                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T01:05:34.414732553Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Revalidated against compat.process-integration v2, on a cycle the dispatcher ordered rather than one this node's own bump stamped for itself.\n\nWHY THE CYCLE, and why sync alone would not do it. `an-interrupt-between-a-recipes-commands-is-lost` bumped the rule from unversioned to +2 while this node had been Done for weeks, so its evidence read `{id compat.process-integration, version 0}` against a rule at version 2 and the node went Stale. Three `nplan sync` runs applied nothing and `nplan start` refuses a Stale node — the no-reopen invariant working as designed. The precedent is `make-ninja-narration`, which carried its evidence across `make.narration`'s bump to +1 without ever leaving Done: a genuine re-verification, a note recording it, and then a commit that NAMES the node, because evidence is rebuilt from the commit that names it and a sync with no such commit to read has nothing to rebuild from (d645a55 says so in as many words). That is the shape followed here.\n\nWHAT THE RULE NOW CLAIMS, and what was checked for each. The base sentence is unchanged — command spawning, working directories, inherited environment, signal and interrupt handling, jobserver participation, child exit interpretation and terminal ownership match Ninja. The +2 text adds three clauses about an interrupt and names one bounded departure. Each was probed FRESH at this tree against upstream Ninja and GNU Make 4.4.1 directly, on a 32-core host, all through `scripts/sandboxed`, every tool exec'd through `perl -e '$SIG{INT}=\"DEFAULT\"; exec @ARGV'` — a background job inherits SIGINT ignored and GNU Make keeps an inherited ignore, so without that GNU finishes the build and says nothing. The signal goes to the tool alone, never to the process group.\n\n* \"The same signal to the same groups, and the same chance to take it.\" A recipe whose trap RECORDS the signal rather than dying of it — `trap 'echo yes > caught' INT` — says whether the signal actually arrived:\n\n| tool | exit | signal to exit | recipe caught the signal | output withdrawn |\n| GNU make 4.4.1 | 130 | 29,998 ms | NO | yes |\n| upstream ninja | 130 | 15 ms | yes | yes |\n| Ronin ninja mode | 130 | 22 ms | yes | yes |\n| Ronin make mode | 130 | 16 ms | yes | yes |\n\nRonin matches Ninja and departs from GNU here, which is the rule as written: GNU relies on the terminal signalling the whole foreground process group and forwards nothing when it alone is signalled, where Ninja and Ronin signal the recipe's group themselves.\n\n* \"No edge that was running is reported as finished or recorded, and every output it had already changed is withdrawn.\" A recipe that DECLINES the signal, so what stops it is the build or nothing:\n\n| tool | exit | signal to exit | `[N/M]` finished lines after the interrupt | build-log entries |\n| upstream ninja | 130 | 20,071 ms | 0 | no log written |\n| Ronin ninja mode | 130 | 121 ms | 0 | 0 |\n| Ronin make mode | 130 | 141 ms | 0 | 0 |\n\n* \"No further command is launched, including the next command line of a recipe the front end gave several of.\" A two-line recipe interrupted inside the first: `second-line-ran` absent for GNU (19,994 ms) and for Ronin (120 ms) alike.\n\n* THE BOUNDED DEPARTURE is visible in the same two tables and is exactly what the rule describes: upstream Ninja blocks in `waitpid` for the declining recipe — 20,071 ms and 29,998 ms, which are the recipes' own sleeps to the millisecond — where Ronin stops what is still standing in 121 to 141 ms. What it does is identical; how long it takes is not, and that is the whole of the departure.\n\nEVIDENCE, all re-run at this tree rather than quoted from the bump:\n* ninja conformance — 425 of 425 pinned upstream tests across 33 suites, 10 CLI/tool differentials, 86 build-outcome cases each on its recorded exit status, 3 invocation-boundary cases, the Ninja → Ronin → Ninja persistence round trip, and build-log content plus dry-run inertness.\n* `cargo test --workspace` 985 passed / 0 failed, which includes every one of the 15 `subprocess::` and 2 `signal::` unit cases this rule's implementation sites carry, and the eight cases of `tests/interrupts.rs`.\n* All 26 annotations claiming the rule read `+2` — six implementation sites across `src/build.rs`, `src/cli.rs`, `src/main.rs` and `src/subprocess.rs`, and twenty verify sites across `src/subprocess.rs`, `src/cli/runtime_options.rs`, `tests/cli.rs` and `tests/interrupts.rs`. Nothing is left at v0.\n\nWHAT MOVED SINCE THE BUMP, and it is why this cycle is worth more than a restamp. `make-mode-leaves-an-interrupt-with-2-where-both-references-say-130` landed between the bump and this verification: Make mode used to leave an interrupt with 2 where both references say 130, and now leaves 130 by reading `BuildStop::Interrupted` rather than the exit code. That is squarely inside this rule's \"signal and interrupt handling ... match Ninja\", so the evidence recorded here is evidence about a tree in which that clause is true — which it was not on the day the rule was bumped. Five of the twenty verify sites are the cases that landing added or tightened, and three of them are in the new `tests/interrupts.rs` suite.\n\nNO CHANGE WAS NEEDED to the implementation. The rule at +2 describes what the code already does, and the two defects this verification did surface — an interrupted `-q` answering zero, and a read-phase interrupt waiting out its `$(shell)` — are filed against their own nodes rather than folded in here, because neither is this rule's subject: the first belongs to `make.question-status`, which owns `-q` and says nothing about interrupts, and the second is the compiler's responsiveness rather than the build's."
                        after_done true
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T01:13:56.97836975Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "ADDENDUM to the revalidation above: the cycle found one clause of the rule with no always-on verification, and closed it.\n\n`+2` says an interrupted edge is \"not reported as finished OR RECORDED\". Those are two places — the report is the `[N/M]` progress line, the record is the build log — and only the first had an assertion. `an_interrupt_stops_an_outliving_recipe` checked the narration for `[1/1]` and stopped there, so a build that stopped narrating an interrupted edge while still logging it would have passed. It is the half a caller feels rather than reads: a recorded edge is treated as up to date by the next build.\n\n`tests/interrupts.rs::an_interrupt_stops_an_outliving_recipe` now also asserts the build log holds no entry for the interrupted output, and `a_finished_edge_is_recorded_in_the_log` is its control — a build left alone records its edge, so the negative assertion is a statement about the interrupt rather than about a log Ronin never writes.\n\nTEETH, measured against a real tree rather than a hand-parked one: `14c6c2c`, the parent of `4e6849a` — the commit that stopped an edge outliving the interrupt — built in a throwaway worktree and run on the same manifest:\n\n| tree | exit | signal to exit | `output` left standing | build-log entries for `output` |\n| 14c6c2c (before `4e6849a`) | 130 | 19,991 ms | YES | 1 |\n| HEAD | 130 | 124 ms | no | 0 |\n| upstream ninja | 130 | 19,999 ms | no | 0, and no log written at all |\n\nSo the assertion would have caught the defect `4e6849a` fixed, and it is not the same statement as the narration assertion beside it. The parked binaries and the worktree were deleted after the measurement.\n\nThis is the same shape the precedent found: `9ee428b` revalidated `make.manifest-equivalence` at v1 and discovered its added clause \"had no always-on verification\", then added one. A rule bump that only ever gets a restamp is a bump nothing checks."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

