                        title "Stop encoding conversion failure as an out-of-range index"
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-07-30T23:48:28.759141087Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @note
                                text "Four sites in the dependency-log reader encode a failed conversion as an out-of-range value and rely on a later bounds check to reject it: src/deps.rs:387 computes size as usize::try_from(encoded_size & 0x7fff_ffff).unwrap_or(usize::MAX), and lines 399, 402 and 436 do the same for output_id, the dependency_ids iterator and the trailing record identifier, each caught downstream by a comparison against nodes.len() or MAX_RECORD_SIZE. This is the C idiom of returning a sentinel and letting the caller's range test sort it out. On every target this crate supports, u32 to usize is infallible, so the fallback is unreachable defensive code for an impossible case, and expressing it as usize::MAX means a genuine future failure would be indistinguishable from a corrupt record whose index merely happens to be large. Make the impossibility explicit instead — the conversion is widening on all supported targets — while preserving the existing validation behaviour exactly: a corrupt log must still be detected and must still trigger the same recovery path, since .ninja_deps parsing is part of the Ninja compatibility surface [spec:ronin:req:compat.ninja-owned-names] and the persistence round trip is covered by the pinned conformance oracle. Purely an expression change; no measurement expected or needed, but the conformance suite must be run because this touches log recovery."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-30T23:58:48.23201281Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
