                        text "Kati 6831eb6 preserves the identity GNU Make assigns to each target-pattern candidate. Second-expanded prerequisites are cached by the candidate's unique definition order and requested output, so two patterns from one rule retain their distinct stems and duplicate patterns retain distinct side-effect epochs. Pattern-rule replacement now happens while rules populate the live list: the new rule's prerequisites must match in GNU's stored dependency-name order, and one new target must equal every target of the old rule. The old Arc's candidates are removed from the trie and the new rule is appended; query-time exact-vector suppression is gone. GNU Make 4.4.1 recordings cover the rejected a%.x candidate followed by the successful %.x stem ab, and a later %.x %.y rule replacing an earlier %.x rule. Before the fix those were the only 2 failures among 277 owned cases; after it all 277 agree. Verification under scripts/sandboxed: nplan check green; kati clippy with warnings denied green; 386-run Make conformance green; direct/manifest equivalence green over 296 makefiles and 272 compared graphs. The first workspace run hit the already tracked ronin-manifest-mtime-test-margin race; its exact warm rerun passed and the complete workspace rerun passed 664 tests with 1 ignored. The real-project gate still stops at the separately recorded explicit duplicate build/browse_py.h."
                    }

