                    {
                        id make-modern-function-evaluation
                        title "Evaluate let, intcmp, and builtin names reached through call"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:28.817043523Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns function-let, function-let-recursive, function-intcmp, and function-call-builtin. Implement GNU Make 4.4.1 let/intcmp semantics and dispatch a builtin function named through call, including recursive let bindings; the upstream intcmp unsupported-feature row is a confirmed member rather than generic residue."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:02.535492661Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:11:02.389849611Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:11:33.6523695Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle before anything moved. Three functions, and the surprising cells are what decided the shape.\n\n`$(let names,list,body)` (function.c func_let, FT_ENTRY 3/3/EXP=0). Each name but the last takes one word; the last takes *the remainder of the string*, not a rejoined word list. `$(let a b,  1   2   3  ,+$(a)+$(b)+)` is `+1+2   3  +`: leading whitespace comes off the remainder and everything inside and after it stays. Names past the end of the list bind to the empty string rather than going unbound. An empty name list is not an error — `$(let ,1 2,body)` expands the body. Bindings are `o_automatic` (`$(origin)` says `automatic`, `$(flavor)` says `simple`) and unwind when the body finishes, so an outer `a` means what it meant again.\n\n`$(intcmp lhs,rhs[,lt[,eq[,gt]]])` (2/5/EXP=0). Parsing is `parse_textint`: optional whitespace, one optional sign, digits, optional whitespace. Leading zeros are dropped and a zero has no sign, so `$(intcmp 007,7)` is `7` and `$(intcmp -0,0)` is `0` — the two-argument form answers with the canonical spelling of the value, not with what was written. The comparison is the surprising one: sign, then digit count, then memcmp of the digits, with no negation for a negative pair. `$(intcmp -5,-6,lt,eq,gt)` is `lt` and `$(intcmp -10,-9,lt,eq,gt)` is `gt` — GNU orders two negatives by magnitude. It is also arbitrary precision by construction: 23-digit values compare correctly. Replicated exactly, quirk included, because the oracle is the specification. Arm defaulting: less takes lt; equal takes eq; greater takes gt if gt was given *and* eq was given, else eq — so `$(intcmp 3,2,lt,eq)` is `eq` and `$(intcmp 3,2,lt)` is empty. Only the chosen arm expands.\n\n`$(call NAME,...)` where NAME is a builtin. `func_call` calls `lookup_function` *before* `lookup_variable`, so a builtin always wins: a Makefile that defines `notdir = SHADOW` cannot reach its own through `$(call)`. Calling nothing is a no-op; `$(call notdir)` is empty rather than an error (`expand_builtin_function` returns early on argc 0), while too few arguments for the function is the same `insufficient number of arguments` it would have refused directly. No maximum-argument clamp applies, because the commas were split by `call`'s unlimited arity — `$(call subst,a,b,banana,ignored)` ignores the fourth. The cell that decided the implementation: `call` expands its arguments first, so a builtin GNU would have handed unexpanded text expands it a *second* time. `$(call foreach,v,1 2,x$(v))` is `x x` (the `$(v)` was consumed before anything was bound) while `$(call foreach,v,1 2,$$(v)y)` is `1y 2y`. `$(call notdir,$$(V))` is the literal `$(V)`.\n\nWhat changed, all in kati/src-rs/func.rs. `FuncInfo` gained `pre_expanded_args`, which is GNU's EXP column and nothing else: every function here expands its own arguments, so it changes nothing about a direct call and decides only what `$(call)` hands over — literal text for an eager function, a re-parsed expression for a lazy one. False for `if`, `and`, `or`, `foreach`, `let`, `intcmp`, and `KATI_foreach_sep` because it is `foreach`. `let_func` binds through the existing `with_bounds`, so a body that fails restores every name at once. `intcmp_func` carries a `MakeInt` that is the parse and the comparison together. `call_func` grew the builtin lookup ahead of the variable lookup, and the GNU no-op for an empty name.\n\nFixtures. The four owned markers are gone: function-let, function-let-recursive, function-intcmp, function-call-builtin all match. Six new cases recorded from the oracle: function-let-binds-the-rest-to-the-last-name, function-let-unwinds-what-it-bound, function-intcmp-reads-sign-and-leading-zeros, function-intcmp-falls-back-to-the-equal-arm (whose lt arm is a `$(shell touch)` that leaves no file, so the laziness is observable as an absence), function-call-hands-a-builtin-expanded-text, function-call-builtin-outranks-a-variable. Three kati unit tests: let_restores_bindings_when_body_fails, intcmp_refuses_a_non_numeric_argument, call_refuses_too_few_builtin_arguments.\n\nUpstream inventory. functions/call.diff, functions/let.diff, .1 and .3 moved unclassified → narration: GNU's own expectations now match line for line and only Ronin's `[N/M]` progress line differs. functions/intcmp.diff.1-4 and functions/let.diff.5-6 stayed unclassified but changed family, ninja-progress+evaluation → product-name+evaluation: those are the error cases, and they now die where they used to evaluate, so what is left is our name and our quoting (`` `intcmp' `` for `'intcmp'`, no `  Stop.` suffix) rather than a different answer. functions/intcmp.diff did not move: it fails on `$(shell echo lt)` inside an arm, which is make-shell-function-evaluation's refusal and not this node's.\n\nTwo defects found and left for follow-ups rather than folded in, both filed under make-subsumption with reproducers: the recursion guard is by name where GNU's is per variable object (reproduces on `foreach` alone, so it predates `let`), and `-e` makes an environment variable's origin `environment override` where GNU says `environment`."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:11:38.150028004Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-filter-percent-escaping
                        title "Preserve escaped percent signs in filter patterns"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:29.042838804Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns function-filter-escaped-percent. A backslash-escaped percent is a literal percent in filter and filter-out patterns, with the remaining wildcard and backslash behavior matching GNU Make."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:02.700515235Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:11:42.808745711Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:12:03.639086166Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "GNU 4.4.1's rule is `find_percent` (read.c:2528), which is `find_char_unquote(pattern, '%')`, and it is a parity rule rather than a \"strip the backslash\" rule. Characterised against the oracle before anything moved.\n\nA run of N backslashes immediately before a `%` collapses to floor(N/2) of them, and the `%` is the wildcard only when N is even. So `foo\\%bar` is the literal name `foo%bar` with no backslash and no wildcard; `foo\\\\%bar` is a literal backslash then the wildcard, matching `foo\\%bar` and `foo\\Xbar`; `foo\\\\\\%bar` is a literal backslash then a literal percent. Verified all three against the oracle.\n\nThe cell that decides where the code goes: GNU stops rewriting the moment it has its answer. `find_char_unquote` returns at the first unescaped `%`, so escaping *after* it is never compressed. `$(filter %x\\%y,ax\\%y aXy ax%y)` is `ax\\%y` — the pattern's suffix is the four characters `x\\%y`, backslash included, and the word that matches carries one. A pattern with several percents keeps only the first as the wildcard: `a%b%c` is prefix `a`, suffix `b%c`.\n\nThe same function runs on the replacement. `$(patsubst abc,\\%,abc def)` is `% def` — the replacement is unescaped even when the pattern has no wildcard at all, because `patsubst_expand` calls `find_percent` on both unconditionally. A substitution reference is the one place it does not: `variable_expand_string` calls `find_percent(replace)` only when the pattern had a percent, so `$(V:abc=\\%)` keeps its backslash (`\\% def`) while `$(V:%c=\\%)` does not (`% def`). And the pattern half is compressed either way — `$(V:\\%=x)` is a suffix substitution on the single character `%`.\n\nWhat changed. `strutil::find_percent` is the rewrite-and-locate pair, and `Pattern::new` is now its only caller, which is where GNU puts it too — `filter`, `filter-out`, `patsubst` and substitution references all construct patterns through it, so one place covers every function that has this question. `append_subst` resolves the replacement through the same function instead of scanning for a bare `%`; `append_subst_ref` still uses the raw replacement on its no-percent branch, which is the asymmetry the oracle showed. The fast path is a `memchr` and returns the original `Bytes` untouched, so a pattern with no escaping copies nothing.\n\nNot done here, deliberately: GNU compresses a *rule's* target and prerequisite names at read time (`find_percent_cached` in read.c), where kati stores them as written. `Pattern::new` now resolves the pattern half of that at use, but a rule target spelled with `\\%` would still have an uncompressed name to match against. No marker owns it and nothing in the corpora reaches it; it is a parser-layer question rather than an evaluator one.\n\nFixtures. function-filter-escaped-percent matches and its divergence file is gone. Two new cases recorded from the oracle: function-filter-halves-backslashes-at-a-percent (the three parities, filter-out, the stops-at-the-wildcard cell, and a two-percent pattern) and function-patsubst-escaped-percent (pattern and replacement escaping, and the substitution-reference asymmetry). Two kati unit tests: escaped_percent_is_a_literal_percent and escaping_stops_at_the_wildcard.\n\nUpstream inventory: functions/filter-out.diff.3, .4 and .5 moved unclassified → narration. Those are GNU's own escaped-percent cases, and the output now matches but for Ronin's progress line."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:12:08.557718165Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-path-function-semantics
                        title "Evaluate suffix and realpath with GNU Make pathname semantics"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:29.271695723Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns function-suffix and function-realpath. suffix examines only the final pathname component, and realpath resolves during Make evaluation to a value rather than leaking a deferred helper command into the compiled recipe."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:02.864863931Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:12:14.087079251Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:12:36.848995249Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Both halves characterised against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle before anything moved.\n\n`$(suffix)` (function.c func_notdir_suffix). GNU scans back from the end of each word for whichever comes first, a dot or a directory separator, and answers only when it found a dot. So a dot inside a directory name names no suffix — `/tmp.c/bar` and `dir.d/file` have none — and the boundary is the same one `$(basename)` takes the stem from. The surprising cells: a name that is nothing but a dotted tail keeps all of it, so `$(suffix .hidden)` is `.hidden` and `$(suffix dir/.hidden)` is `.hidden`; a trailing dot is a one-character suffix, so `$(suffix a.)` is `.` and `$(suffix ..)` is `.`; `./x` has none and `./.x` has `.x`. A word with no suffix contributes nothing at all rather than an empty word, while `$(basename .hidden)` does contribute one.\n\n`$(realpath)` (func_realpath). realpath(3) then stat(2), so resolution is the filesystem's: symlinks followed, `..` and doubled separators collapsed, `//` answered as `/`. A word that names nothing — a missing path, or a symlink with no target — leaves the output entirely rather than appearing as an empty word, so `$(words $(realpath d/f nosuch d/sub))` is 2. `$(abspath)` was probed alongside and already agrees on every cell tried (`//`, `///a`, trailing slashes, `.`, a nonexistent path), so nothing was changed there and no marker asked for it.\n\nWhat changed. `strutil::get_ext` now stops at a directory separator, which makes it the exact complement of `strip_ext` (that one already stopped there, so the pair had disagreed about what a name's suffix was). `$(suffix)` reads it, and so does dep.rs's suffix-rule lookup, where the old answer for `dir.d/file` was the nonsense suffix `.d/file`; GNU converts suffix rules to pattern rules at read time and would never have matched that either, so the fix is in the same direction on both paths.\n\n`realpath_func` no longer checks `avoid_io`. Upstream kati deferred `$(realpath)` reached from a recipe into `$(<self> --realpath ... 2>/dev/null)` for the recipe's shell to run — a different value at a different time, and in Ronin it did not even run, because the compiled command carried the text inside single quotes and the literal `$( ... )` landed in the output. GNU resolves where it reads, and `$(wildcard)` in this same file already answers during evaluation for exactly that reason; realpath now follows the precedent. `rkati --realpath` is left in place: nothing emits that command line any more, but it is a working mode of the binary and removing a CLI surface is a different question from this one.\n\nFixtures. function-suffix and function-realpath both match and their divergence files are gone. Two new cases recorded from the oracle: function-suffix-reads-the-last-component-only (the dotted-tail, trailing-dot and directory-dot cells, with `$(basename)` beside them for the shared boundary) and function-realpath-resolves-links-and-drops-strays. The realpath case is written against `$(CURDIR)` through `$(filter)` rather than emitting absolute paths, because the harness runs each case in a scratch directory whose path is not stable; its dangling symlink is named `.dangling` so the harness's directory listing skips it, since `fs::read` of one is an error rather than a file. One kati unit test: suffix_stops_at_a_directory_separator.\n\nUpstream inventory: functions/suffix.diff, functions/realpath.diff.1 and .2 moved unclassified → narration. GNU's own suffix expectations now match line for line and only Ronin's `[N/M]` progress line differs."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:12:41.838244462Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id make-shell-function-evaluation
                        title "Compile shell function values in the evaluation environment"
                        kind @fix
                        deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:08:29.496249638Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Owns function-shell and variable-curdir. Shell-backed evaluation must produce its value before graph construction, with the evaluation working directory represented correctly so CURDIR comparisons and recipe-context shell expansion do not retain literal command substitution."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-11T10:11:03.038813395Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "sealed — decomposition asserted complete (nplan seal)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:43:34.849576515Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:45:44.617495297Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle before anything moved, then the refusal it was owning was traced to a decision that belongs to the destination rather than to kati.\n\nWhat kati was doing. `avoid_io` is set for the whole of command evaluation, and under it `$(shell)` never runs: at `eval_depth <= 1` the call is written back out as `$(cmd)`, a shell command substitution for the generated command's own shell to answer, and at `eval_depth > 1` — anywhere the value would reach another Make function — it is a hard error, `doesn't support passing results of $(shell) to other make constructs`. The one exception is `has_no_io_in_shell_script`: an empty command, or `echo $((...))`, which Android needs arithmetic from. Outside a recipe nothing defers. So the map was: read time runs, recipe top level defers, recipe nested refuses. `function-shell` was the first (`printf '[%s]' '$(shell echo there)' > out` gave `[$(echo there)]`, because a command substitution inside single quotes is not one), `variable-curdir` and `functions/intcmp.diff` were the second.\n\nThe deferral is not a defect. It is correct when the destination is a build.ninja another program will run later, which is what kati was built for. It is wrong when the destination runs the build itself. So it became `BuildSink::shell_evaluation`, beside the `new_inputs_timing` that already splits the same way for `$?`: a manifest writer keeps `RecipeShell`, Ronin's `GraphSink` asks for `Expansion`, and `NinjaWriter` and the equivalence `Tee` keep the default, so the manifest and the graph it is compared against still agree.\n\nThe GNU matrix, all verified rather than assumed. `func_shell_base` hands `construct_command_argv` a zero for cmd_flags, so `$(shell)` runs `$(SHELL)` with `.SHELLFLAGS` — `SHELL := /bin/echo` makes `$(shell hello world)` answer `-c hello world`, which shows both at once — and `.POSIX:` keeps its `-e` because no `COMMANDS_NOERROR` ever arrives. Environment: `target_environment(NULL, 0)`, whose NULL means `current_variable_set_list` rather than no scope at all. The brief's expectation was that target-specific exports would not be visible; the oracle says they are. `all: export TS = t` reaches a `$(shell)` in `all`'s recipe, and reaches one in a prerequisite's recipe too, because recipe expansion happens with the file's variable set installed. MAKELEVEL arrives incremented, SHELL is the environment's rather than Make's, and MAKEFLAGS is the run's switches (` -j4` under `-j4`) with the jobserver auth invalidated. Output: interior newlines become spaces, `\\r` is dropped only when a `\\n` follows it, the whole trailing run goes for `$(shell)` and all but one of it for `!=`, and a NUL ends the output because GNU Make reads it as a C string. `.SHELLSTATUS` is an `o_override` variable, unset before the first call, 127 for a command that was not there, and 128 plus the signal for one that was killed; a failing `$(shell)` is not a Make error and the run still exits 0. Timing: `:=` runs it once at read, `=` at every expansion, a function arm only when the arm is taken, and a recipe line's during expansion — so its side effect lands before any of the commands that line became. `$(shell $(MAKE) ...)` sees MAKELEVEL 1 and no usable jobserver; Ronin's child sees MAKELEVEL 0, which is the environment gap that belongs to make-export-scope-semantics rather than here.\n\nThree cells of that matrix Ronin also got wrong, all in what comes back rather than in who runs it, and all fixed here because they are one GNU function: `fold_newlines` is now translated exactly, including the CRLF rule and the NUL, and a signalled command reports 128 plus the signal where it used to report 1.\n\nMarkers. function-shell and variable-curdir are gone. function-shell-sees-exported-variable stays: the environment a `$(shell)` child gets is make-export-scope-semantics's, and this node did not touch it.\n\nSeven fixtures recorded from the oracle: function-shell-composes-inside-a-recipe, function-shell-runs-before-the-recipe-line, function-shell-folds-line-endings, function-shell-output-stops-at-a-nul, function-shell-status-after-a-signal, function-shell-uses-shell-and-shellflags, function-shell-runs-once-for-a-simple-value. Four kati unit tests: the two halves of the fold, and one test per side of the sink's choice.\n\nUpstream inventory, 1287 rows, five moved and none the wrong way. `functions/intcmp.diff` compiler → narration: the `$(shell echo lt)` arm was this node's refusal, as the previous dispatch said, and only the progress line is left. `functions/shell.diff.4` and `.16` unclassified/interface → narration, an exported variable read through a recursive value in a recipe and `.SHELLSTATUS` after a SIGINT, both now right. `functions/shell.diff.14` lost `ninja-failure-block`: a `$(shell)` naming a missing program used to be written into the recipe and fail the build, and now it runs, reports 127 and lets the build finish. `features/reinvoke.diff.2` is the known flapper and contains no `$(shell)` at all, so its move cannot be this change.\n\nThree defects found and left as follow-ups under make-subsumption rather than folded in: make-lazy-recipe-expansion (Ronin expands every reachable recipe at graph construction, so an up-to-date target's `$(shell)` side effect now fires anyway — the eagerness predates this and reaches `$(wildcard)` too, but answering `$(shell)` during expansion is what made it observable), make-oracle-recording-trailing-newline (the build-intent recording writes a signal for \"no trailing newline\" that its own reader ignores, so such a case can never match), and make-simple-command-without-a-shell (GNU Make execs a command with no shell syntax directly, so it reports a missing program itself where Ronin reports what /bin/sh said)."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-13T20:46:50.919257137Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

