            {
                id make-remaking-ignores-goal-switches
                title "Remake makefiles whatever -n, -q, -t and -B ask of the goals"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-included-makefile-restarts)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T15:17:05.423911277Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Opened from make-included-makefile-restarts, which characterised the matrix and left this cell divergent.\n\nGNU Make disables -t, -q and -n while it brings the Makefiles up to date and restores them for the goals (remake.c update_goal_chain saves touch_flag/question_flag/just_print_flag and zeroes them unless the Makefile was also named on the command line), and it turns -B off after the first restart so an always-remade Makefile cannot loop (main.c:1702, always_make_flag = always_make_set && restarts == 0). Probed against 4.4.1: `make -n` really writes the generated include and then prints the goal recipe it would run; `make -q` really writes it and then answers about the goals with status 1; `make -n gen.mk` restores -n for gen.mk because it is a command-line target and prints `'gen.mk' is up to date`.\n\nRonin builds the compiler inputs through the ordinary scheduler with the invocation's own options, so -n describes the Makefile instead of making it and stops before the goals, and -q answers about the compiler inputs instead of the goals. Two recorded reproducers carry `divergence` markers naming this node: tests/make/feature-dry-run-still-remakes-a-makefile and tests/make/feature-question-still-remakes-a-makefile.\n\nThe work is not simply clearing dryrun for that build: regeneration targets mix Makefiles with the prerequisites staged for a recursive $(MAKE) unit, and `recursive-dry-run-writes-nothing` says the second kind must stay dry. Loaded::remake_targets already separates the two, so the shape of the fix is two builds, or one build over the Makefile subset with its own options. -B and -t are separately recorded as discovery-only cases (always-make-option, touch-option), so this node should say what, if anything, they mean here."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T15:40:51.448631438Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T15:49:01.681219027Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU 4.4.1, probed cell by cell before any code moved.\n\n`-n` writes the generated include for real and then prints the goal recipe it would run; `-q` writes it for real and then answers about the goals with status 1; `-t` runs the Makefile's recipe (not a touch — the probe's `gen.mk: gen.in ; printf ran >> log; cp $< $@` leaves `ran` in the log and the copied text in gen.mk) and then touches the goals; `-B` remakes an already-current include once and no more, because `always_make_flag = always_make_set && (restarts == 0)` (main.c:1702) turns it off after the first restart, which is what stops it looping forever. In every one of the four the switch stays visible in MAKEFLAGS through both reads — probed with `$(info ...)` — so nothing about the value the Makefile sees changes; only what the update does.\n\nThe exception GNU makes for itself is `file->cmd_target`: a Makefile the command line also named keeps the invocation's own switches (remake.c update_goal_chain saves touch/question/just_print and zeroes them only for the rest). Probed: `make -n gen.mk` prints the recipe, writes nothing, and says `'gen.mk' is up to date`; `make -t gen.mk` touches gen.mk to zero bytes rather than running its recipe; `make gen.mk` with no switch makes it, restarts, and reports it up to date once.\n\nWhat changed. src/make/cli/remake.rs is now two phases over one graph rather than one build over the union. `Loaded::staged_targets` names the other half — the prerequisites staged so a recursive $(MAKE) unit can be evaluated — and it keeps the invocation's own options, so `recursive-dry-run-writes-nothing` still writes nothing and `-q` over staged work still answers about it. The Makefiles go through `Passes::remake_makefiles`, which splits them again: the ones the command line named build under the invocation's options (and under `-q` are not built at all, since GNU records the question and carries on), and every other one builds with dryrun and verbose cleared. The goals list had to be threaded in separately: the `invocation` reaching this point is reparsed from the evaluated MAKEFLAGS and carries switches with no goals at all, which is why the first attempt at the cmd_target rule silently matched nothing.\n\n`-t` and `-B` need nothing here and are recorded as needing nothing: neither reaches Ronin's scheduler at all — they are accepted interface switches whose goal-stage behaviour is the discovery-only touch-option and always-make-option cases — so remaking was never suppressed for them and there is no loop for the `restarts == 0` rule to stop. If either is ever implemented, its implementation has to arrive with the remake-boundary exception, and this note is the record of what that exception is.\n\nFixtures. The two recorded reproducers, feature-dry-run-still-remakes-a-makefile and feature-question-still-remakes-a-makefile, now match and their `divergence` files are deleted. Two new cases recorded from the pristine 4.4.1 oracle: feature-dry-run-is-restored-for-a-makefile-goal (`make -n gen.mk` — nothing produced) and feature-question-is-answered-after-the-makefiles-are-made (`-q` where the goal is current once the Makefile has been made — gen.mk written, status 0). Build-intent corpus 356 cases, all passing; workspace 716/0."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T15:49:06.16272595Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-optional-include-rule-failure
                title "Read on when an optional include's rule fails"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-included-makefile-restarts)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T15:17:15.22539692Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Opened from make-included-makefile-restarts, which reached this cell while characterising Makefile remaking and found it already divergent before that change.\n\n`-include` says the file may be absent, and GNU Make carries that indifference into the rule that would have made it. read.c records the goaldep with RM_DONTCARE; remake.c inherits `dontcare` into the update, and main.c reports `Failed to remake makefile` only for a Makefile it cares about. Probed against 4.4.1: `-include gen.mk` with `gen.mk: ; printf 'gen ran\\n' > log; exit 1` runs the recipe, the recipe fails, and make goes on to build `all` and exit 0.\n\nRonin builds the compiler inputs through the ordinary scheduler and a failed input stops the build, so the goals are never reached and the run fails. Recorded reproducer with a `divergence` marker naming this node: tests/make/feature-optional-include-rule-failure-is-tolerated.\n\nkati already carries the required/optional distinction on MissingInclude and drops an optional include with no rule; what it does not carry across the compiler-input boundary is that an optional include's *failure* is tolerable. A required include is different and must keep failing."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T15:49:31.749822592Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T15:56:23.52664662Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU 4.4.1, probed before anything moved.\n\n`-include gen.mk` with `gen.mk: ; printf 'gen ran\\n' > log; exit 1`: the recipe runs, it loses, and make says *nothing at all* — stderr is empty, there is no `Failed to remake makefile` and no `*** [Makefile:3: gen.mk] Error 1` — then builds the goals and exits 0. The same shape with `include` instead prints `Makefile:2: gen.mk: No such file or directory` and `make: *** [Makefile:3: gen.mk] Error 1` and exits 2. An optional include that is *present but stale* whose refresh rule fails is the same forgiveness: the recipe runs, loses silently, and the goal is built from the text already read (`v=old`).\n\nThe cell that was not in the node when it was filed: a failed optional update does not start the read again either, however the recipe left the file. Probed with a rule that writes `V := built` into gen.mk and then exits 1 — GNU reads once, `MAKE_RESTARTS` stays empty and `$(V)` stays empty, though gen.mk is on disk holding the new text. main.c is explicit about why: in the `us_failed` arm `any_remade` is raised from `file_mtime_no_search != makefile_mtimes[i]` only when `d->file->update_status == us_success`, and the DONTCARE branch beside it raises nothing.\n\nWhat changed. kati 69aea4c: `Evaluator::read_makefiles` is now `Vec<ReadMakefile>` rather than `Vec<Symbol>`, carrying whether any read of that file said it had to be there — raised by `include` and by the Makefile the invocation named, never lowered by a later `-include`. `plan_regeneration` returns `RegenerationRoot { node, required }`, so the answer travels with the root instead of being re-derived. Ronin: `Loaded::forgiven_remake_targets` is the subset, and `Makefiles::subsets` splits the remake phase into the three groups GNU treats differently — command-line-named (invocation's own switches), forgiven, and everything else. A forgiven pass runs with `maxfail: usize::MAX` because GNU considers every Makefile it read before deciding anything, and one it does not need cannot stand in another's way; a lost pass returns `Pass::Lost`, which keeps Ninja's account of the command that really ran and really lost, drops the failure, and skips the pass's own stamp comparison — which is where GNU's `us_success` condition lands.\n\nKnown approximation, deliberately not fixtured: Ronin's scheduler answers per pass, not per file, so a forgiven pass in which one optional Makefile is remade and another's rule loses does not restart the read where GNU would. `maxfail: usize::MAX` at least means every one of them is attempted. A single optional Makefile — which is every recorded case and the `-include $(DEPS)` shape in the wild — is exact.\n\nFixtures. feature-optional-include-rule-failure-is-tolerated now matches and its `divergence` file is deleted. Three new cases recorded from the pristine 4.4.1 oracle: feature-optional-include-failure-does-not-restart-the-read (gen.mk written by the losing recipe, `v= restarts=`, status 0), feature-optional-include-keeps-its-text-when-its-rule-fails (`v=old`, status 0), feature-required-include-rule-failure-stops-the-build (log written, no `out`, outcome failure — the guard that the forgiveness is only ever `-include`'s). Build-intent corpus 359 cases, all passing; kati 112/0; workspace 716/0; kati clippy `-D warnings` clean."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T15:56:28.217626507Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-remake-work-not-repeated
                title "Keep remade Makefile work done when the goals reach it"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-included-makefile-restarts)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T15:17:27.80126465Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Opened from make-included-makefile-restarts, whose delivery made this reachable.\n\nGNU Make brings the Makefiles up to date and then builds the goals inside one process, so a file it already updated is not considered again: remake.c's update_file returns early on a file whose `updated` flag is set. Ronin runs the two phases as two scheduler invocations over the same graph, and dirtiness is recomputed from scratch for the second. For an ordinary rule that costs nothing — the file is current and nothing runs — but a Makefile whose remake rule is always out of date runs its recipe once in each phase.\n\nRecorded reproducer with a `divergence` marker naming this node: tests/make/feature-remade-makefile-is-not-remade-again-for-the-goals. `gen.mk: force` with `force` phony appends one line to `log` under GNU Make and two under Ronin.\n\nAlso visible in the upstream suite: features/include.diff.37 (GNU Make's sv 63484 case, \"included makefiles are not intermediate\") moved narration to unclassified for exactly this reason — Ronin echoes the pattern recipe twice and then rebuilds test.x and test, where GNU Make echoes once and reports `test` up to date. That row is the movement to watch when this node lands.\n\nBuildGraph::mark_subgraphs_prebuilt is the existing machinery for \"work this invocation already did\", used by recursive staging, and is the obvious starting point. Note it is not sufficient on its own: it rewrites an edge's rule to phony but does not clear the always-dirty flag kati derives from is_phony and unconditional_double_colon, which is what keeps this chain dirty."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T16:00:19.940892717Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T16:23:51.71021572Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU 4.4.1, probed before anything moved, and the probes decided the shape of the fix.\n\n`gen.mk: force` with `force` phony and a recipe that appends to a log and leaves gen.mk alone: GNU runs it once and reports `'gen.mk' is up to date` for the goal — `update_file` returns early on a file whose `updated` flag is set (remake.c:458). Two further probes settled what \"already updated\" does and does not stop:\n\n  `copy: gen.mk` with copy newer than gen.mk — `make: 'copy' is up to date`. The dirtiness of `force` does *not* travel through the remade Makefile; what a reader compares against is the Makefile's timestamp.\n\n  `other: force` where `force` has its own recipe — the log reads `force ran / gen ran / other ran`. `force` does not run a second time (it was updated during remaking), but `other` is still remade, because a phony prerequisite is out of date to everything that names it however recently it ran.\n\nSo the marking is two different things, and the fix is `BuildGraph::mark_makefiles_remade`: `mark_subgraphs_prebuilt` over the closure, which is the existing \"no command runs twice\" and leaves always-dirty alone, plus, for the Makefile roots themselves, clearing always_dirty and detaching the edge's inputs so the file on disk is the answer. Applied in `build_compiler_inputs` when the remake phase settles, over exactly the Makefiles whose update was reached and won — a forgiven pass that lost contributes none of its targets, so a goal that really needs an unmakeable optional include still fails.\n\nFixtures. feature-remade-makefile-is-not-remade-again-for-the-goals now matches and its `divergence` file is deleted. Two new cases recorded from the pristine 4.4.1 oracle, one for each half: feature-remade-makefile-timestamp-decides-its-readers (log holds one `gen ran`, copy kept) and feature-remade-phony-prerequisite-still-drives-other-targets (log holds `force ran / gen ran / other ran` — one force, and `other` still built). Both passed against Ronin on the first run after the change. Build-intent corpus 361 cases, all passing; workspace 720/0.\n\n`make-prerequisite-free-rule-currency` is untouched: its five cases (target-prerequisite-free-rule-is-up-to-date, target-commented-prerequisites-leave-no-inputs, target-double-colon-prerequisite-free-in-question, target-question-rule-runs-for-a-newer-prerequisite, target-equal-timestamps-keep-a-question-rule) are in the 361 and none of them remakes a Makefile, so nothing here reaches them. The double-colon-no-prerequisite always-runs rule is untouched for the same reason plus a positive one: always_dirty is cleared only on a Makefile regeneration root, and `is_remakable` refuses both a `.PHONY` Makefile and an unconditional `::` one, so a root can never be either.\n\nUpstream inventory. Six of 1362 rows moved and every one was audited against a rebuilt beb364f binary in a fresh suite worktree. Class counts unclassified 570 → 566, compiler 39 → 38, narration 584 → 589, interface 169 unchanged.\n\n  features/include.diff.37 (sv 63484, the row this node predicted) unclassified → narration: before, Ronin echoed the pattern recipe in the remake phase and again for the goals and then built test.x and test; now it echoes once and has nothing left to do. GNU's `make: 'test' is up to date` against Ronin's `ronin: no work to do.` is all that is left.\n  features/reinvoke.diff.8 and .10 unclassified → narration, same cause: `printf hello.d` ran in both phases and now runs once.\n  variables/undefine.diff.4 (sv 59870) unclassified → narration, same cause reached differently: `%: undefine` gives the Makefile itself a rule, so it becomes a regeneration root and `echo undefine` ran twice. This does not fix make-pattern-rule-cancellation — Ronin still honours the recipe-less pattern rule GNU ignores, which is why the Makefile has a rule at all — it only stops the resulting work being done twice.\n  features/include.diff.5 (sv 13216, \"we don't die when the command fails but we dontcare\") compiler → narration: make-optional-include-rule-failure. Before, `ronin: 'bar', needed by 'foo', missing and no known rule to make it`; now the optional include is forgiven and `all` runs.\n  options/dash-n.diff.5 unclassified → unclassified: make-remaking-ignores-goal-switches. Before, `-n` described `touch inc`; now it runs it, as GNU does. What still differs is the `$(MAKE) -f … bar` line GNU prints because it starts the child to learn what it would do, which is the recorded recursive-dry-run classification.\n\n  features/reinvoke.diff.2, the row noted as flapping within its class, did not move."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T16:23:56.53456572Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-restart-count-is-not-a-compilation-input
                title "Keep the restart count out of a recursive child's identity"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T16:24:55.417979537Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while running the real-project gate at the end of the make-remaking-ignores-goal-switches / make-optional-include-rule-failure / make-remake-work-not-repeated dispatch. Pre-existing and bisected, not caused by any of those three.\n\nReproducer: `scripts/check-make-project.sh` — Ninja's own CMake-generated Unix Makefiles at -j8. At d645a55 it builds ninja and ninja_test in 21s. At 59155b8 (make-included-makefile-restarts) and every commit since, including beb364f which this dispatch started from, it dies with `ronin: manifest 'Makefile' dirty after 100 tries` after running the same `cmake -E cmake_depends … libninja-re2c.dir/DependInfo.cmake` a hundred times. Both binaries rebuilt from clean worktrees and run against the same build directory.\n\nMechanism, instrumented: each pass reports `remakes=0 staged=1 boundaries=1 settled=N` with N rising by one every pass. No Makefile is being remade at all — the loop is the recursive-composition boundary never settling. `record_invocation_variables` records MAKE_RESTARTS into `session.invocation_environment` (cli.rs:1609), `compilation_context` copies that environment into `CompilationContext::environment` (cli.rs:1651), and `extend_compilation_key` hashes the parent's whole environment into every recursive child's cache key (subninja.rs:160, `append_environment_key`). `EvaluationBoundary` is keyed on that compilation key, so every restart mints a fresh boundary and `settled_boundaries` can never contain the one the next pass asks about. CMake's `CMakeFiles/<t>.dir/all` recipe holds two `$(MAKE)` lines, so it always has a ChildGroup(0) boundary, and the depend step restages and reruns forever.\n\nGNU's answer, probed against 4.4.1: MAKE_RESTARTS is `v_noexport`, and a sub-make sees nothing — `$(MAKE_RESTARTS)` is empty in the child and `$$MAKE_RESTARTS` is empty in its recipes, while the parent's own second read sees `1`. So the count is not part of what a child is, and it must not be part of what identifies one.\n\nRonin already matches GNU on the observable side (the same probe gives the same empty answers), so this is only the compilation key — but that is the whole defect."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T16:25:02.471015291Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T16:33:58.097130264Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Fixed in src/make/cli.rs: `compilation_context` now builds the descendant environment through `descendant_environment`, which drops `MAKE_RESTARTS`. One line of behaviour, and the reason is GNU's: the count is `v_noexport` there, so a child never sees it — probed, and Ronin already agreed on the observable side. Where Ronin disagreed was in identity, because a recursive child's compilation key hashes the environment it inherits and an `EvaluationBoundary` is keyed on that compilation key.\n\nReproducer, now a recorded case: feature-restart-keeps-recursive-staging-done. A root Makefile with a `-include` it has to make (so the read restarts once) whose goal runs one sub-make, and that sub-make's goal runs two more (so there is a ChildGroup boundary inside a *child* unit, whose key carries the parent environment — a boundary inside the root unit is keyed on directory, makefile and MAKEFLAGS alone and never showed the defect). GNU Make writes `first` then `second` to the log. beb364f writes `first` a hundred times and dies with `manifest 'Makefile' dirty after 100 tries`; with the fix it writes `first` then `second`.\n\nThe real-project gate, which is what found this, goes from that same hundred-try death to `built ninja 1.14.0.git from its own Makefile in 20s at -j8`.\n\nGates on the fix: build-intent corpus 362 cases, all passing; workspace 720/0; conformance 387 runs, 301 normalised identical / 86 differing, inventory unmoved; equivalence 297/276/0; upstream inventory unmoved at 1362 rows (unclassified 566, compiler 38, interface 169, narration 589); Ninja conformance 425 upstream + 10 differential + 86 build-outcome + 3 invocation-boundary, persistence and log-content; ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery clean, kati `-D warnings` clean."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T16:34:02.840662654Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-optional-include-failure-is-per-file
                title "Forgive one optional include's failure without forgiving its neighbours"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-optional-include-rule-failure)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T16:38:48.003491395Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Opened from make-optional-include-rule-failure, which delivered the forgiveness and recorded this as the one place it is coarser than GNU Make.\n\nGNU Make decides per Makefile. In main.c's `us_failed` arm it walks `read_files` and raises `any_remade` from `file_mtime_no_search (d->file) != makefile_mtimes[i]` for each entry whose own `update_status == us_success`, so a batch in which one optional include is remade and another's rule loses restarts the read for the first and says nothing about the second.\n\nRonin builds the forgiven Makefiles as one scheduler pass and gets one verdict for the whole pass, so `Passes::run` forgives all of them together and `remake_makefiles` skips the pass's stamp comparison entirely. The pass runs with `maxfail: usize::MAX`, so every one of them is still attempted — what is lost is only the restart the successful one should have caused. A single optional include, which is every recorded case and the `-include $(DEPS)` shape in the wild, is exact; two or more where one wins and one loses is not.\n\nNo reproducer recorded yet, because writing one means deciding what the fix is: either a per-target verdict out of the scheduler, or the forgiven Makefiles built one pass each (correct, and O(n) scheduler runs for `-include $(DEPS)`, which is the reason it was not done). Probe GNU for the mixed case first — two `-include`s, one rule that wins and one that loses — and record what MAKE_RESTARTS and the included values come to."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T13:04:48.078823508Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered per-file, without the O(n) scheduler runs the node feared.\n\nProbed the mixed case first, as the node asked. GNU, with `-include one.mk` and `-include two.mk` and `$(info)` reporting `MAKE_RESTARTS` on each pass:\n\n  one wins, two loses    two passes. restarts=[1], ONE=yes, TWO empty. two's rule is attempted AGAIN on the second pass, loses again, is forgiven again, and no third pass. exit 0\n  one loses, two wins    same shape mirrored: restarts=[1], one re-attempted and re-forgiven\n  both win               two passes, restarts=[1]\n  both lose              ONE pass. restarts empty. Both attempted, both forgiven, exit 0\n  rule writes the file then exits 1   NO restart, single or alongside a sibling failure — the file is on disk and the read still stands\n\nThat last cell is the one that decides the implementation. `main.c`'s `us_failed` arm raises `any_remade` from `file_mtime_no_search(d->file) != makefile_mtimes[i]` only for entries whose own `update_status == us_success`; a `RM_DONTCARE` entry that failed contributes nothing however it left the disk. So comparing every stamp on a lost pass would have been wrong, and the verdict really is per file: its own recipe ran and won, and its mtime moved.\n\nShape delivered: the forgiven Makefiles still build as one scheduler pass. What changed is that the pass now reports which targets it lost over. `Builder` records `failed_edges` where a failure is settled, `Outcome::unmade()` names the requested targets whose own edge is among them, `Pass::Lost` carries that list out, and `remake_makefiles` compares the stamps target by target, skipping the ones that lost. A target whose command never ran because a prerequisite failed is deliberately not in the list — nothing was tried and nothing moved, so the mtime test answers correctly on its own.\n\n`Remaking::Settled` narrowed to match its own documentation: it now carries the targets the update reached and won, rather than the whole subset (which is what it carried when every target won) or none of it (which is what a lost pass used to contribute).\n\nThree fixtures: optional-include-restarts-though-another-failed, two-failed-optional-includes-do-not-restart, optional-include-written-by-a-failing-rule-does-not-restart. The existing forgiveness and restart fixtures are unchanged and green, including feature-optional-include-rule-failure-is-tolerated, feature-one-restart-for-several-remade-includes and feature-makefile-rule-that-leaves-the-file-alone-does-not-restart.\n\nOne adjacent difference found while probing and filed rather than fixed: with `-include one.mk` and `all: one.mk`, GNU refuses the goal with `No rule to make target 'one.mk', needed by 'all'` after the forgiven attempt, where Ronin re-runs the rule and fails on it. Both exit 2; the wording and the second attempt differ. Pre-existing, unchanged by this work. See make-a-forgiven-makefile-is-not-remade-for-the-goals."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T13:23:38.276525695Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-signal-killed-recipe-deletion
                title "Delete a target whose recipe died by a signal"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T17:26:31.373537539Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while completing make-delete-on-error-cleanup, and deliberately not folded into it: the mechanism is a different one.\n\nGNU Make deletes a target whose recipe died by a signal whether or not the Makefile said `.DELETE_ON_ERROR`. `job.c` reaps the child and asks `if (exit_sig != 0 || delete_on_error) delete_child_targets (c)` — the switch is one of two independent reasons, and the signal is the other. `.PRECIOUS` and `.PHONY` still protect, because the deletion goes through the same `delete_target`.\n\nReproducer, no `.DELETE_ON_ERROR` anywhere:\n\n    all: out\n    out: ; echo body > $@ ; kill -TERM $$$$\n\nGNU Make 4.4.1 prints `make: *** [Makefile:2: out] Terminated` and removes `out`. Ronin prints `FAILED: [code=143] out` and keeps it.\n\nWhy Ronin cannot see it today. GNU Make runs the recipe line as its own `sh -c`, so the shell that `$$` names is the child it waits on and a signal death is visible in that child's status. Ronin's recipe is already a `/bin/sh -c \"…\"` command line built by kati, and the executor launches commands through a shell of its own, so the process Ronin waits on is the outer shell — which exits 143 perfectly normally after its inner shell dies. `command_interrupted` therefore never fires, and `status_interrupted` would have recognised SIGTERM if the status had carried one. Ronin has the machinery (`Withdraw::Everything` in `finish_edge` already removes changed outputs on interrupt); what it lacks is the signal.\n\nSo this is not a delete-on-error question. It is a question about what the executor can observe of a recipe's death, and any fix has to change that — surface the inner status, or stop adding the outer shell where the command is already a shell invocation. That is a shared-executor change affecting the manifest front end too, which is why it wants its own node rather than a corner of a Make compatibility fix.\n\nAdjacent and worth settling in the same node: `.PRECIOUS` is not honoured on Ronin's interrupt path. `withdraw_outputs(edge, .., Withdraw::Everything)` removes every changed output of a running edge when the build is cut short, while GNU's fatal-signal cleanup runs `delete_target` per file and so skips precious and phony ones. The eligible-output list this node's work put on the graph is already the thing an interrupt-path fix would consult."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T16:31:08.696986522Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T16:57:11.619913396Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle before anything moved, and the node's central doubt — that Ronin \"cannot see\" a recipe's signal death — turned out to be fixable exactly where the node guessed, by not adding the shell that hides it.\n\nThe observation question first, because it decides everything else. A shell reports a child killed by a signal as `128 + signal` and then exits normally; `/bin/sh` is dash here and does not re-raise. Probed through a launcher, with the inner shell killing itself:\n\n  one shell   `sh -c 'kill -TERM $$'`               waited-on process is SIGNALLED(15)\n  two shells  `sh -c \"sh -c 'kill -TERM $$'\"`       waited-on process EXITED(143)\n  two shells  `sh -c \"sh -c 'exit 143'\"`            waited-on process EXITED(143)\n\nSo the node's \"read 128+sig from the outer shell's exit\" is available and is wrong, because the last two cells are the same number and GNU Make gives them opposite answers: it deletes after the first and keeps after the second. The only exact channel is to stop interposing the launcher, which is what `exec` does — the recipe's shell replaces it, one process where GNU Make has one, and `WIFSIGNALED` is the recipe's own. `cd` is a builtin and has to run before the replacement; `env` replaces itself with what it runs, so `exec` goes between them.\n\nThe deletion matrix, every cell probed and every cell now agreeing:\n\n  signal, output written          deleted, `*** Deleting file 'out'`\n  exit 143, no signal             KEPT — `Error 143`, the discriminating cell\n  exit 1                          kept\n  signal + .PRECIOUS              kept\n  signal + .PHONY                 kept\n  signal, output never written    kept — `delete_target` compares mtimes\n  signal, output rewritten        deleted\n  SIGINT / SIGKILL                deleted, `Interrupt` / `Killed`\n  signal, sibling file written    only the target goes\n  signal under -k                 the build CARRIES ON; a signalled recipe is a failed one\n  signal + ignored errors         kept, exit 0\n  grouped `&:`, one member precious   that member kept, its peer deleted\n  multi-target pattern rule, precious pattern   same, per name\n\nTwo reasons and not one, which is `job.c`'s `if (exit_sig != 0 || delete_on_error)`. Both reach `delete_target`, and that is the function that refuses precious and phony names and compares the timestamp — so the eligible set is a single set with two independent reasons to want it. Carrying it as \"the outputs .DELETE_ON_ERROR named\" made the switch look like the reason a name was eligible. It is now `withdrawable_outputs` beside a `delete_on_error` bit, recorded whatever the Makefile said, read per Makefile unit so a recursive Make declaring it does not answer for a parent that did not.\n\nThe interrupt path was the node's second half and had two defects, not one. `Withdraw::Everything` passed no filter, so Ctrl-C took precious and phony names GNU spares — fixed by the same table, since a graph with no entry is a manifest front end that left the question alone and keeps Ninja's answer. The second was found while probing and was not in the filing: the build loop breaks on the first `Interrupted` and every other running edge is killed by the supervisor's teardown without anyone withdrawing its outputs, so a sibling's half-written file outlived the build. GNU says it in one loop — `fatal_error_signal` walks the whole child list calling `delete_child_targets` — and this now does too. Probed with two parallel recipes and a real SIGINT to the process group: GNU keeps the precious one and deletes the other, Ronin did neither before and does both now.\n\nWhat changed. kati: `Node::delete_on_error_outputs` is `withdrawable_outputs` and is filled whatever the switch said, `mark_delete_on_error` is `mark_withdrawable_outputs` and has lost its early return, `SinkEdge` carries both halves, and the manifest writer emits `command = exec …`. Ronin: `graph/withdrawal.rs` holds a `Withdrawal { outputs, on_error }` and distinguishes no entry from an empty one, `Withdraw::Everything` is `Withdraw::Stopped` because it is no longer everything, `command_interrupted` asks whether THIS process was signalled before reading a child's signal death as the build being cut short, a signalled recipe reports `128 + signal` and withdraws as stopped, and `CommandLayout::prefix` writes the `exec`. `needs_shell` learned that `exec` is the shell's business.\n\nReported status: `[code=143]` for a SIGTERM-killed recipe, the same number as before the collapse, because 128+signal is what the launcher used to report. Nothing in the emitted numbers moved.\n\nTwelve fixtures recorded from the oracle, all passing: feature-signal-killed-recipe-removes-the-target, -spares-a-precious-target, -spares-a-phony-target, -keeps-a-target-it-did-not-write, -removes-a-target-it-rewrote, -leaves-other-files-alone, -removes-the-target-under-kill, -spares-one-member-of-a-group, -does-not-stop-keep-going, -with-ignored-errors-keeps-the-target, feature-interrupt-killed-recipe-removes-the-target, feature-an-exit-status-that-looks-like-a-signal-keeps-the-target. That last one is the discriminator: it fails against any fix that reads 128+N from a launcher. The -k one is the pin that a signalled recipe is a failure and not an interrupt.\n\nGates. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace 803 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `-D warnings` clean, both fixed structurally rather than allowed — the manual `Option::map` became one, and the fourth bool on `PendingSubninja` became a `PendingWithdrawal` value. make_port green over the whole corpus, including GPATH's fourteen and the .DELETE_ON_ERROR matrix. Make conformance 387 runs, 318 normalised identical / 69 differing, unmoved. Graph equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 compared / 0 disagreements. Upstream inventory 1287 rows with zero drift: 171 unclassified, 1 compiler, 41 interface, 1074 narration, every count unchanged. The `targets/clean` abort was investigated rather than assumed, since this node changes signal handling: it is still there, still aborting in the perl driver, and its row is still `narration ninja-progress`. Real-project gate builds ninja 1.14.0.git.\n\nAdjacent and not chased: Ronin exits 130 where GNU Make dies by the signal it caught, so `$?` after a Ctrl-C differs. That is Ninja's contract, pinned at `docs/spec/ronin/compatibility.md:77-86`, and deliberately untouched."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T17:00:47.599557658Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-double-colon-keep-going-continues-the-chain
                title "Continue a double-colon chain past a failed entry under keep-going"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-double-colon-rule-freshness)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T17:58:29.782423312Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Reproducer, with n1 and n2 newer than out:\n\n  out:: n1\n  \t@echo R1 >> log\n  \t@false\n  out:: n2\n  \t@echo R2 >> log\n\n`make -k out` under GNU Make 4.4.1 writes log=R1,R2; Ronin writes log=R1 only.\n\nGNU Make's `update_file` walks the double-colon chain and returns early when an entry is still running, but on a failed entry it only abandons the rest of the chain when `keep_going_flag` is clear (reference/gnumake/src/remake.c, the `if (new && !keep_going_flag) return new;` in the chain loop). So under -k the chain continues past a failure.\n\nPre-existing rather than introduced by make-double-colon-rule-freshness: before that node the whole chain compiled into a single action whose recipes were joined with `&&`, so a failing first recipe stopped the rest inside one shell. After it, each record is its own action and the chain order is expressed as an order-only graph edge, so a failed entry leaves its successors unreachable. The observable outcome is the same in both designs; only the mechanism moved.\n\nFixing this needs a scheduler-level edge meaning \"wait for the predecessor to finish, run regardless of its status\", which Ninja's model has no spelling for. That makes it graph/scheduler representation work of the same kind as make-grouped-double-colon-actions, not a rule-compilation change."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:08:30.057551726Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, by building the third edge kind the earlier note said was missing.\n\n`src/graph/forgiven.rs` records a set of `(consumer, order-only input)` pairs whose wait holds but whose status is discarded. Forgiveness is on the relation, not on either end, which is what makes the rest fall out: the same action is still an ordinary dependency of the target's completion join, so the chain runs on but the target is still not remade. kati carries it as `DepNode::forgiven_order_onlys` and hands it to a sink over `BuildSink::forgiven_order_only_inputs`; a sink that writes `build.ninja` drops it, because a Ninja manifest has no spelling for the idea.\n\nOnly the `-k` behaviour changes. Without it the failure exhausts the allowed-failure budget, dispatch stops, and the released successor never starts — the same outcome as before, and the same as GNU Make's.\n\nFour corpus cases, all matching GNU Make 4.4.1 byte for byte: a chain continuing past a failed entry under `-k`; an entry running after a failed *prerequisite* under `-k`; the same chain stopping without `-k`; and a grouped double-colon chain continuing past a failed record, which is the case that proves the edge is per-record rather than per-target."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:10:16.294316845Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-terminal-pattern-rule-needs-an-existing-prerequisite
                title "Apply a terminal pattern rule only when its prerequisite already exists"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T17:58:45.099494517Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while characterising ordinary double-colon freshness; unrelated to it. On a pattern rule the double colon means \"terminal\", not \"one independent rule per record\": a terminal rule's prerequisites are never made by another implicit rule, so the rule applies only when they already exist.\n\nReproducer, GNU Make's own suite (tests/scripts/features/double_colon, sv 60188 subtest 1), with hello.z present and test.x absent:\n\n  all: hello.z\n  %.z:: test.x ; touch $@\n  %.x: ;\n\nGNU Make 4.4.1 prints \"Nothing to be done for 'all'.\" — %.x cannot supply test.x for a terminal rule, so %.z:: does not match. Ronin runs `touch hello.z`.\n\nRonin reaches this through the pattern-rule search rather than through a RuleMerger, so make-double-colon-rule-freshness neither caused nor touched it: that node's split is keyed on an explicit merger holding more than one record. The case is one of the three features/double_colon rows still recorded unclassified in tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:03:36.931538471Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Upstream-residue triage (2026-08-14) attributes features/double_colon.diff.16 to this node, in the `refusal-not-made` family — Make refused the run and Ronin built anyway, which is the direction this node exists to correct.\n\n  all: hello.z\n  %.z:: %.x; touch $@\n  %.x: ;\n  unrelated: hello.x\n\nGNU refuses: `No rule to make target 'hello.x', needed by 'hello.z'`. Ronin runs `touch hello.z`.\n\n`%.z::` is terminal, so its prerequisite `hello.x` has to exist already; it may not be invented by the `%.x: ;` rule below. Ronin applies the terminal rule anyway and then satisfies its prerequisite by chaining, which is the two halves of this node's claim failing together.\n\nWorth reading against `make-match-anything-rule-chaining`, filed by the same triage: that node widens the implicit search to chain through `%:` rules, and this one is the constraint that has to be in place first or the widening will take cases like this one with it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T08:07:51.367468989Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Probed from make-match-anything-rule-chaining (2026-08-14) and left open: that node's compatibility pass does not cover this one, and did not move it in either direction. Both reproducers behave identically before and after it — `touch hello.z` in each case. What the probing did settle is where the two halves live in GNU's source, which should save this node the search.\n\nHalf one, the intermediate pass. `pattern_search` rejects a terminal rule outright when it is looking to make intermediate files: reference/gnumake/src/implicit.c:478, `if (intermed_ok && rule->terminal) continue;`. That is the whole of the sv 60188 reproducer. `%.z:: test.x` fails the strict pass because test.x is neither on disk nor written down anywhere, and it is never offered the second pass, so hello.z ends up with no rule at all and GNU says `Nothing to be done for 'all'.` Ronin has no such rejection: `pick_pattern_rule` in kati/src-rs/dep.rs is handed a `SearchPass` whose `chaining` flag is GNU's `intermed_ok`, and a `::` pattern rule is admitted on both passes.\n\nHalf two, what the chosen rule does to its prerequisite. implicit.c:1044 — when the found rule is terminal and the prerequisite was not itself an invented intermediate, the prerequisite is stamped `tried_implicit = 1`, which bars it from any implicit search of its own. That is the whole of double_colon.diff.16: `hello.x` is written down on `unrelated:`, so the compatibility pass now hands it to `%.z:: %.x` exactly as GNU does, and GNU then refuses `No rule to make target 'hello.x', needed by 'hello.z'` because the terminal rule forbade `%.x: ;` from being consulted for it. Ronin reaches hello.x with an ordinary search and makes it.\n\nSo the constraint and the widening are independent after all: the widening was delivered without taking this cell with it, and this node is still both halves.\n\nNeighbouring cell confirmed against the oracle, for whoever takes this: a terminal match-anything rule IS allowed to make an intermediate, which is what makes half one specifically about `intermed_ok` and not about terminality alone. `all: foo.out` over `%.out: %` and `%:: %.y` with foo.y on disk builds `foo`, uses it and removes it again on both tools — recorded as tests/make/feature-implicit-rule-terminal-match-anything-makes-an-intermediate."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T09:42:00.712693162Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Half one landed in make-builtin-rule-catalogue (2026-08-14), not by choice: the catalogue's five terminal rules cannot be installed without it. This node stays open on half two.\n\nWhy it was forced. `%: %,v` and its four siblings each match every name there is. With no restriction on which pass may use them, `foo` was made from a `foo,v` the search invented, which needed a `foo,v,v` it also invented, and so on — `build_plan` recursed until the stack ran out. tests/make/include-remade-by-a-multi-target-pattern-rule aborted on it, which is how it was found.\n\nHalf one, exactly where this node's last note said it was. implicit.c:478, `if (intermed_ok && rule->terminal) continue;` — a terminal rule is never offered the pass that invents prerequisites, so it applies only to what is already there. In kati/src-rs/dep.rs that is two places, because the pass is explicit at one and implicit at the other: `pick_pattern_rule` skips a double-colon candidate when `pass.chaining` is set, and `implicit_chain_exists` requires a terminal candidate's prerequisites to exist rather than recursing for them.\n\nThe sv 60188 reproducer now agrees. `all: hello.z` over `%.z:: test.x` and `%.x: ;` with hello.z present: both tools say there is nothing to do.\n\nThe neighbouring cell this node recorded is unmoved, which is what confirms half one is about the pass and not about terminality. `all: foo.out` over `%.out: %` and `%:: %.y` with foo.y on disk still builds `foo`, uses it and removes it on both tools — tests/make/feature-implicit-rule-terminal-match-anything-makes-an-intermediate still passes. A terminal match-anything rule is still allowed to be a link in a chain; it is simply never allowed to invent what it reads.\n\nHalf two is untouched and is the whole of what is left. implicit.c:1044 stamps `tried_implicit` on a prerequisite a terminal rule found, barring any implicit search of its own. features/double_colon.diff.16 still diverges: `all: hello.z` over `%.z:: %.x`, `%.x: ;` and `unrelated: hello.x`. The compatibility pass hands `hello.x` to the terminal rule exactly as GNU does, and GNU then refuses `No rule to make target 'hello.x'` because the terminal rule forbade `%.x: ;` from being consulted for it. Ronin reaches hello.x with an ordinary search and makes it, so it runs `touch hello.z` where GNU stops."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T13:40:11.177699144Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T14:49:16.314452159Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle across the whole terminal matrix, and half two turns out to be one stamp with three separate observable consequences rather than the one row that named it.\n\n  cell                                                     GNU 4.4.1\n  `%.z:: test.x`, test.x on disk                           applies\n  `%.z:: test.x`, test.x absent and written nowhere        does not apply — half one, already in\n  `%.z:: test.x`, test.x absent with an explicit rule       applies, and that rule makes it\n  `%.z:: %.x`, hello.x written on an unrelated rule         applies, then REFUSES hello.x\n  `%.z:: %.x`, hello.x written nowhere at all               no rule for hello.z either\n  `%:: %,v` with a `,v` checked in                          checks it out\n  `%:: %,v` with nothing checked in                         no rule at all\n  hello.z reached before `other`, both wanting hello.x      refuses\n  `other` reached before hello.z, same Makefile             builds all three\n  a terminal prerequisite present but behind its own source NOT remade\n\nThe last three are the point. `tried_implicit` is not a rule about the prerequisite, it is a rule about when the prerequisite was reached, and GNU Make's answer to the same Makefile changes with the order of the goals. Swapping `all: hello.z other` for `all: other hello.z` turns a refusal into a build, because in the second the `%.x:` rule reaches hello.x first and gives it a recipe, and the bar the terminal rule sets afterwards has nothing left to bar. That is not an accident of GNU's implementation to be tidied away — it is what `remake.c:555` asking `!file->tried_implicit` means when the update walk is what sets the flag.\n\nAnd a terminal rule's prerequisite that IS there is stamped just the same, which is the cell no reproducer had reached: `%.z:: %.x` over `%.x: %.y` with hello.x on disk and older than hello.y leaves hello.x alone and makes hello.z out of the stale contents. GNU is not choosing not to bother; it has forbidden itself from looking.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, all in kati and all in the search. `implicit_prerequisites_reachable` returned the names it was inventing and now returns how each prerequisite was had: `invented` for the ones a chain search made up, `found` for the ones that existed or that the compatibility pass took on trust. That is GNU's own division, kept in one array where a `patdeps` carrying a file is the first kind and one without is the second, and it is only ever read after a rule is chosen. `can_pick_implicit_rule` then stamps `found` into a new `tried_implicit` set when the chosen rule is terminal, and `pick_rule` skips the pattern search for a stamped name — the other half of the condition it already had for `.PHONY`, which is the same line of `remake.c`.\n\nThe order-dependence needed nothing at all. `build_plan` picks a target's rule before recursing into its prerequisites and memoises on `done`, so a stamp laid while choosing hello.z's rule is read when hello.x is planned afterwards and is moot when hello.x was planned before. That is GNU's update walk with the same shape, and both goal orders agree without either being written down.\n\nMarker: the node owned no divergence marker of its own; its evidence was upstream. `features/double_colon.diff.16` is now `narration product-name+fatal-decoration+shared-refusal` where it was `compiler ninja-progress+fatal-decoration+refusal-not-made` — both tools refuse the same thing and word it differently. It was the only case row in the `refusal-not-made` family, so that family is empty and the compiler bucket falls from 2 to 1. The one left is `features/vpath.diff.1`.\n\nFixtures, six recorded from the oracle, all gating: terminal-pattern-rule-does-not-let-a-rule-make-its-prerequisite (the upstream row), -leaves-an-existing-prerequisite-alone (the stale-but-present cell), -bars-a-prerequisite-reached-through-it-first and -takes-a-prerequisite-already-reached (the two goal orders, written as a pair so the ordering is asserted rather than assumed), -takes-a-prerequisite-with-a-rule-of-its-own (the bar is on searching, not on making), and builtin-terminal-rule-refuses-a-file-that-is-not-checked-in beside the checkout case that was already there.\n\nBoth halves of this node are now in. Nothing about half one moved: tests/make/feature-implicit-rule-terminal-match-anything-makes-an-intermediate still passes, so a terminal rule may still be a link in a chain and still may not invent what it reads."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T14:51:43.062730629Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-default-goal-suffix-rejection
                title "Pass over a suffix-named target when choosing the default goal"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-default-goal-variable-semantics)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T18:42:43.672973977Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while characterising .DEFAULT_GOAL against GNU Make 4.4.1, and deliberately left out of that node: it needs a suffix list Ronin does not have.\n\nGNU Make's `check_specials` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c, the default-goal block) passes over a target on two more grounds than the leading-dot one Ronin now implements. A target whose name is exactly one of `.SUFFIXES`' prerequisites, and a target whose name is two of them concatenated, are both rejected — and the search moves on to the next target rather than stopping.\n\nReproducer, recorded as tests/make/variable-default-goal-suffix-name with a divergence file naming this node:\n\n  .SUFFIXES: foo bar\n  foo:    ; @printf 'foo ran\\n'    > foo.out\n  foobar: ; @printf 'foobar ran\\n' > foobar.out\n  zed:    ; @printf 'zed ran\\n'    > out\n\nGNU Make 4.4.1 builds `zed`: `foo` is a declared suffix and `foobar` is `foo` followed by `bar`. Ronin builds `foo`.\n\nWhy it was not done with the rest. The check reads `.SUFFIXES`' prerequisites as they stand at the moment each target is recorded — probed: `.SUFFIXES: foo` after the `foo:` rule does not reject it, and a bare `.SUFFIXES:` in between clears the list and re-admits it. Ronin's evaluator does not track that list at all; dep.rs reads suffix rules later, and Ronin has no built-in suffix list where GNU starts with thirty-odd entries. So a faithful check needs the list tracked through evaluation *and* the built-in defaults installed, which is the missing-Make-builtins gap. Half of it is worse than none: with only the Makefile's own declarations, a target named `foo.c` under `.SUFFIXES: foo` would still be chosen where GNU rejects it as `foo` + the built-in `.c`.\n\nReachability is the reason this is not urgent: with GNU's default list, every entry begins with a dot, so any name the suffix check could reject already begins with a dot and is rejected by the leading-dot rule first. It bites only where a Makefile declares a suffix that does not begin with a dot."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T09:41:43.947863747Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @unblock
                        text "Unblocked by make-builtin-rule-catalogue (2026-08-14) and deliberately left open: the thing this node was waiting for exists now, and none of its own work is done.\n\nWhat it was blocked on was a built-in suffix list, and there is one — `.SUFFIXES` carries GNU's 35 entries from the bootstrap, and `DepBuilder::read_suffix_list` settles what the whole read left. The note's worry about half a list being worse than none is therefore gone: a target named `foo.c` under `.SUFFIXES: foo` can now be rejected as `foo` followed by the built-in `.c`, which is exactly what GNU's read.c:1953 loop does.\n\nWhat remains is this node's own work, and it is not covered. The check runs at read time, in the evaluator, against `.SUFFIXES` as it stands at the moment each target is recorded — `Evaluator::record_default_goal` in kati/src-rs/eval.rs, which today rejects only a leading dot. dep.rs's list is settled far too late for it, so the evaluator needs to replay the `.SUFFIXES` rules it has accumulated in `ev.rules` the same way read_suffix_list does, honouring the clears in order.\n\nTwo details from GNU's loop for whoever takes it: the single-suffix rejection is guarded by `*dep_name(d) != '.'`, so only a suffix that does not begin with a dot rejects a target of its own name; the two-suffix concatenation check has no such guard, which is what makes the built-in list matter.\n\nIts gated reproducer is unmoved. tests/make/variable-default-goal-suffix-name still carries its divergence file and still diverges the same way: GNU builds `zed`, Ronin builds `foo`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T13:48:25.237422523Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T13:48:45.043460756Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Taken alongside make-suffix-shaped-target-with-prerequisites, which is the same subject read from the other end, and characterised against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle cell by cell.\n\nWHAT GNU DOES. `check_specials` (read.c, the default-goal block) rejects a candidate on two grounds beyond the leading dot, and then considers the next target of the same rule rather than stopping — probed: `foo zed:` under `.SUFFIXES: foo bar` builds `zed`.\n\nThe two tests are not symmetric and the asymmetry is the whole rule.\n\n  The single-suffix test is guarded by `*dep_name(d) != '.'`. Only a suffix written without a dot rejects a target of its own name, so a declared `.foo` does not reject a target named `.foo` — reachable only through a name carrying a slash, since a bare `.foo` is rejected for its leading dot a line earlier.\n\n  The two-suffix test has no such guard, and that is what makes the built-in list matter. `foo.c` under `.SUFFIXES: foo` is the declared `foo` followed by the built-in `.c`, and is passed over. The same makefile under `-r` — no built-in list for `foo` to join to — chooses `foo.c`. That pair is the evidence the earlier note asked for and both halves are fixtures.\n\n  Both loops range over the whole list, so a suffix joins to itself: `foofoo` under `.SUFFIXES: foo` is rejected.\n\nWHEN THE LIST IS READ. As the read stands, which is why this could not be done where the rest of `.DEFAULT_GOAL` was. `suffix_file->deps` is consulted at the moment each target is recorded: a `.SUFFIXES:` below the rule has not happened yet, one above it has, and a bare `.SUFFIXES:` in between clears the list and re-admits what it had rejected. All three probed.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT, in kati b1fc99f on branch nplan/make-default-goal-suffix-rejection. `Evaluator::record_default_goal` gained the check. The list is replayed from `self.rules` — the rules read so far, in order, a bare `.SUFFIXES:` clearing and every other one extending — because the shape of the answer is the shape of the replay and only the order says what is on it. `DepBuilder::read_suffix_list` does the same replay once the read is over, and cannot serve here: by the time dependency analysis settles the list the goal is long since chosen. `.SUFFIXES` became a well-known symbol so the replay costs no interning; it runs only while the goal is unset and only for a candidate that survived the leading-dot test.\n\nThe pure half is a free function with a unit test on the asymmetry, because the asymmetry is invisible in any makefile that does not declare an undotted suffix and nothing else in the file would notice it going wrong.\n\nWHAT CLOSED. tests/make/variable-default-goal-suffix-name carried this node's divergence file since it was recorded; it now agrees with the oracle and the file is deleted. Seven more fixtures were recorded for the cells around it: -suffix-declared-after-the-rule, -suffix-list-cleared-before-the-rule, -suffix-name-joined-to-a-builtin, -suffix-name-joined-to-a-withheld-builtin, -suffix-name-joined-to-itself, -suffix-rejection-tries-the-next-target, -dotted-suffix-does-not-reject-its-own-name.\n\nThe earlier note's worry that half a list is worse than none is settled rather than argued: the built-in list has been there since make-builtin-rule-catalogue, and the `-r` pair shows the check reading it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T13:52:08.1721427Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-private-binding-keeps-the-public-value
                title "Unwind a private and public binding of one name as a single nesting"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-private-variables make-pattern-specific-variable-application)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T11:59:44.677669891Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T12:00:12.195725577Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Re-verified failing at 22b3366 before implementing, as the handoff asked: the errata's reproducer still gave dep9:[outer] where GNU gives dep9:[pub]. The three scope reworks since (LIFO guards, VarExport/target_environment, the bindings stack) had not reached it.\n\nProbing GNU first was what made the fix right, because the errata's own sketch — unwind the run and lay the surviving public bindings down again — is wrong on a cell the errata did not have. The model is not a stack of bindings with the private ones removed. It is `initialize_file_variables` (src/variable.c:591) building a chain of exactly two sets in front of the parent's:\n\n    file->variables       the target's own name bound it       next_is_parent = 0\n    file->pat_variables   every matching pattern, accumulated  next_is_parent = 1\n    parent's chain / global_setlist\n\nEach is a hash table, so a name bound twice inside one set keeps only the last binding — the pattern loop is `do { v = define_variable_loc(...) } while ((p = lookup_pattern_var (p, ...)))`, every iteration into the same set. `lookup_variable` (src/variable.c:459) then walks:\n\n    v = hash_find_item (set->table, &var_key);\n    if (v && (!is_parent || !v->private_var))\n      return v;\n    is_parent |= setlist->next_is_parent;\n\nTwo things fall out, and both are cells a flat model gets wrong. A private binding is *stepped over*, not stopped at: the loop carries on to the next set rather than hiding the name. And `private` is answered per set, on whichever binding the set is left holding — so an earlier public binding overwritten by a later private one in the same set is simply gone.\n\nThe matrix, probed against the oracle with a global V = outer, target q.p, prerequisite dep9, all now fixtures:\n\n  pattern private, target public         q.p:[pub]      dep9:[pub]\n  target private, pattern public         q.p:[priv]     dep9:[pub]      steps over\n  two patterns, public then private      q.p:[privpat]  dep9:[outer]    last in the set decides\n  two patterns, private then public      q.p:[pubpat]   dep9:[pubpat]\n  both private                           q.p:[privtgt]  dep9:[outer]\n  pattern private only                   q.p:[priv]     dep9:[outer]\n  target private only                    q.p:[priv]     dep9:[outer]\n  neither private                        q.p:[pubtgt]   dep9:[pubtgt]\n\nRonin had the first, third and fourth wrong. The errata's sketch would have fixed the first and left the third: keeping every public binding lets a public one show through the private one that overwrote it.\n\nFixed in kati/src-rs/dep.rs. `scopes_for` now returns a `RuleScopes` — patterns and the target's own scope kept apart rather than concatenated into one `Vec` — so a binding can be tagged with the set it came from as it is installed. `apply_rule_vars` records one ordered `Vec<RuleBinding>` carrying the guard, the set, the symbol, the value and the private flag, in place of the two lists it kept before. `release_private` takes the run, reduces it to one surviving binding per name per set, walks own-then-pattern skipping private, unwinds the whole run LIFO and lays down only what the walk found. `ScopedVar::scope` was added so a guard can say which scope to install into again.\n\nSecond expansion reads the same scopes flattened, `private` and all, which is what it should: it is the target's own expansion, so `is_parent` is never set.\n\nTwo fixtures recorded: private-variable-pattern-and-target-are-one-nesting (the errata's case and its mirror) and private-variable-last-in-a-pattern-set-decides (the two multi-pattern cells). The prerequisite names in the first had to stop matching the patterns — `dep.p` matches `%.p` and reads the private binding out of its own set, which is correct and not what the case is about.\n\nOne adjacent cell found and filed rather than fixed: a target-specific `+=` under a private pattern binding. GNU walks the chain a second time when the variable is expanded (`variable_append`, src/expand.c:511) and applies the same private rule there, so the base differs per reader; Ronin resolves the append once at install time. make-target-specific-append-reads-the-readers-scope has the reproducer."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T12:02:55.140217991Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-shadowed-name-is-not-self-reference
                title "Count a recursive expansion per variable rather than per name"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T20:13:02.096271485Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while completing make-modern-function-evaluation, and deliberately not folded into it: `let` only inherits this, and the reproducer needs nothing but `foreach`, so it predates that node's work.\n\n    y = $(foreach y,z,$ydef)\n    all: ; @echo [$y]\n\nGNU Make 4.4.1 prints `[zdef]`. Ronin prints `ronin: Recursive variable \"y\" references itself (eventually).` and builds nothing.\n\nWhy. GNU's guard is per variable object: `func_call` and the recursive-variable expander set `v->exp_count` on the `struct variable` being expanded, and `func_foreach` pushes a new scope in which `y` is a *different* variable, so looking it up from inside the body reaches an object with no count set. Ronin's guard is per name — `Evaluator::lookup_var_for_eval` (eval.rs) tests membership in `symbols_for_eval`, a `HashSet<Symbol>` — so an automatic binding that shadows the name currently being expanded is indistinguishable from the recursion the guard is for.\n\nThe same shape reaches `let`, which is how it was found: GNU's own tests/scripts/functions/let case 3 has `y = $(let y,,$ydef)` and expects `def`. That row is `functions/let.diff.2` in the upstream inventory, still unclassified after this dispatch. `$(call)` is mostly safe by accident, because its bindings are the numeric positional names.\n\nA fix has to make the guard about the binding rather than the name. The natural move is to carry the mark on the `Var` — the thing `with_bound`/`with_bounds` replaces — so that replacing a binding replaces the mark with it, and `var_eval_complete` clears the one it set. Worth checking against the case the guard exists for while doing it: a genuinely self-referential `a = $(a)` must still be refused."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T10:13:37.873660887Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T10:47:33.195816669Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle before anything moved, and the filing's diagnosis was right: the guard is about the variable, not the name.\n\nThe mechanism. reference/gnumake/src/expand.c recursively_expand_for_file sets `v->expanding = 1` on the `struct variable` around the expansion of its own value, and refuses when it finds that already set:\n\n    if (v->expanding)\n      {\n        if (!v->exp_count)\n          OS (fatal, *expanding_var,\n              _(\"Recursive variable '%s' references itself (eventually)\"), v->name);\n        --v->exp_count;\n      }\n\nThe mark is on the object. `func_foreach` pushes a scope in which the loop name is a different `struct variable`, so looking it up from inside the outer variable's expansion reaches something with nothing set on it. `exp_count` is a budget only `$(call)` grants — function.c sets it to EXP_COUNT_MAX around the body and back to 0 after — which is what makes bounded recursion through `$(call)` legal.\n\nThe matrix, GNU on the left, Ronin before the change where it differed:\n\n  x = $(x)                              refused          agreed\n  x = $x                                refused          agreed\n  a = $(b) / b = $(a)                   refused          agreed\n  x := $(x)                             empty            agreed\n  y = $(foreach y,z,$ydef)              zdef             REFUSED\n  y = $(let y,,$ydef)                   def              REFUSED\n  x = $(foreach x,a b,$(foreach x,1 2,$(x)))   1 2 1 2   REFUSED\n  x = $(foreach x,1 2,\"<$(x)>\")         \"<1>\" \"<2>\"      REFUSED\n  y = $(call id,$ydef)                  refused          agreed\n  z = $(foreach y,inner,$(y)) beside y  inner, y intact  agreed\n  all: v = shadowed, over v = $(v)-tail shadowed         agreed\n  f = $(if $(1),$(call f,),done)        done             agreed\n  f = [$(call f)]                       SIGSEGV          stack overflow\n\nThe `$(call)` cells are the two the dispatch asked about and they answer each other. A terminating recursion through `$(call)` works in both, and kati reaches that without a budget because call_func looks its function up with lookup_var rather than lookup_var_for_eval, so nothing is ever marked. A non-terminating one exhausts GNU's own C stack long before EXP_COUNT_MAX and it core dumps (rc 139); Ronin overflows its stack and aborts. Both die, so there is nothing to match here and no cell was written.\n\n`$(call id,$ydef)` is the cell that shows `$(call)`'s bindings are not shadowing: the argument is expanded in the caller's scope, where `y` is still expanding, so GNU refuses — and the numeric names `$(call)` does bind could not shadow `y` anyway.\n\nWhat changed, all in kati. The evaluator's `symbols_for_eval: HashSet<Symbol>` became `expanding_vars: HashSet<usize>` keyed on the `Var`'s address, which is stable and unique for as long as it is marked because whatever put it there holds the Arc until it takes it out. `var_eval_complete` takes the variable `lookup_var_for_eval` answered with instead of the name it was asked for, and expr.rs drops its read guard before calling it. `expand_for_environment` checks and marks the same way.\n\nTwo things fell out that the filing did not predict. A variable reassigned during its own expansion is a different object afterwards, so `VARIABLE = $(eval VARIABLE := echo hi)$(VARIABLE)` — GNU's own varnesting test for Savannah patch #7534 — now reads back rather than being refused. And the `environment_substituted` Vec, which existed to pair a push and a pop by name when a `$(shell)` environment recursion answered from the invocation's environment, has nothing left to do: that path answers with a binding of its own which was never marked, so removing its identity is already a no-op. It is gone.\n\nUpstream inventory, audited against a base binary built from 049247d with kati at f90f42b in a separate worktree. That binary re-runs the suite and reproduces tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv exactly, exit 0 with no drift, so every move below belongs to this dispatch rather than to flap.\n\nNine rows moved, all toward narration, none the wrong way. 1287 rows throughout; unclassified 182 → 177, interface 47 → 43, narration 1075 → 1084, compiler 7 unchanged.\n\nThis node's two:\n  features/varnesting.diff.1  unclassified product-name+evaluation → narration ninja-progress\n  functions/let.diff.2        unclassified product-name+evaluation → narration ninja-progress\nBoth confirmed by hand against the base binary: base refuses `VARIABLE` and `y` as self-referential, this binary prints `hi` and `[def]` as GNU does. let.diff.2 is the row the dispatch predicted would move, and it moved.\n\nmake-environment-origin-precedes-the-switches' seven, recorded here because the re-record ran after that node's commit:\n  functions/foreach.diff            unclassified → narration   TEST 0, $(origin FOOFOO) under -e\n  functions/let.diff.4              unclassified → narration   the masking test under -e\n  functions/origin.diff             unclassified → narration   TEST 0, $(origin MAKETEST) under -e\n  variables/MAKEFLAGS.diff.112-115  interface    → narration   MAKEFLAGS content under -e, family makeflags-content 44 → 40\n`$(origin FOOFOO)` under -e was checked by hand too: base says `environment override`, this binary says `environment`.\n\nThe three watched flappers — features/parallelism.diff.1, features/patternrules.diff.34, features/reinvoke.diff.2 — held their class and their family list byte for byte. The pre-existing targets/clean SIGABRT is still there.\n\nOwned markers: none. Fixtures recorded from the oracle: tests/make/shadowed-name-is-not-a-self-reference (foreach, let, nested foreach reusing the name, a foreach whose body reads the name it binds, and an outer variable left intact) and tests/make/recursive-variable-referencing-itself (the refusal that must survive, reached through a second variable).\n\nOne test defect seen and filed rather than fixed: make-repaint-budget-test-reads-the-wall-clock."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T10:50:22.111468067Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-environment-origin-precedes-the-switches
                title "Read the environment before -e can change what its origin is"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T20:13:13.717620597Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while completing make-modern-function-evaluation, against GNU's own tests/scripts/functions/let case 5 (the masking test), which runs with `-e` and asks for `$(origin)` of an environment variable.\n\nReproducer, with `FOOFOO` set in the environment:\n\n    all: ; @echo [$(origin FOOFOO)]\n\n`make -e WHITE=WHITE` — GNU Make 4.4.1 prints `[environment]`. Ronin prints `[environment override]`.\n\nWhy it is an ordering question rather than a value one. GNU reads the environment in `main` before it decodes the command line, so `env_overrides` is still zero when each variable is defined and every one of them gets `o_env`. The `-e` switch changes which definition *wins* afterwards, not what the variables say they are; `o_env_override` is reachable only for a variable defined from the environment after the switch has been seen, which for a normal invocation is none of them. Ronin decides the origin with the switch already in hand and so labels the whole environment as overriding.\n\nVisible in the upstream inventory as the second half of `functions/let.diff.4`, which stayed unclassified through this dispatch: with `let` implemented, the remaining difference on that row is this one word plus the progress lines. The case reads `$(origin)` of nine names at once, so it is a ready-made check that fixing the label for environment variables does not move `default`, `file`, `command line`, `override` or `automatic`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T10:02:50.503284939Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T10:12:59.280869461Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle before anything moved. The filing had the direction right and the mechanism only half right: the promotion is real, but it is lazy, and it is what implements the precedence rather than a label beside it.\n\nThe mechanism, in GNU's own words. reference/gnumake/src/variable.c define_variable_in_set, at the top of the branch for a name already defined:\n\n    if (env_overrides && v->origin == o_env)\n      /* V came from in the environment.  Since it was defined\n         before the switches were parsed, it wasn't affected by -e.  */\n      v->origin = o_env_override;\n\nand then the ordinary rank test `if ((int) origin >= (int) v->origin)` declines the write, because o_env_override outranks o_file. The identical pair sits in the undefine path. So the environment is read as o_env, and a variable becomes `environment override` at the moment something tries to redefine it and is refused — never before, and never at all for a name nothing writes to.\n\nThe matrix, with `-e` on the left of the slash and without it on the right, GNU and Ronin now agreeing on every cell:\n\n  in the environment, never written    environment / environment\n  in the environment, assigned in file environment override / file\n  in the environment, `undefine`d      environment override / undefined\n  in the environment, `+=` in file     environment override / file\n  in the environment, `?=` in file     environment / environment\n  in the environment, `override` in file  override / override\n  file only                            file / file\n  command line                         command line / command line\n  `override` directive                 override / override\n  built-in (CC)                        default / default\n  unset                                undefined / undefined\n  automatic ($@)                       automatic / automatic\n  MAKEFLAGS                            environment override / file\n\n`?=` is the cell that proves the promotion is lazy: it does not redefine a variable that is already set, so nothing asks `-e` to decide and the origin stays `environment` even under `-e`. Ronin, which stamped the whole environment up front, said `environment override` there.\n\nPrecedence was checked alongside the label at every cell, since the point of `-e` is which value wins. It is unchanged: under `-e` the environment's value is what `$(VAR)` reads for an assigned name, the `undefine` is declined and the variable keeps its value, and `override` still beats the environment. A command-line assignment still outranks it.\n\nMAKEFLAGS is its own cell and was not in the filing. GNU defines it with `env_overrides ? o_env_override : o_file` (main.c) — it has to, or its own definition would be declined by the promotion. Two consequences, both verified: `$(origin MAKEFLAGS)` is `environment override` under `-e` even when MAKEFLAGS is not in the environment at all, and a makefile writing `MAKEFLAGS += -r` under `-e` is outranked, so the flag never arrives and the built-in rules stay. Ronin said `file` and applied the append.\n\n`-e` arriving through the MAKEFLAGS environment variable rather than the command line was probed as the dispatch asked, and every cell above answers the same way — the flag is decoded before the environment is imported either way.\n\nWhat changed, all in kati. evaluate.rs read_invocation_state stamps `VarOrigin::Environment` unconditionally; install_compiler_invocation_variables defines MAKEFLAGS at `EnvironmentOverride` when `-e` is in force. var.rs gained GlobalVars::note_environment_outranks_the_makefile, which promotes the variable in the slot a write would land in, resolved through the same writable_slot the assignment uses so a binding standing in front of a global does not confuse it. session.rs calls it from set_global_var and undefine_global_var when the flag is set, which is GNU's placement.\n\nOwned markers: none. Upstream inventory row functions/let.diff.4, which the filing named, is re-audited with the rest of the sweep.\n\nFixtures recorded from the oracle, all with `-e` in args and using LC_ALL, which the corpus already treats as the one variable a case may call `environment`: tests/make/origin-of-an-untouched-environment-variable-under-e (the node's own reproducer), tests/make/origin-of-a-replaced-environment-variable-under-e (the promotion and the declined write), tests/make/makeflags-outranks-a-makefile-under-e (the MAKEFLAGS rank and the append it declines).\n\nOne adjacent defect found while probing and filed rather than fixed: make-unknown-switch-in-a-makefiles-makeflags — `MAKEFLAGS += -zz` is ignored by GNU and is a fatal usage error in Ronin."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T10:50:17.673502622Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-lazy-recipe-expansion
                title "Expand a recipe when it is about to run"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T20:43:52.832200915Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed from make-shell-function-evaluation, which made the gap observable rather than creating it.\n\nGNU Make expands a recipe line immediately before it runs it, after the target's prerequisites have been brought up to date and only if the target was out of date. Ronin expands every reachable recipe once, during graph construction, before anything runs. Every evaluation-time effect a recipe carries therefore happens at the wrong moment, and now that $(shell) is answered during expansion rather than written through to the recipe's own shell, the difference has side effects.\n\nReproducer, up-to-date target:\n\n    out: in ; @cp in out $(shell echo ran >> log)\n\nwith `out` newer than `in`. GNU Make says \"'out' is up to date\" and leaves no `log`, because it never expanded the line. Ronin says \"no work to do\" and `log` holds `ran`.\n\nReproducer, prerequisite output:\n\n    a: ; @echo A; touch made\n    b: a ; @printf '[%s] [%s]\\n' '$(wildcard made)' '$(shell ls made 2>/dev/null)'\n\nGNU Make prints `[made] [made]`: `b`'s recipe is expanded after `a` has run. Ronin prints `[] []` — both halves, so this predates the $(shell) change and reaches $(wildcard), $? and anything else evaluation can observe.\n\nThe shape is a scheduler question rather than an evaluator one: kati's populate_ninja_nodes walks the goals and compiles each node's commands eagerly because a manifest has to hold text. A destination that runs the build itself could instead keep the unexpanded command list on the edge and expand at launch, which is what NewInputsTiming::SchedulerBoundary already does for $? alone."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T21:40:42.10181034Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T22:27:34.582112569Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Design: recipe expansion is the SINK's choice, one level up from `BuildSink::shell_evaluation`. New `RecipeExpansion::{Construction, Launch}` with `BuildSink::recipe_expansion()` defaulting to Construction. `NinjaWriter` keeps Construction — a manifest is the whole of what it produces, so it needs every command before any of it runs. `GraphSink` answers Launch, but only for the root compilation unit and only when its constructor was given Launch: `load_makefile`'s public graph stays fully expanded, because a caller that takes that graph and runs it later is not present to be asked for a command (tests/public_api.rs asserts exactly that), while Make mode's own `load_with_subninjas` asks for Launch. A recursive child unit keeps Construction: its session and its `-C` directory do not outlive its own compilation.\n\nEquivalence: NO rule bump. `make.manifest-equivalence+1` compares the direct graph against the manifest through the `Tee`, and `Tee` overrides none of the sink knobs, so both sides of the comparison run with the manifest's Construction expansion — the same arrangement dispatch #11 established for `shell_evaluation`. The compared property is unchanged: 297 makefiles, 276 graphs compared, 0 disagreements, as required. What laziness changes is which text the direct sink holds in production, not what the two encodings of the same recipe come to, so there is no proposition in the rule that has moved.\n\nWhat still expands while compiling, and why — the recipe's own text decides the graph's shape in exactly four ways, so a recipe that can reach one of them is read at construction as before: a recursive `$(MAKE)` line (it becomes a child graph), an automatic or declared depfile (`get_depfile` rewrites the script and names a file on the rule), a `$?` (the scheduler binds the value against an edge that must declare the deferral, including the `filter-out` exclusions kati discovers by expanding), and a grouped double-colon action. Plus a recipe of nothing but whitespace, which is a target remade by doing nothing and wants a phony edge rather than a rule.\n\nThe recursion test is the subtle one and the first design was wrong. kati classifies a recursive line dynamically: expanding a reference *named* MAKE inside a command records its value in `expanded_make_in_command`, and a line invoking one of those values is composed as a subninja. A static scan of the recipe for `$(MAKE)` therefore misses `RECURSE = $(MAKE) -C sub` / `all: ; $(RECURSE)`, which tests/cli.rs `recursive_make_compiles_as_subninja` pins — it caught the miss. `command::expansion_can_reach_make` answers the right question instead: can expanding this tree reach a reference named MAKE. It walks the recipe's syntax tree and, through every `SymRef`, the syntax tree of each recursively expanded variable it names (memoised by a visited set, looking in the node's rule_vars before the globals). A simply expanded variable holds text rather than a tree and cannot hold a reference — which is precisely why kati's own classification stops there too. Conservative wherever a name is not knowable without expanding: a computed `$($(x))`, and `call`/`value`/`eval`, all answer yes. The launch-time expansion then re-checks what the expansion actually did and refuses loudly if a recursive invocation appears anyway, so a hole in the walk is a diagnostic rather than a nested Make process.\n\nSession state: the root unit's `Evaluator` and its `DepNode`s are retained past compilation in `make::recipe::PendingRecipes` and carried on `Loaded` to every build over that graph — including the compiler-input builds in `cli/remake.rs`, whose edges can be deferred too. A recipe is expanded in the session that read the Makefile, against the variables that session holds, which is what makes an expansion at launch the same expansion GNU Make would have done there: MAKEFILE_LIST, origin/flavor and the read-time `.SHELLSTATUS` are all still there (probed).\n\nExecutor boundary: `build::LateCommands` is a front-end-agnostic hook — \"the command for this edge, now that it is about to run\" — asked once per edge in `prepare_edge`, deliberately not in `ensure_command`, which runs over every edge the targets reach and would bind every command in the graph. Nothing Make-specific crosses it: command text in, a rendered diagnostic out. It is safe for Make edges because a Make edge's dirtiness never consults its command hash (`generator`, from make-timestamp-only-recipe-freshness), so the placeholder the graph carries until launch cannot make anything dirty; the hash the build log records is computed from the bound command at completion.\n\nErrors: an expansion rejected at launch reads as the compiler diagnostic it would have been at compile time — `ronin: Makefile:1: first argument to 'word' function must be greater than 0.` — via `Outcome::front_end_diagnostic` and `report::ordinary_diagnostic`, rather than as a build that stopped. Without that the 21 `functions/word|intcmp|let|file` rows in the upstream inventory moved from `product-name` to `ninja-failure-block`; with it they stay put. A target that never runs never errors, which is the point."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T22:27:53.130229586Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU Make 4.4.1 matrix, every cell probed against the pristine oracle rather than assumed.\n\nExpansion happens in `new_job`, per recipe, immediately before the recipe runs, and the WHOLE recipe is expanded in one go. `all:` with `@touch made` then `@printf '[%s]' '$(wildcard made)'` prints `[]` on a first run and `[made]` on a second, so a line cannot observe what an earlier line of its own recipe did, while it observes everything every earlier recipe did: `b: a` with `a` touching `made` prints `[made] [made]` for `$(wildcard made)` and `$(shell ls made)` alike. `$(wildcard)` tracks the filesystem at expansion time in both directions — a file an earlier recipe DELETED reads as absent.\n\nAn up-to-date target's recipe is never expanded at all: no `$(shell)` side effect, and an `$(error)` in it does not fire (`make: 'out' is up to date.`, status 0). `-B` expands it. In a double-colon chain only the out-of-date entry's recipe is expanded.\n\nInterleaving. Under `-j1`: expand X, run X, expand Y, run Y. Under `-j2`: expand X, expand Y, then the runs — and with a sleep in X the runs come back Y-then-X, which shows the ordering is \"expanded as each job is launched, in launch order\" rather than any promise about completion. A target whose prerequisite failed is never expanded (`-k` included).\n\n`-n` expands every recipe it would run: `$(info)` prints, `$(shell)` runs and leaves its file, and an `$(error)` in a recipe that would run is fatal. `-q` expands too — same side effects, status 1, nothing run. `-t` does NOT expand: no side effect, no `$(error)`, it just touches.\n\n`.SHELLSTATUS` is scoped, and this is the subtle cell. A `$(shell)` in a recipe sets it where the expansion is happening, which for a recipe is the target's own variable set: within one recipe `$(shell exit 3)$(.SHELLSTATUS)` then `$(shell exit 0)$(.SHELLSTATUS)` reads 3 then 0, but a LATER target's recipe reads the global value the read-time `$(shell)` left (7), not the 3 the earlier recipe set. Recipe-time `$(shell)` must not clobber the global.\n\nAutomatic variables are unaffected — `$@ $< $^ $?` and `$*` are per-rule already, and an `$(info)` in the recipe sees them. The whole evaluation session is available at recipe expansion: `$(MAKEFILE_LIST)`, `$(origin V)`, `$(flavor V)` and an included makefile's variables all answer as they did at read time.\n\nOne cell GNU answers differently from a naive reading: a recipe that expands to NOTHING (`EMPTY =` / `all: ; $(EMPTY)`) is still expanded — an `$(info)` in it prints — and then GNU runs nothing and reports `'all' is up to date`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T22:28:47.032613054Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. kati: `RecipeExpansion` on `BuildSink` plus `SinkRule::deferred_recipe`; `populate_ninja_node` leaves an eligible recipe unread and `sink_node` declares a rule that names it; `ninja::DeferredRecipes::expand` is the launch-time expansion, assembling the same script, description, shell flags and ignore-errors the eager path assembles. `command::expansion_can_reach_make` and `references_new_inputs` and `is_blank_recipe_line` are the eligibility tests; `NewInputsTiming::Launch` answers an indirect `$?` from the real timestamps, which at launch are the ones GNU compares. Two small kati openings the change needed: `Symtab::peek_symbol` (ask about a name without minting it) and `Variable::recursive_definition`; and `Vars::assign` now lets an automatic variable be replaced by another automatic variable, because the front end re-arms `$@`/`$<`/`$?` every time it starts expanding recipes and a Makefile assigning over them is still refused.\n\nRonin: `build::LateCommands`/`LateCommand` and `Builder::bind_late_command`, called from `prepare_edge`; `make::recipe::PendingRecipes` holds the retained session and answers for each deferred edge; `make::sink::CommandLayout` is the `cd`/`env`/shell/response-file wrapper, extracted so the sink and the launch build the identical command line; `Loaded` carries the recipes to every build over the graph, including `cli/remake.rs`'s compiler-input passes.\n\nGates. Workspace tests 737 pass. Make build-intent oracle: 415 gating cases (409 + 6 recorded here), all matching. Direct/manifest equivalence 297 makefiles / 276 graphs compared / 0 disagreements. Conformance 387 runs, 309 identical and 78 differing — unmoved. Upstream GNU Make inventory 1287 rows, no row added or removed, class totals unmoved (unclassified 504, compiler 37, interface 46, narration 700); 4 rows moved, all narration, all the one residue now filed as make-empty-expansion-runs-nothing. Real-project gate: Ninja's CMake Makefile built in 21s at -j8, the recorded figure. fmt clean; Clippy pedantic+nursery clean on ronin lib+bins; plain `-D warnings` clean on kati.\n\nSix oracle-recorded fixtures: recipe-of-a-current-target-is-not-expanded, recipe-error-in-a-current-target-does-not-fire, recipe-expansion-sees-an-earlier-recipes-file, recipe-lines-are-expanded-before-the-first-line-runs, recipe-variable-shell-runs-when-the-recipe-runs (the BUGS.md/CPython shape), dry-run-expands-the-recipe-it-would-run.\n\nThe BUGS.md entry \"Recipe lines expand before their rule runs, freezing $(shell) results\" is closed with the mechanism, the reduced case, and the replay: the failed python@seed tree survives, but its generated Makefile bakes absolute /build paths and remakes itself from a srcdir that exists only inside the package sandbox, so the replay runs CPython's own PYTHON_FOR_BUILD line and pybuilddir.txt rule verbatim against a stand-in interpreter that records the _PYTHON_SYSCONFIGDATA_PATH it was handed. GNU Make 4.4.1 and Ronin now record the same two values.\n\nFollow-ups: make-empty-expansion-runs-nothing, make-recursive-child-recipes-expand-at-launch."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T22:35:56.254395338Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-oracle-recording-trailing-newline
                title "Record a produced file that has no trailing newline"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T20:44:09.279800376Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed from make-shell-function-evaluation, which hit it while recording a fixture.\n\ntests/make_port.rs writes a recording that says whether a produced file ended in a newline and then reads it back as though it always did. `render` counts `content.lines().count() + usize::from(!content.is_empty() && !content.ends_with('\\n'))`, so a file holding \"srr\" is recorded as `file tick touched 2` with one content line — the count is one greater than the lines present, and that is the whole of the signal. `parse`'s `flush` ignores it: it joins the content lines with '\\n' and appends one whenever the result is non-empty.\n\nSo a case whose build leaves a file without a trailing newline can never match its own recording. Recording it succeeds, and the very next run of the gate fails with `'tick' holds \"srr\" where GNU Make left \"srr\\n\"` — a difference the harness invented, and one that reads as a Ronin defect.\n\nReproducer: any case whose recipe is `@printf 'x' > out`. Record it, then run the gate.\n\nThe fix is in the reader rather than the writer: the count is already there to be read, and `flush` should append the newline only when the recorded count matches the number of content lines.\n\nWorked around for now by writing the one fixture that needed it — function-shell-runs-once-for-a-simple-value — with `echo` rather than `echo -n`, which is not a fix and hides the next one."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T09:46:07.023327405Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Hit again while writing make-builtin-rule-catalogue's cases (2026-08-14), and worked around rather than fixed, which is worth recording as a second sighting with a fresh reproducer.\n\nA case whose recipe was `@printf anymatch > $@` recorded as `file all touched 2` with one `| anymatch` line, and replayed as `anymatch\\n`. The gate then failed the case against the very oracle run that recorded it: \"'all' holds \\\"anymatch\\\" where GNU Make left \\\"anymatch\\\\n\\\"\". `render` counts the missing final newline into the line count and `parse` puts one back, so no file without a trailing newline can round-trip — and the line count it already writes is the place the information could live.\n\nThe workaround was to make the case's recipe write `'anymatch\\n'`, which is fine for a case about pattern-rule striking and would not be fine for a case about what a recipe writes. tests/make/pattern-search-cancelled-rule-strikes-nothing carries it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:51:17.026764801Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:56:48.268113764Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Fixed in the reader, which is where the signal already was, and no recording had to be re-made to accept it.\n\nTHE ASYMMETRY, and which way it had to be closed. `render` writes `file <path> <mark> <count>` where the count is `content.lines().count() + usize::from(!content.is_empty() && !content.ends_with('\\n'))` — one greater than the content lines that follow exactly when the last line carried no terminator. `parse`'s `flush` never read the count: it joined the lines and appended a newline to anything non-empty. Fixing the writer instead would have meant a new signal and a re-recording of all 645; the count is already written, already correct, and already in every recording, so the reader was the side that could change without touching the corpus. `parse` now carries the count through `pending` and appends the terminator only when `!lines.is_empty() && recorded == lines.len()`; a `file` line with no count at all (no such recording exists, but the reader is the compatibility surface) falls back to what it used to assume.\n\nTwo shapes round-trip that could not before, and the second was not in the node's account. A file ending `srr` records as count 2 with one content line and now reads back as `srr`. A file that is nothing but a newline records as count 1 with one empty content line, and used to read back as nothing at all, because the join was empty and the old rule keyed on emptiness rather than on the count — which is why the fix keys on `lines.is_empty()` and not on `content.is_empty()`.\n\nPROOF THAT NO RECORDING NEEDED RE-MAKING, which was the risk worth measuring rather than asserting. Before the fix, a scan of all 645 recordings found: zero with count == lines + 1 (the unmatchable shape — the workarounds are why), zero with a single empty content line (the other shape the reader now reads differently), zero with an unparseable or absent count. So the new reader parses all 645 to exactly what the old one did. Then the corpus was re-recorded from the pinned oracle in full, and `git status` reported exactly one changed `expected` — the case this node's workaround lived in, which was edited on purpose. Every other recording came back byte-identical.\n\nTHE WORKAROUND, undone. `feature-file-read-removes-one-trailing-newline` hid two of its inputs behind a leading dot, which is how this harness is told not to list a file, because neither could be recorded as itself. They are ordinary visible files now — `no-newline` holding `a` and `only-newline` holding `\\n` — the Makefile reads them by their plain names, and the recording carries `file no-newline kept 2` with one content line and `file only-newline kept 1` with one empty one. The case is now a test of the fix as well as of `func_file`: with the reader reverted and the fixture left visible, make_port fails with `feature-file-read-removes-one-trailing-newline: 'no-newline' holds \"a\" where GNU Make left \"a\\n\"` — the harness inventing a difference, in the exact words of the first sighting.\n\nThe other two workarounds are left where they are, and are no longer load-bearing. `function-shell-runs-once-for-a-simple-value` writes with `echo` rather than `echo -n`, and `pattern-search-cancelled-rule-strikes-nothing` writes `'anymatch\\n'`; neither case is about what a recipe writes, so re-recording them would change no claim. What has changed is that a future case about exactly that no longer has to be bent.\n\nA round-trip test now pins the format from inside: `make_recording_reads_back_what_the_build_left` renders and re-parses ten contents — empty, one newline, two, `srr`, `srr\\n`, `a\\nb`, `a\\nb\\n`, `a\\n\\nb`, `a\\n\\n`, `a\\n\\n\\n` — and asserts each reads back as itself. make_port is 4 tests now, 635 gated cases of 645, all matching."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:56:58.453119066Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:57:32.221496549Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Erratum to the note above: the round-trip test is `make_recording_round_trips_every_ending`. The name it was written under read as a sentence and the commit gate refused it."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-simple-command-without-a-shell
                title "Run a command with no shell syntax without a shell"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T20:44:25.689972356Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed from make-shell-function-evaluation, which met it as the residue on functions/shell.diff.14 and .15 in the upstream inventory.\n\nGNU Make's construct_command_argv_internal (job.c) tokenizes a command itself and execs it directly whenever nothing in it is shell syntax: no character from `#;\"*?[]&|<>(){}$\\`^`, no shell builtin as the first word, an IFS of only whitespace, and .SHELLFLAGS of `-c` or `-ec`. Only a line that fails one of those tests reaches `goto slow` and is handed to $(SHELL). kati always builds `SHELL -c 'line'`.\n\nWhat that changes is who reports a failure and in whose words. Reproducer, in a recipe:\n\n    all: ; ./nosuchprogram arg\n\nGNU Make: `make: ./nosuchprogram: No such file or directory` then `make: *** [Makefile:1: all] Error 127`. Ronin: `/bin/sh: 1: ./nosuchprogram: not found`, because the shell is the one that failed to find it. The same split shows in $(shell):\n\n    $(shell ./nosuchprogram there)\n\nwhere GNU Make prints its own diagnostic and Ronin prints the shell's.\n\nBoth agree on the status — 127 either way — so this is about the diagnostic, the process tree, and one less fork per simple recipe line, not about what gets built. It reaches recipes and $(shell) alike, so it belongs where the argv is constructed rather than in either caller."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:03:43.523386008Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Upstream-residue triage (2026-08-14) gives this node ten rows of family evidence, now classified `command-not-found-text` and narration rather than unclassified residue.\n\nWhere a recipe names a command that is not there, GNU execs it itself and reports the errno against the command's own name — `./foobarbazbozblat: No such file or directory` — while Ronin hands the line to a shell and reports what the shell said: `/bin/sh: 1: ./foobarbazbozblat: not found`. Both fail the recipe with status 127, so the build outcome agrees and only the sentence differs; the classifier now recognises the pair by matching the command named on each side rather than by either wording, so a case where only one tool failed still shows up.\n\nThat is why they are narration and not this node's debt. What they do record is the observable consequence of always going through a shell, which is what this node proposes to stop doing for a command with no shell syntax in it. Running such a command directly would move these ten rows from an agreed-outcome-differently-worded family to exact agreement, so they are a reasonable acceptance check for the change and a reason to expect the diagnostic to change shape when it lands."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:10:12.04709646Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Half delivered — the argv construction and one of its two callers. Node stays open for the other.\n\nkati `src-rs/simple_command.rs` is GNU Make's `construct_command_argv_internal` (src/job.c) decision and tokenizer: all four gates before any tokenizing, in GNU Make's order — the shell must be the built-in default, `.SHELLFLAGS` exactly `-c` or `-ec`, `IFS` nothing but whitespace, and `.ONESHELL` not turning newlines into separators — then the scan for shell syntax and for a builtin as the first word, then the unquoting. A `SHELL` the makefile set takes the slow path even when it names another POSIX shell, because GNU Make compares against its own default rather than asking what the shell can do.\n\nWired into `$(shell)` through `src-rs/fileutil.rs`, so `$(shell ./nosuchprog there)` now reports `make: ./nosuchprog: No such file or directory` in Make's own voice rather than `/bin/sh: 1: ./nosuchprog: not found`, and the diagnostic prefix threads through `func.rs`, `exec.rs` and `regen.rs` to say it in the right words. That closed functions/shell.diff.14 and .15 in the upstream inventory, which is what filed the node: both are now narration-class on product name and ninja progress only, with the command-not-found text gone from the difference.\n\nRemaining: the recipe-launch caller. A recipe line still goes to `$(SHELL) -c` unconditionally, so `all: ; ./nosuchprog arg` keeps reporting in the shell's voice and keeps the extra fork. The module is caller-agnostic by construction and the recipe path is a second call to it, but it lands in the execution path rather than in expansion, so it wants its own change and its own corpus cases."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T21:33:00.513444584Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T21:34:48.293546939Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The other half, shipped with make-recipe-line-is-its-own-shell because it is the same change: a recipe line cannot be exec'd without a shell until the launch is per line.\n\nTHE MATRIX for the recipe launch, probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. GNU on the left, Ronin before the change on the right.\n\n  ./nosuchprog arg      make: ./nosuchprog: No such file or directory, Error 127\n                        /bin/sh: 1: ./nosuchprog: not found, code 127\n  ./notexec arg         make: ./notexec: Permission denied, Error 127\n                        /bin/sh: 1: ./notexec: Permission denied, code 126\n  ./nosuchprog > out    /bin/sh: 1: ./nosuchprog: not found — the shell's, in BOTH: the `>` made it the shell's errand\n  ./script arg          runs it, though the file has no `#!` line — in both\n  SHELL := /bin/bash    /bin/bash: line 1: ./nosuchprog: No such file or directory, in both: GNU compares\n                        SHELL against its own default and takes the slow path for anything else\n  -./nosuchprog         Error 127 (ignored), and the recipe carries on\n\nThe status is the second cell's point. GNU reports 127 for a program it may not run as well as for one that is not there, because what its child reports is that the exec failed rather than what the command said; a shell distinguishes them and answers 126. So the change moves an exit status and not only a sentence.\n\nThe third cell is why the fast path is a per-line question: the same program, the same absence, and a different reporter, decided by one `>`.\n\nONE ERRNO GNU DOES SOMETHING ABOUT, and the reason the fourth cell agrees. `exec_command` (job.c:2609) switches on errno after `execvp`: `ENOENT` and everything else are reported against `argv[0]`, and `ENOEXEC` alone is retried — the file is executable and is not a program, so it is exec'd again under `getenv(\"SHELL\")` or `/bin/sh` with the script as the first argument. Ronin does the same, and reads SHELL from the environment the recipe is given rather than from the makefile, which is GNU's choice too: what is being answered is how the host runs a script, not what the recipe wants.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, all in Ronin's executor — kati's `simple_command` module was already there and already right, and this is its second caller.\n\n`subprocess::Launch` is what a process the build starts is: a command line for a shell, which is every Ninja edge, or a `DirectLaunch` — an argument list with the directory and environment it needs, because there is no shell here to read a `cd` and an `env`. `ProcessSupervisor::start` is the spawn with its retries, one of them Ninja's shell fallback and two of them GNU Make's. A direct launch that cannot be started is answered for where it happened: `failed_to_start` renders the errno the way the C library would, as the command's own stderr, with 127, so it reaches the caller through the same channel a recipe's own output does.\n\nThe name it is reported against is Ronin's, `ronin: ./nosuchprog: No such file or directory`, which is the same voice `$(shell)` has used since this node's first half and what `product-name` in the upstream inventory is for.\n\n`CommandLayout::launch_step` is the sink's side: it hands over the argv, the unit's `-C` directory and the unit's plus the target's environment as values rather than as words in front of a command line.\n\nINVENTORY, audited row by row rather than by count. Eleven rows moved, all of them this change's, all toward Make, and none the wrong way:\n\n  features/errors.diff.3 .4 .5 .6            narration, lost command-not-found-text\n  features/errors.diff.7 .8 .9               UNCLASSIFIED -> narration, lost the residue with it\n  features/output-sync.diff.14               narration, lost command-not-found-text\n  features/targetvars.diff.26                narration, lost command-not-found-text\n  misc/general4.diff.7 .8                    narration, lost command-not-found-text\n\nEvery one of the eleven is the same fact: both tools now exec the missing command themselves and report the errno against its own name, so the sentences agree and only the product name is left. The `command-not-found-text` family — the ten rows this node was filed on — is now EMPTY. 1283 rows at 163 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1083 narration, against 166/0/37/1080. THE COMPILER BUCKET IS STILL ZERO and no row entered it.\n\nTHE CLASSIFIER WAS CHECKED FIRST, as the dispatch asked, and needed no change: `examples/make_upstream`'s `is_shell_invocation` reads the failure block's middle line, and that line still names the edge's command with its `exec env ... /bin/sh -c` wrapper, because the block names the edge and not the line the edge stopped at. Naming the line was tried and reverted: it left 15 rows of features/implicit_search and features/parallelism unclassifiable, since a directly exec'd line is bare argv text with no shape a recogniser can know. Two family descriptions were corrected, because this change falsified them: `command-not-found-text` now says it is only where the line holds shell syntax, and `recipe-interleave` says Ronin reports an edge's output once the whole recipe has finished rather than that it runs the recipe as one script.\n\nFIXTURES, four recorded from the oracle: recipe-line-with-no-shell-syntax-reports-a-missing-program, recipe-line-reports-a-program-it-may-not-run, recipe-line-with-shell-syntax-lets-the-shell-report (the cell that must NOT change), recipe-line-runs-a-script-with-no-hashbang (the `ENOEXEC` retry, which a corpus can see because the script writes a file). Regression a_missing_program_is_reported_by_make_and_not_by_a_shell carries the wording and the status, which the corpus cannot.\n\nGATES, for both nodes. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean over three passes and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean. Workspace tests 895 passed / 0 failed with `--all-targets`; kati's own 183 pass. make_port 4/4 over 809 cases, no existing recording rewritten, and the two divergence markers deleted because they now pass. Make conformance 387 runs, 318 identical / 69 differing — the baseline, unmoved. Graph equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements: the manifest is byte for byte what it was, which is the decision recorded on the sibling node. Ninja conformance 425 upstream / 10 differential / 86 build-outcome / 3 invocation-boundary, plus persistence and log content. Upstream inventory as above. Real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git from its own CMake Makefile in 46s at -j8, with GNU Make 4.4.1 at 42-43s on the same host in the same window; paired before-and-after runs are within 2%, and the wall-time measurement is on the sibling node."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T21:54:06.818469836Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-empty-expansion-runs-nothing
                title "Run nothing for a recipe that expands to nothing"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T22:28:17.437318077Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed from make-lazy-recipe-expansion, which made the shape reachable.\n\nGNU Make expands a recipe that expands to nothing, runs nothing, and reports the target up to date. `EMPTY =` with `all: ; $(EMPTY)` prints `make: 'all' is up to date.`, and an `$(info)` in the same recipe still prints, so the expansion happened and produced no command line.\n\nRonin decides whether a node has commands when it builds the graph. For an eagerly expanded recipe that decision is made from the expansion — no commands means no rule, so the edge is phony and the build says nothing to do. A deferred recipe has not been expanded when the rule is minted, so the rule exists, and when the expansion turns out empty the edge runs `/bin/sh -c \"\"`: a shell process that does nothing, a `[1/1]` progress line where GNU says `is up to date`, and — the part that is not narration — a `-q` that answers 1 where GNU answers 0.\n\nA recipe of nothing but whitespace is already kept eager for exactly this reason (`is_blank_recipe_line`), which covers the `all:;` that Makefiles actually write. What remains is a recipe whose emptiness only the expansion can discover.\n\nReproducer: `EMPTY =` / `all: ; $(EMPTY)` — Ronin runs an empty shell; and with `-q`, Ronin exits 1 where GNU exits 0.\n\nFour rows of the upstream inventory sit on this: options/dash-n.diff.2 and .3 (`BAR =` / `FOO = +$(BAR)` / `b: c; $(FOO)`), misc/general3.diff, features/patternrules.diff.32 — each moved from `no-work-line` to `ninja-progress` and each is narration except for the question-mode answer.\n\nThe fix wants the launch boundary to be able to say \"nothing to run\" rather than \"run this empty script\": `build::LateCommand` would carry the absence, and `prepare_edge` would complete the edge as it does under a dry run instead of spawning. The plan's own count of work is settled before the expansion, so `already_up_to_date` and therefore `-q` need the same answer to reach them."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T15:17:20.253300062Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T15:40:28.778654864Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU Make 4.4.1 matrix, probed cell by cell against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make before any change.\n\nWHERE THE BEHAVIOUR LIVES. job.c, and in two places rather than one. `job_next_command` advances past every expanded line whose text is empty; reaching the end of them without having started anything leaves `command_state` at `cs_not_started`, which `new_job` reads as `us_success` and hands straight to `notice_finished_file`. No shell, no `commands_started`, and therefore `'x' is up to date.` rather than silence. The `-q` answer is the same fact seen from `start_job_command`, whose question-mode branch is guarded on `argv != 0` — `construct_command_argv` returns NULL for a line with no text — so a recipe of nothing answers 0 and a recipe with one real line answers 1.\n\nTHE MATRIX. Fully empty, whitespace-only (`$(subst x, ,x)` twice), empty after `@`, after `-`, after `@-`, and several lines each empty: all `'all' is up to date.`, exit 0, no file touched. A phony target says `Nothing to be done for 'all'.` instead, which is the ordinary phony wording and not this node's business. Some lines empty among real ones: GNU runs exactly the real ones — the skip is per expanded line, not per recipe. `-n` prints nothing and says up to date, because the emptiness is discovered before `just_print_flag` is consulted. `-t` touches the target, because `-t` is decided from the rule having commands at all, before any expansion — unchanged here and still covered by the discovery-only touch-option case. `$(info)` in such a recipe still prints: the expansion happened.\n\n`-q` genuinely expands. `$(info X)` prints under `-q`, and a `$(shell echo ran > mark)` in a recipe really writes the file. It walks in dependency order and stops at the first target that would run something: two targets with work print only the first one's `$(info)`, a first target that expands to nothing lets the walk reach the second.\n\nWHAT RONIN DID. Ran `/bin/sh -c \"\"` — a process, a `[1/1]` line, and a target counted as built. `-q` answered 1 from the plan's own count of work, which is settled before any expansion and so cannot see this.\n\nDELIVERED. kati: `ExpandedRecipe::runs_nothing`, set where `expand_with` finds no command lines, replacing the empty script it used to hand over. Ronin: `LateBinding { Settled, Run, Nothing }` at the launch boundary in place of the old `Option`; `prepare_edge` returns `None` for `Nothing` before it makes an output directory, writes a response file or records the edge as executed; the build loop completes such an edge through `finish_without_command` and takes it out of the progress total, the same accounting a deferred edge gets when its freshness test comes out negative. `Planned::interrogate` is what `-q` now asks — it expands each planned edge's recipe in turn and stops at the first that yields a command, which is what GNU Make's walk does. `Outcome::ran_a_command` lets Make mode say `no work to do.` about a build that turned out to have none, which is the slot GNU Make's `is up to date` sits in.\n\nTHE DRY-RUN HALF, which is the fourth inventory row. A dry run stands in for the update a prerequisite's command would have made, because it does not run the command. A recipe with no command line makes no such update in either run, so `finish_without_command` now tells the dependents in both — `Unrun::NoCommand` against `Unrun::Skipped`/`Unrun::Phony`, which keep the dry run's assumption — and `deferred_work` stops forcing a candidate to run when every one of its prerequisites came from an edge that ran nothing.\n\nEVIDENCE. Five oracle-recorded fixtures: recipe-that-expands-to-nothing-starts-no-shell and expansion-of-only-whitespace-starts-no-shell (a `SHELL := ./recording-shell` that appends to a file, so \"no shell was started\" is a file fact the build-intent gate can hold), question-mode-answers-for-a-recipe-that-expands-to-nothing (`-q`, where the recorded outcome is the exit status), some-recipe-lines-expand-to-nothing, a-target-remade-by-nothing-satisfies-its-dependents. Two regressions in tests/make_regressions.rs carry what the corpus cannot, because a dry run writes nothing either way and the difference is only in what is said: a_recipe_that_expands_to_nothing_reports_no_command and a_dry_run_does_not_remake_past_a_recipe_that_expands_to_nothing.\n\nINVENTORY. All four rows this node owned moved, and only those four: features/patternrules.diff.32, misc/general3.diff, options/dash-n.diff.2 and options/dash-n.diff.3, each from `ninja-progress` (a progress line for a command GNU Make never ran) to `no-work-line`. dash-n.diff.3 is the dry-run half and is why that part is here rather than filed. Class totals unmoved at 171/1/41/1074 over 1287 rows.\n\nRESIDUE, filed rather than left: make-recipe-output-function-is-not-a-command. A recipe whose expansion is only `$(info ...)` is empty in GNU Make — the text was printed during the expansion and no command line exists — while kati's `avoid_io` turns it into a `printf` command, so `-q` still answers 1 there and the printing happens when the edge runs rather than when it expands.\n\nFix in kati a45d631 on branch nplan/make-empty-expansion-runs-nothing."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T16:18:11.577929057Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recursive-child-recipes-expand-at-launch
                title "Expand a recursive child's recipes when they run"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T22:28:26.835802049Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed from make-lazy-recipe-expansion, which deliberately stopped at the root unit.\n\nA recipe is now expanded when its edge is launched, so an up-to-date target's `$(shell)` never runs and a recipe sees what earlier recipes made. That holds for the root compilation unit only. A recursive `$(MAKE)` child is compiled into the same graph by its own kati session, in its own `-C` directory, and both are dropped when that unit's compilation returns — so `GraphSink::recipe_expansion` answers Construction for a child unit and every child recipe is still expanded before anything runs.\n\nWhat it costs: a Makefile whose real work is in `$(MAKE) -C sub` gets the old timing for that work. The CPython failure this was filed beside is a root-unit recipe, and Ninja's CMake build — 541 recursive lines — is the shape where the child's recipes are most of the build.\n\nTwo things have to be settled to lift it. The session: every child unit's `Evaluator` and `DepNode`s would have to be retained beside the root's, keyed so an edge finds the one that owns it — `make::recipe::PendingRecipes` already holds exactly this for one unit and would become a collection. The directory: a child's recipe expands against its own working directory, and `$(wildcard)` and `$(shell)` read the process's. The build runs from the root with one process directory that parallel spawns depend on, and `make-process-directory-isolation` is the decision that says a chdir during execution is not available. So the child's expansion needs its directory carried into the evaluation rather than into the process — kati resolves relative paths through the session, which is where that would go."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T15:54:35.411140135Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T16:16:27.848876667Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered whole, including the directory, which the node's own text called the harder of the two things to settle.\n\nWHAT WAS TRUE BEFORE. `GraphSink::recipe_expansion` answered Construction for any unit but the root, so every recursive `$(MAKE)` child's recipes were read while the graph was compiled. Probed against 4.4.1 and against Ronin before the change, three shapes and three divergences: a child's recipe-time `$(shell)` ran for a target that was already up to date and never ran (GNU Make runs nothing at all there); a child's `$(wildcard *.made)` was answered before anything was built and came back empty where GNU Make sees the file the child's earlier recipe made; and a `$(file > ...)` in a child's recipe was performed while the graph was built, writing a file for a target that never ran. The directory was the one thing already right: the compilation happened inside `in_directory(&context.directory)`, so a child's `$(shell basename $(pwd))` answered with the child's directory.\n\nTHE SESSION. `CompiledUnit` no longer carries the root's recipes up to the caller. `CompilationState` holds one `PendingRecipes` for the whole compilation and each unit hands its own evaluator, deferred recipes, layout and directory to it as that unit finishes — `state.retain(...)`, taken after the unit compiled rather than inside the guard that compiled it, because what is retained is the session and not the directory. `PendingRecipes` became a collection: a `Vec<RecipeUnit>` and one map from `EdgeId` to the unit and the recipe within it, which works because the graph is one graph and an edge belongs to exactly one unit. A unit served from `state.cache` contributed its recipes the one time it was compiled, and the cache hands back the edges those recipes are keyed by.\n\nTHE DIRECTORY, and it is the existing mechanism rather than a new one. `make-process-directory-isolation` ruled out moving the process during execution because a parallel spawn reads the process directory at the moment it forks — and the same node built an `RwLock` for exactly this, exclusive while kati evaluates and shared while a spawn is prepared (`subprocess.rs` takes the read side in `spawn`). `expanded_in` takes the write side, enters the unit's directory, expands, and restores before returning, so no spawn can observe it and the guard is long gone before the command it produced is launched. On Unix nothing else forks — the threaded spawn path is `cfg(not(unix))` — so the exclusion costs a build loop that is already serial nothing at all. A unit already sitting in the process directory takes none of it, which is the root always and a child invoked without `-C` as well.\n\nTHE FOUR CONSTRUCTION-TIME EXCEPTIONS hold per unit without any new work, because `defers_recipe` was always a test on the node rather than on the unit. Probed in a child: a child that itself recurses two levels deep composes as before; a child's `$?` still resolves through the scheduler; a child's grouped `::` still runs both actions in order. All three match GNU Make.\n\nEVIDENCE. Four oracle-recorded fixtures, of which three fail against the pre-change build and pass after — recursive-child-recipe-shell-runs-when-the-recipe-runs (`sub/probe` produced where GNU Make produces none), recursive-child-recipe-sees-an-earlier-recipes-file (`wildcard saw: ` against GNU Make's `wildcard saw: first.made`), recursive-child-file-write-in-a-recipe-that-does-not-run (`sub/written` produced where GNU Make produces none) — and one that passes both ways on purpose, recursive-child-recipe-expands-in-its-own-directory, which is the guard on the thing the change had to not break. Four parallel children in four `-C` directories under `-j8` were probed by hand and each read its own `seed.txt`, its own `pwd` and its own `$(wildcard)`.\n\nWHAT IT NARROWED. make-recipe-file-operation-at-launch-only listed four kinds of recipe whose `$(file ...)` is performed too early and named this node as the one that had to land before the recursive child's could be fixed. Three are left; its record is updated and its own divergence fixture, which is the `$?` kind, still diverges and keeps its marker.\n\nFOUND AND FILED, present before and after and untouched by this: make-child-automatic-variables-are-child-relative. A child's `$?` is answered with the shared graph's path-qualified names — `sub/a sub/b` where GNU Make writes `a b` — and the recipe that receives them runs with `cd sub` in front of it.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `-D warnings` clean. Workspace tests 800 passed / 0 failed. make_port 4/4 over 611 cases. Graph equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements. Make conformance 387 runs, 318 identical / 69 differing — the baseline, unmoved. Upstream inventory 1287 rows at 171/1/41/1074, nothing moved by this node. Ninja conformance 425 upstream / 10 differential / 86 build-outcome / 3 invocation-boundary, plus persistence and log content. Real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git from its own CMake-generated Makefile — 541 recursive lines, which is the shape this node is about — in 21s at -j8, the recorded figure."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T16:18:16.627523807Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-pattern-peer-is-not-intermediate
                title "Keep the peer output a multi-target pattern rule also made"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T23:16:10.19383936Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while completing make-precious-intermediate-retention, and deliberately not folded into it: the mechanism is a different one, and the reproducer says nothing about .PRECIOUS.\n\nWhen the implicit search invents a name from a rule with several target patterns, GNU Make treats only the name it matched as intermediate. `implicit.c` puts each other pattern's name into `also_make` and sets `new->file->is_target = 1` on it with the comment \"so that this file is not treated as intermediate by the pattern rule search algorithm\" — so `remove_intermediates` never sees it and the peer survives the build.\n\nReproducer, tests/make/multi-target-pattern-peer-is-not-intermediate, recorded from GNU Make 4.4.1 and carrying a divergence marker:\n\n    all: hello.tsk\n    hello.x: ; echo body > $@\n    %.z %.w: %.x ; cat $< > $*.z; cat $< > $*.w\n    %.tsk: %.z ; cat $< > $@\n\nGNU Make prints `rm hello.z` and leaves `hello.w`. Ronin deletes both.\n\nWhere it lives. Kati carries disposability as one flag per DepNode, and `dep.rs` records the other patterns' names as that node's `implicit_outputs`; `Plan::disposable_outputs` in src/build.rs then takes every output of every disposable edge. Both halves are the approximation: the peer is a second output of the same edge and has no flag of its own. The narrow fix is to discard only the name the search asked for; the wider one is to carry the disposable names per output the way `delete_on_error_outputs` already does. Read the mark_delete_on_error comment in kati/src-rs/dep.rs first — the same \"the exclusions are per output and not per edge\" argument applies, and it was written for the other half of the same question.\n\nA `.PRECIOUS` pattern naming the peer does not change the answer either way, because GNU already keeps the peer and Ronin already deletes it; retention by `.PRECIOUS` on the matched pattern does happen to keep both, since the whole edge stops being disposable."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T00:41:21.984314209Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Closed with make-grouped-pattern-targets, which needed the same distinction this node asked for and could not be settled without it.\n\nThe wider of the two fixes this node's own note offered is the one taken: the names are carried per output rather than the sweep being narrowed per edge, which is what `delete_on_error_outputs` already does and for the reason the mark_delete_on_error comment gives. kati's DepNode records a pattern rule's peers separately from its ordinary implicit outputs, and `Plan::disposable_outputs` in src/build.rs spares them — the edge stays disposable, and the invented name still goes.\n\nReproducer tests/make/multi-target-pattern-peer-is-not-intermediate now matches GNU Make 4.4.1 and its divergence marker is gone. `hello.z` is swept up; `hello.w` is left where the recipe wrote it.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T00:41:36.675099955Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-bare-secondary-still-builds
                title "Build the chain a bare .SECONDARY made every target of"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T23:16:31.949482946Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while completing make-precious-intermediate-retention. It is the .SECONDARY half of the intermediate machinery rather than the .PRECIOUS half, and the symptom is severe enough to want its own node: a Makefile that says a bare `.SECONDARY:` builds nothing at all.\n\nReproducer, tests/make/secondary-bare-still-builds-the-chain, recorded from GNU Make 4.4.1 and carrying a divergence marker:\n\n    .SECONDARY:\n    all: hello.tsk\n    hello.x: ; echo body > $@\n    %.z: %.x ; cat $< > $@\n    %.tsk: %.z ; cat $< > $@\n\nGNU Make runs all three recipes and keeps hello.z. Ronin says `no work to do.` and writes nothing.\n\nWhat GNU does with it. `snap_deps` sets `all_secondary` when `.SECONDARY` names nothing, and `snap_file` then sets `f->intermediate = 1` on *every* file — but being intermediate in GNU only decides how `check_dep` treats a prerequisite, and the goal itself is reached through `update_file` rather than through `check_dep`. `update_file_1` finds the goal must be made because it does not exist, and then walks its intermediate prerequisites and makes them (remake.c, \"If it does, update all the intermediate files we depend on\"). `remove_intermediates` punts early on `all_secondary`, which is the deletion half and is the half Ronin already gets right.\n\nWhere Ronin loses it. kati's `treat_as_intermediate` returns true for everything under `all_secondary`, and `plan_root` clears the flag only on the roots themselves — so `hello.tsk`, a prerequisite of the `all` root, stays intermediate. `DirtyEvaluator::push_intermediates` in src/graph/intermediate.rs then only asks for an intermediate when something that reads it has to run anyway, and nothing does: the absence of every file in the chain is excused, so the scan finds nothing dirty. The question this node has to answer is what excuses an absence when the consumer is a goal that does not exist either.\n\nNote that a named `.SECONDARY: hello.z` is correct today (tests/make/secondary-is-kept passes), as is the absent-file case with existing downstream files; only the bare form is broken."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T14:49:45.246067311Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle, fifteen cells, and the matrix moves the question: only one of them was wrong, and it is not the one about `.SECONDARY` at all.\n\n  cell (bare `.SECONDARY:`, chain hello.x -> hello.z -> hello.tsk under `all`)   GNU 4.4.1\n  nothing on disk                                                    runs all three\n  the same with `.PRECIOUS: %.z` beside it                           identical\n  hello.z absent, hello.tsk current                                  nothing to be done\n  hello.z absent, hello.tsk behind hello.x                           runs both\n  hello.z present but behind hello.x                                 runs both\n  everything present and in order                                    nothing to be done\n  hello.tsk absent, hello.z present and current                      runs hello.tsk only\n  `make hello.tsk` with the chain end as the goal                    runs all three\n  `all` declared `.PHONY`                                            runs all three\n  a file named `all` on disk, newest                                 nothing to be done\n  a file named `all` on disk, hello.z and hello.tsk absent           nothing to be done\n  no `.SECONDARY` at all                                             runs all three, then `rm hello.z`\n\nRonin already agreed with ten of those before anything changed, which is what makes the two it failed worth reading carefully rather than reaching for the `.SECONDARY` machinery. It failed the first two — everything absent — and it failed `hello.tsk absent, hello.z present`. It passed both `all`-on-disk cells. So the discriminator is not what a bare `.SECONDARY` does to the intermediates. It is whether the GOAL is there.\n\nThat is `remake.c` exactly, and the node's own filing note had already found the sentence: `update_file_1` sets `must_make` when `this_mtime == NONEXISTENT_MTIME`, and `must_make` is what sends `update_file` over every intermediate prerequisite. Being intermediate excuses an absence only in `check_dep`, which is how a prerequisite is reached; a goal is reached by `update_file`, which has no such clause. The two `all`-on-disk cells are the control that proves it is the goal's own absence doing the work and not the chain's: with `all` there, GNU leaves absent intermediates absent even though nothing else changed.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, all in Ronin and none of it about `.SECONDARY`. `push_intermediates` — the half of the intermediate machinery that already existed because the dirty scan cannot reach it — starts wherever `must_make` would be set, and that is one term wider than being out of date. The term is the goal's own absence, and it needed a fact the scan destroys: an intermediate's mtime is overwritten with the newest thing behind it and a phony output's with its inputs', so by the time anything can ask, `mtime().is_missing()` no longer answers \"was it there\". `NodeRuntime::observe` now records what the syscall said beside the timestamp, at the three places a real stat lands and nowhere else, and `absent_on_disk` is that record.\n\nThe walk also had to carry its answer back. Asking for an excused intermediate makes what reads it out of date, and so on up to the goal, which is the scan's own conclusion re-run over answers the walk has just altered — so the walk became a post-order one, `Enter` pushing `Leave` under its inputs. Without that the files were marked and the goal stayed clean, and a build whose target is not dirty runs nothing however dirty everything under it is.\n\nNothing here reads `all_secondary`, `.SECONDARY`, `.PRECIOUS` or `intermediate` beyond what was already read. A bare `.SECONDARY:` is simply the only ordinary way to make every file in a chain intermediate at once, which is why it is where the gap showed.\n\nMarker: tests/make/secondary-bare-still-builds-the-chain matches GNU Make 4.4.1 and its divergence file is gone.\n\nFixtures, five more recorded from the oracle beside it: secondary-bare-keeps-a-precious-intermediate-and-still-builds, -builds-an-intermediate-an-absent-goal-needs (the hello.tsk cell, which is the change at one level rather than three), -builds-an-absent-intermediate-for-a-stale-target and -excuses-an-absent-intermediate-under-a-current-target (the pair that pins the excusing itself, unchanged), and -leaves-a-settled-chain-under-a-goal-that-exists, which is the control that makes the claim about the goal rather than about the chain. One Ronin test asserts it on the compiled graph, with the goal-on-disk case as its own control, and says that the goal's edge is phony because a Makefile target with no recipe compiles to one.\n\nUpstream inventory: no row moved on this node's account."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T14:51:48.565784913Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-pattern-peer-restart-convergence
                title "Stop re-reading when a remade include's peer never appears"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-13T23:16:44.763215877Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while auditing the upstream GNU Make inventory for make-precious-intermediate-retention, and unrelated to that node's change — it reproduces identically with the change reverted.\n\n`features/patternrules.diff.34` is the upstream case, and it flaps: the same binary produces `ronin: no work to do.` on some runs and `ronin: manifest '...' dirty after 100 tries` on others, which is why the recorded inventory row disagrees with a fresh run about as often as it agrees. In a clean directory the failure is deterministic.\n\nReproducer, tests/make/include-remade-by-a-multi-target-pattern-rule, recorded from GNU Make 4.4.1 and carrying a divergence marker:\n\n    all:;\n    include gta\n    %a %b : ; touch $*a\n\nGNU Make remakes the include once — `touch gta`, then `warning: pattern recipe did not update peer target 'gtb'`, then `'all' is up to date.` — and exits 0. Ronin runs `touch gta` a hundred times and dies with `ronin: manifest 'Makefile' dirty after 100 tries`, exit 2.\n\nThe rule has two target patterns and its recipe makes only one of the two names. GNU notices — that warning is `pattern recipe did not update peer target` — and stops. Ronin's regeneration loop re-reads, finds the peer still missing, decides the edge is dirty again, and never converges. So it is the same multi-target-pattern peer gap as make-pattern-peer-is-not-intermediate seen from the regeneration side rather than the deletion side, and the two are worth reading together; they are separate nodes because a fix for either does not obviously give the other.\n\nTwo things a fix owes: a run that does not diverge, and GNU's peer warning, which is the only reason a reader would understand why the recipe ran once and stopped."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T00:41:32.827753026Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Closed with make-grouped-pattern-targets. This node's own note said it and make-pattern-peer-is-not-intermediate were the same gap seen from two sides and that a fix for either might not give the other; in the event one fix gave both, because the missing thing was under both of them — a pattern rule's peer had no identity separate from the outputs the makefile asked for.\n\nThe read settles now because the peer is no longer part of what makes the edge dirty. `gta` is remade once, exists, and has nothing else to be compared against; `gtb` stays absent and is nobody's reason to run anything. Reproducer tests/make/include-remade-by-a-multi-target-pattern-rule matches GNU Make 4.4.1 and its divergence marker is gone.\n\nThe second thing this node's note said a fix owed — GNU's `pattern recipe did not update peer target` warning — is not delivered here and is re-scoped to make-pattern-peer-warning with its own oracle-recorded reproducer. It is a run-time observation printed to stderr, so nothing in the build-intent corpus can gate it and adding it moves upstream inventory rows; that is a decision worth taking deliberately rather than as a rider. The defect this node is named for — the read that never converged — is gone, and the upstream row it flapped on held its recorded value across two full gate runs.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T00:41:40.942980857Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-upstream-parallelism-interleave-flaps
                title "Stabilise the parallel recipe-interleave row in the upstream inventory"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T00:00:56.464322593Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while auditing inventory movement for make-wildcard-cache-coherence, and unrelated to it.\n\n`features/parallelism.diff.1` alternates between `narration ninja-progress` and `narration ninja-progress+recipe-interleave` on consecutive runs of `scripts/check-make-upstream.sh` with nothing changed in between. Reproduced by running the gate twice and diffing the two `--update` outputs: the second run also moved `features/patternrules.diff.34`, which is the already-known flapper, and moved this row back.\n\nThe class does not change — it is narration either way — so this is not a compiler question. The family does, and the gate fails on family drift, which means every dispatch that touches Make now has to decide whether this row moved for its own reason. That is the cost worth removing.\n\nThe case is a `-j` test, and `recipe-interleave` is recognised by line order: \"Make interleaves each recipe line with the output of running it, and Ronin runs the recipe as one script\". Under parallelism the order the classifier reads is a race, so the fix is on the classifier's side rather than the executor's — either the family is not decidable for a parallel case and should not be assigned, or it needs an order-insensitive comparison. Third known flapper, after `features/reinvoke.diff.2` and `features/patternrules.diff.34`; the three probably want one answer between them.\n\nReproducer: `scripts/sandboxed scripts/check-make-upstream.sh --update` twice, diffing `tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv` after each."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:03:54.320557085Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Upstream-residue triage (2026-08-14) revisited the flapping rows while reclassifying the inventory, and reports no change needed here.\n\nAcross two full runs from fresh disposable worktrees the three watched rows held their class: features/parallelism.diff.1 narration, features/patternrules.diff.34 and features/reinvoke.diff.2 unclassified. The verification run exited 0 against the recording run, so `settled()` did not have to absorb a flip in either direction.\n\nTwo of the three did change their family list, and not by flapping: patternrules.diff.34 and reinvoke.diff.2 both gained `recipe-echo`. That is the triage's new evidence path — Ninja's progress counter carries the command it is about to run, so the counter's payload cancels the recipe line Make echoed — and it fires deterministically. `settled()` still only forgives `recipe-interleave`, which is correct and was not widened.\n\nSo the standing question this node holds — whether `recipe-interleave` needs a family of its own or a sturdier comparison — is unchanged by the reclassification. The count fell from 4 rows to 4 rows; nothing here was resolved by accident."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T18:23:11.607497176Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T18:24:01.280491505Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. `features/parallelism.diff.1` is `narration ninja-progress` and stays there; three consecutive full runs of the gate wrote byte-identical inventories.\n\nWHERE THE COIN WAS FLIPPED. `classify` ends with three branches over what neither narration accounted for: equal line-for-line means nothing is left, equal-once-sorted means `recipe-interleave`, anything else means `evaluation`. The middle branch is the only place in the classifier where the ORDER of two lines decides anything, and that reading is only available to a run where the order was somebody's decision. Under `-j` it is the scheduler's. So two runs of the same case with nothing changed landed either side of the same test and the inventory recorded whichever it saw.\n\nWHAT THE CASE ACTUALLY IS, because it is not the obvious one. The `.run` file for parallelism.diff.1 carries no `-j` at all:\n\n  .../bin/make -f work/features/parallelism.mk.1\n\nThe jobs are in the makefile — `MAKEFLAGS += -j4` on its first line — because that run_make_test is the suite checking that a makefile CAN ask for them. A fix that read the command line alone would have missed this row entirely and fixed nothing.\n\nTHE FIX. `Source` gains `concurrent`, read the same way `debug` already is: from the invoking command line, or from a line of the makefile that names MAKEFLAGS and carries a `-j`. `runs_concurrently` reads the count where one is written — `-j`, `--jobs` and `--jobs=10` are concurrency, `-j1` and `--jobs=1` are the sequential default said out loud and are not, and a separate word after `-j` is a target rather than a count because GNU's option takes its argument attached. The classifier's tail then reads `same_lines || (permuted && source.concurrent)` as \"accounted for\", so a concurrent case gets the same answer whichever order its lines arrived in and never draws the family from that order. It is a declining rather than an assertion: both readings say the same lines came out of both tools, and a concurrent run is simply not entitled to the further claim about which came out first. A SEQUENTIAL case still gets `recipe-interleave`, which is where the family means something — Make interleaves each recipe line with what running it printed and Ronin runs the recipe as one script, and that is a property of the two tools.\n\nEVIDENCE, and the direct probe rather than only the runs. A minimal work directory holding the case's real `.run` and real `.mk` beside a synthesised `.diff` whose two sides are a permutation of each other:\n\n  makefile present (MAKEFLAGS += -j4)   narration  ninja-progress\n  makefile hidden                       narration  ninja-progress+recipe-interleave\n\nwhich is the old behaviour and the new one measured side by side on the same diff, and says the makefile-derived detection is what fires for this row rather than something else being different.\n\nThen three consecutive `scripts/check-make-upstream.sh --update` runs from a clean tree: 1287 rows, 169 unclassified / 0 compiler / 41 interface / 1077 narration each time, and `diff` of the three recorded tsv files empty both ways. The node's own reproducer was two runs and a diff; this is three.\n\nROWS THAT MOVED: one. `targets/WAIT.diff.11` — the `-j10` case that was the first flapper anyone noticed — drops `recipe-interleave` and records as `ninja-progress`. The three sequential cases that carry the family keep it: features/se_explicit.diff.22 and .25, features/se_statpat.diff.8. The other two watched flappers are unmoved and did not flap: features/patternrules.diff.34 stays unclassified with `evaluation`, features/reinvoke.diff.2 stays narration with `recipe-echo`.\n\n`settled()` IS KEPT, and its doc now says what it is for. Its subject is gone — no concurrent case can be assigned the family it forgives — so it is a backstop rather than the answer, for order nondeterminism with some cause other than `-j`. Removing it would make the gate strictly stricter and was not taken: the node's complaint is that a dispatch has to explain a row that moved for nobody's reason, and a forgiveness that never fires costs nothing while a gate that fails on a rerun costs exactly that.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 803 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored (856 with --all-targets); the classifier's own tests 19 -> 21, the two new ones pinning the concurrent reading and the job-count parse. No product code touched."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T18:32:58.130669095Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-multi-target-pattern-trigger-identity
                title "Name the out-of-date target in a multi-target pattern rule's $@"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T00:40:12.084104601Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while completing make-grouped-pattern-targets, and deliberately not folded into it: the grouping question is about which names one edge owns, and this one is about which of them the recipe is told it is making. Different mechanism, and the first is settled without it.\n\nGNU Make's `$@` in a pattern rule with several target patterns is the name whose own state reached the rule, which need not be the first one the rule writes. Measured against the 4.4.1 oracle:\n\n    all: a.c a.h\n    %.c %.h: %.in ; echo 'at=$@' >> log ; cat $< > $*.c ; cat $< > $*.h\n\nwith `a.in` old, `a.c` current and `a.h` absent, GNU considers `a.c` first, finds it up to date, moves on to `a.h`, and runs the recipe with `$@` = `a.h`. Ronin runs the same recipe with `$@` = `a.c`. From scratch — both absent — the two agree on the first name, which is why every other cell of the matrix gates today.\n\nReproducer, tests/make/multi-target-pattern-trigger-is-the-name-that-was-out-of-date, recorded from GNU Make 4.4.1 and carrying a divergence marker.\n\nThe rule that reproduces GNU's answer is small: among the edge's outputs that decide its freshness (`Graph::peer_outputs` names the ones that do not), in walk order, the trigger is the first that is missing or older than the newest ordinary input. What is not small is binding it. `$@` is substituted into the recipe when kati compiles it, so a run-time answer needs the automatic variable bound at launch rather than at compile: src/make/recipe.rs already expands a held recipe as its edge starts, so the deferral path exists, but the edge would have to be forced onto it and kati told which output to bind. That reaches every recipe's expansion, which is why it is a node and not a paragraph.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T14:50:17.413107066Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle over nineteen cells, and the matrix separates two rules that look like one.\n\nA PATTERN rule with several target patterns — `$@` is the first name, in the order the walk reached them, whose OWN state has to be made:\n\n  cell (`%.c %.h: %.in`)                                    GNU 4.4.1\n  `all: a.c a.h`, a.c current, a.h absent                   a.h\n  `all: a.c a.h`, both absent                               a.c\n  `all: a.c a.h`, a.h current, a.c absent                   a.c\n  `all: a.c a.h`, both present, both behind a.in            a.c\n  `all: a.h a.c`, a.c current, a.h absent                   a.h\n  `all: a.c a.h`, both present, only a.h behind a.in        a.h\n  `all: a.h` alone, a.c current and never asked for         a.h\n  `all: a.c` alone, a.c current, a.h absent                 nothing to be done\n  reached through `x: a.c` and `y: a.h`, either order       a.h\n  `%.c %.h &: %.in`, and `%.c %.h:: %.in`                   a.h — identical to the plain form\n  three patterns, only the middle name absent               a.h\n  `$*` in every one of the above                            the stem, unchanged\n\nAn EXPLICIT group is the other rule, and it is not the same one:\n\n  `a.c a.h &: a.in`, `all: a.c a.h`, a.c current, a.h absent   a.c\n  the same with `all: a.h a.c`                                 a.h\n  `all: a.h` alone                                             a.h\n\nSo an explicit `&:` binds `$@` to the member that was REACHED, whatever state it was in — one decision over all its targets, which is what make-grouped-pattern-targets recorded and what Ronin already did. A pattern rule binds it to the member that had to be MADE. Two target patterns are not a group; they are one recipe over targets that each decide for themselves, and this is the cell where saying so has an observable consequence.\n\nTwo cells fall out for free and are worth writing down. From scratch every name is out of date, so the first is both the first reached and the first stale and the two readings coincide — which is why ten fixtures gated before this node and only one did not. And `$*` is the stem, the same for every member, so it does not follow the choice at all: it is recorded in every fixture precisely to say that it does not move.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. `$@` is `recipe_output` read out of the node while the recipe is expanded, and the whole of this node is expanding late enough to know the answer. `src/graph/peer.rs` gains `trigger_output`: among the edge's outputs that are not peers — `Graph::peer_outputs` already names the ones nobody asked for, and those cannot be why anything ran — in the edge's own output order, the first that is absent or behind the newest ordinary input, and the first of them when none is. `bind_late_command` computes it as the edge launches and passes it beside the output that was already crossing that boundary; the response file stays named after the edge, because a response file belongs to the edge rather than to the run.\n\nkati's `DeferredRecipes::expand` takes the name and binds `recipe_output` to it, guarded by a new `DepNode::pattern_group` — set where the implicit search enters a rule's other target patterns as this node's outputs, and false for an explicit group, which is exactly the division the matrix above found. Without that guard the explicit `&:` cells go the wrong way, which is how the guard was arrived at rather than assumed.\n\nThe edge's output order is what the walk order becomes: kati makes the name it reached first the node's own output and the rest implicit outputs, so `all: a.h a.c` and `all: a.c a.h` produce different orders over the same rule, and the reversal cell gates that.\n\nBeing run for a reason no timestamp carries — a rebuilt prerequisite, a changed command — falls to the first name, which is GNU's answer too: that is the first name reached deciding it must be made because a dependency changed.\n\nMarker: tests/make/multi-target-pattern-trigger-is-the-name-that-was-out-of-date matches GNU Make 4.4.1 and its divergence file is gone.\n\nFixtures, six more recorded beside it: multi-target-pattern-trigger-is-the-first-when-every-name-is-out-of-date, -is-the-stale-one-among-present-names, -follows-the-order-the-names-were-reached, -reached-through-separate-dependents, -passes-over-a-peer-nobody-asked-for, -among-three-target-patterns; and grouped-rule-trigger-is-the-member-that-was-reached, which is the explicit-group control and the reason the guard is a claim rather than a coincidence. One Ronin test asserts `trigger_output` on a compiled edge under three arrangements of the same files and says a parsed manifest carries no such distinction.\n\nWHAT IS NOT DELIVERED, and is filed rather than left silent. A recipe naming `$?` cannot be expanded at launch, so it never reaches the binding above — and it turns out it never reaches the build either: the deferred-freshness path takes every output of such an edge out of the scan's stat loop, so an absent second target pattern is invisible and the recipe does not run at all. Confirmed pre-existing at f221c81, so this node neither caused nor touched it, and filed as make-deferred-freshness-hides-a-multi-target-peer with its own reproducer. Every fixture here uses a recipe with no `$?` in it, which is exactly the line the two nodes divide on.\n\nAlso found beside it and unrelated: `$*` for an EXPLICIT rule whose target ends in a known suffix is the basename in GNU and empty in Ronin, again pre-existing at f221c81. Filed as make-explicit-rule-stem-from-a-known-suffix.\n\nUpstream inventory: no row moved on this node's account."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T14:51:53.132682588Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-mixed-implicit-and-normal-rule-severity
                title "Warn and continue for a rule mixing pattern and plain targets"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T01:05:12.449250182Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-static-pattern-nonmatch-semantics; kept out of that node because it is a different diagnostic in a different function, decided before any target pattern exists.\n\nGNU 4.4.1 spells this two ways and means two different severities. read.c:2126 is `fatal` \"mixed implicit and normal rules\" — a pattern rule whose target list has a plain member, which really does stop. read.c:2310 is `error` \"*** mixed implicit and normal rules: deprecated syntax\" — a plain rule whose target list has an unescaped `%` — and `error` prints and returns, so the read continues and make exits 0 having built everything else.\n\nRonin makes the second fatal. kati/src-rs/eval.rs:1368 raises `error_loc!` as soon as a target list is neither all patterns nor none, which covers both of GNU's cases with the harsher one.\n\nReproducer, where GNU exits 0 having written `out` and Ronin exits 2 having written nothing:\n\n    all: ; printf done > out\n    mismatch.c mismatch.dep %.c: ; touch $@\n\nOracle:  Makefile:2: *** mixed implicit and normal rules: deprecated syntax / printf done > out / exit 0\nRonin:   ronin: Makefile:2: mixed implicit and normal rules: deprecated syntax / exit 2\n\nWorth checking while fixing: GNU reduces escaped percents in the surviving target names on that path, so the continuing case needs the target list it leaves behind checked, not just the exit status."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T17:37:14.091933293Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Consolidation, settled before any code was written. This node and make-mixed-implicit-and-normal-rules are one defect filed twice, five hours apart, by two probes that arrived at the same `error_loc!`.\n\nThe other node was filed 2026-08-13 out of a default-goal probe, titled \"Read a mixed implicit and normal rule as deprecated rather than fatal\", with the reproducer `a %.o: ; @echo mixed` — GNU prints the deprecation on stderr, records `a` as the default goal, runs the recipe and exits 0; Ronin prints the same sentence and dies with status 2. It also carried one claim that the oracle disproves: that \"GNU keeps the normal targets and drops the pattern ones\". It does not drop them. `%.o` becomes an ordinary target spelt with a percent in it, carrying the same recipe and the same prerequisites, and `make '%.o'` runs it.\n\nThis node is the same defect characterised correctly and further: it names both of GNU's sites (read.c:2126 and read.c:2310), both severities, the exact kati site that collapses them, and the escaped-percent question the continuing path raises. Everything the other node had is here or is wrong.\n\nSo the other node is removed rather than completed beside this one. Two nodes for one defect is two things to read and one of them misleading; keeping the accurate one and recording what the removed one contributed is the shape that leaves the plan smaller and no less true. Its reproducer is not lost — it is the fixture mixed-rule-deprecated-name-is-an-ordinary-target, recorded from the oracle."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T17:37:24.328946357Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T17:51:55.937225474Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "The matrix, established against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle before anything was written. Every cell was run through both binaries.\n\nWHICH SEVERITY A LINE REACHES is decided by which kind of target came FIRST, which the filing did not say and which is the whole shape of the fix. GNU reads `filenames->name`, asks `find_percent_cached`, and branches on the answer before it looks at any other target.\n\n  a %.o:        `Makefile:1: *** mixed implicit and normal rules: deprecated syntax`, exit 0, `a` built\n  %.o a:        `Makefile:1: *** mixed implicit and normal rules.  Stop.`, exit 2, nothing built\n\nNote the two sentences differ: the fatal one has no `: deprecated syntax`. read.c:2126 and read.c:2310, and the message at 2126 is not historical — it is what 4.4.1 prints today.\n\nWHAT THE DEPRECATED NAME BECOMES, which is the cell the removed sibling node had wrong. It is not dropped. `find_percent_cached` reduces it, `enter_file` records it, `f->cmds = cmds` and `f->is_target = 1` — an ordinary target spelt with a percent in it:\n\n  a %.o: ; @printf 'ran %s\\n' '$@' > out, goal `%.o`   runs, $@ is `%.o`\n  all: foo.o beside a %.o: rule                        No rule to make target 'foo.o'. Not a pattern\n  a %.o: b                                             `a` is made from `b`. Recipe and prerequisites both shared\n  a %.o %.d:                                           the diagnostic twice, once per offending name\n  a %.o::                                              same deprecation, reads on\n  a foo%bar:                                           a percent anywhere in the name, not only leading\n  .a %.o:                                              deprecation, then `No targets` — a name with any `%` in it\n                                                       BREAKS the default-goal loop (read.c:1940) rather than\n                                                       being skipped, so `a` in `\\%.o a:` never becomes the goal\n\nTHE OTHER HALF WAS READING THE PERCENT AT ALL. GNU asks `find_percent`, never \"is there a percent here\": a backslash escapes the wildcard and the escaping is compressed out of the recorded name.\n\n  b\\%c d: ; ...          two plain targets, no diagnostic, exit 0.  Ronin was fatal\n  %.o \\%.x: ; ...        pattern rule with a plain target: FATAL.   Ronin built it happily\n  \\%.o a: ; ...          two plain targets, no diagnostic\n  \\%.o: V = one          an ordinary target-specific binding on `%.o`\n\nRonin got all four wrong, in both directions, because `is_pattern_rule` is `memchr(b'%')`.\n\nTHE CELL THAT MOVED THE CODE. GNU says nothing at all about a mixed target list on a target-specific assignment, in either order:\n\n  a %.o: V = one         silent, both bindings land, exit 0\n  %.o a: V = one         silent, both bindings land, exit 0.       Ronin was fatal\n\nBecause both diagnostics live in `record_files`, which a variable definition never reaches. So the check cannot stay in `parse_rule_targets`, which runs before kati knows whether the line is a rule. The parse now carries out what it found — `RuleTargets { targets, is_pattern_rule, disagreeing }` — and `check_mixed_targets` raises it in `eval_rule` after the assignment branch, beside the `prerequisites cannot be defined in recipes` refusal, which is there for exactly the same reason and which GNU also puts at the top of `record_files`. That ordering is GNU's too: `snapped_deps` is refused first, then the percent is read.\n\nONE NEIGHBOURING SENTENCE CORRECTED. `%.o: %.q: %.c` is GNU's THIRD site, read.c:2103, and it says `mixed implicit and static pattern rules`. rule.rs answered it with the plain-target sentence, naming a defect the line does not have. Same severity, same family, one string literal — corrected here rather than filed, because shipping a fix for this family that leaves a sibling misnamed is worse than the scope it saves. It cannot be fixtured: the build-intent harness compares files, both tools are fatal, and the message is the whole difference.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT, in kati d49463e on branch nplan/make-mixed-implicit-and-normal-rule-severity. `strutil::find_percent` was already there for `Pattern` and only needed exposing. `parse_rule_targets` reduces every target name through it and records whether a wildcard survived; `RuleTargets` carries the first target's answer and how many later ones disagreed; `check_mixed_targets` is the two severities. `is_pattern_rule` is no longer used in eval.rs.\n\nFIXTURES, nine, all recorded from the oracle: mixed-rule-deprecated-name-is-an-ordinary-target (the removed sibling's reproducer, carried forward), -reads-on-and-builds-what-follows (this node's own), -with-a-pattern-first-stops-the-read, -escaped-percent-is-an-ordinary-target, -escaped-percent-does-not-complete-a-pattern-rule, -shares-the-recipe-and-the-prerequisites, -target-specific-assignment-is-not-a-rule, -double-colon-is-deprecated-too, -deprecated-name-is-not-a-pattern. Each was written so the harness can see it: a case where both tools merely refuse is not a gate, so the ones that turn on a refusal were given a goal above them that is or is not reached.\n\nROW AUDIT. Two moved, both this change's, both toward Make: features/statipattrules.diff.66 and .67, unclassified `product-name+no-work-line+evaluation` -> narration `ninja-progress+no-work-line`. They are the last two cases in GNU's own statipattrules script, the pair headed \"Escaped %\", and they are the escaped-percent reading exactly. Nothing else moved. The compiler bucket stays at ZERO: 1287 rows at 226 unclassified / 0 compiler / 41 interface / 1020 narration.\n\nONE CELL LEFT DIVERGENT AND FILED. `a b: \\%.o: %.c` — GNU refuses with `target pattern contains no '%'` and Ronin accepts. That is `find_percent` deciding whether a static rule's target pattern is a pattern at all, a different question in a different function, and make-static-pattern-escaped-percent has it with the reproducer.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 803 passed; clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic/nursery --no-deps clean and kati -D warnings clean; make_port 4/4 over 736 cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 318 identical / 69 differing, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles, 278 graphs, 0 disagreements, unmoved; real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git in 22s."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T17:52:01.750320257Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-pattern-search-specific-rule-match
                title "Let a specific pattern reject a match-anything rule"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T01:40:28.996671844Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-pattern-rule-cancellation's matrix; pre-existing and untouched by it.\n\nGNU's `pattern_search` keeps a flag, `specific_rule_matched` (implicit.c:264). Any pattern rule target that is not a bare `%` sets it when it matches the name (implicit.c:405), including a rule with neither prerequisites nor a recipe — implicit.c:409 says in so many words that such a rule \"exists solely to set specific_rule_matched when it matches\". Once the candidate list is sorted, implicit.c:435 walks it again and strikes out every non-terminal rule that has a bare `%` target. So a specific pattern that matched, even one that cannot make anything, withdraws the match-anything rules from that search.\n\nProbed against 4.4.1 with -r, the two forms come apart exactly where the flag says they should:\n\n  `%: ; @printf anymatch > $@` with `%.tex:` beside it, goal `all: foo.tex` — GNU: `*** No rule to make target 'foo.tex'`. The bare `%.tex:` matched, set the flag, and the `%` rule was struck out.\n\n  the same with `%.tex: %.w` instead — GNU builds foo.tex with the `%` recipe. A rule with prerequisites and no recipe is skipped at implicit.c:320, one line before the flag could be set, so it never reaches the target loop and the `%` rule survives.\n\nRonin builds foo.tex from `%` in both, so the first probe is the reproducer. `matches_anything` in kati/src-rs/dep.rs already names the bare-`%` test; what is missing is the pass that uses it against a specific match, and the fact that a recipe-less rule with no prerequisites still has to be walked far enough to set the flag even though `ordered_candidates` now refuses to collect it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T08:08:01.663725076Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Probed from make-match-anything-rule-chaining (2026-08-14) and left open: unmoved in either direction by that node's delivery. Both of this node's probes behave identically before and after it, so the two are orthogonal in fact as well as in intent — the compatibility pass decides whether a *prerequisite* is acceptable, and this node decides which *candidates* are collected at all, one stage earlier.\n\nWorth recording that the widening does raise this node's stakes rather than lower them. The compatibility pass takes a prerequisite the Makefile merely writes down, and a bare `%` rule matches every name there is, so a `%` rule that GNU would have struck out here is now more likely to find a prerequisite it can take on trust and apply. No inventory row moved that way in this delivery — the movement audit accounts for all sixteen — but the exposure is new and this is the node that closes it.\n\n`ordered_candidates` in kati/src-rs/dep.rs is still the place: it drops every rule with no recipe before the flag could be set, which is the half implicit.c:409 says has to be walked anyway."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T09:41:15.734215542Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered by make-builtin-rule-catalogue (2026-08-14), because the catalogue cannot be installed without it: GNU's declared suffixes ARE this node's shape — a pattern rule with neither prerequisites nor a recipe, whose only effect is to be matched — and 35 of them arrive with the built-in list.\n\n`ordered_candidates` in kati/src-rs/dep.rs now walks the candidates GNU walks rather than only the ones it can use. The order is implicit.c's: a rule with prerequisites and no recipe is passed over first (cancelled, and never counted as a match), then one a search further out is already working through, then the pattern is matched. A match whose target is not a bare `%` sets the flag; a rule with no recipe is then dropped from the list as before. If the flag is set, every non-terminal candidate with a bare `%` target is struck out, wherever it sits in the order — implicit.c:435.\n\nBoth of this node's probes now agree, which is the pair it was filed on:\n\n  `%: ; @printf anymatch > $@` with a bare `%.tex:` beside it, goal `all: foo.tex`, under `-r` — both refuse. Recorded as tests/make/pattern-search-specific-rule-strikes-a-match-anything-rule.\n  the same with `%.tex: %.w` — both build foo.tex from `%`, because a rule with prerequisites and no recipe is a cancellation and is passed over before it can count as a match. Recorded as tests/make/pattern-search-cancelled-rule-strikes-nothing.\n\n`-r` is on both cases deliberately: it takes the built-in disqualifiers out so the only rule doing the striking is the one written in the makefile. The built-in half is recorded separately as builtin-suffix-withdraws-a-match-anything-rule, where `.c` being a suffix is the whole reason `foo.c` has no rule.\n\nThe stakes this node's last note recorded — that the compatibility pass makes a surviving `%` rule likelier to find a prerequisite it can take on trust — are now closed rather than merely smaller."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T09:42:51.576358131Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recipe-expanded-newline
                title "Establish what an expanded newline does to a recipe line"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T02:21:32.803250236Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-continuation-expansion. GNU Make splits a recipe into command lines at read time, so a newline that only appears once a variable is expanded stays inside the command line that referenced it and reaches the shell as a newline. Ronin splits the expanded buffer instead (kati/src-rs/command.rs, find_end_of_line over the evaluated recipe), so the same newline becomes a second command joined with ` && `.\n\nReproducer, where GNU Make 4.4.1 fails with an unterminated quoted string and Ronin succeeds with a value that is not GNU's:\n\n    define NL\n\n\n    endef\n    all:\n    <tab>@printf '%s' 'a$(NL)b' | od -c > out\n\nSame defect from a define body carrying an even backslash run before its newline (`define A` / `a\\\\` / `b` / `endef`), and from `$\\<newline>` written in a recipe, where the reference to the variable named `\\` leaves the newline behind."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T15:49:01.771423477Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T15:53:27.134616904Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The premise this node was filed on is false, and probing it against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make is what showed that. Nothing was changed in the product; what the node produced is the corrected matrix, seven recorded fixtures, and the real defect underneath, filed as make-recipe-line-is-its-own-shell.\n\nWHAT THE NODE SAID. That GNU Make splits a recipe into command lines at read time, so a newline appearing only once a variable is expanded stays inside the command line that referenced it and reaches the shell as a newline.\n\nWHAT GNU MAKE DOES. It splits again after expanding. `new_job` expands each recipe line into `child->command_lines[i]`; `start_job_command` hands that text to `construct_command_argv`, which stops at the first raw newline and writes its position back through `restp`, and `child->command_ptr` is left pointing past it so `job_next_command` picks the remainder up as the next command line. An expansion-produced newline is therefore a command-line boundary exactly as a written one is, and each side of it runs in a shell of its own.\n\nProbed, and the probes are the fixtures: `X=1$(NL)echo \"[$$X]\"` writes `[]`, so the assignment did not survive to the second line; `$(NL)$(NL)` skips the empty line between, because `job_next_command` walks past an empty one; `-` on the recipe line ignores the failure of every line it produced; `.ONESHELL:` keeps them in one shell and `[1]` is written. Two cells are echo and so are narration here: `@` written on the recipe line silences every line the expansion produced, because `lines_flags` is per written line, while an `@` at the start of a later expanded chunk silences only that chunk, because `start_job_command` re-reads the prefix characters at the head of each one.\n\nOne case does not split, and the node's own second shape is it. A backslash immediately before the newline is tested for first — job.c's slow path matches `*p == '\\\\' && p[1] == '\\n'` above its newline case and preserves both bytes — so `define A` / `a\\\\` / `b` / `endef` interpolated into a recipe stays one command line, which is why that shape produced no divergence in either tool. The third shape, `$\\` against a newline written in a recipe, likewise agrees: both tools resolve the reference to the (unset) variable named `\\` and both leave the newline as a boundary.\n\nWHERE RONIN ALREADY AGREES. kati's `CommandEvaluator::eval` runs `find_end_of_line` over the evaluated recipe and drops the lines that came out empty, which is `job_next_command` — so the splitting matches, and so does the per-line prefix parsing. Every cell above matches GNU Make 4.4.1 byte for byte.\n\nWHERE IT DOES NOT, AND WHY IT IS NOT THIS NODE. Ronin joins the command lines into one script as `( a ) && ( b )` and runs one shell. That agrees with GNU Make whenever each line is a complete shell command, which make-recipe-one-shell-per-line established over seven shapes, and disagrees when a line's syntax is left open — the join's own bytes then land inside the quote and make a command out of two fragments. GNU Make fails; Ronin writes a file. The literal-newline counterpart diverges identically, with no expansion anywhere in it, which is the finding: the defect is the joining and not the timing of the split. make-recipe-line-is-its-own-shell owns it, with both reproducers carrying divergence markers that name it.\n\nFIXTURES, all recorded from 4.4.1: expanded-newline-starts-a-new-command-line, expanded-newline-under-one-shell-keeps-the-shell, expanded-newlines-skip-the-empty-lines-between, expanded-newline-keeps-the-lines-ignore-errors, expanded-backslash-newline-stays-in-one-command — five cells where the two tools agree, now held there — and expanded-newline-inside-a-quoted-word plus recipe-newline-inside-a-quoted-word, which carry the divergence."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T15:53:48.055568064Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recipe-continuation-bytes
                title "Hand the shell the backslash and newline a recipe continuation keeps"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T02:21:38.325423978Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-continuation-expansion, which fixed the recipe prefix half of this cell and left the byte fidelity. GNU Make hands the shell both the backslash and the newline of a recipe continuation — read.c's eval copies the recipe line verbatim and only job.c strips one recipe prefix after the newline — so the shell decides what they mean. Outside quotes the shell removes them, which is why most recipes agree; inside single quotes it keeps them, and there Ronin differs.\n\n    all:\n    <tab>@printf '%s' 'a\\\n    <tab>b' | od -c > out\n\nGNU writes four bytes, `a \\ \\n b`. Ronin writes two, `ab`: kati/src-rs/ninja.rs translate_command deletes the pair when it flattens the recipe to one line.\n\nThe flattening is not gratuitous. A Ninja manifest binding cannot carry a newline — there is no escape for one, and `$` before a newline is a continuation that yields nothing — so keeping the bytes means deciding what the retained manifest writer does with a recipe it can no longer spell. That decision, not the graph sink's own command text, is the work here; scripts/check-make-equivalence.sh compares the two paths and would report it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T08:43:02.564612848Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T09:45:09.668765553Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. The filing's mechanism was right and its constraint has been overtaken: make-recipe-line-is-its-own-shell put the launches beside the assembled script, so the manifest's inability to spell a newline is now the manifest's problem alone rather than the whole product's.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, re-read out of GNU Make rather than taken from the filing. `construct_command_argv_internal` (job.c) has two routes and both preserve the pair. The slow path scans the line and tests `*p == '\\\\' && p[1] == '\\n'` before it tests for a bare newline, writing `\\` and `\\n` out under `PRESERVE_BSNL`; the fast path, which builds argv itself, keeps them inside single quotes, drops them inside double quotes — where the shell would have dropped them anyway — and ends the command line only at a bare newline. What the recipe reader edits is one recipe prefix character following the newline and nothing else. So the pair reaches the shell and the shell decides: nothing outside quotes, two characters of text inside them.\n\nTHE MATRIX, eighteen cells, each run through reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and then through Ronin.\n\n  inside single quotes, one tab on the next line     GNU `a \\ <nl> b`; Ronin `ab`        FIXED\n  a second tab on the next line                      the second tab is text              FIXED\n  spaces on the next line                            every one of them is text           FIXED\n  nothing on the next line                           nothing to strip                    FIXED\n  outside quotes                                     both `ab` — the shell removed them  control\n  inside double quotes                               both `ab` — likewise                control\n  two continuations in a row                         two pairs, neither folded           FIXED\n  an empty continuation line                         its own pair as well                FIXED\n  `-` in front of the line                           one logical line: the failure is forgiven and the recipe carries on, on both sides, before and after  FIXED (bytes)\n  `.RECIPEPREFIX = >`                                the `>` goes, a tab is text         FIXED\n  a `;` recipe                                       continued the same way              FIXED\n  `.ONESHELL:`                                       one shell, same bytes               FIXED\n  `.POSIX:`                                          byte for byte identical — the boundary with make-posix-continuation-blanks, which is about a variable's fold and not a recipe's  FIXED (bytes)\n  a comment after the continued line                 unmoved                             FIXED\n  a continuation ending the last line of a recipe    both `ab` — the shell discards it   control, and B's teeth\n  a continuation mid-word outside quotes             both `ab`                           control\n  an odd run of three backslashes                    GNU keeps all three and the newline; Ronin kept two  FIXED\n  an even run of two                                 both refuse: the shell dies on an unterminated quote  control\n\nAll eighteen now agree.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, all in kati on nplan/make-recipe-continuation-bytes at 42540a345d7928850e5421e3e2f24bc50a79293a, pushed before this pin, plus three call sites in Ronin. `translate_command`'s newline arm writes the byte instead of deleting the pair, and the trailing trim stops at a newline — taking the newline off a continuation that ends a recipe would leave the shell an escape with nothing after it.\n\nTHE MANIFEST DECISION, which the filing named as the work. A `build.ninja` binding holds one line: a raw newline ends the value and no escape puts one back, and `rspfile_content` is a binding too. So the retained writer cannot spell a continued recipe at all, and `single_line` is what it writes instead — the pair removed, which outside quotes is exactly what the shell would have done and inside quotes is a manifest that builds something else. Refusing was considered and rejected: a continuation outside quotes is ordinary in real makefiles and the manifest is right about every one of them.\n\nThe equivalence gate would have reported the two paths disagreeing on the `command` binding for the six corpus makefiles that continue a recipe. It now judges those the way it already judges the writer's ignore-errors muting: `SemanticCommand` is recorded at the common sink boundary, where the script is the same on both sides, and the destination-specific spelling is not required to be identical. The count is printed — `manifest respellings: 8` — rather than left to be inferred from a silence.\n\nNARRATION IS ONE LINE, and that is what kept the upstream inventory from moving the wrong way. A description is Ninja's one-line-per-edge narration wherever it is printed, so all three sites that take a description from the recipe's own text — the graph sink's rule bindings, its recipe-lines bindings, and the launch-time `LateCommand` — take `single_line` of it. The command keeps the recipe's bytes; the progress line stays one line. Measured: without this the inventory went 107 → 122 unclassified, because `recipe-echo` cannot cancel a Make echo against a progress payload that has become two lines.\n\nTEETH, each parked and the whole corpus run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the continuation pair is deleted rather than passed on | 9 of 1,026 |\n| the trailing trim crosses a kept newline | 1 of 1,026 |\n| the writer does not respell the script | 8 equivalence cases, each `only the manifest was refused: build.ninja:N: expected 'command =' line` — the manifest is unparseable |\n| the description is not put back on one line | upstream inventory 102 → 122 unclassified |\n\nTWELVE FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten: a-recipe-continuation-hands-the-shell-both-bytes, a-recipe-continuations-bytes-arrive-with-a-second-prefix, a-recipe-continuation-outside-quotes-is-the-shells-to-remove, a-recipe-continuation-inside-double-quotes-is-the-shells-to-remove, two-recipe-continuations-keep-both-pairs, a-recipe-continuation-is-one-logical-line-for-a-dash, a-configured-recipe-prefix-leaves-the-continuations-bytes, a-semicolon-recipes-continuation-keeps-both-bytes, a-continuation-under-one-shell-keeps-both-bytes, a-posix-makefiles-recipe-continuation-is-unchanged, an-odd-run-of-backslashes-before-a-continuation, a-recipe-ending-in-a-continuation-keeps-it. The seven `recipe-continuation-*` fixtures make-continuation-expansion left are about which prefix is stripped and write through double quotes, so none of them could see the bytes; these are their byte-observing counterparts.\n\nINVENTORY AUDIT. 1,285 rows at 102 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,159 narration, five better than the 107 it stood at. Seven rows moved and every one was read: misc/bs-nl.diff.3, .7, .11 and misc/general3.diff.4, .8 went unclassified → narration, because the residue that made them unclassified was the shell receiving different bytes and there is none now; functions/call.diff and misc/bs-nl.diff.18 stayed narration and gained the `recipe-echo` family, because the recipe's own output changed and Make's echo now cancels against the progress payload rather than against Ronin's copy of the same text. No row moved the wrong way and the compiler bucket stays at zero.\n\nGATES: fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; workspace tests 1005 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, unmoved; make_port 4/4 over 1,026 cases, seven divergence sidecars unmoved; conformance 336 normalised identical / 51 differing, 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 refused / 0 disagreements, with 8 respellings now named; upstream inventory as above; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 15 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 26 s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus 10 differentials, 86 build-outcome cases, 3 invocation-boundary cases, persistence round trip and log content; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 773 passed / 0 failed; `scripts/check-release.sh` green end to end."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T09:45:17.469884809Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-posix-continuation-blanks
                title "Keep the blanks a continuation follows when .POSIX: is in force"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T02:21:47.471538436Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-continuation-expansion. GNU Make's collapse_continuations (misc.c) discards the blanks already written before a folded newline only when posix_pedantic is off; under `.POSIX:` each backslash-newline becomes a space and the blanks in front of it stay.\n\n    .POSIX:\n    A = a   \\\n      b\n    all: ; @printf '%s' '$(A)' | od -c > out\n\nGNU writes six bytes, `a` and three spaces and the fold's space and `b`. Ronin writes three, `a b`.\n\nkati/src-rs/eval.rs already threads `is_posix` into collapse_rule_continuations for rule text; the variable path folds in kati/src-rs/expr.rs, which runs during parsing and so cannot see a `.POSIX:` the evaluator has not reached yet. GNU sets posix_pedantic when it reads the target, which is why its folding of every later line already knows. Closing this means giving the parser that read-time knowledge, which is why it is its own node rather than part of the fold fix."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T09:48:40.109734982Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T10:31:02.370890973Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. The filing had the mechanism exactly right and named the obstacle exactly right; what it did not have is that the obstacle is smaller than it looks, because the flag has two readers and only one of them is the evaluator.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, read out of misc.c. `collapse_continuations` walks a logical line and, at each odd backslash run before a newline, does three things in order: skip the blanks AFTER the newline, discard the blanks it has ALREADY WRITTEN — but only `if (! posix_pedantic)` — and write one space. So the difference is entirely about what was written before the backslash. Ordinarily a whole run of continuations separated by nothing but blanks collapses to one space, because each fold takes back the space the one before it wrote; under `.POSIX:` nothing is taken back and the run is as long as it was written. The blanks after the newline are not `.POSIX:`'s and go either way.\n\n`check_specials` (read.c) sets `posix_pedantic` the moment a rule names `.POSIX` among its targets, and it reads the names after expansion. GNU Make reads one logical line, folds it, parses it, and only then reads the next, so the flag is in time for every line below the target and no line above it.\n\nTHE MATRIX, twenty-three cells, each run through reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and then through Ronin.\n\n  .POSIX:, `A = a   \\` over `  b`        GNU `a` + 3 spaces + the fold's = 4; Ronin 1  FIXED\n  the same without .POSIX:               both 1 — the control\n  `.POSIX:` written BELOW the assignment both 1 — read-time, not retroactive; control\n  two folds in a row under .POSIX:       GNU 4 spaces; Ronin 1                         FIXED\n  two folds in a row without it          both 1 — the control for the run\n  blanks on the far side of the newline  both drop them — the control that must not move\n  a tab in front of the backslash        `ISBLANK` is space or tab; GNU keeps it       FIXED\n  `A := `, `A += `                       the same in every assignment flavour          FIXED\n  a fold inside a function call          likewise                                      FIXED\n  a `define` body                        read line by line like anything else          FIXED\n  a `$` against the fold                 the reference takes the space the newline became, and under .POSIX: the SECOND fold's space survives it as text  FIXED\n  an odd run of three backslashes        half survives and is not a blank, so the discarding stops at it in both  control\n  a target-specific assignment           already right — the rule path had `is_posix`  control\n  a prerequisite list                    no cell: `$^` rejoins the words with single spaces\n  an `ifeq` argument                     no cell: the conditional strips its arguments\n  a recipe                               byte for byte identical with and without `.POSIX:` — make-recipe-continuation-bytes' boundary, and it holds\n  `.FEATURES`                            no cell: GNU Make 4.4.1 does not list posix at all\n  `.POSIX:` in the includer, the fold in the included file    GNU folds POSIX; Ronin did not   FIXED\n  `.POSIX:` in the first `-f` makefile, the fold in the second GNU folds POSIX; Ronin did not   FIXED\n  `$(P):` with `P = .POSIX`, then an include holding the fold  GNU folds POSIX; Ronin did not   FIXED\n  a `$(eval)` above a later `.POSIX:`    both fold the old way — the fold happened in the outer parse; control\n  one file included twice with `.POSIX:` set between  both agree\n  `$(P):` or an included `.POSIX:` reaching a line ALREADY READ   still divergent, filed\n\nTwenty-one of the twenty-three now agree; the two that do not are make-a-posix-target-is-read-where-it-is-written.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, in kati on nplan/make-posix-continuation-blanks at 828ebc5bffc3cc7d8be5a45f1438d93f68a58da2, pushed before this pin. `posix_pedantic` is a field of `Session` rather than of `Evaluator`, because it is a property of the READ and the read is what the parser does: `parse_expr_impl_ext` reads it once per line, the trailing-blank trim is taken under it, and `skip_folded` stops absorbing a run under it so each fold in the run is read on its own and writes its own space. `Parser::note_posix_target` sets it off a rule's written target names, as `check_specials` does; the evaluator sets the same flag beside its own `is_posix`, which is what carries an expansion-spelled `.POSIX` to whatever is read after it.\n\nThe filing's obstacle — \"the parser cannot see a `.POSIX:` the evaluator has not reached yet\" — is true only for a name the parse cannot read. A name written down is one the parser can read for itself, and that is the case that matters.\n\nTEETH, each parked and the whole corpus run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the trailing blanks go under `.POSIX:` too | 7 of 1,039 |\n| the parser does not read `.POSIX` off a target | 5 of 1,039 |\n| a run of folds is absorbed under `.POSIX:` too | 2 of 1,039 |\n| the evaluator does not set the read flag | 1 of 1,039 |\n\nTHIRTEEN FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten: a-posix-fold-keeps-the-blanks-in-front-of-it, a-fold-outside-posix-takes-the-blanks-with-it, a-posix-target-does-not-reach-back-up-the-file, two-posix-folds-are-two-spaces, two-folds-outside-posix-are-one-space, a-posix-fold-still-drops-the-blanks-after-it, a-posix-fold-keeps-a-tab-in-front-of-it, a-posix-define-body-folds-the-same-way, a-posix-fold-in-a-file-included-below-it, a-posix-target-in-an-earlier-named-makefile, a-dollar-against-a-posix-fold, a-posix-fold-after-an-odd-backslash-run, a-posix-target-an-expansion-named-reaches-a-later-include.\n\nINVENTORY AUDIT. 1,285 rows at 100 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,161 narration. Two rows moved, both of them this node's subject: misc/bs-nl.diff.16 and .17 went unclassified → narration, because the residue that made them unclassified was the folded value differing and there is none now. Nothing moved the wrong way and the compiler bucket stays at zero.\n\nGATES: fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; workspace tests 1005 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, unmoved; make_port 4/4 over 1,039 cases, seven divergence sidecars unmoved; conformance 336 normalised identical / 51 differing, 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 refused / 0 disagreements / 8 respellings; upstream inventory as above; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 15 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27 s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus 10 differentials, 86 build-outcome cases, 3 invocation-boundary cases, persistence round trip and log content; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 773 passed / 0 failed; `scripts/check-release.sh` green end to end on the second attempt — the first refused on `dependency-log-load Ronin/Ninja ratio regressed to 0.92x from the recorded 0.73x`, measured while the host carried a 15-minute load average of 7.2. Two runs of the performance gate on the quieter host that followed put the ratio at 0.80 and 0.73 and both exited 0."
                    }

