                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-makefiles-variable
                title "Define MAKEFILES and read the makefiles it names"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T04:29:29.179792155Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while completing make-export-scope-semantics, and left out of it because the export half is the smaller half.\n\nGNU Make defines `MAKEFILES` in its built-in catalogue — `define_variable_cname (\"MAKEFILES\", \"\", o_default, 0)` in `src/variable.c` — and gives it the one export attribute nothing else uses, `v_ifset`: exported once it holds something that is not the built-in default. It then reads the makefiles the variable names before the ones the invocation asked for.\n\nReproducer, against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n    $(info [$(origin MAKEFILES)])\n    all: ; @echo \"MF=[$$MAKEFILES]\"\n\nGNU prints `[default]`; Ronin prints `[undefined]`. The environment is the same in both — an unset `MAKEFILES` reaches no child either way — so the divergence visible today is `$(origin)` alone. The reading half was not probed here.\n\nThe export machinery this needs is written and was removed rather than left unconstructible: `VarExport::IfSet` and its arm in `kati::export::should_export`, which is `return origin != VarOrigin::Default`. Defining the variable without the reading behaviour would be worse than leaving both, so this node owns both halves."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T21:28:23.434990385Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T21:28:55.060009384Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised against the pristine oracle before anything moved. Both halves the node owned are done, and the reading half — which the filing had not probed — turned out to be five separate rules rather than one.\n\nTHE EXPORT HALF. GNU's `v_ifset` is decided on the ORIGIN, not on the value, which is the question the dispatch asked to settle. variable.c should_export:\n\n    case v_ifset:\n      if (v->origin == o_default)\n        return 0;\n      break;\n\nand that is the whole arm — it does not consult the name filter and it does not consult `export_all_variables`, both of which the `v_default` arm below it does. So the test is \"has anything but the built-in catalogue touched this name\", and emptiness has nothing to do with it. Probed, GNU and Ronin now agreeing on every cell:\n\n  untouched (o_default)          not in the child's environment\n  `MAKEFILES =` in a makefile    IS in the child's environment, as an empty string\n  `MAKEFILES = later.mk`         exported\n  from the environment           exported\n  from the command line          exported\n  `export MAKEFILES` alone       exported — the directive writes v_export, which outranks the arm\n  `unexport MAKEFILES`           withheld\n\nThe second cell is the one that proves it is origin and not value: an empty assignment in a makefile is a file-origin definition and reaches the child, where the identical empty built-in does not.\n\nTHE DEFINITION. `define_variable_cname (\"MAKEFILES\", \"\", o_default, 0)` sits in `define_automatic_variables`, not in the default-variables catalogue, and that is observable: `make -R` still answers `default` for `$(origin MAKEFILES)` and still reads what the variable names. Verified against the oracle for `-r` and `-R` both. The value is simple and empty, so `$(flavor MAKEFILES)` is `simple` — where the rest of the catalogue is recursive.\n\nTHE READING HALF, which the filing left unprobed. read.c read_all_makefiles reads each word with `RM_NO_DEFAULT_GOAL|RM_INCLUDED|RM_DONTCARE`, before the `-f` files and before the default makefile. Five rules fall out and all were probed:\n\n  order          the named files are read first, in the order written, into one evaluation, so a variable one sets is in scope while the invocation's own makefile is read\n  MAKEFILE_LIST  each name is appended as its file opens, so they stand in front of the makefile the invocation named, and a file reading the variable sees itself last. A name given twice is read twice and listed twice\n  default goal   a target one of these files declares NEVER becomes the default goal — but a `.DEFAULT_GOAL :=` written in one DOES take effect, because that is an assignment and not the implicit first-target rule. Both cells verified\n  missing file   passed over in silence, rc 0, and not listed in MAKEFILE_LIST\n  unreadable     NOT forgiven. `MAKEFILES=.` is `make: *** .: Is a directory.  Stop.`, rc 2 — RM_DONTCARE forgives absence and nothing else\n\nand two neighbours: a goal naming a target one of these files declares is built normally when asked for by name, and `-f other.mk` still reads the MAKEFILES files first. A makefile writing to MAKEFILES is too late to be read, which is why the variable is only useful from the environment or the command line — verified, and it is why the corpus cases drive it as a command-line assignment.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, all in kati.\n\nvar.rs gained `VarExport::IfSet`, the variant that was written and removed under make-export-scope-semantics, and export.rs's `should_export` answers it with `origin != VarOrigin::Default` before the name filter is reached — GNU's arm, in GNU's position.\n\nevaluate.rs gained `install_makefiles_variable`, called beside `install_default_goal` and outside the `-R` guard. It sets the attribute on whatever variable holds the name rather than defining over one: GNU's `define_variable_cname` is an ordinary ranked write that an inherited `o_env` binding declines, and it then writes `v->export` on the variable it was handed back. Defining unconditionally clobbered the environment's value and origin, which the first build of this caught.\n\n`read_makefiles_variable` and `read_makefiles_entry` do the reading, at the top of the `*parse*` phase and before the loop over the invocation's own makefiles. `Source::Absent` returns quietly and `Source::Unreadable` takes the same `error_loc!` that `read_named_makefile` gives a `-f` file, which is what makes the directory cell right. The default-goal suppression is a flag on the evaluator — `Evaluator::default_goal_withheld`, set around the statements of one of these files and read by `record_default_goal` — following the `is_bootstrap`/`is_commandline` phase flags already there rather than inventing a guard type.\n\nFive fixtures recorded from the oracle: makefiles-variable-is-defined (origin, flavor, value, MAKEFILE_LIST, and the child that does not see it), makefiles-variable-reads-what-it-names (the file read, its variable visible, MAKEFILE_LIST order, and `.DEFAULT_GOAL` still `real`), makefiles-variable-passes-over-a-missing-file, a-goal-a-makefiles-file-declares (buildable by name though never the default), makefiles-variable-is-exported-once-set (the empty assignment reaching both a `$(shell)` and a recipe's child).\n\nUpstream inventory: variables/MAKEFILES.diff moved from unclassified to narration, shedding `evaluation` and leaving `ninja-progress` alone — the row this node was filed to move. 1287 rows, one moved, and the compiler bucket stayed at zero.\n\nOwned markers: none."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T21:29:00.116383955Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recipe-time-target-variable-reaches-the-scope
                title "Apply a target-specific variable a recipe assigns"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T04:52:06.261480398Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Split out of make-recipe-eval-mutation-refusal, which drew the boundary that exposes it. GNU Make accepts `$(eval tgt: VAR = value)` at recipe-expansion time — it is `record_target_var`, not `record_files`, so the \"prerequisites cannot be defined in recipes\" refusal does not reach it — and because GNU expands a whole recipe against `current_variable_set_list`, an assignment naming the target being expanded is read by the very next line of that same recipe.\n\nRonin reads nothing. `eval_rule_specific_assign` writes into `Evaluator::rule_vars`, and `DepBuilder::new` took that table out of the evaluator when it compiled the graph, so a recipe-time write lands in a fresh empty map nothing ever consults. The recipe's live scope is the `Arc<Vars>` snapshot on its `DepNode`, reached as `Evaluator::current_scope`.\n\nOwns tests/make/function-eval-in-a-recipe-sets-a-target-variable (recorded, divergence marker). Reproducer:\n\n    all:\n    \t$(eval all: LOCAL = set-in-recipe)\n    \t@printf '%s\\n' 'LOCAL=[$(LOCAL)]' > out\n\nGNU writes `LOCAL=[set-in-recipe]`; Ronin writes `LOCAL=[]`.\n\nThe adjacent question the fix has to answer: an assignment naming a *different* target has to reach that target's node scope, which the evaluator cannot address by symbol once the graph owns the nodes — so this is a plumbing decision (hand the evaluator a way back to the node table, or resolve rule-specific assignments against the live scope when `rules_snapped` and the target is the one being expanded), not a one-line change. The scope-clobbering half of the same defect — the assignment dropping `current_scope` instead of restoring it, so every later recipe line read globals — was fixed under the refusal node and is gated by function-eval-in-a-recipe-keeps-the-target-scope."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T12:00:29.484479819Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T12:00:51.6416224Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The timing matrix, probed against the oracle before implementing. Launch order is the whole question, so every case forces it with a prerequisite chain rather than trusting the scheduler:\n\n  target already expanded    no effect          its recipe is text by the time the eval runs\n  the target running now     visible            on a LATER line of the same recipe\n  a target expanded later    visible\n  that later target's prereqs visible in GNU    Ronin still misses this one; filed separately\n  an existing target-specific binding  overridden\n  `:=` at recipe time        simple, expanded where the eval is\n  `+=` at recipe time        appended to what the target inherits\n\nGNU needs no special case for any of it. `record_target_var` reaches the file's variable set by name at any point in the run, and `initialize_file_variables` has already put that set in front of the recipe's chain, so a write lands where the next expansion reads. \"Already expanded\" is not a rule GNU enforces either — it falls out of the recipe being text by then.\n\nFixed in three places, all kati.\n\n`make_dep` hands the evaluator `planned_scopes`, a `Symbol -> Arc<Vars>` table taken from `DepBuilder::done` once the graph is built. That is the route back the node's handoff said was missing: `Evaluator::rule_vars` left with the graph, so a recipe-time write had been landing in a table nothing consults again. `record_rule_specific_assign` prefers the planned scope when `rules_snapped`, and falls back to the old table otherwise — before the graph exists there is nothing to reach.\n\n`settle_append` finishes a `+=` that has no build-time pass left to finish it. `apply_rule_vars` normally appends a pending `+=` to what the target inherits while it installs rule variables; a recipe-time assignment arrives after that pass has run, so the same paste is made against the same chain at assign time. Without it `$(eval tgt: X += more)` gave the target `more` alone, the inherited value dropped.\n\nThe third is the one that made the matrix's first cell right, and it is not in the assignment path at all. `expansion_can_reach_make` answered `true` for `$(eval)` unconditionally, which kept every recipe holding one out of launch expansion — so such a recipe was expanded while the graph was being compiled, before any recipe ran. That was invisible while a recipe-time eval changed nothing; with this node it decides the timing outright, and got it backwards: a target that ran BEFORE the setter saw the assignment, because the setter's recipe had been expanded first. `$(eval)` is now walked like any other function. It expands to nothing, so it contributes no recipe text, and a `$(MAKE)` it can reach is one written inside its own argument, which is in the tree. `call` and `value` still answer `true` — they reach a variable by a name they are given, which is not. A variable an `$(eval)` defines that a later line expands to `$(MAKE)` is beyond any static walk; the check `DeferredRecipes::expand_with` already makes after expanding catches that and refuses rather than handing the executor a nested Make.\n\nThree fixtures: the divergence marker on function-eval-in-a-recipe-sets-a-target-variable is deleted, function-eval-in-a-recipe-reaches-a-later-target records the three timing cells and both flavours, function-eval-in-a-recipe-overrides-a-target-variable records the override.\n\nThe prerequisite cell is make-recipe-time-target-variable-reaches-prerequisites: it needs node scopes to be links rather than copies, which is a change to the type every scope is expressed in."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T12:02:56.885293405Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recipe-time-export-reaches-the-environment
                title "Put a variable a recipe exports into that recipe's environment"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T04:52:19.96123265Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Split out of make-recipe-eval-mutation-refusal, which characterised recipe-time `$(eval)` and found this cell short. An `export` is session state rather than graph shape, so the refusal boundary lets it through — and GNU Make honours it in the very recipe that wrote it, because the child's environment is built from the export set at the moment the job is started, after the recipe text has been expanded.\n\nRonin builds the recipe environment once, at compile time: `src/make.rs` calls `exported_environment(&mut ev)` and hands the answer to `sink.set_recipe_environment` before any recipe is expanded. Lazy recipe expansion means the `$(eval export V)` now really does run — at launch — but by then the environment prefix the edge carries is fixed, so the variable never reaches the shell.\n\nOwns tests/make/function-eval-in-a-recipe-exports-a-variable (recorded, divergence marker). Reproducer:\n\n    V = exported-value\n    all:\n    \t$(eval export V)\n    \t@printf '%s\\n' \"V=[$$V]\" > out\n\nGNU writes `V=[exported-value]`; Ronin writes `V=[]`.\n\nThe same shape reaches `unexport` at recipe time, and probably a recipe-time assignment to a name that was already exported — worth probing both before deciding whether the environment has to be recomputed per edge at launch or only when the expansion touched the export set."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T12:00:57.475150682Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T12:01:16.810068731Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The timing matrix, probed against the oracle. The node's premise held and the matrix is wider than the two cells it named — what a recipe changes about the export set is not only an `export`:\n\n  `$(eval export V)`, same recipe's later line   the child sees it\n  `$(eval export V)`, a later recipe's child     sees it\n  `$(eval export W = x)`                          sees it, value and all\n  `$(eval unexport A)` on an exported name        withdrawn, from this recipe on\n  `$(eval C = new)` on an already-exported name   the NEW value reaches children\n  `$(shell)` in a later expansion                 already right before this node\n\nThe fourth and fifth are the ones the node's handoff asked to probe before choosing an implementation, and they settle it: the trigger is not the `export` directive, it is any change to what the export set would answer. GNU has no trigger at all — `target_environment` reads the export set when the job starts, so everything any earlier recipe did is already in it, including a plain assignment to an exported name.\n\nRonin settles the compilation unit's environment once, before any recipe runs, and hands it to the sink. There is already a per-edge correction on top of it — `node_environment` calls `scoped_environment` at launch — but that walks the node's own scope, and a recipe-time export marks the global binding, which is not in it.\n\nFixed by naming the names the settlement can no longer be trusted for. `Evaluator::exports_after_snap` collects a name whenever a global assignment lands or `mark_exported` runs while `rules_snapped` — the two choke points, and `export V = x` goes through the first. `export::late_environment` turns that list into environment changes at launch: the current answer for each name, or a withdrawal where the name is no longer exported or no longer there. `node_environment` puts those in front of the scope's own changes, so a target-specific binding still has the last word, which is the order the unit's answer and the scope's already stood in.\n\nOnly what a recipe touched, rather than the whole export set recomputed per job: the settled answer is still right for every name nothing has since spoken about, and recomputing it per edge would cost a walk of every global.\n\nRecipes carrying `$(eval)` reach launch expansion at all only because of the `expansion_can_reach_make` change made under make-recipe-time-target-variable-reaches-the-scope. Without it this node's own reproducer expands while the graph is compiled, and the export lands before the environment is settled rather than after — right answer, wrong reason, and wrong for every later recipe.\n\nTwo fixtures: the divergence marker on function-eval-in-a-recipe-exports-a-variable is deleted, and function-eval-in-a-recipe-exports-to-a-later-recipe records the unexport, the export and the reassignment reaching both the writing recipe and a later one."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T12:03:01.66624019Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-notdir-strips-a-root-directory
                title "Strip a root directory in notdir and the F automatic variables"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T05:21:59.062711553Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while characterising the value function over automatic variables (make-automatic-variable-value-function).\n\nkati's basename does not strip a directory that is the filesystem root, so a\nname whose only directory part is `/` comes back whole. GNU Make's notdir keeps\nnothing before the last slash.\n\nReproducer, `$(notdir /x.o)`:\n\n  all: ; @echo 'notdir=[$(notdir /x.o)] dir=[$(dir /x.o)]'\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  notdir=[x.o]   dir=[/]\n  Ronin      notdir=[/x.o]  dir=[/]\n\nThe same basename backs the `F` automatic variables, so `$(@F)` for a target\n`/x.o` reads `/x.o` where GNU Make reads `x.o`:\n\n  .PHONY: /x.o\n  /x.o: ; @echo 'atD=[$(@D)] atF=[$(@F)]'\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  atD=[] atF=[x.o]\n  Ronin      atD=[] atF=[/x.o]\n\n`$(dir)` and the `D` forms already agree, including the root case, so the defect\nis basename's alone. Fix it in kati's strutil::basename."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T16:23:34.747375931Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T09:45:47.025630729Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GNU Make 4.4.1 characterised cell by cell against the pristine oracle before the change.\n\nnotdir is one rule with no exceptions: scan back from the end of the word for a directory separator and keep only what follows it (reference/gnumake/src/function.c func_notdir_suffix, lines 546-558 — `while (p >= p2 && ! STOP_SET (*p, stop)) --p;` then `++p` and output `len - (p - p2)` bytes). A separator at index 0 is a directory like any other, which is the whole defect: nothing before the last slash survives.\n\nThe matrix, GNU on the left, Ronin before the change on the right where they differed:\n\n  $(notdir /x.o)          x.o        was /x.o\n  $(notdir //x)           x          agreed\n  $(notdir /)             (empty)    agreed, but only via a hand-written special case\n  $(notdir ///)           (empty)    agreed\n  $(notdir a/)            (empty)    agreed\n  $(notdir x)             x          agreed\n  $(notdir /a/b)          b          agreed\n  $(notdir /x.o a/b /y /) \"x.o b y \" was \"/x.o b /y \"\n  $(notdir )              (empty)    agreed\n\nThe trailing space in the list cell is real: the last word `/` contributes an empty word, and WordWriter has already written the separator.\n\ndir was probed alongside and needed nothing: `$(dir /x.o)` is `/`, `$(dir /)` is `/`, `$(dir //x)` is `//`. So is `$(suffix /x.o)` = `.o` and Make's own `$(basename /x.o)` = `/x` (the suffix-stripping function, a different thing from kati's strutil::basename).\n\nThe F automatic variables read the same basename, and moved with it. Probed in a real rule with a root-directory target and a root-directory prerequisite:\n\n  $@ /x.o   $(@D) (empty)  $(@F) x.o    was $(@F) /x.o\n  $< /y     $(<D) (empty)  $(<F) y      agreed\n  $(^F) y                                agreed\n\nThe D forms already agreed, including the root case, because dirname returns empty at index 0 and GNU's `@D` is `$(patsubst %/,%,$(dir $@))` — `%/` against `/` leaves an empty stem. So the defect really was basename's alone, as the filing said.\n\nWhat changed, all in kati: strutil::basename drops the `if found == 0 { return s }` branch and always keeps what follows the last separator; func.rs notdir_func loses the `if tok == b\"/\"` special case, which existed only to patch the one cell the bug made visible and is now what basename answers.\n\nThe other three callers were checked rather than assumed. find.rs uses basename for `-name` matching and for reading a directory entry's own name, where POSIX find matches the basename and the fix is the correct direction. ninja.rs builds `/<stem>.s` for the Android depfile hack; for a root-level output that was `//x.s` and is now `/x.s`, which is the path it was looking for.\n\nOwned markers: none — no divergence file under tests/make/*/divergence named notdir or an F automatic. Upstream inventory row `functions/notdir.diff` was already classified narration/ninja-progress and is re-audited with the rest of the sweep.\n\nFixtures recorded from the oracle: tests/make/function-notdir-of-a-root-directory (the notdir cells) and tests/make/automatic-variable-file-part-of-a-root-name (the F and D automatics over root-directory names). Unit test strutil::test::test_basename holds the same matrix at the function."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T10:50:07.517758933Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-path-automatic-variables-defined-at-read-time
                title "Define the D and F automatic variables before any rule runs"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T05:22:11.626559477Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while characterising the value function over automatic variables (make-automatic-variable-value-function).\n\nGNU Make defines the `D` and `F` automatic variables once, at startup, in\n`define_automatic_variables` (reference/gnumake/src/variable.c). They are\nordinary recursive variables holding a `dir`/`notdir` expression, so they are\ndefined everywhere a makefile can look — including at read time, before any rule\nhas been chosen. Only the base forms are per-file, set in `set_file_variables`.\n\nkati registers every automatic variable from `CommandEvaluator::new`, so none of\nthem exists until a recipe is being expanded. In recipe context the two tools\nnow agree on all of value, flavor and origin. Outside one they do not:\n\n  $(info atD=[$(value @D)] flavor=[$(flavor @D)] origin=[$(origin @D)])\n  $(info at=[$(value @)] flavor=[$(flavor @)] origin=[$(origin @)])\n  all: ; @:\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  atD=[$(patsubst %/,%,$(dir $@))] flavor=[recursive] origin=[automatic]\n             at=[] flavor=[undefined] origin=[undefined]\n  Ronin      atD=[] flavor=[undefined] origin=[undefined]\n             at=[] flavor=[undefined] origin=[undefined]\n\nThe base forms already agree at read time — both read as undefined — so the gap\nis the `D` and `F` forms only. Defining them at session start needs no rule in\nhand, because their value is literal text that only refers to the base form;\n`$(@D)` at read time then expands through an undefined `$@` to the empty string,\nwhich is what GNU Make produces.\n\nDeferred from the value-function node because it is a variable-lifecycle change\nrather than a change to what `value` reports, and the owned corpus case is\nrecipe-context only."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:08:40.213210761Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. `install_path_automatic_variables` (kati `src-rs/builtins.rs`) defines the `D` and `F` form of each per-file automatic once per evaluation, as a recursive variable whose text is the `dir`/`notdir` expression over the base form — GNU Make's `define_automatic_variables` (src/variable.c), in the same place in the order and, like GNU Make, run whatever `-R` says.\n\nBases: `@ % < ? ^ + *`. `|` is deliberately absent — GNU Make defines no `$(|D)`, so it stays an ordinary variable nobody defined.\n\nThe origin ladder came with it. An automatic is the top of it, so a plain assignment, an `override`, a command-line variable and an `undefine` all bounce off `@D` and leave it reading `automatic`, silently, with the run carrying on — the previous code path treated a write to an automatic as the unimplemented case. A target-specific `all: @D = x` still takes, because it lands in the rule's own scope, which is searched first.\n\nThree corpus cases, all byte-identical to GNU Make 4.4.1: the read-time definition itself (`value`/`flavor`/`origin` outside any recipe), the assignment that does not take, and survival under `-R`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:10:20.172129529Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-builtin-rule-catalogue
                title "Install GNU Make's catalogue of built-in rules"
                kind @feat
                deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:01:43.575507671Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Owns four rows of the 2026-08-14 upstream inventory: features/implicit_search.diff.71, .72, .75 and .76, all `no-rule-to-make`.\n\nMechanism, read off the fork rather than inferred: `kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs` bootstraps `.SUFFIXES: .o .c .cc` and exactly two suffix rules, `.c.o` and `.cc.o`, under a comment promising more built-in rules later. The variable half is already there — `kati/src-rs/builtins.rs` defines `COMPILE.c`, `LINK.c`, `COMPILE.cc`, `LINK.cc`, `COMPILE.C`, `COMPILE.cpp`, `OUTPUT_OPTION` and friends — so what is missing is the rules that use them, not the macros.\n\nMeasured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, one empty source file and `all: <target>`:\n\n  hello.f + `all: hello`   → GNU `f77 hello.f -o hello`; Ronin refuses.\n  hello.f + `all: hello.o` → GNU `f77 -c hello.f`;      Ronin refuses.\n\nTwo families of gap, and the second is the wider one:\n\n  Object rules missing for .cpp .C .f .F .p .r .s .S .mod .l .y .w — every suffix GNU compiles to .o except .c and .cc.\n\n  Link rules missing entirely: `%: %.c`, `%: %.cc`, `%: %.f`, `%: %.p`, `%: %.sh`, `%: %.mod`. `all: hello` with hello.c beside it is the oldest idiom in Make and Ronin has no rule for it, which is a larger exposure than the exotic suffixes and is what the four owned rows actually exercise.\n\nTake GNU Make's `default.c` as the catalogue rather than the manual: the fork's own comment already records that the manual and default.c disagree and that default.c is the one that runs."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T08:07:33.093391064Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Two more rows attributed here by make-match-anything-rule-chaining (2026-08-14): features/implicit_search.diff.78 and .80, which that node held.\n\nBoth stay `compiler no-rule-to-make`, but what they are blocked on has changed and is now exactly this node. With the compatibility pass delivered, the search reaches the same rule GNU does and the refusal moves one target inwards:\n\n  .78  all: hello.tsk / %.tsk: %; $(info $@ from $<) / unrelated: hello\n       GNU: `f77 hello.f -o hello`, then `hello.tsk from hello`.\n       Ronin was `'hello.tsk', needed by 'all'`; it is now `'hello', needed by 'hello.tsk'`.\n  .80  the same with `%.tsk: % %.x` and `%.x: %.xx` beside it, same movement.\n\n`hello` is taken on trust because `unrelated: hello` writes it down, so `%.tsk: %` applies; what is then missing is the built-in link rule `%: %.f` this node's second family covers. Neither row needs anything further from the pattern search."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T08:23:17.95565994Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T09:40:59.768803322Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "WHAT WAS PORTED. reference/gnumake/src/default.c, through this host's preprocessor branches — the generic non-VMS Unix ones, without GCC_IS_NATIVE and without __MSDOS__. kati/src-rs/builtin_rules.rs holds the four tables and nothing else:\n\n  default_suffixes          35 entries\n  default_suffix_rules[]    49 entries, 14 with one suffix and 35 with two\n  default_pattern_rules[]    4 entries\n  default_terminal_rules[]   5 entries\n\nThey become 92 pattern rules, which is what `make -p` on the oracle prints and what this was counted against: 35 recipe-less `%.X:`, 14 link rules `%: %.X`, 34 compile rules `%.Y: %.X` — `.lm.m` never converts because `.lm` is not on the list, and a unit test asserts it is the only table entry nothing constructs — then the 4 pattern rules and the 5 terminal rules, in that order. Ronin keeps the two-suffix half in the suffix-rule map it already had and everything else in the pattern chain; that split is a fork difference, so the order between the two halves is asserted by recorded cells rather than by the structure.\n\nWHERE THEY ARE INSTALLED is the whole of the `-r` story. GNU Make sets `.SUFFIXES` before the read and derives every rule from it after — install_default_suffix_rules, convert_to_pattern, install_default_implicit_rules, all after the last makefile closes. Ronin now does the same: the bootstrap writes `.SUFFIXES: <35>` and binds `SUFFIXES` (simple, `default` origin, both probed against the oracle), and DepBuilder::install_builtin_rules runs after handle_special_targets. Three behaviours fall out of the placement rather than needing code of their own:\n\n  a makefile's own `%.out: %` or `.c.o:` keeps the name, because the built-in arrives second and new_pattern_rule's override is off for it;\n  `MAKEFLAGS += -r` withholds the catalogue, because the switch is decoded before the rules are installed rather than after — options/dash-r.diff.1 and .4 close on this;\n  `.SUFFIXES:` withdraws the suffix-derived half and a later line puts back whichever pairs it names.\n\nOne corner of the makefile-set `-r` needed reading rather than inferring: GNU takes the suffix list away only while `suffix_file->builtin` still holds, and enter_file clears that flag the moment a makefile names `.SUFFIXES` at all. So `MAKEFLAGS += -r` beside `.SUFFIXES: .c` keeps `.c` and its disqualifier, and the probe agrees. Recorded as builtin-suffix-list-survives-a-makefile-that-wrote-one.\n\nFOUR THINGS THE CATALOGUE MADE LOAD-BEARING that were not before, each a real GNU mechanism Ronin had done without while it had two built-in rules:\n\n  specific_rule_matched (implicit.c:405,435). A declared suffix is a pattern rule with no prerequisites and no recipe whose only effect is to be matched, and matching a target that is not a bare `%` strikes every non-terminal match-anything rule out of that search. Without it the link rules claim a `foo.o` the way they claim a `foo`. This closes make-pattern-search-specific-rule-match, whose own two probes now agree in both directions.\n\n  rule->in_use (implicit.c:325,534). No rule may be a link in the chain that supplies its own prerequisite. With a catalogue that chains this is what bounds the search rather than tidying it.\n\n  file_impossible (implicit.c:763,922). A name a chain search has proven nothing can make is remembered, and every later search rejects a rule asking for it without walking the subtree again. Recorded against the shallowest depth it failed at, which GNU has no need of because its search has no depth limit and kati's MAX_IMPLICIT_CHAIN does; a failure that ran out of budget at depth five says nothing at depth one.\n\n  found_compat_rule (implicit.c:861,1153). The compatibility retry is conditional in GNU and was unconditional here, which doubled the work at every level of a six-deep search. Only a pass that actually passed over a rule for a written-down name is retried.\n\nTWO DEFECTS THE CATALOGUE EXPOSED, both fixed here because it cannot land without them:\n\n  remake.c:555 asks for an implicit rule only where `!file->phony`. `%: %.c` matches every name there is, so an `all` declared .PHONY beside an `all.c` acquired a recipe that linked a program nobody asked for. Probed and fixed.\n\n  implicit.c:478 rejects a terminal rule outright on the pass that invents prerequisites. Without it the RCS rule `%: %,v` made `foo` from a `foo,v` it would invent from a `foo,v,v`, without end — include-remade-by-a-multi-target-pattern-rule overflowed the stack. That is half one of make-terminal-pattern-rule-needs-an-existing-prerequisite, delivered here and noted there; half two, the tried_implicit stamp at implicit.c:1044, is untouched and that node stays open on it.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS. 0.7ms per evaluation in release — 5.2ms against 4.5ms under `-r` on an empty makefile — and the real-project gate is 21s before and 22s after. Getting there needed the four mechanisms above plus two ordinary economies: `ordered_candidates` takes each candidate's pattern apart once rather than once per comparison, and the pairs are read off the table rather than by constructing all 35x35 names. Before them the same makefile took 330ms and the port corpus took 101s instead of 20s.\n\nThe 53 make lib tests take 0.27s where they took 0.11s, and that is enough extra CPU for build::tests::ronin_scheduler_releases_dependents_on_each_completion to flake about one run in six where it did not before. It asserts that a 0.03s job's dependent is released before a 0.30s job finishes, and one failure poisons the next sandboxed run because its fixture directory is keyed on a pid a pid-namespace reuses. Filed as make-scheduler-completion-test-is-timing-fragile rather than papered over.\n\nINVENTORY MOVEMENT: 18 rows, every one forward, none away from agreement.\n  6 compiler no-rule-to-make to narration: features/implicit_search.diff.71 .72 .75 .76 — this node's own four — and .78 .80, the two re-scoped by make-match-anything-rule-chaining. All six are now pure ninja-progress.\n  2 compiler refusal-not-made to narration shared-refusal: options/dash-r.diff.1 and .4, which make-makefile-set-no-builtin-rules owned. Both tools now refuse.\n  7 unclassified to narration: features/suffixrules.diff.10 .12 .15 (the suffix rules now fire), .11 .13 .14 (both tools now refuse the same thing), and options/dash-k.diff.1.\n  3 narration to narration, family only: features/double_colon.diff.14 loses ninja-progress because Ronin now does no work there, and features/suffixrules.diff.2 and .9 lose recipe-echo. All still fully accounted for by narration recognisers.\nClass counts move 202/26/45/1014 to 195/18/45/1029 on 1287 rows, and the classifier answered the same twice, so no watched flapper flapped."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T09:43:01.256008411Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-match-anything-rule-chaining
                title "Chain an implicit rule through a match-anything rule"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:01:51.948780781Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Owns seven rows: features/implicit_search.diff.48, .49, .58, .78, .80 and features/patternrules.diff.1, .6.\n\nReproducer, minimised against the pristine oracle:\n\n  all: foo foo.out\n  %: %.in ; @echo MADE $@\n  %.out: % ; @echo MADE $@\n  foo.in: ; @touch foo.in\n\nGNU makes both — `MADE foo`, then `MADE foo.out`. Ronin refuses: `'foo.out', needed by 'all', missing and no known rule to make it`.\n\nThe discriminator is that `foo` is itself made by a match-anything rule. Probed either side of it:\n\n  Ordinary chaining works. `%.out: %.mid` over `%.mid: %.in` builds on both tools, which is `make-implicit-rule-chaining` already delivered.\n  An explicit rule for `foo` works. Replace `%: %.in` with `foo: ; @echo made foo` and Ronin chains `%.out: %` onto it happily.\n  No rule for `foo` at all refuses on both tools, so the refusal itself is not the difference.\n\nSo the missing step is narrow: when the implicit search for `foo.out` asks whether prerequisite `foo` can be made, Ronin does not count a match-anything rule (`%:` with prerequisites) among the ways it could be. GNU does, subject to the nonterminal-match-anything constraints in its own pattern_search.\n\nNote the neighbourhood before implementing. `make-pattern-search-specific-rule-match` is the opposite direction — a specific pattern that must reject a match-anything rule — and `make-terminal-pattern-rule-needs-an-existing-prerequisite` is the constraint that stops the chaining being unconditional. Widening the search without those two in view would trade these seven rows for others."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T07:46:44.378834519Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T08:08:31.860589929Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "The title was wrong and the reproducer was right, which is worth recording before the delivery: GNU Make does not chain through a match-anything rule, and adding that would have been a regression.\n\nWHAT GNU ACTUALLY DOES, probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make rather than read.\n\nimplicit.c:342 is the guard, and it is unconditional: `if (recursions > 0 && target[1] == '\\0' && !rule->terminal) continue;`. A rule whose matched target pattern is exactly `%` and which is not terminal is not even collected once the search is recursing, so it can never make an intermediate. Probed both ways with `all: foo.out` over `%.out: %` and `%: %.y`, foo.y on disk — GNU refuses, exactly as Ronin already did. Write the inner rule `%:: %.y` and GNU builds `foo`, uses it and deletes it again: terminal match-anything IS allowed as a chain link, because terminality is what implicit.c:342 exempts. Both cells are recorded, and Ronin agreed with GNU on both before this change and after it.\n\nSo why does the triage's reproducer build on GNU? Not by chaining. Reading the `-d` trace:\n\n  Trying implicit prerequisite 'foo'.\n  Prerequisite 'foo' of rule '%.out: %' does not qualify as ought to exist.\n  ...\n  Looking for a rule with explicit file 'foo'.\n   Avoiding implicit rule recursion for rule '%.out: %'.\n  No implicit rule found for 'foo'.\n  Rejecting rule '%.out: %' due to impossible prerequisite 'foo'.\n  Searching for a compatibility rule for 'foo.out'.\n   Using compatibility rule '%.out: %' due to 'foo'.\n\nThat is GNU Make 4.4's compatibility pass, and it is what the seven rows are about. `pattern_search` carries `allow_compat_rules`. When a rule is rejected for a prerequisite that has a `struct file` — the Makefile wrote the name down somewhere, anywhere — it sets `found_compat_rule` (implicit.c:861) and, if every rule then fails, runs the entire search again with `allow_compat_rules = 1` (implicit.c:1153-1158), in which such a prerequisite is accepted on sight (implicit.c:840) without being found, made, or promised. `foo` qualifies only because `all: foo foo.out` names it. Take it off `all` and GNU refuses exactly as Ronin did — verified, and recorded as the negative control.\n\nThe boundary of \"written down\", probed cell by cell, all five now agreeing with Ronin: a prerequisite of any unrelated explicit rule counts; an order-only prerequisite counts; a `.PHONY` mention counts; a name carrying target-specific variables counts; a command-line goal counts. A prerequisite that appears only on a *pattern* rule does not — there is no such file to know about until the rule is applied.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. kati/src-rs/dep.rs only.\n\n`SearchPass` names the two questions the search relaxes, because there are now four passes and two loose booleans at a call site said nothing. `chaining` is GNU's `intermed_ok`; `compat` is its `allow_compat_rules`. `SearchPass::all()` is the order, and the order is the answer: a rule whose prerequisites are really there beats one whose would have to be invented, and both beat one that only works by taking a name on trust — however far up the candidate list the loser sits. Both orderings are asserted by recorded cells rather than assumed.\n\n`is_written_down` is `lookup_file`: rules, mentioned, phony, rule_vars, goals. It sits after `exists` in `can_pick_implicit_rule` and deliberately before the `invented` push, so a compatibility prerequisite never becomes an intermediate — GNU's is a `patdeps` with a null `file`, and marking it would have had the retention machinery delete a file the Makefile named.\n\nThe recursion gets it too, and had to. `can_be_made_implicitly` runs its strict pass and then its own compatibility pass, unless the search that reached it is already the compatibility pass, which it inherits — which is precisely what implicit.c:896 passing `allow_compat_rules` down, and implicit.c:1157 asserting it is zero on retry, together say. features/implicit_search.diff.48 is the row that needs it: the compatibility prerequisite is accepted inside the recursion for `hello.o`, not at the top.\n\nTwo arguments over the ceiling were the clippy failure and `SearchPass` is the structural answer, not a bypass: both functions are back to seven and read better for it.\n\nWHAT THE SEVEN ROWS DID. Three resolved outright — implicit_search.diff.58, patternrules.diff.1 and .6 are now narration. Two re-scoped to make-builtin-rule-catalogue: .78 and .80 refuse one target further in, at `hello`, needing GNU's built-in `%: %.f` link rule and nothing more from the search. Two re-scoped to a new node, make-circular-dependency-is-dropped: .48 and .49 now find the rules GNU finds and then refuse a cycle GNU drops with a warning and builds through. Nine rows this node never owned moved forward with them, and none moved back."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T08:08:36.672579845Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-pattern-prerequisite-directory-stem
                title "Prefix a pattern prerequisite with the stem's directory"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:02:05.623901172Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Owns four rows: features/patternrules.diff.46, .47, .64 and .65.\n\nWhen a pattern rule's stem carries a directory, GNU splits it: the directory goes in front of the whole prerequisite name and only the remainder is substituted for the `%`. Ronin substitutes the whole stem where the `%` stands, which agrees with GNU whenever `%` is the first character of the prerequisite and disagrees otherwise. That is why the suite's cases look arbitrary — they are the ones whose prerequisite patterns begin with a literal.\n\nBisected against the oracle with `all: lib/bye.x` and `%.x: <prereq>`, stem `lib/bye`:\n\n  prereq `%1%2%`  → both say `lib/bye1%2%`   (agrees: % is first)\n  prereq `%9`     → both say `lib/bye9`      (agrees: % is first)\n  prereq `6%`     → GNU `lib/6bye`;  Ronin refuses (would be `6lib/bye`)\n  prereq `11%12`  → GNU `lib/11bye12`; Ronin refuses (would be `11lib/bye12`)\n\nThe refusal rather than a wrong name is incidental: the misplaced name matches no target, so the rule is rejected and the search runs out. A `.PHONY` declaration naming GNU's spelling is what makes the difference visible as a refusal instead of silence.\n\nOnly the first `%` of a prerequisite is the stem placeholder; the rest are literal. That is the same law `make-second-expansion-pattern-first-percent` records for the deferred path, and the two should be read together even though they sit on different code paths."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:09:55.205339673Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:10:24.140575619Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "The whole `check_lastslash` law, probed against the pristine oracle, and why the node cannot deliver half of it.\n\nThe note filed by the triage characterises the substitution. Probing the matrix the dispatch asked for turned up that the same flag decides which rules match at all, so the two halves are one law and this node implements both.\n\nWHAT SETS IT. GNU Make's `pattern_search` computes `lastslash` as the last `/` in the target name not counting a trailing one, and sets `check_lastslash` when the name has such a slash AND the RULE'S TARGET PATTERN contains none of its own. The prerequisite patterns have no say in it.\n\nWHAT IT DOES TO MATCHING. When set, the pattern is matched against the file part of the name alone, and the directory is held aside. `implicit.c` reaches this by shifting `stem` past `pathlen` and then comparing the pattern's literal prefix against `lastslash + 1` rather than against the start of the name; the arithmetic reduces exactly to matching the basename. Probed, and both directions were wrong here:\n\n  target lib/bye.x, pattern l%.x  → GNU does not match (`l` must match the start of `bye.x`); Ronin matched with stem `ib/bye`\n  target lib/bye.x, pattern b%.x  → GNU matches, stem `lib/ye`; Ronin did not match at all\n  target lib/bye.x, pattern %.x   → both match, stem `lib/bye`\n  target lib/bye.x, pattern lib/%.x or %/bye.x → pattern carries a slash, flag off, matched whole\n\nWHAT IT DOES TO THE STEM. `$*` is the directory put back in front of what `%` stood for: `lib/bye` for `%.x`, `lib/ye` for `b%.x`. The search orders candidates by that full length, which is `stemlen + pathlen` in `tryrules`. Probed: `%.b.x` beats `%.x` for `lib/a.b.x` in both tools already.\n\nWHAT IT DOES TO PREREQUISITES. The directory goes in front of the WHOLE prerequisite name and only the remainder of the stem is substituted for the first `%`:\n\n  6%        → lib/6bye      (not 6lib/bye)\n  11%12     → lib/11bye12\n  %9        → lib/bye9      (agrees with the old code: `%` was first)\n  %1%2%     → lib/bye1%2%   (first `%` only, the sister node's law)\n  sub/%.c   → lib/sub/bye.c (the prerequisite's own directory is not special)\n  /abs/%.c  → lib//abs/bye.c (blind concatenation; the double slash is GNU's)\n  a% b% %c  → lib/abye lib/bbye lib/byec (every prerequisite, not just the first)\n  nopercent.c → nopercent.c (NO directory: a prerequisite with no `%` names one file)\n\nThe last line is the sharp edge and comes straight from the source — the `check_lastslash` prefix is written only inside the branch that found a `%`.\n\nWHERE IT DOES NOT APPLY. Probed and confirmed agreeing already, so these must not move:\n\n  static pattern rules — `lib/bye.x: %.x: 6%` gives `6lib/bye` in both. `check_lastslash` lives in `pattern_search` and a static pattern rule never goes through it.\n  suffix rules — `.c.o` for `lib/bye.o` gives `lib/bye.c`. A suffix rule's prerequisite pattern always begins with `%`, so the two laws cannot disagree.\n  explicit rules — no pattern, nothing to split.\n\nThat boundary is what keeps the change to the implicit search: `ordered_candidates`, `can_pick_implicit_rule`, `implicit_chain_exists` and the deferred expansion they share. `stem_of` is reached only through the rule merger, which is where explicit and static pattern rules live, and is deliberately left alone.\n\nVPATH. Probed: the prerequisite name is built under this law first and vpath resolves the result — `6%` becomes `lib/6bye` and vpath then finds `src/lib/6bye`. Nothing to do; the existing search runs after the substitution.\n\nDEFERRED PATH. GNU substitutes `$(*F)` rather than `$*` when the flag is set, and hands `parse_file_seq` the directory as a prefix, so the name is assembled after the second expansion. `make-second-expansion-pattern-first-percent` had to land first because `$(*F)` did not resolve in a deferred prerequisite at all.\n\nWHY NOT SPLIT THE MATCHING OFF AS A FOLLOW-UP. Because there is no coherent half. Taking the directory off the front of the stem is only meaningful if the stem was measured against the basename; matching `l%.x` to `lib/bye.x` yields a stem of `ib/bye` that does not begin with the directory the substitution then wants to strip. Implementing the substitution alone would mean carrying two contradictory readings of the same flag."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:24:03.780059095Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "What changed, and the four fixtures recorded from the pristine oracle.\n\n`PatternMatch` is the split itself: a directory the search is holding aside and what `%` stood for once it came off. `PatternMatch::of` replaces the bare `Pattern::matches` at every point in the implicit search — `ordered_candidates`, `can_pick_implicit_rule`, `implicit_chain_exists` — so matching, specificity and substitution all read one law instead of three readings of a pattern. `strutil::substitute_stem` is the byte shuffle, kept beside `Pattern` because it needs `find_percent` and deliberately not on it: `$(patsubst)` has no directory rule and must not acquire one.\n\nThree consequences worth naming:\n\nCandidate lookup. The rule index is keyed by the literal a pattern begins with, so `b%.x` is filed under `b` and a walk for `lib/bye.x` never reached it. `candidate_pool` asks the index about the file part as well and drops the duplicates, which is what GNU Make gets for free by having no index and comparing every rule to the name twice over.\n\n`$*` at recipe time. It was recomputed by substituting the target back into the pattern, and no substitution can produce `lib/ye` from `b%.x` and `lib/bye.x`. `DepNode::stem` now carries what the search read, which is GNU's own shape — `pattern_search` computes `stem_str` and hands it to `set_file_variables`. The old reading stays as the fallback for explicit and static pattern rules, where it is right and where a static pattern target that missed its pattern depends on it.\n\nOrder-only prerequisites. They take the same split, probed and confirmed, so they are resolved beside the ordinary ones rather than left for `apply_output_pattern`.\n\nFIXTURES\n\n  pattern-prerequisite-takes-the-stems-directory — `6% 11%12 %9 sub/%.c nopercent.c` against `lib/bye.x` in one rule, so the directory law and the no-percent exemption are pinned side by side. Log `lib/6bye lib/11bye12 lib/bye9 lib/sub/bye.c nopercent.c`, stem `lib/bye`.\n  pattern-prerequisite-directory-not-taken-by-a-static-pattern-rule — the same `%.x: 6%` reached as a static pattern rule, giving `6lib/bye`. This is the fixture that stops the law leaking out of the implicit search.\n  pattern-target-literal-matches-the-file-part — `l%.x` and `b%.x` offered together for `lib/bye.x`; GNU takes the second and leaves stem `lib/ye`. Pins both directions of the matching change.\n  second-expansion-pattern-prerequisite-takes-the-stems-directory — `$$(EMPTY)6% $$*.d`, so the deferred split and the whole stem `$$*` still reads are pinned against each other.\n\nRecording moved no existing case's expectation.\n\nUnit tests: `a_pattern_without_a_directory_matches_the_file_part` covers the split, both matching directions and the three prerequisite shapes; `a_trailing_slash_belongs_to_the_directory_it_names` covers where the split falls; `only_the_first_percent_of_a_segment_stands_for_the_stem` gained the `$(*F)` and no-percent-no-directory cases."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:37:51.837509637Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-second-expansion-pattern-first-percent
                title "Substitute the stem into only the first percent of a deferred prerequisite"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:02:10.298405412Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Owns two rows: features/patternrules.diff.58 and .70.\n\nReproducer against the pristine oracle:\n\n  .SECONDEXPANSION:\n  all: bye.x\n  %.x: $$(wordlist 1, 99, %1%2%) ; $(info $@ from $^)\n  .PHONY: bye1%2%\n\nGNU prints `bye.x from bye1%2%`. Ronin refuses with `'bye1bye2%', needed by 'bye.x', missing and no known rule to make it`, which names the bug outright: the stem `bye` was substituted for the second `%` as well as the first.\n\nOnly the first `%` in a prerequisite pattern is the placeholder; every later one is a literal percent. The immediate path already knows this — the same makefile without `.SECONDEXPANSION:` and without the `$$` agrees with GNU — so this is the deferred prerequisite path having its own substitution that does not carry the rule.\n\n`make-pattern-rule-deferred-prerequisite-identity` and `make-second-expansion-patterns` are the neighbours that already touch this path."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T11:57:34.841026367Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T11:57:53.6308745Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Where the second `%` picked up the stem, and what `$(*F)` needs before the sister node can land.\n\nThe first-`%`-only law was already written down and already right. `stem_references` rewrites the first `%` of each whitespace-separated segment of a deferred word to `$*` and leaves the rest alone, which is exactly what `implicit.c` does when it walks a word replacing `%` with `$*` and then skips to the next blank before looking again. Probed both halves of that: `%a %b` inside one `$(wordlist ...)` gives `byea byeb` in GNU and in Ronin — each segment's first `%` goes, so the rule is per segment and not per word.\n\nThe stem arrived twice because nothing said the deferred expansion had finished the job. `can_pick_implicit_rule` installs the expanded names on the cloned rule (`rule.inputs = inputs`) but leaves `output_patterns` holding `%.x`, and `fill_dep_node_from_rule` then runs `apply_output_pattern` over those finished names — a second `append_subst` against the same pattern. `bye1%2%` was already correct; the second pass read its literal `%` as a placeholder and made `bye1bye2%`. That is why the bug needs a `$` to appear: without one the list is not deferred at all, `rule.inputs` keeps the pattern text, and the single substitution in `apply_output_pattern` is the only one there is.\n\nSo the fix is not in the substitution but in saying when substitution is over. `Rule::prerequisites_are_resolved` records that the implicit search has already filled this rule's `%` in for the name it matched, and `apply_output_pattern` hands such a list back untouched. It matches GNU's shape: `pattern_search` builds the prerequisite names itself and hands the file a finished `struct dep` chain, while an explicit or static pattern rule keeps its pattern text and is substituted where it is read.\n\nSecond finding, from the `$$(*D)` probe the dispatch asked for: the deferred expansion binds `@ < ^ + | *` and nothing else, so `$(*F)` and `$(*D)` in a deferred prerequisite expand to nothing. GNU prints `bye.c` and `lib.c` for `%.x: $$(*F).c` and `%.x: $$(*D).c` against `lib/bye.x`; Ronin refuses both. GNU defines these once at startup as recursive variables over the base form — `$(notdir $*)` and `$(patsubst %/,%,$(dir $*))` — so binding them here as recursive variables holding that same text is the whole fix, and they track whatever `*` is bound to without a second computation. `$|` keeps no D or F form, which the register site already says.\n\nThis is not only parity for its own sake: `make-pattern-prerequisite-directory-stem` needs `$(*F)` to work, because GNU's deferred path substitutes `$(*F)` rather than `$*` whenever it is holding a directory aside. That node is blocked behind this one."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:06:57.404199196Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Fixtures recorded from the pristine 4.4.1 oracle through `tests/make_port.rs`, three of them:\n\n  second-expansion-pattern-prerequisite-first-percent — the node's own reproducer, `%.x: $$(wordlist 1, 99, %1%2%)` against `bye.x`, log `bye1%2%`.\n  second-expansion-pattern-prerequisite-percent-per-segment — `%a %b` inside one reference, log `byea byeb`, which pins the rule as per segment rather than per word so a later simplification cannot quietly make it per word.\n  second-expansion-pattern-prerequisite-stem-path-forms — `$$(*F).c $$(*D).c` against `lib/bye.x`, log `bye.c lib.c`.\n\nRecording moved no other case's expectation. `dep::tests::only_the_first_percent_of_a_segment_stands_for_the_stem` covers `stem_references` directly, since the fixtures reach it only through a whole build."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:37:47.584885131Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-pattern-prerequisite-wildcards
                title "Expand a wildcard in a pattern rule's prerequisite"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:02:18.732445457Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Owns one row: features/rule_glob.diff.1.\n\nReproducer against the pristine oracle, with a.two and a.three on disk:\n\n  .PHONY: all\n  all: a.four\n  %.four : %.t* ; @echo $@: $(sort $^)\n\nGNU prints `a.four: a.three a.two`. Ronin refuses: `'a.four', needed by 'all', missing and no known rule to make it`.\n\nA pattern rule's prerequisite may hold shell wildcards, which are expanded after the stem is substituted — here `%.t*` becomes `a.t*` and then globs to the two files. Ronin substitutes the stem and then looks for a file literally named `a.t*`.\n\n`make-prerequisite-globbing` already delivered this for an ordinary rule's prerequisite list; this is the same expansion on the implicit-rule path, where it has to happen after substitution rather than at read time."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:27:19.078270819Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:27:40.888636253Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "When GNU Make globs a pattern rule's prerequisite, and the one part of that Ronin cannot follow.\n\nWHERE IT HAPPENS. `pattern_search` hands each substituted prerequisite to `PARSE_FILE_SEQ` without `PARSEFS_NOGLOB`, so the glob runs after the stem goes in and not before. Rule definition is too early to glob at — the text is still `%.t*` — and the source confirms it with the comment \"Parse the expanded string. It might have wildcards.\" on the non-deferred branch.\n\nWHAT IT DOES.\n\n  a wildcard is only looked for in `?*[`; `parse_file_seq` skips `glob()` entirely when `strpbrk (name, \"?*[\")` finds nothing, which is the same three bytes `glob_word` already tests\n  no match leaves the literal name standing: `PARSEFS_EXISTS` is not set here, so `GLOB_NOMATCH` falls through to keeping the name as written\n  one prerequisite may become several; `PARSEFS_ONEWORD` stops it splitting on blanks but not on glob results\n\nProbed and agreed already on the deferred path, which has run through `glob_word` since second expansion landed: `%.t?`, `%.t[xy]` and no-match-keeps-literal all match GNU. Only the immediate path was reading the substituted name as a literal file.\n\nTHE ORDER THE DIRECTORY GOES ON. The two paths differ and GNU is the reason. On the immediate path the held-aside directory is written into the buffer before `PARSE_FILE_SEQ` sees it, so the glob matches `lib/6bye*`. On the deferred path the directory is handed over as `parse_file_seq`'s `prefix` argument, which is applied to each name the sequence yields — after the glob. Probed, and the two really do disagree:\n\n  immediate, `%.x: 6%*` against `lib/bye.x`, with lib/6bye1 and lib/6bye2 → lib/6bye1 lib/6bye2\n  deferred, same rule, same files → no rule to make it; the glob looked for `6bye*` in the working directory, found nothing, kept the literal, and `lib/6bye*` is not a file\n  deferred, same rule, with 6bye1 and 6bye2 in the working directory → lib/6bye1 lib/6bye2\n\nRonin already reproduces the deferred order, because `make-pattern-prerequisite-directory-stem` put the prefix on after `glob_word` for exactly this reason. This node adds the immediate one, where the directory is already part of the name by the time the glob runs.\n\nWHAT RONIN CANNOT FOLLOW, AND WHY IT IS A FOLLOW-UP RATHER THAN THIS NODE. GNU globs during the build, so a file an earlier recipe created is visible to a later pattern rule's wildcard. Probed with nothing on disk at all:\n\n  .PHONY: all\n  all: mk a.four\n  mk: ; @touch a.three\n  %.four : %.t* ; @echo \"GOT [$@: $(sort $^)]\"\n\nGNU prints `a.four: a.three`. Ronin compiles the whole graph before any recipe runs, so its glob necessarily reads the filesystem as it stands at compile time and can only see `a.three` if something at evaluation time made it. That is the compiler boundary the whole design rests on, not a defect in this substitution, and it is filed separately."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:36:34.183058506Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivery, fixtures, and the inventory audit for the whole dispatch.\n\nCHANGE. `resolved_prerequisites` builds a matched rule's prerequisite names in one place: substitute under the directory split, then hand the result to `glob_word`, which already tests for the same `?*[` GNU Make's `parse_file_seq` tests for and already keeps the name when nothing matched. Ordinary and order-only prerequisites both go through it, because GNU walks one `rule->deps` chain and globs every entry of it — probed with `%.x: dep | oo*`, which now agrees.\n\nFIXTURES\n\n  pattern-prerequisite-wildcard-expands-after-substitution — the node's own reproducer; log `a.three a.two`.\n  pattern-prerequisite-wildcard-with-no-match-keeps-the-name — log `a.t*`, pinning that a wildcard matching nothing is a name and not a dropped prerequisite.\n  pattern-prerequisite-wildcard-globs-below-the-stems-directory — `%.x: 6%*` against `lib/bye.x`, log `lib/6bye1 lib/6bye2`, pinning that on this path the directory is part of the name before the glob runs.\n\nRecording moved no existing case's expectation.\n\nUPSTREAM INVENTORY, audited row by row. 1287 cases before and after. Eleven rows changed and every one of them improved; nothing regressed.\n\n  compiler     18 → 11\n  unclassified 194 → 190\n  narration    1030 → 1041\n  interface    45 → 45\n\nThe seven owned rows all left the compiler class for narration:\n\n  features/patternrules.diff.46 .47 .64 .65 — make-pattern-prerequisite-directory-stem\n  features/patternrules.diff.58 .70 — make-second-expansion-pattern-first-percent\n  features/rule_glob.diff.1 — this node\n\nFour more moved unclassified → narration without being owned, and both groups are accounted for by this dispatch rather than being unexplained drift:\n\n  features/se_implicit.diff.3 — second-expansion implicit rules, which is where the first-percent law and the newly bound `$(*F)` both land\n  variables/automatic.diff.1 .2 .3 — the automatic variables, which is where `DepNode::stem` and the D and F bindings land\n\nThe three known flappers did not flap and needed no restoring: features/reinvoke.diff.2, features/patternrules.diff.34 and features/parallelism.diff.1 are all byte-identical to their recorded rows. targets/clean still dies in the driver with signal 6, as it did before."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:37:56.076447388Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-suffix-shaped-target-with-prerequisites
                title "Read a suffix-named rule that carries prerequisites as an ordinary rule"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-build-intent-gate)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T06:02:24.706802135Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Owns three rows, and they point in opposite directions, which is what identifies the mechanism: features/suffixrules.diff.4 and .5 (`no-rule-to-make`) and features/suffixrules.diff.6 (`refusal-not-made`). All three run the same makefile.\n\n  .SUFFIXES:\n  .SUFFIXES: .biz .baz\n  .baz.biz: foo.bar ; @echo make $@ from $<\n\nGNU reads a suffix-named rule that carries prerequisites as an ordinary rule for a file called `.baz.biz`, warns `ignoring prerequisites on suffix rule definition`, and installs no `.baz`-to-`.biz` suffix rule at all. So:\n\n  goal `.baz.biz` → GNU warns and builds it; Ronin refuses, `'.baz.biz' missing and no known rule to make it`.\n  goal `foo.biz`  → GNU refuses, `No rule to make target 'foo.biz'`; Ronin builds it from foo.baz.\n\nRonin has made exactly the opposite choice on both counts: it installs the suffix rule and loses the file target. One decision fixes both directions — a suffix-named rule with prerequisites is an ordinary rule — so the two row groups are one node rather than two.\n\nThe warning is narration and not owed. What is owed is which of the two targets exists."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:52:38.961128912Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T12:58:56.774553214Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Characterised against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle before anything was written, and the triage note this node was filed from is half wrong — usefully so, because what is wrong about it is what identifies the mechanism.\n\nTHE MATRIX. Every cell probed through both binaries.\n\n  The shape alone decides nothing. `.baz.biz:` with neither half on `.SUFFIXES` is an ordinary file target and nothing else; with one half declared, likewise. Both halves have to be declared for the pair to be converted, which `keep_only_declared_suffix_rules` already had.\n\n  The file target always survives. `convert_to_pattern` looks the pair's name up in the file database and leaves the entry where it found it, so the conversion adds a pattern rule and takes nothing away. `make .c.o` runs the `.c.o:` recipe. `make .baz.biz` runs the `.baz.biz: foo.bar` recipe with `$<` = `foo.bar`. `.PHONY: .c.o` beside a `.c.o:` rule makes the file target phony and leaves the suffix rule working. This is the whole of what Ronin was getting wrong in the `no-rule-to-make` direction, and it was getting it wrong for every suffix-shaped name rather than only for one with prerequisites.\n\n  Prerequisites, outside POSIX. The pair is still converted; the written prerequisites are dropped on the way; a warning says so, at the recipe's line. Upstream test #8 is the observable consequence and it now agrees: a `foo.biz` newer than `foo.baz` is current however new `foo.bar` is, because `foo.bar` is not one of the converted rule's prerequisites.\n\n  Prerequisites, under POSIX. `rule.c:352` — `if (posix_pedantic) continue;` — passes the pair over entirely, so no `%.biz: %.baz` exists and `foo.biz` cannot be made though `foo.baz` is right there. This is the whole of the `refusal-not-made` direction. `.POSIX:` is latched wherever it stands, before or after the rule, and with prerequisites of its own; the conversion runs after the last makefile is closed, so ordering never matters. A pair with no prerequisites is converted under POSIX as always: the rejection is about prerequisites, not about `.POSIX:`.\n\n  `f->deps` is the whole chain. An order-only prerequisite disqualifies a pair under POSIX and is dropped by the ordinary conversion, exactly as a normal one is.\n\nWHAT THE TRIAGE NOTE HAD WRONG, AND WHY IT MATTERS. It read the three rows as one decision failing in both directions — Ronin installing the suffix rule where GNU installs none, and losing the file target. Only the second half is true. GNU does install the suffix rule; features/suffixrules tests #4 through #7 turn POSIX mode on and off through `$X.POSIX:`, and the note's `foo.biz` cell was GNU's POSIX cell read as its ordinary one. So this is two decisions rather than one direction of a single one, and the second decision — POSIX — had no representation in the fork at all.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT. `is_buildable_target` in dep.rs excluded every suffix-shaped name from the manifest unconditionally. It now takes `has_rule` and asks about it: the exclusion is only ever needed for a suffix-shaped name nothing declared, where emission hands `.c.o` to the built-in `%.o: %.c` with an empty stem and runs `cc -c -o .c.o` against no input. That cell is unmoved and stays a deliberate divergence — GNU runs the cc and fails, Ronin refuses, and no file is written either way.\n\n`populate_suffix_rule` gained the POSIX branch and the warning, and `r.order_only_inputs.clear()` beside the `r.inputs.clear()` that was already there — the dropping was half done, so `.baz.biz: | foo.bar` reached the converted rule and had the stem substituted into it, looking for `foo.foo.bar`. `.POSIX:` is read through a new `Evaluator::is_posix`, because the switch is latched during the read and the conversion happens after it.\n\nFIXTURES. Eight, recorded from the oracle: feature-suffix-shaped-target-with-prerequisites-is-a-file, feature-suffix-shaped-target-is-a-file-when-no-suffix-is-declared, feature-suffix-rule-target-is-a-file-of-that-name, feature-suffix-rule-conversion-drops-the-written-prerequisites, feature-suffix-rule-conversion-drops-an-order-only-prerequisite, feature-posix-suffix-rule-with-prerequisites-makes-no-pattern-rule, feature-posix-suffix-shaped-target-with-prerequisites-is-still-a-file, feature-posix-keeps-a-suffix-rule-without-prerequisites.\n\nDelivered in kati d619930 on branch nplan/make-suffix-shaped-target-with-prerequisites."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T13:49:35.484938391Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Row audit, against a rebuilt base binary and a re-recorded inventory (the tsv landed in 0af6325, the third commit of this dispatch, because that is the run that re-recorded it).\n\nThe three owned rows all left the compiler bucket:\n\n  features/suffixrules.diff.4  compiler product-name+no-rule-to-make → narration ninja-progress\n  features/suffixrules.diff.5  compiler product-name+no-rule-to-make → narration ninja-progress\n  features/suffixrules.diff.6  compiler ninja-progress+fatal-decoration+refusal-not-made → narration product-name+fatal-decoration+shared-refusal\n\n.4 and .5 are the file target, in both POSIX and non-POSIX mode: Ronin builds `.baz.biz` now and the only difference left is Ninja's progress line. .6 is the POSIX half, and `shared-refusal` is the right shape for it — both tools now refuse `foo.biz` because neither has a pattern rule to make it from `foo.baz`, and they word the refusal differently.\n\nTwo more rows came with them, out of unclassified rather than out of compiler, and they are the cells the node's own matrix predicted:\n\n  features/suffixrules.diff.7  unclassified ninja-progress+evaluation → narration ninja-progress\n  features/suffixrules.diff.8  unclassified no-work-line+up-to-date-line+evaluation → narration no-work-line+up-to-date-line\n\n.7 is upstream test #7, the non-POSIX conversion happening at all. .8 is upstream test #8, the written prerequisites being dropped from the converted rule — the cell that `r.order_only_inputs.clear()` and its normal-prerequisite sibling are for.\n\nNo row moved the wrong way. The three watched flappers held their class."
                    }

                    {
