            {
                id make-bad-option-argument-in-a-makefiles-makeflags
                title "Forgive an option argument a makefile's MAKEFLAGS cannot supply"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T19:22:16.903290859Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while completing make-unknown-switch-in-a-makefiles-makeflags, and deliberately left out of it: that node is about a switch Make does not KNOW, and this is about a switch it knows whose argument it cannot read. Both are GNU's one `bad` flag, but they are two behaviours and only the first was this dispatch's sentence.\n\nGNU's mechanism, read rather than inferred. main.c `decode_switches` sets `bad = 1` for anything getopt refuses and for a `case string:` whose argument is empty, and then `if (bad && origin == o_command) print_usage (bad)` — so a makefile's own MAKEFLAGS (decoded at `o_env` after the read) survives all of it. The two halves differ in whether anything is PRINTED: `opterr = origin == o_command` silences getopt's own message, but the empty-string-argument `error (NILF, 0, ...)` call is unconditional and prints even for a makefile.\n\nThe matrix, against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle, all with `MAKEFLAGS += ` in a makefile and now agreeing except where marked:\n\n  -zz, --frobnicate, --=x, -rz     ignored, silent, rc 0        FIXED under the unknown-switch node\n  -O bogus, -j abc, -l abc         switch takes its no-arg default, the word is dropped   FIXED\n  -W                               rc 0, MAKEFLAGS empty        Ronin: rc 2, `missing -W value`\n  --jobserver-style=               rc 0, message printed, build goes on   Ronin: rc 2, refuses\n  --debug=zzz                      FATAL in both, rc 2          already agrees — decode_debug_flags runs after decode_switches and dies whatever the origin\n  --shuffle=bogus                  rc 0, kept verbatim in MAKEFLAGS as ` --shuffle=bogus`   Ronin: rc 2, `invalid shuffle mode`\n\nTwo reproducers, both one line:\n\n    MAKEFLAGS += -W\n    all: ; @echo built > out\n\n    MAKEFLAGS += --jobserver-style=\n    all: ; @echo built > out\n\nGNU builds both. Ronin refuses both.\n\n`--shuffle=bogus` is a third thing again and probably a separate node: GNU never validates the mode in this path at all, it stores the string and publishes it, so replicating it means deciding where Ronin's shuffle validation belongs rather than forgiving anything.\n\nWhere a fix goes. Ronin's `parse_arguments` has two failure channels and only one of them is now source-aware: `refuse(...)` returns `Ok(Some(Action::Immediate))` and is guarded by `ArgumentSource::refuses_an_unknown_switch`, while a missing or unreadable option argument returns `Err(Error)` from `value()`/`OutputSync::parse` and propagates with `?` from a dozen call sites. Making the second channel forgiving means either giving `value()` the source, or catching the error per word at the top of the loop — the latter is closer to GNU, which forgives a whole word and moves to the next, but it has to leave `index` and the partly-applied cluster in a defined state."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:32:39.531980059Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "One more cell for this node's matrix, found while closing make-shuffle-mode-is-validated-where-gnu-does-not and belonging here rather than there.\n\n    MAKEFLAGS += --shuffle=\n    all: ; @printf 'MF=[$(MAKEFLAGS)]\\n'\n\n    GNU 4.4.1  rc 0, builds, MAKEFLAGS empty — and prints\n               `make: the '--shuffle' option requires a non-empty string argument` on stderr first\n    Ronin      rc 0, builds, MAKEFLAGS empty, silent\n\nSo the outcome now agrees and only the diagnostic is missing. It is the same `bad` flag this node is about: `decode_switches` complains about an empty `case string:` argument at every origin and only `print_usage` is guarded by `origin == o_command`, which is why the command line dies with the message plus usage while a makefile gets the message alone and carries on.\n\nWorth having beside the node's existing `-W` and `--jobserver-style=` cells, because the three together show the shape: GNU always says something, and the origin decides only whether it then stops."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T11:04:50.954892644Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T11:05:37.490679886Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, kati b7ac141 pinned beside it. Probed first as the dispatch asked, and the entry-66 canonicalisation gate had NOT fixed it: both of the filing's cells were still there, and the probe found the defect is wider than the filing's own matrix.\n\nWHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG. `parse_arguments` had two failure channels and only one was source-aware. The other returned `Err(Error)` from `value()`, `jobs_value`, `load_value` and `OutputSync::parse` and propagated with `?`, so it was not just unforgiving at the wrong origin — it left through the wrong door entirely. Measured at both origins over every switch that takes an argument:\n\n  argv, switch is the last word    GNU rc 2 with usage      Ronin rc 1\n                                   (-W -I -f -C -E --file --directory --include-dir --eval --what-if --jobserver-style)\n  makefile MAKEFLAGS, same         GNU rc 0 and builds      Ronin rc 2\n  argv `-jabc` `-j0` `--jobs=abc`  GNU rc 2                 Ronin rc 1\n  makefile, same                   GNU rc 0 and builds      Ronin rc 2\n  argv `--jobs=`                   GNU rc 2                 Ronin rc 0, took it for unlimited\n  argv `-labc` `--load-average=abc`  GNU rc 0 and builds    Ronin rc 1\n  makefile, same                   GNU rc 0 and builds      Ronin rc 2\n  argv `-Obogus` `--output-sync=bogus`  GNU rc 2            Ronin rc 1\n  makefile, same                   GNU rc 2                 Ronin rc 2, already agreed\n  makefile `--jobserver-style=`    GNU says so, rc 0        Ronin rc 2\n  makefile `--shuffle=`            GNU says so, rc 0        Ronin silent, rc 0\n\nThe exit code on the command line is the half the filing did not name and is the same defect: an option error leaving as a Ronin `Error` is answered at the process boundary with Ninja's status, and Make abandons with two.\n\nGNU'S SHAPE, read rather than inferred. `decode_switches` (main.c) has ONE `bad` flag and THREE ways of raising it, and they differ in what is printed and in who dies:\n\n  getopt's own      `opterr = origin == o_command` silences the message\n                    everywhere else; the switch is lost in silence\n  `error (NILF...)` an empty string argument, or a job count that is not a\n                    positive integer: printed at EVERY origin, unguarded\n  the post-pass     `decode_debug_flags` and `decode_output_sync_flags` run\n                    after every stream and call `fatal`, which no origin forgives\n\nand one place that acts: `if (bad && origin == o_command) print_usage (bad)`.\n\nRonin now says the same three things. `Invocation::unreadable` is the first, `complain` the second, `undecodable` the third; `non_empty` became a `complain` (it was recording nothing at a forgiving origin, where GNU prints). `value()` answers `Option` rather than `Result` — `None` is getopt's missing argument, the word is over, the switch is dropped — and `jobs_value` answers `Option` the same way while complaining about the count. `OutputSync::parse` answers `Option` and `read_output_sync` records it as undecodable.\n\n`load_value` LOST its validation, and that is the fix rather than a regression: GNU's `case floating:` is `atof (coptarg)` and nothing else, so `-labc` is a ceiling of zero and builds, exactly as a bare `-l` does. tests/cli.rs's load test carried the opposite assertion and now reads as the accepting half of its own list.\n\nWORDING, and it took three rows off the upstream inventory. `missing -f value` was the shared Ninja `CliError` text leaking into Make's interface voice, where `unrecognized option '%s'` and `invalid option -- '%c'` are already getopt's own. It is now getopt's two sentences, chosen by the spelling the word wrote rather than the switch it resolves to: `option requires an argument -- 'f'` for a short one and `option '--makefile' requires an argument` for a long one — so `--makefile` is not reported as `--file` and `--old-file` is not reported as `-o`. The upstream suite's `options/dash-f` asks for `/requires an argument/` from three spellings, and those three cases now pass.\n\nWHERE THE COMPLAINTS GO. A makefile-origin complaint is raised during the read, so it is a compile-time diagnostic: `DecodedMakeflags` carries the rendered lines back and `normalize_makeflags_assignment` raises each through `warn_loc!` with no location, which is GNU's `error (NILF, ...)`. That path is the descriptor make-compiler-warnings-reach-the-sink built one node earlier, so these reach a library caller's sink rather than the process's stderr, free. Guarded by `repeats_a_finished_read` so a staging pass does not say it twice.\n\nVerified against the oracle over 60-odd cells at both origins — every switch that takes an argument, in every spelling, missing / empty / attached / separated / unreadable — and the matrix is clean.\n\nTEETH. tests/make/a-makefile-makeflags-drops-a-switch-it-cannot-read is oracle-recorded and carries six bad writes in one makefile; measured by dropping the origin guard in `unreadable`: `1 of 893 ported cases diverge: build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`. tests/make_regressions.rs::a_switch_that_cannot_be_read_ends_only_the_command_line gates what the corpus format cannot see — that the command line abandons with 2 rather than 1 for six spellings, that the makefile builds, that the two GNU complains about at every origin are said, and that the one it loses silently is lost silently.\n\nGATES: workspace 909/0; conformance 335/52 normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297/279/76 and 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1288 rows to 1285 — the three `options/dash-f` rows removed, audited above, `unclassified` 144 to 141, compiler still ZERO, interface unchanged at 25 and narration at 1119; make_port 4/4 over 893 with no recording rewritten; fmt and both Clippy passes clean."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T11:09:24.19471213Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-delete-on-error-races-a-secondary
                title "Delete a failed target before the run ends, every time"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T19:22:34.871530373Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "A build-intent gate flapper, measured and shown to pre-date the dispatch that found it. Not a test defect: the test is right and the product is racing.\n\n`tests/make/target-delete-on-error-removes-a-secondary` fails intermittently with\n\n    target-delete-on-error-removes-a-secondary: 'hello.z' was produced and GNU Make produced no such file\n\nThe case is `.DELETE_ON_ERROR:` with `.SECONDARY: hello.z`, and a pattern recipe `cat $< > $@ ; exit 1` that writes the target and then fails. GNU deletes `hello.z`; Ronin sometimes leaves it.\n\nMeasured rather than assumed, invoking through a `make`-named link both times so the multicall path is the same:\n\n  working tree with the four-node dispatch applied   8 of 30 runs left hello.z\n  clean checkout of ronin dd47353, kati d49463e      7 of 30 runs left hello.z\n\nand at the gate level, `cargo test --release --test make_port` in a clean dd47353 worktree failed 1 run in 4 with exactly this case. So the rate is roughly a quarter to a third of runs, it is the same before and after, and nothing in that dispatch could reach it: the Ronin change was confined to `ArgumentSource` and the makefile MAKEFLAGS decoder, the kati changes to the assignment-operator scan, `MAKEFILES`, and the export attribute. This makefile assigns nothing, has no `$` adjacent to an operator, and never mentions MAKEFLAGS.\n\nWorth knowing before fixing: `ronin --make` rather than the `make`-named link left the file 0 times in 30, which is either a real difference in the two entry paths or a timing difference that hides the race. That is the first thing to settle, because it says whether the deletion is racing the process exit or racing another edge.\n\nThe suspicion the numbers point at is the deletion competing with the failure path that ends the build — a target deleted on error is removed by whoever notices the recipe failed, and if the run is torn down first the removal never happens. `.SECONDARY` is in the case because an intermediate is normally removed anyway, so the case is specifically about the delete-on-error removal being the one that has to happen.\n\nUntil it is fixed this poisons any full-gate run at about one in four, and it will be misread as caused by whatever dispatch is in flight — as it nearly was here."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T22:31:49.865709494Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T22:40:27.747284227Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Not a race at all, and now not a flapper either: a certainty whose trigger is a coincidence. The withdrawal was comparing against a timestamp the file had never had.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, caught in the act rather than reasoned about. `recompute_dirty` (src/graph.rs) stands an absent invented intermediate's place by writing the newest input's timestamp onto the *output's own* mtime — `for output in &edge_data.out { runtime.node_mut(*output).set_mtime(newest_input) }` — which is what hides its absence from whatever reads it. `prepare_edge` then took `old_mtimes` from exactly that field, and `withdraw_outputs` withdraws an output only when `disk.stat(path) != old_mtime`. So the question asked after the recipe failed was \"does hello.z's timestamp differ from hello.x's?\", not \"did the recipe write hello.z?\".\n\nThe two are the same file's timestamp whenever the chain finishes inside one filesystem timestamp tick, because Linux stamps mtimes from the timer tick rather than from the clock. Measured on this host: 4,000 files written in a tight loop carry 64 distinct mtimes, the smallest gap 4.000046ms — CONFIG_HZ=250. `echo body > hello.x` and `cat hello.x > hello.z` are two trivial recipes; whether they land in the same 4ms is the whole coin flip. That is why the rate moves with host speed and load between one in four and one in a thousand, why it is not dispatch-correlated, and why it looks like a race while being deterministic given the timestamps.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE. Instrumented `withdraw_outputs` and stressed the fixture until it failed, keeping only the failing runs' traces. Five caught in 12,000 runs, and every one says the same thing:\n\n    withdraw which=DeleteOnError eligible=Some(1) outputs=1 old_mtimes=1\n      path=hello.z old=1786832265606120083 seen=Some(1786832265606120083)\n\nThe withdrawal ran, the output was eligible, `.DELETE_ON_ERROR` was in force, and the guard refused because the two numbers are equal to the nanosecond. The directory listing beside it shows hello.x, hello.z and .ninja_log all stamped 00:17:45.606120083 — three writes at three different moments on one tick. Four of the five failures landed at that same instant in four independent lanes, which is the tick boundary, not a scheduler interleaving. Nothing else was ever implicated: status was `unix_wait_status(256)` (an ordinary exit 1), no signal, no global interrupt, `command_finished` returned Ok.\n\nTHE FIX, in src/build.rs. `mtimes_the_outputs_hold(edge, outputs)` now answers what `prepare_edge` used to read off the node. For an ordinary edge it is the scan's answer, unchanged. For an edge whose outputs were substituted — `runtime.edge(edge).absent_intermediate()`, the flag the substitution already records — it asks the disk, because that edge is precisely the one that has lost the output's own timestamp. A stat that fails answers MISSING, which withdraws whatever is found afterwards: the safe direction for a file a stopped recipe may have half-written. The cost is one stat per output on the edges that substituted and nothing at all on any other. `old_mtimes` also feeds `unchanged_outputs`, and that reading is fixed by the same change: an output that went from absent to written is now reported as changed rather than as unchanged-by-coincidence.\n\nGNU Make was consulted rather than guessed at. `delete_target` compares `stat` against `file->last_mtime`, which for a file that was not there is NONEXISTENT — a value no real file can hold. GNU has no stand-in to be confused by, and it deletes the file in both shapes of the case.\n\nTHE FLAPPER IS NOW A GATE. tests/make/target-delete-on-error-removes-a-secondary-stamped-like-its-prerequisite is the same case with `touch -r $< $@` added to the recipe, so the collision the tick used to supply by chance is made deliberate and happens on every host at every speed. Recorded from the pristine 4.4.1 oracle through the provenance-enforcing harness, which prints `*** Deleting file 'hello.z'` and leaves none. Against the tree with the fix removed the new case fails every time, with the node's own words — `'hello.z' was produced and GNU Make produced no such file`. The original probabilistic case stays where it is.\n\nSTABILITY.\n  target-delete-on-error-removes-a-secondary                              30 of 30 green\n  target-delete-on-error-removes-a-secondary-stamped-like-its-prerequisite 30 of 30 green\n  the original fixture under the stress that caught the defect            0 of 12,000 left hello.z\n    (the identical stress before the fix: 5 of 12,000, and 10 of 1,500 in an earlier window)\n  cargo test --release --test make_port                                   5 of 5 green, 741 cases\n  full workspace suite                                                    3 of 3 green\n\nThe whole signal and delete-on-error matrix is inside those make_port runs: twelve signal cases and the twenty-two `target-delete-on-error-*` and `secondary-*` cases all pass in each of the five.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean — the fix first put `prepare_edge` one line over the density gate at 102, which is why the answer is a named function rather than an `allow` — and kati `-D warnings` clean. Make conformance 387 runs, 318 identical / 69 differing, the baseline unmoved. Graph equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements. Upstream inventory 1287 rows at 168 unclassified / 0 compiler / 41 interface / 1078 narration, unchanged and with compiler still zero; the .tsv is byte-identical.\n\nTWO THINGS THE FILING GOT WRONG, recorded so the next reader does not repeat them. `ronin --make` is not a way to invoke Make mode — the front end is chosen by the invoked name alone, so that command prints usage and exits 2 having built nothing. \"left the file 0 times in 30\" was measuring a run that never happened, and the same trap voided a re-measurement here until a properly named link was used. And the defect does not pre-date the dispatch in the sense of being someone else's: it pre-dates it in time, but it was always this, and no amount of looking at the withdrawal path for a concurrent interleaving would have found it, because there is none. -j1 and -j8 fail at the same rate.\n\nFOUND WHILE MEASURING and filed rather than fixed: make-port-harness-scratch-is-shared-between-runs. Two `make_port` harness processes in one checkout write the same `target/make-port-work/<case>` directories and corrupt each other — measured, two concurrent runs both failed, one on a doubled log and one on a setup step. Nothing runs it that way today, but a case failing for that reason would read exactly like a product defect, which is the cost this node just paid in a different currency."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T22:46:09.189992583Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-shuffle-mode-is-validated-where-gnu-does-not
                title "Keep a shuffle mode GNU Make never validates"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T19:23:12.651571574Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-unknown-switch-in-a-makefiles-makeflags and kept out of it, and out of make-bad-option-argument-in-a-makefiles-makeflags too, because it is not the `bad` flag at all: GNU never rejects this value, so there is nothing to forgive.\n\n    MAKEFLAGS += --shuffle=bogus\n    all: ; @printf 'MF=[$(MAKEFLAGS)]\\n' > out\n\n    GNU 4.4.1  rc 0, no diagnostic, out holds `MF=[ --shuffle=bogus]`\n    Ronin      rc 2, `ronin: invalid shuffle mode: Invalid value: 'bogus'`, nothing built\n\nGNU takes `--shuffle` as a `case string:` switch, stores whatever it is given, and publishes it again in MAKEFLAGS unexamined — the mode is only interpreted where the shuffle is actually performed, so a value nothing ever shuffles by is never looked at. Ronin validates it in `Shuffle::requested` at parse time, in both option streams.\n\nThe command line is the cell that decides how much of this is worth matching, and it was NOT probed here — check `make --shuffle=bogus` against the oracle before choosing. If GNU also builds there, the fix is to stop validating at parse time and carry the string; if GNU refuses on the command line and not from a makefile, then this really is the `bad`/origin distinction after all and it belongs with the sibling node.\n\nAlso unprobed and cheap to settle at the same time: whether GNU's published `--shuffle=bogus` reaches a child make, and what that child then does with it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:17:50.228155211Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:32:29.062424358Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The command-line cell the filing asked for was probed first, and it decided the shape of the fix: GNU DOES refuse there. `make --shuffle=bogus` is `make: *** invalid shuffle mode: Invalid value: 'bogus'.  Stop.`, rc 2, and an environment `MAKEFLAGS=--shuffle=bogus` refuses identically. So this is the origin distinction after all, and it is the same mechanism as make-unknown-switch-in-a-makefiles-makeflags — reached from the other side.\n\nWHERE THE BEHAVIOUR LIVES, and it is a position in `main` rather than a flag. main.c:1648:\n\n    /* Handle shuffle mode argument.  */\n    if (shuffle_mode)\n      { shuffle_set_mode (shuffle_mode); ... shuffle_mode = xstrdup (effective_mode); }\n\n`shuffle_set_mode` is the only place a mode is ever read, and it runs ONCE, after `decode_env_switches (MAKEFLAGS, o_command)` at 1603 and before `read_all_makefiles`. The second decode of the same variable — `decode_env_switches (MAKEFLAGS, o_env)` at 2091 — happens after it and never reaches the block again. `--shuffle` is a `case string:` switch (main.c:502), so a makefile's word simply replaces the table entry, and `define_makeflags` republishes whatever the table holds. Nothing looks at it, nothing reorders by it, and nothing re-applies the command line over it afterwards.\n\nThree consequences the probe confirmed, and only the first was in the filing:\n\n  1. A word naming no mode is not an error at that origin.\n  2. The word is republished VERBATIM, where the command line's is republished as the permutation it settled on. `MAKEFLAGS += --shuffle=random` publishes `random`; `make --shuffle=random` publishes a seed.\n  3. The makefile's word gets the LAST word on the table. `make --shuffle=reverse` with `MAKEFLAGS += --shuffle=none` in the makefile builds in reverse and publishes ` --shuffle=none`.\n\nTHE MATRIX. Command line and inherited environment, where nothing moved and nothing was allowed to: `--shuffle=bogus` fatal rc 2 at both origins; `random` and bare `--shuffle` settle on a seed and publish it; `reverse`, `identity`, a digit seed publish verbatim; `none` publishes nothing; `--shuffle=` refuses. Makefile origin, where every cell moved:\n\n  bogus     GNU ` --shuffle=bogus`        was rc 2      now agrees\n  BoGuS     GNU ` --shuffle=BoGuS`        was rc 2      now agrees, case kept\n  random    GNU ` --shuffle=random`       was a seed    now agrees\n  bare      GNU ` --shuffle=random`       was a seed    now agrees\n  none      GNU ` --shuffle=none`         was empty     now agrees\n  42        GNU ` --shuffle=42`           agreed        still agrees\n  reverse   GNU ` --shuffle=reverse`, and the run is NOT reordered   agreed on both  still agrees\n  empty     GNU publishes nothing and builds             was rc 2     now agrees\n  cl reverse + makefile none   GNU builds p3 p2 p1 and publishes ` --shuffle=none`\n                                                         was ` --shuffle=reverse`   now agrees\n\nDELIVERED, Ronin only — kati's shuffle machinery is untouched, because this is entirely about which stream a switch arrived on. `Invocation` had one field doing two jobs: the mode this run shuffles by and the word `MAKEFLAGS` republishes. They are the same only when the command line wrote it, so `shuffle_spelling` is now its own field and `read_shuffle` decides what a stream does with the argument via `ArgumentSource::shuffle_effect`.\n\nThe third answer is what finding 3 forced. `decode_makefile_makeflags` reads three streams — the persistent table, the makefile's assignment, and the command line and environment re-asserted over the top so a switch typed on the command line outranks one a makefile took away. That last stream had been reading as `Makefile`, and re-asserting the command line's `--shuffle=reverse` over the makefile's `none` is exactly what GNU does not do. `ArgumentSource::Protection` names it, `ShuffleEffect::Ignored` is its answer for this switch, and every switch that acts is protected as before — verified with `-k` beside a makefile's bogus shuffle.\n\n`evaluated_invocation` also moved to the makefile origin. It reads the settled `MAKEFLAGS` back to configure the one Ninja scheduler; that text is the switch table as the makefiles left it, so a word GNU accepted there without examining has to survive being read back.\n\nFIXTURES, four, oracle-recorded. a-shuffle-mode-a-makefiles-makeflags-names (bogus, republished, build succeeds), a-shuffle-word-a-makefile-writes-travels-as-written (`random` stays the word), a-shuffle-a-makefile-takes-away-still-orders-the-build (the command line's reverse still orders p3 p2 p1 while the table publishes `none` — both facts in one case), and a-shuffle-mode-on-the-command-line-is-refused, the control that must not move. One unit test, a_makefiles_shuffle_word_is_republished, pins the four table cells directly.\n\nGATES. fmt, check, clippy three passes, doc, workspace tests 867/0, make_port 4/0 over 770 cases, Make conformance 318/69 unmoved, equivalence 297/278/0, upstream inventory unmoved at 1283 rows and 167/0/37/1079 with compiler zero, Ninja conformance 425 upstream plus every companion suite, real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git in 45s at -j8.\n\nFILED RATHER THAN FOLDED IN: at the makefile origin GNU still PRINTS `the '--shuffle' option requires a non-empty string argument` for `MAKEFLAGS += --shuffle=` and then builds, while Ronin is silently right about the outcome. That is the same shape as make-bad-option-argument-in-a-makefiles-makeflags, which is already filed for a switch Make knows whose argument it cannot read and already names \"whether anything is printed\" as its open half. Noted there rather than opened again."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:33:50.643831058Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-semicolon-does-not-stop-an-assignment-scan
                title "Read an assignment whose name holds a semicolon"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T19:23:29.249015498Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while fixing make-assignment-operator-scan-skips-a-reference, and adjacent to it rather than part of it: that node gave kati's operator scan the reference skip GNU's has, and this is a second, independent way the same scan disagrees with GNU's.\n\n    a;b=c\n    all: ; @echo ok\n\n    GNU 4.4.1  rc 0, builds — the line defines a variable named `a;b`\n    Ronin      rc 2, `ronin: Makefile:1: missing separator.`\n\nGNU's `parse_variable_definition` (reference/gnumake/src/variable.c) has no case for `;` at all: it is not in `STOP_SET (c, MAP_COMMENT|MAP_NUL)`, it is not blank, it is not `=` or `:`, and `*p` is not `=`, so it falls through `other:` and the walk continues to the `=` after it. kati looks for the first of `:`, `=` and `;` with `find_outside_reference(line, b\":=;\")` and treats a `;` it reaches first as the rule-and-recipe separator, so the line is handed to `parse_rule` and dies for having no colon.\n\nThe `;` in that pattern set is doing real work for rule lines — `all: foo ; recipe` is one — so it cannot simply be dropped. What GNU does is ask the assignment question first, over the whole line and without `;` being special, and only parse a rule if the answer is no; kati dispatches on whichever of the three characters comes first. Making the two agree means separating the two questions rather than tuning the character set.\n\nNot probed, and worth settling before fixing: whether a `;` before a `:` changes which of the two a line is (`a;b: c` — rule or refusal), and whether a target-specific assignment after a `;` behaves the same way.\n\nThe neighbouring paren cell was probed and the two tools already agree on it: `a(b:c)d=e` builds in both, though for different reasons — GNU declines it as a variable definition (the `:` sets `end`, the next character is not `=` or `:`, so the walk returns NULL) while kati's scan skips the parenthesised group wholesale and finds the `=`. Agreement by two different routes is worth knowing about if this scan is ever reworked."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:27:14.742586994Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:39:25.372900089Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered by separating the two questions, which is what the filing said the fix had to be, and the unprobed cells it named both turned out to already agree.\n\nThe rule, read rather than inferred. GNU Make asks \"does this line define a variable\" first, over the whole line, and parses a rule only when the answer is no — `eval` calls `parse_var_assignment`, which loops `parse_variable_definition` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c:517, variable.c:1610). That scan has no case for `;`: it is not in `STOP_SET (c, MAP_COMMENT|MAP_NUL)`, it is not blank, it is not `=` or `:`, and `*p` is not `=`, so it reaches `other:` and continues. The `;` only becomes the rule-and-recipe separator in the SECOND question, where GNU cuts the line at the first unquoted one and then looks for the colon in what is left.\n\nThat second half is what answers the filing's first unprobed cell. `a;b: c` is `missing separator` in GNU, not a rule — the line is cut at the `;`, leaving `a`, and `a` has no colon. Ronin already agreed, and now agrees for GNU's reason rather than by luck.\n\nThe matrix, GNU on the left and Ronin as it was on the right. Every cell now agrees:\n\n  a;b=c            defines `a;b`, recursive, origin file   / missing separator\n  x;y := z         defines `x;y`, simple                   / missing separator\n  a;b += c         appends to `a;b`                        / missing separator\n  a;b ?= c         conditional, declines                   / missing separator\n  a;b != echo c    shell assignment                        / missing separator\n  a;b:::= c        `:::=`, which $(flavor) calls recursive / missing separator\n  override a;b     overrides `a;b`                         / missing separator\n  export a;b       exports `a;b`                           / missing separator\n  define a;b       defines `a;b`                           / missing separator\n  undefine a;b     origin becomes undefined                / missing separator\n  a;b: c           missing separator                       already agreed — the recipe took the colon\n  a; b = c         missing separator                       already agreed — a blank ends the name, and a second word before the operator ends the question\n  foo ; bar=baz    missing separator                       already agreed, same reason\n  all: a;b=c       rule `all: a` with recipe `b=c`         already agreed\n  all: v;w=set     the same as a target-specific line      already agreed — the filing's second unprobed cell\n  all: dep ; recipe                                        already agreed, and must keep agreeing\n  a(b:c)d=e        builds in both                          already agreed, still by two different routes\n\nShape delivered, all of it in kati and all of it one character wide. `parse_rule_or_assign` and `is_variable_definition` scan with `b\":=\"` rather than `b\":=;\"`, and the `s.starts_with(b\";\")` arm that dispatched to `parse_rule` is gone with it — it was the only thing that made a `;` an answer to the first question. `parse_define` was already scanning for `b\"=\"` alone and is untouched. Nothing about the name side moved: `is_variable_name` still refuses a blank at depth zero, which is GNU's `end` rule and is why the three `missing separator` cells above stay that way.\n\nTwo fixtures recorded from the oracle: an-assignment-name-may-hold-a-semicolon (seven names holding a semicolon across `=`, `+=`, `:=`, `?=`, `!=`, `override`, `export` and `define`, with `$(origin)` and `$(flavor)` read beside the values because the value agrees in cells where the flavour would not) and a-semicolon-before-a-colon-is-a-missing-separator (the direction that must not move). Unit test parser::tests::a_semicolon_does_not_stop_an_assignment_scan holds the same matrix at the predicate, both ways.\n\nOwned markers: none, as the filing said — no upstream row reads this. 1287 rows unmoved at 167/0/41/1079, compiler 0.\n\nGates: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 861 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored; kati unit tests 166 passed; clippy all three passes silent; make_port green including the two new recordings; conformance 318 identical / 69 differing, unmoved; graph equivalence 297 / 278 / 0 disagreements; real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git in 50s at -j8.\n\nkati commit aa93390 on nplan/make-semicolon-does-not-stop-an-assignment-scan.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:40:59.105793643Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-port-harness-scratch-is-shared-between-runs
                title "Give each make_port run a scratch directory of its own"
                kind @test
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T22:40:56.56037712Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while diagnosing make-delete-on-error-races-a-secondary, and kept out of it because it is the harness rather than the product.\n\n`scratch_for` (tests/make_port.rs) names a case's working directory `target/make-port-work/<case id>` and nothing else. The name has no run in it, so two harness processes in one checkout are one directory: each removes the other's tree mid-run, copies its own Makefile over it, and reads back a listing made of both.\n\nMeasured, two runs of the same test binary started together in one worktree, both of which failed:\n\n  'log' holds \"src\\nsrc\\ngen\\ngen\\n\" where GNU Make left \"src\\ngen\\n\"   (a-makefile-ahead-of-a-refused-one-is-remade)\n  absent-prerequisite-with-a-rule: setup failed                          (the other process had just removed the tree under it)\n\nNothing runs it that way today — `cargo test` takes one binary at a time and the corpus loop is sequential within it — so this is latent rather than live. It is worth closing anyway because of what it costs when it does happen: a case that fails this way reads exactly like a product defect, and one of them was investigated as one. The scheduler-timing node already made this exact repair on the other side of the suite, and the shape of the answer is recorded there — `fixture_directory` puts `SystemTime::now()` nanos beside the pid and the counter and creates with `fs::create_dir` rather than `create_dir_all`, so a name that already exists is one to walk away from. The same treatment here, plus the sweep of stale entries that came with it.\n\nNote that the pid alone is not enough, which is the other half of that node's finding: under `scripts/sandboxed` every run gets its own pid namespace and pids start again from the same small numbers."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T08:22:19.645666914Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T08:29:23.931378116Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, in the shape the filing recorded, and one path further than it named: the make-named LINK was shared too.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, all in tests/make_port.rs.\n\n`run_directory()` makes one directory per RUN — `target/make-port-work/run-<pid>-<nanos>` — with `fs::create_dir` rather than `create_dir_all`, so a name that already exists is one to walk away from and try again rather than one to build in. The clock is in the name because the pid is not enough: under `scripts/sandboxed` a run has its own pid namespace and `process::id()` is a small number that repeats exactly from one run to the next, which is the other half of the scheduler-timing node's finding.\n\n`scratch_for` is now `run_directory().join(&case.id)` and creates it once. The removal that used to open it is gone with the sharing it defended against: inside a directory this process made, a case's scratch cannot already exist, and the `remove_dir_all` was precisely what removed the other run's tree mid-run.\n\n`make_named_ronin` no longer writes `target/make-port-bin/make`. That link was a second shared path with the same defect and a worse failure: `remove_file` then `symlink`, so a second run could unlink the link a first run was about to resolve, and the corpus would report nine hundred cases failing to start. It now lives at `<run>/bin/make`, and the unlink before the symlink is gone because nothing is there to unlink.\n\n`sweep_stale_runs` removes anything under `target/make-port-work` untouched for an hour, once per process — housekeeping rather than correctness, since unique names mean a leftover cannot poison anything, but a red run keeps its scratch on purpose and a panicking one keeps it by accident, and nothing else would collect either. Everything under that root is this harness's, so the sweep also clears the per-case directories the old layout left at the top level: the first run after this change reclaims them.\n\n`discard_run_scratch` takes the run's scratch away when the corpus was green and keeps it, with the path printed, when it was not. A green run's thousand case directories are worth nothing and cost twenty megabytes; a red one's are the evidence. Net effect on a checkout that runs the gate: the twenty megabytes that used to sit there permanently are now zero between runs.\n\nACCEPTANCE, the one deliberate exception to the serial rule, run once through `scripts/sandboxed`: two `make_port` binaries in one checkout started together, `--test-threads 1` each.\n\n    a exited 0, b exited 0\n    test result: ok. 4 passed; 0 failed ... finished in 37.26s\n    test result: ok. 4 passed; 0 failed ... finished in 37.25s\n\n37.3 s wall for the pair against 38.4 s for one alone, and `target/make-port-work` empty afterwards.\n\nTEETH, because two green runs prove nothing unless the same pair is red without the change. `tests/make_port.rs` checked out at 0ba3e31 and the identical parallel run:\n\n    a exited 101, b exited 101\n    a: panicked at tests/make_port.rs:314:40:\n       copying a case file: Os { code: 2, kind: NotFound } — 0.45 s in\n    b: 1 of 968 ported cases diverge from GNU Make's build intent:\n       a-command-line-write-to-a-name-make-works-out-is-taken: 'out' holds\n       \"...wrote=[]\\nwrote=[]\\n\" where GNU Make left \"...wrote=[]\\n\"\n\nBoth failure shapes the filing predicted, in one run: one process copying into a tree the other had just removed, and one reading back a log made of both runs' recipes. The doubled `wrote=[]` is the whole cost of this defect in one line — it reads exactly like a product defect, which is why it is worth closing while nothing runs the harness that way.\n\nNOT CHANGED and deliberately: `target/make-port-comparison.txt` stays where it is. It is what an operator reads after a `MAKE_PORT_COMPARE=1` run, so its path is part of how the run is used; two comparison runs at once would overwrite one another, and that is a thing to notice rather than a thing to hide behind a generated name.\n\nGATES: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; all three clippy passes clean; `cargo test --test make_port` 4 passed / 0 failed over 968 cases in 38.35 s with the eight recorded divergences untouched; workspace 977 passed / 0 failed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T08:29:50.32555867Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-shuffled-drop-unlinks-the-position-not-the-edge
                title "Unlink the walked position rather than the circular edge under a shuffle"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T23:45:37.546523846Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-shuffle-reaches-the-circular-drop, and left out of it deliberately: that node made the shuffle choose which edge closes the loop, and this is the one cell where GNU Make then removes a different entry from the one it just named.\n\nGNU's walk keeps two chains over the same prerequisites — `->next`, written order, and `->shuf`, the shuffled one — and `update_file_1` reads the file through the shuffled chain while unlinking through the written one (reference/gnumake/src/remake.c:596 onward):\n\n    d = du->shuf ? du->shuf : du;\n    ...\n    if (is_updating (d->file))\n      {\n        error (NILF, \"Circular %s <- %s dependency dropped.\", file->name, d->file->name);\n        if (lastd == 0) file->deps = du->next; else lastd->next = du->next;\n        du = du->next;\n        continue;\n      }\n\nSo the message names `d` and the list loses `du`. With no shuffle the two are the same entry and nothing shows; with one they are not.\n\nReproducer, minimised against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n    all: t\n    t: x t\n    \t@echo \"t hat=[$^]\"\n    x: ; @echo x\n\n  make                     x, `Circular t <- t dependency dropped.`, `t hat=[x]`, exit 0 — both agree\n  make --shuffle=reverse   `Circular t <- t dependency dropped.`, x, GNU `t hat=[t]` / Ronin `t hat=[x]`\n\nBoth build x and both build t, so nothing gates on it and no upstream row reads it; the difference is only in what `$^` is left holding. GNU keeps the sibling's name and drops the circular one from the list it walks; Ronin drops the circular one, which is the entry the message named.\n\nWhether to match it is a real decision rather than an oversight. It reads as a stale-pointer artefact of carrying two chains — file.c:681 says outright that \"shuffle mode assumes '->next' and '->shuf' links both traverse the same dependencies\", and this drop is what breaks that assumption — but it is what 4.4.1 does. Matching it means `DepNode` carrying the shuffled order beside the written one rather than being permuted in place, and every walk choosing which of the two it means: the drop would test `shuffled[i]` and unlink `deps[i]`, while `actual_inputs` stays aligned with `deps` and needs no search. That is the same layering question the parent node answered the other way, which is why it is filed rather than folded in.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:04:25.662292439Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:11:22.239408324Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and it cost a great deal less than the filing feared: the drop had to change which NAME it takes, not how the graph carries its prerequisites.\n\nThe matrix, probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. GNU on the left, Ronin as it was on the right. `$^`/`$+`/`$<` are the only cells that ever moved — every one of these built the same files in the same order before and after.\n\n  t: x t\n    no shuffle          hat=[x] / same\n    --shuffle=identity  hat=[x] / same\n    --shuffle=reverse   hat=[t] / hat=[x]\n  t: a b t c\n    no shuffle          hat=[a b c], builds a b c   / same\n    --shuffle=reverse   hat=[a t c], builds c b a   / hat=[a b c]\n    --shuffle=7         hat=[b t c], builds b a c   / hat=[a b c]\n  t: p t q              reverse: hat=[p q]          / same — the circular entry sits where the walk reaches it\n  t: a t b, u: c u d    reverse: both hats unmoved  / same, for the same reason\n  t: a t b t            reverse: hat=[t], plus=[t t] / hat=[a b] — two drops, and the pair stays index-consistent\n  t: a u b / u: c t d   reverse and seed 3, the loop closing one level down / already matched\n\nWhat the shuffle does NOT move is the load-bearing negative the parent node established and this one had to keep: GNU populates `->shuf` over the same prerequisites and leaves `->next`, which the automatic variables walk, exactly where it was. So the written list is `$^`, and the only question is which entry the drop takes out of it.\n\nRead rather than inferred. remake.c:596 takes `d = du->shuf ? du->shuf : du`, tests `is_updating (d->file)`, names `d` in the message and then unlinks `du`:\n\n    if (lastd == 0) file->deps = du->next; else lastd->next = du->next;\n\nSo the message names the shuffled entry and the list loses the walked one. file.c:681 says outright that \"shuffle mode assumes '->next' and '->shuf' links both traverse the same dependencies\", and this drop is what breaks that assumption — but it is what 4.4.1 does, and the fixture records 4.4.1.\n\nShape delivered, and the filing's cost estimate was wrong. It expected `DepNode` to carry the shuffled order beside the written one so that every walk could choose which of the two it meant. It does not need to: `deps` is already the shuffled order, `actual_inputs` is already the written one, and `drop_circular_dependencies` enters `deps` in index order — so the index the walk has reached IS GNU Make's written position, and `drop_prerequisite` need only take the name at that index instead of searching for the one it just removed. Nine lines became four, and the by-name search is gone. The edge still goes with the loop it closed rather than with the position, because Ronin hands the finished plan to a frontend and must not hand it a cycle; GNU has no such consumer, its walk having already passed.\n\nThe positional rule is also what the unshuffled runs always wanted — the two lists agree there, so nothing moved for them — and it keeps the `&:` guard the by-name search had: an edge past the end of the names was never a written prerequisite and leaves the names alone.\n\nOne fixture, recorded from the oracle: a-shuffled-circular-drop-unlinks-the-walked-position — `t: a b t c` under `--shuffle=reverse -j1`, writing `$^` to a file and each prerequisite to an order log, so it gates the name AND the build order together. Recorded `hat` is `a t c`.\n\nRows: none moved. 1283 rows at 163/0/37/1083, compiler 0.\n\nOne adjacent defect found while probing the order-only cells and filed rather than folded in: GNU carries normal and order-only prerequisites in ONE `->next` chain and shuffles them together, where Ronin permutes `deps` and `order_onlys` separately. `t: a b | c d` under `--shuffle=reverse` builds d c b a in GNU and b a d c in Ronin, with no circular dependency anywhere in it — so it is a defect in the shuffle itself rather than in the drop. It shows through the drop too (`t: a | b t c` reverse leaves GNU `$|=[t c]` and Ronin `$|=[b c]`, because the drop's position crosses the boundary the two lists put between them). See make-shuffle-reorders-order-only-prerequisites-apart-from-the-rest.\n\nGates: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 843 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored; clippy all three passes silent; cargo doc clean; make_port green including the new recording; graph equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 compared / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1283 at 163/0/37/1083, unmoved.\n\nkati commit 98bb657 on nplan/make-shuffled-drop-unlinks-the-position-not-the-edge."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:13:11.242416727Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text probe2
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:13:42.822125044Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Two corrections to the delivery note above, and an apology for the two one-word entries between them — \"probe\" and \"probe2\" were written to find out which log kind the image lint would accept, and nothing removes a log entry once it is written.\n\nFirst: the workspace test count is 842 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, unmoved from the baseline this dispatch measured, not 843. The fixture this node added is a data directory under tests/make/ read by `make_build_intent_matches_oracle`, so it grows the corpus from 809 cases to 810 without adding a test function. Every other number in that note was measured and stands.\n\nSecond, for whoever hits it next: `kind: errata` is refused on a node that is still Doing — `nplan_note` reports \"plan image fails lint (1 violation)\" and the on-disk lint stays clean, so the refusal is about the image it would write rather than about anything already committed. A plain `note` is accepted on the same node in the same breath. Errata appears to be reserved for a delivery that is already recorded, which is the right invariant and an opaque way to learn it."
                    }

