            {
                id make-a-failed-makefile-rule-still-reports-the-unread-include
                title "Report an unread include whose own rule failed"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T23:52:57.235512602Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-an-unopenable-include-is-reported-where-gnu-reports-it, and kept out of it because it is a complaint Ronin never makes rather than one it makes in the wrong place.\n\n`show_goal_error` has two callers, not one. That node moved the first — `complain()` at reference/gnumake/src/remake.c:414, the no-rule refusal. The second is job.c:581, where a recipe that failed reports the held complaint before it names the failure:\n\n    include gen.mk\n    gen.mk: ; @echo GENFAIL; exit 1\n    all: ; @echo all\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  GENFAIL / `Makefile:1: gen.mk: No such file or directory` / `make: *** [Makefile:2: gen.mk] Error 1`, exit 2\n  Ronin      GENFAIL, the FAILED block, `ronin: build stopped: subcommand failed.`, exit 2 — no complaint at all\n\nBoth exit 2 and both leave the same files, so nothing gates on it; what is missing is the line saying why the file mattered. It is easy to read the failure as being about the rule alone when it is also about a read that did not happen.\n\nThe same hole shows in the mixed case, and there it is louder because Ronin used to print the wrong file's complaint:\n\n    include gen.mk\n    include nope.mk\n    gen.mk: ; @echo GENFAIL; exit 1\n\n  GNU     GENFAIL / `Makefile:1: gen.mk: No such file or directory` / `*** [Makefile:3: gen.mk] Error 1`\n  Ronin   GENFAIL, the FAILED block, the stopped line — gen.mk's complaint is never made, and nope.mk is never reached\n\nWhere. kati's `plan_regeneration` renders the complaint only on the arm that has no rule to offer, so a missing include that IS remakable carries none. The fix is to render one for every required include that would not open — the `missing_includes` list already holds each name and its `include` line — and carry them out as a per-file table beside the refusal, the way GNU carries `goal->error` per goaldep. Ronin's side then emits a file's complaint where its own remake is settled as lost: `Builder::failed_edges` and `Pass::Lost` already name exactly those targets, which is what make-optional-include-failure-is-per-file built them for.\n\nGNU's guard is worth porting with it: `(goal_dep->flags & (RM_INCLUDED|RM_DONTCARE)) != RM_INCLUDED` means a `-include` never complains however it fails, and `goal->error = 0` after printing means once per file however many times it is reached.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:49:29.475919129Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:07:08.866390327Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the probe corrected the filing on one point: the complaint belongs to the goal being updated, not to the recipe that lost.\n\nRead rather than inferred. `show_goal_error` (reference/gnumake/src/remake.c:302) reads the GLOBAL `goal_dep` — the goaldep `update_goal_chain` is currently working on — and not the file whose child died:\n\n    if ((goal_dep->flags & (RM_INCLUDED|RM_DONTCARE)) != RM_INCLUDED) return;\n    for (goal = goal_list; goal; goal = goal->next)\n      if (goal_dep->file == goal->file)\n        { if (goal->error) { OSS (error, &goal->floc, \"%s: %s\", ...); goal->error = 0; } return; }\n\n`child_error` calls it at job.c:581, immediately before the `[Makefile:N: target] Error N` line. So `include gen.mk` whose `gen.mk: dep` loses over `dep` complains about gen.mk while naming dep as the failure — the two lines name different files on purpose, and that is the cell the filing did not have.\n\nThe matrix, GNU on the left and Ronin as it was on the right. Seven cells, of which four moved:\n\n  include gen.mk, its own rule fails\n    GNU  GENFAIL / `Makefile:1: gen.mk: No such file or directory` / `*** [Makefile:2: gen.mk] Error 1`, exit 2\n    was  GENFAIL, the FAILED block, the stopped line — no complaint at all\n  include gen.mk / include nope.mk, gen.mk's rule fails\n    GNU  the same three lines; nope.mk is never reached  / was: no complaint\n  include gen.mk whose rule WRITES the file and then exits 1\n    GNU  the same three lines — the bytes on disk do not answer the question / was: no complaint\n  include gen.mk with `gen.mk: dep` where DEP's rule fails\n    GNU  DEPFAIL / `Makefile:1: gen.mk: ...` / `*** [Makefile:4: dep] Error 1` — the complaint names the goal\n    was  no complaint, and this is the cell that settles which file is named\n  two required includes, both rules fail, -j1\n    GNU  the first one's complaint alone, and the run ends there  / was: none\n  one required include succeeds, the next fails\n    GNU  the SECOND one's complaint — the update walks them in read order / was: none\n  -include gen.mk whose rule fails\n    GNU  GENFAIL then the refusal, and no located line ever  / already agreed, and must keep agreeing\n  include gen.mk where gen.mk exists and is up to date\n    GNU  nothing at all  / already agreed\n\nShape delivered. kati's `plan_regeneration` renders the complaint for every required include the read did not get, rather than only for the one nothing can make, and a `RegenerationRoot` carries its own in `complaint`. The guard is GNU Make's, moved up to where both callers can share it: RM_INCLUDED without RM_DONTCARE, which is `required` and a location — `-include` never complains however it fails, and a Makefile the command line named has no `include` line to point at and was reported by the read.\n\nRonin holds them as `(Node, String)` beside the remakes, through `RegenerationNames`, `UnitRemakes`, `CompilationState` and `Loaded`, and says one where a non-forgiven pass loses. Which one is `complaints_for`: the first target of the pass whose own recipe lost, and failing that the first the pass did not regenerate. The second reading is what the prerequisite cell needs — gen.mk's own edge never ran, so it is not among the pass's failures, and it is still the goal. The update walks the makefiles in read order and ends on the first it cannot bring up to date, so everything before that one succeeded and everything after was never attempted, which is why one answer is the right number of answers.\n\nWhere it is printed is a decision rather than a translation. `finished` became `complained_of` with an empty list, and the complaint goes into stdout ahead of the `build stopped` line rather than onto stderr. The rule is the one `refused_makefile` already follows — the complaint goes on the stream carrying the line it precedes — and there both are stderr because both are diagnostics. Here the line that ends the run is Ninja's `build stopped`, which is narration on stdout, and a complaint written to the other stream is read after everything rather than beside what it explains. Verified in a merged capture: recipe output, complaint, ending line, in that order, which is GNU's shape.\n\nRows: features/include.diff.18 and .34 moved `unclassified -> narration`, both losing the `evaluation` marker and keeping only Ninja's narration families. Auditing them found GNU Make's own suite testing exactly these cells: `# Included file has a prerequisite that fails to build`, which expects `#MAKEFILE#:3: inc1: $ERR_no_such_file` beside `*** [#MAKEFILE#:5: foo] Error 1` — the goal named in one line and the recipe in the next — and `# SV 56301 Verify pattern rules creating optional includes`. 1283 rows at 159/0/37/1087. Compiler stays 0, interface stays 37.\n\nThree tests in tests/make_regressions.rs, where the corpus cannot reach — nothing about these cells touches a file, so the build-intent gate is blind to all of them: lost-remake-reports-its-unread-include (the complaint between the failure and the ending), a-lost-prerequisite-reports-the-goal (the complaint names gen.mk while the failure names dep, and gen.mk's own recipe never ran), a-forgiven-remake-makes-no-complaint (the negative, which is what keeps the guard honest).\n\nOne thing found while auditing the rows that node 4 needs and the filing has wrong. Its note says the `Failed to remake makefile 'X'.` summary is reached only under `-k`. GNU's suite has it without:\n\n    all:; @echo hello\n    -include inc_a.mk\n    include inc_b.mk\n    %_a.mk %_b.mk:; exit 1\n\nexpects `exit 1` then `#MAKEFILE#:4: Failed to remake makefile 'inc_b.mk'.` with NO `-k`, and the suite's next line asserts `-k` changes nothing about it. The summary comes from main.c's `us_failed` arm, which is reached whenever the update returns rather than dying — and a grouped rule whose first attempt was forgiven is one way to return. Probe that shape in make-keep-going-refuses-every-makefile-it-cannot-make rather than assuming the switch is what decides.\n\nGates: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 847 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored (844 + the three new); clippy all three passes silent; make_port green; conformance 387 runs, 318 identical / 69 differing normalised, unmoved; graph equivalence 297 / 278 / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1283 at 159/0/37/1087, the two rows above audited; real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git in 28s at -j8.\n\nkati commit 5e0f6ee on nplan/make-a-failed-makefile-rule-still-reports-the-unread-include."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:44:34.615312085Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-an-unreadable-include-is-refused-rather-than-raised
                title "Treat an include that opens but will not read as a target with no rule"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T23:53:18.626262888Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-an-unopenable-include-is-reported-where-gnu-reports-it, and separate from it: that node asked WHERE the complaint is made, and this asks whether the read may end the run at all.\n\nGNU Make treats every failure to open an `include`d file the same way, whatever errno says. `eval_makefile` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c:347) stores `deps->error = errno` and returns; the only errnos it dies on are the ones that mean make itself is out of resources — EMFILE, ENFILE, ENOMEM. Everything else becomes a makefile that was not read, and the run carries on to the update, which refuses over it as a target with no rule.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n    include secret.mk        (touch secret.mk; chmod 000 secret.mk)\n      GNU    `Makefile:1: secret.mk: Permission denied` / `make: *** No rule to make target 'secret.mk'.  Stop.`, exit 2\n      Ronin  `ronin: In file included from Makefile:1:` / `Makefile:1: secret.mk: Permission denied`, exit 2\n\n    include adir             (mkdir adir)\n      GNU    `make: *** adir: Is a directory.  Stop.` — no location, and no refusal after it\n      Ronin  `ronin: In file included from Makefile:1:` / `Makefile:1: adir: Is a directory`, exit 2\n\nBoth tools exit 2 in both cells, so nothing gates on it. What differs is the shape: Ronin raises the read error from the read, with an inclusion-stack line GNU has no counterpart for, and never reaches the refusal; and it never gets the chance to remake anything ahead of the file, which is the whole of what the positional-refusal work established.\n\nThe directory cell is the odd one and should be probed further before it is matched: GNU says `adir: Is a directory` with its own name and no location, and does NOT go on to refuse, which suggests it comes from a different site than the ENOENT path — `fopen` on a directory succeeds on Linux and the failure arrives at the read rather than at the open. Worth reading `readline`/`eval` in read.c for it rather than assuming it is the same mechanism.\n\nWhere. kati's read raises non-ENOENT errors as evaluation failures instead of recording them the way it records a missing file, so they never reach `missing_includes` and `plan_regeneration` never sees them. Making them reach it is the fix, with `MissingInclude` carrying the errno's words rather than assuming `No such file or directory`.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:14:41.956565437Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:45:24.815849706Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the probe found that the filing had located only half of it. GNU Make records a failed open in two places on two adjacent lines, and the second is what the cells with a rule turn on.\n\nRead rather than inferred. `eval_makefile` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c) does this and nothing else when `fopen` returns NULL:\n\n    errno = 0;\n    ENULLLOOP (ebuf.fp, fopen (filename, \"r\"));\n    deps->error = errno;                        /* :347 — quoted later */\n    switch (deps->error) { case EMFILE: case ENFILE: case ENOMEM:\n        OS (fatal, reading_file, \"%s\", err); }  /* the only three it dies on */\n    ...\n    if (ebuf.fp == 0) {\n        errno = deps->error;\n        deps->file->last_mtime = NONEXISTENT_MTIME;   /* :409 */\n        return deps;\n    }\n\nThe filing named the first line. The second is the one that matters most: a makefile that would not open is a makefile that is not there, for every question after the read. That is what puts its own rule out of date — a recipe with no prerequisites is up to date the moment its target exists, and an unreadable file exists — and so what lets such a rule repair the file and send the read around again.\n\nAnd the directory cell is a different site entirely, as the filing suspected and asked to have read rather than assumed. `fopen` on a directory succeeds on Linux; the failure arrives at the read, where `readline` finds `ferror` and calls `pfatal_with_name` (read.c:2744). That one is not deferred by anything, has no location, and is named under Make's own name.\n\nThe matrix, probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. GNU on the left, Ronin as it was on the right. Ten cells, of which six moved.\n\n  include secret.mk (chmod 000, no rule)\n    GNU   `Makefile:1: secret.mk: Permission denied` / `*** No rule to make target 'secret.mk'.  Stop.`, exit 2\n    was   `ronin: In file included from Makefile:1:` / the complaint, exit 2 — raised at the read, no refusal\n  include locked/one.mk (the DIRECTORY has no search permission — EACCES from a different site)\n    GNU   the located complaint / the refusal          / was: raised at the read\n  include afile/one.mk (afile is an ordinary file — ENOTDIR)\n    GNU   the located complaint / the refusal          / was: raised at the read\n  include secret.mk WITH a rule that chmods and writes it\n    GNU   GEN, restart, the goals see the variable it defines, exit 0\n    was   raised at the read, exit 2\n  include adir (a directory)\n    GNU   `make: *** adir: Is a directory.  Stop.`, no refusal after it\n    was   `ronin: In file included from Makefile:1:` / `Makefile:1: adir: Is a directory` — the same ending, the wrong shape\n  -include secret.mk                    silent in both, and still is\n  -f secret.mk\n    GNU   `make: secret.mk: Permission denied` from the read, then the refusal\n    was   the complaint alone — the refusal was missing\n  -f adir                               `adir: Is a directory` and stop; already agreed\n  MAKEFILES=secret.mk\n    GNU   silent, builds the goals, exit 0\n    was   `ronin: secret.mk: Permission denied`, exit 2\n  MAKEFILES=adir                        stop; already agreed\n\nThe `-f` and `MAKEFILES` rows are the ones that show the deferral is about the open rather than about `include`. GNU reports the `-f` face from `perror_with_name (\"\", *makefiles)` immediately after `eval_makefile` returns (read.c:219) — at the read, because no `include` line exists to point at — and refuses over it later all the same. `MAKEFILES` is read with `RM_DONTCARE` and its errno is never looked at, so it is as quiet as a name nothing is at.\n\nShape delivered. `Source` forks four ways where it forked three: `Absent`, `Unopened` (deferred, like absence, with the errno's words), `Unreadable` (opened and then not — not deferred, unlocated, Make's own name), and `Exhausted` (the three errnos that are about Make). `Makefile::from_file` takes the open and the read apart rather than doing both in one `fs::read`, because it is the only place that still knows which failed. `MissingInclude` gains `reason`, so the complaint quotes what the system said instead of assuming `No such file or directory`, and `note_missing_include` became `note_unread_include`.\n\nThe inclusion-stack line moved rather than being deleted: `In file included from X:` now wraps the included file's statements instead of the whole of `do_include`. A diagnostic about the open or the read already names the file and the line that asked for it, and a second mention of the same line above it says nothing — but a parse error inside the file still wants the stack, and still has it.\n\n`RegenerationRoot::unread` is `last_mtime = NONEXISTENT_MTIME` crossing the boundary. It travels through `RegenerationNames`, `UnitRemakes`, `CompilationState` and `Loaded` beside `forgiven`, and lands in `Graph::unread_makefiles` — a sparse set beside the node arena, where `unmade_makefiles` and `withdrawal` are, and for the same reason: no node of a Ninja manifest is ever in it. `nodestat_with` consults it and observes `FileTime::MISSING` instead of stat'ing, which is one line and puts the whole of the existing freshness machinery behind GNU's answer. Absence sets it too and means nothing there — the filesystem already agrees.\n\nIndependent confirmation, found while auditing the rows rather than while writing the fix: GNU Make's own suite tests this, in `features/include` under `if (defined $ERR_unreadable_file)`. Four cases — the refusal, the refusal with `-I` naming a directory holding a readable file of the same name, and then two headed \"Unreadable files that we know how to successfully recreate should work\", which expect `bar` and are exactly the cell the freshness half was needed for. Two of those four are the only rows in the 1283 that moved.\n\nRows: features/include.diff.24 and .25 moved `unclassified -> narration`, markers `product-name+evaluation -> ninja-progress`. Both residues are gone: the `evaluation` one because the two tools now evaluate to the same thing, and `product-name` because Ronin no longer has a diagnostic to name itself in. 1283 rows at 161/0/37/1085. Compiler stays 0, interface stays 37, and `features/se_explicit` is untouched.\n\nFixtures, recorded from the oracle: an-include-that-would-not-open-is-refused-after-the-remaking (ENOTDIR, and the gate is that `gen.mk` is written at all — the deferral is what lets the makefile ahead of the refusal be remade), a-makefiles-entry-that-would-not-open-is-passed-over (the goals run, where the run used to end). Two more in tests/make_regressions.rs, where the corpus cannot reach: an-unreadable-include-is-repaired-by-its-own-rule needs mode 000, which is not a thing a committed file can be, and asks the host whether it enforces permissions before trusting itself — a run as root would record the opposite case without saying so; a-named-makefile-that-would-not-open-is-refused-after-the-remaking is an ordering, the read's own complaint before the work and the refusal after it, which the build-intent gate cannot see.\n\nTwo adjacent defects found while probing and filed rather than folded in:\n  make-makefiles-variable-entries-are-never-remade — `MAKEFILES=gen.mk` with a rule for gen.mk: GNU runs it, restarts, and the goals see what it defined; Ronin never remakes it. The read notes those names and the plan never makes roots of them.\n  make-an-output-directory-is-created-where-gnu-make-would-refuse — Ronin creates an edge's output directory the way Ninja does, so a target under a path whose parent is an ordinary file ends the run with `File exists` where GNU Make simply runs the recipe.\n\nGates: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 844 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored (842 + the two new regressions); clippy all three passes silent; make_port green including the two new recordings; conformance 387 runs, 318 identical / 69 differing normalised, unmoved; graph equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 compared / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1283 at 161/0/37/1085, the two rows above audited; real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git from its own Makefile in 48s at -j8.\n\nkati commit 8c0aa26 on nplan/make-an-unreadable-include-is-refused-rather-than-raised."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:44:29.697351348Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-keep-going-refuses-every-makefile-it-cannot-make
                title "Report every unmakeable makefile under keep-going, not the first"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T23:53:44.113596898Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-an-unopenable-include-is-reported-where-gnu-reports-it, and filed rather than folded in because it is about `-k` rather than about where a complaint is printed.\n\n`complain()` reads `keep_going_flag` and chooses between `fatal` and `error` (reference/gnumake/src/remake.c). Without `-k` the first unmakeable makefile ends the run — which is the positional refusal make-remake-before-refusing-a-makefile delivered. With `-k` it does not: the update goes on to the next makefile, refuses over that one too, and `main.c`'s `us_failed` arm then adds a `Failed to remake makefile` line for each.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, `include nope1.mk` / `include nope2.mk` / `all: ; @echo all`:\n\n  make -k\n    GNU    Makefile:1: nope1.mk: No such file or directory\n           make: *** No rule to make target 'nope1.mk'.\n           Makefile:2: nope2.mk: No such file or directory\n           make: *** No rule to make target 'nope2.mk'.\n           Makefile:1: Failed to remake makefile 'nope1.mk'.\n           Makefile:2: Failed to remake makefile 'nope2.mk'.\n           exit 2\n    Ronin  the first complaint and the first refusal, exit 2\n\n  make      both tools stop at nope1.mk — this cell already agrees\n\nNote the three shapes in GNU's `-k` output, which are three separate things to port and probably three separate decisions: the per-file complaint (which is the same `show_goal_error` the parent node moved, fired once per file), the undecorated refusal (`*** ` with no `  Stop.`, because `error` rather than `fatal`), and the `Failed to remake makefile 'X'.` summary that only the keep-going path reaches.\n\nSame family, same probe, not measured: `-k` with a makefile whose rule failed rather than one with no rule — GNU printed `Makefile:1: Failed to remake makefile 'gen.mk'.` after the recipe-failure line there too, so the summary is about the update's verdict rather than about which of the two ways it lost.\n\nWhere. `plan_regeneration` stops collecting at the first unmakeable required makefile — the `break` is what reproduces the positional cells — and returns one refusal. Under `-k` it would have to keep walking and return a list, and Ronin's side would raise them together after the remaking rather than raising one.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:08:23.345789867Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:30:07.083208107Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the probe corrected the filing twice — once about what `-k` decides, and once about where the summary comes from.\n\nThe filing described three separate things to port: the per-file complaint, the undecorated refusal, and the summary. Only two of those are real. Ronin's refusal has carried no `*** ` and no `  Stop.` since Make mode was built — that is `diagnostic_body` in src/make/report.rs taking Make's ceremony off every diagnostic — so the difference the filing saw between `make: *** No rule to make target 'x'.  Stop.` and `make: *** No rule to make target 'x'.` is one Ronin does not have to make. Nothing was needed for it and nothing was done.\n\nWhat `-k` actually decides. `complain()` reads `keep_going_flag` and chooses `error` over `fatal` (reference/gnumake/src/remake.c:422), and that one choice has two consequences rather than one:\n\n  the update walks on, so every required makefile nothing can make is reported\n  rather than the first;\n  and the update RETURNS, which lets `main.c` reach two things it otherwise\n  never gets to — the `us_failed` arm that lists each one again (main.c:2544),\n  and, before that, `if (any_remade) goto re_exec`.\n\nThe second consequence is the one the filing did not have, and it is the larger. Without `-k` a refusal outranks a restart, which is what make-remake-before-refusing-a-makefile established. With `-k` it outranks nothing: a makefile that WAS remade sends the read around again, and the refusals are made a second time on the pass that follows.\n\nThe matrix, GNU on the left and Ronin as it was on the right. Nine cells, of which six moved.\n\n  include nope1.mk / include nope2.mk / all\n    make      the first complaint, the first refusal, and stop   / already agreed\n    make -k   complaint 1, refusal 1, complaint 2, refusal 2, then BOTH summaries,\n              exit 2, and `all` never runs\n              / was: the first complaint and the first refusal, exit 2\n  -include nope1.mk / include nope2.mk, -k\n    GNU  nope2 alone, complaint, refusal, summary — the optional one is not\n         mentioned at all                                  / was: no summary\n  -include nope1.mk / -include nope2.mk, -k     both build `all`, exit 0 — agreed\n  three includes, the first optional, -k\n    GNU  the two required ones, in read order, then both summaries  / was: the first only\n  a nested include: Makefile includes sub.mk which includes nope2.mk, -k\n    GNU  `Makefile:1:` and `sub.mk:1:` on their own complaints and their own\n         summaries — the location is the line that asked, at whatever depth\n                                                            / was: the first only\n  -f nope.mk, -k\n    GNU  `make: nope.mk: ...` from the read, `*** No rule...`, then\n         `make: Failed to remake makefile 'nope.mk'.` — unlocated, under Make's\n         own name, because no `include` line asked for it   / was: no summary\n  two missing includes AND a remakable gen.mk, -k\n    GNU  the two refusals, GEN, both summaries, and then THE WHOLE THING AGAIN\n         — refusals and summaries a second time — exit 2\n         / was: the first complaint and refusal, and gen.mk never made at all\n  the same without -k        the first refusal, and gen.mk never made — agreed,\n                             and it must keep agreeing\n  make -k with everything present    nothing to report — agreed\n\nShape delivered. kati's `plan_regeneration` collects into a `Vec<Refusal>` instead of returning at the first, and breaks after one unless `Flags::keep_going`. Each `Refusal` renders its own `summary` beside its `complaint` and `error`, at the `include` line that asked for the file and under the program's own name when nothing did — `color_warn_text` already had both shapes. `Plan` and `Evaluated` carry the list.\n\n`-k` reaches the evaluator the way `--shuffle` does and for the same reason: the makefile update is where GNU Make reads it first, and that update is kati's. It joins the three switches already in the flags literal whose whole effect is on evaluation.\n\nRonin's side. `refusal_report` splits a run's refusals into the two passes GNU Make writes them in — every complaint-and-refusal first, then every summary — because `main.c` makes the second pass after the update has finished and the two lists interleave nowhere. `report::refused_makefile` takes both. Under `-k` a restart no longer loses to a refusal: `settled_makefiles` returns `Again`, and the refusals of the read that is ending are written straight to the diagnostic stream by `report_refusals_now`, because a result is what ENDS a run and that one is not ending.\n\nRefactoring done to keep the gates green rather than to bypass them: `build_compiler_inputs` crossed the hundred-line ceiling twice while this grew, and came back under it by taking out three things that wanted names anyway — `Read::taken_from` for what one read hands the update, `settled_makefiles` for reconciling the update's answer with what the read already knew it could not make, and `refusal_result`. The `Settled` enum keeps `Over` and `Refused` apart for one reason: a build that failed has already said why and a failing persistence flush must not speak over it, where for a refusal the flush is the last thing that can go wrong — which is exactly what the two arms did before they were extracted.\n\nRows: none moved. 1283 at 159/0/37/1087, compiler 0, interface 37.\n\nOne fixture, recorded from the oracle, and it gates a file effect rather than the diagnostics: keep-going-remakes-past-a-makefile-it-cannot-make — two unmakeable includes with a remakable gen.mk behind them, where the recorded effect is that gen.mk is written at all. Without `-k` the run ends before it is reached; the old code ended there under `-k` too. Two tests in tests/make_regressions.rs for the order, which the build-intent gate cannot see: keep-going-refuses-every-makefile (six lines in GNU Make's order, and the goal never running) and one-refusal-without-keep-going (the negative — the second makefile is never mentioned and no summary is reached).\n\nNot delivered, filed with its own matrix: make-a-makefile-whose-recipe-lost-is-listed-under-keep-going. The same summary line for a makefile whose recipe RAN AND LOST rather than one nothing knows how to make. It is a different route to the same `us_failed` arm and lands in a different module — the build's failure rather than the read's refusal — and its third cell shows the filing's `-k`-only reading was wrong in the other direction too: `-include inc_a.mk` / `include inc_b.mk` sharing a losing pattern rule prints `Failed to remake makefile 'inc_b.mk'.` with NO `-k`, and GNU Make's own suite asserts that `-k` changes nothing about it.\n\nGates: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 849 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored (847 + the two new); clippy all three passes silent; make_port green including the new recording; conformance 387 runs, 318 identical / 69 differing normalised, unmoved; graph equivalence 297 / 278 / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1283 at 159/0/37/1087, unmoved; real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git in 21s at -j8.\n\nkati commit f43d7fd on nplan/make-keep-going-refuses-every-makefile-it-cannot-make."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:44:39.993442486Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-question-mode-refuses-a-makefile-it-cannot-make
                title "Refuse under -q over a makefile goal nothing can make"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:21:19.972793858Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-a-forgiven-makefile-is-not-remade-for-the-goals, and pre-existing rather than caused by it: under `-q` neither the old nor the new grouping puts the file in a subset at all, so nothing about that node's change reaches this cell.\n\n    -include one.mk\n    all: ; @echo all ran\n    one.mk: ; @echo GEN-one; echo X=1 > one.mk\n\n    make -q one.mk\n      GNU 4.4.1  `make: *** No rule to make target 'one.mk'.  Stop.`, rc 2, one.mk not created\n      Ronin      no output, rc 1, one.mk not created\n\nBoth decline to build and both leave the tree alone, so nothing gates on it; the exit status is what differs, and `-q` is a status. GNU answers 2 — \"the question could not be answered at all\" — where Ronin answers 1, \"something would have to run\".\n\nWhy GNU refuses. `update_goal_chain` restores `question_flag` for a file the command line named while the makefiles are being rebuilt (`file->cmd_target`, remake.c:169), so `one.mk` is asked about rather than made during the makefile update. The question comes back \"not up to date\", which for a makefile is a failed update; `dontcare` forgives it; and the goal chain then reaches the same file, finds it `updated` with a failing status, and complains — which is a fatal, so 2 wins over the question's 1.\n\nRonin's `Makefiles::subsets` returns an empty target list for both command-line groups under `-q`, on the reasoning that GNU records the question and carries on with the read. That is right about the makefile update doing no work, and wrong about it leaving no verdict behind: the question's answer is what makes the file one the goals must refuse over. The fix is for the `-q` path to record the same third state make-a-forgiven-makefile-is-not-remade-for-the-goals introduced — `mark_makefiles_unmade` — for a makefile the question found wanting, rather than skipping the file entirely.\n\nNot probed and worth settling first: `-q` on a REQUIRED include with the same shape, and `-q` where the makefile is a goal but not an include at all.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:31:13.067739585Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:41:12.041631822Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the two cells the filing asked to have settled first are settled: the required-include face and the not-an-include-at-all face both already agreed, and both had to keep agreeing.\n\nRead rather than inferred, and it turns out to be four lines in three places. `update_goal_chain` restores the invocation's switches for a Makefile the command line named while the makefiles are being rebuilt (`file->cmd_target`, remake.c:169), so that one is asked about rather than made — and it does NOT stop for the answer, because `stop` is withheld while `rebuilding_makefiles` (remake.c:206). `update_file_1` copies `dontcare` into `no_diag` on the way in (remake.c:496). `update_goal_chain` clears `dontcare` when it lets go of the file (remake.c:236). And then the goals' own pass reads the pair back:\n\n    if (file->updated)\n      { if (file->update_status > us_none)\n          { if (file->no_diag && !file->dontcare) complain (file);\n\nSo the difference between 1 and 2 is entirely `no_diag`, and `no_diag` is entirely whether the `include` was forgiving. A required one leaves `us_question` and no flag, `main.c` reads that as \"did nothing\" (main.c:2473), the goals ask again and answer 1. A forgiven one leaves the same status AND the flag, and the goals complain — which is fatal, so 2 outranks the question's 1.\n\nThe matrix, GNU on the left and Ronin as it was on the right. Ten cells, of which one moved and one moved the wrong way.\n\n  -include one.mk, one.mk has a rule, `make -q one.mk`\n    GNU    `*** No rule to make target 'one.mk'.  Stop.`, rc 2, one.mk not created\n    was    silence, rc 1                                        — the filed cell\n  include one.mk (REQUIRED), the same, `make -q one.mk`\n    GNU    silence, rc 1, one.mk not created                    / already agreed\n  one.mk is not included at all, `make -q one.mk`\n    GNU    silence, rc 1                                        / already agreed\n  -include one.mk that EXISTS and is up to date, `make -q one.mk`\n    GNU    rc 0                                                 / already agreed\n  `make -q` and `make -q all` — one.mk not a command-line goal\n    GNU    GEN-one really runs, rc 1 — `-q` does not stop a makefile being made\n                                                                / already agreed\n  `make one.mk` without -q          GEN-one, `'one.mk' is up to date.`, rc 0\n                                                                / already agreed\n  `make -q one.mk all`              rc 2, same as the single goal / was rc 1\n  an ordinary goal with no rule, under every combination of -q and -k\n    GNU    rc 2 in all four                                     / already agreed,\n           and this is the negative that keeps the fix off the ordinary path\n  `make -q -k one.mk`\n    GNU    the refusal, rc 1                                    / was rc 1, now rc 2\n  -include one.mk whose PREREQUISITE would have to be made, `make -q one.mk`\n    GNU    `*** No rule to make target 'src', needed by 'one.mk'.  Stop.`, rc 2\n    was    rc 1; now rc 2 with `one.mk` named instead of `src`\n\nShape delivered, all of it in Ronin. `Makefiles::subsets` stops emptying the forgiven command-line group under `-q` and marks it `question` instead; `remake_makefiles` asks that subset rather than running it, and an answer of \"not up to date\" puts its targets into `settled.unmade` — the third state make-a-forgiven-makefile-is-not-remade-for-the-goals introduced. Everything after that is machinery that already existed: `mark_makefile_unmade`, `Plan::add_node` refusing whether or not the file is there, `MissingInput` naming the dependent when there is one.\n\nThe required command-line group is still left out of the update entirely, which is what `main.c` reading `us_question` as \"did nothing\" amounts to, and the unit test that gates that now asserts the absence of the question as well as the absence of the targets.\n\nTwo cells to be honest about, both filed as make-a-questioned-makefile-keeps-the-questions-status-under-keep-going with their reproducers.\n\n  `-q -k` moved from accidentally right to wrong. Ronin used to answer 1 to every\n  shape of `make -q <a makefile>`, so this cell agreed by having no opinion; it\n  now answers 2 where GNU answers 1. The mechanism is that GNU has TWO failing\n  statuses for a makefile — `us_question` from a question and `us_failed` from a\n  recipe that lost — and `-k`, by making `complain()` report rather than die, is\n  the only thing that lets them be told apart. Ronin has one third state where\n  GNU has two. Nine of the ten cells are right and this one is wrong, where\n  before one was right by accident and nine were wrong.\n\n  The prerequisite cell now answers 2 with GNU rather than 1 against it, and\n  names a different file: GNU's `complain()` recurses into `file->deps`\n  (remake.c:403) and names `src`, which HAS a rule and is named as though it did\n  not because `-q` left it `updated` with `us_question` too. Whether that is\n  worth porting is a decision rather than an omission — it reads as a defect in\n  4.4.1 — and it is recorded as one.\n\nNothing in kati moved, so there is no kati commit for this node.\n\nTwo tests, and neither could be a corpus fixture: the build-intent gate records success or failure and this whole node is the difference between 1 and 2, which it deliberately does not distinguish. question-refuses-a-forgiven-makefile-goal in tests/make_regressions.rs asserts the exact status for both faces of the same Makefile — 2 with `-include` and 1 with `include` — and that neither creates the file. question-asks-about-a-forgiven-named-makefile in src/make/cli/remake.rs gates the grouping that decides it, including that nothing is asked when nothing asked a question.\n\nRows: none moved. 1283 at 159/0/37/1087, compiler 0, interface 37.\n\nGates: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; workspace tests 851 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored (849 + the two new); clippy all three passes silent; make_port green; conformance 387 runs, 318 identical / 69 differing normalised, unmoved; graph equivalence 297 / 278 / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1283 at 159/0/37/1087, unmoved; real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git in 21s at -j8."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:44:45.270928746Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-progress-total-shrinks-to-the-work
                title "Shrink the progress total to the work that will actually run"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:54:02.406423645Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Filed on an explicit operator decision, 2026-08-16: \"the total should be shrunk to what is actually GOING TO RUN\".\n\nThe complaint, from a sed aarch64 install log: `[1/79]` … `[4/79]` and then the build stops. Seventy-nine edges were planned, four ran, and the total never moved.\n\nNATIVE NINJA IS THE AUTHORITY here, under make.narration (\"a Ninja graph is narrated Ninja's way\"), so the convention was probed against reference/ninja-build/ninja 1.14.0.git rather than reasoned about. Ninja's total shrinks the moment an edge leaves the plan, and only lines printed after the prune carry the smaller number:\n\n    rule stamp        (restat = 1, output mtime pinned)\n    build base: stamp src\n    build o1..o8: copy base\n    build z: copy o1..o8 zsrc\n\n  touch src zsrc; ninja -j1\n    [1/10] touch -m -d @1000000000 base\n    [2/2]  touch z\n\nThe mechanism is Plan::CleanNode (build.cc:314): when a restat leaves an output unchanged and a consumer's inputs are all clean, the consumer's want becomes kWantNothing, `--command_edges_`, and `status_->EdgeRemovedFromPlan(edge)` — which is the exact mirror of EdgeAddedToPlan, decrementing total_edges_ AND the ETA bookkeeping (status_printer.cc:89-114). N never exceeds the final M by this route: CleanNode skips edges already erased from want_ (finished ones), and an edge it does prune had pending inputs and so had never started.\n\nRonin prints `[2/10]` on the same manifest — a Ninja-conformance bug, not only a Make one — and `[2/10]` in Make mode on the Makefile of the same shape. One mechanism is behind both.\n\nThis is a CONSISTENCY fix, not a rule bump. `[spec:ronin:sem:build.nodedone-fn]` already says the pruning path \"removes its non-phony total from the status count\"; Ronin's Plan::release_dependents settles the pruned consumer as complete without taking it out of the count."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T00:54:09.451345607Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T11:54:43.908508356Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "DELIVERED. One mechanism behind both graph kinds, in Ronin alone — kati is not involved, because the count of work is the runner's.\n\n`Plan::release_dependents` is Ronin's `Plan::CleanNode`: a dependent reached with every input settled and no dirty output is one a `restat` took out of the work. It settled such an edge as complete and left `wanted` standing, so `command_edge_count` — and therefore the progress total — still counted it. It now calls the new `Plan::unwant` (kWantNothing: the edge stays a dependency barrier and stops being work) and returns the pruned command edges, which `edge_finished` hands to the caller. `Builder::settle_edge` passes them to `forget_pruned_work`, and `BuildState::forget_edge` is the exact mirror of the two `expect_` calls, because Ninja's `EdgeRemovedFromPlan` is the exact mirror of `EdgeAddedToPlan` — the ETA weighting loses the edge along with the count, or `%P` would chase a total that no longer exists.\n\nThe two Make-mode paths that already decremented the total by hand — a recipe that expanded to no command, and a deferred edge whose freshness test came out negative — now go through the same `Plan::unwant` via `forget_unrun_edge`, so `wanted` and the count cannot drift apart. That matters beyond tidiness: the deferred-activation path recomputes the total from `command_edge_count`, and an edge pruned without being unwanted would have come back.\n\nWHY THE SED CASE NEVER SHRANK. Its prunes were not the Skip path that already decremented; they were consumers going clean under `release_dependents`, which is the path that did not. The same fixture reproduces in both languages:\n\n  before   ninja [1/10] [2/2]    ronin manifest [1/10] [2/10]    ronin make [1/10] [2/10]\n  after    ninja [1/10] [2/2]    ronin manifest [1/10] [2/2]     ronin make [1/10] [2/2]\n\nand the two-chain shape is byte-identical to `reference/ninja-build/ninja` now: [1/8] [2/5] [3/5] [4/5] [5/5].\n\nFIXTURES. tests/cli.rs `a_pruned_edge_leaves_the_progress_total` (the manifest, whose expected two lines are the reference ninja's own output on that manifest) and `make_mode_drops_a_re_observed_edge_from_the_progress_total` (the same shape as a Makefile — the reported case). src/build/tests.rs `plan_names_the_command_edges_a_restat_pruned` pins the plan-level answer directly.\n\nGATES. fmt, check, clippy three passes, doc, workspace tests 864/0 (861 + the three above). Ninja conformance: 425 upstream tests, 10 differential, 86 outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence and log-content — all pass. Make conformance 318/69 normalised, unmoved. Equivalence 297 makefiles / 278 graphs / 0 disagreements. make_port 4/0. Upstream inventory 1287 rows at 167/0/41/1079, unmoved, compiler zero. Real-project gate built ninja 1.14.0.git from its own Makefile in 23s at -j8.\n\nNOT MOVED, and worth saying because the brief expected it might: no inventory row moved. The narration families read the progress line's payload, not its counter, so shrinking the total changes no classification.\n\nPRE-EXISTING RED, not this node's: `nplan port check --wave 4` fails at 0/170 on every wave. Confirmed identical on a pristine worktree at 0065e35, so scripts/check-release.sh has been stopping there before it reaches the conformance and performance steps; those were run individually here."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T11:54:48.76750394Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-release-gate-stops-at-the-port-ladder
                title "Reach the conformance and performance gates past a port ladder that is 0/170"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T12:43:57.096571215Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while running the release gate for make-progress-total-shrinks-to-the-work, and filed rather than folded in because it is about the gate rather than about Make.\n\n    scripts/sandboxed scripts/check-release.sh\n\ndies at `nplan port check --wave 4` with\n\n    Wave 1 · 170 symbols · markup 0/170 · translate 0/170 · tests 0/170\n    [✗] wave-1-markup: 0/170 symbols marked up\n    [✗] wave-2-translate: 0/170 symbols ported\n    [✗] wave-3-tests: 0/170 symbols verified\n    [✗] wave-4-idiomatize: idiomatization is the last wave; complete waves 1–3 first\n    error: port: port check: one or more gates failed\n\nReproduced on a pristine worktree at 0065e35, so it is not any recent change's doing — the ladder reads 0/170 across every wave, which is what an unpopulated port register looks like rather than what a port that has not started looks like. The tree carries 396 formalization items and a full `docs/spec/port/**`, so the symbols exist; something in the register or its configuration is not seeing them.\n\nWhat it costs, and why it is worth a node rather than a shrug: `check-release.sh` is `set -eu`, and the port check sits AHEAD of\n\n    scripts/check-ninja-conformance.sh\n    scripts/check-performance.sh\n    nplan spec uncovered / nplan spec stale\n    nplan lint / nplan audit\n\nso the one command that is supposed to say whether a release is sound has not reached the Ninja conformance suite or the performance baseline for as long as this has been red. Every dispatch that runs \"the gates\" has been running the tail by hand, which works only for as long as someone remembers the list.\n\nTwo ways out, and the choice is the work: either the port register is repopulated so the ladder measures something (and the gate keeps its position), or the ladder moves behind the suites that produce a verdict about the code, so a bookkeeping gap cannot hide a conformance regression. The second is cheap and the first is the one that makes the number mean anything."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T08:58:33.929597413Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T09:24:10.878555792Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. `scripts/check-release.sh` runs END TO END and exits 0 — the first time it has, on the evidence of this node and its twin, in about forty dispatches.\n\n    exit=0 seconds=163\n\nThat is the acceptance run, timed from outside the wrapper on a quiet host with warm artifacts. The same script from a tree whose release binaries were stale took about seventeen minutes, nearly all of it `cargo test --all-targets` and two fat-LTO release builds; nothing in the gate itself is slow.\n\nWHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG, which is neither of the two ways out the filing named. `nplan port check --wave 4` gates four waves and each wave is per-symbol, so the ladder is cumulative: wave 1 wants, for every symbol, a rule written AND an annotation in the source file the symbol was ported from. Ronin's source was C samurai, and that corpus was retired from this repository, so `build.c` and its neighbours cannot carry an annotation. Wave 1 therefore reads 0/170 whatever the port has done, and waves 2, 3 and 4 read 0/170 because wave 1 does.\n\nThe consequence is the part worth writing down: THAT LINE VERIFIED NOTHING. A gate that is red for a reason nothing can change cannot distinguish a lost claim from a retired source — it says exactly the same thing either way. And `check-release.sh` is `set -eu`, so it was not merely uninformative, it was consuming every gate below it: Ninja conformance, the performance baseline, `nplan spec uncovered`, `spec stale`, `lint` and `audit` had not run from that script since the source was retired.\n\nWHAT THE LADDER WOULD HAVE VERIFIED, AND STILL CAN. `nplan port status` answers four columns per symbol — R rule written, S source claimed, T target ported, V verified. Three of the four have subjects that exist, and all 170 symbols read ✓ in all three today. `scripts/check-port-ledger.sh` asserts exactly that, per symbol, failing closed, and asserts the fourth column is EMPTY rather than ignoring it: a source annotation reappearing means the C corpus is back, and the script says so and tells the reader to put the ladder back and delete it. The manifest's symbol count is pinned at 170 in the script, because a manifest whose source was retired cannot grow by porting and must not shrink by accident.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT A WEAKENING, measured rather than argued. The question is whether anything else in the release script already asserts what the ledger asserts, and the answer is NO for the half that matters:\n\n  - `nplan spec uncovered` tracks 225 rules — the 55 `req` and the 170 `def`. The 151 `sem` rules, which are the ones that say what each ported function actually DOES, are outside it.\n  - Probe: `// [spec:ronin:sem:build.buildreset-fn]` removed from src/build.rs. `nplan spec uncovered` → \"No uncovered rules.\" `nplan port status` → that symbol's row goes `✓ · · ✓`. `scripts/check-port-ledger.sh` → exit 1, `check-port-ledger: build.buildreset-fn: no Rust code claims its rules`.\n\nSo the ledger check catches a class of loss that no other gate in the script sees, and the line it replaced caught nothing at all. The frozen-count branch was exercised the same way (expected 169 against 170 in the manifest → exit 1 with the reason).\n\nWHERE IT WENT. `scripts/check-port-ledger.sh` (new, and it carries the whole of this reasoning in its header, because the next reader will meet the script before the node); `scripts/check-release.sh`, which calls it where the ladder used to be and has lost the comment explaining why `check-make-projects.sh` had to sit AHEAD of a red gate — nothing is red any more, so nothing has to duck; `tests/release_gate.rs`, whose wiring assertion now names the ledger and says in its comment what happened to the ladder.\n\nTHE OTHER TWO OPTIONS, and why not. Retiring `plan/.port-manifest.styx` would take the ledger with it — the 170 rows are what say which Rust symbols descend from which C ones, and that is the only remaining record of the port's shape. Moving the ladder to the end of the script would leave it red at the end instead of red in the middle, which is not \"green end to end\" by any reading. A third, floated in the twin node — teach the adoption config that the source side is gone — is nplan's to offer, not ours: `mode @sidecar` exists for a pristine source tree, not an absent one, and `nplan annotations check` validates that each sidecar entry's path is on disk, so entries for `build.c` would be as unusable as the annotations they stand in for.\n\nTHE FULL BATTERY, from the acceptance run's own log unless stated:\n  fmt clean; `cargo check --all-targets` clean\n  `scripts/check-lints.sh` — both passes clean\n  `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean\n  `cargo test --all-targets --no-fail-fast` green, twice (the gate runs it, and the conformance script runs it again)\n  graph equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements\n  vim 9.2 built from its own Makefile in 16s at -j8; zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s\n  port ledger 170 symbols, every rule written, claimed and tested, no source side\n  Ninja conformance: all 425 pinned upstream tests, 10 differential CLI/tool cases, 86 build-outcome cases, 3 invocation-boundary cases, persistence round trip, log content\n  performance: all eight workloads inside their thresholds — Ronin ahead of pinned Ninja on seven (large-manifest-parse 141.0 ms against 299.9, clean-tree-noop 8.5 against 12.3, manifest-command-evaluation 6.5 against 12.0) and behind on scheduler-barrier at 42.8 against 40.1, which is where it has always been\n  `nplan spec uncovered` none; `nplan spec stale` none; `nplan lint` 0 violations; `nplan audit` no transitions proposed\n\nRun beside it rather than by it, because the release script does not carry them: workspace 978 / 0; `--no-default-features` 759 / 0; make_port 4 of 4 over 968 cases with the eight recorded divergences intact; Make conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised and 286 raw; upstream inventory regenerated at 1285 rows / 132 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1129 narration, byte-identical.\n\nONE THING LEFT UNFIXED AND NAMED: the Windows type check inside `check-ninja-conformance.sh` (`cargo check --target x86_64-pc-windows-gnu --lib`) prints 40 warnings, among them `associated items event_key and from_event_key are never used`. It has no `-D warnings`, so it passes, and it is outside this node. Worth a look by whoever owns that target — it is the only place in the tree where a warning is allowed to stand."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T09:26:05.44423272Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-target-specific-append-expands-its-tail-where-it-is-read
                title "Expand a target-specific append's own text in the scope that reads it"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:42:11.042349194Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while shipping make-target-specific-append-reads-the-readers-scope and deliberately not folded into it: that node is about which BASE the second chain walk finds, and this is about where the appended TEXT is expanded. Both were wrong before it and only this half is still wrong.\n\nReproducer, against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n    V = g\n    W = outerW\n    %.o: private V += p\n    a.o: V += <$(W)>\n    a.o: W = aoW\n    dep: W = depW\n    all: a.o\n    a.o: dep ; @echo \"a.o:[$(V)]\" >> log\n    dep: ; @echo \"dep:[$(V)]\" >> log\n\nGNU: a.o:[g p <aoW>], dep:[g <depW>]. Ronin: a.o:[g p <aoW>], dep:[g <aoW>]. The base is now right in both; the tail is not — `<aoW>` where GNU reads `<depW>`.\n\nWhy. `variable_append` (src/expand.c:534) ends each level with `variable_expand_string (buf, v->value, ...)` when the level is recursive, and that expansion runs in whatever `current_variable_set_list` the reader set — `recursively_expand_for_file` is reached per read. So every level of the chain, tail included, is expanded in the reader's scope and not in the writer's. A simple level is the exception and is copied verbatim (`if (! v->recursive) return variable_buffer_output (buf, v->value, ...)`), which is what makes the distinction observable rather than academic.\n\nRonin expands the tail once, in `DepBuilder::apply_rule_vars` (kati/src-rs/dep.rs), while the rule scope is being built — deliberately, with a comment saying why: `all: A += $(Z)` beside `all: Z = changed` must append `changed` rather than whatever Z was outside the rule, and expanding while the scope is half built reads the outer one. That reasoning is right about the target's own read and says nothing about a prerequisite's.\n\nA fix has to keep the binding lazy: the value a reader gets would be a recursive expression whose text is the base's text joined to the tail's, with any simple level spliced in as a literal so it is not expanded a second time. kati already has the machinery — `appended_values`/`appended_text` in var.rs build exactly that shape for an ordinary `+=`. What it changes is the flavour of the result: the settled simple string becomes recursive, so `$(flavor)`, `$(value)` and any `$(shell)` in the tail all move with it, and the recursion guard has to answer for `a.o: V += $(V)`. That is why it is worth its own node rather than a follow-on hunk.\n\nNot urgent: it needs a `+=` whose text names a variable that two readers in the same chain bind differently."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T00:47:11.335822857Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. Filing's claim re-verified against the 4.4.1 oracle before touching anything: `%.o: private V += p` / `a.o: V += <$(W)>` with `a.o: W = aoW` and `dep: W = depW` gave Ronin `dep:[g <aoW>]` where GNU gives `dep:[g <depW>]` — the tail baked in the writer's scope.\n\nPROBED FIRST, and it widened the fix from the filing's own framing. Against the oracle:\n* A target-specific `+=` is RECURSIVE whatever the base's flavour — `$(flavor V)` is `recursive` for both `V = g` and `V := g` bases, and the tail is read per reader in both.\n* The WHOLE chain is re-expanded per reader, not just the tail: `V = base<$(X)>` / `a.o: V += tail<$(W)>` gives `a.o[base<aoX> tail<aoW>]` and `dep[base<depX> tail<depW>]`. The base moves with the reader too. Ronin baked both.\n* A `$(shell)` in the tail runs once PER READER (measured: two readers, two runs) — the sibling deliberately ran it once, which was the wrong count.\n* `V += $(V)` reaches the recursion guard and GNU dies; Ronin read the old value and did not.\n\nSo the fix is not \"expand the tail elsewhere\" but \"hold the whole `+=` lazily\", which is GNU Make's variable_append walking the chain under the reader's `local`.\n\nDELIVERED, kati b0049b3d777da8d12d6dc83f2ad20f377cdd0c94 on branch nplan/make-target-specific-append-expands-its-tail-where-it-is-read, pushed before this pin. `apply_rule_vars` (src-rs/dep.rs) builds the `+=` result as a recursive value rather than an eval'd simple string, for the guard (the target's own read) and the public binding (the reader across the parent link) alike. `Variable::append_source` hands back each level's expression — a recursive level as its own `Value`, a simple level as a `Value::Literal` GNU Make copies verbatim — and `appended_recursive_value` (src-rs/var.rs) joins base to tail the way `append_var` already does for an ordinary `+=`. `DepBuilder::append_expression` wraps `append_source` with the eval fallback for the rare non-recursive/non-simple base. The guard and public bindings share one tail expression and differ only in their base, so which base a reader gets is still the sibling's guard/public decision — now each base re-expands where it is read.\n\nEvery probed cell now matches GNU: the filing's reproducer (`a.o[g p <aoW>] dep[g <depW>]`), simple and recursive bases, the whole-chain case, a three-level chain (`a.o[g<aoW> a<aoW>] dep[g<depW> a<depW> d<depW>]`), flavor recursive, and the `V += $(V)` recursion guard.\n\nFIXTURES, two, oracle-recorded, teeth measured on the pre-fix tree (dep baked to `<aoW>`, flavor simple): a-target-specific-append-reads-its-tail-where-it-is-read (the filing's reproducer, gating dout `g <depW>` and the private base per reader) and a-target-specific-append-reads-its-whole-chain-where-it-is-read (base and tail per reader, gating dout `base<depX> tail<depW>`).\n\nFILED, not folded: make-target-specific-append-value-holds-only-the-appended-text. GNU's `$(value)` of a target-specific `+=` is the appended tail alone (`<$(W)>`), because it stores only that and walks the parent for the base; Ronin stores the joined text, so `$(value)` reads `g <$(W)>`. The expansion is right; only `$(value)`'s raw text differs, and matching it needs GNU's parent-walk storage model — a larger change than the tail. Pre-existing and narrowed by this fix (Ronin used to read the fully expanded `g <aoW>` there); no corpus row turns on it.\n\nGATES (full battery): fmt both trees clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; scripts/check-lints.sh EXIT 0; workspace tests 0 failed / 1 ignored; make_port 4/4 over the corpus, six divergence sidecars, no existing recording rewritten; make_corpus_inventory clean, tsv byte-identical; conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised, 286 raw — unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements, respellings 8 — unmoved; upstream inventory 1285 @ 95 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1166 narration — compiler ZERO, unmoved."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T00:47:17.707684832Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-deferred-freshness-binds-at-before-the-timestamps-are-known
                title "Bind a deferred recipe's $@ to the name that was out of date"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T13:42:34.673071186Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The last cell of make-multi-target-pattern-trigger-identity, reachable only now that make-deferred-freshness-hides-a-multi-target-peer has made the edge run at all. Filed from that node rather than folded into it: making the edge run is a question about which outputs decide its freshness, and this is a question about when `$@` can be chosen.\n\nReproducer, with `a.in` old, `a.c` current and `a.h` absent:\n\n    all: a.c a.h\n    %.c %.h: %.in ; echo \"at=$@ q=$?\" >> log ; cat $< > $*.c ; cat $< > $*.h\n\nGNU: `at=a.h q=a.in`. Ronin now runs the recipe and writes both files, with `q=a.in` right, and `at=a.c`. Take `$?` out of the recipe and Ronin says `at=a.h` too — that case is tests/make/multi-target-pattern-trigger-is-the-name-that-was-out-of-date and it gates.\n\nWhy the two part company. `$@` for a pattern group is the member whose own state sent the recipe to the shell, which no compile-time reading can know: `trigger_output` (src/graph/peer.rs) is a stat-time answer, and `bind_late_command` asks for it as the edge launches. A recipe naming `$?` cannot be held for launch — `references_new_inputs` refuses it in `defers_recipe` — so its text is expanded while the graph is compiled and `AutoCommand::At` writes `node.recipe_output`, the first name, before any timestamp exists.\n\nThe route is the one `$?` itself already takes. `DEFERRED_NEW_INPUTS_REFERENCE` puts `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}` in the command text and `resolve_deferred_new_inputs` (src/build/deferred.rs) substitutes the value as the edge launches, in the inline and response-file forms alike. `$@` would need the same: a second reference kati emits when it is expanding for a destination that cannot hold the recipe AND the node is a `pattern_group`, and a second substitution the executor makes from `trigger_output`. Both halves are small; what makes it a node is what moves with them — the command text carries a reference rather than a name, so the edge hash, `-n` output, the narration and the equivalence check all read the new text, and `escape_double_quoted_shell` has to be right for a path.\n\nScope note: only a pattern group is affected. An explicit `&:` group binds `$@` to the member that was reached, which is one decision over all its targets and is settled at plan time — make-grouped-pattern-targets recorded that and it is unaffected here."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T00:19:05.876342755Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T00:21:29.736125263Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Re-verified and delivered, with the filing's central design OVERTURNED — the cleaner fix was already in reach. Filing's claim reproduced first at ffcbfe6's parent: `%.c %.h: %.in ; @echo \"at=$@ q=$?\"` with a.c current and a.h absent gave Ronin `at=a.c` where GNU gives `at=a.h`, and taking `$?` out made it agree — so the `$?` reference was the whole reason `$@` was bound early.\n\nThe filing proposed emitting a `$@` placeholder kati fills at launch from trigger_output, the way `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}` carries `$?`. That is unnecessary. make-a-scheduler-bound-recipe-is-expanded-though-its-target-is-current taught defers_recipe to hold a `$?` recipe unexpanded, and a deferred pattern group's `$@` was ALREADY a launch-time value: DeferredRecipes::expand binds recipe_output to the trigger when node.pattern_group && implicit_outputs.contains(trigger) (kati src-rs/ninja.rs). That code existed for the non-`$?` case (make-multi-target-pattern-trigger-identity) and simply could not be reached by a `$?` recipe until the recipe deferred. So no new code: this node is a consequence of node 3's deferral, gated by its own fixture.\n\nProbed against the 4.4.1 oracle, GNU on the left, all matching after the fix:\n  a.c current, a.h absent   at=a.h   (the out-of-date member)\n  both absent               at=a.c   (the first name reached, both out of date)\n  a.h current, a.c absent   at=a.c   (the out-of-date member)\n`$?` in the same recipe is `a.in` throughout and correct. Teeth measured on a reverted binary: `at=a.c` where GNU says `at=a.h`.\n\nFIXTURE: tests/make/a-scheduler-bound-pattern-group-binds-at-to-the-name-that-was-out-of-date, oracle-recorded, gating `at=a.h q=a.in` — the `$?` sibling of multi-target-pattern-trigger-is-the-name-that-was-out-of-date (which is the non-`$?` version and was already green). Scope note in the filing holds: only a pattern group is affected; an explicit `&:`/`&::` group settles `$@` at plan time.\n\nGATES (shared with node 3's landing at ffcbfe6, re-run for this fixture): make_port green over the corpus with the new fixture; workspace tests 0 failed; conformance 336/51 normalised, 286 raw; equivalence 297/280/75/0 respellings 8; upstream inventory 1285 @ 95/0/24/1166 compiler ZERO — all unmoved."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T00:21:45.341007653Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
