            {
                id make-archive-member-globbing
                title "Expand a member pattern inside an archive name"
                kind @feat
                deps (make-archive-member-targets)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:09:03.302420553Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Split out of make-archive-member-targets, which shipped the archive name shape, the built-in `(%): %` rule, `$%`, and member freshness read off the archive index — but not this.\n\n`lib.a(*.o)` written as a target or prerequisite is expanded by GNU Make's `ar_glob` (reference/gnumake/src/ar.c) against the archive's own member list rather than against the filesystem: the pattern matches names the index holds, and the result is sorted and re-emitted as one `lib.a(...)` name per member. `ar_glob` is reached from `parse_file_seq` only when the member part holds a glob metacharacter, so an archive name without one costs nothing.\n\nRonin reads the name shape now, so `lib.a(*.o)` parses as archive `lib.a` and member `*.o` and then looks for a member literally called `*.o`. The reading is right and the expansion is absent.\n\nNeeds the index scan that make-archive-member-targets already built (`src/os/archive.rs` walks the members to date one), turned around to enumerate rather than to look up, and called from wherever a target or prerequisite name is expanded — which is before the graph exists, so it is front-end work rather than scheduler work."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:25:55.231294498Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. `lib.a(*.o)` is expanded against the archive's index wherever a target or prerequisite is written.\n\nThe characterisation, measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make rather than read off `ar.c` and assumed:\n\n* The index, not the filesystem. With `q.o` sitting on disk beside an archive that does not hold it, GNU matches `a.o b.o c.o` and never `q.o`. The converse holds too — a member whose object has been deleted still matches.\n* Sorted, and the index's own order is not it. An archive written `m3.o m1.o m2.o` answers `m1.o m2.o m3.o`; `ar_glob` sorts the finished `lib.a(...)` names with `alpha_compare`, which is `strcmp` once the first byte ties, so sorting the members is the same order.\n* `ar_glob_pattern_p` is deliberately not the filename test. `[` counts only once a `]` follows it, so `lib.a(m[13.o)` names a member spelt exactly that way and refuses; `m[13].o` globs. A backslash quotes the next character.\n* `fnmatch` runs with `FNM_PATHNAME|FNM_PERIOD`, so `*.o` does not match `.hidden.o` and `.*.o` does. Verified both directions.\n* No match is not an empty list: the member name stands as written and the build refuses over `lib.a(*.zzz)` with the wildcard still in the diagnostic. An archive that is not there behaves the same way.\n* Both halves glob, in their own places. `parse_file_seq` globs the archive name on the filesystem and only then asks that archive's index about the member, so `lib*.a(m.o)` works and costs no index scan.\n\nWhere it went. kati `src-rs/rule.rs` gains `file_sequence`, which is GNU's `parse_file_seq` in the order it does its three jobs — split into words, strip a leading `./`, reopen any archive group — and `glob_word` grew the archive branch that globs the two halves separately. The three sites that read a rule's names now share it: `eval.rs`'s `parse_rule_targets`, `rule.rs`'s `parse_inputs`, and `dep.rs`'s `.EXTRA_PREREQS` reader. The hot path is untouched: a word with none of `?*[` still interns without a syscall, and the group machinery is only entered for text holding a `(`.\n\nkati `src-rs/archive.rs` gains `member_is_a_pattern`, an `ar_scan` port that enumerates member names, and `glob_members`. The format read is the SysV/GNU one `ar` writes here, including the `//` long-name table and 4.4BSD `#1/LEN` extended names, and anything that does not parse is an archive with no members — which is what `ar_scan` reports to a caller that only wanted to match names.\n\nRecorded, not swept up: that scan is the second reader of the `ar` header format in the tree. `src/os/archive.rs` has the other, and it answers a different question — one member's date, at build time, on Ronin's side of a one-way crate dependency that kati cannot cross. Filed as make-archive-index-reader-is-written-twice.\n\nFixtures, oracle-recorded: archive-member-pattern-expands-against-the-archive-index (which carries the `q.o` decoy and the deliberately unsorted index), archive-member-pattern-that-matches-nothing-stands-as-written, and archive-member-pattern-and-name-expand-where-they-were-written. Five kati unit tests cover the pattern test, the index scan, sorting, long names and `FNM_PERIOD`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:27:26.019376578Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:29:38.872498647Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-archive-suffix-rule-conversion
                title "Convert a .X.a suffix rule into its archive-member pattern too"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-archive-member-targets)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:09:17.541899528Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Split out of make-archive-member-targets, where it was noted at filing and left there deliberately: until the archive name shape existed, the pattern this makes could not have fired.\n\n`convert_to_pattern` (reference/gnumake/src/rule.c:363) turns a suffix rule whose target suffix is `.a` into **two** pattern rules, not one: `%.a: %.X` and `(%.o): %.X`. Ronin's suffix conversion makes only the first. The second is what lets a makefile that writes its own `.c.a:` rule file an object straight into an archive.\n\nThere are no built-in `.X.a` entries, so this is reachable only from a makefile that writes one — which is why it is separate from the built-in catalogue and why it was not urgent. Now that `(%): %` fires and `$%` is bound, the conversion has somewhere to land.\n\nMeasured by GNU Make's own `features/archives` script, which the parent node leaves at eight compiler-class differences; this is one of the four pieces those eight are made of."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:26:30.136684195Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. A `.X.a:` suffix rule now makes both of the pattern rules GNU Make makes from it.\n\nProbed first, because the filing note asserted the conversion without saying which rules GNU actually installs. `make -p` over a makefile with `.SUFFIXES: .a .o .c` and a `.c.a:` recipe lists, in this order:\n\n    (%.o): %.c\n    %.a: %.c\n\nboth carrying the suffix rule's own recipe. So the conversion is two rules, the archive one first, and there are no built-in `.X.a` entries at all — `default.c`'s only archive rule is the pattern `(%): %` with `$(AR) $(ARFLAGS) $@ $<`, which the catalogue already had. The `$(AR) r $@ $*.o` + `$(RM) $*.o` shape the dispatch asked about is the GNU manual's example of a makefile-written `.c.a:` body, not a builtin; it is in the fixture's doc comment as the thing the conversion exists to serve.\n\nThe target pattern is literally `(%.o)` whatever the source suffix is — `convert_suffix_rule`'s null-target case writes it as a constant. That is what makes the two archive rules differ where it matters: matching `(foo.o)`, the converted rule binds `$*` to `foo` and `$<` to `foo.c`, where the built-in `(%): %` would bind them to `foo.o` and `foo.o`. Confirmed against the oracle, both halves: `at=libx.a pct=foo.o star=foo lt=foo.c` for the member, and `at=libz.a pct= star=libz lt=libz.c` for the `%.a` half.\n\nGating is `.SUFFIXES`, both ends. `convert_to_pattern` walks the declared list twice, so `.c.a:` converts only while both `.c` and `.a` are on it; with either withdrawn the rule makes nothing and GNU falls through to compiling the object and letting `(%): %` file it. Both directions verified, including a list cleared AFTER the rule was written — the list that counts is the one the last makefile left.\n\nThe corner that changed the implementation. The two conversions are installed independently, each with `new_pattern_rule`'s override off, so a makefile that also writes `%.a: %.c` by hand takes the name of the second and leaves the first standing — GNU still runs the `.c.a:` recipe for the member. The obvious implementation loses exactly that: deriving `(%.o): %.c` from whichever suffix rules survived kati's written-rule comparison finds nothing, the written rule having already emptied the entry. Caught by probing rather than by reasoning, and it is now a fixture of its own. So the `.X.a:` rules are kept aside in `DepBuilder::archive_suffix_rules` when they are read, and converted in `install_archive_suffix_rules` — after `.SUFFIXES` has settled, before the catalogue's `(%): %`, which is the order GNU installs them in and the reason the more specific pattern is reached first.\n\nWhere it went: kati `src-rs/dep.rs` — the new field, the capture in `populate_suffix_rule`, and `install_archive_suffix_rules` called from `install_builtin_rules`.\n\nFixtures, oracle-recorded: archive-suffix-rule-files-an-object-into-the-archive, archive-suffix-rule-also-makes-the-plain-archive-rule, archive-suffix-rule-survives-a-written-rule-taking-the-other-half."
                    }

