            {
                id make-archive-member-list-in-one-name
                title "Read several members named inside one pair of parentheses"
                kind @feat
                deps (make-archive-member-targets)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:09:32.012119616Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Split out of make-archive-member-targets, which reads exactly one member per archive name.\n\n`lib.a(a.o b.o)` is one word to the tokenizer and several targets to GNU Make: `parse_file_seq` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c) reopens the parenthesis, splits the members on whitespace, and emits `lib.a(a.o)` and `lib.a(b.o)` as separate names. The same reopening handles `lib.a(a.o) lib.a(b.o)` written out longhand, which is why the two spellings behave identically in GNU Make and why a makefile is entitled to use either.\n\nRonin's splitter (kati `src-rs/archive.rs`) takes the whole of the parenthesised text as the member name, so `lib.a(a.o b.o)` looks for a member with a space in it. Nothing else is wrong: once the name is split into two, both halves already work, because everything downstream of the split — the `(%): %` rule, `$%`, the index lookup for freshness — is per-member and shipped.\n\nFront-end work at the same site as make-archive-member-globbing, and the two want doing together: both are `parse_file_seq` reopening a parenthesis, one splitting on whitespace and one expanding a pattern."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:26:10.99254707Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. `lib.a(a.o b.o)` names two members, and does so at the same site as the member globbing — the two were done together, as the filing note said they wanted to be.\n\nThe tokenizer is a small state machine and every part of it is load-bearing. Measured against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle:\n\n* `lib.a(a.o b.o)` and `lib.a(a.o) lib.a(b.o)` produce the same two names. So do uneven runs of spaces and tabs.\n* A group only opens when some LATER word ends in `)`. GNU looks ahead over the remaining words before it reopens the parenthesis, and with none there the text is not a group at all — `lib.a(a.o b.o` names a file called `lib.a(a.o` and another called `b.o`, and the refusal names the first of those. That lookahead is the part an implementation is most likely to skip, and skipping it silently changes what a malformed line means.\n* A word that is only `lib.a(`, or only `)`, separates rather than names. That is what makes `lib.a(  a.o    b.o   )` mean what `lib.a(a.o b.o)` means, and it is why the suite's whitespace case passes.\n* A word beginning with `(` never opens a group, because the member name would be empty.\n* A closed group lets the words after it alone: `lib.a(a.o b.o) plain.txt` is three names, and `lib.a(a.o b.o) other.a(c.o)` is three too.\n* Globbing and listing compose in written order. `lib.a(c.o *.o)` gives `c.o a.o b.o` — each word inside the parentheses expanded on its own, where it stands, so a name keeps its place and a pattern contributes its sorted matches after it.\n* The split is textual and does not consult the index, so a list against an archive that does not exist yet still names every member.\n\nImplemented as `reopen_groups` in kati `src-rs/archive.rs`, reached from `file_sequence` in `src-rs/rule.rs` — see make-archive-member-globbing's note for the shared plumbing. Everything downstream of the split was already per-member and needed nothing: `$%`, the `(%): %` rule, the index lookup for freshness.\n\nFixtures, oracle-recorded: archive-member-list-in-one-name-names-every-member, archive-member-list-tolerates-spaces-inside-the-parentheses, archive-member-group-that-never-closes-is-not-a-group. Five kati unit tests cover the group shapes directly, including the two spellings asserted equal to each other."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T00:27:31.019997649Z
