            {
                id make-archive-member-touch
                title "Touch an archive member's index date under -t"
                kind @feat
                deps (make-archive-member-targets)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T19:09:46.040308826Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Split out of make-archive-member-targets, which made the member's date readable but not writable.\n\n`-t` on an ordinary target is a `utime`. On `lib.a(member.o)` it cannot be: the date lives in the archive's own index, not in the filesystem, so GNU Make calls `ar_touch` (reference/gnumake/src/ar.c), which opens the archive, finds the member's header, and writes a fresh date into it in place — and warns and does nothing when the archive is one whose format has no date to write.\n\nRonin under `-t` treats the member name as a filename and touches a file called `lib.a(member.o)`, which is both wrong and litter.\n\nThe read half is already here — `src/os/archive.rs` locates a member's header to date it — so this is the same walk with a write at the end, plus the format check that decides whether writing is possible at all. Worth noting that on a modern Linux host `ar` defaults to deterministic mode and records every date as zero, so the touched date is the only real date such an archive will ever have."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T23:55:37.524545627Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @blocker
                        text "Blocked, and on a premise rather than on difficulty: this node was filed saying \"Ronin under `-t` treats the member name as a filename and touches a file called `lib.a(member.o)`, which is both wrong and litter.\" That is not what happens. `-t` does nothing at all — it is accepted and ignored, for every target, archive member or not.\n\nMeasured, ronin at e238d39 built release, invoked as `make` (argv[0]), against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle:\n\n    all: out.txt\n    out.txt: in.txt ; @echo \"REAL RECIPE RAN\" > out.txt\n\n  GNU `make -t`  → prints `touch out.txt`, out.txt is empty.\n  Ronin `make -t` → prints `[1/1] echo \"REAL RECIPE RAN\" > out.txt`, out.txt holds `REAL RECIPE RAN`.\n\nSo `-t` runs the recipe. `Switch::Touch` is read in exactly one place in the whole tree — src/make/cli.rs:498, the `propagated()` table that puts `-t` back into `MAKEFLAGS` for a sub-make. It is declared `OptionClass::NoOp` at src/make/cli.rs:250. There is no `touch` field on `BuildConfig` and no touch primitive on the disk interface.\n\nThere used to be. `Builder::touching`/`Builder::touch_outputs` in src/build/command.rs and `RealDiskInterface::touch` in src/os.rs arrived in aa4f3c3 and were removed in 95452af, \"refactor: Remove Make provenance from graph execution\".\n\nThat removal is authoritative, not incidental. docs/make-compiler-boundary-audit.md:99 lists \"src/build/command.rs: Make touch behavior\" under what the boundary removes, and the WBS disposition at :165-169 is explicit:\n\n  ### Accept without emulation (`N`)\n  - `make-option-output-sync`, `make-option-debug-and-trace`, `make-option-shuffle`,\n    and `make-option-touch-and-what-if` collapse into the complete interface table\n    owned by `make-interface-surface`. Their spellings are accepted; their Make\n    runtime behavior is not implemented.\n\nTouching an archive member's index date is therefore not the missing piece here. The missing piece is `-t` itself, and it is missing on purpose. Delivering this node means first reinstating the whole touch execution path — reversing a recorded architecture decision — and only then teaching it that a member's date lives in the archive index rather than in the filesystem. That is an operator's decision about the compiler boundary, not something to settle inside an archive dispatch.\n\nWhat the archive half would be, recorded now so it is not re-probed later. GNU's `ar_touch` (reference/gnumake/src/ar.c:132) parses the name, calls `f_mtime` on the archive, then `ar_member_touch` (reference/gnumake/src/arscan.c:923), which finds the member's header with `ar_member_pos`, opens the archive O_RDWR, `fstat`s it, and formats that mtime into the 12-byte `ar_date` field in place, space-padded. It writes the ARCHIVE's mtime, not \"now\", and it is read before the write, so the member ends a touch marginally older than the archive it sits in. The four outcomes, all measured against the oracle:\n\n  member present   → `touch lib.a(a.o)`, header rewritten, exit 0\n  member absent    → `make: touch: Member 'zzz.o' does not exist in 'lib.a'`, exit 2\n  archive absent   → `make: touch: Archive 'nope.a' does not exist`, exit 2, no file created\n  not an archive   → `make: touch: 'bad.a' is not a valid archive`, exit 2\n\nAnd the cell worth keeping: on a deterministic archive (`ar rc`, every date zero, so every member reads as absent), `-t` is the only thing that will ever put a real date in it. Measured — before `ar tv` shows `Jan 1 1970`, after `make -t` it shows the archive's own mtime, and the member is up to date from then on, so the second `make -t` says `'all' is up to date`. `-n` and `-q` leave the index alone; `-q` exits 1.\n\nNot a blocker for the other three nodes or for the `.FEATURES` advertisement: GNU's own `features/archives` script, which is the measure for `archives`, never invokes `-t`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T12:01:39.271799271Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Operator decision, Brendan, 2026-08-17, explicit: implement `-t` fully. This reverses the accept-without-emulation disposition recorded for `make-option-touch-and-what-if` in docs/make-compiler-boundary-audit.md:165-169, under which the touch path was removed in 95452af. `docs/make-compiler-boundary-audit.md` is updated to carry the reversal and its reason.\n\nWhy the original disposition did not hold. The `N` bucket is for options whose Make behaviour is reporting or scheduling — things `[spec:ronin:req:make.semantics+1]` explicitly excludes from conformance. `-t` is not one of those. It decides what the run writes to disk, and filesystem effects ARE a conformance criterion under that same rule. Measured before this dispatch, `make -t` on a two-rule Makefile ran the recipes and overwrote the outputs: not an unimplemented no-op but the opposite of what was asked for.\n\nA second operator ruling, same day, scoped the reversal: the BEHAVIOUR lands as specified, the NARRATION does not. The brief's `print `touch <file>`` was withdrawn — that is GNU's runtime voice and goes through the same lens as every other Make-voiced output. So a touched edge is reported by the ordinary `[N/M]` progress line under `[spec:ronin:req:make.narration+1]`, and no fixture records GNU's `touch <file>` stdout. The fixtures gate the filesystem effect, which is what the harness can see and what the semantics rule makes binding.\n\nThe matrix as implemented, each cell measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n  ordinary target, out of date  → output dated, recipe not run, exit 0\n  target with no recipe         → not touched (nothing to stand in for)\n  `.PHONY` target               → not touched, recipe not run, no file of that\n                                  name invented. GNU declines it in\n                                  `update_file_1`; Ronin declines an edge whose\n                                  `always_dirty` is set, which is `.PHONY`.\n  `-n` with `-t`                → `-n` wins: reported, nothing written\n  `-s` with `-t`                → touched, nothing reported\n  `-q` with `-t`                → `-q` wins: question answered, nothing runs\n  MAKEFLAGS                     → carries `t`; a recursive child sees `-t`\n  makefile remake pass          → `-t` is turned OFF for it, as GNU turns it off\n                                  across `update_goal_chain`: a makefile left\n                                  dated and unwritten would make the read guess\n                                  what it said. Measured: GNU really runs the\n                                  remake recipe under `-t`.\n  makefile the command line named → keeps `-t` and IS touched rather than\n                                  remade, GNU's `file->cmd_target` exception.\n                                  Measured: `touch bye.mk`, no REMADE.\n  `::` chain and deferred rules → the file the Makefile named is touched, not\n                                  the `.ronin_grouped_join/N` name the graph\n                                  invented to sequence them. Found by the\n                                  upstream suite's own `options/dash-t` TEST 0,\n                                  which failed with `No such file or directory`\n                                  until the indirection was followed.\n\n  lib.a(member.o), present      → the member's index entry is dated, exit 0\n  lib.a(member.o), absent       → `Member 'zzz.o' does not exist in 'lib.a'`,\n                                  exit 2\n  archive absent                → `Archive 'nope.a' does not exist`, exit 2,\n                                  no file created\n  not an archive                → `'bad.a' is not a valid archive`, exit 2\n  deterministic archive         → the cell worth keeping: `ar` writes every date\n                                  as zero, so every member reads as absent, and\n                                  `-t` is the only thing that will ever put a\n                                  real date in that index. Measured identical to\n                                  GNU: before, `ar tv` says Jan 1 1970; after, it\n                                  says the archive's own mtime; the second run\n                                  finds it up to date.\n\nThe three archive failures are reported in Ronin's own shape — `ronin: build stopped: <reason>.` and exit 2 — rather than GNU's `make: touch: <reason>`. They are failures that must be reported at all, which is exactly the case the narration contract allows, and the information (which of the three ways it could not be filed) is what the diagnostic carries.\n\n`ar_member_touch` is `src/os/archive.rs::touch_member`, the same walk `member_date` makes stopped one step earlier — at the header's address rather than the date in it — with three details of GNU's `arscan.c:923` kept: the date written is the ARCHIVE's mtime and is read before the write, so the member ends fractionally behind the file holding it; the field is space-padded to its full 12 bytes so no tail of a longer previous date survives; and a member whose recorded date is zero still counts as found, because dating it is the point.\n\n`-W` (`--what-if`/`--new-file`/`--assume-new`) is NOT part of this ruling and stays an accepted no-op. Filed as make-what-if-assumed-new."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-17T12:05:21.638451061Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-escaped-percent-prerequisite-pattern-keeps-its-backslash
                title "Leave a prerequisite pattern that holds no wildcard exactly as it was written"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T20:02:45.199214188Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-static-pattern-escaped-percent's adjacent cells against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle; left out of it because the target-pattern side is one refusal and this is a substitution that has to stop rewriting.\n\nGNU Make compresses the percent escaping into a COPY of a prerequisite pattern and throws the copy away when no wildcard survived it. file.c:476 `enter_prereqs`:\n\n    char *nm = alloca (nl);\n    memcpy (nm, dp->name, nl);\n    percent = find_percent (nm);\n    if (percent) { ... o = patsubst_expand_pat (...); dp->name = ...; }\n\nso `dp->name` is untouched when `percent` is null, and a prerequisite pattern that is not a pattern reaches the build spelt exactly as the makefile wrote it — backslash included. kati's `strutil::substitute_stem` returns `find_percent`'s reduced text on that branch instead of the original.\n\nReproducer, with both `\\%.c` and `%.c` on disk:\n\n    a.o b.o: %.o: \\%.c ; @echo \"ran[$@]<$<\" >> log\n\nGNU writes `ran[a.o]<\\%.c` and reads the file called `\\%.c`; Ronin writes `ran[a.o]<%.c` and reads the one called `%.c`. Two different files, so this is build intent and not wording. With neither on disk the refusals name different files too — GNU `No rule to make target '\\%.c'`, Ronin `'%.c'`.\n\nThe one-line half is `substitute_stem` returning `prerequisite.clone()` rather than the reduced text when `find_percent` found nothing. What makes this a node rather than that line is the implicit-rule counterpart, which diverges further and in a different direction:\n\n    %.o: \\%.c ; @echo \"made $@ from $<\" > log\n    all: a.o\n\nGNU refuses outright — `No rule to make target 'a.o', needed by 'all'` — even with `\\%.c` present, so `pattern_search` is doing something to a non-pattern prerequisite that is not merely \"use the name as written\". Ronin matches the rule, builds `a.o` from `%.c`, and carries on into the builtin link rule. Whoever takes this has to establish what implicit.c does with such a prerequisite before changing the shared substitution, because both paths run through `substitute_stem`.\n\nThe neighbouring cells already agree and are fixtured: an ordinary rule's `\\%.o` prerequisite keeps its backslash in both tools, and `.PRECIOUS: \\%.b` names a file in both since make-static-pattern-escaped-percent."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T02:19:21.157973638Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T02:39:04.76813331Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing's warning was right to insist: `implicit.c` does something to a non-pattern prerequisite that is not \"use the name as written\" and not \"compress the escaping\" either. It is a THIRD answer, and finding it turned a one-line change into a split.\n\nWHAT GNU DOES, read out of the source before anything moved and then measured cell by cell.\n\nA static pattern rule's prerequisites go through `enter_prereqs` (file.c:476) with the stem in hand. It compresses the escaping into a COPY, `nm`, and uses the copy only inside `if (percent)` — so a prerequisite pattern that holds no wildcard reaches the build spelt exactly as the makefile wrote it, backslash included.\n\nAn implicit rule's prerequisites go nowhere near it. `record_files` (read.c:2085) enters prerequisites only `if (! pattern && ! implicit_percent)`, with the comment saying why — \"We don't want to enter pattern rules at all so that we don't think that they ought to exist\". So a pattern rule's dependency text is stored RAW, and `pattern_search` (implicit.c:562) then finds its wildcard with\n\n    const char *cp = strchr (nptr, '%');\n\nwhich knows nothing about escaping and never compresses anything. The backslash is just a byte of the name, ahead of the stem. That is the answer the filing could not have guessed from the refusal it saw: `%.o: \\%.c` does not look for `\\%.c` and does not look for `%.c` — it looks for `\\a.c`, and refuses `a.o` when that file is not there.\n\nTHE MATRIX, fifteen cells, each run through both binaries with the files written by exact bytes rather than by a shell that would eat the backslashes. GNU on the left, and every one of them agrees now.\n\n  static  \\%.c   disk \\%.c        reads `\\%.c`              was: read `%.c`\n  static  \\%.c   disk %.c         refuses `\\%.c`            was: built from `%.c`\n  static  \\%.c   disk both        reads `\\%.c`              was: read `%.c`\n  static  \\%.c   disk neither     refuses `\\%.c`            was: refused `%.c`\n  static  \\\\%.c  disk \\a.c        reads `\\a.c`              agreed already\n  static  \\\\%.c  disk a.c         refuses `\\a.c`            agreed already\n  static  \\\\%.c  disk \\\\a.c       refuses `\\a.c`            agreed already\n  implicit \\%.c  disk \\a.c        makes a.o from `\\a.c`     was: refused a.o\n  implicit \\%.c  disk \\%.c        refuses a.o               agreed already\n  implicit \\%.c  disk %.c         refuses a.o               was: built from `%.c`\n  implicit \\%.c  disk a.c         refuses a.o (both)        agreed already\n  implicit \\\\%.c disk \\\\a.c       makes a.o from `\\\\a.c`    was: refused a.o\n  implicit \\\\%.c disk \\a.c        refuses a.o               was: built from `\\a.c`\n  ordinary \\%.c  disk \\%.c        reads `\\%.c`              agreed already\n  ordinary \\%.c  disk %.c         refuses `\\%.c`            agreed already\n\nThe ordinary rule is the third code path and needed nothing: `enter_prereqs (deps, NULL)` skips the whole `if (stem)` block, so the name is never touched.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT, kati 1e68f7f7396c2830138bd9c48183576e845b42af on branch nplan/make-escaped-percent-prerequisite-pattern-keeps-its-backslash. One substitution became two, because GNU asks the question in two places that share no routine.\n\n* `strutil::substitute_stem` is the implicit search's, and it stops calling `find_percent` at all: a raw `memchr(b'%')` into the raw text, which is `strchr (nptr, '%')`. Its only caller is `PatternMatch::prerequisite`, and every caller of THAT is the implicit search.\n* `Pattern::append_subst_prerequisite` is the static pattern rule's, added beside `append_subst`: when no wildcard survives the compression it hands back the ORIGINAL text rather than the compressed copy, which is `enter_prereqs` throwing `nm` away. `apply_output_pattern`'s generic branch is the one call site.\n\n`Pattern::append_subst` is deliberately left as it was, and the reason is a cell that would have broken had the branch gone into the primitive: `func_patsubst` runs `find_percent` over the replacement IN PLACE and uses what it left either way, so `$(patsubst abc,\\%,abc)` is a literal percent and must stay one. The two callers want different answers out of the same compression, which is why the difference is a caller's and not the routine's. Measured in both tools before and after.\n\nADJACENT CELLS PROBED and unmoved, twelve of them: a prerequisite whose second `%` is a literal (`x%y%.c`), an escaped percent AHEAD of the wildcard in both rule forms (`a\\%b%.c` — static reads `a%ba.c`, implicit refuses), the directory hold-aside (`%.x: 6%` on `lib/bye.x`), plain `%.c` in both forms, a prerequisite with no percent in both forms, both forms under `.SECONDEXPANSION:`, `$(patsubst)`'s escaped replacement, and a suffix rule.\n\nFIXTURES, two, oracle-recorded, each gating file CONTENTS rather than existence so that which file was read is the evidence:\n\n* `tests/make/a-static-patterns-escaped-prerequisite-is-not-a-pattern` — `\\%.c` and `%.c` both on disk with different contents, plus the `\\\\%.c` cell as its guard. On the parent, `a.o` and `b.o` hold `bare` where GNU leaves `escaped`.\n* `tests/make/an-implicit-rules-escaped-prerequisite-is-not-escaped-at-all` — `\\a.c`, `\\\\a.c`, `a.c` and `\\%.c` all on disk, and the rule picks two of them. On the parent the rule does not match at all and the build falls through to the builtin `cc` rule and FAILS.\n\nTeeth measured in a worktree at 252ca6c/f9e94d4 rather than assumed. kati unit test `an_escaped_prerequisite_is_one_name_in_each_rule_form` holds both directions and the `$(patsubst)` cell beside them.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 900 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 156/0/37/1106, tsv byte-identical, no row moved and the compiler bucket ZERO; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential.\n\nA NOTE ON RUNNING THE GATES: two of them started concurrently both died with `No such file or directory (os error 2)` on an unrelated corpus case. They share a work directory, so they have to be run one at a time; run serially each is green."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T02:40:49.681188141Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-construction-expanded-recipe-is-one-shell
                title "Launch a recipe read at graph construction line by line too"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T21:34:08.762666165Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The residue make-recipe-line-is-its-own-shell left, recorded rather than hidden: two shapes of recipe still reach one shell as one assembled script.\n\nA recipe expanded when its edge launches is handed over as the sequence of launches GNU Make would perform. A recipe expanded at graph construction is not, because it never goes through that boundary at all — the sink emits its command into the graph as a rule binding, and the launch answers `LateBinding::Settled`. `defers_recipe` (kati/src-rs/ninja.rs) refuses four kinds for reasons that have nothing to do with this: a recursive `$(MAKE)` line becomes a child graph, a declared or detected depfile rewrites the script, a grouped double-colon action and a `$?` are values the scheduler binds against an edge that has to declare them. Of the four, `$?` is the one an ordinary Makefile reaches.\n\n    out: a b\n    \t@printf '%s' 'x$?\n    \ty' > out\n\nis the reproducer shape: an open quote across two lines in a recipe naming `$?`. GNU Make fails; Ronin joins and writes the file, exactly as it did for every recipe before the per-line launch landed.\n\nThe second shape is narrower and is a decision rather than an omission: a recipe holding a command line longer than the 100,000-byte response-file threshold keeps the assembled script, because the response file such a line needs is named per edge (`<output>.rsp`) and several long lines in one recipe would want one name. `CommandLayout::launches_line_by_line` is that test.\n\nWhat it would take. For the first shape, the sink would have to carry the per-line steps it already has — `translate_recipe` produces them for every recipe, deferred or not — across the boundary for an edge whose command it also settled, which is a second kind of entry in `PendingRecipes` rather than a new mechanism. The `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}` substitution would then have to reach the step holding `$?` rather than the whole command, which is one call site in `Builder::prepared_steps`. For the second, a response file named per step rather than per edge.\n\nNeither is urgent. Both are the old behaviour rather than a new defect, and the second needs a hundred-kilobyte recipe line to reach."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T02:41:16.912249235Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T03:23:28.6585698Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered as the filing sketched it, after a smaller-looking alternative was tried first and MEASURED WRONG — which is the part of this worth reading.\n\nTHE ALTERNATIVE THAT FAILED, and why it is recorded rather than forgotten. `defers_recipe` refuses a `$?` recipe, and `NewInputsTiming::Launch` already exists and already answers `$?` from the timestamps on disk at expansion time — which is where GNU Make's `set_file_variables` reads it in `new_job`. So the obvious move was to delete the refusal: a deferred recipe already launches line by line, and the reproducer passed on the first build. Three more cells improved with it, all measured against the oracle: `$(?D)`/`$(?F)` started answering about the names instead of the placeholder, an `$(error)` in the recipe of an up-to-date target stopped firing, and the grouped double-colon cell agreed.\n\nThen the corpus caught it: SEVEN cases fail, because the launch-time stat loop is a strictly weaker model than GNU's `d->changed`.\n\n  an-archive-member-is-current-in-the-second-it-was-filed   `$?` empty, GNU `old.o`\n  an-archive-rule-refiles-the-members-that-are-new         nothing filed\n  an-empty-recipe-leaves-its-target-absent                 `ran []`, GNU `ran [mid bare]`\n  circular-dependency-leaves-the-prerequisite-list         `q=[]`, GNU `q=[b c]`\n  question-variable-drops-a-member-the-archive-already-holds\n  question-variable-names-an-archive-member-by-its-member\n  variable-automatic-absent-prerequisite-is-in-question    `[]`, GNU `[bar]`\n\nThree shapes a stat cannot see: a prerequisite that does not exist counts as changed for GNU and is not newer than anything on disk; one remade without its mtime moving — an empty recipe, a phony, a restat — counts and does not stat as newer; and an archive member's index date is not a file's. The scheduler's list is the accurate one, which is why the edge declares it, and `NewInputsTiming::Launch` is a fallback for a `$?` the compiler could not see rather than an equal. The refusal is load-bearing. Reverted.\n\nWHAT LANDED, kati 21f2b5fa0f7d1d93922e5f3961cfba7751d8340e on branch nplan/make-construction-expanded-recipe-is-one-shell, and it is the filing's own sketch.\n\n`SinkRule::steps` carries `translate_recipe`'s per-line launches for a rule whose command was SETTLED, beside that command. The assembled script stays the rule's command — a progress line, a log entry and a `-n` all want the whole of it — and a destination that can launch several runs the steps instead. `GraphSink` builds them into `LateStep`s with the unit's own layout as it declares the rule, hands them to the edge that names it, and `PendingRecipes` grows a second map keyed by edge with no session and no expansion behind it. `LateBinding::Steps` is the answer that says \"the command stands; these are the processes\", and `Builder::prepared_steps` was already substituting `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}` per step, so the placeholder reaches the step holding `$?` and no other — the one call site the filing named.\n\nONE DEFECT THIS INTRODUCED AND THE INVENTORY CAUGHT, worth recording because the gate is the only reason it was found. Three rows of GNU Make's own suite moved out of `narration` — `targets/WAIT.diff`, `.1` and `.2` — and the reproducer is `all: .WAIT pre1 ... ; @echo '?=$?'`. kati's `direct_argv` had decided that line needed no shell, so the step was an argv to exec and `prepared_steps` substitutes only into shell launches; the recipe printed `?=${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}` where GNU printed `?=pre1 pre2`. `recipe_steps` now refuses a direct launch for any line still holding `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}`: `construct_command_argv` decides from the FINISHED text and a scheduler placeholder is not it. The line goes to a shell, which is where the substitution lands, and where the whole recipe went before it was one line at a time. Inventory back to 156/0/37/1106 with the tsv byte-identical.\n\nFIXTURE. `tests/make/a-recipe-naming-new-inputs-dies-where-its-line-does` is the filing's own reproducer, oracle-recorded and written so the refusal is the evidence: `out` is never written. Teeth measured in a worktree at 252ca6c/f9e94d4, where it writes `xa b ) && (y` — the join's own `) && (` fallen inside the quote and become data.\n\nTHE SECOND SHAPE, decided rather than delivered, as the filing allowed. A recipe holding a line over the 100,000-byte response-file threshold keeps the assembled script and its single launch, because the response file such a line needs is named per edge and several long lines would want one name. `CommandLayout::launches_line_by_line` is that test and the settled path asks it too. Delivering the sketched alternative — a response file named per step — means `PreparedStep` carrying its own, written before that step spawns and removed after it finishes, which is a change to the build engine's response-file lifetime and to `take_step`, and it has to leave every Ninja edge exactly as it is. Measured cost against measured benefit: it needs a hundred-kilobyte recipe LINE whose recipe also breaks its syntax across lines. Not taken here; filed as make-a-composed-recipe-is-still-one-shell with the two composed shapes.\n\nNote that the threshold is tested per STEP and not on the assembled script, so a recipe of 500 short lines totalling 138KB now launches line by line where it used to reach one shell through a response file. Measured against the oracle: 500 lines written, both tools, identical.\n\nWHAT STILL REACHES ONE SHELL, measured after the fix rather than predicted, all three with the same open-quote reproducer: a recipe beside a recursive `$(MAKE)` (the edge runs a segment, not the whole recipe), one with a declared depfile (the extraction rewrites the script, and it is reachable only through kati's `.KATI_DEPFILE` or `--detect_depfiles`), and one holding a line over the threshold. The grouped double-colon kind the filing named is FIXED and agrees. All three are on make-a-composed-recipe-is-still-one-shell with what each would take.\n\nTWO PRE-EXISTING DEFECTS FOUND BY THESE PROBES, both unmoved by this fix and both reproducing identically on the parent, each filed with an oracle-recorded corpus case carrying a `divergence` file naming its owner: make-new-inputs-path-forms-read-the-placeholder (`$(?D)` answers `.` and `$(?F)` the whole placeholder, where GNU answers `d .` and `a b`) and make-a-scheduler-bound-recipe-is-expanded-though-its-target-is-current (an `$(error)` in a `$?` recipe fires for a target that is already up to date). Both were briefly fixed by the alternative above, which is how they were found and what their notes record about the cost of fixing them properly. A third, make-always-make-rebuilds-nothing, came out of the same matrix and is unrelated to recipes: `-B` builds nothing at all.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 900 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 853 cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 0 disagreements — the manifest is byte-identical, which it has to be since a manifest writer ignores the new field by nature; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 156/0/37/1106, tsv byte-identical, no row moved and the compiler bucket ZERO; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 713 / 0 in a cold target directory; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 20s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 36s — no slower than the runs before this change, which is expected: only a recipe the compiler had to read for itself gains a process per line, and neither project has one."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T03:31:17.251403752Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-shuffle-reorders-order-only-prerequisites-apart-from-the-rest
                title "Shuffle a target's order-only prerequisites in the same chain as the rest"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:13:03.159371574Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text probe
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:14:04.854803672Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Ignore the one-word \"probe\" entry above it — that was written to find out which log kinds the image lint accepts, and nothing removes a log entry once written.\n\nFound while probing make-shuffled-drop-unlinks-the-walked-position over the order-only cells, and separate from it: that node is about which entry a circular drop takes out of the list, and this is about the list the shuffle permutes at all.\n\nGNU Make carries a target's normal and order-only prerequisites in ONE `->next` chain, distinguished by `d->ignore_mtime` rather than by being in separate lists, and `shuffle_deps_recursive` permutes that one chain. Ronin keeps `deps` and `order_onlys` apart and `crate::shuffle::reorder` permutes each on its own, so the two groups shuffle within themselves and never across.\n\nReproducer, minimised against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, and note there is no circular dependency anywhere in it:\n\n    all: t\n    t: a b | c d\n    \t@echo \"t hat=[$^] bar=[$|]\"\n    a: ; @echo a\n    b: ; @echo b\n    c: ; @echo c\n    d: ; @echo d\n\n  make                     a b c d, hat=[a b] bar=[c d]  — both tools agree\n  make --shuffle=reverse   GNU builds d c b a / Ronin builds b a d c\n                           hat and bar agree in both, because the automatic\n                           variables read the written order either way\n\nSo what differs is only the build order, and only when a shuffle is asked for. Nothing gates on it and no upstream row reads it.\n\nIt shows through the circular drop too, and there it reaches `$|`, because the drop takes the entry standing at the walk's position and GNU's position counts across the boundary Ronin's two lists put between them:\n\n    all: t\n    t: a | b t c\n    \t@echo \"t hat=[$^] bar=[$|]\"\n\n  make --shuffle=reverse   GNU bar=[t c] / Ronin bar=[b c]\n\n  Written chain [a, b, t, c], reversed [c, t, b, a]. GNU walks index 1, reads\n  `t` from the shuffled chain, and unlinks written position 1 — which is `b`, a\n  member of the OTHER group. Ronin's order-only walk reaches `t` at index 1 of\n  its own three-entry list and unlinks `b` from that list's position 1... which\n  happens to be `t` itself in the reversed list, so it drops the loop and leaves\n  `b`. The two answers coincide only when the groups happen to line up.\n\nWhere. `crate::shuffle::reorder` (kati/src-rs/shuffle.rs) calls `draw.permute` on `node.deps` and `node.order_onlys` separately. Matching GNU means permuting the concatenation and splitting it back, with `actual_inputs` and `actual_order_only_inputs` left alone as they already are — but the split-back is the hard half, because an entry may cross from one group to the other under the permutation and the two lists are what the graph's compared and order-only edges are built from. GNU has no such split to preserve: `ignore_mtime` travels on the entry, so its chain can hold them in any order at all. The honest shape here is probably for `DepNode` to carry one prerequisite list with a per-entry order-only flag, which is a wider change than the shuffle.\n\nWorth settling before it is matched: whether `.WAIT`'s synthesised order-only prerequisites should travel in the shuffled chain too. The evaluator turns each barrier into order-only edges before this point, so they would be permuted with everything else and the barrier would stop meaning what it said — GNU dodges the question by leaving a list holding a single `.WAIT` group alone."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T14:38:48.253416578Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Probed shuffle.c to settle how GNU Make shuffles order-only prerequisites, and the answer is that it does not shuffle them apart at all — there is nothing to shuffle apart, because there is only one list.\n\n`shuffle_deps` (reference/gnumake/src/shuffle.c:152) takes `struct dep *deps`, counts the whole `->next` chain, copies every entry into one array, permutes that array, and writes the result back as a second `->shuf` chain over the same entries. It never looks at `ignore_mtime`. `shuffle_file_deps_recursive` (:190) hands it `f->deps` and nothing else — there is no second call for a second list, because `f->deps` already holds a target's normal and order-only prerequisites together and tells them apart per entry.\n\nThat per-entry flag is the whole mechanism. `update_file_1` walks the WRITTEN chain and reads the SHUFFLED one at each step — `d = du->shuf ? du->shuf : du` (remake.c:596) — and everything it then asks about the prerequisite it asks of `d`: `d->ignore_mtime`, `d->file`, `d->wait_here`. So order-only-ness travels with the entry under the permutation, which is exactly what lets GNU permute across the boundary Ronin's two lists draw.\n\nTwo switches gate the whole thing and both are already Ronin's: `sm_none` returns early, and `not_parallel` returns early (:225). `.WAIT` is the third — `shuffle_deps` returns without touching a list holding one (:162), where Ronin needs no exception because the evaluator has already turned the barrier into edges.\n\nThe chain genuinely interleaves, which is the finding that decides the cost. The note above reproduced the divergence from a single rule line, where the written chain happens to be normals-then-order-onlys and a group-preserving split could have been mistaken for a fix. It is not one — across rule lines the groups alternate:\n\n    all: t\n    t: a | c\n    t: b | d\n    \\t@echo \"t hat=[$^] bar=[$|]\"\n\n  make                     GNU builds b d a c;  Ronin builds b a d c\n  make --shuffle=reverse   GNU builds c a d b;  Ronin builds a b c d\n  hat=[b a] bar=[d c] in both tools under every mode — the automatic\n  variables already agree, because both read the written order.\n\nSo GNU's chain here is [b(normal), d(order-only), a(normal), c(order-only)] and reversing it gives [c, a, d, b]. Ronin holds the same entries as deps=[b,a] and order_onlys=[d,c] — the correct per-group orders, with the interleaving discarded at construction. Permuting the concatenation and splitting it back by group reproduces exactly what Ronin already does, because the walk visits deps before order_onlys whatever the split says. The interleaving is not recoverable after the fact; it has to be recorded where the two lists are first written to.\n\nWhat the fix costs, measured rather than guessed. Five sites push a prerequisite (kati/src-rs/dep.rs:3016, 3929, 3934, 4000, 4023, 5365, 5398 — two of them synthetic: the double-colon serialisation and the `.WAIT` barriers, which have no written position at all), and three consumers read the order: `drop_circular_dependencies` (dep.rs:2513), `populate_ninja_node` (ninja.rs:703), and the emission at ninja.rs:1300. `drop_prerequisite` is the subtle one — it takes the edge out by the loop-closer's identity and the NAME out by the walk's position, and under one chain those two positions live in different groups, which is the whole of the `$|` cell below.\n\nDeliberately not attempted in this dispatch, and the reason is blast radius rather than difficulty. Making the walk honour a combined order changes the order edges are minted in for EVERY Makefile, not only shuffled ones, and mint order is what the equivalence gate compares and what the upstream inventory's 1101 compiler rows are recorded against. Gating the combined walk on a live shuffle would contain that, at the price of two representations of one chain. Neither is a change to make beside four other deliveries and a full gate run, for a divergence that moves no file, no exit code and no automatic variable — only the order two independent targets are built in, under a debugging switch.\n\nWhat is still owed, and it is the honest reason the node stays open: the circular drop under a shuffle, which is the one place this reaches something observable.\n\n    all: t\n    t: a | b t c\n    \\t@echo \"t hat=[$^] bar=[$|]\"\n\n  make --shuffle=reverse   GNU bar=[t c] / Ronin bar=[b c]\n\n  Written chain [a, b, t, c] reversed to [c, t, b, a]. GNU stands at written\n  index 1 — `b`, an order-only entry — reads `t` off the shuffled chain, finds\n  it updating, and unlinks the entry it was standing on. Ronin's order-only\n  walk reaches `t` at index 1 of its own three-entry list and unlinks that\n  list's position 1, which is `t` itself. The two coincide only when the groups\n  happen to line up."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T08:56:00.449018007Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "EXPLANATION for the operator to rule on. No decision taken here, no code changed, node left open. Every cell below re-measured 2026-08-24 against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left, Ronin on the right, through a make-named link to the debug binary.\n\nWHAT DIVERGES, AND WHAT AGREES.\n\n1. Build ORDER of independent targets, under --shuffle only.\n     t: a b | c d   (a b normal, c d order-only), --shuffle=reverse\n       GNU builds  d c b a\n       Ronin builds b a d c\n       Automatic variables AGREE in both: hat=[a b] bar=[c d].\n   GNU carries a b c d in ONE ->next chain and reverses it whole (d c b a). Ronin holds deps=[a,b] and order_onlys=[c,d] and reverses each within itself (b a, then d c). The written lists $^ and $| are read from an order neither tool shuffles, so they agree; only the order the four independent recipes run in differs.\n\n2. Nothing reachable WITHOUT --shuffle diverges. Verified now: the same makefile under no switch and under --shuffle=identity builds a b c d in BOTH tools, hat=[a b] bar=[c d] in both. The two lists agree wherever they are not permuted, which is every unshuffled run, so this reaches nothing a real build without the debugging switch can observe.\n\n3. ONE cell reaches an observable value, and it additionally needs a circular order-only prerequisite.\n     t: a | b t c   (a normal; b t c order-only; t depends on itself), --shuffle=reverse\n       GNU   $| = [t c]\n       Ronin $| = [b c]\n   Both drop the circular t<-t, both exit 0, both build the same files. Only the name the drop removes from $| differs. Written chain [a,b,t,c] reverses to [c,t,b,a]; GNU stands at written index 1 (b, an order-only entry), reads t off the shuffled chain, finds it updating and unlinks the entry it was standing on (b) — so $| loses b and keeps [t c]. Ronin's order-only walk reaches t at index 1 of its own three-entry order_only list and unlinks that list's position 1, which is t itself — so $| loses t and keeps [b c]. The two coincide only when the groups line up. This is the whole of the observable surface: build order under a debugging switch, plus this one $| cell that also requires a self-dependency.\n\nWHY IT IS ONE MECHANISM. GNU (shuffle.c:152 shuffle_deps, :190 shuffle_file_deps_recursive) counts the whole f->deps ->next chain — a target's normal AND order-only prerequisites together, told apart per entry by d->ignore_mtime — copies it into one array, permutes that, and writes it back as a second ->shuf chain. It never looks at ignore_mtime and never makes a second call for a second list, because there is no second list. update_file_1 walks the WRITTEN chain and reads the SHUFFLED one per step (remake.c:596, d = du->shuf ? du->shuf : du), asking d->ignore_mtime / d->file of the shuffled entry, so order-only-ness travels with the entry under the permutation. The chain genuinely interleaves across rule lines (t: a | c then t: b | d gives GNU's chain [b(normal) d(order-only) a(normal) c(order-only)]); the interleaving is discarded at construction in Ronin, where the two per-group orders are kept correctly but their alternation is not, so it cannot be recovered after the fact.\n\nTHE TWO CANDIDATE FIX SHAPES AND WHAT EACH COSTS EDGE-MINT ORDER. Both reorder the order in which the graph's compared and order-only edges are minted, which is exactly what the equivalence gate compares (make.manifest-equivalence) and what the upstream inventory's compiler rows are recorded against.\n\n  (A) DepNode carries ONE prerequisite list with a per-entry order-only flag, GNU's shape exactly. The interleaving is recorded where the two lists are first written, and the shuffle permutes the one list. This is the honest shape and the only one that reproduces cell 3 without special-casing, but the combined list becomes what populate_ninja_node and the ninja.rs emission walk to mint edges, so the mint order changes for EVERY Makefile the compiler touches, shuffled or not — five push sites (dep.rs, two of them synthetic: the double-colon serialisation and the .WAIT barriers, which have no written position) and three consumers (drop_circular_dependencies dep.rs:2513, populate_ninja_node ninja.rs:703, the emission ninja.rs:1300) all move to reading one list. drop_prerequisite is the subtle consumer: it removes the edge by the loop-closer's identity and the NAME by the walk's position, and under one chain those two positions live in what used to be different groups — which is precisely cell 3.\n\n  (B) A combined walk gated on a live shuffle: keep deps and order_onlys as they are, and build a combined shuffled view only when a shuffle is active, so the mint order moves for shuffled runs alone and every unshuffled Makefile mints exactly as it does today. This contains the blast radius the equivalence gate and the inventory would otherwise see, at the price of two representations of one chain that must be kept consistent, and it still has to answer drop_prerequisite's position-across-the-boundary question for cell 3.\n\n  Neither is a code change to make casually: (A) moves an ordering that two gates and 95→(post-triage) inventory rows are pinned against for every Makefile; (B) buys back that containment with dual state. The divergence they would close moves no file, no exit code, and no automatic variable except the single $| cell in 3.\n\nTHE .WAIT QUESTION, unresolved and load-bearing for either shape. The evaluator turns each .WAIT barrier into synthesised order-only prerequisites BEFORE this point. If order-only prerequisites travel in the shuffled chain, those synthesised barriers are permuted with everything else and the barrier stops meaning what it said. GNU sidesteps the question by leaving a list that holds a .WAIT group alone (shuffle_deps returns without touching it, :162). Whichever fix is chosen has to decide whether Ronin's synthesised .WAIT order-only edges are excluded from the shuffle the same way — which is a second, narrower decision riding on the first.\n\nTHE RULING THIS ASKS FOR. Whether closing a divergence confined to (i) the build order of independent targets under the --shuffle debugging switch and (ii) one $| cell that additionally requires a circular dependency is worth an every-Makefile edge-mint reordering (A) or a dual-representation containment (B), given that both re-baseline the equivalence gate and the inventory's compiler rows. The node stays open pending that ruling."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T12:30:50.711489691Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "EXPLAINED TO THE OPERATOR ON 2026-08-24; NO RULING YET. This node is section 1 of docs/make-oracle-divergences.md. He read that section on that date and ruled on seven of the nine numbered divergences; this was not one of them.\n\nNothing here has changed and nothing has been decided. The node stays open, the two candidate fix shapes on it stand, and the survey's Authority line for section 1 now reads \"none — explained to the operator 2026-08-24; awaiting his ruling\" so that a later reader does not mistake the survey's completeness for approval of this cell.\n\nRecorded by the dispatch delivering his rulings, which was instructed to leave sections 1 and 6 exactly as they were."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T13:50:21.395291593Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "RETIRED AS A RECORDED, OPERATOR-RULED DIVERGENCE. Filed as a defect while probing the shuffled circular drop; ruled on 2026-08-24, by the operator, to be Ronin's behaviour rather than a bug.\n\nOPERATOR DECISION, Brendan, 2026-08-24, on docs/make-oracle-divergences.md section 1, verbatim:\n\n    \"leave it.\"\n\nHe had read the section once that day and left it unruled; asked again the same day, that is his answer. What he was reading is the cost paragraph: both candidate fix shapes reorder edge-minting for every Makefile the compiler touches — (A) one prerequisite list with a per-entry order-only flag, GNU's shape, which the equivalence gate and the inventory's compiler rows are pinned against; or (B) a combined walk gated on a live shuffle, two representations of one chain — and the reachability is a switch nothing in any corpus passes.\n\nRE-MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24 before this entry was written, both cells, through scripts/sandboxed.\n\n    t: a b | c d          --shuffle=reverse -j1\n      GNU 4.4.1   builds d c b a   hat=a b   pipe=c d\n      Ronin       builds b a d c   hat=a b   pipe=c d\n\n    t: a | b t c          --shuffle=reverse -j1\n      GNU 4.4.1   pipe=t c   builds c b a\n      Ronin       pipe=b c   builds a c b\n\nBoth drop the circular t <- t, both exit 0, both build the same files. The automatic variables agree in the first cell; the second is the one cell where the two-list representation reaches a value a makefile can read, and it needs a circular order-only prerequisite to get there.\n\nGATED, so a later change reopens the decision rather than passing quietly. Two make_port cases recorded from the oracle, each carrying a `divergence` sidecar naming this ruling: tests/make/shuffled-order-only-prerequisites-permute-with-the-rest (the build order, read through an order log) and tests/make/a-shuffled-circular-drop-crosses-the-order-only-boundary (the $| cell). make_port fails a recorded divergence that has been \"repaired\" as loudly as it fails an unrecorded one, so a change that starts permuting the two groups together lands as a test failure pointing here. That takes the build-intent corpus from three divergence markers to five, which is the intended and reported movement.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER. make-order-only-and-normal-prerequisite-of-the-same-name is the same two-list representation seen from the other end — a file declared BOTH ways, where GNU's one chain clears the ignore_mtime bit on the entry already there and Ronin leaves the name in $| as well. That one is reachable without any switch at all and is NOT ruled by this; it stays where it is."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T13:50:45.540924212Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
