            {
                id make-makefiles-variable-entries-are-never-remade
                title "Remake the Makefiles the MAKEFILES variable named"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-16T22:45:51.277130728Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-an-unreadable-include-is-refused-rather-than-raised, and separate from it: that node asked what happens when a `MAKEFILES` entry will not open, and this asks what happens when one is not there and a rule says how to make it.\n\nGNU Make reads the variable's names with `eval_makefile (name, RM_NO_DEFAULT_GOAL|RM_INCLUDED|RM_DONTCARE)` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c:204), and the goaldep that comes back joins `read_files` like any other. So the makefile update considers it, remakes it if a rule says how, and restarts the read on what it wrote — `RM_DONTCARE` forgives the failure, it does not excuse the attempt.\n\nReproducer, minimised against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make:\n\n    all: ; @echo all X=$(X)\n    gen.mk: ; @echo GEN; echo X=1 > gen.mk\n\n  make MAKEFILES=gen.mk\n    GNU 4.4.1  GEN / `all X=1`, gen.mk on disk, exit 0\n    Ronin      `all X=`, no gen.mk, exit 0\n\nBoth exit 0 and neither reports anything, so nothing gates on it; what differs is that GNU built the file and read it and Ronin did neither, which means a Makefile that expects the variable to bootstrap a fragment gets an empty one.\n\nWhere. `read_makefiles_entry` (kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs) returns `Ok(())` for a name that is not there and never calls `note_read_makefile` or `note_unread_include`, so the name reaches neither `read_makefiles` nor `missing_includes`, and `plan_regeneration` has nothing to make a root of. The `include` path does both and works. The fix is for the `MAKEFILES` path to note the name too, as not required — the forgiven grouping already exists and `-include` travels through it — and to note it whether the open failed with ENOENT or otherwise, since a `MAKEFILES` entry is forgiven either way.\n\nWorth settling with it, because the same line decides both: an entry that IS read should be noted as a read makefile as well, so that a `MAKEFILES` fragment which later becomes out of date restarts the read the way an `include`d one does. `read_makefiles_entry` does call `note_read_makefile(name, false)` on the arm where the file opened, so that half may already hold — probe it rather than assume, since nothing gates it today."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T14:39:56.580795773Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

