            {
                id make-a-worked-out-names-value-is-its-own-name
                title "Hand back the live list for the value of a name Make works out, not the name itself"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T04:38:09.294936689Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-a-worked-out-name-answers-the-wrong-origin, and left out of it because that node is about the origin ladder — where a binding stands and what may replace it — and this is about what `$(value)` hands back once it stands in the right place.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left:\n\n    $(firstword $(value .VARIABLES))    a real name from the table  /  `.VARIABLES`\n\n`$(value)` reads the variable's stored text without expanding it, and GNU Make's `.VARIABLES` is a SIMPLE variable whose value is the list `lookup_special_var` rebuilt — so `$(value .VARIABLES)` is the list, the same bytes `$(.VARIABLES)` gives, and it holds the `D`/`F` automatic forms exactly as the expansion does.\n\nRonin answers `.VARIABLES` — the name — because `Variable::string` (kati/src-rs/var.rs) maps `InnerVar::VariableNames { name, .. }` to `Cow::Borrowed(name)`. That is the branch `$(value)`, `$(origin)`'s neighbours and every unexpanded-text reader go through. `.KATI_SYMBOLS` answers its own name the same way.\n\nThe fix is to build the list there as `Variable::eval`'s `InnerVar::VariableNames` arm does, which means lifting that loop into a helper both call — it needs `&Session` for `global_var_names` and `peek_global_var`, and `string` already takes one. Watch the `all` flag: `.KATI_SYMBOLS` filters out the function-valued names and `.VARIABLES` does not, and the base automatic variables are excluded from both as of the sibling node.\n\nGate it on a file a recipe writes: `$(words $(value .VARIABLES))` agreeing with `$(words $(.VARIABLES))`, and a name defined in the makefile appearing in the `$(value)` answer. Do not gate the list verbatim — it holds the environment."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:20:02.438175848Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:31:17.335736073Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was right on every point including the shape of the repair. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left.\n\nTHE CELL, re-measured before touching anything:\n\n    $(firstword $(value .VARIABLES))    a real name from the table  /  `.VARIABLES`\n\nand the three that say what the answer has to be, all now identical in both tools: a name the makefile defined appears in `$(value .VARIABLES)`; `$(words $(value .VARIABLES))` equals `$(words $(.VARIABLES))`; and neither of the two answers holds a word the other does not.\n\nWHY THE VALUE IS THE LIST, which is the part worth writing down. `$(value)` reads a variable's stored text without expanding it, and GNU Make's `.VARIABLES` is a SIMPLE variable whose stored text `lookup_special_var` rebuilds whenever the table has changed since it last did. So the text and the expansion are the same bytes BY CONSTRUCTION over there — there is no second code path that could disagree, and no other answer `$(value)` could give. Here they were two code paths, which is exactly how they came to disagree: the list was built in `Variable`'s `Evaluable::eval` arm and nowhere else, and `Variable::string` — the branch `$(value)` and every other unexpanded-text reader goes through — returned a name the `InnerVar::VariableNames` variant carried for that one purpose.\n\nTHE FIX is the filing's: lift the loop into `write_variable_names` and call it from both. `&Session` is all the loop ever needed — `global_var_names`, `peek_global_var` and `symtab` — and `string` already takes one, so nothing had to be threaded anywhere. The variant's `name` field went with it, because after the change nothing reads it and the two definition sites already say which name they are (`Symbol::VARIABLES` and `Symbol::KATI_SYMBOLS`).\n\nSharing the loop rather than copying it is the point: it is what stops the two answers drifting apart a second time, and it carries the two rules the filing warned about for free rather than by remembering them. `all` is the whole of what separates the two names — `.KATI_SYMBOLS` leaves out the function-valued bindings and `.VARIABLES` does not — and both leave out the BASE automatic variables, which is the split the sibling node established against GNU: `$@` and its siblings are bound in the FILE's own variable set by `set_file_variables`, and this walks the global table.\n\n`.KATI_SYMBOLS` is repaired by the same construction and is NOT gated by a fixture, because there is no oracle answer to compare against — GNU has no such name. It is kati's own extension and it stands, which is the judgement already recorded across the conformance corpus's kati-extension family. Measured by hand instead: `$(value .KATI_SYMBOLS)` and `$(.KATI_SYMBOLS)` now hold the same words.\n\nFIXTURE, one, oracle-recorded, branching the BUILD on the cell:\n\n* `tests/make/the-value-of-a-worked-out-name-is-the-list-it-answers` — `ifeq ($(filter MY_OWN_NAME,$(value .VARIABLES)),MY_OWN_NAME)` chooses the goal, gating `mine=[MY_OWN_NAME] words=[same] difference=[]`.\n\nEverything it gates is environment-independent although the list is not: each cell compares two answers the SAME tool gave in the SAME run, so what the environment holds cancels. The list is never gated verbatim, in either direction — the filing said so and it is right, and it is the reason the case is written as a comparison rather than as a transcript.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed: with the fix reverted and the recording in place, the case fails by building the other target, and the file it leaves says exactly what the defect was — `value=[.VARIABLES]`.\n\nWHERE, kati ae4bd58c8ae2e672cc60ebd5850a71b98e76b411 on branch nplan/make-a-worked-out-names-value-is-its-own-name. No Ronin-side change beyond the one fixture.\n\nGATES. fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 903 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 867 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 151 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1111 narration, tsv byte-identical, no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 15s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream plus every differential band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 714 passed / 0 failed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:31:21.569690838Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-four-names-gnu-works-out-are-missing
                title "Define the four names GNU works out for itself that Ronin leaves undefined"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T04:38:30.229063049Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-a-worked-out-name-answers-the-wrong-origin. That node made the `.VARIABLES` list agree with GNU's about the automatic variables; what it did not do — and could not, because it is a different mechanism — is add the names Ronin never defines at all. They show up as the whole of one side of the list diff and each is also its own `$(origin)` divergence.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make in a clean environment, diffing `$(sort $(.VARIABLES))` at read time and again inside a recipe. Identical both times. GNU has and Ronin has not:\n\n    .INCLUDE_DIRS   origin default, flavour simple / undefined\n    .LOADED         origin default, flavour simple / undefined\n    MAKE_COMMAND    origin default, flavour simple / undefined\n    MAKE_HOST       origin default, flavour simple / undefined\n\n`MAKE_HOST` is the configure triplet — `x86_64-pc-linux-gnu` on this host, and the oracle provenance already records it under that name, so what Ronin should answer is a decision to take rather than a value to copy. `MAKE_COMMAND` is the invoked path, which Ronin already works out for `$(MAKE)`: `make_named_invocation` in src/make/cli.rs produces exactly it, and GNU's `MAKE` is `$(MAKE_COMMAND)` with the switches appended. `.INCLUDE_DIRS` is the search list `-I` accumulates, which make-include-directories already maintains. `.LOADED` is the `load`ed object list and is empty here; the oracle provenance records `default .LOADED` with an empty value.\n\nSo three of the four are values Ronin already has and does not publish, and the fourth is a value to decide. None of them is a graph question, which is why this is a small node and not a risky one.\n\nGate it on effects: a recipe writing `$(origin)` and `$(flavor)` for all four, plus `$(MAKE_COMMAND)` being the same word `$(MAKE)` starts with, plus `.INCLUDE_DIRS` naming a directory a `-I` put there."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:34:10.998139354Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:49:02.600310554Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was right about three of the four and wrong about the fourth in a way that turned into its own node. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make throughout, GNU on the left.\n\nTHE FILING'S TABLE re-measured and confirmed before anything was written — all four `default`/`simple` there and undefined here — and a fifth cell found in the same probe that the filing named without listing: `$(value MAKE)` is `$(MAKE_COMMAND)` there and was the path itself here. GNU's `MAKE` is a REFERENCE, not a copy, which is what makes a makefile's write to MAKE_COMMAND change the program `$(MAKE)` runs.\n\nTHREE OF THE FOUR were values this evaluator already had and did not publish, exactly as the filing said.\n\n* `.LOADED` — empty at default rank, and empty for good rather than for now: `load` is not a directive this parser knows, so no object can join the list. GNU's is empty on every makefile in the corpus for the same reason its own is empty on one that loads nothing.\n* `MAKE_COMMAND` — `session.flags.subkati_args`, which is what `make_named_invocation` in src/make/cli.rs already worked out and what the bootstrap's `MAKE?=` line already carried. The line now carries `$(MAKE_COMMAND)` instead, which is GNU's `define_variable_cname (\"MAKE\", \"$(MAKE_COMMAND)\", o_default, 1)` said the same way — and it takes the path out of makefile text, where a `#` or a `$` in it would have been read as syntax. That was a latent defect nothing had reached.\n* `.INCLUDE_DIRS` — the `-I` list, in the spelling `construct_include_path` (read.c) records rather than the spelling the switch was given: every directory stat'ed, the ones that are not there left out, trailing slashes discarded. Both rules measured against the oracle — `-I nosuchdir` leaves the list unchanged and `-I inc/` joins it as `inc`.\n\nMAKE_HOST WAS THE DECISION, and it is recorded on `make_host` in kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs rather than only here. GNU bakes its configure triple, so there is nothing to copy. What a makefile does with the name is `$(findstring mingw32,...)`, `cygwin`, `darwin`, `linux` — it is asking what platform it is ON. So Ronin answers the host the RUN is on, assembled from the target this build was made for, with the vendor field spelled the way `config.guess` spells it: `apple` on Apple systems, `pc` for the x86 family elsewhere, `unknown` otherwise. On this host that produces `x86_64-pc-linux-gnu`, which is the oracle's own answer character for character. It was not written to match it — the rule was taken from config.guess first and the agreement checked afterwards.\n\nTHE FILING'S ONE ERROR, and it became a node. It said `.INCLUDE_DIRS` is \\\"the search list `-I` accumulates, which make-include-directories already maintains\\\". GNU's list is the `-I` directories FOLLOWED BY a built-in default search path — `/usr/local/include /usr/include` on this host, from `default_include_directories` in read.c — and this evaluator has no such path and does not search there. So `.INCLUDE_DIRS` here holds what `-I` put there and no more, which is an honest answer about a search that really does stop at the `-I` list, and the gap is filed as make-the-built-in-include-search-path-is-missing. It is a build question rather than a variable one: `include foo.mk` with the file only in `/usr/local/include` is one GNU finds and this does not. Every other `-I` cell agrees, including the two where `-I -` turns the list off, which was worth measuring because GNU's `-I -` disables the defaults permanently rather than merely restarting the list.\n\nNINE INVOCATIONS MEASURED: bare, `-R`, `-I inc`, `-I inc -I inc2`, `-I nosuchdir`, `-I inc/`, `-I -`, `-I inc -I -`, `-I - -I inc`. `-R` is in the list because it withholds the tool catalogue and these four are not in it — GNU keeps all four under `-R` and so does Ronin now.\n\nFIXTURES, two, oracle-recorded, both branching the BUILD on the cell:\n\n* `tests/make/the-four-names-make-works-out-for-itself-are-there` — `ifeq ($(origin MAKE_COMMAND),undefined)` chooses the goal, gating `inc=[default,simple] loaded=[default,simple,] cmd=[default,simple] host=[default,simple] make=[default,recursive,$(MAKE_COMMAND)] same=[same]`. The two values that legitimately differ between the tools — and between any two hosts — are deliberately not compared; `same=[same]` compares `$(MAKE)` with `$(MAKE_COMMAND)` instead, which is what makes the reference a reference.\n* `tests/make/a-search-directory-joins-the-list-make-works-out` — args `-I inc -I nosuchdir -I inc2/`, branching on `$(filter inc,$(.INCLUDE_DIRS))`, gating `named=[inc] absent=[] stripped=[inc2] as-written=[]`. Every cell is a `filter` over the list rather than the list itself, so the case says the same thing after the default search path lands.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed: with the fix reverted and the recordings in place, both fail by building the OTHER target — `branch=[missing]` and `branch=[absent]`.\n\nINVENTORY MOVED AND AUDITED. Three rows, all in `variables/INCLUDE_DIRS`, from `unclassified`/evaluation to `narration`/ninja-progress: `.diff.1` is upstream's test #2 (`-I$dir` must be in the list), `.diff.4` is `-I somedir`, `.diff.5` is `-I - -I somedir`. All three now agree on everything but the progress line. `variables/INCLUDE_DIRS.diff` — test #1, whose whole content is `ifeq ($(.INCLUDE_DIRS),) $(warning .INCLUDE_DIRS is empty)` — is still unclassified, and it is precisely the default-search-path node's row. Counts moved 151→148 unclassified and 1111→1114 narration over the same 1299 rows; compiler stays ZERO. The tsv is re-recorded with `--update` because the movement is understood and is in the improving direction.\n\nWHERE, kati 8b096c5752756d330945f71034d10a242ef11caa on branch nplan/make-four-names-gnu-works-out-are-missing. No Ronin-side change beyond the two fixtures and the inventory record.\n\nGATES. fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 903 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 869 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 148 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1114 narration, three rows audited above; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 17s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream plus every differential band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 714 passed / 0 failed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:49:08.529047756Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-two-worked-out-names-exist-before-gnu-defines-them
                title "Define MAKECMDGOALS and MAKEOVERRIDES only when GNU does, not always"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T04:38:52.267039419Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-a-worked-out-name-answers-the-wrong-origin, and the remaining half of that node's list divergence once the automatic variables were taken out of it. It is a question about WHEN a name comes into existence rather than about where it stands in the ladder, which is why it is filed apart.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left, three invocations of the same makefile:\n\n    make            MAKECMDGOALS undefined      / default, recursive\n                    MAKEOVERRIDES undefined     / default, recursive\n    make all        MAKECMDGOALS default,simple / default, recursive\n                    MAKEOVERRIDES undefined     / default, recursive\n    make FOO=1      MAKECMDGOALS undefined      / default, recursive\n                    MAKEOVERRIDES default,recursive, value `FOO=1` / default, recursive, value `FOO=1`\n\nGNU defines `MAKECMDGOALS` inside `handle_non_switch_argument` (main.c), on the branch that enters a goal target — so with no goal word there is no name. `MAKEOVERRIDES` is defined by `define_makeflags` only when `command_variables` is non-empty, and its value is `${-*-command-variables-*-}`, which is why it reads back `recursive` while `MAKECMDGOALS` reads back `simple`.\n\nIt reaches real makefiles: `ifdef MAKECMDGOALS` and `ifeq ($(origin MAKEOVERRIDES),undefined)` are both idioms for \"was I invoked bare\", and under Ronin both answer the same whatever the command line said.\n\nThe values already agree — this is only about the name existing and about the flavour it reads back with. Where: the two are defined unconditionally in Ronin's session setup (`record_invocation` / `compiler_flag_variables` in src/make/cli.rs feed them). Making them conditional means not defining them rather than defining them empty, since `$(origin)` distinguishes the two.\n\nBeware one interaction: `MAKEOVERRIDES` is also what a recursive child reads to inherit command-line variables, so withdrawing the name when there are none must not withdraw the mechanism. Probe a recursive child with and without a command-line variable before and after.\n\nGate it on effects through the port harness's `args` sidecar: three cases, or one case whose recipe writes `$(origin MAKECMDGOALS)` and `$(origin MAKEOVERRIDES)` with a goal and a variable among the arguments and one without."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T05:43:07.015470443Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:00:40.89578292Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and one thing the filing predicted is not true — which changes which idiom this repairs. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make throughout, GNU on the left.\n\nTHE FILING'S CELLS ALL RE-MEASURED AND ALL CONFIRMED, four invocations of one makefile:\n\n    make            MAKECMDGOALS  undefined / default,recursive\n                    MAKEOVERRIDES undefined / default,recursive\n    make all        MAKECMDGOALS  default,simple / default,recursive\n                    MAKEOVERRIDES undefined / default,recursive\n    make FOO=1      MAKECMDGOALS  undefined / default,recursive\n                    MAKEOVERRIDES default,recursive `FOO=1` / agrees\n    make all FOO=1  both defined, both tools\n\nand a third name in the same block, probed because GNU's guard covers it too: `-*-command-variables-*-` is `undefined` there and `automatic` here whenever nothing overrode anything. It is askable — `$(origin -*-command-variables-*-)` — and it is in `.VARIABLES`, so it is a cell like the other two.\n\nWHAT IS NOT TRUE. The filing says `ifdef MAKECMDGOALS` \"cannot work under Ronin\". It always worked. `ifdef` asks whether the value is NON-EMPTY, not whether the name exists — measured: `EMPTY :=` followed by `ifdef EMPTY` takes the false branch under GNU. Both names were defined EMPTY here, so `ifdef` answered exactly as GNU's does in every one of the four invocations. The guard that genuinely could not work is the other one the filing names: `ifeq ($(origin MAKEOVERRIDES),undefined)`, and its sibling over MAKECMDGOALS. `$(origin)` is the only question that can tell an empty binding from an absent one, which is why the fixtures branch the build on `$(origin)` and not on `ifdef` — an `ifdef` fixture would have had no teeth at all.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, both halves at their own site. `read_bootstrap_makefile` (kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs) emitted `MAKECMDGOALS?=` unconditionally; it now emits the line only when the target list is non-empty, which is GNU defining the name from inside the goal branch of `handle_non_switch_argument` (main.c) rather than up front. `install_compiler_invocation_variables` defined `-*-command-variables-*-` and `MAKEOVERRIDES` unconditionally; the pair is now inside `if !make_overrides.is_empty()`, which is GNU's `if (command_variables != 0)` in `define_makeflags`.\n\nTHE INTERACTION THE FILING WARNED ABOUT, probed rather than reasoned about. Withdrawing the name must not withdraw the inheritance. It does not, and the reason is that the table Ronin gates on is filled the same way GNU's is: an assignment arriving in an inherited `MAKEFLAGS` reaches `invocation.variables` exactly as an argv one does, so `make_overrides` is non-empty in a child of `make FOO=1` and the pair is defined there. Measured over a recursive `$(MAKE) -C sub` in three invocations — bare, `FOO=1`, and a goal word — and the child agrees with GNU's child on all six cells including `MAKEFLAGS`. The env-only case is measured too and is where Ronin used to be wrong in the other direction: `MAKEOVERRIDES=BAR=2 make` with no MAKEFLAGS gives `-*-command-variables-*-` `undefined` under GNU and gave `automatic` here; it now agrees, and `MAKEFLAGS` still reads ` -- BAR=2` because the reference resolves to the environment's own binding.\n\nThe `?=` spelling is left alone deliberately: it is what makes an environment `MAKECMDGOALS` outrank the bootstrap, which is the same answer GNU's `o_default` define gives — `MAKECMDGOALS=zzz make all` reads back `environment`/`zzz` under both tools, GNU having computed `zzz all` and then been refused by origin precedence.\n\nFIXTURES, three, oracle-recorded, each branching the BUILD on the cell rather than only printing it, so the teeth are a different target running:\n\n* `tests/make/a-bare-invocation-works-out-neither-of-the-two-names` — no args; `ifeq ($(origin MAKECMDGOALS),undefined)` chooses the goal, gating `branch=[bare] goals=[undefined] over=[undefined] hidden=[undefined]`.\n* `tests/make/a-goal-word-works-out-the-goals-name-alone` — args `deliver`; branches on `$(origin MAKEOVERRIDES)`, gating `branch=[none] goals=[default] value=[deliver] hidden=[undefined]`.\n* `tests/make/a-command-line-write-works-out-the-overrides-name-alone` — args `FOO=1`; branches on `$(origin MAKECMDGOALS)`, gating `branch=[none] over=[default] value=[FOO=1] hidden=[automatic] table=[FOO=1] foo=[1]`.\n\nThe two crossing cases are the ones that matter: an implementation gating both names on \"any non-switch word arrived\" would pass a bare case and a both-given case and fail these.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed: with the fix reverted and the recordings in place, all three fail, and each fails by building the OTHER target — `branch=[goal]`, `branch=[some]`, `branch=[some]`.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT AND WHOSE IT IS. `$(flavor MAKECMDGOALS)` still reads `recursive` where GNU says `simple`, because the bootstrap's `?=` makes a recursive variable. That cell is make-worked-out-names-answer-the-wrong-flavour's and is deliberately not gated here — this node's fixtures ask `$(origin)` only, so neither recording moves when that node lands.\n\nWHERE, kati b2dde7d72e75349a4ecc20c90fd1d5c692c93f18 on branch nplan/make-two-worked-out-names-exist-before-gnu-defines-them. No Ronin-side change beyond the three fixtures.\n\nGATES. fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 903 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 864 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 151 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1111 narration, tsv byte-identical, no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 32s; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream plus every differential band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 714 passed / 0 failed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:00:45.454570974Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-worked-out-names-answer-the-wrong-flavour
                title "Give the built-in names the flavour and origin GNU defines them with"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T04:39:15.399431148Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-a-worked-out-name-answers-the-wrong-origin, which fixed `.VARIABLES` and `.KATI_SYMBOLS`. This is the same question asked of the names both tools DO define, and the answers were surveyed in the same pass rather than guessed.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make with `make FOO=1 all`, GNU on the left, every built-in name that disagreed:\n\n    CURDIR          file, simple        / default, simple\n    MAKE_VERSION    default, simple     / default, recursive\n    MAKELEVEL       environment, simple / environment, recursive\n    MAKECMDGOALS    default, simple     / default, recursive\n    MAKEOVERRIDES   default, recursive  / default, recursive   (agrees)\n\nNames checked and already agreeing, so the survey is not open-ended: `.FEATURES`, `.RECIPEPREFIX`, `.DEFAULT_GOAL`, `MAKEFILE_LIST`, `MAKEFLAGS`, `MAKE`, `SUFFIXES`, `.SHELLSTATUS`, `MAKE_RESTARTS`, `MAKE_TERMOUT`, `MAKE_TERMERR`.\n\nTWO MECHANISMS. `CURDIR` is GNU's `o_file` — `define_variable_cname (\"CURDIR\", current_directory, o_file, 0)` in main.c, deliberately at file origin so a makefile's own `CURDIR = x` is a peer rather than an override. Ronin has it at `default`, so a makefile assignment behaves the same but `$(origin CURDIR)` — which is what a makefile checks before deciding to trust it — does not.\n\nThe flavour cells are one mechanism: Ronin defines these as recursive variables holding text, where GNU defines them simple. `$(flavor)` sees the difference and so does `$(value)`: a recursive `MAKE_VERSION` hands back its text, and for these names the text and the value are the same string, so nothing but `$(flavor)` and `$(value)` can tell. Both are makefile-visible.\n\n`MAKECMDGOALS`'s row here is about flavour only; whether it should exist at all is make-two-worked-out-names-exist-before-gnu-defines-them.\n\nWhere: `GlobalVars::with_builtins` in kati/src-rs/var.rs for the catalogue, and src/make/cli.rs's `record_invocation` for the invocation-derived ones. Both build with `Variable::with_simple_string` or the recursive constructor, so this is choosing the right one per name.\n\nGate it on a file a recipe writes: `$(origin)` and `$(flavor)` for the four names, plus a `CURDIR = x` line proving the assignment still lands the way it did."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:04:45.474891332Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:19:25.910510918Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. Every cell in the filing's table re-measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and every one confirmed, and the survey was widened past the names the filing listed — which found one more.\n\nTHE FILING'S TABLE, re-probed with `make FOO=1 all`, GNU on the left, all four now agreeing:\n\n    CURDIR          file, simple        / was default, simple\n    MAKE_VERSION    default, simple     / was default, recursive\n    MAKELEVEL       environment, simple / was environment, recursive\n    MAKECMDGOALS    default, simple     / was default, recursive\n    MAKEOVERRIDES   default, recursive  / agrees, and still does\n\nONE MECHANISM, not two, and the filing was right that it was one: three of the four were bootstrap MAKEFILE LINES, and a makefile line cannot say what GNU says here. `?=` builds a recursive variable and every bootstrap assignment lands at default rank, so neither the flavour nor CURDIR's file rank was expressible. GNU says both in the same call — `define_variable_cname (name, value, origin, 0)`, and the trailing zero IS the recursive flag. So the fix is to stop writing them as makefile text: `install_worked_out_variables` (kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs) now makes the calls, and `read_bootstrap_makefile` is three lines shorter and no longer takes the goal list.\n\nEach name goes through the path its own GNU call implies rather than through one shared path:\n\n* MAKE_VERSION and MAKECMDGOALS through `claim_at_default`, which is `crate::builtins::claimable` — the same test `define_variable_cname` applies with `o_default`: another default may be replaced, an environment or command-line or makefile binding may not.\n* CURDIR through the ordinary ladder, `Session::set_global_var` with `VarOrigin::File`. That is not a detail: kati's `assign` declines a File write over CommandLine, Override and EnvironmentOverride and allows it over Environment, which is exactly GNU's `o_file` rank, so `-e` and a command-line write stay above it without a special case.\n* MAKELEVEL simple as the environment is imported, which is GNU defining it a second time in `define_automatic_variables` once the environment is in scope.\n\nNINE INVOCATIONS MEASURED, not four, because the ladder either side of a rank change is where a rank change goes wrong. All nine now identical: clean; an environment CURDIR (overridden by both, value becomes the real cwd); an environment MAKE_VERSION (kept by both at `environment`/`recursive`); an environment MAKELEVEL (value kept, `environment`/`simple`); a makefile's own `CURDIR = x` and `MAKE_VERSION = 9.9` (both `file`/`recursive` in both tools); a command-line `CURDIR=cl` (`command line` in both); `-R`; `-e` over an environment CURDIR; `-e` over an environment MAKELEVEL.\n\nThat last one moved during the work and is worth recording. MAKELEVEL under `-e` is `environment override` in GNU and was `environment` here, and it is a THIRD mechanism rather than the promotion the other names need: `define_variable_in_set` lifts an INCOMING `o_env` define to `o_env_override` when `-e` is in force, and only a name Make defines over again is ever incoming. kati says that at the import — the origin the constructor is handed — rather than by defining twice. Both tools now agree, including when MAKELEVEL is absent from the environment entirely.\n\nTWO DEFECTS FOUND BY THE SAME WIDENED SURVEY AND FILED, not fixed, each because it is a whole-surface question rather than these four names':\n\n* make-the-e-rank-does-not-show-on-a-name-make-defines-over — `$(origin CC)` under `-e` with `CC` in the environment is `environment override` there and `environment` here, and so is `$(origin MAKE_VERSION)`. `claimable` reads the binding's rank without promoting it; GNU promotes the binding it is about to define over and THEN declines the write. Some fifty catalogue names have the same cell, so fixing two of them here would have left the surface inconsistent. An ordinary environment name is `environment` under `-e` in both tools — measured, and it is what makes the eager reading of `-e` wrong.\n* make-the-shell-name-stands-at-the-wrong-rank-and-flavour — `$(origin SHELL)` is `file` there and `default` here. GNU's answer depends on whether the environment had a SHELL, because the o_default define is declined and a following block rewrites rank and value while leaving the import's recursive flag alone. Two answers from one site, plus the NoExport/`shell_var` interactions, is its own node.\n\nFIXTURES, two, oracle-recorded, both branching the BUILD on the cell rather than only printing it:\n\n* `tests/make/the-names-make-works-out-for-itself-are-simple` — args `deliver`; `ifeq ($(flavor MAKE_VERSION),simple)` chooses the goal, gating `version=[default,simple] level=[environment,simple] goals=[default,simple] curdir=[file,simple]`.\n* `tests/make/a-makefile-writing-the-directory-name-is-its-peer` — `ifeq ($(origin CURDIR),file)` chooses the goal, and the makefile then writes `CURDIR = written`, gating `before=[file] after=[file recursive written]` — the rank before, and the ladder cell the filing asked for, in one line.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed: with the fix reverted and the recordings in place, both fail, and each fails by building the OTHER target — `branch=[recursive]` and `branch=[elsewhere] before=[default]`.\n\nWHERE, kati 389234bb8178264fa0aa2d6107497bd1426d56dd on branch nplan/make-worked-out-names-answer-the-wrong-flavour. No Ronin-side change beyond the two fixtures.\n\nGATES. fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 903 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 866 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 151 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1111 narration, tsv byte-identical, no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 15s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream plus every differential band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 714 passed / 0 failed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:19:29.847920174Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-the-e-rank-does-not-show-on-a-name-make-defines-over
                title "Promote the environment's rank on a name Make defines over, as -e makes GNU do"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-worked-out-names-answer-the-wrong-flavour)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:18:28.038795453Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-worked-out-names-answer-the-wrong-flavour, and left out of it because it is a whole-surface question rather than those four names': the same cell is wrong for every name in the `-R` catalogue.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left, `CC=gcc PLAIN=p make -e`:\n\n    $(origin CC)            environment override / environment\n    $(origin MAKE_VERSION)  environment override / environment      (with MAKE_VERSION=9.9 in the environment)\n    $(origin PLAIN)         environment          / environment      (agrees — nothing ever defines over it)\n\nSo the rule is not \"`-e` promotes the environment\". It promotes exactly the names Make itself defines over, and it promotes them whether or not the definition then lands. GNU's `define_variable_in_set` (variable.c) does it in two steps as it is ABOUT to write:\n\n    if (env_overrides && v->origin == o_env)\n      v->origin = o_env_override;\n    if (v->origin <= origin) { ...write... }\n\nThe promotion happens first, the rank comparison then declines the write, and the promoted rank is what `$(origin)` answers for the rest of the run. A name nothing ever defines over — `PLAIN` above — is still `environment`, which is why an eager promotion of the whole environment would be wrong. This was measured rather than assumed, and it is the reason `note_environment_outranks_the_makefile` exists and is called from `Session::set_global_var` alone.\n\nWHERE. `crate::builtins::claimable` (kati/src-rs/builtins.rs) is the \"would `define_variable_cname` with `o_default` write here\" test, and it reads the binding's origin without promoting it. Its callers are `install_default_variables` — the whole tool catalogue, some fifty names — and `claim_at_default` in evaluate.rs, which is MAKE_VERSION and MAKECMDGOALS. Every one of them is a name GNU defines over, so every one should promote. `note_environment_outranks_the_makefile` (kati/src-rs/var.rs) is already the promotion and already does the right thing; it just is not reached from here.\n\nTwo cells to check before writing it, because `claimable` is called in a loop over a table and the promotion has to happen even for entries whose write is declined: an environment `CC` under `-e` keeps `gcc` AND reads `environment override`, and `.POSIX:`'s second pass over the same names must not double-promote into something else.\n\nMAKELEVEL is already right and is a different mechanism, recorded here so the next reader does not try to unify them: GNU lifts the INCOMING `o_env` define to `o_env_override` under `-e` (the first line of the same function), and kati says that at the import instead of defining twice.\n\nGate it on a file a recipe writes: `$(origin CC)` and `$(origin MAKE_VERSION)` with each name in the environment and `-e` among the arguments, beside an ordinary environment name that must still read `environment`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T07:49:47.826687774Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T07:50:54.735255058Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. The filing's mechanism was right and its scope was too small: the survey widened from the three cells it listed to THIRTY NAMES across FIVE INVOCATION SHAPES — 150 cells — and found six more sites, one of which the filing's own reasoning would have got backwards.\n\nTHE FILING'S THREE CELLS re-measured and confirmed first, `CC=gcc PLAIN=p make -e`: `$(origin CC)` and `$(origin MAKE_VERSION)` `environment override` there and `environment` here, `$(origin PLAIN)` `environment` in both.\n\nTHE RULE, and the filing quoted only half of it. `define_variable_in_set` (variable.c) does TWO promotions, not one, and the comparison runs the other way from what the filing wrote:\n\n    if (env_overrides && origin == o_env)\n      origin = o_env_override;                    /* the INCOMING origin */\n    if (! HASH_VACANT (v))\n      {\n        if (env_overrides && v->origin == o_env)\n          v->origin = o_env_override;             /* the binding in the slot */\n        if ((int) origin >= (int) v->origin)      /* >=, so a tie writes */\n          { ...write... }\n      }\n\nThe second is the one the catalogue needs. The first is the one MAKELEVEL already had, and it turns out MFLAGS needs it too — see below.\n\nIT IS THE WRITE THAT CARRIES THE ANSWER, NOT THE NAME. That is the finding, and three cells make it visible, each measured before anything was written:\n\n* `-e -R` leaves an inherited `CC` at plain `environment`, because `define_default_variables` (default.c) returns without defining anything when `-R` is in force. MAKE_VERSION, written outside the catalogue, is lifted under `-R` exactly as it is without it. Ronin gets this free, because `install_default_variables` is already gated on the same switch — but it had to be measured before that could be called correct rather than lucky.\n* `.LOADED` and `.FEATURES` are NEVER lifted. GNU writes them at main.c:1436 and main.c:1475, ahead of `decode_switches` at main.c:1624, so `env_overrides` is still zero. Had `claimable` been made to promote unconditionally — which is what the filing's \"every one of them is a name GNU defines over, so every one should promote\" says — `.LOADED` would have BROKEN, because it agreed before by not promoting. That is the filing's one error and the probe caught it.\n* `.SHELLFLAGS` is written in that same early window and IS lifted the moment `.POSIX:` writes it again during the read. So the early names are not exempt; only the early WRITE is. This is the cell the filing asked about as \".POSIX:'s second pass must not double-promote\" — the answer is that the second pass promotes for the first time, and the promotion is idempotent because a rank already `environment override` is not `environment`.\n\nWHERE. `crate::builtins::claimable` (kati/src-rs/builtins.rs) now lifts, which puts the promotion on every caller at once: the tool catalogue, `install_posix_variables`, `install_suffixes_variable`, and `claim_at_default`. `claimable_before_the_switches` beside it is the same test without the lift, and has exactly two callers. The three names `GlobalVars::with_builtins` binds — `.RECIPEPREFIX`, `.SHELLFLAGS`, `.VARIABLES` — need nothing, because they are bound ahead of the environment import and the import overwrites them.\n\nSIX MORE SITES THE FILING DID NOT NAME, all found by widening the survey and all now agreeing:\n\n* `MAKEFILES` — `install_makefiles_variable` returned early on any existing binding, so it never lifted. It goes through the claim now, and still writes `v_ifset` on whichever variable holds the name.\n* `MAKEOVERRIDES` — the same early return, inside the `if (command_variables != 0)` guard it shares with GNU. Without a command-line assignment there is no define and no lift in either tool, which was measured both ways.\n* `SUFFIXES` — free, via `claimable`, in both of `install_suffixes_variable`'s calls.\n* `.FEATURES` — was a bootstrap `.FEATURES :=` line, which stands at FILE rank and therefore took the name from the environment, where GNU's default-rank define is declined by it. It is a non-promoting default claim now, which fixes the `-e` cell and a second divergence the filing never mentioned: an inherited `.FEATURES` is now kept, as GNU keeps it.\n* `MAKE` — was a bootstrap `MAKE?=` line, and `?=` is the one assignment GNU Make declines WITHOUT promoting (`do_variable_definition` returns on a bound name before it reaches `define_variable_in_set`, measured on an ordinary name to be sure). It is now the recursive default claim `define_variable_cname (\"MAKE\", \"$(MAKE_COMMAND)\", o_default, 1)` makes — the one name in `install_worked_out_variables` whose flag is a 1.\n* `MFLAGS` — the other half of the function. GNU writes it at `o_env` (`define_variable_cname (\"MFLAGS\", flagstring, o_env, 1)`), so under `-e` the INCOMING origin is lifted as well as the one in the slot, the two ranks meet, and `>=` lets the write land at the promoted rank. Ronin hands MFLAGS to the evaluator through the invocation environment, so it joins MAKELEVEL in the import's special case. `MAKE_RESTARTS`, which arrives the same way and which GNU never defines over, stays `environment` — measured, and it is what makes the pair a pair rather than a class.\n\nMEASURED: 150 cells — thirty names × {`-e`, no switch, `-e -R`, `-e -r`, `-R`}, each name in the environment — plus the same thirty with NOTHING in the environment across five shapes, plus the makefile-write ladder either side (`?=`, `:=`, `=`, `+=`, `override`, an unrelated write, and no write at all), plus `.POSIX:` three ways, plus a recursive child. Zero differences. The child was checked because the filing asked: `-e` descends through MAKEFLAGS and the compiled child unit runs the same evaluator, so it sees the same origins GNU's child sees.\n\nFIXTURES, three, oracle-recorded, each branching the BUILD on the cell:\n\n* `tests/make/the-e-switch-lifts-only-the-names-make-defines-over` — env carries CC, MAKE_VERSION, MAKE, PLAIN, .FEATURES and .LOADED, args `-e`; `ifeq ($(origin CC),environment override)` chooses the goal, gating `cc=[environment override,from-the-environment] ver=[environment override,9.9] make=[environment override] mflags=[environment override] plain=[environment] feat=[environment,before-the-switches] loaded=[environment,before-the-switches]`. All three kinds of name in one invocation.\n* `tests/make/the-catalogue-a-dash-r-withheld-is-not-lifted` — env carries CC and MAKE_VERSION, args `-e -R`; branches on MAKE_VERSION being lifted, gating `cc=[environment,from-the-environment] ver=[environment override,9.9]`.\n* `tests/make/a-posix-target-lifts-the-name-it-writes-again` — env carries `.SHELLFLAGS=-c` and CC, args `-e`, makefile has `.POSIX:`; branches on `.SHELLFLAGS` being lifted, gating `sf=[environment override,-c] cc=[environment override,from-the-environment] arf=[default,-rv]`.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed: with the whole kati diff reverted and the recordings in place, all three fail, and each fails by building the OTHER target — `branch=[environment]` in every case.\n\nWHERE, kati c83c59632085a7731f62129cd69d6a2b1d0b3557 on branch nplan/make-the-e-rank-does-not-show-on-a-name-make-defines-over. No Ronin-side change beyond the three fixtures.\n\nGATES. fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 904 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 877 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 148 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1114 narration, tsv byte-identical, no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream plus every differential band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 714 passed / 0 failed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T07:51:31.632282692Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-the-shell-name-stands-at-the-wrong-rank-and-flavour
                title "Stand SHELL where GNU Make stands it, which depends on whether the environment had one"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-worked-out-names-answer-the-wrong-flavour)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:18:51.929473019Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-worked-out-names-answer-the-wrong-flavour, by widening that node's survey past the names its filing listed. SHELL was not among the eleven the earlier survey checked and agreed on, and it disagrees.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left, with SHELL in the environment (which is the ordinary case, and the case the port harness runs in — it removes MAKEFLAGS and MAKELEVEL and nothing else):\n\n    $(origin SHELL)   file    / default\n    $(flavor SHELL)   recursive / recursive   (agrees)\n    $(SHELL)          /bin/sh / /bin/sh       (agrees)\n\nand under `-e` Ronin says `environment override`, which is a third answer neither tool's ladder would give for this name.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, and it is why this is its own node rather than a line beside CURDIR's. GNU's answer is not a constant: it depends on whether the environment had a SHELL at all. `define_automatic_variables` (variable.c) does\n\n    v = define_variable_cname (\"SHELL\", default_shell, o_default, 0);\n    /* Don't let SHELL come from the environment.  */\n    if (*v->value == '\\0' || v->origin == o_env || v->origin == o_env_override)\n      { v->origin = o_file; v->value = xstrdup (default_shell); }\n\nso with no SHELL in the environment the first line lands and the name stands `default` and SIMPLE; with one, the first line is declined, the block rewrites the VALUE and the RANK and leaves the recursive flag the environment import set — so the name stands `file` and RECURSIVE. Two different pairs of answers from one call site. Predicted rather than measured for the no-SHELL case; probe `env -u SHELL make` before writing anything.\n\nRonin says `default` and recursive in both, from the bootstrap line `SHELL=/bin/sh` in `read_bootstrap_makefile` (kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs), which is a makefile assignment and therefore recursive at bootstrap rank whatever the environment held.\n\nTWO INTERACTIONS TO PROBE FIRST, because SHELL is not an ordinary name. The import already marks it `VarExport::NoExport` — POSIX says a makefile's SHELL must not change the one subprocesses are given — and `crate::eval` reads `shell_var` separately for what actually runs recipes. Changing the rank must not change either. GNU also consults MAKESHELL before any of this on platforms that have it; this host does not, and the branch is `#ifdef`ed out.\n\nGate it on a file a recipe writes: `$(origin SHELL)` and `$(flavor SHELL)` with SHELL in the environment and again with it removed, plus a recipe line proving the shell that ran it is still `/bin/sh`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T07:10:47.281457121Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T07:27:31.016465253Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was right about both halves of the mechanism — including the half it flagged as predicted rather than measured. `env -u SHELL` was probed before anything was written and GNU answers `default`/SIMPLE there, exactly as the filing predicted from the source.\n\nTHE FILING'S TABLE re-measured and confirmed, and the survey widened from the four cells it listed to TWENTY invocations, which found two more divergences the filing did not name — one of them a build failure.\n\n    with SHELL in the environment      file, recursive, /bin/sh   / was default, recursive\n    with no SHELL in the environment   default, simple, /bin/sh   / was default, RECURSIVE\n    -e over an environment SHELL       file, recursive, /bin/sh   / was `environment override`, AND THE ENVIRONMENT'S SHELL RAN THE RECIPE\n    -e over an environment SHELL,\n      makefile assigning SHELL         file, recursive, assigned  / was `environment override`, the makefile write declined\n    a command-line `SHELL=` (empty)    file, recursive, /bin/sh   / was `command line`, empty, and the build DIED 127\n\nThe two the filing did not name are the last two, and they are effects rather than cells. `SHELL=/bin/zsh make -e` executed `/bin/zsh -c` where GNU Make executes `/bin/sh -c` — the whole point of GNU's special case is that the environment's shell must not run a recipe, and `-e` was the one door it was still coming through here. `make SHELL=` ran `'' -c` and exited 127 where GNU Make builds.\n\nONE MECHANISM, one call site, two pairs of answers, and the filing quoted it correctly. `define_automatic_variables` (variable.c) offers the built-in shell at default rank and then refuses to let the environment's answer survive:\n\n    v = define_variable_cname (\"SHELL\", default_shell, o_default, 0);\n    /* Don't let SHELL come from the environment.  */\n    if (*v->value == '\\0' || v->origin == o_env || v->origin == o_env_override)\n      { v->origin = o_file; v->value = xstrdup (default_shell); }\n\nWith no SHELL in the environment the offer lands: `default`, and SIMPLE because the trailing zero is the recursive flag. With one, the offer is declined on rank and the block rewrites the RANK and the TEXT while leaving the import's recursive flavour alone: `file`, RECURSIVE, holding `/bin/sh`. Ronin said `default` and recursive in both because it said it as a bootstrap makefile line — `SHELL=/bin/sh` — and no makefile line can say either of those two things.\n\nWHY `-e` IS INVISIBLE HERE, which is the part worth writing down: the promotion `-e` performs on an environment binding is to `o_env_override`, and `o_env_override` is one of the two ranks this block overwrites. So the promotion happens and is then overwritten by `o_file` in the next statement. That is why SHELL is the one environment name `-e` does not lift — and why, at `file` rank, a makefile's own `SHELL =` is a peer that may replace it even under `-e`.\n\nTHE EMPTY TEST IS NOT ABOUT THE ENVIRONMENT AT ALL, which the filing did not say and the probe found: `*v->value == '\\0'` reads whatever binding won the slot, so a command-line `SHELL=` — which outranks everything — stands back at the built-in shell at `file` rank. That is what keeps a recipe running when an empty one arrives.\n\nWHERE. `stand_the_shell` (kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs) is the call site, and `Variable::restate_at` (kati/src-rs/var.rs) is the in-place rewrite the second half needs — defining the name again would be declined on rank and would install the wrong flavour, which is exactly why GNU assigns `v->origin` and `v->value` directly rather than calling `define_variable` twice. The bootstrap line is gone.\n\nPLACED WHERE GNU PLACES IT, and the placement is load-bearing rather than tidy. `decode_switches` enters the command line's variables at main.c:1624 and `define_automatic_variables` runs at main.c:2032, so GNU stands SHELL after the command line and before the first makefile. Ronin applies command-line assignments after the bootstrap, so the call sits after that phase. Two things follow that a call earlier would have got wrong: a command-line `SHELL=` is the binding the empty test sees, and an expansion DURING the command line reads the environment's shell in both tools because neither has stood this one yet — measured, `make 'FOO:=$(shell echo $$BASH_VERSION)'` under `SHELL=/bin/bash` answers `5.2` in both and answered empty here before.\n\nTHE TWO INTERACTIONS THE FILING ASKED TO PROBE FIRST, both probed and both unchanged. The import's `VarExport::NoExport` on SHELL still stands, so a child is given the invocation's own SHELL and not the makefile's — measured with `printenv SHELL` inside the recipe rather than `$$SHELL`, which bash sets for itself and would have confounded the reading. An `export SHELL` directive still overrides that and hands the child `/bin/sh` in both tools. `crate::eval::get_shell` reads the same binding and now reads the right one, which is what took the `-e` recipe back off `/bin/zsh`. MAKESHELL is `#ifdef`ed out on this host and was not probed.\n\nA HARNESS CHANGE WAS NEEDED AND IS THE RONIN-SIDE DELIVERY. `tests/make_port.rs` cases could say their arguments and their setup but not their environment, and this node's answer turns on whether the environment had a name at all. Cases may now write an `env` sidecar — `NAME=value` per line, `-NAME` for a name the run must not carry. Both the recording and the replay go through the same code, so the oracle and Ronin are asked the same question rather than each inheriting the host's.\n\nFIXTURES, three, oracle-recorded, each branching the BUILD on the cell:\n\n* `tests/make/the-shell-the-environment-gave-is-not-the-one-a-recipe-runs` — env `SHELL=./impostor`, args `-e`; `ifeq ($(origin SHELL),file)` chooses the goal, gating `origin=[file] flavor=[recursive] value=[/bin/sh]`. `impostor` is a checked-in executable that touches a file and then execs `/bin/sh \"$@\"`, so a build whose shell came from the environment still succeeds and is caught by what it left behind rather than by a failure that could have had any cause.\n* `tests/make/a-shell-the-environment-did-not-carry-is-simple` — env `-SHELL`; `ifeq ($(flavor SHELL),simple)` chooses the goal, gating `origin=[default] flavor=[simple] value=[/bin/sh]`.\n* `tests/make/an-empty-shell-stands-back-at-the-built-in-one` — args `SHELL=`, env `SHELL=/bin/sh` so the ambient environment cannot decide it; gating `origin=[file] value=[/bin/sh]`, and the recipe running at all is half the assertion.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed: with the bootstrap line put back and `stand_the_shell` unreached, all three fail. Two fail by building the OTHER target — `branch=[recursive]` and `branch=[environment override]` — and the third fails on the outcome, `build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`. The first also leaves `shell-came-from-the-environment` in the run directory, which is the impostor having run the recipe.\n\nWHERE, kati a490e27973e3c90623d79622d2b25f3bd8a73e9b on branch nplan/make-the-shell-name-stands-at-the-wrong-rank-and-flavour.\n\nGATES. fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 904 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 874 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1299 rows at 148 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1114 narration, tsv byte-identical, no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream plus every differential band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 714 passed / 0 failed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T07:29:00.877787502Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-the-built-in-include-search-path-is-missing
                title "Search the built-in include directories an -I did not name, as GNU Make does"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-four-names-gnu-works-out-are-missing)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T06:46:07.756828586Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-four-names-gnu-works-out-are-missing, and it CORRECTS that node's filing. The filing said `.INCLUDE_DIRS` is \\\"the search list `-I` accumulates, which make-include-directories already maintains\\\". It is not: GNU Make's list is the `-I` directories FOLLOWED BY a built-in default search path, and this evaluator has no such path at all.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left, after that node landed:\n\n    make                 /usr/local/include /usr/include        / (empty)\n    make -I inc          inc /usr/local/include /usr/include    / inc\n    make -I -            (empty)                                / (empty)          agrees\n    make -I inc -I -     (empty)                                / (empty)          agrees\n    make -I - -I inc     inc                                    / inc              agrees\n\nSo the divergence is exactly the default path and nothing else — every `-I` cell already agrees, including the two that turn the defaults off.\n\nIt is a BUILD question and not only a variable one, which is why it was filed rather than folded in: `include foo.mk` with `foo.mk` in `/usr/local/include` and not in the working directory is a file GNU Make finds and this one does not. `at_include_dirs` (kati/src-rs/eval.rs) searches `flags.include_dirs` and stops; with nothing in it, the name is returned unchanged and the include fails.\n\nWHAT THE PATH IS. `default_include_directories` (read.c:103) is the configure-time `INCLUDEDIR`, then `/usr/gnu/include`, `/usr/local/include`, `/usr/include`. `construct_include_path` stats each and keeps the ones that are there, which is why the oracle answers with two of the four on this host. Ronin has no configure step, so `INCLUDEDIR` is a decision like `MAKE_HOST` was — probably the same three GNU ships without it.\n\nTWO RULES TO CARRY, both already measured against the oracle and both currently invisible because the list is empty:\n\n* `-I -` does not merely restart the list, it DISABLES the defaults for the rest of the run — `disable = 1` in `construct_include_path`, never cleared. `make -I - -I inc` gives `inc` alone and not `inc` plus the defaults. Ronin's `include_dir` (src/make/cli.rs) clears the list on `-` and would grow the defaults back at the end unless the switch sets a flag of its own.\n* A `-I ~/x` is tilde-expanded before it is stat'ed (`tilde_expand`, read.c). Ronin's `path_of` does not expand it, which is a second small divergence at the same site and belongs here rather than in a node of its own.\n\nTHE INVENTORY ROW THAT PROVES IT. `variables/INCLUDE_DIRS.diff` is upstream's test #1 and its whole content is `ifeq ($(.INCLUDE_DIRS),) $(warning .INCLUDE_DIRS is empty)`. It is the one row of that case still unclassified after the sibling node moved the other three to narration, and it will move when this lands.\n\nGate it on effects, not on the variable: an `include` of a file that exists only under a default directory, reaching a rule the build then runs. `/usr/include` cannot be written to from a test, so the case needs the `INCLUDEDIR` decision to be something a fixture can create, or the effect gated through `-I` plus the `-I -` disable rule instead."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T08:00:51.713004257Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T08:25:07.46101088Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. Every cell in the filing was re-measured and confirmed, the survey widened from five invocations to twenty-one, and both of the questions the filing left open — what the path honestly is, and what a default-path include does about remaking — were answered by measurement rather than by argument.\n\nTHE FILING'S TABLE re-probed and confirmed exactly: bare gave `/usr/local/include /usr/include` there and nothing here; `-I inc` gave `inc` plus those two there and `inc` alone here; the three `-I -` cells already agreed. So the divergence really was the default path and nothing else.\n\nWHAT THE PATH IS, and it is a decision recorded on `DEFAULT_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES` (kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs) rather than only here. GNU's `INCLUDEDIR` is `$(includedir)` from its own configure run — the place THAT installation puts headers. Ronin has no configure step and installs no include tree of its own, so there is nothing honest to prepend: a path named after this program would name a directory nothing ever puts a fragment in. What is left is the convention, `/usr/gnu/include /usr/local/include /usr/include`, which is exactly the list GNU Make ships when it is built WITHOUT an `INCLUDEDIR`. The oracle is such a build — `am__append_1 = -DINCLUDEDIR=...` is commented out of its generated Makefile, which is why it answers with two of the three on this host. So the agreement is by construction, not by matching: the rule was taken from read.c first and the oracle's own build checked afterwards.\n\nONE LIST, WHERE THERE WERE TWO. `construct_include_path` now does what its name says and both consumers read it: `.INCLUDE_DIRS` publishes it and `at_include_dirs` searches it, through a new `Session::include_path`. Before, the variable stat'ed and stripped trailing slashes while the SEARCH did neither — `-I inc/` was published as `inc` and searched as `inc/`, and `-I nosuchdir` was published as absent and searched anyway. That was a latent second opinion, and the node's whole point is that the variable is an answer about the search.\n\nTHE TWO ADJACENT RULES, both carried, both measured.\n\n* `-I -` is a LATCH and not a list operation. `disable = 1` in `construct_include_path` is never cleared, so `make -I - -I inc` searches `inc` alone. Ronin's `include_dir` (src/make/cli.rs) cleared the list and would have grown the defaults back at the end; it now sets `no_default_include_dirs`, which travels to kati as a flag of its own. Measured in three positions — `-I -`, `-I inc -I -`, `-I - -I inc`.\n* `-I ~` is tilde-expanded before it is stat'ed. `~` and `~/sub` both agree now, with an absolute HOME, a relative one and none at all.\n\nTHE ONE FORM DELIBERATELY NOT IMPLEMENTED, and it is a finding rather than a shortfall: `~user`. GNU answers it out of the passwd database. `getpwnam` on a name that is not in /etc/passwd falls through to `libnss_systemd`, which glibc has to `dlopen` — and Ronin is built `+crt-static` (.cargo/config.toml) into a static-pie binary, where that `dlopen` is unsupported. It does not fail, it SEGFAULTS inside `_nss_systemd_is_blocked`. That was measured twice: through `ronin -I ~zzz` under gdb, and again in a plain `cargo test -p kati` binary, which rules out anything about the front end and pins it on the static link. A dynamically linked C program and a plain `rustc` binary on the same host both answer correctly, which is the same finding from the other side. So Ronin resolves `~` from the environment and leaves `~user` as written — which then fails the directory test and drops out of the path, exactly what GNU Make does for a user IT cannot resolve. Anyone wanting `~user` has to give up static linking first, and that is a product decision rather than a compatibility one.\n\nTHE REMAKING QUESTION THE FILING ASKED, answered by measurement. A makefile found down the search path is entered under the JOINED path, not the name as written, so it is the joined path a rule must name to remake it: GNU's `-p` dump reads `MAKEFILE_LIST := k.mk /usr/include/ar.h` for an `-include ar.h` that the default path found. (Its diagnostic says `ar.h:1`, which is the location's spelling and not the file's identity — narration, not a second answer.) Ronin does the same, measured on the `-I` half of the same list, where both tools give `MAKEFILE_LIST = ml.mk inc/frag.mk`. The default entries are appended to that same array in both tools and go through the same code, so nothing about remaking needed changing — which is the answer, not an absence of one.\n\nTWENTY-ONE INVOCATIONS MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make: bare, `-R`, `-I inc`, `-I inc -I inc2`, `-I nosuchdir`, `-I inc/`, `-I inc///`, `-I .`, `-I /usr/include` (which GNU lists TWICE, once from the switch and once from the default path — nothing is de-duplicated, and neither does this), `-I -`, `-I inc -I -`, `-I - -I inc`, `-I ~`, `-I ~/work`, `-I ~` with HOME relative, `-I ~/x` with HOME naming a directory that is not there, `-I ~nosuchuser`, and the four search cases below. Every one agrees but `~user`.\n\nFIXTURES, four, oracle-recorded, each gating an EFFECT rather than the variable:\n\n* `tests/make/an-include-no-directory-named-is-found-on-the-built-in-path` — `-include stdio.h` and nothing else. Finding it stops the build on that file's first line; not finding it is the silent successful case that writes `out`. GNU fails, and so does Ronin now. A `note` beside the case records what it assumes about /usr/include.\n* `tests/make/a-dash-i-dash-turns-the-built-in-include-path-off-for-good` — the same include with `-I - -I inc`: the build succeeds and gates `dirs=[inc]`, so the later `-I` adds one directory rather than restoring the defaults beside it.\n* `tests/make/a-named-directory-comes-before-the-built-in-include-path` — `inc/stdio.h` is a real fragment; branching on `$(FOUND)` and gating `found=[inc] first=[inc] tail=[there]`, which says both that the named directory won and that the built-in path is still behind it.\n* `tests/make/a-tilde-in-a-search-directory-is-expanded-before-it-is-searched` — env `HOME=inc`, args `-I ~`, and a fragment reachable only through the expansion; gates `found=[yes] named=[inc]`.\n\nTEETH MEASURED, not assumed. With the whole diff reverted, three of the four fail — two by building the OTHER target (`branch=[]` and `tail=[absent]`) and one on the outcome (`build succeeded where GNU Make's build failed`). The fourth guards the new code rather than the old defect, so its teeth were measured by breaking the latch alone (`if !no_default_include_dirs` forced true), and it then fails with `build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`.\n\nINVENTORY MOVED AND AUDITED, and one row of the movement is new rather than moved. Counts 1299 → 1300 rows, unclassified 148 → 147, narration 1114 → 1116, interface 37, compiler ZERO.\n\n* `variables/INCLUDE_DIRS.diff` — unclassified/ninja-progress+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress. This is the row the filing named: upstream's test #1 is `ifeq ($(.INCLUDE_DIRS),) $(warning .INCLUDE_DIRS is empty)`, and the warning no longer fires because the list is no longer empty. Only the progress line differs now.\n* `options/dash-I.diff.8` — a row that did not exist before, classified narration/product-name+fatal-decoration+shared-refusal. Upstream's last dash-I case is CONDITIONAL: it asks the tool for `$(.INCLUDE_DIRS)`, looks for a default directory that is not `.` and holds at least one file, and skips itself if there is none. Ronin answered with an empty list, so the case never ran; it runs now. Reproduced by hand to check the classification: `include aio.h` under `-I-` gives `d8.mk:2: aio.h: No such file or directory` in both tools, then `make: *** No rule to make target 'aio.h'.  Stop.` there against `ronin: No rule to make target 'aio.h'.` here — the same refusal, the product name and the fatal decoration apart. The count grew because the tool reaches a case it could not reach before, which is the improving direction, and the tsv is re-recorded with `--update`.\n\nTWO DEFECTS FOUND BY THE WIDENED SURVEY AND FILED, not fixed, each because it is a different site's question:\n\n* make-a-command-line-file-argument-is-not-canonicalised — `expand_command_line_file` (main.c) is the gate every `filename`-typed switch goes through, and Ronin has no counterpart. Leading `./` is stripped there (`-I ./inc` is `inc` and `-f ./x.mk` puts `x.mk` in MAKEFILE_LIST), an empty argument is fatal (`the '-I' option requires a non-empty string argument`, and the same for `-f` and `-W`), and duplicate arguments are filtered for every list switch but `-f`.\n* make-a-makefiles-own-makeflags-does-not-reach-the-include-path — `MAKEFLAGS += -I inc2` in a makefile adds to the search path in GNU Make and `.INCLUDE_DIRS` updates immediately, because `reset_makeflags` calls `construct_include_path` again. Ronin's search path is built once.\n\nWHERE, kati a12795a2ec183313dd91cf1f8d49eec4f16a1ef4 on branch nplan/make-the-built-in-include-search-path-is-missing. Ronin side: the `-I -` latch in src/make/cli.rs and the four fixtures.\n\nGATES. fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 904 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 881 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1300 rows at 147 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1116 narration, two rows audited above, tsv re-recorded, compiler bucket ZERO; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream plus every differential band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 714 passed / 0 failed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T08:27:26.935234185Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T09:09:03.966543964Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Operator ruling, Brendan, 2026-08-20, verbatim: \"a static binary on glibc is basically not supported, so, this isn't something we need to care about. musl can't break in that use case either.\"\n\nThis supersedes the rationale recorded on this node's completion note for leaving `~user` unimplemented. That note read that `getpwnam` on a name not in /etc/passwd falls through to `libnss_systemd`, which glibc has to `dlopen`, and that this SEGFAULTS in a `+crt-static` binary — concluding that \"anyone wanting `~user` has to give up static linking first, and that is a product decision rather than a compatibility one.\"\n\nThe measurement stands; the conclusion does not. The crash is glibc's own documented NSS limitation in a configuration this product does not ship: static glibc. The shipped static link is musl, whose `getpwnam` reads the passwd files directly with no `dlopen` and cannot fail this way. So what was recorded as a product constraint is an artifact of one development host, and the blocker is withdrawn.\n\nWHAT CHANGED IN THE CODE. The comment at kati's tilde site (`tilde_expand`, src-rs/evaluate.rs) is corrected to say this: `~user` is unimplemented rather than unimplementable, the recorded crash is a static-glibc artifact, and musl-static is unaffected. Landed with make-a-command-line-file-argument-is-not-canonicalised, kati 220974aaee349cb0abf0f86c3ee68ef98595c5fd.\n\n`~user` IS STILL NOT IMPLEMENTED, and the ruling did not order it. It did not fall out of the file-argument gate naturally — that work moved `tilde_expand` to one shared function and gave it a second caller, but adding a passwd lookup is a new dependency rather than a line in it — and this development host is the crashing configuration, so a test exercising a `getpwnam` miss would kill the suite here rather than fail it. Whoever takes it should file it as its own node and reckon with how to test it on a host that cannot safely run the miss.\n\nBehaviour today, unchanged and correct as far as it goes: `~user` is left exactly as written, then fails the directory test and drops out of the search path — which is what GNU Make itself does for a user IT cannot resolve."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-command-line-file-argument-is-not-canonicalised
                title "Canonicalise a filename switch's argument the way GNU Make's expand_command_line_file does"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-the-built-in-include-search-path-is-missing)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T08:25:37.842112217Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-the-built-in-include-search-path-is-missing, and left out of it because it is a switch-table question rather than a search-path one: it is the same defect at `-f` and `-W` as at `-I`, and fixing it for one switch would leave the other two inconsistent.\n\nGNU Make runs every argument of a `filename`-typed switch through `expand_command_line_file` (main.c:744) as the switches are decoded, and `decode_switches` filters duplicates for every list switch but `-f` on the way in. Ronin's `path_of` (src/make/cli.rs) does neither. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left:\n\n    make -I ./inc          .INCLUDE_DIRS is `inc`              / `./inc`\n    make -I .//inc         `inc`                               / `.//inc`\n    make -I inc -I inc     `inc` once                          / `inc inc`\n    make -f ./lst.mk       MAKEFILE_LIST is `lst.mk`           / `./lst.mk`\n    make -f .//lst.mk      `lst.mk`                            / `.//lst.mk`\n    make -I ''             *** the '-I' option requires a non-empty string argument, exit 2  / accepted silently\n    make -f ''             *** the '-f' option requires a non-empty string argument, exit 2  / `ronin: : No such file or directory`\n    make -W ''             *** the '-W' option requires a non-empty string argument, exit 2  / accepted silently\n\nTHREE RULES, one site. The function fatals on an empty argument, tilde-expands a leading `~` (already done for `-I` in the include-path node, and NOT done for `-f`), and then strips leading `./` sequences repeatedly — `.//foo` is `foo`, not `/foo`, and a name that is nothing but `./` becomes `./`. The duplicate filter is separate and one line earlier: `if (cs->c != 'f')`, so `-I a -I a` keeps one and `-f a -f a` keeps both, which is deliberate and observable (the second `-f` re-reads the file and warns about overriding the recipe).\n\nWHY IT SHOWS UP IN THE SPELLING RATHER THAN THE BEHAVIOUR, mostly: `-I ./inc` searches the same directory either way, and `-f ./x.mk` reads the same file. What differs is what a makefile READS BACK — `$(.INCLUDE_DIRS)` and `$(MAKEFILE_LIST)` — which is branch-worthy, and what an empty argument does, which is the difference between a refusal and a build.\n\nThe `-I ~` half of `expand_command_line_file` is already implemented, in kati's `tilde_expand` beside `construct_include_path`, because the include path is where it is observable. Whoever takes this node should decide whether that moves to the switch table or stays where GNU also does it twice — GNU expands `~` in BOTH places, and read.c's comment says so.\n\nGate it on a file a recipe writes: `$(.INCLUDE_DIRS)` and `$(MAKEFILE_LIST)` under `-I ./dir` and `-f ./file`, a duplicate `-I` beside a duplicate `-f`, and the exit status of an empty argument to each of `-I`, `-f` and `-W`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T08:41:30.691566245Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T09:08:48.287286439Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. Every cell in the filing was re-probed and held, and the survey around it found three more that the filing could not have seen — two of them wrong in the opposite direction from the defect it named.\n\nTHE FILING'S TABLE, re-measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and confirmed exactly: `-I ./inc` was `./inc` here and `inc` there, `-I .//inc` was `.//inc`, `-I inc -I inc` was `inc inc`, `-f ./lst.mk` put `./lst.mk` in `MAKEFILE_LIST`, and `-I ''`, `-W ''` and `-f ''` were accepted, accepted and mis-refused.\n\nWHERE THE GATE IS, and it is TWO gates rather than one. `expand_command_line_file` (main.c:744) canonicalises; `decode_switches` (main.c:3205) refuses an empty argument and filters duplicates, one line each, BEFORE it calls the first. Reading them as one function would have got the duplicate filter wrong, because of the next paragraph.\n\nTHE DUPLICATE FILTER COMPARES THE WORD AS WRITTEN AGAINST NAMES ALREADY CANONICALISED, `streq (sl->list[k], coptarg)` where `sl->list` holds expanded names and `coptarg` does not. That asymmetry is not a reading, it is measured: `make -I ./inc -I inc` searches ONE directory, because the second word matches what the first was stored as; `make -I inc -I ./inc` searches TWO, because `./inc` matches nothing that is there. GNU prints `inc` and `inc inc` respectively and so does this now.\n\nTHE SCOPE IS EVERY SWITCH THAT TAKES A STRING, not the three the filing named, because that is where GNU puts it: `case string: case strlist: case filename:` share the empty test, and the duplicate filter is `if (cs->c != 'f')` over `strlist` and `filename` alike. Nineteen spellings measured refused identically — `-I -f -W -o -C -E` standing alone, and `--include-dir= --file= --makefile= --directory= --what-if= --new-file= --assume-new= --old-file= --assume-old= --eval= --debug= --output-sync= --sync-mutex=` attached — all exit 2 in both tools with no build, where twelve of them exited 0 and built here before. `-O ''` exits 0 in BOTH, and that is not an exception: `-O` takes an optional argument, so getopt never consumes the separate word and the empty string becomes a goal that `handle_non_switch_argument` passes over.\n\nTHE THREE THINGS THE SURVEY FOUND, none of them in the filing.\n\n* `-C` IS A FILENAME SWITCH AND IS FILTERED. `make -C sub -C sub` enters `sub` ONCE in GNU Make and entered `sub/sub` here — a build running in the wrong directory, which is the sharpest cell in the whole node. `-C sub -C ./sub` still enters `sub/sub` in both, by the asymmetry above.\n* `-E` IS FILTERED TOO. `make -E 'N+=x' -E 'N+=x'` evaluates the statement once there and evaluated it twice here, so `N` read `x` against `x x`.\n* THE `-I -` LATCH WAS THE WRONG MODEL, and the duplicate filter is what exposes it. GNU stores `-` IN the list and `construct_include_path` reaches it where it was written, re-reading the list from the start on every call. Two cells measured, both wrong here: `make -I inc -I - -I inc` searches NOTHING — the second `inc` is filtered as a duplicate of the first, so the reset has nothing after it — where a latch that cleared the list would let it back in and did (`dirs=[inc]`); and `make -I - -I inc -I -` searches `inc` alone, because the second dash is itself a duplicate and resets nothing, where clearing on it gave `dirs=[]`. The `no_default_include_dirs` flag added last dispatch is therefore gone from the boundary, and `Flags::include_dirs` is the switch table's own list with the dash in it.\n\nA MAKEFLAGS VALUE ENDING IN WHITESPACE ENDS IN AN EMPTY WORD, which is how the empty-argument gate is reachable from a stream that is not argv at all. `decode_env_switches` (main.c:3380) skips leading blanks once, consumes a blank run between words whole, and terminates the last word wherever the value ran out — so `MAKEFLAGS=-I ` hands `-I` an empty argument and exits 2, while `MAKEFLAGS=-I  -R` hands it `-R` and builds. `makeflags_words` dropped the empty and turned the first into a switch missing its argument, which is a different complaint and, from a makefile's own write, a different outcome. Both measured; both agree now.\n\nWHICH STREAMS DIE OF IT, which is the same question `refuses_an_unknown_switch` already answered and is now named for what it is. GNU has ONE `bad` flag for every way a word can be wrong and one place it answers for them: `if (bad && origin == o_command)`. The environment's `MAKEFLAGS` is decoded at `o_command` (main.c:1603) and dies; a makefile's own write is decoded at `o_file` and only loses the switch. Measured on both sides — `MAKEFLAGS=-I ` in the environment exits 2 with nothing built, and `MAKEFLAGS += --include-dir= -R` written in a makefile leaves the build running and the `-R` behind it still honoured, in both tools. The predicate is now `refuses_a_bad_switch`, and it is the only one.\n\nWHERE IT WENT. `command_line_file` and `already_listed` in src/make/cli.rs, with the empty gate on `Invocation::non_empty` and one recording method per list — `makefile`, `directory`, `include_dir`, `eval_statement`, `debug_spec`, `discarded_argument` — so every spelling of every switch reaches the same three rules and a new switch cannot quietly miss them. `Invocation::bad` is read once per word rather than where it is set, because GNU consumes the whole switch before it gives up on it: abandoning the word where the emptiness was noticed would read the argument as a goal. `~` is expanded through kati's `tilde_expand`, made public and taking the home directory rather than the session, which is GNU's own arrangement — one function, called from the switch table and again from the search path.\n\nFIXTURES, seven, oracle-recorded, each gating an effect and each measured by reverting the whole diff — all seven fail with it off, none by accident:\n\n* `a-command-line-file-name-loses-its-leading-dot-slash` — `-f .//mk.mk`, branching on `$(MAKEFILE_LIST)`; off, it builds the other target and writes `unstripped=[.//mk.mk]`.\n* `a-search-directory-named-twice-is-searched-once` — `-I ./inc -I inc`; off, `repeated=[./inc inc]`.\n* `a-makefile-named-twice-is-read-twice` — `-f ./dup.mk -f dup.mk`, gating `n=[x x] list=[dup.mk dup.mk]`: the one list switch that is NOT filtered, and both spellings the same name.\n* `an-empty-include-directory-refuses-the-run` and `an-empty-what-if-file-refuses-the-run` — `-I ''` and `-W ''`; off, both build. `-f ''` is deliberately not a fixture: it already failed, for the unrelated reason that the empty name would not open, so it gates nothing this node changed.\n* `a-directory-named-on-both-sides-of-a-dash-i-dash-is-searched-neither-time` — `-I inc -I - -I inc`, gating `dirs=[]`; off, the fragment is found.\n* `a-second-dash-i-dash-is-a-duplicate-and-resets-nothing` — `-I - -I inc -I -`, gating `found=[yes] dirs=[inc]`; off, `dirs=[]`.\n\nWHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE. `--jobserver-style=` refuses an empty argument from every stream rather than only the two that die, which is its own node's decision and untouched here. `-W` and `-o` still record nothing but their refusal, because `-W` is an accepted no-op under make-what-if-assumed-new and the duplicate filter over a list nothing reads is unobservable. `--temp-stdin` is GNU's own exception — it is the one `filename` switch `expand_command_line_file` is not applied to — and Ronin's standard-input handling is not that switch.\n\nWHERE, kati 220974aaee349cb0abf0f86c3ee68ef98595c5fd on branch nplan/make-a-command-line-file-argument-is-not-canonicalised.\n\nGATES. fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps --all-features` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 904 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 888 gated cases, seven new, no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1300 rows at 147 unclassified / 0 compiler / 37 interface / 1116 narration, no row moved and the tsv untouched, compiler bucket ZERO; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 15s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream plus every differential band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 714 passed / 0 failed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T09:09:08.786561618Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-makefiles-own-makeflags-does-not-reach-the-include-path
                title "Rebuild the include search path when a makefile writes MAKEFLAGS, as reset_makeflags does"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-the-built-in-include-search-path-is-missing)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T08:26:01.526181034Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-the-built-in-include-search-path-is-missing. That node builds the search path once, where GNU Make rebuilds it every time a makefile writes `MAKEFLAGS`.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make with\n\n    MAKEFLAGS += -I inc2\n    $(info dirs=[$(.INCLUDE_DIRS)])\n    all: ; @printf 'dirs=[%s]\\n' '$(.INCLUDE_DIRS)'\n\nGNU says `inc2 /usr/local/include /usr/include` on BOTH lines — at `$(info)` time, in the middle of the read, and again in the recipe. Ronin says the default path alone on both: the `-I` in the write never reaches the search. An `-I` in an INHERITED `MAKEFLAGS` does arrive in both tools, which is what makes this specifically the makefile's own write.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. `reset_makeflags` (main.c:3123) is called when a makefile assigns the variable, and it does three things in order: `decode_env_switches` over the new value, then `construct_include_path (include_dirs ? include_dirs->list : NULL)`, then `define_makeflags`. Ronin already has the first and the third — the `MakeflagsAssignment` decoder in kati's flags, and `install_suffixes_variable` called a second time for a `-r` that arrives this way — but not the second. So the shape is there and this is the missing call.\n\nTWO THINGS TO WORK OUT before writing it. The decoded switches come back from the front end through `DecodedMakeflags`, which carries `no_builtin_rules` and `no_builtin_variables` but no directory list — an `-I` in a makefile's write has nowhere to land today, so the boundary type has to grow, and the `-I -` latch has to travel with it. And `.INCLUDE_DIRS` is rewritten by `construct_include_path` at `o_default` with `f_simple` then `f_append` per directory, which is a definition rather than an edit: a makefile that assigned `.INCLUDE_DIRS` itself outranks it and keeps its value, and that ladder cell should be measured before the rewrite is written.\n\nGate it on a file a recipe writes, and on an effect rather than the variable: a fragment reachable only through a directory a makefile's own `MAKEFLAGS += -I` names, included after the write."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T09:14:43.56105389Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T09:38:16.64952017Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. The filing's mechanism was right and its two open questions are answered by measurement; the survey around it found a third thing the filing did not name, and that turned out to be the larger half of the work.\n\nTHE FILING'S PROBE re-measured and confirmed exactly: `MAKEFLAGS += -I inc2` gave `inc2 /usr/local/include /usr/include` at `$(info)` time and again in the recipe there, and the default path alone on both lines here.\n\nTHE MISSING CALL, as filed. `reset_makeflags` (main.c:3123) makes three calls when a makefile assigns `MAKEFLAGS` — `decode_env_switches`, `construct_include_path (include_dirs ? include_dirs->list : NULL)`, `define_makeflags` — and kati had the first and the third. The second is now `crate::evaluate::construct_include_path(&mut self.session)` from `normalize_makeflags_assignment` (kati/src-rs/eval.rs), which is where the other two already were.\n\nTHE THING THE FILING DID NOT NAME, and it is why the node is bigger than one call: `-I` NEVER REACHED `MAKEFLAGS` AT ALL. GNU's switch table entry is `{ 'I', filename, &include_dirs, 1, 1, ... }` — env AND toenv — and `define_makeflags` writes one ` -I<dir>` word per list entry, between the letter group and `-j`. Ronin published nothing, so `make -I inc` handed a child an empty `MAKEFLAGS` where GNU hands it ` -Iinc`, and a child of a makefile that wrote its own `-I` learned nothing either. Without this the missing call could not have worked in any case: `decode_makefile_makeflags` reads the previous table back as one of its three streams, so a `-I` that was never published is a `-I` the next write forgets.\n\nMeasured across nine invocations, all agreeing now and none before: ` -Iinc`; ` -Iinc -Iinc2`; `k -Iinc -j4` for `-I inc -j4 -k`, which fixes the POSITION as well as the presence; ` -I-` for the bare reset, which travels as the list entry it is; ` -Inosuchdir`, because the table holds what was written and it is the far end's `construct_include_path` that decides what a search reaches; ` -Ia\\ b`, escaped by `quote_for_env`'s own rule; ` -Iinc -- FOO=bar` with the assignments still behind the `--`; and `MFLAGS` matching in every one.\n\nTHE TIMING, which the filing asked about and is answered rather than argued. An include ABOVE the write stays failed — the attempt was made against the path as it stood and nothing retries it — and one BELOW succeeds. Measured as one makefile with `-include frag.mk` on both sides of the write: `before=[] after=[yes]` in both tools. So the rebuild is a rebuild and not a replay.\n\nTHE LATCH WRITTEN FROM A MAKEFILE, also measured: `MAKEFLAGS += -I -` gives `dirs=[]` immediately, and with a command-line `-I inc2` in front of it gives `mf=[ -Iinc2 -I-] dirs=[]`. It works because make-a-command-line-file-argument-is-not-canonicalised had already moved the dash INTO the list — `construct_include_path` re-reads the list from the start on every call and reaches the dash where it was written, which is the only arrangement in which a second call can honour it at all. That node was worth doing first for this reason and not only the one in the brief.\n\nTHE LADDER CELL THE FILING ASKED TO MEASURE BEFORE WRITING: measured, and it needed no special handling. `construct_include_path` publishes at `o_default` with `f_simple`, so a makefile with `.INCLUDE_DIRS := mine` above the write keeps `mine` through every rebuild — GNU says `mine` and so does this, because `claim_at_default` already refuses a name bound above DEFAULT rank. That is the same test the startup call goes through, which is the point of moving the publish INTO `construct_include_path`: the path and the variable are now rebuilt by one function, so there is no way to rebuild one without the other.\n\nREMAKING, the case the brief flagged as delicate: a makefile that includes a generated fragment, writes `MAKEFLAGS += -I inc2`, and is restarted after the fragment is made. Both tools read twice and print `dirs=[inc2 ...] found=[yes]` on both passes, with `gen=[]` then `gen=[made]`. Nothing about the restart needed writing — the decoder runs again on the second read and the path is rebuilt from the list, which is what GNU does too.\n\nFOUR MORE CELLS, all agreeing: the same write twice does not grow the list (the switch table's duplicate filter settles the three-stream replay); `MAKEFLAGS :=` does NOT take a command-line `-I` away, because the protected stream restores it; `$(eval MAKEFLAGS += -I inc2)` works, being the same assignment; and `MAKEFLAGS += -I inc2 -I inc` names two directories in the order written.\n\nFIXTURES, three, oracle-recorded, each gating an effect and each measured by reverting the whole diff — all three fail with it off:\n\n* `a-makefiles-own-makeflags-reaches-the-include-path` — the fragment lives only in the directory the write names, and the makefile branches on finding it; gates `before=[] after=[yes] dirs=[inc2]`, so the timing is in the same assertion. Off, it builds the other target with `unreached=[/usr/local/include /usr/include]`.\n* `a-makeflags-write-can-turn-the-built-in-include-path-off` — `-I inc` on the command line and `MAKEFLAGS += -I -` in the makefile; gates `dirs=[]`. Off, `found=[yes] dirs=[inc ...]`.\n* `a-makeflags-write-carries-the-include-path-to-a-child` — the recipe runs `$(MAKE) -f child.mk` and the CHILD writes the file; gates `child found=[yes] named=[inc2]`. Off, `child found=[] named=[/usr/local/include]`.\n\nINVENTORY MOVED AND AUDITED, twelve rows gone and three reclassified, all in the improving direction. Counts 1300 → 1288 rows, unclassified 147 → 144, interface 37 → 25, narration 1116 → 1119, compiler ZERO. The suite's own count fell from 1302 failing to 1290.\n\n* TWELVE ROWS REMOVED, every one `interface/…+makeflags-content`: `variables/MAKEFLAGS.diff.36 .40 .44 .48 .64 .68 .72 .76 .80 .81 .82 .83`. That family is \"a Makefile read MAKEFLAGS and saw different switches\", and the switch it was not seeing was `-I`. Reproduced by hand on upstream's own case — `MAKEFLAGS += -Ibar` under `-I- -Ifoo` — where both tools now print `MAKEFLAGS= -I- -Ifoo -Ibar` for the makefile-visible value AND for the exported one.\n* `variables/MAKEFLAGS.diff.3` — unclassified/ninja-progress+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress. The `evaluation` residue was the same disagreement; only the progress line differs now.\n* `options/dash-I.diff.3` and `.7` — unclassified/product-name+directory-announce+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress+directory-announce. These are upstream's own \"Check that -I is passed down through MAKEFLAGS\" and \"Check that -I- is passed down through MAKEFLAGS\". Reproduced both by hand: the child now finds `ifile.mk` and prints the same line, where before it refused with a Ronin-named diagnostic. The tsv is re-recorded with `--update`.\n\nONE DEFECT FOUND AND FILED, not fixed: make-makeflags-holds-its-switches-as-literal-text. `-I` is the first switch value Ronin inlines into `MAKEFLAGS` that can carry a `$`, and it exposes that Ronin stores the switch text as a literal where GNU stores parsed text. `make -I 'a$b'` gives `$(MAKEFLAGS)` = ` -Ia$b` there and ` -Ia$$b` here, while `$(value MAKEFLAGS)` is ` -Ia$$b` in BOTH — so what is stored is right and what an expansion does to it is not. The round trip is unaffected and Ronin's is the better answer of the two: GNU's own child ends up with `-Ia` and loses the directory, because the value is expanded once more on the way in.\n\nWHERE, kati 925989fe68b1fc11481f46d5cc15729bbdc4d25e on branch nplan/make-a-makefiles-own-makeflags-does-not-reach-the-include-path. Ronin side: the `-I` words in `compiler_flag_variables` and the list on `DecodedMakeflags`, both in src/make/cli/interface.rs.\n\nGATES. fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps --all-features` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; workspace 904 passed / 0 failed; make_port 4/4 over 891 gated cases, three new, no existing recording rewritten; conformance 334 identical / 53 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 279 graphs / 76 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1288 rows at 144 unclassified / 0 compiler / 25 interface / 1119 narration, fifteen rows audited above, tsv re-recorded, compiler bucket ZERO; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 28s — reported without a timing claim, because another session was building on this host throughout; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream plus every differential band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 714 passed / 0 failed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T09:38:53.840166284Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-makeflags-holds-its-switches-as-literal-text
                title "Store MAKEFLAGS as the expression GNU Make stores, so a `$` in a switch value halves on expansion"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-makefiles-own-makeflags-does-not-reach-the-include-path)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T09:38:44.931826284Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-a-makefiles-own-makeflags-does-not-reach-the-include-path, and left out of it because it is a question about how `MAKEFLAGS` is STORED rather than about what goes into it.\n\n`-I` is the first switch value Ronin writes inline into `MAKEFLAGS` that can carry a `$`, and publishing it exposed the difference. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left:\n\n    make -I 'a$b'   $(MAKEFLAGS)        ` -Ia$b`    / ` -Ia$$b`\n                    $(value MAKEFLAGS)  ` -Ia$$b`   / ` -Ia$$b`\n                    $(flavor MAKEFLAGS) recursive   / recursive\n\nSo WHAT IS STORED IS RIGHT and what an expansion does to it is not. GNU's `define_makeflags` runs each switch argument through `quote_for_env`, which doubles a `$` precisely so that the RECURSIVE variable halves it again when read — `define_variable_cname (\"MAKEFLAGS\", flagstring, o_env, 1)`, the trailing 1 being the recursive flag. Ronin stores the switch text as `Value::Literal` (`makeflags_value`, kati/src-rs/eval.rs), which is flagged recursive but expands to itself, so the doubling survives into the reading.\n\nWHY IT DID NOT SHOW BEFORE. No switch value Ronin published inline could contain a `$`. `--debug=b$x` publishes verbatim without the doubling, so it prints the same as GNU by a second mistake cancelling the first; command-line assignments and `--eval` fragments both print `$$` in BOTH tools, because GNU keeps them behind `$(-*-command-variables-*-)` and `$(-*-eval-flags-*-)` and a reference's value is not expanded a second time. That one-level shielding is the whole shape: GNU's `MAKEFLAGS` is parsed text holding TWO variable references, and Ronin's is one literal with the references' contents inlined.\n\nRONIN'S ANSWER IS THE BETTER ONE OF THE TWO, which is why this is a node and not a patch. Measured across a recursion: `make -I 'a$b'` then `$(MAKE) -f child.mk`. GNU's child reports `dirs=[a ...]` and `mf=[ -Ia ...]` — it LOST the `$b`, because the exported value was already halved to `-Ia$b` and the child's `decode_env_switches` expands `$(MAKEFLAGS)` once more, reading `$b` as an undefined variable. Ronin's child reports `dirs=[a$b ...]` and keeps the directory. Whoever takes this has to decide whether matching GNU here means reproducing that loss, and the honest answer may be that only `$(MAKEFLAGS)`'s own expansion should change while the descent keeps working.\n\nWHAT IT WOULD TAKE. `makeflags_value` builds a `Value::Literal` for the switch prefix and a `Value::List` with a `SymRef` for `MAKEOVERRIDES`. Matching GNU means parsing the switch prefix as an expression — which needs `&mut Session` where the function has none — and moving the `--eval` fragments out of the inline text and behind a reference of their own, so their `$$` keeps the shielding GNU gives it. Doing only the first half would fix `-I` and break the fragments.\n\nGate it on a file a recipe writes: `$(MAKEFLAGS)` and `$(value MAKEFLAGS)` under `-I 'a$b'`, an `--eval` fragment carrying a `$` beside it so the shielding is asserted rather than assumed, and whatever the recursion cell is decided to be."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T22:37:07.086757028Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T23:08:28.703827233Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "DECISION: (b), INTENTIONAL DIVERGENCE — `$(MAKEFLAGS)` reads back the text it stores, and GNU Make 4.4.1's halving is not reproduced. The filing predicted this and the probe confirmed it, but the grounds are stronger and more specific than the filing had, and one third of what looked like the same question turned out to be a plain defect that is now fixed.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, both sides of it, read off the source rather than inferred. GNU Make's `define_makeflags` (reference/gnumake/src/main.c) runs ONE `quote_for_env` over every switch argument — `p = quote_for_env (p, flags->arg)`, with no question about which switch it belongs to — and then binds the result RECURSIVELY: `define_variable_cname (MAKEFLAGS_NAME, flagstring, env_overrides ? o_env_override : o_file, 1)`. So reading the variable expands it, and the doubling the quoting added is halved again. Ronin quotes identically and holds the result as a `Value::Literal`, which expands to itself.\n\nWHY NOT (a). Because GNU Make expands `MAKEFLAGS` TWICE on the way to a child and the second expansion destroys the switch. `target_environment` (variable.c) carries an explicit exception for this one name — \"If it came from the environment, it should go back into the environment unchanged... except MAKEFLAGS\" — so the child's environment gets the halved ` -Ia$b`; the child's `decode_env_switches` (main.c) then builds the text `$(MAKEFLAGS)` and calls `variable_expand` on it before splitting into words, reads `$b` as an undefined variable, and the directory arrives as `-Ia`. Measured, with the directory present and holding an includable fragment:\n\n                            GNU Make 4.4.1                       Ronin\n  child $(MAKEFLAGS)        ` -Ia --no-print-directory`          ` -Ia$$b --no-print-directory`\n  child $(.INCLUDE_DIRS)    `/usr/local/include /usr/include`    `a$b /usr/local/include /usr/include`\n  child found the fragment  no                                   yes\n\n`--debug=b$x` loses its tail the same way and reaches the child as `--debug=b`. Conforming on the parent's spelling means reproducing that, and reproducing a silent data loss is the one thing this campaign's architecture line forbids.\n\nWHY NOT (c), the hybrid that occurred to me and is not in the filing — halve on `$(MAKEFLAGS)`, keep the descent lossless by exporting and decoding the STORED text. It conforms on every cell except the child's, costs the same work as (a), and is rejected on coherence rather than effort: under it, `$(MAKEFLAGS)` and what a child receives are two different strings, and a makefile reading the variable to work out what its child was told would be misled. Ronin's model is one string with one meaning — what `$(MAKEFLAGS)` reads is what descends is what the child's decoder unquotes, and the unquoting is the exact inverse of the quoting that wrote it. (c) also breaks on a value carrying a real `$$`: quoting `a$$b` gives `a$$$$b`, expanding gives `a$$b`, and unquoting that gives `a$b`. Exporting the expansion is lossy in general and only looks lossless on the single-dollar case.\n\nTHE DISPATCH'S OWN CRITERION SELECTS (b): \"conform to GNU if the probe shows real-world Makefiles depend on GNU's spelling\". Nothing does. The divergence needs a LITERAL `$` inside a published switch value, and there is none in the 932-case build-intent corpus, the 387-case vendored kati corpus, GNU Make's own test suite at d66a65a, or the Makefiles of vim 9.2, zsh 5.9.2, abseil or Ninja. Searched for, not assumed.\n\nWHAT WAS A DEFECT AND IS NOW FIXED, and it is the reason this node carries code at all. `--debug` is the second switch whose argument is arbitrary text, and Ronin published it RAW where GNU Make quotes it. That was three wrong answers wearing one right one:\n\n    make '--debug=b\\x'      GNU / Ronin was              is\n      $(MAKEFLAGS)          ` --debug=b\\\\x` / ` --debug=b\\x`      agrees\n      $(value MAKEFLAGS)    ` --debug=b\\\\x` / ` --debug=b\\x`      agrees\n      $(MFLAGS)             `--debug=b\\\\x`  / `--debug=b\\x`       agrees\n\nThe backslash cell has nothing to do with `$` at all — it is the round trip the quoting exists for, and a `--debug` spec holding a backslash or a blank was being handed to a child as a word that would split or re-quote differently. One `quote_for_makeflags` in `compiler_flag_variables` (src/make/cli/interface.rs) fixes all three, and moves the `$` cell from an accidental agreement to the recorded divergence, which is the right trade: what is STORED is now GNU Make's for every switch kind, and the one thing Ronin does differently is what READING does.\n\n    make '--debug=b$x'      GNU / Ronin was              is\n      $(MAKEFLAGS)          ` --debug=b$x` / ` --debug=b$x`       ` --debug=b$$x`   <- the recorded divergence\n      $(value MAKEFLAGS)    ` --debug=b$$x` / ` --debug=b$x`      agrees\n      $(MFLAGS)             `--debug=b$x` / `--debug=b`           agrees\n\nThat `$(MFLAGS)` cell is worth naming because it was NOT there this morning. MFLAGS reaches kati through the invocation environment (`record_invocation`, src/make/cli.rs), so make-an-environment-variable-is-a-recursive-one — landed an hour before this — started expanding it, exactly as GNU Make does. For `-I`, already quoted, that moved MFLAGS INTO agreement; for `--debug`, published raw, it moved it out and ate the `$x`. Measured by rebuilding at the previous commit rather than reasoned about. The quoting is what makes that expansion correct for both.\n\nTHE PROBE, 33 shapes against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. GNU on the left.\n\n  WHICH SWITCHES CAN CARRY A `$` INTO MAKEFLAGS AT ALL\n    -I 'a$b'                published inline, quoted        the divergent one\n    --debug=b$x             published inline, was raw       fixed here\n    --eval='X := a$b'       behind $(-*-eval-flags-*-)      see below\n    V=a$b                   behind $(MAKEOVERRIDES)         agrees, both sides\n    -W 'a$b', -o 'a$b', --what-if 'a$b', -C .   not published at all, in either tool\n    -f, -j, -l, -O, --shuffle                   numeric, fixed-vocabulary, or unpublished\n  So the surface is exactly two switches, and one of them was the defect.\n\n  THE DIVERGENT CELLS, after the fix\n    -I 'a$b'        $(MAKEFLAGS)        ` -Ia$b`  / ` -Ia$$b`\n                    $(value MAKEFLAGS)  ` -Ia$$b` / same          agrees\n                    $(MFLAGS)           `-Ia$b`   / same          agrees\n    two -I switches, both with dollars                            same shape, both words\n    a makefile's own `MAKEFLAGS += -Ia$$b`      ` -Ia$b` / ` -Ia$$b`\n    `MAKEFLAGS := $(MAKEFLAGS)` under -I 'a$b'  ` -Ia$b` / ` -Ia$$b`, flavour `recursive` in both\n    the child of an -I 'a$b' parent             ` -Ia` / ` -Ia$$b`   <- GNU lost it\n\n  CELLS THAT AGREE AND WOULD HAVE BROKEN UNDER (a), which is the trap the filing named\n    --eval='X := a$b'   $(MAKEFLAGS)   ` --eval=X\\ :=\\ a$$b`  / same\n      GNU's fragment is shielded by exactly ONE level: `$(-*-eval-flags-*-)` names a\n      SIMPLE variable, so substituting it does not halve the fragment's `$$`. A literal\n      gives the same one level. Parsing the switch prefix without moving the fragments\n      behind a reference of their own would have halved them and broken this row.\n    V=a$b               $(MAKEFLAGS)   ` -- V=a$$b` / same\n                        $(MAKEOVERRIDES) `V=a$$b` / same\n                        $(value MAKEOVERRIDES) `${-*-command-variables-*-}` / same\n    -I 'a\\b' and -I 'a b'   all three observables agree, before and after\n\n  CONTROLS\n    --debug=bx, --debug=b, --debug=basic    agree, before and after\n    -l 2, -k, -rR, --no-print-directory     agree\n\nSTILL DIVERGENT AND NOT PART OF THIS DECISION: `$(value MAKEFLAGS)` for an `--eval` fragment reads ` $(-*-eval-flags-*-)` in GNU Make and the inlined text here, and `$(-*-eval-flags-*-)` is `automatic` there and undefined here. That is a variable Ronin does not have rather than a spelling it chose, and GNU Make's own suite asks about it (tests/scripts/options/warn-undefined-variables lists it among the names `--warn-undefined-variables` must not warn about). Filed as make-makeflags-keeps-its-eval-fragments-behind-a-reference, deps on this node because this node decided the switch prefix stays a literal.\n\nRECORDED, three ways, which is the pattern:\n* This note.\n* `docs/make-oracle-divergences.md` gains a section — \"Where Ronin deliberately disagrees with the oracle\" — carrying both tables above and what was searched before choosing. The document was four builds of GNU Make against each other; it is now that plus the one disagreement Ronin chose.\n* Two `divergence` sidecars in the corpus, which make the gate work in BOTH directions: `tests/make_port.rs` fails on an unrecorded divergence and also fails on a recorded one that has started to agree. So a later change that quietly conformed either cell would stop the gate and have to reopen this decision rather than route around it.\n\nFIXTURES, three, oracle-recorded:\n* `a-switch-value-is-stored-in-makeflags-as-gnu-make-quotes-it` — GATES. `-I 'a$b'` and `--debug=b\\x$y` together, read through `$(value MAKEFLAGS)` and `$(MFLAGS)`. Teeth measured by parking the quoting: it fails on both, `val` keeping the raw backslash and single dollar and `mfl` truncating at the `$` to `--debug=b\\x`.\n* `makeflags-hands-back-the-switch-text-it-stores` — RECORDS the divergence. GNU left ` -Ia$b --debug=b$x`; Ronin answers ` -Ia$$b --debug=b$$x`.\n* `a-child-keeps-a-switch-value-a-dollar-was-written-in` — RECORDS what the divergence buys. GNU's child reports `-Ia`; Ronin's reports `-Ia$b`. This is the case that makes the decision reviewable: it is the reason the one above is not repaired.\n\nPlus `src/make/cli/interface_tests.rs::a_switch_argument_is_quoted_on_its_way_into_the_table`, which pins the quoting at the function and the unquoting at the decoder in one place, and fails with the change parked (` --debug=b\\x$y` against ` --debug=b\\\\x$$y`)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T23:08:42.068481762Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "WHERE: Ronin only — one `quote_for_makeflags` in `compiler_flag_variables` (src/make/cli/interface.rs), a unit test beside it, three corpus cases with two `divergence` sidecars, and the new section in docs/make-oracle-divergences.md. No kati change: MAKEFLAGS's storage is composed on the Ronin side and kati only holds what it is handed, so the submodule pin does not move for this node.\n\nGATES, run serially on a quiet host. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; clippy pedantic+nursery over ronin lib+bins --no-deps clean and plain `-D warnings` over kati --all-targets clean; workspace 959 passed / 0 failed (958 plus the one new unit case); `--no-default-features` 753 / 0; make_port 4/4 over 931 gated cases, with the two recorded divergences live in both directions and no existing recording rewritten; conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved, 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact and nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 1 both-refused / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 at 134 unclassified / 0 compiler / 25 interface / 1126 narration, tsv byte-identical with no drift line and no row moved, compiler bucket ZERO; ninja conformance 425 upstream across 33 suites plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 17s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 30s at -j8.\n\nThe inventory's `makeflags-content` family — 23 interface observations where a Makefile read MAKEFLAGS and saw different switches — did not move. Nothing in GNU Make's suite puts a `$` in a switch value, which is the same finding the decision rests on, arrived at from the other side."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T23:08:45.814542412Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T12:30:33.929844723Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "RATIFIED BY THE OPERATOR. This node's divergence is section 2 of docs/make-oracle-divergences.md, and it had carried the delivering dispatch's authority only — \"no operator ruling\", grounded in the argument that reproducing a data loss is not compatibility of build intent.\n\nOPERATOR DECISION, Brendan, 2026-08-24, on that section, verbatim:\n\n    \"We keep our behaviour.\"\n\nWhat he was ratifying, re-measured with `make -I 'a$b'` on 2026-08-24: GNU Make's $(MAKEFLAGS) reads back ` -Ia$b` where Ronin reads back ` -Ia$$b`; $(value MAKEFLAGS) and $(MFLAGS) agree in both tools. What is stored agrees; only reading $(MAKEFLAGS) differs, and only where a switch value carries a literal `$`, which is `-I` and `--debug`. GNU's own consequence is a data loss on the way to a child: it expands MAKEFLAGS a second time in the child's decode_switches, reads `$b` as an undefined variable, and the directory arrives as `-Ia`. Ronin passes the switch whole.\n\nThe two `divergence` sidecars that gate it are unchanged and still name this node: tests/make/makeflags-hands-back-the-switch-text-it-stores and tests/make/a-child-keeps-a-switch-value-a-dollar-was-written-in. Their text already says \"Decided on make-makeflags-holds-its-switches-as-literal-text and recorded in docs/make-oracle-divergences.md\"; that record now carries the operator's words and the date beside the dispatch's reasoning."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-ifeq-closing-paren-need-not-end-the-line
                title "Find an ifeq's closing paren by counting rather than by reading the last byte"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-endif-trailing-text-is-a-warning)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T10:27:02.23827157Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by the probe for make-endif-trailing-text-is-a-warning, which swept the four directives that can carry trailing text. `else`, `endef` and `define` already agree; `endif` was that node; this is the fourth cell and it is a different mechanism, so it is a node of its own.\n\n    ifeq (a,a) junk\n    all: ; @echo hi\n    endif\n\n    GNU 4.4.1  `Makefile:1: extraneous text after 'ifeq' directive`, then builds, rc 0\n    Ronin      `ronin: Makefile:1: invalid syntax in conditional.`, rc 2\n\nThe cause is one test. kati's `parse_ifeq` (kati/src-rs/parser.rs, and `ParseIfeq` in kati/src/parser.cc) decides it is looking at the parenthesised form with\n\n    line.first() == Some(&b'(') && line.last() == Some(&b')')\n\nso a line whose last byte is not `)` is not the paren form at all, and it falls through to the quoted form, finds no quote, and dies with `invalid syntax in conditional.` — GNU's other refusal, raised for the wrong reason.\n\nGNU never reads the last byte. `conditional_line` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c) takes `termin = *line == '(' ? ',' : *line`, walks forward counting nested parens for the first string, then walks forward again for the second with `termin = ')'`, and whatever is left after that closing paren reaches the shared\n\n    NEXT_TOKEN (line);\n    if (*line != '\\0')\n      EXTRATEXT ();\n\nwhich is `error` — it prints and the read carries on. Exactly the severity the `endif` node settled, reached by a scan rather than by a byte test.\n\nBoth implementations have the same test, so both want the same change: find the matching `)` by counting from the front, take the pair from between the parens, and warn about whatever follows instead of refusing over it. The `warn_loc!` for `extraneous text after 'ifeq' directive` is already there — it is simply unreachable for this shape, because the shape never gets that far.\n\nTwo things to settle while doing it, both readable from the source above: GNU counts nesting, so `ifeq ($(f a,b),x) junk` has to find the right paren rather than the first one; and the second string's scan is `count <= 0` on `)` rather than a plain match, which is the same counting on the other side.\n\nAcceptance: an oracle-recorded fixture beside tests/make/text-after-an-endif-does-not-end-the-read, carrying both a plain `ifeq (a,a) junk` and one whose left-hand side holds a nested call, building where GNU builds."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T12:34:13.87444687Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T12:57:37.38539735Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered in kati b6fcb02 (pinned below). The filing was right about the cause and understated the consequence: reading the close by counting is not only about which diagnostic comes out, it decides WHAT IS COMPARED, and three shapes answered the other way round before.\n\nTHE PROBE, every cell measured against the 4.4.1 oracle before anything moved, GNU on the left:\n\n  `ifeq (a,a) junk`          warns, builds, rc 0        Ronin refused, rc 2      THIS NODE\n  `ifneq (a,b) junk`         warns `'ifneq'`, rc 0      Ronin refused, rc 2      and kati's warning said `'ifeq'`\n  `ifeq ((a),(a)) x`         warns, builds, rc 0        Ronin refused, rc 2\n  `ifeq ($(subst a,b,aaa),bbb) junk`  warns, rc 0       Ronin refused, rc 2\n  `ifeq (a,a)<TAB>junk`      warns, builds, rc 0        Ronin refused, rc 2\n  `ifeq (a,a);`              warns, builds, rc 0        Ronin refused, rc 2\n  `ifeq (a,a) $(info SIDE)`  warns, does NOT expand it  Ronin `unterminated call to function 'info'`\n  `ifeq \"a\" \"a\" junk`        warns, builds, rc 0        already agreed, both spellings\n  `ifeq 'a' 'a' junk`        warns, builds, rc 0        already agreed\n  `ifeq (a,a) # comment`     silent, builds             already agreed -- the comment is stripped before the directive is read\n  `ifeq (a,a junk`           FATAL `invalid syntax in conditional.`, rc 2   already agreed\n\nand the four that were not about the diagnostic at all:\n\n  `ifeq (a(b,c),a(b,c))`     TAKEN                      Ronin NOT taken\n  `ifeq (a,a),c)`            warns, TAKEN               Ronin NOT taken\n  `ifeq (a,a))`              warns, TAKEN               Ronin NOT taken\n  `ifeq (a,(a)`              FATAL, rc 2                Ronin built, rc 0\n\nTHE MECHANISM. kati's `parse_ifeq` decided it was looking at the parenthesised form with `line.first() == Some(&b'(') && line.last() == Some(&b')')`, so a line whose last byte was not `)` was not that form: it fell through to the quoted form, found no quote, and refused. GNU's `conditional_line` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c) never reads the last byte. `termin = *line == '(' ? ',' : *line` picks the form off the OPENING byte, then two forward scans find the close -- the first string ends at a comma at paren depth zero, the second at the paren that balances the opening one (`)` breaks at `count <= 0` and decrements otherwise) -- and whatever follows reaches the shared `NEXT_TOKEN (line); if (*line != '\\0') EXTRATEXT ();`, which is `error`: it prints and the read carries on.\n\n`split_ifeq_condition` (kati/src-rs/parser.rs) is that scan, returning where the two compared strings sit and where the rest begins; `SplitIfEqCond` in kati/src/parser.cc is the same one, so the C++ and the Rust port still agree with each other and now both agree with Make. The blank handling is GNU's and is asymmetric on purpose: blanks before the comma are stripped off the first string, blanks before the second string are skipped, and blanks after it are kept -- probed on `ifeq (  a  ,a)` (not equal) against `ifeq (a,  a)` (equal), and both tools answer alike. The trailing text is discarded WITHOUT being expanded, which is why `ifeq (a,a) $(info SIDE)` prints nothing extra.\n\nThe count is GNU's raw paren count rather than an expression-aware one, and that is deliberate: it is what makes `ifeq (a(b,c),...)` work, and it also reproduces GNU's blind spot for braces. `ifeq (${subst a,b,aaa},bbb)` splits at the comma inside the braces in both tools and both die `unterminated call to function 'subst': missing '}'`; so does the pathological `ifeq ($(subst (,),(a)),)a))`, where both now say `insufficient number of arguments (2) to function 'subst'`. Those two were Ronin-builds/GNU-refuses before.\n\nTEETH, three fixtures, each recorded from the oracle and each biting on its own mechanism. Measured by restoring the old `parse_ifeq`: `3 of 902 ported cases diverge from GNU Make's build intent`.\n\n  tests/make/text-after-an-ifeq-does-not-end-the-read      `build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`\n      the four trailing-text shapes -- plain, a nested call, nested parens on both sides, and the `ifneq` spelling\n  tests/make/an-ifeq-closes-where-its-parens-balance       `'comma' holds \"uncounted\\n\" where GNU Make left \"counted\\n\"`\n      the three that flip the ANSWER, each written with an `else` so the wrong branch is a wrong file rather than a failure\n  tests/make/an-ifeq-whose-parens-never-balance-is-refused `build succeeded where GNU Make's build failed`\n      `ifeq (a,(a)`, whose `(a)` balances itself and leaves the condition open\n\nROW MOVEMENT. Conformance went 335/52 to 336/51 normalised, raw unmoved at 286: `err_invalid_ifeq5.mk#test` -- `ifeq (foo, bar) XXX` under the corpus's own fix-me annotation -- now matches after normalisation, and the gate said so on its own last line. Its row is deleted from tests/make_corpus_inventory.tsv, taking one row off `evaluation` and emptying nothing; the pinned count in tests/make_corpus_inventory.rs moves 52 to 51 and `recorded` 3 to 2. Equivalence moved with it, 279 graphs compared to 280 and `make rejected` 76 to 75, because a makefile that used to be refused now compiles; disagreements stayed 0.\n\nFOUND AND NOT FIXED, filed as make-a-conditional-in-an-ignored-branch-is-not-read. GNU decides it is ignoring BEFORE it parses an `if...` condition (`conditional_line`'s `for (i = 0; i < o; ++i) if (conditionals->ignoring[i]) { ignoring[o] = 1; return 1; }` sits above the ifdef/ifeq arms), so a condition inside a branch that is not being taken is never read at all. kati builds an AST at read time and parses it regardless. Measured: `ifeq (a,a junk` nested inside a false `ifeq` is silent and rc 0 under GNU and rc 2 under Ronin -- a build-intent divergence, not only a diagnostic one -- and after this node `ifeq (a,a) junk` in the same place warns where GNU says nothing. `endif junk` and `else junk` are NOT affected: their arms sit above the ignoring check, so both tools warn there, and that agreement is already gated.\n\nGATES: workspace 909/0; conformance 336/51 normalised and 286 raw with the one row removed; equivalence 297/280/75 and 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 rows at 134/0/25/1126, compiler at zero, not one row moved; make_port 4/4 over 902 with no recording rewritten; ninja conformance 425/425; scripts/check-make-projects.sh built vim in 31s and zsh in 53s at -j8; cargo test --workspace --no-default-features 716/0; fmt and both Clippy passes clean."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T12:57:47.39369638Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-question-mode-runs-a-plus-line
                title "Run a recipe's + lines while question mode is asking"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-plus-line-runs-under-pretending)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T11:27:37.914558739Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Split out of make-plus-line-runs-under-pretending, which delivered the marker and `-t` and left this cell measured rather than fixed. It is a node of its own because it needs the question path to be able to start a process, which it cannot today, and that is a decision about what `-q` is rather than a gap in the plumbing.\n\nWHAT GNU DOES, measured against the 4.4.1 oracle. `start_job_command` (job.c) reads:\n\n    if (question_flag && !(flags & COMMANDS_RECURSE))\n      { child->file->update_status = us_question; notice_finished_file (child->file); return; }\n\nso a `+` line is not an answer — it RUNS, and the walk goes on to the next line. The answer comes from the first line that is not `+`.\n\n    target: ; +@echo PLUS > c.txt\n      GNU    rc 0, c.txt written — every line ran, nothing is left to do\n      Ronin  rc 1, nothing written\n\n    target:\n    \t+@echo PLUS > c.txt\n    \t@echo A > a.txt\n      GNU    rc 1, c.txt written — the `+` line ran, the ordinary one answered\n      Ronin  rc 1, nothing written\n\n    .ONESHELL: with `+` on the first line\n      GNU    rc 0, the whole script ran\n      Ronin  rc 1, nothing written\n\nEvery other `-q` cell already agrees, including the one that matters most: `+$(MAKE) -C sub` under `-q` answers 1 in both, because Ronin composed the child into the graph and interrogated its edges rather than starting it. That is the same reason `-n` and `-t` are right for a recursive `+` line, and it is why this is only ever about a `+` line that is NOT Make.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT PLUMBING. `Builder::interrogate` walks `reportable_work_edges`, asks the front end to bind each one, and answers on whether the binding came to anything. It has no process supervisor and starts nothing; the expansion it performs is deliberate (`$(shell)` in an interrogated recipe runs, as GNU's does), but a `+` line is a whole process. Making `-q` able to start one means either giving the question path a supervisor or running the plan in a mode where only marked steps launch and the first unmarked one stops the walk with the answer — and the second is closer to what GNU does, since GNU answers from inside its ordinary update.\n\nThe marker itself is already there and needs nothing: `LateStep::runs_while_pretending`, `PreparedStep`, and `Pretending`, which today has `EveryStep` for `-n` and `AllButRunning` for `-t`. A `-q` that could launch would ask the same question of the same field.\n\nAcceptance: an oracle-recorded fixture beside tests/make/a-touch-runs-a-plus-line-instead-of-touching carrying the three shapes above, and the exit status gated — the corpus format records success or failure rather than the code, so `-q`'s 0-versus-1 wants a regression test beside it as make-bad-option-argument-in-a-makefiles-makeflags did."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T13:00:48.725990464Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T13:39:03.441748459Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "IMPLEMENTED, and the architecture turned out not to be in the way. The filing said the question path \"cannot start a process\" and called that a decision about what `-q` is; the probe says it is a decision the campaign already made twice — `-t` and `-B`/`-W` — and this cell falls on the same side. `-q`'s contract is its exit status AND the filesystem effects of lines the Makefile explicitly marked always-run, which is build intent, so it is implemented.\n\nTHE PROBE, every cell measured against the 4.4.1 oracle, GNU on the left. The three the filing carried, and then the ones it did not.\n\n  `+` only                              rc 0, c.txt written    Ronin rc 1, nothing\n  `+` then an ordinary line             rc 1, c.txt written    Ronin rc 1, nothing\n  ordinary line then `+`                rc 1, nothing          agreed\n  `.ONESHELL` with `+` on line one      rc 0, whole script ran Ronin rc 1, nothing\n  `.ONESHELL` with `+` on a later line  rc 1, nothing          agreed\n\nTHE EXIT STATUS OF THE LINE IT RAN, which the filing did not ask about and which decides `-q`'s own status:\n\n  `+` line exits 0                      rc 0                   recipe carries on\n  `+` line exits 1                      rc 1, SILENT           recipe stops there; a following `+` line does not run\n  `+` line exits 2                      rc 2, `*** [Makefile:2: all] Error 2`\n  `+` line exits 7                      rc 2, `Error 7`\n  `-+` line exits 1                     rc 0, both files       forgiven, `Error 1 (ignored)`\n\nThat middle row is `reap_children` (job.c:954): `else if (exit_sig == 0 && exit_code == 1 && question_flag && c->recursive) child_failed = MAKE_TROUBLE;` — a status of exactly one from a line run while questioning IS the question's answer rather than a failure, and `reap_children` then leaves the file `us_question` instead of `us_failed`. Two or more is an ordinary lost build and 2 outranks the question.\n\nWHERE THE QUESTION IS ASKED, which is the half that made this more than plumbing:\n\n  `all: dep` with `+` on `all`, ordinary on `dep`      GNU rc 1, NOTHING written\n  the same under `-k`                                  GNU rc 1, NOTHING written\n  `all: dep` with ordinary on `all`, `+` on `dep`      GNU rc 1, d.txt written\n  a three-deep chain, all marked                       GNU rc 0, all three written\n  the same chain with the BASE unmarked                GNU rc 1, nothing\n  a marked branch BESIDE an answered one, under `-k`   GNU rc 1, only the beside one\n\nSo the walk is depth-first and an answer is a barrier: `update_file_1` does not start a recipe whose dependencies did not come back updated, so a marked line ABOVE an answer never runs, `-k` or not. What `-k` reaches is the branches beside it — `update_goal_chain`'s `stop = (question_flag && !keep_going_flag && !rebuilding_makefiles)` (remake.c:206).\n\nAn earlier build of this node walked `reportable_work_edges`, which is graph order, and got two of those six wrong in opposite directions — running a marked line above an answer, and failing to run one below. Recorded here because the fixture that catches it is in the corpus now and the mistake is easy to make again.\n\nTHE REST OF THE MATRIX, all measured and all agreeing after the fix: `-q -t` (the question outranks the touch, both orders, and nothing is touched), `-q -B`, `-q -W`, `-q -s`, `-q -j2`, `.PHONY` with a `+` line, a double-colon target with one marked entry each, a recipe that expands to nothing, `$(shell)` inside an interrogated recipe, a goal with no rule (rc 2 in both), a target already current (nothing runs, rc 0 in both), and a target stale against its prerequisite.\n\nTWO CELLS THE FILING HAD NOT SEEN, both now right:\n\n  `+$(MAKE) -C sub child` where the CHILD's recipe is all `+` lines\n      GNU rc 0 and the child's file written, because the child it started was itself under `-q` and answered 0.\n      Ronin composes the child into the graph, so the question walks the child's own edges and finds the marker\n      there. Same answer, same file, no child process. It was rc 1 and nothing before.\n  `@$(MAKE) --version > v.txt`, no `+` at all\n      GNU classifies a line REFERENCING `$(MAKE)` as recursive too, so it runs and answers 0. kati's\n      `references_make` already sets `recursive_line` for it, so this came right for free — the 2026-08-08\n      handoff note saying kati marks only a leading `+` is stale.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. `Builder::interrogate` (src/build/command.rs) was a read-only sweep of `reportable_work_edges` that answered at the first edge that bound to anything. It is now the plan's own walk: `prepare_queue`, then `find_work`/`edge_finished` for as long as work becomes ready. An edge that answers is finished as `EdgeResult::Failed`, which is how the answer becomes a barrier — `abandon_dependents` takes its dependents out of the plan exactly as GNU declines to start a recipe above a dependency that did not update, and the siblings stay reachable, which is `-k`. Without `-k` the walk returns at the first answer, as it always did.\n\nPer edge, `question_edge` takes the steps the front end bound and walks them: a step with `LateStep::runs_while_pretending` runs, and the first step without it is the answer. `question_step` is the one new thing that starts a process — a `ProcessSupervisor` created lazily, so an ordinary `-q` still creates none — and reads the finished status by GNU's three-way split above. What the line wrote goes to the sinks the build writes to, because suppressing the output of a line the Makefile said runs anyway would be the one thing `-q` must not do to it.\n\n`Plan::reportable_work` is `reportable_work_edges` asked about one edge, which the ready-order walk needs because it reaches the barriers between the work as well as the work.\n\n`-n` COMPOSES RATHER THAN BEING RESTATED, and this is the precise overlap the dispatch asked for. `make-recipe-dry-run`'s 2026-08-08 decision governs `-n` and is untouched: dry run is Ninja's dry run, print the commands and run none of them. The question walk asks `self.pretending()` the same question the build loop asks it, so under `-q -n` the marked step is stood in for and nothing is written. Measured: GNU under `-q -n` echoes `echo PLUS > c.txt` AND runs it, so the file appears; Ronin's does not. The ANSWER is identical in both tools in every `-q -n` cell probed (1 for a mixed recipe, 0 for a `+`-only one), so what differs is the effect, and it differs for the reason `dry-run-skips-a-plus-line` is in `DISCOVERY_ONLY_CASES` rather than for a reason of this node's. The decision does NOT constrain `-q` alone; it constrains the intersection, and only there.\n\nTEETH. Five oracle-recorded corpus cases and two regression tests, because the corpus records success or failure and not which number.\n\n  tests/make/a-question-runs-a-plus-line                                     `build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`\n  tests/make/a-question-answers-past-the-plus-line-it-ran                    `'plus.txt' was not produced`\n  tests/make/a-one-shell-question-runs-the-whole-recursive-script            `build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`\n  tests/make/a-question-runs-a-plus-line-a-composed-child-holds              `build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`\n  tests/make/a-question-reaches-a-plus-line-beside-an-answer-and-not-above-it `'beside.txt' was not produced`\n\nMeasured by restoring the old `interrogate`: `5 of 907 ported cases diverge from GNU Make's build intent`. The last of the five is the dependency-order one and it is the sharpest: it holds a marked line that MUST run beside an answer and one that must NOT run above it, so it fails against both the old sweep and against a graph-order walk.\n\ntests/make_regressions.rs gains `question_status_reads_the_plus_line` — 0, 1, 1 and 2 for the four shapes above, each also asserting the `+` line's file was written and the line it answered on was not — and `keep_going_passes_an_answered_goal`, which is the `-k` cell as a status plus a file. Both fail against the old code.\n\nNOT CHANGED, and checked rather than assumed: the makefile-remake pass. GNU clears `question_flag` while `rebuilding_makefiles` unless the makefile is a command-line target, and Ronin's remake pass answers through `already_up_to_date` rather than through `interrogate`, so no `+` line runs there and `question_refuses_a_forgiven_makefile_goal` and `keep_going_keeps_the_questions_status` are untouched.\n\nSTILL NARRATION, and none of it new: GNU echoes the `+` line it runs (`recipe-echo`), says `*** [Makefile:2: all] Error 2` where Ronin says `ronin: subcommand failed: <the edge's command, wrapper included>` (`recipe-error-line`, and the wrapper is the same text Ronin's ordinary FAILED: block carries), and says `Error 1 (ignored)` for a forgiven line where Ronin says nothing — which is what an ordinary build already does with `-`.\n\nGATES: workspace 911/0, up two for the new regression tests; conformance 336/51 normalised and 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297/280/75 and 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 rows at 134/0/25/1126, compiler at zero, not one row moved; make_port 4/4 over 907 with no recording rewritten; ninja conformance 425/425, every differential, build-outcome and persistence case matched, which is the gate that matters most for a change that now drives the plan queue; scripts/check-make-projects.sh built vim in 16s and zsh in 36s at -j8; cargo test --workspace --no-default-features 716/0; fmt and both Clippy passes clean."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T13:39:15.118914789Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-conditional-in-an-ignored-branch-is-not-read
                title "Leave an if directive's condition unread while the branch around it is being ignored"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-ifeq-closing-paren-need-not-end-the-line)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T13:00:18.400013399Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by the probe for make-ifeq-closing-paren-need-not-end-the-line and left out of it, because it is a different mechanism: not how a condition is split, but WHETHER IT IS READ AT ALL.\n\nGNU decides it is ignoring before it looks at an `if...` condition. `conditional_line` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c) reaches\n\n    for (i = 0; i < o; ++i)\n      if (conditionals->ignoring[i])\n        {\n          /* We are already ignoring, so just push a level to match the next\n             \"else\" or \"endif\", and keep ignoring.  We don't want to expand\n             variables in the condition.  */\n          conditionals->ignoring[o] = 1;\n          return 1;\n        }\n\nBEFORE either the ifdef arm or the ifeq arm, so a conditional nested inside a branch that is not being taken is never parsed, never expanded, and never complained about. kati builds an AST at read time and parses every condition it passes, ignoring or not.\n\nMeasured against the 4.4.1 oracle, GNU on the left, all after the ifeq node landed:\n\n    ifeq (x,y)\n    ifeq (a,a junk        <- the close never arrives\n    $(info INNER)\n    endif\n    endif\n    all: ; @echo hi\n      GNU    rc 0, builds, silent          Ronin  rc 2, `invalid syntax in conditional.`\n\n    same shape with `ifeq (a,a) junk`\n      GNU    rc 0, builds, silent          Ronin  rc 0, builds, warns `extraneous text after 'ifeq' directive`\n\n    same shape with `else ifeq (a,a junk` under a TAKEN outer branch\n      GNU    rc 0, builds, silent          Ronin  rc 2, `invalid syntax in conditional.`\n\nThe first and third are build-intent divergences and not only diagnostic ones — GNU builds where Ronin refuses — which is what makes this worth a node rather than a footnote. The second is the diagnostic half of the same cause.\n\nNOT AFFECTED, and the probe says so rather than assuming: `endif junk` and `else junk` inside an ignored branch warn in BOTH tools, because their arms in `conditional_line` sit ABOVE the ignoring loop. `ifdef X junk` inside an ignored branch is silent in both, because kati's `parse_ifdef` reads the whole rest of the line as the name and never had a complaint to make. So the cell is exactly the two `ifeq`/`ifneq` spellings.\n\nWHAT IT WOULD TAKE. kati reads and evaluates in two phases where GNU does one, so the fix cannot be \"skip the parse\" — the parser does not know which branch will be taken. It has to be a deferral: `parse_ifeq` keeps the unread condition text on the `IfStmt` when it cannot split it, and the evaluator raises `invalid syntax in conditional.` only if it reaches that statement while not ignoring. The `extraneous text` warning wants the same treatment, held on the statement and emitted at evaluation. Both `kati/src-rs/parser.rs` and `kati/src/parser.cc` carry the same shape and would take the same edit.\n\nGate it on a file a recipe writes, since the interesting halves are a build that happens rather than a diagnostic: a false outer conditional wrapping an `ifeq` whose parens never balance, with the goal outside it, so GNU's silence is a built file and Ronin's refusal is not; plus the `else ifeq` spelling, which reaches the same arm by a different road."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T16:07:23.9175724Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T16:16:29.709490849Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing's reading of the cause was right while its reading of the fix was one step short: the deferral it sketched is only half of what the ordering costs, and the other half was found by the conformance gate rather than by the probe.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. `IfStmt` (kati/src-rs/stmt.rs) carries a `CondComplaint` — `Unreadable` or `ExtraneousText`, what the split found and did not say — and `eval_if` says it. `parse_ifeq` no longer refuses: it records, parses two empty expressions where it could not split, and ENTERS THE IF either way. That last part is the counting half the dispatch asked about, and it is what makes a nested pair inside an ignored branch land where GNU puts it: the `endif` closes the conditional above it and not the one its branch belongs to. Gated by `an_unreadable_condition_still_holds_its_nesting_open` (kati/src-rs/parser.rs), which reads the shape and asserts the outer branch holds two statements and the file holds two.\n\nThe `extraneous text` warning moved with it and is said BETWEEN the two expansions, where `conditional_line` says it: expand the first string, find the second string's close, `EXTRATEXT`, expand the second. Ronin's diagnostics are collected and flushed rather than interleaved, so that position is not observable through Ronin today; it is where GNU puts it and it costs nothing to put it there.\n\nWHAT THE FILING DID NOT SEE, and it is the interesting half. `missing 'endif'.` was raised as the read ended — which in a read-then-evaluate model is BEFORE any statement is evaluated. So the moment an unreadable condition stopped speaking at parse time, the unclosed-conditional complaint spoke in its place:\n\n    ifeq X                GNU  `invalid syntax in conditional.` at 1:1\n                          Ronin after the deferral alone: `missing 'endif'.` at 1:2\n\nThree corpus rows said so — `err_invalid_ifeq.mk`, `err_invalid_ifeq2.mk`, `err_keyword_in_rule.mk`, all three unclassified — which is the conformance gate catching what the probe matrix did not think to hold. The fix is the same fix: `missing 'endif'.` is now a `ParseErrorStmt` pushed at the FILE's level, mirroring the type kati's C++ has always had for its own parse errors. At the file's level rather than in the branch still open, because GNU checks `conditionals->if_cmds` whether or not it was ignoring — and that placement is exactly what makes both orderings come out right:\n\n    ifeq (a,a junk  alone            GNU `invalid syntax`, 1:1   Ronin the same\n    the same under a false ifeq      GNU `missing 'endif'`, EOF  Ronin the same\n\nThe second cell is the one that proves the placement. GNU reads line 2 while ignoring, never looks at the condition, and finds the count wrong at the end of the file; a complaint parked in the open branch would never have been evaluated at all and the build would have succeeded.\n\nTHE PROBE, 55 shapes against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left, `was` is what Ronin did before:\n\n  ifeq (a,a junk in a false branch        builds, silent, 0    was refused, 2\n  ifneq (b,b junk                         the same             was refused, 2\n  ifeq \"c\" c   (second terminator bad)    the same             was refused, 2\n  ifeq junk    (no opener at all)         the same             was refused, 2\n  ifeq ($(subst a,b,aaa,bbb)              the same             was refused, 2\n  a nested pair inside the false branch   builds, silent, 0    was refused, 2\n  three levels of them                    the same             was refused, 2\n  the false half of a TAKEN ifeq's else   the same             was refused, 2\n  else ifeq (y,y junk under a taken arm   the same             was refused, 2\n  else ifeq (s,s junk below a taken arm   the same             was refused, 2\n  ifeq (a,a) junk in a false branch       silent, builds       was warned about\n  the statement after the inner endif     stays in the branch  was never reached\n  ifeq (a,a junk with no endif at all     invalid syntax 1:1   was missing endif\n  the same nested in a false branch       missing endif, EOF   agreed already\n  ifeq X / ifeq (   (the corpus rows)     invalid syntax 1:1   was missing endif\n  a $(shell) above either refusal         its file is written  was not written\n  a define left open beside both          missing endef        was missing endif\n  ifeq (a,a junk in a TRUE branch         still fatal, 2       agreed already\n  ifdef FOO junk in a false branch        silent               agreed already\n  ifdef FOO junk at the top level         fatal, 2             agreed already\n  endif junk / else junk while ignoring   both warn            agreed already\n\n`ifdef` needed nothing, and the reason is worth recording: its complaint was ALREADY made at evaluation — `eval_if` trims the expanded name and refuses a space in it — so the arm this node is about was the only one still complaining at read time. The filing said as much and it held up.\n\nFIXTURES, four, oracle-recorded, all gating effects:\n\n* `tests/make/a-condition-in-an-ignored-branch-is-not-read` — five unreadable spellings in one ignored branch, plus the counting half written as an assignment after the inner `endif` that must stay unset. Gates `inside=[] unread=[] after=[after-the-outer-endif]`.\n* `tests/make/an-else-if-under-a-taken-branch-is-not-read` — the `else if...` road, both under a taken first arm and below a taken later arm. Gates `taken=[first] unread=[] chain=[second]`.\n* `tests/make/a-condition-that-cannot-be-read-stops-the-read-where-it-stands` — the refusal that still happens, with a `$(shell)` above it whose file must be there afterwards.\n* `tests/make/an-unclosed-conditional-is-refused-at-the-end-of-the-read` — the same for the `missing 'endif'` half.\n\nTEETH MEASURED by restoring the old `parse_ifeq` and the old end-of-read complaint: `3 of 910 ported cases diverge from GNU Make's build intent` — the two ignored-branch cases as `build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`, and the refusal case as `'early' was not produced`. The fourth was written after that measurement and fails the same way with the complaint put back.\n\nPlus `tests/make_regressions.rs::an_ignored_branch_draws_no_complaint_about_its_conditions`, which holds the warning half — an effect gate cannot see it, because `ifeq (a,a) junk` compares `a` against `a` and builds either way. It asserts silence in the ignored branch and the same line complained about where the read does reach it, so the quiet is the branch's doing rather than the complaint having been dropped.\n\nFOUND AND FILED, not fixed, both probed in the same pass:\n\n* make-a-refused-conditions-first-string-is-not-expanded — GNU expands the first string before it goes looking for the second, so `ifeq ($(shell touch marker; echo x),y junk` leaves `marker` behind and Ronin's refusal leaves nothing. Both refuse; what differs is the ground afterwards.\n* make-a-function-call-is-checked-where-it-is-read — kati checks a call's arity and termination while PARSING, GNU while expanding, so `X := $(subst a)` inside a false branch is exit 2 here and exit 0 there. Not a conditional defect at all, which is why it is not folded in: the assignment cell proves it.\n\nWHERE: kati bf2f818e119babbddbad790f18720800fd1d0293 on nplan/make-a-conditional-in-an-ignored-branch-is-not-read, pushed before this pin. Both the Rust and the C++ took the same edit, so `src/parser.cc` and `src-rs/parser.rs` still agree with each other.\n\nGATES. fmt clean both crates; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace 935 passed / 0 failed (933 plus the two new tests); Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; make_port 4/4 over 911 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved, and the three unclassified rows the deferral alone produced are gone; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 rows at 134 unclassified / 0 compiler / 25 interface / 1126 narration, tsv byte-identical, no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO; ninja conformance 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log-content; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 734 passed / 0 failed. `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 29s at -j8 — the host was carrying an unrelated `pkg` release build throughout, so those are reported as the gate passing rather than as measurements."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T16:17:43.516977343Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-refused-conditions-first-string-is-not-expanded
                title "Expand a refused condition's first string, as the read that refuses it does"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-conditional-in-an-ignored-branch-is-not-read)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T16:18:13.70587276Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by the probe for make-a-conditional-in-an-ignored-branch-is-not-read and left out of it, because that node is about WHETHER a condition is read at all and this is about how much of one is read before the reading gives up. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make after that node landed, GNU on the left.\n\nGNU's `conditional_line` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c) splits the first string, EXPANDS it — `s2 = variable_expand (s1)` — and only then goes looking for the second. Both `return -1` paths below that line therefore run with the first string's expansion already done, side effects and all:\n\n    ifeq ($(shell touch marker; echo x),y junk\n    endif\n    all: ; @echo built > out\n      GNU    exit 2, `marker` written        Ronin  exit 2, nothing written\n\nTwo shapes reach it — the second string's close never arriving, as above, and a second terminator that is neither `)` nor a quote:\n\n    ifeq \"$(shell touch marker; echo x)\" a\n\nand two where GNU expands nothing and the two tools already agree, which is what makes the cell exactly the two above:\n\n    ifeq ($(shell touch marker; echo x) junk    the FIRST string's terminator never arrives\n    ifeq junk                                    the opener is neither a paren nor a quote\n\nBoth tools refuse in all four, so this is not a build that happens where one did not. What differs is what the refused read left on the ground, and a `$(shell)` is only the plainest way to leave something: a `$(file >)` or an `$(eval)` in the same place is the same divergence.\n\nWHAT IT WOULD TAKE. `split_ifeq_condition` (kati/src-rs/parser.rs) answers `Option<IfeqCondition>` — it either read the condition or it did not — and it would have to say WHICH of the four it hit and, for the two that matter, where the first string sits. The statement already carries `CondComplaint::Unreadable` and two empty expressions; it would carry the first string's parsed expression instead, and `eval_if` would expand it before raising. `SplitIfEqCond` in kati/src/parser.cc is the same function and takes the same edit.\n\nGate it on effects through the port harness: a `$(shell)` in the first string writing a file, under a condition whose close never arrives, with the build refused in both tools and the file present in both."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T17:24:01.114158629Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T17:59:47.731688643Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing's count of the shapes was right while its count of the ROADS was one short.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, which is exactly what the filing sketched. `split_ifeq_condition` (kati/src-rs/parser.rs) answered `Option<IfeqCondition>` — it either read the condition or it did not — and now answers `SplitCondition::Read` or `SplitCondition::Invalid { lhs: Option<Range<usize>> }`. The `lhs` is the whole of the fix: it is `Some` from every refusal below the line where `conditional_line` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c) does `s2 = variable_expand (s1)`, and `None` from every refusal above it. `parse_ifeq` puts that text on the statement's `lhs` instead of an empty expression, and `eval_if` (kati/src-rs/eval.rs) expands `stmt.lhs` BEFORE it says `invalid syntax in conditional.` rather than after. A split that read no first string leaves an empty expression there, so the ordering costs nothing on the paths that must run nothing — which is why one move of one line covers all five exits.\n\n`SplitIfEqCond` and `EvalIf` in the C++ took the same edit, so `src/parser.cc` and `src-rs/parser.rs` still agree with each other and `src/eval.cc` with `src-rs/eval.rs`.\n\nTHREE ROADS, NOT TWO. The filing named two shapes that reach the expansion — the second string's close never arriving, and a second terminator that is neither `)` nor a quote. There is a third, and it is a distinct exit in both tools: the quoted form whose text ENDS after the first string's close quote, with nothing at all where the second terminator should be.\n\n    ifeq \"$(shell touch first; echo x)\"\n    endif\n\nGNU reads `termin = *line` off the terminating NUL, which is neither `)` nor a quote, and refuses — with `first` written. In kati that was `*s.get(i)?`, one of the two `?`s in the function, and it needed an arm of its own rather than falling out of the change. Two spellings of it were probed: the close quote at end of line, and the close quote followed by blanks.\n\nTHE PROBE, 33 shapes against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left, `was` is what Ronin did before. Every cell that names a file wrote it with `$(shell touch)`; `first` sits in the condition's first string and `second` in its second.\n\n  REFUSED WITH THE FIRST STRING ALREADY RUN — this node\n    ifeq (first,second junk           refuses, first          was refused, nothing\n    ifneq (first,second junk          refuses, first          was refused, nothing\n    ifeq \"first\" a                    refuses, first          was refused, nothing\n    ifeq 'first' a                    refuses, first          was refused, nothing\n    ifeq \"first\" \"second              refuses, first          was refused, nothing\n    ifeq \"first\" second               refuses, first          was refused, nothing\n    ifeq \"first\"                      refuses, first          was refused, nothing   <- the third road\n    ifeq \"first\"<blanks>              refuses, first          was refused, nothing   <- the same\n    ifeq (first,y junk                refuses, first          was refused, nothing\n    ifeq ($(file > first,written),y junk  refuses, first      was refused, nothing\n    ifeq ($(error boom),y junk        says `boom`             was `invalid syntax in conditional`\n\n  REFUSED BEFORE ANYTHING RAN — the control, and it must stay this way\n    ifeq (first junk                  refuses, nothing        agreed already\n    ifeq junkfirst                    refuses, nothing        agreed already\n    ifeq                              refuses, nothing        agreed already\n    ifeq ($(subst a,b,first) junk     refuses, nothing        agreed already\n    ifeq (x,second junk               refuses, nothing        agreed already\n\n  THE SECOND STRING IS NEVER THE ONE THAT RAN\n    ifeq (first,second junk           second not written      neither written\n    ifeq \"first\" second               second not written      neither written\n\n  UNTOUCHED, AND CHECKED\n    ifeq (first,second)               builds, both written    agreed already\n    ifeq (first,second) junk          builds, both, warns     agreed already\n    the same inside an ignored branch builds, nothing         agreed already\n    else ifeq (...) under a taken arm builds, nothing         agreed already\n    ifdef $(shell touch first) junk   refuses, first          agreed already\n\nThe `ifdef` row is the one that says the arm this node is about was the only one still refusing early: its complaint has always been made at evaluation, after the name is expanded, which the previous node's filing recorded and which held up.\n\nFIXTURES, three, oracle-recorded, all gating effects:\n\n* `tests/make/a-refused-condition-has-expanded-its-first-string` — a `$(shell touch)` in each string with the close missing. Refused in both; the first file must be there and the second must not, so one case gates the fix and its boundary.\n* `tests/make/a-refused-conditions-second-terminator-is-read-after-the-first-string` — the quoted form whose second terminator is neither quote. The same two-way assertion by the other road.\n* `tests/make/a-condition-refused-before-its-first-string-runs-nothing` — the control: the two refusals above the expansion, neither of which may run anything.\n\nTEETH, measured by parking the whole kati diff and rebuilding: `2 of 920 ported cases diverge from GNU Make's build intent`, both as `'the-first-string-ran' was not produced`. The third is the control and passes either way against the parked diff; measured against the wrong implementation instead — the first string's terminator scan handing back `lhs_start..i` when it ran off the end, which is the plausible mistake — it fails as `'the-first-string-ran' was produced and GNU Make produced no such file`.\n\nPlus `kati/src-rs/parser.rs::a_refusal_carries_the_first_string_it_had_already_read`, nine shapes over the split itself, which pins which of the five exits carries a first string and which do not — the one fact the whole fix rests on, and the one an effect gate can only reach two exits of.\n\nFOUND AND FILED, not fixed:\n\n* make-an-unreadable-condition-after-else-is-extraneous-text — an `else ifeq` whose condition cannot be read is a WARNING in GNU, not a refusal, and it leaves an extra open conditional behind. `ifeq (a,b) / else ifeq (x,y junk / endif / endif` builds under GNU and is refused here, which is a build divergence rather than a wording one. The node records which spelling is safe to gate and which reads uninitialised memory on GNU's side, because the branch-content spelling does.\n\nWHERE: kati 8af9768de6cba0767ae045681c902c5d90d8a98c on nplan/make-a-refused-conditions-first-string-is-not-expanded, pushed before this pin.\n\nGATES, run serially. THE HOST WAS NOT QUIET — an unrelated build harness held the machine at a load average of 55 to 75 throughout, so these are reported as the gate passing and not as measurements. fmt clean both crates; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace 937 passed / 0 failed (936 plus the one new kati case), measured twice; Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; make_port 4/4 over 920 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved, 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact and nothing unclassified; equivalence 75 rejected and 0 disagreements; upstream inventory exits clean with no drift line and `tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv` byte-identical, its family table identical row for row to the run before it; ninja conformance 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log-content; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 736 passed / 0 failed; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules.\n\nOne earlier workspace run reported 836 passed / 1 failed while a second cargo invocation was live in the same target directory; two serial runs afterwards were 937 / 0 and the failing case did not recur. Recorded rather than dismissed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T17:59:52.512839719Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-function-call-is-checked-where-it-is-read
                title "Check a function call where it is expanded rather than where it is read"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T16:18:50.601608542Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by the probe for make-a-conditional-in-an-ignored-branch-is-not-read, which fixed the conditional half of something wider and left this. kati checks a function call's shape while it PARSES the text; GNU Make checks it while it EXPANDS. Text a read never expands is therefore refused here and passed over there — and the plainest case has no conditional in it at all.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left, each over a makefile whose goal is `all: ; @echo built > out`:\n\n    X = $(subst a)                        builds, exit 0    `insufficient number of arguments (1) to function 'subst'.`, exit 2\n    X := $(subst a)                       refuses, exit 2   the same, and the two tools agree\n    X = $(subst a) with `$(X)` in a recipe refuses, exit 2   the same, and the two tools agree\n\nThat first line is the whole node in three words: a recursive binding nothing ever expands. GNU never runs the call, so it never counts its arguments; kati counts them while building the `Value` and the makefile is refused before anything is built. The second and third lines are the control — where GNU does expand, the two tools say the same thing, and GNU names the line the call was WRITTEN at (`Makefile:1`) even when the expansion happens in a recipe on another line, so the call's own location travels with it.\n\nThe same cause shows up in every branch a read never takes, which is where it was found:\n\n    ifeq (x,y)\n    X := $(subst a)\n    endif                                 builds, exit 0    refuses, exit 2\n\n    ifeq (x,y)\n    ifeq ($(subst a),b) ... endif\n    endif                                 builds, exit 0    refuses, exit 2\n\n    ifeq (x,y)\n    ifeq (${subst a,b,aaa},bbb) ... endif\n    endif                                 builds, exit 0    `unterminated call to function 'subst': missing '}'.`, exit 2\n\nThe third is worth reading twice. The brace form is well formed, and it is GNU's OWN comma-splitting blind spot that breaks it: `conditional_line` counts parens and not braces, so the condition splits inside `${subst a,b,aaa}` and leaves `${subst a` behind. Both tools do that identically and both refuse the shape when the branch is taken; the divergence is only that GNU never looks when it is ignoring.\n\nTWO CELLS THAT AGREE, which say where the boundary already is:\n\n    X := ${subst a,b,aaa}   in an ignored branch    builds in both — the call is well formed, so there is nothing to check\n    weird: ; @echo $(subst a)  in an ignored branch builds in both — a recipe is not expanded at read time in either tool\n\nSo this is not about conditionals: `parse_expr` (kati/src-rs/expr.rs) raises `insufficient number of arguments` and `unterminated call to function` while building the `Value`, where GNU's `expand_builtin_function` (func.c) counts arguments as it runs the call and `handle_function` complains about nothing until then.\n\nWHAT IT WOULD TAKE, and it is bigger than it looks. The parsed `Value` has to be able to hold a call the parse has not approved of, and the complaint has to arrive carrying the location the call was written at rather than the location the expansion reached it from — measured above, GNU says `Makefile:1` for a call expanded from line 2. `unterminated call to function` may be a different question from arity: an unterminated call cannot be parsed into anything at all, so the shape it takes is probably a `Value` that is a held complaint rather than a call.\n\nThe narrow alternative — deferring the whole condition's parse, the way make-a-conditional-in-an-ignored-branch-is-not-read defers its split — would close the two conditional cells and leave both assignment cells open. That is half a fix for half the shapes and is recorded here as the option that exists rather than as the plan.\n\nGate it on effects through the port harness: `X = $(subst a)` with a goal that writes a file, which GNU builds and Ronin refuses, plus the ignored-branch assignment beside it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T16:52:32.310460183Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T17:23:03.519347032Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. The filing read the cause correctly and was four shapes short on the inventory, one of which was a panic.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, in two halves, both in kati and both in `src-rs/expr.rs`.\n\nThe COUNT half needed no new state at all, which the filing did not expect. `parse_func` reported `nargs - 1`, and `nargs - 1` is `args.len()` at every exit — so `Value::Func` already carries the count, and the complaint moved to the `Value::Func` arm of `Evaluable::eval` where it is now made against the args the value is holding. The one thing that had to come with it is GNU's ORDER: `handle_function` expands an argument-expanding function's arguments and `expand_builtin_function` counts them afterwards, so a call about to be refused has already run everything its arguments do. `fi.pre_expanded_args` is GNU's `expand_args` column, and it is exact rather than approximate at this site — the only functions the complaint can reach are those wanting two arguments or more (a call parses with at least one), and every one of those carries the same value for the flag that GNU Make's table does.\n\nThe TERMINATION half needed the held value the filing sketched. `parse_func` answers `ParsedCall::Args` or `ParsedCall::Unterminated` in place of raising, and `parse_dollar` turns the second into `Value::Unreadable(Loc, Unreadable)` — a value whose only behaviour is to raise when something expands it. The same variant carries `unterminated variable reference.`, which is the third parse-time complaint on this surface and was not in the filing: `X = $(FOO` never expanded builds under GNU and was exit 2 here.\n\nWHAT THE FILING DID NOT SEE, four things, all measured.\n\n* `$(subst ` at end of text was a PANIC, not a refusal — exit 101 out of `subst_func` indexing an empty argument list. `parse_func`'s early return handed back a call with no arguments at all where GNU reads a missing close. It is now `Unterminated` and says what GNU says.\n* Two more shapes reached the wrong complaint: `$(subst a,` and `$(subst a,b,` — a comma as the last byte — said `insufficient number of arguments` where GNU says `unterminated call`. Same edit, same exit.\n* `unterminated variable reference` is the same defect one directive over, as above.\n* The sharpest cell in the whole matrix has no conditional and no lazy variable in it: `X := $(if ,$(subst a))`, a SIMPLE assignment expanded the moment the read reaches it. The untaken arm of `$(if)` is not expanded, so GNU never counts the call. That cell is what says this is about expansion and not about reading.\n\nWHERE THE COMPLAINT LANDS, which the filing asked about and the probe answered: GNU reports `*expanding_var`, the location of the VARIABLE being expanded, and kati reports where the call was written. For everything on one line the two are the same line, and they measured the same everywhere but the two `define` shapes (GNU names the `define`'s first line, kati the line the call is on). Left as it is and filed, because kati's answer points at the call and GNU's points at the binding, and changing it is a diagnostic-location question rather than this one.\n\nTHE PROBE, 69 shapes against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left, `was` is what Ronin did before.\n\n  X = $(subst a)              never expanded    builds, 0     was refused, 2\n  X = $(subst a,b,c           never expanded    builds, 0     was refused, 2\n  X = ${subst a,b,aaa         never expanded    builds, 0     was refused, 2\n  X = $(FOO                   never expanded    builds, 0     was refused, 2\n  X := $(if ,$(subst a))      untaken arm       builds, 0     was refused, 2\n  the same with a $(shell) in the arm           builds, 0     was refused, 2\n  X := $(subst a) in an ignored branch          builds, 0     was refused, 2\n  X := $(subst a,b,c in an ignored branch       builds, 0     was refused, 2\n  a define holding it, never called             builds, 0     was refused, 2\n  all: X = $(subst a)   target-specific         builds, 0     was refused, 2\n  X = $(eval Y := $(subst a))  never expanded   builds, 0     was refused, 2\n  a bad call in the recipe of a target NOT built builds, 0    was refused, 2\n  $(shell ...)$(subst a,b,c never expanded      builds, 0     was refused, 2\n  X := $(subst $(shell touch m))   refuses, 2, m written      was refused, nothing written\n  the same with two arguments      refuses, 2, both written   was refused, nothing written\n  $(word ...) and $(patsubst ...)  the same                   the same\n  X := $(if $(shell touch m))      refuses, 2, NOTHING written  agreed already\n  $(foreach), $(intcmp), $(let)    the same                   agreed already\n  $(shell touch a)$(subst $(shell touch b),c,d  a written, b not  neither written\n  X := $(subst              was a PANIC, 101              now unterminated call, 2\n  X := $(subst a,           unterminated call             was insufficient arguments\n  X := $(subst a,b,         unterminated call             was insufficient arguments\n  X = $(subst a,b, / newline / c    missing separator at line 2  was insufficient arguments\n  X := $(subst a)           refuses, 2        agreed already\n  X = $(subst a) with $(X) in a recipe  refuses, 2  agreed already\n  the call written in the recipe itself, line 3   names line 3   agreed already\n  in a prerequisite list, a target name, an include name  refuses  agreed already\n  export X = $(subst a)     refuses, 2        agreed already\n  $(call subst,a) expanded / not expanded  refuses / builds  agreed already\n  $(value X), $(origin X), $(flavor X), .VARIABLES over a held call  all four agree\n  a call inside a define, called       GNU names the define's line 1, kati the call's line 2\n\n`$(call)` needed nothing and the reason is worth recording: `call_builtin` has always counted at evaluation, so `X = $(call subst,a)` was already the GNU answer. The eval-time check this node adds is the same check one layer down.\n\nFIXTURES, four, oracle-recorded, all gating effects:\n\n* `tests/make/a-call-no-expansion-reaches-is-never-checked` — eight calls nothing expands, in one file: the four recursive spellings, both ignored-branch spellings, the untaken `$(if)` arm, and the recipe of a target that is not the default goal. Builds, and gates `chosen=[the-other-arm] ignored=[]`.\n* `tests/make/a-call-with-too-few-arguments-has-already-run-them` — `$(subst $(shell touch ...),$(shell touch ...))`. Refused in both, and both files must be there.\n* `tests/make/a-lazy-call-with-too-few-arguments-has-run-nothing` — `$(if $(shell touch ...))`. Refused in both, and the file must NOT be there.\n* `tests/make/an-unterminated-call-expands-none-of-its-own-text` — a `$(shell)` before the call and a `$(shell)` inside it. Refused in both, the first file there and the second not.\n\nTEETH, measured by parking the whole kati diff and rebuilding: `3 of 917 ported cases diverge from GNU Make's build intent` — the first as `build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`, the second and fourth as a file `was not produced`. The third is the control and passes either way against the parked diff, because what it guards is the implementation's own choice; measured against the wrong choice instead — the `pre_expanded_args` guard removed — it fails as `'the-argument-ran' was produced and GNU Make produced no such file`.\n\nPlus `kati/src-rs/expr.rs::a_call_a_read_cannot_finish_is_held_until_it_is_expanded`, six shapes read and then expanded, which holds the diagnostics an effect gate cannot see — including the panic, whose only observable difference from a refusal is the exit status this harness deliberately does not compare.\n\nRUST ONLY, deliberately. `src/expr.cc` did not take this edit and the reason is that it cannot take half of it: the C++ `FuncInfo` has no `expand_args` column and `src/func.cc` has no `$(call)` builtin dispatch at all, so the flag the count half rides on exists only on the Rust side. An unbuilt mirror that is nearly right is worse than one that plainly has not moved.\n\nFOUND AND FILED, not fixed, both probed here:\n\n* make-a-diagnostic-inside-a-define-names-the-call-not-the-define — the location cell above.\n* make-an-unterminated-call-with-no-space-is-read-as-a-reference — `X := $(subst` with nothing after the name says `unterminated variable reference` where GNU says `unterminated call to function 'subst'`. Both refuse; only the word differs.\n\nWHERE: kati aafe46186eda0be73a3bfa3cb49bff4d95aa8f5e on nplan/make-a-function-call-is-checked-where-it-is-read, pushed before this pin.\n\nGATES, run serially on a quiet host. fmt clean both crates; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace 936 passed / 0 failed (935 plus the one new kati case); Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; make_port 4/4 over 917 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved, every row still classified and none unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 rows at 134 unclassified / 0 compiler / 25 interface / 1126 narration, tsv byte-identical with no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO; ninja conformance 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log-content; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 735 passed / 0 failed. `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` green, vim 9.2 in 27s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 44s at -j8."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T17:23:08.257206179Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-diagnostic-inside-a-define-names-the-call-not-the-define
                title "Name the binding being expanded where GNU Make names it"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-function-call-is-checked-where-it-is-read)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T17:21:53.047695886Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by the probe for make-a-function-call-is-checked-where-it-is-read and left out of it, because that node is about WHEN a call is judged and this is about where the judgement points.\n\nGNU Make raises a function-call fatal at `*expanding_var` — `fatal (*expanding_var, ...)` in `expand_builtin_function` and `handle_function` (reference/gnumake/src/function.c) — which is the location of the VARIABLE being expanded, not of the call inside it. kati raises at the call's own written location. For everything written on one line the two are the same line, which is why 68 of the 69 probed shapes agreed:\n\n    X = $(subst a)          on line 1, expanded from line 3\n      GNU Makefile:1        Ronin Makefile:1        the binding and the call are the same line\n\n    all: ; @echo $(subst a) written and expanded on line 3\n      GNU Makefile:3        Ronin Makefile:3        a recipe is not a binding, so GNU falls back to the line\n\nThe cell where they differ is a `define`, which is the one binding whose text spans lines:\n\n    define D            <- line 1\n    $(subst a)          <- line 2\n    endef\n                        \n    all: ; @echo $(call D) > out\n      GNU Makefile:1    Ronin Makefile:2\n\nWHICH IS RIGHT is not obvious and that is why this is a node rather than a fix. GNU's answer names the binding a reader would go and edit; kati's names the call a reader would go and count. Both are defensible and only one is compatible. Whatever is decided, it is a diagnostic-location question that reaches every fatal raised from inside an expansion, not just the two this probe used — `$(error)` raised from a `define` is the same question, and worth measuring before anything moves.\n\nGate it through the conformance corpus rather than the port harness: both tools refuse and neither writes a file, so the only observable is the line number, and that is narration."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T21:45:07.38166694Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T21:51:00.349628885Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was right about the divergence, wrong about what kati was doing, wrong about the shape of the question, and two cells short.\n\nWHICH IS RIGHT — the question the node existed to answer. The filing put it as \"GNU's answer names the binding a reader would go and edit; kati's names the call a reader would go and count. Both are defensible and only one is compatible.\" That framing does not survive the probe, because kati was not naming the call:\n\n    define D            <- 1\n    ok                  <- 2\n    ok                  <- 3\n    $(subst a)          <- 4\n    endef\n    all: ; @echo $(D) > out\n      GNU    Makefile:1     the `define` line\n      Ronin  Makefile:2     the define's FIRST BODY line -- not the call, which is on 4\n\nEvery define-body diagnostic came out at `define`-line-plus-one wherever in the body the call was written. So there was no third position to defend: the answer was neither the binding nor the call, and GNU's is the only one of the three that names something a reader can act on. It went to GNU's without a trade.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, all in kati and all Rust.\n\nGNU Make raises these at `*expanding_var` (reference/gnumake/src/expand.c). That is a pointer to a pointer: it points at `reading_file` until `recursively_expand_for_file` installs `&v->fileinfo` -- the location the VARIABLE WAS DEFINED, not where the text inside it was written -- and it does so only `if (v->fileinfo.filenm)`, so a variable with no location of its own leaves whatever was standing. A `define` is the one binding whose text spans lines, which is why every other shape agrees by accident.\n\n`Evaluator::expanding_var_locs: Vec<Option<Loc>>` is that pointer, as a stack. `enter_expanding_var` pushes the entered variable's own location or, where it has none, the one already standing; `leave_expanding_var` pops; `expanding_var_loc()` reads the top and falls back to `ev.loc`, which is `reading_file`. It is pushed at every site GNU reaches a variable's value through a reference: `Value::SymRef`, `Value::VarRef`, `Value::VarSubst` -- `$(V:a=b)` calls `recursively_expand` on that arm too -- and `$(call)`, which GNU implements by building the text `$(NAME)` and expanding THAT, so the body arrives through an ordinary reference.\n\nIt is deliberately separate from the `expanding_vars` set beside it, because GNU keeps `v->expanding` (the recursion guard) and `expanding_var` (the location) separate and `func_call` installs the second without the first -- which is what lets `$(call)` recurse. Folding them together would have made `$(call)` a recursion error.\n\n`Variable::expansion_loc()` is GNU's `if (v->fileinfo.filenm)` said in kati's terms. GNU fills in a `fileinfo` only for a variable a makefile line defined, so `VarOrigin::File` and `VarOrigin::Override` have one and `Default`, `Environment`, `EnvironmentOverride`, `CommandLine` and `Automatic` do not. That single test is what fixes the command-line cell below.\n\nWHICH COMPLAINTS MOVED, read off GNU's `fatal (*expanding_var, ...)` sites rather than chosen: the two in expr.rs (the held `Unreadable`, and a call with too few arguments), `parse_numeric`'s three, `$(word)`'s and `$(wordlist)`'s three, `$(call)`'s builtin-dispatch argument count, `$(intcmp)`'s two, the four structural `$(file)` complaints, and the recursive-self-reference one.\n\nWHICH DID NOT, and this is the filing's second correction. The filing said \"`$(error)` raised from a `define` is the same question\". It is not. `func_error` (function.c) raises at `reading_file`, not at `*expanding_var` -- as do `$(warning)`, `$(info)`, and the open/read/write/close failures inside `$(file)`, which sit one line away from the structural complaints in the same function. Ronin already agreed on all of them and still does; they are gated as controls precisely because they look like they should have moved.\n\nTWO CELLS THE FILING DID NOT HAVE, neither of which needed a define to show up:\n\n* `$(word 0,a b c)` inside a define was located at the RECIPE line -- a third answer again, because `word_func` raised at `ev.loc` where the argument-count complaint raised at the value's own location. One rule now covers both.\n* `X='$(subst a)'` on the command line was located at `*bootstrap*:0`, a file that does not exist, where GNU names the line the reference was written on. kati gives command-line bindings a synthetic location and GNU gives them none; `expansion_loc` is where the two are reconciled.\n\nTHE PROBE, 30 shapes against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. GNU on the left, `was` is what Ronin did before.\n\n  THE DEFINE CELLS — this node\n    a define at lines 1-3, reached by $(D)          1     was 2\n    the same, reached by $(call D)                  1     was 2\n    the same with arguments, $(call D,x)            1     was 2\n    a define starting at line 3                     3     was 4\n    the bad call on the define's 3rd body line      1     was 2\n    a define whose body references another define   1     was 2   (the inner one's line)\n    the same with the inner define written LAST     5     was 6   (still the inner one's)\n    a define expanded by $(eval $(D))               1     was 2\n    a define expanded at read time by `X := $(D)`   1     was 2\n    a define expanded in a prerequisite list        1     was 2\n    a define reached through $(D:x=y)               1     was 2\n    an `override define`                            1     was 2\n    a define in an included file             inc.mk:1     was inc.mk:2\n    the same, referenced from the parent file inc.mk:1     was inc.mk:2\n    $(word 0,...) inside a define                   1     was 5   (the recipe line)\n    $(file >) inside a define                       1     was 5\n    $(intcmp x,1,...) inside a define               1     was 5\n    an unterminated call inside a define            1     was 2\n    an unterminated reference inside a define       1     was 2\n    a define that references itself                 1     agreed already\n\n  NOT A DEFINE, AND STILL WRONG\n    X='$(subst a)' on the command line              3     was *bootstrap*:0\n\n  CONTROLS — these must not move, and did not\n    X = $(subst a) on line 1, used from a recipe    1     agreed already\n    the call written in the recipe itself           2     agreed already\n    X := $(subst a), a simple assignment            2     agreed already\n    a target-specific `all: X = $(subst a)`         1     agreed already\n    X = $(subst a) reached through Y = $(X)         1     agreed already\n    a variable $(eval) defined, raising later       5     agreed already\n    $(error boom) inside a define                   5     agreed already   <- reading_file\n    $(warning careful) inside a define              5     agreed already\n    $(error boom) in a one-line recursive variable  2     agreed already\n\nFIXTURE: `tests/make_regressions.rs::a_define_body_names_the_define`, nine cells run against the real executable. Not the port harness, and the filing said why: both tools refuse and neither writes a file, so the only observable is the line number. It reports every cell that moved rather than the first, which is what made the teeth measurable in one run.\n\nTEETH, measured by parking the whole kati diff and rebuilding: six of the nine cells fail -- the plain reference, `$(call)`, the define's own line, the innermost define, the `$(word)` complaint, and the command-line binding -- while the three controls pass either way, which is what a control is for.\n\nRUST ONLY, and for the reason the node before it gave. `src/expr.cc`, `src/eval.cc` and `src/func.cc` have no `expanding_var` at all, and half of what this rides on -- the held `Unreadable` value -- does not exist on that side either. The C++ would need the same field, the same four push sites and the same fifteen complaint sites, in a tree that is not compiled or tested here. An unbuilt mirror that is nearly right is worse than one that plainly has not moved.\n\nA NOTE ON COST, because this runs on the hottest path there is: `enter_expanding_var` takes the location rather than the variable, so the caller's existing read guard does the work and no reference pays a second lock. The stack entry is one `Option<Loc>` push and pop per reference.\n\nFOUND AND FILED, not fixed: make-an-environment-variable-is-a-recursive-one. GNU Make enters every environment variable as recursive (`define_variable_in_set (..., o_env, 1)`), so a `$` in an environment value is make syntax; kati enters them as simple and passes the bytes through unread, so `FROMENV='$(words a b c)'` reaches the recipe verbatim and the shell tries to run `words`. kati already REPORTS the flavour as recursive, which is what makes it a plain defect. Found here because the probe was asking where a diagnostic raised inside an environment variable's expansion points, and discovered there is no expansion."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T21:58:16.315773594Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "WHERE: kati a4e7dc842a9bf2e369e71ec02aa1e91f29709754 on nplan/make-a-diagnostic-inside-a-define-names-the-call-not-the-define, pushed before this pin.\n\nGATES, run serially on a quiet host, the whole battery on the final tree. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; all three clippy passes clean — ronin lib+bins `--no-deps` pedantic+nursery, ronin `--all-targets --no-deps` with the groups off, and `-p kati --all-targets` at plain `-D warnings`; workspace 958 passed / 0 failed; `--no-default-features` 753 / 0; make_port 4/4 over 924 gated cases with no recording rewritten (this node adds no port case — its gate is `tests/make_regressions.rs`); conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved, 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact and nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 1 both-refused / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 at 134 unclassified / 0 compiler / 25 interface / 1126 narration, tsv byte-identical, no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO; ninja conformance 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s at -j8, against 17s and 28s on the tree before this node — no cost visible at that resolution, which is the answer to the one thing a per-reference push and pop could have cost.\n\nNothing moved in any corpus. Every case that reaches one of these diagnostics writes it on the line the binding is on, which is why the divergence needed a `define` to show at all."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T22:00:07.152218267Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-an-unterminated-call-with-no-space-is-read-as-a-reference
                title "Read a name with no space after it as the call GNU Make reads"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-function-call-is-checked-where-it-is-read)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T17:22:01.910338257Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by the probe for make-a-function-call-is-checked-where-it-is-read, which fixed every other unterminated shape and left this one because it is a different question: not when the complaint is made, but which complaint it is.\n\n    X := $(subst        text ends immediately after the name, no space\n      GNU    Makefile:1: *** unterminated call to function 'subst': missing ')'.  Stop.\n      Ronin  Makefile:1: unterminated variable reference.\n\nBoth refuse, both exit 2, and nothing is written either way — so the divergence is one word of narration and no build moved. The neighbours all agree after that node:\n\n    X := $(subst        (a space after the name)   unterminated call, both\n    X := $(subst a,     (a trailing comma)         unterminated call, both\n    X := $(foo                                     unterminated variable reference, both\n\nWHY IT IS THE ODD ONE. GNU's `lookup_function` (function.c) accepts a name terminated by whitespace, a comma, a close paren OR a NUL — `MAP_NUL` is in the set — so `$(subst` at the end of the text is a call with nothing after its name, and `handle_function` then finds no close. kati's `parse_dollar` (kati/src-rs/expr.rs) only enters the call arm when the byte after the name is a space or a backslash, so a name that runs into the end of the text falls through to the reference arm and reaches the reference's own complaint.\n\nThe fix is to let the call arm be entered by end-of-text as well as by a space, which means deciding what `$(subst` with no close and no arguments parses to before it can be held — the `Unreadable::UnterminatedCall` that node introduced is exactly the shape, so this is a small edit on top of it rather than new machinery."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T20:58:14.342556487Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T21:10:17.210328646Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing read the cause exactly right while badly understating what it cost. It called the divergence \"one word of narration and no build moved\". The cause it named — kati enters the call arm only on a space or a backslash where GNU Make's `lookup_function` ends a name on MAP_NUL|MAP_SPACE — has FIVE more bytes in it than the filing counted, and every one of them is a silently wrong build.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, one place, `parse_dollar` in kati/src-rs/expr.rs.\n\n`lookup_function` (reference/gnumake/src/function.c) walks the name over `MAP_USERFUNC` and then requires `STOP_SET(*e, MAP_NUL|MAP_SPACE)`. `MAP_SPACE` is `MAP_BLANK|MAP_NEWLINE`, and `initialize_stopchar_map` (main.c) gives MAP_BLANK to space and tab and MAP_NEWLINE to every other `isspace` character — so the full terminator set is NUL, space, tab, newline, carriage return, vertical tab and form feed. kati's name scan used `terms = [cp, ':', ' ']`, so only a space ended the name; a tab or a newline was swallowed INTO the name, and the whole `subst\\ta,b,aaa` became the symbol looked up.\n\nThree edits. `terms` is now every one of those bytes, which the existing `terms.truncate(2)` drops as a group exactly as it dropped the single space, so the not-a-function fallback that re-reads with the plain terminators needed nothing. The call arm is entered by any of them OR by the end of the text (`t.is_empty()`), stepping over the terminator only where there is one. And the call arm is asked BEFORE the reference arm, because `variable_expand_string` (expand.c) tries `handle_function` before it goes looking for the close — which is the whole reason `$(subst)` is a reference to a variable called `subst` and `$(subst` is a call whose close is missing. The backslash stays in the set because a continuation reaches this decision unfolded, and the newline it stands for is one of the six.\n\nWHAT THE FILING MISSED, all measured against the oracle:\n\n* `$(subst<TAB>a,b,aaa)` was EMPTY where GNU Make says `bbb`. Same for a newline (which a `define` body is full of), a carriage return, a vertical tab and a form feed.\n* `$(shell<TAB>echo dep)` in a prerequisite list read as an unset variable, so the prerequisite was not there, the rule that would have built it never ran, and the build succeeded having done less. A silent graph difference, not a diagnostic.\n* `X := $(subst<TAB>$(shell touch m),b,c` with no close BUILT here and is refused by GNU Make. The tab kept it out of the call arm, the argument's own close paren then satisfied the unmatched-parentheses arm, and that arm swallows to the end of the text and answers a symbol. A whole unterminated call disappeared into a variable name.\n\nWHERE THE FILING WAS RIGHT AND IT MATTERS: the end-of-text half really is narration. Both tools refuse, both exit 2, neither writes anything, and only the word differs. That half is gated by the unit test rather than by a fixture, which is the honest instrument for it.\n\nTHE PROBE, 38 shapes against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. GNU on the left, `was` is what Ronin did before.\n\n  THE NAME TERMINATOR — where the whole node lives\n    $(subst<TAB>a,b,aaa)              bbb            was empty\n    $(subst<NL>a,b,aaa) in a define   bbb            was empty\n    $(subst<VT>a,b,aaa)               bbb            was empty\n    $(subst<FF>a,b,aaa)               bbb            was empty\n    $(subst<CR>a,b,aaa)               bbb            was empty\n    $(word<TAB>1,a b c)               a              was empty\n    $(if<TAB>,then,else)              else           was empty\n    $(call<TAB>F,hi) over F = [$(1)]  [hi]           was empty\n    $(shell<TAB>echo hi)              hi             was empty, and the shell never ran\n    all: $(shell<TAB>echo dep)        dep built      was no prerequisite at all\n    a recipe holding $(subst<TAB>...) bbb            was empty\n    $(subst a,b,aaa) with a space     bbb            agreed already\n    $(subst\\<newline> a,b,aaa)        bbb            agreed already\n\n  WHAT DOES NOT END A NAME, and must not start doing so\n    $(subst)                          empty          agreed already   <- the close is not whitespace\n    $(subst,a,b,aaa)                  empty          agreed already   <- nor is a comma\n    $(FOO<TAB>BAR)                    empty          agreed already   <- not a function's name\n    $(FOO<TAB>BAR) as a rule line     missing separator, both\n    $((                               unterminated variable reference, both\n    $(subst(                          unterminated variable reference, both\n    $(V:a=z) over V = aaa bbb         aaz bbb        agreed already\n    $(strip $(subst a,b, aaa ))       bbb            agreed already\n    $() and $(<TAB>)                  empty          agreed already\n\n  THE END OF THE TEXT — the shape the node was filed for\n    X := $(subst                      unterminated call 'subst'   was unterminated variable reference\n    X := ${subst                      unterminated call, missing '}'  was unterminated variable reference\n    X := $(shell                      unterminated call 'shell'   was unterminated variable reference\n    X := $(call                       unterminated call 'call'    was unterminated variable reference\n    X := $(subst at end of line 1, more lines after   the same    the same\n    $(subst as the last line of a define body         the same    the same\n    X := $(substx                     unterminated variable reference, both\n    X := $(sub                        unterminated variable reference, both\n    X := $(subst,a,b                  unterminated variable reference, both\n    X = $(subst   never expanded      builds                      agreed already\n    the same in an ignored branch     builds                      agreed already\n\n  THE UNTERMINATED CALL A TAB NAMED — a build divergence, not a wording one\n    X := $(subst<TAB>a,b,c            refuses                     was refused (no inner paren)\n    X := $(subst<TAB>$(shell touch m),b,c   refuses, m not written    WAS BUILT, exit 0\n\nFIXTURES, three, oracle-recorded, all gating effects:\n\n* `tests/make/a-function-name-a-tab-ends-is-still-a-call` — a tab-named `$(subst)` in a binding and a tab-named `$(shell)` in a prerequisite list, in one file. Gates both the substituted text and the file the vanished prerequisite's rule writes.\n* `tests/make/a-function-name-a-newline-ends-is-still-a-call` — the same question by the other byte, from a `define` body, which is where a call meets a newline.\n* `tests/make/an-unterminated-call-a-tab-named-is-still-a-call` — the refusal. GNU Make fails and writes nothing; before the fix Ronin built.\n\nTEETH, measured by parking the whole kati diff and rebuilding: `3 of 923 ported cases diverge from GNU Make's build intent` — `'made-by-a-tab' was not produced`, `'out' holds \"[]\" where GNU Make left \"[bbb]\"`, and `build succeeded where GNU Make's build failed`. One divergence per fixture, no overlap.\n\nPlus `kati/src-rs/expr.rs::a_name_is_a_call_wherever_gnu_make_ends_one`, thirteen shapes over the classification itself: the six whitespace bytes and the end of the text on one side, the close paren and the comma on the other, and the two names `lookup_function` would refuse. It holds the half no effect gate can see — `$(subst` and `$(substx` both refuse and both write nothing, so the only thing separating them is which complaint is made.\n\nTHE C++ MIRROR took the terminator half. `ParseDollar` in src/expr.cc has the same `terms` array and the same branch, so both readers end a name in the same place. The end-of-text half stays out of it for the reason the previous node gave for staying out entirely: it rides on the held `Unreadable` value, and the C++ raises at that site instead of holding, so half of this cannot exist there. What is mirrored is complete on its own terms and does not make the two files disagree anywhere they agreed before.\n\nWHERE: kati b189c565a00f3f567c82c51dcbc8966cd991f371 on nplan/make-an-unterminated-call-with-no-space-is-read-as-a-reference, pushed before this pin.\n\nGATES, run serially on a quiet host. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; all three clippy passes clean; workspace 956 passed / 0 failed (955 plus the one new kati case); `--no-default-features` 752 / 0; make_port 4/4 over 923 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved, 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact and nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 1 both-refused / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 at 134 unclassified / 0 compiler / 25 interface / 1126 narration, tsv byte-identical, no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO; ninja conformance 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 20s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 29s at -j8.\n\nNothing moved in any corpus, which is worth saying plainly: no case in the conformance suite, the equivalence suite, the upstream inventory, vim or zsh writes a tab between a function's name and its arguments. The three fixtures are the only witnesses, and they were written because the probe found the shapes, not because a corpus did."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T22:00:02.795768926Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-an-unreadable-condition-after-else-is-extraneous-text
                title "Warn about an else-if that cannot be read, as GNU Make warns rather than refuses"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-refused-conditions-first-string-is-not-expanded)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T17:35:35.066090172Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by the probe for make-a-refused-conditions-first-string-is-not-expanded and left out of it, because that node is about WHAT a refused condition has already done and this is about whether it refuses at all.\n\nAn `ifeq` whose condition cannot be read is a fatal. The same condition written after an `else` is NOT: GNU Make's `conditional_line` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c) recurses into the text after `else`, and on `< 0` reaches `EXTRATEXT()`, which is `error` — it prints and the read carries on. So an unreadable `else if...` degrades to a plain `else` with a warning.\n\n    ifeq (a,b)\n    else ifeq (x,y junk\n    endif\n    endif\n    all: ; @echo built > out\n\n      GNU    exit 0, `out` written, `Makefile:2: extraneous text after 'else' directive`\n      Ronin  exit 2, `Makefile:4: extraneous 'endif'.`\n\nThere are TWO endifs there and that is not a typo — it is the second half of the defect. The recursive `conditional_line` has already done `o = conditionals->if_cmds++` by the time the `ifeq` arm returns -1, and the `else` branch only decrements `if_cmds` on the success path, so a refused `else if` leaves an extra open level behind. GNU is then one `endif` short for the rest of the file, which is why the shape above balances with two and why the one-endif spelling dies at EOF instead:\n\n    ifeq (a,b)\n    else ifeq (x,y junk\n    endif\n    all: ; @echo built > out\n\n      GNU    Makefile:2: extraneous text after 'else' directive, then Makefile:5: *** missing 'endif'.  Stop.\n      Ronin  Makefile:2: invalid syntax in conditional.\n\nBoth refuse there, so only the first shape is a build divergence — but it is a real one: GNU builds and Ronin does not.\n\nWHAT MUST NOT BE COPIED. A third spelling looked like a third cell and is not:\n\n    ifeq (a,b)\n    X := from-then\n    else ifeq (x,y junk\n    X := from-else\n    endif\n    endif\n    all: ; @echo [$(X)] > out       GNU exit 0, `[from-else]`\n\nThat answer is not a contract. `conditionals->ignoring[o]` is never assigned when the `ifeq` arm returns -1 — the array is `xmalloc`ed and never initialised — and `DONE:` then reads it to decide whether the following lines are ignored. The oracle answers `from-else` on this build and is reading uninitialised heap to do it. So the branch's CONTENT is out of scope; only the nesting count and the warning are, and the two-endif cell above is the one to gate because its outcome does not depend on that byte.\n\nReproducing it means letting `parse_else`'s recursion into a following `if...` record `CondComplaint::ExtraneousText` on the `else` and still push the level the failed conditional would have pushed, rather than raising. The `else` machinery bf2f818 and 3db667f left is the right place; the nesting-count half is the same question `an_unreadable_condition_still_holds_its_nesting_open` already gates one level up.\n\nGate the two-endif cell through the port harness: GNU builds and writes the file, so the effect is comparable without touching either tool's wording."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T21:23:33.819793953Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T21:29:57.848873006Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "NOT FIXED, AND THE REASON IS THE FIX ITSELF. The filing read GNU Make's mechanism exactly right and named a real build divergence. What it did not see is that GNU Make has TWO answers for the same text and chooses between them on a value, and that a reader which compiles the whole file before evaluating any of it has to answer once. It already answers with the better of the two, and the other answer is worse in both directions.\n\nWHAT THE FILING GOT RIGHT. `conditional_line` (reference/gnumake/src/read.c) recurses into the text after an `else`. The recursion does `o = conditionals->if_cmds++` before it looks at the condition, and the `else` gives that level back with `--conditionals->if_cmds` only on the success path — the `< 0` path reaches `EXTRATEXT()`, which is `error`: it prints and the read carries on. So a refused `else if...` leaves a level standing and the rest of the file is one `endif` short. All of that is confirmed.\n\nWHAT THE FILING DID NOT SEE. The recursion does not always read the condition. Before it does, it runs\n\n    for (i = 0; i < o; ++i)\n      if (conditionals->ignoring[i])\n        { conditionals->ignoring[o] = 1; return 1; }\n\nso an `else if...` sitting under a branch that is already being ignored returns SUCCESS without touching the condition, and the level IS given back. The same unreadable text therefore takes ONE `endif` when the `else` arm is dead and TWO when it is live, and which of those happens is decided by the value of the enclosing condition — a runtime fact.\n\nTHE 2x2, all four cells measured. The text is `ifeq (<a>,<b>)` / `else ifeq (x,y junk` / one or two `endif`s / `all: ; @echo built > out`.\n\n                          one endif                       two endifs\n  else arm LIVE     missing 'endif', refuses        warns, BUILDS\n  else arm DEAD     builds, silently               extraneous 'endif', refuses\n\nRonin answers the bottom row for both columns, because that is the row where the `endif` closes the `else` and the conditional the `else` carried — the same rule a readable `else if...` follows, which is what `num_nest` already encodes in `parse_else`.\n\nWHY THAT ROW AND NOT THE OTHER. Not by counting cells — by the direction of the disagreement.\n\n* Ronin's row disagrees only in the top-right cell: GNU Make builds a file with a second `endif` and Ronin refuses it. Ronin never builds a file GNU Make refuses.\n* The other row disagrees in both bottom cells: it would refuse `ifeq (a,a) / else ifeq (x,y junk / endif` which GNU Make builds, AND it would build `... / endif / endif` which GNU Make refuses. The second of those is the dangerous direction.\n\nThe other row also contradicts a case the corpus already holds. `tests/make/an-else-if-under-a-taken-branch-is-not-read` gates exactly the dead-arm reading, including a chain whose third link is unreadable, and it was recorded from the oracle. Implementing the filing would have broken it — measured, not reasoned: with the failed `else if...` given an ordinary nesting level instead, `make_port` reports `3 of 925 ported cases diverge`, and one of the three is that existing case as `build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`.\n\nAND THE NATURALLY WRITTEN FILE ALREADY AGREES. The divergent cell needs a SECOND `endif` — a file written to balance GNU Make's accounting slip. A file written the ordinary way has one, and there Ronin matches GNU Make's outcome in both rows: dead arm, both build; live arm, both refuse. Only the wording of the refusal differs, and the two wordings are two faces of one read — GNU Make says `Makefile:2: extraneous text after 'else' directive` and then `Makefile:5: *** missing 'endif'.`, Ronin says `Makefile:2: invalid syntax in conditional.` at the line that caused it. Making Ronin say GNU's words is not available either: under this row the file HAS no missing `endif`, so the only way to reach that diagnostic is to stop refusing, which turns the cell into a build divergence in the dangerous direction.\n\nWHY THE FIX IS NOT EXPRESSIBLE, rather than merely awkward. The two rows do not differ only in how many `endif`s are consumed; they differ in WHERE the statements after the first `endif` live. Measured:\n\n    ifeq (m,m)\n    ifeq (a,b)\n    else ifeq (x,y junk\n    endif\n    endif\n    X := reached\n    endif\n    all: ; @echo [$(X)] > out          GNU: [reached]. Ronin: Makefile:7: extraneous 'endif'.\n\nFor GNU Make with the arm live, line 5 closes the level the refusal left and `X := reached` is inside `ifeq (m,m)`; with the arm dead, line 5 closes `ifeq (m,m)` and line 7 is the fatal. A statement tree can hold one placement or the other, not one that is chosen later — deferring the decision to evaluation would mean carrying the tail of the file twice. That is a different reader, not a fix.\n\nTHE UNSAFE SPELLING, and it is worse than the filing recorded — which is the point of re-probing it. The filing said the branch-content spelling reads uninitialised heap on GNU's side and recorded the answer as `from-else`. Measured today the same makefile answers `[]`. Both are real, and the difference was reproduced deliberately inside one session on one binary:\n\n    ifeq (a,b) / X := from-then / else ifeq (x,y junk / X := from-else / endif / endif\n      alone                                      GNU: []\n      with `ifeq (p,p)` / `ifeq (q,q)` / `endif` / `endif` in front of it   GNU: [from-else]\n\nThe prefix is balanced and changes nothing about the shape. What it changes is what is left in `conditionals->ignoring[o+1]`, which the `< 0` path never assigns and `DONE:` then reads. So the filing's `from-else` and this dispatch's `[]` are the same undefined read answering differently, and the caution the filing recorded is correct and now has a reproducer for both answers rather than one.\n\nWHAT WAS DELIVERED, since no behaviour moved:\n\n* `tests/make/a-second-endif-after-an-unread-else-if-is-extraneous`, oracle-recorded. `ifeq (a,a) / else ifeq (x,y junk / endif / endif` — GNU Make refuses and writes nothing. Nothing gated the `endif` COUNT before; the existing case gates only which branch is read. Teeth measured against the plausible wrong implementation — the failed `else if...` given an ordinary nesting level — where it fails as `build succeeded where GNU Make's build failed`.\n* `kati/src-rs/parser.rs::an_unreadable_else_if_is_closed_by_the_endif_that_closes_the_else`, which pins both halves of the answer (one `endif` closes both levels; a second is extraneous) and carries the reasoning above at the site, so the next reader who finds the divergent cell finds the argument before rewriting `parse_else`.\n\nThe dead-arm branch content is defined behaviour and was checked for stability before anything was recorded: the ignored level's `ignoring[o]` IS assigned by the loop above, and the case answers `the-then-branch` unchanged under the prefix that flips the live-arm cell and under four levels of nesting.\n\nWHAT STAYS DIVERGENT, recorded rather than fixed: a makefile carrying an unreadable `else if...` in a live branch AND a second `endif` builds under GNU Make 4.4.1 and is refused here. Nothing in the conformance corpus, the equivalence corpus, the upstream inventory, vim or zsh contains one, and the only way to write one is to have balanced the file against GNU Make's own slip."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T21:32:35.509579582Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "WHERE: kati 00a11e2877762a9cbf4b31fd4b69435edbf7f2c1 on nplan/make-an-unreadable-condition-after-else-is-extraneous-text, pushed before this pin. The kati commit adds the unit test and its reasoning and changes no behaviour; the fixture is on the Ronin side.\n\nGATES, run serially on a quiet host. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; all three clippy passes clean; workspace 957 passed / 0 failed (956 plus the one new kati case); `--no-default-features` 753 / 0; make_port 4/4 over 924 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved, 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact and nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 1 both-refused / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 at 134 unclassified / 0 compiler / 25 interface / 1126 narration, tsv byte-identical, no drift line, compiler bucket ZERO; ninja conformance 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 17s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 28s at -j8."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T21:32:58.812572939Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T22:01:17.61489056Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "The kati SHA in the note above was written wrong and is corrected in place. It read `00a11e2b7a58e6ff2a30c4a8e0e2eb1c26ea9b5c`, whose tail was invented from the short form rather than read; the commit is `00a11e2877762a9cbf4b31fd4b69435edbf7f2c1`, which `git rev-parse nplan/make-an-unreadable-condition-after-else-is-extraneous-text` answers and which `origin` carries. The seven-character prefix was right, so nothing pointed anywhere else; the rest of it pointed nowhere at all. Recorded rather than quietly fixed, because a SHA nobody can resolve is exactly the kind of thing a later reader would assume was a rebase."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-an-environment-variable-is-a-recursive-one
                title "Expand an environment variable's value, as GNU Make expands a recursive one"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T21:48:19.455310411Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by the probe for make-a-diagnostic-inside-a-define-names-the-call-not-the-define, which was checking where a diagnostic raised inside an environment variable's expansion points and discovered there is no expansion.\n\nGNU Make enters every environment variable as RECURSIVE — `define_variable_in_set (..., o_env, 1)` in main.c, where the last argument is `recursive` — so a `$` in an environment value is make syntax and is expanded at every reference. kati enters them as simple and hands the bytes through unread, which is a silent wrong build rather than a diagnostic: the unexpanded text reaches the recipe and the shell tries to run it.\n\n    FROMENV='$(words a b c)' make\n\n    all: ; @echo [$(FROMENV)] > out\n      GNU    exit 0, out holds `[3]`\n      Ronin  exit 0, out holds `[]`, and stderr carries `/bin/sh: 1: words: not found`\n\n    X := $(FROMENV)\n    all: ; @echo [$(X)] > out\n      GNU    exit 0, out holds `[3]`\n      Ronin  the same failure one step earlier -- `$(words a b c)` is stored in X verbatim\n\n    FROMENV=literal                     both `[literal]`, so it is only values holding a `$`\n\nThe flavour is already REPORTED correctly, which is what makes this a plain defect rather than a design question: `$(flavor FROMENV)` answers `recursive` on both tools, `$(origin FROMENV)` answers `environment` on both, and `$(value FROMENV)` answers `$(words a b c)` on both. Only the expansion is missing — kati says the variable is recursive and then does not treat it as one.\n\nWHERE IT LIVES: whatever builds the environment bindings at startup makes them with `Variable::with_simple_string`, where a recursive one wants the parsed value a `=` assignment produces. Worth checking `-e` / `EnvironmentOverride` and `MAKEFLAGS`-carried bindings at the same time, and worth checking what `$(shell)` and the exported set do with a value that now expands, since an exported variable holding a `$(shell)` is what `environment_recursion` exists for.\n\nNot fixed here because it is a variable-flavour question rather than a diagnostic-location one, and because the blast radius is every environment variable in every build — it wants its own gates."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T22:10:50.572658197Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T22:35:28.602098506Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was right end to end — the first one in a while that needed no correction. What the probe added is the boundary: which environment names are NOT this, and how much of the answer was already standing.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, one branch in kati and all Rust. `read_invocation_state` (kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs) built each imported binding as `Variable::new_recursive(Arc::new(Value::Literal(None, bytes)), ...)` — flagged recursive, holding a literal, and a literal expands to itself. GNU Make's `main` enters the same text with `define_variable_in_set (name, len, value, o_env, 1)`, the trailing 1 being the recursive flag, so the value is makefile text. The literal is now `parse_expr(&mut ev.session, &mut loc, v.clone(), ParseExprOpt::Normal)?` — the same call `X = text` already made, in the same file, four hundred lines up (`claim_recursive_at_default`).\n\nPARSING IS NOT EXPANDING, which is the whole reason this is safe to do at startup. The expression is built once and evaluated only where something reads the name, so every laziness GNU Make has survives: an environment value nothing refers to is never read, one referred to twice runs its `$(shell)` twice, one holding `$(error)` raises only at the reference, and one whose call is unterminated is a held `Value::Unreadable` that raises where it is read. That last one is the load-bearing detail — kati's parser holds a missing close rather than refusing at parse time, so `UNREAD=$(words a b` in the environment of a makefile that never mentions it is not a diagnostic in either tool.\n\nTHE DIAGNOSTIC LOCATIONS CAME FREE, and they came from the node before this one. Every complaint an environment value can raise now points where GNU Make points — `Makefile:1`, the line the reference is written on — because `Variable::expansion_loc()` answers `None` for `VarOrigin::Environment` and `expanding_var_loc()` falls back to `ev.loc`, which is `reading_file`. That was make-a-diagnostic-inside-a-define-names-the-call-not-the-define's work; without it these would all have pointed at the parse's own `Loc::default()`, which is nowhere.\n\nMAKELEVEL IS THE EXCEPTION AND IT IS GNU MAKE'S. `define_automatic_variables` writes it over again from the depth it parsed, `define_variable_cname (MAKELEVEL_NAME, buf, o_env, 0)` — recursive flag OFF. So the one imported name whose value is a number stays simple even when the invocation puts an expression there, and the branch for it stays.\n\nNOTHING ABOUT EXPORT MOVED, and it was checked rather than assumed. `target_environment` (variable.c) reads: \"If V is recursively expanded and didn't come from the environment, expand its value. If it came from the environment, it should go back into the environment unchanged... except MAKEFLAGS.\" kati's export path already skipped environment-origin bindings for exactly that reason, so a child still receives the bytes the parent was given — measured on both tools, and it is what makes the parent/child pair below agree.\n\nTHE PROBE, 40 shapes against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. GNU on the left; `was` is what Ronin answered before.\n\n  THE VALUE IS READ — this node\n    $(FROMENV) in a recipe, FROMENV=$(words a b c)        [3]            was []\n    X := $(FROMENV) at read time                          [3]            was []\n    $(FROMENV) in a prerequisite list                     a.dep b.dep    was a refusal on '$(addsuffix'\n    $(FROMENV) as a goal's prerequisite name              built          was a refusal on '$(strip'\n    ifeq ($(FROMENV),3) chooses a branch                  yes            was no\n    an env value reaching another env value               inner          was []\n    an env value reaching a MAKEFILE variable             from-makefile  was []\n    an env value reaching a makefile $(call)              <q>            was []\n    $(FROMENV:x=y) through a substitution reference       a.o b.o        was the text\n    $(eval) inside an env value                           set            was []\n    a target-specific `all: V := $(FROMENV)`              [3]            was []\n    $@ inside an env value, read in a recipe              all            was []\n    FROMENV := $(FROMENV), makefile re-assigns            make=3 child=3 was the text, both sides\n    the include directive's file name                     inc.mk         was '$(strip'\n    a literal dollar, FROMENV=a$$b, read via $(file)      [a$b]          was [a$$b]\n    the same read through a recipe                        [a]            was [a<pid>]  <- the shell ran $$\n\n  LAZINESS — must not become eager, and did not\n    $(error) in an env value nothing refers to            builds         agreed already\n    the same env value referenced                         Makefile:1     was silently empty\n    $(shell) in an env value nothing refers to            never runs     agreed already\n    $(shell) referenced twice                             runs twice     agreed by accident (the SHELL ran it twice)\n    an unterminated call nothing refers to                builds         agreed already\n    the same referenced          Makefile:1 unterminated call           was a shell syntax error\n    an env value referencing itself      Recursive variable references itself   was [x]\n\n  NOT THIS — the names the environment does not simply hand over\n    MAKELEVEL=$(words a b c)          [0][simple][environment]     agreed already\n    MFLAGS=$(words a b c)             [][recursive][environment]   agreed already\n    SHELL=$(words a b c)              [/bin/sh][file][recursive]   agreed already   <- a490e27's non-import\n    CURDIR=$(words a b c)             [file]                       agreed already\n    -e promotes the env binding       [3][recursive][environment override]   was []\n    no -e, the makefile wins          [from-makefile][file]        agreed already\n    a command-line X=$(words a b c)   [3][recursive][command line] agreed already\n    a command-line X:=$(words a b c)  [3][simple]                  agreed already\n\n  WHAT DESCENDS — raw, in both tools, before and after\n    \"$$FROMENV\" in a recipe's shell            $(words a b c)     agreed already\n    \"$$FROMENV\" inside a $(shell)              $(words a b c)     agreed already\n    $(FROMENV) beside it in the same recipe    parent=3 raw=$(words a b c)   was raw on both sides\n    a makefile that re-assigns simple          make=3 child=3     was the text on both sides\n    MAKEFLAGS to a child under -e              ek                 agreed already\n\n  STILL DIVERGENT — filed, not fixed\n    MAKEFLAGS=$(subst X,-,Xk)         k / invalid option -- '$'\n    GNUMAKEFLAGS=-k                   k / not read at all\n\nFIXTURES, four, oracle-recorded, under tests/make:\n\n* `an-environment-values-dollar-is-makefile-syntax` — the filing's [3] cell as a build that BRANCHES on it: `ifeq ($(COUNT),3)` selects which of two rules `all` depends on, so the graph and not just the bytes carry the answer. Beside it in the same recording: `$(value)`, `$(flavor)` and `$(origin)` (all three of which already agreed, which is what made this a plain defect), `a$$b` halving to `a$b` while `$(value)` keeps both dollars, an env value reaching another env value, and an env value reaching a variable the makefile defined.\n* `an-environment-value-names-what-the-build-depends-on` — `all: $(DEPS)` with `DEPS=$(addsuffix .dep,a b)` in the environment. Two files that only exist if the expansion happened.\n* `an-environment-value-is-read-only-where-it-is-referenced` — the laziness gate. `$(error)`, a `$(shell)` that would leave a file, and an unterminated call, all in the environment and none of them referenced; a fourth name referenced twice so its `$(shell)` appends twice. An eager reader refuses this makefile outright.\n* `an-environment-name-make-defines-over-again-is-not-an-expression` — MAKELEVEL's flavour and SHELL's non-import, with a `$` in both environment values.\n\nTEETH, measured by parking the whole kati diff and rebuilding: three of the four fail — a refusal, a refusal, and `out` holding `branch=[the value was bytes]` where GNU left the three expanded lines. The fourth has teeth against the plausible OVER-broad fix rather than against the parked one: dropping MAKELEVEL's branch so every imported name becomes recursive fails it on `flavor=[recursive]` against GNU's `simple`, and takes the existing `the-names-make-works-out-for-itself-are-simple` with it. Both measured, not reasoned.\n\nRUST ONLY, for the reason the last three nodes gave: `src/eval.cc` has no `parse_expr` call on this path and the C++ tree is neither built nor tested here.\n\nFOUND AND FILED, not fixed: make-gnumakeflags-carries-switches-too. `GNUMAKEFLAGS=-k` in the environment is `-k` in GNU Make and nothing at all here, which is a wrong build and not a wrong report — a keep-going build writes its second output under GNU and writes nothing here. The MAKEFLAGS half of the same question (GNU's `decode_env_switches` expands `$(MAKEFLAGS)` before splitting it into words; Ronin splits the raw bytes) is recorded on make-makeflags-holds-its-switches-as-literal-text, which owns the expansion."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T22:35:41.147440389Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "WHERE: kati 4ba7622e539aa20482ab4b2297774501e4dac08a on nplan/make-an-environment-variable-is-a-recursive-one, pushed before this pin. One branch in `read_invocation_state`; the four fixtures are on the Ronin side.\n\nGATES, run serially on a quiet host. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; clippy pedantic+nursery over ronin lib+bins --no-deps clean and plain `-D warnings` over kati --all-targets clean; workspace 958 passed / 0 failed, unmoved (the four new cases live inside make_port's single test); `--no-default-features` 753 / 0; make_port 4/4 over 928 gated cases with no existing recording rewritten; conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved, 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact and nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 1 both-refused / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 at 134 unclassified / 0 compiler / 25 interface / 1126 narration, tsv byte-identical with no drift line and no row moved, compiler bucket ZERO; ninja conformance 425 upstream across 33 suites plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 20s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 34s at -j8.\n\nA note on the inventory, since env-var rows were the ones expected to move: none did. The suite's cases set their environment through the harness rather than through the invocation, and the 134 unclassified are the same 134 — byte-identical tsv is the evidence."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T22:35:44.716324909Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-gnumakeflags-carries-switches-too
                title "Read GNUMAKEFLAGS's switches and clear it, as GNU Make does before MAKEFLAGS"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T22:34:39.09774989Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing which of the environment's o_env entries GNU Make expands, for make-an-environment-variable-is-a-recursive-one. It is a separate defect and a plain one: GNU Make reads switches out of GNUMAKEFLAGS and Ronin does not read them at all.\n\nGNU Make's `main` (reference/gnumake/src/main.c) does this immediately before the same call on MAKEFLAGS:\n\n    decode_env_switches (STRING_SIZE_TUPLE (GNUMAKEFLAGS_NAME), o_command);\n    /* Clear GNUMAKEFLAGS to avoid duplication.  */\n    define_variable_cname (GNUMAKEFLAGS_NAME, \"\", o_env, 0);\n\nso the switches fold into MAKEFLAGS and the name is emptied so a child does not read them twice. kati knows the name -- `(\"GNUMAKEFLAGS\", \"\")` in builtins.rs's default catalogue -- and does nothing else with it.\n\nPROBED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left, with `GNUMAKEFLAGS=-k` in the environment:\n\n    $(MAKEFLAGS) / $(GNUMAKEFLAGS)      `k` / ``       is     `` / `-k`\n    unset (control)                     `` / ``        agrees\n\nAND IT IS A BUILD DIVERGENCE, not a reporting one:\n\n    all: bad good\n    bad: ; @false\n    good: ; @echo [ran] > out\n      GNU    exits 2 and `out` holds `[ran]` -- -k arrived, so `good` still ran\n      Ronin  exits 2 and writes nothing\n\nWorth doing together with what MAKEFLAGS's own decode does to a `$`, because both live in whatever reads switches out of the environment: GNU's `decode_env_switches` expands `$(MAKEFLAGS)` through `variable_expand` before splitting it into words, and Ronin splits the raw bytes. `MAKEFLAGS='$(subst X,-,Xk)'` is `k` in GNU and `invalid option -- '$'` here. That second half is recorded on make-makeflags-holds-its-switches-as-literal-text, which owns the expansion question; this node owns the name."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T23:26:15.808746906Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T23:57:16.902853105Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was right about the defect and short by one site. The probe found a SECOND decode GNU Make performs on the same name, at the other end of the read, and it is a build divergence too — so the node carries both.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, and there are two of them because GNU Make has two.\n\nSTARTUP, main.c 1592-1597, before the same call on `MAKEFLAGS`:\n\n    if (lookup_variable (STRING_SIZE_TUPLE (GNUMAKEFLAGS_NAME)))\n      {\n        decode_env_switches (STRING_SIZE_TUPLE (GNUMAKEFLAGS_NAME), o_command);\n        define_variable_cname (GNUMAKEFLAGS_NAME, \"\", o_env, 0);\n      }\n\n`parse` (src/make/cli.rs) now reads two streams instead of one, in that order, both at `ArgumentSource::Inherited` — which is `o_command`, so a word either of them got wrong ends the run with the usage GNU prints. Nothing else in the parser knows there are two: everything either stream contributes is published as `MAKEFLAGS`, which is the whole architecture of the name. The clearing is `record_invocation_variables`, which empties rather than withdraws — GNU Make's guard exists precisely so a Make handed no second stream does not invent one for its children, and emptying and withdrawing are what tell those two apart.\n\nAFTER THE READ, main.c 2086-2089, which the filing did not have:\n\n    decode_env_switches (STRING_SIZE_TUPLE (GNUMAKEFLAGS_NAME), o_env);\n    define_variable_cname (GNUMAKEFLAGS_NAME, \"\", o_override, 0);\n\nunconditional this time, and it is why `$(origin GNUMAKEFLAGS)` answers `override` to every recipe in every run. `GNUMAKEFLAGS` is not a special variable, so unlike `MAKEFLAGS` it is never intercepted where it is written (`set_special_var`, variable.c) — its whole effect is decided once, here, over whatever the last makefile left in it. `decode_gnumakeflags_after_read` (kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs) sits exactly where GNU Make's does, before the `-R` catalogue withdrawal, so a `-R` written there still takes the catalogue away. The folding half of `normalize_makeflags_assignment` became `fold_switches_into_makeflags` and both sites call it.\n\nTHE PROBE, 30 shapes against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. GNU on the left; `was` is what Ronin answered before.\n\n  THE ENVIRONMENT'S STREAM — the filed defect\n    GNUMAKEFLAGS=-k, all: bad good     out holds [ran]   was nothing written\n    $(MAKEFLAGS) / $(MFLAGS)           k / -k            was empty / empty\n    $(GNUMAKEFLAGS)                    empty             was -k\n    $(origin) at read time             environment       agreed by accident\n    $(flavor) / $(value)               simple / empty    was recursive / -k\n    -Iincdir, an -include beside it    the fragment is found   was not found\n    GNUMAKEFLAGS=FOO=bar               command line, $(MAKEOVERRIDES)=FOO=bar   was undefined\n    GNUMAKEFLAGS=k (no dash)           k                 the prepended dash, was empty\n    --no-print-directory -e -r -R --trace   erR --trace --no-print-directory   was empty\n    GNUMAKEFLAGS=--zzz                 unrecognized option, usage, exit 2   was a clean build\n    beside MAKEFLAGS=-s                ks                was s\n    under -e                           ek, origin environment override        was e\n    unset (control)                    empty, origin default, flavor recursive    agreed already\n    GNUMAKEFLAGS= (control)            empty, origin environment, flavor simple   was recursive\n\n  THE DESCENT — upstream's own question (tests/scripts/variables/GNUMAKEFLAGS)\n    child's $$MAKEFLAGS                -Itst/bad         was empty\n    child's $$GNUMAKEFLAGS             empty             was -Itst/bad, decoded twice\n    the recursion case verbatim        every line        now every line\n    no GNUMAKEFLAGS given              child's origin is `default`, the name is not in its environment   agreed already\n\n  THE MAKEFILE'S OWN — found here, not in the filing\n    GNUMAKEFLAGS := -k in a makefile   out holds [ran]   was nothing written\n    read time                          -k, origin file, MAKEFLAGS empty      agreed already\n    recipe time                        empty, origin override                was -k / file\n    undefine GNUMAKEFLAGS              read time undefined, recipe time override    was undefined at both\n    GNUMAKEFLAGS := mine               decodes as -m -i -n -e, so the build is a dry run  was ignored\n    a run that was handed none         recipe time still override / simple   was default / recursive\n\nWHAT THE STREAM CANNOT DO, checked rather than assumed: nothing in it reaches a name of its own. There is no `$(GNUMAKEFLAGS)` after startup to read, its `-I` lands in `.INCLUDE_DIRS` beside every other `-I`, and its assignments land in `MAKEOVERRIDES` beside every other one. That is what makes this a small change to a parser rather than a second option system.\n\nFIXTURES, five, oracle-recorded, under tests/make. Teeth measured by parking the three pieces of the change and rebuilding — four of the five fail:\n\n* `a-second-option-stream-in-the-environment-carries-switches-too` — the filed defect as a BUILD: `-k` in the environment's second stream, `all: bad good`, and `kept-going` exists only because `good` ran after `bad` failed. Parked: `kept-going` was not produced. Beside it in the same recording, the four cells of the emptied name.\n* `the-second-option-stream-names-directories-and-binds-names` — `-Ifragments FOO=…` in one value, an `include` that resolves only through the search path and a `$(origin FOO)` of `command line`. Parked: the build FAILS, because the fragment is not found.\n* `a-child-reads-the-second-option-stream-emptied-or-not-at-all` — upstream's recursion case. Parked: the child reads `gmf=[-Itst/bad]` and a `MAKEFLAGS` without the switch — decoded twice and published never.\n* `a-makefiles-own-second-option-stream-is-read-once-the-read-is-over` — the site the filing missed, again as a build: `GNUMAKEFLAGS := -k` written in the makefile, `kept-going` written by the recipe that only runs because the write was read. Parked: not produced. The recipe line also carries the `override` origin.\n* `a-make-given-no-second-option-stream-invents-none-for-its-child` — the one case with NO teeth against the parked change, and it is the one that matters most for review: its teeth are against the plausible OVER-broad fix. Clearing the name unconditionally instead of only where the invocation was given one fails exactly this case and nothing else, on the child reading `environment` where GNU Make leaves `default`. Measured both ways.\n\nPlus `both_environment_option_streams_are_read_gnumakeflags_first` (src/make/cli.rs), which pins the order and the leading-cluster rule at the parser. Every one of its eight assertions was read off the oracle rather than reasoned about.\n\n`tests/make_port.rs` now removes `GNUMAKEFLAGS` from the environment it hands every case, beside `MAKEFLAGS`. A host that exported one would have been handing all 936 cases switches they never asked for, and the recording would have carried them.\n\nTHE SECOND HALF THE FILING RECORDED — `MAKEFLAGS='$(subst X,-,Xk)'` — IS NOT DONE, and the reason is not the one the dispatch expected. It does NOT collide with the literal-text decision; it composes with it, and cleanly. Probed precisely:\n\n  GNU's `decode_env_switches` builds the text `$(MAKEFLAGS)` and calls\n  `variable_expand` on it before splitting into words. At that point in `main`\n  the only names in scope are the ENVIRONMENT'S: `$(FOO)` with FOO=-i in the\n  environment gives `-i`, `$(CC)` gives nothing because `define_default_variables`\n  has not run yet, `$(warning W)` prints, `$(error)` dies, `$(shell)` runs.\n  Ronin's `makeflags_words` halves `$$` and leaves a lone `$` alone, which is the\n  exact inverse of `quote_for_makeflags` and agrees with GNU on every value a\n  Make ever WROTE. The two differ only on a value a human wrote by hand.\n\n  It composes because Ronin exports the STORED text where GNU exports the halved\n  one. `-I 'a$b'` is stored ` -Ia$$b`; a full expansion of that at the child's\n  decode gives `a$b`, which is what the `$$`-halving already gives. The recorded\n  divergence's own descent fixture would stay green under it.\n\n  What stops it is cost and shape, not correctness: expanding at that site means\n  standing up an evaluator with the environment loaded BEFORE the switches that\n  construct the session have been decoded — `-C`, `-f`, `-r`, `-R`, `--eval` all\n  come out of the stream being expanded. That is a bootstrap evaluator, a\n  different mechanism from anything in this node, and it makes `$(shell)` and\n  `$(error)` live at a point where Ronin currently has no diagnostics routing.\n  Filed as make-an-environment-option-stream-is-expanded-before-it-is-split with\n  the composition argument and the scope probe, so the operator decides whether\n  a hand-written `$` in MAKEFLAGS is worth a startup evaluator.\n\n  Note that the divergence is now UNIFORM across both streams: `GNUMAKEFLAGS='$(subst X,-,Xk)'` reads exactly as `MAKEFLAGS` does, because both go through `makeflags_arguments`. Nothing about this node made it worse or better.\n\nGATES, all green and all serial: workspace 960/0 (959 + the new parser test); make_port 4/4 over 936 with both recorded divergences intact; conformance 336/51 normalised, 286 raw, zero unclassified; equivalence 297/280/75/0; ninja conformance 425/425 plus differentials; `--no-default-features` 753/0; all three clippy passes clean; vim 9.2 in 15s and zsh 5.9.2 in 26s at -j8.\n\nINVENTORY: 1285 rows, unchanged in total, four moved and all four in `variables/GNUMAKEFLAGS` — the suite file this node is about. Compiler stays ZERO.\n\n    .diff    unclassified(ninja-progress+evaluation)          -> narration(ninja-progress)\n    .diff.1  unclassified(ninja-progress+evaluation)          -> unclassified(ninja-progress+recipe-echo+evaluation)\n    .diff.2  unclassified(…+recipe-echo+directory-announce+evaluation) -> narration(ninja-progress+directory-announce)\n    .diff.4  interface(ninja-progress+makeflags-content)      -> narration(ninja-progress)\n\n  Three conformed outright. `.diff.1` did not, and its residue was read rather than assumed: the switches now agree exactly (`erR --trace --no-print-directory` on both sides), and what is left is GNU Make's `--trace` line `Makefile:2: update target 'all' due to: target does not exist`, which Ronin does not produce — the standing debug-trace family. Class totals 134/0/25/1126 -> 132/0/24/1129."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T00:05:25.725832453Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-makeflags-keeps-its-eval-fragments-behind-a-reference
                title "Keep --eval fragments in a -*-eval-flags-*- variable, as GNU Make's MAKEFLAGS references them"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-makeflags-holds-its-switches-as-literal-text)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T22:56:54.329106641Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing the full switch matrix for make-makeflags-holds-its-switches-as-literal-text, and deliberately left out of it: that node decided what `$(MAKEFLAGS)`'s EXPANSION does, and this one is about a variable GNU Make has and Ronin does not.\n\nGNU Make's `define_makeflags` (reference/gnumake/src/main.c) writes a REFERENCE where the fragments would go:\n\n    const char evalref[] = \"$(-*-eval-flags-*-)\";\n    ...\n    if (eval_strings)\n      { *p++ = ' '; p = mempcpy (p, evalref, CSTRLEN (evalref)); }\n\nso `MAKEFLAGS`'s stored text names `-*-eval-flags-*-`, which is a separate variable holding the quoted fragments. Ronin writes the fragments inline into the published text (`compiler_flag_variables`, src/make/cli/interface.rs), which the code there already explains as a deliberate placement — the reasoning is right and the shape is one reference short.\n\nPROBED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make with `--eval='X := a$b'`, GNU on the left:\n\n    $(MAKEFLAGS)                ` --eval=X\\ :=\\ a$$b`   / the same        AGREE\n    $(value MAKEFLAGS)          ` $(-*-eval-flags-*-)`  / ` --eval=X\\ :=\\ a$$b`\n    $(-*-eval-flags-*-)         `--eval=X\\ :=\\ 1`       / `` (undefined)\n    $(origin -*-eval-flags-*-)  `automatic`             / `undefined`\n\nThe first row agrees and is why this is small: the reference names a SIMPLE variable, so expanding `MAKEFLAGS` substitutes the fragment text without halving its `$$` a second time. That one-level shielding is exactly what a literal gives too — which is why Ronin's inline spelling reads back correctly today and why fixing this must not disturb it.\n\nIt is not only cosmetic. GNU Make's own suite asks: reference/gnumake/tests/scripts/options/warn-undefined-variables lists `$(-*-eval-flags-*-)` among the special names `--warn-undefined-variables` must not warn about. A run of Ronin that implemented that warning would warn where GNU Make does not.\n\nWHAT IT WOULD TAKE: publish `-*-eval-flags-*-` as an automatic simple variable holding the quoted fragments, and build `MAKEFLAGS`'s value as a list carrying a `SymRef` to it, beside the `MAKEOVERRIDES` `SymRef` already there (`makeflags_value`, kati/src-rs/eval.rs). The trap the parent node recorded still stands and is now measured: whatever is done must leave `$(MAKEFLAGS)` reading ` --eval=X\\ :=\\ a$$b`, because the fragment's `$$` is shielded by exactly one level and no more. Gate it on `$(value MAKEFLAGS)`, `$(-*-eval-flags-*-)`, `$(origin -*-eval-flags-*-)` and the unchanged `$(MAKEFLAGS)` in one recording.\n\nDepends on make-makeflags-holds-its-switches-as-literal-text because that node decides whether the switch prefix stays a literal; it did, so the fragments' reference is the only structural change left."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T00:05:54.930271309Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T00:36:45.788627474Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was right about the shape and short about the shape's oddest part. The probe found that GNU Make's REFERENCE and the VARIABLE behind it are answered by two different questions, deliberately, and that the pair is observable in four separate cells. Reproducing only the tidy half would have been a plausible wrong answer, and two of the cases here exist to fail it.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, read off main.c rather than inferred.\n\n    const char evalref[] = \"$(-*-eval-flags-*-)\";\n    ...  if (eval_strings) { *p++ = ' '; p = mempcpy (p, evalref, CSTRLEN (evalref)); }\n\nin `define_makeflags`, beside the `$(MAKEOVERRIDES)` it writes after a ` -- `; and\n\n    define_variable_cname (\"-*-eval-flags-*-\", value, o_automatic, 0);\n\nin `main`, inside `if (eval_strings)`, once. Ronin's `compiler_flag_variables` (src/make/cli/interface.rs) now answers `base` and `eval_flags` where it answered `published`, and kati's `makeflags_value` (eval.rs) assembles both references rather than one. The variable is defined in `install_compiler_invocation_variables` beside the `-*-command-variables-*-` pair that was already there — AFTER them, because that is the order GNU Make defines them in and `$(.VARIABLES)` is a list in the order names arrived, which is a cell.\n\nTWO PROPERTIES CARRY EVERYTHING, and both were checked rather than assumed:\n\n* SIMPLE. Substituting a simple variable's bytes does not expand them a second time, so a fragment's doubled `$` is halved exactly once on the way back through `$(MAKEFLAGS)`. A literal shields it exactly once too — which is why `$(MAKEFLAGS)` agreed before this change and must go on agreeing, and it is the trap make-makeflags-holds-its-switches-as-literal-text recorded. The first fixture below fails with the change parked on four lines and passes on the `mf=[...]` line, which is that trap demonstrated rather than described.\n* AUTOMATIC. `o_automatic` outranks every origin a makefile can write at, so `-*-eval-flags-*- := mine` is declined and `undefine -*-eval-flags-*-` is declined too. Both came free from kati's origin ladder; both are gated, because free is not the same as intended.\n\nWHERE THE FILING WAS SHORT. The reference is written whenever `eval_strings` is non-empty, and `eval_strings` is a list only ever appended to — by the command line, by an inherited `MAKEFLAGS`, and by a makefile's own write to it. The VARIABLE is filled once, at startup, from what the invocation carried, and is never written again. The two are not the same question, and the difference shows in both directions:\n\n    with `--eval=X=1` on the command line\n      MAKEFLAGS := -k         GNU: mf=[k --eval=X=1]    the write cannot take the fragment off\n      MAKEFLAGS :=            GNU: mf=[ --eval=X=1]     nor can clearing it\n      MAKEFLAGS += --eval=Y=2 GNU: mf=[ --eval=X=1]     Y is named by nothing and held by nothing\n    with nothing on the command line\n      MAKEFLAGS := --eval=Y=2 GNU: mf=[ ] val=[ $(-*-eval-flags-*-)] origin=[undefined]\n      then MAKEFLAGS := -k    GNU: mf=[k ]              still naming it, still empty\n\nThat last pair is the giveaway: GNU Make writes a reference to a variable it never defined, and `$(MAKEFLAGS)` ends in the space that was to precede it. Modelled as `MakeflagsAssignment::has_evals`, sticky in one direction, beside the `has_overrides` that was already there for the same reason.\n\nTHE PROBE, 20 shapes against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. Every one of them now agrees; `was` is what Ronin answered before.\n\n  THE FOUR CELLS THE FILING NAMED\n    $(MAKEFLAGS)                 ` --eval=X\\ :=\\ a$$b`   agreed already, and must\n    $(value MAKEFLAGS)           ` $(-*-eval-flags-*-)`  was the inlined text\n    $(-*-eval-flags-*-)          `--eval=X\\ :=\\ a$$b`    was empty\n    $(origin -*-eval-flags-*-)   `automatic`             was `undefined`\n\n  AND THE ONES IT DID NOT\n    $(flavor -*-eval-flags-*-)   `simple`                was `undefined`\n    $(value -*-eval-flags-*-)    the same bytes          was empty\n    $(.VARIABLES), both odd names  `-*-command-variables-*- -*-eval-flags-*-`  was the second alone\n    two fragments                one variable, space-joined, in order          was inlined\n    a fragment beside V=cmd      ` $(-*-eval-flags-*-) -- $(MAKEOVERRIDES)`    was half a reference\n    MFLAGS                       carries no fragment     agreed already — `define_makeflags` writes\n                                                         MFLAGS before it appends the reference\n    a composed $(MAKE) child     its own automatic variable, from the fragment it decoded   was undefined\n    .EXPORT_ALL_VARIABLES        the odd name reaches no child   agreed already (automatic never exports)\n    no fragment at all           the name stays undefined and MAKEFLAGS names nothing   agreed already\n\n  THE STICKY PAIR, five shapes, all listed above, all `was` the fragment silently lost or invented.\n\n  A MAKEFILE INTERFERING\n    -*-eval-flags-*- := mine     declined, origin stays automatic   was `file`, and it landed\n    undefine -*-eval-flags-*-    declined                           was `undefined`, and it took\n\nFIXTURES, four, oracle-recorded, under tests/make. Teeth measured three ways by rebuilding, which is what the shape of this one needs — the parked change and the two nearby WRONG answers:\n\n* `makeflags-names-its-eval-fragments-rather-than-holding-them` — the cell set in one recording: `$(MAKEFLAGS)`, `$(value MAKEFLAGS)`, the odd name's value/`$(value)`/origin/flavour, both odd names' order in `$(.VARIABLES)`, MFLAGS, MAKEOVERRIDES, and `X` holding `a$b` so the shielding is a build fact and not a spelling. Parked: five of its six lines differ and `mf=[...]` does not, which is the recorded trap in one diff.\n* `a-makefile-cannot-reach-the-variable-makeflags-names` — the assignment and the `undefine`, both declined. Parked: `origin=[undefined]` and the inlined `$(value)`.\n* `a-makefiles-write-to-makeflags-cannot-take-its-fragments-away` — `MAKEFLAGS := -k` over a command-line fragment. Parked: `mf=[k]`, the fragment gone. And this is the case with teeth against the NARROWER wrong answer: deriving the reference from the write's own text instead of a flag that only ever turns on fails exactly this case and nothing else.\n* `a-makefiles-own-eval-is-named-by-makeflags-and-held-by-nothing` — `MAKEFLAGS := --eval=Y=2` with nothing on the command line, so `mf=[ ]` names an undefined variable. Parked: `mf=[ --eval=Y=2]`. And this is the case with teeth against the BROADER wrong answer: re-deriving the variable from each write, which is the obvious thing to do and which GNU Make does not do, fails exactly this case and nothing else.\n\nPlus `propagates_eval_fragments` (src/make/cli.rs), extended rather than joined by a new function — `base`, `eval_flags` and `makeflags` asserted together, because their relationship is the thing being pinned: `base`, one space and `eval_flags` is exactly what the reference resolves to.\n\nWHAT DID NOT COLLIDE. The dispatch asked for the one cell where the reference's expansion might meet the stored-text decision. There is none. The decision is about what READING `$(MAKEFLAGS)` does to the switch table's own text, and the reference resolves to a simple variable, which is one level of shielding — the same one a literal gives. Both recorded divergences and all three of their fixtures stay green, checked rather than assumed, and `--eval` descent (ec1a12a) is gated by the composed-child cell above.\n\nNOT THIS NODE, and found while probing it: `--warn-undefined-variables` is not published into `MAKEFLAGS` by Ronin at all — GNU Make's ` --warn-undefined-variables --eval=X=1` reads ` --eval=X=1` here. The suite case that made this node interesting (options/warn-undefined-variables) needs the switch itself before it needs the name, and the name is now there for it. The switch is a separate gap in the option surface and is not filed here because it is not about the fragments.\n\nGATES, all green and all serial: workspace 960/0; make_port 4/4 over 940 with both recorded divergences intact; conformance 336/51 normalised, 286 raw, zero unclassified; equivalence 297/280/75/0; upstream inventory 1285 @ 132/0/24/1129 UNMOVED, compiler ZERO; ninja conformance 425/425 plus differentials; `--no-default-features` 753/0; all three clippy passes clean; vim 9.2 in 21s and zsh 5.9.2 in 28s at -j8."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T00:38:12.832729047Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-an-environment-option-stream-is-expanded-before-it-is-split
                title "Expand MAKEFLAGS and GNUMAKEFLAGS as makefile text before splitting them into switches"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-makeflags-holds-its-switches-as-literal-text make-gnumakeflags-carries-switches-too)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T23:57:57.894817898Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Recorded on make-makeflags-holds-its-switches-as-literal-text, re-probed in full on make-gnumakeflags-carries-switches-too, and filed here rather than done because it is a different mechanism from either — not because it collides with the literal-text decision. IT DOES NOT COLLIDE. It composes. The argument is below, so the operator can decide on cost rather than on risk.\n\nTHE SITE. GNU Make's `decode_env_switches` (main.c) does not read the environment's bytes. It builds the text `$(NAME)`, calls `variable_expand` on it, and splits the RESULT into words:\n\n    p = varref; *(p++) = '$'; *(p++) = '(';\n    p = mempcpy (p, envar, len); *(p++) = ')'; *p = '\\0';\n    value = variable_expand (varref);\n\nSo the value is makefile source, and `MAKEFLAGS='$(subst X,-,Xk)'` is `-k`. Ronin's `makeflags_words` (src/make/cli/interface.rs) halves `$$` and leaves a lone `$` literal, which is the exact inverse of `quote_for_makeflags` and nothing more. `MAKEFLAGS='$(subst X,-,Xk)'` dies here with `invalid option -- '$'`.\n\nWHAT IS IN SCOPE AT THAT MOMENT, probed rather than assumed — GNU Make on the left, with the name in the environment:\n\n    MAKEFLAGS=$(FOO), FOO=-i in the environment    -i        the environment IS visible\n    MAKEFLAGS=$(CC)                                empty     `define_default_variables` (main.c:2039) has not run\n    MAKEFLAGS=$(MAKELEVEL)                         empty     nor `define_automatic_variables` (2032)\n    MAKEFLAGS=$(CURDIR), $(MAKE_HOST)              empty     the same\n    MAKEFLAGS=$(warning W)-k                       prints `make: W` with no location, then decodes -k\n    MAKEFLAGS=$(error boom)                        dies, *** boom.  Stop.\n    MAKEFLAGS=$(shell echo -k)                     runs the shell — and fails, because SHELL is not defined yet\n    MAKEFLAGS=$(.VARIABLES)                        expands, and the words decode as options\n\n  So the table holds the environment and almost nothing else. Everything a\n  makefile would recognise as a builtin is defined AFTER this point.\n\nWHY IT COMPOSES WITH THE LITERAL-TEXT DECISION (make-makeflags-holds-its-switches-as-literal-text, c8cc5a1). That decision is about what READING `$(MAKEFLAGS)` does — Ronin hands back the stored text where GNU Make halves it — and therefore about what a parent EXPORTS. The env decode is the other end of the same round trip, and the two ends stay inverse under full expansion:\n\n    directory `a$b` -> quote_for_makeflags -> stored ` -Ia$$b` -> exported ` -Ia$$b`\n      full expansion at the child's decode -> ` -Ia$b` -> directory `a$b`      lossless\n    directory `a$$b` -> stored ` -Ia$$$$b` -> full expansion -> ` -Ia$$b`      lossless\n\n  which is what the `$$`-halving already gives. `a-child-keeps-a-switch-value-a-dollar-was-written-in` — the fixture that RECORDS what the divergence buys — stays green under it, checked cell by cell. The change would be invisible to every value a Make ever wrote and visible only to one a human wrote by hand.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT IN THE GNUMAKEFLAGS NODE. Expanding at that site means standing up an evaluator with the environment loaded BEFORE the switches have been decoded — and `-C`, `-f`, `-r`, `-R` and `--eval` all come out of the stream being expanded, so the session those switches construct cannot be the one doing the expanding. That is a bootstrap evaluator: a second, smaller kati session whose only visible names are the environment, running before `enter_directories`. It also makes `$(shell)`, `$(error)` and `$(warning)` live at a point where Ronin has no diagnostics routing and no working directory settled, and GNU Make's own answers there are odd enough to need pinning (a `$(warning)` with no location prefix; a `$(shell)` with no SHELL).\n\nWHAT WOULD HAVE TO BE DECIDED, beyond the mechanism:\n* Whether `$(error)` in an inherited MAKEFLAGS ends the run before `-C` has been applied, as GNU Make's does.\n* Where a `$(warning)` raised there is reported from, given it has no makefile and no line.\n* Whether `$(shell)` runs at all, and with what SHELL, given the catalogue is not installed yet.\n* Whether the expansion runs once for the process or again for every composed `$(MAKE)` child, which decodes its parent's MAKEFLAGS the same way.\n\nTHE SURFACE IS TWO NAMES AND ONE SHAPE. Both `MAKEFLAGS` and `GNUMAKEFLAGS` go through `makeflags_arguments`, so the divergence is uniform across them: `GNUMAKEFLAGS='$(subst X,-,Xk)'` reads exactly as `MAKEFLAGS` does. Nothing in the corpora depends on it — searched for a literal `$` in an env-supplied MAKEFLAGS across the 936-case build-intent corpus, the 387-case vendored kati corpus, GNU Make's own suite at d66a65a, vim 9.2, zsh 5.9.2, abseil and Ninja, and there is none. That is the same search that decided the parent node, and it points the same way.\n\nGATE IT ON, if it is done: `MAKEFLAGS='$(subst X,-,Xk)'` reaching `k`; `MAKEFLAGS='-Ia$b'` losing its tail to an undefined `$b` exactly as GNU Make's does; `MAKEFLAGS='-Ia$$b'` still reaching the child whole, which is the recorded divergence and must not move; and the same three under `GNUMAKEFLAGS`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T07:44:25.993122929Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T08:25:52.72856009Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered in full. The filing held on re-probe — the reproducer `MAKEFLAGS='$(subst X,-,Xk)'` dies `invalid option -- '$'` under Ronin and decodes `-k` under the 4.4.1 oracle, exactly as recorded — and the blocker it named turned out to be smaller than it looked: all four function classes close, with no deferral and no remainder filed.\n\nTHE SITE, re-verified. `parse` (src/make/cli.rs) reads each of GNUMAKEFLAGS then MAKEFLAGS through `makeflags_arguments` (src/make/cli/interface.rs) and splits into switches. GNU Make's `decode_env_switches` never splits the bytes: it builds `$(NAME)`, hands it to `variable_expand`, and splits the RESULT. So the fix is a bootstrap evaluator standing before the split.\n\nWHERE IT WENT. kati gains `expand_environment_option_stream` (kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs) — a throwaway `Session` with every environment name loaded RECURSIVE (a plain loader, not `read_invocation_state`'s, which folds a switch table that does not exist yet), expanding the stream value with the default evaluator. The frontend's `makeflags_arguments` now calls it when the stream holds a `$`, then splits WITHOUT re-halving `$$`; a stream with no `$` skips the evaluator and its whole environment load, so the common case is byte-identical to before.\n\nTHE DOUBLE-HALVING TRAP, found and avoided. `makeflags_words` halved `$$` as a STAND-IN for expansion. Adding real expansion on top would halve twice — a directory's `-Ia$$$$b` would lose a `$`. So the env path expands (which halves once, GNU's way) and splits with halving OFF; `makeflags_words` gained a `halve_dollars` flag, true only for the makefile-write path (`assigned_makeflags_arguments`), which receives kati-expanded text and is unchanged. `evaluated_invocation` — Ronin reading its OWN canonical MAKEFLAGS table back — keeps the non-expanding split (`evaluated_option_arguments`), because GNU keeps that table and never hands it to `variable_expand` again.\n\nTHE TWO RECORDED DIVERGENCES STAY GREEN, and the reason is that the literal-text decision lives on the EXPORT side, not here. `a-child-keeps-a-switch-value-a-dollar-was-written-in`: the parent EXPORTS the stored `-Ia$$b` (the literal-text decision), and the child's import expands `-Ia$$b` to `-Ia$b` whether by `$$`-halving or by full expansion — there is no live `$b` in an escaped `$$b`, so both give `-Ia$b` and the child still keeps the directory GNU loses. `makeflags-hands-back-the-switch-text-it-stores`: its `$` arrives on the COMMAND LINE, which never touches the env path. Both markers verified live in make_port. The eval-fragment reference is untouched too: `propagated_makeflags` is the resolved switch table (`.base`), which `quote_for_makeflags` guarantees can hold `$$` but never a live `$(-*-eval-flags-*-)` — those travel beside it.\n\nTHE FOUR OPEN QUESTIONS, answered against the oracle (probes sandboxed where a shell ran):\n1. Does `$(error)` end the run before `-C`? YES. kati's `error_func` returns an Err; `makeflags_arguments` turns it into a refusal `parse` returns as `Action::Immediate`, before `enter_directories`. `MAKEFLAGS='$(error boom)'` → Ronin exit 2 `ronin: *** boom.  Stop.`, oracle exit 2 `make: *** boom.  Stop.` — both abandon before any target.\n2. Where is a `$(warning)` reported from? The invocation's own diagnostics sink (`raised`, threaded into `parse`), with NO makefile location — the bootstrap evaluator's loc is None, so `MAKEFLAGS='$(warning W)-k'` prints `W` where GNU prints location-less `make: W`, then the `-k` still decodes. The text is runtime narration and not gated; the switch surviving is.\n3. Does `$(shell)` run, and with what SHELL? YES, against the environment's SHELL (`get_shell` = `eval_var SHELL`). `MAKEFLAGS='$(shell echo -k)'` with SHELL=/bin/sh: both tools produce `-k` and build. With SHELL unset: both fail the exec (GNU `make: -c: No such file or directory`, Ronin `/bin/sh: 1: -c: not found`) and both fall through to an empty value and build — the outcome agrees, only the runtime text differs.\n4. Once per process, or per composed `$(MAKE)` child? Per invocation. A composed child re-runs `parse` (src/make/cli/subninja.rs) with the parent's diagnostics, re-expanding — the way GNU re-execs and re-decodes. It cannot double-run a `$(shell)`, because a composed child inherits the resolved switch table (only `$$`, never a live call), so its expansion does `$$`-halving alone.\n\nWHY NO DEFERRAL. The filing's blocker was \"$(shell)/$(error)/$(warning) live at a point with no diagnostics routing.\" Routing was one line: `session.diagnostics = <the invocation's collector>`. `$(error)` is Err-propagation; `$(shell)` is kati's existing machinery, which spawns exactly the one command GNU spawns. The pure-expansion subset and the three effectful functions all close together, so there is no diagnostics-routing remainder to file.\n\nSIX FIXTURES, oracle-recorded under tests/make from the pinned 4.4.1 oracle, teeth measured by parking the expansion and running the 1184-case build-intent corpus:\n* an-environment-option-stream-expands-a-function-into-a-switch — the reproducer as a build: `$(subst X,-,Xk)`, `all: bad good`, `kept-going` only if `-k` decoded. Parked: not produced.\n* an-environment-option-stream-reads-an-environment-name — `MAKEFLAGS=$(CARRIED)`, CARRIED=-k in the environment, same build. Parked: not produced.\n* both-environment-option-streams-expand-the-same-way — `GNUMAKEFLAGS=$(subst ...)`, uniform across both streams; also records the emptied name (`gmf=[] mf=[k]`). Parked: not produced.\n* an-environment-option-stream-warns-and-carries-its-switch — `$(warning ...)-k`, the switch survives a non-fatal warning. Parked: not produced.\n* an-environment-option-stream-runs-a-shell-for-its-switch — `$(shell echo -k)` with SHELL set. Parked: not produced.\n* an-environment-option-stream-that-errors-abandons-the-run — `$(error ...)`, `ran` never written. NO tooth against parking (the parked invalid-option path also abandons with outcome=failure), so this one locks the abandon-point rather than distinguishing the mechanism; it does exercise the `$(error)`→refusal path.\nFive of six fail when the expansion is parked. Plus one unit test in interface.rs (`a_reference_in_the_stream_expands_before_it_is_split`).\n\nGATES, all green and serial: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; cargo doc -D warnings clean; workspace tests 1087/0/1 (1086 + the new unit test); check-lints.sh clean (run by hand); make_port 4/4 over 1184 cases with BOTH recorded divergence markers live and the literal-text/eval-fragment paths untouched; conformance 336 normalised / 51 differing, 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 0 disagreements / 8 manifest respellings, unmoved; upstream inventory 1285 rows at 95 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1166 narration, NO drift, COMPILER STAYS ZERO; check-make-projects.sh built vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 31s; ninja conformance all 425 pinned Ninja tests + 10 differential + persistence round trip (Make-only change, feature-gated, provably cannot touch Ninja mode); cargo test --workspace --no-default-features 834/0, unmoved (the change is behind the make feature); check-port-ledger.sh 170 symbols green. check-performance.sh is a Ninja-mode benchmark unaffected by a Make env-decode change and was not re-run.\n\nkati on nplan/make-an-environment-option-stream-is-expanded-before-it-is-split at 2c165bbcc9e9e2ce6b2c6597924923d724ac0ca7, pushed before this pin."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T08:32:04.195843423Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
