            {
                id make-upstream-intermediate-sweep-narration-unrecognised
                title "Give the classifier a family for GNU Make's intermediate-sweep line"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-vpath-name-survives-only-a-current-target)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T04:46:11.835156242Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by auditing the one upstream row that stayed unclassified after make-vpath-name-survives-only-a-current-target moved it.\n\nfeatures/vpathplus.diff.3 is the suite's \"intermediate file handling (part 2)\". Before that node it was `ninja-progress+evaluation` — Ronin built the chain in the wrong directory. It is now `ninja-progress+recipe-echo+evaluation`, and reproducing the case by hand against both binaries shows the graph intent is IDENTICAL:\n\n  GNU Make 4.4.1                          Ronin\n  cat vp/inter.d > inter.c                [1/3] cat vp/inter.d > inter.c\n  cat inter.c > inter.b 2>/dev/null ...   [2/3] cat inter.c > inter.b 2>/dev/null ...\n  cat inter.b > inter.a                   [3/3] cat inter.b > inter.a\n  rm inter.c                              (nothing)\n\nSame commands, same order, same files left on disk — `inter.c` is swept by both. The whole remaining difference is that GNU Make ANNOUNCES the sweep and Ronin does not, and the classifier has no family for that line, so it falls into `evaluation`, which is the unclassified residue.\n\nThis is a classifier node and not a runtime one. `[dec:ronin:make-compiles-to-ninja]` and the campaign's standing rule are that the runtime speaks Ninja: Ronin does not print GNU Make's recipe echo, its `Nothing to be done` line or its `Entering directory` bracketing either, and each of those has a narration family (`recipe-echo`, `no-work-line`, `directory-announce`) saying so. `rm <files>` is the same kind of line and wants the same kind of family — recognised from the sweep's own payload rather than guessed at, the way `recipe-echo` is recognised from the counter's payload.\n\nWhat it is worth: one row out of the 131 that are still unclassified, and the honesty of the count. A row whose only difference is a line Ronin deliberately does not print should not be sitting in the bucket that means \"exact output cannot distinguish graph intent from runner behaviour\" — it can, and it says they agree."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T05:37:26.010797754Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T05:47:52.25333859Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. The family is `intermediate-sweep`, narration, and it moved twenty-two rows out of the unclassified bucket rather than the one the filing named.\n\nTHE LINE, read out of GNU Make's own source rather than guessed at. `remove_intermediates` (reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/src/file.c:361) walks the file table at the end of a build, and for each file that is intermediate, not secondary, not precious and not named on the command line it writes `rm ` once with `fputs`, then each file's own `f->name` with a single space between them, then a newline. No options, no quoting, no shell in front of it, and nothing else on the line. So the whole recognisable shape is `rm ` plus a payload of plain names — which is too weak to read on its own, exactly as `is_switch_cluster` and the debug-trace regex are too weak on their own.\n\nWHAT SETTLES IT, and this is the discipline the filing asked for. The line is recognised on GNU Make's side only, in `read_their_side`, AFTER the two recipe-echo readings that already run there: `normalise` cancels a line the case's own makefile wrote as a recipe, and the payload comparison cancels a line Ronin named in a progress counter. What is left is offered to `swept_intermediates`, which returns the names, and the family is claimed only when EVERY name is one this run's own commands named — `names_file` asks by word rather than by substring, so `cat inter.c > inter.b` names `inter.c` and `cat winter.city` names nothing. A recipe that runs `rm` is therefore already accounted for above; a build that never made the files is not explained by a line about deleting them; and a run where Ronin refused and printed no commands cannot reach the family at all. That is the conservative direction in all three.\n\nIT EXPLAINS ONLY ITSELF, and the corpus demonstrates it rather than the test alone. `features/patternrules.diff.35` gained `intermediate-sweep` and STAYED unclassified: GNU Make also says `warning: pattern recipe did not update peer target 'foo.h'.` there, Ronin does not, and `evaluation` stands beside the new family. Asserted in `a_sweep_line_is_the_intermediates_going_away`, which runs the same case three ways — sweep alone is narration, sweep plus one unaccounted line is unclassified with the family still named, and an `rm` over a file no command mentioned is not the sweep at all.\n\nPROBED, not assumed. The family's reason says Ronin sweeps the same files and says nothing, so that was measured on both binaries: `%.d: %.e` over `%.e: %.f` with only `foo.f` on disk leaves GNU Make printing `cp foo.f foo.e`, `cp foo.e foo.d`, `rm foo.e` and leaving `foo.d foo.f Makefile`; Ronin prints the two progress lines, says nothing about the sweep, and leaves `foo.d foo.f Makefile`. Same files, same absence of `foo.e`.\n\nTWENTY-THREE CASES exhibit the family and every one was read by hand from its own `.diff`:\n  features/vpathplus.diff.2 (`rm inter.b inter.c`) and .3 (`rm inter.c`) — the filing's row and its sibling, which the filing did not have\n  targets/INTERMEDIATE.diff, .2, .3, .5, .7, .9\n  targets/SECONDARY.diff.8, .9, .10, .11\n  features/patternrules.diff.4, .11, .21, .22, .23, .24, .25, .26, .27\n  features/archives.diff.10 (`rm 1234567890123456a 1234567890123456b`)\n  features/patternrules.diff.35 — recognised, still unclassified, as above\nTwenty-two moved unclassified -> narration; one stayed. No case lost a family and no narration row moved.\n\nINVENTORY, 1285 rows: 107 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1154 narration, from 129 / 0 / 24 / 1132. THE COMPILER BUCKET STAYS ZERO. The only non-case line the diff carries is the new `family` row itself.\n\nGATES: `cargo fmt` clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; workspace tests 1004 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, unmoved — the classifier's own tests live in an example target and are reached by `--all-targets`, not by `cargo test --workspace`, so the two added there (24 in the example, from 22) do not move that number; upstream inventory as above, regenerated and every moved row audited. Nothing else in the battery can see this change: it is confined to `examples/make_upstream.rs` and the inventory it records."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T05:47:56.494706891Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-gpath-rename-keeps-the-recorded-prerequisites
                title "Keep a static pattern rule's recorded prerequisites across a GPATH rename"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-vpath-found-name-merges-two-declared-targets)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T05:09:45.476033798Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The \"adjacent thing seen and not chased\" that make-vpath-gpath-output-location recorded, now probed against the pristine 4.4.1 oracle and measured on both binaries. It is a live refusal, not a difference of wording.\n\nReproducer, with `build/out.o` present and older than `out.c`, and `out.o` absent:\n\n  VPATH = build\n  GPATH = build\n  all: out.o ; @printf 'all sees %s\\n' '$^' > seen\n  out.o: %.o: %.c ; @printf 'made [%s] from [%s] stem [%s]\\n' '$@' '$<' '$*' > $@\n\nGNU Make 4.4.1 leaves `build/out.o` holding `made [build/out.o] from [out.c] stem [out]` and `all sees build/out.o`. Ronin REFUSES: `'build/out.c', needed by 'build/out.o', missing and no known rule to make it`, exit 2.\n\nWHY. A static pattern rule's prerequisites are worked out when the rule is READ: `record_files` matches the target against the target pattern, substitutes the stem into each prerequisite pattern, and hands the file object a finished dependency chain. `GPATH` then renames that object — `rename_file` moves ONE object, chain and all — so the target the recipe makes is `build/out.o` while the prerequisite it carries is still the `out.c` the read derived from `out.o`. The stem stays `out` for the same reason.\n\nkati keys everything by name and re-derives: `apply_output_pattern` substitutes the rule's target pattern into whatever name the node is being planned under, which after the rename is `build/out.o`, giving `build/out.c` — a file nothing makes. `$*` would be wrong the same way, read off `output_pattern` against `recipe_output`.\n\nWHAT A FIX LOOKS LIKE. The name a pattern is applied to has to be the name the rule was DECLARED for, which `DepBuilder::written_as` already answers — it is how `lookup_rule_merger` and `lookup_rule_vars` reach a renamed target's rule at all. So `fill_dep_node_from_rule` and `recorded_prerequisites` want `written_as(output)` where they currently pass `output`, and `AutoCommand::Star`'s pattern branch wants the same. What has to stay the renamed name is `$@`: GNU makes the file at `build/out.o`, which is the whole point of `GPATH`.\n\nNot attempted under make-vpath-found-name-merges-two-declared-targets, which owns the OTHER thing `rehash_file` does and left this one measured rather than guessed at. It wants its own run of the corpus: every static pattern rule in the build-intent corpus is planned through the same substitution, and a change there moves recorded manifests if it is wrong.\n\nNo upstream row is known to turn on it: the suite's one GPATH case, features/vpathgpath, is a build where nothing is out of date."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T05:49:27.779996601Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T06:23:24.501566488Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and it was two defects under one rename rather than one. The second was a PANIC, and the filing had no way of knowing: it is reached by `::` under `GPATH` whether or not a static pattern is involved.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, read out of GNU Make rather than inferred, and the filing had it right. `record_files` (read.c) works a static pattern rule's prerequisites out AS THE RULE IS READ — it matches the target against the target pattern, substitutes the stem into each prerequisite pattern, and hands the file object a finished chain. `GPATH` then renames THAT OBJECT: `rename_file` moves one object, chain and all. So the target is made at `build/out.o` from the `out.c` the WRITTEN name derived, and `$*` is `out`. kati keys everything by name and re-derives, which after the rename asks `%.o: %.c` about `build/out.o` and produces `build/out.c` — a file nothing makes, hence the refusal.\n\nTHE DISTINCTION IS EXACT, and the corpus already carried the other half of it. An IMPLICIT rule records nothing at read time, so GNU matches it against the RENAMED name and looks for `build/out.c`, finds none, and leaves the found file where it is. Measured on both binaries and unmoved: `feature-gpath-renames-before-the-implicit-rule-search` is that control and is the guard that the change did not over-apply. `feature-gpath-keeps-the-stem-an-explicit-rule-reads` is the other one — an explicit rule has no target pattern, so `$*` is the RENAMED name with a known suffix taken off, `build/out`, and it must stay that way.\n\nTHE MATRIX, every cell run through reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and then through Ronin. `VPATH = GPATH = build`, `build/out.o` present, `out.o` absent:\n\n  static pattern, target stale      GNU makes build/out.o from out.c, $* is `out`; Ronin REFUSED, exit 2\n  static pattern, target current    GNU does nothing and `all` reads build/out.o; Ronin REFUSED — the refusal is at plan time, so it does not need the target to be stale\n  the prerequisite is itself found  `VPATH = build src` — GNU reads src/out.c, $* still `out`; Ronin REFUSED\n  build/out.c EXISTS                GNU still reads out.c; Ronin BUILT, silently, from build/out.c. Not a refusal at all — a wrong input\n  two targets, one renamed          GNU makes build/out.o from out.c and two.o from two.c; Ronin REFUSED\n  order-only `| %.h`                GNU reads out.h; Ronin REFUSED\n  `::` static pattern               GNU runs both records, $@ build/out.o, $* `out`; Ronin PANICKED, exit 101\n  `::` explicit                     GNU runs both records, $* `build/out`; Ronin PANICKED — no static pattern needed\n  `.SECONDEXPANSION` with `%`       GNU fills `%` from the declared stem and reads out.h; Ronin REFUSED\n  target-specific variable          GNU reads it under the renamed name; Ronin REFUSED\n  static pattern, plain vpath       both agree, both cells (stale keeps out.o, current takes build/out.o) — the control\n  implicit rule under GPATH         both do nothing — the control that must not move\n  suffix rule under GPATH           both do nothing — the same control\n  target misses its target pattern  both warn and carry an empty chain — unmoved\n  explicit rule under GPATH         both make build/out.o from out.c with $* of build/out — the control for `$*`\n\nEvery one of those now matches, the two controls included. `.SECONDEXPANSION` even reproduces GNU's `Circular build/out.o <- build/out.o dependency dropped.` where Ronin used to refuse.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, all in kati on nplan/make-gpath-rename-keeps-the-recorded-prerequisites at f1add07f9d733992341992c6547306c1b359d6a3, pushed before this pin. `DepNode::declared_output` is the name the rule was written for, beside `recipe_output`, which is the name the file moved to; `written_as` — which already existed for `lookup_rule_merger` — is what fills it. Five readers take the first: `apply_output_pattern` through `fill_dep_node_from_rule` (the prerequisites and the order-only half), `prerequisites_reach` and `stem_of` where `build_plan` picks the deferred half, `recorded_prerequisites`, `double_action_id` on both sides of the `::` record, and `AutoCommand::Star`'s pattern branch. `$@` still reads `recipe_output`, and so does `$*`'s suffix branch.\n\nTHE PANIC. `populate_explicit_rule` files a `::` record under `(rule, declared output)`. `build_double_action` looked it up under the trigger, which after a rename is the found path, so `self.double_action_indices[&action]` indexed a map that had no such key. It is a crash rather than a wrong answer and it needs no static pattern — `out.o:: out.c` with `GPATH` is enough.\n\nTEETH, each parked and the whole 996-case build-intent corpus run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the static pattern's record is re-derived against the renamed name | 5 |\n| `$*` is read off the renamed name | 5 |\n| the `::` record is keyed by the renamed name | 2, both the panic |\n| the deferred half is selected and stemmed against the renamed name | 1 |\n| `recorded_prerequisites` reaches its rule under the renamed name | 0 — RECORDED AS SUCH |\n\nThe last one was chased rather than assumed: `recorded_prerequisites` is read by `expanded_pattern_inputs` and by the grouped-peer path, and both are reached through the implicit search, which under a rename is matching the RENAMED name and so needs `build/out.c` to exist before it applies at all. Two attempts at a fixture for it left GNU Make choosing its builtin `cc` rule instead. It is kept because reaching a rule under a name it was not declared under is wrong whether or not a fixture notices — the same reading the vpath node gave `found_new_inputs`.\n\nEIGHT FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle: -searches-again-for-a-recorded-prerequisite, -keeps-a-recorded-order-only-prerequisite, -renames-one-target-of-a-static-pattern-rule, -keeps-the-recorded-prerequisite-over-a-derived-one, -runs-a-double-colon-record-under-the-found-name, -keeps-a-record-for-each-double-colon-entry, -second-expands-a-percent-to-the-declared-stem, and -keeps-the-stem-an-explicit-rule-reads. No existing recording was rewritten.\n\nMARKER RESOLVED: tests/make/feature-gpath-keeps-a-static-patterns-recorded-prerequisites/divergence, deleted, now matching the oracle. Seven markers remain (the tree held eight, not the ten the dispatch carried; counted with `find tests/make -name divergence`).\n\nADJACENT, PROBED AND FILED rather than fixed: make-gpath-rename-carries-a-targets-own-flags. `rehash_file`'s closing run of `MERGE(field)` carries the losing object's own booleans and `command_flags` onto the survivor, and Ronin keys those by the planned name too. `.IGNORE: out.o` under `GPATH` leaves GNU at exit 0 with the failure ignored and Ronin at exit 2; `.PRECIOUS: out.o` under `.DELETE_ON_ERROR:` leaves GNU holding build/out.o and Ronin deleting it. `.SILENT` has no cell because Ronin never echoes, and `.PHONY` can have none because `at_vpath` declines a phony name outright. Not folded in: it is a second matrix over the same rename and wants its own corpus run.\n\nGATES: fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; workspace tests 1004 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, unmoved; make_port 4/4 over 996 cases with no existing recording rewritten, seven divergence sidecars; conformance 336 normalised identical / 51 differing, 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 refused / 0 disagreements, unmoved; upstream inventory 1285 rows at 107 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1154 narration, no drift; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 15 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27 s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus 10 differentials, 86 build-outcome cases, 3 invocation-boundary cases, persistence round trip and log content; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 773 passed / 0 failed; `scripts/check-release.sh` green end to end. The recorded trap held: the Make-less binary at the shared debug path was rebuilt with `--features make` before anything else was probed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T06:23:32.539880754Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-gpath-rename-carries-a-targets-own-flags
                title "Carry a target's own special-target flags across a GPATH rename"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-gpath-rename-keeps-the-recorded-prerequisites)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T06:21:35.545060211Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-gpath-rename-keeps-the-recorded-prerequisites, measured on both binaries, and filed rather than folded in because it is the OTHER half of the rename: the flags the file object carries rather than the prerequisite chain the read recorded for it.\n\nWHY IT IS ONE THING. GNU Make renames one file object when `GPATH` says the search's answer is where the name belongs — `file.c` `rename_file` calls `rehash_file`, and `rehash_file` ends with a run of `MERGE(field)` over the object's own booleans plus `to_file->command_flags |= from_file->command_flags`. So `.IGNORE: out.o` is still in force for the object now standing at `build/out.o`. Ronin keys every one of those off the name the node is PLANNED under, which after the rename is the found path, so a flag declared for the name as written is looked up and not found.\n\nMEASURED, `VPATH = GPATH = build`, `build/out.o` present and older than `out.c`, `out.o` absent:\n\n  .IGNORE: out.o, recipe writes $@ then exits 1\n    GNU   exit 0, `make: [Makefile:5: build/out.o] Error 1 (ignored)`, `all` runs and writes seen\n    Ronin exit 2, nothing written\n\n  .DELETE_ON_ERROR: with .PRECIOUS: out.o, recipe writes $@ then exits 1\n    GNU   exit 2 and build/out.o is STILL THERE\n    Ronin exit 2 and build/out.o has been deleted\n    The same case WITHOUT `.PRECIOUS` deletes it on both sides, so what is measured\n    here is the flag and not the deletion.\n\n  .SILENT: out.o          no difference. Ronin does not echo a recipe at all.\n\nTHE SITE. `DepBuilder::build_plan` reads `self.ignore_errors`, `self.restat`, `self.phony`, `self.secondary` and `treat_as_intermediate` off `output`, and `RuleMerger`/the sink reach `is_precious(node.recipe_output, node.output_pattern)`. `written_as` is already the answer and was applied to the prerequisite half here — but do NOT apply it across the board without probing: `rehash_file`'s MERGE list is explicit and shorter than the set of things Ronin keys by name, and at least one entry cannot be reached at all — `at_vpath` declines a `.PHONY` name outright, so a phony target is never renamed and `.PHONY` has no cell.\n\nWHAT TO PROBE: `.IGNORE`, `.PRECIOUS`, `.SECONDARY`, `.INTERMEDIATE`, `.NOTINTERMEDIATE`, `.SILENT`, `.DELETE_ON_ERROR` (which is per makefile rather than per target and so probably has no cell), and kati's own `.KATI_RESTAT`, each against the 4.4.1 oracle with and without the rename, and the same again where the flag is declared for the FOUND name instead of the written one — `rehash_file` merges in one direction and the losing object's flags are the ones that travel."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T07:39:33.944271484Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T08:41:21.695703689Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. The filing had the mechanism half right and the half it had wrong is the whole of the interest: `rehash_file` does not merge `command_flags` at all.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, read out of file.c rather than inferred. `rehash_file (from_file, to_hname)` ends TWO ways and the filing only saw one of them.\n\n  The found path's hash slot is VACANT — `hash_insert_at (&files, from_file, file_slot); return;`. The searched name's object is reinserted under the new name and that is the whole of it: every field it holds arrives with it, `command_flags` and `intermediate` included, because it is the same object.\n\n  Something already stands there — TO_FILE is RETAINED and the searched name's is merged into it. Recipes, prerequisites and variable set lists are merged by hand, and then a closing run of `MERGE(field)` takes `precious`, `loaded`, `tried_implicit`, `updating`, `updated`, `is_target`, `cmd_target`, `phony`, `is_explicit`, `secondary`, `notintermediate`, `ignore_vpath` and `snapped`. `command_flags` is NOT in that run, and neither is `intermediate` — which carries its own comment: \"Don't merge intermediate because this file might be pre-existing\".\n\nSo the flags split into two readings, and the split is measurable. `.IGNORE` reaches the found path only by riding the whole object there; `.PRECIOUS` reaches it either way.\n\nWHAT MAKES A SLOT NON-VACANT is any mention at all, which is wider than a rule: `record_files` enters every target, `record_target_var` every name given a binding of its own, and `expand_deps` every prerequisite — the special targets' prerequisites included, so `.PRECIOUS: build/out.o` is enough. Measured: `.IGNORE: out.o` with build/out.o named only as some unrelated rule's prerequisite already loses the ignore.\n\nTHE MATRIX, seventeen cells, each run through reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and then through Ronin. `VPATH = GPATH = build`, `build/out.o` present and older than `out.c`, `out.o` absent throughout.\n\n  .IGNORE: out.o                     GNU exit 0, `all` runs; Ronin exit 2      FIXED\n  .IGNORE: build/out.o               both exit 0                              already agreed\n  .IGNORE: out.o + build/out.o named GNU exit 2 — the ignore does NOT travel   CONTROL, held\n  .PRECIOUS: out.o, .DELETE_ON_ERROR GNU holds build/out.o; Ronin deleted it   FIXED\n  the same + build/out.o named       GNU still holds it — precious MERGEs      FIXED\n  .DELETE_ON_ERROR alone             both delete                              control\n  .INTERMEDIATE: out.o               GNU `rm build/out.o`; Ronin kept it       FIXED\n  .INTERMEDIATE: out.o + named       GNU keeps it — intermediate does NOT MERGE  CONTROL, held\n  .INTERMEDIATE: build/out.o         both sweep                               control\n  .INTERMEDIATE found + .SECONDARY written    GNU keeps it; Ronin swept        FIXED\n  .INTERMEDIATE found + .NOTINTERMEDIATE written  GNU keeps it; Ronin swept    FIXED\n  .INTERMEDIATE written + .NOTINTERMEDIATE found  both keep it                 control\n  .SECONDARY: bare + .NOTINTERMEDIATE written     both build                   control\n  .SILENT: out.o                     no cell — Ronin does not echo a recipe\n  .PHONY: out.o                      no cell — `at_vpath` declines a phony name, so nothing is renamed and both write ./out.o\n  target-specific variable           both read it under the renamed name       control\n  no flag at all                     both fail at build/out.o                  control\n\nONE CELL IS STILL DIVERGENT AND IS NOT THIS NODE'S. `.SECONDARY: out.o` with a stale found file under a newer goal: GNU says `Nothing to be done` and Ronin builds. The found-name spelling of it diverges identically, so the cause cannot be the flag's name — it is that GNU forgives a stale intermediate and Ronin only forgives an absent one. Filed as make-an-existing-intermediate-does-not-force-its-dependent, together with the GPATH-free defect underneath it, make-an-intermediate-the-build-found-is-not-swept-up: `f_mtime` turns the declared intermediate bit OFF for a file that was already there, and the `GPATH` branch returns above that code, which is exactly why the rename cell and the no-rename cell answer differently.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, all in kati on nplan/make-gpath-rename-carries-a-targets-own-flags at 4b1ee98e32966659443cb00a901da2b1e7015ee4, pushed before this pin. `merged_flag` and `moved_flag` are the two readings, both over `written_as`, which the prerequisite half already established; `names_an_object` is the vacancy test and is `rules` ∪ `mentioned` ∪ `rule_vars`, which is GNU's `enter_file` set. `.IGNORE` (both the planning path and `build_double_action`), `.INTERMEDIATE` and `.KATI_RESTAT` take `moved_flag`; `.PRECIOUS`, `.SECONDARY` and `.NOTINTERMEDIATE` take `merged_flag`. The sweep now asks for `intermediate` and against `secondary` and `notintermediate` separately, as `remove_intermediates` (file.c) does, because a rename is the one way a file object comes to hold `.INTERMEDIATE` and `.NOTINTERMEDIATE` at once — the two written for one name are a read-time error in both tools.\n\nTEETH, each parked and the whole 1,014-case build-intent corpus run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| `.IGNORE` keyed by the planned name | 1 |\n| `.PRECIOUS` keyed by the planned name | 2 |\n| `.INTERMEDIATE` keyed by the planned name | 1 |\n| `.SECONDARY` keyed by the planned name | 1 |\n| `.NOTINTERMEDIATE` does not stop the sweep | 1 |\n| the whole-object guard dropped, so every flag travels | 2, and they are the two controls |\n| `.NOTINTERMEDIATE` keyed by the planned name in the FRESHNESS answer | 0 — RECORDED AS SUCH |\n| `.KATI_RESTAT` keyed by the planned name | 0 — RECORDED AS SUCH |\n\nThe last two were chased rather than assumed. `.NOTINTERMEDIATE`'s freshness half is masked by the divergence just filed: the cell that would reach it needs Ronin to forgive a stale intermediate, and today it does not, so the answer is the same either way — the fixture for that cell is recorded and will gain teeth when make-an-existing-intermediate-does-not-force-its-dependent lands. `.KATI_RESTAT` has no cell at all: probed two-phase with and without the rename and with the flag on either name, Ronin rebuilds the dependent in every one, so restat is not observable in this shape. Both are kept for the same reason the vpath and prerequisite nodes kept theirs — reaching a flag under a name it was not declared under is wrong whether or not a fixture notices.\n\nNINE FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, none of the existing recordings rewritten: -ignores-an-error-for-the-name-as-written, -leaves-a-flag-behind-when-the-found-name-is-spoken-for, -keeps-a-target-the-name-as-written-called-precious, -merges-precious-onto-a-found-name-in-use, -sweeps-up-what-the-name-as-written-made-intermediate, -keeps-an-intermediate-the-found-name-is-spoken-for, -merges-secondary-onto-the-found-name, -merges-notintermediate-onto-the-found-name, -merges-notintermediate-against-a-bare-secondary.\n\nGATES: fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; workspace tests 1005 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, unmoved; make_port 4/4 over 1,014 cases, seven divergence sidecars unmoved; conformance 336 normalised identical / 51 differing, 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 refused / 0 disagreements, unmoved; upstream inventory 1,285 rows at 107 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,154 narration with no drift in the tsv; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 15 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 26 s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus 10 differentials, 86 build-outcome cases, 3 invocation-boundary cases, persistence round trip and log content; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 773 passed / 0 failed; `scripts/check-release.sh` green end to end. One run of the release gate failed in the release-profile lib tests and did not reproduce — three further runs of that suite and two more full release runs were green, and the failing output was not captured. Recorded rather than dismissed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T08:42:25.80922143Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-an-intermediate-the-build-found-is-not-swept-up
                title "Leave behind an intermediate that was there before the build"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-gpath-rename-carries-a-targets-own-flags)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T08:40:14.979757989Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-gpath-rename-carries-a-targets-own-flags, measured on both binaries, and GPATH-free in its main cell.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. `f_mtime` (remake.c) ends with\n\n    if (mtime != NONEXISTENT_MTIME && file->command_state == cs_not_started\n        && !file->tried_implicit && file->intermediate)\n      file->intermediate = 0;\n\nwith the comment saying why: a file that has never been built by us but is found now existed before make started, so make must not delete it, because it did not create it. `.INTERMEDIATE:` and `.SECONDARY:` are the only ways to reach that code with `intermediate` already set and `tried_implicit` still clear — a file an implicit chain made intermediate had `try_implicit_rule` run against it first, in `check_dep`, which sets `tried_implicit` before `file_mtime` is asked.\n\nMEASURED, no VPATH and no GPATH anywhere:\n\n  .INTERMEDIATE: out.o, out.o present and older than out.c, `app: out.o`\n    GNU   remakes out.o, makes app, and LEAVES out.o\n    Ronin remakes out.o, makes app, and deletes it\n\n  the same case with out.o ABSENT — the control\n    both remake out.o, make app, and delete it (`rm out.o`)\n\nTHE GPATH EXCEPTION, which is why this was found here. The `GPATH` branch of `f_mtime` is `rename_file (file, name); check_renamed (file); return file_mtime (file);` — it RETURNS, above the turn-off, so a target the search found through `GPATH` keeps the bit its declaration gave it and is swept up after all. That cell is gated:\ntests/make/feature-gpath-sweeps-up-what-the-name-as-written-made-intermediate. So a fix here must not move it.\n\nTHE SITE. kati's `treat_as_intermediate` (src-rs/dep.rs) answers from the declaration alone. The existence question is one the planner can ask — `at_vpath` already stats — but the condition is `command_state == cs_not_started`, which is a build-time state, and the only reading of it available at plan time is \"the file is there now and this run has not made it\", which is what `exists_cache` already knows.\n\nFiled rather than folded in: it is the intermediate bit's lifetime rather than the rename's flag merge, and it wants its own corpus run."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T10:35:43.18441088Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T11:25:49.735998939Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. The filing had the mechanism right and one step of its reasoning wrong, and the matrix around it found a SECOND clause of the same function wrong in the same direction — closing only the filed one would have left a file deleted under `-t` that GNU Make keeps.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, and the correction. `f_mtime` (remake.c) ends with the turn-off the filing quoted, guarded on `!file->tried_implicit`. The filing reasoned that a name reached through `check_dep`'s intermediate branch has `try_implicit_rule` run against it before `file_mtime` is asked, so a `.INTERMEDIATE:` name with only a pattern rule would keep the bit. It does not, because that is not where the first stat happens: `update_file_1`'s prerequisite loop calls `file_mtime (d->file)` ONE LINE ABOVE `check_dep (d->file, ...)` (remake.c:602), and a goal's own `this_mtime = file_mtime (file)` sits above `try_implicit_rule` too. So the stamp is still clear for every name a makefile wrote down, whether its recipe is explicit or a pattern's — MEASURED, and the two cells answer identically.\n\n`tried_implicit` is set with the bit only for a name `pattern_search` invented (implicit.c:1014-1015), and such a name is only invented because nothing was at it. So the rule is one sentence: a name `.INTERMEDIATE:` or `.SECONDARY:` made intermediate stops being intermediate the moment the search finds a file at it, and an invented one never reaches the question at all.\n\nWHAT ALREADY AGREED, and it is worth naming because it is the other half of GNU Make's protection. `update_file_1` sets `file->secondary = 1` when `!must_make` (remake.c:876), so an intermediate make decided not to remake is spared whatever the bit says. Ronin's sweep reads `plan.wanted_edges` and so answers that case already — which is why a pre-existing intermediate that is CURRENT was never a cell.\n\nTHE SECOND CLAUSE. `remove_intermediates` (file.c:368) opens `if (question_flag || touch_flag || all_secondary || no_intermediates) return;` — it gives up on the whole run rather than on a file, and it reads the switches where it runs, which is once, at the end of `main`. Two of those four were answered where the manifest is compiled and two were not answered at all. `-t` is the sharper of the two because `update_goal_chain` sets both flags ASIDE while the Makefiles are remade, so a pass can run an intermediate's recipe in earnest and the sweep still never reach it. Closed here rather than filed: it is the same early return, in the same function, reached by the same node's matrix, and a file deleted under `-t` is the same file-loss class the filing named.\n\nTHE MATRIX, twenty cells, each run through reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and then through Ronin. `.INTERMEDIATE: out.o`, `all: app`, `app: out.o`, `out.o: out.c` unless said otherwise.\n\n  out.o present and stale                     GNU keeps it; Ronin deleted it            FIXED\n  out.o absent                                both `rm out.o` — control\n  out.o present, `%.o: %.c` instead of a rule GNU keeps it; Ronin deleted it            FIXED\n  out.o present and CURRENT                   both keep it — `!must_make` sets `secondary`, and `wanted_edges` already says so; control\n  out.o present, under -B                     GNU keeps it; Ronin deleted it            FIXED\n  out.o present, under -W out.o               both keep it and neither remakes — control\n  out.o present, under -t                     GNU keeps it; Ronin deleted it            FIXED\n  out.o ABSENT, under -t                      GNU touches it into being and keeps it; Ronin deleted it   FIXED, the second clause\n  an invented chain under a REMADE makefile, -q   GNU keeps gen.z; Ronin deleted it     FIXED\n  the same under -t                           GNU keeps gen.z; Ronin deleted it         FIXED\n  VPATH without GPATH, build/out.o present    GNU keeps the out.o it made here; Ronin deleted it         FIXED\n  VPATH = GPATH, build/out.o present          both sweep build/out.o — the gated fixture, unmoved; control\n  .PHONY: out.o beside .INTERMEDIATE          both sweep it — a phony carries NONEXISTENT_MTIME from the read and is never stated   FIXED (it had no fixture)\n  .SECONDARY: out.o present and stale         both keep it — `secondary` is never swept; control\n  bare .SECONDARY:                            both punt the whole sweep — control\n  bare .NOTINTERMEDIATE:                      both punt the whole sweep — control\n  .INTERMEDIATE + .NOTINTERMEDIATE on one name   a read-time error in both — control\n  -q with the goals absent                    both build nothing — control\n  a real chain whose intermediate EXISTS      no cell: `pattern_search` tries each rule once without intermediates first, so a file that is there is a prerequisite and never intermediate. The filing asked for this cell and it does not exist.\n  -o out.o                                    STILL DIVERGENT and not this node's: filed as make-an-old-file-is-not-remade, together with the whole switch, which is an accepted no-op today.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, in kati on nplan/make-an-intermediate-the-build-found-is-not-swept-up at fb63376b7a43c006a8448dc76d6fec51c93659f0, pushed before this pin. `found_before_the_build` is the turn-off, asked of the declared arm of `treat_as_intermediate` and of the bare-`.SECONDARY:` arm: the name as written or the directory search's answer for it, and neither a `.PHONY` name nor a `GPATH` rename. The set of invented names is read before it, because a bare `.SECONDARY:` reaches every other name there is and that arm answers without going to the filesystem.\n\nIn Ronin, `sweeps_nothing` beside `discard_intermediates`' `-n`: the invocation's `-q` and `-t` rather than the pass's, carried onto `Passes` so the Makefile passes that had them set aside still decline the sweep.\n\nTEETH, each parked and the whole corpus run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the turn-off on the declared arm | 3 of 1,047 |\n| the `GPATH` exception | 1, and it is the control |\n| the `VPATH` half of the search | 1 |\n| the `.PHONY` guard | 1 |\n| the -q and -t punt on the sweep | 3 |\n| the punt asked of the pass rather than the invocation | 2 |\n| the turn-off on the bare `.SECONDARY:` arm | 0 — RECORDED AS SUCH |\n\nThe last was chased rather than assumed. Under a bare `.SECONDARY:` the sweep is already off for the whole run, so the bit's only remaining reader is the freshness answer, and Ronin's `absent_intermediate` needs every output missing before it acts — which a file that is there is not. The cell that would reach it is make-an-existing-intermediate-does-not-force-its-dependent's, and it will gain teeth there. Kept for the same reason the `GPATH` node kept its two: the bit's lifetime is wrong whether or not a fixture notices.\n\nEIGHT FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten: intermediate-already-there-is-not-the-builds-to-remove, intermediate-the-build-made-itself-is-still-removed, intermediate-already-there-outlives-always-make, intermediate-the-search-found-is-not-the-builds-to-remove, intermediate-that-is-also-phony-is-swept-up-whatever-lies-at-the-name, intermediate-touched-rather-than-made-is-not-removed, intermediate-under-a-remade-makefile-outlives-a-question, intermediate-under-a-remade-makefile-outlives-a-touch.\n\nINVENTORY AUDIT. 1,285 rows at 100 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,161 narration, the tsv untouched and no row moved in either direction. The compiler bucket stays at zero."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T11:33:27.725399812Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T13:29:01.996443594Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GATES for the landing above, run serially on this tree at 93ef3c76de58383ffdce712cf1147dc205cbe0b2 with kati pinned at fb63376b7a43c006a8448dc76d6fec51c93659f0.\n\nfmt clean in both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; workspace tests 1005 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, unmoved; make_port 4/4 over 1,047 gated cases with eight new recordings and none rewritten, seven divergence sidecars unmoved; conformance 336 normalised identical / 51 differing, 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 manifest respellings, unmoved; upstream inventory 1,285 rows at 100 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,161 narration, tsv untouched, no row moved; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 17 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 29 s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus 10 differentials, 86 build-outcome cases, 3 invocation-boundary cases, persistence round trip and log content; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 773 passed / 0 failed.\n\n`scripts/check-release.sh` was not run against this commit alone. The host carried another tenant's build all afternoon — 15-minute load averages between 5 and 25 — and the gate's last stage is the performance comparison, which is the recorded flake under load. It was run end to end against the final tree of the dispatch instead; the result is recorded on the last node landed."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-an-existing-intermediate-does-not-force-its-dependent
                title "Let an intermediate that is merely stale leave its dependent alone"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-gpath-rename-carries-a-targets-own-flags)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T08:40:34.433651795Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-gpath-rename-carries-a-targets-own-flags, measured on both binaries. Ronin forgives an intermediate that is ABSENT and does not forgive one that is merely STALE, and GNU Make forgives both.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. `check_dep` (remake.c) splits on `file->phony || !file->intermediate`. An intermediate takes the other branch, and there the question asked of it is not \"are you out of date\" but \"are you NEWER THAN THIS_MTIME\" — the mtime of the target on whose behalf the check is running. When it is not, the branch updates the intermediate's own NON-intermediate prerequisites and asks whether any of THOSE is newer than this_mtime; only then is `*must_make_ptr` set. So a stale intermediate under a goal that is newer than everything reachable leaves the goal alone, whether or not the intermediate itself is remade.\n\nMEASURED. `out.o` present and older than `out.c`, `app` present and newest, `all: app`, `app: out.o`:\n\n  %.o: %.c implicit chain, no declaration\n    GNU   remakes out.o and LEAVES app alone\n    Ronin remakes out.o and remakes app\n\n  VPATH = GPATH = build, build/out.o present and stale, `.SECONDARY: out.o`\n    GNU   `Nothing to be done for 'all'.` — nothing runs at all\n    Ronin remakes build/out.o and remakes app\n    The same with `.SECONDARY: build/out.o`, and with a bare `.SECONDARY:`, and\n    the same answer each time.\n\n  no VPATH, out.o present and stale, `.SECONDARY: out.o`\n    both remake out.o and remake app — because `f_mtime` turns the declared\n    intermediate bit OFF for a file that was already there, which is\n    make-an-intermediate-the-build-found-is-not-swept-up's subject. Under\n    `GPATH` the rename returns above that code and the bit survives, which is\n    why the GPATH cells above are the ones that diverge.\n\n  out.o ABSENT, `.SECONDARY: out.o`, app newest — the control\n    both do nothing. Ronin already forgives an absent intermediate.\n\nTHE SITE. Ronin's freshness answer for an intermediate reads its absence and nothing else; what GNU Make compares is the intermediate's mtime against the DEPENDENT's, which is a question about the pair rather than about the file. The two nodes share `treat_as_intermediate` and should be probed together, but they are separate answers: one decides whether the bit is there, this one decides what the bit does."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T11:43:52.577748201Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T12:30:26.081572739Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, Ronin only — no kati change. The filing had the mechanism exactly right and its first measured cell wrong, and the correction is what makes the node small: after make-an-intermediate-the-build-found-is-not-swept-up landed there is only ONE way left for a file that is there to still carry the intermediate bit, and every cell of this node is that way.\n\nTHE FILING'S FIRST CELL DOES NOT EXIST. It recorded `%.o: %.c` with out.o present and stale, `all: app`, `app: out.o`, app newest, and said GNU leaves app alone. Re-measured: GNU remakes out.o AND remakes app, exactly as Ronin does. It has to — out.o is a prerequisite the makefile wrote down, so nothing ever made it intermediate; only a name `pattern_search` invented is, and `pattern_search` tries each rule once WITHOUT intermediates first, so a file that is already there is found as an ordinary prerequisite and never invented. There is no such thing as a pre-existing chain-derived intermediate.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT, and it is the filing's second cell. `f_mtime`'s turn-off takes the bit off any declared intermediate the search finds a date for, so the only present file that keeps it is one `GPATH` renamed — the rename leaves `f_mtime` through the `return` above the turn-off. Every cell below is therefore a `GPATH` cell, and the plain `VPATH`-less spelling of each is a control that must not move.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, read out of remake.c. `check_dep` splits on `file->phony || !file->intermediate`. An intermediate takes the other branch and is asked whether it is NEWER THAN THIS_MTIME — the date of the file being checked — and when it is not, the branch walks the intermediate's own prerequisites with THE SAME this_mtime and asks whether any of THOSE is newer. So what a dependent sees through an intermediate is the later of the file's own date and the newest thing under it, and never the fact that the file is stale. The staleness is not lost: `update_file_1`'s second loop (remake.c:674) goes back over the intermediate prerequisites once `must_make` is set and calls `update_file` on each — which UPDATES it rather than forcing it, so one that is current on its own account still does not run.\n\nTHE MATRIX, sixteen cells, `VPATH = GPATH = build`, `build/out.o` present, `all: app`, `app: out.o`, `out.o: out.c`. Dates written (intermediate, source, dependent).\n\n  (2020, 2021, 2023) stale, dependent newest    GNU `Nothing to be done`; Ronin remade both   FIXED\n  the same with `.INTERMEDIATE:`                the same answer                               FIXED\n  the same with a bare `.SECONDARY:`            the same answer                               FIXED\n  the same without VPATH or GPATH               both remake both — the bit is off; control\n  (2020, 2024, 2023) source newest              both remake both — what is behind it reaches\n                                                the dependent through it; control, and it has teeth\n  (2024, 2021, 2023) intermediate newest        both remake the dependent alone; control\n  (2020, 2021, 2019) dependent oldest, stale    both remake both — the second loop; control\n  (2022, 2021, 2019) dependent oldest, current  both remake the dependent alone — the second\n                                                loop UPDATES rather than forces; control\n  a chain of two stale intermediates            GNU `Nothing to be done`; Ronin remade all three  FIXED\n  a second, ordinary prerequisite newer than\n    the dependent                               both remake the intermediate too; control\n  `.INTERMEDIATE:` where the second loop made it   both sweep build/out.o afterwards; control\n  `.INTERMEDIATE:` where nothing ran               both keep it — `update_status == us_none`; control\n  under -B                                      both remake everything; control\n  under -t                                      both do nothing at all; control\n  under -W out.c                                both remake both; control\n  under -q                                      both answer 0 and build nothing; control\n  out.o ABSENT under a current dependent        both do nothing — already gated\n\nWHAT CHANGED, all in Ronin.\n\n* `recompute_edge_dirty_with` (src/graph.rs) gained the arm for an intermediate that IS there, beside the one for an intermediate that is not: its outputs stand in at the later of their own date and the newest input, and the edge reports `input_dirty` rather than the date comparison — so its own staleness reaches nothing above it.\n* `EdgeRuntime::intermediate_pending` (src/runtime.rs) is the answer the scan reached and declined to pass on. Sticky within a build for the same reason the absent flag is: an output carrying the date of what stands behind it no longer looks stale, so a second scan of the same edge cannot reach the answer again. Cleared in `build.rs` where the absence flag is, the moment the edge's own command observes real outputs.\n* `push_intermediates` (src/graph/intermediate.rs) asks that flag instead of asking about absence. That is `update_file_1`'s second loop, and the difference between the two questions is the whole of the (2022, 2021, 2019) control: an intermediate that is merely THERE is not asked for, one with work of its own is.\n* The date comparison came out of `recompute_edge_dirty_with` into `out_of_date_by_dates` unchanged, because the new arm needs it as a value rather than as the function's answer — and because the function was one line over the density gate with the arm inlined.\n\nTEETH, each parked and the whole corpus run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the present-intermediate arm | 3 of 1,054 |\n| the substitution of what stands behind it | 1 |\n| `push_intermediates` asking about absence rather than about pending work | 6 |\n| the pending answer not kept between scans | 6 |\n| the absent arm not recording pending work | 20 |\n| the pending answer not cleared when the command runs | 0 — RECORDED AS SUCH |\n\nThe last is state hygiene rather than an observable: after the command runs the outputs are fresh, so the next scan recomputes the answer as false anyway, and a finished edge is not re-entered by the plan. It is kept because a flag saying there is work left after the work is done is wrong, and because the absence flag beside it is cleared in the same breath for the same reason.\n\nAND ONE PARKING THAT WAS PROMISED. make-gpath-rename-carries-a-targets-own-flags recorded `.NOTINTERMEDIATE` keyed by the planned name in the FRESHNESS answer as ZERO teeth, and said the cell would gain them when this node landed. Re-measured with that node's guard parked: 1 of 1,054 — `feature-gpath-merges-notintermediate-against-a-bare-secondary`. The promise is kept.\n\nSEVEN FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten: feature-gpath-a-stale-intermediate-leaves-its-dependent-alone, feature-gpath-a-stale-intermediate-is-made-once-its-dependent-must-be, feature-gpath-a-current-intermediate-is-left-alone-by-a-dependent-that-runs, feature-gpath-a-source-newer-than-the-dependent-reaches-it-through-an-intermediate, feature-gpath-a-chain-of-stale-intermediates-leaves-its-dependent-alone, feature-gpath-a-stale-intermediate-a-dependent-made-is-swept-up, feature-gpath-a-stale-intermediate-nothing-needed-is-not-swept-up.\n\nINVENTORY AUDIT. 1,285 rows at 100 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,161 narration, the tsv untouched and no row moved. Compiler stays at zero."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T12:32:00.710102757Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T13:29:22.00814476Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GATES for the landing above, run serially on this tree at 7d9d12a614b1ef2a1613ab816fa75e4e3c57ded9. No kati change: the submodule stayed pinned at fb63376b7a43c006a8448dc76d6fec51c93659f0, this node's whole subject being the dirty scan.\n\nfmt clean in both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; workspace tests 1,009 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored — up four from 1,005, and the four are this node's own, the unit tests `src/runtime/edge.rs` gained when `EdgeRuntime` moved into a module of its own; make_port 4/4 over 1,054 gated cases with seven new recordings and none rewritten, seven divergence sidecars unmoved; conformance 336 normalised identical / 51 differing, 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 manifest respellings, unmoved; upstream inventory 1,285 rows at 100 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,161 narration, tsv untouched, no row moved; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 31 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 46 s, both on a host carrying another tenant's build; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential, outcome, boundary and persistence band — the gate that matters most for a change inside the dirty walk, and it was run twice, once before the extraction into `graph::intermediate` and once after; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 777 passed / 0 failed, up four for the same reason.\n\nTwo code-quality gates fired and were fixed rather than bypassed: `recompute_edge_dirty_with` went over the line count, which is why the intermediate arms moved to `graph::intermediate`; and `src/runtime.rs` went over the function count, which is why `EdgeRuntime` has a module of its own — and the module then owed the test-footprint floor, which is where the four new tests came from."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-posix-target-is-read-where-it-is-written
                title "Fold the lines a .POSIX: only the evaluation could find still had to reach"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-posix-continuation-blanks)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T10:30:03.917357584Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The residue of make-posix-continuation-blanks: two cells out of twenty-three, and both of them are about WHEN the flag turns on rather than what it does.\n\nGNU Make reads one logical line, folds it, parses it, and only then reads the next — so `check_specials` (read.c) setting `posix_pedantic` on a rule's EXPANDED target names is in time for every line after it and no line before it. kati reads a whole file into statements and evaluates them afterwards, so the fold for line N is decided before the evaluation of line N-1 has happened. Where the two orders agree the flag is now right; where they do not, these are the cells:\n\n  include inc.mk        inc.mk holds `.POSIX:`, the fold is in the INCLUDER below the include\n    GNU   folds the POSIX way; Ronin does not — the includer was parsed whole before\n          the include was evaluated\n  $(P):                 with `P = .POSIX`, the fold below it in the SAME file\n    GNU   folds the POSIX way; Ronin does not — the parser sees `$(P)` and cannot\n          know what it spells, and by the time the evaluator does the line has been read\n\nBoth directions that CAN be reached are already right and gated: `.POSIX:` written down above the fold in the same file (a-posix-fold-keeps-the-blanks-in-front-of-it), in a file `-f` named earlier (a-posix-target-in-an-earlier-named-makefile), in the includer above an include (a-posix-fold-in-a-file-included-below-it), and an expansion-named one above a LATER include (a-posix-target-an-expansion-named-reaches-a-later-include).\n\nWhat closing this costs. Either the parse becomes lazy — one statement produced at a time, so the evaluator's answer is available when the next line is folded, which is GNU Make's own shape — or the evaluator re-parses what is left of a file when the flag turns on under it. The second is cheaper and worse: `Session::makefiles` caches a file's statements by name, so a re-parse has to decide what to do with the cache, and the statement list is shared.\n\nNot urgent. Both cells need `.POSIX:` and a continuation whose trailing blanks are load-bearing, in that order, which is a shape no real makefile has been seen to write."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T12:53:41.802927303Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T13:20:16.405241586Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "NOT DELIVERED, and probed far enough to say exactly what delivering it costs. The residue is SIX cells rather than the filed two, one of them was moving the wrong way, and one bounded defect the filing did not have was split out and fixed on its own — make-a-posix-target-is-read-off-text-that-names-none.\n\nTHE MATRIX, twelve cells, each run through reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and then through Ronin. Every one is `.POSIX` reaching a fold whose trailing blanks are load-bearing: `A = a   \\` over `  b`, read back as `[$(A)]`. GNU writes `[a    b]` under `.POSIX:` and `[a b]` without it.\n\n  `.POSIX:` written above the fold            both POSIX — control, gated\n  `# .POSIX:` in a comment                    both plain — control\n  `$(P):` with `P = .OTHER`                   both plain — control\n  `.POSIX:` in a `define` body                both plain — control\n  `$(info .POSIX:)`                           GNU plain; Ronin was POSIX      FIXED, split out\n  `.POSIX:` in an `ifeq` branch that loses    GNU plain; Ronin was POSIX      FIXED, split out\n  `.POSIX:` in an `ifdef` branch that loses   GNU plain; Ronin was POSIX      FIXED, split out\n  `.POSIX:` in an `ifeq` branch that WINS     GNU POSIX; Ronin plain          DIVERGENT, marked\n  `$(eval .POSIX:)` above the fold            GNU POSIX; Ronin plain          DIVERGENT, marked\n  `$(P):` with `P = .POSIX`                   GNU POSIX; Ronin plain          DIVERGENT (the filing's)\n  `$(E).POSIX$(E):` with `E` empty            GNU POSIX; Ronin plain          DIVERGENT\n  `include` whose file holds `.POSIX:`        GNU POSIX; Ronin plain          DIVERGENT (the filing's)\n  the same one include deeper                 GNU POSIX; Ronin plain          DIVERGENT\n\nThe three now fixed were all Ronin being POSIX where GNU is not, which is the direction that matters: it changes the value of every folded variable below a line in a makefile that never asked for POSIX, and `ifdef POSIXLY_CORRECT` / `.POSIX:` / `endif` is a shape a real makefile would write. The two newly marked are the price — reading a name the read cannot read was what made them agree, and they were agreeing by accident. Both are recorded as divergences beside the corpus so the gate keeps them rather than forgetting them.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT IS ONE MECHANISM. Every remaining cell is the same sentence: the flag turns on during the EVALUATION of a statement, and the fold it should have reached was decided when the file was PARSED, which happened first. GNU Make reads one logical line, folds it, parses it, evaluates it, and only then reads the next.\n\nWHY THE OBVIOUS FIX IS NOT AVAILABLE. Making kati's read lazy is not a matter of yielding statements one at a time: `Parser::enter_if` redirects `out_stmts` into an `IfStmt`'s `true_stmts` and `false_stmts` and the EVALUATOR chooses between them afterwards. A lazy read has to know which branch is taken while reading, which means moving conditional evaluation out of the evaluator and into the read — GNU Make's own arrangement, and a restructuring of the whole front end.\n\nTHE DESIGN THAT IS BOUNDED, sketched here so the next dispatch does not have to find it again. The flag changes the READ in exactly three places, all of them in `parse_expr_impl_ext` (src-rs/expr.rs) and all of them about literal blanks:\n\n  1. the discard — `let text = if kept == 0 && !posix { trim_right_space(text) } else { text }`\n  2. the run absorption — `skip_folded(loc, &s, .., posix)` stops at the first fold under POSIX and swallows the whole run without it\n  3. the same two at the `$`-against-a-fold site a few lines above\n\nNothing else about the parse differs: the same bytes split into the same logical lines, the same statements, the same expression structure. So the parse does not have to be deferred — only the CHOICE does. Give `Value` one more variant carrying both readings as finished bytes:\n\n    Folded { plain: Bytes, posix: Bytes }\n\nBoth readings consume the same input if the run is always skipped maximally (the skipped region is blanks and backslash-newlines and nothing else), and `posix` is then `text + \" \" + blanks1 + \" \" + blanks2 + \" \" ...` over that region while `plain` is `trim_right_space(text) + \" \"`. Computable where the fold is read, in one helper.\n\nWHEN THE CHOICE IS MADE is the other half, and it is smaller than it looks. A statement's own values are expanded while that statement is being evaluated, and at that moment `session.posix_pedantic` holds exactly what GNU Make's flag held when it read the line — everything above has been evaluated and nothing below has been read. So `Value::Folded` can simply read the session flag. The ONE exception is a value stored for later: a recursive variable's right-hand side is expanded at use, which may be after a `.POSIX:` the definition preceded. Give `Var` a `posix_at_definition: bool` recorded where the variable is created, and have `Evaluator::enter_expanding_var` carry it the way it already carries `expansion_loc`; `Value::Folded` reads the innermost such answer, falling back to the session's. A `:=` variable needs nothing — it is expanded at definition and stored as literal text.\n\nWHAT THAT COSTS, honestly: one variant added to `Value`, which every exhaustive match over it must answer; a helper at three call sites in the hottest parse path; a field on `Var` and two lines in `enter_expanding_var`. The risk is the parse path — `large-manifest-parse` is a performance workload and `parse_expr_impl_ext` is most of it — so the change wants its own measurement rather than a ride on someone else's.\n\nWHY IT WAS NOT FORCED THROUGH HERE. The dispatch asked for what is sound at bounded cost and a precise filing for the rest, and not for a restructuring taken as a side effect. The three false positives were the sound bounded part and are landed. The six that remain need the change above, want their own performance measurement, and are behind a shape the filing already judged: `.POSIX:` and a continuation whose trailing blanks carry meaning, in that order.\n\nThe node stays open and unclaimed, with the six cells measured and two of them gated by recorded divergences."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T06:38:19.410547901Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Doing → Todo, by retracting the assertion, because the machinery offers no transition that does it and leaving the node Doing made it UNSTARTABLE. The dispatch of 2026-08-22 measured six cells, delivered none of them, and said so in the decision entry above — but the node kept the `doing` assertion that dispatch's `nplan start` wrote, and nothing since could take it back.\n\nWHAT THE MACHINERY SAYS, each line measured in a throwaway worktree off 82aa058 rather than guessed:\n\n  nplan start make-a-posix-target-is-read-where-it-is-written\n    error: transition: node is Doing; start only transitions Todo → Doing\n\n  nplan note --kind errata make-a-posix-target-is-read-where-it-is-written \"...\"\n    error: store: plan image fails lint (1 violation(s))\n    — the same text under --kind note appends without complaint, so it is the\n      KIND that is refused: errata is for correcting a completion record, and\n      this node has none to correct\n\n  nplan unclaim make-a-posix-target-is-read-where-it-is-written\n    unclaim: (no claim was held)   — the claim had already been released; the\n      status outlived it, which is the whole shape of the problem\n\n  nplan note --kind status ... \"Doing → Todo\"\n    appended, and the tree still rendered [P1/doing]\n\nSo the node was visible in `nplan tree` and invisible to `nplan ready`, `nplan next` and `nplan start`: open work no dispatch could be handed, and no verb in the lifecycle to free it. `block` goes Doing → Blocked, `unblock` comes back to Doing, `complete` and `sync` go forward to a completion state. There is no abandon and no reset.\n\nTHIS IS NOT THE NO-REOPEN INVARIANT. `lc.no-reopen` governs COMPLETION states, and the binary says so where it fires: \"a completion state; completion is a record, not a rewrite (lc.no-reopen)\". Doing is not one. Nothing forbids the return; the tooling has no word for it.\n\nWHERE THE STATUS LIVED. Not in this log. It is the single `{id ..., kind @doing, ...}` row in the `assertions` block of plan/.state.styx — one row out of 414, the other 413 all `@complete`. Removing that row returns the node to Todo, and every question the machinery can be asked then agrees: `nplan lint` clean at 0 violations, `nplan audit` proposing no transitions, `nplan sync` declining to write the row back and reporting \"no observations recorded\". All measured in the worktree before this tree was touched.\n\nWHAT WAS RETRACTED AND WHAT WAS NOT. The retraction is of an assertion in the derived state store — the file `nplan sync` stamps and regenerates. This log is untouched: \"Todo → Doing (nplan start)\" of 2026-08-22T12:53:41Z still stands, the decision that recorded the non-delivery still stands, and this entry records the withdrawal. Nothing was in flight to withdraw except the claim that something was.\n\nWORTH FILING UPSTREAM IN NPLAN, and outside this repo's scope: a Doing node whose work is abandoned has no way home, and an `errata` cannot even be written on it to say so.\n\nThe six measured cells and the bounded `Value::Folded` design in the decision entry above are unchanged, and are still what this node owes."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T07:27:54.100783004Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T07:28:35.518727526Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "DELIVERED in full. The bounded `Value::Folded` design closes ALL SIX residual cells with no remainder: the honest fix did not need the lazy-read/conditional-in-the-read front-end restructuring the filing feared. Re-verified against the current oracle and current code (kati 8441be6) before building, per the skepticism instruction.\n\nRE-VERIFICATION. Each of the six cells was `A = a   \\` over `  b` read back as `[$(A)]`: oracle `[a    b]`, Ronin `[a b]` — still divergent. The sketch was right about the three fold sites in `parse_expr_impl_ext` (src-rs/expr.rs) and about `Value::Folded { plain, posix }`, and WRONG on one byte detail: it wrote a run's posix reading as `text + \" \" + blanks1 + \" \" + blanks2`, keeping the blanks between folds. The oracle keeps only the blanks in front of the FIRST backslash and drops every blank between and after folds — one space per folded newline (`two-posix-folds-are-two-spaces` = `a` + four spaces + `b`, verified live). The implementation follows the oracle, not the formula.\n\nTHE SIX CELLS, each now `[a    b]` matching the oracle: `.POSIX:` in an ifeq branch that WINS; `$(eval .POSIX:)` above the fold; `$(P):` with `P = .POSIX`; `$(E).POSIX$(E):` with E empty; an included file holding `.POSIX:` with the fold below the include; the same one include deeper.\n\nWHY NO REMAINDER. Every residual cell is a `.POSIX:` the EVALUATION reaches before it evaluates the fold's own definition — never one reaching a line already evaluated. GNU does not fold retroactively either (control `a-posix-target-does-not-reach-back-up-the-file`, and the matrix's \"written below → both plain\"), so no cell needs the restructuring. The bounded fix is the whole fix.\n\nTWO CORRECTIONS to the sketch, both from re-verification, both load-bearing:\n1. The resolve-flag is NOT `session.posix_pedantic`. The PARSER sets that flag (`note_posix_target`) and it is sticky through the whole eval pass, so a fold above a literal `.POSIX:` written below it would wrongly read posix at eval. The evaluator's own `is_posix` (eval.rs:2385, set only at the rule it reaches, never by the parser, in eval order) is the correct flag — and needs no new field.\n2. The reading must be settled at the DEFINITION, not at expansion (the sketch's `posix_at_definition` on Var carried by `enter_expanding_var`). A single per-variable flag CANNOT do it: `A = a\\b` (plain) then `.POSIX:` then `A += c\\d` (posix) needs the two folds read differently, which one flag on A cannot express. Resolving each fold into finished bytes where its definition stores its value reads each where GNU folds it. Teeth #2 is exactly this case.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, in kati on nplan/make-a-posix-target-is-read-where-it-is-written at 7b4b24bbcc74c71573e2deeb767ea09f6ef84959, pushed before this pin.\n- `Value::Folded { plain, posix }`: a fold whose two readings DIFFER, emitted at the two fold sites only when the read has not already seen `.POSIX:` AND the readings differ — so a makefile with no `.POSIX:`, or a fold with no load-bearing blanks, pays nothing, and a `.POSIX:` written above the fold still resolves at parse.\n- `Value::resolve_folds(posix)`: settles every Folded in a tree into the selected reading, sharing untouched subtrees. Called at `eval_rhs` where a definition stores its recursive value — which covers global, target-specific, pattern-specific and `define`, all of which route through it — and at the two re-parse assignment paths (`::=`, `!=`).\n- `Value::eval` answers a Folded that reaches it un-settled (an immediate context — a rule word, a function argument, `$(info ...)`) from `ev.is_posix()`.\n- Three read-only recipe scans in command.rs get the exhaustive arm (a Folded is finished bytes and references nothing).\n\nTEETH, each parked and the whole 1,178-case corpus run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the fold's reading is not deferred to the evaluation | 9 of 1,178 |\n| the fold is settled at expansion rather than at the definition | 2 of 1,178 |\n| the `$`-reference's run is not held | 1 of 1,178 |\nThe second is the `+=` case and the defined-above-`.POSIX:` control: proof the settling must be per-definition, not a per-variable flag.\n\nSEVEN FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, none rewritten. Four gate the residual cells: a-posix-target-an-expansion-named-reaches-a-later-fold, a-posix-target-an-empty-expansion-named-reaches-a-later-fold, a-posix-target-an-include-declares-reaches-a-later-fold, a-posix-target-an-include-two-deep-declares-reaches-a-later-fold. Three gate the mechanism: two-appended-folds-split-on-a-posix-between-them (per-definition settling), a-dollar-against-a-fold-an-eval-declares-reaches (the `$`-site), a-posix-define-body-an-eval-declares-folds-that-way (the define path).\n\nDIVERGENCE MARKERS: four → TWO. This node's two — a-posix-target-a-taken-branch-declares-reaches-a-later-fold and a-posix-target-an-eval-declares-reaches-a-later-fold — now match the oracle, so their `divergence` sidecars are deleted (the make_port gate fails a recorded divergence that has been fixed). The two remaining belong to other nodes (a-child-keeps-a-switch-value-a-dollar-was-written-in, makeflags-hands-back-the-switch-text-it-stores).\n\nPERFORMANCE. The sketch's named risk was a fold at every affected site in the hot parse path. Because Folded is emitted ONLY where the two readings differ, `large-manifest-parse` ran at 144.8 ms against Ninja's 298.9 ms (ratio 0.48) on a quiet host — no regression; the release performance gate validated and exited 0."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T07:29:04.426376786Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GATES, run serially on this tree with kati pinned at 7b4b24bbcc74c71573e2deeb767ea09f6ef84959 (branch nplan/make-a-posix-target-is-read-where-it-is-written, pushed before this pin).\n\nfmt clean in both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; workspace tests 1,086 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, unmoved; make_port 4/4 over 1,178 cases with seven new recordings and none rewritten — divergence sidecars four → TWO, the two removed being this node's; conformance 336 normalised identical / 51 differing, 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 manifest respellings, unmoved; upstream inventory 1,285 rows at 95 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,166 narration, tsv untouched, no row moved, compiler ZERO; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 15 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 28 s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus 10 differential, 92 build-outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence round trip; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 834 passed / 0 failed.\n\n`scripts/check-release.sh` was run END TO END and exited 0, performance comparison included, on a quiet host (15-minute load average ~1 on 32 cores). The three recorded workloads: large-manifest-parse Ronin 144.8 ms vs Ninja 298.9 ms; clean-tree-noop 8.279 vs 12.077; dependency-log-load 3.201 vs 4.032 — each Ronin-favouring and inside the gate's band."
                    }

