            {
                id make-an-old-file-is-not-remade
                title "Leave alone what --old-file said is older than everything"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-what-if-assumed-new)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T11:24:57.921089381Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-an-intermediate-the-build-found-is-not-swept-up, measured on both binaries. `-o` / `--old-file` / `--assume-old` is `OptionClass::NoOp` in src/make/cli.rs and does nothing at all, so a build the switch was meant to hold back runs in full.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, and it is `-W`'s own with the sign flipped. `-W` collects into `new_files` and `main` stamps `f->last_mtime = f->mtime_before_update = NEW_MTIME`; `-o` collects into `old_files` and stamps `OLD_MTIME`, which is 1 rather than `INTEGER_TYPE_MAXIMUM`. That one line is the whole of the switch, and `file_mtime_1` (filedef.h) is what makes it stick: with `last_mtime` already set, `f_mtime` is never called for that name at all.\n\nMEASURED, `out.o` present and older than `out.c`, `app` absent, `all: app`, `app: out.o`, `out.o: out.c`:\n\n  -o out.c   the name of the SOURCE\n    GNU   nothing runs; app is written from the out.o that was there, and holds `old`\n    Ronin remakes out.o and app, and app holds `made`\n\n  -o out.o   the name of the TARGET\n    GNU   the same: `-o` says do not remake this file either\n    Ronin the same divergence\n\n  no switch — the control\n    both remake out.o and app\n\nTHE SECOND HALF, which is why this is filed from the intermediate node. `f_mtime` never running means the declared intermediate bit is never turned off, so a file `-o` named is swept up after all where the same file without the switch is kept. Measured with `.INTERMEDIATE: out.o` and out.o present: GNU says `rm out.o` under `-o out.o` and keeps the file without it. Ronin now keeps it either way — the plan-time turn-off make-an-intermediate-the-build-found-is-not-swept-up landed does not know that an `-o` name's date is asserted rather than stated. So this node has to reach `treat_as_intermediate` as well as the dirty scan: a name the invocation stamped is one the search never asks about.\n\nWHERE. `make-what-if-assumed-new` is the shape to copy and its note is the design: not a compiler input and not an executor override but a SCAN POLICY, carried on `BuildOptions` as names rather than node ids because one Make run scans the same graph twice. `RuntimeState::assume_new` and `observe_node`'s `FileTime::NEWEST` have an obvious counterpart in `FileTime`'s other end, and `Invocation::assume_new` already routes `-o` through `command_line_file` for the empty-argument and canonicalisation gates — the switch is parsed and validated today and only its effect is missing. The restart guard `-W` needed (Savannah #7566) has no counterpart here: an assumed-OLD makefile cannot send the read around forever."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T14:21:29.480580357Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T14:50:47.719153282Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing had both halves right. `-o` / `--old-file` / `--assume-old` is a scan policy in the shape `-B`, `-W` and `-t` established, plus one thing none of those three had: two answers the READ draws, which is why kati moved as well.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, and it is `-W`'s with the sign flipped and one extra clause. `main` stamps `f->last_mtime = f->mtime_before_update = OLD_MTIME` (main.c:2312), and `OLD_MTIME` is 2 — one below `ORDINARY_MTIME_MIN` and one above `NONEXISTENT_MTIME` (filedef.h) — so the name reads as THERE and as older than every real file. `file_mtime` (filedef.h) calls `f_mtime` only for a date that is still unknown, so for an `-o` name the filesystem is never asked at all. The extra clause is the line beside the date: `f->updated = 1`, `f->update_status = us_success`, `f->command_state = cs_finished`, and `update_file_1` returns on `file->updated` (remake.c) before it looks at a recipe, a prerequisite, or a switch. That one return is four measured behaviours at once.\n\nTwo differences from `-W` that the filing predicted and the probe confirmed. There is no `restarts` guard on the `-o` stamp where `-W` has one (main.c:2312 against main.c:2325 and main.c:2837), because an assumed-OLD makefile cannot send the read around forever. And the two are stamped in a fixed order, `-o` then `-W`, so a name given to both is NEW whichever order the words were written in — measured both ways.\n\nTHE MATRIX. Ninety-one cells, each run through reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and then through Ronin. Ronin now agrees with GNU Make on eighty-nine of them; the two it does not are one filed defect, below.\n\n  THE DUAL CONTRACT\n    -o out.o, out.o stale                     out.o is not remade                       FIXED\n    -o out.o, dependent EXISTS and is older   the dependent is NOT rebuilt              FIXED\n      — this is the cell that settles which mechanism: GNU asserts the name VERY OLD,\n        not current, so an existing dependent is newer than it and stays\n    -o out.c, the name of a SOURCE            nothing under it runs                     FIXED\n    -o app, the name of a target that is ABSENT   nothing runs, and out.o BENEATH it is\n        not remade either — `update_file_1` returns before it reaches a prerequisite    FIXED\n    -o nosuch, a name no rule mentions        the control; `enter_file` makes a file\n        nothing depends on\n\n  ABSENCE\n    -o missing, no rule and not there, needed by a goal\n        without: `No rule to make target 'missing'`, exit 2.  With: the goal runs.      FIXED\n    -f nope.mk -o nope.mk, a REQUIRED makefile that is not there and has no rule\n        without: refused, exit 2.  With: the read carries on, and the open's own\n        warning is printed either way.                                                  FIXED\n    -o app where app is a goal with a recipe and is absent   `'app' is up to date.`     FIXED\n\n  AGAINST THE OTHER SWITCHES\n    -o out.o -W out.o  /  -W out.o -o out.o   -W wins in BOTH orders                    FIXED\n    -o out.o -B                               -B remakes everything else; the -o name\n        is the one target -B does not reach                                             FIXED\n    -B -o app app                             `'app' is up to date.`                    FIXED\n    -o out.o -t                               the -o name is not touched                FIXED\n    -t -o app                                 app is not touched; app stays absent      FIXED\n    -o app -q                                 exit 0 where the same -q exits 1          FIXED\n    -o out.o -n                               nothing to be done                        FIXED\n    -k -o a over a failing a                  the control\n\n  SHAPES\n    .PHONY, -o ph                             the phony stops being forced AND stops\n        dragging its dependent — what forces a phony is that it does not exist          FIXED\n    .PHONY, -o ph -B                          -B rebuilds the dependent; ph still\n        does not run                                                                    FIXED\n    double-colon, -o out                      INERT, twice: with the dependent older\n        than the target and with it newer. `enter_file` returns the entry it found only\n        when that entry is not a double-colon target (file.c:177), so the stamp lands on\n        a file object it has just made and nothing consults.                            FIXED\n    grouped `a b &:`, -o a                    the record still runs and writes both     control\n    grouped `a b &:`, -o a -o b               nothing runs                              FIXED\n    order-only `out: | oo`, -o oo             oo does not run                           FIXED\n    archive `lib.a(m.o)`, -o m.o              the control\n    a chain intermediate a pattern invented, -o out.c    it is never made and the chain\n        runs on around it                                                               control\n\n  INTERMEDIATES — the half that reaches the read\n    .INTERMEDIATE: out.o present, -o out.o    GNU says `rm out.o`; the same build\n        without the switch KEEPS it. `f_mtime` never runs, so the turn-off at the end of\n        it never runs, and `remove_intermediates` finds the bit still set and\n        `update_status` no longer `us_none` — `-o` wrote `us_success` over it.          FIXED\n    .INTERMEDIATE: out.o ABSENT, -o out.o     nothing made, nothing removed             FIXED\n    .SECONDARY: out.o, -o out.o               kept; secondary is never swept            control\n    bare .SECONDARY:, -o out.o                kept; the sweep is off for the whole run  control\n    .PRECIOUS + .INTERMEDIATE, -o out.o       kept                                      control\n    the same four under -q, -n, -t, -B        each agrees\n\n  MAKEFILES\n    include gen.mk, -o gen.mk                 gen.mk is not remade and the read does\n        not go around — the stamp is before the makefile update on EVERY pass           FIXED\n    include gen.mk, -o gen.src                the same, from the prerequisite side      FIXED\n    a makefile remade so the read RESTARTS, then -o out.o for the goals    honoured\n        after the restart, which is where `-W` is deliberately withheld                 FIXED\n    -include opt.mk, -o opt.mk                the control\n\n  DESCENT AND SPELLING\n    $(MAKE) child, -o sub                     the child is NOT told and remakes sub.\n        `-o` shares `-W`'s cleared `toenv` (main.c:484), so neither reaches MAKEFLAGS.\n        MAKEFLAGS and MFLAGS both read empty under `-o`, measured.                      control\n    -o pre.out over a recursive recipe's own target    the recipe does not run — the\n        question is asked where the graph is composed                                   FIXED\n    MAKEFLAGS='-o out.o' in the ENVIRONMENT   honoured; `decode_env_switches` passes\n        `o_command` for the startup read (main.c:1603)                                  agreed\n    `MAKEFLAGS += -o out.o` written BY A MAKEFILE   INERT; the second pass reads\n        `o_env` and `-o` has `env` clear                                                agreed\n    -o '' , --old-file= , --assume-old=       `the '-o' option requires a non-empty\n        string argument`, exit 2 — every spelling names `-o`                            agreed\n    --old-file / --assume-old / -o with the argument MISSING   `option '--old-file'\n        requires an argument` and `option requires an argument -- 'o'`, each naming the\n        spelling that was written                                                       agreed\n    -o ./out.o                                names out.o; only a LEADING ./ is stripped FIXED\n    -o d/./out.o                              names a file no rule mentions             control\n    -o out.o -o out.o                         one stamp, said twice                     FIXED\n    -oout.o attached, and -no out.o in a cluster    both read                           FIXED\n    VPATH without GPATH, -o out.o             held through the search; `vpath.c:430`\n        preserves OLD_MTIME rather than stating what it found                           FIXED\n    VPATH without GPATH, -o src/out.o         names nothing                             control\n\nWHAT CHANGED, in kati on nplan/make-an-old-file-is-not-remade at 4cd013b4db27703ea96a5cee481bc27dead77fb6, pushed before this pin. `Flags::old_files` carries the names to the read, and two places decline them: `found_before_the_build` — beside the `.PHONY` and `GPATH` names it already declined — and the refusal `plan_regeneration` raises over a required unread include with no rule.\n\nIn Ronin, `FileTime::OLDEST` beside `NEWEST`, `BuildOptions::assumed_old` beside `assumed_new`, `RuntimeState::assumed_old` beside it again, and a new `AssertedDates` that carries both lists together and resolves them against whichever graph a scan is about to read — because two `&[BString]` parameters side by side swap silently. Four readers: `nodestat_with` answers OLDEST without a syscall; the dirty walk STOPS at such a node, which is `update_file_1`'s early return and is what keeps the scan from finding a stale prerequisite and remaking the very name the switch named; `load_depfiles_for` stops at the same node, because an edge that walk called out of date on the way past would stay called that; and `disposable_outputs` sweeps an `-o` name whose edge is disposable even though the plan never wanted it, which is `remove_intermediates`' `update_status == us_none` test. `Graph::is_written_undeclared` is the double-colon clause, and `-o` alone reads it — see below.\n\nTEETH, each parked and the whole corpus of 1,078 gated cases run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the whole switch, stamping nothing | 13 |\n| the walk stopping at the name | 9 |\n| the same stop on the depfile walk | 3 |\n| the batched stat honouring it | 2 |\n| the sweep reaching an -o name | 1 |\n| the intermediate turn-off declining an -o name (kati) | 1 |\n| the refusal an -o makefile is spared (kati) | 1 |\n| the double-colon name the stamp misses | 1 |\n| the switch reaching a recursive recipe | 1 |\n| `nodestat_with`'s own OLDEST branch | 0 — RECORDED AS SUCH |\n\nThe last was chased rather than assumed. Every name a scan reaches is either pre-stated by the batched stat, which answers OLDEST for these, or met by the walk, which stops before any stat. The branch is what answers a name that reaches `nodestat_with` through neither — the deferred-freshness path and the dyndep path both can — and it is kept for the reason the `GPATH` node kept its two: the date is wrong whether or not a fixture notices.\n\nNINETEEN FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten. Eighteen gate the fix: an-old-file-is-not-remade, an-old-file-does-not-force-what-reads-it, an-old-file-hides-what-lies-beneath-it, an-old-file-outranks-always-make, a-what-if-outranks-an-old-file, an-old-file-that-is-phony-stops-being-forced, an-old-file-that-is-intermediate-is-swept-up, an-old-makefile-is-not-remade, an-old-makefile-that-is-not-there-is-not-refused, an-old-file-with-no-rule-is-not-refused, an-old-file-a-double-colon-declares-is-left-alone, an-old-file-under-a-question-is-up-to-date, an-old-file-is-not-touched, an-old-file-the-search-path-found, an-old-file-does-not-reach-a-recursive-child, an-old-file-over-a-recursive-recipes-own-target, an-empty-old-file-refuses-the-run, an-old-file-a-group-shares-still-runs-the-group. One carries a divergence sidecar and gates the gap: an-old-file-a-gpath-renamed-target-was-written-as.\n\nTHE SWITCH TABLE. `-o` moves from `OptionClass::NoOp` to `OptionClass::NinjaControl`, which empties the set of no-op switches that take an argument AND have a short letter — the comment in `accept_noop_long` that used `--old-file=` as its example now says so rather than naming a switch that has left.\n\nTWO DEFECTS FILED, both found here and both `-W`'s as much as `-o`'s:\n  make-a-what-if-file-that-is-double-colon-refuses — `-W` over a `::` target makes GNU Make refuse with `No rule to make target 'out'`, because the fresh `enter_file` entry carries a date and no recipe. `-o` gets away with the same entry only because it also writes `us_success`. This is why `is_written_undeclared` is read by `-o` alone: declining the `-W` stamp too would turn a refusal into a quiet success and hide the gap.\n  make-a-switch-names-a-target-gpath-renamed — a `GPATH` rename replaces the written name with the found path in Ronin's graph, so `-o out.o` reaches nothing and `-o src/out.o` reaches a target GNU Make says the switch never named. `-W` has the same hole from the other side, and it was divergent before this node.\n\nINVENTORY AUDIT. 1,285 rows at 100 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,161 narration, the tsv untouched and no row moved in either direction. The compiler bucket stays at zero. `-o` was already accepted, so no `option-refused` row could move; its effects are build effects, which the inventory reads as narration and the build-intent gate reads as evidence."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T14:53:53.177148372Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T14:59:09.698287403Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GATES for the landing above, run serially on this tree with kati pinned at 4cd013b4db27703ea96a5cee481bc27dead77fb6 (branch nplan/make-an-old-file-is-not-remade, pushed before the pin).\n\nfmt clean in both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean — it caught `read_cluster` at 109 lines and the two switches were merged into one arm, which is what they are; workspace tests 1,009 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, unmoved; make_port 4/4 over 1,078 gated cases with nineteen new recordings and none rewritten, divergence sidecars nine → ten and the tenth is the filed GPATH cell; conformance 336 normalised identical / 51 differing, 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 manifest respellings, unmoved; upstream inventory 1,285 rows at 100 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,161 narration, tsv untouched, no row moved; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 17 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 33 s; ninja conformance 425/425 across 33 suites plus 10 differentials, 86 build-outcome cases, 3 invocation-boundary cases, persistence round trip and log content; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 777 passed / 0 failed.\n\n`scripts/check-release.sh` was run END TO END against this tree and exited 0, performance comparison included. The host carried another tenant's nextest run for part of the dispatch — 15-minute load averages between 8 and 30, which is why the teeth measurements took as long as they did — but it had left by the time the release gate reached its last stage, and the run needed no retry."
