            {
                id make-a-posix-target-is-read-off-text-that-names-none
                title "Read .POSIX off a name the read can read and off no other text"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-posix-continuation-blanks)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T13:21:01.439056692Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found and delivered while probing make-a-posix-target-is-read-where-it-is-written, which owns the opposite half. That node's residue is the flag turning on too LATE; this one is the flag turning on where GNU Make never turns it on at all — and it is the half that reaches a makefile someone would actually write.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. `check_specials` (read.c) is handed a rule's EXPANDED target names, and it is handed them only for a line GNU Make actually read. `Parser::note_posix_target` was reading the words of the line as written, out of any line it classified as a rule, and neither guard was there.\n\nMEASURED, `A = a   \\` over `  b` read back as `[$(A)]` — GNU writes `[a    b]` under `.POSIX:` and `[a b]` without it:\n\n  $(info .POSIX:)\n    GNU   expands the line to nothing at all and reads no target; `[a b]`\n    Ronin found the word `.POSIX` in the text before the colon; `[a    b]`\n\n  ifeq (1,0) / .POSIX: / endif        the same, and\n  ifdef POSIXLY_CORRECT / .POSIX: / endif   the same again\n    GNU   chooses the branch as it reads, so the line is never read; `[a b]`\n    Ronin buffers both branches for the evaluation to choose between and read\n          the losing one anyway; `[a    b]`\n\nThe third is the one that matters. Offering POSIX behaviour behind a variable is a shape a real makefile writes, and a makefile that wrote it got POSIX whether or not the variable was set — silently, in the value of every folded variable below.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, in kati on nplan/make-a-posix-target-is-read-where-it-is-written at 74c5fc2b3fdf8563e0f8a9d12d84be8c53382d52, pushed before this pin. `note_posix_target` declines two kinds of text: a target list holding a `$`, which is text rather than names, and any line reached while `if_stack` is not empty. What it declines falls to the evaluator, which sets the same flag when it reaches such a rule — in time for whatever is read after it, and too late for what was read before, which is the other node's subject.\n\nTHE PRICE, and it is recorded rather than hidden. Two cells were agreeing because of the misreading and now do not: `.POSIX:` in the branch a conditional TAKES, and `$(eval .POSIX:)` above the fold — the second only ever agreed because `.POSIX` happened to appear before a colon in the eval's argument text. Both are recorded as divergences beside their cases, owned by make-a-posix-target-is-read-where-it-is-written, so the gate keeps them and fails when they are fixed.\n\nTEETH, each parked and the whole corpus run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the guard against a target list holding a `$` | 1 of 1,059 |\n| the guard against a line inside a conditional | 2 of 1,059 |\n\nFIVE FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten. Three gate the fix: a-posix-target-a-function-call-only-mentions-is-not-one, a-posix-target-in-a-branch-the-conditional-drops-is-not-read, a-posix-target-an-undefined-conditional-drops-is-not-read. Two carry a divergence sidecar and gate the gap: a-posix-target-a-taken-branch-declares-reaches-a-later-fold, a-posix-target-an-eval-declares-reaches-a-later-fold.\n\nINVENTORY AUDIT. 1,285 rows at 100 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,161 narration, the tsv untouched and no row moved. Compiler stays at zero."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T13:21:19.608991641Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T13:29:52.81082318Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GATES for the landing above, run serially on this tree at fc7d63183cbaba6a556c9d045bd7bcda00a7157c with kati pinned at 74c5fc2b3fdf8563e0f8a9d12d84be8c53382d52.\n\nfmt clean in both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; workspace tests 1,009 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, unmoved; make_port 4/4 over 1,059 gated cases with five new recordings and none rewritten — the divergence sidecars go from seven to NINE, and the two added are named above and owned by make-a-posix-target-is-read-where-it-is-written; conformance 336 normalised identical / 51 differing, 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 manifest respellings, unmoved; upstream inventory 1,285 rows at 100 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,161 narration, tsv untouched, no row moved; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 32 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 58 s, on a host carrying another tenant's build; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential, outcome, boundary and persistence band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 777 passed / 0 failed.\n\n`scripts/check-release.sh` was run stage by stage on this tree rather than as one command, because its last stage is the performance comparison and the host was not this dispatch's alone: another tenant's build kept the 15-minute load average between 5 and 25 from early afternoon onward. Every deterministic stage is green here — `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, `cargo check --all-targets`, `scripts/check-lints.sh`, `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps`, `cargo test --all-targets --no-fail-fast`, equivalence, the real-project builds, `scripts/check-port-ledger.sh` at 170 symbols with every rule written, claimed and tested, ninja conformance, `nplan spec uncovered` clear, `nplan spec stale` clear, `nplan lint` clean and `nplan audit` clean. The performance comparison is the one recorded flake under load and is the only stage this tree has not been measured against on a quiet host."
