            {
                id make-a-what-if-file-that-is-double-colon-refuses
                title "Refuse over a --what-if name a double-colon rule declares"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-an-old-file-is-not-remade)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T14:23:18.177218184Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-an-old-file-is-not-remade, measured on both binaries. `-W NAME` where NAME is a double-colon target: GNU Make 4.4.1 REFUSES the run with `*** No rule to make target 'out'.  Stop.` and exit 2, building nothing. Ronin accepts it and builds on.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, and it is the same clause the `-o` node had to model. `enter_file` (file.c:177) returns the entry it found only when that entry is not a double-colon target: `if (! HASH_VACANT (f) && !f->double_colon) return f;`. So a switch that stamps a name by entering it appends a FOURTH `struct file` to a `::` chain of three, and that new entry has a date and no `cmds`. `-o` gets away with it because it also writes `updated`, `us_success` and `cs_finished`, so `update_file_1` returns before it can look for a recipe; `-W` writes only the date, so the update reaches the recipe-less entry and `complain()` refuses over it.\n\nMEASURED, `all: app`, `app: out`, `out:: a`, `out:: b`, with a and b at 2021, out at 2022 and app at 2020:\n\n  no switch   nothing runs for out, app is remade — control\n  -o out      the same as the control; the stamp landed where nothing reads it\n  -W out      `make: *** No rule to make target 'out'.  Stop.`, rc 2, nothing built\n  -W out out  the same refusal with the name as the goal\n\nThe same refusal appears with app NEWER than out, so it is not a freshness answer: it is the update meeting a target with no way to make it.\n\nWHAT LANDED ALREADY, and what did not. make-an-old-file-is-not-remade added `Graph::is_written_undeclared` — a name an edge writes while no edge declares it an output, which in this graph is exactly a double-colon target — and `AssertedDates::mark_on` drops an `-o` name that answers to it, which is what makes `-o` inert there as GNU Make is. `-W` deliberately does NOT use that gate, because declining the stamp would turn a refusal into a quiet success and hide the gap. So the machinery to reach the case is in place and only the refusal is missing.\n\nWHERE. The refusal is a read-time answer about a name the invocation gave, so it belongs beside the other switch-name gate rather than in the scan: a `-W` name that `is_written_undeclared` says is a double-colon target has to become the same refusal kati raises for a required makefile nothing can make. Beware the double-colon record that HAS no prerequisites and the one declared `.PHONY`, which `is_remakable` already treats apart."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T22:42:45.728868046Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T23:37:08.770731037Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing named the site correctly: this is a read-time answer, not a scan's. What the filing did not have is the two things the probe settled — that the refusal is conditional on the update REACHING the name, and that a lone `::` record is refused over exactly as a chain of three is, which is the fact that decides where the reading can live at all.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, finished. `main` stamps a `-W` name by entering it (main.c:2325). `enter_file` (file.c:177) returns the entry it found only when that entry is not a double-colon target, so the stamp appends a fresh `struct file` to the chain: a date, and no `cmds`. `update_file` walks the chain by `f->prev`, the fresh entry is last, and `remake_file` (remake.c:1287) meets a file with no commands that is neither `phony` nor `is_target` and calls `complain()` over it. The refusal is not about the date at all — `-o` writes the same kind of entry and escapes because it also writes `updated` and `us_success`, and `update_file_1` returns on `file->updated` before it can look for a recipe.\n\nTHE MATRIX. Twenty-four cells, each run through reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and then through Ronin. Ronin now agrees with GNU Make on twenty; the four it does not are one recorded difference, below.\n\n  THE REFUSAL, over `all: app` / `app: out` / `out:: a` / `out:: b`\n    -W out, chain up to date, app older        `No rule to make target 'out'`, rc 2,\n        app not remade                                                            FIXED\n    -W out, app NEWER than out                 the same refusal — it is not a freshness\n        answer                                                                    FIXED\n    -W out out, the name as the goal too       the same refusal                   FIXED\n    -W out over a LONE `out:: a`               the same refusal, and the shape whose\n        graph has nothing to tell it from an ordinary rule                        FIXED\n    -W a, a `::` prerequisite                  builds; the chain is not the target\n        the switch named                                                          control\n    -o out                                     inert, both — make-an-old-file-is-not-remade\n        already gated this                                                        control\n\n  REACHED, WHICH IS THE WHOLE CONDITION\n    -W other where nothing the goals reach wants `other`   `Nothing to be done`, rc 0,\n        silent                                                                    FIXED\n    -W other other, the same name as a goal    refused                            FIXED\n    -W out where `app: | out` is order-only    refused; the update reaches an\n        order-only prerequisite too                                               FIXED\n    include gen.mk beside it, -W out           refused over `out`                  FIXED\n    `gen.mk:: seed` and -W gen.mk              refused over `gen.mk`, and the goals are\n        never reached                                                             FIXED\n\n  AGAINST THE OTHER SWITCHES — every one refuses, and none of them changes it\n    -W out -o out  /  -o out -W out            refused in both orders              FIXED\n    -W out -k                                  refused; GNU adds `Target 'all' not\n        remade because of errors` and remakes nothing either way                   FIXED\n    -W out -n, -W out -q, -W out -t            refused, rc 2 — `-q`'s 1 becomes 2   FIXED\n    -W out -B                                  refused                             FIXED\n\n  SPELLING\n    --what-if out, --new-file=out, --assume-new out   each refuses                 FIXED\n    -W ./out                                   names `out`; only a LEADING ./ is\n        stripped, as the `-o` node measured                                        FIXED\n\n  WHERE THE REFUSAL FALLS — the four cells that do not agree\n    -W out with a prereq-less `out::` rule     GNU runs `touch out` and THEN refuses\n    -W out with the `::` target `.PHONY`       GNU runs both rules and THEN refuses\n    -W out -B                                  GNU runs both rules and THEN refuses\n    -W other other, other absent               GNU runs both rules and THEN refuses\n  In every one of them both tools refuse, both exit 2 and both leave the dependent\n  alone; the difference is the file the chain's own recipe wrote on GNU's way to the\n  same answer. GNU Make updates the chain entry by entry and meets the fresh\n  recipe-less one last; a compiled graph is planned before any of it runs, so the\n  refusal comes first. Recorded as discovery rather than chased — reproducing it\n  would mean running a recipe in order to throw it away, which is Make's update walk\n  and not a graph.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, in kati on nplan/make-a-what-if-file-that-is-double-colon-refuses at f2246875fa61dabcb8dfafafbaca7dafc3378a13, pushed before this pin. `Flags::new_files` beside `old_files`, filled by the Make front end from the same invocation field the scan reads. `DepBuilder::refuse_an_asserted_double_colon` refuses over any `-W` name that the plan reached (`done`) and that a `::` record declares (`double_memberships`), after the makefiles have had their say — GNU Make dies in the makefile update one pass earlier when both apply — with `complain()`'s own wording, which is the read's voice here as it already is for a required include nothing can make.\n\nWHY NOT THE SCAN, which is where the first attempt went and where it can only half work. A `::` record with more than one rule compiles to an action per rule plus a completion join, and the name the Makefile wrote is a node in NO edge's inputs or outputs — reached only through `deferred_freshness` and `completion_join_output`. A refusal keyed on `Graph::is_written_undeclared` and raised on the plan walk was written, measured, and it does answer the chain cells. It cannot answer the LONE `::` record, because kati compiles that to the single-node shape it shares with an ordinary rule (dep.rs: \"a lone `::` record keeps the single-node shape it already had\") and nothing in the graph says `::` any more. Carrying the fact across the sink to say so would be a new field, a new node property and a new refusal — to reach a case the read already knows about. That attempt was reverted whole.\n\nTEETH, each parked and the whole corpus of 1,092 gated cases run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the refusal itself | 2 |\n| the `reached` test, so every `-W` name a `::` declares refuses | 1 |\n| the `::` test, so every reached `-W` name refuses | 9 |\nThe last is the measurement that the guard is what keeps ordinary `-W` working: it takes down `what-if-option`, `what-if-forces-a-phony-to-be-current`, `what-if-names-a-file-the-search-path-found`, `what-if-reads-a-makefile-once-and-not-forever`, `a-what-if-outranks-an-old-file` and four more.\n\nFOUR FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten. Three gate:\n  a-what-if-file-a-double-colon-declares-refuses-the-run — the chain, up to date.\n  a-what-if-file-a-lone-double-colon-declares-refuses-the-run — one `::` rule, which is\n    the case the graph cannot see and the read can.\n  a-what-if-file-no-goal-reaches-is-not-refused — the control: `other` is a `::` target\n    the switch names and nothing reaches, and the build runs and remakes its goal.\nOne is discovery: a-what-if-file-that-is-double-colon-refuses-after-the-chain-ran, in\n`DISCOVERY_ONLY_CASES` with the reason written beside the others, holding what GNU Make\nran before it refused.\n\nGATES\n  workspace                1044/0/1, unmoved\n  --no-default-features    806/0, unmoved\n  make_port                4/4 over 1,092 gated cases (1,089 plus three), ten divergence markers unmoved, eight discovery-only cases\n  conformance              336/51 normalised, 286 raw, class and family tables identical\n  equivalence              297/280/75/0, manifest respellings 8, unchanged\n  upstream inventory       1285 at 100/0/24/1161, compiler zero, TSV byte-identical, no row moved\n  ninja conformance        425/425, 10 CLI, 86 build outcome, 3 invocation, persistence, log content\n  real projects            vim 9.2 in 31s, zsh 5.9.2 and 36 modules in 50s, both at -j8\n  scripts/check-release.sh exit 0 end to end\n  fmt, check-lints.sh      clean\nThe performance stage refused twice before it passed, on a host carrying a 15-minute load average of 14 to 15: `deep-graph-evaluation` at 0.91x against the recorded 0.72x and then `wide-noop-build` at 1.10x against 0.82x — a different workload each time, which is what noise looks like. Measured again on the same tree: deep-graph 6.561 ms against Ninja's 8.075 for 0.81, wide-noop 9.784 against 10.832 for 0.90, and `check-release.sh` green end to end."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T23:40:21.885268195Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-switch-names-a-target-gpath-renamed
                title "Let a switch name a GPATH-renamed target as the makefile wrote it"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-an-old-file-is-not-remade)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T14:23:38.321932528Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-an-old-file-is-not-remade, measured on both binaries, and it is `-o`'s and `-W`'s in equal measure — one hole reached from two sides.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. `GPATH` says a directory the search looks in is also the directory a target found there is remade in, so `f_mtime` calls `rename_file` and the file object moves to the found path while the rule the makefile wrote goes on making it (remake.c). GNU Make's switch names are matched BEFORE any of that — `main` enters them and stamps them (main.c:2312, main.c:2325) at the name as written — and the rename never revisits the answer, because `file_mtime` calls `f_mtime` only for a date that is still unknown. So `-o out.o` holds through a rename to `src/out.o`, and `-o src/out.o` names a file GNU Make's `enter_file` invents and nothing depends on.\n\nRonin renames in the compiler: kati's `gpath_origin` maps the found path back to the name that carries its rule, and the node in the graph is the FOUND path. The name the makefile wrote is not in the graph at all, so both switches answer backwards.\n\nMEASURED, `VPATH = src`, `GPATH = src`, `all: app`, `app: out.o`, `out.o: out.c`, with src/out.c at 2021 and src/out.o at 2022:\n\n  app at 2020 — app is out of date without any switch\n    -o out.o        GNU leaves app alone; Ronin remakes it        DIVERGENT\n    -o src/out.o    GNU remakes app; Ronin leaves it alone        DIVERGENT, the other way\n  app at 2024 — nothing is out of date without any switch\n    -W out.o        GNU remakes app; Ronin leaves it alone        DIVERGENT\n    -W src/out.o    both leave app alone — the same control\n\nPlain `VPATH` without `GPATH` agrees in every cell, because there the node keeps the name as written and carries the found path beside it.\n\nWHERE. The graph has to keep the written name for a renamed target, the way it already keeps `searched_at` for the other half of the search: `Graph::searched_at` is the found path for a node left where it was written, and this is the same pair with the survivors swapped. `AssertedDates::mark_on` is then the one reader that has to look through it, and it is the only place either switch resolves a name.\n\nONE FIXTURE ALREADY GATES THE GAP: tests/make/an-old-file-a-gpath-renamed-target-was-written-as, with a divergence sidecar naming this node. The `-W` half has no fixture yet and should gain one here."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T23:41:00.659614998Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T00:43:49.742570529Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. The filing had the mechanism right and one of the four cells wrong, and the cell it got wrong is the one that explains the other three: GNU Make has a kludge here, it says so in its own source, and it is why the two switches answer the found spelling differently.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, finished. `main` stamps both switches after the read and before the update (main.c:2312, main.c:2325), so `enter_file` matches a name against the file database as the makefiles left it — before any rename. `f_mtime` then searches for a target it cannot find here, and where `gpath_search` says the found directory is one `GPATH` names it calls `rename_file` (remake.c:1443). Two consequences, one per spelling.\n\nThe name as WRITTEN is the one the stamp landed on, and the file object it landed on is the one that gets renamed, so the stamp travels with it. For `-o` there is a second reason it holds: `file_mtime` calls `f_mtime` only for a date that is still unknown, so an `-o` name is never searched for at all.\n\nThe name as FOUND was not a target when the switch was read, so it gets a file object of its own — and the rename then meets it. `rehash_file` merges the two and keeps the LATER of the two dates:\n\n    if (from_file->last_mtime > to_file->last_mtime)\n      /* %%% Kludge so -W wins on a file that gets vpathized.  */\n      to_file->last_mtime = from_file->last_mtime;\n\nAn asserted NEW date is later than any real file's and wins; an asserted OLD one is earlier than any real file's and loses to the date the search has just taken. So `-W src/out.o` reaches the target and `-o src/out.o` is inert — which is where the filing was wrong: it recorded `-W src/out.o` as a control where both tools leave the dependent alone, and 4.4.1 remakes it.\n\nTHE MATRIX, `VPATH = src` / `GPATH = src`, `app: out.o` / `out.o: out.c`, src/out.o at 2022 and src/out.c at 2021.\n\n  app at 2020 — app is out of date without any switch\n    control              app is remade                                        agreed\n    -o out.o             app is NOT remade                                    FIXED\n    -o src/out.o         app IS remade; the switch reaches nothing            FIXED\n  app at 2024 — nothing is out of date without any switch\n    control              nothing runs                                         agreed\n    -W out.o             app is remade                                        FIXED\n    -W src/out.o         app is remade — the kludge                           agreed\n  VPATH without GPATH, all four spellings                                     agreed\n    the node keeps the name as written and carries the found path beside it,\n    which make-an-old-file-is-not-remade already settled\n\n  THE DOUBLE-COLON CELL, `out:: a` / `out:: b` moved into src, no out.c anywhere\n    -W out               `No rule to make target 'src/out'`, rc 2 — the appended\n        entry is carried into the GPATH directory with the rest of the chain,\n        so the complaint names the path it was moved to                       FIXED\n    -W src/out           the run is not refused and app is remade — the found\n        spelling is not a `::` name, which\n        make-a-what-if-double-colon-refusal-reads-the-implicit-search settled  agreed\n    -o out               nothing to be done                                    agreed\n\nWHAT CHANGED. In kati on nplan/make-a-switch-names-a-target-gpath-renamed at 63b607ecd94bc2e1dfd4c5f91b8f903701ccb899, pushed before this pin: `SinkEdge::written_as` is `searched_at` with the survivors swapped — the name the Makefile wrote, for the few outputs the search moved, and only where the edge's own output is the target the rule was written for, because a `::` action's output is a private name the compiler invented and the rename is the target's rather than the action's. `DepBuilder::found_as` reads `gpath_origin` backwards for the one caller that starts from a name rather than from a node.\n\nIn Ronin: `Graph::written_as` beside `searched_at` and read by exactly one thing — the invocation, not the build. `AssertedDates::mark_on` resolves a switch's name through `moved_from_written` when the arena does not hold it, and declines a `-o` name that IS a moved node's found path, which is the kludge written out as the `Moved` enum.\n\nTEETH, each parked and the whole corpus of 1,099 gated cases run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the written name reaching the moved node | 2 — one per switch |\n| the `-o` decline of the found spelling | 1 |\n| kati's `found_as`, so the refusal cannot reach a moved `::` record | 1 |\n\nFOUR FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, and ONE DIVERGENCE SIDECAR DELETED:\n  an-old-file-a-gpath-renamed-target-was-written-as — the fixture make-an-old-file-is-not-remade left gating this gap; its `divergence` file is gone and it gates the fix.\n  an-old-file-spelt-as-a-gpath-rename-found-it-reaches-nothing\n  a-what-if-file-a-gpath-renamed-target-was-written-as\n  a-what-if-file-spelt-as-a-gpath-rename-found-it-wins\n  a-what-if-file-a-gpath-moved-double-colon-declares-refuses\n\nGATES\n  workspace                1044/0/1, unmoved\n  --no-default-features    806/0, unmoved\n  make_port                4/4 over 1,099 gated cases, NINE divergence markers — one fewer, the one this node owned\n  conformance              336/51 normalised, 286 raw, class and family tables identical\n  equivalence              297/280/75/0, manifest respellings 8, unchanged\n  upstream inventory       exit 0, TSV byte-identical, no row moved\n  ninja conformance        425/425 plus the differentials\n  real projects            vim 9.2 in 16s, zsh 5.9.2 and 36 modules in 31s, both at -j8\n  scripts/check-release.sh exit 0 end to end\n  fmt, check-lints.sh      clean"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T00:45:46.255938746Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-recursive-invocation-spans-a-continued-line
                title "Read a lifted recursive invocation whose command line is continued with a backslash"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T15:20:05.115441477Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T15:43:07.452116672Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed and delivered from a priority operator report: OpenZFS 2.4.4 external modules fail under Ronin, with what looked like a dependency-ordering race — `CC [M] zfs/spa_history.o` at [178/307], `LD [M] zfs.o` at [306/307] failing `ld.lld: error: cannot open ./zfs/spa_history.o`, then `ronin: 'spl.ko', needed by '__modfinal', missing`. The brief's hypothesis was two graph nodes for one file across a composed-child boundary. It is not that, and the log says so plainly once read past the first error.\n\nWHAT THE LOG ACTUALLY SAYS\n\nLine 809 of ~/.cache/necessary/runs/1787409540954-3624113/logs/openzfs-kmod--x86-64-x86-64-.log:\n\n  /build/work/include/zfs_gitrev.h:2:9: error: 'ZFS_META_GITREV' macro redefined [-Werror,-Wmacro-redefined]\n  1 error generated.\n\nThe compile of zfs/spa_history.o FAILED, on a genuine defect in the packaged source — zfs_gitrev.h carries two `#define ZFS_META_GITREV` lines, a git hash and then a dist string. That is a NOS packaging problem and not Ronin's. Ronin recorded the edge as succeeding and ran on; the link then could not open the object nobody made.\n\nSo the ordering was never wrong. The link ran at 306 of 307, after every compile, which is a graph that ordered correctly. Two things went wrong on top of a bad source tree, and only the second is this node.\n\nWHY A FAILED RECIPE WAS RECORDED AS SUCCEEDING\n\n`Ninja::gen_shell_script` assembles a multi-line recipe into `( L1 ) && ( L2 )`. POSIX ignores `set -e` when the failing command is part of an AND-OR list other than the last, and the suppression reaches into a subshell standing in one — measured identically on dash, bash and nsh. Kbuild's `cmd` macro puts `set -e;` at the head of every recipe line, and `rule_cc_o_c` is six lines, so every kernel compile loses it. The log shows the suppression directly at line 1076: `objtool` ran after `ld.lld` had already failed inside the same `set -e`.\n\nThat half is already fixed on main for what Ronin executes: ddc1139 made the sink run each recipe line as its own process, reading `TranslatedRecipe::lines` rather than the joined script, and a broken source in the toy module below now fails the build correctly. It is NOT fixed in what kati emits — `rkati --ninja` still writes the joined form, and running that manifest creates the output and exits 0 where GNU Make fails. Filed with the reproduction, the measurement and the shape of the fix as make-manifest-recipe-line-keeps-its-errexit. The NOS run predates ddc1139 in its cached ronin, which is why it saw the executed form.\n\nWHAT THIS NODE FIXED\n\nBuilding a toy external module against the host's real kernel Kbuild — obj-m with seven objects across two subdirectory levels, `make -C $KDIR M=$PWD modules` — found a second defect, and this one is live on main and blocks every external module.\n\n  ronin: '\n  -f', needed by '__sub-make', missing and no known rule to make it\n\nThe kernel's top-level Makefile re-invokes itself once to set `--no-print-directory`:\n\n  __sub-make:\n          $(Q)$(MAKE) $(no-print-directory) -C $(abs_objtree) \\\n          -f $(abs_srctree)/Makefile $(MAKECMDGOALS)\n\n`shell_words_with` in src/make/cli/subninja.rs resolved a backslash escape by pushing the escaped byte into the current word. A backslash before a newline is the one escape that stands for nothing: the shell removes the pair and joins what is on either side, which is how a long argument list gets written at all. Read as an escaped newline it became the first byte of the next word, and the child was asked for a goal spelled with a newline in front of `-f`. Two lines, in both the unquoted and the double-quoted arm, where the shell removes it in both.\n\nReduced to five lines and checked in as tests/make-project-reductions/recursive-invocation-continued-over-two-lines, gating both arms. Verified it gates: with the fix stashed the test fails on `No rule to make target 'chi\\nld.mk'`.\n\nACCEPTANCE\n\nThe toy external module now builds end to end through the real Kbuild — compile, modpost and modfinal — in ONE invocation, producing a tmod.ko with BTF. All six subdirectory objects are in the composite (`nm tmod.o` shows ta..tf), so the link waited for every compile. 32 clean builds from scratch with four spinners saturating the box: 20 at -j8 and 12 at -j16, every one correct, no failure and no reordering. GNU Make 4.4.1 is the control and builds the same tree the same way.\n\nThat answers the operator's question about the three-phase split: for this shape it is now unnecessary. Ronin composes the kernel's own recursion — `__sub-make`, `Makefile.build`, `Makefile.modpost`, `Makefile.modfinal` — into one graph and runs it correctly at -j8. What remains before zfs itself is proven is the source defect above, which is NOS's to fix, and the manifest half of the `set -e` finding for anyone consuming an emitted manifest rather than Ronin's own execution. zfs was not built here: the brief said the toy through real Kbuild is sufficient acceptance, and pkg was not run.\n\nGATES\n\nworkspace 1010/0/1 (1009 baseline plus this node's test); conformance 336/51 normalised, 286 raw; equivalence 297/280/75/0 with the 8 manifest respellings intact; upstream inventory 1285 at 100/0/24/1161, compiler still zero and no row moved; make_port 4/4 with all ten divergence markers present; ninja conformance 425/425 plus the differentials; --no-default-features 777/0; vim and zsh green at 15s and 27s; port ledger, lints, fmt and check-release.sh green end to end."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T15:45:41.623229893Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-manifest-recipe-line-keeps-its-errexit
                title "Keep a manifest recipe line's own set -e, which the && chain takes away"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T15:28:57.342838156Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while diagnosing the OpenZFS external-module report (see make-recursive-invocation-spans-a-continued-line for the whole trail). Recorded with the reproduction, the measurement and the shape of the fix, so whoever takes it does not have to find it again.\n\nWHAT IS WRONG\n\n`Ninja::gen_shell_script` in kati/src-rs/ninja.rs assembles a multi-line recipe into one script by putting each line in a subshell and joining them with ` && `:\n\n    ( L1 ) && ( L2 )\n\nThe comment there says the subshells and the `&&` chain \"are what stands in for the several shells GNU Make would have started\". They are not, in one respect, and it is the respect that matters most: POSIX says `set -e` is ignored when the failing command is part of an AND-OR list other than the last, and that suppression reaches INTO a subshell standing in such a list. Measured identically on dash, bash and Ronin's own nsh:\n\n    sh -c '( set -e; false; echo LEAK ) && ( : )'   ->  LEAK, status 0\n    sh -c '( set -e; false; echo LEAK )'            ->  status 1\n\nSo a recipe line that arms `set -e` for itself loses it the moment it is not the last line of the recipe. The line runs to the end and the script reports the status of whatever it finished on.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nEvery Kbuild compile is that shape. scripts/Kbuild.include defines\n\n    cmd = @$(if $(cmd_$(1)),set -e; $(log_print) $(delete-on-interrupt) $(cmd_$(1)),:)\n\nand `rule_cc_o_c` is six `$(call cmd,...)` lines, most of which expand to nothing and become `:`. The compile is line one of six, so its `set -e` is always suppressed, and the compiler's exit status is replaced by the `rm -f` that ends the line.\n\nThat is visibly what happened in the failing OpenZFS run\n(~/.cache/necessary/runs/1787409540954-3624113): zfs/spa_history.o fails to compile with a genuine `-Werror` diagnostic at [178/307], the edge is recorded as succeeding, the build runs on to [307/307], `ld.lld` cannot open the object it was never given, `objtool` runs anyway after `ld.lld` failed inside the same `set -e` — which is the suppression showing itself in the log — and modpost then reports both modules missing.\n\nREPRODUCTION, side by side\n\n    all: out\n    out:\n    \t@set -e; echo line-one; false; echo LEAK; touch $@\n    \t@echo second-line-ran\n\nGNU 4.4.1: fails, `out` not created.\nRonin's Make mode: FAILED [code=1], `out` not created — correct, because ddc1139 made the sink run each recipe line as its own process and it reads TranslatedRecipe::lines rather than this script.\n`rkati --ninja` then running the manifest: LEAK prints, second-line-ran prints, `out` is created, exit 0.\n\nSo the executed path is already right and the EMITTED path is not, which is exactly the divergence make.manifest-equivalence exists to forbid. The current gate compares graph structure and not command semantics, which is why it passes.\n\nWHAT A FIX LOOKS LIKE\n\nThere is no way in POSIX sh to keep a compound command's inner `set -e` while it stands in an AND-OR list. The two escapes are a separate process per line, or not being in such a list. Measured, this works and is in the vocabulary the function already speaks — it has a `relax` path that emits `set +e ; ` inside a line's subshell when the line's flags differ from the script's:\n\n    set -e\n    ( set +e ; L1 )\n    ( set +e ; L2 )\n\nNewline separator instead of `&&`; a leading `set -e` so the script still stops at the first failing line; `set +e` inside each subshell so a line that did NOT ask for `-e` still gets GNU's permissive default rather than inheriting the script's. Verified: a line that arms `set -e` aborts and stops the script; a line that does not keeps running past a failure exactly as GNU's own shell would.\n\nTwo cases need care. `wholly_ignored` recipes already use ` ; ` and no chain, so they want no `set -e` at all and are unchanged. A muted line (`ignore_error` inside a mixed recipe) currently emits `{ ( … ) ; true ; }`, which under an outer `-e` exits at the subshell before reaching the `true`; `set +e ; ( … ) ; set -e` was measured to be both faithful and quiet, and has the bonus that the line's own `-e` works there too.\n\nBlast radius is the reason this is its own node rather than folded in: it respells the command of every multi-line recipe, so the conformance goldens, the equivalence manifest respellings and the upstream inventory all have to be re-read row by row. Single-line recipes are untouched — `needs_subshell` is already false for them — and those are most of the corpus.\n\nA NARROWER DEFECT IN THE SAME FUNCTION, not fixed and not reproduced in the field: inside double quotes the same assembler drops a backslash before any character, where POSIX keeps both bytes unless the next one is one of `$` `` ` `` `\"` `\\` or newline. `\"a\\b\"` is `a\\b` to a shell and `ab` here. Worth folding into the same pass."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T15:53:02.261625481Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T16:52:26.706768414Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Landed. The filing's diagnosis was right in every particular and its predicted blast radius was wrong in two of the three places it named; both are audited below.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, CONFIRMED\n\n`( set -e ; false ; echo LEAK ) && ( : )` prints LEAK and exits 0 on dash, on bash and on nsh. POSIX ignores `-e` while executing any command of an AND-OR list other than the last, and the shell's \"I am in an AND-OR list\" state is not reset by entering a subshell. It IS reset by entering a new process: `sh -c \"sh -c 'set -e; false; echo LEAK' && sh -c ':'\"` does not print, which is why GNU Make -- one shell per line -- never had this and why Ronin's own execution has not had it since ddc1139.\n\nGNU 4.4.1 was probed for the whole matrix before anything was written. What it says, in effects:\n\n  plain line, no inner -e, fails mid-line      line runs to its end; its LAST command decides\n  line arms -e for itself, fails mid-line      line stops there; recipe stops\n  `-` prefix, line arms -e                     line stops there; recipe CARRIES ON\n  .IGNORE: / -i, line arms -e                  same: the line keeps -e, the status is forgiven\n  .POSIX:, unprefixed line                     shell is `-ec`: line stops at its first failure\n  .POSIX:, `-` prefixed line                   shell is `-c`: line runs to its end\n  .POSIX: + .IGNORE:                           shell is STILL `-ec` -- .IGNORE forgives a status\n                                               without taking -e off the line, because job.c reads\n                                               only `lines_flags` for that\n  -k                                           changes nothing inside a recipe\n  .ONESHELL:                                   one shell for the whole recipe, exempt by design\n\nTHE SPELLING, AND WHY THIS ONE\n\n    set -e ; ( set +e ; L1 ) ; ( set +e ; L2 )\n\nSeparated, not conjoined, and the script arms `-e` for itself. Nothing is then in an AND-OR list, so a line's own `set -e` works where the line stands. Each line states its own setting inside its own subshell, where it dies with the line: `set +e` for a line GNU would have given `-c`, nothing for a line `.POSIX:` gave `-ec` (the script is already strict), `set -e` for a line that wants it where the script is not strict. A forgiven line is spelled\n\n    set +e ; ( L ) ; set -e\n\nrather than the old `{ ( L ) ; true ; }`, because under a strict script the group cannot survive its own failure -- and every construct that swallows a status instead (`||`, an `if` condition, a `!` pipeline) is one POSIX suppresses the line's own `-e` inside, which is the defect itself. The trailing `set -e` is the discard as well as the restoration: it is the fragment's last command and it succeeds.\n\nThree spellings were measured and rejected. `X || exit $?` and `if X ; then : ; fi` both put X in a suppressing context. `( L ) ; s=$? ; test $s -eq 0 || exit $s` avoids errexit entirely and was rejected for putting a shell variable in the script's scope where GNU has none, and for `exit` with no argument reporting the test's status rather than the line's. One shell process per line -- `sh -c '...' ; sh -c '...'` -- is the most faithful of all and was rejected for the nested quoting it would need inside an already doubly-escaped Ninja binding, and for doubling the process count of every Kbuild compile.\n\nA SECOND SHAPE IN THE SAME FUNCTION, ALSO FIXED\n\n`.POSIX:` with `.IGNORE:` gives every line `-e` and forgives every status, so `wholly_ignored` is true and `script_flags` is `-ec`. The old assembly chained the lines with `;` and let the strict shell stop at the first one, and the writer's whole-recipe mute `( SCRIPT ) ; true` never reached its `true` because the outer `-e` killed the shell at the subshell. Measured: GNU makes m1 and m3 and succeeds; the manifest made m1 and failed. The script now opens `set +e` where the flags arm errexit, each line puts its own setting back inside its subshell, and `muted_script` takes the setting off before the subshell it has to survive. This is not the `&&` mechanism, but it is the same sentence -- the emitted manifest must keep the recipe's errexit semantics -- and it is nine lines in the function being rewritten, so it was folded in rather than filed.\n\nTHE PROOF, ON STOCK NINJA\n\nKbuild's own two macros, reduced -- `cmd = @set -e; <echo>; $(cmd_and_fixdep)` and `cmd_and_fixdep = $(cmd_$1); echo fixdep > $@.cmd; rm -f $@.d` -- over a hello.c that does not compile, with objtool and a record line after it. This is the shape from the field: the compile is the FIRST of three commands on the line, so the line's own status is the `rm -f`'s.\n\n  GNU Make 4.4.1          rc=2, nothing made.\n  manifest BEFORE, run by stock ninja 1.14.0.git\n                          rc=0, and hello.o.cmd, hello.o.objtool and hello.o.record ALL made.\n                          That is the OpenZFS log exactly: objtool running after the compiler\n                          failed inside the same `set -e`, and the edge recorded as succeeding.\n  manifest AFTER, same ninja\n                          rc=1, nothing made.\n\nTwenty shapes were run this way, GNU beside `rkati --ninja` piped into stock ninja, comparing which marker files exist and whether the build failed. Sixteen agreed before, five moved from disagreeing to agreeing, and nothing moved the other way. The four still disagreeing are all pre-existing and none is this mechanism; three nodes were filed for them.\n\nTEETH\n\nSix tests were added in kati that run the assembled script the way a Ninja edge runs it -- one shell, started with the script's own flags, handed the script as one argument, in a directory of its own -- and assert the files it left and whether it failed, against GNU 4.4.1's recorded answer quoted on each. With the emitter parked back on the `&&` chain (separator, header, per-line restore and the mute all reverted, tests left in place) four of the six fail:\n\n  a_kbuild_line_keeps_the_errexit_it_armed_for_itself                FAILED\n  a_forgiven_line_keeps_its_own_errexit                              FAILED\n  posix_stops_a_line_at_its_first_failing_command                    FAILED\n  ignore_under_posix_keeps_the_line_strict_and_the_recipe_running    FAILED\n  a_line_that_armed_nothing_runs_to_its_end                          ok  (guard)\n  a_wholly_forgiven_recipe_runs_every_line                           ok  (guard)\n\nTHE GOLDEN AUDIT, PER FAMILY\n\nkati's own script-spelling tests, 7 re-recorded, all in src-rs/ninja.rs. Three narration cases (an_echo_with_nothing_to_announce_stays_in_the_command, a_recipe_with_a_line_left_loud_keeps_its_command, a_run_that_shows_no_description_keeps_the_echo): the spelling moved and what they assert -- which text stayed in the command and which moved to the description -- did not. Representative:\n  before  \"(echo '  CC      misc.o' ) && (cc misc.c )\"\n  after   \"set -e ; (set +e ; echo '  CC      misc.o' ) ; (set +e ; cc misc.c )\"\nTwo muting cases (an_ignored_line_lets_the_rest_of_the_recipe_run_even_past_exit, a_line_whose_failure_counts_still_stops_a_recipe_that_ignores_another): `{ (L) ; true ; }` became `set +e ; (L) ; set -e`; both still assert that one line's failure is forgiven and the other's is not. One `.POSIX:` case (posix_relaxes_only_the_prefixed_line) re-recorded, and its inner `set +e` is gone because the muting around the line already took the setting off.\n\nOne test was RENAMED rather than re-recorded, because its claim became false by design. `a_plain_recipe_never_relaxes_a_line` asserted that a recipe read without `.POSIX:` never writes the word `set`. It now writes one per line, and that is the fix: the script is strict so that it stops, and each line takes the strictness off again because a line GNU gave `-c` did not ask for it. It is now `a_plain_recipe_gives_each_line_the_shell_it_asked_for` and asserts the exact script instead of an absence.\n\nTwo wholly-ignored cases did NOT move (a_wholly_ignored_recipe_keeps_the_status_it_ends_on, posix_drops_errexit_for_a_wholly_prefixed_recipe): that path was already right, because a `;` chain puts no line in an AND-OR list.\n\nmake_port: 4/4, all TEN divergence markers present, ZERO re-records. The filing predicted this family would move and it does not: make_port asserts effects through Ronin's Make mode, which reads `TranslatedRecipe::lines` and runs each line as its own process, and never sees the assembled script at all.\n\nequivalence: \"manifest respellings: 8\", UNCHANGED, also predicted to move. `respelled` in src/make/equivalence.rs counts rules whose script holds a NEWLINE -- the `.ONESHELL` recipes the manifest cannot write down -- and this change puts `;` where `&&` stood and adds no newline anywhere.\n\nconformance and the upstream inventory: not one row moved, and for the same reason as make_port -- rkati executes per line too. The inventory TSV is byte-identical.\n\nGATES\n\n  workspace                1016/0/1  (1010 baseline plus the six new kati tests)\n  --no-default-features    783/0     (777 baseline plus the same six)\n  conformance              336/51 normalised, 286 raw, family table identical\n  equivalence              297/280/75/0, manifest respellings 8\n  upstream inventory       1285 at 100/0/24/1161, compiler still zero, TSV unmodified\n  make_port                4/4, ten divergence markers\n  ninja conformance        425/425, 10 CLI, 86 build outcome, 3 invocation, persistence, log content\n  real projects            vim 9.2 in 35s, zsh 5.9.2 and 36 modules in 70s, both at -j8\n  port ledger              170 symbols\n  fmt, check-lints.sh      clean\n\nWHAT IS OPEN\n\nscripts/check-release.sh stops at scripts/check-performance.sh, which reported wide-noop-build at 1.00x against a recorded 0.82x on one run and manifest-command-evaluation at 0.61x against 0.49x on the next. The host was carrying another workload throughout -- load average 34 to 43 on 32 cores, dozens of clang++ processes belonging to nothing in this tree -- and both workloads are pure Ninja mode: `ronin -t commands all` over a generated manifest, and a no-op build over another. Neither reads a Makefile, so neither can reach anything this node touched. The two runs naming different workloads and different ratios is itself the signature of measurement noise rather than a regression. Re-measure on a quiet host; nothing else in the battery is outstanding.\n\nTHREE NODES FILED from what the twenty-shape matrix turned up, none of them this mechanism:\n\nmake-manifest-oneshell-recipe-is-not-a-manifest -- a `.ONESHELL` recipe emits a syntactically invalid manifest, not merely a wrong command: the newline separator ends the binding, and stock ninja answers `build.ninja:11: expected 'command =' line`. This is what the equivalence gate's eight respellings are, said honestly.\n\nmake-kati-option-table-reads-i-as-n -- `flags.rs:387` reads `-i` as `is_dry_run`, so `rkati -i` runs nothing and reports success where GNU ignores errors; `-n` and `-k` panic with `Unknown flag`. Ronin's Make mode parses all three correctly, so only the standalone binary is affected -- but that binary is the conformance gate's front end.\n\nmake-manifest-response-file-keeps-the-shell-flags -- the response-file destination writes `exec /bin/sh $RESPONSE_FILE` with no flags, so a `.POSIX:` recipe over 100 kB loses `-e` entirely.\n\nThe narrower defect the filing noted at the end -- a backslash dropped before any character inside double quotes, where POSIX keeps both bytes unless the next is one of `$` backtick `\"` `\\` or newline -- was NOT folded in. It is in `translate_command` rather than in the assembly, it changes what every quoted recipe line means rather than how the lines are joined, and it wants its own oracle matrix. It remains unfiled and unfixed; it belongs to whoever takes the double-quote reading next."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T16:54:34.942966915Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Correcting the last paragraph of the decision above, which said the filing's narrower defect \"remains unfiled and unfixed\". It is not filed because it does not reproduce, and leaving that unsaid would have left a referral to outlive its subject.\n\nThe filing recorded that inside double quotes the assembler drops a backslash before any character, where POSIX keeps both bytes unless the next one is a dollar, a backtick, a double quote, a backslash or a newline. Measured end to end, five backslash shapes in one recipe line printed through printf, covering a backslash before a plain letter inside double quotes, before a dollar inside double quotes, inside single quotes, unquoted, and before a double quote inside double quotes:\n\n  GNU Make 4.4.1                       a\\b|a\\|c\\d|ef|g\"h|\n  Ronin's Make mode                    a\\b|a\\|c\\d|ef|g\"h|\n  rkati --ninja, run by stock ninja    a\\b|a\\|c\\d|ef|g\"h|\n\nByte-identical on all three. Reading the code says the same. translate_command copies every byte through its catch-all arm and only ever drops a backslash that is the last byte of the line. simple_command::direct_argv never sees a double-quoted word at all, because the double quote is in SHELL_CHARACTERS, so such a line returns None and goes to the shell whole; outside quotes it drops the backslash and keeps the next byte, which is what POSIX says an unquoted backslash does.\n\nSo either the claim was about a path neither probe reached, or it was written from reading rather than from running. Anyone who finds the shape it names should file it with the reproduction. There is no node for it because there is nothing here to reproduce."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T16:55:39.766026385Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T17:08:43.235665722Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The one open item, measured rather than asserted: scripts/check-performance.sh could not be run on a quiet host, and its failures are noise.\n\nThe host carried another workload for the whole dispatch — a sandboxed build under `silo-`, dozens of clang++ and cc1 processes belonging to nothing in this tree, holding the load average between 28 and 43 on 32 cores for over an hour, never once dropping below 28.\n\nThree runs of the gate, three DIFFERENT workloads flagged:\n\n  wide-noop-build                 1.00x against a recorded 0.82x\n  manifest-command-evaluation     0.61x against a recorded 0.49x\n  clean-tree-noop                 0.87x against a recorded 0.65x\n\nA regression does not move between workloads run to run. Two back-to-back runs of the baseline example on the same unchanged release binary say the same thing directly:\n\n  manifest-command-evaluation   run 1: ronin 17.482 ms, ninja 36.123 ms -> 0.484x\n                                run 2: ronin 13.496 ms, ninja 26.224 ms -> 0.515x\n  wide-noop-build               run 1: ronin 21.735 ms, ninja 23.095 ms -> 0.941x\n                                run 2: ronin 19.616 ms, ninja 22.598 ms -> 0.868x\n\nBoth of those runs would have PASSED validation; the run before them, on the same binary, reported 0.61x for the first of them. The spread across consecutive runs is wider than the gate's own 20% tolerance, and the absolute medians are around three times the recorded ones (6.015 ms recorded against 17.482 measured), which is the load and nothing else.\n\nAttribution, independently of the noise: both flagged workloads are pure Ninja mode. `manifest-command-evaluation` runs `ronin -t commands all` over a generated manifest and `wide-noop-build` a no-op build over another; `clean-tree-noop` likewise. None reads a Makefile, so none reaches kati at all, and this node changed one function in kati's manifest writer.\n\nRe-run `scripts/check-release.sh` on a quiet host. Everything else in the battery is green and recorded in the note above."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-manifest-oneshell-recipe-is-not-a-manifest
                title "Write a .ONESHELL recipe into a manifest a ninja can read"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-manifest-recipe-line-keeps-its-errexit)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T16:48:33.781428067Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while measuring the emitted manifest against GNU 4.4.1 for make-manifest-recipe-line-keeps-its-errexit. A `.ONESHELL` recipe does not produce an invalid command; it produces an invalid MANIFEST, and stock ninja refuses to read the file at all.\n\n`gen_shell_script` separates a `.ONESHELL` recipe's lines with a newline, because GNU Make really does hand the whole recipe to one shell and its own newlines are the separators. A Ninja binding ends at a newline and no escape puts one back, so the writer emits the bytes as they are and the stanza breaks in two.\n\n    .ONESHELL:\n    all: out\n    out:\n    \t@touch m1; false\n    \t@touch m2\n\n`rkati --ninja` writes:\n\n    rule rule0\n     description = touch m1; false\n    touch m2\n     command = exec /bin/sh -c \"touch m1; false\n    touch m2\"\n     generator = 1\n\nand `ninja -f build.ninja` answers `ninja: error: build.ninja:11: expected 'command =' line`. The description binding breaks too, one line earlier. Both stock ninja 1.14.0.git and Ronin's own reader refuse it.\n\nThe equivalence gate does not see this: `src/make/equivalence.rs` counts a script holding a newline as a `respelled` rule — eight of them across the corpus — and judges those at the sink boundary rather than through the manifest, precisely because the manifest cannot write them down. That count is honest about what it skips, and this is what it skips.\n\nWhat a fix has to decide is what one Ninja edge can be for a recipe GNU gives to one shell. The line separator cannot be `;`, because `.ONESHELL` is the case where a `cd` on one line is meant to reach the next and a `;` chain gives that but a failing line does NOT stop the rest, which `;` also gives — so the semantics may in fact be reachable. Probe GNU first: `.ONESHELL` with `.POSIX:`, with a `-` on the first line, with a `+` on the first line, and with a line holding an unterminated quote, before assuming `; ` is equivalent. The response-file destination is the other candidate: a script file holds newlines natively, and `SinkCommand::ResponseFile` already exists — see make-manifest-response-file-keeps-the-shell-flags, which has to be settled first because that path drops the shell flags today.\n\nMeasured against GNU 4.4.1: `.ONESHELL` with a plain failing first line leaves m1 and m2 both made and make succeeds (the recipe is one script with no `-e`); with `set -e` at the head of the first line, m1 alone and `*** Error 1`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T18:26:40.224134206Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T19:10:49.889683865Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Landed. The filing's diagnosis reproduced byte for byte, and one thing it asserted alongside is false — said below, because it is the reason the respellings count did not move.\n\nREPRODUCED\n\n    .ONESHELL:\n    all: out\n    out:\n    \t@touch m1; false\n    \t@touch m2\n\n`rkati --ninja` wrote the recipe's own newlines into the binding, the stanza broke in two, and stock ninja 1.14.0.git answered `build.ninja:11: expected 'command =' line`. Ronin's own reader refuses it in the same words.\n\nWHAT A BINDING CAN HOLD, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED\n\nA hand-written manifest was put to stock ninja with a `$` before a newline in a `command` and again in an `rspfile_content`, which is the only escape ninja has near a newline. Both yielded `ab` and `line1line2`: it is a line continuation that produces nothing at all. So neither destination can carry a newline, and the response file — which the filing named as the other candidate — is not one, because a file written from a binding is a binding.\n\nTHE SPELLING\n\nThe manifest cannot write the script down; it can write down how to rebuild it. Each line becomes a single-quoted operand of `printf '%s\\n'`, which puts the newlines back where they were and interprets nothing else: the format holds the only escape and the operands are bytes. `'` is the one byte single quotes cannot hold and is spelled `'\\''`.\n\n    inline    command = exec /bin/sh -c \"$(printf '%s\\n' 'touch m1; false' 'touch m2')\"\n    response  rspfile_content = eval \"$(printf '%s\\n' 'case x in' 'x) : … ; touch m1' 'esac')\"\n              command = exec /bin/sh $out.rsp\n\nInline, the substitution is done by the shell ninja starts and the result becomes the recipe shell's `-c` argument — one argument, which is exactly where GNU Make puts it — and `exec` still leaves one process where Make has one. Through a file, `eval` runs the reconstruction in the shell already started for it rather than in one of its own, so the process count is unchanged there too. Both destinations were run end to end; the response-file one needed a recipe over 100 kB and a `case` spanning lines, and GNU, the manifest under stock ninja, and Ronin's Make mode all made m1 and m2 and succeeded.\n\nWHY NOT `; `, MEASURED\n\nThe filing said the separator \"cannot be `;`\" and then said the semantics \"may in fact be reachable\" with one. They are not. Run as a plain shell command line:\n\n    case x in ; x) touch m1 ;; ; esac ; touch m2      rc=2, nothing made\n    if true ; then ;   touch m1 ; fi ; touch m2       rc=2, nothing made\n    # a comment ; touch m1                            rc=0, NOTHING made\n\nagainst the same three reconstructed with newlines, which make their markers and succeed. The comment case is the one that fails silently, and it is also the one kati is accidentally safe from: `translate_command` strips a recipe line's own leading comment before any of this, so the corpus cannot reach it. The constructs are the argument that stands.\n\nGNU PROBED FIRST, AS THE FILING ASKED\n\nThirteen shapes, GNU Make 4.4.1 beside `rkati --ninja` piped into stock ninja and beside Ronin's Make mode, comparing the marker files made, their contents where they have any, and the exit status:\n\n  plain                 both markers, 0            — one shell, no `-e`, a failing line stops nothing\n  posix                 m1 alone, 2                — `.ONESHELL` takes the flags from the FIRST line\n  dash-first-line       both markers, 0\n  plus-first-line       both markers, 0\n  comment-line          m1, 0\n  case-construct        both markers, 0\n  cd-carries            both markers, and `pwd` says `sub` — the property `.ONESHELL` exists for\n  set-e-in-script       m1 alone, 2                — the script arms it for itself\n  dollar-in-script      `hello` and `$notvar`      — expansion and quoting both survive\n  quotes-and-backslash  `a\\\\b` and `it's`\n  multiline-if          both markers, 0\n  wholly-ignored        both markers, 0\n  unterminated-quote    m1 holding `one\\ntwo`, 2   — see below\n\nTen agree in all three columns. `posix` and `set-e-in-script` agree on effects and differ only on the status stock ninja gives a failed build, 1 against GNU's 2, which is the pre-existing `exit-status` family and shows on every failing shape; Ronin agrees with GNU on both.\n\n`unterminated-quote` is the thirteenth and is GNU's own. A quote spanning a `.ONESHELL` line boundary WITH a shell metacharacter on the line makes GNU write the file correctly and then report `/bin/sh: 1: Syntax error: Unterminated quoted string` and exit 2. It needs all three conditions: the same recipe with `SHELL = /bin/dash` — a named shell, so GNU's fast path is off — succeeds with rc=0 and the same file, and so does the same recipe with no redirect in it. So it is GNU's internal command scanner, not a semantics anyone should reproduce, and the manifest and Ronin both give the answer GNU gives when its own fast path is out of the way.\n\nWHAT THE FILING GOT WRONG\n\n\"This is what the equivalence gate's eight respellings are, said honestly.\" It is not. The gate was instrumented to print every script it counts, and all eight are backslash CONTINUATIONS — `echo foo\\<newline>bar`, `for d in foo bar; do \\<newline> echo $d ; done`, and six more of that shape. `.ONESHELL` appears nowhere in kati's testcase corpus: grepping it finds no file, and every one of the 387 manifests the corpus produces was put to stock ninja, which parsed all of them (the five it complained about were missing inputs, not syntax).\n\nSo the count did not move and could not have: `respelled` is `script.contains(&b'\\n')` measured at the sink boundary, before the writer respells anything, and it marks a rule whose two destinations cannot hold the same bytes — which is still true of all eight and now also true of a `.ONESHELL` rule, in the same way and for the same reason. **manifest respellings: 8, unchanged.** The machinery's purpose is intact and is now documented by a second shape using it.\n\nTHE DESCRIPTION, AND ONE ROW THAT MOVED AND WAS PUT BACK\n\nA description is one line too, so the writer emits none for a script it had to respell — the rule the response-file destination already had. Making Ronin's own sink do the same moved three upstream rows: `targets/ONESHELL.diff`, `.diff.1` and `.diff.2` went from `narration` to `unclassified`, because the classifier recognises a recipe echo from Ronin's progress-line payload and there was suddenly nothing in it. That is a narration regression, so it was reverted: the sink that keeps the script in memory has somewhere to put every byte and still narrates with `single_line`, and the comparison now reads a respelled rule's description as destination-specific, beside the `command` it already read that way. Inventory back to 1285 at 100/0/24/1161 with the TSV byte-identical.\n\nTEETH\n\n  ronin  make::equivalence::a_one_shell_recipe_is_a_manifest_a_reader_takes\n         parked: \"the fixture did not produce two graphs: ronin refused the manifest:\n         error: build.ninja:11: expected 'command =' line\" — the filed error, exactly.\n  kati   a_printed_script_comes_back_byte_for_byte     six scripts through a real shell\n  kati   a_printed_script_is_one_line\n  kati   a_one_shell_recipe_runs_to_its_end\n  kati   a_posix_one_shell_recipe_stops_at_a_failure\n  kati   a_directory_and_a_construct_cross_the_lines\n\n`one_shell_edge` runs the manifest's own command line the way ninja runs an edge — ninja starts a shell for the line, the line substitutes the printer's output into the recipe's shell — in a directory of its own, and answers in the files left behind and whether the edge failed, against GNU 4.4.1 recorded on each case. The equivalence one is where the parked failure lands because that is where the manifest is written and read back: the kati tests are about the reconstruction being right, and the writer using it is what the reader proves.\n\n`a_printed_script_comes_back_byte_for_byte` puts the printer's whole claim to `/bin/sh` and compares the output with the script: six scripts covering `'`, `\"`, `$`, `\\`, a comment line, a single line, and an empty line in the middle.\n\nA NEW DEFECT, FILED\n\nmake-a-one-shell-line-keeps-the-semicolons-it-ends-on. `translate_command` trims every trailing `;` off every recipe line, which is a redundant separator where lines are joined with ` ; ` and is a `case` item's terminator under `.ONESHELL`. A two-arm `case` therefore loses both `;;` and the shell refuses the script: GNU makes m1 and m3 and exits 0, the manifest and Ronin's Make mode make nothing and exit 2. Older than this node and not the manifest — Ronin's Make mode is handed `TranslatedRecipe` and never sees a manifest — but this node is what made it visible, because before it the manifest could not be run at all. A one-arm `case` survives it, which is why the shape looked fine until a second arm was written.\n\nGATES\n\n  workspace                1033/0/1  (1027 plus six)\n  --no-default-features    796/0     (791 plus the five kati ones)\n  conformance              336/51 normalised, 286 raw, class and family tables identical\n  equivalence              297/280/75/0, manifest respellings 8, unchanged and explained above\n  upstream inventory       1285 at 100/0/24/1161, compiler zero, TSV byte-identical\n  make_port                4/4 over 1080 cases, ten divergence markers\n  ninja conformance        425/425, 10 CLI, 86 build outcome, 3 invocation, persistence, log content\n  real projects            vim 9.2 in 16s, zsh 5.9.2 and 36 modules in 30s, both at -j8\n  fmt, check-lints.sh      clean"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T19:17:03.927957914Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-kati-option-table-reads-i-as-n
                title "Read -i, -n and -k in rkati's own option table as GNU spells them"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T16:48:41.106203386Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while measuring the emitted manifest against GNU 4.4.1 for make-manifest-recipe-line-keeps-its-errexit, which ran the corpus through `rkati` with option flags on the command line.\n\n`kati/src-rs/flags.rs:387` reads\n\n    b\"-i\" => flags.is_dry_run = true,\n\nGNU Make's `-i` is `--ignore-errors` and `-n` is `--dry-run`. So `rkati -i` runs nothing and reports success, and `rkati -n` panics with `Unknown flag: -n`, as does `rkati -k`. The field `flags.ignore_errors` that `-i` should set already exists and is already read — `command.rs:1566` folds it into every line's `ignore_error` — so the wiring is there and only the letter is wrong.\n\nMeasured: `all: out` / `out:` with `@touch m1; false` then `@touch m2`.\n  GNU `make -i`: both markers, exit 0.\n  Ronin's Make mode `make -i`: both markers, exit 0 — the front end in `src/make/cli.rs` parses its own options and is correct.\n  `rkati -i --ninja`: emits a manifest with no muting at all, which stock ninja then fails at the first line.\n  `rkati -n --ninja`, `rkati -k --ninja`: panic at flags.rs:521.\n\nOnly kati's standalone binary is affected, but that binary is not incidental: it is the front end `scripts/check-make-conformance.sh` classifies the corpus with, and it is the manifest emitter. Worth checking as part of the fix whether any conformance case passes `-i`, `-n` or `-k` in its `args`, because such a case has been classified against a runner that did the wrong thing.\n\nThe panic is its own finding: an unknown option should be a diagnostic and a status, not `panic!`. GNU answers `make: invalid option -- 'q'` plus usage, and exits 2."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T17:17:06.851992073Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T17:44:03.237001889Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Landed. The filing reproduced exactly, and the survey it asked for found the transposition was one of six wrong letters rather than one of three.\n\nTHE DEFECT, REPRODUCED\n\n`kati/src-rs/flags.rs` read `b\"-i\" => flags.is_dry_run = true`. Over `all: out` / `out:` with `@touch m1; false` then `@touch m2`:\n\n  GNU 4.4.1 `-i`   m1 and m2 made, exit 0\n  rkati `-i`       nothing made, exit 0\n  rkati `-n`       panicked at flags.rs:521, `Unknown flag: -n`\n  rkati `-k`       same panic\n\nBoth tools answer \"it worked\" under `-i`, so the only place the difference is visible is what is on the disk afterwards — which is why this survived.\n\nTHE SURVEY, WHICH WAS THE POINT\n\nEvery letter in GNU Make 4.4.1's short-option row was run through the standalone binary and read against `MAKE_OPTION_SURFACE` in src/make/cli.rs, which is Ronin's Make mode's own classification of the same row. Before:\n\n  accepted and correct   -d  -s   (and -f, -C, -j, plus kati's own -c)\n  accepted and wrong     -i        read as the dry run\n  panicked               -b -m -B -e -h -k -L -n -p -q -r -R -S -t -v -w\n  panicked, with an arg  -I -o -W -l -O -E\n\nSo the row had ONE transposition and NINETEEN letters with no arm at all. Six of those nineteen name a field kati already writes and already reads, and they are what this node added: `-n` (is_dry_run), `-k` and `-S` (keep_going, set and taken back off), `-e` (environment_overrides), `-r` (no_builtin_rules), `-R` (no_builtin_variables). Nothing else in the file changed — every field was already written by an embedding frontend through `Flags` and already read by the evaluator, so this was the spelling on the command-line side and only that.\n\nThe remaining fifteen were deliberately NOT folded in, and make-kati-option-row-past-the-letters-it-can-honour carries the reasoning and the table. The short version: `-b -m -L -w -O -l -p` could be accepted and dropped, but `-B -t -q -W -v -h -E` name front-end behaviour kati has no field for, and a silent `rkati -q` that builds the tree and reports success is a worse answer than a crash. `-I` and `-o` do have fields but take an argument whose canonicalisation the frontend owns. The panic itself, and the missing option clustering (`-rR`), are filed there too.\n\nPROOF, AGAINST THE ORACLE\n\nSame Makefile, `reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make` beside the release `rkati`, comparing the markers made and the exit status:\n\n  -i    GNU m1 m2 / 0     rkati m1 m2 / 0\n  -n    GNU none  / 0     rkati none  / 0\n  -k    GNU m1    / 2     rkati m1    / 2\n  -e    GNU m1    / 2     rkati m1    / 2\n  -r    GNU m1    / 2     rkati m1    / 2\n  -R    GNU m1    / 2     rkati m1    / 2\n  -S    GNU m1    / 2     rkati m1    / 2\n  none  GNU m1    / 2     rkati m1    / 2\n\nand the three letters whose meaning is not visible in that shape, each measured on its own: `-e` with `V=makefile` in the file and `V=env` in the environment writes `env` on both; `-R` over `[$(CC)][$(AR)]` writes `[][]` on both; `-r` leaves `hello.o` unmade on both where without it both make it.\n\nTEETH, BY PARKING\n\nSix tests, five in kati's own suite and one in Ronin's equivalence module. With the option-table arms reverted to the single wrong `-i` line and the tests left in place, all six fail:\n\n  flags::a_dash_i_ignores_errors_rather_than_running_nothing   FAILED (asserts !is_dry_run too)\n  flags::a_dash_n_is_the_dry_run                               FAILED (panicked: Unknown flag: -n)\n  flags::keep_going_is_read_and_can_be_taken_back_off          FAILED (panicked: Unknown flag: -k)\n  flags::the_letters_that_change_the_evaluation_are_read       FAILED\n  flags::the_switches_reach_a_sub_make                         FAILED\n  make::equivalence::the_ignore_errors_switch_reaches_both_sinks  FAILED\n\nThe last one is the effects-level one and is where it is on purpose. `src/make/equivalence.rs`'s `Case` hands its flags straight to `Session::from_args`, so a test there runs the letter through kati's own option table and out into both sinks. Under `-i` every rule must cross the sink muted and the two graphs must still agree; under no switch and under `-n` the semantic map must be empty, because an override is recorded only for a rule that ignores errors or had to be respelled. Before the fix the manifest carried no muting and the direct graph bound nothing — the two sides agreed with each other and both disagreed with Make, which is the one shape an equivalence comparison cannot catch by itself.\n\nCONFORMANCE MOVEMENT: NONE, AND MEASURED RATHER THAN HOPED\n\nThe filing asked whether any conformance case passes `-i`, `-n` or `-k`. Every `${mk}` invocation in `kati/testcase/*.sh` was read: the only GNU short options the corpus hands the front end are `-C`, `-j1`/`-j4`/`-j8` and `-s`. The `.mk` cases are given no options at all beyond `-s` for the recursive one. So the gate could not have been exercising this, and it did not move: 336/51 normalised and 286 raw, family table and class counts identical.\n\nThe 26 cases under `tests/make/` that do pass `-i`, `-n` or `-k` belong to make_port, which drives Ronin's Make mode — correct all along — and it is 4/4 with all ten divergence markers and no re-records.\n\nGATES\n\n  workspace                1022/0/1  (1016 baseline plus the six new tests)\n  --no-default-features    788/0     (783 baseline plus the five kati tests)\n  conformance              336/51 normalised, 286 raw, class and family tables identical\n  equivalence              297/280/75/0, manifest respellings 8, unchanged\n  upstream inventory       1285 at 100/0/24/1161, compiler zero, TSV byte-identical\n  make_port                4/4, ten divergence markers\n  ninja conformance        425/425, 10 CLI, 86 build outcome, 3 invocation, persistence, log content\n  real projects            vim 9.2 in 38s, zsh 5.9.2 and 36 modules in 72s, both at -j8\n  fmt, check-lints.sh      clean"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T19:16:50.278912053Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-manifest-response-file-keeps-the-shell-flags
                title "Give a response-file recipe the shell flags the inline one gets"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-manifest-recipe-line-keeps-its-errexit)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T16:48:55.785354446Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by reading `Ninja::declare_rule` while fixing make-manifest-recipe-line-keeps-its-errexit. Not reproduced in the field, because the path needs a recipe over 100 kB.\n\nThe inline destination writes the shell, the flags and the script:\n\n    command = exec <env> /bin/sh -ec \"<script>\"\n\nand the response-file destination writes the shell and the file:\n\n    rspfile_content = <script>\n    command = exec <env> /bin/sh $RESPONSE_FILE\n\nwith no flags at all. `script_shell_flags` computed `-ec` for a `.POSIX:` recipe and the second spelling drops it, so such a recipe emitted through a response file runs with no `-e` anywhere: every line runs to its end and only the last line's status is read. The threshold is `script.len() > 100 * 1000`, so it is a long recipe — a generated link line, an `ar` invocation over a large object list — that silently changes semantics on crossing it.\n\nThe flags cannot be passed through as they stand: `-ec` says the next argument IS the command, and here the next argument is a file name. The `c` has to come off, which means reading the value rather than copying it — and `.SHELLFLAGS` is a Makefile-settable variable, so the reader has to answer for whatever a Makefile put there. `NinjaGenerator::arms_errexit`, added by the errexit node, already reads the one bit that matters; the alternative is to make the script self-sufficient by writing its own `set -e` header whenever the flags arm it, which costs a header on every single-line `.POSIX:` recipe in every manifest and is the reason the errexit node did not simply do that.\n\nSame file, same function: `SinkSubninja::preceding` and `residual_command` choose between the two spellings on the same threshold, so whatever is decided has to cover all three."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T17:49:44.445551984Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T18:21:59.082290541Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Landed. The filing was right about the mechanism and understated the blast radius: it named one spelling of the file-borne launch and there are FOUR, in two crates, and the one it named is not the one that reaches a build.\n\nREPRODUCED, THEN MEASURED\n\nA one-line `.POSIX:` recipe with 110,000 bytes of argument on it —\n\n    .POSIX:\n    all: out\n    out:\n    \t@: <110KB of x> ; false ; touch m1\n\n  GNU Make 4.4.1                       nothing made, exit 2\n  rkati --ninja + stock ninja 1.14.0   m1 MADE, exit 0\n  Ronin's Make mode                    m1 MADE, exit 0\n\nand the manifest said why: `command = exec /bin/sh $out.rsp`, against `exec /bin/sh -ec \"...\"` for the same recipe one byte under the threshold. The length of a recipe decided whether it stopped at its first failure.\n\nWHY A SINGLE LINE, WHICH IS THE SHAPE OF IT\n\nAn assembled script of several lines writes its own `set -e ; ` header — that is what the errexit node put there. A script that is ONE command writes no header, because there is nothing to stop between, and under `.POSIX:` its strictness is the shell's `-ec` and nothing else. So the exposure is exactly: a single-line recipe, or a whole `.ONESHELL` recipe, over the threshold, under flags that arm errexit. `long-posix-2line` in the matrix below is right before the fix and after it, and that is the reason.\n\nTHE FIX\n\n`kati::ninja::script_file_flags` takes the `c` off and copies the rest. `-ec` → `-e`, `-c` → nothing, `-e -c` → `-e`, `-xec` → `-xe`, `-o pipefail -ec` → `-o pipefail -e`, `--posix -c` → `--posix` (a long option keeps every letter it has), empty → empty. Read as an option cluster because that is the form `.SHELLFLAGS` takes, left alone from `--` onwards and on any word that is an option's argument rather than an option, and a cluster that was nothing but `-c` disappears rather than becoming a bare `-`.\n\nCopying the rest rather than writing a `set -e` header is the choice, and the reason is that errexit is not the only thing in there. `.SHELLFLAGS` is Makefile-settable: a header answers for `-e` and silently drops `-x`, `-u` and `-o pipefail`, which are the same kind of instruction about the same shell. The letter that had to come off is the only one that is about how the script ARRIVED.\n\nFOUR SITES, NOT ONE\n\nThe filing named `Ninja::declare_rule` and said `SinkSubninja::preceding` and `residual_command` choose on the same threshold. Reading for those found two more, both in Ronin, and neither reachable from the manifest:\n\n  kati/src-rs/ninja.rs   declare_rule, the `ResponseFile` arm        the manifest\n  src/make/sink.rs       command_bindings, the `ResponseFile` arm    the graph's command binding\n  src/make/layout.rs     launch_script, the `Script::File` arm       Ronin's direct launch\n  src/make/layout.rs     launch, the over-threshold branch           the composed command line\n\n`preceding` and `residual_command` need no separate change: every one of them funnels through `command_bindings` on Ronin's side and through `declare_rule` on kati's, which is what \"all three\" turned out to mean.\n\nThe third and fourth were found by measuring rather than by reading. After the first two were in, Ronin's Make mode still made m1 — because a settled launch never goes through the command binding at all. Fixing `launch_script` made the default-shell case right and left a `SHELL = /bin/bash` case wrong, because a shell the Makefile named is not one a direct launch may start and falls back to the composed command line, which was the fourth. Each was found by a fixture failing, not by inspection.\n\nTHE MATRIX, BEFORE AND AFTER\n\nFourteen shapes, each run three ways — GNU 4.4.1, `rkati --ninja` piped into stock ninja, and Ronin's Make mode — comparing the markers made and the exit status. Three rows moved, all of them `long`:\n\n  long-posix          before  GNU 2/none   manifest 0/m1   ronin 0/m1\n                      after   GNU 2/none   manifest 1/none ronin 2/none\n  long-shellflags-x   before  GNU 2/none   manifest 0/m1   ronin 0/m1     (.SHELLFLAGS = -ec)\n                      after   GNU 2/none   manifest 1/none ronin 2/none\n  long-shellflags-u   before  GNU 2/none   manifest 0/m1   ronin 0/m1     (.SHELLFLAGS = -uc)\n                      after   GNU 2/none   manifest 2/none ronin 2/none\n\nNothing moved the other way, and the eleven unmoved rows are the controls: `long-plain` (no `.POSIX:`, so no flags to lose), `long-posix-dash` (a `-` prefix takes `-e` off the line in GNU too), `long-posix-ignore`, `long-posix-2line` (writes its own header), and every `short-` counterpart. Effects now agree in all fourteen. The residual disagreements are exit status only — GNU 2 where stock ninja says 1 — which is the pre-existing bounded difference the conformance inventory carries as the `exit-status` family, and it shows on the `short-` rows just as much.\n\nTEETH, BY PARKING\n\nSix, over three gates, with `script_file_flags` parked to return nothing — which is exactly the old contract at all four sites at once:\n\n  kati  a_posix_line_read_from_a_file_still_stops_at_its_failure   FAILED  (got m1, success)\n  kati  script_file_flags_take_off_the_command_letter              FAILED\n  kati  a_plain_line_read_from_a_file_runs_to_its_end              ok      (guard)\n  ronin make::layout_tests::a_long_posix_script_keeps_its_errexit  FAILED\n  ronin make::layout_tests::a_long_script_keeps_the_flags_...      FAILED\n  make_port  a-posix-recipe-too-long-to-be-an-argument-keeps-errexit      FAILED\n  make_port  a-long-recipe-under-a-named-shell-keeps-its-flags             FAILED\n\n`ran_from_a_response_file` is the other half of the errexit node's `ran_as_an_edge`: same assembly, but the script is written to a file and the shell is started with the flags the file-borne launch leaves, in a directory of its own, and the answer is the files left behind and whether the edge failed.\n\nTHE FIXTURES\n\nTwo make_port cases, both oracle-recorded from `reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make`, both `outcome failure` with no marker file. The padding is generated by the Makefile itself — `xxxxxxxxxx` and four doublings by ten, then eleven of those, 110,000 bytes — so the case stays a case rather than a checked-in blob, and the recorded `expected` stays twenty lines. The second names `SHELL = /bin/bash`, which is what sends the launch down the composed command line: it is the only one of the four sites a default-shell case cannot reach.\n\nGATES\n\n  workspace                1027/0/1  (1022 plus five: three in kati, two in layout_tests)\n  --no-default-features    791/0     (788 plus the three kati ones)\n  conformance              336/51 normalised, 286 raw, class and family tables identical\n  equivalence              297/280/75/0, manifest respellings 8, unchanged\n  upstream inventory       1285 at 100/0/24/1161, compiler zero, TSV byte-identical\n  make_port                4/4 over 1080 cases (1078 plus the two new), ten divergence markers\n  ninja conformance        425/425, 10 CLI, 86 build outcome, 3 invocation, persistence, log content\n  real projects            vim 9.2 in 20s, zsh 5.9.2 and 36 modules in 34s, both at -j8\n  fmt, check-lints.sh      clean\n\nWHAT THIS LEAVES FOR THE NEXT NODE\n\nmake-manifest-oneshell-recipe-is-not-a-manifest named the response file as one candidate destination for a `.ONESHELL` recipe, and said this node had to be settled first because that path dropped the flags. It no longer does: a `.ONESHELL` script reaching the shell through `$out.rsp` now carries whatever `.SHELLFLAGS` the first line asked for, which is what `script_shell_flags` computes for it under `one_shell`. Whether that is the right destination is still that node's question — the file holds newlines natively, but it is one file per edge and the threshold that selects it is a length rather than a property of the recipe."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T19:16:59.394982602Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-kati-option-row-past-the-letters-it-can-honour
                title "Answer the rest of GNU's option row in rkati without panicking on it"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-kati-option-table-reads-i-as-n)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T17:43:27.194356326Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed from the survey make-kati-option-table-reads-i-as-n was asked to do. That node fixed every letter with a live field behind it; this is what the survey found beyond them.\n\n`rkati -f M.mk <letter>` against GNU Make 4.4.1's whole short-option row, before that fix:\n\n  -d  ok        -i  ok (WRONG: read as the dry run)   -s  ok\n  -b -m -B -e -h -k -L -n -p -q -r -R -S -t -v -w      PANIC\n  -I DIR   -o FILE   -W FILE   -l N   -O TYPE   -E STRING   PANIC\n\nand after it, `-i -n -k -S -e -r -R` are correct against the oracle on effects and status. What is left is two separable things.\n\nONE. Fifteen letters still reach `panic!(\"Unknown flag: ...\")` at flags.rs. They split three ways:\n\n  ignored by GNU itself      -b -m\n  no-op in Ronin's own table -L -w -O -l -p\n  front-end behaviour        -B -t -q -W -o -I -v -h -E\n\n`src/make/cli.rs`'s MAKE_OPTION_SURFACE already classifies all of them, and that table is the reference. The first two groups can be accepted and dropped, matching what Ronin's Make mode does with them today. The third group cannot: kati has no field for always-make, touch, question or what-if, and accepting one silently would make `rkati -q` build the tree and report success, which is worse than crashing. `-I` and `-o` are the two in that group kati DOES have fields for (`include_dirs`, `old_files`) — both take an argument whose canonicalisation the frontend owns (`-I -` resets the search where it stands; `old_files` is stored \"as the switch canonicalised them\"), which is why they were left out rather than folded in.\n\nTWO. The panic itself. GNU answers `make: invalid option -- 'Z'` plus the usage block and exits 2; rkati panics with a Rust backtrace note and exits 101. That is a diagnostic and a status, not a crash — but `Flags::from_args` is a library function that Ronin's own equivalence gate calls (`src/make/equivalence.rs:693`), so it must not `process::exit`. Either it returns the complaint and `main` renders it, or `Flags` carries it out. The same arm holds three more panics of the same kind: an unparseable `-j`, an unparseable `--remote_num_jobs`, and `--variable_assignment_trace_filter` without its partner.\n\nTHREE, found while surveying and not yet anywhere: rkati has no option CLUSTERING. GNU's getopt reads `-rR` as `-r -R`; rkati reads it as one unknown word and panics. The corpus under `tests/make/` passes `-rR` seven times and `-rR-q` once — through Ronin's Make mode, which handles it — so nothing measures this today.\n\nMeasured scope: kati's own testcase corpus passes rkati no GNU short option other than `-C`, `-j1` and `-s`, so none of this moves the conformance gate. The pressure is on anyone driving `rkati` directly and on the equivalence gate's argv."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T20:37:17.957496393Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T21:14:41.930292971Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Landed. The filing's three parts all reproduced; its count of the first was one short.\n\nTHE SURVEY, RE-RUN\n\nEvery letter through the standalone binary, before:\n\n  accepted and correct   -c -C -d -e -f -i -j -k -n -r -R -s -S\n  panicked (rc 101)      -b -m -B -h -L -p -q -t -v -w        ten with no argument\n                         -I -o -W -l -O -E                    six with one\n\nThat is SIXTEEN, not the fifteen the filing's headline said — its own three groups already summed to sixteen (2 + 5 + 9), so the number was the only thing wrong with it. Each panic exits 101 with a Rust backtrace note; GNU Make 4.4.1 answers a command line it will not take with a diagnostic and exit 2.\n\nONE. SEVEN ACCEPTED AND DROPPED\n\n`-b` and `-m` GNU Make ignores itself (`ignore` in its switch table). `-L -p -w -O -l` name something no compiled graph can carry: whether symlink times are checked, whether the database is printed, where a directory announcement goes, how a runner interleaves its output and how heavily it loads the machine. `MAKE_OPTION_SURFACE` in src/make/cli.rs classifies the same seven as no-ops and is what this follows.\n\nDropping one is not free, because how much of the command line it takes is still observable — a word too many and a goal disappears, one too few and an argument becomes a goal. GNU's shapes, read out of `switches[]` and then measured:\n\n  -O   `string` with a `noarg_value`, which getopt spells `O::`\n       GNU `-O line zz` → *** No rule to make target 'line'.   rkati now the same\n       GNU `-Oline zz`  → built zz                             rkati now the same\n  -l   `floating` with one, plus main.c's own extra peek at the following word\n       when it opens with a digit or a `.` (main.c:3336)\n       GNU `-l 2 zz`   → built zz          rkati now the same\n       GNU `-l 2.5 zz` → built zz          rkati now the same\n       GNU `-l foo`    → *** No rule to make target 'foo'.     rkati now the same\n\nTWO. NINE REFUSED, AND REFUSED AS A VALUE\n\n`-B -t -q -v -h -E -I -o -W`. Each says what to do INSTEAD of building, or names a field whose argument the front end canonicalises rather than stores — `-I -` resets the search path where it stands rather than adding a directory named `-`, and an old file is stored as the switch canonicalised it, so reading either argument in raw would put the wrong bytes in a real field. That is the filing's reasoning and it stands.\n\n  rkati: unsupported option -- 'q'      exit 2\n\nin this binary's own words, beside the `Unknown flag: -Z` the row already had for a letter nothing knows. It is not GNU Make and does not print GNU Make's usage block.\n\nThe delivery is what the filing insisted on: `Flags::from_args` returns `Result<Flags, Refusal>` rather than exiting, because Ronin's equivalence gate builds a session straight from an argv and a `process::exit` there would take the gate with it. `Session::from_args` carries the `Result` up; the standalone binary renders and exits 2; the gate turns it into `Outcome::NotAccepted`. The three malformed-argument panics on the same arm — an unparseable `-j`, an unparseable `--remote_num_jobs`, and `--variable_assignment_trace_filter` without its partner — travel the same way keeping the exact words they already had.\n\nTHREE. CLUSTERING\n\n`-rR` is two switches and GNU's getopt reads it as two. The split reaches only a word opening with a single `-` and a letter that takes no argument, which leaves `-j8`, `-Csub` and every `--long` spelling exactly where they were — so it can only affect words that panicked before, and the regression surface is zero. The first letter that DOES take an argument ends the split and keeps the rest of the word attached to itself, which is what getopt does with `-Cdir`; that also guarantees nothing is left pending when an option reaches for its argument.\n\n  rkati -rR    both no_builtin_rules and no_builtin_variables\n  rkati -rRs   and is_silent_mode\n  rkati -rj8   -r plus num_jobs 8\n  rkati -rZ    rkati: Unknown flag: -Z            — the letter, as GNU names it\n  rkati -rRq   rkati: unsupported option -- 'q'\n  rkati -rR-q  rkati: Unknown flag: -rR-q\n\nThe last is deliberate and probed: `-` is the byte getopt keeps for itself, and GNU Make answers `-rR-q` with `invalid option -- '-'` and exit 2. A word carrying one among its letters is therefore not a cluster at all and is complained about whole. The filing said the corpus passes `-rR-q` once and that Ronin's Make mode handles it — that is Ronin's Make mode's own front end, unchanged here.\n\nCONFORMANCE MOVEMENT: NONE, AS THE FILING PREDICTED\n\nThe corpus hands rkati only `-C`, `-j` and `-s`, so nothing here could move it, and nothing did: 336/51 normalised, 286 raw, class and family tables identical. Upstream inventory 1285 at 100/0/24/1161 with the TSV byte-identical. The pressure this relieves is on anyone driving rkati directly and on the equivalence gate's argv, which is where the teeth went.\n\nTEETH, BY PARKING\n\nFive tests. With the letter classification and the cluster split reverted and the tests left in place, all five fail:\n\n  kati  flags::tests::the_letters_a_compiler_can_drop_are_taken                        FAILED\n  kati  flags::tests::a_dropped_switch_takes_the_words_gnu_takes                       FAILED\n  kati  flags::tests::a_switch_this_front_end_cannot_honour_is_refused_and_not_crashed_on  FAILED\n  kati  flags::tests::a_cluster_of_short_switches_is_read_as_the_switches_it_is        FAILED\n  ronin make::equivalence::a_refused_switch_is_an_outcome_rather_than_a_crash          FAILED\n\nThe last is in the equivalence gate on purpose, because that is the caller the panic would have killed: it asserts `-q` arrives as `make rejected the fixture: rkati: unsupported option -- 'q'` and, beside it, that `-rR` compiles a graph both sinks agree on — a cluster read as one unknown word would have been a different graph, since `-r` and `-R` are compiler input.\n\n`a_dropped_switch_takes_the_words_gnu_takes` is the one that would fail silently otherwise: it reads the GOALS off the parsed flags for the five shapes above, so a `-l` that swallowed `foo` or an `-O` that swallowed `line` shows up as a missing or extra goal rather than as nothing at all.\n\nGATES\n\n  workspace                1042/0/1  (1037 plus five)\n  --no-default-features    804/0     (800 plus the four kati ones)\n  conformance              336/51 normalised, 286 raw, class and family tables identical\n  equivalence              297/280/75/0, manifest respellings 8, unchanged\n  upstream inventory       1285 at 100/0/24/1161, compiler zero, TSV byte-identical\n  make_port                4/4 over 1086 cases, ten divergence markers\n  ninja conformance        425/425, 10 CLI, 86 build outcome, 3 invocation, persistence, log content\n  real projects            vim 9.2 in 22s, zsh 5.9.2 and 36 modules in 37s, both at -j8\n  scripts/check-release.sh EXIT 0 end to end, performance included\n  fmt, check-lints.sh      clean\n\nOne lint refusal was hit and fixed rather than bypassed: `clippy::while_let_loop` on the option loop's new `loop { let Some(arg) = ... else { break } }`, rewritten as the `while let` it is.\n\nkati nplan/make-kati-option-row-past-the-letters-it-can-honour at 219ac5dd3e365b8696d16f5873ae0402c18e58b4, pushed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T21:14:51.644103896Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-one-shell-line-keeps-the-semicolons-it-ends-on
                title "Stop taking a .ONESHELL line's own trailing semicolons off it"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-manifest-oneshell-recipe-is-not-a-manifest)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T19:07:40.451710025Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-manifest-oneshell-recipe-is-not-a-manifest, and older than it: this is not the manifest, and Ronin's Make mode has it too.\n\n`translate_command` ends by trimming the line:\n\n    while let Some(&c) = cmd_buf.last() {\n        if c == b'\\n' || (!c.is_ascii_whitespace() && c != b';') {\n            break;\n        }\n        cmd_buf.truncate(cmd_buf.len() - 1);\n    }\n\nso every trailing `;` comes off every recipe line. Where lines are joined with ` ; ` that is a redundant separator and taking it off changes nothing. Under `.ONESHELL` the lines are joined with NEWLINES and a trailing `;;` is a `case` item's terminator — a token of the script, not a separator between lines.\n\nMeasured, GNU Make 4.4.1 against `rkati --ninja` through stock ninja 1.14.0.git and against Ronin's Make mode:\n\n    .ONESHELL:\n    all: out\n    out:\n    \t@case x in\n    \tx) touch m1 ;;\n    \ty) touch m2 ;;\n    \tesac\n    \ttouch m3\n\n  GNU Make 4.4.1     m1 and m3 made, exit 0\n  manifest + ninja   nothing made, exit 2\n  Ronin's Make mode  nothing made, exit 2\n\nand the manifest says why: `printf '%s\\n' 'case x in' 'x) touch m1' 'y) touch m2' 'esac' 'touch m3'` — both `;;` gone, so `y)` follows an unterminated item and the shell refuses the whole script. A ONE-arm case survives only because POSIX lets the last item omit its `;;`, which is why the shape looked fine until a second arm was written.\n\nRonin's Make mode fails identically and never sees the manifest at all: it is handed `TranslatedRecipe`, so the trim is upstream of both destinations. That is what makes this its own node rather than part of the manifest one.\n\nWhat a fix has to decide is which trailing bytes a line may lose and when. The trim also takes trailing whitespace, and it is right to for a line that will be joined; the question is whether `one_shell` should reach `translate_command`, which today takes only the line's bytes and knows nothing about the recipe it is in. Probe GNU for what it does with a line ending in `;`, in `;;`, in ` ; ` and in whitespace, both under `.ONESHELL` and not, before deciding — GNU hands `.ONESHELL` the recipe's own bytes with the prefix characters removed, so the answer there is likely \"nothing at all\".\n\nTeeth: the two-arm `case` above as a make_port fixture (it fails through Ronin's Make mode, which is what make_port drives), and a kati `one_shell_edge` case beside the ones make-manifest-oneshell-recipe-is-not-a-manifest added."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T19:31:36.75976882Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T19:57:30.423561833Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Landed. The filing reproduced exactly, and probing GNU first — as the filing asked — turned the question inside out: the answer is not \"where does GNU trim semicolons\" but \"GNU trims none, so what is the trim FOR\". That reading found a second defect, older and broader than the filed one, that has nothing to do with .ONESHELL.\n\nWHERE GNU TRIMS, READ IN ITS OWN SOURCE\n\nNowhere. `chop_commands` (commands.c:321) splits `cmds->commands` at unescaped newlines and hands each line on as written; under `one_shell` it does not split at all — nlines is 1, the whole recipe is one string, and the ONLY byte it removes is a single trailing newline. `construct_command_argv_internal` (job.c) removes leading blanks and [@+-] per logical line under .ONESHELL and touches no trailing byte. There is no trailing-separator trim anywhere in GNU Make 4.4.1.\n\nSo kati's trim is not a reproduction of anything Make does. It is a tidying of the separator the ` ; ` join would otherwise double — `touch m1;` joined to the next line as `touch m1; ; touch m2` is a syntax error — and that is the whole of its warrant. Which means it may only reach bytes that separator is redundant with, and the fix is to say exactly that.\n\nTHE FILED DEFECT\n\n    .ONESHELL:\n    all: out\n    out:\n    \t@case x in\n    \tx) touch m1 ;;\n    \ty) touch m2 ;;\n    \tesac\n    \ttouch m3\n\n  GNU Make 4.4.1     m1 m3, exit 0\n  manifest + ninja   nothing, exit 2      (before)\n  Ronin's Make mode  nothing, exit 2      (before)\n\nBoth `;;` came off, `y)` followed an unterminated item, and the shell refused the whole script. Under .ONESHELL the lines are joined with newlines, so there is no separator to be redundant with and nothing comes off at all.\n\nTHE SECOND DEFECT, FOUND BY PROBING RATHER THAN FILED\n\n    all: out\n    out:\n    \t@find . -name Makefile -exec cp {} m1 \\;\n    \t@touch m2\n\n  GNU Make 4.4.1     m1 m2, exit 0\n  manifest + ninja   nothing, exit 1      (before)\n  Ronin's Make mode  nothing, exit 2      (before)\n\nThe manifest said why: `command = exec /bin/sh -c \"find . -name M.mk -exec cp {} m1 \\\\\"`. The `;` was an ARGUMENT — the backslash in front of it says so — and taking it off left the backslash last, where it escapes the join's own ` ; ` and runs the line on into the next. `find` then exits complaining its `-exec` has no terminator. This is wrong in BOTH modes and with or WITHOUT .ONESHELL, and `find -exec \\;` is a far more common Makefile shape than a two-arm case. It was not in the filing.\n\nTHE SPELLING\n\n`translate_command` gained the recipe's `one_shell`, and the trim gained two things it did not know:\n\n  protected  — the walk already tracks quoting and the backslash; it now records how far back a blank or `;` the shell would read as TEXT reaches, and the trim stops there.\n  one `;`    — of the bare ones it drops exactly one, and only when `!one_shell`. `;;` is a token, not two separators, so a line ending in one keeps both bytes in either mode; GNU keeps them and a non-.ONESHELL line ending `;;` is already a syntax error in GNU too, so keeping the bytes is agreeing with it.\n\nTrailing blanks still go, protected the same way — an escaped blank is a blank the command is carrying.\n\nPROBED PER SHAPE, ALL THREE COLUMNS\n\nEight shapes, GNU 4.4.1 beside `rkati --ninja` through stock ninja beside Ronin's Make mode, on the markers made and the exit status. Before the fix os-case2, nos-findexec and os-findexec disagreed; after it all eight agree in all three columns:\n\n  os-case2      two-arm case under .ONESHELL      m1 m3 / 0\n  nos-case2     the same, backslash-continued     m1 m3 / 0   (agreed before too)\n  nos-semi      per-line recipe, lines end in `;` m1 m2 / 0\n  os-semi       .ONESHELL, lines end in `;`       m1 m2 / 0\n  os-if-semi    .ONESHELL if/then spanning lines, each ending `;`   m1 m2 / 0\n  os-trailws    .ONESHELL, trailing blanks        m1 m2 / 0\n  nos-findexec  find -exec \\; per-line            m1 / 0\n  os-findexec   find -exec \\; under .ONESHELL     m1 / 0\n\nTEETH, BY PARKING\n\nFive tests; with the trim reverted to the one-line loop and the tests left in place, three fail:\n\n  kati  ninja::tests::a_line_keeps_the_trailing_bytes_that_are_script   FAILED\n  kati  ninja::tests::a_case_arm_keeps_the_terminator_it_ends_on        FAILED  (\"\", false) against (\"m1 m3\", true)\n  kati  ninja::tests::an_argument_semicolon_survives_the_join           FAILED  (\"M.mk\", false) against (\"M.mk m1 m2\", true)\n\n  make_port  a-one-shell-case-arm-keeps-its-terminator                  FAILED  build failed where GNU succeeded\n  make_port  an-argument-semicolon-is-not-a-recipe-lines-separator      FAILED  build failed where GNU succeeded\n\nand exactly those two of 1082, no others. The two edge tests run the assembled script the way the destination runs it — `one_shell_edge` substitutes the printer's output into the recipe's shell as a ninja command line does, `ran_as_an_edge` hands the ` ; `-joined script to one shell — in a directory of their own, answering in the files left behind and whether the edge failed, against GNU 4.4.1 recorded on each case. The two make_port fixtures are oracle-recorded from `reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make` and drive Ronin's Make mode, which is the destination that never sees a manifest.\n\nThe unit test is the third because the two edge tests cannot reach every shape a shell would refuse to parse: it pins the quoted `;`, the escaped blank, `;;` in BOTH modes, and the .ONESHELL/`!one_shell` difference on a single `;`.\n\nGATES\n\n  workspace                1036/0/1  (1033 plus three)\n  --no-default-features    799/0     (796 plus the three kati ones)\n  conformance              336/51 normalised, 286 raw, class and family tables identical\n  equivalence              297/280/75/0, manifest respellings 8, unchanged\n  upstream inventory       1285 at 100/0/24/1161, compiler zero, TSV byte-identical\n  make_port                4/4 over 1082 cases (1080 plus two), ten divergence markers\n  ninja conformance        425/425, 10 CLI, 86 build outcome, 3 invocation, persistence, log content\n  real projects            vim 9.2 in 19s, zsh 5.9.2 and 36 modules in 34s, both at -j8\n  fmt, check-lints.sh      clean\n\nkati nplan/make-a-one-shell-line-keeps-the-semicolons-it-ends-on at 4468643b1ade7e1cc12b53ed52be0295118124ba, pushed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T19:57:35.172898565Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-one-shell-recipe-reads-its-prefixes-off-the-first-line
                title "Read a .ONESHELL recipe's prefix characters the way GNU reads them"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-manifest-oneshell-recipe-is-not-a-manifest)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T19:20:58.739138612Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-manifest-oneshell-recipe-is-not-a-manifest, and older than it: Ronin's Make mode has it too, and never sees a manifest.\n\nGNU Make's manual says the prefix characters `@`, `-` and `+` are removed from the FIRST line of a `.ONESHELL` recipe and apply to the whole of it, and that prefix characters on later lines are not removed and are passed to the shell. kati reads them per line, as it does for every other recipe.\n\nMeasured, GNU Make 4.4.1 beside `rkati --ninja` through stock ninja 1.14.0.git and beside Ronin's Make mode, on the exit status and the markers made:\n\n    .ONESHELL:\n    all: out\n    out:\n    \t-@touch m1\n    \tfalse\n\n  GNU Make 4.4.1     m1 made, exit 0   — the `-` on the first line forgave the recipe\n  manifest + ninja   m1 made, exit 1\n  Ronin's Make mode  m1 made, exit 2\n\nThe `-` never reaches `wholly_ignored`, which kati computes as every line being prefixed, so nothing mutes the script and the failing last line decides. It shows only when the first line carries the prefix AND a later line fails: `-@touch m1; false` then `@touch m2` agrees, because the last line succeeds either way, and a recipe every line of which is prefixed agrees because `wholly_ignored` is true by accident.\n\nThe same reading question covers `@` and `+`, and they were probed and are NOT settled. GNU's answer for a prefix on a LATER line differs between its own two paths: with `SHELL` named by the Makefile — the slow path — a spy shell records `arg2=[touch m1\\n-touch m2\\ntouch m3]`, the `-` passed through as text; with the default `/bin/sh` — the fast path — the same recipe makes m1, m2 and m3 and exits 0, which a literal `-touch m2` could not do. So GNU's own behaviour there depends on a scan the manual does not describe, and reproducing the manual rather than the fast path is a decision this node has to make explicitly. The same fast path is what makes an unterminated quote spanning a `.ONESHELL` line boundary report a syntax error while still writing the file correctly, recorded on make-manifest-oneshell-recipe-is-not-a-manifest.\n\nScope: `dep.rs` reads `.ONESHELL` into `flags.one_shell` and `command.rs` reads the prefixes; neither knows the other's answer at the point the prefix is read. Teeth: the recipe above as a make_port fixture — it fails through Ronin's Make mode, which make_port drives — and a `one_shell_edge` case beside the ones the manifest node added.\n\nSibling: make-a-one-shell-line-keeps-the-semicolons-it-ends-on is the other `.ONESHELL` reading defect that landing turned up. Both are about what a line of such a recipe means rather than about how the recipe is written down, and either could be taken first."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T19:58:28.096434199Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T20:36:05.845245858Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Landed. The filing said `@` and `+` were \"probed and NOT settled\" and that reproducing the manual rather than the observed behaviour was a decision this node had to make explicitly. It is settled, and no such decision was needed: GNU's source says exactly what it does, the manual describes only half of it, and the filing's \"fast path vs slow path\" reading of the other half was wrong.\n\nWHERE GNU READS THEM, IN ITS OWN SOURCE\n\nTwo functions, and they answer two different questions.\n\n`chop_commands` (commands.c:321) decides the FLAGS. Under `one_shell` it does not chop: `nlines = 1`, `lines[0]` is the whole recipe with its final newline stripped. The prefix scan below it then runs over `lines[0]` — over the whole recipe's first characters, which are its first line's — and what it finds becomes `lines_flags[0]` for all of it. `start_job_command` (job.c:1198) scans the EXPANDED text the same way and stops at the first newline: its skip-over is blanks and prefixes, and a newline is neither. So an expansion that produced the first line carries prefixes too, and no line below the first ever contributes one.\n\n`construct_command_argv_internal` (job.c:3293) decides the TEXT, and separately: it removes leading blanks and `[@+-]` from every logical line, but only under `is_bourne_compatible_shell`.\n\nTHE FILING'S UNSETTLED HALF, SETTLED\n\nThe filing recorded that a `-` on a later line \"passed through as text\" under a named SHELL and vanished under the default `/bin/sh`, and called that a fast path / slow path difference. It is not: `is_bourne_compatible_shell` (job.c:433) is a BASENAME match against seven names — sh, bash, dash, ksh, rksh, zsh, ash. Measured with a spy shell that is `#!/bin/sh` / `exec /bin/sh \"$@\"`, so its name is the only thing about it that differs:\n\n    .ONESHELL: / all: / echo one / @echo two / echo three\n\n  SHELL default (/bin/sh)   one, two, three\n  SHELL = /bin/dash         one, two, three          — on the list, so stripped just the same\n  SHELL = ./spy             one, `/bin/sh: 2: @echo: not found`, three\n\nThe filing's \"slow path\" was a shell whose name was not on the list. That half is now its own node — make-a-one-shell-prefix-survives-a-shell-make-does-not-know — because kati strips unconditionally and fixing it needs the shell's name at a point that does not have it. It changes no flag: in all three columns above the recipe stays LOUD, because the `@` was on the second line.\n\nTHE MATRIX, MEASURED ON 4.4.1\n\n  `-` first line, later line fails   m1, exit 0          — the filed defect\n  `-` second line, later line fails  m1 m2, exit 2       — reaches nothing\n  `-` from an expansion, first line  m1, exit 0          — `$(FIRST)` = `-touch m1`\n  `@` first line                     nothing echoed\n  `@` second line                    every line echoed   — reaches nothing\n  `+` first line, under -n           whole script runs\n  `+` second line, under -n          nothing runs        — reaches nothing\n  `$(MAKE)` on any line, under -t    whole recipe runs instead of being touched\n\nThe last is GNU's own widening and the one asymmetry: the `$(MAKE)` search `chop_commands` runs after the prefix scan reads the whole line it chopped, which under `.ONESHELL` is the whole recipe. So `+` is first-line-only and `$(MAKE)` is anywhere, and the difference is visible.\n\nTHE SPELLING\n\n`CommandEvaluator::eval` builds one `Command` per expanded line and each carries its own prefixes. Under `.ONESHELL` a post-pass now gives every one of them the first's — echo, dash_prefixed, ignore_error and shell_flag — and gives them all `first.recursive_line || references_make_anywhere`, which is exactly `+`-on-the-first-line OR `$(MAKE)`-anywhere because `first.recursive_line` already carries the first line's `+` beside its own reference and the OR subsumes that reference.\n\nThat is one place rather than several: `script_shell_flags` already read the first line's flags, `recipe_steps` already read the first kept line's `recursive_line` — which is why the `-q` and `-t` `+` fixtures were already green before this — and `translate_recipe`'s `wholly_ignored` is `all(ignore_error)`, which the post-pass now makes true exactly when the first line's `-` says so. Nothing downstream changed.\n\nThe `$(MAKE)` widening is genuinely new: `recipe_steps`' local first-kept-line reading could not see a reference on a later line, so `-t` touched the target where GNU ran the recipe.\n\nWHAT WAS NOT A DEFECT, CHECKED BEFORE FILING\n\n`+` under `-n` runs the line in GNU and runs nothing in Ronin — with AND without `.ONESHELL`, so it is not this node. It is also not a defect: `dry-run-skips-a-plus-line` is in make_port's DISCOVERY_ONLY_CASES with the reason written out — GNU runs the child because starting it is the only way it can learn what it would do, and Ronin compiled the child instead, so its `-n` is Ninja's. Recorded, decided, and left alone.\n\nTEETH, BY PARKING\n\nFour new make_port fixtures, oracle-recorded from reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, plus a kati unit test. With the post-pass reverted:\n\n  a-one-shell-recipe-is-forgiven-by-its-first-lines-dash              FAILED  build failed where GNU succeeded\n  a-one-shell-expansion-carries-the-first-lines-dash                  FAILED  build failed where GNU succeeded\n  a-one-shell-recipe-is-recursive-from-a-later-lines-make-reference   FAILED  'one.txt' was not produced\n  command::tests::a_one_shell_recipe_reads_its_prefixes_off_the_first_line  FAILED\n\nand those three alone of 1086, no pre-existing case moving either way.\n\n`a-later-one-shell-line-carries-no-dash-for-the-recipe` passes with the fix parked and with it in place, and is here on purpose: it is the guard that the reading is not applied in the other direction. A `-` on the second line must NOT forgive, and a post-pass written as \"any line's\" rather than \"the first line's\" would break it while the other three stayed green.\n\nThe unit test is the one that reaches `@` and `+`. Neither is visible in files — `@` is narration, which Ronin speaks in its own voice, and `+`'s effect under `-n` is the decided architectural difference above — so the reading itself is what is asserted, over the whole matrix in both directions.\n\nGATES\n\n  workspace                1037/0/1  (1036 plus one)\n  --no-default-features    800/0     (799 plus one)\n  conformance              336/51 normalised, 286 raw, class and family tables identical\n  equivalence              297/280/75/0, manifest respellings 8, unchanged\n  upstream inventory       1285 at 100/0/24/1161, compiler zero, TSV byte-identical\n  make_port                4/4 over 1086 cases (1082 plus four), ten divergence markers\n  ninja conformance        425/425, 10 CLI, 86 build outcome, 3 invocation, persistence, log content\n  real projects            vim 9.2 in 33s, zsh 5.9.2 and 36 modules in 47s, both at -j8\n  fmt, check-lints.sh      clean"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T20:36:27.351541314Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-one-shell-prefix-survives-a-shell-make-does-not-know
                title "Leave a .ONESHELL script's inner prefixes alone for a shell GNU would not strip them for"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-one-shell-recipe-reads-its-prefixes-off-the-first-line)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T20:35:22.13978359Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-a-one-shell-recipe-reads-its-prefixes-off-the-first-line, which settled which line's prefixes become FLAGS. This is the other half of the same code in GNU: which lines' prefix characters are removed from the script's TEXT.\n\n`construct_command_argv_internal` (job.c:3293) removes leading blanks and `[@+-]` from every logical line of a `.ONESHELL` recipe ONLY under `is_bourne_compatible_shell (shell)`, and the comment above it says why: \"if we're in .ONESHELL mode and have changed SHELL to something non-standard, we should leave those alone because they could be part of the script. In this case we must also leave in place any leading [@+-] for the same reason.\" `is_bourne_compatible_shell` (job.c:433) is a basename match against a fixed list — `sh`, `bash`, `dash`, `ksh`, `rksh`, `zsh`, `ash` — and nothing else.\n\nkati's `parse_command_prefixes` strips them from every expanded line unconditionally, with no idea what the shell is called.\n\nMeasured, GNU Make 4.4.1 over\n\n    .ONESHELL:\n    all:\n    \techo one\n    \t@echo two\n    \techo three\n\n  SHELL default (/bin/sh)   prints one, two, three          — `@` stripped as text\n  SHELL = /bin/dash         prints one, two, three          — same, the basename is on the list\n  SHELL = ./spy             prints one, then `/bin/sh: 2: @echo: not found`, then three\n                                                            — `@` left in place and handed over\n\nwhere `./spy` is `#!/bin/sh` / `exec /bin/sh \"$@\"`, so the only thing that differs between it and `/bin/sh` is its NAME. Ronin's Make mode prints one, two, three in all three, because the strip is unconditional.\n\nThis is what the sibling node's filing called a difference between GNU's \"fast path\" and \"slow path\". It is not: it is the shell's basename, and the source settles it. The same filing also recorded an unterminated quote spanning a `.ONESHELL` line boundary behaving differently under a named SHELL — THAT one is the fast path (`construct_command_argv_internal`'s own scanner giving up to `goto slow`), and the two are unrelated.\n\nScope: `parse_command_prefixes` in kati/src-rs/command.rs is called from `ExpandedRecipeLines::next` for each expanded line and knows nothing about the shell. Under `.ONESHELL` the per-line strip is the ONLY thing that call does that matters — the flags now come from the first line regardless — so the decision is whether to make that call conditional on the shell's basename. `Command::shell_flag` is already chosen per line with the rule's own scope in hand, so the shell itself is reachable at the same point.\n\nFringe by construction: it needs a `SHELL` the Makefile set to something whose basename is not one of seven names, AND a `.ONESHELL` recipe, AND a prefix character below its first line. Filed because it is a real divergence with a source-level answer, not because anything is pressing on it. Teeth would be a make_port fixture carrying its own spy shell."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T22:37:09.080965122Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T22:38:42.185796065Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Landed, and the filing was right about the mechanism and understated the damage. The prefix characters are the half you can see in a probe; the half that breaks a real Makefile is the indentation beside them, and it is the same conditional in the same GNU function.\n\nWHAT GNU DOES, IN ITS OWN SOURCE\n\nTwo removals, and only one of them asks what the shell is.\n\n`start_job_command` (job.c:1198) skips blanks and `[@+-]` off the front of the script it is about to run and stops at the first newline — a newline is neither a blank nor a prefix — so the recipe's FIRST line loses both whatever the shell is. This is the same scan the sibling node measured for the flags; it moves the text too.\n\n`construct_command_argv_internal` (job.c:3293) removes them from every logical line, first included, and only under `is_bourne_compatible_shell (shell)`. Its comment says why: a `SHELL` changed to something non-standard may be reading those characters, \"and in this case we must also leave in place any leading [@+-] for the same reason\". `is_bourne_compatible_shell` (job.c:433) walks back to the last `/` and compares what follows against seven names exactly — `sh`, `bash`, `dash`, `ksh`, `rksh`, `zsh`, `ash`.\n\nTHE MATRIX, measured on reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make with a `SHELL` that is `#!/bin/sh` / `exec /bin/sh \"$@\"`, so its NAME is the only thing about it that differs, and with a second one that writes `\"$2\"` to a file so the script itself can be read back.\n\n  BY NAME — `.ONESHELL:` over `echo one` / `@echo two` / `echo three`\n    SHELL default (/bin/sh)     one two three            stripped   — the control cell\n    SHELL = /bin/dash           one two three            stripped\n    SHELL = ./dash              one two three            stripped   — the basename, not the file\n    SHELL = ./sub/sh            one two three            stripped\n    SHELL = ./bsh//sh, ./bsh/./sh   one two three        stripped\n    SHELL = ./spy               `@echo: not found`       LEFT       — FIXED\n    SHELL = ./flash             `@echo: not found`       LEFT       — FIXED, `sh` is not a suffix match\n    SHELL = ./python            `@echo: not found`       LEFT       — FIXED\n    SHELL = ./ashx              `@echo: not found`       LEFT       — nor a prefix match\n\n  WITH ARGUMENTS — the value is `$(SHELL)` whole, so the basename carries them\n    SHELL = ./bsh/sh            stripped\n    SHELL = ./bsh/sh -x         LEFT — the basename is `sh -x`, which is not `sh`\n    SHELL = ./bsh/sh --         LEFT\n    SHELL = './bsh/sh   '       LEFT — trailing blanks survive the assignment and the basename\n    SHELL = ./dumper -x         LEFT\n  Read off the echo, which `start_job_command` prints AFTER `construct_command_argv`\n  has rewritten the buffer in place, so the echoed text is the strip's own answer.\n\n  WHAT THE SHELL IS HANDED — `SHELL = ./capture`, which writes `\"$2\"` to a file\n    `-@ touch m1` / `  @touch m2` / `    touch m3`\n      GNU     `touch m1\\n  @touch m2\\n    touch m3`\n      Ronin   `touch m1\\ntouch m2\\ntouch m3`                        — FIXED\n    the same recipe under `SHELL = ./bsh/sh`\n      both    `touch m1\\ntouch m2\\ntouch m3`                        — the control\n\n  THE CASE THAT MATTERS — `SHELL := /usr/bin/python3`, `.SHELLFLAGS := -c`\n    `import sys` / `if True:` / `    open(\"out.txt\",\"w\").write(\"hello\\n\")` / `print(\"done\")`\n      GNU     writes out.txt, prints `done`\n      Ronin   `IndentationError: expected an indented block after 'if' statement`   — FIXED\n  This is the whole point of GNU's conditional and the filing did not name it: a\n  shell Make cannot name may be one that counts columns.\n\n  UPSTREAM'S OWN TEST — `tests/scripts/targets/ONESHELL`, the perl case\n    `SHELL = /usr/bin/perl`, `.SHELLFLAGS = -e`, a recipe whose second line is\n    ` +7;` and whose third is `@y=qw(a b c);`. GNU prints `a = 12, y = (a b c)`.\n    Ronin ate the continuation's indentation and perl's array sigil, and the build\n    failed. It now prints `a = 12, y = (a b c)`. That row moved in the inventory,\n    audited below.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, in kati on nplan/make-a-one-shell-prefix-survives-a-shell-make-does-not-know at d652791e7b0a953c88a0d5ee7d008494d14f9def, pushed before this pin.\n\n`is_bourne_compatible_shell` is GNU's basename test, written out. `PrefixStripping` says which of a recipe's lines Make reads the leading blanks and prefixes off — `interior` for the lines below the first, `past_first` for where the recipe has got to — and `ExpandedRecipeLines` carries it, returning the line exactly as the expansion left it where it is the script's own. `Command::keeps_indent` carries that answer to the one other place that trimmed: `translate_recipe` in ninja.rs, which took `trim_ascii_start` to every line.\n\nThe shell is read once per recipe with the rule's own scope in hand, which is where GNU Make's `lookup_variable_for_file` reads `SHELL` from too, and only under `.ONESHELL` — no other recipe evaluates `$(SHELL)` that did not before, and no other recipe's reading changes.\n\nTHE FIRST LINE is the first line that carried a command, whichever written line it came from, which is the convention the sibling node already set for the flags. GNU's is the first PHYSICAL line, so a recipe whose first written line expands to nothing is read one line later here than there. Recorded rather than chased: the flags have had the same seam since the sibling landed and nothing has stood on it.\n\nTEETH, by parking, over the whole corpus of 1,089 gated cases:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the conditional, so every line is stripped again | 2 |\n| `keeps_indent`, leaving the prefix fix without the indent one | 1 |\nand those alone — no pre-existing case moved in either direction.\n\nTHREE FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten:\n  a-one-shell-prefix-survives-a-shell-make-does-not-know — `./spy` is `/bin/sh`\n    under another name; the interior `@touch m2` reaches it and m2 is never made.\n  a-one-shell-prefix-is-eaten-for-a-shell-named-like-a-bourne-one — the control,\n    and the one that gates the basename test rather than the conditional: the same\n    file under the name `./dash`, where m2 IS made. A strip keyed on anything but\n    the basename breaks this while the other two stay green.\n  an-unknown-shells-one-shell-script-arrives-as-it-was-written — the script itself,\n    written to a file by the shell, so the recording holds the exact bytes GNU Make\n    handed over: the first line's blanks and prefixes gone, every line below it\n    untouched.\n\nTWO UNIT TESTS in kati: the basename test over the seven names, their paths, the\nnear-misses (`flash`, `shell`, `ashx`, `sh.exe`) and a value with arguments; and\nthe line reading in both directions, including a first line made of nothing but a\nprefix.\n\nGATES\n  workspace                1044/0/1  (1042 plus the two unit tests)\n  --no-default-features    806/0     (804 plus two)\n  make_port                4/4 over 1089 cases (1086 plus three), ten divergence markers\n  conformance              336/51 normalised, 286 raw, class and family tables identical\n  equivalence              297/280/75/0, manifest respellings 8, unchanged\n  upstream inventory       1285 at 100/0/24/1161, compiler zero, ONE row moved (below)\n  ninja conformance        425/425, 10 CLI, 86 build outcome, 3 invocation, persistence, log content\n  real projects            vim 9.2 in 17s, zsh 5.9.2 and 36 modules in 32s, both at -j8\n  scripts/check-release.sh exit 0 end to end, performance stage included\n  fmt, check-lints.sh      clean\n\nINVENTORY AUDIT. One row: `targets/ONESHELL.diff.5` loses `ninja-failure-block` and keeps `unclassified` + `ninja-progress+evaluation`. That is upstream's perl `.ONESHELL` case above: the build no longer fails. It stays unclassified because Ronin narrates in its own voice, which is what the bucket is for. Totals and every other row unchanged; the compiler bucket stays at zero. The first run of the performance stage refused `deep-graph-evaluation` at 0.87x against the recorded 0.72x under a 15-minute load average of 8.7 on a 32-way host; the second run measured 6.686 ms against Ninja's 8.177, a ratio of 0.82, and the gate passed. Load noise, per the standing note.\n\nTWO DEFECTS FILED, both found here and neither this node's:\n  make-a-target-specific-shell-is-not-the-one-that-runs — `all: SHELL := ./dumper`\n    is ignored outright; Ronin reads `SHELL` once for the whole graph. `.SHELLFLAGS`\n    IS read per target beside it, so the two halves of the same lookup disagree.\n  make-a-one-shell-shell-is-one-word — under `.ONESHELL` GNU Make execs the whole\n    `$(SHELL)` value as one path and reports `./dumper -x: No such file or directory`;\n    Ronin splits it into a program and its arguments, as GNU Make does for every\n    recipe that is NOT `.ONESHELL`, and the build succeeds where GNU's fails."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T22:41:59.60655073Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-target-specific-shell-is-not-the-one-that-runs
                title "Run a recipe under the SHELL its own target set"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T22:40:10.615186877Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-a-one-shell-prefix-survives-a-shell-make-does-not-know, measured on both binaries. A `SHELL` set for one target is ignored: Ronin runs the recipe under the global one.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. GNU Make expands `$(SHELL)` per recipe line with the file's own scope — `construct_command_argv` (job.c:3725) calls `allocated_variable_expand_for_file (\"$(SHELL)\", file)`, and the `.SHELLFLAGS` beside it goes through `lookup_variable_for_file`. Ronin reads the shell ONCE, in `NinjaGenerator::new` (kati/src-rs/ninja.rs), with no scope at all, and carries that one value on the generator for every node it emits. `.SHELLFLAGS` is read per line with the rule's scope in hand (`get_shell_flag`, called from `CommandEvaluator::eval`), so the two halves of the same lookup already disagree with each other.\n\nMEASURED, with `./dumper` a shell that writes its arguments to a file:\n\n    all: SHELL := ./dumper\n    all:\n    \\techo one\n\n  GNU     argv[1]=<-c> argv[2]=<echo one> — dumper ran, nothing was printed\n  Ronin   no argv.txt at all, `one` on stdout — /bin/sh ran\n\n  The same file with `SHELL := ./dumper` at the top rather than on the target:\n  both run dumper. So it is the scope and not the assignment.\n\n  With `.ONESHELL:` beside it the same thing happens, which is how it was found:\n  the strip decision that node added reads the shell with the rule's scope in\n  hand, so it can answer for a shell the run then does not use.\n\nWHERE. The generator's one `shell` field is the whole of it; a per-node read with `node.rule_vars` in scope is what GNU Make does. Beware what else reads that field: `recipe_steps` hands it to `simple_command::direct_argv`, whose first gate is that the shell is the built-in default, and the manifest sink writes it into the rule. A node-scoped shell has to reach all three or none."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T01:37:54.880383536Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T02:12:11.592024395Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Landed, and the filing was right about the mechanism and about where the plumbing goes. What it got wrong is the one cell it offered as a control, and the correction is the thing that says the fix is complete rather than partial.\n\nTHE FILING'S PROBE LIST SAID `$(shell)` IS UNAFFECTED, GLOBAL `SHELL` GOVERNING THE READ. Half right. A `$(shell)` in a SIMPLE assignment is expanded while the makefile is read, before any target has a scope, and there the global `SHELL` governs — agreed on both binaries before and after. But a `$(shell)` reached while a recipe is being expanded is inside the file's scope, and GNU Make starts it under THAT TARGET's shell:\n\n    V = $(shell echo readtime)\n    all: SHELL := ./dumper\n    all: ; echo $(V)\n\n      GNU ran ./dumper twice — once for `echo readtime`, once for the recipe —\n      so V came out empty and the recipe was `echo `.\n      Ronin already did the first of those and not the second. It is the same\n      lookup, and Ronin had it right in the one place and wrong in the other.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. `CommandEvaluator::eval` (kati/src-rs/command.rs) already puts the node's scope in `current_scope`, and already read the shell there under `.ONESHELL` — the sibling node's basename test needed it. That read is now unconditional and its answer is carried out on `recipe_shell`. The generator hands it to `recipe_steps`, to the `SinkRule` it declares, and to `ExpandedRecipe`, which are the three the filing named: `simple_command::direct_argv` gates on the shell being the built-in default, and so does the sink's `launch_step`, so a node-scoped shell had to reach all of them or none. A deferred recipe reads its own shell in `expand_with`, when the recipe is read — reading it when the provisional rule is declared is the one thing deferral exists to avoid, and a deferred rule's bindings never look at the shell the declaration carried.\n\nMEASURED, with two shells that write their own `$0` and arguments to a file and then exec `/bin/sh`:\n\n    all: SHELL := ./own                          GNU ./own      Ronin /bin/sh   FIXED\n    SHELL := ./own at the top                    both ./own                     the control\n    global ./global, target ./own                GNU ./own      Ronin ./global  FIXED\n    global ./global, target /bin/sh              GNU the builtin, `one` printed\n                                                 Ronin ./global                 FIXED\n    two target-specific SHELLs, last wins        GNU ./other    Ronin /bin/sh   FIXED\n    %.o: SHELL := ./own                          GNU ./own      Ronin /bin/sh   FIXED\n    all: SHELL := ./own over `dep`, a prerequisite of all\n                                                 GNU ./own for BOTH             FIXED\n    default: all aside / all: SHELL := ./own     GNU ./own for all and its dep,\n                                                 ./global for aside             FIXED\n    all: private SHELL := ./own over `dep`       GNU ./own for all alone        agreed\n    .ONESHELL: beside a target-specific SHELL    GNU ./own, one script          FIXED\n    .SHELLFLAGS := -x -c beside it               GNU ./own -x -c                FIXED\n    export SHELL := ./own                        both ./own                     agreed\n    all: SHELL := ./nosuch                       both rc 2, nothing made        agreed\n    a deferred recipe — $(shell) or $(wildcard)\n      in the line, target-specific SHELL         GNU ./own      Ronin /bin/sh   FIXED\n    a recursive line under a target-specific\n      SHELL, that shell really being a shell     effects agreed; Ronin composes\n                                                 the child rather than handing\n                                                 the line over                  architecture\n\nAN avoid_io LIFT WAS WRITTEN AND THEN TAKEN BACK OUT. Expanding `$(SHELL)` is Make's own errand rather than the recipe's, so it looked as though the compiler's deferral had to be lifted around the read or a `$(shell)`/`$(file)`/`$(info)` in the value would be written into the recipe naming the shell. Parked, it turns out to be inert in every shape reachable: `$(file >picked.txt,yes)./shell` writes the file either way and `$(info)` prints either way, because `file_evaluation` and `output_evaluation` are not in their deferring states here. An unmeasurable change is a hypothesis, so it is not in the diff.\n\nTEETH, parked over the whole corpus of 1,105 gated cases:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the node's shell, so every node reads the generator's one again | 3 |\nand those alone; no pre-existing case moved either way. The third failure is the one that names the descent — `asked-global.txt` holds all three recipes instead of one.\n\nTHREE FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten. Each `SHELL` is a script that writes its own name and arguments to a file and then execs `/bin/sh`, so the recording holds who was asked as well as what happened:\n  a-target-specific-shell-runs-that-targets-recipe — the plain cell.\n  a-target-specific-shell-runs-the-prerequisites-built-for-that-target — the\n    descent AND its boundary: `dep` is built for `all` and gets `all`'s shell,\n    `aside` is built for `default` and keeps the global one. Each shell logs to\n    its own file so the recording does not depend on the scheduler's order.\n  a-pattern-rules-target-specific-shell-runs-its-recipe — the same lookup\n    reached through a pattern-specific assignment.\n\nINVENTORY AUDIT — THREE ROWS MOVED, and they are upstream's own tests for this.\n`misc/general3.diff.11`, `.12` and `.13` go from `unclassified` +\n`ninja-progress+ninja-failure-block+evaluation` to `narration` + `ninja-progress`.\nThey are `tests/scripts/misc/general3`'s \"slow path with odd setups\" block:\n\n    slow: SHELL := echo / slow: .SHELLFLAGS := hoho / slow:; @foo bar   `hoho foo bar`\n    slow: SHELL := echo hi / .SHELLFLAGS := ho ho                       `hi ho ho foo bar`\n    slow: SHELL := echo hi / .SHELLFLAGS := 'ho;ho'                     `hi ho;ho foo bar`\n\nRonin ran `foo bar` under `/bin/sh`, which is not a command, and the build failed.\nIt now prints each of those three lines exactly, and stays `narration` only\nbecause Ronin's progress line is beside it. Totals: 1285 unchanged, unclassified\n100 → 97, narration 1161 → 1164, interface 24 unchanged, compiler stays ZERO.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, in kati on nplan/make-a-target-specific-shell-is-not-the-one-that-runs at 998405c953c9044e086f929006263329d1cf2261, pushed before this pin. Nothing in Ronin: the per-step shell already travelled to `launch_step` and the sink's rule, and was only ever handed the wrong value.\n\nGATES\n  workspace                1044/0/1, unmoved\n  --no-default-features    806/0, unmoved\n  make_port                4/4 over 1,105 gated cases (1,102 plus three), nine divergence markers unmoved\n  conformance              336/51 normalised, 286 raw, class and family tables identical\n  equivalence              297/280/75/0, manifest respellings 8, unchanged\n  upstream inventory       1285 at 97/0/24/1164, compiler zero, THREE rows moved, audited above\n  ninja conformance        425/425, 10 CLI, 86 build outcome, 3 invocation, persistence, log content\n  real projects            vim 9.2 in 17s, zsh 5.9.2 and 36 modules in 32s, both at -j8\n  fmt, check-lints.sh      clean\n\nONE DEFECT FILED, found here and PRE-EXISTING — measured against the release binary built at 13aee39, before this node touched anything:\n  make-a-shell-that-needs-a-shell-to-name-is-a-recursive-variable — `SHELL =\n    $(shell echo ./sh)` is `Recursive variable 'SHELL' references itself\n    (eventually).` in GNU Make and a stack overflow here, rc 134 with a core\n    dump. Ronin's guard catches `A = $(A)` and `SHELL = $(SHELL)`; it does not\n    survive the trip out through a function and back in."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T04:06:35.763237463Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-one-shell-shell-is-one-word
                title "Exec a .ONESHELL recipe's SHELL as the one path GNU Make execs"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T22:40:33.628852872Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-a-one-shell-prefix-survives-a-shell-make-does-not-know, measured on both binaries. Under `.ONESHELL` GNU Make treats the whole `$(SHELL)` value as one program path; Ronin splits it into a program and its arguments, as GNU Make itself does for every recipe that is NOT `.ONESHELL`.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, and it is a real asymmetry in GNU rather than an oversight here. `construct_command_argv_internal`'s slow path (job.c) says \"SHELL may be a multi-word command\" and builds `$(SHELL) $(.SHELLFLAGS) LINE` as text, then recurses to tokenize it — which is what makes `SHELL = /bin/sh -x` work for an ordinary recipe. The `if (one_shell)` branch above it does not: it writes `new_argv[0] = xstrdup (shell)` and execs that, so a value with a space in it is a path with a space in it.\n\nMEASURED, `.ONESHELL:` with `all:` over `echo one` / `@echo two`:\n\n  SHELL = ./dumper -x     GNU   `make: ./dumper -x: No such file or directory`,\n                                `*** [Makefile:4: all] Error 127`, rc 2\n                          Ronin runs `./dumper` with `-x -c <script>`, rc 0\n  SHELL = ./bsh/sh -x     the same refusal from GNU, the same success here\n  without `.ONESHELL:`    both split the value and both run it — agreed\n\nSo the divergence is exactly the `.ONESHELL` cell, and it is a build effect: GNU makes nothing and exits 2, Ronin builds.\n\nWHERE. Wherever the shell value becomes a process — `simple_command::shell_process` and the sink's rule — the split has to be declined under `.ONESHELL`. Very fringe by construction: it needs `.ONESHELL` and a `SHELL` of more than one word, which is a combination GNU Make cannot run at all, so nothing that works today depends on it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T02:30:53.000323369Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T03:03:50.031331899Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Landed. The filing had the mechanism exactly right and called the case \"very fringe by construction\". It is not: matching GNU Make's exec meant matching how the same branch reads `.SHELLFLAGS`, and upstream's own suite has two tests standing on that. Doing only the half the filing named REGRESSED them, and the inventory is what said so.\n\nTHE FIRST HALF, which is the filing's. `construct_command_argv_internal`'s slow path (job.c) says \"SHELL may be a multi-word command\", builds `$(SHELL) $(.SHELLFLAGS) LINE` as text and recurses to tokenise it. The `if (one_shell)` branch above it writes `new_argv[0] = xstrdup (shell)` and execs that. So under `.ONESHELL` the whole value is a path, and a path with a space in it is not there.\n\nTHE SECOND HALF, which nothing named until the gate did. Twenty lines below that exec, the same branch parses `.SHELLFLAGS` with `construct_command_argv_internal` itself — the comment says \"Parse shellflags using construct_command_argv_internal to handle quotes\" — so `-E 'use warnings FATAL => \"all\";'` is TWO words, not five, and the quotes come off. Ronin's launch split the flags on whitespace, which is right for every launch that was going to put them on a command line and wrong for this one. Splitting the shell without also splitting the flags GNU's way took upstream's `targets/ONESHELL.diff.7` and `.10` from `narration` to `unclassified` + `ninja-failure-block`: two perl cases that had been passing began to fail. Both are back.\n\nMEASURED, on both binaries:\n\n  .ONESHELL:, `SHELL = ./shell -x`, two recipe lines\n    GNU   `make: ./shell -x: No such file or directory`, Error 127, rc 2,\n          nothing made\n    Ronin ran the recipe, rc 0                                          FIXED\n  the same without `.ONESHELL:`      both split it and both run          agreed\n  .ONESHELL:, `SHELL = /bin/sh -e`   GNU refuses 127; Ronin ran it       FIXED\n  .ONESHELL:, target-specific `SHELL = ./shell -x`\n                                     GNU refuses 127; Ronin ran it       FIXED\n                                     (reachable at all only since the\n                                     target-specific shell landed)\n  .ONESHELL:, `SHELL = ./shell`, `.SHELLFLAGS = -x -c`\n                                     both run it — where the flags live  agreed\n  .ONESHELL:, `SHELL = './shell   '` both refuse against the same name   agreed\n  .ONESHELL:, `SHELL =` (empty)      both rc 2, nothing made; the\n                                     diagnostics word it differently     agreed\n  a 399-line `.ONESHELL:` recipe, multi-word SHELL\n                                     both refuse, no log written         FIXED\n  the same recipe under `/bin/sh`    both write 399 lines                agreed\n  .SHELLFLAGS = -w -E 'one two' -E   GNU passes 4 words; Ronin passed 5  FIXED\n  .SHELLFLAGS = -c 'a b'             both pass `-c` and `a b`, rc 127    FIXED\n\nONE SHAPE HAS NO CONTRACT TO MATCH. `.ONESHELL:` with `.SHELLFLAGS = set` — a lone word the tokeniser hands back to a shell — kills GNU Make 4.4.1 outright: `malloc(): unaligned tcache chunk detected`, rc 134. It sizes `new_argv` from `sflags_len` before it knows the parse failed. Ronin drops the flags, which is what `if (argv)` says to do; recorded here rather than gated, because an oracle that dies is not an answer.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, in kati on nplan/make-a-one-shell-shell-is-one-word at edc067e27d115e1c03ee23df449f70d3919a225c, pushed before this pin: `RecipeStep::shell_is_a_path`, which says which of GNU Make's two branches a step came from and is `.ONESHELL` and nothing else; and `shell_flag_argv`, with `direct_argv`'s tokeniser lifted out of its four gates as `tokenize` so the flags can go through it without being asked whether they are a command line. In Ronin, `Layout::launch_step` names the shell as the program for such a step — the same launch the default shell already got, asked after it so `/bin/sh` under `.ONESHELL` still gets the builtin substitution.\n\nTEETH, each parked over the whole corpus of 1,109 gated cases:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the one-path exec | 1 |\n| the quote-aware flag split | 1 |\nand those alone; no pre-existing case moved either way. The upstream inventory is the second measurement of the same thing and is sharper: parking the flag split alone puts two upstream rows back into `unclassified`.\n\nFOUR FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten:\n  a-one-shell-shell-of-more-than-one-word-is-a-path-of-more-than-one-word —\n    the case itself: `outcome failure`, and neither file made.\n  a-one-shell-shell-of-one-word-takes-its-flags-from-shellflags — the control\n    that holds the boundary in place: a splitting launch passes this too, and\n    a launch that stopped splitting without keeping this working would not.\n  a-shell-of-more-than-one-word-is-split-without-one-shell — the other side of\n    GNU Make's asymmetry, differing from the first in the `.ONESHELL:` line\n    and nothing else.\n  a-one-shell-reads-its-flags-with-the-shells-own-quoting — the shell writes\n    each argument on a line of its own, so the recording holds the split.\n\nONE UNIT TEST in kati over `shell_flag_argv`: the plain flags, the quoted one\nwith blanks, a `;`, a `>` and a pair of double quotes in it, the empty value,\nan unterminated quote, and the lone builtin word.\n\nGATES\n  workspace                1045/0/1  (1044 plus the unit test)\n  --no-default-features    807/0     (806 plus one)\n  make_port                4/4 over 1,109 gated cases (1,105 plus four), nine divergence markers unmoved\n  conformance              336/51 normalised, 286 raw, class and family tables identical\n  equivalence              297/280/75/0, manifest respellings 8, unchanged\n  upstream inventory       1285 at 97/0/24/1164, compiler zero, TSV byte-identical to\n                           the target-specific-shell landing — no row moved\n  ninja conformance        425/425, 10 CLI, 86 build outcome, 3 invocation, persistence, log content\n  real projects            vim 9.2 in 15s, zsh 5.9.2 and 36 modules in 28s, both at -j8\n  fmt, check-lints.sh      clean"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T03:06:27.313017913Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "scripts/check-release.sh exit 0 end to end, performance stage included, on the first attempt with the host at a one-minute load average of 2.6.\n\nRecorded beside it because the two landings before this one had to re-measure: a foreign silo suite held the host at a 15-minute load average of 14 to 16 for about half an hour, and the performance stage refused four runs in a row on a DIFFERENT workload each time — path-canonicalization at 0.82x, then clean-tree-noop at 0.83x, then wide-noop-build at 1.14x, then dependency-log-load at 0.88x — before passing on the fifth. A refusal that moves between workloads is the load and not the build, which is the standing note; this run on a quiet host is what closes it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T04:06:43.076186233Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
