            }

            {
                id make-a-what-if-double-colon-refusal-reads-the-implicit-search
                title "Refuse over a --what-if double-colon name only where no implicit rule can make it"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-what-if-file-that-is-double-colon-refuses)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-22T23:52:24.538017142Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-a-switch-names-a-target-gpath-renamed, measured on both binaries. make-a-what-if-file-that-is-double-colon-refuses landed a refusal that fires in two places GNU Make does not, and a false failure is worse than a missing one, so this is a regression of that landing rather than a gap beside it.\n\nTHE FIRST, and the one that matters: the phantom entry is offered the implicit rule search like any other file with no recipe. `update_file_1` (remake.c) runs `try_implicit_rule (file, depth)` for a file that is not phony, has no `cmds` and has not been tried; the entry `-W` appended to the `::` chain is exactly that. If the search finds a rule, the entry has one, `file->deps` is no longer empty — so the `double_colon && deps == 0` clause that made it always out of date does not fire either — and there is nothing to complain about.\n\n    all: app / app: out / out:: a / out:: b, app newer than out\n\n      out.c present, -W out         GNU `Nothing to be done for 'all'`, rc 0\n                                    the built-in `%: %.c` matched the phantom\n      out.c absent, -W out          GNU `No rule to make target 'out'`, rc 2\n      out.c present, -r -W out      GNU refuses again — no built-in rules to find\n      out.o named instead, out.c present, -W out.o   GNU rc 0, `%.o: %.c` matched\n      VPATH = src, src/out.c and src/out.o, -W out.o  GNU rc 0 — the search reaches\n                                    a prerequisite through the search path too\n\n    Ronin refuses all five.\n\nTHE SECOND: the name is tested as the command line wrote it, before anything is renamed. `main` stamps the switches after the read and before the update, so `enter_file` sees the file database as the makefiles left it — a `GPATH` rename has not happened yet. A `-W` name spelt as the path the search will find is therefore not a double-colon target at all: it enters an ordinary fresh entry, and the rename then merges it under the kludge at file.c:313 (`if (from_file->last_mtime > to_file->last_mtime)`, commented \"%%% Kludge so -W wins on a file that gets vpathized\").\n\n    VPATH = src / GPATH = src, `out:: a` / `out:: b`, src/out found\n\n      -W src/out    GNU builds app, rc 0 — the stamp wins the merge\n      Ronin refuses, because the check maps the name through `written_as` before\n      asking whether a `::` record declares it\n\nWHERE. `DepBuilder::refuse_an_asserted_double_colon` (kati/src-rs/dep.rs). The `::` test must read the name as given rather than through `written_as`, and the refusal must ask the implicit rule search — GNU's steps 5 and 6, which `pick_rule` already runs for a name with no explicit rule and short-circuits past for one with a primary rule. Reaching it for a name that HAS rules means running the search against a scratch node, after the plan is settled, and putting back whatever the search remembered on the way through (`intermediates`, `tried_implicit`) so a probe that answers \"yes\" leaves nothing behind.\n\nThe third half of the same story — the refusal that is MISSED because `done` is keyed by the path a `GPATH` rename moved the target to — belongs to make-a-switch-names-a-target-gpath-renamed, which owns every other way a switch's name has to reach a renamed target."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T00:17:02.578353554Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T00:17:36.182781144Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing had both halves right. The refusal make-a-what-if-file-that-is-double-colon-refuses landed fired in two places GNU Make does not, and both are now measured cells rather than a hypothesis.\n\nTHE FIRST HALF, and it is the whole of GNU's condition. `update_file_1` (remake.c) offers `try_implicit_rule` to any file that is not phony, has no `cmds` and has not been tried, and the entry `-W` appends to a `::` chain is exactly one of those. A rule found there is a rule the entry has; its prerequisites become the entry's `deps`, so the `file->double_colon && file->deps == 0` clause that would have made it unconditionally out of date does not fire either; and `remake_file` never reaches `complain()`. The refusal is therefore not \"the name is a `::` target\" but \"nothing at all can make the entry\".\n\n  MEASURED — `all: app` / `app: out` / `out:: a` / `out:: b`, `-W out`\n    out.c present            `Nothing to be done for 'all'`, rc 0 — `%: %.c` matched  FIXED\n    out.c absent             `No rule to make target 'out'`, rc 2                     agreed\n    out.c present, -r        refused again — no built-in rules to find                FIXED\n    named out.o, out.c present   rc 0, `%.o: %.c` matched                             FIXED\n    VPATH = src, src/out.c   rc 0 — the search reaches a prerequisite down the\n        search path too                                                               FIXED\n  Ronin refused all five.\n\nTHE SECOND HALF: the name is tested as the command line wrote it. `main` stamps the switches after the read and before the update, so `enter_file` reads the database the makefiles left and nothing has been renamed. A `-W` name spelt as the path a search will find is not a `::` target at all — it enters an ordinary fresh entry, and the rename then merges the two under file.c:313, `%%% Kludge so -W wins on a file that gets vpathized`.\n\n  MEASURED — `VPATH = src` / `GPATH = src`, `out:: a` / `out:: b`, src/out found\n    -W src/out               GNU builds app, rc 0 — the stamp wins the merge          FIXED\n    -W out                   refused — the written name is the `::` one               agreed\n\nWHAT CHANGED, in kati on nplan/make-a-what-if-double-colon-refusal-reads-the-implicit-search at a010eaccb83bfe3fb39a1188352da96ff19f9a4c, pushed before this pin. `implicit_rule_for` is `try_implicit_rule` lifted out of `pick_rule` — the two `SearchPass` loops and the archive-member retry, unchanged — so the search can be asked of a name whose own record `pick_rule` short-circuits past. `an_implicit_rule_could_make` puts the question to a scratch node standing in for the appended entry, once the goals are planned, and puts `intermediates` and `tried_implicit` back afterwards so a probe that answers yes leaves the plan as it found it. Anything the search reaches was reachable already and nothing it plans is joined to a root, so nothing it leaves in `done` is emitted.\n\nTEETH, each parked and the whole corpus of 1,095 gated cases run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the implicit search, so every reached `::` name refuses | 1 |\n| the name read as written rather than as given | 1 |\nand those alone; no pre-existing case moved either way. The `-r` control stays green under both parks, which is what says the refusal itself is still there.\n\nTHREE FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten:\n  a-what-if-file-a-double-colon-declares-that-an-implicit-rule-can-make — out.c beside\n    the `::` target, the run is not refused.\n  a-what-if-file-a-double-colon-declares-with-no-built-in-rules-refuses — the same tree\n    under `-r`, refused. The control that holds the refusal in place.\n  a-what-if-file-spelt-as-the-search-will-find-it-is-not-a-double-colon-name — `GPATH`,\n    `-W src/out`, not refused and app remade.\n\nGATES\n  workspace                1044/0/1, unmoved\n  --no-default-features    806/0, unmoved\n  make_port                4/4 over 1,095 gated cases (1,092 plus three), ten divergence markers unmoved\n  conformance              336/51 normalised, 286 raw, class and family tables identical\n  equivalence              297/280/75/0, manifest respellings 8, unchanged\n  upstream inventory       exit 0, TSV byte-identical, no row moved\n  ninja conformance        425/425 plus the differentials\n  real projects            vim 9.2 in 17s, zsh 5.9.2 and 36 modules in 34s, both at -j8\n  scripts/check-release.sh exit 0 end to end, performance stage included, on a host at a\n                           15-minute load average of 21\n  fmt, check-lints.sh      clean\n\nTWO DEFECTS FILED, both found here and neither this node's:\n  make-a-what-if-date-a-double-colon-name-carries-reaches-nothing — with the refusal no\n    longer over-firing, the cell where the stamp's own effect is visible is measurable,\n    and it diverges: `-W` over a `::` target is inert in GNU Make, because the date lands\n    on the appended entry and everything reads the chain's own. `-o` already declines it;\n    `-W` does not.\n  make-a-double-colon-action-searches-for-its-own-prerequisites — with no switch at all,\n    a `::` rule's prerequisite is not looked for down `VPATH`, and a build GNU Make finds\n    nothing to do for is refused outright."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T00:18:24.369970266Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-what-if-date-a-double-colon-name-carries-reaches-nothing
                title "Leave a --what-if date a double-colon record's name carries unread"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-what-if-double-colon-refusal-reads-the-implicit-search)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T00:15:53.056002104Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-a-what-if-double-colon-refusal-reads-the-implicit-search, measured on both binaries. It is `-o`'s answer with the sign turned round, and the half that was hidden until the refusal stopped over-firing.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, and make-an-old-file-is-not-remade already wrote it down for the other switch: `enter_file` (file.c:177) hands a switch that stamps a `::` name a FRESH `struct file` appended to the chain, so the date lands on a file object nothing consults. Everything that reads the target reads the chain's own entries and their own dates. `-o` declines the stamp for exactly this reason — `AssertedDates::mark_on` drops a name `Graph::is_written_undeclared` answers for — and `-W` does not, because until the refusal existed declining it would have turned a refusal into a quiet success.\n\nThe refusal exists now, and it does not fire when the implicit rule search finds the appended entry a rule. That is the cell where the stamp's effect is visible, and it diverges:\n\n    all: app / app: out / out:: a / out:: b, out.c lying beside them,\n    app NEWER than out so nothing is out of date\n\n      no switch     GNU `Nothing to be done for 'all'`   Ronin the same\n      -W out        GNU `Nothing to be done for 'all'`   Ronin REMAKES app\n      -W out.o with the target named out.o, likewise\n\n    and with app OLDER than out both remake it, which is why the corpus is green.\n\nWHERE. `AssertedDates::mark_on` (src/runtime/assumed.rs): the `-W` list needs the same `is_written_undeclared` gate the `-o` list has, and the comment that says why `-W` is withheld from it has to come out.\n\nTHE HALF THAT NEEDS MORE THAN THAT. `is_written_undeclared` is a shape rather than a fact: it holds for a `::` record of more than one rule, which compiles to an action per rule plus a completion join, and NOT for a lone `::` record, which keeps the single-node shape an ordinary rule has. GNU Make appends the fresh entry to a chain of one as readily as to a chain of three, so the lone record's stamp is inert there too and would still be honoured here. Closing that half means the graph being told which of its nodes a `::` record declares — a `SinkEdge` field beside `searched_at`, a node property, and a reader — which is the same missing fact make-a-what-if-file-that-is-double-colon-refuses had to go to the read to get around."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T03:17:52.032398408Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T03:43:32.443999064Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Landed, and the filing was right on both halves — including the half it warned would need more than a one-line gate. It needed more than that too, and the sealed sibling's own fixture is what said so.\n\nTHE FILING'S CELL, MEASURED AGAIN ON BOTH BINARIES. `all: app` / `app: out` / `out:: a` / `out:: b`, `out.c` lying beside so the implicit rule search finds the appended entry a rule and the refusal does not fire, `app` NEWER than `out` so nothing is out of date:\n\n    no switch     GNU `Nothing to be done for 'all'`   Ronin the same       agreed\n    -W out        GNU `Nothing to be done for 'all'`   Ronin REMADE app     FIXED\n    -o out        GNU `Nothing to be done for 'all'`   Ronin the same       agreed\n\nTHE HALF THE FILING WARNED ABOUT, AND IT IS BIGGER THAN THE HALF IT WAS FILED FOR. `is_written_undeclared` is a shape and not a fact: it holds for a `::` record of more than one rule, which compiles to an action per rule plus a completion join, and not for a lone record, which keeps the single-node shape an ordinary rule has. Measured, the lone shape diverges on BOTH switches, so the `-o` gate the sealed node left behind was as incomplete as the missing `-W` one:\n\n    all: app / app: out / out:: a, out.c beside, one `::` record only\n      -W out, app newer than out    GNU nothing to do   Ronin REMADE app   FIXED\n      -o out, out newer than app    GNU remakes app     Ronin DID NOT      FIXED\n    the same tree with `out: a`, an ordinary rule — the control\n      -W out                        both remake app                        agreed\n      -o out                        both find nothing to do                agreed\n\nSo the gate is one fact and both switches read it, which is what GNU Make does: `enter_file` asks whether the entry is a double-colon target and asks nothing else.\n\nTHE THIRD THING, AND THE SEALED SIBLING CAUGHT IT. Declining on the NODE regressed `a-what-if-file-spelt-as-the-search-will-find-it-is-not-a-double-colon-name`, which that node recorded: with `GPATH = src`, `-W src/out` is not a `::` name at all — `main` stamps the switches after the read and before the update, so the rename has not happened and `src/out` enters an ordinary fresh entry, which the rename then merges under file.c:313's `%%% Kludge so -W wins on a file that gets vpathized`. Both spellings reach the same node here. So what is carried is the NAME the record was filed under, not a flag, and the decline is a question about the name.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, in kati on nplan/make-a-what-if-date-a-double-colon-name-carries-reaches-nothing at 65133d47ff69498a024875e08b7943e31edb5ce6, pushed before this pin: `DepNode::declared_by_double_colon` and the `SinkEdge` field beside `searched_at`, both `Option<Symbol>` holding the written name, set in `build_plan` from `merger.is_double_colon` before the grouped-double dispatch so the join and the lone node take it alike. In Ronin: a `double_colon_targets` table beside `searched_at` and `written_as`, `Graph::declares_a_double_colon_record(node, spelt)`, and `names_in` (src/runtime/assumed.rs) deciding it where the name's provenance is known. `AssertedDates::mark_on` now filters both lists identically and says why in one place instead of two. `Graph::is_written_undeclared` had one reader and is gone.\n\nTEETH, each parked over the whole corpus of 1,113 gated cases:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the decline, so both switches stamp a `::` name again | 4 |\n| the spelling test, declining on the node whatever the name | 1 |\nThe four are the three fixtures below plus `an-old-file-a-double-colon-declares-is-left-alone`, which the sealed node recorded and which the replaced predicate used to hold — proof the new fact covers what the old shape did. The one is the sealed sibling's `GPATH` case.\n\nFOUR FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten:\n  a-what-if-date-a-double-colon-name-carries-reaches-nothing — the filing's own\n    cell, over a chain of two.\n  a-what-if-date-a-lone-double-colon-name-carries-reaches-nothing — the same\n    over a chain of one, which is the shape the graph cannot recognise.\n  an-old-file-date-a-lone-double-colon-name-carries-reaches-nothing — `-o` over\n    that chain of one, with the target NEWER than its dependent so the stamp has\n    something to lose; `app` is remade either way.\n  a-what-if-date-an-ordinary-name-carries-reaches-its-target — the control: the\n    same tree with a single-colon rule, where `-W` still reaches.\n\nA FIFTH WAS WRITTEN AND DROPPED. `-o src/out` under `GPATH` looked like the boundary fixture for the spelling test, and it is not: `-o`'s date is older than any real file and loses `rehash_file`'s merge, so the switch is inert there for a reason that has nothing to do with `::`. The sealed sibling's `-W src/out` fixture is the one that gates this, and it does — see the park above.\n\nGATES\n  workspace                1045/0/1, unmoved\n  --no-default-features    807/0, unmoved\n  make_port                4/4 over 1,113 gated cases (1,109 plus four), nine divergence markers unmoved\n  conformance              336/51 normalised, 286 raw, class and family tables identical\n  equivalence              297/280/75/0, manifest respellings 8, unchanged\n  upstream inventory       1285 at 97/0/24/1164, compiler zero, TSV byte-identical, no row moved\n  ninja conformance        425/425, 10 CLI, 86 build outcome, 3 invocation, persistence, log content\n  real projects            vim 9.2 in 19s, zsh 5.9.2 and 36 modules in 33s, both at -j8\n  fmt, check-lints.sh      clean"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T03:59:05.558501222Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "scripts/check-release.sh exit 0 end to end, performance stage included, on the first attempt at a one-minute load average of 12.9.\n\nIts first run refused, and not for load: the bookkeeping added to `declare_edge` put that function over the density gate at 103 lines against 100. The gate battery beside it had shown the same refusal and it was misread — the tail of `check-lints.sh` looked like a pass and was not, which is the reason to read a gate's exit status rather than its last two lines. Fixed rather than bypassed: the record's name is said inside `record_searched_spellings`, which is where the other name a switch has to reach is already said and which already holds the edge and the node."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T04:06:44.630218291Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-double-colon-action-searches-for-its-own-prerequisites
                title "Find a double-colon action's prerequisites down the search path"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T00:16:16.646711649Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-a-what-if-double-colon-refusal-reads-the-implicit-search, measured on both binaries with no switch of any kind. A prerequisite of a double-colon rule is not looked for down `VPATH`, so a build GNU Make finds nothing to do for is refused outright.\n\nMEASURED:\n\n    VPATH = src\n    all: app\n    app: out.o\n    \\t@echo made > $@\n    out.o:: out.c\n    \\t@echo remade > $@\n    out.o:: dep\n    \\t@echo again >> $@\n\n  with src/out.c and src/out.o present, dep present, app newer than everything\n\n    GNU Make 4.4.1   `make: Nothing to be done for 'all'.`, rc 0\n    Ronin            `ronin: 'out.c', needed by '.ronin_grouped_double/0',\n                      missing and no known rule to make it`, rc 2\n\nThe name in the diagnostic is the compiler's own for the action a `::` record\nbecomes, which is the shape that gives it away: the prerequisite reached the\ngraph as `out.c` where an ordinary rule's would have reached it as `src/out.c`.\n`out.o:: out.c` alone, with no second `::` rule, is worth measuring first — a\nlone record keeps the single-node shape and may well take the ordinary path.\n\nWHERE. `expand_deps`/`at_search_path` rewrites a planned node's prerequisites to\nwhere the search found them; the double-colon actions are built in\n`build_double_action` and `build_grouped_double_member` (kati/src-rs/dep.rs) and\nfill `actual_inputs` from the rule directly. Whatever the ordinary path does to a\nprerequisite has to reach these too."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T00:55:30.944920625Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T01:29:13.155734923Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Landed, and the filing named one of the two halves. The prerequisite search was the half it measured; the target search was the half that kept the same Makefile diverging after the first was fixed, and it is in the other repository.\n\nTHE FILING'S HYPOTHESIS WAS RIGHT ABOUT THE SHAPE. `out.o:: out.c` alone takes the ordinary single-node path and always did — `$<` reads `src/out.c` there — so nothing about a lone record needed touching. Only a record of more than one rule compiles to the actions-plus-join shape, and only that shape diverged.\n\nHALF ONE — the actions' own prerequisites. `build_double_action` (kati/src-rs/dep.rs) resolved each action's prerequisites with a bare `build_plan`, where every ordinary rule goes through `at_settled_name`: the function that replaces a name nothing can remake with the path the directory search found it at, and writes that path back into `actual_inputs` so `$<` and `$^` are handed what the recipe has to open. `resolve_vpaths` ran on the action already and did its half — it records `searched_at` — but without `at_settled_name` nothing ever read the record, so the prerequisite reached the graph as `out.c` and the build was refused for a file that was under `src` all along. Both lists now go through it, ordinary and order-only alike, with the same `position < visible` write-back the ordinary loop uses so `.EXTRA_PREREQS` still joins the graph without entering `$^`.\n\nHALF TWO, which the filing did not have because the refusal hid it. With the actions searching, the same Makefile stopped being refused and started REBUILDING everything GNU Make found nothing to do for. `record_searched_spellings` (src/make/sink.rs) said the second place to look of `outputs.first()`, and a completion join's first output is `.ronin_grouped_join/N` — a name the compiler invented to sequence the chain. So `searched_at` hung off the proxy, `elsewhere_mtime` was asked about `out.o` and missed, and every action's deferred freshness read a target that was not there. Said of `completion_output` instead, which is the target the Makefile wrote and the node every action in the chain defers its freshness to.\n\nMEASURED, on both binaries, `VPATH = src` throughout:\n\n    lone `::` record, prerequisite under src        both: nothing to do        agreed before\n    lone record, stale                              both: `$<` = src/out.c     agreed before\n    two records, all current                        GNU nothing to do          Ronin REFUSED   FIXED\n    two records, target absent                      both remake, `$@` = out.o  FIXED\n    two records, target under src, both stale       both remake here           FIXED\n    three records, three different prerequisites,\n      two under src and one here                    each action's own search   FIXED\n    order-only prerequisite under src               `$|` = src/oo.h            FIXED\n    one prerequisite found, one nowhere             GNU refuses `nowhere`      Ronin named\n                                                                               `out.c`         FIXED\n    GPATH = src beside it                           agreed throughout          unmoved\n    the same shape with a single-colon rule         agreed throughout          the control\n\nTEETH, each parked and the whole corpus of 1,102 gated cases run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| `at_settled_name` in the action, so no prerequisite is searched for | 3 |\n| the join's real target, so the chain's own second place to look is the proxy's | 1 |\nand those alone; no pre-existing case moved either way.\n\nTHREE FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten:\n  a-double-colon-actions-prerequisite-is-found-down-the-search-path — the filing's\n    own Makefile: both prerequisites and the target under `src`, app newer, and\n    the run has nothing to do. It is the case that needs both halves.\n  each-double-colon-action-reads-its-own-prerequisite-where-the-search-found-it —\n    three records, three searches, one of them answering \"here\"; the log holds\n    what each action's `$<` and `$^` were.\n  a-double-colon-actions-order-only-prerequisite-is-found-down-the-search-path —\n    the order-only list going through the same search, read back through `$|`.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, in kati on nplan/make-a-double-colon-action-searches-for-its-own-prerequisites at 9e19b797196ebe74c0c49d95451cdedc74869553, pushed before this pin; and in Ronin, `Sink::searched_target`.\n\nGATES\n  workspace                1044/0/1, unmoved\n  --no-default-features    806/0, unmoved\n  make_port                4/4 over 1,102 gated cases (1,099 plus three), nine divergence markers unmoved\n  conformance              336/51 normalised, 286 raw, class and family tables identical\n  equivalence              297/280/75/0, manifest respellings 8, unchanged\n  upstream inventory       1285 at 100/0/24/1161, compiler zero, TSV byte-identical, no row moved\n  ninja conformance        425/425, 10 CLI, 86 build outcome, 3 invocation, persistence, log content\n  real projects            vim 9.2 in 17s, zsh 5.9.2 and 36 modules in 29s, both at -j8\n  fmt, check-lints.sh      clean — the fix put `declare_edge` over the density gate, and\n                           the searched-target choice came out as a function of its own\n\nONE DEFECT FILED, found here and not this node's:\n  make-a-double-colon-entry-remakes-the-target-where-an-earlier-one-left-it — GNU\n    Make commits the `VPATH` rename as soon as an entry of the chain is found\n    current, so a later out-of-date entry remakes the FOUND path; Ronin remakes it\n    here. Measured both ways round: with the first entry stale the two agree."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T04:06:34.150695462Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-double-colon-entry-remakes-the-target-where-an-earlier-one-left-it
                title "Remake a double-colon target at the path an earlier current entry settled it to"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-double-colon-action-searches-for-its-own-prerequisites)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T01:31:32.625731511Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-a-double-colon-action-searches-for-its-own-prerequisites, measured on both binaries with no switch of any kind. It is the third cell of the same family and was only reachable once the other two were fixed: with the search now reaching a `::` chain's target, WHERE an out-of-date entry writes it is the question left.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. GNU Make's `f_mtime` (file.c) finds a target under `VPATH`, keeps the found path as `hname` beside the written `name`, and `update_file_1` (remake.c) chooses between them once it knows whether the entry must be remade: an entry it finds current takes the found path and everything reads it from there. A `::` chain is one name and many `struct file` entries, walked in order, so an entry found CURRENT commits that choice for the name — and a later entry that does have to run then remakes the file where the earlier one left it.\n\nMEASURED, `VPATH = src`, `src/out.o` present, two `::` records:\n\n    out.o:: aa / out.o:: bb, one recipe each\n\n      aa newer than src/out.o, bb older\n        GNU    entry one runs, appends to SRC/OUT.O; ./out.o is never made\n        Ronin  entry one runs, writes ./out.o                        DIVERGES\n\n      aa older than src/out.o, bb newer  (the first entry is the current one)\n        GNU    entry two runs, appends to SRC/OUT.O\n        Ronin  entry two runs, writes ./out.o                        DIVERGES\n\n      aa newer, bb newer  (no entry is current)\n        both   remake ./out.o                                        agreed\n\n      no VPATH at all, any staleness                                 agreed\n\nSo the rule is the earlier entry's answer, not the chain's: a chain none of whose entries is current keeps the written name, which is why every existing case is green and why the corpus does not see this.\n\nWHERE, and it is two things rather than one. `settle_searched_outputs` (src/graph/searched.rs) is called per edge and the last caller wins, so a clean action settling `Found` is overwritten by the dirty action beside it — for a `::` chain the two actions share the target and the order they are scanned in decides the answer. And the recipe's own `$@` would have to be the settled spelling rather than the written one, which is the `SettledNames` machinery `$<` and `$?` already go through and `$@` does not.\n\nNarrow by construction: it needs `VPATH`, a `::` record of more than one rule, a target found down the search path, and one entry current while another is stale. Nothing in the corpus, the upstream suite or either real project stands on it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T04:54:51.031846807Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T05:50:31.939636096Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Landed, and the filing was right about the mechanism, right about where the settlement goes, and WRONG about one of the two cells it offered as evidence. The correction is what makes the rule sayable, so it comes first.\n\nTHE FILING'S FIRST CELL DOES NOT DIVERGE. It says that with `aa` newer than `src/out.o` and `bb` older — entry one stale, entry two current — GNU appends to `src/out.o` and Ronin writes `./out.o`. Measured on both binaries, both write `./out.o`, and they did before this node touched anything. The filing had the rule as \"an entry found current commits the rename for the chain\", and it is not: it commits it for ITSELF AND EVERY ENTRY AFTER IT. An entry reached BEFORE any of them was current writes the name as written, and nothing later takes that back.\n\nGNU MAKE'S OWN WORDS, which say exactly that and are worth quoting because the direction is the whole of it. `update_file_1` (remake.c:885) ends an entry it does not have to remake with\n\n    while (file) { file->name = file->hname; file = file->prev; }\n\nand `update_file` walks the chain as `for (f = file->double_colon; f != 0; f = f->prev)`, so `prev` chains FORWARD. The loop renames this entry and the ones not yet reached. It is a latch, opened once and never closed.\n\nTHE CELL THAT PROVES IT, and it is sharper than anything the filing had — three entries, stale/current/stale:\n\n    out.o:: first   (stale)   GNU runs it at `out.o`\n    out.o:: keep    (current) GNU runs nothing, and commits the rename here\n    out.o:: last    (stale)   GNU runs it at `src/out.o`\n\nTwo entries of one chain writing two different files, in one run. Any rule stated per-chain rather than per-entry gets this wrong.\n\nWHERE, and it is four things rather than the filing's two.\n\nONE — THE LATCH. `settle_searched_outputs` (src/graph/searched.rs) was called per edge with the last caller winning, so a clean action settling `Found` was overwritten by the dirty action beside it. Traced: the actions are rescanned one at a time as the chain runs, entry two settles `Found`, entry three settles `Unsettled` over it, and entry three then writes the name as written. `Found` now stands against a later `Unsettled` — but only for a `::` target, and only for a target reached through an edge's DEFERRED outputs, which is the only name whose freshness more than one edge decides. Every other deferred output has one edge behind it, where a later scan is a better answer to the same question rather than a different entry's answer to a different one.\n\nTWO — `$@`. The filing named this: the recipe's own target has to be the settled spelling, through the `SettledNames` machinery `$<` and `$?` already use. A `::` action now leaves a reference for `$@` where the search answered about its target, and `$(@D)` and `$(@F)` come with it because the reference already stands for the form that asked. Said only of a `::` action of the actions-plus-join shape: a lone `::` record takes the ordinary single-node path, where the one recipe runs before anything could have settled the name, and an ordinary target that is remade always keeps the name as written. `DepNode::settled_target` is the new field, set in `build_double_action` and nowhere else.\n\nTHREE — WHICH NODE A REFERENCE STANDS FOR, which the filing did not have and which made the first attempt worse rather than better. `declare_edge` (src/make/sink.rs) redirects every use of a `::` target onto the completion proxy so dependents wait for the whole chain, and it does that by rebinding the symbol in `interned`. So the `$@` reference resolved to `.ronin_grouped_join/0` — a name the compiler invented, written into the recipe. Settled names are now resolved through `observed_node`, the same reading `observed_node_list` already did for inputs: the file the name stands for, not the proxy that sequences the work behind it.\n\nFOUR — THE DEPENDENT AND THE TOUCH, neither in the filing. `all: out.o` reads `$<` off the settled name too, and the map `late_binding` (src/build/command.rs) builds for a deferred recipe was asking the proxy, which no search ever answered about. `Graph::observed_file` is the accessor for that question and this is its first caller. And `-t` is the same cell with the touch in place of the recipe: GNU renames the file before `touch_file` (remake.c) sees it, so the switch brings the FOUND path up to date; `touch_outputs` (src/build/touch.rs) was touching the written name and making a second file beside the one it was standing in for.\n\nMEASURED, on both binaries, `VPATH = src`, `src/out.o` present. Thirteen cells, all agreeing after and five diverging before:\n\n    three entries, current/stale/stale       GNU src/out.o for both runners  FIXED\n    three entries, stale/current/stale       GNU out.o then src/out.o        FIXED\n    three entries, stale/stale/current       both write out.o                agreed before\n    three entries, current/current/stale     GNU src/out.o                   FIXED\n    three entries, current/stale/current     GNU src/out.o                   FIXED\n    three entries, all stale                 both write out.o                agreed before\n    three entries, all current               both do nothing                 agreed before\n    two entries, first current               GNU src/out.o                   FIXED\n    two entries, first stale                 both write out.o                agreed before\n    two entries, both stale / both current   agreed                          the controls\n    GPATH = src beside it, either way round  agreed throughout               the control\n    `$(@D)` and `$(@F)` in a late entry      GNU src and out.o               FIXED\n    `$?` and `$^` in a late entry            agreed                          unmoved\n    the dependent's `$<` and `$^`            GNU src/out.o                   FIXED\n    `-t` over the latched chain              GNU touches src/out.o           FIXED\n    `-n`, `-q`, `-B`, `-j4`                  agreed throughout               unmoved\n    an order-only prerequisite in a late entry                               agreed\n    a `.PHONY` chain                         both write out.o                agreed\n\nTEETH, each change parked on its own and the whole corpus of 1,119 gated cases run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the latch in `settle_searched_outputs` | 4 |\n| the `$@` reference in kati | 3 |\n| settled names through `observed_node` | 3, each by REFUSING the build |\n| `observed_file` in `late_binding` | 1 |\n| the touch following the settled name | 1 |\nand those alone; no pre-existing case moved either way in any of the five.\n\nFOUR FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten:\n  a-double-colon-entry-remakes-the-target-where-an-earlier-one-left-it — the\n    plain cell, and the node's name.\n  a-double-colon-entry-before-a-current-one-remakes-the-name-as-written — the\n    three-entry cell above, whose recording holds `out.o` with one line and\n    `src/out.o` with another. It is the case that makes the rule per-entry.\n  a-double-colon-rename-is-read-by-the-dependent-too — `$@`, `$(@D)`, `$(@F)`\n    in the entry and `$<`, `$^` in the dependent, all off one rename.\n  a-touch-over-a-double-colon-chain-follows-the-rename — `-t` in the `args`\n    file, gating which file the switch brought up to date.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, in kati on nplan/make-a-double-colon-entry-remakes-the-target-where-an-earlier-one-left-it at 6bd06e2a252031dc320cfba1a2fb2374284bee78, pushed before this pin; and in Ronin, `settle_shared` and `Graph::is_double_colon_target` (src/graph/searched.rs), `Graph::observed_file` (src/graph/deferred.rs), `Sink::observed_node` (src/make/sink.rs), the settled map in `late_binding` (src/build/command.rs) and the touched path in `touch_outputs` (src/build/touch.rs).\n\nTWO DEFECTS FILED, both found here:\n  make-a-recipe-less-double-colon-entry-decides-nothing-about-the-chains-name —\n    the one cell of this matrix still standing. An entry with NO recipe that is\n    found current commits the rename in GNU and cannot here: `build_double_action`\n    fills `grouped_double_action` only for a rule with commands, so a recipe-less\n    entry compiles to an edge with no deferred freshness, never names the chain's\n    target, and is scanned dirty because its only output is a proxy that never\n    exists. Reaching it moves when that entry's freshness is decided, which is a\n    measurement of its own rather than a line in this one.\n  make-a-double-colon-chains-name-reaches-a-dependents-new-inputs-as-the-proxy —\n    PRE-EXISTING and needing no `VPATH`: `$?` in a dependent of a two-rule `::`\n    record is `.ronin_grouped_join/0`, a name Ronin invented, where GNU says\n    `out.o`. Verified against the release binary built at 52d1e48. `observed_file`\n    is the answer and this is its third caller.\n\nONE MEASUREMENT NOTE. `scripts/check-release.sh` refused once on\n`dependency-log-load Ronin/Ninja ratio regressed to 0.90x from the recorded\n0.73x`. It is host noise and was demonstrated to be: three further runs of\n`scripts/check-performance.sh` on the same tree flagged `dependency-log-load`\nat 0.95x, then NOTHING at all, then `clean-tree-noop` at 0.81x — three\nverdicts over three workloads, while foreign silos held the load average\nbetween 8 and 12. Nothing in this change is on the path any of those three\nmeasures. Re-run on a quiet host for the number that counts."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T06:02:56.34174913Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T06:24:00.435742307Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "The completion note's measurement note said the `check-release.sh` performance refusal was host noise and gave the evidence as a spread of verdicts. That was an inference. It has since been measured against a control, and the control settles it.\n\n`scripts/check-release.sh` was run six times over the landed tree. Five refused and one passed clean, and the five named FIVE DIFFERENT workloads: `dependency-log-load` at 0.90x and again at 0.95x, `path-canonicalization` at 0.77x, `clean-tree-noop` at 0.81x, `manifest-command-evaluation` at 0.67x, `deep-graph-evaluation` at 0.97x. One run of `scripts/check-performance.sh --warmups 3 --repetitions 15` over the same tree passed clean, so the variance is in the sampling rather than in the code.\n\nTHE CONTROL. A worktree at 52d1e48 — the tree whose recorded baseline this dispatch started from, and whose `check-release.sh` the handover records as EXIT 0 — was built to a release binary and measured through the same harness under the same load. It refused three times out of three, naming `dependency-log-load` at 0.89x, `manifest-command-evaluation` at 0.60x and `path-canonicalization` at 0.82x. The binary was deleted and the worktree removed.\n\nSo the gate refuses on this host for the tree it was recorded on. Foreign silos held the load average between 9 and 17 throughout. Nothing in either node's diff is on the path of any of the six workloads named: they are Ninja-side manifest, graph and log workloads, and the changes are a `::`-specific settlement, a Make-frontend shell read, and the `-t` touch.\n\nEvery other stage of `check-release.sh` passed on every run. The performance stage wants a quiet host and did not get one; re-run it for the number that counts."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-shell-that-needs-a-shell-to-name-is-a-recursive-variable
                title "Refuse a SHELL whose own value has to start a shell to expand"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T01:43:23.020722718Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-a-target-specific-shell-is-not-the-one-that-runs, and PRE-EXISTING — measured against the release binary built at 13aee39, before that node touched anything, which overflows exactly the same way. Not a regression of it.\n\nMEASURED:\n\n    SHELL = $(shell echo ./dumper)\n    all:\n    \\techo one\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   `Makefile:1: *** Recursive variable 'SHELL' references\n                       itself (eventually).  Stop.`, rc 2\n      Ronin            `thread 'main' has overflowed its stack`, rc 134\n\nThe recursion is indirect and that is the whole of it: expanding `SHELL` reaches `$(shell)`, `$(shell)` has to know what shell to start, asking that expands `SHELL`. GNU Make's guard is `variable->expanding`, set around the whole expansion of a recursive variable wherever the expansion goes, so the second entry to `SHELL` is caught however far around the houses it came.\n\nRonin's own guard is there and works for the direct shapes — `A = $(A)` and `SHELL = $(SHELL)` both give `Recursive variable \"…\" references itself (eventually).`, agreed with the oracle and already in the corpus. What it does not survive is the trip out through a function and back in.\n\nWHERE. The guard the direct cases hit, and whatever `get_shell` does to reach `SHELL` that steps around it. `func_shell` asking for the shell is the only path known to reach it, but the fix belongs to the guard rather than to that caller: any function that has to consult a variable while that variable is being expanded is the same shape.\n\nA stack overflow aborts the process, so the failure is worse than the divergence: 134 rather than 2, a core dump, and no diagnostic naming the makefile."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T04:11:48.703031346Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T04:50:56.111458537Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Landed, and the filing named the mechanism exactly. What it did not have is the second half: the guard was one of two things wrong, and fixing only the guard would have turned a crash into a refusal for a whole class of makefile GNU Make runs to completion.\n\nTHE GUARD, which is where the filing pointed. `expanding_vars` was already right and already caught `A = $(A)` and `SHELL = $(SHELL)`. It sat on one path only — the one a `$(NAME)` written in the text takes, through `lookup_var_for_eval`. Make's own reads went through a second door with no mark on it: `eval_var` (kati/src-rs/eval.rs) looked the binding up and called `eval_to_buf` straight, and `get_shell_flag` did the same by hand. `get_shell` is `eval_var(SHELL)`, so `$(shell)` asking what shell to start re-entered `SHELL` unmarked and the second ask was a descent with no floor.\n\nGNU Make has no second door, and that is the whole of the fix. Everything that consults a variable goes through `recursively_expand_for_file` (expand.c) and meets `v->expanding` there, however far around the houses it came. `lookup_var_for_eval` is split into the lookup and `begin_var_expansion`, so a caller that had to find the binding itself still enters the expansion the same way; `eval_var` and `get_shell_flag` both take it, and `eval_var` installs `expanding_var` too.\n\nThe evidence that this is the right door rather than a door: the INDIRECT cell. `OTHER = $(shell ...)` / `SHELL = $(OTHER)` was already caught here — and named OTHER at line 1, where GNU names SHELL at line 2. GNU is right and for a reason: SHELL is the variable re-entered, and `recursively_expand_for_file` installs it as `expanding_var` BEFORE it tests `v->expanding`, so the diagnostic carries SHELL's own definition line. With `get_shell` marking SHELL the two agree to the byte, name and line, without touching the diagnostic.\n\nWHEN THE READ HAPPENS, which the filing did not have. GNU Make expands `$(SHELL)` in `construct_command_argv` (job.c), once per recipe it is about to start — so a makefile with no recipe to RUN never asks what the shell is, and a `SHELL` that could not be expanded is one GNU Make runs to completion. Three reads here were eager and made that makefile a refusal: `NinjaGenerator::new`, `Executor::new`, and `CommandEvaluator::eval` (which asked for every node, recipe or not). All three now read when a recipe asks.\n\nThe boundary is the recipe AS WRITTEN rather than what it expands to, and GNU Make's is the same one — `chop_commands` (commands.c) drops a blank line before anything is expanded, while `all: ; $(EMPTY)` is a command line that survives to be expanded and therefore does ask. `is_blank_recipe_line` already draws exactly that line, so it is the condition.\n\nThe generator's read moving is the one that needed an argument, because it moves to the far side of `avoid_io`. It is reached only by a DEFERRED recipe, which only a destination that runs the build produces, whose `ShellEvaluation` is `Expansion` — so the `$(shell)` in a value read there is answered rather than written into the recipe, exactly as before. This is the same finding the `avoid_io` lift in make-a-target-specific-shell-is-not-the-one-that-runs reached and recorded.\n\nMEASURED, on both binaries, and on rkati beside them where the answer differs:\n\n    SHELL = $(shell echo /bin/sh)               GNU Makefile:1 SHELL rc 2\n                                                Ronin rc 134, core dump    FIXED\n    SHELL := $(shell echo /bin/sh)              both run it; := expands at\n                                                read under the old shell   the control\n    OTHER = $(shell ..) / SHELL = $(OTHER)      GNU Makefile:2 SHELL\n                                                Ronin Makefile:1 OTHER     FIXED\n    all: SHELL = $(shell ...)                   GNU Makefile:1 SHELL\n                                                Ronin rc 134               FIXED\n    all: SHELL := $(shell ...)                  both run it                the control\n    X := $(shell echo hi) beside a recursive\n      SHELL — the read-time ask                 GNU Makefile:1  Ronin 134  FIXED\n    export SHELL = $(shell ...)                 GNU Makefile:1  Ronin 134  FIXED\n    $(info $(SHELL)) beside it                  GNU Makefile:1  Ronin 134  FIXED\n    A = x$(shell echo $(A)) — not SHELL         both name A at line 1      agreed before\n    SHELL = /bin/sh$(shell true)                GNU refuses; the value\n                                                needing a shell at all is\n                                                the whole condition        FIXED\n    SHELL = $(shell ..) then SHELL := /bin/sh   both run it: the value at\n                                                the ask is what decides    agreed\n    all: ;                  (empty recipe)      GNU 'all' is up to date\n                                                Ronin 134, then REFUSED\n                                                after the guard alone      FIXED by the read\n    .PHONY: all / all:      (no recipe)         GNU Nothing to be done     FIXED by the read\n    all: dep / dep: ;                           GNU Nothing to be done     FIXED by the read\n    a recipe line of nothing but the prefix     GNU 'all' is up to date    FIXED by the read\n    all: ; $(EMPTY)         (expands to blank)  GNU REFUSES — still a\n                                                command line               agreed, the boundary\n    all: ;@:                                    GNU REFUSES                agreed\n    .SHELLFLAGS = $(shell echo -c)              GNU SEGFAULTS, rc 139,\n                                                core dumped                see below\n\n.SHELLFLAGS IS A GNU MAKE DEFECT AND IS NOT REPLICATED. `.SHELLFLAGS = $(shell echo -c)` recurses in GNU Make 4.4.1 until it segmentation faults — rc 139, core dumped, no diagnostic. A bounded refusal naming `.SHELLFLAGS` is this fork's own answer to a question the oracle cannot be asked, and it is the one cell in the matrix with no recording behind it. It is in the regression case, marked as such.\n\nTEETH, each change parked on its own:\n| parked | what fails |\n| the guard in `eval_var` | both regression cases, BY CRASH — `status.code()` is None, the process killed by signal |\n| `get_shell_flag` through the guard | the refusal case, by crash, on the `.SHELLFLAGS` row |\n| the blank-recipe boundary in `CommandEvaluator::eval` | 1 of 1,116 ported cases, and the unasked-for regression case |\n| the generator's lazy read | the unasked-for regression case |\n| the executor's lazy read | rkati refuses `all: ;` and `.PHONY: all / all:` where GNU has nothing to do |\nand those alone; no pre-existing case moved either way.\n\nTWO REGRESSION CASES in tests/make_regressions.rs, which is where the teeth are because the port gate cannot hold them: a crash and a refusal are both \"build failed\" to a gate that compares effects, so only a case asserting the STATUS can fail by crash. `a_shell_that_needs_a_shell_to_name_is_refused` walks five shapes and asserts rc 2, the named variable and the line, and that nothing was built. `a_shell_that_needs_a_shell_is_not_asked_for_when_no_recipe_runs` walks the four that must succeed and the one boundary that must not.\n\nTWO FIXTURES, both recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten:\n  a-shell-that-needs-a-shell-to-name-is-refused — the plain shape. It gates the\n    effect (nothing built) and has no teeth against THIS fix, said plainly:\n    parked, Ronin crashes, and a crash is a failure outcome like any other. It\n    is here for the regression it can catch — a refusal that stopped refusing.\n  a-shell-nothing-needs-is-never-asked-for — the read's own boundary in one\n    makefile: a blank recipe, a target with none at all, a prefix-only line, and\n    `later: ; $(EMPTY)` standing beside them as the line that WOULD ask.\nA third was written for the indirect shape and DELETED before landing: its build\nintent is identical to the plain one's, and what distinguishes it — the name and\nthe line — is what the port gate does not compare and the regression case does.\n\nONE PRE-EXISTING PRECEDENCE DIFFERENCE OBSERVED AND NOT FILED. With a missing prerequisite beside a bad `SHELL`, GNU reports `No rule to make target 'dep'` and Ronin reports the SHELL diagnostic: the compiler expands recipes before the scheduler discovers the missing input. It is not this fix's — `SHELL = $(error boom)` shows the same order on a path this node did not touch — and it is the compiled-intent ordering rather than a defect: a compile error before a graph error.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, in kati on nplan/make-a-shell-that-needs-a-shell-to-name-is-a-recursive-variable at e10849e1aacd168a2cf8bd9cdec603bf56e1b147, pushed before this pin. Nothing in Ronin.\n\nGATES\n  workspace                1047/0/1 — 1045 plus the two regression cases\n  --no-default-features    807/0, unmoved\n  make_port                4/4 over 1,116 gated cases (1,113 plus three, less the\n                           one deleted, is 1,115 — the harness counts 1,116 because\n                           the two new directories are two cases and the deleted\n                           third never entered a passing run), nine divergence\n                           markers unmoved\n  conformance              336/51 normalised, 286 raw, class and family tables identical\n  equivalence              297/280/75/0, manifest respellings 8, unchanged\n  upstream inventory       1285 at 97/0/24/1164, compiler zero, TSV byte-identical,\n                           no row moved\n  ninja conformance        425/425, 10 CLI, 86 build outcome, 3 invocation,\n                           persistence, log content\n  real projects            vim 9.2 in 22s, zsh 5.9.2 and 36 modules in 37s, both at\n                           -j8. Slower than the 17s/32s of the last dispatch and not\n                           chased: the host was not quiet, and nothing here is on a\n                           build's hot path — the shell reads that moved happen once.\n  fmt, check-lints.sh      clean\n  check-release.sh         exit 0\n\nONE DEFECT FILED, found here and NOT this node's:\n  make-a-target-specific-shell-does-not-reach-katis-own-executor — `Executor`\n    (kati/src-rs/exec.rs) runs every recipe under the GLOBAL shell, so\n    `all: SHELL := ./own` is ignored in rkati's exec mode where GNU Make and\n    Ronin both honour it. The target-specific-shell landing (998405c) reached\n    the compiled path and not this one. Measured on the same Makefile: GNU and\n    Ronin both run `./own -c echo one`, rkati runs `/bin/sh`.\n"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T04:52:09.425144741Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Correction to the note above, before it is sealed: the make_port count is 1,115, not 1,116, and the parenthetical explaining it is confused. 1,123 case directories less the 8 in DISCOVERY_ONLY_CASES is 1,115 — the 1,113 baseline plus the two fixtures that landed. The 1,116 quoted is real but it is the teeth measurement's count, taken while the third fixture (the indirect shape, deleted before landing) was still on disk. The teeth are unaffected: 1 of 1,116 failed then, and it was the new case."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T04:53:05.470548848Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T04:53:42.330067329Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The two regression cases landed under shorter names than the completion note gives them. The commit gate's `function-name` check caps a function name at six words; both were written past it, and the refusal was fixed rather than bypassed. Read the note's names as:\n\n* `a_shell_that_needs_a_shell_to_name_is_refused` → `a_recursive_shell_is_refused`\n* `a_shell_that_needs_a_shell_is_not_asked_for_when_no_recipe_runs` → `a_shell_nothing_needs_is_unasked`\n\nEach case's doc comment carries what the name no longer spells out, and the teeth transcript quoted in the note names them by their original spelling."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-target-specific-shell-does-not-reach-katis-own-executor
                title "Run a recipe under its target's own SHELL in kati's executor too"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-shell-that-needs-a-shell-to-name-is-a-recursive-variable)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T04:53:01.654314363Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-a-shell-that-needs-a-shell-to-name-is-a-recursive-variable, on the same line that node had to touch for a different reason. Not a regression of it: the gap is what make-a-target-specific-shell-is-not-the-one-that-runs (998405c) did not reach.\n\nMEASURED, one Makefile, three binaries. `./own` writes its own name and arguments to stderr and then execs `/bin/sh`:\n\n    all: SHELL := ./own\n    all:\n    \techo one\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   echo one / OWN ./own -c echo one / one\n      Ronin            [1/1] echo one / OWN ./own -c echo one / one\n      rkati            echo one / one            -- ./own never asked\n\nTHE MECHANISM. `Executor` (kati/src-rs/exec.rs) holds one `shell` and hands it to every `run_command` it starts. `CommandEvaluator::eval` reads the shell with the node's own scope in front of it and leaves the answer on `recipe_shell` — which is what 998405c added and what the compiled path reads — but the executor runs `self.shell` beside it and never looks. So the value is right there on the evaluator it just called and is dropped on the floor.\n\nWHERE. `Executor::exec_node`, the `run_command` call: `self.shell()` becomes `self.ce.recipe_shell`. The read is already done by the `self.ce.eval(n)` immediately above it, in the same iteration, so nothing new has to be expanded — and the laziness that landed with the recursion fix falls out of it, since `recipe_shell` is only set for a node with a recipe to run.\n\nProbe the matrix the sibling node already established for the compiled path and check each cell against rkati: the plain target-specific `SHELL`, the descent to a prerequisite built for that target and its boundary, a pattern rule's, `private`, two assignments where the last wins, `.SHELLFLAGS` beside it, and `.ONESHELL`. The recordings for those cells exist under tests/make/ and were taken from the oracle; what is missing is a gate that runs rkati rather than Ronin over them.\n\nNarrow in reach and not narrow in kind: rkati's exec mode is what the conformance corpus measures, so every cell that diverges here is a row the corpus cannot see the truth of."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T07:05:00.159050865Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T07:27:13.617080966Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was right about the mechanism, right about the line and right about what it costs. What it did not have is WHY the executor could not simply ask again — which is the half that makes the fix a READ of a field rather than a second expansion — and a second gap on the same executor, now filed.\n\nVERIFIED FIRST, against the binaries at a63dead before anything was touched. `./own` writes its own name and arguments to stderr and then execs `/bin/sh`:\n\n    all: SHELL := ./own\n    all:\n    \\t@echo one\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   OWN ./own -c echo one / one\n      Ronin            [1/1] echo one / OWN ./own -c echo one / one\n      rkati            one            -- ./own never asked\n\nWHY ASKING AGAIN WAS NOT AVAILABLE, which is the half the filing did not have. `CommandEvaluator::eval` (kati/src-rs/command.rs) sets `self.ev.current_scope = node.rule_vars.clone()` at its first line, reads `SHELL` through it, leaves the answer on `recipe_shell` — and takes the scope back down at its last (`self.ev.current_scope = None`). `Executor::shell` then called `self.ce.ev.get_shell()` one line later with nothing in front of the evaluator, and memoised what it got for the whole build. So the executor was not merely ignoring the value beside it: a second `get_shell()` at that point CANNOT be right, because the scope it would need has already been dismantled. That is why the answer is the field and not the call.\n\nWHERE. `Executor::exec_node`'s `run_command` reads `self.ce.recipe_shell` (kati/src-rs/exec.rs). The `shell` field and the `Executor::shell` method are gone with it, and the laziness their doc comment argued for is now a property of `eval`: `recipe_shell` is only filled for a node whose recipe is not entirely blank lines, so a build with nothing to run still never asks. `get_shell` is now called once per node rather than once per node plus one, which is the same reads minus one.\n\nTHE MATRIX, thirteen cells, each run through reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, then Ronin, then rkati. All three now agree on all thirteen; rkati diverged on seven.\n\n  WHOSE SHELL\n    a plain target-specific `SHELL`                    ./own runs the recipe        FIXED\n    it descends to a prerequisite built for it         ./own runs both recipes      FIXED\n    `private` stops the descent                        ./own runs the target's\n        recipe and `/bin/sh` runs the prerequisite's — the boundary cell            FIXED\n    a pattern rule's own `SHELL`                       ./own runs `%.o`, and the\n        goal beside it keeps the global                                             FIXED\n    two assignments for one target                     the last written wins        FIXED\n    a global `SHELL` and no target-specific one        the control — agreed before\n    `.SHELLFLAGS := -xc` beside a target's `SHELL`     ./own -xc, and the trace it\n        turns on appears                                                            FIXED\n\n  WHEN IT IS ASKED FOR\n    `SHELL = $(shell ...)` with a recipe to run        refused, exit 2 — agreed\n    the same with nothing to run                       runs to completion — agreed\n    a target-specific `SHELL = $(shell ...)`           refused, exit 2 — agreed\n    `all: ;`                                           nothing runs, exit 0 — agreed\n    a recipe line of nothing but the prefix            the same — agreed\n    `all: ; $(EMPTY)`                                  the same — agreed; the three\n        blank shapes are the cells that would break if the empty `recipe_shell`\n        ever reached a launch, and they are why they are here\n\nTEETH, the change parked and the new gate run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the read of `recipe_shell` | 5 of the 6 |\nThe sixth is `a_shell_no_recipe_needs_is_unasked`, which passes either way and is RECORDED AS SUCH: it gates the laziness the change had to keep while moving the read, and a test that cannot fail today is still the one that fails when the read moves again.\n\nTHE GATE, and it is new surface: kati/tests/exec_shell.rs, six cases running the `rkati` binary over a makefile whose `SHELL` announces itself. It lives in kati rather than in the ported corpus because the corpus runs Ronin — and the filing's last line is the whole reason this node is not narrow: rkati's exec mode is what the conformance corpus measures, so every cell that diverges here is a row the corpus cannot see the truth of. `CARGO_BIN_EXE_rkati` and `CARGO_TARGET_TMPDIR` are what make it hermetic.\n\nGATES, run serially on this tree: fmt clean in both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace tests 1,053 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored — up six from 1,047, and the six are this node's own gate; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; make_port 4/4 over 1,124 gated cases, no recording touched, nine divergence sidecars unmoved; conformance 336 normalised identical / 51 differing, 286 raw — UNMOVED, and that is the audit rather than a pass: the vendored suite has no case that sets a target-specific `SHELL`, which is why the gap survived a corpus that drives this very binary; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 manifest respellings, unmoved; upstream inventory 1,285 rows at 97 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,164 narration, tsv untouched, no row moved, compiler still zero; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 17 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 30 s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential, outcome, boundary and persistence band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 813 passed / 0 failed, up six for the same reason.\n\nONE DEFECT FILED, found here on the same executor and PRE-EXISTING — measured against the parked build:\n  make-a-one-shell-recipe-is-one-shell-in-katis-own-executor-too — rkati runs a\n    `.ONESHELL:` recipe as one shell per LINE. A variable set on one line is gone\n    by the next, a `cd` does not persist, and the first line's failure status\n    becomes the recipe's where under one shell it would not be. Every reader of\n    `flags.one_shell` in kati is in ninja.rs; exec.rs names it nowhere, which is\n    this node's shape exactly — a decision the compiled path makes and the\n    executor beside it never learned."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T07:29:06.46416779Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-double-colon-chains-name-reaches-a-dependents-new-inputs-as-the-proxy
                title "Name a double-colon chain's target in a dependent's new inputs, not the proxy"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-double-colon-entry-remakes-the-target-where-an-earlier-one-left-it)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T05:42:16.406650764Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-a-double-colon-entry-remakes-the-target-where-an-earlier-one-left-it, and PRE-EXISTING — measured against the release binary built at 52d1e48, before that node touched anything, which answers the same way. It needs no `VPATH` and no rename: a plain `::` record of two rules is the whole of it.\n\nA NAME THE COMPILER INVENTED REACHES THE RECIPE.\n\n    all: out.o\n    \\t@echo \"new=$?\" >> log.txt\n    out.o:: p1\n    \\t@echo v1 >> $@\n    out.o:: p2\n    \\t@echo v2 >> $@\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   new=out.o\n      Ronin            new=.ronin_grouped_join/0\n\n`$?` is bound by the scheduler from the edge's own input list, and a `::` chain's target reaches its dependents as the COMPLETION PROXY: `declare_edge` (src/make/sink.rs) redirects every use of the target onto the join so a dependent waits for every entry of the chain. The proxy is a name the compiler made to sequence work, and the value handed to the recipe is `graph.node_path` of it.\n\nSo a Makefile sees `.ronin_grouped_join/N` — Ronin's own working name in a value GNU Make fills with the Makefile's. Worse than a wrong path: it is a name that exists nowhere, so a recipe that passes `$?` to a compiler names a file that cannot be opened.\n\nWHERE. `Builder::new_inputs_value` (src/build/deferred.rs), the arm that spells each `state.new_inputs()` entry. `Graph::observed_file` was added for this node and answers exactly the question the arm needs — the file a node stands for, which for a completion proxy is the target the Makefile wrote — and the same reading already went into `late_binding`'s settled map and into the sink's reference resolution. This is the third caller, and the only one still asking the proxy.\n\nProbe the neighbours while there: `$(?D)` and `$(?F)` over the same value, `$?` where the dependent's recipe is read early rather than at launch (`new_input_names` is consulted first and may already answer), and the same shape with `VPATH` on top, where the settled spelling is `src/out.o` rather than `out.o` — that cell is recorded in this node's own probe and is the one GNU answers `src/out.o` for.\n\nNot gated anywhere: no ported case, no upstream case and neither real project puts a `::` chain of more than one rule on the prerequisite side of a recipe that reads `$?`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T06:39:00.162385154Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T07:01:08.143601033Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was right about the cell, right about the answer and right about where it goes — third caller of `observed_file`, one line, and the only one of the three that was still asking the proxy. What the filing did not have is how far the cell reaches: it is not the dependent's alone, and the halves go with it.\n\nVERIFIED FIRST, against the binary built at 82aa058 before anything was touched. `all: out.o` over a two-rule `out.o::` record, `$?` in the dependent:\n\n    GNU Make 4.4.1   new=out.o\n    Ronin            new=.ronin_grouped_join/0\n\nThe filing stands. Neither `VPATH` nor a rename is needed; a plain `::` record of two rules is the whole of it.\n\nTHE MATRIX, seventeen cells, each run through reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and then through Ronin. Ronin now agrees on all seventeen, and diverged on nine of them before.\n\n  THE VALUE AND ITS HALVES\n    `$?` in a dependent of a two-rule chain      GNU out.o                        FIXED\n    `$(?D)` beside it                            GNU `.`; Ronin `.ronin_grouped_join`  FIXED\n    `$(?F)` beside it                            GNU out.o; Ronin `0` — the proxy's\n        own filename half, which is the index the compiler counted with          FIXED\n    `$^` and `$<` in the same recipe             agreed; they read the target already,\n        and are the control that says which reading was missing\n\n  WHICH LIST, AND WHERE IN IT\n    a chain and a plain prerequisite together    GNU `out.o other`; the proxy stood in\n        the chain's place and the ordinary name beside it was right               FIXED\n    two chains on one list                       GNU `a.o b.o`, `$(?D)` = `. .` and\n        `$(?F)` = `a.o b.o` — the reading is per word, and both proxies were wrong FIXED\n    a chain of ONE rule                          agreed; a lone `::` record takes the\n        ordinary single-node path and has no proxy at all — the control\n    an order-only chain prerequisite             agreed; `$?` is empty and `$|` names\n        the target — the control\n    an archive member prerequisite               agreed; the published-name arm above\n        answers first and is untouched by this — the control\n    a grouped `g1 g2 &:` prerequisite            agreed — the control\n\n  WHICH NAME\n    `VPATH = src`, first entry current           GNU src/out.o, `$(?D)` = src,\n        `$(?F)` = out.o — `$?` follows the latch the sibling node landed          FIXED\n    `GPATH = src` beside it                      agreed after, and the same name       FIXED\n    a chain target in a subdirectory             GNU sub/out.o, `sub`, `out.o`         FIXED\n    every entry current, dependent up to date    both do nothing — the control\n    every entry current, dependent ABSENT        GNU `out.o other`: what forces the\n        dependent is its own absence, and the chain is in `$?` all the same       FIXED\n\n  WHO ELSE READS IT\n    a `.PHONY` chain                             GNU `ph`                              FIXED\n    a chain inside a recursive child             GNU `out.o`, `.`, `out.o`; Ronin said\n        `../.ronin_grouped_join/0` — the proxy relativised from the parent, which is\n        the qualification the value must never carry                             FIXED\n    an ENTRY of one chain waiting on another     GNU `b.o` for the entry whose\n        prerequisite is a chain and `p2` for the entry beside it, off one list    FIXED\n\nWHERE, and it is one line. `Builder::deferred_new_inputs_value` (src/build/deferred.rs) resolves each `state.new_inputs()` entry through `Graph::observed_file` before it asks the search or the graph for a path. The published-name arm above it is deliberately left on the raw node: those spellings are keyed by the node kati named them for, and an archive member is the only name that reaches them.\n\nTEETH, the change parked and the whole corpus of 1,124 gated cases run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| `observed_file` in the new-inputs value | 5 |\nand those five are this node's own fixtures; no pre-existing case moved either way.\n\nFIVE FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten:\n  a-double-colon-chains-name-reaches-a-dependents-new-inputs — the plain cell and\n    the node's name, with `$(?D)` and `$(?F)` beside `$?` and `$^`/`$<` as the control.\n  two-double-colon-chains-reach-one-dependents-new-inputs — two proxies in one\n    list, whose recording holds `dir=. .`: the case that makes the reading per word.\n  a-double-colon-chains-settled-name-reaches-the-new-inputs — the latch, where the\n    recorded value is `src/out.o` and its halves are `src` and `out.o`.\n  a-double-colon-chain-a-recursive-child-holds-names-its-own — the child's own\n    spelling, gating that the value is never qualified from the parent's directory.\n  a-double-colon-entry-names-the-chain-its-prerequisite-is — an entry of a chain\n    reading another chain, with the entry beside it as the control.\n\nGATES, run serially on this tree, kati unmoved at 6bd06e2a252031dc320cfba1a2fb2374284bee78: fmt clean in both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace tests 1,047 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, unmoved; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; make_port 4/4 over 1,124 gated cases with five new recordings and none rewritten, nine divergence sidecars unmoved; conformance 336 normalised identical / 51 differing, 286 raw, unmoved — and it cannot move here, the gate driving rkati's own executor rather than Ronin's scheduler; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 manifest respellings, unmoved; upstream inventory 1,285 rows at 97 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,164 narration, tsv untouched, no row moved, the compiler bucket still zero; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 19 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 34 s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential, outcome, boundary and persistence band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 807 passed / 0 failed.\n\nONE DEFECT FILED, found here and NOT this node's:\n  make-a-dry-run-prints-the-placeholder-it-would-have-filled-in — `-n` narrates the\n    recipe with `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}` in it rather than the names it stands for, and\n    it needs no `::` at all: `all: p1 p2` with `$?` in the recipe is enough. The\n    deferred substitution happens as the command launches and a dry run never\n    launches one, so the placeholder — a name the compiler invented, exactly this\n    node's subject — reaches the reader."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T07:01:45.138425592Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-recipe-less-double-colon-entry-decides-nothing-about-the-chains-name
                title "Let a recipe-less double-colon entry settle the chain's name as GNU does"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-double-colon-entry-remakes-the-target-where-an-earlier-one-left-it)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T05:45:42.296089131Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-a-double-colon-entry-remakes-the-target-where-an-earlier-one-left-it, and it is the one cell of that node's matrix still standing. Not a regression of it: the same Makefile diverged before and diverges after, for a reason the settlement cannot reach.\n\nTHE CELL. `VPATH = src`, `src/out.o` present at 100, `p1` at 50, `p2` at 200:\n\n    VPATH = src\n    out.o:: p1\n    out.o:: p2\n    \\t@echo \"e2 at=$@\" >> log.txt\n    \\t@echo v2 >> $@\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   e2 at=src/out.o, and src/out.o is what it writes\n      Ronin            e2 at=out.o,     and ./out.o is what it writes\n\nThe first entry has NO RECIPE and its prerequisite is older than the target, so GNU Make's `update_file_1` finds nothing to remake and commits the rename for it and every entry after it — the same `while (file) { file->name = file->hname; file = file->prev; }` the sibling node landed. A recipe-less entry decides the chain's name exactly as a recipe-bearing one does; having nothing to run is not the same as having nothing to say.\n\nWHY THE SETTLEMENT CANNOT REACH IT. `build_double_action` (kati/src-rs/dep.rs) only fills `grouped_double_action` when the rule has commands, and `deferred_freshness_outputs` (kati/src-rs/ninja.rs) reads exactly that. So a recipe-less entry compiles to an edge with no deferred freshness and no member list — it never names the chain's target, so `settle_searched_outputs` (src/graph/searched.rs) is never asked about it and the latch never sees a current entry. Traced: the recipe-less action's only settlement is of its own invented output, and the target is settled by the OTHER entry alone.\n\nWorse than not settling: the edge is scanned dirty, because its only output is a virtual proxy that never exists. So the entry Ronin thinks is stale is the one GNU found current.\n\nWHERE. The `has_recipe` guard in `build_double_action`, and everything that reads `grouped_double_action` behind it — `deferred_freshness_always_dirty`, the join's `actions_pending`, the `$?` timing. Making a recipe-less entry an action with members is not a one-line change; it moves when that entry's freshness is decided, from the plan to the scheduling boundary. That is the measurement this node owes: park it and run the corpus, and probe the shape where the recipe-less entry is STALE, which must still run nothing at all.\n\nProbe the matrix from the sibling node again with the recipe-less entry in each position — first, middle, last — and both ways round on staleness; and without `VPATH` at all, where the two tools agree today and must keep agreeing.\n\nNarrow by construction: it needs `VPATH`, a `::` record of more than one rule, one of them recipe-less AND current, another stale, and a target found down the search path. Nothing in the corpus, the upstream suite or either real project stands on it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T07:29:37.576430865Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T08:16:26.610719133Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was right about the cell, right about the guard and right about the cost — reaching it does move when the entry's freshness is decided. It was WRONG about the rule, in a way the filing could not have known without asking GNU Make the second question: the entry is not decided by dates at all.\n\nWHAT THE FILING HAD, and it holds. `build_double_action` (kati/src-rs/dep.rs) filled `grouped_double_action` only for a rule with commands, so a recipe-less `::` entry compiled to an edge with no deferred freshness, never named the chain's target, and was scanned dirty because its only output is a proxy that never exists. Measured against the binary at 98dc6e8, the filing's cell diverged exactly as written.\n\nWHAT THE FILING HAD WRONG, and it is the whole of the rule. The filing says the entry is found current because \"its prerequisite is older than the target\". It is not. `update_file_1` (remake.c:849) overrules the comparison outright:\n\n    else if (!noexist && file->is_target && !deps_changed && file->cmds == 0\n             && !always_make_flag)\n      must_make = 0;\n\n`deps_changed` is gathered a few lines above as `deps_changed |= d->changed` — a bit `update_file` set where it REMADE the prerequisite, read before `d->changed |= noexist || d_mtime > this_mtime` adds this target's own comparison to it. So a prerequisite that is merely NEWER decides nothing, and only one this run actually rebuilt does. The measurement that settles it: the same makefile with the prerequisite at 300 against a target at 100 renames the chain exactly as it does with the prerequisite at 50. Three clauses stand ahead of or beside that one and each is its own cell — no prerequisites at all (`file->double_colon && file->deps == 0`, which counts an order-only prerequisite as a prerequisite), an absent target (`noexist`), and `--always-make`, which withholds the overruling and hands the dates back.\n\nTHE MATRIX, twenty-two cells, each run through reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and then through Ronin. Ronin now agrees on twenty; the two it does not are one defect, filed and gated.\n\n  WHAT DECIDES THE ENTRY\n    recipe-less first entry, prerequisite older    GNU src/out.o — the filing's  FIXED\n    the same with the prerequisite NEWER           GNU src/out.o, unchanged —\n        the cell that says no date decides this entry                            FIXED\n    the prerequisite is REMADE by this run         GNU out.o: a rebuild reached\n        withholds the overruling                                                 FIXED\n    the prerequisite is a PHONY, so it runs        GNU out.o, same reason        agreed\n    no prerequisites at all                        GNU out.o: never current, and\n        still nothing to run — \"out of date\" and \"has work\" come apart here      agreed\n    an ORDER-ONLY prerequisite, newer              GNU src/out.o                 FIXED\n    an order-only prerequisite this run REMAKES    GNU src/out.o: the sum is\n        gathered under `if (! d->ignore_mtime)`                                  FIXED\n    an order-only PHONY, which is never current    GNU src/out.o — the sharpest\n        reading of the same clause, because this one cannot settle by waiting    FIXED\n    `--always-make`, prerequisite older            GNU src/out.o: `-B` reaches a\n        target through `file->cmds != 0` and never forces one with no recipe     FIXED\n    `--always-make`, prerequisite NEWER            GNU out.o: the comparison is\n        back, and it is the pair that proves the switch changes the rule         agreed\n\n  WHERE IN THE CHAIN\n    recipe-less entry FIRST                        renames the whole chain       FIXED\n    recipe-less entry in the MIDDLE of three       GNU out.o then src/out.o —\n        one chain, two files written in one run                                  FIXED\n    recipe-less entry LAST                         renames itself and nothing\n        follows it                                                               agreed\n    two recipe-less entries in a row               GNU src/out.o                 FIXED\n    a lone recipe-less `::` record                 the single-node path — control\n\n  BESIDE IT\n    no `VPATH` at all                              agreed throughout — control\n    `GPATH = src`                                  agreed — the name is already\n        the found one\n    `-t` over the chain                            GNU touches src/out.o         FIXED\n    `-q`                                           exit 1 both — control\n    the dependent's `$<`, `$^` and `$?`            GNU src/out.o                 FIXED\n    a `-t` whose head is remade and second current agreed — control\n    the dependent where the HEAD is remade         DIVERGENT, filed and gated\n\nWHERE, and it is six places rather than the filing's one.\n\nONE — kati says the entry exists. `build_double_action` fills `grouped_double_action` for every entry, with `has_recipe` and `without_prerequisites` recorded on it. `unconditional_double_colon` keeps its `has_recipe` guard, deliberately: that flag is what `is_remakable` refuses a Makefile for, and an entry with nothing to run cannot restart a read however out of date it is. The never-current answer a prerequisite-less recipe-less entry needs rides on the action's own `deferred_freshness_always_dirty` instead.\n\nTWO — kati says which rule decides it. `SinkEdge::deferred_freshness_ignores_dates`, set for an action with no recipe and for nothing else.\n\nTHREE — the scheduler stops asking about dates. `recompute_deferred_freshness` (src/graph/deferred.rs) skips the timestamp loop for such an edge, unless `--always-make` gives it back.\n\nFOUR — `-B` stops treating the outputs as absent for such an edge, in `capture_deferred_freshness`. The same withholding from the other side, and without it the switch would answer for an edge it never reaches.\n\nFIVE — the rebuild bit is REMEMBERED. `DeferredRuntime::deps_changed` is GNU Make's `d->changed`, set the first time an ordinary prerequisite of such an edge is out of date and never cleared. It has to be: the prerequisite that forced the entry stops being out of date the moment it is made, and this edge is rescanned afterwards — asking again would find nothing wrong and rename a chain GNU Make does not.\n\nSIX — an ORDER-ONLY prerequisite is left out of that sum, but the chain's own ordering link is not. Both are order-only inputs; they are told apart by `is_virtual_output`, because the link is a name the compiler invented to make this entry wait its turn rather than a prerequisite anybody wrote. Without the distinction an entry settles the chain's name before its turn and answers for the entries in front of it.\n\nTEETH, each change parked on its own and the whole corpus of 1,134 gated cases run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the recipe-less entry is not an action at all (kati) | 5 |\n| the never-current flag for a prerequisite-less one (kati) | 1 |\n| the timestamp comparison is asked anyway | 1 |\n| `--always-make` treating the outputs as absent here too | 1 |\n| the remembered rebuild bit | 1 |\n| the chain link told apart from an order-only prerequisite | 2 |\nand no pre-existing case moved either way in any of the six.\n\nONE MEASUREMENT NOTE ON THE LAST ROW, because it is the reason a second fixture exists for one clause. With the distinction parked, `a-recipe-less-double-colon-entrys-order-only-prerequisite-settles-nothing` fails INTERMITTENTLY — two runs in eight, measured — because the parked answer is a race between the order-only prerequisite finishing and the entry being rescanned. `...-order-only-phony-settles-nothing` fails six times in six, because a phony never stops being out of date and there is no race to win. Both are deterministic with the change in; the phony one is the tooth.\n\nTEN FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten. Eight gate the fix: a-recipe-less-double-colon-entry-settles-the-chains-name, ...-is-not-decided-by-a-date, ...-a-rebuild-reached-settles-nothing, ...-in-the-middle-settles-what-follows, ...-with-no-prerequisites-settles-nothing, ...-order-only-prerequisite-settles-nothing, ...-order-only-phony-settles-nothing, an-always-make-run-decides-a-recipe-less-entry-by-its-dates. Two carry divergence sidecars and gate the gap below.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, in kati on nplan/make-a-recipe-less-double-colon-entry-decides-nothing-about-the-chains-name, pushed before this pin: `GroupedDoubleAction` (dep.rs) gains `has_recipe` and `without_prerequisites`, `build_double_action` fills the metadata for every entry, `SinkEdge` (build_sink.rs) gains `deferred_freshness_ignores_dates` and ninja.rs sets it. In Ronin: `DeferredFreshness::dates_do_not_decide` (src/graph/deferred.rs) and the two clauses that read it, `DeferredRuntime::deps_changed` (src/runtime/deferred.rs), and the plumbing through `DeferredSpec` (src/frontend/deferred.rs) and the sink.\n\nGATES, run serially on this tree: fmt clean in both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace tests 1,053 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, unmoved; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; make_port 4/4 over 1,134 gated cases with ten new recordings and none rewritten, and ELEVEN divergence sidecars where there were nine — the two new ones are the defect below, and both are shapes the gate could not see before; conformance 336 normalised identical / 51 differing, 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 manifest respellings, unmoved; upstream inventory 1,285 rows at 97 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,164 narration, tsv untouched, no row moved, compiler still zero; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 28 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 44 s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential, outcome, boundary and persistence band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 813 passed / 0 failed.\n\nONE DEFECT FILED, found here and PRE-EXISTING in its recipe-bearing form — measured on the binary at 98dc6e8 in a shape with no recipe-less entry in it:\n  make-a-double-colon-chains-dependent-reads-the-heads-name — the rename reaches\n    forward through the chain and a dependent does not follow it: GNU Make hands\n    a dependent the entry the record was filed under, which is the FIRST, so a\n    first entry that is remade keeps the name as written for every dependent\n    however many entries after it renamed themselves. `settle_shared` holds one\n    verdict per target and a dependent reads it after every entry, so it gets the\n    last entry's. Four cells measured, all four diverging, and three controls\n    that agree. Both gated shapes carry the sidecar: the recipe-bearing one,\n    which diverged before this node too, and the recipe-less-last one, which\n    agreed before only because a recipe-less entry then answered for nothing."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T08:18:03.178053696Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T08:27:57.552735347Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "A completeness follow-up on the landing above, found by auditing what the equivalence gate compares rather than by a failing run.\n\n`DeferredSemantics` (src/make/equivalence.rs) mirrors every deferred field a `SinkEdge` carries, and that mirror is the whole of what the gate can judge: the direct-graph path reads its answer off `DeferredFreshness`, the manifest path reads it off what the writer emitted, and any property missing from both records is one the two encodings could disagree about in silence. `deferred_freshness_ignores_dates` was the one new field and it was in neither.\n\nIt is now the eighth question the two sides answer, beside `always_dirty_output` and its six neighbours, and the comparison prints it. No row moved: 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 manifest respellings, unchanged — which is the answer expected, and the point is that the gate is now able to say so."
                        after_done true
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T08:34:12.016439934Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "THE PERFORMANCE ANSWER FOR THIS DISPATCH, measured rather than held, because the host went quiet for long enough to ask properly and then did not stay that way.\n\n`scripts/check-release.sh` PASSED IN FULL, exit 0, on the tree at 7d28297 — every stage including the performance one. Load average was 4.63 falling to 2.96 across the run, and the only tenant on the machine was this session's own build. That is the real answer the standing note asked for, and it retires the hold: the refusals recorded on 2026-08-23 against the same workloads were host noise.\n\nWHAT HAPPENED AFTERWARDS IS THE CONTROL, and it is worth recording because it is the same noise arriving again. A foreign silo returned — five `build-script-build` processes at about 100% each — and the load went back to between 9.5 and 9.8. Three further measurements on the same tree, with no code between them:\n\n    check-release.sh          refused: deep-graph-evaluation 0.87x from 0.72x\n    check-performance.sh      refused: dependency-log-load   0.97x from 0.73x\n    check-performance.sh      PASSED, exit 0\n\nThree verdicts over two different workloads and a pass, on one tree, under one load. A regression does not move between workloads and does not go away.\n\nAND NOTHING THIS DISPATCH CHANGED IS ON EITHER PATH. `deep-graph-evaluation` and `dependency-log-load` are Ninja-manifest workloads. Every runtime change here is inside `recompute_deferred_freshness`, `capture_deferred_freshness` or `deferred_new_inputs_value`, each reached only by an edge carrying deferred freshness — which is a relation a Ninja manifest has no way to declare — or inside kati, which those workloads never load. The one clause added to a shared path is a bool test guarded by the same relation.\n\nThe number that counts is the first line: the whole release gate, green, on a quiet host."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-dry-run-prints-the-placeholder-it-would-have-filled-in
                title "Narrate a dry run's recipe with the new-input names, not the placeholder"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-double-colon-chains-name-reaches-a-dependents-new-inputs-as-the-proxy)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T07:01:39.431561462Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-a-double-colon-chains-name-reaches-a-dependents-new-inputs-as-the-proxy, and PRE-EXISTING — it needs no `::` record, no `VPATH` and no rename. A recipe that reads `$?` and a `-n` are the whole of it.\n\nMEASURED, `p1` and `p2` present, `all` absent:\n\n    all: p1 p2\n    \\t@echo \"new=[$?]\" > log.txt\n\n  make -n\n    GNU Make 4.4.1   echo \"new=[p1 p2]\" > log.txt\n    Ronin            [1/1] exec env 'MAKEFLAGS=n' 'MAKELEVEL=1' 'MFLAGS=-n' \\\n                       /bin/sh -c \"echo \\\"new=[\\${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}]\\\" > log.txt\"\n\nTHE MECHANISM. `KATI_NEW_INPUTS` is the name kati writes into a recipe where the Makefile wrote `$?`, and `KATI_NEW_INPUTS_D` / `KATI_NEW_INPUTS_F` are its two halves. The substitution is `Builder::deferred_launch_command` (src/build/deferred.rs), which runs as the command LAUNCHES — and a dry run never launches one, so what the reader is shown is the recipe with the placeholder still in it.\n\nThe value is not unknowable at that moment: `deferred_work` decides the deferred edge's freshness under a dry run too, and `set_new_inputs` has already filled the list the substitution reads. Only the call site is missing. So this is not a case of narrating what could not be computed; it is the one path where the computed value is not asked for.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT MERELY NARRATION. Ronin's `-n` speaks Ninja and that is settled — the progress counter, the `exec env` prefix and the argv spelling are all Ronin's voice by design, and none of them is this. `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}` is a name the COMPILER invented to carry a value across the boundary, in exactly the sense `.ronin_grouped_join/N` was: a working name that stands for something the Makefile wrote, leaking into a place a reader reads. The sibling node above took that name out of `$?`'s value; this one is the same name in the same recipe's text.\n\nWHERE. The dry-run narration path, which prints `Edge`'s command without going through `deferred_launch_command`. Probe which of the three placeholders reach it (`$?`, `$(?D)`, `$(?F)`), the settled-name references beside them (`KATI_SETTLED_N`, which the same launch-time pass resolves and which a `VPATH` build's `-n` will show), the response-file spelling, and `-q` and `--what-if` — every switch that reports a command it does not run. Check `-n` over a recursive child too, where the value is spelt from the child's own directory.\n\nNot gated anywhere: the corpus records files and exit status rather than narration, and the upstream inventory reads a differing command line as `ninja-progress` or `recipe-echo` before it could ever reach the placeholder."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T09:10:21.69858247Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T09:11:10.160100378Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was right about the mechanism, right about where the value comes from and right that it needs no `::`. It was WRONG about the scope in one way that matters: this is not `-n`'s defect. An ORDINARY build narrates the placeholder too, and it does so through a different text and a different spelling, so a fix aimed at the dry run alone would have left every reader of every ordinary build looking at the same working note.\n\nVERIFIED FIRST, against the binary at 93130da before anything was touched, `p1` and `sub/p2` present:\n\n    all: p1 sub/p2\n    \t@echo \"new=[$?] d=[$(?D)] f=[$(?F)]\" > log.txt\n\n  GNU Make 4.4.1  -n   echo \"new=[p1 sub/p2] d=[. sub] f=[p1 p2]\" > log.txt\n  Ronin           -n   [1/1] exec env ... /bin/sh -c \"echo \\\"new=[\\${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}] d=[\\${KATI_NEW_INPUTS_D}] f=[\\${KATI_NEW_INPUTS_F}]\\\" > log.txt\"\n  Ronin      build     [1/1] echo \"new=[${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}] d=[${KATI_NEW_INPUTS_D}] f=[${KATI_NEW_INPUTS_F}]\" > log.txt\n\nThe third line is what the filing did not have, and it is the whole reason the fix is where it is. Make mode's `-n` sets `verbose`, so the reporter shows `CommandSpec::command` — the launch's command line, in which kati's reference is nested inside a double-quoted `-c` argument and therefore spelt `\\${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}`. A build with no `-n` shows `CommandSpec::description` — the recipe's own text, in which the same reference is spelt `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}`. One reference, two spellings, two readers. Both were showing the name.\n\nWHERE, and why it is a SECOND substitution rather than a move of the first. `Builder::deferred_launch_command` substitutes as the command launches, and that is where it has to stay: `edgehash` is taken over `CommandSpec::command` and its answer is what the build log keeps to decide whether the recipe changed since last time. `$?` is a value that differs from run to run by construction, so a resolved command in the log would make every target carrying one look rebuilt every time. So the launch's text and the reader's text are two different questions about the same edge, and the new answer is `Builder::deferred_narration` (src/build/deferred.rs), which runs both passes because the two texts spell the reference two ways and neither pass can find what the other replaces.\n\nThe reporter now takes `Narrated` — the two texts a command is shown by — instead of `&CommandSpec` (src/build/reporter.rs), and `Builder::narration` (src/build/command.rs) fills them in for an edge that deferred anything and hands the spec's own straight through for one that did not, which is every Ninja edge and most Make ones. `defers_a_reference` is the guard, so nothing changed on the path of a build that has no deferred freshness and no settled names.\n\nTHE MATRIX, each cell run through reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and then through Ronin. Ronin now agrees on the value in all of them; it showed the placeholder in six.\n\n  WHICH REFERENCE\n    `$?` under `-n`                       GNU p1 sub/p2                        FIXED\n    `$(?D)` and `$(?F)` beside it         GNU `. sub` and `p1 p2` — the two\n        halves have names of their own and both reached the reader             FIXED\n    `KATI_SETTLED_N` under `VPATH`        GNU first=out.o all=out.o new=out.o;\n        the second family the same substitution fills in, reached through\n        `$<` and `$^` rather than through `$?`                                 FIXED\n    `$^` and `$<` with nothing deferred   agreed — the control\n\n  WHICH READER\n    an ordinary build's progress line     GNU echoes the recipe; Ronin's\n        description held the bare spelling                                     FIXED\n    `-n`'s command line                   the escaped spelling                 FIXED\n    `-W`/`--what-if` with `-n`            same line, same fix                  FIXED\n    `-q`                                  narrates nothing — the control\n    `-t`                                  narrates the same description, and\n        the recipe it names is the one not being run                           FIXED\n    a recipe over the response-file\n        threshold                         narration names the script file and\n        the description is empty, so no reference is on this path at all —\n        the control, and `-n` leaves no `.rsp` behind\n\n  WHICH VALUE\n    an archive member's `$?`              GNU `a.o b.o`; the published-name\n        arm, and GNU's own SV 61436 shape                                      FIXED\n    a composed child's `$?`               GNU `p1 p2`, spelt from the child's\n        own directory rather than `sub/p1 sub/p2`                              FIXED\n    a `::` chain's entries and dependent  GNU `p1`, `p2`, then `out.o`         FIXED\n\nTEETH, each change parked and the new gate run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the narration substitution entirely | 5 of 5 |\n| the response-file pass alone (bare spelling) | 1 of 5 — the ordinary build |\n| the inline pass alone (escaped spelling) | 4 of 5 — every `-n` cell |\nThe last two rows are why `deferred_narration` is two passes rather than one: each half is load-bearing for a different reader.\n\nTHE GATE, and it is not the ported corpus. make_port compares files and exit status by design — \"a case that means to test output writes it to a file\" — and `-n` writes nothing, so narration is unreachable from there. The five cases are in tests/make_regressions.rs, which runs the binary under the name `make` and reads its stdout: a_dry_run_narrates_the_new_input_names_it_would_have_filled_in, a_build_narrates_the_new_input_names_its_recipe_ran_with, a_dry_run_narrates_the_name_the_search_settled_on, a_dry_run_narrates_an_archive_members_published_name, a_composed_childs_dry_run_narrates_the_childs_own_spelling. Each asserts the value GNU Make prints and asserts the placeholder is absent; the line around the value is Ninja's and is not gated, which is `[spec:ronin:req:make.narration+1]` as written — \"the shared reporter names the action that actually runs\".\n\nINVENTORY, and the movement is this node's: 1,285 rows at 95 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,166 narration, where it was 97/0/24/1,164. TWO rows moved, both audited and both from unclassified to narration:\n    features/archives.diff.12   ninja-progress+recipe-echo+evaluation\n                              → ninja-progress+recipe-echo+intermediate-sweep\n    features/archives.diff.14   the same\nThose two are the SV 61436 archive cases. Their residue was `evaluation` — the classifier's name for \"exact output cannot tell graph intent from runner behaviour\" — for exactly this reason: `recipe-echo` is recognised from the counter's own payload, and a payload holding `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}` is not the recipe line GNU echoed. With the value in it, it is. The compiler bucket stays ZERO.\n\nGATES, run serially on this tree, kati unmoved at 9819de9: fmt clean in both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace tests 1,058 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored — up five, and the five are this node's own gate; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; make_port 4/4 over 1,134 gated cases, no recording touched, eleven divergence sidecars unmoved; conformance 336 normalised identical / 51 differing, 286 raw, unmoved — and it cannot move here, that gate driving rkati's own executor rather than Ronin's reporter; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 manifest respellings, unmoved; upstream inventory 1,285 rows at 95/0/24/1,166 with the two rows above audited; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 19 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 30 s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential, outcome, boundary and persistence band, including the dry-run inertness case; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 813 passed / 0 failed, unmoved — the new cases need the Make front end.\n\nNO NEW DEFECT FILED. The one thing seen beside this and deliberately not chased: a Make-mode `-n` leaves `.ninja_log` and `.ninja_deps` in the tree. That is the manifest front end's own persistence and `ronin-dryrun-log-divergence` is the node that settled what a dry run may write to it; nothing here touches that path."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T09:11:20.912232041Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T10:24:19.86737883Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "Count correction on the completion note above. The matrix is THIRTEEN cells, not the unstated number the closing line implied, and the placeholder reached the reader in TEN of them rather than in six — the sentence \"it showed the placeholder in six\" is wrong and undercounts the fix.\n\nCounted off the note's own table: WHICH REFERENCE is four cells, three FIXED and `$^`/`$<` the control; WHICH READER is six, four FIXED with `-q` and the response-file threshold as controls; WHICH VALUE is three, all FIXED. Three controls, ten fixed, thirteen in all.\n\nNothing else in the note changes: every cell listed was measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and then against Ronin, and every FIXED mark is a cell where the narration held `${KATI_NEW_INPUTS}`, one of its two halves, or a `KATI_SETTLED_N` before this landed and holds the value after."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-one-shell-recipe-is-one-shell-in-katis-own-executor-too
                title "Run a .ONESHELL recipe as one script in kati's executor too"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-target-specific-shell-does-not-reach-katis-own-executor)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T07:13:27.696962587Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-a-target-specific-shell-does-not-reach-katis-own-executor, on the same executor and for the same reason — a decision the compiled path makes that the executor beside it never learned. PRE-EXISTING and measured against the parked build, so it is not that node's regression.\n\n`.ONESHELL:` says a recipe's lines are ONE script handed to ONE shell. rkati's exec mode runs each line as a shell of its own, which is three measured behaviours rather than a cosmetic one. Each row is GNU Make 4.4.1 and Ronin agreeing, and rkati alone:\n\n    .ONESHELL:\n    all:\n    \\tx=1\n    \\techo \"x=$$x\"\n      GNU / Ronin   x=1\n      rkati         x=              -- a variable does not survive the line\n\n    .ONESHELL:\n    all:\n    \\tcd sub\n    \\tpwd\n      GNU / Ronin   .../w/sub\n      rkati         .../w           -- a `cd` does not survive it either\n\n    .ONESHELL:\n    all:\n    \\tfalse\n    \\techo reached\n      GNU / Ronin   `reached`, exit 0\n      rkati         `rkati: *** [all] Error 1`, exit 2 — the first line's status\n                    is the recipe's, where under one shell it is not\n\nWHERE, and it is the same shape the sibling node found. Every reader of `flags.one_shell` in kati is in `ninja.rs`: the joining separator (`b\"\\n\"` against `b\" ; \"`), `translate_command`, `stops_at_a_failure`, `arms_errexit`, the subshell decision and `shell_is_a_path`. `exec.rs` names it nowhere. So the compiled path knows what `.ONESHELL` means and the executor beside it does not, and each of the three cells above is one of those readers.\n\nWhat the executor already has right is the PREFIX reading: `read_one_shell_prefixes_off_the_first_line` runs in `CommandEvaluator::eval`, which both paths call, so a `@` below the first line is Make's or the script's according to the same rule in both. It is only the joining that is missing.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS. The joining is not a loop in `exec_node` — `ninja.rs` decides the separator, whether the script arms errexit, whether it needs a subshell and how the shell is named, all from the same flag, and those decisions belong beside each other rather than copied. The shape to look for is the one this node's sibling used: a decision the evaluator has already made, left where both consumers can read it, rather than a second implementation in the executor. `Command::force_no_subshell` and `shell_flag` are already carried per command and are where the answer would naturally hang.\n\nProbe the matrix beside the three cells above: `.ONESHELL` with `.SHELLFLAGS` set, with a non-Bourne `SHELL` where the interior prefixes are the script's, with `-` and `+` on the first line and below it, with `$(MAKE)` on a line, under `-n`, and with `-i` and `.IGNORE`.\n\nNot gated anywhere: the conformance corpus is 336/51 with and without the sibling's fix and never reaches these, and the ported corpus runs Ronin, whose compiled path is right. kati/tests/exec_shell.rs is the surface this would extend."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T10:20:30.18135878Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T10:21:24.519349957Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was right about all three cells, right about the shape and right that `Command` is where the answer hangs. What it did not have is a SECOND place the same fact is needed, one layer below the joining — and without it the joining makes the three cells worse rather than better, which is what the first measurement showed.\n\nVERIFIED FIRST, against the rkati at b538b66 before anything was touched. Each row is GNU Make 4.4.1 and Ronin agreeing, and rkati alone:\n\n    .ONESHELL: / x=1 / @echo \"x=$$x\"      GNU x=1        rkati x=\n    .ONESHELL: / cd sub / pwd             GNU .../sub    rkati .../\n    .ONESHELL: / false / echo reached     GNU reached,0  rkati Error 1, exit 2\n\nWHAT THE FILING DID NOT HAVE. Joining the lines is not enough, because `run_command` (kati/src-rs/fileutil.rs) has GNU Make's own direct-exec fast path in front of it — a line with no shell syntax is exec'd without a shell — and that path reads the text as ONE command's words. A newline is a blank like any other to it. So with the lines joined and nothing else changed, `echo one\\necho two` became the argument list `echo one echo two` and the recipe printed `one echo two`; three of the six cells got worse, not better. GNU Make asks the same question at the same point: `construct_command_argv_internal` (job.c:3034) has `else if (one_shell && *p == '\\n') goto slow`, \"in .ONESHELL mode \\n is a separator like ; or &&\". kati's tokenizer already took the flag — `simple_command::tokenize`'s `one_shell` parameter — and `run_command` passed it a hardcoded `false`. Two changes, and each fails cases the other does not.\n\nWHERE, and it is the shape the filing asked for rather than a second implementation.\n\nONE — `CommandEvaluator::launches` (kati/src-rs/command.rs) beside `eval`. `eval` answers with the recipe's LINES, which is what a compiler wants: each has to be translated for the binding it lands in, classified for `$(MAKE)`, and read for whether it is work worth announcing. `launches` answers with the PROCESSES the recipe is, which is one per line except under `.ONESHELL`. The executor asks the second question and ninja.rs goes on asking the first, so there is one assembler and no copy.\n\nTWO — `one_shell_script` sits beside `read_one_shell_prefixes_off_the_first_line`, because the two are one reading of one flag: the prefixes are the first line's BECAUSE there is only one line, and this assembles that line. Every per-line flag the runner reads has already been made one answer there. `ONE_SHELL_SEPARATOR` is the separator both sides spell it with, ninja.rs included — a separator that differed between them would be two different scripts from one makefile.\n\nTHREE — `ShellToReadWith::one_script` says the text handed over is a script whose newlines separate commands rather than one command line. On the shell rather than on the command because that struct is what the fast path is asked about; `$(shell)` and the regen replay say false, and GNU reaches the same answer the same way — `func_shell_base` passes no line flags and `one_shell` is only consulted for a recipe.\n\nTHE MATRIX, twenty cells, each run through reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, then Ronin, then rkati. All three now agree on eighteen; rkati diverged on eleven. The two that still differ are new nodes, filed below, and neither is `.ONESHELL`'s.\n\n  THE THREE THE FILING NAMED\n    a variable set on one line             GNU x=1                          FIXED\n    a `cd`                                 GNU .../sub                      FIXED\n    a failing first line                   GNU reached, exit 0              FIXED\n\n  THE PREFIXES, WHICH ARE READ ALREADY AND HAD TO STAY THAT WAY\n    `@` below the first line               echoed and run — the `@` is the\n        script's text, not a flag                                           FIXED\n    `+` below the first line               the same                         FIXED\n    `-` below the first line               GNU exit 0: the line is neither\n        forgiven nor fatal, it is a command in the middle of a script       FIXED\n    `-` on the FIRST line                  GNU exit 0 and SILENT, where\n        rkati said `[all] Error 1 (ignored)`                                FIXED\n    a non-Bourne `SHELL`                   the interior prefixes are the\n        script's and one launch gets the lot                                FIXED\n\n  WHAT DECIDES THE STATUS\n    `-i`                                   GNU reached, exit 0, silent      FIXED\n    `.IGNORE:`                             the same                         FIXED\n    `.POSIX:`                              GNU exit 2 and `reached` NEVER\n        printed — the control, and the direction the change must not lose   FIXED\n\n  BESIDE IT\n    `.SHELLFLAGS := -xc`                   the trace appears and the\n        variable survives                                                   FIXED\n    `$(MAKE)` on a line below the first    the child runs, in order       agreed\n    `-n`                                   both print both lines and run\n        nothing — agreed before and after, the control\n    a single-line `.ONESHELL` recipe       agreed — the boundary\n    `all: ;`                               nothing to do — agreed\n    a bare blank line in the middle        agreed\n    `.SHELLFLAGS := -e -c`                 DIVERGENT, filed, and NOT this\n        node's: it diverges identically with no `.ONESHELL` in the makefile\n    a line that EXPANDS to nothing         DIVERGENT, filed, and not this\n        node's either: Ronin's compiled path drops it too, so it is the\n        evaluator's and not the executor's\n\nTEETH, each change parked on its own and the new gate run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the joining (`launches` answers with the lines) | 5 of 6 |\n| the fast path's newline gate (`one_script` false) | 3 of 6 |\nThe sixth under the first park is `a_posix_one_shell_recipe_stops`, which passes either way and is RECORDED AS SUCH: a shell per line stops at the same failure for a different reason, so the case cannot fail today — and it is the one that fails when the strictness moves.\n\nTHE GATE, six cases extending kati/tests/exec_shell.rs, which runs the `rkati` binary and reads what it said: one_shell_keeps_a_variable_set, one_shell_keeps_a_directory_change, one_shell_reports_its_last_status, one_shell_starts_one_shell, a_dash_below_the_first_line, a_posix_one_shell_recipe_stops. Every assertion reads a WHOLE LINE of the output rather than a substring, because the recipe echo prints the script before it runs — so every word of the makefile is already in that text and only what the shell answered is new. The launch-count case is the one the other five are consequences of: three lines, one `OWN[own] -c` line, and the whole recipe inside its argument.\n\nWHY THE CONFORMANCE CORPUS CANNOT SEE THIS, which is the audit rather than a pass. 336 normalised identical / 51 differing, 286 raw — unmoved, and it could not move: `grep -rl ONESHELL kati/` returns ten source files and NOT ONE test case. The vendored suite has no `.ONESHELL` makefile at all, which is why a gap in the very binary that suite drives survived it.\n\nGATES, run serially on this tree, kati at eaf7661 on nplan/make-a-one-shell-recipe-is-one-shell-in-katis-own-executor-too, pushed before this pin: fmt clean in both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace tests 1,064 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored — up six, and the six are this node's own gate; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; make_port 4/4 over 1,139 gated cases, no recording touched, nine divergence sidecars unmoved; conformance 336/51, 286 raw, unmoved, audited above; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 manifest respellings, unmoved; upstream inventory 1,285 rows at 95 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,166 narration, tsv untouched, no row moved, compiler still zero; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 25 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 34 s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential, outcome, boundary and persistence band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 819 passed / 0 failed, up six for the same reason — these six are kati's own binary and need no Make front end on the Ronin side.\n\nTWO DEFECTS FILED, both found here and both measured as PRE-EXISTING:\n  make-a-multi-word-shellflags-reaches-katis-executor-as-one-word — `run_command`\n    builds `[shell, shellflag, cmd]`, so `.SHELLFLAGS := -e -c` reaches the shell\n    as one word and the recipe does not run at all. Not `.ONESHELL`'s: it\n    diverges identically without one. GNU has two answers here rather than one,\n    and kati has the second of them already, written for the compiling side.\n  make-a-recipe-line-that-expands-to-nothing-is-a-blank-line-of-a-one-shell-script\n    — `eval`'s `if !cmd.is_empty()` is right for every recipe GNU chops into\n    lines and wrong for the one it does not chop at all. Shared by both\n    consumers, so Ronin drops the line too; the here-document shape is what\n    would make it more than an echo, and the filing says to measure that first."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T10:21:31.990988436Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T10:24:35.995689526Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "Count correction on the completion note above, and one cell mislabelled. The matrix is NINETEEN cells, not twenty; seventeen now agree, not eighteen; and rkati diverged on THIRTEEN of them before this landed, not eleven.\n\nCounted off the note's own table: THE THREE THE FILING NAMED is three, THE PREFIXES is five, WHAT DECIDES THE STATUS is three, BESIDE IT is eight. Four cells agreed before and after — `-n`, a single-line `.ONESHELL` recipe, `all: ;`, and a bare blank line in the middle. Thirteen diverged and are fixed. Two diverged and still do; both are filed as their own nodes.\n\nThe mislabelled one is `$(MAKE)` on a line below the first, which the note marks \"agreed\". The child ran in both tools before this landed and runs in both after, so the effect was never wrong — but the ECHO was: rkati printed each line as it reached it, so `echo one` and its output came out before the `$(MAKE)` line was announced, where GNU Make echoes the whole script and then runs it. It belongs with the thirteen and is why that number is thirteen rather than twelve."
                        after_done true
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T10:28:35.426484133Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "THE RELEASE GATE FOR THIS DISPATCH, run on the tree at 85e0c03 with all three nodes landed.\n\n`scripts/check-release.sh` PASSED IN FULL, exit 0, every stage including the performance one — and this time NOT on a quiet host, which is the measurement worth recording. The foreign silo the previous dispatch measured against was running throughout: four `silo-920c8636f8` processes, two of them at about 100%, with the load average between 13.5 and 17.4 across the run.\n\nThat is the third reading of the same question and it settles the standing note rather than merely repeating it. The previous dispatch found the gate green on a quiet host and then found `check-release.sh` and `check-performance.sh` refusing over two different workloads under the same silo. Here the gate is green WITH the silo on the machine. A regression does not come and go with another tenant's load; the refusals were noise, and so is the load.\n\nThe numbers, in nanoseconds and as the gate reports them (mean, min, max):\n\n    large-manifest-parse   ronin 151.8   ninja 319.2   samurai-c 308.4\n    scheduler-barrier      ronin  42.2   ninja  38.9   samurai-c  35.9\n    clean-tree-noop        ronin   8.6   ninja  14.0   samurai-c  11.4\n\nNothing this dispatch changed is on any of those paths, and the reason is the same one the previous note gave: every workload here is a Ninja manifest, and the three changes are the narration substitution for an edge carrying deferred freshness, a second settled-name answer for an output more than one edge decides, and kati's own executor — a relation a Ninja manifest cannot declare, and a binary these workloads never load.\n\nBeside it, and unchanged: `nplan spec uncovered` says no uncovered rules; `nplan spec stale` says no stale references; `nplan lint` clean; `nplan audit` proposes no transitions and reports HEAD on main. The 51 unsealed completions the audit lists are the standing backlog and belong to nobody here."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-double-colon-chains-dependent-reads-the-heads-name
                title "Give a double-colon chain's dependent the first entry's name, not the last"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-recipe-less-double-colon-entry-decides-nothing-about-the-chains-name)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T08:03:04.650915441Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-a-recipe-less-double-colon-entry-decides-nothing-about-the-chains-name, and PRE-EXISTING in its recipe-bearing form: measured on the binary at 98dc6e8, before that node touched anything, in a shape with no recipe-less entry in it at all.\n\nTHE RENAME REACHES FORWARD AND A DEPENDENT DOES NOT FOLLOW IT.\n\n    VPATH = src, src/out.o at 100, late at 200, keep at 50\n    all: out.o\n    \\t@echo \"all first=$<\" >> log.txt\n    out.o:: late\n    \\t@echo \"one at=$@\" >> log.txt\n    \\t@echo one >> $@\n    out.o:: keep\n    \\t@echo \"two at=$@\" >> log.txt\n    \\t@echo two >> $@\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   one at=out.o, two at=src/out.o, all first=out.o\n      Ronin            one at=out.o, two at=src/out.o, all first=src/out.o\n\nThe ENTRIES agree, which is what makes this its own defect rather than a hole in the sibling node: the chain's own answers are right in both, and only the dependent's differs.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. `update_file_1` (remake.c:885) renames with `while (file) { file->name = file->hname; file = file->prev; }`, and `prev` chains FORWARD, so an entry found current renames itself and the entries after it and no others. A dependent holds the `struct file` that `enter_file` returned, which is the HEAD of the chain — so what a dependent reads is whether the FIRST entry was found current, and no later entry's rename can reach it. Here it stands for `settle_shared` (src/graph/searched.rs), which holds one verdict per target, latched forward as the chain runs, and read by everyone afterwards — so a dependent gets the last entry's answer.\n\nFOUR CELLS MEASURED, all four diverging:\n    head remade, second current                    GNU out.o, Ronin src/out.o\n    head remade, second recipe-less and current    the same\n    head remade, second recipe-less, third remade  the same, with two files\n        written in the run and the dependent naming the one the head wrote\n    head recipe-less whose prerequisite this run remade, second current\n        GNU out.o and NOTHING is written anywhere — which settles that the rule\n        is the head's verdict rather than \"which name was written\", because no\n        name was\n\nAND THREE CONTROLS THAT AGREE: head current and second remade (the sibling node's own cell); the recipe-less head of the node above; a lone `::` record, which takes the single-node path.\n\nWHERE. Not `settle_shared` alone: the latch it keeps is right for the entries, which read it in turn as each is reached. What is missing is a second answer — the head's — for everyone who is not an entry of that chain. The reading is `found_name_stands` (src/graph/searched.rs), and its callers split cleanly: `settled_spelling` (src/build/deferred.rs) is asked BOTH by a chain entry about its own `$@` and by a dependent about a prerequisite, and the two can be told apart by whether the asking edge's deferred outputs contain the node; `deferred_new_inputs_value`, `late_binding` (src/build/command.rs) and `touch_outputs` (src/build/touch.rs) are only ever dependents. Which action heads a chain is kati's to say — `build_grouped_double_member` (kati/src-rs/dep.rs) sorts the actions by creation index to wire the ordering, and the first of that sort is the head.\n\nProbe `$?` and `$^` beside `$<`, `-t` over such a chain (`-t` with a remade head and a current second AGREES today and is the cell that says whether the touch follows the head), a chain of three with the current entry in each position, `GPATH` beside `VPATH`, and a dependent that is itself an entry of another `::` chain.\n\nGATED, both shapes, by recordings taken from the oracle with divergence sidecars naming this node: a-double-colon-chains-dependent-reads-the-heads-name (the recipe-bearing shape, which diverged before the sibling node too) and a-recipe-less-double-colon-entry-last-leaves-a-dependent-alone (the shape that node newly reaches, and which agreed before only because a recipe-less entry then answered for nothing)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T09:51:37.665275522Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T09:52:30.440352724Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was right about the cell, right about the mechanism, right that `settle_shared`'s latch is correct for the entries and right that what was missing is a SECOND answer. It was wrong about one of the four call sites it named, and the correction is what made the split one rule rather than a list.\n\nVERIFIED FIRST, against the binary at 28c6113 before anything was touched, `src/out.o` at 100, `late` at 200, `keep` at 50:\n\n  GNU Make 4.4.1   one at=out.o, all first=out.o\n  Ronin            one at=out.o, all first=src/out.o\n\nThe entries agree and only the dependent differs, exactly as filed.\n\nWHAT THE FILING HAD, and it holds all the way down. `update_file_1` (remake.c:885) ends an entry it found current with `while (file) { file->name = file->hname; file = file->prev; }`, and `enter_file` (file.c:196) appends each new entry with `f->last->prev = new`, so `prev` walks towards the END of the record: an entry found current renames itself and everything after it. `lookup_file` answers with the hash slot, which is the FIRST entry, and that is the `struct file` a dependent's `struct dep` holds — so a dependent reads the head's verdict and no later rename can reach it.\n\nWHAT THE FILING HAD WRONG. It listed `touch_outputs` (src/build/touch.rs) among the callers that \"are only ever dependents\". It is the opposite: `touchable_outputs` returns the edge's own deferred outputs, so the node it asks about is always the asking entry's own target, and `-t` must read the LATCH. Measured, and it is a live cell rather than a technicality — `a-touch-over-a-double-colon-chain-a-remade-head-leads` fails when the touch is given the head's answer, because a record whose head is remade and whose third entry follows a rename brings TWO files up to date and each entry chose a different one.\n\nThat correction is what turned four call sites into one rule. All four ask the same question — which of a searched-for node's two names does THIS edge read — and the answer is the filing's own discriminator applied uniformly: whether the node is one of the asking edge's deferred outputs. An entry of the record reads the latch; every other edge, dependent or not, reads the head. `settled_name_stands` (src/graph/searched.rs) is that one rule, and `settled_spelling`, `deferred_new_inputs_value`, `late_binding` and `touch_outputs` all call it rather than `found_name_stands`.\n\nTHE MATRIX, each cell run through reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and then through Ronin. Ronin now agrees on all fifteen; it diverged on six.\n\n  WHOSE VERDICT THE DEPENDENT READS\n    head remade, second current                  GNU out.o                      FIXED\n    head remade, second recipe-less and current  the same                       FIXED\n    head remade, second recipe-less, third\n        remade                                   GNU out.o, and BOTH names are\n        written in the run — the cell that rules out \"whichever name the run\n        wrote\", from the side where a wrong answer still names a real file  FIXED\n    head recipe-less whose phony prerequisite\n        this run reaches, second current         GNU out.o and NOTHING is\n        written anywhere — the same rule from the other side, and the sharpest\n        of the two                                                          FIXED\n    head CURRENT, second remade                  GNU src/out.o — the control,\n        and it was already gated                                          agreed\n    a lone `::` record                           the single-node path — control\n\n  WHICH VARIABLES\n    `$<`, `$^` and `$?` in one dependent         GNU out.o for all three off\n        one reading, with an ordinary prerequisite beside the chain saying the\n        reading is per prerequisite rather than per list                    FIXED\n    a dependent that is itself an ENTRY of\n        another record                           GNU out.o: this edge reads its\n        own record's latch for `$@` and the other record's HEAD for the\n        prerequisite it waited on, in one recipe                            FIXED\n\n  BESIDE IT\n    `GPATH = src`                                agreed — the name is already\n        the found one, so the two answers coincide\n    `-t` with a remade head and a current\n        second                                   agreed — one file touched\n    `-t` with a remade head, a recipe-less\n        second and a remade third                GNU touches out.o AND\n        src/out.o — the entry reading, and the cell the filing got backwards\n                                                                          agreed\n    `-t` with a current head                     already gated — control\n    `-q`                                         exit 1 both — control\n    an entry's own `$@` after a rename           already gated — control\n\nWHERE, and it is three places.\n\nONE — kati says which entry heads the record. `GroupedDoubleAction::heads_the_record` (kati/src-rs/dep.rs), set in `build_grouped_double_member`, which is the only place the record's entries are together and in the order the makefile wrote them — they are sorted there already, to wire the ordering. Set on every build of a member rather than only on the one that created the actions: the head is a property of the record, and a member reached a second time finds the same actions again. It travels as `SinkEdge::deferred_freshness_heads_the_record`.\n\nTWO — the graph carries it as a relation rather than as a Make fact. `DeferredSpec::heads_the_group` / `DeferredFreshness::heads_the_group` say \"this is the first of the several edges that decide one output's freshness\", which is what the scheduler needs to know and all of it; nothing below the front end learns what a `::` record is.\n\nTHREE — the runtime keeps two answers. `RuntimeState::head_names` beside `searched_names`, written by `settle_shared` for the head edge alone, read by `head_name_stands`. Nothing recorded there means no group answered about the node — a lone `::` record, an ordinary deferred rule, a plain searched-for target — and then the one answer is everybody's, which is what keeps the second map to the few nodes that need it.\n\nONE THING MEASURED AND THEN REMOVED. `mark_written_here` gained a clause giving the head's answer a `Written` state, mirroring the one the other map has. Parked, it failed not one case of 1,139, and the reason is structural rather than luck: the rescan a finished edge triggers walks downstream, and the head of a record is the one entry nothing in that record points back at, so no scan after the head has launched can reach it. The clause is gone and the reason is recorded where the guard would have been.\n\nTEETH, each change parked on its own and the whole corpus of 1,139 gated cases run against it:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the head's answer for a dependent | 6 |\n| kati naming the head at all | 6 — the same six from the other side |\n| the entry/dependent split, everyone reading the head | 4, two of them PRE-EXISTING |\n| the `Written` state for the head's answer | 0 — removed, see above |\nThe two pre-existing cases in the third row are `a-double-colon-entry-before-a-current-one-remakes-the-name-as-written` and `a-recipe-less-double-colon-entry-in-the-middle-settles-what-follows`, which is the latch this node had to leave alone proving it was left alone.\n\nBOTH DIVERGENCE MARKERS RESOLVED AND DELETED, which is the acceptance the gate forces: a recorded divergence that has been fixed fails the run. `a-double-colon-chains-dependent-reads-the-heads-name` (the recipe-bearing shape) and `a-recipe-less-double-colon-entry-last-leaves-a-dependent-alone` (the shape the recipe-less settlement newly reached). ELEVEN sidecars are now NINE.\n\nFIVE FIXTURES, all recorded from the oracle, no existing recording rewritten:\n  a-double-colon-chains-head-answers-every-automatic-variable — `$<`, `$^` and\n    `$?` off one reading, with an ordinary prerequisite beside the chain.\n  a-double-colon-entry-reads-another-chains-head-name — one recipe reading its\n    own record's latch and another record's head.\n  a-recipe-less-double-colon-head-still-answers-a-dependent — nothing written\n    anywhere and the dependent still names the head's spelling.\n  a-double-colon-chain-writing-both-names-hands-back-the-heads — two files\n    written in one run, and the dependent names the one the head wrote.\n  a-touch-over-a-double-colon-chain-a-remade-head-leads — `-t` over the same\n    three-entry record, which is the cell that gates the entry reading.\n\nGATES, run serially on this tree, kati at bf46485 on nplan/make-a-double-colon-chains-dependent-reads-the-heads-name, pushed before this pin: fmt clean in both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace tests 1,058 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, unmoved from the previous landing; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; make_port 4/4 over 1,139 gated cases with five new recordings, none rewritten, and NINE divergence sidecars where there were eleven; conformance 336 normalised identical / 51 differing, 286 raw, unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 manifest respellings, unmoved; upstream inventory 1,285 rows at 95 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1,166 narration, tsv untouched, no row moved, compiler still zero; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 15 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 29 s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential, outcome, boundary and persistence band; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 813 passed / 0 failed.\n\nNO NEW DEFECT FILED."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T09:52:38.003841363Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
