            {
                id make-a-multi-word-shellflags-reaches-katis-executor-as-one-word
                title "Split .SHELLFLAGS into words in kati's own executor"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-one-shell-recipe-is-one-shell-in-katis-own-executor-too)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T10:19:24.839568585Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-a-one-shell-recipe-is-one-shell-in-katis-own-executor-too, on the same executor, and PRE-EXISTING — it needs no `.ONESHELL` at all and was measured with and without one.\n\n`.SHELLFLAGS` holding more than one word reaches rkati's executor as a single argv word, so the shell is handed `-e -c` where GNU Make hands it `-e` and `-c`. Measured:\n\n    .SHELLFLAGS := -e -c\n    all:\n    \\t@echo one\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   one, exit 0\n      Ronin            [1/1] echo one / one, exit 0\n      rkati            /bin/sh: 0: Illegal option -\n                       rkati: *** [all] Error 2, exit 2\n\nSo the recipe does not run at all, and the diagnostic blames the shell for a spelling nobody wrote.\n\nWHERE. `run_command` (kati/src-rs/fileutil.rs) builds its argv as `[shell, shellflag, cmd]` — three words, whatever the flags were. `ShellToReadWith::flag` is one slice and every caller hands it one.\n\nWHAT GNU DOES, and it is two different answers rather than one. `construct_command_argv_internal` (job.c) builds the ordinary path's command as the TEXT `$(SHELL) $(.SHELLFLAGS) LINE` and then re-tokenizes the whole of it, so the flags are split like any other words on a command line and quoting applies to them. The `.ONESHELL` branch does not: it parses the flags with `construct_command_argv_internal` itself — \"Parse shellflags using construct_command_argv_internal to handle quotes\" (job.c:3293 onwards) — passing no shell and no flags of its own, so `-E 'use warnings FATAL => \"all\";'` is two words and the quotes come off. kati already has the second of those as `simple_command::shell_flag_argv`, written for the compiling side, and the executor uses neither.\n\nProbe both branches, and probe the quoted form specifically, because that is where the two answers come apart: a `.SHELLFLAGS` whose value contains a quoted argument is split one way by the ordinary path and another by the one-shell branch. Probe a `.SHELLFLAGS` set per target beside the global one — that reading already works, `a_targets_own_shell_flags_arrive_with_it` gates it — and the empty value, which GNU hands over as no word rather than as one empty one.\n\nNot gated anywhere: the vendored conformance corpus has no case that sets `.SHELLFLAGS` to more than one word, and the ported corpus runs Ronin, whose compiled path splits them correctly. kati/tests/exec_shell.rs is the surface this extends."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T17:25:23.944374109Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Delivered. The filing was right about the mechanism, right about where the code was, and right that GNU has two answers rather than one — and WRONG about the blast radius in the one way that matters: this is not rkati's alone. Ronin's compiled path splits a recipe's flags itself, so a recipe was never the exposure, but `$(shell)` is run by kati's executor whoever the front end is. `.SHELLFLAGS := -e -c` was emptying every `$(shell)` in a RONIN build too, silently, with `/bin/sh: 0: Illegal option -` on stderr and an empty expansion behind it. That cell is in the matrix below and it is the reason this node was worth more than its title says.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, confirmed. `run_command` (kati/src-rs/fileutil.rs) built `[shell, shellflag, cmd]` — three words whatever the flags were — so `-e -c` reached the shell as one argv word. `dash` reads it as the option `-e -c`, refuses with `Illegal option - `, and the recipe never runs.\n\nWHAT GNU DOES, read off job.c and then measured. Both branches were probed and the filing's guess about where they come apart is right, though not for the reason it gives:\n\n  * the ordinary branch assembles the TEXT `$(SHELL) $(.SHELLFLAGS) LINE` — the shell escaped, the flags copied in as they stand, the line escaped whole — and re-tokenizes all of it (job.c:3586). The flags are therefore split and quoted exactly like words on a command line, and the line comes back as the single argument its escaping made of it. Tokenizing the flags ALONE reaches the same words, because the tokenizer's leading-word rules are about the first word of a command line and there the shell is already ahead of them;\n  * the one-shell branch parses the flags with `construct_command_argv_internal` itself (job.c:3437) and builds `[SHELL] ++ flags ++ [script]` directly, never falling back to a command line.\n\nWHERE THEY COME APART, measured rather than reasoned. `SHELL := ./argv.sh`, `.SHELLFLAGS := \"-e\" -c`, recipe `@echo one`:\n\n    ordinary   GNU  [-e] [-c] [echo one]\n    .ONESHELL  GNU  [/bin/sh] [-c] [\"-e\" -c] [echo one]\n\nThe double quote is a shell character, so the flags go slow in both. The ordinary branch's whole line goes to a shell, which splits them properly; the one-shell branch's `if (argv)` guard is NEVER REACHED, because the inner call has already gone slow and answered with the argument list that starts a shell — so those three words are injected ahead of the script. That contradicts what `shell_flag_argv`'s doc comment claimed (\"flags the tokenizer hands back to a shell contribute nothing at all\"), which is corrected here, and the remaining behaviour is filed as make-one-shell-flags-a-shell-would-split-are-three-words-not-none rather than fixed in this landing.\n\nWHAT LANDED. `argv_to_exec` in fileutil.rs now gives GNU's three answers in GNU's order: direct exec, the one-shell argument list, and the ordinary shell-flags-line argument list, with `None` for the assembled command line that a shell reads. `command_line_flag_argv` is new in simple_command.rs beside the existing `shell_flag_argv`; the two are separate functions because GNU has two, and the doc on each says which branch it is. Where the ordinary tokenizer refuses — shell syntax in the flags, or a leading-word rule the shell ahead of them would have answered — the launch falls through to the assembled command line, which is GNU's own slow path and reaches the same words by letting a shell do the splitting.\n\nTWO CELLS CAME WITH IT, both named in the filing and both measured:\n\n  * an EMPTY `.SHELLFLAGS` is no argument rather than an empty one. GNU's assembled line has a run of blanks where the flags were, and a run of blanks is not a word, so the script arrives as the shell's FIRST argument — a file operand. 4.4.1 says `/bin/sh: 0: cannot open echo one: No such file`; rkati said `cannot open : No such file`, having passed an empty word ahead of it. Ronin was already right here;\n  * a `.ONESHELL` recipe's `SHELL` is a program and not a command line. Routing every one-shell launch through the one-shell argument list — rather than only those with a simple shell — is what fixes it, and it is in scope because the one-shell branch is where the flags splitting had to go anyway. `SHELL := ./argv.sh -q` under `.ONESHELL` gets `No such file or directory` against the whole spelling and `Error 127` from 4.4.1; rkati handed the spelling to `/bin/sh`, which split it and ran it. Ronin was already right here too, and says so in layout.rs's `shell_is_a_path`.\n\nTHE MATRIX. TWELVE cells, each one makefile run three ways — GNU Make 4.4.1, Ronin, rkati — with the binary at 85e0c03 as the \"before\" column. `argv.sh` is a shell that prints each argument on its own line, which is the only way to tell one word holding a blank from two words.\n\n  WHAT REACHES THE SHELL                       before          after\n    .SHELLFLAGS := -e -c, plain recipe         rkati Error 2   FIXED\n    .SHELLFLAGS := -e -c, .ONESHELL            rkati Error 2   FIXED\n    .SHELLFLAGS := -e -c, SHELL := argv.sh     rkati one word  FIXED\n    .SHELLFLAGS := -e 'a b' -c, plain          rkati one word  FIXED\n    .SHELLFLAGS := -e 'a b' -c, .ONESHELL      rkati one word  FIXED\n    .SHELLFLAGS := \"-e\" -c, plain              rkati one word  FIXED\n    all: .SHELLFLAGS := -e -c (target-scoped)  rkati one word  FIXED\n  THE EMPTY VALUE\n    .SHELLFLAGS :=, plain                      rkati empty arg FIXED\n    .SHELLFLAGS :=, .ONESHELL                  rkati empty arg FIXED\n  THE SHELL ITSELF\n    SHELL := argv.sh -q, .ONESHELL             rkati ran it    FIXED\n    SHELL := argv.sh -q, plain                 agreed          agreed\n  WHAT ELSE ASKS\n    $(shell) under .SHELLFLAGS := -e -c        BOTH broken     FIXED BOTH\n\nEleven of the twelve diverged before this landed and eleven are fixed; the twelfth agreed before and after and is the control that says the ordinary path's assembled command line was already splitting a multi-word SHELL correctly. One cell not in the count still diverges — `.SHELLFLAGS := \"-e\" -c` under `.ONESHELL`, the three-words case — and is its own node.\n\nGATES, run serially on the tree with this landed. Workspace 1070 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, which is the 1064 baseline plus this node's six new tests. `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 825 / 0, the 819 baseline plus the same six. Conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised, 286 raw, every family count unmoved. Equivalence 297 makefiles, 280 graphs compared, 0 disagreements, manifest respellings 8. Upstream inventory 1285 discovery differences at 95 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1166 narration — compiler stays ZERO. make_port 4 / 4 with all NINE divergence markers live. Real projects: vim 21s, zsh 37s, both at -j8. Ninja conformance 425 / 425 plus every differential. Lints clean. Port ledger 170 symbols. `scripts/check-release.sh` EXIT 0 IN FULL under a load average of about 14.\n\nNothing moved a corpus row, and the reason is the one the filing gave: no vendored conformance case sets `.SHELLFLAGS` to more than one word, and no ported case does either. kati/tests/exec_shell.rs is the surface, and it grew five cases — flags as words, the one-shell launch's own quoting, the blank value, the `$(shell)` reach, and the one-shell shell being a program. All five were measured by parking the source change and rerunning: five fail without it, and the twelve that were there pass either way.\n\nTHE HOST. `user@1000.service` died a fourth time mid-run, killed by SIGKILL with a 3.5G peak, taking a `cargo test --workspace` with it. Reset-failed, started, verified with `systemd-run --user true`, and the rerun passed identically.\n\nONE THING SEEN AND NOT FILED. `.ONESHELL:` with a NON-BOURNE `SHELL` and `@` on every recipe line: GNU keeps the interior `@` (`is_bourne_compatible_shell` gates the stripping), so the script is `echo AAA\\n@echo BBB`; kati's `hoisted_narration` then reads the leading `echo AAA` as the edge description and drops the line from the script, because what remains — `@echo BBB` — passes `is_work_worth_announcing` on the strength of the `@` that a Bourne shell would not have left there. Ronin's manifest comes out `description = AAA` / `command = argv.sh -c \"@echo BBB\"`. That is the standing narration hoist behaving as designed on an input the design did not have in mind, not a new defect, and it is the compiling side rather than this node's. Recorded here so it is not lost; `echo` without `@` on the same shape agrees in all three tools."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T17:26:03.008643868Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-recipe-line-that-expands-to-nothing-is-a-blank-line-of-a-one-shell-script
                title "Keep a blank line in a .ONESHELL script where the expansion left one"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-one-shell-recipe-is-one-shell-in-katis-own-executor-too)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T10:19:54.955562774Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-a-one-shell-recipe-is-one-shell-in-katis-own-executor-too, and PRE-EXISTING on BOTH sides — this one is not the executor's, it is the evaluator's, and Ronin's compiled path drops the line exactly as rkati's executor does.\n\nUnder `.ONESHELL` a recipe line whose expansion is empty is still a LINE of the one script, and GNU Make keeps it. Measured:\n\n    .ONESHELL:\n    EMPTY =\n    all:\n    \\t$(EMPTY)\n    \\techo after\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   <blank line> / echo after / after\n      Ronin            [1/1] echo after / after\n      rkati            echo after / after\n\nThe blank line GNU echoes is the script's own first line. Without `.ONESHELL` the three agree: the line is its own command, it expands to nothing, and GNU starts no shell for it either.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT ONLY THE ECHO, which is what it looks like at first. A blank line in a shell script is a no-op, so the three runs above do the same thing — but under `.ONESHELL` the lines are the text of one script, and a script can be reading its own text. A here-document is the shape that makes it plain:\n\n    .ONESHELL:\n    all:\n    \\tcat <<'EOF' > out\n    \\t$(EMPTY)\n    \\tline\n    \\tEOF\n\nwhere a dropped line changes what `out` holds. Probe that before deciding the size of this: if the here-document case really does differ, the defect is a wrong file rather than a missing blank line in a log.\n\nWHERE. `CommandEvaluator::eval` (kati/src-rs/command.rs) keeps a line only `if !cmd.is_empty()`, which is right for every recipe GNU chops into lines and wrong for the one it does not chop at all: `chop_commands` leaves a `.ONESHELL` recipe whole, so an expansion that came out empty leaves a blank line inside it rather than removing a command. The guard is shared by both consumers, which is why both drop it.\n\nMind what the guard is also doing. `is_blank_recipe_line` decides whether a recipe asks what the shell is at all, and `expanded.runs_nothing` decides whether an edge has a command — a `.ONESHELL` recipe every line of which expands to nothing must still come out as nothing to run, not as a script of blank lines. The boundary GNU draws is the text as written against what it expands to, and `make-a-target-specific-shell-does-not-reach-katis-own-executor` records the three shapes that sit on it.\n\nNot gated anywhere: the ported corpus records files and exit status, and this writes no file until the here-document case says otherwise; the conformance corpus has no `.ONESHELL` case at all."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T18:12:49.033696602Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Delivered. THE FILING'S OWN INSTRUCTION WAS TO MEASURE THE HERE-DOCUMENT FIRST, and the answer is that this is a wrong FILE and not a cosmetic echo — so the node is worth its fixtures and the fixtures gate an effect rather than a line of narration.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT THAT DECIDED IT. The shape the filing wrote out, run three ways:\n\n    .ONESHELL:\n    EMPTY =\n    all:\n    \\t@cat <<'BODY' > out\n    \\t$(EMPTY)\n    \\tline\n    \\tBODY\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   `out` holds an empty line and then `line`\n      Ronin            `out` holds `line`\n      rkati            `out` holds `line`\n\nThe script is reading its own text, so the blank line is content. Every other shape the filing named — blank at the start, in the middle, at the end, a literally empty recipe line, a line of nothing but whitespace — differs only in the echo, and all six are fixed by the same change.\n\nTHE FILING NAMED ONE SITE AND THERE WERE TWO, which is the correction worth recording. `eval`'s `if !cmd.is_empty()` is real and is one of them, but it is only ever reached for a line that expanded to BLANKS. A line that expanded to NOTHING never got that far: `ExpandedRecipeLines::next` opens with `if self.rest.is_empty() { return None }`, so a written line whose whole expansion was empty produced no lines at all and vanished one level up. The `$(EMPTY)` case — the filing's own case — went through the second door.\n\nThe iterator now counts lines the way a shell counts them: one more than the newlines in the text, and never fewer than one. That is GNU Make's model exactly, because `chop_commands` (commands.c:335) makes ONE line of a `.ONESHELL` recipe and `new_job` expands that one line whole, so the newlines between the makefile's lines are just characters in the middle of it.\n\nTWO CELLS CAME WITH IT, neither of them narration and both measured:\n\n  * WHICH LINE THE PREFIXES COME FROM. The `lines_flags` scan runs over the front of the whole chopped text, so a first line that expanded to nothing means the recipe carries NO prefix — and the `-` on the line below it is script text rather than a flag. Under `.POSIX:` that decides whether the one shell is started with `-e`, and so whether the lines after a failure run at all. Before this, the first surviving line was read as the first line: a recipe whose blank line stood above a `-false` was forgiven, ran on, and wrote a file GNU Make never writes. Exit status and a file, both.\n\n  * WHERE \"NOTHING TO RUN\" IS, which the filing warned about from the other side. `construct_command_argv_internal` opens by skipping blanks and answering no argv if the text ends there. A NEWLINE IS NOT A BLANK. So ONE line that expanded to nothing is nothing to run — no shell, target remade by doing nothing — and TWO are a script of one newline that 4.4.1 really does start a shell on. Measured with a `SHELL` that appends to a file every time it is started: GNU launches it once for the two-line recipe and not at all for the one-line one. Both tools launched it for neither.\n\nWHERE IT WENT. `ExpandedRecipeLines` gained `keeps_blank_lines` (`.ONESHELL` is the whole of the condition) and an `owed` flag that says a text which ended ON a newline still owes the line after it; `eval` keeps a line whose expansion came out empty under the same condition, and collapses a whole recipe that came out blank back to nothing to run; `NinjaGenerator::translate_recipe` now distinguishes a line it ABSORBED — the narration hoist, the output `mkdir` — from a line that was simply empty, and only the absorbed one leaves no trace. Everything downstream of `eval` was left alone: the collapse means `commands.is_empty()` still answers \"runs nothing\" for every reader that asks it.\n\nTHE MATRIX. SIXTEEN cells, each one makefile run three ways against GNU Make 4.4.1, with the binary at e99c85d as the \"before\" column.\n\n  WHAT THE SCRIPT HOLDS\n    here-document with a `$(EMPTY)` line inside  BOTH wrong    FIXED — a FILE\n    `$(EMPTY)` above `echo after`                BOTH dropped  FIXED\n    `$(EMPTY)` below `echo before`               BOTH dropped  FIXED\n    `$(EMPTY)` between two lines                 BOTH dropped  FIXED\n    a literally empty recipe line between two    BOTH dropped  FIXED\n    a whitespace-only expansion between two      BOTH dropped  FIXED\n    the same, with a non-Bourne SHELL            Ronin dropped LINE FIXED, blanks not\n  WHAT RUNS AT ALL\n    one line that expanded to nothing            agreed        agreed\n    two lines that both expanded to nothing      BOTH ran none FIXED — a PROCESS\n    one line of whitespace only                  agreed        agreed\n    one line of whitespace only, no .ONESHELL    agreed        agreed\n  WHICH PREFIXES GOVERN\n    .POSIX: + blank first line above `-false`    BOTH forgave  FIXED — a STATUS\n    .POSIX: + `-false` as the real first line     agreed        agreed (control)\n  THE CONTROLS\n    `$(EMPTY)` above `echo after`, no .ONESHELL  agreed        agreed\n    a whitespace line above `echo after`, no one agreed        agreed\n    `$(EMPTY)` alone, no .ONESHELL               agreed        agreed\n\nNINE cells diverged before this landed and nine are fixed, one of them only in part; seven agreed before and after, four of them the controls that say every recipe GNU Make DOES chop still loses the line it emptied.\n\nThe partial one is filed as make-a-one-shell-scripts-lines-keep-the-blanks-they-end-on: under a non-Bourne shell GNU keeps the blanks a line ends on and `translate_command` trims them, so Ronin's blank line is empty where GNU's holds a space. The line and its newline are right now; what stands on it is not, and only on the compiling side — rkati agrees with the oracle because its executor never translates.\n\nFIXTURES, oracle-recorded with `MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1` against the pinned 4.4.1 and each with measured teeth, taken by parking the source change and rerunning:\n\n    a-one-shell-here-document-holds-the-line-that-expanded-to-nothing\n      parked: `'out' holds \"line\\n\" where GNU Make left \"\\nline\\n\"`\n    a-one-shell-recipes-blank-first-line-carries-no-prefix\n      parked: `build succeeded where GNU Make's build failed`\n    a-one-shell-script-of-two-blank-lines-starts-a-shell\n      parked: `'ran.out' was not produced`\n\nThree different kinds of evidence — a file's contents, an exit status, a process having run — which is the argument for three cases rather than one. Beside them, a unit test on the line counting itself, with the chopped recipe's answer as its control.\n\nGATES, run serially. Workspace 1071 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, which is 1070 plus the one new unit test — the three corpus cases are rows inside `make_build_intent_matches_oracle` rather than tests of their own. `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 826 / 0. Conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised, 286 raw, every family count unmoved. Equivalence 297 makefiles, 280 graphs, 0 disagreements, respellings 8. Upstream inventory 1285 at 95 / 0 / 24 / 1166 — compiler stays ZERO. make_port 4 / 4 with all NINE divergence markers live. vim 17s, zsh 29s at -j8. Ninja conformance 425 / 425 plus every differential. Lints clean.\n\nTHE RELEASE GATE, and the reading it forced. `scripts/check-release.sh` refused TWICE on its performance stage before passing on the third run — and the refusal is the host, proved by parking rather than by re-measuring:\n\n    with the fix, run 1   dependency-log-load  0.88x against a recorded 0.73x\n    with the fix, run 2   dependency-log-load  0.88x against a recorded 0.73x\n    with the fix, alone   path-canonicalization 0.88x against a recorded 0.59x\n    FIX PARKED, alone     deep-graph-evaluation 1.03x against a recorded 0.72x\n    with the fix, run 3   EXIT 0, every stage\n\nThe fourth line is the one that settles it: the tree WITHOUT this change refuses too, on a third workload again, at the same moment and the same load. A different workload trips on every run, which no regression does. Six `silo-920c8636f8` processes at about 100% each were on the machine for the refusing runs — load average 5 to 8 — and two for the passing one, at 2.36. The numbers from the passing run, in nanoseconds (mean, min, max):\n\n    large-manifest-parse   ronin 160.4   ninja 329.0   samurai-c 328.7\n    scheduler-barrier      ronin  45.0   ninja  41.9   samurai-c  38.7\n    clean-tree-noop        ronin   8.3   ninja  12.2   samurai-c  10.9\n    dependency-log-load    ronin   3.4   ninja   4.0   samurai-c   3.1\n\nNothing this change touches is on any of those paths: every one of those workloads is a Ninja manifest, and this is a Make recipe's expansion, in a binary they never load.\n\nTHE HOST, again. `user@1000.service` died a fourth time earlier in this dispatch, SIGKILLed with a 3.5G peak, taking a `cargo test --workspace` with it. Reset-failed, started, verified with `systemd-run --user true`; the rerun passed identically and the count is now four."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T18:12:56.314981001Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-one-shell-flags-a-shell-would-split-are-three-words-not-none
                title "Give a one-shell launch the three words GNU makes of flags a shell would split"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-multi-word-shellflags-reaches-katis-executor-as-one-word)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T17:25:58.454574796Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-a-multi-word-shellflags-reaches-katis-executor-as-one-word, and PRE-EXISTING on BOTH sides: this is `shell_flag_argv`'s answer, which the compiling side has used for a `.ONESHELL` launch since it was written, and which the executor now uses too.\n\nA `.SHELLFLAGS` the tokenizer hands back to a shell contributes NO WORDS here and THREE in GNU Make 4.4.1. Measured, with a shell that prints each argument on its own line:\n\n    .ONESHELL:\n    SHELL := ./argv.sh\n    .SHELLFLAGS := \"-e\" -c\n    all:\n    \\t@echo one\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   [/bin/sh] [-c] [\"-e\" -c] [echo one]\n      Ronin            [echo one]\n      rkati            [echo one]\n\nThe control beside it is the same makefile without `.ONESHELL`, where all three agree on `[-e] [-c] [echo one]`: the ordinary branch assembles a command line and the shell reading it splits the flags properly, quotes and all. Only the one-shell branch differs.\n\nWHY. `shell_flag_argv` reads GNU's guard as \"no words\": `if (argv)` around the flag tokenizer's result, with `unwrap_or_default()` standing in for it. But the guard is only ever false for flags that are BLANK. For flags with shell syntax in them the inner `construct_command_argv_internal` does not return null — it has gone slow and answered with the argument list that STARTS A SHELL, `[/bin/sh, -c, <the flags text>]`, and the one-shell branch copies those three words into the launch ahead of the script (job.c:3437 onwards, the `for (a = argv; *a; ++a)` loop). So `.SHELLFLAGS := \"-e\" -c` makes `/bin/sh` the shell's first argument.\n\nWHAT TO PROBE BEFORE FIXING IT. The third word is the flags text VERBATIM in the case above, because GNU's escape-then-tokenize round trip restores it — but that is one measurement and not a rule. Probe: flags whose second-level tokenize ALSO goes slow (a newline in the value, an unterminated quote), which should nest another `/bin/sh -c` rather than round-trip; `.POSIX:` beside it, because the recursion defaults its own shellflags to `-ec` rather than `-c` when `posix_pedantic` is on and `COMMANDS_NOERROR` is clear, so the second word is not always `-c`; and a `SHELL` other than the default, which does NOT change the injected `/bin/sh` because the recursion passes no shell and falls back to `default_shell`.\n\nMIND THE ORACLE'S OWN LIMIT. 4.4.1 sizes `new_argv` as `4 + sflags_len/2` pointers BEFORE it knows the parse failed, and the slow answer needs six — shell, three injected words, the script, and the null. `.SHELLFLAGS := \"-e\" -c` is seven bytes and fits; a shorter one that goes slow does not, and the existing unit test on `flag_words(\"set\")` already records 4.4.1 dying of heap corruption on that shape. So there is a band where the oracle cannot be asked, and the fix has to say what it does there rather than claiming agreement.\n\nWHERE. `shell_flag_argv` in kati/src-rs/simple_command.rs — one function, both consumers. Its doc comment records the divergence as measured; the unit test `one_shell_flags_are_read_with_the_shells_own_quoting` asserts the current answer and says so.\n\nNot gated anywhere against GNU: no vendored conformance case and no ported case sets `.SHELLFLAGS` to anything a shell would have to split. kati/tests/exec_shell.rs is the executor's surface and src/make/layout.rs the compiling side's."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T19:34:53.568876079Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T19:38:24.131383368Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. The filing's central claim held under probe — `.SHELLFLAGS := \"-e\" -c` under `.ONESHELL` really does reach 4.4.1's launch as `/bin/sh`, `-c`, `\"-e\" -c` ahead of the script, and Ronin really did hand over nothing — but its ACCOUNT OF WHY was wrong in the way that decides what the fix is, and its instruction to probe the third word before treating it as a rule was the right instruction.\n\nWHAT THE FILING SAID. That the inner call goes slow, assembles `$(SHELL) $(.SHELLFLAGS) LINE` as text, recurses to tokenize it, and hands back the argument list that starts a shell — so the third word is the flags text restored by an escape-then-tokenize ROUND TRIP, verbatim in the one case measured.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENS, read off job.c and then measured on every shape. `one_shell` is a GLOBAL, not a parameter — three uses in the whole file, and one of them is `if (one_shell)` sitting INSIDE the slow path at job.c:3293. So the recursion that parses the flags reaches the slow path and lands in the ONE-SHELL BRANCH AGAIN, one level down, with the flags text as its recipe. It never reaches the `new_line` assembly at all; there is no round trip. What that branch does before building its argv is strip the blanks and `[@+-]` off the front of every line of the text it is about to hand over — and it does that here because the shell it has is the DEFAULT `/bin/sh`, `shell` having been passed as 0, which `is_bourne_compatible_shell` says yes to. Then it builds `[/bin/sh, <its own defaulted flags>, <that stripped text>]` and returns, and the branch above copies all three in.\n\nThe difference is visible the moment the flags start with a prefix character, which the filing's one measurement did not:\n\n    .SHELLFLAGS      third word 4.4.1 passes\n    \"-e\" -c          \"-e\" -c        (nothing to strip — the filing's case)\n    -e $X -c         e $X -c        (the leading `-` gone)\n     +- e \"x\" -c     e \"x\" -c       (a run of them, and the blanks between)\n    @e \"x\" -c        e \"x\" -c\n    -e<nl>-c         e<nl>c         (BOTH lines' prefixes, and the newline kept)\n\nThe last one is also the answer to the filing's \"flags whose second-level tokenize also goes slow\": a newline in the value is what SENDS it slow, because `one_shell` makes a newline a separator in the fast path too — so `tokenize` is now asked with one_shell true here, where it was asked with false.\n\nTHE SECOND WORD IS NOT ALWAYS `-c`, which the filing predicted and which measured true. The recursion is handed no flags, so `construct_command_argv_internal` defaults them: `posix_pedantic && NONE_SET (flags, COMMANDS_NOERROR) ? \"-ec\" : \"-c\"`. Measured:\n\n    .POSIX: + .ONESHELL:                     -ec\n    .POSIX: + `-` written on the recipe line  -c\n    .POSIX: + .IGNORE:                        -ec\n\nThe third of those is the one worth keeping: `.IGNORE:` forgives the status without clearing COMMANDS_NOERROR, so only the line's own `-` moves this. Under `.ONESHELL` the line is the first written one, which is where GNU reads the recipe's prefixes.\n\nAND THE SHELL IS ALWAYS `/bin/sh`, whatever `SHELL` says — the recursion passes no shell and `default_shell` is a compile-time constant on this host. Measured with `SHELL := ./argv.sh`, which is how every cell above was read: the injected word is `/bin/sh` while the program started is `./argv.sh`.\n\n4.4.1 REACHES ALL OF THIS THROUGH A HEAP OVERFLOW, and the band the filing warned about is wider than it thought. The filing had the POINTER array — `4 + sflags_len/2` where the slow answer needs six. The STRING buffer is worse and is undersized for EVERY input on this path: `xmalloc (shell_len + sflags_len + line_len + 3)` is exact for words that came out of the flags text, and these three are `/bin/sh` and `-c` longer than that, about eleven bytes over. Valgrind on the filing's own case:\n\n    Invalid write of size 1 at stpcpy, job.c:3452\n    Address is 0 bytes after a block of size 27 alloc'd at job.c:3429\n\n`.SHELLFLAGS := set` and `:= exec` segfault 4.4.1 outright (exit 139); `export`, `unset`, `readonly`, `ulimit -c` and every longer value survive with the argv the source says they should have. So the oracle cannot be asked below about five bytes, and what landed is what the SOURCE says — measured against every value long enough for 4.4.1 to answer. The overflow is 4.4.1's bug, not a behaviour to reproduce; it is recorded on `shell_flag_argv` so the next reader does not go looking for agreement in the band where there is none.\n\nWHERE IT WENT. `shell_flag_argv` (kati/src-rs/simple_command.rs) takes the flags GNU would default to and answers three words where the tokenizer refuses, with a new `one_shell_prefixes_stripped` beside it that is GNU's own strip loop — it counts backslashes rather than splitting on `\\n`, because an escaped newline does not end a line. The blank guard moved in front of the tokenizer, which is GNU's own first test and the whole of what `if (argv)` is ever false for. `Evaluator::default_shell_flag` is new beside the `.SHELLFLAGS` lookup that already made that decision for the default value; the decision now travels to the launch two ways — `RecipeStep::default_shell_flags` for the compiled path, and `ShellToReadWith::one_script` which became `Option<&[u8]>` for the executor, so a one-script launch is exactly the one that carries them and a `$(shell)` cannot accidentally.\n\nTHE MATRIX. Nine cells, each one makefile run three ways — GNU Make 4.4.1, Ronin, rkati — with the binaries at 56bd51b as the \"before\" column. `argv.sh` prints each argument on its own line, `$0` included, which is the only way to see the injected `/bin/sh` beside the `SHELL` that was actually started.\n\n  WHAT THE LAUNCH CARRIES\n    .SHELLFLAGS := \"-e\" -c                 BOTH no words   FIXED\n    .SHELLFLAGS := -e $X -c                BOTH no words   FIXED\n    .SHELLFLAGS :=  +- e \"x\" -c            BOTH no words   FIXED\n    .SHELLFLAGS := export (a builtin word) BOTH no words   FIXED\n    .SHELLFLAGS := -e<newline>-c           BOTH no words   FIXED\n  WHICH FLAGS THE RECURSION DEFAULTS TO\n    .POSIX: beside it                      BOTH no words   FIXED — `-ec`\n    .POSIX: + `-` on the recipe line        BOTH no words   FIXED — `-c`\n  THE CONTROLS\n    the same value with NO .ONESHELL         agreed         agreed\n    .SHELLFLAGS := (blank), .ONESHELL        agreed         agreed\n    quoted flags the tokenizer accepts       agreed         agreed\n\nSeven diverged and seven are fixed; three agreed before and after. The blank one is the control that says the guard this was mistaken for is still there and still answers no words, and the quoted one says the fast path — the corpus's existing `a-one-shell-reads-its-flags-with-the-shells-own-quoting` — did not move when `tokenize` started being asked with one_shell true.\n\nFIXTURES, oracle-recorded with `MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1` against the pinned 4.4.1, each on the corpus's existing `./shell`-writes-`args.txt` shape and each with measured teeth taken by parking the source change and rerunning:\n\n    a-one-shell-flags-a-shell-would-split-are-three-words\n      parked: `'args.txt' holds \"<echo hello>\" where GNU Make left \"</bin/sh><-c><\\\"-e\\\" -c><echo hello>\"`\n    a-one-shell-flags-a-shell-would-split-lose-their-leading-prefixes\n      parked: `'args.txt' holds \"<echo hello>\" where GNU Make left \"</bin/sh><-c><e \\\"x\\\" -c><echo hello>\"`\n    a-posix-one-shell-flags-a-shell-would-split-carry-errexit\n      parked: `'args.txt' holds \"<echo hello>\" where GNU Make left \"</bin/sh><-ec><\\\"-e\\\" -c><echo hello>\"`\n\nThree cases for the three things that are separately wrong: that there are words at all, what the third one holds, and what the second one is. Beside them a new unit test on `shell_flag_argv` itself, carrying the newline and escaped-newline shapes the corpus cannot spell and the `set` shape the oracle segfaults on.\n\nGATES, run serially. Workspace 1072 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, which is 1071 plus the one new unit test — the three corpus cases are rows inside `make_build_intent_matches_oracle`. `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 827 / 0. Conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised, 286 raw, every family count unmoved. Equivalence 297 makefiles, 280 graphs, 0 disagreements, respellings 8. Upstream inventory 1285 at 95 / 0 / 24 / 1166 with the TSV unchanged — compiler stays ZERO. make_port 4 / 4, corpus 1148 cases, all NINE divergence markers live. vim 24s, zsh 44s at -j8. Ninja conformance 425 / 425 plus every differential. Lints clean.\n\nTHE RELEASE GATE, and the load evidence for it. `scripts/check-release.sh` refused once on its performance stage — `large-manifest-parse` 0.63x against a recorded 0.51x — and passed in full on the re-measure, EXIT 0. The host was not quiet: another session's dash compatibility harness was running `dscase.sh` under `xargs -P`, nine of them at a time out of `~/.local/share/nplan/dash-05c1076b`, with the load average between 15 and 22 across both runs. The passing run's own number for that workload is 169.4ns against Ninja's 352.0 — 0.48x, inside the recorded ratio rather than near it — which is what says the 0.63x was the machine and not the change. Nothing this touches is on a Ninja manifest path in any case: it is the argument list a Make recipe's shell is started with.\n\nkati branch nplan/make-one-shell-flags-a-shell-would-split-are-three-words-not-none at 14a6ded7f70b0cbb7f207e5830047cbbbebf15f9, pushed before this pins it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T19:38:33.70455866Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-one-shell-scripts-lines-keep-the-blanks-they-end-on
                title "Keep the trailing blanks on a .ONESHELL script's lines"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-recipe-line-that-expands-to-nothing-is-a-blank-line-of-a-one-shell-script)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T18:11:56.426567775Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-a-recipe-line-that-expands-to-nothing-is-a-blank-line-of-a-one-shell-script, and PRE-EXISTING: it is the compiling side's, and it is what that node's fix left behind rather than anything it caused.\n\nA `.ONESHELL` script's line keeps the blanks it ends on in GNU Make 4.4.1 and loses them here. Measured with a shell that says the script it was handed:\n\n    .ONESHELL:\n    SHELL := ./mark.sh\n    SP = $(subst x, ,x)\n    all:\n    \\techo one$(SP)\n    \\techo two\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   `echo one `, newline, `echo two`\n      Ronin            `echo one`,  newline, `echo two`\n      rkati            `echo one `, newline, `echo two` — agrees\n\nand the same with the blanks as the whole of the line:\n\n    .ONESHELL:\n    SHELL := ./mark.sh\n    SP = $(subst x, ,x)\n    all:\n    \\techo one\n    \\t$(SP)\n    \\techo two\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   `echo one`, ` `, `echo two`\n      Ronin            `echo one`, ``,  `echo two`\n      rkati            `echo one`, ` `, `echo two` — agrees\n\nThe line COUNT is right in all three now; only what is on the blank line differs, and only on the compiling side. rkati's executor agrees with the oracle because it assembles the script out of the expanded lines directly and never translates them.\n\nWHERE. `NinjaGenerator::translate_command` (kati/src-rs/ninja.rs) trims the blanks a line ends on, through the `blanks_before` walk near its end. It runs per written line, `.ONESHELL` or not. GNU Make trims nothing: `chop_commands` leaves a one-shell recipe whole and the one-shell branch of `construct_command_argv_internal` strips only the blanks and `[@+-]` at the START of each interior line, and only for a Bourne-compatible shell.\n\nThe function already has a `.ONESHELL` exception beside this one — the trailing `;` is kept under one shell and trimmed otherwise, \"because the join supplies one too, and only one of it\" — so the shape of the fix is written already. What it needs is the measurement the `;` exception has: a trim that exists because a manifest binding joins lines with ` ; ` has nothing to do under a join that is a newline.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS AT ALL, which the sibling node's here-document measurement answers: a `.ONESHELL` script can be reading its own text, and a here-document writes what the lines hold into a file. A trailing blank is a difference in that file. The gate is therefore the same shape as `a-one-shell-here-document-holds-the-line-that-expanded-to-nothing` in the ported corpus — a here-document with a line that ends on blanks — and the corpus records file contents, so it can hold it.\n\nPROBE BEFORE FIXING. Whether GNU keeps trailing blanks written in the MAKEFILE as well as ones an expansion produced (read.c may take them off the recipe line before anything else sees it — the measurement above got its blank from `$(SP)`, so it says nothing about that). Whether a trailing backslash-newline still behaves, which is what `protected` in the trim exists for. And whether the leading blanks agree: a `.ONESHELL` recipe under a non-Bourne shell keeps its indentation, which `keeps_indent` already carries, so leading and trailing may already be answered in different places."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T18:28:49.05235692Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T18:49:24.257067204Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. THE FILING WAS RIGHT — mechanism, site, and blast radius — and the probe it asked for came back with an answer it had explicitly left open, which changes what the fix has to be about. Its own measurement got its trailing blank from `$(SP)`, so it could not say whether a blank WRITTEN IN THE MAKEFILE survives read.c. Measured: it does. GNU Make 4.4.1 hands `echo one   ` to the one shell with all three spaces, from a recipe line that was typed that way. So this is not \"an expansion left something behind\"; it is that GNU takes nothing off the end of a recipe line at all, and the trim was always the odd one out.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, confirmed. `NinjaGenerator::translate_command` (kati/src-rs/ninja.rs) ran `blanks_before(cmd_buf.len())` unconditionally before it asked anything about the join. GNU Make's counterpart strips only at the START of a line, and only for a Bourne-compatible shell: `chop_commands` (commands.c) never chops a `.ONESHELL` recipe, and the one-shell branch of `construct_command_argv_internal` (job.c) walks each interior line taking off leading blanks and `[@+-]`. Nothing walks backward from the end.\n\nWHERE IT WENT. The trailing-blank trim moved inside the `!one_shell` guard the trailing-`;` trim was already in, and the two now read as one exception with one reason: what comes off a line comes off because the ` ; ` join made it redundant. Under a newline join the join supplies nothing, so nothing is redundant, so nothing comes off. One `if`, no new state.\n\nWHY THE ORDINARY BRANCH KEEPS ITS TRIM, measured rather than assumed. GNU keeps the blanks there too — `.SHELLFLAGS`-style probing with a non-Bourne `SHELL` shows 4.4.1 handing `[-c] [echo one   ]` for a recipe line with no `.ONESHELL` in sight. But Ronin's compiled path joins that recipe's lines into ONE manifest binding with ` ; ` between them, and a blank in front of a ` ; ` is whitespace between tokens. The difference is unobservable in effect, and the corpus gates effects. So the trim stays where the join justifies it and the control case agrees before and after.\n\nTHE THIRD CONSTRUCT, which neither the filing nor the sibling node had. A double-quoted string that SPANS lines makes an end-of-line blank content, and `protected` — the quote-and-escape tracking that already saves a quoted trailing blank — cannot see it, because the quote opened on a PREVIOUS line and `translate_command` is called once per line with fresh quote state. So `printf '%s' \"a<nl>b <nl>c\"` lost its space where a single-line `printf '%s' \"b \"` never did. That is the case for fixing this at the end of the function rather than teaching `protected` to carry state across lines: the only answer that is right whether or not the reader can see the quote is to leave the end of every line alone.\n\nTHE MATRIX. Ten cells, each one makefile run three ways — GNU Make 4.4.1, Ronin, rkati — with the binary at a9d3e89 as the \"before\" column. Where the script is what is being measured, `SHELL` is a script that prints each argument on its own line; where a file is, the recipe writes one.\n\n  WHAT THE SHELL IS HANDED\n    trailing spaces from `$(SP)`, mid-script      Ronin trimmed  FIXED\n    trailing spaces WRITTEN IN THE MAKEFILE       Ronin trimmed  FIXED\n    trailing spaces on the script's LAST line     Ronin trimmed  FIXED\n    a whitespace-only line, non-Bourne SHELL      Ronin emptied  FIXED\n  WHAT A FILE HOLDS\n    here-document line ending on spaces           Ronin trimmed  FIXED\n    here-document line ending on a TAB            Ronin trimmed  FIXED\n    quoted string spanning lines, blank interior  Ronin trimmed  FIXED\n    a capture compared against its own text       Ronin failed   FIXED\n  THE CONTROLS\n    the same trailing spaces, NO .ONESHELL        agreed         agreed\n    a whitespace-only line, Bourne SHELL          agreed         agreed\n    leading blanks under one shell, Bourne        agreed         agreed\n\nEight cells diverged before this landed and eight are fixed. The last two controls are the ones that say the leading side was already right and is answered somewhere else: GNU strips the front of an interior line for a Bourne shell, `keeps_indent` carries the other case, and a whitespace-only line therefore comes out empty under `/bin/sh` in all three tools.\n\nrkati agrees with the oracle throughout, before and after, because its executor assembles the script out of the expanded lines and never translates them. This was the compiling side's alone, exactly as filed.\n\nFIXTURES, oracle-recorded with `MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1` against the pinned 4.4.1, three constructs and two kinds of evidence, each with measured teeth taken by parking the source change and rerunning:\n\n    a-one-shell-here-document-holds-the-blanks-a-line-ends-on\n      parked: `'out' holds \"line one\\nline two\\n\" where GNU Make left \"line one \\nline two\\n\"`\n    a-one-shell-scripts-quoted-string-keeps-the-blanks-a-line-ends-on\n      parked: `'out' holds \"a\\nb\\nc\" where GNU Make left \"a\\nb \\nc\"`\n    a-one-shell-capture-keeps-the-blanks-the-captured-line-ends-on\n      parked: `build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`\n\nThe third one is recorded as an exit status and nothing else, and getting there taught something worth keeping: the comparison has to be the recipe's LAST line. `.ONESHELL` arms no `-e`, so a `[ ... ]` in the middle of a script fails and the script runs on regardless, touches its file and reports the status of whatever ended it. The first spelling of the case put the test above `touch out` and had NO teeth for exactly that reason — it passed parked. Moved to the end, the script's own status is the test's.\n\nBeside them, the existing unit test `a_line_keeps_the_trailing_bytes_that_are_script` grew four assertions: trailing spaces and a trailing tab kept under one shell, the trailing `;` now keeping the blanks in front of it too, and a line that is nothing but a blank staying one blank under a newline join and coming out empty under a ` ; ` join.\n\nGATES, run serially. Workspace 1071 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored — unmoved, because the three corpus cases are rows inside `make_build_intent_matches_oracle` and the unit work went into a test that already existed. `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 826 / 0. Conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised, 286 raw, every family count unmoved. Equivalence 297 makefiles, 280 graphs, 0 disagreements, manifest respellings 8. Upstream inventory 1285 at 95 / 0 / 24 / 1166 with the TSV unchanged — compiler stays ZERO. make_port 4 / 4, corpus now 1145 cases, all NINE divergence markers live. vim 26s, zsh 38s at -j8. Ninja conformance 425 / 425 plus every differential. Lints clean. `scripts/check-release.sh` EXIT 0 in full, first run, under a load average of 8.5 with four `silo-` processes on the host.\n\nkati branch nplan/make-a-one-shell-scripts-lines-keep-the-blanks-they-end-on at 82a6ed1b350840f04b6947d6cc23e35dffec8c1e, pushed before this pins it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T18:49:28.03441464Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-shell-function-under-one-shell-is-read-by-the-one-shell-branch
                title "Read a $(shell) under .ONESHELL: the way GNU's one-shell branch reads it"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-one-shell-flags-a-shell-would-split-are-three-words-not-none)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T19:44:00.259451764Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-one-shell-flags-a-shell-would-split-are-three-words-not-none, and PRE-EXISTING on BOTH sides.\n\nGNU Make's `one_shell` is a GLOBAL, not a parameter — three uses in the whole of job.c, and none of them asks who is calling. `func_shell_base` reaches `construct_command_argv` like everything else, so a `$(shell)` under `.ONESHELL:` is read by the one-shell branch too. Both tools read it as an ordinary command line whatever `.ONESHELL:` says, and `func.rs` says so in a comment that is wrong: \"one_shell is only consulted for a recipe\".\n\nTWO CELLS MEASURED, each one makefile run three ways.\n\nA NEWLINE IS A SEPARATOR. `construct_command_argv_internal` (job.c:3034) has `else if (one_shell && *p == '\\n') goto slow`, so a `$(shell)` whose command text holds a newline goes to a shell under `.ONESHELL:` and is exec'd directly without it:\n\n    define CMD\n    echo a\n    echo b\n    endef\n    .ONESHELL:\n    V := $(shell $(CMD))\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   V=[a b]        — two commands, two lines of output\n      Ronin            V=[a echo b]   — one exec, the newline a word separator\n      rkati            V=[a echo b]\n\nand the same makefile WITHOUT `.ONESHELL:` agrees in all three at `V=[a echo b]`, which is the control that says this is the global and nothing else.\n\nTHE INTERIOR PREFIXES COME OFF. The slow path's one-shell branch strips the blanks and `[@+-]` off the front of each line of the text it is about to hand over, for a Bourne-compatible shell. That is the recipe's stripping being applied to a `$(shell)`:\n\n    .ONESHELL:\n    V := $(shell @echo hi > out ; cat out)\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   V=[hi], and `out` written\n      Ronin            `/bin/sh: 1: @echo: not found`, V=[], no `out`\n      rkati            the same\n\nagain with the no-`.ONESHELL:` control agreeing in all three. A FILE, not narration.\n\nWHAT IS LIKELY THERE AND NOT MEASURED: the third of the one-shell branch's behaviours, the three-word answer for a `.SHELLFLAGS` the tokenizer refuses, which by the same reasoning should reach a `$(shell)` under `.ONESHELL:` as well. Probe it before assuming — 4.4.1's heap overflow on that path (recorded on `shell_flag_argv`) means the oracle cannot be asked below about five bytes of flags.\n\nWHERE. `crate::fileutil::ShellToReadWith::one_script`, which is `None` at `func.rs:688` and at `regen.rs:428` and carries the flags GNU would default to at `exec.rs`. The fix is presumably that a `$(shell)` under `.ONESHELL:` is a one-script launch too — but `one_script` currently means two things at once (the newline gate AND which flag splitting), so check that GNU really joins them before making them one field's answer. `func_shell_base` passes no line flags, which is what keeps `.POSIX:`'s `-ec` out of it.\n\nNot gated anywhere: no vendored conformance case and no ported case puts a `$(shell)` under `.ONESHELL:`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T20:22:29.312161757Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T20:23:45.118010914Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. THE FILING WAS RIGHT on both measured cells and on its reading of the mechanism, and the third cell it left reasoned-but-unmeasured is MEASURED and comes out the way it reasoned. But the probe found a fourth thing the filing did not have, and it is the one that decides where the fix goes: GNU LATCHES `.ONESHELL` WHERE THE LINE STANDS.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. `one_shell` is a global (main.c:580), set by `read.c:1925` the moment the target is recorded, and read by `construct_command_argv_internal` in three places: the newline gate at job.c:3034, the interior `[@+-]` strip at job.c:3293, and the flags recursion at job.c:3440. `func_shell_base` reaches `construct_command_argv` like every other launch, so a `$(shell)` gets all three. func.rs's comment claiming \"one_shell is only consulted for a recipe\" was wrong and is gone.\n\nTHE FOURTH THING. Because the latch is at READ time and a `$(shell)` in a `:=` is expanded at read time, WHERE the `.ONESHELL:` line stands decides how a `$(shell)` is read. Measured on 4.4.1, one makefile each way:\n\n    V := $(shell @echo hi > out ; cat out)   above `.ONESHELL:`   V=[]   (out empty)\n    V := $(shell @echo hi > out ; cat out)   below `.ONESHELL:`   V=[hi] (out holds hi)\n    V  = $(shell ...) below, expanded in the recipe                V=[hi]\n\nkati latched the flag in dep.rs, AFTER the whole read, so a naive fix reading `flags.one_shell` from func.rs would have been inert for every read-time `$(shell)` — the filing's own two cells among them. The latch therefore moved up into `Evaluator::eval_rule`, beside the `.POSIX:` and `.SECONDEXPANSION` latches that are there for the same reason. dep.rs's own set stays: it is the same answer, later, and costs nothing.\n\nTHE THIRD CELL, MEASURED — record this as a claim, not a probe instruction. YES: the three-word refused-flag fallback reaches `$(shell)` under `.ONESHELL:`. With `SHELL = ./shell` (an argv-logging script), `.SHELLFLAGS = \"-e\" -c` and `V := $(shell echo hello)`, 4.4.1 hands the shell\n\n    </bin/sh> <-c> <\"-e\" -c> <echo hello>\n\nwhich is `[/bin/sh, <the defaulted flags>, <the flags text with its line prefixes off>]` copied in ahead of the command, exactly as 14a6ded built it for a recipe. Both tools gave `<-e> <-c> <echo hello>` before — the command-line splitting, with the quotes eaten by the `/bin/sh` in the way. The measurement needs the argv written to a FILE: capturing it through `$(shell)` and echoing it in a recipe loses the double quotes to the recipe's own shell, which is how the first attempt at this cell nearly recorded the wrong answer.\n\nThe 4.4.1 heap overflow the filing warned about is real and bit here: with the exotic `SHELL` still in force when the recipe launched, 4.4.1 aborted with a core dump AFTER logging the `$(shell)` argv. The fixture puts `SHELL` back to `/bin/sh` before any recipe runs, which is enough — one launch down that path survives, two do not.\n\nWHERE IT WENT.\n  * `Evaluator::eval_rule` (kati/src-rs/eval.rs) latches `.ONESHELL` where the target is recorded.\n  * `shell_func_with` and `shell_no_rerun_func` (func.rs) build a `ShellToReadWith` whose `one_script` is `flags.one_shell.then(|| ev.default_shell_flag(false))`. `false` because `func_shell_base` passes no line flags, which is what keeps `.POSIX:`'s `-e` out of the default the flags recursion falls back on.\n  * `argv_to_exec` (fileutil.rs) strips the script's interior prefixes on the ONE-SCRIPT SLOW PATH ONLY, and only for a Bourne-compatible shell. `one_shell_prefixes_stripped` and `is_bourne_compatible_shell` were already written for the recipe side; both became `pub(crate)`.\n  * `shell_func_impl` now takes the `ShellToReadWith` whole rather than three arguments, which is what kept the arity lint green.\n\nTHE FAST PATH IS NOT THE ONE-SHELL BRANCH, which the filing did not distinguish and which decides the shape of the fix. GNU's strip is on the far side of `goto slow`, so a `$(shell)` whose text the tokenizer can take apart keeps its prefixes even under `.ONESHELL:`. Measured: `.ONESHELL:` with `V := $(shell @echo hi)` reports `@echo: No such file or directory` in all three tools, before and after, while the same text with a `>` in it prints `hi` only in GNU before this landed. That is why the strip is in `argv_to_exec` and not in `func.rs`.\n\n`one_script` was already carrying both the newline gate and the flag splitting, and the filing asked whether GNU really joins them. It does: all three behaviours are the same global read in the same function, so one field is the right shape and no second field was needed.\n\nTHE MATRIX. Five cells, one makefile each, run three ways — GNU Make 4.4.1, Ronin, rkati.\n\n  A NEWLINE IS A SEPARATOR\n    define CMD / echo a / echo b, `$(shell $(CMD))`   both tools [a echo b]   FIXED\n  THE INTERIOR PREFIXES COME OFF\n    `$(shell @echo hi > out ; cat out)`               both tools [] no file   FIXED\n  THE REFUSED FLAGS ARE THREE WORDS\n    `.SHELLFLAGS = \"-e\" -c`, argv logged to a file    both tools 3 args       FIXED\n  WHERE THE LINE STANDS\n    the same `$(shell)` ABOVE `.ONESHELL:`            agreed                  agreed\n  THE FAST PATH IS UNTOUCHED\n    `$(shell @echo hi)`, no shell character in it     agreed                  agreed\n\nrkati and Ronin moved together throughout, because this is kati's evaluator on both sides.\n\nFIXTURES, oracle-recorded with `MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1` against the pinned 4.4.1, with measured teeth taken by parking the source change and rerunning:\n\n    a-one-shell-shell-function-separates-the-lines-of-its-command\n      parked: `'out' holds \"v=<a echo b>\" where GNU Make left \"v=<a b>\"`\n    a-one-shell-shell-function-loses-the-prefixes-inside-its-command\n      parked: `'down.txt' holds \"\" where GNU Make left \"down\\n\"`\n    a-one-shell-shell-function-takes-the-three-words-a-refused-flag-makes\n      parked: `'args.txt' holds \"<-e>\\n<-c>\\n<echo hello>\\n\" where GNU Make left \"</bin/sh>\\n<-c>\\n<\\\"-e\\\" -c>\\n<echo hello>\\n\"`\n\nThe second fixture carries the positional latch as well as the strip: it has a `$(shell)` on each side of the `.ONESHELL:` line and asserts the file each one leaves. Parked the OTHER way — the latch made global instead of positional, which is the plausible wrong fix — it fails on its own: `'out' holds \"before=<up> after=<down>\" where GNU Make left \"before=<> after=<down>\"`. Two errors, one case, both measured.\n\nBeside them a unit test, `a_one_script_launch_keeps_the_prefixes_a_shell_may_be_reading` (fileutil.rs), pins the three answers `argv_to_exec` gives a one-script launch: stripped for `/bin/dash`, left alone for `/opt/myshell`, and untouched on the fast path.\n\nLEFT OPEN, with a node of its own: the regen replay. `regen.rs` replays a recorded `$(shell)` before any makefile has been read, so it cannot know the recorded call was one-script, and the kati stamp's field order is ckati's — recording the bit is a format change. The cost is a replay that can answer differently and regenerate when it need not. The comment there now says so rather than claiming the replay asks the same question.\n\nGATES, run serially. Workspace 1073 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, up one for the new unit test. `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 828 / 0, up one for the same. Conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised, 286 raw, every family count unmoved. Equivalence 297 makefiles, 280 graphs, 0 disagreements, manifest respellings 8. Upstream inventory 1285 at 95 / 0 / 24 / 1166 with the TSV unchanged — compiler stays ZERO. make_port 4 / 4, corpus now 1151 cases, all NINE divergence markers live. vim 17s, zsh 31s at -j8. Ninja conformance 425 / 425 plus the ten differential cases and the persistence round trip. Lints clean, `cargo doc` clean, port ledger 170 / 170.\n\nThe arity lint refused the first spelling of `shell_func_impl` — eight arguments — and was FIXED rather than bypassed by giving the function the `ShellToReadWith` the three of them were about.\n\nkati branch nplan/make-a-shell-function-under-one-shell-is-read-by-the-one-shell-branch at 9c659115ba9c76668082bce227f3be5ebcf0c888, pushed before this pins it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T20:27:18.250061996Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-shellflags-value-is-expanded-again-where-the-launch-reads-it
                title "Expand the .SHELLFLAGS value a second time where the launch reads it"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-one-shell-flags-a-shell-would-split-are-three-words-not-none)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T19:44:24.162768098Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-one-shell-flags-a-shell-would-split-are-three-words-not-none, and PRE-EXISTING on BOTH sides.\n\nGNU Make expands the `.SHELLFLAGS` value a SECOND time, where the launch reads it. `construct_command_argv` (job.c) looks the variable up and then does\n\n    shellflags = allocated_variable_expand_for_file (var->value, file);\n\non the VALUE — not on the reference. A simply-expanded `.SHELLFLAGS` was already expanded once when it was assigned, so a `$` its assignment left behind is expanded again here, against the target's own scope.\n\nMEASURED, with a shell that prints each argument on its own line:\n\n    X := ZZZ\n    .ONESHELL:\n    SHELL := ./argv.sh\n    .SHELLFLAGS := -e $$X -c\n    all:\n    \\t@echo one\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   [-e] [ZZZ] [-c] [echo one]\n      Ronin            [/bin/sh] [-c] [e $X -c] [echo one]\n      rkati            the same as Ronin\n\nBoth tools pass the `$X` through as text, so the value still holds a shell character and goes down the refused-flags path that\nmake-one-shell-flags-a-shell-would-split-are-three-words-not-none just built. GNU never gets there, because by the time it looks the value has no `$` in it at all. The divergence is therefore the expansion and not the splitting, and it would show under any recipe — the `.ONESHELL:` above is only what makes the words easy to read.\n\nWHAT TO PROBE BEFORE FIXING IT. `SHELL` is NOT read this way and must not be given the same treatment: measured on the same shape, `SHELL := ./shell$$X` reaches 4.4.1's exec as the literal path `./shell$X` whether or not `X` is defined. So this is `.SHELLFLAGS` alone, and the asymmetry is the thing to preserve. Probe also which scope the second expansion sees — `allocated_variable_expand_for_file` is handed the FILE, so a target-specific variable should be visible to it — and what a RECURSIVE `.SHELLFLAGS` does, where the value has not been expanded yet and this would be the second expansion of a first-expansion result.\n\nWHERE. `Evaluator::get_shell_flag` (kati/src-rs/eval.rs) ends at `eval_bound_var(Symbol::SHELLFLAGS, var)`, which is the reference's own expansion and not a second one over the result. The `.POSIX:` default branch above it is unaffected: GNU takes that branch before it reaches the expansion at all.\n\nNot gated anywhere: no vendored conformance case and no ported case leaves a `$` in a `.SHELLFLAGS` value."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T20:53:55.161397432Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T20:55:00.052426054Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. THE FILING WAS RIGHT — mechanism, site, asymmetry and all three things it asked to be probed came back the way it expected. It is the first of these three that survived its own probe intact, and the probe still found one thing the filing did not have: the missing expansion is not only a wrong word, it is an EFFECT. A `.SHELLFLAGS` value holding `$(...)` reaches a shell as parentheses, and rkati's launch runs what is inside them.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, confirmed at the source. `construct_command_argv` (job.c) reads the two variables differently in the same block:\n\n    shell      = allocated_variable_expand_for_file (\"$(SHELL)\", file);   <- the REFERENCE\n    shellflags = allocated_variable_expand_for_file (var->value, file);   <- the VALUE\n\nExpanding the reference is flavour-aware: a simply-expanded `SHELL` hands back its stored bytes untouched. Expanding the value is not: a simply-expanded `.SHELLFLAGS` has its stored bytes — already expanded once at assignment — expanded AGAIN. A recursive `.SHELLFLAGS` is unaffected, because there the stored bytes are the unexpanded text and this is its first expansion. So the asymmetry has two halves and both had to be kept.\n\nWHAT THE PROBES ANSWERED, each one makefile run three ways with a `SHELL` that prints each argument to a file.\n\n  WHEN the second expansion happens: AT THE LAUNCH.\n    X := one / .SHELLFLAGS := -e $$X -c / X := two   GNU [-e][two][-c]     both tools [-e][-c]\n  WHICH SCOPE it sees: THE TARGET'S.\n    all: X := pertarget                              GNU [-e][pertarget]   both tools [-e][-c]\n  A RECURSIVE value: EXPANDED ONCE, not twice.\n    .SHELLFLAGS = -e $$X -c                          GNU [-e][-c]          agreed, before and after\n  A TARGET-SPECIFIC `.SHELLFLAGS`                    GNU [-e][ZZZ][-c]     both tools [-e][-c]\n  A FUNCTION CALL in the value                       GNU [-e][3][-c]       both tools [-e][-c]\n  THE `$(shell)` PATH gets it too                    GNU [-e][ZZZ][-c]     both tools [-e][-c]\n  `SHELL` does NOT                                   GNU execs `./argv$X.sh` literally, and fails 127\n\nThe tools' `[-e][-c]` is not the flags being dropped: the unexpanded `$X` made the value shell syntax, so the launch went down the assembled-command-line path and the `/bin/sh` in the way expanded `$X` to nothing and dropped the empty word. That is the filing's reading exactly.\n\nTHE EFFECT THE FILING DID NOT HAVE. `.SHELLFLAGS := -c $$(touch pwned)` is Make's `$(...)` to expand, and 4.4.1 expands it into nothing — an unknown function name is read as a variable reference — so the build succeeds and NO file is written. Copied through unexpanded, the parentheses reach whatever reads the assembled line and are not Make's any more:\n\n    GNU Make 4.4.1   no `pwned`, `out` written\n    rkati            `pwned` WRITTEN, `out` written\n    Ronin            build FAILS: its own shell splits the line at the blank and\n                     `/bin/sh` is handed the half-substitution `$(touch`\n\nTwo different ways of being wrong about the same missing expansion, and the corpus gates Ronin's, which is the failure. rkati's is a written file and is recorded here.\n\nWHERE IT WENT. `Evaluator::get_shell_flag` (kati/src-rs/eval.rs), which ended at `eval_bound_var` — the reference's own expansion. It now takes the variable's stored text through `Variable::string` and expands THAT once, which is GNU's line transcribed: one expansion for a recursive value, a second for a simple one. The `.POSIX:` default branch above it is untouched, because GNU takes that branch before it reaches the expansion at all, and `get_shell` is untouched, because the asymmetry is the point.\n\nTWO THINGS ADDED THAT GNU DOES NOT HAVE, both deliberate.\n\nA `$`-free value is returned without a parse. `variable_expand_string` walks the text for a `$` and hands it back where it finds none, so this is the same answer; it matters because `get_shell_flag` runs once per recipe line of every build and the parse would otherwise be on the hot path of Android-sized makefiles.\n\nA recursion mark. GNU sets none here and dies of it: `.SHELLFLAGS := -e $$(shell echo hi) -c` SEGFAULTS 4.4.1, because answering the `$(shell)` needs the flags it is in the middle of expanding. Measured, with a core dump. `begin_var_expansion`/`var_eval_complete` around the expansion turns that into the ordinary \"Recursive variable references itself\" refusal. It is an unmeasurable cell — the oracle crashes there, so no fixture can hold it — and a compiler that recurses without a floor is the worse of the two answers.\n\nFIXTURES, oracle-recorded with `MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1` against the pinned 4.4.1, four cases, with measured teeth taken by parking and rerunning:\n\n    a-shellflags-value-is-expanded-where-the-launch-reads-it\n      parked: `'args.txt' holds \"<-e>\\n<-c>\\n<echo hello>\\n\" where GNU Make left \"<-e>\\n<two>\\n<-c>\\n<echo hello>\\n\"`\n    a-shellflags-value-expands-in-the-scope-of-the-target-it-launches-for\n      parked: `'args.txt' holds \"<-e>\\n<-c>\\n<echo hello>\\n\" where GNU Make left \"<-e>\\n<pertarget>\\n<-c>\\n<echo hello>\\n\"`\n    a-shellflags-value-holding-a-substitution-is-makes-to-expand\n      parked: `build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`\n    a-recursive-shellflags-value-is-expanded-once-and-not-twice\n      parked THE OTHER WAY: `'args.txt' holds \"<-e>\\n<ZZZ>\\n<-c>\\n<echo hello>\\n\" where GNU Make left \"<-e>\\n<-c>\\n<echo hello>\\n\"`\n\nThe fourth is the control the filing asked for and it has real teeth, just not against the change that landed: parking the source leaves it passing, because it always agreed. What it refuses is the OTHER spelling of the fix — expand the reference and then expand the result — which is the natural one to reach for and which expands a recursive value twice. Measured by writing that spelling and rerunning.\n\nGATES, run serially. Workspace 1073 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, unmoved from the landing before this — the four new cases are rows inside `make_build_intent_matches_oracle`. `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 828 / 0. Conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised, 286 raw, every family count unmoved. Equivalence 297 makefiles, 280 graphs, 0 disagreements, manifest respellings 8. Upstream inventory 1285 at 95 / 0 / 24 / 1166, TSV unchanged, compiler ZERO. make_port 4 / 4, corpus now 1155 cases, all NINE divergence markers live. vim 19s, zsh 34s at -j8. Ninja conformance 425 / 425 plus the ten differential cases and the persistence round trip. Lints clean, `cargo doc` clean, port ledger 170 / 170.\n\nkati branch nplan/make-a-shellflags-value-is-expanded-again-where-the-launch-reads-it at cf46e83b9f7550c61437c86381a3e5f80a7bf026, pushed before this pins it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T20:55:07.791549757Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-shell-holding-shell-syntax-is-escaped-into-the-assembled-line
                title "Escape a SHELL holding shell syntax into the assembled command line"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-one-shell-flags-a-shell-would-split-are-three-words-not-none)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T19:44:47.317685868Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while landing make-one-shell-flags-a-shell-would-split-are-three-words-not-none, and PRE-EXISTING on BOTH sides. This one RUNS A COMMAND GNU Make never runs.\n\nWhere a launch has to go to a shell, GNU Make assembles `$(SHELL) $(.SHELLFLAGS) LINE` as text and escapes the shell into it, one character at a time, with the reason written beside the loop (job.c):\n\n    /* Copy SHELL, escaping any characters special to the shell.  If\n       we don't escape them, construct_command_argv_internal will\n       recursively call itself ad nauseam, or until stack overflow,\n       whichever happens first.  */\n    for (cp = shell; *cp != '\\0'; ++cp)\n      {\n        if (strchr (sh_chars, *cp) != 0)\n          *(ap++) = '\\\\';\n        *(ap++) = *cp;\n      }\n\nBoth tools copy the value in unescaped, so whatever shell syntax the value holds is the shell's to read.\n\nMEASURED, and the effect is a file:\n\n    SHELL := ./sh$$(touch pwned)\n    all:\n    \\t@echo one\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   `make: ./sh$(touch: No such file or directory`, Error 127, NO `pwned`\n      Ronin            `pwned` WRITTEN, then `env: './sh': No such file or directory`\n      rkati            `pwned` WRITTEN, then `/bin/sh: 1: ./sh: not found`\n\nGNU escapes the `$` and the `(`, so its own tokenizer hands the assembled line back as the words `./sh$(touch`, `pwned)`, `-c`, `echo one` and execs the first as a path. Both tools hand `./sh$(touch pwned) -c \"echo one\"` to a shell, which runs the substitution. Make's own exit status is 2 in all three, so the gate is the file and not the status.\n\nWHAT ELSE THE SAME MISSING ESCAPE REACHES, measured and NOT an effect: a `SHELL` holding a plain `$X` differs only in the diagnostic — GNU names `./shell$X` and both tools name `./shell`, because the shell expanded the reference to nothing — and both exit 127 through Make's 2. That is narration, and it is recorded here only so the fix is not written to chase it.\n\nWHERE. `run_command`'s assembly in kati/src-rs/fileutil.rs — `cmd_with_shell.put_slice(shell)` with no escaping, where `crate::strutil::escape_shell` is already applied to the command below it — and the same shape in `Layout::launch_step` (src/make/layout.rs), which builds `Launch::Shell` out of the shell, the flags and the escaped text. Note GNU escapes the SHELL and the LINE but copies the FLAGS in as they stand, which is what\nmake-one-shell-flags-a-shell-would-split-are-three-words-not-none's `command_line_flag_argv` already records; the fix here is the shell alone.\n\nPROBE BEFORE FIXING. Which character set: GNU uses `sh_chars`, `#;\"*?[]&|<>(){}$`^~!`, and NOT the backslash or the quotes that `escape_shell` also takes — the escaping the LINE gets is a different, wider loop in the same function. A `SHELL` with a plain space must keep working: measured, 4.4.1 answers `./shell: No such file or directory` for `SHELL := ./shell space.sh`, because a space is not in `sh_chars` and the tokenizer therefore splits it into two words, which is the documented \"SHELL may be a multi-word command\"."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T21:35:07.899001578Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T21:36:21.06682547Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. THE SAFETY EFFECT IS CONFIRMED AND GATED: `pwned` IS NOT WRITTEN. Six oracle-recorded cases hold a build in which the file does not exist, four of them fail with the harness's own words — `'pwned' was produced and GNU Make produced no such file` — the moment the source change is parked.\n\nTHE FILING WAS RIGHT about mechanism, site and character set, and UNDERSTATED one thing it explicitly told the fix not to chase. It said a `SHELL` of a plain `$X` \"differs only in the diagnostic\". It does not. Measured: put a real `./sh` beside a makefile that says `SHELL := ./sh$$X`, and the shell in the way strips the reference and RUNS `./sh` — a program the makefile never named — while 4.4.1 looks for `./sh$X`, does not find it, and stops at 127. GNU Make's build fails and this one succeeded. That cell is now a fixture rather than a note.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. job.c's slow path builds `new_line` out of three pieces and treats each differently: the SHELL is walked character by character with a backslash before every `sh_chars` byte, the FLAGS are `mempcpy`'d in as they stand, and the LINE gets a wider loop that also escapes the backslash, both quotes and every blank. The assembled text is then handed to `construct_command_argv_internal` AGAIN, with no shell and no flags — so it is GNU's own tokenizer that takes it apart, and the backslashes are what survive that. The recursion terminates because the second level's shell is `/bin/sh`, which has no `sh_chars` in it, and its line is the whole of level one escaped into a single word.\n\nTHE MATRIX, twelve cells, each one makefile run three ways with the payload written as a FILE rather than narrated. `pwned` before → after:\n\n  THE SUBSTITUTIONS\n    SHELL := ./sh$$(touch pwned)          GNU never   both tools WROTE IT   FIXED\n    SHELL := ./sh`touch pwned`            GNU never   both tools WROTE IT   FIXED\n    SHELL := ./sh`>pwned`                 GNU never   both tools WROTE IT   FIXED\n    SHELL := ./sh$${x=`>pwned`}           GNU never   both tools WROTE IT   FIXED\n  THE OPERATORS\n    SHELL := ./sh;>pwned;                 GNU never   rkati WROTE IT        FIXED\n    SHELL := ./sh|>pwned|                 GNU never   both tools WROTE IT   FIXED\n    SHELL := ./sh&>pwned&                 GNU never   both tools WROTE IT   FIXED\n  WHERE THE VALUE CAME FROM\n    a target-specific SHELL               GNU never   both tools WROTE IT   FIXED\n    the shell a $(shell) runs under       GNU never   both tools WROTE IT   FIXED\n  THE CONTROLS\n    a plain $X, with ./sh present         GNU fails 127; the tools RAN ./sh FIXED\n    .ONESHELL:, which assembles no line   agreed, before and after\n    the direct-exec path                  agreed, before and after\n    SHELL := /usr/bin/env sh              agreed, before and after\n\nThe `;` row is the one where the two tools differ from each other: rkati assembles and hands the line to `/bin/sh`, which runs the second command, while this front end's own shell parses the line differently and dies instead. Both are wrong against GNU and both are fixed by the same escaping.\n\nThe `.ONESHELL:` control is the one that says where the escaping does NOT belong. GNU's one-shell branch execs `[shell, flags..., script]` and assembles no line at all, so there is nothing to escape and 4.4.1 reports `./sh$(touch pwned): No such file or directory` against the whole value. Both tools already agreed there and still do.\n\nWHERE IT WENT. `crate::simple_command::escaped_shell_name` (kati), which is GNU's loop and its character set — `SHELL_CHARACTERS`, already spelled out there as `sh_chars` — and returns the value untouched when there is nothing to escape. It is applied at every place a `$(SHELL)` goes into a command line, which is FOUR:\n\n  * `run_command`'s `goto slow` assembly (kati/src-rs/fileutil.rs), which is rkati's launch and every `$(shell)`;\n  * `Layout::launch` and `Layout::launch_step` (src/make/layout.rs), the front end's runtime launches;\n  * `Sink::command_bindings`, both arms (src/make/sink.rs), which is what the COMPILED MANIFEST holds.\n\nThe manifest arm is the one worth naming: the escaping composes with the writer's own, so a `\\$` written here becomes `\\$$` in the build file and reaches the shell as one literal `$`. A fix that had only touched the runtime would have left every compiled build with the hole in it.\n\nWHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT ESCAPED, and both halves are gated. The FLAGS: GNU copies `$(.SHELLFLAGS)` in as it stands, so a backquote there still reaches a shell in 4.4.1 too — measured, and it writes the file in all three tools. The BLANK: it is not in `sh_chars`, so `SHELL := /usr/bin/env sh` is still two words, which is the documented \"SHELL may be a multi-word command\". Parking a variant that adds the blank to the escape set fails two corpus cases with `build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`.\n\nFIXTURES, oracle-recorded with `MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1` against the pinned 4.4.1, with measured teeth taken by parking the source change and rerunning:\n\n    a-shell-holding-a-substitution-is-escaped-into-the-assembled-line\n      parked: `'pwned' was produced and GNU Make produced no such file`\n    a-shell-holding-a-backquote-is-escaped-into-the-assembled-line\n      parked: `'pwned' was produced and GNU Make produced no such file`\n    a-target-specific-shell-holding-a-substitution-is-escaped-too\n      parked: `'pwned' was produced and GNU Make produced no such file`\n    a-shell-function-runs-under-a-shell-escaped-into-its-line-too\n      parked: `'pwned' was produced and GNU Make produced no such file`\n    a-shell-holding-a-variable-reference-is-a-name-and-not-a-command\n      parked: `build succeeded where GNU Make's build failed`\n    a-shell-of-more-than-one-word-is-still-more-than-one-word\n      parked THE OTHER WAY (blank in the escape set): `build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`\n\nBeside them a unit test, `a_shell_name_is_escaped_for_the_line_and_not_for_the_word` (simple_command.rs), which pins the character set from both sides: every `sh_chars` byte escaped, and the blank, the backslash and the two quotes left alone.\n\nFOUND AND NODED, not fixed here: `make-a-default-shell-launch-splits-shellflags-a-shell-would-have-read`. Probing the flags control turned up a divergence of this front end's own — `Layout::launch_step` asks whether the SHELL is the default and never whether the FLAGS can be split, so `.SHELLFLAGS := -c \\`>pwned\\`` beside `/bin/sh` execs an argv where GNU assembles a line, the empty substitution stays a real argument, and the recipe DOES NOT RUN AT ALL. GNU writes `out`; rkati writes `out`; Ronin writes nothing and says nothing.\n\nGATES. `scripts/check-release.sh` EXIT 0 in full — fmt, check, lints, `cargo doc`, `cargo test --all-targets` 1675 passed / 0 failed / 2 ignored, equivalence, the two real projects, the port ledger, ninja conformance and the performance suite — on a host at load average 5.6. Workspace 1074 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, up two over the campaign baseline for the two unit tests added across these three landings. `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 829 / 0. Conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised, 286 raw, every family count byte-identical to the pre-change run. Equivalence 297 makefiles, 280 graphs, 0 disagreements, manifest respellings 8. Upstream inventory 1285 at 95 / 0 / 24 / 1166, family table byte-identical, TSV unchanged, compiler ZERO. make_port 4 / 4 over 1161 cases with all NINE divergence markers live. vim 19s, zsh 32s at -j8. Ninja conformance 425 / 425 plus the ten differential cases and the persistence round trip. Port ledger 170 / 170.\n\nTHE HOST RAN OUT OF DISK mid-battery, twice, at 100% of 149G with other work on the machine. No gate failed on code: both failures were `rustc-LLVM ERROR: IO failure on output stream: No space left on device`. Reclaimed from this repository's own rebuildable cache — `target/.../debug` including a 1.2G incremental directory that the ninja gate recreates because it does not set `CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0` — and every gate above was then run to completion. Nothing outside `target/` was touched.\n\nkati branch nplan/make-a-shell-holding-shell-syntax-is-escaped-into-the-assembled-line at aaa812d782f7242bdc2fb518c77600b97eea37d6, pushed before this pins it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T21:36:28.823235097Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-regen-replay-reads-a-one-script-shell-as-a-command-line
                title "Replay a recorded one-script $(shell) the way it was recorded"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-shell-function-under-one-shell-is-read-by-the-one-shell-branch)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T20:26:06.990284765Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Opened while landing make-a-shell-function-under-one-shell-is-read-by-the-one-shell-branch, which is what made it a divergence: before that landing the record and the replay both read a `$(shell)` as one command line, so they agreed by accident.\n\n`StampChecker::check_shell_command` (kati/src-rs/regen.rs) replays a recorded `$(shell)` to ask whether it still answers the same thing, and it passes `one_script: None` unconditionally. A `$(shell)` expanded below a `.ONESHELL:` line was RECORDED as a script — newlines separating commands, `[@+-]` off the front of each line, the three-word answer for refused `.SHELLFLAGS` — and is REPLAYED as a command line. Where the two readings differ the replay's answer differs from the recording's for a command that did not change, and the build regenerates when it need not. Nothing is built wrongly; the cost is a needless regeneration.\n\nThe replay cannot ask the session, because it runs before any makefile has been read: `flags.one_shell` is false there whatever the makefile says. The bit has to come from the stamp, and the stamp is the obstacle. `ShellResult` is loaded field by field in a fixed order (`op`, `shell`, `shellflag`, `cmd`, `result`, then the two ignored debug fields) that ckati writes and `regen_dump.rs` also reads, with no version word to branch on. So this is a stamp FORMAT question first and a launch question second.\n\nPROBE BEFORE FIXING. Whether ckati's stamp is still read by anything here — if `kati/ckati` and `Makefile.ckati` are the only readers and neither is gated, the field can simply be appended; if a stamp written by one tool has to be read by the other, it cannot. Check also whether the two ignored debug fields (`loc.filename`, `loc.line`) give somewhere to hide it without moving the others, and whether an unrecognised trailing field makes `regen_dump.rs` report a corrupt stamp or merely stop early — a reader that stops early is the difference between an appendable format and a frozen one.\n\nNot gated anywhere: the regen path has no corpus case, and `$(shell)` under `.ONESHELL:` is now three of them but none of them regenerates."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T22:34:22.086596595Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T23:13:19.779721679Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and THE CENTRAL CLAIM IS CONFIRMED AND GATED: a `$(shell)` expanded below `.ONESHELL:` no longer regenerates the ninja file when it has not changed. Measured before the fix, `.ONESHELL:` + `V := $(shell @echo hi; echo bye)` generated, then re-checked with byte-identical arguments, reports `$(shell @echo hi; echo bye) was changed, regenerating...` with nothing changed; after, `shell \"...\": clean (rerun)` then `No need to regenerate ninja file`. Nothing was ever built wrongly — the cost was the needless regeneration, exactly as filed.\n\nWHAT THE PROBE SETTLED — the three questions the filing left open:\n\n  * IS CKATI'S STAMP STILL READ BY ANYTHING HERE? No. There are ZERO `.cc` files left in the submodule — the C++ writer and its `regen.cc`/`regen_dump.cc` are retired; only a stale `kati/ckati` binary and a `Makefile.ckati` naming sources that no longer exist remain, in no gate. The stamp format is this tool's alone, read only by `regen.rs` (the check) and `regen_dump.rs` (`--dump_stamp_tool`). So the format could change; the only constraint is that rkati's own write and read agree and an old rkati stamp reads safely.\n\n  * DO THE IGNORED DEBUG FIELDS GIVE SOMEWHERE TO HIDE IT? They do (`loc.filename`, `loc.line`), but `regen_dump.rs` PRINTS `loc.line`, so packing a bit there would corrupt a displayed line number. The op int is the better host: it is `0..=5`, has room above, and both readers already run it through `CommandOp::from_int`.\n\n  * DOES AN UNRECOGNISED TRAILING FIELD STOP EARLY OR CORRUPT? The `load!` macro forces a regeneration on any incomplete read (`incomplete kati_stamp, regenerating...`), so a trailing field is not silently skipped — but an OLD stamp lacking it would misalign every field after it. A packed bit avoids that entirely.\n\nTHE FIX. The one-script bit rides in a high bit (`0x100`) of the op int the stamp already writes. `CommandOp` gains `as_stamp_int(one_script)` / `from_stamp_int(i) -> (op, one_script)`; `CommandResult` gains a `one_script` field, set at record time from the same `Option` `func_shell` already computes (`one_script.is_some()`) and to `false` for the three `$(file)` results that are not shell launches; `CommandOp` gains a `Debug` derive for the unit test. `generate_stamp` writes `cr.op.as_stamp_int(cr.one_script)`; `regen.rs` decodes to `(op, one_script)`, carries it on `ShellResult`, and the replay passes `sr.one_script.then_some(b\"-c\")` in place of the unconditional `None`; `regen_dump.rs` decodes the same and prints `one script: <bool>`.\n\nBACKWARD COMPAT, by construction and measured. There is NO field added and NO layout change — only a bit in an int old stamps hold as `0..=5` with the bit clear, which `from_stamp_int` reads as `one_script = false`: exactly the launch such a stamp was replayed with before. Measured: a plain (non-oneshell) `$(shell)` stamp is byte-for-byte what a pre-bit kati wrote, and the new `--dump_stamp_tool --cmds` reads it cleanly as `one script: false`, while a oneshell stamp reads `one script: true`. (In practice an old stamp forces a full regeneration before the CR section is even reached, because the stamp records the kati binary's mtime and a rebuilt binary trips that check first — the bit-clear read is the belt to that suspenders.) A standalone build of the pre-change kati to produce a real old stamp failed on an unrelated libc/feature-resolution error when built outside the workspace, so the byte-identical plain-shell stamp stands in for it — which is the same bytes an old kati writes.\n\nTHE BYTES vs THE BIT. A one-script launch carries `Option<&[u8]>` — the flags GNU Make's recursion defaults to — not just a bool, and the replay passes `Some(b\"-c\")`. Those bytes are read only in `shell_flag_argv`'s fallback, reached only where the recorded `.SHELLFLAGS` itself holds shell syntax and has to go back to a shell; `-c` (`-ec` under `.POSIX:`, which also tokenizes cleanly so the bytes stay unused) is the default for every `$(shell)` whose flags are ordinary — all of them bar a case that would have changed the recorded `shellflag` too and regenerated regardless. Recording the exact bytes would have needed a variable-length field and broken the byte-compat above for no reachable gain.\n\nA NOTE ON `$(...)` IN `.SHELLFLAGS`, off this node but recorded so the next probe does not chase it: a `$(...)` written in `.SHELLFLAGS` is expanded by Make as a variable reference (to empty), never reaching the shell — so it is not a way to put shell syntax in the flags (`.SHELLFLAGS := -c $$(touch pwned)` reaches a launch as `-c`). The differentiator that exercises the replay divergence is a metacharacter in the `$(shell)` COMMAND under `.ONESHELL:` — the `[@+-]` stripping and the newline-as-separator of the one-shell branch — which is what the gate uses.\n\nGATES (kati branch nplan/make-a-regen-replay-reads-a-one-script-shell-as-a-command-line at b0d6f3282e25c8bab964cc3970d0df053bf37c8f, pushed before this pins it). New end-to-end gate `kati/tests/regen_stamp.rs`, 3 cases: a one-shell `$(shell)` that did not change no longer regenerates; a plain one still does not (its stamp being the old-format bytes, read safely); a genuine change to a file the shell reads still regenerates. Plus two unit tests in `func.rs`: the op int round-trips the bit for every op, and a bare `0..=5` reads as `one_script = false`. kati 273 lib + 5 new + 17 exec_shell all pass.\n\nFULL BATTERY, all green. fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` exit 0; workspace 1082 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored (up eight over the 1074 campaign baseline across both landings); ronin lints and kati clippy exit 0; make_port 4/4 over 1165 with the NINE divergence markers intact; make conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised, 286 raw, family-identical; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 0 disagreements / respellings 8; upstream inventory TSV unchanged, compiler ZERO, narration 1166; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 834 / 0 (up five for the kati tests); `scripts/check-release.sh` EXIT 0 — 425 pinned Ninja tests, 10 differential, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence round trip, log content, package. The scheduler-barrier performance line read 1.34x against a recorded 1.02x, but the gate passed (EXIT 0, within threshold) and a regen-stamp change cannot touch the scheduler — host load, not this code; the same host built vim in 17s and zsh in 31s minutes later. All build/probe work under scripts/sandboxed; CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0. The compiled ninja output and every build-behaviour gate are byte-identical: this change touches only the regeneration stamp, which nothing but `--ninja --regen` reads."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T23:14:30.155753654Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-default-shell-launch-splits-shellflags-a-shell-would-have-read
                title "Send a launch to a shell when .SHELLFLAGS holds syntax a shell would read"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-a-shell-holding-shell-syntax-is-escaped-into-the-assembled-line)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T21:15:20.285827836Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while probing make-a-shell-holding-shell-syntax-is-escaped-into-the-assembled-line, as the control for \"GNU copies the FLAGS in unescaped\". It does, and that half agrees; what does not agree is what Ronin does with flags it should not have split.\n\nRONIN'S OWN, not kati's. `Layout::launch_step` and `Layout::launch_script` (src/make/layout.rs) take the argv path whenever `step.shell == DEFAULT_SHELL`, and split `.SHELLFLAGS` into words with `shell_flag_words`, which is `split(is_ascii_whitespace)` and nothing else. kati's model of the same decision refuses: `argv_to_exec` (fileutil.rs) asks `command_line_flag_argv`, which returns `None` for flags holding shell syntax, and the launch goes to a shell — which is GNU Make's `goto slow`. Ronin asks about the SHELL and not about the FLAGS, so it builds an argv where GNU builds a command line.\n\nMEASURED, one makefile run three ways:\n\n    SHELL := /bin/sh\n    .SHELLFLAGS := -c `>pwned`\n    all:\n    \\t@echo one > out\n\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   `pwned` written, `out` holds \"one\"\n      rkati            the same\n      Ronin            `pwned` written, `out` NOT WRITTEN AT ALL\n\n`pwned` is not the finding — GNU writes it too, because GNU copies the flags into the assembled line as they stand and the shell that reads the line runs the backquotes. The finding is `out`. GNU assembles `/bin/sh -c \\`>pwned\\` \"echo one > out\"`, and the shell it hands that to substitutes the backquotes into NOTHING, drops the empty field, and runs `/bin/sh -c \"echo one > out\"`. Ronin execs `[/bin/sh, -c, \"\\`>pwned\\`\", \"echo one > out\"]` directly, so the empty word is a real argument: `-c` takes the backquote word as the command, the recipe becomes `$0`, and the recipe never runs. A build that quietly does not run a recipe line is worse than one that runs it in the wrong shell.\n\nThe same shape reaches the compiled manifest, because `Sink::command_bindings` is fed by the same steps.\n\nWHERE. `Layout::launch_step` (src/make/layout.rs), the `step.shell == kati::simple_command::DEFAULT_SHELL || step.shell_is_a_path` branch, and `Layout::launch_script` beside it. The condition wants a third question: whether the flags can be split HERE, which is exactly `kati::simple_command::command_line_flag_argv` returning `Some`. `shell_is_a_path` (a `.ONESHELL` launch) is not affected — there GNU's one-shell branch really does parse the flags itself and really does exec an argv, and `shell_flag_argv` already models it.\n\nPROBE BEFORE FIXING. Whether the answer belongs in Ronin's layout or in the step kati hands it: `RecipeStep` already carries `direct`, `shell_is_a_path` and `default_shell_flags`, and a fourth field saying \"these flags are words\" would put the whole decision on kati's side of the boundary, where the rest of `construct_command_argv_internal` already lives. Probe also what the manifest should hold for a step that has to go to a shell with the default `SHELL` — the substitution Ronin makes for `/bin/sh` (its own executable under `argv[0]` of `/bin/sh`) is only available on the argv path, so a step that leaves it loses the builtin shell, and that trade has to be measured rather than assumed.\n\nNot gated anywhere: no ported case puts shell syntax in `.SHELLFLAGS` beside the default `SHELL`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T21:45:17.592316283Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T22:22:31.579850379Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. THE RECIPE NOW RUNS: a default-`SHELL` recipe whose `.SHELLFLAGS` hold shell syntax writes its output where it wrote nothing before, and a fixture holds it to that with measured teeth.\n\nTHE FILING WAS RIGHT about mechanism and site. One detail of its measurement is worth recording exactly. It said Ronin writes `pwned` and not `out`. Measured against the current binary run under its `make` name: `pwned` written, `out` NOT written, AND EXIT 0 — a build that silently skips a recipe line and reports success, which is the worst shape of the defect. (A first reproduction that wrote neither was my error, not Ronin's: I ran the `ronin` binary directly, so Ninja mode looked for a build.ninja that was not there. Run as `make`, the filing's shape is exact.) The nuance the filing reasoned to and got right: the empty backquote word makes `-c` take the recipe for `$0`, and because Ronin substitutes its builtin shell for `/bin/sh`, the builtin running `-c '`>pwned`'` is what leaves `pwned` behind while the recipe becomes the ignored `$0`.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. `construct_command_argv_internal` (job.c) decides direct-vs-shell per line, and it decides it about the whole assembled `$(SHELL) $(.SHELLFLAGS) LINE`, not the SHELL alone: GNU Make builds that text, re-tokenizes it, and where the tokenizer meets shell syntax it takes `goto slow` and hands the line to a shell that splits the flags. `Layout::launch_step` asked only whether `step.shell == DEFAULT_SHELL` and then split `.SHELLFLAGS` on whitespace with `shell_flag_words`, so a flag holding a backquote became a real argv word: Ronin exec'd `[/bin/sh, -c, `>pwned`, echo one > out]` where GNU assembled `/bin/sh -c `>pwned` \"echo one > out\"` and let the shell substitute the backquote to nothing and drop the empty field. kati's own model of the same decision — `argv_to_exec` (fileutil.rs) asking `command_line_flag_argv`, which returns `None` for flags holding shell syntax — already refuses; Ronin's layout never asked.\n\nWHERE IT WENT. `src/make/layout.rs`, and it stayed in Ronin's layout rather than becoming a fourth `RecipeStep` field. The boundary was already \"layout asks kati's tokenizer\": `launch_step`'s `.ONESHELL` arm calls `kati::simple_command::shell_flag_argv`, and the fix adds the parallel call for the ordinary arm, `command_line_flag_argv`, whose `Some` is the argv words and whose `None` is GNU's `goto slow` — a fall-through to the existing `Launch::Shell` path. `launch_script` (the whole-script path, `!launches_line_by_line`) got the same: it now returns `None` where the tokenizer refuses, so the caller uses the composed command line it already builds for a non-default shell. The combined `shell == DEFAULT || shell_is_a_path` branch is now split, with the `.ONESHELL` (`shell_is_a_path`) arm asked first, so `/bin/sh` under `.ONESHELL` keeps its builtin substitution unchanged. `shell_flag_words` is gone; it only ever differed from the tokenizer for quoted flags, where it was wrong — it kept the quote bytes, so `'-c'` reached `/bin/sh` as a file name to open and the recipe did not run.\n\nTHE MANIFEST NEEDED NOTHING — the filing's one overstatement. It said \"the same shape reaches the compiled manifest, because `Sink::command_bindings` is fed by the same steps.\" Measured, it does not: `command_bindings` ALWAYS assembles `<escaped shell> <flags> \"<script>\"` into one shell command line — GNU's slow-path form, already correct for flags a shell must split. The defect is the runtime line-by-line executor's alone (`SettledSteps` via `launch_step`); the `command` binding, which runs a `!launches_line_by_line` recipe and every `-n`/log/narration, was right before this and is right after. So this landing pins no compiler change and moves no compiler gate.\n\nTHE SUBSTITUTION TRADE, MEASURED not assumed. On the fall-through the outer shell that reads the assembled line is still the builtin — `shell_command`'s `Auto`/`Compat` arm substitutes for `/bin/sh` exactly as the argv path does — so `out` is written by the builtin doing the command substitution and dropping the empty field. Verified directly: the builtin run over `/bin/sh -c `>pwned` \"echo one > out\"` produces `pwned` and `out=one`, byte-for-byte GNU's result. The recipe's own `/bin/sh` inside the assembled line is the real one, which is exactly where GNU also ends up; nothing is lost that GNU keeps.\n\nTHE MATRIX, ten shapes, each run three ways; `out` written = the recipe ran:\n\n  THE FIX\n    .SHELLFLAGS := -c `>pwned`         GNU out+pwned  Ronin out+pwned  FIXED (was out MISSING, exit 0)\n    target-specific -c `>pwned`        GNU out+pwned  Ronin out+pwned  FIXED (was out MISSING, exit 0)\n    .SHELLFLAGS := '-c'                GNU out        Ronin out        FIXED (was build failed, exit 2)\n  AGREEMENT ON FAILURE\n    .SHELLFLAGS := -c |>pwned|         GNU no-out ex2 Ronin no-out ex2 agreed (GNU's own pipeline fails)\n    empty .SHELLFLAGS                  GNU no-out ex2 Ronin no-out ex2 agreed\n  CONTROLS, unchanged (direct argv path preserved)\n    default -c / .SHELLFLAGS := -c / -e -c      all out on all three\n    .ONESHELL: + -c `>pwned`           GNU aborts 134 (its documented heap overflow); Ronin, rkati exit 2 — unchanged, shell_is_a_path arm untouched\n  MAKE-LEVEL, not a shell case\n    .SHELLFLAGS := -c $$(touch pwned)  all out, no pwned — `$(...)` is a make variable ref that expands to empty, leaving `-c`; not the shell fall-through, so no fixture for it\n\nFIXTURES, oracle-recorded with `MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1` against pinned 4.4.1; teeth taken by parking the source (manual backup/restore, no stash) and rerunning make_port:\n\n    a-default-shell-flag-holding-a-backquote-sends-the-launch-to-a-shell\n      parked: `'out' was not produced`\n    a-target-specific-shell-flag-holding-a-backquote-sends-the-launch-to-a-shell\n      parked: `'out' was not produced`\n    a-default-shell-flag-of-a-single-quoted-word-still-launches-directly\n      parked: `build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded`\n    a-default-shell-flag-that-is-only-dash-c-launches-directly\n      the pure control — passes parked and fixed alike, pinning the direct path as unchanged\n\nBeside them three unit tests in `layout.rs` pinning the decision at the boundary: plain and single-quoted flags are a `Launch::Direct`, syntax-holding flags a `Launch::Shell` with the flags copied in unescaped.\n\nGATES (runtime-only; the compiled-output gates are audited unchanged rather than moved). fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` exit 0; workspace 1077 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, up three over 1074 for the unit tests; `scripts/check-lints.sh` exit 0; make_port 4/4 over 1165 build-intent cases with the NINE divergence markers intact and four new non-divergence cases; make conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised, 286 raw, every family byte-identical to base; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 0 disagreements / respellings 8; upstream inventory TSV unchanged, compiler ZERO, narration 1166; the two real projects vim 29s and zsh 42s at -j8. All build/probe work under scripts/sandboxed; CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0. No kati change: `command_line_flag_argv` was already in the submodule, so no new kati SHA is pinned by this landing."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-23T22:33:07.826113105Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-target-specific-append-value-holds-only-the-appended-text
                title "Hand back only the appended text for the value of a target-specific append"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-target-specific-append-expands-its-tail-where-it-is-read)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T00:45:21.53291964Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while delivering make-target-specific-append-expands-its-tail-where-it-is-read, and pre-existing rather than introduced by it — left out because it is a different mechanism: that node is about WHERE the tail expands (per reader), and this is about WHAT $(value) hands back for a target-specific `+=`.\n\nProbed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, GNU on the left:\n\n    V = g\n    W = outerW\n    a.o: V += <$(W)>\n    a.o: W = aoW\n    all: a.o\n    a.o: ; @printf 'value=[%s] expanded=[%s]' \"$(value V)\" \"$(V)\" > out\n\n  GNU:   value=[<$(W)>]      expanded=[g <aoW>]\n  Ronin: value=[g <$(W)>]    expanded=[g <aoW>]\n\n$(value) hands back the variable's stored text unexpanded. GNU Make stores ONLY the appended part on a target-specific `+=` binding — `<$(W)>` — and `variable_append` walks the parent chain to prepend the base at expansion time, so `$(value)` sees the tail alone while `$(V)` sees `g <aoW>`. Ronin now stores the whole chain joined (base<sep>tail) as the recursive binding's `orig` text — appended_recursive_value in kati src-rs/var.rs — so `$(value)` reads `g <$(W)>`, the base included.\n\nNote the EXPANSION is right in both; only $(value)'s raw text differs, and only because the base is prepended in the stored text rather than walked at read. Before make-target-specific-append-expands-its-tail-where-it-is-read the divergence was larger — Ronin settled the whole thing to a simple string, so $(value) read the fully expanded `g <aoW>`. So this node is a narrowing of a pre-existing gap, not a new one.\n\nWHAT A FIX TAKES: GNU's model is a per-binding stored value of the appended text alone, with the base found by a parent walk at expansion. Ronin's guard/public machinery (make-target-specific-append-reads-the-readers-scope) settled the base into each binding instead. Matching $(value) means the target-specific binding holding only its own appended expression and the outward walk being performed at read rather than baked — which is the parent-walk `variable_append` is, and a larger change to how apply_rule_vars stores a `+=` than the tail fix was. Not urgent: it needs `$(value)`/`$(flavor)`-style introspection of a target-specific `+=`, which a build rarely branches on.\n\nNo corpus row turns on it: make_port, conformance, equivalence and the upstream inventory are all unmoved by the tail fix, and none carries a `$(value)` case over a target-specific `+=`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T04:21:48.091344266Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T04:43:59.976421696Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. The filing's claim re-verified against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make before touching anything, capturing the raw text with `$(file >…,$(value V))` rather than a shell arg (the `$(W)` in the value is a command substitution to the shell): `V = g` / `a.o: V += <$(W)>` gave Ronin `$(value V) = g <$(W)>` where GNU gives `<$(W)>` — the base baked into the stored text.\n\nPROBED FIRST, and it defined the boundary the fix turns on. GNU's `$(value)` of a target-specific `+=` is NOT always the tail alone — it is the target-specific SET's own accumulated text, which excludes a base inherited from a PARENT set but INCLUDES a base the same set put there. Against the oracle:\n* Global base (`V = g`, `a.o: V += <p>`): `$(value)` = `<p>` — parent base excluded.\n* Chained (`a.o: V += <p>` then `a.o: V += <q>`): `$(value)` = `<p> <q>` — both appends, base `g` excluded.\n* Same-set base (`a.o: V := inset` then `a.o: V += <t>`): `$(value)` = `inset <t>` — base included, because the `:=` is in a.o's own set. Ronin already matched this one.\n* No base (`a.o: V += <t>`, V undefined): `$(value)` = `<t>`.\nEvery `$(V)` expansion carries the base regardless (`g <p>`, `g <p> <q>`, `inset <t>`), because `$(V)` walks to the base whether it was stored or found.\n\nSo the model is GNU's `variable_append`: a target-specific binding stores only its own set's text and sets `v->append` when the base lives in a parent, walking that parent to prepend the base at lookup. `$(value)` reads the stored text; `$(V)` triggers the walk.\n\nDELIVERED, kati 8441be641f78cfb44e492cbfdccb810232ba1dd7 on branch nplan/make-target-specific-append-value-holds-only-the-appended-text, pushed before this pin. `appended_recursive_value` (src-rs/var.rs) gains a `base_in_scope` flag: the text beside the expression (`$(value)`) carries the base only when it is set — the current `appended_text` join — and is the tail alone otherwise. The expression is UNCHANGED (`appended_values` with the real base_text, so the separator and the base for `$(V)` are exactly as before), which is why every expansion stays byte-identical and only `$(value)` moves. `apply_rule_vars` (src-rs/dep.rs) computes `base_in_scope` for the guard and the public binding alike: `outer` already held what a name stood at before the run bound it, so a base still ptr-equal to that value came from a parent (excluded) and any other came from this run's own earlier assignment or append (included).\n\nEvery probed cell now matches GNU: the filing's reproducer (`value=<$(W)>`, `exp=g <aoW>`, flavor recursive, origin file), chained, same-set `:=` base, simple `:=` base, no base, a `$(shell)` in the tail read back unexpanded, and the guard/public divergence across a parent link (a `%.o: private V += …` — dep reads plain `g`, a.o reads `value=<$(W)>`/`exp=g <aoW>`). The `V += $(V)` recursion guard still dies.\n\nFIXTURE, oracle-recorded, teeth measured on the pre-fix tree. `a-target-specific-append-value-holds-only-the-appended-text` writes three files with `$(file)`: `v-value.out=[<one> <two>]` (V's base `g` is a parent, excluded), `w-value.out=[inset <tail>]` (W's base is a `:=` in a.o's own set, included), and `expanded.out=[g <one> <two>] [inset <tail>]` (both walk to the base). It gates both halves of the boundary — parent-excluded and same-set-included — in one case; recorded via MAKE_PORT_RECORD against the pinned oracle, `git status` confirmed only this case was created. Teeth: on the pre-fix binary `v-value.out` was `[g <one> <two>]`, base wrongly included.\n\nWHY THE EXPANSION COULD NOT MOVE, checked rather than assumed. The change touches only the `orig` text; the `Value` expression is built the same way, so `$(V)` is byte-identical and no build effect changes. The chained case confirms it end to end: the second `+=` reads the first binding's `orig` (now `<p>`) as its base text and its expression as its base value, and produces `orig=<p> <q>` with expression still expanding to `g <p> <q>`.\n\nGATES (full battery, serial, all green): fmt both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets`; `scripts/check-lints.sh`; workspace 1086 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored; `--no-default-features` 834 / 0; make_port 4/4 with the new value case, four divergence sidecars live, none rewritten; conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised, 286 raw — unmoved; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 respellings — unmoved; upstream inventory 1285 @ 95 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1166 narration — compiler ZERO, unmoved; `check-make-projects.sh all` vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 + 36 modules in 29s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential; `scripts/check-release.sh` EXIT 0, no uncovered rules, no stale references. No corpus row outside the new fixture turns on it, as the filing said."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T04:44:04.835765305Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-define-directive-whose-name-reads-as-an-assignment-panics
                title "Stop panicking on a define directive whose name reads as an assignment"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T09:09:29.783666172Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by make-upstream-residue-triage while surveying the 95 unclassified upstream rows: variables/define.diff.16 (the suite's variables/define script, `make -f define.mk.12`) has Ronin PANIC where GNU Make 4.4.1 builds cleanly. This is a crash on valid input, not a narration difference, so the row stays unclassified and this owns it.\n\nMEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, 2026-08-24. The suite makefile define.mk.12 is:\n\n    define = define\n    $(define) : ;@echo $@\n    %:define\n    all: define foo\n    %.x : define\n    foo:;\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  prints `define`, exit 0.\n  Ronin      thread 'main' panicked at kati/src-rs/parser.rs:1125:5: assertion failed: sep != 0\n             exit 101 (a Rust panic, no graph produced).\n\nMINIMISED to two lines — the trigger is a `define` DIRECTIVE whose remainder reads as an assignment:\n\n    define = x\n    all:;\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  `all` is up to date, exit 0.\n  Ronin      same panic, exit 101.\n\n`define = x` alone (no `all:`) panics too; `V = x` / `%:V` / an ordinary assignment do not — the trigger is the `define` keyword followed by ` = value` on the same line, which GNU reads as a define directive (the variable name being `=`, or the assignment operator being read as the name) without complaint. kati's parser hits `assertion failed: sep != 0` at kati/src-rs/parser.rs:1125 while splitting the directive line.\n\nWHERE: kati/src-rs/parser.rs:1125 (our code). Whoever takes this should probe GNU's read.c handling of a `define` line carrying an `=` to settle what the correct parse is (GNU tolerates it; Ronin must at minimum not crash), then replace the assertion with the tolerant read. A panic reachable from a plain makefile line is the sharpest kind of defect this triage found."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:42:13.538451193Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Row attribution for traceability, 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch: this node owns upstream inventory row `variables/define.diff.16`, which is still recorded `unclassified` and is counted as a DUPLICATE (already-filed defect) in docs/make-upstream-residue.md rather than as a new finding. Re-measured on 2026-08-24 against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make: unchanged — GNU prints `define` and exits 0, Ronin panics at kati/src-rs/parser.rs:1125 with `assertion failed: sep != 0`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T21:19:42.738522504Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T21:20:13.203898331Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CORRECTED THE FILING, and the defect is wider than it was filed as.\n\nThe filing guessed GNU reads `define = x` as a define directive \"the variable name being `=`, or the assignment operator being read as the name\". It does not. Measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make and confirmed in its own source:\n\n    define = x\n    all:;@echo \"define=[$(define)] origin=$(origin define) flavor=$(flavor define)\"\n      GNU: define=[x] origin=file flavor=recursive\n\nGNU reads the line as an ORDINARY ASSIGNMENT to a variable literally named `define`. read.c `parse_var_assignment` calls `parse_variable_definition` (variable.c) BEFORE it looks for any keyword, and only when that walk returns NULL does it reach the `export`/`override`/`private`/`define`/`undefine` arms. The walk sets its `end` at the first blank and then wants an assignment operator: reaching one makes the first word the name; reaching any other character with `end` already set (`goto other`, `if (end) return NULL`) makes the line no assignment at all. So the boundary is exactly \"does an operator stand where the directive's argument would\":\n\n  ASSIGNMENT to `define`  — `define = x`, `define := x`, `define ::= x`, `define :::= x`,\n                            `define += x`, `define ?= x`, `define != cmd`, `define  =  x`,\n                            and with a modifier in front: `export define = x`, `override define = x`\n  DIRECTIVE               — `define V =`, `define X= x`, `define a b = c`, `define $(N) = v`\n\nMEASURED TEETH, all against the oracle, before the fix:\n  * PANIC (exit 101, `assertion failed: sep != 0`, kati/src-rs/parser.rs:1125) on the four spellings whose\n    operator opens with `=` or `:`, plus both modifier paths. Six spellings crash.\n  * WRONG WITHOUT CRASHING on `+=`, `?=`, `!=`: read as a directive, they waited for an `endef` the makefile\n    never owed and the run died `*** missing 'endef', unterminated 'define'.` Three more spellings.\nSo nine of the ten spellings the case covers were wrong; the filing named one.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT: kati (Make semantics, the compiler) — kati 4a7270fa4faaca178fbb7dee29aab1939abb5d2d,\nbranch nplan/make-a-define-directive-whose-name-reads-as-an-assignment-panics. `handle_make_directive` and\n`handle_assign_directive` now guard their `define` arm with `starts_assignment(&rest)`, which is the guard\n`undefine`, `export`, `override` and `private` already carried and is exactly the two answers GNU's walk\ngives once the argument has its blanks off. Two one-line guards; the `parse_assign_statement` invariant that\nwas being breached is left standing, because with the dispatch right nothing can reach it at position zero.\nThe second table matters on its own: `export define = x` and `override define = x` reach `define` through\n`handle_assign_directive`, and both panicked.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX:\n  * kati parser unit test `an_assignment_operator_after_define_names_define` FAILS BY PANIC when parked —\n    `panicked at kati/src-rs/parser.rs:1125:5: assertion failed: sep != 0`, which is the defect itself\n    reached in-process. Passes with the fix.\n  * build-intent case tests/make/a-define-carrying-an-assignment-operator-defines-define/ FAILS when parked:\n    \"build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded\". Oracle-recorded, ten spellings in one Makefile\n    (all seven operators, the modifier path, a double-blank spelling, and `define KEPT =` + `endef` for the\n    side that must stay a directive). Recorded with MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1 against make-4.4.1.\n\nINVENTORY ROW MOVED — exactly one, audited by name against the pre-run tsv:\n  variables/define.diff.16  unclassified/evaluation → narration/ninja-progress\nThat is the row this node owns. Its residue is now Ronin's progress counter against GNU's recipe echo and\nnothing else; the semantics agree. unclassified 57 → 56, narration 1200 → 1201, compiler ZERO throughout,\ninterface 28 unchanged, total 1285 unchanged.\n\nGATES (all serial, all through scripts/sandboxed): fmt (root + kati) clean; check --workspace --all-targets\nEXIT 0; workspace tests 1101 passed / 0 failed (1100 before, +1 the new parser test); check-lints.sh EXIT 0;\nmake_port 4/4 over 1199 cases with the five divergence markers intact; conformance 354 runs, 326/28\nnormalised, 276 raw — unchanged, no regression; equivalence 274/276/56/0 disagreements + 8 respellings;\ncheck-make-projects.sh all — vim 9.2 and zsh 5.9.2 both built; ninja conformance 425/425 + 10 differential\n+ 92 build-outcome; --no-default-features EXIT 0.\n\nNOT FIXED, deliberately out of scope and left as it was: `define X= x` draws GNU's\n`extraneous text after 'define' directive` warning where Ronin is silent. That is narration, it is not what\nthis node owns, and the build is identical either way."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-25T00:07:15.155724388Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-command-line-assignment-with-an-unterminated-reference-is-refused
                title "Read a command-line assignment whose value never terminates a reference, as GNU does"
                kind @fix
                implements {
                    target-impl ([spec:ronin:req:make.exported-value-charged-to-the-job])
                }
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T09:09:36.893493731Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by make-upstream-residue-triage while surveying the 95 unclassified upstream rows: variables/MAKEFLAGS.diff.119 has Ronin REFUSE a command line GNU Make 4.4.1 builds. The residue is Ronin's own diagnostic, a genuine build-intent divergence rather than narration, so the row stays unclassified and this owns it.\n\nMEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, 2026-08-24. Makefile:\n\n    all:; $(info good)\n\ninvoked as `make 'hello=$(world'` — a command-line variable assignment whose VALUE holds an unterminated `$(` reference:\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  prints `good`, `make: 'all' is up to date.`, exit 0. The value `$(world` is stored and never expanded, so its unterminated reference is never an error.\n  Ronin      `ronin: Makefile:1: unterminated variable reference.` and does not build — it parses/validates the command-line assignment's value eagerly and refuses the unterminated reference.\n\nWithout the `hello=$(world` argument the makefile builds identically in both tools, so the divergence is entirely in how a command-line assignment's value is handled: GNU defers expansion of a command-line assignment (a lazily-expanded, recursively-assigned variable) and so never reads the `$(` unless something uses `$(hello)`; Ronin reads it at the point of assignment. The fix is to hold a command-line assignment's value unexpanded the way GNU does, so an unused value with an unterminated reference is not an error. GNU only complains about `$(world` if the variable is actually expanded."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:42:16.410465346Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Row attribution for traceability, 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch: this node owns upstream inventory row `variables/MAKEFLAGS.diff.119`, which is still recorded `unclassified` and is counted as a DUPLICATE (already-filed defect) in docs/make-upstream-residue.md rather than as a new finding. Re-measured on 2026-08-24 against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make: unchanged — `make -f Makefile 'hello=$(world'` over `all:; $(info good)` has GNU print `good` and exit 0, Ronin refuse with `unterminated variable reference.`"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T23:52:31.565759765Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @blocker
                        text "STOPPED FOR THE OPERATOR. The filing's symptom is real and reproduces exactly; its EXPLANATION is wrong,\nand once corrected the node asks for something that is an architectural decision rather than a defect\nrepair. Not classified, not closed, no code written. Everything below is measured against\nreference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, 2026-08-25.\n\nTHE FILED REPRODUCER STILL HOLDS:\n    Makefile: `all:; $(info good)`   invoked `make 'hello=$(world'`\n    GNU 4.4.1  `good` / `make: 'all' is up to date.`  exit 0\n    Ronin      `ronin: Makefile:1: unterminated variable reference.`  exit 2\n\nWHAT THE FILING SAYS, AND WHY IT IS WRONG. It says \"GNU defers expansion of a command-line assignment (a\nlazily-expanded, recursively-assigned variable) and so never reads the `$(` unless something uses\n`$(hello)`\". GNU does read it, and something using `$(hello)` is not what makes it read it. A command-line\nvariable is EXPORTED, and GNU expands every exported recursive variable's value each time it builds a\nchild's environment — `target_environment`, variable.c: \"If V is recursively expanded and didn't come from\nthe environment, expand its value.\" So the same command line over a makefile that launches ANY recipe\nrefuses in GNU too, with `hello` mentioned nowhere:\n\n    all:;@echo ran      +  make 'hello=$(world'   → GNU: *** unterminated variable reference.  Stop.\n    all:;@echo [$$hello]+  make 'hello=$(world'   → GNU: *** unterminated variable reference.  Stop.\n    all:;@env|grep hello+  make 'hello=$(world'   → GNU: *** unterminated variable reference.  Stop.\n    all:;@:             +  make 'hello=$(world'   → GNU: silent, exit 0   (`:` launches no shell)\n    all:; $(info good)  +  make 'hello=$(world'   → GNU: silent, exit 0   (recipe expands to nothing)\n\nSo GNU's rule is not \"never expands\"; it is \"expands once for each job it actually launches\". The filed\ncase is simply one where GNU launches none — the recipe `$(info good)` expands to no command, so no\nenvironment is ever built.\n\nCOUNTED DIRECTLY, with `hello=$(info SIDE)x` so each expansion announces itself:\n    all: a b / a:;@echo A / b:;@echo B    GNU: SIDE A SIDE B    Ronin: SIDE SIDE A B\n    all:;@echo A                          GNU: SIDE A           Ronin: SIDE SIDE A\n    all:; $(info good)                    GNU: good             Ronin: SIDE SIDE good\nGNU: once per launched job — two, one, zero. Ronin: twice, always, before any recipe runs.\n\nWHY THAT IS THE ARCHITECTURE AND NOT A BUG TO PATCH. Ronin settles a compilation unit's exported\nenvironment ONCE, at load time, before any recipe — `exported_environment` at src/make.rs:813, and\nkati/src-rs/export.rs says so in as many words (\"Ronin settles the compilation unit's environment once,\nbefore any recipe runs\"). GNU builds one per job inside `job.c`. Expanding an exported value at compile\ntime is inherent to compiling the environment rather than emulating its construction, and the refusal the\nnode was filed over is that choice meeting a makefile that launches nothing. Making Ronin match would mean\ndeferring the expansion of exported values to job launch and raising the failure there — which is the\nruntime side of the boundary this campaign has a standing rule about, and is a decision for the operator\nrather than for a dispatch.\n\nWHAT I WOULD FIX IF TOLD TO, AND IT IS NOT THE SAME QUESTION. The DOUBLING is a plain defect independent of\nall of the above: Ronin expands every command-line variable twice at settle time, once in\n`exported_environment` (src/make.rs:813, because a command-line variable is exported) and once in\n`command_line_environment` (src/make.rs:1856, building what a semantic subninja inherits). One settle should\nexpand once. That is bounded and does not touch when the settle happens — but it does not resolve this\nnode's reproducer either: with the doubling gone Ronin would print SIDE once and still refuse the\n`$(world` case, because it would still be expanding at settle time.\n\nTWO SMALLER DIVERGENCES NOTED IN PASSING, both narration and neither fixed:\n  * `make 'hello:=$(world'` (SIMPLE flavour, which both tools expand at assignment time): GNU refuses with\n    no location, `make: *** unterminated variable reference.  Stop.`; Ronin refuses with\n    `ronin: *bootstrap*:0: unterminated variable reference.` — an internal filename reaching a user\n    diagnostic.\n  * Where both refuse over the recursive form, GNU names no location and Ronin names the makefile line the\n    read happened to be on (`M7:1`), which is not where the text came from either.\n\nFOR THE OPERATOR — the question to rule on: should Ronin defer the expansion of an exported variable's\nvalue from unit settle to job launch, so that a value nothing ever reads is never expanded and a value read\nby N jobs is expanded N times? If yes, this node is a defect and I will take it. If no, this node is a\ndeliberate divergence and wants a `divergence` record rather than a repair, and the doubling above should\nbe filed separately as the one genuine defect in the area."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T23:54:16.538307127Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T23:58:39.956069167Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBED, DIAGNOSED AND COSTED. NOT FIXED, and not because it needs a ruling — because the honest design is\none node's work of its own and a half-plumbed one would be worse than none. Everything below is measured\nagainst reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-27, after\nmake-a-command-line-variables-value-is-settled-once landed.\n\nTHE 2026-08-25 BLOCKER'S DIAGNOSIS IS CONFIRMED AND ITS RULE IS NOW EXACT. GNU Make expands an exported\nrecursive value exactly when it MATERIALISES AN ENVIRONMENT — for a job it really launches, or for a\n`$(shell)` — and never otherwise. Ten cells, `make 'hello=$(world'` over each makefile:\n\n    makefile / switches                         GNU        Ronin\n    all:                          (no recipe)   0          2   DIVERGES\n    all:; $(info good)            (no command)  0          2   DIVERGES\n    all:; @:                      (a no-op)     0          2   DIVERGES\n    all:; @echo ran   with -n                   0          2   DIVERGES\n    all:; @echo ran   with -q                   1          2   DIVERGES\n    all:; @echo ran                             2          2   agree\n    all:; @echo [$(hello)]                      2          2   agree\n    X := $(shell echo hi) / all:; @echo ran     2          2   agree   (the $(shell) builds one)\n    all:; @echo ran   goal `nope`               2          2   status agrees, reason does not:\n                                                                GNU refuses over the missing rule and\n                                                                never reaches a launch.\n\nTwo of those five are NEW and neither the filing nor the blocker had them: `-n` and `-q`. They are what\nmakes the fix bigger than \"settle later\", and they are the reason a smaller fix does not exist.\n\nTHE `@:` CELL HAS ITS OWN MECHANISM, and it is written down in GNU Make's source rather than inferred.\n`start_job_command` (job.c:1370): \"Optimize an empty command. People use this for timestamp rules, so\navoid forking a useless shell.\" A line whose argv is a Bourne-compatible shell, `-c` or `-ec`, and exactly\n`:` goes to `next_command` — after `commands_started++`, so the target still counts as remade. No process,\nso no environment, so no expansion. Ronin forks a shell for `:`.\n\nWHY THERE IS NO SOUND PARTIAL, having looked for one. Three were tried on paper and each fails a measured\ncell:\n  * Make the unit's environment lazy and force it at the first launch. Fails `-n` (0 against 2): Ronin's\n    dry run still ASSEMBLES the command, and the assembled command string is where the environment lives —\n    `CommandLayout::launch` writes `env NAME=value` into the text a `-n` narrates.\n  * Defer the FAILURE rather than the environment: keep the name, record that expanding it raised, raise\n    at the launch that would carry it. Fails `-n` and `-q` for the same reason, and fails `@:` outright.\n  * Skip the settle for a unit that declares no rule. Gets `all:` alone and nothing else.\n\nWHAT THE FIX IS, named and costed. Three pieces, and all three are needed for the five cells:\n  1. The unit's exported environment stops being a value settled at read and becomes one the retained\n     evaluator answers on demand. The forcing points are exactly three and all are enumerable:\n     `descendant_context.environment` (only where a child unit is composed — and a `$(MAKE)` line is a job\n     GNU launches, so forcing there is right), `descendant_context.recipe_environment` →\n     `GraphSink::set_recipe_environment` → `CommandLayout::recipe_environment`, and kati's\n     `generate_shell`. The second is the one that has to become lazy; `RecipeUnit` already retains the\n     session that would fill it, which is why this is tractable at all.\n  2. The environment leaves the assembled command TEXT and is applied at spawn. Today\n     `CommandLayout::launch` bakes `env NAME=value ... exec sh -c '<script>'` into one string, so anything\n     that reads the command — the progress line, `-n`, the build log — reads the environment with it.\n     GNU's `-n` prints the recipe line and builds no environment at all. This is the piece that makes the\n     change cross-cutting rather than local, and it is also the piece that would take the `env` prefix out\n     of Ronin's own dry-run narration.\n  3. A recipe line that comes to exactly `:` starts no process, per job.c:1370.\nEstimated at two to three hundred lines across `src/make.rs`, `src/make/sink.rs`, `src/make/layout.rs`,\n`src/make/recipe.rs` and kati's `export.rs`, plus the consequence that a class of refusal moves from read\ntime to build time — which changes where the diagnostic is printed relative to other output and wants the\nconformance and build-outcome corpora re-read rather than assumed.\n\nWHAT IS ALSO TRUE AND IS THE OPERATOR'S ALONE. There is a defensible reading in which Ronin is right here:\na command-line assignment whose value cannot be read is a malformed invocation, and refusing it at once\nbeats building most of a tree and failing at the first recipe. That is an ACCEPT question, which the\nstanding ruling keeps for him. It is recorded, not parked: unless he accepts it, this stays a defect and\nthe design above is what closes it. The node is left Todo with that design rather than half-built.\n\nROW: `variables/MAKEFLAGS.diff.119` is still unclassified and still belongs here."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-27T14:56:55.041257795Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "DELIVERED ON THE OPERATOR'S RULING (Brendan, 2026-08-27): FIX, do not accept — GNU builds successfully\nhere, so refusing is a false failure. All ten cells now agree, and five more shapes measured beside them\ndo too.\n\nTHE TEN CELLS, RE-MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-27, before and after.\n`make -f M <switches> 'hello=$(world'` over each makefile, exit status:\n\n    makefile / switches                         GNU   before   after\n    all:                          (no recipe)    0      2       0\n    all:; $(info good)            (no command)   0      2       0\n    all:; @:                      (a no-op)      0      2       0\n    all:; @echo ran   with -n                    0      2       0\n    all:; @echo ran   with -q                    1      2       1\n    all:; @echo ran                              2      2       2\n    all:; @echo [$(hello)]                       2      2       2\n    X := $(shell echo hi) / all:; @echo ran      2      2       2\n    all:; @echo ran   goal `nope`                2      2       2\n\nThe 2026-08-27 blocker's rule is confirmed exactly and its five diverging cells are the five that moved.\nFive further shapes measured after the fix, all agreeing at 2 where GNU refuses: a single job, two jobs\nunder `-k`, a composed `$(MAKE)` child, a grouped `&:` target, and a `::` chain.\n\nWHAT THE PROBE CORRECTED IN THE COSTED DESIGN. That design named three pieces and put a LAZY unit\nenvironment first. The lazy half is not what any of the five cells needs, and GNU's own source says why:\n`start_job_command` (job.c) reaches `target_environment` at line 1431 — AFTER the empty-command\n`goto next_command` at 1370, after the `-n` return and after the `-q` return. GNU is not lazy about the\nVALUE; it is strict about the MOMENT. What has to move is not when the expansion happens but where its\nFAILURE is charged, which is smaller and is a compiled fact rather than a runtime one.\n\nThat distinction also settles what this does NOT close, said plainly: how MANY times such a value is\nexpanded is unchanged. GNU expands it once per job it launches; Ronin settles one environment per\ncompilation unit and expands it once for the unit. That remainder belongs to\nmake-a-command-line-variables-value-is-settled-once, which recorded it, and closing it would mean\nre-expanding per job — runtime emulation of Make rather than compiled intent.\n\nTHE FIX, in three places. kati acdf96003bcd28c2d7445077864e5218a6fa9afa, branch\nnplan/make-a-command-line-assignment-with-an-unterminated-reference-is-refused.\n  1. kati `export.rs`: `settled_environment` beside `exported_environment` — the same walk with a value\n     that cannot be read HELD rather than raised, handed back as `Unreadable { name, why }`. Only the\n     first, because only the first is reachable: GNU builds the environment name by name and the job dies\n     at the one it cannot read. `exported_environment` is unchanged and is still what a `$(shell)`\n     (func.rs), the emitted graph's environment script (ninja.rs) and kati's own executor (main.rs) call\n     — each of those materialises an environment right there and each must still refuse. That is why the\n     `X := $(shell echo hi)` cell agrees at 2 with nothing done for it.\n  2. Ronin carries the refusal to the launch instead of to the read. `CommandLayout::unreadable`\n     (src/make/layout.rs), set per compilation unit through the sink, turns every launch of that unit\n     into `Launch::Refused(diagnostic)` — a third answer to \"how is this started\" beside a command line\n     and an argument list. `Builder::prepare_edge` raises it as `BuildError::LateCommand` for exactly the\n     steps the run would really start, using the same predicate `take_step` uses to decide whether to\n     spawn; `question_step` asks it again for the one line `-q` really runs. So `-n`, `-q` and a run with\n     no command reach it unmoved, and the diagnostic lands where a process would have.\n  3. GNU's empty command, `start_job_command` (job.c:1370): \"People use this for timestamp rules, so\n     avoid forking a useless shell.\" A Bourne-compatible shell, a lone `-c` or `-ec`, and a script of\n     exactly `:` starts no process. `kati::simple_command::is_the_empty_command` is that test on the argv\n     GNU compares; the front end asks it as it builds the launch and the engine reads the answer off\n     `DirectLaunch::starts_no_process` without knowing what a Makefile is — the shape\n     `runs_while_pretending` already has. The ORDER is GNU's: `direct_launch` asks whether the line starts\n     a process BEFORE it refuses, because job.c reaches the optimisation above its `target_environment`\n     call. The line is still narrated and the target is still remade; only the fork is skipped.\n\nAND ONE THING THE PROBE FOUND THAT NOBODY HAD FILED. The diagnostic pointed at the wrong line. GNU's\n`recursively_expand_for_file` (variable.c) sets `expanding_var` to the variable's own `fileinfo` before\nexpanding, so a value that will not expand is reported against where it was DEFINED. Ronin's\n`expand_for_environment` walked the table without entering the variable at all, so `expanding_var_loc`\nfell back to wherever the read had got to. Over `# a comment` / `export FOO = $(bar` / `all:; @echo ran`:\n\n    GNU    M:2: *** unterminated variable reference.  Stop.\n    before ronin: M:3: unterminated variable reference.\n    after  ronin: M:2: unterminated variable reference.\n\nREMAINING NARRATION DIVERGENCE, unfixed and named. For a value the COMMAND LINE supplied GNU names no\nlocation at all — `make: *** unterminated variable reference.  Stop.` — because `fileinfo` is empty and\n`error()` falls back to the program name. Ronin still says `ronin: M:1: …`. The mechanism is exact:\n`Evaluator::expanding_var_loc` (kati/src-rs/eval.rs) cannot tell \"inside a variable that has no\ndefinition site\" from \"inside no variable\" — both are `None` on its stack, and the `.or_else(self.loc)`\nfallback then supplies the read position. Distinguishing them means a three-state stack entry; resolving\nthe fallback at push time instead would change what a diagnostic raised inside an `$(eval)` points at.\nLeft as it was rather than half-moved.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING EACH PIECE SEPARATELY. Five oracle-recorded cases, recorded with\nMAKE_PORT_RECORD=1 against make-4.4.1; the recording rewrote no existing expectation — checked with\n`git status` over tests/make afterwards, which showed nothing but the five new directories.\n  * the deferred refusal parked → 4 of 1236 corpus cases fail, and they are exactly the four success-side\n    ones: a-value-no-job-carries-is-never-read, a-recipe-that-expands-to-nothing-reads-nothing,\n    an-empty-command-builds-no-environment, a-dry-run-builds-no-environment — each \"build failed where\n    GNU Make's build succeeded\".\n  * the empty command parked → 1 of 1236 fails: an-empty-command-builds-no-environment, the one cell the\n    other two pieces cannot reach.\n  * the definition-site location parked → 1 of 62 make_regressions cases fails,\n    an_unreadable_export_names_where_it_was_written, on `M:3` against GNU's `M:2`.\n  * a-launch-refuses-a-value-it-cannot-read is the fifth case and is the one that must STILL fail: it\n    records GNU's refusal, so a change that starts building it lands as a test failure.\nFive Rust cases beside the corpus carry what the recording format cannot: `-q`'s status of 1 rather than\n2, `-q`'s refusal of the one `+`-marked line it really runs (GNU: exit 2, `out` not written), the\ndiagnostic's text and location, and that an empty command is still counted as work rather than reported\nas nothing to do.\n\nINVENTORY: exactly ONE row moved, and it is the row this node owns.\n    variables/MAKEFLAGS.diff.119   unclassified/product-name+up-to-date-line+evaluation\n                                 → narration/no-work-line+up-to-date-line\n1277 cases before and after; nothing left the corpus and nothing new entered it. unclassified 19 → 18,\ncompiler 0 → 0, interface 20 → 20, narration 1238 → 1239. Classifier EXIT 0 against the new record.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean both crates; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0;\nworkspace tests 1111 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1236 cases\nwith every divergence marker intact; inventory as above."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

