            {
                id make-an-escaped-hash-in-a-function-argument-keeps-its-backslash
                title "Keep a backslash-escaped hash as written inside a function call argument"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:39:09.334677154Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from features/escape.diff.8 (suite script features/escape:85). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer:\n\n    self = $1\n    foo := $(call self,#foo#)#foo\n    bar := $(call self,\\#bar\\#)#bar\n    all:;@echo \"$(foo) $(bar)\"\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  #foo# \\#bar\\#\n  Ronin      #foo# #bar#\n\nBoth agree on the unescaped `#foo#`: inside a function-call argument a bare `#` is not a comment, so it survives on both sides. They disagree on the ESCAPED one. GNU removes a `\\#` escape only where the `#` would otherwise open a comment; inside a function argument it would not, so the backslash is left standing and the value keeps both characters. Ronin removes the backslash unconditionally, so `\\#bar\\#` collapses to `#bar#`.\n\nThe difference reaches a recipe's command line — the echoed text differs — so it is build intent, not narration."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T18:49:34.738472594Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T18:49:57.084940731Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING and widened the case beyond `$(call)`. Measured against\nreference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\nThe filing's case held exactly: GNU `#foo# \\#bar\\#`, Ronin `#foo# #bar#`. Three more shapes were measured:\n  * `$(patsubst %,[%],\\#)` — GNU `[\\#]`, Ronin `[#]`. So it is not about `$(call)`: any reference's\n    contents behave this way.\n  * `x\\#y` at the top level of an assignment — the backslash DOES come off on both sides. That is the\n    control that says the two questions are separate, and it is what keeps the fix from being \"never\n    unquote\".\n  * `$(subst \\#,S,a\\#b)` — `aSb` on both sides, because both halves are literal `\\#` either way and they\n    still match each other. Agreement here would survive either reading, which is why it is a control\n    rather than evidence.\n\nMECHANISM. GNU Make never unquotes a `\\#` inside a reference because it never LOOKS inside one.\n`remove_comments` calls `find_map_unquote (line, MAP_COMMENT|MAP_VARIABLE)` (read.c); `MAP_VARIABLE` makes\nthe scan stop on a `$` and, for `$(` or `${`, skip to the matching close before resuming the search. The\nbackslash-halving that turns `\\#` into `#` happens only where the scan is actually looking, so it reaches\nthe top level of a line and nothing nested inside a reference. A `define` body and a recipe line are the\nother two places the scan does not reach, and Ronin already had both.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati 5187320bd47362f483f9febb26bb986765d21d04, branch\nnplan/make-an-escaped-hash-in-a-function-argument-keeps-its-backslash, one file:\n  * `expr.rs::should_handle_comments`: `ParseExprOpt::Func` joins `Define` and `Command`. The read already\n    parses a reference's contents under that option, so the place GNU Make's scan skips is the place this\n    already knew it was in. The doc comment names `MAP_VARIABLE` so the next reader can find the reason.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX:\n`tests/make/an-escaped-hash-inside-a-reference-keeps-its-backslash` fails without it — `inside=[#bar#]` and\n`deeper=[[#]]` where GNU left `inside=[\\#bar\\#]` and `deeper=[[\\#]]`. Three controls in the same file agree\non both sides: `plain=[x#y]` (the top-level unquoting that must stay), `bare=[#foo#]` (an unescaped `#`\ninside a call is not a comment either) and `matched=[aSb]`.\n\nINVENTORY ROW MOVED — exactly one, audited by name against the pre-run tsv:\n  features/escape.diff.8  unclassified/ninja-progress+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress\nThat is the row this node owns. unclassified 36 → 35, narration 1221 → 1222, compiler ZERO, interface 28\nunchanged, total 1285 unchanged.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0; workspace\ntests 1101 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1205 ported cases; conformance 354\nruns, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8 respellings."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T18:50:07.663151433Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-normal-prerequisite-outranks-the-same-name-as-order-only
                title "Drop a name from the order-only list once it is also an ordinary prerequisite"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:39:16.414649997Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from features/order_only.diff.3 and .4 (suite script features/order_only:40 and :57). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer:\n\n    foo: bar | baz\n    \t@echo \"pipe=$|\"\n    \t@echo \"hat=$^\"\n    foo: baz\n    .PHONY: baz bar foo\n    bar baz: ; @:\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  pipe=          hat=bar baz\n  Ronin      pipe=baz       hat=bar baz\n\nGNU's manual is explicit: a file declared as both a normal and an order-only prerequisite is a normal prerequisite, and the normal declaration takes precedence. GNU carries one `->next` chain with an `ignore_mtime` bit per entry, so the second, ordinary mention clears the bit on the entry that is already there. Ronin keeps two lists (`deps`, `order_onlys`) and adds `baz` to both, so `$|` still names it.\n\n`$^` already agrees, so the graph edges agree; what differs is the text `$|` puts on the command line, which is build intent. Related but not the same as make-shuffle-reorders-order-only-prerequisites-apart-from-the-rest, which is about the same two-list representation seen from the shuffle end."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T15:56:35.419307546Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T15:57:00.877583983Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING and narrowed where the answer is decided. Measured against\nreference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\nThe filing's case held exactly: `pipe=` / `hat=bar baz` from GNU, `pipe=baz` / `hat=bar baz` from Ronin. Two\nthings were added by measurement:\n  * Writing the two rule lines the other way round — the recipe-less `foo: baz` FIRST — changes nothing on\n    either side. So the reconciliation is not about which line was read first.\n  * A control: an order-only name that is ONLY order-only stays in `$|` on both sides, which is what keeps\n    the fix from being \"empty `$|`\". It is in the recorded case as `qux`.\n  * `$+` and `$?` were measured too and already agreed, so `$|` is the whole of it.\n\nMECHANISM, and it is narrower than the filing's \"GNU carries one `->next` chain ... so the second, ordinary\nmention clears the bit on the entry that is already there\". The bit is not cleared as the makefile is read.\nBoth entries stay in the chain with their own bits until the recipe's automatic variables are computed, and\n`set_file_variables` (commands.c) reconciles them there: it hashes the chain by name, and on a collision\nwhose two entries disagree it writes `d->ignore_mtime = hd->ignore_mtime = 0` — its own comment is \"if so\nthen we need to 'upgrade' one that is order-only\". The three lists are read off afterwards, so the name\nleaves `$|` and stays in `$^`. Entries carrying `need_2nd_expansion` or `ignore_automatic_vars` are skipped\nby that pass, which is why `.EXTRA_PREREQS` does not take part.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati b2a53e1cb7e2ca0d0eda5295ced994d75bb9b940, branch\nnplan/make-a-normal-prerequisite-outranks-the-same-name-as-order-only, one file:\n  * `command.rs::eval_impl`, `AutoCommand::Bar`: the duplicate-suppressing set `$|` walks with now starts\n    holding every ordinary prerequisite, so a name the ordinary list already has is not written. That is\n    the two-list spelling of GNU's upgrade. `.EXTRA_PREREQS` needs no exclusion here because Ronin already\n    splits those out of `actual_inputs` before any automatic variable sees the node.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX: `tests/make/a-name-declared-both-ways-is-an-ordinary-prerequisite`\nfails without it — `out` holds `pipe=[baz qux]` where GNU left `pipe=[qux]`, with `$^`, `$+` and `$?`\nidentical on both sides in the same file, which is the evidence that only `$|` was wrong.\n\nINVENTORY ROWS MOVED — exactly two, audited by name against the pre-run tsv, and they are the two this node\nowns:\n  features/order_only.diff.3  unclassified/ninja-progress+recipe-echo+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress+recipe-echo\n  features/order_only.diff.4  unclassified/ninja-progress+recipe-echo+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress+recipe-echo\nunclassified 48 → 46, narration 1209 → 1211, compiler ZERO, interface 28 unchanged, total 1285 unchanged.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0; workspace\ntests 1101 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1200 ported cases; conformance 354\nruns, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8 respellings."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T15:57:15.822139062Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-foreach-variable-name-is-trimmed-of-whitespace
                title "Trim whitespace from the variable name a foreach binds"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:39:23.866915754Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch. Seven suite rows, one cause: functions/foreach.diff.2 (functions/foreach:55) and misc/bs-nl.diff.20 through .25 (misc/bs-nl:144, 150, 155, 166, 176, 190). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer — the variable-name argument reaches `foreach` with whitespace around it, here from a backslash-newline continuation:\n\n    $(foreach \\\n      a \\\n    , b c d \\\n    , $(info [$a]))\n    all:;@:\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  [b] [c] [d]\n  Ronin      [] [] []\n\nThe list argument is split identically on both sides — Ronin runs the body three times — so the fault is only in the NAME. GNU strips whitespace from the variable name before binding it (`func_foreach` in function.c), so the name is `a` and `$a` finds it. Ronin binds a name that still carries the surrounding spaces and newline-derived whitespace, so `$a` expands to nothing.\n\nmisc/bs-nl 20-25 are the same shape written five more ways — continuations, carriage returns, tabs, a `define` body, and the whole thing inside a recipe — and all five differ from GNU in exactly this one way. Fixing the trim should move all seven rows."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T18:29:17.622382835Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T18:29:38.232308525Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING and corrected the RULE it names. Measured against\nreference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\nThe filing's case held exactly, and the filing said GNU \"strips whitespace from the variable name\". It does\nmore than strip: `func_foreach` (function.c) reduces the argument to its FIRST TOKEN — `next_token` skips\nthe leading whitespace, then `end_of_token (vp)[0] = '\\0'` cuts off everything from the next blank on. The\ndifference is measurable and is in the recorded case:\n\n  $(foreach a b, x y, ...)   GNU binds `a`, and `$(a b)` names nothing.\n\nTwo more things the probe settled, both narrowing the filing:\n  * The continuation is incidental. `$(foreach  a , b c d , ...)` written on ONE line with plain spaces\n    fails the same way, so this is not about backslash-newline folding at all — the six misc/bs-nl rows are\n    the same defect written six ways, which the filing suspected and this confirms.\n  * An all-whitespace name argument leaves the name empty on both sides, and the body still runs once per\n    word. That is the boundary the `unwrap_or_default` covers.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati bf7fc02b88f6d28f1fa672ae7508cfa6cce679bb, branch\nnplan/make-a-foreach-variable-name-is-trimmed-of-whitespace, one file:\n  * `func.rs::foreach_func`: the expanded first argument is reduced to its first token before it is\n    interned. One line of code and a comment saying which two of GNU's calls it stands for.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX:\n`tests/make/a-foreach-binds-the-first-token-of-its-name-argument` fails without it, with all four of its\nanswers wrong at once — `spaced=[[] [] []] folded=[[] [] []] first=[[] []] whole=[[x] [y]]` where GNU left\n`spaced=[[b] [c] [d]] folded=[[b] [c] [d]] first=[[x] [y]] whole=[[] []]`. The last pair is the one the\nfiling's wording would have got wrong: with a two-token name argument the name is the first token, so\n`$(a b)` is empty and not the loop word.\n\nINVENTORY ROWS MOVED — exactly seven, audited by name against the pre-run tsv, and they are the seven this\nnode owns:\n  functions/foreach.diff.2                       unclassified/ninja-progress+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress\n  misc/bs-nl.diff.20 .21 .22 .23 .24 .25         the same, all six\nunclassified 43 → 36, narration 1214 → 1221, compiler ZERO, interface 28 unchanged, total 1285 unchanged.\nBiggest single move of this batch.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0; workspace\ntests 1101 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1204 ported cases; conformance 354\nruns, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8 respellings."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T18:29:48.531505461Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-shell-assignment-keeps-a-hash-its-command-printed
                title "Keep a hash a shell assignment's command printed out of comment stripping"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:39:28.286542058Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from features/shell_assignment.diff.1 (suite script features/shell_assignment:27). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer:\n\n    foo1!=echo bar#baz\n    hash != printf '\\043'\n    foo2!= echo \"bar$(hash)baz\"\n    all: ; @echo \"<$(foo1)> <$(hash)> <$(foo2)>\"\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  <bar> <#> <bar#baz>\n  Ronin      <bar> <> <barbaz>\n\nBoth agree on `foo1`: the `#baz` there is a makefile comment on the assignment line and is stripped before the command runs. They disagree on what the COMMAND'S OUTPUT may contain. `printf '\\043'` prints a `#`, and GNU stores that `#` verbatim: a `!=` assignment's value is the command's output with newlines turned into spaces and nothing else. Ronin puts the captured output back through comment stripping, so the `#` and everything after it on the line disappear — which then also empties `$(hash)` out of `foo2`.\n\nThe value reaches a recipe's command line, so this is build intent."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T19:13:15.620296086Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T19:13:38.892334638Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING exactly and drew the boundary around it. Measured against\nreference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\nThe filing's case held: GNU `<bar> <#> <bar#baz>`, Ronin `<bar> <> <barbaz>`. What the probe added is the\nset of things that must NOT change, because the fix is \"stop reading this text as makefile source\" and that\ncould take too much with it:\n  * `$(shell printf '\\043')` — already `#` on both sides. So it is `!=` alone, not the shell capture.\n  * a backslash the command printed (`a\\b`, `a\\\\b`) — identical on both sides before and after.\n  * a `$` the command printed — `a$b` reads `$b` as a reference on BOTH sides, and `x$(inner)y` expands to\n    `xINNERy` on both. So the captured text is still a recursive value that expands when read, which is\n    what GNU Make stores.\n  * a newline in the output is a space on both sides.\n  * `echo bar#baz` — the `#baz` there IS a comment, on the assignment line, and is gone before the command\n    runs on both sides. That is the control the recorded case keeps.\n\nMECHANISM. GNU Make's `!=` (`f_shell` in `do_variable_definition`, variable.c) runs the command and defines\nthe result as a recursive variable. Nothing re-reads it as a line: `remove_comments` ran once, over the\nmakefile line, before the command was launched, and the only reader afterwards is\n`variable_expand_string`, which handles `$` and nothing else. So a `#` in the output is a `#`.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati 0832004e28bbd9db58046c4a2eaa572dbb115957, branch\nnplan/make-a-shell-assignment-keeps-a-hash-its-command-printed, two files:\n  * `expr.rs`: a fourth `ParseExprOpt`, `Captured`, for text a command wrote — not makefile source, never\n    read as a line, so nothing in it opens a comment. It joins `Define` and `Command` in\n    `should_handle_comments` and behaves like `Normal` everywhere else, which is what keeps `$` references\n    and backslashes reading as they did.\n  * `eval.rs::eval_rhs`, `AssignOp::ShellEq`: the captured output is parsed under it.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX:\n`tests/make/a-shell-assignment-keeps-every-byte-its-command-wrote` fails without it, four of its five\nanswers wrong — `hash=[] quoted=[barbaz] escaped=[a#b] folded=[a]` where GNU left\n`hash=[#] quoted=[bar#baz] escaped=[a\\#b] folded=[a#b c#d]`. `trailing=[bar]` agrees on both sides and is\nthe control: a `#` on the assignment LINE is still a comment.\n\nINVENTORY ROW MOVED — exactly one, audited by name against the pre-run tsv:\n  features/shell_assignment.diff.1  unclassified/ninja-progress+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress\nThat is the row this node owns. unclassified 35 → 34, narration 1222 → 1223, compiler ZERO, interface 28\nunchanged, total 1285 unchanged.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0; workspace\ntests 1101 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1206 ported cases; conformance 354\nruns, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8 respellings."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T19:13:49.601562374Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-second-expansion-sees-target-and-pattern-specific-variables
                title "Expand a second-expanded prerequisite with the target's own variables in scope"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:39:41.998394301Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch. Three suite rows, one cause: features/se_explicit.diff.3 (se_explicit:66), features/se_implicit.diff.1 (se_implicit:61), features/se_statpat.diff.1 (se_statpat:41). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nExplicit-rule reproducer:\n\n    .SECONDEXPANSION:\n    .DEFAULT: ; @echo 'default $@'\n    foo.x: $$a $$b\n    foo.x: a := bar\n    %.x: b := baz\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  default bar / default baz\n  Ronin      ronin: no work to do.\n\nImplicit-rule reproducer (features/se_implicit.diff.1) — Ronin gets HALF of it:\n\n    .SECONDEXPANSION:\n    foo.x:\n    foo.%: $$(%_a) $$(%_b) bar ; @:\n    foo.x: x_a := bar\n    %.x: x_b := baz\n    bar baz: ; @echo '$@'\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  bar / baz\n  Ronin      bar          (the pattern-specific `%.x: x_b := baz` half is missing)\n\nStatic-pattern reproducer (features/se_statpat.diff.1) behaves like the first: GNU `bar` and `baz`, Ronin nothing.\n\nGNU expands the deferred prerequisite text with the target's variable set installed — target-specific and pattern-specific bindings included — which is the point of the feature. Ronin's second expansion runs with only the global set in scope, so `$$a` and `$$(%_b)` come back empty and the prerequisites vanish from the graph. This is a graph-shape difference: GNU builds two targets, Ronin builds none.\n\nmake-second-expansion and make-second-expansion-patterns are complete and are not this: they made the deferred expansion happen at all. What is missing is the SCOPE it happens in."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T22:17:31.024824799Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T22:18:02.918667441Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING'S CAUSE and corrected one of its three measurements.\n\nRe-measured all three reproducers against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. The explicit and\nstatic-pattern ones hold exactly as filed: GNU builds two targets, Ronin built none.\n\nCORRECTION to the implicit case. The filing says \"Ronin gets HALF of it — the pattern-specific `%.x: x_b :=\nbaz` half is missing\". It gets NEITHER half. The `bar` Ronin built was the LITERAL prerequisite `bar`\nwritten in that rule, not `$$(%_a)`. Dropping the literal from the makefile:\n\n    .SECONDEXPANSION:\n    foo.x:\n    foo.%: $$(%_a) $$(%_b) ; @:\n    foo.x: x_a := bar\n    %.x: x_b := baz\n    bar baz: ; @echo '$@'\n      GNU  bar / baz     Ronin  (nothing)\n\nSo all three cases were the same total failure, not two-and-a-half.\n\nWHAT GNU DOES, read in its source then measured. The target's variable set is installed around the deferred\nexpansion in BOTH places a deferred prerequisite is reached — `initialize_file_variables` then\n`set_file_variables` in front of `variable_expand_for_file`: read.c/file.c for an explicit or static pattern\ntarget, and implicit.c (inside `pattern_search`, per candidate) for an implicit rule. Global and automatic\nvariables were already visible to Ronin's second expansion; it is only the target's own scope that was not.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT: kati — kati 74e18ad0eb38ffe91d9666c50ec0232d69827d1f, branch\nnplan/make-second-expansion-sees-target-and-pattern-specific-variables. TWO sites, because kati has two:\n  * `build_dep_node`'s deferred loop (dep.rs) pushed the expansion scope only when `grouped_outputs` was\n    non-empty. Blamed to 864f32e \"Compile grouped double-colon actions\", which added the push FOR grouped\n    targets; the ordinary case was never wired. Now always pushed.\n  * `expanded_pattern_inputs` (dep.rs), the implicit search's per-candidate expansion, pushed nothing at\n    all. Now pushes `applicable_rule_vars(output)` — which is exactly `initialize_file_variables`. The scope\n    follows the target's NAME rather than the candidate, so the existing `(candidate_order, output)` cache\n    stays sound.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING BOTH SITES: the build-intent case\ntests/make/a-second-expanded-prerequisite-reads-the-targets-own-variables/ FAILS when parked —\n`'bar.out' holds \"bar.x <- \" where GNU Make left \"bar.x <- from-static from-pattern\"`. It covers all three\nrule forms in one makefile (explicit, static pattern, implicit-through-`$$*`) and each of target-specific\nand pattern-specific bindings.\n\nBROKE NOTHING: make_port 4/4, conformance unchanged at 326/28 and 276 raw, workspace 1101/0, equivalence 0\ndisagreements, ninja conformance 425/425, vim and zsh both built.\n\nINVENTORY: the three owned rows CHANGED FAMILY but stayed unclassified, and the count did not move —\n55/0/28/1202 before and after. Audited by name against the pre-run tsv:\n  features/se_explicit.diff.3  unclassified no-work-line+evaluation → unclassified ninja-progress+evaluation\n  features/se_implicit.diff.1  unclassified no-work-line+evaluation → unclassified ninja-progress+evaluation\n  features/se_statpat.diff.1   unclassified no-work-line+evaluation → unclassified ninja-progress+evaluation\nThe family change is the movement: Ronin no longer says \"no work to do\", it builds and narrates. Nothing\nelse in the tsv moved.\n\nWHY THEY DID NOT CLEAR, run to ground rather than assumed — and it is NOT this defect. Run the three suite\nscripts in a clean worktree and the residue is PURE progress lines, which the classifier puts in narration\n(verified: the classifier's unclassified set for that isolated run holds four other rows and none of these\nthree). Run the whole suite and each loses its `bar` line, because a FILE named `bar` is sitting in the\nsuite's working directory by then and `.DEFAULT` does not fire for a target that exists. Bisected to the\nsource: `features/output-sync` leaves `bar` behind under Ronin, and `features/patternrules` leaves `a.15`\nand `a.2`. The same full suite run against the oracle leaves NOTHING — GNU's run ends with a clean tests\ndirectory and passes all three cases. That is a separate defect, reported to the operator rather than\ntouched here.\n\nDIRECT VERIFICATION that this node's work is done: each of the three suite makefiles, run byte for byte in\na clean directory, now produces GNU's build. features/se_explicit test #2 → `bar` and `baz`;\nfeatures/se_statpat test #2 → `bar` and `baz`; features/se_implicit test #2 → `bar` and `baz`. Before the\nfix all three produced nothing.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean root and kati; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0;\nworkspace tests 1101 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1201 cases, five\ndivergence markers intact; conformance 326/28 normalised, 276 raw; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8\nrespellings; check-make-projects.sh all — vim and zsh built; ninja conformance 425/425 + 10 differential +\n92 build-outcome; --no-default-features EXIT 0."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-25T00:07:23.895553496Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-second-expanded-prerequisites-keep-their-rule-line-order
                title "Order second-expanded prerequisites the way GNU orders its rule lines"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:39:47.048944521Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from features/se_explicit.diff.20 (suite script features/se_explicit:324). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer:\n\n    .SECONDEXPANSION:\n    all: hello.tsk\n    dep1:=hello.o\n    dep2:=hello.h\n    hello.tsk: $$(dep1)\n    hello.tsk: $$(dep2); $(info $@ from $^)\n    hello.o:; $(info $@)\n    hello.h:; $(info $@)\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  hello.h / hello.o / hello.tsk from hello.h hello.o\n  Ronin      hello.o / hello.h / hello.tsk from hello.o hello.h\n\nThe same two prerequisites in the opposite order, so the build order and `$^` both differ. GNU puts the prerequisites of the rule line that CARRIES THE RECIPE ahead of the ones accumulated from the recipe-less lines; Ronin appends in source order. `$^` reaches the command line, so this is build intent rather than a scheduling detail.\n\nThe `is up to date` / `no work to do` half of the same suite row is narration (up-to-date-line) and is not this."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T17:49:39.938013099Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T17:50:09.692228196Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING and corrected its account of the rule. Measured against\nreference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\nThe filing's case held exactly, and its sentence — \"GNU puts the prerequisites of the rule line that\nCARRIES THE RECIPE ahead of the ones accumulated from the recipe-less lines\" — is right about GNU but\nwrong about what Ronin was doing. Ronin ALREADY did that: the same makefile with the `$$` taken off gave\n`hello.h hello.o` on both sides. What Ronin did not do was keep a DEFERRED line in its place: every\nsecond-expanded line was expanded after the written-out ones and appended to the end. Measured on three\nshapes:\n\n  t: A / t: $$(d) ; recipe                        GNU [D A]      Ronin [A D]\n  t: $$(p) / t: $$(q) ; recipe / t: $$(r)         GNU [Q P R]    Ronin [P Q R]\n  t: A / t: $$(d) / t: B ; recipe                 GNU [B A D]    Ronin [B A D]  (agreed by accident)\n\nMECHANISM. `record_files` (read.c) builds `f->deps` as the lines are read — a recipe-carrying line's\nprerequisites go in FRONT of what the target already had, a recipe-less line's go on the END — and\n`expand_deps` (file.c) later walks that one chain and replaces each `need_2nd_expansion` entry IN PLACE.\nSo the deferred line's own prerequisites land at that line's position, not after everything.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati b0d0ad6213deaf9948ecf981b1733483b9b11670, branch\nnplan/make-second-expanded-prerequisites-keep-their-rule-line-order, one file:\n  * `dep.rs::RuleMerger::fill_dep_node` already walks the rules in GNU's order — primary first, then as\n    written — so it now returns a `DeferredSegment` per deferred rule line, carrying where in\n    `actual_inputs` and `actual_order_only_inputs` that line's prerequisites belong.\n  * `dep.rs::build_plan`'s deferred loop splices each expansion in at that position instead of extending,\n    tracking what earlier segments already inserted in front of it.\n\nA REGRESSION I CAUGHT AND RESCOPED FOR. The first cut also noted the PICKED rule as a deferred segment,\nwhich brought `.DEFAULT` in: features/se_explicit.diff.28 (the sv 62706 `.DEFAULT` case) moved BACKWARDS\nfrom narration to unclassified, because Ronin started second-expanding `.DEFAULT`'s prerequisite line and\nprinting an `$(info)` GNU never runs. GNU reads nothing off `.DEFAULT` but its recipe — `update_file_1`\ntakes `default_file->cmds` and stops — so the picked-rule branch is now deliberately not noted, which is\nalso the set of segments Ronin had before. Every other rule that reaches that branch is an implicit one\nwhose deferred prerequisites the search has already expanded. The `.DEFAULT` shape is in the recorded case\nas a control.\n\nA TEST THAT WAS ENCODING THE DEFECT. `tests/cli.rs::make_mode_expands_a_static_pattern_rules_prerequisites_again`\nasserted `built one.o from first.c first.c.one one.h`. The oracle was asked about that exact makefile and\nanswers `built one.o from first.c.one one.h first.c` — the recipe-carrying static pattern line first. The\nassertion was written to Ronin's old order and is corrected here, with the reason in a comment beside it.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX:\n`tests/make/second-expanded-prerequisites-keep-their-rule-lines-place` fails without it — `mixed.out` holds\n`[A D]` where GNU left `[D A]`, and the parked binary also gets `spread.out` and `tsk.out` wrong\n(`[P Q R]` and `[hello.o hello.h]`). Two controls in the same case agree on both sides: `written.out`,\nwhere nothing is deferred, and `default.out`, where `.DEFAULT`'s `$$(shell)` prerequisite must never run —\nthat one is what the rescope above is for, and its evidence is a file that must NOT be there.\n\nINVENTORY ROW MOVED — exactly one, audited by name against the pre-run tsv:\n  features/se_explicit.diff.20  unclassified/no-work-line+up-to-date-line+evaluation\n                              → narration/no-work-line+up-to-date-line\nThat is the row this node owns. unclassified 45 → 44, narration 1212 → 1213, compiler ZERO, interface 28\nunchanged, total 1285 unchanged.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0; workspace\ntests 1101 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1202 ported cases; conformance 354\nruns, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8 respellings."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T17:50:19.924661077Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-an-existing-target-is-not-rebuilt-through-a-declined-implicit-chain
                title "Leave an existing target alone where GNU declines the implicit chain under it"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:39:54.090502765Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from features/se_implicit.diff.13 and .18 (suite script features/se_implicit:275 and :343). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24. Both rows need the state the suite leaves behind, which is why they are written here with it.\n\nReproducer 1 — `hello.z` exists on disk, `hello.x` does not:\n\n    .SECONDEXPANSION:\n    dep:=.x\n    all: hello.z\n    %.z: %$$(dep) ; @echo $@\n    %.x: ;\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  make: Nothing to be done for 'all'.\n  Ronin      [1/1] echo hello.z   (rebuilds it)\n\nReproducer 2 — `hello.tsk` and `hello.x` exist, `hello.z` does not:\n\n    .SECONDEXPANSION:\n    dep:=.z\n    all: hello.tsk\n    %.tsk: %$$(dep) ; @echo $@\n    %.z : %.x ; @echo $@\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  make: Nothing to be done for 'all'.\n  Ronin      builds hello.z, then hello.tsk\n\nWith every file present, or with none, the two tools agree; the disagreement is exactly the case where the chain's intermediate is missing and the goal is not. GNU declines to build a chain of implicit rules to remake a target that already exists — the missing intermediate does not make the existing goal out of date — and Ronin takes the chain. Ronin builds work GNU does not, which is a graph difference in the direction that costs a build rather than fails one."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T15:30:34.48333424Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T15:31:08.649283575Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED BOTH REPRODUCERS AND CORRECTED THE FILING'S DIAGNOSIS. All measured against\nreference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\nThe filing said Ronin \"takes the chain\" where GNU \"declines to build a chain of implicit rules to remake a\ntarget that already exists\". That is not what GNU does, and the correction matters because the filing's\nsentence would have been the wrong fix. GNU does not decline the chain; it WALKS THROUGH the missing\nintermediate and asks whether anything BELOW it is newer than the target. Measured on reproducer 2 with the\ntimestamps set by hand rather than left to how fast the setup ran:\n\n  hello.x OLDER than hello.tsk   GNU: Nothing to be done      Ronin: built hello.z and hello.tsk\n  hello.x NEWER than hello.tsk   GNU: built hello.z, hello.tsk  Ronin: the same\n\nSo GNU builds the chain readily; what it will not do is build it BECAUSE the intermediate is missing. The\nfiling's own transcript had them agreeing on the second row by accident: its setup wrote both files inside\nthe same nanosecond, so `hello.x` was not newer and GNU declined. That is the cell the fixture now pins\nexplicitly.\n\nRONIN ALREADY HAD THE RULE. Written without `.SECONDEXPANSION`, both reproducers already agreed, and so did\nthe classic shape — `hello`, `hello.c` present, `hello.o` missing under the built-in `%: %.o` and `%.o: %.c`\n— where GNU says `'hello' is up to date`. The defect was narrower than filed: a prerequisite reached\nthrough a SECOND EXPANSION was never marked intermediate. Narrowed by measurement to the exact trigger: a\n`%`-bearing word on the pattern rule of the target being made (`%.tsk: %$$(dep)`). `.SECONDEXPANSION:` on\nits own does not do it, and a `$$` on the rule BELOW does not either.\n\nMECHANISM. Two halves in GNU, and the second is where Ronin parted company:\n  * `update_file_1` (remake.c) forces a remake for a prerequisite that does not exist only\n    `if (d_mtime == NONEXISTENT_MTIME && !d->file->intermediate)`, and `check_dep` reads a missing\n    intermediate by recursing into its own prerequisites rather than treating its absence as staleness.\n  * WHICH names are intermediate is settled per raw word, before any expansion: `is_explicit` is\n    `strchr (nptr, '%') == 0` on the word as written, and every dep the word expands to inherits it\n    (implicit.c). GNU writes that assignment out twice — once in the plain branch, once in the\n    second-expansion branch — so the two paths agree by construction.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati 6c1d241bbf31b7464579a0a111773f14044549ca, branch\nnplan/make-an-existing-target-is-not-rebuilt-through-a-declined-implicit-chain, one file:\n  * `dep.rs::can_pick_implicit_rule` had `Some((inputs, _)) => inputs.iter().map(|input| (*input, false))`\n    with the comment \"a deferred list is one string until it is expanded, so which word the `%` was in is no\n    longer knowable\". It is knowable: `expand_pattern_prerequisites_again` already splits the retained text\n    into the words GNU Make's `get_next_word` yields and expands each on its own, and `stem_references`\n    already reports which of them held a `%` — the same bit that decides whether the held-aside directory\n    goes in front of the result.\n  * `dep.rs::expand_deferred_prerequisites` now pairs every name it produces with that flag, so the\n    directory prefix and the pattern origin are read off one answer instead of two. The explicit-rule\n    caller drops the flags through a named `forget_pattern_origin`, and the pair has a\n    `SecondExpandedPrerequisites` alias so the type-complexity lint stays quiet.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX: `tests/make/a-missing-intermediate-does-not-remake-an-existing-target`\nfails without it, with all three of its answers wrong at once — `log` holds\n`made kept.x / made older.z / made newer.z / made kept.z / made older.tsk / made newer.tsk` where GNU left\n`made newer.z / made newer.tsk`. The case carries the control as well as the defect: `newer.tsk` is older\nthan `newer.x`, so its chain DOES run on both sides, which is what keeps the fix from being \"never build\".\nTimestamps are set with `touch -d` in `setup` rather than left to the clock.\n\nINVENTORY ROWS MOVED — exactly two, audited by name against the pre-run tsv, and they are the two this node\nowns:\n  features/se_implicit.diff.13  unclassified/ninja-progress+no-work-line+evaluation → narration/no-work-line\n  features/se_implicit.diff.18  unclassified/ninja-progress+no-work-line+evaluation → narration/no-work-line\nunclassified 50 → 48, narration 1207 → 1209, compiler ZERO, interface 28 unchanged, total 1285 unchanged.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean root and kati; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0;\nworkspace tests 1101 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1199 ported cases;\nconformance 354 runs, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8 respellings."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T15:31:19.577906167Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-library-prerequisite-is-the-file-libpatterns-names
                title "Read a -lNAME prerequisite as the file LIBPATTERNS resolves it to"
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:40:00.494966197Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from features/se_explicit.diff.10 (suite script features/se_explicit:168). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24, with the suite's own makefile (work/features/se_explicit.mk.9) and goal `foo`.\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  Makefile:16: Recipe was specified for file '-lcat' at Makefile:16,\n             Makefile:16: but '-lcat' is now considered the same file as 'libcat.a'.\n             Makefile:16: Recipe for '-lcat' will be ignored in favor of the one for 'libcat.a'.\n             exit 0, leaves libcat.a\n  Ronin      [1/3] touch libcat.a / [2/3] true / [3/3] true\n             exit 0, leaves libcat.a\n\nThe filesystem effect is the same file. What differs is the GRAPH: GNU resolves the `-lcat` prerequisite through `.LIBPATTERNS` to `libcat.a` and then treats the two names as ONE file node — which is what the warning is about, and which is why GNU runs one recipe where Ronin runs three. Ronin keeps `-lcat` as a node of its own, satisfies it through the makefile's `-l%: lib%.a` rule, and so carries two extra no-op edges.\n\nTwo things to settle when this is taken: whether `.LIBPATTERNS` resolution is implemented at all, and whether GNU's three-line duplicate-recipe warning is owed (see make-a-warning-gnu-emits-about-a-makefile-is-emitted for the wider question). The edge count is the part that is build intent."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T20:05:41.931483028Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "MEASURED, AND THE FILING'S BUILD-INTENT CLAIM DOES NOT HOLD. Not claimed as accepted — recorded for the\noperator, and the node is left open. All measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on\n2026-08-26.\n\nTHE FILING SAID: \"GNU resolves the `-lcat` prerequisite through `.LIBPATTERNS` to `libcat.a` and then\ntreats the two names as ONE file node — which is what the warning is about, and which is why GNU runs one\nrecipe where Ronin runs three ... The edge count is the part that is build intent.\"\n\nGNU RUNS THREE RECIPES, not one. `make -d foo` on the suite's own makefile, read off the trace:\n    Must remake target 'foo.o'      -> `%.o: ; @true`        ran\n    Must remake target 'libcat.a'   -> `libcat.a: ; @touch $@` ran, and the trace echoes `touch libcat.a`\n    Must remake target '-lcat'      -> `-l%: lib%.a ; @true`  ran\n    ONLY THEN: the three-line `Recipe was specified for file '-lcat'` warning\nRonin runs `touch libcat.a`, `true`, `true` — the same three. Same files left, same exit status. The edge\ncount the filing called build intent is already identical.\n\nWHY THE WARNING COMES LAST, which is the thing that matters. `f_mtime` reaches `library_search` only after\n`name_mtime` and `vpath_search` have both failed, and `library_search` finds only files that ALREADY EXIST.\nAt the first ask `libcat.a` is not there, so nothing is renamed and `-lcat` keeps its own rule and runs it.\nThe rename happens on a LATER `f_mtime`, once the build has created `libcat.a` — and `rehash_file` then\nfinds `-lcat` carrying a recipe of its own and says so. The warning is a post-build observation about a\nfile that appeared during the build.\n\n`.LIBPATTERNS` RESOLUTION IS IMPLEMENTED AND AGREES, which was the filing's first open question. Four more\nshapes were measured and all four are identical on both tools:\n  * `libcat.a` a declared target, no `-l%` rule            both: `No rule to make target '-lcat'`, exit 2\n  * `libcat.a` on disk, nothing declared                   both: `foo from [libcat.a]`, no work\n  * nothing named `libcat` anywhere                        both: refuse, exit 2\n  * `.LIBPATTERNS = made_%.a` naming a declared target     both: refuse, exit 2\n  * `libcat.a` on disk WITH both rules present             both: `foo from [libcat.a]`, and GNU does not\n    warn either — at that ask `-lcat` has no recipe yet, so `rehash_file` has nothing to complain about.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT, and why it is not a compile-time answer. Only the three-line diagnostic. Its precondition is\n\"a file that was absent when the build started is present now, and the name that was searched for has\nacquired a recipe in the meantime\" — an event in the middle of a build. A compiled graph is planned before\nany of it runs, so there is no moment at which Ronin holds both halves of that condition. Predicting it\nwould mean the compiler asserting what the build is going to do, which is emulation rather than\ncompilation.\n\nThis is a divergence question rather than a defect, and accepting a divergence is not a dispatch's to take\n(the 2026-08-26 standing ruling exempted correctness calls, not acceptance). The two static makefile\ndiagnostics the node was grouped with — `--warn-undefined-variables` and `extraneous text after 'define'` —\nwere fixed under make-a-warning-gnu-emits-about-a-makefile-is-emitted, which is where this one would have\ngone had it been static."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-an-empty-static-pattern-prerequisite-is-not-an-empty-path
                title "Build a static pattern rule whose stem leaves an empty prerequisite name"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:40:11.758516957Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from features/statipattrules.diff.3 (suite script features/statipattrules:46). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer — two lines, and the whole run is refused:\n\n    .DEFAULT: ; @echo 'D:$@'\n    foo: foo%: % %.x % % % y.% % ; @echo $@\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  D:.x / D:y. / foo          exit 0\n  Ronin      ronin: empty path          exit 2\n\n`foo` matched against `foo%` has an EMPTY stem, so five of the seven prerequisites expand to the empty string and two to `.x` and `y.`. GNU drops the empty ones and keeps the two real names, building both through `.DEFAULT`. Ronin refuses the whole run with `empty path`, so nothing is built at all: a refusal where GNU builds, which is the sharpest class of divergence after a crash."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T21:42:06.200358354Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T21:42:32.366281763Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING and found a second symptom it did not name.\n\nRe-measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. The filing's reproducer holds exactly:\n\n    .DEFAULT: ; @echo 'D:$@'\n    foo: foo%: % %.x % % % y.% % ; @echo $@\n      GNU 4.4.1  D:.x / D:y. / foo   exit 0\n      Ronin      ronin: empty path   exit 2\n\nWHAT THE FILING DID NOT SAY: rkati, the compiler on its own, does not refuse — it diverges differently\nand worse. It interns the empty name, then goes looking for a FILE with no name, which falls into the\nbuilt-in suffix rules: `rkati: *** No rule to make target '.w', needed by '.c'.` and, where .DEFAULT is\npresent, `ctangle .w .ch .c` / `Error 127`. So there were two faces of one defect: kati inventing a\nCWEAVE build out of nothing, and Ronin's graph refusing the empty path that reached it. Both come from the\nsame missing drop, and both are gone.\n\nWHAT GNU ACTUALLY DOES, read in its source and then measured. `enter_prereqs` (file.c:476) special-cases\nan empty stem by REMOVING the `%` character rather than running patsubst, and then:\n\n    /* If the name expanded to the empty string, ignore it.  */\n    if (variable_buffer[0] == '\\0') { ...unlink dp from the chain...; continue; }\n\nMeasured on `foo: foo%: % a%b %c d% %`:  `a%b`→`ab`, `%c`→`c`, `d%`→`d`, and the two `%` are DROPPED —\n`$^` is `ab c d` and `$*` is empty. Confirmed with the oracle before writing a line of the fix.\n\nkati's substitution was already right: `Pattern::append_subst` computes the stem geometrically, so an\nempty stem takes the `%` out and leaves `ab`, `c`, `d` exactly as GNU does. What was missing was only the\ndrop.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT: kati (Make semantics, the compiler) — kati ce4bfd17ebad76505552cd6dacf863a3f34ebaea,\nbranch nplan/make-an-empty-static-pattern-prerequisite-is-not-an-empty-path. `apply_output_pattern`\n(kati/src-rs/dep.rs) now skips a substituted prerequisite whose name came out empty, at the push beside\nthe `enter_prereqs` comment it belongs to.\n\nSCOPE CHECKED BEFORE COMMITTING, because a fix that catches implicit rules too would be wrong: only static\npattern rules reach that loop. An implicit rule's prerequisites are resolved by `pattern_search` and return\nat the `prerequisites_are_resolved` early exit above it, which is GNU's own shape — `record_files`\ndeliberately skips `enter_prereqs` for a pattern rule. The `.SECONDEXPANSION` path re-scans its expansion\nwith a word scanner, so empty names already vanish there. src/frontend.rs's `EmptyPath` guard was left\nstanding: the graph SHOULD refuse a nameless node, and with the compiler right nothing hands it one.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX: the build-intent case\ntests/make/a-static-pattern-prerequisite-a-stem-empties-is-no-prerequisite/ FAILS when parked —\n\"build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded\". Oracle-recorded; it pins `$^`, `$*`, and one file per\nsurviving prerequisite (one file each rather than one appended file, so what is recorded is which\nprerequisites were asked for and not the order a scheduler reached them in). The two `%` prerequisites are\nwritten on either side of the real ones so the record shows they were dropped rather than emptied in place.\n\nINVENTORY ROW MOVED — exactly one, audited by name against the pre-run tsv:\n  features/statipattrules.diff.3  unclassified/product-name+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress\nThat is the row this node owns. unclassified 56 → 55, narration 1201 → 1202, compiler ZERO, interface 28\nunchanged, total 1285 unchanged.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean root and kati; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0;\nworkspace tests 1101 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1200 cases, five\ndivergence markers intact; conformance 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0\ndisagreements + 8 respellings; check-make-projects.sh all — vim and zsh built; ninja conformance 425/425 +\n10 differential + 92 build-outcome; --no-default-features EXIT 0."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-25T00:07:19.633062339Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-grouped-pattern-target-keeps-its-own-explicit-prerequisites
                title "Keep the explicit prerequisites of one target of a multi-target pattern rule"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:40:16.697564299Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from features/patternrules.diff.7 (suite script features/patternrules:161). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer:\n\n    final: x ; @echo $@\n    x: x.t1 x.t2 ; @echo $@\n    x.t2: dep\n    dep: ; @echo $@\n    %.t1 %.t2: ; @echo $*.t1 ; echo $*.t2\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  dep / x.t1 / x.t2 / x / final\n  Ronin      x.t1 / x.t2 / x / final          (dep is never built)\n\n`x.t2: dep` is a recipe-less rule adding a prerequisite to `x.t2`. `x.t2` is then produced by the multi-target pattern rule `%.t1 %.t2:`. GNU keeps the explicit prerequisite and orders `dep` ahead of the pattern rule's recipe; Ronin drops it when the pattern rule takes over the target, so `dep` is not in the graph at all.\n\nA prerequisite missing from the graph is build intent, and this one loses a whole edge."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T18:09:27.766071397Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T18:09:51.359545774Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING and settled how the prerequisite reaches the graph, which the filing left open.\nMeasured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\nThe filing's case held exactly: GNU built `dep` and Ronin did not. The control it implies was measured too\n— the same makefile with a SINGLE-target pattern rule `%.t2:` — and there the two already agreed, which\npins the fault to the multi-target shape rather than to pattern rules generally. Three more shapes were\nmeasured:\n  * `$^` must NOT show the peer's prerequisite. With `%.t1 %.t2: base`, GNU's `$^` is `base` alone while\n    `dep` is still built. So this is not \"add it to the prerequisite list\".\n  * `$|` likewise, for a peer that declared an order-only prerequisite.\n  * A peer with a RECIPE of its own: GNU still uses the pattern rule (the peer was marked made by\n    `also_make`) and builds the peer's `dep` first. Ronin already agreed here and still does.\n\nMECHANISM, and it is stated in GNU's own comment. `pattern_search` (implicit.c) records the other targets\non the matched file as `also_make` and does NOT merge their prerequisites into `file->deps` — which is why\n`$^` does not show them. `update_file_1` (remake.c) then walks them anyway: \"we need to walk our deps, AND\nthe deps of any also_make targets to ensure everything happens in the correct order\". That is the same loop\nthat decides `must_make`, so the peer's prerequisites are ordinary prerequisites of the shared action for\nscheduling and freshness, and invisible to the automatic variables.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati 37d28242f172abb641bf6607cfa6701ad6a31137, branch\nnplan/make-a-grouped-pattern-target-keeps-its-own-explicit-prerequisites, one file:\n  * `dep.rs::build_plan`: the peers a multi-target pattern rule claimed (`DepNode::peer_outputs`) now\n    contribute their own declared prerequisites to `grouped_peer_inputs` /`grouped_peer_order_only` — the\n    same hidden channel an explicit `&:` peer's prerequisites already ride, which puts them in the graph\n    and the freshness test and out of `$^`, `$<` and `$|`. Read where `peer_outputs` is still full: a peer\n    later reached in its own right is struck off that list, and that happens after this point.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX:\n`tests/make/a-multi-target-pattern-rule-builds-its-peers-prerequisites` fails without it — `dep.out` was\nnot produced at all. Four controls in the same case agree on both sides: `x-hat.out` and `y-hat.out` hold\n`[base]` (the rule's own prerequisite and nothing else), `x-bar.out` and `y-bar.out` are empty, and\n`oo.out` proves an order-only prerequisite declared on a peer is built too.\n\nINVENTORY ROW MOVED — exactly one, audited by name against the pre-run tsv:\n  features/patternrules.diff.7  unclassified/ninja-progress+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress\nThat is the row this node owns. unclassified 44 → 43, narration 1213 → 1214, compiler ZERO, interface 28\nunchanged, total 1285 unchanged.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0; workspace\ntests 1101 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1203 ported cases; conformance 354\nruns, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8 respellings."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T18:10:02.226741215Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-every-matching-pattern-specific-assignment-composes-in-order
                title "Compose every matching pattern-specific assignment in the order GNU applies them"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:40:22.906544033Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from features/patspecific_vars.diff.3 (suite script features/patspecific_vars:60, its TEST #4 \"multiple patterns matching the same target\"). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer:\n\n    a%: AAA = aaa\n    %b: BBB = ccc\n    a%: BBB += ddd\n    %b: AAA ?= xxx\n    %b: AAA += bbb\n    .PHONY: ab\n    ab: ; @echo $(AAA); echo $(BBB)\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  aaa bbb / ccc ddd\n  Ronin      xxx bbb / ccc\n\nTwo independent losses in one target. For `AAA`, GNU applies `a%: AAA = aaa` first, so the later `%b: AAA ?= xxx` finds it already set and does nothing, and `%b: AAA += bbb` appends: `aaa bbb`. Ronin lets the `?=` win, so it starts from `xxx`. For `BBB`, GNU appends `a%: BBB += ddd` onto `%b: BBB = ccc`: `ccc ddd`. Ronin drops the append entirely.\n\nBoth values reach the recipe's command line, so this is build intent. It is the sibling of make-pattern-specific-variable-application (done, every matching pattern applies) — what is missing is the ORDER they compose in and the interaction of `?=` and `+=` across two patterns."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T16:51:44.571340209Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T16:52:31.20818726Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING and found a SECOND defect of the same cause, in the opposite direction. All\nmeasured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\nThe filing's case held exactly: GNU `aaa bbb` / `ccc ddd`, Ronin `xxx bbb` / `ccc`. Four more shapes were\nmeasured beside it:\n  * `a%: X = 1`, `%b: X = 2`, `a%: X += 3` — one name, so the order IS the answer. GNU `2 3`, Ronin `2`.\n  * a global value under a pattern `?=` and a pattern `+=` — already agreed on both sides, and is in the\n    recorded case as the control that keeps the fix from being about the pattern sets alone.\n  * a `private` pattern variable — already agreed, unchanged.\n  * THE SECOND DEFECT: `a%: override APP += a1` beside `%b: APP += b1`. GNU `a1`, Ronin `a1 b1`. The\n    upstream suite's own TEST #8 is the same shape under one pattern, and Ronin passed that one; written\n    across two patterns it did not.\n\nMECHANISM. GNU Make holds one `pattern_var` per ASSIGNMENT, not per pattern (`create_pattern_var`,\nvariable.c), inserted shortest-pattern-first and, within one length, at the end of the pack of that length\n— which is definition order. `initialize_file_variables` then builds ONE set for the target that matched\nand replays every matching entry into it through `do_variable_definition`, with\n`current_variable_set_list` pointed at that set. So `+=` appends to whatever the previous entry left\nwhichever pattern it belonged to, and `?=` finds the name already there.\n\nTwo consequences, and Ronin had one of each wrong:\n  * Collecting a pattern's assignments into a set of its own settles them against each other first. That is\n    what lost the interleaving.\n  * Because the replay is one SET, `define_variable_in_set`'s ladder applies across every pattern in it:\n    \"if the old definition is from a stronger source than this one, don't redefine it\". That is what made\n    `override APP += a1` refuse the `%b: APP += b1` after it.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati a6f52f344d39137d6f27c4c9f5832b0a4d4cac9f, branch\nnplan/make-every-matching-pattern-specific-assignment-composes-in-order, three files:\n  * `eval.rs`: `pattern_rule_var_order: Vec<Symbol>` became `pattern_rule_var_sets:\n    Vec<(Symbol, Arc<Vars>)>` — one fresh `Vars` per pattern-specific assignment, in read order.\n    `record_rule_specific_assign` writes a pattern assignment into a set of its own, which is what leaves\n    the composing to the replay rather than doing it as the makefile is read.\n  * `dep.rs`: `pattern_var_order` holds `(Pattern, Arc<Vars>)` per assignment and is stable-sorted by\n    pattern length, so equal-length patterns keep definition order. `matching_pattern_vars` pushes one\n    scope per matching assignment.\n  * `dep.rs::apply_rule_vars`: a `pattern_set_origins` map applies the origin ladder ACROSS the pattern\n    scopes of one run, because those scopes are one GNU set. The target's own scopes are genuinely\n    separate sets (`rehash_file` merges LISTS, not contents) and are deliberately not compared this way.\n  * `var.rs`: the ladder itself is now `origin_outranks`, read by `Vars::assign` and by the above, so the\n    two askers cannot drift.\n\nA REGRESSION I CAUGHT AND RESCOPED FOR. The first cut had only the per-assignment sets, and it moved\nfeatures/patspecific_vars.diff.7 and .8 BACKWARDS from narration to unclassified — the upstream suite's\nTEST #8, `a%: override FOO += f1` / `a%: FOO += f2`, which had been passing because both lines shared a\n`Vars` and `Vars::assign` refused the second. That is what the `pattern_set_origins` half is for; the two\nrows are back where they were, and the same rule now also holds across patterns, which it did not before.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX:\n`tests/make/every-matching-pattern-assignment-composes-in-the-order-it-was-written` fails without it, with\nfour of its seven answers wrong — `AAA=[xxx bbb] BBB=[ccc] X=[2] APP=[a1 b1]` where GNU left\n`AAA=[aaa bbb] BBB=[ccc ddd] X=[2 3] APP=[a1]`. The other three (GGG, OVR, WEAK) agree on both sides and\nare the controls: a global reached from a pattern set, an `override` refusing a plain line, and a plain\nline still there for a later `override` to append to.\n\nINVENTORY ROW MOVED — exactly one, audited by name against the pre-run tsv:\n  features/patspecific_vars.diff.3  unclassified/ninja-progress+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress\nThat is the row this node owns (the suite's TEST #4). unclassified 46 → 45, narration 1211 → 1212,\ncompiler ZERO, interface 28 unchanged, total 1285 unchanged.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0; workspace\ntests 1101 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1201 ported cases; conformance 354\nruns, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8 respellings."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T16:53:03.744393967Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-command-line-variable-outranks-a-target-specific-assignment
                title "Let a command-line variable outrank a plain target-specific assignment"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:40:27.016924075Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from features/targetvars.diff.1 (suite script features/targetvars:60). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer, invoked as `make one two FOO=1 BAR=2`:\n\n    export FOO = foo\n    export BAR = bar\n    one: override FOO = one\n    one two: ; @echo $(FOO) $(BAR)\n    two: BAR = two\n    .PHONY: one two\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  one 2 / 1 2\n  Ronin      one 2 / 1 two\n\nBoth agree on `one`, where the target-specific assignment says `override` and so does beat the command line. They disagree on `two`, where `two: BAR = two` is a PLAIN target-specific assignment: GNU's command-line origin outranks it and `$(BAR)` stays `2`; Ronin lets the target-specific value win.\n\nThe value reaches the recipe's command line, so this is build intent. The rule is the ordinary origin ladder — command line beats a makefile assignment unless the assignment says `override` — applied to the target-specific scope."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T20:22:52.042519989Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T20:23:17.668555437Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING and corrected its account of the rule, which matters because the filing's\nversion would have produced a different answer in three of the five cases now recorded. All measured\nagainst reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\nThe filing's case held exactly: GNU `one 2` / `1 2`, Ronin `one 2` / `1 two`. The filing called the rule\n\"the ordinary origin ladder ... applied to the target-specific scope\". It is not a ladder question at all:\nGNU Make's file variable set is searched BEFORE the global one, so a ladder would have let the\ntarget-specific `o_file` binding win. What actually happens is a REPLACEMENT made when the rule line is\nread. Measured:\n\n  t: BAR += appended   with BAR=2   GNU [2], origin command line, flavour recursive — Ronin [2 appended]\n  %b: PAT = pattern    with PAT=cli GNU takes the command line's — Ronin took the pattern's\n  t: BAR = mine        with nothing named on the command line  both take `mine`\n  t: override BAR      with BAR=2   both take `mine`, origin override\n  t: BAR = mine  -e    with BAR in the environment  both take `mine` (the environment is not lifted by\n                                    `-e` for this test unless it was an override origin)\n\nThe `+=` row is the sharp one: the append is dropped WHOLE rather than pasted onto the command line's\nvalue, because the same block clears `append` along with the value.\n\nMECHANISM. `record_target_var` (read.c) ends with: \"If it's not an override, check to see if there was a\ncommand-line setting. If so, reset the value.\" — copying `gv->value`, `gv->origin` and `gv->recursive` onto\nthe per-target variable and setting `v->append = 0`. The block sits AFTER the pattern/file split, so a\npattern-specific assignment takes it too, and `o_env_override` is named beside `o_command` so `-e` counts.\nThe lookup happens with `current_variable_set_list` restored to the global one.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati 42af07b02652edb385f10ec6605a9426a5dc80c4, branch\nnplan/make-a-command-line-variable-outranks-a-target-specific-assignment, one file:\n  * `eval.rs::take_command_line_value`, called from `record_rule_specific_assign` for every non-`override`\n    target-specific assignment that actually binds. It asks `lookup_var_global` — not the scope being\n    written into — and, on a `CommandLine` or `EnvironmentOverride` answer, replaces the binding's whole\n    content and settles its `assign_op` to `Eq` so no later pass appends to it. What the rule line said\n    about `private` and `export` is put back on top, because `record_target_var` writes those after the\n    copy.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX:\n`tests/make/a-command-line-value-outranks-a-target-specific-one` fails without it — `patb.out` holds\n`[1][2][pattern][global]` where GNU left `[1][2][from-the-command-line][global]`, and the parked binary\nalso gets `two.out` and `plus.out` wrong. Two controls in the same case agree on both sides: `one.out`,\nwhere `override` takes the command line back, and `own.out`, where nothing was named on the command line\nand the target's own assignment stands. Each target writes its own file, so the recording does not depend\non the order two independent recipes ran in.\n\nINVENTORY ROW MOVED — exactly one, audited by name against the pre-run tsv:\n  features/targetvars.diff.1  unclassified/ninja-progress+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress\nThat is the row this node owns. unclassified 30 → 29, narration 1227 → 1228, compiler ZERO, interface 20\nunchanged, corpus 1277 unchanged.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0; workspace\ntests 1104 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1208 ported cases; conformance 354\nruns, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8 respellings."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T20:23:29.685967244Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-target-specific-export-flag-reaches-the-prerequisites
                title "Inherit a target-specific export or unexport down the prerequisite chain"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:40:39.750811824Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from features/targetvars.diff.39 and .43 (suite script features/targetvars:396 and :411). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24. Both need the environment stated, so it is stated here.\n\nReproducer A (row .43) — `mid` EXPORTS, run with `hello` unset in the environment:\n\n    all: mid\n    mid: base\n    mid: export hello=mid\n    base: hello=base\n    all mid base:; @echo $@ make=$(hello) shell=$$hello\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  base make=base shell=base / mid make=mid shell=mid / all make= shell=\n  Ronin      base make=base shell=      / mid make=mid shell=mid / all make= shell=\n\nReproducer B (row .39) — `mid` UNEXPORTS, run with `hello=environ` in the environment:\n\n    all: mid\n    mid: base\n    mid: unexport hello=mid\n    base: hello=base\n    all mid base:; @echo $@ make=$(hello) shell=$$hello\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  base make=base shell=       / mid make=mid shell= / all make=environ shell=environ\n  Ronin      base make=base shell=base   / mid make=mid shell= / all make=environ shell=environ\n\nIn both, the two tools agree about `mid` itself and about `all`, and disagree about `base` — the PREREQUISITE. GNU inherits the export/unexport decision down the prerequisite chain along with the value: `mid: export hello` makes `base`'s own `hello=base` exported too, and `mid: unexport hello` keeps `base`'s out of the environment. Ronin inherits the value (both tools print `make=base`) but not the flag, so it gets the export decision backwards in both directions.\n\nThe recipe's environment is a filesystem-visible effect of the build, so this is build intent. Neighbouring node: make-inherited-export-reassignment (done) covers the value; this is the flag."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T23:05:36.745469444Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T23:06:02.929053113Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING EXACTLY — both reproducers, both directions, and the observation that the two\ntools agree about `mid` and `all` and disagree only about `base`. Re-measured against\nreference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make with the environment stated as the filing states it.\n\nWHAT GNU DOES, read in its source and then measured. `target_environment` (variable.c) walks the file's\nwhole variable-set list from most specific outward. The first definition it meets keeps the VALUE; the\nexport attribute is filled in separately, by the next set out that has one:\n\n    if (HASH_VACANT (*evslot)) { ...insert... }\n    else if ((*evslot)->export == v_default)\n      /* We already have a variable but we don't know its status.  */\n      (*evslot)->export = v->export;\n\nSo value and export answer come from DIFFERENT sets when the inner one said nothing about exporting. That\nis the whole defect: Ronin carried the value across and not the flag.\n\nProbes that bounded it before any code was written: with `base` having NO value of its own the exported\nvalue already descended correctly in both tools, and a `mid: export other=x` naming a different variable was\nalready right. It is only the case where the prerequisite gives its OWN value and no export answer.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT: kati — kati 137fc4fe0c71e4041b65aafef8b4182dc399c6f3, branch\nnplan/make-a-target-specific-export-flag-reaches-the-prerequisites. `apply_rule_vars` now installs a\nbinding carrying the export answer already standing for that name in the rule scope, when the binding has\nnone of its own. The outer target scopes are flattened into `cur_rule_vars` by then, so what stands there\nIS the nearest outward answer; the GLOBAL set is asked separately and later by\n`export::scoped_environment`, which was already doing the other half of GNU's walk. The two together are\nthe whole walk, and they compose in GNU's order — a nearer answer wins over the global one.\n\nCarried onto a COPY rather than written through the binding: the `Var` is the one stored against the rule,\nand one rule's scope can be reached for more than one target — a pattern-specific set most of all — so\nwriting the inherited answer into it would leak this target's reading of the name to every other target\nthat shares the set.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX: tests/make/a-target-specific-export-descends-to-the-prerequisites/\nFAILS when parked — `'ubase.out' holds \"ubase make=[base] shell=[base]\" where GNU Make left\n\"ubase make=[base] shell=[]\"`. One makefile carries both directions: an `export` chain and an `unexport`\nchain, three targets deep each, run with `hello=environ` in the environment so the unexport side has\nsomething real to suppress. Every recipe records `$(hello)` beside `$$hello`, which is what separates the\nvalue from the flag — the value half is identical in all six and only the environment half moves.\n\nBROKE NOTHING: make_port 4/4 over 1202 cases; conformance unchanged at 326/28 and 276 raw; workspace\n1101/0; equivalence 0 disagreements; vim and zsh both built; ninja conformance green.\n\nINVENTORY ROWS MOVED — both of the node's own, audited by name against the pre-run tsv:\n  features/targetvars.diff.39  unclassified/ninja-progress+recipe-echo+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress\n  features/targetvars.diff.43  unclassified/ninja-progress+evaluation             → narration/ninja-progress\nNothing else moved. unclassified 54 → 52, narration 1203 → 1205, compiler ZERO, interface 28 unchanged,\ntotal 1285 unchanged.\n\nFOR THE OPERATOR — an rkati-only observation this dispatch did not touch and does not own. Running the two\nreproducers through `target/release/rkati` rather than through Ronin's Make mode, rkati gets `mid` ITSELF\nwrong, before and after this fix and unchanged by it: in reproducer A it prints `mid make=mid shell=` where\nGNU and Ronin both print `shell=mid`, and in B `mid ... shell=environ` where both print `shell=`. So rkati\nappears not to apply a target-specific export to the recipe's environment at all. Ronin's Make mode — the\nproduct — is correct on every case measured here. The conformance corpus did not move either way. Flagging\nit rather than filing or classifying it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-25T00:07:32.656607643Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-vpath-resolved-name-keeps-its-explicit-rule
                title "Prefer the explicit rule for a VPATH-resolved name over a pattern rule"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:40:45.719279921Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from features/vpath.diff.4 (suite script features/vpath:105). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer — a `vpa/` directory exists and is empty; goal is `foo.x`:\n\n    VPATH = vpa\n    %.x: ; @echo pattern $@\n    vpa/foo.x: ; @echo vpath $@\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  vpath vpa/foo.x\n  Ronin      pattern foo.x\n\nGNU searches VPATH for the goal, finds that `vpa/foo.x` is a target with an EXPLICIT rule, and uses that rule — an explicit rule for the vpath-resolved name outranks a pattern rule for the bare name. Ronin does not consider the vpath-resolved name a candidate at all and falls to the pattern rule.\n\nDifferent rule, different command, different work: build intent. Owner adjacent to make-vpath-search (done) and make-vpath-gpath-output-location (done); this is the rule-search precedence those did not cover."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T14:55:02.860110076Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T14:55:40.374599843Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING and widened it by one shape. All measured against\nreference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\nThe filing's case held exactly as written: with an empty `vpa/`, `VPATH = vpa`, a `%.x:` rule and a\n`vpa/foo.x:` rule, GNU echoed `vpath vpa/foo.x` and Ronin echoed `pattern foo.x`. Two more shapes were\nmeasured beside it:\n\n  * the same names reached as a PREREQUISITE rather than as the goal — `all: foo.x` — behave identically\n    on both sides of the fix, so this is not a property of being a goal;\n  * a RECIPE-LESS rule at the found path, `vpa/bar.x: dep`, is the more telling half: GNU renames the\n    target to `vpa/bar.x`, keeps `dep`, and THEN applies the pattern rule to it — `pattern vpa/bar.x\n    [dep]`. Ronin built `bar.x` from the pattern rule with no prerequisites at all, so `dep` was not in\n    the graph.\n\nThe two shapes that already agreed were checked too and were left alone: a found path that is on disk and\nnot a target, and one that is on disk AND a target, both already answered `vpa/foo.c`.\n\nMECHANISM. GNU looks a name up in the directory search BEFORE it looks for an implicit rule.\n`update_file_1` (remake.c) calls `file_mtime` above `try_implicit_rule`, and its own comment says why:\nreading the modtime first means the file \"may not need an implicit rule\". `f_mtime` runs\n`vpath_search`, and `selective_vpath_search` (vpath.c) accepts a candidate the makefile merely MENTIONS —\n\"makefile-mentioned file need not exist\". On a hit, `f_mtime` calls `rehash_file` (file.c), which merges\nthe two file objects into the one standing at the found path: recipe, prerequisites, variable set lists\nand all.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati 566ede8cb4047ed04ed4003ad7cd71ae99e3dbe5, branch\nnplan/make-a-vpath-resolved-name-keeps-its-explicit-rule, one file:\n  * `dep.rs::at_found_name` and `dep.rs::at_gpath`: the merge onto the found name was conditioned on\n    `declares_a_target(found) && is_declared(name)`. `rehash_file` asks nothing about the searched name —\n    it rehashes whatever object `f_mtime` was called for — so the second half of the condition is not\n    GNU's. Dropping it leaves `declares_a_target(found)`, and `is_declared` had no other caller and is\n    gone. `merge_into_found_name` already handled a searched name with no rule of its own.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX: `tests/make/a-vpath-found-name-that-carries-a-rule-outranks-the-pattern-rule`\nfails without it — `all.out` holds `all [foo.x bar.x]` where GNU left `all [vpa/foo.x vpa/bar.x]`. One\ncase, both halves in it: the explicit rule at the found path, and the recipe-less rule whose prerequisite\nthe pattern rule then inherits. Each recipe writes its own file, so the recording does not depend on the\norder two independent recipes ran in.\n\nA RECORDING HAZARD worth writing down: `MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1` re-records EVERY case, not the new one. This\nrun's re-record flipped `feature-restart-count`'s `gen.mk` from `touched` to `kept` — the recorder's\ntouch detection is mtime-resolution-sensitive on a makefile GNU remakes and then restarts over. That file\nwas restored from the index and is unchanged in this landing; only the new case's `expected` is added.\n\nINVENTORY ROW MOVED — exactly one, audited by name against the pre-run tsv:\n  features/vpath.diff.4  unclassified/ninja-progress+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress\nThat is the row this node owns. unclassified 51 → 50, narration 1206 → 1207, compiler ZERO, interface 28\nunchanged, total 1285 unchanged.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean root and kati; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0;\nworkspace tests 1101 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1198 ported cases (1206\nin the corpus), eight discovery-only cases unchanged; conformance 354 runs, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw —\nunchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8 respellings."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T14:55:45.286414252Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-byte-order-mark-is-not-part-of-the-first-target
                title "Skip a UTF-8 byte order mark at the head of a makefile"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:40:48.982598478Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from features/utf8.diff (suite script features/utf8:8). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer — a makefile whose first three bytes are the UTF-8 BOM (EF BB BF), then `all: ; @echo $@`:\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  all\n  Ronin      ﻿all\n\nGNU skips a UTF-8 byte order mark at the head of a makefile, so the first target is named `all`. Ronin reads the BOM as part of the target's name, so the default goal is a three-byte-prefixed name and `$@` echoes it.\n\nSmall, but it is a target NAME: a makefile saved by an editor that writes a BOM would have every reference to its first target miss. Build intent."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T19:34:04.152562808Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T19:34:25.830754852Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING and found the rule has two more edges than it named. Measured against\nreference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\nThe filing's case held exactly: GNU `all`, Ronin a three-byte-prefixed name. Two shapes were measured\nbeside it, and both are in the recorded case:\n  * An INCLUDED makefile whose first bytes are the mark: GNU skips it there too, so `V := from-inc` in\n    `inc.mk` defines `V`. Ronin defined a prefixed name and `$(V)` was empty. So the filing's \"at the head\n    of a makefile\" is per makefile BUFFER, not per run.\n  * A mark on a LATER line: ordinary text on both sides, so the name it makes is not the one written and\n    the reference to it is empty. That is the control, and it is what says the rule is about the line\n    number rather than about the bytes.\n\nMECHANISM. `eval` (read.c) tests for `EF BB BF` and advances past it only inside\n`if (ebuf->floc.lineno == 1)`. Every makefile buffer starts at line 1, which is why an `include`d file is\ncovered and a later line is not.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati 484c2f53141ee6f8a5b83a76ee87c74ce47415b6, branch\nnplan/make-a-byte-order-mark-is-not-part-of-the-first-target, one file:\n  * `file.rs::Makefile::from_bytes`, which is the single point every makefile's bytes pass through on their\n    way to statements: `without_byte_order_mark` takes a leading mark off the text handed to `parse_file`.\n    The bytes are still noted as they were READ, because `read_sources`/`supply_makefile` hands them back\n    to a pass that re-reads this unit and that pass must see what the file holds. `--eval` fragments do not\n    go through here — they are parsed by `parse_buf` — which matches GNU, whose eval buffers inherit the\n    enclosing line number and so almost never satisfy the test.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX:\n`tests/make/a-byte-order-mark-at-the-head-of-a-makefile-is-skipped` fails without it — `out` holds\n`goal=[\\u{feff}all] inc=[]` where GNU left `goal=[all] inc=[from-the-included-file]`. Both halves in one\ncase: the goal's own name, and the variable an included file with its own mark defines. `mid=[]` agrees on\nboth sides and is the control for a mark that is not on line 1.\n\nINVENTORY ROW MOVED — exactly one, audited by name against the pre-run tsv:\n  features/utf8.diff  unclassified/ninja-progress+recipe-echo+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress\nThat is the row this node owns. unclassified 34 → 33, narration 1223 → 1224, compiler ZERO, interface 28\nunchanged, total 1285 unchanged.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0; workspace\ntests 1101 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1207 ported cases; conformance 354\nruns, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8 respellings."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T19:34:37.404605983Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-makefile-written-during-evaluation-is-there-to-include
                title "Include a makefile an earlier line of the same makefile wrote"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:40:53.833886261Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from features/include.diff.27 (suite script features/include:362). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer, in a directory with no `hello.mk`:\n\n    default:; @echo $(hello)\n    -include hello.mk\n    $(shell echo hello=world >hello.mk)\n    include hello.mk\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  world                                    exit 0\n  Ronin      Makefile:2: hello.mk: No such file or directory\n             ronin: No rule to make target 'hello.mk'.  exit 2\n\nRonin DOES run the `$(shell)` — `hello.mk` is on disk after both runs — but the `include` on the following line still fails, and the failure is reported against line 2, the `-include`, which GNU keeps silent. So there are two things wrong in one case: an `-include` that is not silent, and an `include` that does not see a file an earlier line of the same makefile created.\n\nA refusal (exit 2, nothing built) where GNU builds. Neighbouring node: make-include-semantics (done)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T21:13:17.73939869Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T21:13:43.293464111Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING and found the fault is TWO defects that only show together — which is why the\nfiling saw \"two things wrong in one case\". All measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\nThe filing's case held. What the probe added is the set of shapes that ALREADY worked, and they are what\nlocalised it:\n\n    include after a $(shell) that wrote the file, no `-include` above it   both: world\n    a bare `-include` of a name nothing creates                            both: silent, exit 0\n    `-include a.mk` (missing) then $(shell) then `include hello.mk`        both: world  (different names)\n    `include hello.mk` then `-include hello.mk`                            both: world  (order reversed)\n    `-include hello.mk` then $(shell) then `-include hello.mk`             both: world  (both optional)\n    `-include hello.mk` then $(shell) then `include hello.mk`              GNU world, Ronin refused\n\nSo it needed the SAME NAME, the optional include FIRST, and the second one REQUIRED. Each of the two\ndefects is invisible without the other.\n\nDEFECT ONE — absence was remembered. `get_makefile` cached `Source::Absent` as a `None` entry, so the\nsecond `include` was told what the first was told. GNU Make's `eval_makefile` (read.c) calls `fopen` every\ntime the directive names the file; a read that did not get a file has learnt nothing about it except that\nit did not have it then.\n\nDEFECT TWO — a name the read GOT was still recorded as a name it went without. `note_unread_include` from\nthe `-include` left a `MissingInclude` keyed by name; the later `include` succeeded and did not clear it,\nand `plan_regeneration` then found that entry beside a `read_makefiles` entry whose `required` the\nsuccessful include had just raised to true. The result was the complaint and the refusal — and it is why\nthe diagnostic pointed at line 2, the `-include`, which is where the stale entry's location came from.\nGNU Make keeps ONE GOALDEP PER `include` LINE: the failed `-include` and the successful `include` are two\nentries, only the first carries an errno, and that one is `RM_DONTCARE`, which `show_goal_error` (remake.c)\nreturns from without a word.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati e2aeddd09c0019af4246bbe6a5db9b851459dc0b, branch\nnplan/make-a-makefile-written-during-evaluation-is-there-to-include, two files:\n  * `file_cache.rs`: absence joins the same `unread` set as a file that would not open, rather than the\n    cache. The cache now holds only makefiles actually READ, so its type lost its `Option`. The name is\n    still in `all_filenames`, which is what it was in the cache for.\n  * `eval.rs::do_include`: a successful read takes the name off `missing_includes`.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX:\n`tests/make/an-include-reads-a-makefile-an-earlier-line-wrote` fails without it — \"build failed where GNU\nMake's build succeeded\". The case carries the control in the same file: `never.mk` is optionally included\nand never created, and must stay silent and not refuse.\n\nINVENTORY ROW MOVED — exactly one, audited by name against the pre-run tsv:\n  features/include.diff.27  unclassified/product-name+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress\nThat is the row this node owns. unclassified 28 → 27, narration 1229 → 1230, compiler ZERO, interface 20\nunchanged, corpus 1277 unchanged.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0; workspace\ntests 1104 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1210 ported cases; conformance 354\nruns, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8 respellings."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T21:13:54.626502462Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-an-eval-only-invocation-needs-no-makefile-on-disk
                title "Treat --eval text as makefile content when no makefile is on disk"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:41:05.344715129Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from features/include.diff.36 (suite script features/include:461). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nMinimal reproducer, in an EMPTY directory:\n\n    make -E 'all:;@echo hi'\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  hi                                              exit 0\n  Ronin      ronin: no targets specified and no makefile found  exit 2\n\nThe same `-E` text WITH a makefile present works on both:\n\n    (directory holds `other:;@echo other`)\n    make -E 'all:;@echo hi' all   ->  both print `hi`\n\nSo `--eval` is read and its rules do take effect; what is missing is that the eval'd text does not satisfy the \"a makefile must exist\" requirement. GNU treats `-E` text as makefile content in its own right.\n\nThe suite's own case goes further, and is the second half of this: with `MAKEFILES='foobar barfoo'` set and `-E '%:;@echo $@' -E 'all:;' -E '-include bizbaz' -E '-include bazbiz'`, GNU echoes bizbaz, bazbiz, foobar, barfoo, GNUmakefile, makefile, Makefile — the eval'd pattern rule participates in remaking every makefile GNU looks for, including the default names. Ronin builds none of it.\n\nA refusal where GNU builds: build intent."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T23:45:52.745884997Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T23:46:26.075975689Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING — both halves, exactly as written — AND FOUND THREE MORE DIVERGENCES with the\nsame cause. All measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\nThe filing's two halves held: `make -E 'all:;@echo hi'` in an empty directory had GNU print `hi` and Ronin\nrefuse, and the suite's own case had GNU echo bizbaz/bazbiz/foobar/barfoo/GNUmakefile/makefile/Makefile\nwhere Ronin built none of it. What the probe added:\n\n  * `make all` in an EMPTY directory, no `-E` anywhere — GNU: `No rule to make target 'all'.` Ronin:\n    `no targets specified and no makefile found`. So the refusal was not about `-E` at all; Ronin gave up\n    on \"no makefile\" whenever there was none, where GNU gives up only on \"no goal\".\n  * ORDER: `-E` ran AFTER `MAKEFILES` in Ronin and BEFORE it in GNU. Measured with a fragment and a\n    MAKEFILES file each appending to one variable: GNU `EVAL: V=` then `MF: V=from-eval from-mkfiles`;\n    Ronin had them the other way round.\n  * `MAKEFILE_LIST` DURING a fragment: GNU empty, Ronin `Makefile`.\n\nONE CAUSE. Ronin spliced the fragments into the parsed statements of the first NAMED makefile. GNU\nevaluates each as its own buffer — `eval_buffer (p, NULL)`, main.c — above the call to\n`read_all_makefiles`. The `NULL` is the second half of the meaning: the text came from no file, so it joins\nneither `MAKEFILE_LIST` nor the makefiles that can be remade. Splice them into a file and they run after\n`MAKEFILES`, they inherit that file's `MAKEFILE_LIST`, and where there is no file they are silently lost —\nwhich is the filing's defect.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati 9e6057614f53a9d00470d361a82e790f1bbc8c6b, branch\nnplan/make-an-eval-only-invocation-needs-no-makefile-on-disk, plus the Ronin side in the same landing:\n  * kati `evaluate.rs`: the fragments are read as their own buffer, first, ahead of `MAKEFILES` — carried\n    on `Flags::command_line_evals` — and note no makefile.\n  * kati `evaluate.rs`: having read no makefile, the three default names go on as don't-care remake\n    candidates, which is GNU's `RM_DONTCARE` block in read.c. Don't-care, so an ordinary empty directory\n    stays silent.\n  * kati `dep.rs`: the no-goal refusal now picks between GNU's two messages on `MAKEFILE_LIST`, exactly as\n    main.c does — `No targets.` when something was read, `No targets specified and no makefile found.`\n    when nothing was.\n  * src/make/cli.rs: the early \"no makefile\" refusal is GONE. It was made before anything was read; GNU\n    makes it after everything is. `src/make/report.rs::no_makefile` went with it.\n  * src/make/cli/interface.rs + cli/subninja.rs: `prepend_command_line_evals` became\n    `carry_command_line_evals` — it hands the fragments to the read instead of splicing them, and no longer\n    has to enter the child's directory to parse a file it no longer touches.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX — two build-intent cases, both FAILING when parked:\n  * tests/make/an-eval-fragment-carries-a-run-with-no-makefile/ — \"build failed where GNU Make's build\n    succeeded\". Runs `-C empty` in a directory `setup` leaves empty, with a `%:` fragment and an `all:`\n    fragment; records the three default names GNU reaches and the goal it builds.\n  * tests/make/an-eval-fragment-is-read-before-any-makefile/ — parked, `out` holds\n    `eval: order=[from-mkfiles]list=[extra.mk Makefile]` where GNU left `eval: order=[]list=[]`, and the\n    whole four-line record is in the wrong order. Covers the ordering and the `MAKEFILE_LIST` halves at\n    once, with a fragment, a MAKEFILES file and the named makefile each recording what it found.\nBoth cases write their recipes without a space in them, because a case's `args` are whitespace-separated\nwords with no quoting — noted in the Makefiles so the next reader does not rediscover it.\n\nINVENTORY ROW MOVED — exactly one, audited by name against the pre-run tsv:\n  features/include.diff.36  unclassified/product-name+up-to-date-line+evaluation\n                          → narration/ninja-progress+no-work-line+up-to-date-line\nThat is the row this node owns. unclassified 52 → 51, narration 1205 → 1206, compiler ZERO, interface 28\nunchanged, total 1285 unchanged.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean root and kati; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0;\nworkspace tests 1101 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1204 cases, five\ndivergence markers intact; conformance 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8\nrespellings; check-make-projects.sh all — vim and zsh built; ninja conformance 425/425 + 10 differential +\n92 build-outcome; --no-default-features EXIT 0.\n\nONE WORDING CHANGE, stated because it is a narration change and not a silent one. The empty-directory\nrefusal was Ronin's own lowercase sentence, `ronin: no targets specified and no makefile found`, written\nby the front end before it read anything. It is now the compiler's, in GNU Make's words with the `***` and\n`Stop.` ceremony still taken off: `ronin: No targets specified and no makefile found.` That follows the\nprecedent already standing beside it — kati's `*** No targets.` is deliberately GNU's own wording, because\nthe upstream suite matches that string to learn what the program under test is called — and it moves the\nrow toward identical rather than away. The suite's name probe is unaffected: it runs `-f null.mk` and reads\n`No targets.`, which is unchanged."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-25T00:07:37.933854104Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-makeoverrides-reaches-a-recursive-invocation
                title "Carry an appended MAKEOVERRIDES into a recursive invocation"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:41:11.443163762Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from features/recursion.diff.2 (suite script features/recursion:57). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer:\n\n    ifeq ($(MAKELEVEL),0)\n    MAKEOVERRIDES += FOO+=bar\n    endif\n    .PHONY: M R\n    M: ; @$(MAKE) --no-print-directory -f Makefile R\n    R: ; @echo '[$(FOO)]'\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  [bar]\n  Ronin      []\n\n`MAKEOVERRIDES` is how a makefile adds its own definitions to what a recursive invocation inherits: GNU writes it into the `-- ` half of MAKEFLAGS, the child reads `FOO+=bar` as a command-line definition, and `R` sees it. Ronin composes the recursive invocation into the same graph and does not carry the appended override across the boundary, so `$(FOO)` is empty in the composed sub-unit.\n\nThe value reaches the recipe's command line, so this is build intent, not choreography. Neighbouring nodes: make-subninja-recursion, make-makefile-set-makeflags (both done)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T20:44:29.348568645Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T20:44:59.459810797Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING and narrowed it to one missing step. All measured against\nreference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\nThe filing's case held: GNU `[bar]`, Ronin `[]`. The narrowing came from asking what the PARENT saw, which\nthe filing did not:\n\n  parent, MAKEOVERRIDES appended, nothing on the command line\n      GNU   OVR=[FOO+=bar] FLAGS=[ -- FOO+=bar] value=[ -- $(MAKEOVERRIDES)]\n      Ronin OVR=[FOO+=bar] FLAGS=[]             value=[]\n  parent, the same append AND a real command-line override `Z=9`\n      both  OVR=[Z=9 FOO+=bar] FLAGS=[ -- Z=9 FOO+=bar] value=[ -- $(MAKEOVERRIDES)]\n\nSo Ronin already carried `MAKEOVERRIDES` into `MAKEFLAGS` and across the composed boundary — it did it\nwhenever the COMMAND LINE had overrides. What was missing is the other way the variable comes to hold\nsomething: a makefile defining it from nothing.\n\nMECHANISM. `MAKEOVERRIDES` is defined at startup only inside `if (command_variables)` (main.c), as\n`${-*-command-variables-*-}`. `define_makeflags` writes the ` -- $(MAKEOVERRIDES)` suffix when\n`v && v->value && v->value[0] != '\\0'` — the STORED TEXT, so the default definition qualifies by being a\nreference. And that question is asked a second time once the read is over: `define_makeflags (0)` under\n\"Set up 'MAKEFLAGS' again for the normal targets\". A makefile appending to an undefined `MAKEOVERRIDES`\ndefines it, and the second pass is what notices.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati 141a23e2d18dee9ddd972272e59df7f73041e4a8, branch\nnplan/make-makeoverrides-reaches-a-recursive-invocation, plus the Ronin side in the same landing:\n  * kati `evaluate.rs::republish_makeflags_after_read`, run where GNU Make runs its second\n    `define_makeflags`: if `MAKEOVERRIDES` now has stored text and the suffix was not written, write it and\n    record that it is written. Sticky in one direction, as the startup answer already was.\n  * kati `flags.rs`: `MakeflagsAssignment::published` — the switch table as last published, which is what\n    the second pass rebuilds the value on top of. Written at startup and on every `MAKEFLAGS` assignment.\n  * `src/make/cli.rs`: seeds it.\n\nA SECOND DIFFERENCE MEASURED AND NOT TAKEN, recorded so the next dispatch does not rediscover it. GNU Make\nrepublishes `MAKEOVERRIDES` as the SETTLED variables rather than as the assignments were written —\n`quote_for_env(name)`, a `:` if the variable came out simple, `=`, then the value. Measured at MAKELEVEL 0,\nwhere it has nothing to do with this fix:\n\n    command line        GNU              Ronin\n    FOO+=bar            FOO=bar          FOO+=bar\n    FOO?=bar            FOO=bar          FOO?=bar\n    FOO!=echo hi        FOO=hi           FOO!=echo\\ hi\n    FOO::=x             FOO:=x           FOO::=x\n    FOO:::=y            FOO=y            FOO:::=y\n    FOO=plain           FOO=plain        FOO=plain   (the only spelling that agrees)\n\nThe last row of that table is the sharp one: republishing `FOO!=echo hi` as an unsettled `!=` means a child\nRUNS the command again, where GNU ran it once and passed the answer. Doing it GNU's way needs each\ncommand-line assignment settled — expanded, or run — before `MAKEOVERRIDES` is rendered, which is\nevaluator work rather than command-line work, and it is the same ground as the \"every command-line variable\nis expanded twice at settle time\" defect this batch was also handed. Left for that one rather than folded\nin here.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX:\n`tests/make/an-appended-makeoverrides-reaches-a-recursive-invocation` fails without it — `child.out` holds\n`child FOO=[] level=1` where GNU left `child FOO=[bar] level=1`. `parent.out` carries the other half in the\nsame case: the parent's own `$(MAKEFLAGS)` and `$(value MAKEFLAGS)`, which show the suffix and the\nreference it is made of.\n\nINVENTORY ROW MOVED — exactly one, audited by name against the pre-run tsv:\n  features/recursion.diff.2  unclassified/ninja-progress+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress\nThat is the row this node owns. unclassified 29 → 28, narration 1228 → 1229, compiler ZERO, interface 20\nunchanged, corpus 1277 unchanged.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0; workspace\ntests 1104 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1209 ported cases; conformance 354\nruns, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8 respellings."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T20:46:51.040613437Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-command-line-variable-reaches-a-shell-functions-recursive-make
                title "Carry a command-line variable into a recursive Make run from a shell function"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:41:17.248406399Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from variables/MAKEFLAGS.diff.116 and .117 (suite script variables/MAKEFLAGS:349 and :355, GNU's own sv 63347 regression). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer — a `bye/makefile` holding `hello=moon` and `all:; $(info $(hello))`, and:\n\n    v:=$(shell $(MAKE) -C bye --no-print-directory)\n    all: ; $(info $(v))\n\ninvoked as `make hello=world`:\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  world make[1]: 'all' is up to date.\n  Ronin      moon ronin: no work to do.\n\nRow .117 is the same case written with the shell-assignment operator (`v != $(MAKE) -C bye --no-print-directory`) and behaves identically.\n\nThe command-line definition `hello=world` must outrank the sub-makefile's own `hello=moon`, and it reaches the child through MAKEFLAGS. Ronin's `$(shell $(MAKE) …)` launches a real child process at evaluation time — this one is NOT composed into the graph, since its output is captured as a variable's value — and that child is not given the parent's command-line definitions.\n\nThe captured value reaches `$(info)` and could as easily reach a recipe, so this is build intent."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T21:33:34.433978311Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T21:33:58.260132761Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE FILING and found the cause is one missing bit rather than anything about recursion.\nAll measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\nBoth filed rows held — the `$(shell)` and `!=` spellings behave identically. The probe that settled it read\nthe child's ENVIRONMENT rather than its output:\n\n    parent's own $(MAKEFLAGS)   both:  ` -- hello=world`\n    child's environment          GNU:  MAKEFLAGS= -- hello=world|MFLAGS=|\n                                Ronin:  (nothing — no MAKEFLAGS at all)\n\nSo Ronin worked out the right `MAKEFLAGS` and did not hand it to anyone. `MAKELEVEL` reached the child as 1\non both sides, so the environment-building machinery was working; `MAKEFLAGS` alone was absent from it.\n\nMECHANISM. GNU Make marks `MAKEFLAGS` exported BY NAME rather than leaving it to origin:\n`define_makeflags (0)->export = v_export` (main.c). A file-origin variable is not exported otherwise, and\nRonin defines it at file origin (or environment-override under `-e`), so it never travelled.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati 88fe1ec4ca97e23fe919967708e7085c8bcbbc31, branch\nnplan/make-a-command-line-variable-reaches-a-shell-functions-recursive-make, one file:\n  * `evaluate.rs`: the `MAKEFLAGS` binding is given `VarExport::Export` where it is defined.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX:\n`tests/make/a-command-line-value-reaches-a-shell-functions-child-make` fails without it — `captured.out`\nholds `moon` where GNU left `world`, and `shelled.out` (the `!=` spelling) goes the same way. Each child\nwrites what it saw to a file of its own rather than to stdout, because what a child prints on stdout is\nnarration and the two tools narrate differently; `out` carries the halfway point, the environment's own\n`MAKEFLAGS`, so a failure says which half went wrong.\n\nINVENTORY: NO ROW MOVED, and that is worth stating rather than glossing. variables/MAKEFLAGS.diff.116 and\n.117 are still `unclassified/no-work-line+up-to-date-line+evaluation`. Their output is now right to the\nnarration:\n    GNU    world make[1]: 'all' is up to date.  /  make: 'all' is up to date.\n    Ronin  world ronin: no work to do.          /  ronin: no work to do.\nThe captured value now begins `world` on both sides — the whole of what the case was testing. What is left\nis the child's own up-to-date line INSIDE that captured value, where the classifier has to compose\n`product-name` with `up-to-date-line` inside a variable's contents and does not. The defect is closed and\ngated by the recorded case; the row is narration residue the classifier cannot yet explain, and belongs to\nwhoever widens it.\n\nA SECOND, SMALLER DIFFERENCE MEASURED AND NOT TAKEN: GNU's child environment also carries `MFLAGS=` and\nRonin's does not. `$(MFLAGS)` reads the same on both sides at every level — the child derives it from its\nown switches — so nothing observable follows from it inside make; only a recipe reading `env` would see it.\nRecorded here rather than fixed.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0; workspace\ntests 1104 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1211 ported cases; conformance 354\nruns, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8 respellings; upstream classes\nunchanged at unclassified 27 / compiler 0 / interface 20 / narration 1230."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T21:34:10.813314491Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-link-rule-does-not-inherit-the-compile-rules-source
                title "Keep a chained implicit rule's source out of the link rule's prerequisites"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:41:21.618330816Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from misc/general4.diff.1 (suite script misc/general4:24). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer — an empty `foo.c` exists, the makefile is one line `foo: foo.o`, invoked as `make CC=\"@echo cc\" OUTPUT_OPTION=`:\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  cc -c foo.c\n             cc foo.o -o foo\n  Ronin      cc -c foo.c\n             cc foo.c foo.o -o foo\n\nThe compile step agrees. The LINK step does not: GNU's built-in `%: %.o` rule gives `$^` as `foo.o` alone, and Ronin's gives `foo.c foo.o` — the source of the chained compile rule has leaked into the link rule's prerequisite list.\n\n`$^` is on the command line, so the linker would be handed a `.c` file it was never meant to see. Build intent, and the kind that breaks a real makefile rather than a test."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T22:44:08.130240267Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T22:44:34.882227682Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED THE SYMPTOM AND CORRECTED THE DIAGNOSIS. The filing is right about what happens and wrong\nabout why, and the difference decides where the fix goes.\n\nThe filing says \"the source of the chained compile rule has leaked into the link rule's prerequisite list\".\nIt has not leaked. Ronin picked a DIFFERENT LINK RULE, and `foo.c foo.o` is that rule's correct\nprerequisite list.\n\nMeasured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. With `foo.c` on disk and `foo: foo.o`:\n\n    .SUFFIXES: .o .c        GNU: .o: rule, $^ = foo.o          Ronin: .c: rule, $^ = foo.c foo.o\n    .SUFFIXES: .c .o        GNU: .c: rule, $^ = foo.c foo.o    Ronin: the same\n\nGNU ITSELF produces `foo.c foo.o` whenever `%: %.c` is the rule that wins — the pattern prerequisite\n`foo.c` plus the explicitly written `foo.o`. So the prerequisite list was never the defect. The defect is\nCANDIDATE SELECTION: `%: %.o` and `%: %.c` both match `foo` with the stem `foo`, they are tried in\n`.SUFFIXES` order, and GNU takes the first one it can satisfy without inventing anything.\n\nWHY GNU CAN SATISFY `%: %.o` WITH NO `foo.o` ON DISK, isolated to one condition by three probes:\n  (a) `foo.o` is a target of some other rule            → Ronin already matched GNU (its `exists()` asks\n                                                           `self.rules.contains_key`, which is `is_target`)\n  (b) `foo.o` is a prerequisite of the target being made → Ronin diverged. THIS ONE.\n  (neither, `all: foo` alone)                            → both pick `%: %.c`; GNU does prefer a candidate\n                                                           that invents nothing, and Ronin agreed there\nGNU's `pattern_search` (implicit.c) walks `file->deps` for the name and, on a hit, accepts it with\n`\"'%s' ought to exist.\"` — no intermediate, strict pass, candidate wins. Ronin had no per-target\n`file->deps` scan at all; the nearest thing was the GLOBAL `mentioned` set, reached only through\n`is_written_down`, which is the far weaker `lookup_file` claim and is honoured only on the compatibility\npass.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT: kati — kati d0ae73065e88cba864dc8e89abd28ac0c88b3f57, branch\nnplan/make-a-link-rule-does-not-inherit-the-compile-rules-source. `ImplicitSearch` now carries the target's\ndeclared prerequisites, read ONCE per search from `recorded_prerequisites` (which is `file->deps`:\n`snap_deps` has settled it before `pattern_search` runs, so it is the same for every candidate and every\npass). Both paths a candidate reaches the search by consult it — the pattern candidates in\n`implicit_prerequisites_reachable`, and the suffix map in `pick_pattern_rule`, because GNU has one code\npath for both.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX: tests/make/a-prerequisite-this-target-asks-for-needs-no-intermediate/\nFAILS when parked — `'link.out' holds \"link.c foo <- [foo.c foo.o]\" where GNU Make left\n\"link.o foo <- [foo.o]\"`. Written with explicit `.SUFFIXES` and `-r` so the case pins the rule that is\nchosen rather than the catalogue that happens to be installed, and its comment records that `.c:` is\nwritten FIRST and still loses — the order is `.SUFFIXES`', not the makefile's.\n\nBROKE NOTHING, which mattered here because this is the implicit search: make_port 4/4 over 1201 cases;\nconformance unchanged at 326/28 and 276 raw; workspace 1101/0; equivalence 0 disagreements; vim and zsh\nboth built; ninja conformance 425/425. Thirteen hand-written probes covering both suffix orders, both\navailability conditions, single- and double-suffix rules, user-written pattern chains and the built-in\ncatalogue all match GNU byte for byte.\n\nINVENTORY ROW MOVED — exactly one, audited by name against the pre-run tsv:\n  misc/general4.diff.1  unclassified/ninja-progress+evaluation → narration/ninja-progress\nThat is the row this node owns; its residue is now the progress counter alone. unclassified 55 → 54,\nnarration 1202 → 1203, compiler ZERO, interface 28 unchanged, total 1285 unchanged.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean root and kati; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0;\nworkspace tests 1101 passed / 0 failed; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4, five divergence markers\nintact; conformance 326/28 normalised, 276 raw; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8 respellings;\ncheck-make-projects.sh all — vim and zsh built; ninja conformance 425/425 + 10 differential + 92\nbuild-outcome; --no-default-features EXIT 0."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-25T00:07:28.561437168Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-touch-touches-a-target-whose-recipe-expands-to-nothing
                title "Touch a target under -t whose recipe expands to no command"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:41:35.398450464Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch, from options/dash-n.diff.4 (suite script options/dash-n:59). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer — `c` exists, `a` and `b` do not:\n\n    .SUFFIXES:\n    BAR =     # nothing\n    FOO = +$(BAR)\n    a: b; echo > $@\n    b: c; $(FOO)\n\nUnder `-t` alone, which is the filesystem-effect half:\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  touch b / touch a        leaves a AND b\n  Ronin      [1/1] echo > a           leaves a only — b is never touched\n\nUnder the suite's own `-t -n`, which is what the row records:\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  touch b / touch a        writes nothing\n  Ronin      [1/1] exec env ... /bin/sh -c \"echo > a\"   writes nothing\n\n`b`'s recipe is `$(FOO)`, which expands to a lone `+` — a recipe-line modifier with no command behind it. GNU still counts `b` as a target with a recipe and touches it. Ronin makes no edge for a recipe that expands to nothing, so there is nothing to touch, and `b` is left absent.\n\nA control probe pins the cause: with `b: c; @:` instead, Ronin touches both files and agrees with GNU.\n\nTwo claims here, and the first is the one that matters: `-t` leaves a file GNU creates, which is a filesystem effect and so inside the contract under [spec:ronin:req:make.semantics+1] and the 2026-08-17 `-t` ruling. The second is that under `-t -n` Ronin's dry-run report names the recipe it would run rather than the touch it would perform; nothing is written either way, but the two tools report different intended work, which is why this row is NOT covered by the `touch-announce` narration family (that family is deliberately switched off under `-n`)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T21:39:44.518500403Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBED AND DIAGNOSED, NOT FIXED. Measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-26.\nLeft for the next dispatch with the mechanism named and the fix's shape costed; nothing here is a request\nfor a ruling.\n\nTHE FILING HELD, and the case is wider than it says. Measured under `-t`, with `c` present and `a`, `b`\nabsent, `b`'s recipe varied:\n\n    b: c                       no recipe          neither touches `b`   (POSIX: \"-t doesn't affect\n                                                                        targets with no cmds\")\n    b: c;                      empty recipe       GNU touches, Ronin does not\n    b: c \\n <tab>$(NOTHING)    blank line         GNU touches, Ronin does not\n    b: c; $(FOO)  FOO =        expands to nothing GNU touches, Ronin does not\n    b: c; $(FOO)  FOO = +      the filing's case  GNU touches, Ronin does not\n    b: c; +                    a LITERAL `+`      NEITHER touches\n    b: c; @:                   a real command     both touch\n\nSo the filing's `+` is incidental: any recipe that comes to nothing behaves this way, and the one shape\nthat does NOT touch is the literal `+` — because that flag is read off the line AS WRITTEN\n(`lines_flags[i] & COMMANDS_RECURSE`), so `$(FOO)` expanding to `+` is not a recursive line and a written\n`+` is.\n\nMECHANISM. `notice_finished_file` (remake.c) touches when `touch_flag && update_status == us_success`, the\nfile is not phony, and `file->cmds != 0` — with one exception, `cmds->any_recurse` when EVERY line is\n`COMMANDS_RECURSE`. What the recipe expands to is never consulted: \"According to POSIX, -t doesn't affect\ntargets with no cmds\" is the whole test, and an empty recipe is cmds.\n\nWHERE RONIN LOSES IT — two places, and they need one thing between them:\n  * The immediate path. `ninja.rs`: `let rule_id = if commands.is_empty() && !deferred_recipe { None }`. An\n    empty recipe mints no rule, so the edge is declared with `rule: None` — indistinguishable in the graph\n    from a target that has no recipe at all, which is exactly the distinction `-t` turns on.\n  * The deferred path. `src/build.rs::prepare_edge`: `if bind_late_command(..) == Runs::Nothing { return\n    Ok(None) }`, whose own comment says \"Nothing below this point happens for such an edge\" — and\n    `touch_outputs` is below it. `src/build/touch.rs::touch_outputs` then wants `stood_in_for_a_step`,\n    which a recipe with no steps cannot set.\n\nWHAT THE FIX COSTS. The graph has to carry one bit the compiler already knows and currently drops: whether\nthe target HAS a recipe, as against whether that recipe produced a command. That is a field on `SinkEdge`\n(kati `build_sink.rs`), set from `!node.cmds.is_empty()` in `ninja.rs`, carried through `src/make/sink.rs`,\n`src/frontend.rs` and `src/graph.rs` the way `intermediate` and `disposable` already are — the plumbing is\nmechanical and about forty lines. Then `-t` touches an edge that has a recipe and ran none of it, which\ncovers both paths at once, and declines the one where every step was a written `+`. `touch_outputs` already\ndeclines a phony target, which is GNU's other guard.\n\nNOT ATTEMPTED HERE because it is a sink-protocol change and this dispatch had the full battery still to\nrun; a half-plumbed protocol is worse than an unstarted one. The second half of the node — that under\n`-t -n` Ronin's dry-run report names the recipe rather than the touch — is untouched and is narration\neither way: nothing is written on either side."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T22:47:58.15107186Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T23:20:53.659880682Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "FIXED. Batch 2's diagnosis held exactly as written and its costing was right: one bit, two places,\nabout forty lines of sink plumbing. Everything below is measured against\nreference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-27.\n\nWHAT THE PROBE CORRECTED, and it is small: batch 2 named `notice_finished_file`'s test correctly but\ndescribed the carve-out as \"cmds->any_recurse\". GNU Make's `any_recurse` is set when ANY line is\nrecursive; the touch is declined only when EVERY line is — `if (file->cmds->any_recurse)` then a loop\nthat jumps to `have_nonrecursing` the moment it meets a line that is not. And `remake.c:1311` says what\nhappens instead: `if (!touch_flag || file->cmds->any_recurse) execute_file_commands (file)`, so an\nall-recursive recipe is RUN under `-t` and the target is made by making it. That is why the carve-out is\npart of the bit rather than a separate guard.\n\nTHE EIGHT SHAPES, before and after, `c` present and `a`, `b` absent, invoked `-t`:\n\n    b's recipe                        GNU leaves   before   after\n    b: c            no recipe         a            a        a\n    b: c;           empty             a b          a        a b\n    b: c \\ <tab>$(NOTHING)  blank     a b          a        a b\n    b: c; $(FOO)    FOO =             a b          a        a b\n    b: c; $(FOO)    FOO = +           a b          a        a b\n    b: c; +         a LITERAL plus    a            a        a\n    b: c; @:        a real command    a b          a b      a b\n    b: c; +@:       all recursive     a            a        a\n\nEight of eight agree on files and on exit status. The only difference left in any of them is the\nprogress line's wording, which is the 2026-08-17 narration ruling.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati 3808a919b0dee9ab46af597dbcc5607b3e4ca573, branch\nnplan/make-touch-touches-a-target-whose-recipe-expands-to-nothing, and the Ronin side in the same landing:\n  * kati `command.rs`: `written_line_recurses`, GNU Make's `lines_flags[i] & COMMANDS_RECURSE` for one\n    written recipe line — the `+` among its opening prefixes, or a `$(MAKE)` reference anywhere in it.\n    Built from the `references_make` and `scan_written_prefixes` that were already there for the two\n    halves of `chop_commands`.\n  * kati `build_sink.rs`, `ninja.rs`: `SinkEdge::has_touchable_recipe`, set from the node's own recipe\n    lines: it has some, and not every one of them is a written recursive line.\n  * `src/make/sink.rs`, `src/frontend.rs`, `src/graph.rs`: carried through as `intermediate` and\n    `disposable` are. A recursive wrapper and a `.ronin_recipe_stage` proxy each declare it false, with\n    the reason written where it is declared.\n  * `src/build/touch.rs`: `touch_the_recipe_that_ran_nothing`, the other touch — the existing one is per\n    step and needs a step to have been stood in for, which a recipe with no steps cannot supply.\n  * `src/build.rs::finish_phony_edge` and `src/build/deferred.rs::finish_without_command` call it, which\n    are batch 2's two places. The deferred one fires for `Unrun::NoCommand` only: an edge skipped because\n    its outputs were already newer is one GNU Make does not remake, and a target it does not remake is a\n    target it does not touch.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING EACH PIECE IN TURN. Four oracle-recorded cases added, and every part of the\ncondition is load-bearing:\n  * both call sites parked      2 of 1215 fail  (`...recipe-that-expands-to-nothing`, `...written-as-nothing`)\n  * the deferred call parked    3 shapes lose `b` (the two `$(FOO)` ones and the blank line)\n  * the phony call parked       1 shape loses `b` (the `b: c;` one)\n  * the recursive test parked   1 of 1215 fails (`a-touch-leaves-a-recipe-of-a-bare-plus-alone`)\n  * the whole bit forced true   8 of 1215 fail, including `a-touch-runs-a-plus-line-instead-of-touching`,\n                                `an-old-file-is-not-touched` and `a-one-shell-recipe-is-recursive-from-\n                                its-first-line`, which are the cases that were already pinning the\n                                boundary from the other side.\nThe four new cases are `a-touch-stands-in-for-a-recipe-that-expands-to-nothing`,\n`a-touch-stands-in-for-a-recipe-written-as-nothing`, `a-touch-leaves-a-target-with-no-recipe-alone` and\n`a-touch-leaves-a-recipe-of-a-bare-plus-alone`. Recording rewrote no existing expectation — checked by\n`git status` over tests/make after the record, which is batch 2's own hazard note.\n\nTHE SECOND HALF OF THE NODE IS NARRATION AND IS LEFT. Under `-t -n` GNU says `touch b` / `touch a` and\nRonin says `[1/1] echo > a`: nothing is written on either side, the difference is which words each tool\nreports its intent in, and the count differs because Ronin does not report a commandless edge as work in\nANY mode — that is Ninja's accounting and is uniform across the product rather than anything `-t` does.\nThe 2026-08-17 ruling on `make-archive-member-touch` covers it: \"the BEHAVIOUR lands as specified, the\nNARRATION does not\". The filing hedged on this because `touch-announce` is gated off under `-n`; the\ngating is about not letting that family swallow a row whose FILESYSTEM half was a real defect, and that\nhalf is now closed.\n\nINVENTORY: no row moved, and none was expected to. `options/dash-n.diff.4` runs `-t -n`, where both tools\nwrite nothing, so the suite never exercised the half that was wrong. It stays `unclassified\nninja-progress+evaluation` and docs/make-upstream-residue.md now records what was fixed and what is left.\n1277 rows, 27 unclassified, 0 compiler, 20 interface, 1230 narration — tsv byte-identical.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean both crates; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0;\nworkspace tests 1104 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1215 ported\ncases (1211 + 4); conformance 354 runs, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274 makefiles\n/ 276 graphs / 56 make-rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 respellings; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream, 10\ndifferential, 92 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T23:21:32.879577603Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-warning-gnu-emits-about-a-makefile-is-emitted
                title "Emit the makefile warnings GNU emits, or record why they are declined"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:41:47.416453263Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch. Three suite rows, one question: options/warn-undefined-variables.diff.2 and .3 (options/warn-undefined-variables:33 and :40) and variables/define.diff.7 (variables/define:161). MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\n`--warn-undefined-variables` is accepted and does nothing:\n\n    EMPTY =\n    EREF = $(EMPTY)\n    UREF = $(UNDEFINED)\n    SEREF := $(EREF)\n    SUREF := $(UREF)\n    all: ; @echo ref $(EREF) $(UREF)\n\n  make --warn-undefined-variables\n  GNU 4.4.1  Makefile:5: warning: undefined variable 'UNDEFINED'\n             Makefile:6: warning: undefined variable 'UNDEFINED'\n             ref\n  Ronin      ref\n\nA malformed `define` draws no complaint:\n\n    NAME =\n    define NAME = $(NAME)\n    ouch\n    endef\n    all: ; @echo ok\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  Makefile:2: extraneous text after 'define' directive / ok\n  Ronin      ok\n\nIn every one of the three the BUILD is identical — same work, same effects, same exit status — and the whole of the difference is a diagnostic GNU emits and Ronin does not. That is why this is one node rather than three: the question is a single one, and it is for the operator rather than for a dispatch.\n\nNot filed as narration on this dispatch's own authority, and deliberately so. The 2026-08-17 narration ruling covers Make-voiced RUNTIME chatter — progress, up-to-date announcements, `Entering directory`. These are static diagnostics about the makefile's own text, which the same ruling keeps inside the contract (\"legitimate ONLY where a failure must be reported at all\"), and `--warn-undefined-variables` in particular is a switch Ronin ACCEPTS whose entire purpose is the warning: accepting it and doing nothing is an interface claim Ronin does not honour. features/se_explicit.diff.10's three-line duplicate-recipe warning is a fourth instance of the same question and is noted on make-a-library-prerequisite-is-the-file-libpatterns-names.\n\nEither outcome is defensible and both need a ruling: implement the warnings, or record a decision that makefile-text diagnostics are declined and add the family that says so."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T20:02:18.563647522Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T20:02:27.703493191Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "RULED AND IMPLEMENTED. The node asked for an operator decision between emitting these diagnostics and\nrecording that they are declined. The operator's standing ruling of 2026-08-26 — verbatim: \"none of this\nneeds my call. If it's wrong, don't do it. Make it correct.\" — settles it: they are emitted.\n\nBoth are static facts about the makefile's own text, so both are compile-time answers and neither is\nMake-voiced runtime chatter. `--warn-undefined-variables` in particular is a switch Ronin ACCEPTS whose\nentire purpose is the warning."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T20:02:56.622806149Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PROBE CONFIRMED BOTH FILED CASES and found a THIRD defect neither the filing nor the suite rows named,\nwhich turned out to be the biggest part of the landing. All measured against\nreference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make.\n\nBoth filed cases held exactly as written. What the probe added:\n  * The exact firing rule for the undefined-variable warning, measured rather than assumed:\n    `$(origin NOPE) $(flavor NOPE) $(value NOPE)` are SILENT on GNU, while `$(call NOPE,x)` and\n    `$(NOPE:a=b)` warn. Asking about a name is not expanding one.\n  * `UREF = $(UNDEFINED)` on line 3 does not warn; the two warnings come from line 5 (`SUREF := $(UREF)`,\n    which expands it) and the recipe line. So the location is where the READ has got to, not where the\n    value was written — GNU passes `reading_file`.\n  * The `define` complaint is narrower than \"text after the operator\": `define NAME = # hi` and\n    `define NAME =   ` are silent on GNU. The comment and the trailing blanks are off before the check.\n  * THE THIRD DEFECT: `--warn-undefined-variables` was not written into `MAKEFLAGS` at all. GNU puts it in\n    the long-option group after `--trace` — measured: `MAKEFLAGS=[i --warn-undefined-variables]` and\n    `[k --trace --warn-undefined-variables]`. Ronin published `[i]`. That is eight upstream rows of its\n    own, and they were sitting in the INTERFACE class where nobody was looking for them.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati 50f95a1a0a4c832068bfca77db6a7da8f56900d8, branch\nnplan/make-a-warning-gnu-emits-about-a-makefile-is-emitted, and the Ronin side in the same landing:\n  * kati `eval.rs`: `Evaluator::warn_undefined`, carrying GNU Make's ten-name exemption list from\n    `defined_vars` beside `warn_undefined` (variable.c) and reading the location off the read's own\n    position. Called from `lookup_var_for_eval`, which is the single funnel `$(NAME)` and `$(NAME:a=b)`'s\n    name lookup already go through — the two callers are exactly `reference_variable`'s counterpart.\n  * kati `expr.rs`: the `$(V:a=b)` arm asks it too, since that one looks the name up for itself.\n  * kati `func.rs::call_func`: `func_call` asks `warn_undefined` about the name it was handed; kati's own\n    `undefined user function` warning is a separate thing and stays behind its own flag.\n  * kati `parser.rs::parse_define`: the `extraneous text after 'define' directive` complaint, beside the\n    `endef` one that was already there.\n  * kati `flags.rs`, `eval.rs`: `warn_undefined_variables` on `Flags` and on `DecodedMakeflags`, so a\n    makefile's own write to `MAKEFLAGS` turns it on and off like every other switch.\n  * `src/make/cli.rs`: `--warn-undefined-variables` moved from `OptionClass::NoOp` to `CompilerInput` and\n    became a `Switch`, and `src/make/cli/interface.rs` writes it into `MAKEFLAGS` where GNU Make's switch\n    table puts it — after `--trace`.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX: three cases in `tests/cli.rs`, all three failing without it. They live\nthere rather than under `tests/make/` because the make_port harness reads FILES and a diagnostic is not\none.\n  * `undefined_variables_are_warned_about_when_asked` — two warnings at the right lines, and silence for\n    `$(origin)`, `$(flavor)` and `$(value)`.\n  * `the_undefined_warning_is_off_and_inherited` — nothing said without the switch, and the switch in\n    `MAKEFLAGS` with it.\n  * `extra_text_after_a_define_directive_is_named` — the complaint once, and NOT for the `define` whose\n    tail is a comment and blanks.\n\nINVENTORY — ELEVEN ROWS MOVED, and eight of them left the inventory ENTIRELY, which is the first time this\nbatch a case has become byte-identical to GNU Make rather than merely explained. Audited by name against\nthe pre-run tsv:\n  options/warn-undefined-variables.diff.2  unclassified → narration/ninja-progress\n  options/warn-undefined-variables.diff.3  unclassified → narration/no-work-line+up-to-date-line\n  variables/define.diff.7                  unclassified → narration/ninja-progress\n  variables/MAKEFLAGS.diff.53 .56 .59 .62 .84 .85 .86 .87   interface/makeflags-content → GONE\nThe eight MAKEFLAGS rows produce no diff at all now, so the corpus is 1285 rows → 1277.\nunclassified 33 → 30, interface 28 → 20, narration 1224 → 1227, compiler ZERO.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0; workspace\ntests 1104 passed / 0 failed (1101 + the three new ones); check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1207\nported cases; conformance 354 runs, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274/276/56/0 + 8\nrespellings."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T20:04:52.340422823Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-keep-going-builds-what-it-can-past-an-unmakeable-prerequisite
                title "Build the reachable work under -k when one prerequisite has no rule"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T10:41:56.537819393Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-24 upstream residue dispatch. Found IN PASSING while diagnosing options/dash-k.diff — that row itself turned out to be a suite artifact (see docs/make-upstream-residue.md), but the reproducer built to check it exposed this. MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24.\n\nReproducer — a `work/` directory holding main.c, defs.h, command.h, commands.c, display.c, buffer.h, command.c, and deliberately NO kbd.c:\n\n    VPATH = work\n    edit:  main.o kbd.o commands.o display.o\n    \t@echo cc -o edit main.o kbd.o commands.o display.o\n    main.o : main.c defs.h\n    \t@echo cc -c main.c\n    kbd.o : kbd.c defs.h command.h\n    \t@echo cc -c kbd.c\n    commands.o : command.c defs.h command.h\n    \t@echo cc -c commands.c\n    display.o : display.c defs.h buffer.h\n    \t@echo cc -c display.c\n\n  make -k\n  GNU 4.4.1  cc -c main.c\n             make: *** No rule to make target 'kbd.c', needed by 'kbd.o'.\n             cc -c commands.c\n             cc -c display.c\n             make: Target 'edit' not remade because of errors.\n             exit 2\n  Ronin      ronin: 'kbd.c', needed by 'kbd.o', missing and no known rule to make it\n             exit 2\n\nThe exit status agrees and the refusal agrees. What differs is that GNU under `-k` builds the three objects it CAN build and only then gives up on `edit`, while Ronin refuses at graph-load time and builds nothing. That is the point of `-k`, and it is the whole of what the suite's dash-k script is for: \"make builds the rest of the dependencies even though it knows that at the end it will fail\".\n\nThis is a filesystem-effect difference — three files GNU writes and Ronin does not — so it is inside the contract. It is adjacent to, but not the same as, divergence #8 of docs/make-oracle-divergences.md (recursive keep-going choreography): there is no recursion here, only a missing prerequisite in one graph. The likely cause is that an unresolvable prerequisite is a load-time refusal rather than a per-edge failure the scheduler can route around under keep-going."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T12:30:22.735148645Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "RETIRED AS A RECORDED, OPERATOR-RULED DIVERGENCE. Filed on 2026-08-24 as a defect; ruled the same day, by the operator, to be Ronin's behaviour rather than a bug.\n\nOPERATOR DECISION, Brendan, 2026-08-24, on docs/make-oracle-divergences.md section 10, verbatim:\n\n    \"Ronin superior. Accepted divergence.\"\n\nAsked as a follow-up whether the node should be kept open or the behaviour kept, he answered KEEP the behaviour: fail fast is right, and refusing at graph-load when the goal provably cannot complete beats doing work that gets thrown away.\n\nRE-MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24 before this entry was written. Under `-k`, with `kbd.c` absent and no rule that makes it, GNU Make builds `main.o`, `commands.o` and `display.o` and only then abandons `edit`; Ronin refuses at graph load and builds none of them. Exit status agrees at 2 in both. Three fewer files, and none of the three was going to be used, because the goal that wanted them cannot be made.\n\nGATED, so a later change reopens the decision rather than passing quietly. `tests/make/keep-going-refuses-the-goal-before-building-what-it-can` records GNU's answer from the oracle — the three objects present, `edit` and `kbd.o` absent, outcome failure — and carries a `divergence` sidecar naming this ruling. `make_port` fails a recorded divergence that has been \"repaired\" as loudly as it fails an unrecorded one, so a change that starts building the three objects lands as a test failure pointing at this note. That takes the corpus from two divergence markers to three, which is the intended and reported movement.\n\nWHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not the same thing as section 8 of the survey, which is adjacent and has no recursion in it either but IS a defect: there the two tools write different files AND leave different exit statuses, the operator's ruling on it was conditional on behavioural equivalence, and the measurement came back negative. That one is filed as `make-recursive-keep-going-choreography-writes-different-files`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T12:42:22.932638022Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

