            {
                id an-interrupt-leaves-with-the-signal-that-caused-it
                title "Leave an interrupt with the signal that caused it, in both front ends"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-mode-leaves-an-interrupt-with-2-where-both-references-say-130 question-mode-answers-up-to-date-after-an-interrupt)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T12:29:23.50868123Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "OPERATOR DECISION, Brendan, 2026-08-24, on docs/make-oracle-divergences.md section 5 (\"An interrupt leaves Ninja's 130, not GNU's 128 + signal number\"), verbatim:\n\n    \"I think ronin should actually follow the GNU case even for ninja. It seems semantically smarter.\"\n\nAsked as a follow-up whether that meant re-raising in BOTH modes, accepting that it diverges from upstream Ninja, he answered yes.\n\nThis REVERSES what `[spec:ronin:req:product.build-outcome]` said in as many words — \"an interrupt leaves with Ninja's 130 rather than re-raising the signal, so the status does not depend on how far the build had got; C samurai re-raised here, and Ninja is the contract\" — and reverses the disposition `make-mode-leaves-an-interrupt-with-2-where-both-references-say-130` delivered on 2026-08-22 under that rule. The rule is now +1 and states the ruling; all 30 source references were re-read against the new body and re-pinned; nplan_spec_stale is empty.\n\nWHAT GNU ACTUALLY DOES, measured rather than assumed. `fatal_error_signal` (commands.c) sets the disposition back to SIG_DFL, kills the children, deletes the non-precious targets they were making, reaps them, removes the intermediates, and then `kill (make_pid (), sig)` — so the process dies of what it was sent and a shell reads 128 + the signal number. SIGQUIT is carved out in the source with its reason written down: \"We don't want to send ourselves SIGQUIT, because it will cause a core dump. Just exit instead.\" — `exit (MAKE_TROUBLE)`, which is 1.\n\nSo GNU's rule is NOT \"128 + signum\". It is \"die of the signal, except the one whose default action dumps core\". Following the GNU case means following it there too, which is why Ronin now leaves 1 for SIGQUIT rather than 131, and writes no core file. This is the one fact in the delivery that the operator's stated facts did not include, and it is called out in the report and in the survey so he can overrule it if he meant the arithmetic literally.\n\nMEASURED MATRIX, 2026-08-24, all through scripts/sandboxed, the signal sent to the tool's own pid and never to the process group, every tool exec'd through `perl -e '$SIG{$_}=\"DEFAULT\" for qw(INT QUIT HUP TERM); exec @ARGV'`. That reset is load-bearing: a background job inherits SIGINT and SIGQUIT ignored, GNU Make keeps an inherited ignore, and without the reset the SIGQUIT row measures an artefact. The first pass of this dispatch measured exactly that artefact and it was caught by reading GNU's source against the number.\n\n    cell                          GNU 4.4.1              Ronin before   Ronin after\n    SIGINT   mid-recipe           dies of sig 2  -> 130  exit 130       dies of sig 2  -> 130\n    SIGINT   read phase           dies of sig 2  -> 130  exit 130       dies of sig 2  -> 130\n    SIGINT   -q with a + line     dies of sig 2  -> 130  exit 130       dies of sig 2  -> 130\n    SIGTERM  mid-recipe           dies of sig 15 -> 143  exit 130       dies of sig 15 -> 143\n    SIGTERM  read phase           dies of sig 15 -> 143  exit 130       dies of sig 15 -> 143\n    SIGTERM  -q with a + line     dies of sig 15 -> 143  exit 130       dies of sig 15 -> 143\n    SIGHUP   mid-recipe           dies of sig 1  -> 129  exit 130       dies of sig 1  -> 129\n    SIGHUP   read phase           dies of sig 1  -> 129  exit 130       dies of sig 1  -> 129\n    SIGHUP   -q with a + line     dies of sig 1  -> 129  exit 130       dies of sig 1  -> 129\n    SIGQUIT  mid-recipe           exit 1, no core        exit 130       exit 1, no core\n    SIGQUIT  read phase           exit 1, no core        exit 130       exit 1, no core\n    SIGQUIT  -q with a + line     exit 1, no core        exit 130       exit 1, no core\n\nTwelve Make-mode cells, all twelve now agreeing with GNU exactly. Four Ninja-mode cells (mid-recipe, one per signal) follow the same rule, which is the ruled divergence from upstream Ninja: `ExitInterrupted` is hard-coded 130 at reference/ninja/src/exit_status.h:30 and returned at reference/ninja/src/ninja.cc:1679, subprocess-posix.cc installs handlers for SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGHUP only, and none of them is re-raised.\n\nTHE CHANGE. `Signal::reraise` existed from the C samurai heritage and had no caller. It is now `Signal::die_of`, carries the SIGQUIT carve-out, and is called from the two places `main` can leave by — the Ok path where the front end reported 130 as a status, and the Err path where the build reported the interrupt as an error. The Ok path is guarded on the reported status being INTERRUPTED_EXIT_CODE as well as on a signal having reached this process, so a recipe that exited 130 of its own accord and a build that finished before a stray signal arrived both keep the status they earned. Nothing inside the build engine moved: 130 is still what a stopped build reports as a value, and only the ending changed.\n\nWHAT WAS PRESERVED, checked case by case rather than assumed. tests/interrupts.rs still holds: the cut-short edge is not recorded in the build log (and a finished edge is, so the assertion is about the interrupt); partial outputs are removed; the target is withdrawn; .PRECIOUS is spared; no further recipe line is launched into the gap between two lines; an interrupted read abandons its $(shell) child rather than waiting for it, in under ten seconds, and starts no further shell function; an interrupted -q refuses to answer the affirmative zero and outranks an answer the walk had already reached; a recipe's own 130 is still 2; a recipe killed by a signal nobody sent the build is still 2; and a signalled + line under -q is still 2.\n\nTHE -q FACE. `question-mode-answers-up-to-date-after-an-interrupt` gated at 130 and now reads the signal instead. GNU's answer is unchanged and still matched: an interrupted question answers neither of the two things it could, and the number a shell sees for SIGINT is the same 130 it always was — it is now reached the way GNU reaches it. Under SIGTERM and SIGHUP the -q face moves with everything else, to 143 and 129, which is what GNU leaves there too (measured above, three cells).\n\nTESTS. `make_termination_leaves_ninjas_status` is renamed `make_termination_dies_of_the_signal` and now asserts 143; the rename is recorded in its own doc comment. Three cases added: `a_manifest_build_dies_of_the_signal_too` (the ruled divergence from upstream Ninja, stated in the front end where a reader could otherwise mistake it for Make emulation), `make_hangup_dies_of_the_signal` (129 — the third number, which SIGINT alone cannot distinguish because Ninja's fixed 130 and GNU's 128+2 are the same number), and `make_quit_leaves_the_trouble_status_without_dumping`. A shared `died_of` helper reads the wait status as a signal rather than as a number, because `code()` is None for a process a signal killed and only `signal()` tells 130-by-dying from 130-by-exiting."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T12:42:02.185445736Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T12:42:07.982849256Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id an-outputs-directory-is-left-to-the-recipe-in-make-mode
                title "Leave an output's directory to the recipe when run as make"
                kind @fix
                deps (make-an-output-directory-is-created-where-gnu-make-would-refuse)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T12:29:54.166820379Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "OPERATOR DECISION, Brendan, 2026-08-24, on docs/make-oracle-divergences.md section 7 (\"An output's directory is created where GNU Make would leave it to the recipe\"), verbatim:\n\n    \"Make's rule applies when run as make.\"\n\nThis REVERSES the retirement recorded on `make-an-output-directory-is-created-where-gnu-make-would-refuse` on 2026-08-19, which read in part: \"Decided: recorded intentional divergence. Ronin keeps creating an output's directory before the recipe runs, and this node is retired rather than fixed.\" That decision was taken by the delivering dispatch, grounded in the compiler-boundary audit, and it said so itself: \"there is no operator ruling on directory creation itself.\" There is now, and it goes the other way. An errata entry stands on that node pointing here.\n\nWHAT MOVED. In MAKE MODE nothing creates $@'s directory before the recipe runs. In NINJA MODE the launcher still creates it, unchanged — a manifest is generated and its generator is entitled to assume the directory is there, and the ninja conformance gate is the proof that this half did not move.\n\nMEASURED, before and after, all through scripts/sandboxed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-24. The survey's section 7 had recorded only the first shape. Three of four diverged, and two of those diverged SILENTLY — Ronin succeeding where GNU fails, which is the worse direction and the one a hand probe would not have looked for:\n\n  1. a recipe replaces a file with a directory of the same name (the survey's reproducer)\n       all: ; @echo \"all X=$(X)\"\n       afile/one.mk: ; @echo GEN; rm -f afile; mkdir afile; echo X=1 > afile/one.mk\n       include afile/one.mk\n     with `afile` an ordinary file.\n       GNU     GEN / all X=1 / exit 0\n       before  ronin: build stopped: File exists. + Makefile:3: afile/one.mk: Not a directory, exit 2\n       after   GEN / all X=1 / exit 0\n\n  2. an output in a directory nothing creates\n       all: sub/out\n       sub/out: ; @echo made > $@\n       GNU     exit 2, sub/out absent   (\"/bin/sh: cannot create sub/out: Directory nonexistent\")\n       before  exit 0, sub/out WRITTEN\n       after   exit 2, sub/out absent\n\n  3. the recipe makes its own directory\n       sub/out: ; @mkdir -p $(@D); echo made > $@\n       GNU / before / after all agree: exit 0, sub/out written\n\n  4. `-t` over an output in a directory nothing creates\n       GNU     exit 2, sub/out absent   (\"make: touch: open: sub/out: No such file or directory\")\n       before  exit 0, sub/out WRITTEN\n       after   exit 2, sub/out absent\n\nHOW, WITHOUT PUTTING MAKE PROVENANCE IN THE GRAPH. The 2026-08-19 retirement was right about the obstacle it named: plan/decisions/make-compiles-to-ninja.md and docs/make-compiler-boundary-audit.md forbid an edge property that exists only to make the executor behave differently because the graph came from a Makefile, and \"do not create my output's directory\" is not a Ninja graph idiom. Nothing was written into the graph. `BuildOptions::create_output_directories` is a RUN-LEVEL setting, sitting beside the answers the Make front end already gives in the same function — command_status_interrupts, recipe_signal_fails, archive_members, touch, always_make. What creates the directory is the launcher, so which launcher behaviour a run wants is the front end's to say once, at the boundary, rather than a property carried per edge. A graph loaded from a manifest still gets Ninja's launcher; a manifest Make mode emits still describes a Ninja build.\n\nTHE HALF THAT HAD TO MOVE WITH IT, and which the survey had not found. kati's ninja generator absorbs a leading, silent `mkdir -p` of exactly dirname($@) from a recipe (`is_output_mkdir`, src-rs/ninja.rs) — sound while Ninja is going to make that directory anyway, and a DELETED BUILD STEP once nothing does. `Flags::recipes_own_output_directories` turns the absorption off and src/make/cli.rs sets it; rkati, whose output is a manifest for Ninja to run, leaves it false. This was found by two tests/cli.rs cases going red — `recursive_evaluation_waits_for_parent_inputs` and `nested_recursive_evaluation_boundary` — whose makefiles write `@mkdir -p installed` and whose recipes had been running without that line all along, invisibly, because the launcher got there first. Diagnosed by narrowing: a two-line pattern recipe keeps both lines; the same recipe whose first line is `mkdir -p <the output's own directory>` kept only the second.\n\nWHAT RONIN'S OWN STAGING STILL DOES. A name the compiler invented for itself — the `.ronin_recipe_stage/N` proxy of a composed recipe's preceding segment — is not a Make output and never was. Its directory is still made, in `Builder::prepare_response_file`, because the tool that chose the path is the one that can place it, and a dry run still writes nothing for it. `is_virtual_output` keeps the two apart exactly as it did; the make_dirs loop skipped virtual outputs before this change and still does.\n\nGATES. Four oracle-recorded corpus fixtures were added and all four agree with GNU: an-outputs-directory-is-the-recipes-to-arrange, an-output-in-a-directory-nothing-creates-is-refused, a-touched-output-in-a-directory-nothing-creates-is-refused, a-recipes-own-mkdir-of-its-output-directory-runs. Recording rewrote no existing expectation. Two kati unit tests pin the absorption from both sides: a_redundant_output_mkdir_is_absorbed_for_ninja and a_recipes_own_output_mkdir_is_kept_where_it_owns_the_directory.\n\nIF THIS IS EVER REOPENED, the 2026-08-19 note's advice still stands and is worth keeping: the general question is who owns an output's directory for EVERY front end, and that is a plan/decisions-level question. What landed here is narrower and is what the operator asked for — Make's rule when run as make."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T12:38:13.736925389Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "GATE MOVEMENT, audited by name. Exactly one row of tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv moved, and it moved the right way:\n\n    -case  misc/general4.diff.4  unclassified  ninja-progress+evaluation\n    +case  misc/general4.diff.4  narration     ninja-progress\n\nCounts: 1285 recorded cases before and after; unclassified 58 -> 57; compiler 0 -> 0; interface 28 -> 28; narration 1199 -> 1200.\n\nWHICH CASE, established by running the suite scoped to `misc/general4` and reading the driver's own diff files rather than by counting `run_make_test` calls: `.diff.N` numbers the FAILING runs, so `.diff.4` is the fifth `run_make_test` in the script — \"Make sure that subdirectories built as prerequisites are actually handled properly... this time with '$'\":\n\n    all: dir/subdir/file.$$a\n    dir/subdir: ; @echo mkdir -p '$@'\n    dir/subdir/file.$$b: dir/subdir ; @echo touch '$@'\n    dir/subdir/%.$$a: dir/subdir/%.$$b ; @echo 'cp $< $@'\n\nexpecting three lines: `mkdir -p dir/subdir`, `touch dir/subdir/file.$b`, `cp dir/subdir/file.$b dir/subdir/file.$a`. Its diff now holds nothing but the three `[N/M]` progress lines beside the three expected ones, which is the `ninja-progress` family and the operator's 2026-08-17 narration ruling. Before, the diff also carried missing OUTPUT lines, which is what the `evaluation` residue was.\n\nWHY IT MOVED, and why an isolated probe of that makefile shows nothing. The driver runs every script's tests in ONE work directory, and the FIRST test in `misc/general4` is the same shape without the `$`: it also names `dir/subdir` and `dir/subdir/file.b`. Under the old behaviour Ronin's launcher created `dir/` and then `dir/subdir/` as real directories while running that first test, so by the time the fifth test ran, `dir/subdir` existed on disk with a fresh date, its `@echo mkdir -p` target was up to date, and the line did not run — two lines instead of three. Nothing creates those directories now, so the fifth test sees an empty tree and produces all three lines exactly as GNU does. Run the makefile on its own with `dir/` removed first and both tools agree either way, which is why this needed the suite's own state to reproduce and was measured through the suite rather than guessed at.\n\nThis is the second thing #7 was masking, after the two tests/cli.rs cases whose `@mkdir -p installed` line kati was absorbing. Both are the same class of finding: the launcher's directory creation was standing in for work the makefile asked for, so removing it made the makefile's own instructions visible again.\n\nNINJA CONFORMANCE: no movement, and the reason is worth writing down because movement was expected. 425/425 upstream tests, 10 differential CLI/tool cases, 92 build-outcome cases, 3 invocation-boundary cases, persistence and log content — every one unchanged. The output-directory change is Make-mode only by construction, so nothing there could move for #7; and the interrupt change (`an-interrupt-leaves-with-the-signal-that-caused-it`) could not move it either, because every signal case in that corpus sends the signal to a COMMAND (`kill -TERM $$$$`, the recipe shell's own pid) and never to the tool. `recorded_status_rejects_a_signalled_tool` states that outright: \"No shape here asks either tool to die by a signal.\" The new ending is conditioned on a signal reaching this process, so a build whose command died of SIGTERM still reports Ninja's 130 and still exits with it — which is what upstream Ninja does and what those 92 cases pin. The deliberate Ninja-mode divergence is therefore gated by `tests/interrupts.rs::a_manifest_build_dies_of_the_signal_too` and by nothing in the conformance corpus, which is why that case was added."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T12:42:12.598859664Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
