            {
                id make-recursive-keep-going-choreography-writes-different-files
                title "Reproduce per-child keep-going choreography across a composed recursive Make"
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T12:28:33.775574819Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the dispatch that delivered the operator's rulings on docs/make-oracle-divergences.md, 2026-08-24. It is section 8 of that survey, and it is filed as a DEFECT rather than ratified, because his ruling on it was CONDITIONAL and the condition failed.\n\nHIS WORDS, verbatim, 2026-08-24, on section 8: \"if it's behaviourally equivalent I don't care.\"\n\nSo it was measured rather than assumed. All eight DISCOVERY_ONLY_CASES were run through GNU Make 4.4.1 and Ronin on 2026-08-24 under scripts/sandboxed, mirroring the make_port harness exactly: copy the case into a scratch directory, run its `setup` under sh, invoke the tool through a make-named link with the case's `args` and `env`, then read back the exit status and every file the run left, by relative path and content.\n\n    case                                              exit          files\n    makeflags-keep-going-precedence                   GNU 0/Ronin 2 GNU wrote `went`; Ronin wrote `stopped`\n    makeflags-outranked-by-command-line               0/0           identical (8 entries)\n    makeflags-value-switch-precedence                 0/0           identical (7 entries)\n    makeflags-withdrawal-outranked-by-command-line    0/0           identical (6 entries)\n    phony-runs-though-the-file-is-current             0/0           identical (4 entries)\n    a-what-if-file-that-is-double-colon-...           2/2           identical by content — the §9 difference is WHEN the file was written, which this probe reads by content and the harness reads by mtime\n    dry-run-skips-a-plus-line                         0/0           GNU wrote `plus`  (that is §6, open and unruled)\n    dry-run-skips-a-make-reference-line               0/0           GNU wrote `named` (that is §6, open and unruled)\n\nThe keep-going cell differs in BOTH halves of his condition — a different file on disk AND a different exit status — so it is not behaviourally equivalent and his sentence does not cover it. The four other makeflags cases and the phony case ARE behaviourally identical and differ only in narration, which is what makes the failing cell a finding rather than a category.\n\nREPRODUCER, `tests/make/makeflags-keep-going-precedence`, run with `-k`:\n\n    Makefile:\n      all:\n      \t-@$(MAKE) -S -f stop.mk\n      \t-@$(MAKE) -k -f go.mk\n    stop.mk:\n      all: failing stopped\n      failing: ; @false\n      stopped: ; @echo reached > stopped\n    go.mk:\n      all: failing went\n      failing: ; @false\n      went: ; @echo reached > went\n\n  GNU 4.4.1  `went` written, `stopped` absent, exit 0 (both lines are `-@`-ignored)\n  Ronin      `stopped` written, `went` absent, exit 2\n\nA parent whose recipe is `-@$(MAKE) -S -f stop.mk` then `-@$(MAKE) -k -f go.mk` reaches two separate GNU processes: the first takes `-S` and stops at the first failure, the second takes `-k` and carries on to the target beside the failure. Ronin composes both sub-makes into one graph with one scheduler, so the `-S`/`-k` precedence across the composed boundary does not reproduce the per-process choreography, and the whole run takes the first setting it met.\n\nWHAT THIS COSTS TO FIX, honestly. It needs a per-child keep-going policy preserved across composition — scheduling composed sub-makes as if they were separate runners, which is the recursive-jobserver model `make-single-ninja-scheduler` deliberately replaced with one scheduler. That is why the survey recorded it as an architectural consequence and why the harness holds `makeflags-keep-going-precedence` as discovery-only. None of that makes it accepted; the operator has not accepted it, and the only thing he said about it was conditional on a measurement that came back negative.\n\nWHAT IS NOT CLAIMED HERE. This node does not propose the fix shape. It records that the divergence is real, reachable (a recursive build relying on different keep-going settings per child, under -k, builds different files and exits differently), and unruled."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-27T00:02:28.129317263Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "EVIDENCE ADDED FROM ANOTHER DIRECTION, 2026-08-27. Not fixed; the filing's cost estimate stands and is\nnow better supported, and the concept it needs turns out to explain a second symptom nobody had connected\nto it.\n\nMeasured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make while diagnosing\nmake-a-run-leaves-no-directory-the-suite-did-not-ask-for: a recipe line's `-` prefix does not reach a\nCOMPOSED child at all.\n\n    parent's line                       child                     GNU   Ronin\n    -@$(MAKE) -f child.mk               $(error d1 failed)        0     2\n     @$(MAKE) -f child.mk               $(error d1 failed)        2     2   agree\n    -@$(MAKE) -f child.mk               all:; @false              0     2\n    -@$(MAKE) -f child.mk               all: nope  (no rule)      0     2\n    -@false                             (no child at all)         0     0   agree\n\nThis node's own reproducer is written with `-@$(MAKE)` on both lines, so the exit-status half of its\ndivergence — GNU 0, Ronin 2 — is THIS and not the keep-going precedence. The FILE half (`went` against\n`stopped`) is the keep-going precedence and is separate. Two mechanisms in one cell, and they were being\nread as one.\n\nIt also aborts GNU Make's own `features/output-sync` script at line 331 (`t1: ; -@$(MAKE) -f $m1` over a\nchild holding `$(error)`), which is why that category reports 17 tests where GNU reports 18 — the driver\nstops the script rather than running one test fewer.\n\nTHE CONCEPT, named once for all three: a composed child is not a FAILURE DOMAIN. The parent's graph has no\nnotion of a sub-graph that stops at its own first failure, reports a status of its own, and hands that\nstatus to the recipe line that invoked it as if a process had exited. Given one, all three symptoms close:\nthis node's exit status, this node's per-child `-S`/`-k`, and the `-`-prefixed composed child.\n\nThe pieces, and none of them is speculative:\n  * a domain tag per edge — the subninja scope already is one, and `EdgeSpec::scope` carries it;\n  * a policy per domain — keep-going from the child invocation's own switches, ignore-failure from the\n    invoking recipe line's `-` prefix, which `SinkSubninja` does not carry today and would gain a field\n    for beside `preceding_ignore_errors`;\n  * a scheduler that charges a failure to its domain rather than to the run: the domain stops dispatching\n    unless its own policy says otherwise, and the run's failure budget is charged only where the domain's\n    status is not ignored.\n`graph::forgiven` is the nearest existing machinery and is NOT enough by itself — its own module says why:\n\"Without `-k` this changes nothing observable. The failure exhausts the allowed-failure budget, the build\nstops dispatching, and the released successor never starts.\"\n\nStill unruled and still a defect. What has changed is that it is now one design with three tests rather\nthan one architectural regret."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-27T14:42:23.10045228Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "MEASURED AGAIN 2026-08-27 by defect-batch-4, against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. Not fixed;\nthe shape the two earlier notes named still stands, and what this probe adds is a THIRD spelling of the\nsame gap and a narrowing of which half is actually missing.\n\nA THIRD SPELLING, which nobody had: `.IGNORE:` does not reach a composed child either.\n\n    parent                                child                       GNU  Ronin\n    -@$(MAKE) -f child.mk                 $(error d1 failed)           0    2   DIVERGES\n     @$(MAKE) -f child.mk                 $(error d1 failed)           2    2   agree\n    -@$(MAKE) -f child.mk                 all:; @false                 0    2   DIVERGES\n    -@$(MAKE) -f child.mk                 all: nope   (no rule)        0    2   DIVERGES\n    .IGNORE: +  @$(MAKE) -f child.mk      all:; @false                 0    2   DIVERGES   <- NEW\n    -@false                               (no child at all)            0    0   agree\n\nSo it is not only the recipe line's `-` prefix. Whatever carries \"this failure is not the run's\" has to\ncarry the makefile-wide spelling of the same thing, and `-i` with it.\n\nWHAT ALREADY WORKS, which makes the fix narrower than \"schedule composed sub-makes as separate runners\".\nWith child.mk = `all: a b` / `a:; @touch A; false` / `b:; @touch B`:\n\n    parent line                     GNU                  Ronin\n    -@$(MAKE) -f child.mk           rc 0, A only         rc 2, A only     FILES AGREE\n    -@$(MAKE) -k -f child.mk        rc 0, A and B        rc 2, A only     files diverge\n\nWithout `-k` the composed child already stops exactly where GNU's child PROCESS stops, and the whole of\nthe difference is the exit status. So the first half is not \"make the child stop\"; it is \"do not charge\nthe child's failure to the run\".\n\nWHAT IS STILL MISSING BESIDE IT, each measured:\n  * the parent's own following recipe line does not run —\n      all: / <tab>-@$(MAKE) -f child.mk / <tab>@touch AFTER\n      GNU rc 0 with A and AFTER; Ronin rc 2 with A alone.\n  * a target BESIDE the failing child does not run —\n      all: t1 t2 / t1: ; -@$(MAKE) -f child.mk / t2: ; @touch T2\n      GNU rc 0 with A and T2; Ronin rc 2 with A alone. So forgiving the failure is not enough on its own:\n      the run has to keep dispatching everything OUTSIDE the domain that failed.\n  * the child's own `-k` does not reach it, which is this node's original reproducer.\n\nTHE PIECES, costed against the code rather than sketched:\n  1. A domain tag per edge. The subninja scope already is one — `EdgeSpec::scope`, opened per composed\n     unit by `GraphSink::begin_subninja`.\n  2. A policy per domain: keep-going from the child's own switches, ignore-failure from the invoking\n     line's `-` or the invoking makefile's `.IGNORE:`. The `-` is NOT carried across the sink today and\n     is available where it would have to be: `SinkSubninja` (kati/src-rs/build_sink.rs) carries\n     `preceding_ignore_errors` for the lines AHEAD of the invocation and nothing for the invocation's own\n     line, while the `Command` whose `recursive_make` produced it has `dash_prefixed` right there — the\n     `flat_map` that builds `subninja_storage` in kati/src-rs/ninja.rs.\n  3. A scheduler that charges a failure to its domain rather than to the run: a failure inside a domain\n     with an ignoring policy stops dispatching THAT domain, releases the wrapper edge as made, and does\n     not count against `maxfail` or the exit status. `Graph::forgive_order` (src/graph/forgiven.rs) is\n     the nearest existing thing and covers only the wait, never the charge — its own module says so.\n\nNOT ATTEMPTED IN BATCH 4. Two ruled items were delivered there and this is a scheduler-core change whose\nblast radius is every recursive case in the conformance and build-intent corpora. It wants a dispatch of\nits own."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id remove-the-kati-extensions-from-the-product
                title "Remove the kati-only extensions from the product"
                kind @refactor
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T13:55:00.037009748Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "OPERATOR RULING, Brendan, 2026-08-24, verbatim:\n\n    \"fuck kati extensions\"\n\nClarified in a follow-up as: REMOVE the kati-only extensions from the product. Ronin implements GNU Make; the kati extensions are inherited surface nobody asked for.\n\nTHIS REVERSES A STANDING CAMPAIGN RULE. Until today the rule was \"kati extensions are deliberate product surface — never delete one under cover of a compatibility fix\". That rule was an AGENT-ERA CONVENTION, not his ruling: no operator ever said the extensions were product, and the convention existed to stop a dispatch quietly deleting surface while claiming to fix conformance. His ruling supersedes it. The convention's purpose survives in a narrower form and is worth stating, because it is what makes the reversal safe rather than a licence: an extension still may not be deleted as a SIDE EFFECT of a conformance fix. It is deleted deliberately, by name, with its corpus cases audited, under this node.\n\nWHAT IS AN EXTENSION HERE. Makefile text — or a switch — that reaches kati-only behaviour that GNU Make 4.4.1 does not have. Not: implementation markers a makefile cannot name (KATI_NEW_INPUTS and friends are compiler-invented shell variable names, and tests/make_regressions.rs already asserts they never reach a reader). Not: gaps where kati lacks something GNU has ($(guile ...), load/-load) — those are the opposite of extensions.\n\nREACHABILITY, established once. Ronin's Make front end drives kati's SHARED evaluator (src/make.rs:57, :810), so everything implemented in eval.rs / func.rs / dep.rs / var.rs / parser.rs is reachable from `ronin make`. What is NOT reachable is anything gated on a Flags field only kati::flags::Flags::from_args sets: Ronin builds Flags by hand and closes with ..Flags::default() (src/make/cli.rs:1365-1454), and refuses unknown long options outright (src/make/cli.rs:1139-1145, :1169-1186). So no --kati_* switch is product surface for Ronin's make.\n\nTHE SURVEY. docs/make-kati-extensions.md holds the full inventory, its dependents in this tree, and what each removal costs. It is the plan; this node is where the removals land."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T14:17:04.468395301Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T15:19:55.541622643Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "DELIVERED WHOLE. Every makefile-visible kati extension is gone from the product, in four commits, each with its corpus movement audited by name.\n\n  1. the twelve KATI_* builtin functions          kati 0b4b3c9 / ronin 573a453\n  2. the readonly family: .KATI_READONLY, $=,\n     .KATI_ALLOW_RULES, .KATI_SYMBOLS             kati 7731bd8 / ronin 5a08bfd\n  3. the KATI variable                            kati 8ea644e / ronin a825f2d\n  4. the six .KATI_* Ninja-edge target variables  kati cf3af0b / ronin 0ce81c1\n\nCONFORMANCE, the whole movement in one place. The vendored kati corpus goes 387 runs to 349; normalised 336 identical / 51 differing to 326 / 28; raw 286 identical to 276. THE IDENTICAL COUNT WENT DOWN BY TEN AND THE DIFFERING COUNT BY TWENTY-THREE, so the corpus got smaller and what is left agrees more often. Forty-three cases were deleted and every one was audited by name; twenty-three inventory rows left and EVERY ONE was class `extension`, family `kati-extension`, except `file_func.sh#script`, which was the last `artefact` case and left because the two tools started AGREEING. Nothing that was byte-identical stopped being so. The `kati-extension` family went 31 rows to 9, and the nine that remain are all kati-only SWITCHES rather than makefile syntax.\n\nFour cases were rewritten rather than deleted, because half of each was always about Make: foreach.mk (keeps GNU's $(foreach)), variables.mk (keeps .VARIABLES), ninja_regen.sh (keeps the regeneration test), file_func.sh + ninja_regen_filefunc_read.sh + shellstatus_in_rule.mk (all three lost an `ifdef KATI` gate that was standing in front of a GNU feature).\n\nWHAT WAS NOT REMOVED, and it is a judgement worth the operator's eye: every kati-only COMMAND-LINE SWITCH. Ronin's make builds its Flags by hand and refuses any long option it does not know, so --detect_depfiles, --regen, --use_find_emulator, --warn_*/--werror_*, --writable, --default_pool and the rest are unreachable from the product. They reach only rkati, which is the evaluator with nothing else on top and exists so the conformance gate can measure evaluation against GNU Make — the measurement apparatus rather than the product's surface. Removing them would take the apparatus with them. Written down in docs/make-kati-extensions.md so it can be overruled.\n\nTHE ONE CAPABILITY LOSS, said plainly rather than buried. `.KATI_DEPFILE` was the only makefile text in Make mode that put a depfile on an edge, so a Make-compiled graph now reaches Ninja's dependency log by no route a Makefile can ask for. A GNU makefile says this with `-include`, which Ronin compiles, and the user-visible property is unchanged except for the one-run lag that IS GNU Make's behaviour. tests/make_state.rs was rewritten to that idiom. It also DISSOLVED divergence #4 of docs/make-oracle-divergences.md, which existed only because of that extension.\n\nFULL GATE BATTERY, all green: fmt both crates; cargo check --workspace --all-targets; workspace tests 1092 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1199 cases with FIVE recorded divergences; conformance at the new baseline with no unclassified difference; equivalence 274 makefiles / 276 graphs / 56 make-rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 respellings; upstream inventory UNMOVED at 1285 rows, 57 unclassified / 0 COMPILER / 28 interface / 1200 narration, tsv byte-identical; ninja conformance 425/425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 92 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log-content; --no-default-features 836 passed / 0 failed; check-make-projects.sh all green, vim 9.2 in 18s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 31s at -j8 on a host carrying this session's own load; check-release.sh EXIT 0 with no uncovered rules, no stale references and lint clean."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T15:20:00.673750394Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-24T15:21:43.334420343Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "ARITHMETIC CORRECTION to the delivery note above and to ronin 0ce81c1's commit message. The vendored corpus ends at 354 RUNS, not 349.\n\nMeasured: raw 276 identical byte for byte / 78 differing; normalised 326 identical / 28 differing, so 354 runs. The last family deleted SEVEN cases but the run count falls by only TWO, because five of the seven are `ninja_*` scripts the make oracle never enumerates. The error was subtracting all seven from the run total.\n\nEvery other number in the delivery note is measured and stands. The whole movement, corrected, is now a table in docs/make-kati-extensions.md:\n\n  before                          387 runs  336 identical  51 differing  286 raw  31 kati-extension rows\n  after the twelve functions      365       324            41            275      20\n  after the readonly family       356       324            32            275      11\n  after the KATI variable         356       326            30            275      11\n  after the six edge variables    354       326            28            276       8\n\nForty-three cases deleted, all named; twenty-three inventory rows left, all class `extension` except file_func.sh#script, which was the last `artefact` case and left because the two tools started AGREEING."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-command-line-variables-value-is-settled-once
                title "Settle a command-line variable's value once, not twice"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T22:12:34.553352437Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-26 defect-batch-2 dispatch, on the coordinator's instruction to file what batch 1\nfound and did not take. MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-26, in three\nshapes, and the answer is the same each time: Ronin settles a command-line variable's value EXACTLY ONE\nTIME MORE than GNU Make does.\n\nThe value's expansion is observable because a `$(shell)` in it has an effect. Invoked as\n`make 'V=$(shell echo hit >> counted; echo x)'`, counting the lines of `counted` the run leaves:\n\n    the makefile never mentions $(V)          GNU 1   Ronin 2\n    the recipe reads $(V) once                GNU 2   Ronin 3\n    the recipe reads $(V) twice               GNU 3   Ronin 4\n\nAlways +1, never a doubling and never proportional to the number of references — so it is one extra settle\nof the definition and not an extra expansion per read. `$(V)` itself reads `x` on both sides in every\nshape, so nothing about the VALUE is wrong; what differs is how many times the command ran to produce it.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS. A command-line assignment whose value holds a `$(shell)` is ordinary — `make\n'CFLAGS=$(shell pkg-config --cflags foo)'` — and the extra run is a command the build makes that GNU does\nnot. Where the command has a side effect, as here, the extra run is an extra effect.\n\nADJACENT AND PROBABLY THE SAME GROUND: make-makeoverrides-reaches-a-recursive-invocation measured a second\ncommand-line-variable defect that also lives in how the definition is settled. GNU Make republishes\n`MAKEOVERRIDES` as the SETTLED variables — `quote_for_env(name)`, a `:` if the variable came out simple,\n`=`, then the value — while Ronin republishes the assignment as it was written:\n\n    command line        GNU              Ronin\n    FOO+=bar            FOO=bar          FOO+=bar\n    FOO?=bar            FOO=bar          FOO?=bar\n    FOO!=echo hi        FOO=hi           FOO!=echo\\ hi\n    FOO::=x             FOO:=x           FOO::=x\n    FOO:::=y            FOO=y            FOO:::=y\n    FOO=plain           FOO=plain        FOO=plain   (the only spelling that agrees)\n\nThe `!=` row is build intent on its own: republishing an unsettled `!=` means a CHILD runs the command\nagain, where GNU ran it once and passed the answer down. Doing it GNU's way requires each command-line\nassignment to be settled — expanded, or run — before `MAKEOVERRIDES` is rendered, which is the same\nquestion this node's counting exposes from the other end. Whoever takes this should expect one fix."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T23:22:01.736618399Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T23:51:41.49934544Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "FIXED, IN BOTH HALVES. The filing's counting held exactly; the sealed\nmake-makeoverrides-reaches-a-recursive-invocation handed this node its second half and that held too.\nAll measured against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make on 2026-08-27.\n\nWHAT THE PROBE CORRECTED. The filing said the two halves were \"probably the same ground\" and \"whoever\ntakes this should expect one fix\". They are NOT one fix — they are two, in two places, and each has its\nown teeth:\n  * the doubling is `src/make.rs`, where one settle expanded every command-line variable twice;\n  * the `MAKEOVERRIDES` rendering is the evaluator, where the table was published as argv wrote it.\nThey are one QUESTION — when is a command-line assignment settled — which is why they belong on one node,\nand the second is measurably not fixed by the first.\n\nHALF ONE: THE DOUBLING. `exported_environment` (src/make.rs:813) expands every exported variable, and a\ncommand-line variable is exported without being asked; `command_line_environment` (:814) then expanded\nevery command-line variable AGAIN for the descendant compiler's environment. It now reads the value the\nexport pass already settled and expands only a name that pass declined — one a shell could not read back,\nor one `unexport` took away. Counting the runs of a `$(shell)` in the value:\n\n    the makefile never mentions $(V)     GNU 1   before 2   after 1\n    the recipe reads $(V) once           GNU 2   before 3   after 2\n    the recipe reads $(V) twice          GNU 3   before 4   after 3\n\nHALF TWO: THE RENDERING. GNU Make writes `MAKEOVERRIDES` from the variable STORE — `quote_for_env(name)`,\na `:` where the variable came out simple, `=`, `quote_for_env(v->value)` (`define_makeflags`, main.c) — so\nan assignment reaches a child in the flavour and value it settled to. Nine spellings measured, all nine\nnow byte-identical where six differed:\n\n    command line      GNU          before            after\n    FOO=plain         FOO=plain    FOO=plain         FOO=plain\n    FOO=$(BAR)        FOO=$$(BAR)  FOO=$$(BAR)       FOO=$$(BAR)\n    FOO=a b           FOO=a\\ b     FOO=a\\ b          FOO=a\\ b\n    FOO=a\\b           FOO=a\\\\b     FOO=a\\\\b          FOO=a\\\\b\n    FOO:=$(BAR)       FOO:=        FOO:=$$(BAR)      FOO:=\n    FOO+=bar          FOO=bar      FOO+=bar          FOO=bar\n    FOO?=bar          FOO=bar      FOO?=bar          FOO=bar\n    FOO!=echo hi      FOO=hi       FOO!=echo\\ hi     FOO=hi\n    FOO::=x           FOO:=x       FOO::=x           FOO:=x\n    FOO:::=$(BAR)     FOO=         FOO:::=$$(BAR)    FOO=\n\n`$(FOO)` itself and `$(flavor FOO)` agreed with GNU in every row before and after; the whole difference was\nwhat a CHILD is told. The `!=` row is build intent: measured with a parent that runs a sub-make, GNU ran\nthe command once and passed the answer down, and Ronin ran it a second time in the child. Now once.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati 5830d7de6cb5af0f1c4d33b6f6d3873b1798e2b9, branch\nnplan/make-a-command-line-variables-value-is-settled-once, and the Ronin side in the same landing:\n  * `src/make.rs::command_line_environment` takes what the export pass settled and reuses it.\n  * kati `evaluate.rs::resettle_command_variables`, called where GNU Make renders the table — the command\n    line's variables entered, no makefile read — rewriting `-*-command-variables-*-`, which\n    `$(MAKEOVERRIDES)` and the `-- $(MAKEOVERRIDES)` suffix of `$(MAKEFLAGS)` are both made of.\n  * kati `eval.rs`: `Evaluator::command_line_names`, the order of first introduction. GNU Make keeps the\n    same record as a `command_variable` per distinct variable, prepended as it is introduced; it is not\n    recoverable from a variable table keyed by name, and the table is published in the reverse of it.\n  * kati `flags.rs`: `quote_for_makeflags` moves here from `src/make/cli/interface.rs`, which now calls\n    it. Two renderings of one table that quoted separately would drift.\nThe frontend still renders the table once from argv and must: `MAKEFLAGS` has to hold one while the\ncommand line is being read. Both spellings are the same table asked for at different moments, and both\ndoc comments now say so.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING EACH HALF SEPARATELY. Four oracle-recorded cases, two per half:\n  * doubling parked   → 2 of 1219 fail: `a-command-line-values-shell-runs-once-for-the-environment`\n    (`counted` holds two lines where GNU left one) and\n    `a-command-line-value-settles-once-more-for-each-read` (four where GNU left three).\n  * rendering parked  → 2 of 1219 fail: `makeoverrides-carries-the-settled-variables-not-the-operators`\n    (`E:=5 D:::=4 C::=3 B?=2 A+=1` where GNU left `E:=5 D=4 C:=3 B=2 A=1`) and\n    `a-command-line-shell-assignment-reaches-a-child-settled` (the child told `FOO!=./gen`, and `counted`\n    left with two lines rather than one).\nRecording rewrote no existing expectation — checked by `git status` over tests/make after the record.\n\nINVENTORY: no row moved and none was expected to. The suite's own command-line-variable rows\n(`variables/MAKEFLAGS.*`) were already narration or gone; the one that is still unclassified,\n`variables/MAKEFLAGS.diff.119`, belongs to\nmake-a-command-line-assignment-with-an-unterminated-reference-is-refused and turns on WHEN the settle\nhappens rather than how often. 1277 rows, 27 unclassified, 0 compiler, 20 interface, 1230 narration —\ntsv byte-identical.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT CLOSE, said plainly. GNU settles an exported command-line value once PER JOB IT\nLAUNCHES; Ronin settles it once per compilation unit. With the doubling gone the two agree wherever the\nrun launches exactly one job, which is every shape the filing measured, and still differ where it launches\nnone or several:\n\n    all: a b (two jobs)                 GNU 2   Ronin 1\n    all:; @:  (a recipe launching none) GNU 0   Ronin 1\n    all:; $(info good)                  GNU 0   Ronin 1\n\nThat is the question make-a-command-line-assignment-with-an-unterminated-reference-is-refused is about,\nand it is a different fix in a different place.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean both crates; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0;\nworkspace tests 1104 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1219 ported\ncases; conformance 354 runs, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274 makefiles / 276\ngraphs / 56 make-rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 respellings; upstream inventory unmoved; ninja conformance\n425/425 upstream, 10 differential, 92 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T23:53:33.987813081Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-run-leaves-no-directory-the-suite-did-not-ask-for
                title "Leave behind no directory GNU Make's own run does not"
                kind @fix
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T22:12:58.683752881Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-26 defect-batch-2 dispatch, on the coordinator's instruction to file what batch 1\nfound and did not take. MEASURED on 2026-08-26 with BOTH tools, in matched disposable worktrees of\nreference/gnumake at d66a65ad, running GNU Make's own `features/output-sync` script alone:\n\n    GNU Make 4.4.1   18 Tests in 1 Category Complete ... No Failures :-)\n                     leftovers in tests/: NONE\n    Ronin            17 Tests in 1 Category Failed\n                     leftovers in tests/: `bar`, a directory\n\nWHY IT IS NOT A NARRATION ROW. The script's own `output_sync_clean` does `rmfiles('foo/Makefile',\n'bar/Makefile', @syncfiles)` then `rmdir('foo')` and `rmdir('bar')`. `foo` goes and `bar` does not, so the\n`rmdir` failed — something was still in `bar` when the cleanup ran, and was gone by the time the directory\nwas listed afterwards. The suite's tests share one working directory, so a directory that outlives its own\nscript is state a LATER script runs on top of. Batch 1 traced three second-expansion rows that changed\nfamily but did not clear to exactly this contamination.\n\nThe test count differs too — 18 against 17 — which is worth a look at the same time: one case does not run\nat all under Ronin.\n\nWHERE TO START. The script runs several sub-makes in parallel under `-O`, synchronised through sentinel\nfiles in `../mksync.*`, with `bar/Makefile` written and removed per case. The likeliest shape is a child\nthat outlives the run that started it, still holding or writing inside `bar` after the parent has been\nreaped — the same family of question as the interrupt-path work, and it is why the timing looks like a race\nrather than a missing `rm`.\n\nNOT the same as a build leaving an output: what is left is a directory the suite created and expected to\nremove, so the fix is about when Ronin's children are finished with the tree, not about what a recipe\nwrites."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-26T23:58:55.22777276Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-27T00:02:09.657521152Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "MECHANISM FOUND, AND THE FILING'S GUESS WAS WRONG IN BOTH HALVES. Nothing races and no child outlives\nanything. Measured on 2026-08-27 against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, and reproduced in nine\nlines rather than through the suite.\n\nTHE LEFTOVER `bar` IS RONIN'S BUILD LOG. Caught directly, by listing the suite's own working directory\nwhile `scripts/check-make-upstream.sh` was mid-run:\n\n    target/make-upstream.*/gnumake/tests/bar/  →  .ninja_deps  .ninja_log\n    target/make-upstream.*/gnumake/tests/      →  .ninja_deps  .ninja_log  (the top level too)\n\nThe script's `output_sync_clean` does `rmfiles('foo/Makefile','bar/Makefile',@syncfiles)` then\n`rmdir('foo')` and `rmdir('bar')`. `rmdir` on a directory holding two dotfiles fails. `foo` went and `bar`\ndid not, which read like a race and is not one: the two dotfiles are not listed by an ordinary `ls`, so\nthe directory looked empty by the time anyone looked.\n\nWHY `bar` AND NOT `foo`, WHICH IS THE FINDING. The suite's makefile is\n\n    all: make-foo make-bar\n    make-foo: ; $(MAKE) -C foo\n    make-bar: ; #HELPER# -q sleep 1 ; $(MAKE) -C bar\n\n`make-foo`'s line is one static invocation and Ronin LIFTS it — the child is composed into the parent's\ngraph and there is one build, with one log, at the top. `make-bar`'s line has a `;` in front of the\ninvocation, so nothing can lift it, and a real `ronin make -C bar` process runs; that process opens its\nown persistence in the directory it was told to build in. Reduced to nine lines and measured both ways:\n\n    GNU 4.4.1   foo:[]  bar:[]                        top:[]\n    Ronin       foo:[]  bar:[.ninja_deps .ninja_log]  top:[.ninja_deps .ninja_log]\n\nSo the same recursive build leaves different files depending only on whether the compiler could lift the\nrecipe line. Also measured: the two files appear for a run that is already up to date, for a `-n`, and for\na makefile whose only target has no recipe — `Persistence::open` creates both before anything is decided.\n\nWHAT THAT MAKES IT. Ronin keeps build state where it builds, and GNU Make keeps none; `-C bar` means Make\nwas invoked in `bar`, so `bar` is where Ronin's convention puts it. That is the whole of incremental Make\nin this product and it cannot be repaired away by a dispatch. TWO THINGS ARE FOR THE OPERATOR HERE and\nboth are recorded rather than parked:\n  * Does `ronin make` write build state into the directory it builds in? Today yes, by design, and it is\n    what `-t`, restat and command-change detection are read from. Accept or not is his.\n  * If yes, is it acceptable that composition decides WHETHER a subdirectory gets it? Composition is meant\n    to be invisible and here it is not. Making it consistent the other way — a nested child not keeping a\n    log — costs that child its incrementality, which is worse.\n\nTHE OTHER HALF, `18 TESTS AGAINST 17`, IS NOT ABOUT DIRECTORIES AT ALL AND IS A REAL DEFECT. The driver\ndoes not run one test fewer; it ABORTS. From the run's own log:\n\n    features/output-sync: Error running .../bin/make (expected 0; got 512)\n    scripts/features/output-sync:331: .../bin/make -f work/features/output-sync.mk.12 -j -Oline\n\nLine 331 is `all: t1` / `t1: ; -@$(MAKE) -f $m1` over an `m1` holding `$(error d1 failed)`. GNU exits 0 —\nthe `-` on the recipe line ignores the child's status — and Ronin exits 2. Measured over five shapes:\n\n    parent's line                       child                     GNU   Ronin\n    -@$(MAKE) -f child.mk               $(error d1 failed)        0     2\n     @$(MAKE) -f child.mk               $(error d1 failed)        2     2   agree\n    -@$(MAKE) -f child.mk               all:; @false              0     2\n    -@$(MAKE) -f child.mk               all: nope  (no rule)      0     2\n    -@false                             (no child at all)         0     0   agree\n\nSo the `-` prefix reaches a plain command and does NOT reach a composed child. Three of five diverge, and\nthe exit status is the whole of the difference in each.\n\nTHIS IS THE SAME MISSING CONCEPT AS make-recursive-keep-going-choreography-writes-different-files, which\nis why it is written down here rather than filed again. A composed child is not a failure domain: the\nparent's graph has no notion of a sub-graph that stops at its own first failure, reports a status of its\nown, and hands that status to the recipe line that invoked it as if a process had exited. Give it one and\nthree symptoms close together — this row's abort, the `-` prefix over composition, and that node's\nper-child `-S`/`-k`. The pieces are: a domain tag per edge (the subninja scope already is one), a policy\nper domain (keep-going from the child's own switches, ignore-failure from the invoking line's `-`), and a\nscheduler that charges a failure to its domain rather than to the run. `forgiven_order` is the nearest\nexisting thing and is not enough on its own — its own module says so: \"Without `-k` this changes nothing\nobservable. The failure exhausts the allowed-failure budget, the build stops dispatching.\"\n\nNOT ATTEMPTED HERE. It is a scheduler-core change and this dispatch had the full battery still to run.\nNothing about the directory half is fixed either, because nothing about it is a dispatch's to fix."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-a-shared-rules-outputs-are-each-swept-on-their-own-answer
                title "Decide per output whether a multi-target rule's file is swept"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-27T00:08:49.20282345Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the 2026-08-27 defect-batch-3 dispatch, found by chasing the OTHER leftover\nmake-second-expansion-sees-target-and-pattern-specific-variables reported and nobody had measured:\n\"`features/patternrules` leaves `a.15` and `a.2`\". MEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make\non 2026-08-27, first through the suite in matched disposable worktrees and then reduced to four makefiles.\n\n    features/patternrules, alone, both tools, matched worktrees\n      GNU 4.4.1   72 Tests Complete ... No Failures     leftovers in tests/: none\n      Ronin       72 Tests Failed                       leftovers in tests/: a.15, a.2\n\nFOUR CELLS, and Ronin errs in BOTH directions — it deletes a file GNU keeps and keeps two GNU deletes:\n\n    subtest 3   a.4: / %.4: %.1 %.15 a.3 / %.1 %.15 %.3: ; touch $*.1 $*.15 $*.3\n                GNU  a.3 a.4      Ronin  a.4              Ronin DELETED a.3\n    subtest 4   the same with `%.3 %.1 %.15:`\n                GNU  a.3 a.4      Ronin  a.4              Ronin DELETED a.3\n    subtest 5   a.4: / %.4: a.3 %.1 %.15 / %.1 %.15 %.3: ; touch $*.1 $*.15 $*.3\n                GNU  a.3 a.4      Ronin  a.1 a.15 a.3 a.4 Ronin KEPT a.1 and a.15\n    subtest 6   a.3: / %.3: %.1 %.2 / %.1 %.2: ; touch $*.1 $*.2 / install: a.1 / .PHONY: install\n                GNU  a.1 a.3      Ronin  a.1 a.2 a.3      Ronin KEPT a.2\n\nSubtests 3 and 5 differ only in where `a.3` sits in the prerequisite list, and Ronin answers them\ndifferently. That order-dependence is the tell.\n\nMECHANISM, traced rather than guessed. GNU decides intermediacy PER FILE:\n`f->intermediate |= !f->is_explicit && !f->notintermediate` (implicit.c:1014), and a name written without a\n`%` in a pattern rule's prerequisite list counts as mentioned — the suite says so in its own words a few\ntests later, \"A target explicitly listed as a prerequisite of a pattern rule, is still considered mentioned\nand 'ought to exist'\". So `a.3` is not intermediate and `a.1`/`a.15` are, whatever order they were written\nin.\n\nkati has the right per-name answer and throws it away. `pick_rule` (dep.rs) puts the other target patterns'\nnames into ONE DepNode — `self.done.insert(sym, n.clone())`, `implicit_outputs.push(sym)`,\n`peer_outputs.push(sym)` — and `build_plan` asks `treat_as_intermediate` only for the name it is planning,\nwhich is whichever of them the walk reached FIRST. `build_plan`'s memoised branch un-peers a name reached\nin its own right (`peer_outputs.retain(...)`) and does not ask that name's own question. `SinkEdge` then\ncarries one `disposable` bool for the edge and `Plan::disposable_outputs` (src/build.rs) sweeps every\noutput of a disposable edge that is not still a peer.\n\n  subtest 3/4  `a.1` reached first, intermediate → edge disposable → `a.3` swept with it.\n  subtest 5    `a.3` reached first, mentioned → edge not disposable → `a.1`/`a.15` survive.\n  subtest 6    `a.1` reached first, mentioned by `install:` → edge not disposable → `a.2` survives.\nAll three explained by one bit read off the wrong name.\n\nNOT THE SAME AS make-pattern-peer-is-not-intermediate, which is Done and correct: that one is about a name\nNEVER reached in its own right, which GNU marks `is_target` in `also_make` so it is not intermediate at all.\nThis is the complement — names that ARE each reached, sharing one edge, whose answers differ from each\nother.\n\nTHE FIX SHAPE, which that node's own note already named and half-took: carry the disposable names PER\nOUTPUT rather than one flag per edge, \"the way `delete_on_error_outputs` already does\". `SinkEdge` already\nhas `withdrawable_outputs: &[Symbol]` as the precedent, with the argument written out beside it — \"The\nnames rather than a flag, because the exclusions are per output and not per edge\".\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE FOUR CELLS: `a.15` and `a.2` outliving their script is state every LATER script\nin GNU Make's suite then runs on top of, which is half of why six rows of the upstream residue are\nunclassified with no honest family (docs/make-upstream-residue.md records the seven-row table)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-27T00:08:55.321365011Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-27T00:25:44.901312162Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "FIXED, in the half that decides what is DELETED. The half that decides what is REMADE is the same\n\"per file, not per edge\" question and is left, named and measured, below.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT — kati (branch nplan/make-a-shared-rules-outputs-are-each-swept-on-their-own-answer)\nand the Ronin side in the same landing:\n  * kati `dep.rs`: `DepNode::disposable_outputs`, every name this action writes that the build may sweep.\n    Seeded with the node's own name in `DepNode::new`, and added to in `build_plan`'s memoised branch — the\n    one that un-peers a name reached in its own right — which is exactly the moment GNU Make's\n    `!is_explicit` question becomes askable about that name. `disposable_name` factors the answer out of\n    the `DepNode::new` call site so both places ask it the same way.\n  * kati `dep.rs`: `keep_precious_intermediates` and `plan_root` narrow the list as well as the flag, so a\n    `.PRECIOUS` name and a goal are taken out of it by name.\n  * kati `build_sink.rs`, `ninja.rs`: `SinkEdge::disposable` becomes `disposable_outputs: &[Symbol]`,\n    beside `withdrawable_outputs` and for the reason written there — \"The names rather than a flag,\n    because the exclusions are per output and not per edge\".\n  * `src/graph/peer.rs`, `src/frontend.rs`, `src/make/sink.rs`: a per-node `disposable_outputs` set on the\n    graph, filled by `BuildGraph::set_disposable_outputs`, replacing `EdgeSpec::disposable` and\n    `Edge::disposable` outright.\n  * `src/build.rs::sweep_disposable` asks the OUTPUT. The old `peer_outputs` guard went with the flag: a\n    name that is still a peer is not in the set and never was, which is GNU Make stamping `is_target` on\n    an `also_make` entry.\n\nSEVEN CELLS, all seven now byte-identical with GNU Make 4.4.1 where three diverged:\n\n    a.4: / %.4: %.1 %.15 a.3 / %.1 %.15 %.3:        GNU a.3 a.4   before a.4               after a.3 a.4\n    a.4: / %.4: %.1 %.15 a.3 / %.3 %.1 %.15:        GNU a.3 a.4   before a.4               after a.3 a.4\n    a.4: / %.4: a.3 %.1 %.15 / %.1 %.15 %.3:        GNU a.3 a.4   before a.1 a.15 a.3 a.4  after a.3 a.4\n    a.3: / %.3: %.1 %.2 / install: a.1              GNU a.1 a.3   before a.1 a.2 a.3       after a.1 a.3\n    a.4: / %.4: %.1 %.15 / %.1 %.15:                GNU a.4       both a.4\n    a.4: / %.4: %.1 / %.1:                          GNU a.4       both a.4\n    a.4: / %.4: %.1 %.15 a.3 / %.1 %.15: / %.3:     GNU a.3 a.4   both a.3 a.4\nRonin erred in BOTH directions before: it deleted `a.3`, a name the makefile mentioned, and it kept `a.1`,\n`a.15` and `a.2`, three the search invented.\n\nAND THE SUITE'S OWN LEFTOVER IS GONE. `features/patternrules` alone, both tools, matched worktrees:\n  before  GNU leaves nothing beside the driver's `work/`; Ronin leaves `a.15`, `a.2`\n  after   Ronin leaves nothing beside `work/` and its own `.ninja_log`/`.ninja_deps`\nThe two logs are make-a-run-leaves-no-directory-the-suite-did-not-ask-for's product-scope question and are\nnot this node's.\n\nA TEST THAT ENCODED THE APPROXIMATION WAS CORRECTED, with the evidence.\n`src/make/equivalence.rs::peers_reach_only_the_direct_graph` asserted that the disposable list held BOTH\n`hello.w` and `hello.z`, and its own message said why — \"the edge is disposable; sparing the peer is the\nbuild's reading of this list\". The list was the edge's and the build narrowed it. It is now the outputs'\nand holds `hello.z` alone. The build's answer is unchanged and is still gated by\n`tests/make/multi-target-pattern-peer-is-not-intermediate`: `hello.z` swept, `hello.w` kept.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX: 3 of 1222 ported cases fail —\n`a-mentioned-name-survives-a-rule-whose-peers-do-not` ('a.15' produced where GNU produced none),\n`a-mentioned-name-survives-wherever-it-was-written` ('a.1'), and\n`a-peer-goes-though-the-name-beside-it-was-mentioned` ('a.2'). Recording rewrote no existing expectation.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT, and it is the same question read the other way. Intermediacy has a second job in GNU Make:\nan intermediate that is not there is no reason to remake what reads it. Ronin carries THAT per edge too\n(`Edge::intermediate`, `record_absent_intermediate`, `stand_in_for_an_intermediate`), so a mixed action —\none mentioned name beside two invented ones — takes the mentioned name's answer for all three. Measured\nby running the same case twice:\n\n    a.4: / %.4: a.3 %.1 %.15 / %.1 %.15 %.3:\n      run 1  GNU a.3 a.4          Ronin a.3 a.4        agree\n      run 2  GNU \"'a.4' is up to date.\"                Ronin re-runs both recipes\n    a.3: / %.3: %.1 %.2 / install: a.1\n      run 2  GNU \"'a.3' is up to date.\"                Ronin re-runs both recipes\n\nSaid plainly, because this landing is what exposed it: before the fix the second of those two shapes\nagreed on run 2 BY ACCIDENT — nothing had been swept, so nothing was missing. The files each run leaves\nare the same either way and the recipes Ronin runs on a second pass are not. The fix shape is the one\ntaken here, applied to the other flag: a per-node intermediate set, and an output that is intermediate and\nabsent contributing no MISSING to the freshness comparison. It is larger than this half because\n`intermediate` feeds `record_absent_intermediate`, `stand_in_for_an_intermediate` and\n`push_intermediates`, each of which quantifies over the whole edge today, and each of which has corpus\ncases of its own.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed): fmt clean both crates; check --workspace --all-targets EXIT 0;\nworkspace tests 1104 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored; check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over 1222 ported\ncases; conformance 354 runs, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — unchanged; equivalence 274 makefiles / 276\ngraphs / 56 make-rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 respellings; upstream inventory unmoved at 1277 rows, 27\nunclassified / 0 compiler / 20 interface / 1230 narration, tsv byte-identical — the six contamination rows\nof docs/make-upstream-residue.md's table are held by `bar/` rather than by `a.15`, so clearing this one\ndoes not move them."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-27T00:32:26.336278837Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id make-mode-keeps-no-build-state-beside-the-build
                title "Stop writing Ninja's two logs into the directory Make builds in"
                kind @fix
                implements {
                    target-impl ([spec:ronin:req:make.state-outside-the-tree])
                }
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-27T14:46:06.674261874Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-27T14:49:52.931379397Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "DELIVERED ON THE OPERATOR'S RULING (Brendan, 2026-08-27): `ronin make` writes no `.ninja_log` and no\n`.ninja_deps` into the directory it builds in. Ninja mode is untouched — those two files are Ninja's own\ncontract, and the manifest front end still opens, reads and appends both.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE THE RULING RESTS ON, VERIFIED BEFORE ANY CODE MOVED. A lifted (composed) sub-make already\nleaves no log in its subdirectory and builds correctly, so the log is not load-bearing in Make mode. With\n`make-foo`'s line liftable and `make-bar`'s not (a `;` in front of the invocation):\n\n    before   top/[.ninja_deps .ninja_log]   foo/[]   bar/[.ninja_deps .ninja_log]\n    after    top/[]                         foo/[]   bar/[]\n    GNU      top/[]                         foo/[]   bar/[]\n\nTHE TWO KNOWN READERS, PROBED RATHER THAN ASSUMED — neither reads either file, and neither had to be\ngiven anything to replace it.\n  * `-t` reads nothing. `Builder::touch_outputs` (src/build/touch.rs) stats the file and touches it;\n    nothing on that path consults a log. Measured: `make -t` over a fresh tree touches the target and the\n    run after it says there is no work to do, with no log ever written.\n  * restat / `outputs_reobserved` reads nothing either. `Builder::reobserves` (src/build/freshness.rs)\n    compares the mtimes an edge's own outputs hold AFTER its command ran against the moment the command\n    started — one build, off the disk, no history.\n  * And the two things the log DOES hold were already out of Make's freshness before this change:\n    `FreshnessHistory::FilesystemOnly` keeps `log_mtime` out of the comparison (src/graph.rs), and the\n    `generator` binding every Make rule carries keeps the command hash out of it\n    (`refresh_command_hash`, src/build/command.rs). Measured directly: deleting both files between two\n    builds changes nothing — the second still reports no work to do.\n  * `.ninja_deps` was unreachable from makefile text already. `.KATI_DEPFILE` went on the 2026-08-24\n    ruling, and the only remaining route to a `depfile`/`deps` binding is kati's `--detect_depfiles`,\n    which `ronin make` refuses: `ronin: unrecognized option '--detect_depfiles'`, exit 2.\nSo nothing in Make mode needed either file and no defect had to be filed against the ruling.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT. `Persistence` (src/frontend/execute.rs) holds `Option`s and gains\n`Persistence::none()`; both Make-mode openings use it — src/make/cli.rs for the goal build and\nsrc/make/cli/remake.rs for the makefile update. The engine already took `Option<&mut BuildLog>` /\n`Option<&mut DepsLog>` and skips both the record and the hydration when they are absent, so the absence is\nread as an absence rather than as an empty log: no entry is written and no previous run's opinion is read.\n`CompiledInvocation::directory` went with it — it existed only so the run could open persistence where it\nbuilt. The one Ninja-only caller that needs both logs (the `-t` log tools, src/cli.rs) goes through\n`Persistence::logs_mut`. kati 9cdbc1f7acfd94a2ca39b90a24d18f89983e103c, branch\nnplan/make-mode-keeps-no-build-state-beside-the-build, carries the emitted graph's side of the rule.\n\nSPEC: `make.state-outside-the-tree` +2 → +3, inverted. It used to say a Makefile-derived graph uses\nNinja's placement and that \"front-end provenance does not ... suppress state beside the build\". It now\nsays Make mode keeps none, and keeps the `generator` half unchanged and explicit — an EMITTED manifest is\nbuilt by stock Ninja against Ninja's own state, and reaches the same verdict because the control is on the\ngraph rather than in the front end. Every pinned reference moved with it.\n\nA TEST THAT ENCODED THE OLD BEHAVIOUR, corrected with its evidence. `tests/lint.rs`'s\n`a_remade_include_recurses_here` gated \"the child was one of ours\" on finding a `.ninja_log` in the child's\nown directory. The case already writes down the `$(MAKE)` the child was reached by — an absolute path\nending in the staged `make` link, which only this Make hands a child — so the assertion moved onto that and\nthe doc comment says so. `tests/make_regressions.rs`'s `symlink_freshness_ignores_old_build_log` was named\nafter a file that no longer exists; it is now `symlink_freshness_reads_the_filesystem_alone` and its\ncomment says what the property actually is.\n\nTEETH, MEASURED BY PARKING THE FIX — the two Make-mode openings put back and everything else left alone.\nSIX of the eight `tests/make_state.rs` cases fail:\n  a_make_build_leaves_only_what_it_made        the two dotfiles are back in the listing\n  a_build_with_nothing_to_do_writes_nothing    `[]` left something behind, for a run that had none to do\n  a_recursive_child_leaves_no_state_either     `bar/` holds them again where `foo/` never did\n  a_build_writes_where_it_was_invoked          the `-f`-elsewhere working directory holds them\n  equivalent_frontends_execute_identically     `!make_tree.join(\".ninja_log\").exists()` fails\n  concurrent_builds_settle_on_the_filesystem   same, after eight racing builds\nThe other two pass parked and unparked, which is the point: incrementality does not depend on the log.\nmake_port stays 4/4 with the fix parked — build intent never depended on it either, which is why this had\nto be gated by what the directory holds.\n\nINVENTORY MOVED, and every moved row is audited by name against the pre-run tsv. 1277 cases before and\nafter; NOTHING left the corpus and nothing new entered it. unclassified 27 → 19, compiler 0 → 0,\ninterface 20 → 20, narration 1230 → 1238.\n\n  EIGHT rows unclassified → narration, all of them losing an `evaluation` residue:\n    features/patspecific_vars.diff.5   ninja-progress+evaluation      → ninja-progress\n    features/patspecific_vars.diff.6   ninja-progress+evaluation      → ninja-progress\n    features/se_explicit.diff.3        ninja-progress+evaluation      → ninja-progress\n    features/se_implicit.diff.1        ninja-progress+evaluation      → ninja-progress\n    features/se_statpat.diff.1         ninja-progress+evaluation      → ninja-progress\n    targets/DEFAULT.diff               no-work-line+directory-announce+evaluation\n                                                                      → ninja-progress+directory-announce\n    variables/automatic.diff.7         no-work-line+evaluation        → ninja-progress\n    variables/private.diff.7           ninja-progress+evaluation      → ninja-progress\n  FOUR already-narration rows lost a `recipe-echo` residue: features/se_explicit.diff.2,\n  features/se_implicit.diff, features/se_implicit.diff.6, variables/MAKECMDGOALS.diff.2.\n  ONE stayed unclassified with less residue: options/dash-n.diff.5 lost `no-work-line`.\n\nWHY THOSE ROWS AND NOT OTHERS, said rather than assumed. The suite's scripts share one working directory.\n`features/output-sync`'s own `output_sync_clean` does `rmfiles(...)` then `rmdir('foo')` and `rmdir('bar')`;\n`rmdir` on a directory holding two dotfiles fails, so `bar` outlived its own script and every later script\nran on top of it. The second-expansion family (se_explicit, se_implicit, se_statpat), patspecific_vars,\nDEFAULT, automatic and private are exactly the later scripts, and their residue was `evaluation` — \"exact\noutput cannot distinguish graph intent from runner behavior\". With the leftover gone they classify. The\n`no-work-line` → `ninja-progress` moves are the same thing seen from the other side: a script that found\nfiles a previous script had left said there was nothing to do, and now does the work it was written to do.\nMeasured, not inferred: a second full run of check-make-upstream.sh over the same tree produced a\nbyte-identical tsv and exits 0 against it, so this is the fix and not suite non-determinism.\n`variables/MAKEFLAGS.diff.119` is NOT here and must not be: it belongs to\nmake-a-command-line-assignment-with-an-unterminated-reference-is-refused and moves with that node.\n\nGATES (serial, through scripts/sandboxed, on this node's slice alone): fmt clean both crates; check\n--workspace --all-targets EXIT 0; workspace tests 1104 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored (the two new\nmake_state cases are inside that count, which was 1104 before with two fewer state cases and two\nnow-removed elsewhere — `nplan check` reports the suite green); check-lints.sh EXIT 0; make_port 4/4 over\n1231 cases; conformance 354 runs, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw — UNCHANGED; equivalence 274 makefiles / 276\ngraphs / 56 make-rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 respellings; check-make-projects.sh all — vim 9.2 and zsh\n5.9.2 both built from their own Makefiles; upstream inventory as above, classifier EXIT 0."
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-08T03:10:23.934566535Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Five nodes worked in parallel in isolated worktrees on 2026-08-08 and merged as one wave: make-second-expansion-patterns, make-option-output-sync, make-option-touch-and-what-if, make-jobserver-explicit-under-parent, make-option-debug-and-trace + make-option-shuffle.\n\nThe merged classification, measured after the merge rather than summed from the lanes — each lane measured against the same 767-semantic baseline, so their movements overlap and adding them would be wrong:\n\n                 before   after\n  capability       126      54\n  option-refused    93      20\n  narration        439     522\n  semantic         767     772\n  counted        1,332   1,348\n\nSemantic is up five and that is the honest number, but it is not the reading. 72 capability refusals were removed, which makes previously uncountable cases countable and lands most of them in semantic; against that, second expansion moved 65 cases from semantic to narration. `no-rule-to-make` fell 105 → 45 and `unsupported-feature` to 35, both from second expansion.\n\nThe real-project gate on a quiet host: Ronin 21s against GNU Make's 20s on Ninja's own CMake-generated Makefile at -j8. It was 64.4s against 22.9s on 2026-08-07.\n\nThree lanes found and fixed defects outside their own node, which is the argument for running them concurrently rather than serially: `|` did not end the word it fell in, so `%.o: %.c|bar.h` silently fell through to the built-in cc rule; `-O` arriving in an inherited MAKEFLAGS was read letter by letter and silently turned on -e and -r; and `-t` was undone by the next build, because an output with no build-log entry is dirty here.\n\nThree lanes also found the recorded defect on their node incomplete or wrong and said so. make-second-expansion-patterns named one of five causes, and the largest — that a prerequisite list should be deferred only when it still holds a `$`, as GNU's read.c does — was not among them.\n\nThe cost was contention on src/make/cli.rs, which four lanes edited. It ended three functions over the density gate; the extraction into src/make/report.rs (ea942c5) cleared it. One wave of this size is about the limit for one shared file.\n\nHandoffs recorded by the lanes and not yet noded: `$(MAKEFLAGS)` cannot report this build's own budget, because the fifo does not exist until after evaluation; `announcing()` implements only the -C half of GNU's should_print_dir, missing `makelevel > 0`; the directory announcement carries no `[N]` level; kati marks a recipe recursive only for a leading `+` and not for `$(MAKE)`; `*** No targets.` fires when only pattern rules exist even with a goal named; `--debug=i`/`v` narrate the dependency search and need kati; and SinkEdge::loc is now read, which recipe-error-line (78 cases) wants next."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-exit-status-parity
        title "Exit with the status Ninja exits with when a command fails"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-09T17:25:12.172102097Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Opened from the dangling referral on ronin-command-launcher's 2026-08-04 note: Ronin exits 1 where Ninja propagates the failing command's exit status. The claim about Ninja's behaviour must be verified against real Ninja before matching it — the oracle is Ninja, not the note. The failure-surface conformance scenarios from ronin-failure-surface-parity are the natural place for exit-status assertions."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-09T18:16:09.775678397Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Exit-status parity: the referral was stale, and the real defect was the interrupt.\n\nThe provenance claim — ronin-command-launcher's \"Ronin exits 1 when a command fails where Ninja exits with the command's own status\" — is REJECTED ON MEASUREMENT. It was true when written on 2026-08-03 and fixed hours later by ronin-failure-surface-parity, whose own note opens its defect list with it. The referral was never retracted, so it outlived its subject by six days. Pinned Ninja b51a1e3 does propagate the status (subprocess-posix.cc ParseExitStatus returns WEXITSTATUS transparently; build_result.h carries it out through BuildResult::exit_status), and Ronin already did the same before this node: `exit 7` was 7 on both sides, and compare_build_case had been comparing rc all along.\n\nProbing 66 shapes found three real divergences, none of them the one referred. All three are fixed.\n\nThe first was predicted, in writing, by ronin-failure-surface-parity: \"Ninja returns immediately when it cannot finish a command, where Ronin counts it as a keep-going failure and carries on. I could not produce a case where the two differ — the obvious one, a missing depfile under -k 0, behaves identically in both — so I did not restructure the scheduler on a hypothesis.\" The case exists; it is an interrupt rather than a finish failure. That note's discipline is why this one found it.\n\nPROBE TABLE (ninja / ronin, both after the fix; \"was\" is what Ronin did before it)\n\n  Direct command status\n    exit 0 (control)                      0 / 0\n    exit 1                                1 / 1\n    exit 3                                3 / 3\n    exit 111                          111 / 111\n    exit 255                          255 / 255\n    exit 130                          130 / 130   was 130, but with a FAILED: line and no early stop\n    command not found                 127 / 127\n\n  Signal-killed command (raw wait status + 128, truncated to & 0xff on exit)\n    SIGSEGV                             11 / 11   (FAILED: [code=267])\n    SIGBUS                                7 / 7\n    SIGQUIT                               3 / 3   (FAILED: [code=259])\n    SIGKILL                           137 / 137\n    SIGINT                            130 / 130\n    SIGTERM                           130 / 130\n    SIGHUP                            130 / 130\n\n  Multiple failures\n    exit 3 then exit 5, -k 0              5 / 5   (last non-zero wins)\n    exit 5 then exit 3, -k 0              3 / 3\n    exit 3 then exit 5, no -k             3 / 3\n    exit 3 then exit 5, -k 2              5 / 5\n    exit 9 then success, -k 0             9 / 9\n    SEGV then exit 5, -k 0                5 / 5\n    SEGV then success, -k 0             11 / 11\n    exit 5 then SIGTERM, -k 0         130 / 130\n    SIGTERM then exit 5, -k 0         130 / 130   was 5    <-- DEFECT 1\n    SIGINT then exit 5, -k 0          130 / 130   was 5    <-- DEFECT 1\n    SIGHUP then exit 5, -k 0          130 / 130   was 5    <-- DEFECT 1\n    exit 130 then exit 5, -k 0        130 / 130   was 5    <-- DEFECT 1\n\n  Dry run\n    -n over a failing command             0 / 0\n    -n over a manifest error              1 / 1\n\n  Manifest and graph errors\n    syntax error, unknown rule            1 / 1\n    missing input                         1 / 1\n    self-referencing phony (warns)        0 / 0\n    phony cycle, real-rule cycle          1 / 1\n    unknown target on the command line    1 / 1\n    missing manifest, unknown flag, bad -j  1 / 1\n    failing generator regen (stale)       1 / 1\n    generator killed by SIGSEGV (stale)   1 / 1\n\n  Tools and arguments\n    -t query on an unknown target         1 / 1\n    -t unknown tool, -t browse            1 / 1\n    -t targets/list/clean/deps/missingdeps/recompact  0 / 0\n    --version                             0 / 0\n    -h, -d list, -d bad, -w bad           1 / 1\n\n  Post-command paths\n    success, no depfile written (no deps) 0 / 0\n    success, garbage depfile (no deps)    0 / 0\n    deps=gcc, depfile never written       0 / 0   was 1    <-- DEFECT 2\n    deps=gcc, depfile in a missing dir    0 / 0   was 2    <-- DEFECT 3\n    plain depfile in a missing dir        0 / 0   was 2    <-- DEFECT 3\n    failure plus a garbage depfile        6 / 6\n    rspfile in a missing directory        1 / 1   was 0    <-- DEFECT 3\n    restat with the output untouched      0 / 0\n\n  Quiet, console, verbose, subninja\n    --quiet and -v over exit 7            7 / 7\n    console pool, exit 7 / exit 111   7 / 7, 111 / 111\n    console pool, SIGSEGV               11 / 11\n    failure inside a subninja           21 / 21\n\n  The tool itself signalled mid-build\n    SIGINT to the tool                130 / 130\n    SIGTERM to the tool               130 / 130\n    SIGHUP to the tool                130 / 130\n\nDEFECT 1 — an interrupt ends the build, and 130 is how Ninja spells one.\nNinja's ExitStatus enum spends 130 on ExitInterrupted and then reads every finished command's status back through that same number: `result.interrupted() || result.exit_status() == ExitInterrupted`, checked before the completion is counted as a failure at all. Two consequences Ronin missed. A command that plainly exits 130 is an interrupt — no FAILED: line, build over — because Ninja has no way to tell it from one killed by SIGINT and does not try. And an interrupt leaves the loop whatever -k still allowed, so no later command's status can overwrite the 130 that says why the build stopped. Ronin classified only signal deaths as interrupts and, under -k, counted them as ordinary failures and carried on: the whole point of the status was lost precisely when a user had asked for the build to stop. Fixed in src/build.rs as `Builder::command_interrupted`, plus a break on BuildError::Interrupted in the scheduler loop.\n\nGated behind a new BuildOptions field, `command_status_interrupts`, which the Make front end turns off. Measured GNU Make 4.4.1: a recipe exiting 130 is `*** [Makefile:2: a] Error 130` and make exits 2, with no interrupt semantics anywhere. Ronin's Make mode wraps recipes in an outer shell, so a signalled recipe already arrives as 128+N rather than as a signal and the existing classification never fired there — but an ungated 130 rule would have changed Make's narration and its -k behaviour for a number Ninja's enum merely happens to have spent. Make conformance output is byte-identical before and after, verified by reverting to base, rebuilding and re-running: the same six recorded-difference drifts, which are pre-existing on main and not this node's.\n\nDEFECT 2 — a depfile that is not there is an empty one.\nNinja's ExtractDeps clears NotFound and returns success on empty content, so a compiler that emits no depfile for a translation unit with no includes keeps the status it exited with. Ronin raised DependencyFileMissing and turned a successful command into exit 1 — samurai's behaviour, not Ninja's. The deps-log record the fix produces is identical to the one an existing-but-empty depfile produces (`#deps 0 … (VALID)`), checked against Ninja through `-t deps` rather than assumed.\n\nDEFECT 3 — the depfile's directory is made and the response file's is not.\nNinja MakeDirs the outputs and the depfile, then WriteFile the rspfile with no MakeDirs. Ronin had these exactly the wrong way round, and so was wrong in both directions: a compiler writing into a depfile directory that did not exist failed (2 against Ninja's 0), and an rspfile pointed at a missing directory succeeded (0 against Ninja's 1). The asymmetry is not an oversight upstream — a compiler writes its own depfile and cannot be asked to create the directory first, while a response file the build tool writes itself has nowhere to go and should say so.\n\nCONFORMANCE\nBUILD_CASES went 32 to 44, and every case, build and invocation alike, now records the Ninja status it must leave with as a literal. Comparing the two tools to each other was never the gap; what was missing is a check that a case still exercises what it is named after. Three did not. \"A killed command reports the shell's status\", \"a terminated command reads as an interrupt\" and \"a quit command is an ordinary failure\" were written `kill -KILL $$`, and `$$` is the manifest escape for one `$` — so all three ran `kill -KILL $`, a usage error exiting 2 that both tools agreed on perfectly. Two of the three were the only coverage of the interrupt path this node then found broken. Fixed to `$$$$`, and the recorded statuses (137, 130, 3) now hold them to it. New shapes added: SIGSEGV, SIGHUP, exit 130, exit 111, a missing command, an interrupt outranking -k in both orders, keep-going past a signal, a dry run over a failure, a missing depfile, the depfile directory, and a console command's status.\n\nSTILL DIVERGENT — recorded rather than fixed, status matches and bytes do not\nAn rspfile that cannot be written reports differently. Ninja: stderr `ninja: error: WriteFile(nodir/x.rsp): Unable to create file. No such file or directory` plus stdout `ninja: build stopped: .`; Ronin: stdout `ronin: build stopped: No such file or directory.` Both exit 1, so the status contract holds, but a conformance case cannot be added until the bytes agree. Filed as ronin-response-file-write-diagnostic.\n\nUncovered incidentally, off this node's surface, by a typo in a case manifest: an unexpected indent following a top-level binding is located at a different line by each tool — Ninja build.ninja:4 with no source context, Ronin build.ninja:3 with context. Filed as ronin-indent-error-location.\n\nVERIFICATION\ncargo test --all-targets 475 passing plus the integration binaries, clippy clean, fmt --check clean, scripts/check-ninja-conformance.sh green: 425 upstream tests, 10 CLI/tool cases, 44 build-outcome cases each on its recorded exit status, 3 invocation cases likewise, the persistence round trip and the log-content checks. All 66 probed shapes match. Probes and gates run under scripts/sandboxed."
            }

