    {
        id ronin-manifest-mtime-test-margin
        title "Separate the manifest-reload test's mtimes by more than load can eat"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-09T20:08:45.886769483Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Filed after two independent sightings on 2026-08-09: cli::tests::rust_cli_rebuilds_and_reloads_manifest_before_targets fails with MissingInput on its own template file under load (both failures immediately after a full recompile; 0 in 22 warm-cache runs across two branches). The test separates two manifest mtimes with a 20 ms sleep in src/cli.rs, which load can eat. Fix the mechanism, not the number: write the mtimes explicitly (filetime-style) or stat-loop until the clock has visibly advanced, rather than sleeping and hoping."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-24T04:55:31.72916852Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-24T05:12:48.211129628Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Fixed the racy mechanism as filed, and in doing so refuted the filing's central causal claim. Done on ronin main 1866135, no kati change (ronin-only).\n\nTHE FIX (src/cli.rs, rust_cli_rebuilds_and_reloads_manifest_before_targets). Removed the 20 ms sleep between writing build.ninja and next.ninja and replaced it with an explicit backdate: after both writes, stamp the manifest ten seconds into the past via std::fs::FileTimes::set_modified / File::set_times (the same call os.rs:437 already uses). The template is now unambiguously newer than the manifest by construction, immune to a loaded scheduler eating the gap or a coarse-granularity filesystem (1 s / 2 s) dropping both writes into one tick. The manifest goes into the PAST, never the future: a future template would leave the regenerated copy (cp stamps it at the current time) still older than its input, looping the rebuild to the 100-attempt ManifestRetryLimit. This is the precedent pattern from 6f7dae0 / 9300219 — assert the property by construction, do not race the clock.\n\nREFUTATION OF THE MARGIN THEORY (probe evidence). The filing says the test flaps because the mtime margin between manifest and template is too small. It is not: this test's outcome does not depend on that margin at all. On the first run there is no build log, so the regeneration edge is dirty via command_dirty() (graph.rs:628 — the command has never been recorded), NOT via any mtime comparison. Proven by parking: I stamped the manifest EQUAL to the template, and separately 30 s NEWER than the template (both the conditions a \"too-small margin\" would produce, and worse) — the test PASSED both times (cp ran, printf new, out == \"new\", manifest reloaded). So widening the margin cannot cure a flap the margin does not cause. The old 20 ms sleep was decorative for correctness; it only ever affected which mtime tick the two files landed in, which this test never reads.\n\nTHE OBSERVED SYMPTOM (MissingInput on next.ninja) IS NOT GATED BY THIS MARGIN. MissingInput on next.ninja can arise by exactly one code path: nodestat observes next.ninja as mtime 0 (plain_stat maps ENOENT/NotADirectory to Ok(0), os.rs:310-319), i.e. next.ninja must be genuinely absent at planning time. The test writes next.ninja before run() and never removes it, so on any normal filesystem it is present. Could not reproduce across ~1560 runs of the test binary — 480 warm, 480 under heavy dd+sync I/O and CPU churn, 600 under sustained CPU load — all on tmpfs (nanosecond mtime), 0 failures. The documented 2/2 failures \"immediately after a full recompile\" (0/22 warm) point at a cold-cache / resource-pressure environment condition, not at the mtime margin. Precedent for this exact split is 6f7dae0, whose note recorded \"the flapper the node was filed as is NOT this.\" If MissingInput recurs it is a distinct cause and wants its own node with the corrected understanding.\n\nADJACENT LATENT SMELL (not this node, not filed pending evidence it bites): RealDiskInterface::directory_fd (os.rs:170) caches the cwd-open Result in a OnceLock and consults it before every statat even for absolute paths; a single transient open failure (e.g. EMFILE under a full recompile's fd pressure) would be cached permanently and surface as an I/O error on every subsequent stat in that run. It yields a Stat error, not MissingInput, so it does not explain the symptom either. Flagged for the operator.\n\nVERIFICATION (sandboxed, SANDBOX_MEM=16G): cargo fmt --all --check clean (kati fmt nit already resolved at the current pin); scripts/check-lints.sh green (ronin + kati clippy -D warnings); cargo test --workspace green, 0 failures; the target test 600/600 under CPU load. Ninja/Make conformance untouched (test-only change) — deferred to the batch's final full battery."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-24T05:49:01.987426631Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-regeneration-progress-total
        title "Carry a regeneration edge into the progress total the way Ninja does"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-09T20:09:58.21138517Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Measured on ronin-response-file-write-diagnostic, 2026-08-09, against pinned Ninja b51a1e3. Found while probing what a failed manifest regeneration does after it stops reporting itself as an error; it is not caused by that fix and reproduces on a plainly successful regeneration.\n\nManifest — a generator edge for build.ninja and two ordinary commands, with build.ninja backdated so the regeneration has something to do:\n\n  rule regen\n    command = touch $out\n    generator = 1\n  rule cp\n    command = cp $in $out\n  build build.ninja: regen conf\n  build a: cp source\n  build b: cp source\n  default a b\n\n  ninja -j1  rc=0  stdout: [1/1] touch build.ninja / [1/3] cp source a / [2/3] cp source b\n  ronin -j1  rc=0  stdout: [1/1] touch build.ninja / [1/2] cp source a / [2/2] cp source b\n\nNinja's denominator is three where Ronin's is two. `StatusPrinter::total_edges_` is moved only by `EdgeAddedToPlan` and `EdgeRemovedFromPlan` (status_printer.cc:89, 103), and `BuildStarted()` resets `started_edges_`, `finished_edges_` and `running_edges_` but deliberately not the total (status_printer.cc:286). One `Status` object serves both the manifest-regeneration builder and the build that follows, so the regeneration edge stays in the total for the rest of the process. Ronin builds each with a fresh `Builder`, so nothing carries.\n\nEvery field derived from the total is affected with it — `%t`, `%r`, `%p`, and the ETA weighting — so this is one accounting question, not four. It also shows after a regeneration that failed and reported itself, which is the shape that surfaced it.\n\nExit status agrees (0), so this is a progress-rendering defect. A conformance BuildCase needs `stale_manifest: true` and a generator edge, which the harness already supports; add it with the fix."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-24T05:17:23.556573821Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-24T05:41:59.920756814Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Fixed. Ronin now carries a manifest regeneration's edges into the following build's progress total, the way Ninja does. Done on ronin main 2e1b9c7, ronin-only (no kati change).\n\nUPSTREAM-NINJA PROBE (pinned b51a1e3, reference/ninja). Ninja makes one Status object before the rebuild loop (ninja.cc:1844) and passes the SAME object to both RebuildManifest (ninja.cc:283) and RunBuild (ninja.cc:1646/1909). StatusPrinter::total_edges_ is moved only by EdgeAddedToPlan/EdgeRemovedFromPlan (status_printer.cc:90, 104), and BuildStarted (status_printer.cc:285) resets started_edges_/finished_edges_/running_edges_ and nothing else. So the regeneration edge stays in total_edges_ for the rest of the process while the numerator resets: a plain regeneration then two commands prints `[1/1] touch build.ninja`, then `[1/3] cp source a` / `[2/3] cp source b`. This reconciles with the operator's shrink-to-what-runs ruling: total_edges_ IS what runs — EdgeRemovedFromPlan decrements it when a restat prunes an edge — and the regeneration edge belongs in it because it ran. The denominator is the whole invocation's run count, not one phase's.\n\nMECHANISM (Ronin). Ronin builds each phase with a fresh Builder, so `progress.total = command_edge_count` reset per phase and the regeneration edge was lost — it printed `[1/2] [2/2]`. Added a `ProgressCarry` (src/build/status.rs): the six counters BuildStarted leaves standing — total plus the ETA predictable/unpredictable weighting. BuildState gains carry()/adopt_carry(); Builder holds a progress_carry, seeds it in build() before its own edges join (total = carry.total + command_edge_count, prediction adopted then seed_prediction adds on top), and exposes it after the run via progress_carried(). Threaded through the Build/Planned/Outcome facade (src/frontend/execute.rs: carrying() in, Outcome::progress_carry() out) and the cli loop (src/cli.rs): one ProgressCarry lives across the 0..100 remake loop, rebuild_manifest updates it from each regeneration that ran, build_targets seeds the requested build with it. The deferred-work total recompute (src/build/deferred.rs:589, a Make-mode late-binding path) now adds the same base; zero carry there means no behaviour change for Make. Every field derived from the total moves with it — %t, %r (=total-started), %p (=100*finished/total), and the %P/%E/%W weighting — one accounting fix, not four.\n\nVERIFICATION / TEETH. Manual differential against pinned Ninja b51a1e3 (reference/ninja-build/ninja) on the filing's exact manifest under `-C dir -j1`: both now print `[1/1] touch build.ninja`, `[1/3] cp source a`, `[2/3] cp source b`, rc 0 — byte-identical. Parked (build_targets carries ProgressCarry::default()) reproduces the old `[1/2] [2/2]`, so the new conformance case bites. Added that case to examples/conformance.rs (a successful regeneration + generator edge, stale_manifest:true, -j1, status 0), which runs real Ninja and real Ronin and compares byte-for-byte: build-outcome cases 86 -> 87. scripts/check-ninja-conformance.sh green — 425/425 upstream, 10 differential, 87 build-outcome each on its recorded status, 3 invocation, persistence + log-content. Also green: cargo fmt --all --check; scripts/check-lints.sh (clippy -D warnings, ronin + kati); cargo test --workspace, 0 failures. All under scripts/sandboxed, SANDBOX_MEM=16G.\n\nNOTE (not this node): `cargo test --all-targets` prints dead-code warnings for SHELL_SIGNIFICANT / needs_shell / retry_with_shell / direct_argv in src/subprocess.rs (from the recent shell-launch commits); they are cfg-conditional dead in the plain test build and clippy --all-targets does not flag them, so the lint gate stays green. Pre-existing, untouched here."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-24T05:49:03.533332093Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

