                        kind @note
                        text "Ran the 324-case corpus in comparison mode against Fedora 44 (make-4.4.1-12.fc44, fedora@sha256:6c75d5bf...) and Arch (make 4.4.1-3, archlinux@sha256:b0deabeb...) through a Docker shim that mounts each case directory at its own path and runs as the invoking user, so every case saw the distribution's own /bin/sh and shell utilities as well as its Make. Both distributions ship 4.4.1. Fedora: 0 of 324 cases differ; sole identity departure is MAKE_HOST=x86_64-redhat-linux-gnu, the configure triple, with every built-in variable and every .FEATURES entry matching the released source. Arch: 0 of 324 cases differ; sole identity departure is a guile entry in .FEATURES, a capability no case reaches. So the classification across all three distributions is: one distro-unique divergence in total — Debian's POSIX ARFLAGS — and nothing that matches Debian in either of the other two, which is what makes the -rvU value a Debian patch rather than a platform convention. No case in either run classified as matches-Debian, and none as host-environment, so running two distinct userlands produced no shell-dialect or coreutils differences in this corpus either."
