            {
                id lint-hermetic-read
                title "Decide whether a Makefile can be linted without running it"
                deps (lint-makefile-nesting-census)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T13:56:03.083372458Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the lint design decision on `ronin-lint-surface` rather than built into v1.\n\nThe want: lint a Makefile from an untrusted or unconfigured tree without evaluating it — no `$(shell)`, no remade include, nothing executed. What makes it a decision rather than a flag: GNU Make's read phase runs `$(shell)` at read time, and a great many Makefiles compute the very things a census is about that way — the directory a `$(MAKE)` enters, the goals it is given, whether a guard is written at all. An unrun `$(shell)` has to expand to SOMETHING, and whichever answer is chosen changes what the census reports. Reporting a census gathered under a different evaluation as though it described the build is the failure `[spec:ronin:req:make.nesting-census]` exists to prevent.\n\nSo the decision to make is what a hermetic read claims about itself: a second, weaker report that says outright which facts it could not settle, or nothing at all. Not a quieter version of the same output."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id lint-machine-readable
                title "Emit lint findings as records a tool can read"
                deps (lint-makefile-nesting-census lint-manifest-checks)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T13:56:07.908107057Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the lint design decision on `ronin-lint-surface` rather than built into v1.\n\nThe want: `-t lint --json`, or some other record form, so a packaging pipeline can consume the census instead of parsing diagnostics. Deliberately deferred until the human-readable shape has been used against real trees: a record format pins field names and a schema on the first release that emits it, and the census's own vocabulary — what a composed invocation names, how a nested reason is spelled — is exactly what a first pass over vim, zsh and a kernel tree is likely to change.\n\nDeferring costs nothing that cannot be recovered: the findings are already a structured value internally and the text is rendered from it, so a second renderer is a renderer and not a redesign."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id lint-reads-with-ronins-own-make
                title "Give a lint's read the Make it is reporting about"
                deps (lint-makefile-nesting-census)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T15:32:21.19635038Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while running `-t lint` over zsh's `Src/` tree, and filed rather than fixed in v1 because the fix is a choice about identity rather than a bug in the census.\n\nWHAT HAPPENS. `-t lint` builds a Make argv of its own — `make -C DIR -f FILE goals...` — and `make_named_invocation` resolves that `make` by searching PATH for an entry that canonicalizes to this executable. Under `ronin -t lint` there usually is none, and the documented fallback is the bare name, so `$(MAKE)` is `make`. That is right for the census — the value is what a Makefile expects to see and what `invokes_make` matches against — and wrong for the one thing the read still runs: a recipe the read must run to remake an include, which starts whatever `make` PATH holds. Linting zsh's `Src/Makefile` prints `make[2]: Entering directory ...` lines, which are GNU Make 4.4.1's narration and not Ronin's, because GNU Make 4.4.1 is what ran.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS. `[spec:ronin:req:tools.lint]` says the read is the read phase a build would perform. A read whose remakes are performed by a different Make is a read of a different build, in the one place where lint's own contract says it must not be.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT OBVIOUS. Handing the invocation this executable's path instead makes `$(MAKE)` name Ronin, and Ronin reached by its own name is Ninja mode — so a recipe that runs `$(MAKE)` would fail rather than quietly use another tool. Failing loudly during a read that a build would have completed is not obviously better than the current answer, and `[spec:ronin:req:product.make-identity]` is the reason there is no third option: no flag selects a front end.\n\nWorth measuring first: how often a read has to run a recipe at all. Every gate here does it exactly once, for vim's `src/auto/config.mk` and zsh's generated `Makemod`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T01:25:51.147931739Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T01:54:26.318936635Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the answer turned out to be smaller than the filing feared: no PATH is touched, and nothing outside `src/lint.rs` changed.\n\nWHAT THE PROBE SAID, and it is the whole design. A build's `$(MAKE)` is not resolved when a recipe runs it — it is resolved ONCE, at startup, by `make_named_invocation`, which searches PATH for an entry that canonicalizes to this executable and hands the recipe that entry's ABSOLUTE PATH. Measured, on the reduction below with a `make` symlink to the release binary on PATH:\n\n    [1/1] printf 'GENERATED := …' > gen.mk; /tmp/…/probe/bin/make -C sub marker\n\nSo a build never asks PATH for a Make at recipe time, and a recipe that runs `$(MAKE)` execs a path whose last component is `make` — which is exactly what makes it re-enter Make mode, since the front end is selected by the invoked name and by nothing else. The read was missing that path and nothing else: `-t lint` supplies the literal `make` as its own argv[0], the PATH search finds nothing that is this executable, and the documented fallback is the bare name.\n\nSo the read stages one. `make_of_this_reads_own` (src/lint.rs) makes a directory of this run's own under TMPDIR holding `make` → the canonical path of this executable, and the Makefile arm names it as `arguments[0]`. `make_named_invocation` sees a multi-component path and takes it as it stands, so `$(MAKE)` is that link, a recipe execs it, and the child is a Make of ours. The value is held for the length of the read and the directory goes with it; two reads at once cannot collide because each has its own.\n\nWHY NOT A PATH PREPEND, which is what the filing and the dispatch both expected. Because it would overreach. A recipe that runs a BARE `make` — or a `$(shell make …)` — resolves it through PATH under GNU Make and under a Ronin build alike, so whatever the machine has under that name is the right answer for both, and a read that put its own directory in front of PATH would answer differently from the build it is reporting on. The only thing a build resolves specially is `$(MAKE)`, and that is the only thing this changes.\n\nWHY NOT PREFER A REAL `make` ON PATH WHEN THERE IS ONE. Considered and refused: a read whose `$(MAKE)` depended on whether this machine has Ronin installed under that name would be a report that could differ between machines. The staged link is always the answer, and it is a Make of ours either way.\n\nWHAT THE FIELD CASE DOES NOW. `-t lint` over zsh 5.9.2's `Src/Makefile`, the tree the defect was found on: the invocation in `Makemod:356` reads `( cd $subdir && /tmp/ronin-make-…/make … )`, the progress lines are Ronin's own, and the `make[1]: Entering directory …` narration the node was filed over is gone. The census is unchanged — 5 composed at `Makefile:448`, the two nested `Makemod:356` warnings, `Makemod:422`, `411` and `271` composed — because the report prints the recipe's own text and never the value.\n\nTHE BUILD PATH DOES NOT WANT THIS, which the dispatch asked to be settled either way. There is one build shape with the same symptom: a name supplied rather than followed — `exec -a make /path/to/ronin` — where the PATH search finds nothing, `$(MAKE)` falls back to `make`, and a recipe starts whatever the machine has. GNU Make 4.4.1 does the identical thing there, probed against the oracle on the same reduction: `$(MAKE)` is `make` and the child is `/usr/bin/make`. So matching it is compatibility rather than a defect, and staging a link for a build would be a divergence from 4.4.1. Lint is the only consumer, because lint is the only caller that invents the name rather than being reached by it.\n\nFIXTURE: tests/lint.rs `a_remade_include_recurses_here`. A read whose `include gen.mk` must be remade, whose remake recipe runs `$(MAKE) -C sub marker` behind a `;` — zsh's shape, and nested rather than composed on purpose (see the defect filed below). It gates effects: the child's own directory holds a `.ninja_log` afterwards, which only a Make of ours writes; the `$(MAKE)` the child reports is an absolute path whose last component is `make`; and that path no longer exists, because the read took its directory with it. TEETH: with the staging parked the test fails at the first of those — \"the child left no build log, so it was not this Make\".\n\nGATES, serial, sandboxed, on a quiet host: fmt clean; check clean on both feature sets; all three clippy passes clean; `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean; workspace 962 passed / 0 failed (960 baseline, one test from each node this dispatch); `--no-default-features` 753 / 0; make_port 4/4 over 948 with nothing rewritten; conformance 387 runs, 286 raw and 336 normalised identical, 51 differing at 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact, nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 / 280 / 1 both-refused / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 at 132 / 0 / 24 / 1129 with the tsv byte-identical; ninja conformance 425 plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content; `check-make-projects.sh all` built vim in 23s and zsh with 36 modules in 35s at -j8.\n\nFOUND WHILE BUILDING THE FIXTURE, filed as `a-remade-makefile-that-composes-a-recursion-runs-the-probe`: the first shape tried for the fixture was a remake recipe whose `$(MAKE)` line COMPOSES, and it does not work at all — the read runs `false` and reports `FAILED: [code=1] gen.mk`. Reproduced on the parent commit 1dc5603 with the source restored, so it is not this dispatch's doing; the fixture uses the nested shape, which is also the field's."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T01:55:22.609685575Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id lint-reports-a-recursion-whose-makefile-is-missing
                title "Report a composition whose child makefile is missing, and carry on reporting"
                kind @fix
                deps (lint-makefile-nesting-census)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T17:52:14.858569202Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found in the field, running `-t lint` over ~/git/lang-sme, a real tree of relic Makefiles.\n\nWHAT HAPPENS. A recipe line whose own command is the invocation COMPOSES, so the compiler goes to read the child — and when the child's directory holds no makefile, `subninja::compile` (src/make/cli/subninja.rs) raises `no makefile found for recursive compilation in '<dir>'`. That error leaves the whole read, `read_without_building` (src/make/cli.rs) propagates it with `?`, and `-t lint`'s Makefile arm (src/lint.rs) reports the refusal and closes with `nothing further to report: the read did not finish`. Everything the compile had already classified is sitting in the census ledger and is never taken out of it.\n\nThe tree's own shape, from devtools/testing-from-old-infra/make-dictindex:\n\n    SRCDIR = ../../\n    ...\n            @cd $(SRCDIR)/ && $(MAKE) ifst GTLANG=sme\n\n`../../` is the repository root, which has a `Makefile.am` and a `Makefile.in` and no `Makefile`. Reduced to a scratch tree of four targets — one composed `cd present && $(MAKE) all`, the missing child, one nested behind an `if test`, and a target declared twice — the whole report today is, verbatim:\n\n    Makefile:26: warning: overriding commands for target 'noise'\n    Makefile:23: warning: ignoring old commands for target 'noise'\n    ronin: no makefile found for recursive compilation in '<abs>/nomakefile'\n    ronin: nothing further to report: the read did not finish\n\nExit 2, and NOT ONE census line — neither the composed invocation written above the missing one nor the nested invocation written below it. On a tree like lang-sme the missing child is itself the most useful finding lint could report, and it is delivered as the reason there is no report.\n\nWHAT IT SHOULD DO. Under `-t lint`, a composition site whose child cannot be read is a FINDING rather than a refusal: a warning at the invocation's own file and line saying the invocation composes and its makefile is missing at that directory, and the census carries on over everything else the compile classified — before the missing child and after it, and into the children that do exist. The summary line still prints and the exit status reflects the warning.\n\nA BUILD KEEPS TODAY'S REFUSAL, and that is the constraint that makes this more than a message change. The graph genuinely cannot be compiled: there is no child to compose, and GNU Make would start a Make that fails. Only the reporting path changes, so whatever selects it has to be something a build never sets, and the build path's refusal has to be provably unmoved — the port harness and the upstream inventory are what say so.\n\nWHAT IT LOOKS LIKE IT WILL TAKE, read off the code rather than guessed:\n\n* A census disposition of its own beside `Composed` and `Nested` (kati/src-rs/census.rs), naming the directory. The census is the deliverable, so a missing child is a third answer to \"what became of this invocation\" rather than an error bolted onto the side of two.\n* A location on the invocation the sink hands over. `SinkSubninja` (kati/src-rs/build_sink.rs) carries the command and the `MAKE` value and no `Loc`, while the `Command` it was flattened out of has one — `record_census` (kati/src-rs/ninja.rs) already renders exactly the string a finding wants, unit prefix included, so that rendering wants factoring out rather than writing twice. Without it the finding has nowhere to point.\n* Something on `CompilationContext` (src/make.rs) that says this compile is a report. `Census::is_recording()` is that flag today by accident, since `Census::collected()` is only ever built by `read_without_building` — accident is not a contract, and reading \"a census was asked for\" as \"a missing child is survivable\" is how a build path quietly acquires a lint's tolerance.\n* A distinguishable error out of `subninja::compile`, so `load_with_subninjas_unlocked`'s `resolve(...)?` can record and carry on for that one invocation rather than for every `MakeError::Evaluate`. Carrying on means the pending wrapper completes with one fewer child group than it has invocations, which `complete_subninja` (src/make/sink.rs) has not been asked for before.\n\nACCEPTANCE. A checked-in reduction under `tests/make-project-reductions/`, copied into scratch by the test the way `recipe-mixes-liftable-and-unliftable-recursion` is, holding a composable `$(MAKE)` into a directory with no makefile beside real census content on both sides of it and a duplicate-target override. The lint output shape is pinned in `tests/lint.rs` — findings from before AND after the missing child, the missing child's own warning with its file and line, and the summary — which is the one place this repository asserts on Ronin's own bytes, because the report IS the deliverable there."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T18:00:37.406863328Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T18:31:07.959293419Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. The filing's reading of what it would take was right on all four counts, and the reduction surfaced a fifth thing that was a defect rather than an addition.\n\nWHAT `-t lint` SAYS NOW, on the checked-in reduction, verbatim and exit 1:\n\n    Makefile:26: warning: overriding commands for target 'noise'\n    Makefile:23: warning: ignoring old commands for target 'noise'\n    Makefile:12: note: recursion composed into the graph: cd present && $(MAKE) all\n    Makefile:15: note: recursion composed into the graph: cd nomakefile/ && $(MAKE) all\n    Makefile:15: warning: the invocation composes and its makefile is missing: nothing a Make reads is in `nomakefile`\n    Makefile:15: note: a composed invocation is compiled where it points, so that directory needs a makefile under one of the names a Make looks for, or the invocation needs a `-f` naming one\n    Makefile:18: warning: recursion nests at run time: the invocation is not the recipe line's own command, so a shell construct stands between them\n    Makefile:18: note: a line whose own command is the invocation composes: `$(MAKE) ...`, or `cd DIR && $(MAKE) ...`, with any test settled where the Makefile can answer it\n    present/Makefile:5: warning: recursion nests at run time: ... (same shape)\n    present/Makefile:5: note: ... (same shape)\n    ronin: 4 recursive invocations: 2 composed, 2 nested; 1 of them found no makefile\n\nagainst the four lines it said before, of which the last two were the refusal and `nothing further to report: the read did not finish`. Every finding is there: the two above the missing child, the missing child's own, the one below it, and the one inside `present/Makefile` — a child reached BECAUSE the invocation naming it composed. The census descends past the sibling it could not read.\n\nTHE FIELD REPRODUCER ITSELF, run on the shape it came from rather than only on the reduction. `devtools/testing-from-old-infra/make-dictindex` and the `Makefile.am`/`Makefile.in` two levels above it, copied into a scratch tree so nothing was written into the real one, `-t lint --make savefile`, exit 1:\n\n    make-dictindex:427: note: recursion composed into the graph: cd ../..// && $(MAKE) ifst GTLANG=sme\n    make-dictindex:427: warning: the invocation composes and its makefile is missing: nothing a Make reads is in `<scratch>/langsme`\n    make-dictindex:427: note: a composed invocation is compiled where it points, so that directory needs a makefile under one of the names a Make looks for, or the invocation needs a `-f` naming one\n    ronin: 1 recursive invocation: 1 composed, 0 nested; 1 of them found no makefile\n\nThe directory is absolute there and relative in the reduction, and that is the rule working rather than failing: `SRCDIR = ../../` points ABOVE the build's root, so there is no root-relative way to write it and the whole path is the honest answer.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, in the four places the filing named.\n\n* `kati::census::Disposition::MissingMakefile { directory }`, a third answer beside `Composed` and `Nested`. Recorded by whoever went to read the child rather than by the classifier, and it FOLLOWS the `Composed` entry for the same line rather than replacing it, because both are true: composing is what the compiler decided about the recipe, and this is what happened when it acted on the decision. The summary says it the same way — `2 composed` counts the missing one, and `1 of them found no makefile` says how many of those found nothing, rather than a fourth number that would count one invocation twice.\n* `SinkSubninja::location`, so the finding has somewhere to point. `record_census` already rendered exactly the string a finding wants, unit prefix and all, so that rendering came out into `NinjaGenerator::written_at` and both callers use it — a report that spelled the same line two ways would read as two places. Rendered only when a census is being collected, so a build pays for neither the interner lookup nor the string.\n* `CompilationContext::reporting`, written down rather than inferred. `Census::is_recording()` would have worked today and would have been an accident: `Census::collected()` is only ever built by `read_without_building`, and reading \"a census was asked for\" as \"a missing child is survivable\" is how a build path quietly acquires a report's tolerance. It travels to children through `subninja::compile` like `census` and `diagnostics` do, and it is grouped with the census in `cli::Purpose` because the two are one decision.\n* `MakeError::MissingChildMakefile { directory }`, its own variant so `resolve(...)?` can be answered for that one invocation rather than for every `Evaluate` string. Its rendering is the same text the build always printed. Under a report the arm records the census entry and pushes an empty child group, because `complete_subninja` completes the wrapper against one group per invocation and this one composed nothing.\n\nTHE DEFECT THE REDUCTION SURFACED. `written_as_make_reference` wrote `$(MAKE)` over every occurrence of the `MAKE` value in the command text, so the census reported the reduction's line as `cd no$(MAKE)file/ && $(MAKE) all`. The value is a program name: an occurrence with a path character on either side of it belongs to something longer, and a directory called `nomakefile` contains the word `make` without being one. Now written back only where the value stands as a word of its own, gated by `a_make_reference_is_written_back_only_where_the_value_is_a_word` over seven shapes including the absolute-path spelling and two occurrences in one line. Found because the reduction was written from the real tree rather than around it.\n\nTHE BUILD DID NOT MOVE, and that is asserted rather than assumed. `building_a_composition_with_no_makefile_is_still_refused` runs the same reduction through the `make`-named link and requires exit 2 and the same words. Measured against the wrong implementation — the build handed `Purpose::report` — it fails, so the control has teeth of its own rather than merely passing. GNU Make 4.4.1 fails the same tree at the child it starts, also with exit 2, so the two tools still agree about what this build is worth.\n\nTHE REDUCTION, `tests/make-project-reductions/recursion-into-a-directory-with-no-makefile`, holds `@cd $(SRCDIR)/ && $(MAKE) all` with `SRCDIR = nomakefile`, a directory holding one text file, beside a composition that works, a nesting that does not, a child with a census of its own, and a target declared twice — a finding that is not a census entry, and the proof that what the compiler raised before the census survives too.\n\nTHE RULE MOVED, to `[spec:ronin:req:make.nesting-census+2]`. `+1` closed by saying \"a refusal ends the lint where it would have ended the build, and is reported as the refusal it is\", which is exactly what this node stops being true of one refusal. `+2` says which one, says the build is still refused there, and says why the two are different judgements about the same fact. Every other refusal still ends the lint. The five pinned annotations were re-read against the new body and re-pinned.\n\nTEETH: with the whole fix parked and the tests kept, `a_composition_with_no_makefile_is_reported_and_the_census_carries_on` fails on the exit status alone — 2 where a complete report leaves 1 — before it reaches the bytes.\n\nWHERE: kati 43d78d0d40e2c2f005b0b242184f953f7512a138 on nplan/lint-reports-a-recursion-whose-makefile-is-missing, pushed before this pin.\n\nGATES, run serially, the host quiet again by the end of them. fmt clean both crates; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace 940 passed / 0 failed (937 plus the two lint cases and the kati one); Clippy ronin lib+bins pedantic+nursery `--no-deps` clean and kati `--all-targets -D warnings` clean — two findings this work introduced, an eighth argument on `compile_invocation` and a `compose_subninjas` grown past a hundred lines, were answered by grouping the report's two channels into `cli::Purpose` and by lifting the two staging blocks out, not by an allow; make_port 4/4 over 920 gated cases with no recording rewritten; conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised and 286 raw, unmoved, nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 rows at 134 unclassified / 0 compiler / 25 interface / 1126 narration with the tsv byte-identical and no drift line; ninja conformance 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log-content; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 737 passed / 0 failed; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 31s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 60s at -j8, and vim's own census is line for line what it was before this work.\n\nThe five annotation re-pins landed after that battery started and are comments; fmt, `cargo check --workspace --all-targets`, both Clippy runs and the lint suite were re-run over the exact committed tree afterwards."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T18:31:13.405969886Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T18:45:53.345470936Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The whole battery was re-run over the exact committed tree, on a host that had gone quiet, because two files moved between the first run and the commit: the commit gate's function-density and function-name checks fired, and the answer was to inline `Purpose`'s two constructors at their call sites and to shorten two test names. Neither can change behaviour and both were in the Make CLI, so the numbers are worth having rather than reasoning about.\n\nConformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised and 286 raw, 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact, nothing unclassified. Equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 rejected / 0 disagreements. Upstream inventory 1285 rows at 134 unclassified / 0 compiler / 25 interface / 1126 narration, exit clean with no drift line and `tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv` byte-identical. Ninja conformance 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log-content. `check-make-projects all` built vim 9.2 in 18s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 33s at -j8. `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 737 passed / 0 failed. `nplan check` over the same tree: check, format and the whole workspace suite, all passing."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-20T13:55:52.052761078Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Originating decision. Operator ask, Brendan, 2026-08-20, verbatim: \"One thing that keeps popping up is that ninja can't statically analyse all Makefiles. But we know where the static failures occur, right? So we could have a ninja lint subcommand that works on Makefiles and ninja.builds?\"\n\nWHY THIS TREE EXISTS AT THE ROOT, NOT UNDER make-subsumption. `make-subsumption` is a compatibility container: every node in it closes a gap between what GNU Make 4.4.1 does and what Ronin does. Lint closes no such gap — GNU Make has no lint and Ninja has no lint, so there is nothing to be compatible with and no oracle to probe. It is product surface over both front ends: one tool that reads a Makefile or a manifest and reports what compiling it established. It is grounded on `[arch:ronin:cli]`, which now owns `[spec:ronin:req:tools.lint]` and `[spec:ronin:req:tools.manifest-lint]`, and on `[arch:ronin:make-frontend]`, which owns `[spec:ronin:req:make.nesting-census]`.\n\nDECISION 1 — SPELLING. `-t lint`, and only that.\n\n`ninja -t <tool>` is the native extension point: `-t` already terminates top-level options and hands the rest of argv to the tool, tools already declare whether they run before the manifest is read, and `-t list` already enumerates them. Lint becomes the first entry Ronin adds to that set. It is marked as ours the way `--compat`, `--shell`, `--output` and `--color` are marked as ours — a `[spec:ronin:req:...]` rule and a usage line — because there is no naming convention for a Ronin-owned tool and inventing one would be worse than the annotation the repository already uses. No Ninja-owned tool name changes meaning: `lint` is a name upstream does not use.\n\n`ronin lint` as a subcommand is REFUSED, and the reason is not taste. In Ninja mode an operand is a target: `ronin lint` today means \"build the target named lint\". Reading it as a subcommand would give one operand two readings depending on what the manifest happens to contain, which `[spec:ronin:req:compat.cli-and-tools]` forbids and which would break silently for any project that has a `lint` target — which is most of them. The multicall binary has no subcommands at all and this is not the feature to introduce one for.\n\nA Makefile is linted through the same tool, selected by the kind of the input file rather than by the invoked name. That is not a second door into Make mode. `[spec:ronin:req:product.make-identity]` says the front end is selected by the invoked name and by nothing else, and its stated reason is that `$(MAKE)` must be a path: a front end reached through a flag needs the flag carried in `MAKE`, and software execs that value. Lint starts no process, spawns no child, and never appears in a `MAKE` value. `-t lint` runs in Ninja mode, always; what it does there is call the Make compiler as a library and read what the compile recorded. Make mode itself is untouched — in Make mode `-t` is GNU's `--touch` and stays that, so the two spellings cannot collide.\n\nInput kind: the file the invocation names, `-f`, defaulting to Ninja's `build.ninja`. `GNUmakefile`, `makefile`, `Makefile` or a `.mk` suffix is a Makefile; every other name is a manifest; `--make` and `--ninja` name the kind outright. Deliberately NOT sniffing the directory for whichever of the two exists: `src/multicall.rs` already rejects directory-sniffing as a way of answering \"which language is this\", and the answer should not be reached one way for builds and another for lint.\n\nDECISION 2 — READ-PHASE IMPURITY, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. Linting a Makefile evaluates it, exactly as a build's read phase would: `$(shell)` runs, `$(warning)` and `$(info)` print, and a makefile the read must remake is remade. That is not a concession, it is the only way to be about the build in question — GNU Make's read phase does all three, kati reproduces it, and a report gathered from a quieter read would be a report about a different Makefile. `-t lint -h` says so in as many words, so nobody runs it on an untrusted tree believing it is inert.\n\nA hermetic or sandboxed read is a real want and a separate decision, filed as `lint-hermetic-read` rather than half-built here. It cannot be a flag bolted on later without an answer to what an unrun `$(shell)` expands to, and that answer changes what the census reports.\n\nDECISION 3 — VOICE AND EXIT STATUS. Findings are compiler diagnostics in Ronin's established shapes, per the narration-contract table on make-narration-contract-audit: `<loc>: <message>` located, `<loc>: warning: <message>` for a problem, `<loc>: note: <message>` for the line that says what would answer it, `ronin: <message>` unlocated and for the closing summary. `note:` is the one addition, and it is the established located shape with a severity word in the place `warning:` already occupies — it exists because the operator's ask is that a packager see \"what edit would lift it\", and a remedy on its own line is how every compiler has ever said that. Nothing is rendered in GNU Make's voice; there is no GNU counterpart to imitate and none is invented.\n\nSeverities and status: note = 0, warning = 1, error or unreadable input = 2. Three levels rather than two because the census is mostly notes — a composed invocation is not a finding, it is the census saying the build composes — and a tool that exited nonzero for saying so would be useless in CI. The worst finding sets the status.\n\nNote that lint is a tool the user asks for by name, which is what makes it admissible at all. make-narration-contract-audit retired `make-pattern-peer-warning` on the principle that a warning on a SUCCESS path fails the contract. That principle is intact here: a successful build says nothing new, and every word below is printed only because someone typed `-t lint`.\n\nDECISION 4 — SCOPE OF v1. In: the Makefile nesting census with file, line and reason; refusal sites; the compile-time warnings kati already raises, surfaced without building anything; and the manifest checks for what parsing accepts. Out, and filed: hermetic read (`lint-hermetic-read`), machine-readable output (`lint-machine-readable`).\n\nOf the five manifest checks asked for, three are lint-only findings — a build binding no reachable rule reads, a cycle anywhere rather than only under the requested targets, phony misuse — and two, duplicate outputs and dangling rule references, are already unconditionally fatal in the parser. Lint does not re-implement those; it reports the parse error as the finding it is, in lint's shape, so one command still answers for all five. Making the parser stricter is explicitly not in scope: a manifest that lints with findings builds exactly as it did.\n\nWHAT THIS COSTS, HONESTLY. The premise that the compiler already computes every fact lint would report is nearly true and not entirely: the disposition is computed and thrown away, but no Makefile location is ever stamped on a classified recipe line, and the reason a line was left to nest is collapsed into one bit (`Command::uncomposable_recursion`) whose only consumer is a `bail!`. So the census needs three things added in kati — a `Loc` on the classified line, a discriminated reason in place of the bit, and a collector shared the way `Diagnostics` already is — and none of them may change what compiles. The upstream inventory, conformance and equivalence gates are the proof of that, and a moved row is a defect in this work rather than a number to re-record."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-20T15:32:52.582538187Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "v1 delivered. `-t lint`, the Makefile nesting census, and the manifest checks are in and pushed: ronin 8a36950, b9b74fd, e0b5ad0 over the design commit 6a96cfa, with kati b300685 on `nplan/lint-makefile-nesting-census`.\n\nTHE OPERATOR'S OWN REPRODUCER, run and quoted verbatim. `ronin -f Makefile -t lint all` in `reference/make-projects/vim-9.2.0957`, exit 1:\n\n    Makefile:44: note: recursion composed into the graph: cd src && $(MAKE) all\n    Makefile:46: warning: recursion nests at run time: the invocation is not the recipe line's own command, so a shell construct stands between them\n    Makefile:46: note: a line whose own command is the invocation composes: `$(MAKE) ...`, or `cd DIR && $(MAKE) ...`, with any test settled where the Makefile can answer it\n    Makefile:51: warning: recursion nests at run time: the invocation is not the recipe line's own command, so a shell construct stands between them\n    Makefile:51: note: a line whose own command is the invocation composes: `$(MAKE) ...`, or `cd DIR && $(MAKE) ...`, with any test settled where the Makefile can answer it\n    src/Makefile:1655: warning: ... (same shape)\n    src/Makefile:2148: warning: ... (same shape)\n    src/Makefile:2152: warning: ... (same shape)\n    ronin: 6 recursive invocations: 1 composed, 5 nested\n\nThe top-level Makefile is exactly the census the acceptance asked for: one composed `cd src && $(MAKE)` at line 44, two nested at 46 and 51 — the dead-branch guards — each with its file, its line, its reason and its remedy. The other three are `src/Makefile`, reached because line 44 COMPOSES and the child is compiled into the same graph; the census descends with the composition rather than stopping at the file it was handed, which is what makes it a report about the build rather than about one file. `src/Makefile:1655` is `cd auto/wayland; $(MAKE)`; 2148 and 2152 are the two `if test -n \"$(MAKEMO)\" ...; then cd $(PODIR); ... $(MAKE) ...; fi` guards under `languages`.\n\nzsh runs clean of refusals. `-t lint` on the top-level Makefile finds the `for subdir in Src Doc; do (cd $$subdir && $(MAKE) ...) done` loop — 1 nested, 0 composed — and on `Src/Makefile` finds 13 invocations, 10 composed, 3 nested, with no error finding anywhere. The three nested are all `Makemod:356`, reached once per module.\n\nA `.ninja` case exercises each manifest check: an unexpanded build binding, a binding on a phony statement, a rule named `phony` shadowing the built-in, a cycle no requested target reaches, a manifest that will not parse (reported with its caret, exit 2), and Ninja's own generated `build.ninja`, which lints clean — the property that makes the checks worth running.\n\nWHAT THE DESIGN NOTE PROMISED AND WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENED. The claim that the compiler already computes every fact a lint would report was nearly true and not entirely, as the note said. Three things had to be added in kati: a `Loc` on the classified recipe line, a discriminated reason in place of `Command::uncomposable_recursion`, and a ledger shared the way `Diagnostics` already is. Two more were DEFECTS the census surfaced rather than additions: `spawns_make` read the head of every command a shell would begin, but not through the reserved words a construct puts in front of one, nor past the `\\` a recipe line written across several source lines keeps. `if test x; then $(MAKE) sub; fi` — vim's shape, twice in its top-level Makefile — answered no, so the compiler was blind to a nested Make it should have known about. Both fixed in kati; the full battery proves compilation did not move.\n\n`make.nesting-census` went to `+1` before any of it landed. The v0 text drew the three nested reasons from the remainder `[spec:ronin:req:make.recursive-invocation+2]` admits and said anything else was \"not admitted\" — which would have made the census argue about whether `cd auto/wayland; $(MAKE)` is a gap in the compiler or a boundary of the contract. `+1` says the census names the shape and leaves the judgement to whoever reads it. A report that argued would be worth less than one that reported.\n\nGATES, all run serially on a quiet host after the last commit: workspace 933/0 (911 baseline plus 22), `--no-default-features` 733/0 (716 plus 17), conformance 336/51 normalised and 286 raw, equivalence 297/280/75 with 0 disagreements, upstream inventory 1285 @ 134/0/25/1126 with the compiler class still zero and no TSV drift, ninja conformance 425/425, make_port 4/4, `check-make-projects all` building vim in 32s and zsh in 55s at -j8, clippy pedantic and nursery clean on the ronin lib and bins, clippy `-D warnings` clean on kati, fmt clean.\n\nLEFT OPEN, each with a node: `lint-hermetic-read` (a read that runs nothing), `lint-machine-readable` (records rather than lines), and `lint-reads-with-ronins-own-make`, which was found while running the zsh tree and is the sharpest of the three — a read that has to remake an include starts whatever `make` is on PATH, which under `ronin -t lint` is usually GNU Make rather than Ronin."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id ronin-builtin-shell
        title "Run what needs a shell under Ronin's own dash-compatible shell"
        kind @feat
        children (
            {
                id builtin-shell-design
                title "Decide how a builtin shell stands in for the default /bin/sh"
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T19:01:44.016286399Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T19:02:45.49457836Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "THE THREE RULINGS, verbatim, Brendan, 2026-08-20.\n\n1. The ask: \"earlier on we said we should replace /bin/sh nonsense with a builtin shell. https://github.com/necessary-nu/nsh is a dash compatible drop-in as a lib\"\n\n2. The model, confirming exec-self after a parse-to-argv alternative was weighed: \"hmm, no, the original approach is the foolproof one isn't it\"\n\n3. The dependency: \"no, I want to use a normal git dep\"\n\nAll three are recorded in `plan/decisions/builtin-shell.md` as `[dec:ronin:builtin-shell]`, which carries the reasoning; this entry is the ledger copy so the words are on the node that decided.\n\nWHY EXEC-SELF RATHER THAN AN EMBEDDED SHELL, from nsh's own documentation rather than from a guess. `nsh::Shell` reaps EVERY child of its process — `wait3(status, flags, NULL)` is `waitpid(-1)` — so a shell driven inside Ronin's supervisor would collect the compiler the scheduler is waiting on, and the scheduler's wait would get `ECHILD` for a status sitting in a job table it cannot see. The crate says plainly that this is not fixable by tracking pids: reaping is destructive, the only peek-without-reap primitive returns the same foreign child forever and turns a blocking wait into a spin, and dispatching properly would need the shell to own SIGCHLD for the whole process, which is precisely what a build engine cannot delegate. Separately, `fork` without `exec` from a threaded host carries only the calling thread and the library's children allocate before they exec — the `Command::pre_exec` hazard, same cause, not removable. Exec-self answers both by construction and removes the simple-versus-complex classification seam as a bonus: every line that reaches the shell gets complete dash semantics.\n\nTHE MECHANISM: `Command::new(std::env::current_exe()?).arg0(<the spelling the build named>)`. `std::os::unix::process::CommandExt::arg0` is safe and stable, so no `/proc/self/exe` string is spelled by hand and no `unsafe` is written. Failure modes, and what each does: `current_exe()` fails (no /proc, or a platform that cannot answer) — fall back to spawning the named shell exactly as today, which is the behaviour being replaced, so a failure degrades to correct-but-external rather than to a broken build. `current_exe()` answering a deleted path (Linux appends \" (deleted)\") — same fallback, tested for by asking whether the answer is a file.\n\nWHY argv[0] IS `/bin/sh` AND NOT `sh`, measured before it was written down. dash prefixes every diagnostic with argv[0] exactly as it was written:\n\n    $ /bin/sh -c nosuchcmd      ->  /bin/sh: 1: nosuchcmd: not found   (127)\n    $ sh      -c nosuchcmd      ->  sh: 1: nosuchcmd: not found        (127)\n\nThat text is on a failing build's stderr and a consumer reads it. Passing the spelling the build was about to exec makes the substituted shell byte-identical to the one it replaced, and it generalises: stand in for `/usr/bin/sh` and the diagnostic says `/usr/bin/sh`. `Path::file_name` reduces `/bin/sh` to `sh`, which is what `multicall::select` matches, so identity and fidelity do not conflict. Confirmed against dash with an argv[0]-overriding exec: `echo $0`, `nosuchcmd`, `$#`, `kill -INT $$` and a TERM trap are all byte-identical and status-identical.\n\nTHE SUBSTITUTION BOUNDARY. The builtin shell stands in for the DEFAULT shell and nothing else, and both front ends already decide \"default\" without being asked to:\n\n* kati `simple_command.rs:52` — `DEFAULT_SHELL = b\"/bin/sh\"`, and `direct_argv` refuses its fast path unless `shell == DEFAULT_SHELL`, because a makefile-set SHELL is a statement about which interpreter runs the recipe. `stand_the_shell` (kati evaluate.rs:497, commit a490e27) is what puts SHELL at the rank where a makefile's own assignment is a peer — so `$(SHELL)` is already the authoritative answer and reading it is all this needs.\n* Ninja mode `subprocess.rs:1088` — `SYSTEM_SHELL = \"/bin/sh\"`, reached by `ShellMode::Auto` (needing a shell) and `ShellMode::Compat`; `--shell X` is `ShellMode::Program(X)`.\n\nSo `SHELL = /bin/bash`, a target-specific SHELL, a command-line `SHELL=`, and `--shell /bin/zsh` all get what they asked for. `--shell /bin/sh` gets the builtin one, because the test is on the program and not on how it was requested.\n\nWHAT THE MANIFEST WRITES: `/bin/sh`, unchanged, and that is the reason the substitution is a spawn-time act rather than a compile-time rewrite. An emitted manifest must stay runnable by a stock Ninja, which has no builtin shell, so the shell named in it has to be one that exists on the machine. The same choice keeps the graph, `-n`, and the build log's command hash on their existing spelling, which is what makes the equivalence gate meaningful rather than re-recorded. Ninja mode's default output does not change at all: the shell is transparent.\n\nTHE DEPENDENCY. `nsh = { git = \"https://github.com/necessary-nu/nsh\" }`, the workspace member `crates/nsh`, package name `nsh`, 0.5.13, pinned by Cargo.lock's rev — HEAD of master at 1644cd66dd289f538b56b29015f3e692ea56e294 when this was decided. No vendor submodule: kati is a submodule because it is our fork and we commit into it, and nsh is upstream with its own plan discipline, so a fix there arrives by lock bump. The crates.io `nsh` is an unrelated project (nuta/nsh) and a registry fallback must never resolve to it — the git source is what the lock records, so it cannot. The frontend also needs `nsh-platform` from the same repository: `process_arguments()` keeps non-UTF-8 argv bytes intact where `std::env::args()` would panic, and `restore_shell_process_runtime_state()` undoes the three things Rust's runtime does that dash does not — SIGPIPE to SIG_IGN, /dev/null over closed fds 0/1/2, and a SIGSEGV handler on an alternate stack. Each is inherited or observable, and SIGPIPE is the one a build notices: a recipe's `cmd | head` needs the default disposition.\n\nThe closure is small: bstr (already ours), libc, rustix (already ours), nshedit, nshedit-plat, nshterm, postcard, unicode-width. Declared under `cfg(unix)` beside kati and rustix, because Windows has no shell in this position at all — Ninja hands the command line to CreateProcess — so there is nothing there to substitute for. NOT behind the `make` feature: a Ninja-only build wants its own shell too, which is what the ask says.\n\nA HOST NOTE, not a project constraint. This machine rewrites `https://github.com/` to `git@github.com:` via gitconfig `insteadOf`, and cargo's built-in fetcher cannot authenticate that rewrite from this session. `CARGO_NET_GIT_FETCH_WITH_CLI=true` on the first fetch populates `~/.cargo/git/db` and every later build resolves the locked rev from the cache. Nothing is committed for it: on a host without the rewrite the https URL fetches anonymously.\n\nWHAT THE FLIP HAS TO TOUCH, read off the code rather than guessed. Every site that would exec `/bin/sh`:\n\n* `subprocess.rs:1267` `shell_command`, `Auto`-needing-a-shell and `Compat` — the Ninja path.\n* `subprocess.rs:1245` `shell_command`, `ShellMode::Program(p)` where p is the default.\n* `subprocess.rs:1179` `direct_command`'s ENOEXEC interpreter, when `script_interpreter` answered the default.\n* kati `fileutil.rs:79` `run_command` — `$(shell)`, the in-process executor, and the regen replay. Two arms name a shell: the `/bin/sh -c \"<shell> <flags> \\\"<cmd>\\\"\"` wrapper used when `$(SHELL)` is relative or holds a space or `$`, and the plain `<shell> <flags> <cmd>` arm.\n* `src/make/cli/subninja.rs:433` — the compile-time `$(shell)` inside a recursive recipe.\n\nAND ONE SITE THAT IS NOT A SPAWN, which is the finding that shapes the flip. The Make executor does not hand the shell to the spawner as a program; `CommandLayout::launch` (src/make/layout.rs:110) composes it into a command STRING — `cd 'dir' && exec env -u 'X' 'Y=1' /bin/sh -c \"script\"` — and that string is handed to `subprocess.rs` as `Launch::Shell`. `needs_shell` rejects it (it starts with `exec`), so the outer shell is spawned and substituting at the spawn site substitutes the OUTER shell only. The outer shell then `exec`s away into `env`, which `exec`s the INNER `/bin/sh` named in the text — the real dash, resolved by path. So the recipe still runs under the system shell, and a string cannot say otherwise: naming Ronin's binary there would both break the emitted manifest and select Ninja mode, since argv[0]'s basename would be `ronin`.\n\nThe answer is to put the shell back in program position for the executor's path. `Launch::Direct`/`DirectLaunch` already carries argv, a directory and environment deltas as VALUES — it is what `launch_step` builds for a line kati marked direct — so the non-direct case can build `Launch::Direct { argv: [shell, flags, script], directory, environment }` instead of composing the same three things into text. The composed string stays exactly as it is for the graph, the manifest and the command hash; only what the executor spawns changes. As a side effect it deletes a transient `sh` and an `env` exec per recipe line, which is the opposite of the cost exec-self adds.\n\nRISK CARRIED INTO THE FLIP: the extra `execve`. Ronin's binary is statically linked and carries kati; dash is ~120 kB. A probe binary of nsh alone measured 2.4 MB and cost 3785 us per `-c :` invocation against dash's 2608 us — but the host was at load average 40 when that ran, so the ratio is indicative and the numbers are not results. Re-measure on a quiet host against the real binary before quoting anything.\n\nDIVERGENCES ALREADY FOUND, before any flip, by running nsh against dash directly (Debian's /bin/sh IS dash, so this is the differential suite):\n\n* `set -u` on an unset parameter. dash: `/bin/sh: 1: UNSETVAR: parameter not set`, status 2. nsh: `UNSETVAR: parameter not set`, status 1. Both the argv[0] prefix and the status differ. Not in nsh's sanctioned register.\n* `$((1/0))`. dash: `arithmetic expression: division by zero`. nsh: `arithmetic expression: division error`. Status 2 on both. Not in the register.\n* A command-file operand that cannot be opened. dash 2, nsh 127 — this one IS sanctioned upstream as `missing_command_file_status`, and it is reachable here only through the response-file path, whose file Ronin has just written.\n\nForty-nine other cases spanning pipelines, errexit under `-c` and `-ec`, a script file with and without `-e`, here-documents, subshells, traps, job control, quoting, globbing, `IFS`, arithmetic, `local`, `type`, `ulimit`, signals and non-UTF-8 bytes are byte-identical and status-identical to dash."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T19:03:02.522366214Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id builtin-shell-personality
                title "Answer to the name sh as a dash-compatible shell"
                implements {
                    target-impl ([spec:ronin:req:product.shell-identity])
                }
                deps (builtin-shell-design)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T19:04:47.019747117Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T19:26:15.16979018Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. Invoked as `sh`, Ronin is a shell, and it is the same shell whether or not the Make front end was compiled.\n\nWHAT IT IS. `multicall::run_as_shell` is the process entry point's first act, ahead of signal handlers and buffered streams — a shell must present the process it inherited, and every arrangement a build makes is one its children would see. It reads the invoked name the way `is_make_name` reads Make's (whole file name, `.exe` stripped), and on a match it calls `nsh_platform::restore_shell_process_runtime_state()` and hands `nsh::shellmain::main_fn` the argv bytes and `Streams::INHERIT`. That is the same three-line frontend nsh's own `nsh-cli` is, which is the point: anything the shell needs that is not public in the crate is a compile error rather than something a reader has to notice.\n\nThe shell is NOT a `FrontEnd` variant. `select` still answers Ninja or Make and knows nothing about this, because the shell is not a build language — it is the question \"is this a build at all\", asked earlier. `sh` is the one name; `dash` and `bash` are deliberately absent, because what Ronin stands in for is the shell a build resolved and answering to a second name would put it in the way of a shell somebody installed on purpose.\n\n`restore_shell_process_runtime_state` is not optional and not tidiness. Rust's runtime, between `_start` and `main`, ignores SIGPIPE, opens `/dev/null` over any closed standard descriptor, and installs a SIGSEGV handler on an alternate stack. dash does none of the three, and all three are inherited or observable — a recipe's `cmd | head` wants SIGPIPE's default disposition, and a test reads `SigIgn` out of `/proc/self/status` to prove it is the default here.\n\nWHY argv[0] IS `/bin/sh` RATHER THAN `sh`, which is the finding that shaped the whole design. dash prefixes its diagnostics with argv[0] as written. Measured:\n\n    /bin/sh -c nosuchcmd   ->  /bin/sh: 1: nosuchcmd: not found   (127)\n    sh      -c nosuchcmd   ->  sh: 1: nosuchcmd: not found        (127)\n\nThe first line is on a failing build's stderr. So a substitution passes the spelling the build was about to exec, `Command::arg0` puts it there, and `Path::file_name` reduces it to `sh` for the selector. The test asserts all three: `$0`, the not-found diagnostic byte for byte, and that `/usr/bin/sh` says `/usr/bin/sh` — an imitation of one fixed shell could not.\n\nTHE TESTS, 11 of them in `tests/shell.rs`, all green in both the default and the `--no-default-features` build: the name selects the shell; no other name does (`ronin -c 'echo hello'` is a build reading options, not a shell); the argv[0] spelling; a pipeline and its status; `-c` against `-ec`, which is the whole of `.POSIX:`; a script file, which is the response-file shape; a shell that interrupts itself leaving BY the signal rather than reporting `128+n`; the inherited process state; a non-UTF-8 argument surviving; and a twenty-case differential against the host's `/bin/sh` where that is dash. Nothing in the file signals anything but the shell's own process — `kill -INT $$` and nothing else — because a shell owns process groups and a test that reaches outside its own can reach the session.\n\nTHE FILED DIVERGENCES, asserted rather than omitted. `the_differences_from_dash_are_the_ones_that_were_filed` pins both sides of each, so a fix upstream fails the test and says which filing to close. Three are defects to file against nsh and one is sanctioned upstream:\n\n1. A FAILED WRITE. `echo a > /dev/full` — dash: `/bin/sh: 1: echo: echo: I/O error`, status 1. nsh: `/bin/sh: echo: I/O error`, status 2. Both the prefix (no line number, no repeated builtin name) and the status differ. This one is a build's business: it is what a full disk looks like.\n2. `set -u` ON AN UNSET PARAMETER. `set -u; echo \"${UNSETVAR}\"` — dash: `/bin/sh: 1: UNSETVAR: parameter not set`, status 2. nsh: `UNSETVAR: parameter not set`, status 1. Prefix and status again.\n3. ARITHMETIC DIVISION BY ZERO. `echo $((1/0))` — dash says `division by zero`, nsh says `division error`. Wording only; status 2 on both.\n4. A COMMAND FILE THAT CANNOT BE OPENED — dash 2, nsh 127. SANCTIONED upstream as `missing_command_file_status` (POSIX gives the failure 127; dash routes it through its generic shell-error 2). Ronin writes the response file it hands over, so no build reaches it.\n\nEverything else agrees byte for byte and status for status across some seventy probed behaviours: pipelines, errexit under `-c` and `-ec` and in `&&` chains and `if` and subshells and pipelines, script files with and without `-e`, here-documents, traps including EXIT and an ignored INT, `IFS` splitting, globbing, arithmetic, `case`, functions and `local`, `type`, `ulimit`, `umask`, `export -p`, `wait`, backgrounded jobs, quoting, `exec`, non-UTF-8 bytes, a 500-command script, and a child killed by a signal.\n\nTHE DEPENDENCY BROKE A STANDING CONSTRAINT, and this is the part worth reading twice. `plan/main.styx` records, from the kati workspace wiring: \"the constraint on the fork is: no proc-macro dependencies at all\", because `.cargo/config.toml` applies `-C target-feature=+crt-static` to every unit when target and host are the same triple, a proc-macro must be a dylib, and a statically linked target cannot produce one. nsh's closure holds exactly one: nsh -> nshedit (its line editor) -> postcard -> serde with the `derive` feature -> serde_derive. Nothing in Ronin or kati needs any of it, and no arrangement on this side can decline it, because features unify additively and nsh declares `nshedit` with its defaults on.\n\nThe note that recorded the constraint also recorded the escape hatch — \"setting build.target so cargo separates host from target flags ... it moves every artifact under target/<triple>/\" — and that is what is taken here, because the static link is worth MORE under a builtin shell than it was without one, not less: exec-self runs Ronin's own binary once per shell-needing recipe line, so the dynamic loader's work would be paid per line rather than per build. Measured on the probe before the tree was touched: `--target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` compiles serde_derive for the host without the target's flags and leaves crt-static on everything else. `target-applies-to-host = false` is accepted in the config and silently does nothing on stable, which is worth knowing before anyone tries it.\n\nWhat moved with it: `.cargo/config.toml` gains `[build] target` and `[net] git-fetch-with-cli` (nsh's own repository carries the same net setting, for the same reason — a gitconfig that rewrites github HTTPS to SSH hands cargo's built-in client a URL it cannot authenticate); six gate scripts compute `release=target/$(rustc -vV | sed -n 's/^host: //p')/release`; three examples derive their sibling binaries from `current_exe()` rather than spelling a profile path, which is strictly better than what they had; README says where the executable is. The pin costs an in-repo build on another host its own `--target`, and it is two lines to delete the day nsh's closure holds no proc-macro. That is the filing to raise upstream: a shell library an embedder links should not force a serialization framework's derive macro on a statically linked host, and `nshedit` is the place it enters.\n\nThe release gate that asserted \"every integration test that links the executable under a name of its own is reaching for the Make front end\" was true until today and is not any more, so it now names the two Make names it means. A shell test must NOT declare the `make` feature: the Ninja-only build is the one that most needs to know its shell is there.\n\nBUILD COST, recorded rather than claimed: the closure grows by bstr's default features (nsh asks for them, so regex-automata joins the build), libc, nshedit, nshedit-plat, nshterm, postcard, cobs, thiserror, serde, unicode-width and the proc-macro chain. The host was running two other agents' suites throughout, at load average 40 falling to 8, so no wall-clock number from this session is a result. A probe of nsh alone measured 3785 us per `sh -c :` against dash's 2608 us under that load; re-measure against the real binary on a quiet host before quoting anything."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id builtin-shell-flip
                title "Spawn the builtin shell wherever the default shell was resolved"
                implements {
                    target-impl ([spec:ronin:req:product.builtin-shell])
                }
                deps (builtin-shell-personality)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T19:31:08.570284247Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T20:14:33.324451827Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the battery is green under it. Every command a build hands to the default shell is now read by Ronin's own, and nothing a consumer reads changed.\n\nWHERE THE SUBSTITUTION HAPPENS. Five places, and each of them is a spawn where the shell was already a program rather than a word in a command line:\n\n* `subprocess::shell_command` — Ninja mode's `Auto`-needing-a-shell and `Compat`, and `--shell` when the program it names is the default.\n* `subprocess::direct_command` — a launch that names its own program, which is where the Make front end now puts the shell, and separately the `$SHELL` interpreter an executable file with no `#!` line is re-exec'd under.\n* kati `fileutil::run_command` — `$(shell)`, the in-process executor and the regen replay, covering both the plain `<shell> <flags> <cmd>` arm and the `/bin/sh -c \"…\"` wrapper kati uses when `$(SHELL)` is relative or holds a space or `$`.\n* `src/make/cli/subninja.rs` — a `$(shell)` deferred out of a recursive recipe and run at compile time.\n* `src/make/layout.rs::launch_step` — the one that needed a shape change, below.\n\n`shell_process(named)` is the whole rule: if `named` is `/bin/sh` and a builtin shell was declared, spawn this executable with `arg0(named)`; otherwise spawn `named`. `Command::arg0` is safe and stable, so no `/proc` path is spelled by hand and no `unsafe` is written.\n\nTHE DECLARATION, which is the part that took a second attempt. The first spelling asked `current_exe()` directly, and that is wrong in exactly the place it matters: `current_exe()` in a test binary linking this library names the TEST binary, which does not dispatch on its invoked name and would have been spawned as `/bin/sh` to run libtest. So the executable declares instead — `ronin::declare_builtin_shell()`, called by the process entry point immediately after the `sh` personality declined the invocation, which is the proof that this executable answers to `sh`. A test binary and an embedder declare nothing and get the machine's shell, exactly as before.\n\nTHE SHAPE CHANGE. The Make executor did not hand the shell to the spawner at all: `CommandLayout::launch_step` composed `cd 'dir' && exec env -u 'X' 'Y=1' /bin/sh -c \"script\"` and passed that STRING. Substituting at the spawn would have replaced only the OUTER shell, which then `exec`s `env`, which execs the inner `/bin/sh` spelled in the text — the machine's. And a string cannot say what the substitution needs to say: the program is this executable while `argv[0]` stays `/bin/sh`. Naming Ronin's binary in the text would both select Ninja mode (argv[0]'s basename would be `ronin`) and break the emitted manifest.\n\nSo a recipe line whose shell is the default now builds a `Launch::Direct` — argv `[shell, flags…, text]`, with the unit's directory and environment as VALUES, which `DirectLaunch` already carried for the shell-free lines kati marks. Two details are load-bearing and both are measured against what the string did: `.SHELLFLAGS` is split on whitespace with empty words dropped, because the command line spelled `sh  \"script\"` and a shell splits that into `sh` and a file operand — an empty argument would have been a different thing entirely — and a `.SHELLFLAGS` of several words split for the same reason. A recipe that names its own `SHELL` keeps the composed string untouched, which is the substitution boundary again: the argv form exists to name the default shell, and nothing else needs it.\n\nThat change also removed a transient `sh` and an `env` exec per recipe line, which is the opposite of the cost exec-self adds.\n\nThe deferred `$?` values a scheduler-bound recipe leaves for the build to fill in now substitute into both launch shapes: a command line takes the value quoted for the shell that will read it, an argument list takes it raw, because the word reaches the program unread. That is the response file's context exactly, so the two already-existing contexts covered it and the invariant comment claiming a `Launch::Direct` never carries one is gone with the thing that made it true.\n\nTHE FLAG, NOT A GLOBAL — caught by kati's own gate rather than by review. The stand-in was first a `OnceLock<PathBuf>` in `simple_command.rs`, and `kati/tests/no_globals.rs` refused it: `[spec:ronin:req:make.no-ambient-state]` is a gate, and a mutable process global is what it exists to keep out. It is `Flags::default_shell_program` now, set per compilation unit in `compile_unit` — per unit rather than once, because a recursive child reads with its own session and must not read with a different shell than its parent. `rkati` sets nothing and reads `$(shell)` with the machine's `/bin/sh`, exactly as before. kati commit 5120ed7 on `nplan/builtin-shell-flip`.\n\nWHAT THE MANIFEST WRITES: `/bin/sh`, unchanged, and the equivalence gate is the proof rather than the claim — 280 graphs compared against kati's own manifests, zero disagreements. The graph, `-n`, the description line and the build log's command hash are all untouched, which is what makes the rest of the battery a measurement rather than a re-recording. Verified by hand as well: `-n` still prints `exec env 'MAKEFLAGS=n' … /bin/sh -c \"…\"`.\n\nTHE BATTERY, run serially on the final tree, every one of them through `scripts/sandboxed`:\n\n* Ninja conformance — 425/425 pinned upstream tests, 10 differential CLI/tool cases, 86 build-outcome failure and directory cases each on its recorded exit status, 3 invocation-boundary cases, the Ninja→Ronin→Ninja persistence round trip, and the build-log content check. All green.\n* Make conformance — 387 runs against GNU Make 4.4.1: 286 identical byte for byte, 336 identical normalised, 51 differing, classified 17 defect / 2 recorded / 31 extension / 1 artefact. Zero unclassified. Identical to the baseline in every number.\n* Equivalence — 297 makefiles, 280 graphs compared, 1 both-refused, 75 make-rejected, 0 disagreements. Identical to the baseline.\n* Upstream inventory — 1285 discovery differences at unclassified 134 / compiler 0 / interface 25 / narration 1126. Identical to the baseline, compiler bucket still zero, and no row moved, so there was nothing to audit.\n* Projects — vim 9.2 and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules, each built from its own Makefile at `-j8`. Thousands of recipe lines through the builtin shell, which is the evidence the corpora cannot give.\n* Workspace tests 955/0, up from 940 by the 15 tests these two nodes added. `--no-default-features` 751/0, up from 737 by 14 — the Ninja-only build has the shell too, which is what the ask asked for.\n* make_port 4/4 over 920, inside the workspace run.\n\nNO DIVERGENCE APPEARED UNDER THE FLIP. The four filed against nsh were found by running it against dash directly, before any of this landed, and none of them is reachable from a build: a failed write's prefix and status, `set -u`'s prefix and status, arithmetic division-by-zero's wording, and the upstream-sanctioned status for a command file that cannot be opened. Debian's `/bin/sh` IS dash, so the whole battery above is a differential suite for the substitution, and it did not move by a byte.\n\nWHAT IS STILL OPEN, filed as `builtin-shell-oversized-recipe-line` with a reproducer: a recipe holding a line over the response-file threshold keeps the assembled script and its single `Launch::Shell`, whose text names `/bin/sh` — so that one script is read by the machine's shell. Nothing in any corpus, in vim or in zsh reaches it.\n\nNOT DELIVERED, and this is the one the dispatch asked about: `lint-reads-with-ronins-own-make` does not fall out of this. Its problem is that `$(MAKE)` is a STRING a recipe runs, and a string cannot carry an `argv[0]` override any more than it could for the shell — `exec -a` is a bashism and dash has none. What would answer it is a `make`-named symlink in a per-run directory, which is a filesystem side effect per build rather than something this node produced, so it stays filed.\n\nTIMING: not reported. The host carried two other agents' suites throughout, at load averages between 8 and 46, and a number taken against that is not a result. What is worth measuring on a quiet host, and is not measured here: the extra `execve` per shell-needing recipe line. The release binary is 6.1 MB, statically linked, against dash's ~120 kB — which is why `crt-static` had to survive the dependency rather than be traded away for it."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id builtin-shell-oversized-recipe-line
                title "Give a recipe too long to be an argument the builtin shell too"
                deps (builtin-shell-flip)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T20:13:46.416017248Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by the flip that closed everything else, with a reproducer, because it is the one recipe shape still read by the machine's shell.\n\nWHAT HAPPENS. `CommandLayout::launches_line_by_line` (src/make/layout.rs) refuses the per-line split when any line exceeds the response-file threshold — the file is named per edge, so several lines would want one name. Such a recipe keeps the assembled script and the single launch that goes with it, and that launch is `Launch::Shell` over the composed command line:\n\n    cd 'dir' && exec env -u 'X' 'Y=1' /bin/sh out.rsp\n\nRonin's own shell runs THAT line — `needs_shell` sends it to a shell and the substitution puts this executable there — but the line's own work is to `exec env`, and `env` execs the `/bin/sh` spelled inside it, which is the machine's. So the script itself is read by dash rather than by the shell the rest of the build uses.\n\nREPRODUCER, measured on the release binary:\n\n    printf 'out:\\n\\t@readlink /proc/$$$$/exe > out; : %s\\n' \"$(python3 -c 'print(\"x\"*120000)')\" > Makefile\n    make            # `make` a symlink to ronin\n    cat out         # /usr/bin/dash\n\nAgainst the same recipe under the threshold, which answers with the path of the ronin binary. Everything else already does: an ordinary recipe line, a line kati marked shell-free, a line holding a scheduler-bound `$?`, a nested `$(MAKE)` line, a `$(shell)` in the makefile, a `$(shell)` deferred out of a recursive recipe, and every Ninja-mode command including `--compat` and `--shell /bin/sh`.\n\nWHY IT WAS NOT CLOSED WITH THE REST. The other shapes reach the spawn as a `RecipeStep`, whose shell is a field, so the launch can name a program and an `argv[0]` separately. This one reaches it as a command STRING, and a string cannot say \"the program is this executable while `argv[0]` stays `/bin/sh`\" — which is the whole mechanism. The string is also the edge's command: the graph's, the manifest's, `-n`'s and the build log's hash, and none of those may change, so rewriting it is not available either.\n\nWHAT IT LOOKS LIKE IT WILL TAKE. The whole-script launch has to become a `Launch::Direct` the way the per-line one did — argv `[shell, flags-or-rsp-path, script-or-nothing]`, with the unit's directory and environment as values rather than as a `cd` and an `env` in front of text — while the composed string stays exactly as it is for everything that reads it. The pieces are in hand at both places that build it (`src/make/recipe.rs` at launch time, `src/make/sink.rs` at declare time); what is missing is somewhere on `LateCommand` and `SinkRule` to carry a launch for the whole recipe, and a decision about the two per-line fields a whole recipe has no single answer for — `ignore_errors` is the recipe's own and `runs_while_pretending` would have to be true if any line carries the marker.\n\nHOW BIG IT IS IN PRACTICE. A single recipe line over 100 kB. Nothing in the conformance corpus, the equivalence corpus, the upstream suite, vim or zsh reaches it — the full battery is green with this open. The consequence while it stands is not a wrong build but an inconsistent one: one recipe in a hundred thousand is read by a different dash-compatible shell than its neighbours, and the two agree on everything the battery measures."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T00:49:48.851882348Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T01:15:52.721058793Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "THE FILING NAMED ONE SHAPE AND THERE ARE THREE. Probed on the pinned release binary at 1dc5603 before anything was written, each recipe a two-command line so the shell is not exec'd away (`readlink /proc/$$/exe > out; : ...`, the trick tests/shell.rs already uses):\n\n* a line over the response-file threshold — `/usr/bin/dash`. The filed shape.\n* a recipe with a depfile, `out: .KATI_DEPFILE := out.d` — `/usr/bin/dash`, with no 100 kB anywhere in it. kati withholds `SinkRule::steps` when `depfile.is_some()` because the extraction rewrote the script and the lines are no longer what runs, so the sink records no launches and the edge falls back to the composed command line.\n* the segments a recipe is cut into around a composed `$(MAKE)` — the lines written ahead of the invocation and the lines written after it, both `/usr/bin/dash`. `steps` is withheld for the same reason (`contains_recursive`), and each segment becomes an executor rule of its own on an edge of its own, neither of which passes the place the flip fixed.\n\nAn ordinary line and the child Make's own recipe came back as Ronin, as the flip recorded. Its note said \"nothing in any corpus, in vim or in zsh reaches it\", which was true of the shape it measured and not of the defect: a recipe with a depfile is ordinary, and `.KATI_DEPFILE` is product surface.\n\nTaken as one node, because it is one defect with one cause — a recipe that reaches the executor as the composed command line rather than as its launches — and one mechanism answers all three. Splitting it would have landed the same helper twice."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T01:16:30.754788563Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered. Every script the Make front end composes is now launched as a program, and nothing a consumer reads moved.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. `CommandLayout::launch_script` (src/make/layout.rs) is `launch_step`'s answer for a whole script: argv `[shell, flags…, script]` where the script is an argument, argv `[shell, path]` where it is a response file — the two shapes `launch` writes into a command line, said to the spawn instead, with the unit's directory and environment as values rather than as a `cd` and an `env` in front of text. It returns `None` for a recipe naming a shell of its own, which is the same substitution boundary `launch_step` draws: there is no executable to stand in for a shell that is not the default one. The `.SHELLFLAGS` splitting the flip wrote inline came out into `shell_flag_words` and both callers use it, because an empty `.SHELLFLAGS` has to vanish rather than become an empty argument in either.\n\nWHERE IT IS APPLIED, which is where the three shapes are:\n\n* src/make/recipe.rs, the recipe expanded at launch. The `else` arm that used to hand over an empty step list now hands over one step, reading the response file when `launch` wrote one and taking the script as an argument when it did not.\n* src/make/sink.rs, an ordinary rule the compiler read for itself — the depfile shape. `declare_rule` records a `SettledScript` where it cannot record per-line launches, and `record_late_bindings` turns it into the step once the edge exists.\n* src/make/sink.rs, the segments of a recursive recipe. `SubninjaInvocation::preceding_script` and `SubninjaRule::residual_script` carry one each to `stage_preceding_lines` and `complete_subninja`, which are where those segments get their edges.\n\nWHY IT IS HELD RATHER THAN FINISHED AT DECLARE TIME, which is the whole reason there is a `SettledScript` at all: a script too long to be an argument is read out of a response file named `$out.rsp`, and a rule does not know its edge's output. The launch is settled where the edge is, from `graph.path(output)` through the same `CommandLayout::response_file` the launch-time path uses — one function, two callers, so the two spellings of that path cannot drift. The layout travels on the held value too, because the unit that declared the rule is no longer the current one by the time a recursive recipe's segments reach their edges: the children have been compiled in between, each with a layout of its own. `stage_preceding_lines` and `complete_subninja` run after the parent's `take_late_edges`, so `take_settled_edges` drains what they pushed once the composition is over (src/make.rs).\n\nTHE TWO FIELDS A WHOLE RECIPE HAS NO SINGLE ANSWER FOR. `ignore_errors` is the recipe's own, exactly as the filing said and exactly what the command binding already carried: kati sets it only where every line said so. `runs_while_pretending` is `false` — NOT the filing's \"true if any line carries the marker\", and the reason is `-q` rather than `-t`. A step marked as one that runs anyway is one `question_edge` RUNS while the question is being asked; today an assembled-script edge answers \"there is work to do\" and starts nothing. Marking it would have `-q` and `-t` run a hundred kilobytes of shell that GNU Make would not have run — Make runs the marked LINES of a recipe and skips the rest, and a script assembled into one process can do neither. Between doing something Make does not and not doing something Make does, under two modes whose promise is to change nothing, the second is the one to keep. So this landing says nothing new about `-t` or `-q`, which is what makes the battery a measurement of the identity fix and nothing else. The fidelity question is filed as `pretending-over-an-assembled-recipe`.\n\nFIXTURE, tests/shell.rs `a_whole_recipe_reads_through_this_shell`, beside the flip's own. It gates all three shapes plus the boundary and the failure answer, on effects: which binary `/proc/$$/exe` names, the exit status of an all-`-` recipe over the threshold, and the status of one that fails. The 120 kB line is generated in the test rather than checked in.\n\nTEETH, measured by parking each half in turn rather than asserted:\n\n* `launch_script` returning `None` unconditionally — fails at the oversized assertion, `left: \"/usr/bin/dash\"`.\n* `settle_script` returning `None` — fails at the depfile assertion (shell.rs:567).\n* the `stage_preceding_lines` push alone parked — fails at `before` (shell.rs:582).\n* the `complete_subninja` push alone parked — fails at `after` (shell.rs:584).\n\nEach shape is held by its own assertion, and the tree restored between measurements.\n\nWHAT DID NOT MOVE. The composed command line is untouched: it is still the edge's `command`, still what the manifest holds, still what `-n` prints and what the build log hashes, and still what a failing recipe is reported as. The response file is still named per edge and still written from the manifest binding — the launch reads the path the build wrote rather than one it recomputed. Equivalence is the proof: 280 graphs against kati's own manifests, 0 disagreements.\n\nGATES, run serially through scripts/sandboxed on a quiet host, after the tree was restored from the last teeth measurement: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean and the same with `--no-default-features`; workspace 961 passed / 0 failed, up from 960 by the one test added; `--no-default-features` 753 / 0, unchanged because the fixture is Make's; all three clippy passes clean — the first found two lints on one `match` in the new code and they were fixed rather than allowed; `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean; make_port 4/4 over 948 cases with no recording rewritten; conformance 387 runs, 286 identical raw and 336 normalised, 51 differing at 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact, nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 1 both-refused / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 at 132 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1129 narration, `tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv` byte-identical and no drift line; ninja conformance 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content; `check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 17s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 31s at -j8 — thousands of recipe lines and both trees' recursive recipes through the builtin shell, which is the evidence a corpus cannot give.\n\nFOUND WHILE PROBING, unrelated to this and filed as `a-recursive-recipe-that-writes-its-own-target-first`: a recipe whose first line writes the target it is the recipe for, and whose second line is a composed `$(MAKE)`, stops after the first line. The staged preceding-lines edge creates the target, and the wrapper is then current. GNU Make 4.4.1 runs all three lines and the child."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T01:18:09.00992716Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id builtin-shell-flip-left-the-clippy-gate-red
                title "Clear the redundant closure the shell flip left in the lint gate"
                kind @fix
                deps (builtin-shell-flip)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T20:41:26.336535563Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by the next dispatch's first gate run, not by the flip's own battery: `cargo clippy --lib --bins --no-deps -- -D warnings -W clippy::pedantic -W clippy::nursery` — the exact line scripts/check-release.sh runs — fails on a tree that is otherwise clean at e8caf4b.\n\n    error: redundant closure\n       --> src/make/layout.rs:197:28\n        |\n    197 |                     .split(|byte| byte.is_ascii_whitespace())\n        |  help: replace the closure with the method itself: `u8::is_ascii_whitespace`\n\n`git blame` puts the line in e8caf4b itself — it is the `.SHELLFLAGS` split the flip added when `launch_step` moved a default-shell recipe line from a composed command string to `Launch::Direct`. `redundant_closure_for_method_calls` is pedantic, so `cargo check` never says a word about it and the workspace test run does not either; only the clippy pass does, and the flip's recorded battery does not list one.\n\nConfirmed pre-existing rather than inherited from the work in flight: measured with the kati submodule restored to its pinned 5120ed7 and the tree free of any other change. Behaviour is not in question — the closure and the method are the same call — so the whole of it is that the gate every dispatch runs before committing is red, and a red gate teaches the next reader to skip it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T20:41:30.691706625Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T20:55:01.479515339Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Delivered, and the filing was one finding short of what the gate actually held: the shell wave left THREE lint findings, one in each of the three clippy passes scripts/check-release.sh runs, and each pass stops at the first crate that fails so only running all three finds all three.\n\n1. `src/make/layout.rs:197` — `redundant_closure_for_method_calls` on `.split(|byte| byte.is_ascii_whitespace())`, the `.SHELLFLAGS` split the flip added. Now `.split(u8::is_ascii_whitespace)`. Pedantic, so only the first pass (ronin lib+bins, `--no-deps`, pedantic+nursery) sees it.\n\n2. `tests/shell.rs:305` — `type_complexity` on `[(&str, (&str, i32), (&str, i32)); 4]`, the filed-divergence table `builtin-shell-personality` added. Now a `FiledDifference` alias, which also names what the three parts are. This one is in the SECOND pass (all-targets, pedantic and nursery off), so it is a correctness-register lint rather than a style one, and it was invisible until the first pass was clean enough to reach it.\n\n3. `kati/src-rs/fileutil.rs:87` — `too_many_arguments` (8/7) on `run_command`, which the flip pushed over by adding `default_shell_program`. Fixed by gathering the three arguments that always travel together — the shell's program, its flag, and the stand-in executable — into `ShellToReadWith`, since all three call sites (`exec.rs`, `regen.rs`, `func.rs`) pass all three and none passes one without the others. Six parameters again. Third pass (`-p kati --all-targets -D warnings`), and likewise unreachable until the second was clean.\n\nNo behaviour is in question in any of the three: two are spellings of the same call and the third destructures the struct into the same three bindings the parameters were.\n\nWHERE: kati c9539ac on nplan/builtin-shell-flip-left-the-clippy-gate-red, pushed before this pin.\n\nGATES, run serially. The host was quiet. cargo fmt --all --check clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; all three clippy passes clean — ronin lib+bins `--no-deps` pedantic+nursery, ronin `--all-targets --no-deps` with the groups off, and `-p kati --all-targets` at plain `-D warnings`; `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean; workspace 955 passed / 0 failed; `--no-default-features` 751 / 0; make_port 4/4 over 920 with no recording rewritten; conformance 336 identical / 51 differing normalised and 286 raw, 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact, nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 1 both-refused / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 at 134 unclassified / 0 compiler / 25 interface / 1126 narration with the tsv byte-identical and no drift line; ninja conformance 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 15s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s at -j8.\n\nA NOTE FOR THE NEXT DISPATCH, because it cost a rebuild here: `cargo test --workspace` runs the dev profile and `target/<triple>/debug/incremental` reached 2.0 GB on a host with 4 GB free. `CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0` on the gate runs keeps it from coming back; the directory is safe to delete."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-20T22:00:16.906492482Z
