            {
                id pretending-over-an-assembled-recipe
                title "Say what -t and -q do to a recipe that reaches one shell whole"
                kind @fix
                deps (builtin-shell-oversized-recipe-line)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T01:17:08.33679817Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by `builtin-shell-oversized-recipe-line`, which had to decide the field and decided it the conservative way.\n\nWHAT IS AT STAKE. `LateStep::runs_while_pretending` is GNU Make's `COMMANDS_RECURSE` in words the engine can act on: a step marked so runs even where the build is standing in for its edge. `-t` touches the outputs and runs the marked lines; `-q` answers \"there is work to do\" without running anything, EXCEPT the marked lines, which it runs and reads the status of (job.c:954 — one is \"something to do\", zero is success, anything else is a failure). A recipe handed over as its command lines gets this per line, which is exactly what GNU Make does.\n\nA recipe that reaches ONE shell as an assembled script cannot: it is one step, and GNU Make's answer for the recipe it came from is \"run these lines and skip those\". Three shapes are in that position — a line too long to be an argument, a script a depfile extraction rewrote, and the segments a recipe is cut into around a composed `$(MAKE)` — and the shell landing left the field `false` on all of them, which is what the command line it replaced was given.\n\nWHY FALSE RATHER THAN THE FILING'S \"TRUE IF ANY LINE CARRIES THE MARKER\". Under `-q` a marked step RUNS. A recipe of a hundred kilobytes, or a depfile compile, running because the user asked a question, is a mode breaking its own promise; not propagating a `-t` into a child Make is a smaller wrong than running work nobody asked for. It is also not a regression: nothing about `-t` or `-q` moved in that landing.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY WRONG TODAY, and what to measure first: under `-t`, a recipe whose lines include a `+`-prefixed or `$(MAKE)`-naming line that could not be lifted out is touched instead of recursed, so a child tree that GNU Make would have touched is not. Under `-q` such a recipe answers \"work to do\" without running the line GNU Make would have run, which can change the answer. Both need an oracle probe against 4.4.1 before anything is written, and the interesting question is whether the marked lines can be split out of the assembled script for these two modes ALONE — the response file is per edge and only one line can use it, but under `-t` and `-q` the other lines are not running, so the constraint that forced the assembly may not be there.\n\nThe sink knows which lines were marked only where kati handed the steps over at all: a depfile recipe and a recursive recipe arrive with `SinkRule::steps` empty, so the marker would have to come from somewhere else for those two."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T05:24:15.113785729Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T05:24:51.839394876Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Delivered as the probe's answer plus a recorded, gated divergence. The gap is real, it is smaller than the filing said, and it is not one thing.\n\nWHAT THE PROBE FOUND, oracle at reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, effects and exit status only.\n\nFIRST, THE FILING NAMED THREE SHAPES AND ONLY TWO OF THEM REACH THIS. A depfile recipe does NOT: Make mode compiles with `RecipeExpansion::Launch` (src/make/cli.rs), every recipe is expanded when its edge is about to run, and `DeferredRecipes::expand` computes `recipe_steps` unconditionally — the `depfile.is_some() || contains_recursive` exclusion that empties `SinkRule::steps` is on the CONSTRUCTION path, which is the library entry for a caller that runs the graph itself. Measured rather than reasoned: a `goal.o: goal.c` recipe with `-MD -MF goal.d -c` and a `+` line runs the marked line and skips the compile under both `-t` and `-q`, byte-identical to 4.4.1. So the depfile shape was already right and needs nothing.\n\nThe two that do reach it:\n\n| shape | `-t` | `-q` |\n| a composed `$(MAKE)`'s segments | GNU runs both `+` lines and touches `goal` and the child's target; Ronin touches `goal` and runs neither | GNU writes `before.out` and exits 1; Ronin exits 1 having written nothing |\n| a recipe held together by a line over 100 kB | GNU runs the `+` line either side of the long one; Ronin runs neither | GNU runs a `+` line written BEFORE the long one; Ronin does not. With the long line first, GNU does not run it either and Ronin agrees |\n\nAnd the row that is NOT this node's, established by probing an ordinary line-by-line recipe: under `-n` GNU runs a `+` line and Ronin does not, whether the recipe is assembled or not. That is `Pretending` (src/build/command.rs) saying what it says — \"`-t` steps aside for a step the front end says runs anyway, and `-n` does not, because a dry run over a graph that already holds every edge has nothing to learn by starting one\" — which is the compiled-intent architecture and a standing decision. It is written down here so the next reader does not fold it into the assembled-recipe question, which it has nothing to do with.\n\nSECOND, THE FILING'S OWN QUESTION — can the marked lines be split out for these two modes alone? — HAS TWO DIFFERENT ANSWERS.\n\n* For the composed segments: NOT YET, and not for a reason of Ronin's. kati hands the segments over as text and withholds their lines (`SinkRule::steps` empty, `SinkSubninja::preceding` a `SinkCommand`), so nothing downstream knows which line carried the `+`. The lines exist inside kati and giving them out is an ordinary change to code we own. Filed as `a-composed-recipe-segment-arrives-without-its-lines`, with the route.\n* For the oversized-line recipe: the lines are already in hand — `expanded.steps` is populated for every launch-expanded recipe — and the obstacle is the response file. `$out.rsp` is named per EDGE and written once from the WHOLE assembled script, so a segment that used it would read the whole recipe rather than itself. Splitting only under `-t` and `-q` sidesteps that entirely, because the long line runs in neither mode — but it makes an edge's steps depend on the switch the run was started with, which is a hazard to own rather than a free lunch. Filed as `an-oversized-recipes-marked-lines-cannot-be-split-out`, with all three shapes weighed.\n\nTHIRD, WHAT WAS NOT DONE AND WHY. Marking the assembled script `runs_while_pretending: true` remains rejected for the reason `builtin-shell-oversized-recipe-line` rejected it, and the probe strengthens the case rather than weakening it: under `-q` GNU Make runs the marked lines it meets BEFORE the first unmarked one and stops there. An assembled script has no \"before\"; marking it would run every line of the recipe — a hundred kilobytes of shell, or a whole recursive segment — under a mode whose promise is to change nothing. Between doing something Make does not and not doing something Make does, under `-t` and `-q`, the second stays the one to keep.\n\nWHAT WAS DELIVERED, so that none of this can be quietly lost:\n\n* Two corpus cases carrying a `divergence` file each — `a-touch-over-a-composed-recipes-marked-line` and `a-question-over-a-composed-recipes-marked-line` — naming the node that owns the gap. `tests/make_port.rs` fails in BOTH directions, so the day someone hands the segments their lines over, the gate says so rather than the record quietly becoming a lie.\n* Two corpus cases with no `divergence` file — `a-marked-line-runs-while-a-recipe-is-touched` and `a-marked-line-runs-while-a-recipe-is-questioned` — which are the positive claim: where the lines exist, the marker works, under `-t` and under `-q`. They pass in both states on purpose. They are the guard on the path that is right, and without them a fix for the two above could break this one without anything noticing.\n\nDELIBERATELY NOT ADDED to `docs/make-oracle-divergences.md`. That document's last section is for the one place the disagreement is a DECISION rather than a defect, and this is a defect with two owners and a route out of each. The `divergence` sidecar covers both kinds — its own doc says \"a defect this case is known to reach, and the node that owns it\" — and that is the mechanism this belongs in. Should `an-oversized-recipes-marked-lines-cannot-be-split-out` end in a decision rather than a fix, that is when the document gains a section.\n\nTEETH, measured by removing the two `divergence` files and keeping everything else:\n\n    2 of 960 ported cases diverge from GNU Make's build intent:\n    a-question-over-a-composed-recipes-marked-line: 'before.out' was not produced\n    a-touch-over-a-composed-recipes-marked-line: 'after.out' was not produced\n\nThe two agreement cases have no teeth against this tree by construction; their teeth are against the future, which is what a guard is for.\n\nGATES for this landing, which adds four corpus directories and touches no product code: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; workspace 969 passed / 0 failed, unchanged because corpus cases are directories rather than test functions; make_port 4/4 over 960 case directories with four recorded divergences live, none of the six pre-existing ones rewritten. The remaining corpus gates read no file this commit touches; they are measured in the end-of-dispatch battery, which covers this commit as much as the four before it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-21T05:25:01.872267434Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id a-recursive-recipe-that-writes-its-own-target-first
        title "Finish a recursive recipe whose earlier lines already made its target"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T01:18:00.62267473Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found while probing recursive recipes for `builtin-shell-oversized-recipe-line`, on a shape reduced from that probe rather than from a real tree — but it is an ordinary shape: a recipe that produces its target and then recurses.\n\nTHE REPRODUCER, both tools run in the same scratch directory with nothing built:\n\n    pre.out:\n    \t@echo before > pre.out\n    \t@$(MAKE) -f sub.mk sub\n    \t@echo after >> pre.out\n\nwith `sub.mk` making `sub.out`. GNU Make 4.4.1 (reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make) runs all three lines: `pre.out` holds `before after`, and `sub.out` exists. Ronin runs the first line and stops:\n\n    [1/1] echo before > pre.out\n    ronin: no work to do.\n\n`pre.out` holds `before`, `sub.out` was never made, and the exit status is zero — so a build that did a third of the work reports success.\n\nWHY. A composed `$(MAKE)` cuts the recipe into segments: the lines ahead of the invocation are staged as an edge of their own, because the child Makefile is read off the disk those lines write to, and the wrapper edge carries the lines after it. The staging edge runs in a build of its own, and it writes `pre.out` — which is the wrapper's own target. When the composition finishes and the real build reaches the wrapper, the target exists and has no prerequisite newer than it, so the wrapper is current and neither the child goals nor the residual lines run. `stage_recursive_wrapper` asked the freshness question BEFORE the preceding lines ran, and got the right answer then; nothing asks it again, and by the time the wrapper could run, the preceding lines have made the answer stale.\n\nGNU Make cannot have this problem: it decides once, before the recipe starts, and then runs the whole recipe.\n\nWHAT IT LOOKS LIKE IT WILL TAKE, read off the code rather than guessed. The wrapper's freshness decision has to survive its own recipe's earlier lines: either the wrapper is marked out of date once `stage_recursive_wrapper` says Dirty and the staging runs (the decision was taken; the target's date afterwards is the recipe's own work, not evidence of currency), or the staged preceding-lines edge declares the wrapper's outputs as things it has already written so the freshness comparison reads them as the recipe's own product. The first is closer to what GNU Make means: a target whose recipe has begun is a target being remade.\n\nNOT CAUSED BY THE SHELL WORK. Reproduced against the tree at 1dc5603's behaviour and unchanged by the launch substitution, which adds no freshness logic at all. Filed rather than fixed there because it is a freshness defect and that node was an identity fix."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T02:44:15.576286688Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T02:44:57.544114912Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered, and taken FIRST rather than second because the other two nodes of this family cannot be finished without it. `a-remade-makefile-that-composes-a-recursion-runs-the-probe` reaches exactly this shape one pass later — its staged line writes `gen.mk`, and every fix for the probe leaves the wrapper judged current unless this one is already in. So the order the dispatch suggested is inverted, and the reason is a dependency rather than a preference.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, confirmed against the filing rather than assumed. A composed `$(MAKE)` cuts a recipe into segments: the lines ahead of the invocation become an edge of their own — the child Makefile is read off the disk they write to — and the wrapper edge carries what is left. `stage_recursive_wrapper` asks whether the wrapper is out of date BEFORE that staging, gets the right answer, and the compilation then stops at the boundary and starts over. On the pass after, the target is on the ground, written by the recipe's own first line, with nothing newer behind it. Every ordinary reading says the target is current, so the wrapper short-circuits: `RecursiveWrapper::Current` pushes the probe edge and `continue`s, the child is never composed, and the residual lines never run. Exit 0 with a third of the recipe done.\n\nGNU Make cannot be in that position. It decides once, before the first line, and then runs the whole recipe. So the verdict has to survive the recipe's own work, and that is what this landing adds.\n\nWHERE IT WENT, three small pieces and nothing else:\n\n* `Edge::recipe_begun` (src/graph.rs) — a fact about work that has already happened, folded into the dirty computation ahead of every other arm. Deliberately NOT `always_dirty`: that field is `.PHONY` and yields to `-W` (`always_dirty && !assumed_new`), because a `.PHONY` name has no date for the switch to write over and a file does. This is not a claim about dates at all, and no switch can make it untrue.\n* `CompilationState::recipe_begun` (src/make.rs) — whether a settled boundary for this recipe is one of the recipe's OWN: `PrecedingLines(_)` or `ChildGroup(_)`. `ParentPrerequisites` is excluded on purpose — GNU Make settles a recipe's prerequisites before the recipe begins, so building them is no reason to call it started.\n* the two ends: `stage_recursive_wrapper` takes `begun` and lets it outrank the disk, and `complete_subninja`'s wrapper is marked so the BUILD runs it too. Marking only the compile-time decision is not enough: the completed wrapper carries the child targets as ORDER-ONLY inputs when it has residual lines, and an order-only input never makes an edge dirty, so the child would have run and `echo after` would not.\n\nAND ONE THING CLEARED. `mark_makefiles_settled` already unsets `always_dirty` on a Makefile the update reached and won, because from there the goals compare against the file rather than the rule; `recipe_begun` is unset in the same place for the same reason — a recursive recipe the update ran to its end is a recipe that is over.\n\n`compose_subninjas` came apart into itself and `compose_child_groups` because the added lines took it to 103 (limit 100). The extraction is the inner per-invocation loop whole, `None` where it used to return `incomplete_unit`; the commit gate found it and it was fixed rather than allowed.\n\nPROBE EVIDENCE, oracle at reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, effects and exit status compared, never text. The filed reproducer and four variants, all now byte-identical to 4.4.1:\n\n| shape | GNU 4.4.1 | Ronin before | Ronin after |\n| the filed one: first line writes the target | `pre.out` = `before after`, `sub.out` = `sub` | `pre.out` = `before`, no child, exit 0 | matches |\n| the CHILD writes the target | `pre.out` = `child after` | already matched | matches |\n| `.PHONY` wrapper | all three lines | already matched | matches |\n| two invocations in one recipe | `sub.out` = `a b`, `pre.out` = `before after` | `pre.out` = `before` | matches |\n| invocation first, target written after | `pre.out` = `after` | already matched | matches |\n| `-q` | exit 1, nothing on disk | matched | matches |\n| `-t` | `pre.out` and `sub` touched, `sub.out` absent | matched | matches |\n| `-B` | all three lines | ran the first line, then `false` and exit 2 | matches |\n\nThe `-B` row is the one worth reading twice: before this, a `-B` build of the filed shape ran the staged line and then executed the freshness probe, which is `false`. That is `a-remade-makefile-that-composes-a-recursion-runs-the-probe`'s symptom arriving through a switch rather than through a makefile, and it goes away here because the wrapper is now composed instead of being re-judged.\n\nSTILL DIVERGENT, measured and left: `-W pre.out`. GNU says `'pre.out' is up to date` and runs nothing; Ronin runs the whole recipe. The compile-time freshness probe is blind to `-W` — it stats the disk with a runtime that has no assumed-new set — so it says dirty and the staging follows. Before this landing the same blindness produced ONE line of the recipe instead of three, which is the same wrong answer with less of it; the fix makes the run self-consistent and does not make it right. Filed as `a-recursive-recipes-freshness-probe-cannot-see-dash-w`.\n\nFIXTURES, four cases in the make_port corpus, recorded from 4.4.1 with `MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1 MAKE_PORT_ORACLE=…` and nothing existing rewritten: `a-recursive-recipe-whose-earlier-line-writes-its-own-target`, `a-recursive-child-that-writes-the-recipes-own-target`, `two-recursions-in-a-recipe-that-writes-its-own-target`, `a-question-over-a-recursive-recipe-that-makes-its-own-target`. The corpus gates effects and exit status, which is what a Make compatibility claim is about, and it ignores dot-names so the compilation's own `.ronin_recipe_stage` proxy stays out of the recording.\n\nTEETH, measured by parking the source and keeping the corpus, not asserted:\n\n    2 of 944 ported cases diverge from GNU Make's build intent:\n    a-recursive-recipe-whose-earlier-line-writes-its-own-target: 'pre.out' holds \"before\\n\" where GNU Make left \"before\\nafter\\n\"\n    two-recursions-in-a-recipe-that-writes-its-own-target: 'pre.out' holds \"before\\n\" where GNU Make left \"before\\nafter\\n\"\n\nTwo of the four have teeth and two do not, and that is worth saying plainly: the child-writes-the-target case and the `-q` case already passed, and they are in the corpus as the boundary of the fix rather than as evidence for it — one says the fix did not have to reach the child's own writes, the other says a question still answers without running anything.\n\nGATES, serial, through scripts/sandboxed, on a quiet host: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; all three clippy passes clean; `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean; workspace 963 passed / 0 failed; `--no-default-features` 754 / 0; make_port 4/4 over 951 case directories, 944 run, nothing rewritten; conformance 286 identical raw and 336 normalised, 51 differing at 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact, nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 1 both-refused / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 at 132 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1129 narration with `tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv` byte-identical; ninja conformance 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content; `check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 17s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 30s at -j8. The `--no-default-features` check carries 28 dead-code warnings; the same 28 are at the parent commit, measured by parking the source, so nothing here moved them."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T03:41:39.090550018Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id a-remade-makefile-that-composes-a-recursion-runs-the-probe
        title "Compose a remade makefile's recursion instead of running the freshness probe"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T01:54:53.158405328Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found while building the fixture for `lint-reads-with-ronins-own-make`: it was the first shape tried, and it does not work at all.\n\nTHE REPRODUCER, four lines and a child:\n\n    all: ; @printf '%s\\n' '$(GENERATED)' > out\n\n    include gen.mk\n\n    gen.mk:\n    \t@printf 'GENERATED := from-generated\\n' > $@\n    \t@$(MAKE) -C sub marker\n\nwith `sub/Makefile` making `marker`. GNU Make 4.4.1 remakes `gen.mk`, runs the child, restarts the read and builds `all`. Ronin, whether the goal is `all` or `gen.mk` itself, and whether run as a build or under `-t lint`:\n\n    [1/1] false\n    FAILED: [code=1] gen.mk\n    false\n    ronin: build stopped: subcommand failed.\n    Makefile:3: gen.mk: No such file or directory\n\nexit 2, and nothing was made. Reproduced on 1dc5603 with the source restored to it, so it predates the shell and lint work of 2026-08-21.\n\nWHAT `false` IS. Two internal rules carry that command — the deferred-recipe placeholder (`deferred_rule_bindings`) and `subninja_probe`, \"used only while deciding whether a recursive wrapper's recipe is current. It is never allowed to execute.\" A recipe holding a composed invocation never reaches the deferred branch, `declare_rule` returning early into `subninja_rules` before it, so the one that ran is the freshness probe — an edge that reached the executor still wearing the rule it was staged with.\n\nWHY IT REACHES THE EXECUTOR, read off `compose_subninjas` (src/make.rs) rather than measured: a wrapper whose recipe has lines written ahead of the invocation is staged, and `state.stage(...)` answering false — the staged work has to be BUILT before the child Makefile can be read — returns `incomplete_unit` for the whole unit. `complete_subninja` never runs for that wrapper, so `set_edge_rule` never replaces the probe. For the GOAL pass the read goes around again and the second pass completes it; the compiler-input pass has no such round, and the makefile whose remake was left incomplete is exactly what that pass then tries to build.\n\nTHE FAMILY. `a-recursive-recipe-that-writes-its-own-target-first` is the same construction seen from the other side: there the staging DID run, wrote the wrapper's own target, and the wrapper was then judged current and skipped. Both are about a wrapper edge whose fate is decided while its own recipe is half-run, and a fix for one should be read against the other.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS TODAY. Any makefile that is remade by a recipe whose `$(MAKE)` line composes — `cd DIR && $(MAKE) …` or `$(MAKE) -f …` as a line's own command, which is the shape the census calls the good one — cannot be remade at all. Writing the same invocation behind a `;` works, because a nested invocation stays in the recipe as something to run. So the compiler's preferred shape is the one that fails, which is the sharpest part of this."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T02:55:47.274418169Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T03:34:05.026536622Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered. The compiler's preferred recursion shape works on the makefile that has to be remade before the read means anything — as a build, as a lint, and under `-B`.\n\nTHE FILING'S MECHANISM WAS RIGHT, and the probe confirmed it before anything was written: `state.stage(...)` answers false at the preceding-lines boundary, `compose_subninjas` returns `incomplete_unit`, `complete_subninja` never runs, and the wrapper edge for `gen.mk` is still wearing `subninja_probe`. What the filing did not have is WHO runs it, and that turned out to be the interesting half — the makefile update, `remake_makefiles`, which runs BEFORE the staged pass and whose targets are exactly the makefiles. The read's own graph was handed to a build that was then asked for a makefile the graph had not finished compiling.\n\nTHREE THINGS, and each of them is a separate way for the probe to be reached.\n\n1. A MAKEFILE MADE THROUGH AN UNFINISHED WRAPPER IS HELD BACK FROM THE UPDATE. The compilation now records the outputs of every wrapper it staged and did not finish (`CompilationState::unfinished`, carried out as `Loaded::unfinished_targets`), and `makeable_now` (src/make/cli/remake.rs) drops from the update whichever Makefiles reach one — `BuildGraph::blocked_targets` walks the whole input closure, because a Makefile made from something a recursive recipe produces is as blocked as one the recipe makes itself. The staged pass below then settles the boundary, and the pass after that has a wrapper carrying the recipe and remakes the Makefile through it.\n\n   HELD BACK RATHER THAN THE WHOLE UPDATE DEFERRED, and this is the part that was got wrong first and measured wrong. The first shape of this fix skipped the makefile update entirely whenever the read was incomplete. It fixed the node and it moved an inventory row: `variables/MAKE_RESTARTS.diff.2` went narration → unclassified, because deferring the update moves a GOAL's staged recipe lines ahead of it. GNU Make's order is makefiles first, then goals; that shape ran `echo recurse MAKE_RESTARTS=$MAKE_RESTARTS` before `MAKE_RESTARTS` had been counted. The surgical version leaves the order alone and the row went back. The inventory found this, not a test.\n\n2. THE STAGED WORK THAT REMADE A MAKEFILE RESTARTS THE READ. Without it, the fix above gets `gen.mk` written and then reads it as absent: `read_units` pins the bytes each unit's read got, on purpose, so that a staged repeat sees the text GNU Make's one read had. But a pass whose staged work WAS the recipe that remade a makefile is not a repeat, it is GNU Make's restart. So the Makefiles' stamps are compared across the staged pass exactly as `remake_makefiles` compares them across its own, and a move clears the journals and returns `Settlement::Restart`. The boundaries stay settled across it — `feature-restart-keeps-recursive-staging-done` — so the staged lines do not run twice.\n\n3. THE PROBE NEVER SURVIVES THE QUESTION. `subninja_is_dirty` became `settle_subninja_freshness`: ask, and if the answer is \"current\", take the question off the edge — phony, with `outputs_unaliased` so its outputs are read as the files they are rather than as names it stands in for. One act rather than two, because a wrapper that must run reaches `complete_subninja` and one that must not reaches nothing, and only the second could carry `false` into the graph the build is made from.\n\nAND `-B`, which is how that third route was found. `make -B` over a recursive recipe whose target already exists ran `false` and exited 2 — the filed symptom with no makefile anywhere near it. GNU Make's `always_make_flag` makes every target with a recipe out of date, and a recursive recipe is a recipe, but the compile-time freshness question was asked with a runtime that had never heard of the switch. `CompilationContext::always_make` carries it now, inherited by every child compilation because `-B` reaches a recursive child through `MAKEFLAGS`. With it the wrapper is composed instead of short-circuited, and sealing (3) is what makes the remaining short-circuit safe rather than merely quiet.\n\nPROBE EVIDENCE, against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, on effects and exit status:\n\n| shape | GNU 4.4.1 | Ronin before | Ronin after |\n| the filed reproducer, goal `all` | gen.mk made, child ran, `out` = `from-generated` | `[1/1] false`, `FAILED: [code=1] gen.mk`, exit 2 | matches |\n| the same, goal `gen.mk` | gen.mk made, child ran | exit 2 | matches |\n| the same under `-B` | as above | exit 2 | matches |\n| a remade include ABOVE the composing recipe, beside a plain one | both remade, child ran, `out` has both | exit 2 | matches |\n| the includes BELOW both rules | same | exit 2 | matches |\n| no line ahead of the invocation | gen.mk made by the child, `out` = `from-child` | already matched | matches |\n| `-B` over an already-current recursive wrapper | runs the whole recipe and the child | `[1/1] false`, exit 2 | matches |\n| `-t lint` over the filed reproducer | n/a | `FAILED: [code=1] gen.mk`, exit 2 | census reports 1 composed, gen.mk made, child ran, exit 0 |\n\nThe \"no line ahead of the invocation\" row is the boundary of the defect and is in the corpus for that reason: with nothing to stage the composition finishes in one pass, the wrapper carries a rule by the time the update wants it, and it worked all along.\n\nSTILL DIVERGENT, measured and filed rather than left to be discovered:\n* `-n`, `-t` and `-q` over a makefile remade by a COMPOSING recipe: GNU Make turns all three off across the makefile update and remakes the file for real; Ronin's staged pass honours the invocation's own switches, so gen.mk is never written and the read is truncated. The nested `;` shape already gets this right, which is what makes it a gap rather than a design. Filed as `a-staged-recipe-segment-obeys-n-and-t-where-a-remake-would-not`.\n* `-W` over a recursive recipe's target: the compile-time freshness question is blind to it. Filed as `a-recursive-recipes-freshness-probe-cannot-see-dash-w`.\n* the staged segment's proxy name leaves an empty `.ronin_recipe_stage/` in the build root. Pre-existing, newly visible because these shapes now run. Filed as `the-recipe-stage-proxy-leaves-a-directory-in-the-tree`.\n\nFIXTURES. Five make_port cases recorded from 4.4.1 with `MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1 MAKE_PORT_ORACLE=…`, nothing existing rewritten: `a-makefile-remade-by-a-recipe-that-composes-a-recursion`, `a-composing-remade-makefile-asked-for-by-name`, `a-remade-include-below-the-recipe-that-composes-a-recursion`, `forcing-a-recursive-recipe-whose-target-is-current`, `a-composing-remake-with-no-line-before-the-invocation`. Plus `tests/lint.rs::a_composed_remake_recursion_runs`, because the filing said the read hits it too and a report that dies is worse than a build that dies — a lint is the thing you run when the build already failed.\n\nTEETH, measured by parking the six source files and keeping the fixtures:\n\n    4 of 949 ported cases diverge from GNU Make's build intent:\n    a-composing-remade-makefile-asked-for-by-name: build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded\n    a-makefile-remade-by-a-recipe-that-composes-a-recursion: build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded\n    a-remade-include-below-the-recipe-that-composes-a-recursion: build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded\n    forcing-a-recursive-recipe-whose-target-is-current: build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded\n\n    thread 'a_composed_remake_recursion_runs' panicked at tests/lint.rs:621:5:\n    assertion `left == right` failed: [1/1] false\n    FAILED: [code=1] gen.mk\n      left: Some(2)\n     right: Some(0)\n\nFour of the five corpus cases and the lint fixture have teeth; the fifth is the boundary case and passes in both states, which is what it is there to say.\n\nTWO FUNCTIONS CAME OUT under the clippy line limit rather than being allowed: `makeable_now`, which is the held-back computation with its reasoning, and `after_staging`, which is what building the staged work comes to — one place where a pass either settles, restarts, or goes around.\n\nGATES, serial, through scripts/sandboxed, on a quiet host: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; all three clippy passes clean; `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean; workspace 964 passed / 0 failed (963 after the node before this, plus the lint fixture); `--no-default-features` 754 / 0, unchanged because everything added is Make's; make_port 4/4 over 956 case directories, 949 run, no recording rewritten; conformance 286 identical raw and 336 normalised, 51 differing at 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact, nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 1 both-refused / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 at 132 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1129 narration with `tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv` byte-identical — audited by regenerating it and diffing, which is how the MAKE_RESTARTS row above was caught and how the final shape was confirmed to move nothing; ninja conformance 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content; `check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 23s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 35s at -j8."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T03:41:28.62907032Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id lint-tests-leave-their-scratch-behind
        title "Take the lint suite's scratch directories away with it"
        kind @test
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T01:55:13.069707938Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Noticed while checking that `lint-reads-with-ronins-own-make`'s own staged directory was removed: it was, and hundreds of others were not.\n\n    $ ls -d /tmp/ronin-lint-* | wc -l\n    …several hundred, oldest from earlier dispatches\n\n`tests/lint.rs::scratch` names its directory `ronin-lint-<case>-<pid>` under `TMPDIR`, removes whatever is already there and creates it — and never removes it again. The removal at the start means a rerun under the same pid is clean, which is all the tests need to pass, so nothing has ever complained; what accumulates is one directory per case per pid, each holding a makefile or a manifest and whatever the case built. On this host that is `/tmp` entries in the hundreds, one of which was mistaken for a leak in the code under test before the test was read.\n\nEvery other suite in the tree uses `tempfile::tempdir()`, which removes on drop, and `tests/shell.rs` right beside it does. The fix is to use it here too; the only thing the named form buys is a directory a human can look at after a failure, which `TempDir::into_path` or a `--nocapture` print can give when it is actually wanted.\n\nTwo places, both in tests/lint.rs: `scratch` and the second helper at the manifest cases that spells the same thing again.\n\nNot urgent and not a defect in the product. Filed because a suite that leaves several hundred directories on a machine is the kind of thing that gets read as evidence of something else."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T02:01:29.754791396Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T02:08:38.09752365Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered, and it is smaller than the filing allowed for: one file, two helpers, and a gate so that nothing has to notice this by hand again.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG. `tests/lint.rs::scratch` and `reduction_at` both named a directory `ronin-lint-<label>-<pid>` under TMPDIR, removed whatever was there and created it. The removal is at the START, which is why nothing ever failed: a rerun under the same pid finds a clean directory. Nothing removes it at the end, so a run leaves one directory per case behind, and pids rotate. Measured on this host: 1354 directories, 6.6 MB, the oldest from earlier dispatches.\n\nWHAT IT IS NOW. A `Scratch` newtype over `tempfile::TempDir`, which removes on drop — the pattern `tests/shell.rs` beside it already uses. It carries `Deref<Target = Path>` and `AsRef<Path>`, so every case reads exactly as it did: `ronin(&directory, …)`, `directory.join(\"sub\")`, `Command::current_dir(&directory)` all still compile against the handle. The name is now a PREFIX rather than the whole of it (`tempfile::Builder::prefix`), which removes the collision the old form had to defend against with an up-front `remove_dir_all` — two runs at once cannot land in one directory any more, so the defence came out with it. `reduction_at` was rewritten to build on `scratch` rather than spell the naming a second time, which is the other half of the filing (\"the second helper that spells the same thing again\"): there is now one place that makes a directory in this file. `PathBuf` left the imports because nothing names it any more.\n\nTHE GATE, `a_scratch_directory_goes_away_with_its_test`. Takes a scratch, asserts it is a directory, drops it inside a block, and asserts the path is gone. It is deliberately written against BEHAVIOUR rather than against the type, so it compiles against the old helpers too and fails on what they do — which is what makes it a measurement rather than a tautology.\n\nTEETH, measured by parking rather than asserted. With the pre-fix helpers restored from HEAD and the gate kept:\n\n    thread 'a_scratch_directory_goes_away_with_its_test' panicked at tests/lint.rs:96:5:\n    the scratch outlived the test that made it: /tmp/ronin-lint-self-removing-42\n\nAnd the leak itself, measured the same way: `/tmp` swept to zero, then `cargo test --test lint` with the fix parked leaves 19 directories; with the fix in place it leaves 0. The sandbox shares `/tmp` with the session — probed rather than assumed — so those counts are of the same directory a developer would look at.\n\nTHE SWEEP. 1354 `/tmp/ronin-lint-*` removed on this host, and nothing else; `/tmp` holds none now, and none after the workspace suite ran twice.\n\nGATES for this landing, which is confined to one test file: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; all three clippy passes clean; `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean; workspace 963 passed / 0 failed (962 baseline plus this gate); `--no-default-features` 754 / 0 (753 plus the same gate, which is not behind the `make` feature); `--all-targets --no-fail-fast` 1016 / 0. The corpus gates — conformance, equivalence, upstream inventory, make_port, ninja conformance, check-make-projects — were not run for THIS commit and are not evidence it moved anything: the delta is `tests/lint.rs` alone, no product code, and no corpus gate reads that file. They are measured in the end-of-dispatch battery, which covers this commit as much as the two beside it. Said plainly rather than left to be inferred, because the host is at 2.5 GB free and running the battery three times was the wrong spend against a test-hygiene fix."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T03:41:43.537557598Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id a-recursive-recipes-freshness-probe-cannot-see-dash-w
        title "Let -W reach the question a recursive recipe is asked before it runs"
        kind @fix
        deps (a-remade-makefile-that-composes-a-recursion-runs-the-probe)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T03:32:09.911096577Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found while probing the switch matrix for `a-recursive-recipe-that-writes-its-own-target-first`, and left alone there because it is a different question from the one that node answered.\n\nTHE REPRODUCER, in an empty directory:\n\n    pre.out:\n    \\t@echo before > pre.out\n    \\t@$(MAKE) -f sub.mk sub\n    \\t@echo after >> pre.out\n\nwith `sub.mk` making `sub.out`. Run as `make -W pre.out pre.out`:\n\n    GNU Make 4.4.1: make: 'pre.out' is up to date.   nothing on disk, exit 0\n    Ronin:          runs all three lines and the child, exit 0\n\n`-W FILE` says \"pretend FILE is infinitely new\", so a target the switch names is up to date and its recipe does not run. Ronin runs the whole recipe.\n\nWHY. Whether a recursive recipe has to run is decided at compile time, not by the build: `stage_recursive_wrapper` (src/make.rs) adds the wrapper edge, then asks `GraphSink::settle_subninja_freshness`, which reaches `BuildGraph::edge_dirty_with` — and that builds a `RuntimeState` of its own, with no assumed-new set and no `-B`. `-B` was given to the compilation by `a-remade-makefile-that-composes-a-recursion-runs-the-probe` (`CompilationContext::always_make`), for the same reason and by the same route; `-W` was not, because it is a set of nodes rather than a flag and the plumbing is a different size.\n\nThe wrapper is therefore judged dirty, its earlier lines are staged, and from there the recipe is committed: `Edge::recipe_begun` says the recipe has started and nothing may un-start it.\n\nNOT A REGRESSION, measured on the parent commit: before that node, `-W` over this shape ran the FIRST line of the recipe and stopped — the same wrong answer with less of it. What changed is that the wrong answer is now self-consistent.\n\nWHAT IT WOULD TAKE. `-W`'s file list has to reach the compilation the way `always_make` now does. `BuildOptions` holds it (see `RuntimeState::assumed_new` and the `-W` reasoning in src/graph.rs's dirty computation), `CompilationContext` is where a per-invocation fact that the compile has to know already lives, and `edge_dirty_with` would take the set. Probe first: `-W` on a PREREQUISITE of a recursive recipe rather than on its target is the other half, and 4.4.1's answer there (`-W ph` over `out: in ph`) is already written down in src/graph.rs."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T05:07:20.098055516Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T05:07:58.537656562Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered, and it is the smaller of the two shapes the filing sketched: `-W` reaches the compile-time freshness question by the route `always_make` already takes, and stops there rather than being inherited.\n\nTHE FILING WAS RIGHT ABOUT THE MECHANISM. `stage_recursive_wrapper` (src/make.rs) asks `GraphSink::settle_subninja_freshness`, which reaches `BuildGraph::edge_dirty_with`, which builds a `RuntimeState` of its own — with nothing marked assumed-new. So a target `-W` named was judged dirty, its earlier lines were staged, `Edge::recipe_begun` said the recipe had started, and nothing could un-start it.\n\nTHE ONE THING THE FILING DID NOT KNOW, and it is what shaped the fix: `-W` IS NOT INHERITED. Probed against 4.4.1 before anything was written — under `make -W foo`, a recursive child's `MAKEFLAGS` reads ` --no-print-directory` and nothing else, where under `make -n` the same child reads `n --no-print-directory`. GNU Make does not put the switch in `MAKEFLAGS`, so a child never hears about a file the parent was told to pretend was new. `CompilationContext::always_make` is inherited by every child for exactly the opposite reason; `assumed_new` is deliberately EMPTY in `subninja.rs`, and that is the only interesting line of the change.\n\nTHE FIX, four small pieces:\n* `CompilationContext::assumed_new` — the names the invocation gave, carried into the compilation beside `always_make`, filled from `invocation.assumed_new` at the root and left empty for a child and for the library entry point that has no invocation at all.\n* `stage_recursive_wrapper` takes them and hands them on.\n* `GraphSink::settle_subninja_freshness` passes them through.\n* `BuildGraph::edge_dirty_with` marks them on the `RuntimeState` it makes, resolved against the graph by `nodeget` and looked up as the switch stored them — the same resolution `Build::plan` does, including not canonicalising, because GNU Make's `expand_command_line_file` strips a leading `./` and stops.\n\nNothing new was written in `src/graph.rs`: the dirty computation already knew what to do with an assumed-new node (`FileTime::NEWEST` ahead of every stat, and `always_dirty && !assumed_new` for the `.PHONY` interaction). The defect was that the set was empty, not that the rule was wrong.\n\nPROBE EVIDENCE, oracle at reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, on effects and exit status. The filed reproducer and seven variants:\n\n| shape | GNU 4.4.1 | Ronin before | Ronin after |\n| `-W pre.out pre.out` | `'pre.out' is up to date`, nothing on disk | ran all three lines and the child | matches |\n| `-W pre.out -n` | nothing | described the first staged line | matches |\n| `-W pre.out -t` | nothing touched | ran the recipe, so `-t` wrote real contents | matches |\n| `-W pre.out -q` | exit 0 | exit 1 | matches |\n| `-W pre.out -B` | runs everything | already matched | matches |\n| `-W pre.out -W sub.out` | up to date | ran everything | matches |\n| target already on disk, with and without `-W` | up to date | already matched | matches |\n| `-W dep` on a PREREQUISITE of the wrapper | runs the whole recipe, `dep` not made | already matched | matches |\n| `-W sub/marker` or `-W marker`, a CHILD's target | child rebuilds it, whole recipe runs | already matched | matches |\n| `-W gen.mk` on a composed remade makefile | gen.mk not remade, child never runs, `out` empty | already matched | matches |\n\nThe prerequisite row and the child row are the boundary the filing asked to be probed, and they say the same thing from two sides: `-W` is an answer about one file in one invocation's own directory, and neither a prerequisite's own recipe nor a child Make is entitled to it. The `-W gen.mk` row is worth reading beside `a-staged-recipe-segment-obeys-n-and-t-where-a-remake-would-not`, which landed an hour before this: a Makefile the switch has already answered about is not remade at all, so the makefile-staged split and the `-W` set agree without either being told about the other.\n\nFIXTURES, four make_port cases recorded from 4.4.1 with `MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1 MAKE_PORT_ORACLE=…`, nothing existing rewritten: `a-what-if-over-a-recursive-recipes-own-target`, `a-touched-what-if-over-a-recursive-recipe`, `a-question-with-what-if-over-a-recursive-recipe`, `a-forced-what-if-over-a-recursive-recipe`.\n\nTEETH, measured by parking the five source files and keeping the corpus:\n\n    3 of 956 ported cases diverge from GNU Make's build intent:\n    a-question-with-what-if-over-a-recursive-recipe: build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded\n    a-touched-what-if-over-a-recursive-recipe: 'pre.out' was produced and GNU Make produced no such file\n    a-what-if-over-a-recursive-recipes-own-target: 'pre.out' was produced and GNU Make produced no such file\n\nThree of the four have teeth. The fourth is `-B`, which passes in both states because `-B` outranks `-W` — `always_make_flag` makes every target with a recipe out of date whatever a date says, and `-W` only writes a date — and it is in the corpus to hold that boundary rather than to prove the fix.\n\nThe `-n` row has no corpus case, on purpose: its file effects are identical either way (nothing reaches the disk), so a build-intent recording could not tell the two apart. What changed there is what is DESCRIBED, and that is narration.\n\nGATES, serial, through scripts/sandboxed on a quiet host: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; all three clippy passes clean; `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean; workspace 969 passed / 0 failed, unchanged because the four fixtures are corpus directories rather than test functions; `--no-default-features` 754 / 0; make_port 4/4 over 956 case directories, nothing rewritten; conformance 286 identical raw and 336 normalised, 51 differing at 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact, nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 1 both-refused / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 at 132 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1129 narration, audited by regenerating `tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv` with `--update` and comparing digests — `2f09deebd5a3495ea06bfcaedc092bf5` before and after; ninja conformance 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content; `check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 28s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 48s at -j8."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T05:09:35.270455339Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id a-staged-recipe-segment-obeys-n-and-t-where-a-remake-would-not
        title "Remake a makefile for real when its recipe composes, under -n, -t and -q"
        kind @fix
        deps (a-remade-makefile-that-composes-a-recursion-runs-the-probe)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T03:32:34.278044569Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Measured while probing the switch matrix for `a-remade-makefile-that-composes-a-recursion-runs-the-probe`. The build path there is now right; these three switches are not.\n\nTHE REPRODUCER is that node's own, `-f Makefile <switch> all`:\n\n    all: ; @printf '%s\\n' '$(GENERATED)' > out\n\n    include gen.mk\n\n    gen.mk:\n    \\t@printf 'GENERATED := from-generated\\n' > $@\n    \\t@$(MAKE) -C sub marker\n\n| switch | GNU Make 4.4.1 | Ronin |\n| `-n` | remakes gen.mk FOR REAL, runs the child, restarts, then prints `printf … > out` | prints the first staged line and stops; gen.mk is never written |\n| `-t` | remakes gen.mk for real, runs the child, then touches `all` | runs the child but leaves gen.mk absent |\n| `-q` | remakes gen.mk for real, runs the child, exits 1 | exits 1 having made nothing |\n\nGNU Make turns `-n`, `-t` and `-q` OFF across the makefile update and back on for the goals (`update_goal_chain`, main.c), because a Makefile it only pretended to remake is a Makefile it would then have to guess the contents of. Ronin already does this for an ordinary remake — `remaking_options` (src/make/cli/remake.rs) clears `dryrun`, `verbose` and `touch` — and the nested `;` shape shows it working: under all three switches gen.mk IS written.\n\nWHY THE COMPOSED SHAPE IS DIFFERENT. Its recipe is cut into segments around the `$(MAKE)` line, and the segment ahead of the invocation runs at a compilation boundary through `Passes::stage`, which uses the INVOCATION's own switches. That is deliberate and documented there — GNU Make has no such phase, so there is nothing of its behaviour to copy — but it is the wrong call when the segment belongs to a Makefile: it is then part of the makefile update, and the update is exactly the phase these switches do not reach.\n\nWHAT IT WOULD TAKE. The staged pass has one flat list and does not know which staged node belongs to which wrapper. `compose_subninjas` does: it has `pending.outputs()` beside `state.remakes` at the moment it stages. Recording the makefile-owned staged nodes separately would let `build_compiler_inputs` run those under `remaking_options` and the rest under the invocation's own switches — which is exactly the split GNU Make makes.\n\nThe `-n` answer for an ORDINARY target's staged segment stays what it is: GNU Make describes the whole recipe without running any of it, and Ronin cannot describe a child it has not compiled. That half is the `-n` truncation, and it belongs with `pretending-over-an-assembled-recipe` rather than here."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T04:38:11.260615316Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T04:38:55.859530326Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered. A Makefile whose recipe composes is now remade for real under `-n`, `-t` and `-q`, which is what GNU Make does and what the read needs in order to mean anything.\n\nTHE FILING'S MECHANISM WAS RIGHT and its plan was nearly right. GNU Make turns the three switches off across `update_goal_chain`'s makefile pass and back on for the goals, because a Makefile it only pretended to remake is one whose contents it would then have to guess. Ronin does that for an ordinary remake (`remaking_options`, src/make/cli/remake.rs) and the composed shape escapes it, because a recipe cut into segments around a `$(MAKE)` has those segments built at a compilation boundary — a phase GNU Make has nothing corresponding to, so the code deliberately used the invocation's own switches there.\n\nWHERE THE FILING WAS WRONG, and it matters. It said `compose_subninjas` has `pending.outputs()` beside `state.remakes` and that recording the makefile-owned staged nodes would be enough. The first half is right and the second half is not a mere subset test: GNU brings a Makefile up to date by updating everything it is MADE FROM, all inside the same switch-free phase. A `gen.mk: helper` whose `helper` holds the composed recursion is as much part of the makefile update as one whose own recipe does — probed against 4.4.1 before writing anything, and it builds `helper`, runs the child, and writes `gen.mk` under all three switches. So the question asked at staging time is over the whole input closure, not over the wrapper's outputs alone.\n\nTHE FIX, four pieces and one extraction:\n\n* `BuildGraph::any_reaches` (src/frontend/staging.rs) — whether making any of `roots` reaches any of `targets`, over the input closure. `blocked_targets` was already that walk under a different name and a different question, so the walk came out into `roots_reaching` and both are one line over it. One traversal, two domain names.\n* `GraphSink::reached_by_makefiles`, and the question asked once per pending recursive recipe in `compose_subninjas` (src/make.rs). It is asked THERE and nowhere else because there is the only moment both halves exist: by the time the staged work is built, the wrapper still wears the freshness probe and the staged edge is not yet linked to it, so no later closure walk could find the connection. That was measured, not assumed — the first design tried to classify at build time and could not see the staged proxy at all.\n* `CompilationState::makefile_staged` and `Loaded::makefile_staged_targets`, carrying the answer out beside `staged_targets` (which now excludes it). Both are subsets of `regenerations`; the split is the phase, not the kind.\n* `Passes::stage_all` (src/make/cli/remake.rs) — the makefile-owned list first, under `remaking_options`, never questioning; then the goals' list under the invocation's own switches, exactly as before. Two lists rather than a flag, because it is two phases and GNU runs them in that order.\n\n`build_compiler_inputs` went to 102 lines (limit 100) and the staged half came out whole as `build_staged_work` with a `StagedWork` carrying what it needs. The clippy pass found it and it was fixed rather than allowed, as was an `unused_mut` the extraction left behind.\n\nPROBE EVIDENCE, oracle at reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, on effects and exit status, four shapes × five switches, every row run against both tools:\n\n| shape | switch | GNU 4.4.1 | Ronin before | Ronin after |\n| composed remake | (none) | gen.mk, child, out=from-generated, 0 | matched | matches |\n| composed remake | `-n` | gen.mk MADE, child ran, out described, 0 | gen.mk absent, child never ran | matches |\n| composed remake | `-t` | gen.mk MADE, child ran, `all` touched, 0 | gen.mk absent | matches |\n| composed remake | `-q` | gen.mk MADE, child ran, exit 1 | nothing made, exit 1 | matches |\n| composed remake | `-B` | all of it, 0 | matched | matches |\n| plain remake (no recursion) | all five | — | matched | matches, unmoved |\n| ordinary GOAL that composes | all five | — | matched on effects | matches, unmoved |\n| nested `;` remake | `-n`/`-t`/`-q` | child runs for real | child pretends | STILL DIVERGES — see below |\n\nThe composed child is byte-identical to GNU's in all three modes: `sub/marker` holds `child`, six bytes. That is the sharpest statement of the fix, because it says the switch did not reach the child either.\n\nTHE FILING'S REFERENCE SHAPE IS NOT A REFERENCE. It said \"the nested `;` shape already gets this right: under all three switches gen.mk IS written\". gen.mk is written, and that is all that is right about it. Probed here: under `-n` the child of that recipe describes its recipe and makes nothing; under `-t` it TOUCHES `marker` to zero bytes where GNU leaves six; under `-q` the child answers 1, the shell command carrying it exits 1, the makefile's own recipe reads as FAILED and Ronin exits 2 where GNU exits 1. One cause for all three — the recipe's `MAKEFLAGS` still carries the switch the update turned off — and it cannot be reached from here, because `MAKEFLAGS` is composed per unit at compile time and the phase an edge is built in is not known then. Filed as `a-remade-makefiles-recipe-hands-its-child-the-pretending-switches`, with the reasoning and two candidate shapes.\n\nALSO FOUND, and confirmed PRE-EXISTING by parking this whole fix: `gen.mk: helper` with the recursion on `helper` remakes `gen.mk` on every pass and dies at `manifest 'Makefile' dirty after 100 tries`, exit 2, on all four switch settings. This landing changes that shape's work — `helper`, `marker` and `gen.mk` are now all made under `-n`, where before none of them were — and does not stop the loop. Filed as `a-makefile-made-through-a-recursive-prerequisite-never-settles` with the `recipe_begun` lead.\n\nFIXTURES. Three make_port cases recorded from 4.4.1 with `MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1 MAKE_PORT_ORACLE=…`, nothing existing rewritten: `a-pretended-build-of-a-composing-remade-makefile`, `a-touched-build-of-a-composing-remade-makefile`, `a-question-over-a-composing-remade-makefile`. Plus two in `tests/make_regressions.rs`, because the corpus records an outcome as success or failure and `-q`'s whole answer is the exit STATUS: `a_composing_remade_makefile_is_made_for_real` gates 0/0/1 across the three switches along with gen.mk's contents, the child's target and whether `all` was touched; `a_goals_staged_segment_keeps_the_switches` gates the other side of the split and passes in both states on purpose — it is what says the goals' half did not move.\n\nTEETH, measured by parking the four source files and keeping the fixtures:\n\n    3 of 952 ported cases diverge from GNU Make's build intent:\n    a-pretended-build-of-a-composing-remade-makefile: 'gen.mk' was not produced\n    a-question-over-a-composing-remade-makefile: 'gen.mk' was not produced\n    a-touched-build-of-a-composing-remade-makefile: 'gen.mk' was not produced\n\n    thread 'a_composing_remade_makefile_is_made_for_real' panicked at tests/make_regressions.rs:1950:9:\n    assertion `left == right` failed: -n: the makefile was pretended rather than remade\n      left: None\n     right: Some(\"GENERATED := from-generated\\n\")\n\nGATES, serial, through scripts/sandboxed on a quiet host: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; all three clippy passes clean; `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean; workspace 969 passed / 0 failed (967 plus the two fixtures); `--no-default-features` 754 / 0, unchanged because everything added is Make's; make_port 4/4, nothing rewritten; conformance 286 identical raw and 336 normalised, 51 differing at 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact, nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 1 both-refused / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 at 132 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1129 narration, audited by regenerating `tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv` with `--update` and comparing digests — `2f09deebd5a3495ea06bfcaedc092bf5` before and after, so no row moved; ninja conformance 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content; `check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 17s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 29s at -j8."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T04:39:56.290674607Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T04:52:42.687749598Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Two amendments to the note above, both made because the commit gate refused and both fixed rather than bypassed.\n\nTHE CLOSURE WALK MOVED, and the shape is better for it. The first version added `BuildGraph::any_reaches` in src/frontend/staging.rs and a `GraphSink::reached_by_makefiles` forwarding to it. The `function-density` gate refused: src/make/sink.rs went to 51 functions against a limit of 50. Rather than raise the limit or split a file to satisfy a count, the walk went where it belongs — `made_for_a_makefile` in src/make.rs, over `GraphSink::prerequisites_of`, which is the accessor `dependency_ordered` in the same file already walks the ordinary ground with, and for the same reason: held recursive edges are not in the graph while this decision is being made, so everything between a wrapper and a Makefile is an ordinary target. src/frontend/staging.rs is untouched by this landing, and the gate found a design that reads better than the one it refused.\n\nTHE TWO FIXTURES WERE RENAMED. `function-name` refused both as sentences (8 and 7 words against a 6-word limit). They are `a_composing_remake_is_not_pretended` and `a_goals_segment_keeps_the_switches`; nothing about what they assert changed, and the teeth measurement re-run against the final shape reads\n\n    thread 'a_composing_remake_is_not_pretended' panicked at tests/make_regressions.rs:1950:9:\n    assertion `left == right` failed: -n: the makefile was pretended rather than remade\n      left: None\n     right: Some(\"GENERATED := from-generated\\n\")\n\nwith the same three corpus cases diverging beside it and `a_goals_segment_keeps_the_switches` passing in both states.\n\nEvery gate in the note above was re-run against the reshaped tree and every number is unchanged, including the inventory digest."
                after_done true
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id the-recipe-stage-proxy-leaves-a-directory-in-the-tree
        title "Stop a staged recipe segment leaving .ronin_recipe_stage in the build tree"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T03:32:55.383298826Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Noticed while reading the tree a composed recursion had just built. Small, and a leak of Ronin's own working names into a user's directory, which is the kind of thing a Makefile author reports as a mystery.\n\nWHAT HAPPENS. A recipe whose `$(MAKE)` composes has the lines ahead of it staged as an edge of their own, and that edge needs a name to be asked for by: `GraphSink::recipe_stage_proxy` (src/make/sink.rs) mints `.ronin_recipe_stage/0`, `.ronin_recipe_stage/1`, … Its own comment says the name is \"never written and never read\". The BUILD does not know that: it creates an output's parent directory before running the command, so an empty `.ronin_recipe_stage/` is left in the build root.\n\n    $ make -f Makefile pre.out     # a recipe with a line ahead of its $(MAKE)\n    $ ls -a\n    .  ..  Makefile  .ninja_deps  .ninja_log  .ronin_recipe_stage  sub.mk\n\nPRE-EXISTING, from the mixed-recipe machinery (6eb714b) rather than from anything since: the staged edge has always run and has always had that output name. It is newly VISIBLE because the shapes that reach it now work — `a-recursive-recipe-that-writes-its-own-target-first` and `a-remade-makefile-that-composes-a-recursion-runs-the-probe` both used to fail before the directory could matter.\n\nWHAT IT IS NOT. Not a corpus failure: the make_port harness ignores dot-names, which is why five cases exercising this shape record clean. That is the harness being right about what a build's answer is, and it is also why nothing caught this.\n\nWHAT IT WOULD TAKE, in rough order of preference: not creating a directory for an output nothing writes (the build already knows an edge's outputs; a name under a directory that does not exist is the only reason it is made), or minting the proxy as a flat name with no directory component, or removing it with the intermediates. The first is the honest one — the proxy is a name and not a file, and the build should be told so rather than the name being bent around what the build assumes."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T04:12:53.675845203Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T04:13:27.275618784Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered the way the filing said was the honest one: the build is TOLD the name is not a file, rather than the name being bent around what the build assumes.\n\nTHE DEFECT IS WORSE THAN FILED, and the probe is what says so. The filing had an empty `.ronin_recipe_stage/` after a build. It is left after `-n` as well:\n\n| mode | Ronin before | GNU 4.4.1 |\n| (no switch) | `.ronin_recipe_stage/` in the build root | nothing |\n| `-n` | `.ronin_recipe_stage/` | nothing, and nothing else either |\n| `-t` | `.ronin_recipe_stage/` | nothing |\n| `-B` | `.ronin_recipe_stage/` | nothing |\n| `-q` | absent | nothing |\n\n`-n` is the row that makes this more than untidiness: a mode whose whole promise is that nothing reaches the disk was creating a directory. `-q` is the boundary — it reaches nothing, so there was nothing to leave — and it is in the fixture for that reason rather than as evidence.\n\nWHERE IT CAME FROM. `Builder::prepare_edge` (src/build.rs) calls `Disk::make_dirs` for every output an edge is about to write, which is Ninja's behaviour and right for a file. `GraphSink::recipe_stage_proxy` mints `.ronin_recipe_stage/N` for the edge a composed `$(MAKE)` cuts off the front of a recipe, and that name is a handle: nothing writes it, nothing reads it, the edge is `always_dirty`. The build had no way to know, so it made the directory the name appears to sit in.\n\nWHY THE OTHER TWO INVENTED NAMES NEVER SHOWED THIS, probed rather than assumed. `.ronin_grouped_join/N` and the deferred-freshness proxies are already invisible to `make_dirs`, and not by luck: `prepare_edge` takes its output list from `deferred_freshness(edge)` when there is one, so it is the REAL outputs whose directories get made. `Graph::is_virtual_output` existed for exactly this idea and derives it off the edge — a deferred-freshness rule or a completion join points at the real file through the edge, so the invented name can be recognised. `tests/make_grouped.rs` already asserts `.ronin_grouped_join` never appears. A staged recipe segment has no such indirection to be recognised BY, which is why it was the one that leaked.\n\nTHE FIX, four small pieces:\n\n* `Graph::invented_outputs` (src/graph.rs) — a `RapidHashSet<NodeId>` beside the arena for the reason `withdrawal` is: no node of a Ninja manifest is ever in it, and `Node` is the largest structure a large manifest builds, so it does not pay a bool per node for a Make feature.\n* `Graph::is_virtual_output` (src/graph/deferred.rs) now consults that set ahead of the two derived cases. One predicate with three sources rather than a second predicate meaning almost the same thing.\n* `Graph::mark_invented_output`, forwarded through `BuildGraph` (src/frontend/deferred.rs) beside `set_completion_join`, and called by `recipe_stage_proxy` where the name is minted. The claim is made where the name is made.\n* `prepare_edge` skips `make_dirs` for a virtual output. Written as a filter on the loop rather than a special case for one name, so the next invented name is covered by the same line.\n\nThe field is `invented_outputs` and not `graph_only_outputs` because the commit gate's first clippy pass rejected the latter: a field on `Graph` whose name starts with `graph` stutters. Fixed rather than allowed.\n\nTEETH, measured by parking the five source files and keeping the fixture:\n\n    thread 'a_staged_recipe_segment_leaves_no_name_of_its_own' panicked at tests/make_regressions.rs:1876:9:\n    a staged segment left its own name in the tree under []\n\nThe parked binary leaves the directory under no switch, `-n`, `-t` and `-B`, and not under `-q` — four rows with teeth and one without, which is the same shape as the table above.\n\nFIXTURES, two, in `tests/make_regressions.rs` rather than the make_port corpus — the corpus harness ignores dot-names, which is right about what a build's answer is and is also why nothing caught this:\n\n* `a_staged_recipe_segment_leaves_no_name_of_its_own`, the five modes.\n* `a_staged_recipe_segment_still_runs_the_whole_recipe`, which passes in both states on purpose: it is the guard that says the name stopping being a file did not take the work with it. `pre.out` still holds `before\\nafter` and `sub.out` is still made.\n\nWHAT DID NOT MOVE, and this is a change in the shared Ninja build path so it needs saying: for a Ninja manifest `is_virtual_output` is false for every node — no deferred freshness, no completion joins, nothing marked — so the filter is a predicate that never fires. Ninja conformance is the proof: 425 upstream tests plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content, all matched.\n\nGATES, serial, through scripts/sandboxed on a quiet host: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; all three clippy passes clean; `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean; workspace 967 passed / 0 failed (965 plus the two fixtures); `--no-default-features` 754 / 0, unchanged because both fixtures are Make's; make_port 4/4 over the corpus with the two recorded divergences unmoved; conformance 286 identical raw and 336 normalised, 51 differing at 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact, nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 1 both-refused / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 at 132 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1129 narration, audited by REGENERATING `tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv` with `--update` and comparing digests — `2f09deebd5a3495ea06bfcaedc092bf5` before and after, so no row moved; ninja conformance as above; `check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 15s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27s at -j8."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T04:14:26.679311129Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T04:15:05.852560737Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Erratum to the note above, so the fixture names in it can be found: the commit gate's `function-name` check rejected both as sentences (10 words / 49 chars against a 6-word, 45-char limit), and they were renamed rather than bypassed. They are `a_stage_proxy_makes_no_directory` and `a_staged_segment_runs_the_recipe`; the teeth measurement is unchanged and reads\n\n    thread 'a_stage_proxy_makes_no_directory' panicked at tests/make_regressions.rs:\n    a staged segment left its own name in the tree under []"
                after_done true
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id make-regression-tests-leave-their-scratch-behind
        title "Take the Make regression suite's scratch directories away with it"
        kind @test
        deps (lint-tests-leave-their-scratch-behind)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T03:33:12.86989688Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "The same defect `lint-tests-leave-their-scratch-behind` was filed and fixed for, in the file next door. Found by looking for it once the lint suite's own leak was swept.\n\n    $ ls -d /tmp/ronin-make-regression-* | wc -l\n    353\n\n`tests/make_regressions.rs::test_directory` names its directory `ronin-make-regression-<label>-<pid>` under TMPDIR, removes whatever is there and creates it. Some cases end with `fs::remove_dir_all(directory)` and some do not, and a case that fails never reaches its own removal at all — so the leak is partial, which is worse than total: what is left behind is exactly the runs that went wrong, mixed with the ones that happened to forget, and nothing says which is which.\n\nTHE FIX IS WRITTEN, in `tests/lint.rs`: a `Scratch` newtype over `tempfile::TempDir` carrying `Deref<Target = Path>` and `AsRef<Path>`, so every call site reads as it did, plus the gate `a_scratch_goes_with_its_test` which fails against the old helper rather than merely asserting the new one. Copy both. The explicit `remove_dir_all` calls at the ends of cases come out with it — a handle that removes on drop makes them noise, and the one at the START goes too, because a prefixed temp directory cannot collide.\n\nSweep `/tmp/ronin-make-regression-*` on the host as part of landing, and nothing else: `/tmp` on this machine also holds a few hundred hand-made probe directories from earlier dispatches, which are somebody's evidence and not a leak."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T03:52:33.535495147Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T03:53:01.941877233Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered, and NOT by copying: the pattern came out of `tests/lint.rs` into `tests/support/scratch.rs` and both suites include it, because a second copy of a twenty-line lifetime is a second thing to drift. `tests/support/` is already this repo's shared-test-code convention — `support/oracle.rs` is `#[path]`-included by `make_port.rs`, and cargo does not build a subdirectory of `tests/` as a target of its own, which is what makes that arrangement work.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG, and the filing understated one half of it. `test_directory` named `ronin-make-regression-<label>-<pid>` under TMPDIR, cleared it, and created it. 42 call sites; 42 explicit `fs::remove_dir_all` at the ends of cases. So the steady-state leak on a passing run is not 42 — it is ONE: `shell_function_names_the_missing_program` is the single case that never removes. That is exactly what the host census said before the sweep, and it is the number worth writing down:\n\n    328  ronin-make-regression-shell-direct-*\n     17  ronin-make-regression-stdin-twice-*\n      2  ronin-make-regression-symlink-freshness-*\n      2  ronin-make-regression-submake-shell-boundary-*\n    ...  356 in total\n\nOne case leaking every run, and a scatter of others leaking only the runs that FAILED — a case that panics never reaches the removal written at its end. The scatter is the more useful half: what survives is precisely the evidence of runs that went wrong, mixed with one that always survives, and nothing distinguishes them.\n\nWHAT IT IS NOW. `Scratch(tempfile::TempDir)` with `Deref<Target = Path>` and `AsRef<Path>`, constructed by `Scratch::named(prefix)`; each suite keeps a one-line wrapper that spells its own prefix (`scratch` in lint.rs, `test_directory` here), so every call site reads exactly as it did. The removal is now a drop, which is why it covers the failing run as well as the passing one. The 42 explicit removals came out, and so did the up-front `remove_dir_all` — a prefixed temp directory cannot collide with a concurrent run, so the defence it existed for has no subject. `unreadable_include_repaired_by_its_rule`'s early return loses its removal too and simply returns.\n\nTEETH, measured by parking rather than asserted, `/tmp` swept to zero before each:\n\n* full suite passing, fix parked: 1 directory left (`ronin-make-regression-shell-direct-43`). With the fix: 0.\n* one case made to fail on purpose (`assert!(false)` in `a_define_body_names_the_define`), fix parked: `ronin-make-regression-define-diagnostic-location-43` survives the failure. With the fix: 0. This is the case the explicit removals cannot cover and the drop does.\n* the gate `a_scratch_goes_with_its_test`, with `Scratch` changed to hold the path from `TempDir::keep()` — which is the old behaviour exactly, a named directory left behind:\n\n      thread 'scratch_directory::a_scratch_goes_with_its_test' panicked at tests/support/scratch.rs:69:5:\n      the scratch outlived the test that made it: /tmp/ronin-scratch-self-removing-I1Zb3j\n\n  The gate is written against behaviour rather than against the type, which is what lets it be parked that way at all.\n\nTHE SWEEP: 356 `/tmp/ronin-make-regression-*` removed on this host and nothing else; zero remain, and zero after the workspace suite ran. Deliberately not swept: `/tmp` also holds `ronin-rust-cli-manifest-*` (24), `ronin-runner-directory-*` (12), `ronin-jobserver-*` (8) and a few hundred hand-made probe directories from earlier dispatches. The probe directories are somebody's evidence. The others are the same defect in other suites, filed rather than swept.\n\nFOUND WHILE SWEEPING and filed as `more-suites-leave-their-scratch-behind`: `tests/public_api.rs`, `tests/cli.rs`, `tests/progress.rs`, `tests/make_projects.rs`, `src/build/tests.rs`, `src/tool/test_support.rs` and `src/lint/manifest.rs` all name a directory after their own pid the same way. The shared module is now the place to point them at, which is the other reason it is a module.\n\nGATES for this landing, which is two test files and one new support file: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; all three clippy passes clean; `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean; workspace 965 passed / 0 failed, up from 964 by the gate now compiling into `make_regressions` as well as `lint`; `--no-default-features` 754 / 0, unchanged because `make_regressions.rs` is `#![cfg(feature = \"make\")]` and the gate is not reached there. The corpus gates read no file this commit touches and are not evidence for it; they run in the end-of-dispatch battery."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T03:54:16.316343018Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id more-suites-leave-their-scratch-behind
        title "Point the remaining suites at the scratch that goes with its test"
        kind @test
        deps (make-regression-tests-leave-their-scratch-behind)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T03:54:09.083133866Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "The same defect `lint-tests-leave-their-scratch-behind` and `make-regression-tests-leave-their-scratch-behind` were filed and fixed for, in every other suite that makes a directory. Found by census rather than by reading: `/tmp` on this host, after the make-regression sweep, still holds the leavings of six more.\n\nWHERE IT IS, with the prefix each one leaves behind and what it counts on this host:\n\n| site | naming | explicit removals | leaked here |\n| `src/lint/manifest.rs:119` | `ronin-manifest-lint-<pid>-<n>.ninja` | none at all | 24 |\n| `src/tool/test_support.rs:15` | `ronin-tool-<label>-<pid>-<n>` | 1 | (via `ronin-runner-directory-*`, 12) |\n| `tests/public_api.rs` | `std::env::temp_dir().join(...)` ×5 | 6 | — |\n| `tests/cli.rs:9` | one helper, per-pid | 82 | — |\n| `tests/progress.rs:25` | `ronin-<label>-<pid>-<n>` | 3 | — |\n| `tests/make_projects.rs:31` | `ronin-make-project-<name>-<pid>` | 6 | 6 |\n\nThe shape is identical everywhere: name after the process, `let _ = remove_dir_all` up front, `create_dir_all`, and a removal written at the end of each case that a panicking case never reaches. `src/lint/manifest.rs` has no removal at all, which is why it is the biggest count after the one already swept.\n\nTHE FIX IS WRITTEN AND SHARED: `tests/support/scratch.rs` holds `Scratch::named(prefix)` — a `tempfile::TempDir` newtype with `Deref<Target = Path>` and `AsRef<Path>` — plus the gate `a_scratch_goes_with_its_test`, which is written against behaviour and so fails against a helper that returns a bare path. `tests/lint.rs` and `tests/make_regressions.rs` both `#[path]`-include it and keep a one-line wrapper naming their own prefix. Point the other test crates at the same file the same way.\n\nTHE TWO IN `src/` ARE A DIFFERENT PROBLEM and should be probed before being lumped in: `tests/support/` is not reachable from a `#[cfg(test)]` module inside the library, so either the newtype moves somewhere both can include it, or those two get their own. Decide that from the code rather than assuming the module can simply be reached.\n\nSweep each prefix as its own landing lands, and nothing else — `/tmp` here also holds a few hundred hand-made probe directories from earlier dispatches, which are somebody's evidence and not a leak."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T05:34:39.099787565Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T05:50:51.25692194Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered, and the filing's census was wrong about two of its six rows — worth writing down because the numbers were real and pointed somewhere else.\n\nWHAT THE CENSUS ACTUALLY MEASURED. The table attributed 24 leaked directories to `src/lint/manifest.rs` and 12 to `src/tool/test_support.rs`. Neither site had left anything on this host: `ronin-manifest-lint-*` was 0 and `ronin-tool-*` was 0. The 24 were `ronin-rust-cli-manifest-*` and `ronin-rust-cli-manifest-restat-*`, and the 12 were `ronin-runner-directory-*` — all three from the `cli::tests` module in `src/cli.rs`, which the table does not name at all. `src/tool/test_support.rs` already had a `Drop` that removed its directory, so it was never the defect; `src/lint/manifest.rs` writes a FILE and removes it, and leaks only when `load_manifest_reporting` panics first. So the two `src/` rows were a misread of prefixes that sort next to each other, and the real `src/` leaker was a third file.\n\nFixed anyway, all of it: the six the node names — where the shape is genuinely leak-on-panic even at count zero — plus the eight scratch directories in `src/cli.rs`'s test module, which is what the node's own 24 and 12 were counting.\n\nTHE `src/` REACHABILITY QUESTION, answered from the code as the filing asked. `tests/support/` is not reachable from a `cfg(test)` module inside the library by ordinary means, but `#[path]` is a path, not a module route: `src/lib.rs` is a crate root, so `#[path = \"../tests/support/scratch.rs\"]` resolves against `src/` and lands on the same file the integration crates include. One `#[cfg(test)] mod scratch_directory;` in `lib.rs` and every unit test in the library reaches `crate::scratch_directory::Scratch`. No second copy, and no move: the newtype stays where the integration suites already include it from. `tempfile` is an ordinary dependency rather than a dev-dependency, so nothing had to move for that either. The one concession is an `#[allow(unreachable_pub)]` on the mod declaration with a reason — the file is `pub` for the integration crates that include it, and inside the library it is a private module, which is exactly what that lint is for.\n\nWHERE IT WENT, seven files:\n\n* `src/lib.rs` — the shared include, under `cfg(test)`.\n* `src/cli.rs` — eight `temp_dir().join(format!(\"…-{pid}-{n}\"))` sites became `Scratch::named`, and `NEXT_RUN` with its `AtomicUsize`/`Ordering` import went with them; the counter existed only to keep two names in one process apart, which a random suffix does better.\n* `src/lint/manifest.rs` — `checked` now writes `build.ninja` inside a scratch instead of a bare `.ninja` under TMPDIR, so the `.expect(\"the source parses\")` above the removal no longer leaks when it fires.\n* `src/tool/test_support.rs` — `Fixture::directory` is a `Scratch`, and the hand-written `Drop` came out. `&fixture.directory` still coerces to `&Path` at all three consumers.\n* `tests/cli.rs` — `test_directory` returns `Scratch`; `make_case` and `makefile_tree` return it too, which is the whole cascade. 48 redundant `create_dir_all(&directory)` and 81 explicit removals came out for 64 scratches — more removals than scratches, because `make_io_failures_name_their_source` makes four in one body and removed each.\n* `tests/progress.rs`, `tests/make_projects.rs`, `tests/public_api.rs` — the same, plus one shape worth naming: `public_api_preserves_manifest_io_causes` wants a path nothing has written, so it takes a `Scratch` and names a file inside it rather than naming a directory that must not exist.\n\nTEETH, measured by parking rather than asserted. A PASSING run at the parent commit leaks nothing from these sites — every case reaches its removal — so the tooth has to be a failing run, which is the whole point of the defect. Two cases made to fail on purpose, one on each side of the crate boundary, `/tmp` swept first:\n\n    parked (parent commit's code, same two failures):\n      /tmp/ronin-rust-cli-manifest-45-5\n      /tmp/ronin-warn-dupbuild-1041-18\n\n    with the fix, same two failures: 0\n\nThe first insertion of that measurement was wrong and is worth recording: `assert!(false)` placed immediately after `let directory = test_directory(...)` left NOTHING behind, because at the parent commit that helper only NAMES a path and the `create_dir_all` on the next line is what makes it. The panic has to be after the directory exists. That is also the sharpest statement of what changed — the fixed helper hands back a directory, so there is no window in which the name exists and the removal does not.\n\nThe gate itself, `a_scratch_goes_with_its_test`, now compiles into five more targets rather than two, which is where the test-count movement comes from.\n\nSWEPT, and only these: `ronin-make-project-*` (6), `ronin-runner-directory-*` (12), `ronin-rust-cli-manifest*-*` (24), and three stale `ronin-make-graph-direct-*` / `ronin-make-collected-warnings-*` carrying the old `<pid>-<thread-name>` spelling from a tree where those tests had different names. 45 in total; zero of any swept prefix remain after a full workspace run. `/tmp` still holds 164 `ronin-*` — the hand-made probe directories from earlier dispatches, `ronin-jobserver-*`, and `ronin-build-*`/`ronin-plan-test-*`, none of which are this node's.\n\nDELIBERATELY NOT TOUCHED, with reasons. `src/build/tests.rs` is not in the node's table and should not be: it already collects, through `fixture_directory` + `sweep_stale_fixtures`, and its naming carries the clock for a documented reason — under `scripts/sandboxed` a run has its own pid namespace, so `std::process::id()` repeats exactly and a leftover from a failed run used to poison the next one. It leaves nothing on this host. `ronin-jobserver-*` (8) is PRODUCT code, `ServedJobserver::create`, and it has a `Drop` that removes the fifo; those eight are the leavings of runs that were killed, which GNU Make's fifo does the same way. Neither is a leak this node fixes.\n\nGATES: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; all three clippy passes clean (`--lib --bins --no-deps` pedantic+nursery, `--all-targets --no-deps`, `-p kati --all-targets`); `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean; workspace 974 passed / 0 failed, up from 969 by the five new gate instances; `--no-default-features` 758 / 0, up from 754 by four — `make_projects.rs` is the one of the five that is gated off without the feature. The corpus gates read no file this commit touches and run in the end-of-dispatch battery."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T05:50:55.204798458Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id a-remade-makefiles-recipe-hands-its-child-the-pretending-switches
        title "Keep -n, -t and -q out of the MAKEFLAGS a remaking recipe hands its child"
        kind @fix
        deps (a-staged-recipe-segment-obeys-n-and-t-where-a-remake-would-not)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T04:37:40.83717381Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found while probing the switch matrix for `a-staged-recipe-segment-obeys-n-and-t-where-a-remake-would-not`, on the shape that node's own filing called the one that already works. It does not: it writes the Makefile and then hands the recursion the switch it was supposed to have turned off.\n\nTHE REPRODUCER is the composed one with the `$(MAKE)` moved behind a `;`, which is what stops it being composed and leaves it a run-time recursion:\n\n    all: ; @printf '%s\\n' '$(GENERATED)' > out\n\n    include gen.mk\n\n    gen.mk:\n    \\t@printf 'GENERATED := from-generated\\n' > $@; $(MAKE) -C sub marker\n\nwith `sub/Makefile` making `marker` by `@printf 'child\\n' > marker`.\n\n| switch | GNU Make 4.4.1 | Ronin |\n| `-n` | gen.mk written, child RUNS, `marker` holds `child` | gen.mk written, child describes its recipe and makes nothing |\n| `-t` | gen.mk written, child RUNS, `marker` holds `child` (6 bytes) | gen.mk written, child TOUCHES, `marker` is 0 bytes |\n| `-q` | gen.mk written, child runs, exit 1 | `FAILED: [code=1] gen.mk`, `ronin: build stopped: subcommand failed.`, exit 2 |\n\nONE CAUSE FOR ALL THREE. GNU Make clears `just_print_flag`, `touch_flag` and `question_flag` for the makefile update AND recomputes `MAKEFLAGS` from the cleared set, so a `$(MAKE)` inside a Makefile's own recipe starts a child that is not pretending either. Ronin clears them for the update's own `BuildOptions` — `remaking_options`, src/make/cli/remake.rs — and that is all: the recipe's environment still carries `MAKEFLAGS=n`, `=t` or `=q`, so the child pretends. Under `-q` the child then answers 1, the shell command carrying it exits 1, and the parent reads the makefile's own recipe as having FAILED — which is where the extra exit status comes from. GNU's 1 is a question's answer; Ronin's 2 is a build that broke.\n\nWHY THE COMPOSED SHAPE IS NOT AFFECTED, measured rather than assumed: with the node above landed, the composed child's `marker` is byte-identical to GNU's under all three switches. A composed recursion never travels through `MAKEFLAGS` at all — it is compiled into the graph, and its edges are built under whichever `BuildOptions` the phase holds. That is the compilation being right about something the environment cannot express, and it is why this defect survives only on the run-time path.\n\nWHAT IT WOULD TAKE, read off the code rather than guessed. `MAKEFLAGS` is composed per UNIT at compile time (`compile_unit`, src/make.rs, `makeflags` out of `read_unit`) and baked into the recipe environment of that unit's edges. The phase an edge is built in is not known then. Two shapes to weigh: recompose the switches into the environment at launch for an edge the makefile update is running — the classification `Loaded::makefile_staged_targets` now records is the same question asked one layer up — or carry a second `MAKEFLAGS` per unit, the pretending one and the remaking one, and choose at launch. Probe first whether `-t` alone is enough to tell them apart on a tree that recurses both ways in one run."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T06:51:05.484879297Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T07:16:26.987744145Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered. The filing's mechanism was right, its two candidate shapes were both partly right, and the probe answered its own question outright.\n\nWHAT GNU DOES, asked before anything was written and answered twice — from the source and from the ground.\n\nFrom the source: `define_makeflags (1)` is called once, immediately ahead of `update_goal_chain (read_files)`, and `define_makeflags (0)` puts it back for the goals (main.c:2439, main.c:2831). Its `makefile` argument is a filter — `if (cs->toenv && (!makefile || !cs->no_makefile))` — and exactly three entries of the switch table carry `no_makefile`: `n`, `q` and `t`. Nothing else does. So GNU does not filter `MAKEFLAGS` for remake recipes as a special case and the child does not re-read anything: `MAKEFLAGS` is REBUILT for the phase, from the same switch table, minus three bits.\n\nFrom the ground, with a Makefile that prints what each phase's recipe was handed:\n\n| invocation | GNU's remaking recipe sees | GNU's child sees |\n| `-n` | `MAKEFLAGS=[]` `MFLAGS=[]` | ` --no-print-directory` |\n| `-nk` | `[k]` `[-k]` | |\n| `-t -j3` | `[ -j3 --jobserver-auth=…]` `[-j3 …]` | |\n| `-n -B -s` | `[Bs]` `[-Bs]` | |\n| `-nrR --warn-undefined-variables` | `[rR --warn-undefined-variables]` | |\n| `-n FOO=1` | `[ -- FOO=1]` `[]` | |\n\nEverything but the three letters survives, and the letter group is the only thing that moves. Ronin's own composition (`compiler_flag_variables`, src/make/cli/interface.rs) already produces byte-identical values in the goals phase, so the operation this node needed is precisely \"take `n`, `q` and `t` out of the leading single-letter group\" — and every one of those six rows now matches GNU on both variables.\n\nAND THE OTHER SIDE, probed because a fix could break it silently: a `+`-marked line in a GOAL's recipe is handed `MAKEFLAGS=[t]` / `[q]` by GNU, because `define_makeflags (0)` has already put them back. So the strip belongs to the PHASE and not to the unit — a per-unit answer would have taken them off the goals too.\n\nWHERE IT WENT, and the interesting part is that the filed reproducer does not travel the path the filing named.\n\n* `src/make/layout.rs` — `makeflags_while_remaking` and `mflags_while_remaking`, plus `CommandLayout::while_remaking_makefiles`, which hands back only what would MOVE. `MAKEFLAGS` opens with the letter group, so taking a letter out is taking it out of the first word; `MFLAGS` is the same switches with a leading `-` and no `--` assignments, and a group that empties takes its whole word with it — `-t -j3` becomes `-j3`, not `- -j3`. The change is appended rather than substituted because `env` reads its arguments in order and `environment_prefix` settles them last-wins, which is the same thing that already lets a target's own `export` beat its unit's.\n* `src/make/recipe.rs` — `PendingRecipes::remaking_makefiles`, said by the front end at each phase. Held in the front end rather than read off the build on purpose: the engine schedules a graph and has no notion of a makefile update, and `LateCommands` is the boundary no front-end vocabulary crosses.\n* `src/make/cli/remake.rs` — `Passes::remaking_makefiles`, called at the top of `remake_makefiles`, again for `stage_all`'s makefile-owned list, and with `false` in `stage`. Three calls because there are three builds and GNU changes the answer between them.\n\nTHE FILING SAID `MAKEFLAGS` IS BAKED PER UNIT AT COMPILE TIME AND IT IS, but that only settles half of it, and the half it settles is not the reproducer's. A recipe with no `$(MAKE)` is expanded when its edge launches, so its environment is composable then — that half was one line and the flags probe went green immediately while the reproducer did not move at all. A recipe HOLDING a `$(MAKE)` is one of the four shapes kati has to read while the graph is built, so its launches are settled early with the environment already written into the command line. That is the path the reproducer takes, and no launch-time composition can reach it.\n\nSo the filing's second candidate is what this needed, in the shape it suggested: `SettledSteps` (src/make/layout.rs) carries a recipe's launches in both environments, and `SettledSteps::during` picks. `while_remaking` is an `Option` and is `None` whenever `while_remaking_makefiles` finds nothing to change — which is every invocation carrying none of the three — so a run without `-n`, `-t` or `-q` builds exactly one set of launches and pays nothing.\n\nPROBE EVIDENCE, oracle at reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, on effects and exit status:\n\n| switch | GNU 4.4.1 | Ronin before | Ronin after |\n| (none) | gen.mk written, child ran, `marker` = `child` (6B), out=from-generated, 0 | matched | matches |\n| `-n` | gen.mk written, child RAN, `marker` 6B, 0 | child described its recipe, `marker` ABSENT | matches |\n| `-t` | gen.mk written, child RAN, `marker` 6B, `all` touched, 0 | child TOUCHED, `marker` 0B | matches |\n| `-q` | gen.mk written, child ran, exit 1 | `FAILED: [code=1] gen.mk`, exit 2 | matches |\n| `-B` | all of it, 0 | matched | matches |\n\nThe matrix the dispatch asked for, every row run against both tools:\n* the nested child that REMAKES A MAKEFILE OF ITS OWN, under all four switches — the child runs both its phases with the switches off and its goal reads `from-child` out of the makefile it remade. Matched after, diverged before.\n* the composed cousin, all four shapes and three switches: a makefile whose own recipe composes, a makefile made through a composed recursive prerequisite, two staged recursive recipes in one unit, and a three-line recipe with residual lines after its invocation. Unmoved, and all still byte-identical to GNU.\n* the goal's `+` line under `-t` and `-q`: still handed `t` and `q`, which is what says the split did not leak into the goals.\n\nFIXTURES. Four make_port cases recorded from 4.4.1, nothing existing rewritten: `a-pretended-build-of-a-nested-remade-makefile`, `a-touched-build-of-a-nested-remade-makefile`, `a-question-over-a-nested-remade-makefile`, `a-nested-remakes-child-that-remakes-its-own-makefile`. The child writes `marker.out` rather than its own goal, so the recording carries the BYTES: a child handed `-t` touches and leaves the file empty where GNU's child writes what the recipe said. Plus two in tests/make_regressions.rs: `a_nested_remake_hides_the_switches` for `-q`'s exit STATUS, which the corpus records only as success or failure and which is the whole of what a question answers; and `a_goals_marked_line_keeps_the_switches`, which passes in both states on purpose — it is what says the goals' half did not move.\n\nTEETH, measured by parking the five source files and keeping the fixtures:\n\n    4 of 968 ported cases diverge from GNU Make's build intent:\n    a-nested-remakes-child-that-remakes-its-own-makefile: 'sub/marker.out' was not produced\n    a-pretended-build-of-a-nested-remade-makefile: 'sub/marker.out' was not produced\n    a-question-over-a-nested-remade-makefile: 'sub/marker.out' was not produced\n    a-touched-build-of-a-nested-remade-makefile: 'sub/marker.out' was not produced\n\n    thread 'a_nested_remake_hides_the_switches' panicked at tests/make_regressions.rs:2112:9:\n    assertion `left == right` failed: -n: the child of the remaking recipe pretended:\n      left: None\n     right: Some(\"child\\n\")\n\nSTILL DIVERGENT AND NOT THIS NODE'S: under `-t -j3` GNU's `MAKEFLAGS` carries `--jobserver-auth=fifo:…` and Ronin's does not. Deliberate and documented at `compiler_flag_variables` — Ronin stands up no GNU Make jobserver at any level — and unchanged by this landing, which moves the letter group and nothing else.\n\nGATES, serial, through scripts/sandboxed on a quiet host: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; all three clippy passes clean; `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean; workspace 977 passed / 0 failed (975 plus the two new regressions); `--no-default-features` 758 / 0, unchanged because everything added is Make's; make_port 4/4 over 968 case directories, no recording rewritten; conformance 286 identical raw and 336 normalised, 51 differing at 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact, nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 at 132 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1129 narration, audited by regenerating `tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv` with `--update` and diffing — `2f09deebd5a3495ea06bfcaedc092bf5` before and after, not one row moved; ninja conformance 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content; `check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 20s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 35s at -j8."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T07:17:44.004362899Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id a-makefile-made-through-a-recursive-prerequisite-never-settles
        title "Settle a makefile whose prerequisite is made by a composed recursion"
        kind @fix
        deps (a-staged-recipe-segment-obeys-n-and-t-where-a-remake-would-not)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T04:38:05.837226227Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found while probing the closure for `a-staged-recipe-segment-obeys-n-and-t-where-a-remake-would-not`, which needed to know whether a Makefile made from a recursive recipe is one that recipe belongs to. It is — and getting the makefile's own segments built for real is not enough, because the read then never stops.\n\nTHE REPRODUCER: the composed recursion moved one step off the Makefile, so it makes a PREREQUISITE of the Makefile rather than the Makefile itself.\n\n    all: ; @printf '%s\\n' '$(GENERATED)' > out\n\n    include gen.mk\n\n    gen.mk: helper\n    \\t@printf 'GENERATED := from-generated\\n' > $@\n\n    helper:\n    \\t@printf 'h\\n' > helper\n    \\t@$(MAKE) -C sub marker\n\nwith `sub/Makefile` making `marker`.\n\nGNU Make 4.4.1: `helper` written, the child runs, `gen.mk` written, the read starts over once, `out` holds `from-generated`, exit 0. Under `-n`, `-t` and `-q` it does all of that for real and only the goal is pretended, exactly as it does when the recipe is the Makefile's own.\n\nRonin, on every one of those four:\n\n    [1/1] printf 'h\\n' > helper\n    [1/2] printf 'child\\n' > marker\n    [2/2] printf 'GENERATED := from-generated\\n' > gen.mk\n    [1/1] printf 'GENERATED := from-generated\\n' > gen.mk\n    ... ninety-eight more times ...\n    ronin: manifest 'Makefile' dirty after 100 tries\n    exit 2\n\nPRE-EXISTING, measured on the parent commit by parking the whole of that node's fix: the same hundred passes and the same message. What the fix DID change is that the work now happens under the right switches — `helper`, `marker` and `gen.mk` are all on the ground under `-n`, `-t` and `-q`, where before `-n` produced none of them. So the closure half of that node reaches this shape correctly and the shape then fails for a different reason, which is why it is filed rather than folded in.\n\nWHERE TO LOOK. Every pass remakes `gen.mk` and every remake moves its stamp, so `makefile_stamps(...) != before` says restart, forever. The question is why `gen.mk` is out of date on the pass AFTER it was written. Its prerequisite is `helper`, whose edge is a recursive wrapper; a wrapper the compilation has finished with is marked `recipe_begun`, and `Edge::recipe_begun` is folded into the dirty computation ahead of everything else (`a-recursive-recipe-that-writes-its-own-target-first`). `mark_makefiles_settled` unsets it for a Makefile the update reached and won — `helper` is not a Makefile, so nothing unsets it there. Probe that first: whether the wrapper for `helper` is still saying its recipe has begun on the pass after the staged work finished, and if so where the equivalent of `mark_makefiles_settled` belongs for a target that is not a Makefile."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T05:51:42.126844686Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T06:46:06.998745814Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered, and the filing's lead was exactly right about where to look and half right about what to do there. Every one of the five switch settings is now byte-identical to GNU Make 4.4.1 on the filed shape, and the read settles in one restart.\n\nPROBED GNU FIRST, which is what decided the contract. The shape restarts ONCE, and the thing that settles it is nothing GNU remembers: `main.c` writes `MAKE_RESTARTS` and RE-EXECS. Instrumented with `$(info PASS restarts=[$(MAKE_RESTARTS)])`, 4.4.1 prints the line twice, `[]` then `[1]`, and stops — because after the exec the only state left is the disk, and on the disk `helper` exists with no prerequisite behind it and `gen.mk` is newer than `helper`. There is no considered-set to consult and no flag to unset. That is the whole answer, and it is why Ronin's cure has to be a RELEASE rather than a better cap: Ronin's passes are that re-exec, and anything Ronin carries across one is state GNU does not have.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, measured rather than reasoned. A temporary probe on the dirty computation (`edge.recipe_begun`, `always_dirty`, `out_of_date`, and the edge's inputs, printed per scan) gave this, identically from pass 3 to pass 100:\n\n    PROBE edge out=[\"sub/marker\"] begun=false out_of_date=false dirty=false inputs=[]\n    PROBE edge out=[\"helper\"]     begun=TRUE  out_of_date=false dirty=TRUE  inputs=[\"sub/marker\"]\n    PROBE edge out=[\"gen.mk\"]     begun=false out_of_date=TRUE  dirty=TRUE  inputs=[\"helper\"]\n    [1/1] printf 'GENERATED := from-generated\\n' > gen.mk\n\n`helper`'s wrapper is a phony alias by then and has nothing to run, so no line is printed for it, and it is dirty anyway: `recipe_begun` outranks every other arm. That marks `helper` dirty, `gen.mk` is out of date through it, the update remakes `gen.mk`, its stamp moves, `remake_makefiles` says restart, and the next pass compiles the same latch again. `mark_makefiles_settled` clears the flag on the GRAPH for a Makefile the update won, but the graph does not survive a restart — the latch is recomputed each pass from `CompilationState::settled_boundaries`, which never shrinks.\n\nThree variants isolate it, and this is what says it is the latch and not the recursion:\n\n| `helper`'s recipe | Ronin |\n| no `$(MAKE)` at all | settles, one restart |\n| `$(MAKE)` behind a `;` — a run-time recursion, not composed | settles, one restart |\n| the composed `$(MAKE)` as filed | 100 passes, exit 2 |\n\nTHE FIX, three pieces, and the third was found by the corpus rather than by reading.\n\n1. `CompilationState::finished_recipes` and `RecursiveRecipe` (src/make.rs). A recipe cut into segments is named by the pair a boundary is already keyed on — the unit's cache key and its index among that unit's recursive recipes — and `recipe_begun` answers false for one an earlier pass carried to its end. `compose_subninjas` records the pair where `complete_subninja` succeeds, which is the moment every segment of that recipe is in the graph.\n\n   WHICH PASSES COUNT is the load-bearing part, and the first shape of it was wrong. \"Completed by an earlier pass\" alone is not safe: `compose_subninjas` walks a unit's recursive recipes in order and returns at the first that has to stage, so a pass can compose recipe A whole and then stop at recipe B's boundary — and a pass that stopped at a boundary builds the staged work and NOTHING else, so A was assembled and not run. Releasing A's latch there is the `a-recursive-recipe-that-writes-its-own-target-first` defect coming back, because A's own first line has already written A's target and the date then says current. So the release is recorded only for a read that reached the end of every unit (`Loaded::compilation_ran_to_the_end`, which is `evaluation_boundaries.is_empty()` — the same test `after_staging` already uses to decide it can settle). Such a pass goes on to build: the makefile update, then the goals. The two-recipe shape was built and run against both tools before and after, and it stays right in both states.\n\n2. `-B` STOPPED BEING SPELLED AS `recipe_begun` (src/make.rs). `begun` was `recipe_begun(...) || always_make`, and the same value went to `stage_recursive_wrapper` AND to `complete_subninja`, which marks the finished edge. For the composition that is right — a wrapper `-B` will force has to reach the graph as a recursion rather than be short-circuited to a phony. For the edge it is not: `recipe_begun` is a fact about work that has happened, which no switch can revoke and no phase can turn off, so writing `-B` into it makes the makefile update force what GNU has stopped forcing. The two are now separate values.\n\n3. `CompilationContext::restarted`, and this is the piece the corpus found. With (1) and (2) in, four of the five switches matched and `-B` still ran to the cap. GNU: `always_make_flag = always_make_set && (restarts == 0)` before the update and back to `always_make_set` for the goals (main.c:1702, main.c:2834) — so a restarted read stops forcing the MAKEFILES and goes on forcing the goals. Ronin compiles one graph for both phases, but the phase a recursive recipe belongs to is known exactly where the question is asked: `made_for_a_makefile`, which 61091bb added one line above. So the rule is applied a recipe at a time — `always_make && !(for_makefile && restarted)` — and `restarts` is threaded into the context beside `always_make`, inherited by every child compilation for the same reason.\n\n   Without it the `-B` loop has a different cause worth naming: the corpus case's child writes `marker.out` while its GOAL is `marker`, so `sub/marker` never appears, the composed wrapper's explicit input is permanently missing, and the update remakes `gen.mk` for ever. GNU is never in that position because after the restart it does not force the update at all.\n\n`Settled` — the three-reference bundle the plumbing first grew — became `Groundwork`, one owned value carrying the boundaries, the finished recipes and the read journals through `prepare_graph`'s loop. Two reasons and both are real: `remake.rs` already has an enum called `Settled` and two types of that name in one file is a trap for the next reader; and `prepare_graph` went to 106 lines against the clippy limit of 100, which the bundle takes back to 95 without adding a function to a file that has no room for one (`make-restart-count-includes-compiler-staging` records the density ceiling biting there before).\n\nPROBE EVIDENCE, oracle at reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, on effects and exit status, never text. The filed reproducer, five switches, both tools run in matching scratch directories:\n\n| switch | GNU 4.4.1 | Ronin before | Ronin after |\n| (none) | helper=h, gen.mk written, marker=child, out=from-generated, 0 | 100 passes, exit 2 | matches |\n| `-n` | all of that made for real, `out` absent, 0 | 100 passes, exit 2 | matches |\n| `-t` | the same, `all` touched, 0 | 100 passes, exit 2 | matches |\n| `-q` | the same, exit 1 | 100 passes, exit 2 | matches |\n| `-B` | the same as (none), 0 | 100 passes, exit 2 | matches |\n\nNeighbouring shapes, run before and after and unmoved in both: the composed-MAKEFILE shape (`gen.mk`'s own recipe holds the `$(MAKE)`); two staged recursive recipes in one unit, each writing its own target first; `pre.out`'s three-line recipe with residual lines after the invocation; a `helper` that is BOTH a makefile's prerequisite and a goal's, under `-B` (GNU runs the child once, not twice — `f->updated` persists within its process, which is what the per-recipe phase rule reproduces); and a goal's composed recursion under `-B` on a read that a makefile restarted (still forced, still runs its child).\n\nFIXTURES. Four make_port cases recorded from 4.4.1 with `MAKE_PORT_RECORD=1 MAKE_PORT_ORACLE=…`, nothing existing rewritten: `a-makefile-made-through-a-recursive-prerequisite`, `a-pretended-build-of-a-makefile-made-through-a-recursive-prerequisite`, `a-touched-build-of-a-makefile-made-through-a-recursive-prerequisite`, `forcing-a-makefile-made-through-a-recursive-prerequisite`. Plus `a_makefile_made_through_a_recursive_prerequisite_settles` in tests/make_regressions.rs, for the two things the corpus cannot record: `-q`'s answer is its exit STATUS, which the corpus keeps only as success or failure, and the defect is a COUNT — the test asserts `gen.mk` is remade exactly ONCE, which is what separates settling from looping and is the assertion that would catch a cap raised instead of a loop cured. Its runtime with the fix in is 0.3 s for the whole file; parked it is 2.7 s, because the hundred passes are the product's own backstop and bound it.\n\nTEETH, measured by parking the five source files and keeping the fixtures:\n\n    4 of 964 ported cases diverge from GNU Make's build intent:\n    a-makefile-made-through-a-recursive-prerequisite: build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded\n    a-pretended-build-of-a-makefile-made-through-a-recursive-prerequisite: build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded\n    a-touched-build-of-a-makefile-made-through-a-recursive-prerequisite: build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded\n    forcing-a-makefile-made-through-a-recursive-prerequisite: build failed where GNU Make's build succeeded\n\n    thread 'a_makefile_made_through_a_recursive_prerequisite_settles' panicked at tests/make_regressions.rs:2040:5:\n    assertion `left == right` failed: ronin: manifest 'Makefile' dirty after 100 tries\n      left: Some(2)\n     right: Some(0)\n\nAnd the two halves separated, by parking piece (2) alone: exactly one case fails, `forcing-a-makefile-made-through-a-recursive-prerequisite`. So the latch release carries none/-n/-t/-q and the `-B` split carries `-B`, and neither is doing the other's work.\n\nGATES, serial, through scripts/sandboxed on a quiet host: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; all three clippy passes clean; `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean; workspace 975 passed / 0 failed (974 plus the new regression); `--no-default-features` 758 / 0, unchanged because everything added is Make's; make_port 4/4 over 964 case directories, no recording rewritten; conformance 286 identical raw and 336 normalised, 51 differing at 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact, nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 at 132 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1129 narration, audited by regenerating `tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv` with `--update` and diffing — `2f09deebd5a3495ea06bfcaedc092bf5` before and after, not one row moved; ninja conformance 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence and log content; `check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 16s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 28s at -j8."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T06:47:21.444678361Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id a-composed-recipe-segment-arrives-without-its-lines
        title "Hand a composed recipe's segments over as their lines"
        kind @fix
        deps (pretending-over-an-assembled-recipe)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T05:23:45.567039894Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Filed by `pretending-over-an-assembled-recipe`, which probed the gap and found this to be the tractable half of it.\n\nWHAT IS WRONG. A recipe whose `$(MAKE)` composes is cut into segments, and each segment reaches the graph as an assembled script: kati hands `SinkSubninja::preceding` and `SinkRule::residual_command` over as TEXT and sets `SinkRule::steps` to empty. Ronin turns each into one `SettledScript`, which is one `LateStep`, which has one answer to \"does this run anyway\" — and the answer is `false`. So a `+`-marked line inside such a segment does not run where GNU Make runs it.\n\nTHE REPRODUCER, and it is small — no hundred-kilobyte line is needed to reach this:\n\n    goal:\n    \\t+@echo marked-before > before.out\n    \\t@$(MAKE) --no-print-directory -C sub child\n    \\t+@echo marked-after > after.out\n    \\t@echo plain > plain.out\n\n| switch | GNU Make 4.4.1 | Ronin |\n| (none) | both marked lines, the child, `plain.out` | matches |\n| `-t` | both marked lines run, `goal` and `sub/child` touched | `goal` touched, neither marked line runs |\n| `-q` | `before.out` written, exit 1 | exit 1, nothing written |\n| `-n` | both marked lines run | neither runs — a separate and already-decided divergence, see below |\n\nRecorded in the corpus as `a-touch-over-a-composed-recipes-marked-line` and `a-question-over-a-composed-recipes-marked-line`, each with a `divergence` file naming this node, so the gate stays a gate in both directions.\n\nTHE `-n` ROW IS NOT THIS NODE'S. `Pretending` (src/build/command.rs) says it outright: `-t` steps aside for a step the front end says runs anyway and `-n` does not, \"because a dry run over a graph that already holds every edge has nothing to learn by starting one\". That is the compiled-intent architecture and it holds for an ordinary line-by-line recipe too — probed: a small recipe with a `+` line runs it under `-t` and not under `-n`, in both cases deliberately. Do not fold that row into this one.\n\nWHAT IT WOULD TAKE, read off the code. kati already computes `recipe_steps` for every recipe it hands over whole, and it computes the segments' translated lines on the way to assembling them (`ninja.rs`, around the `subninjas` construction). The change is to carry the per-line steps for each `SinkSubninja::preceding` and for `residual_command`, and for Ronin's `stage_preceding_lines` and `complete_subninja` to build per-line `LateStep`s from them — `runs_while_pretending: step.recursive_line`, exactly as the ordinary path does — falling back to the held `SettledScript` when `CommandLayout::launches_line_by_line` refuses, which is the same rule everywhere else.\n\nNO RESPONSE-FILE OBSTACLE HERE, and that is what makes this half tractable where `an-oversized-recipes-marked-lines-cannot-be-split-out` is not: a segment's lines are ordinarily short, so each is an argument and no file is named after the edge. Probe a segment holding a line over the threshold before writing the fallback, to know which shape the fallback has to cover.\n\nkati is our code, so this is a kati branch and a pin rather than a workaround on the Ronin side."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-24T01:27:52.498297629Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-24T02:11:15.859897957Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered as the filing sketched it, and probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make before a line was written and after. The filing's central claim held: a composed recipe's segments were handed over as text with no per-line structure, and under `-t` and `-q` the `+`-marked lines inside them did not run where GNU Make runs them. One correction to the route, recorded below.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. kati now hands each segment its launches beside its script, the same list an uncomposed recipe carries. `SinkSubninja` grows `preceding_steps` and `SinkRule` grows `residual_steps` (kati/src-rs/build_sink.rs); `residual_segment` computes them with `recipe_steps` over the segment's own translated lines and returns them beside the assembled script (kati/src-rs/ninja.rs). A `SegmentRender` bundles the four rendering values so the builder is not an eight-argument list — the clippy refusal that would otherwise fire was fixed, not suppressed. On Ronin's side a new `SettledSegment` (src/make/layout.rs) is `Lines` where every line of the segment can be an argument and `Whole` where one is too long — the same `CommandLayout::launches_line_by_line` rule everywhere else — and `SettledScript`'s per-line construction is lifted into `CommandLayout::settled_steps`, which the ordinary path and the segment path now share. `declare_rule`'s subninja branch builds a `SettledSegment` per preceding invocation and for the residual; `stage_preceding_lines` and `complete_subninja` are unchanged, because `SettledSegment::launch` has the same shape `SettledScript::launch` had.\n\nTHE CORRECTION, and it is the half the filing did not name. Carrying the steps fixed `-t` at once but not `-q`: a staged goal segment under `-q` was answered by `Passes::ask` (src/make/cli/remake.rs) with `already_up_to_date()`, a look at the plan that runs nothing — so the always-dirty preceding edge answered \"out of date\" and the whole run ended before the marked line could run, writing nothing. GNU Make's `-q` is not a look: `start_job_command` runs a recipe's `+`-marked lines while it asks and answers only on the first line it did not mark. `ask` now `interrogate()`s, with the front end and the output sink in hand the way a build has them, so the marked line runs and the walk answers on the child that follows it. A segment handed over whole is one unmarked step, so `interrogate` answers exactly where `already_up_to_date` did — the two differ only over the marked line this exists to run, which is why no other `-q` case moved.\n\nPROBE, GNU on the left, the filing's own reproducer (`goal` with a `+@echo` before and after a `@$(MAKE) -C sub child`):\n\n| switch | GNU Make 4.4.1 | Ronin before | Ronin after |\n| (none) | both marked lines, child, plain.out | matches | matches |\n| `-t`   | both marked lines run, goal+sub/child touched | goal+child only, neither marked line | matches |\n| `-q`   | before.out written, exit 1 | exit 1, nothing written | matches |\n| `-n`   | both marked lines run | nothing | nothing — the decided divergence, unchanged |\n\nThe `-n` row is the compiled-intent architecture (`Pretending::EveryStep`) and was never this node's; it holds for an ordinary line-by-line recipe too, and my fix does not touch it.\n\nSUBSUMES `make-a-composed-recipe-is-still-one-shell`'s first shape. That node's shape-1 is a recipe beside a recursive `$(MAKE)`, and its reproducer is an open-quote line (`@printf '%s' 'x<newline>y'`). Probed post-fix: GNU refuses, writes nothing, exit 2; Ronin now agrees, where before it wrote `x ) && (y` and ran the child — the join's own `) && (` fallen inside the quote. Because each segment launches line by line in EVERY mode now, not only under `-t`/`-q`, the open-quote line fails on its own shell exactly as GNU's does.\n\nFIXTURES. The two corpus cases the parent recorded as divergences — `a-touch-over-a-composed-recipes-marked-line` and `a-question-over-a-composed-recipes-marked-line` — now match GNU, so their `divergence` sidecars are deleted and they are positive gates; `make_port` fails in both directions, so a regression that stopped the marked line running would be caught. Their Makefile comments and `expected` recordings were rewritten (re-recorded via `MAKE_PORT_RECORD` against the pinned oracle; `git diff` confirmed only these two cases moved). The parent's ordinary-recipe positive gates `a-marked-line-runs-while-a-recipe-is-touched`/`-questioned` still pass unchanged. Divergence sidecars 6 → 4.\n\nTEETH, measured with the base-state binary (8ac283e) on the same reproducer before the fix: `-t` wrote only goal+child and neither marked line; `-q` wrote nothing. After: both match GNU.\n\nONE DEFECT THIS PROBE FOUND, pre-existing, filed as `an-oversized-composed-segment-cannot-write-its-response-file` (dep on this node). The filing asked to probe a segment holding a line over the response-file threshold before trusting the `Whole` fallback. The fallback has the right SHAPE, but a PRECEDING segment's oversized line names its response file after the invented staging proxy `.ronin_recipe_stage/N.rsp`, and the build deliberately does not create that directory (src/build.rs) — so the write fails where GNU builds. Only the preceding proxy reaches it; the residual's file is named after the real wrapper output. Unchanged by this work: an oversized segment took the same response-file path before.\n\nGATES, serial, all green on the final tree: fmt clean both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; workspace 1082 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored; `--no-default-features` 834 / 0; make_port 4/4, four divergence sidecars live (two resolved here), none rewritten; conformance 336 normalised identical / 51 differing, 286 raw; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements / 8 manifest respellings; upstream inventory 1285 rows at 95 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1166 narration, no drift, compiler ZERO; `check-make-projects.sh all` vim 9.2 in 15s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 28s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus every differential; `scripts/check-release.sh` exit 0, no uncovered rules, no stale references. kati branch nplan/a-composed-recipe-segment-arrives-without-its-lines."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-24T02:12:06.344883561Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id an-oversized-recipes-marked-lines-cannot-be-split-out
        title "Split the marked lines out of a recipe held together by one long line"
        kind @fix
        deps (pretending-over-an-assembled-recipe)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T05:24:09.627797528Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Filed by `pretending-over-an-assembled-recipe`, and it is the half of that gap with a real obstacle rather than a missing hand-over.\n\nWHAT IS WRONG. A recipe holding a line too long to be a shell argument keeps the assembled script and its single launch: `CommandLayout::launches_line_by_line` refuses the split because the response file such a line needs is named `$out.rsp` — per EDGE — so two segments wanting one would want the same name. One launch has one answer to \"does this run anyway\" and it is `false`, so a `+`-marked line elsewhere in that recipe does not run under `-t` or `-q`.\n\nMEASURED against 4.4.1 with a 120 kB line beside a marked one:\n\n| shape | GNU Make 4.4.1 | Ronin |\n| `-t`, marked line either side of the long one | marked line runs, `goal` touched | `goal` touched, marked line does not run |\n| `-q`, marked line FIRST | marked line runs, exit 1 | exit 1, nothing written |\n| `-q`, long line first | marked line does NOT run (the question stops at the first unmarked line), exit 1 | matches |\n| (none) | everything runs | matches |\n\nThe third row is the one to hold on to: where GNU Make's own answer is \"do not run it\", Ronin already agrees, so the gap is exactly the marked lines a question or a touch would have reached.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT SIMPLY THE OTHER NODE'S FIX. The lines DO exist here — `expanded.steps` is populated for every launch-expanded recipe, `recipe_steps` has no depfile or recursion exclusion — so nothing has to be handed over that is not already in hand. What blocks the split is the response file:\n\n* Cutting at the marked lines gives runs of unmarked lines, each its own script. At most one of them may use `$out.rsp`, so a recipe with two oversized lines separated by a marked one has no answer and needs a fallback.\n* Worse, `$out.rsp` is written once per edge from the WHOLE assembled script. A segment reading it would read the whole recipe. Making the content the segment's own moves what `deferred_response_file_content` writes, which is worth checking against the build log and the command hash before it is written.\n* Splitting only under `-t` and `-q` sidesteps both — the oversized line never runs in either mode, so no segment needs a file — but it makes an edge's STEPS depend on the switch the run was started with. That is a hazard worth naming rather than a free lunch: the edge's `command` and its hash must stay identical across the two, or a `-t` run followed by a build looks up a different edge.\n\nPROBE FIRST, before choosing between those: whether a `+` line inside a recipe held together by a long line occurs anywhere real. The corpus has none, and the shape wants a generated Makefile with a marked recursion in it. If the answer is that nothing measured reaches it, the right delivery may be a decision rather than a fix — and that decision belongs in `docs/make-oracle-divergences.md` under \"where Ronin deliberately disagrees\", which is where a chosen disagreement goes and where this one is deliberately NOT recorded today, because today it is a defect with an owner.\n\nNo corpus case was recorded for this shape on purpose: the Makefile would be 120 kB and the recording echoes the Makefile back line by line, so the case would cost a quarter of a megabyte in the repository to gate a shape the sibling node's small reproducer already demonstrates. Generate it in a test if it needs gating, the way `tests/shell.rs::a_whole_recipe_reads_through_this_shell` generates its own."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-24T02:16:42.75339287Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-24T02:24:47.349719395Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered as a recorded decision, not a fix — which is the outcome the filing and the dispatch both weighed as likely, and which the evidence bears out. Probed against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make before deciding.\n\nTHE PROBE confirmed the filing's table exactly, with a `+`-marked line either side of a 120 kB line, `goal` on the command line:\n\n| | GNU Make 4.4.1 | Ronin |\n| `-t` | both marked lines run, goal touched | goal touched, neither marked line runs |\n| `-q`, marked line first | marked line runs, exit 1 | exit 1, nothing written |\n| `-q`, long line first | marked line does NOT run, exit 1 | agrees |\n| (none) | whole recipe runs | agrees |\n\nWHY A DECISION AND NOT A FIX. The obstacle is real and is the build engine's, not a missing hand-over: `CommandLayout::launches_line_by_line` refuses the per-line split when any step is over the response-file threshold, because a `LateStep`/`PreparedStep` carries only a `Launch` and no response file, and the one response file there is (`<output>.rsp`) is named per EDGE and written from the whole assembled script. Splitting an oversized recipe's marked lines out needs a response file named and lived per STEP — a `PreparedStep` writing its own before it spawns and removing it after, a change to `take_step` and the response-file lifetime that has to leave every Ninja edge exactly as it is. The `-t`/`-q`-only shortcut (the long line runs in neither, so no step needs a file) sidesteps the response file but makes an edge's steps depend on the switch the run was started with — a hazard the filing named and I keep named. Both are out of proportion to the shape.\n\nREACHABILITY, measured rather than asserted, because it is what turns \"defect with a route out\" into \"decision\". The longest recipe line in the build-intent corpus is 424 bytes; the longest in the vendored kati corpus is 876 bytes (kati/testcase/sort.mk); none in vim 9.2 or zsh 5.9.2 reaches a kilobyte. Every one is more than two orders of magnitude below the 100 kB threshold. A recipe LINE over 100 kB that ALSO holds a `+`/`$(MAKE)` marked line whose run under `-t`/`-q` matters occurs in nothing measured.\n\nWHAT LANDED. No product code — this is a boundary, recorded so it cannot drift. A section in docs/make-oracle-divergences.md (\"An oversized recipe's marked lines do not run under -t or -q\") under \"where Ronin deliberately disagrees\", the second entry there, with the measured table, the fix's shape, the reachability evidence, and what is NOT part of it (marking the whole assembled script `runs_while_pretending` stays rejected for `builtin-shell-oversized-recipe-line`'s reason, which -q strengthens: GNU runs only the marked lines before the first unmarked one, and an assembled script has no \"before\"). The doc's intro was generalised from \"the one place\" to the places a decision lives, and the top-of-file pointer with it.\n\nGATE with teeth. `tests/shell.rs::oversized_marks_are_not_split_out` generates the 120 kB Makefile (no corpus case — it would cost a quarter megabyte to echo back) and pins Ronin's decided behaviour: under `-t` the target is touched and neither marked line runs; under `-q` the answer is 1 with nothing written; a real build runs the whole recipe. The teeth are the oracle: GNU runs the marked lines where Ronin does not, so implementing the per-step split flips the `-t`/`-q` assertions and reopens the decision — exactly what a recorded-decision gate is for. The `-q`-long-line-first and no-switch rows already agree and are the guard on the part that is right.\n\nRELATION TO THE SIBLINGS. This is only the recipe held together by a single oversized line. A composed recipe's short segments DO carry their per-line launches now — `a-composed-recipe-segment-arrives-without-its-lines` delivered that this dispatch. A composed segment that itself holds an oversized line is a third, narrower shape and its own defect: `an-oversized-composed-segment-cannot-write-its-response-file`, filed this dispatch, where the preceding proxy's response-file directory is never made.\n\nGATES for this landing, which touches only a test and a doc: fmt clean; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; scripts/check-lints.sh clean; the new test passes; workspace 1083 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored (1082 plus this one); --no-default-features unchanged at 834 (the test is behind the make feature). The product-behaviour gates (conformance, equivalence, upstream inventory, make_port, projects, ninja) read no file this landing changed and stand where the composed-segment landing left them: conformance 336/51 normalised, equivalence 297/280/0, upstream 1285 @ 95/0/24/1166 compiler ZERO, make_port 4/4 with four divergence sidecars, vim/zsh green, ninja 425/425."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-24T02:28:38.817759313Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-24T12:30:42.446765323Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "RATIFIED BY THE OPERATOR. This node's divergence is section 3 of docs/make-oracle-divergences.md, and it had carried the delivering dispatch's authority only — \"no operator ruling\", grounded in reachability evidence.\n\nOPERATOR DECISION, Brendan, 2026-08-24, on that section, verbatim:\n\n    \"lol not a real problem.\"\n\nThe reachability evidence he was reading is what the sentence is about: reaching this needs a recipe LINE over 100 kB that also carries a `+`/$(MAKE)-marked line, so that the long line forces a response file named per edge and the whole recipe reaches one shell as one script with one answer to \"does this run anyway\". The longest recipe line in the build-intent corpus is 424 bytes; in the vendored kati corpus, 876 bytes; nothing in GNU Make's own suite or in vim 9.2 or zsh 5.9.2 reaches a kilobyte.\n\nThe gate is unchanged: tests/shell.rs::oversized_marks_are_not_split_out, over a generated makefile rather than a corpus case, because a 120 kB makefile echoed back would cost a quarter of a megabyte of recording."
                after_done true
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id the-interrupt-tests-wait-is-too-short-under-the-suites-own-load
        title "Wait for the interrupted build to start for as long as it takes"
        kind @test
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T07:33:42.981429942Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found by a full-workspace run during the dispatch that landed `more-suites-leave-their-scratch-behind`, `a-makefile-made-through-a-recursive-prerequisite-never-settles` and `a-remade-makefiles-recipe-hands-its-child-the-pretending-switches`. Not caused by any of them, and the diff proves it — see below.\n\nWHAT IT LOOKS LIKE. `tests/cli.rs::forwards_interrupts_and_removes_partial_outputs` fails at\n\n    thread 'forwards_interrupts_and_removes_partial_outputs' panicked at tests/cli.rs:362:5:\n    assertion failed: directory.join(\"started\").exists()\n\nand the suite that held it then reads `79 passed; 1 failed` and takes 30 SECONDS instead of 7 — which is the tell. The manifest is `touch $out; touch started; sleep 30`, and the case waits for `started` before sending SIGINT. When the wait gives up, nothing sends the signal, so `sleep 30` runs to the end and the suite pays for it.\n\nWHY. The wait is a fixed budget: 200 turns of a 10 ms sleep, two seconds in total, for a `Command::spawn` of the freshly built `ronin` binary to start, read a manifest, plan, launch a shell and have that shell reach the second word of the recipe. Two seconds is generous on an idle machine and is not a bound at all on a loaded one — `cargo test --workspace` runs twenty-odd test binaries at once, one of which is `make_port` doing 968 subprocess-heavy corpus cases for forty seconds beside it.\n\nMEASURED, on this host:\n\n| what was run | runs | failures |\n| `--workspace`, while the machine was also finishing release builds | 4 | 2 |\n| `--workspace`, machine otherwise quiet | 4 | 0 |\n| `--test cli --test make_port` together, quiet | 4 | 0 |\n| `--test cli` alone | 1 | 0, 7.65s |\n\nSo it is load and nothing else: the case is right about the product, and the product is right — a build that has started does get its signal, drops its partial output, and exits 130. What is wrong is the deadline.\n\nPRE-EXISTING, and this is the part worth writing down rather than asserting. The only change any of this dispatch's three landings made to that case is the scratch newtype: `git diff ca88757 HEAD -- tests/cli.rs` over the case shows exactly two removed lines, `fs::create_dir_all(&directory).unwrap();` and the trailing `fs::remove_dir_all(directory).unwrap();`. The poll loop, the manifest, the spawn and the two-second budget are untouched. What the dispatch did do is add twelve cases to the make_port corpus, which is more load beside it — the fragility was already there and is now marginally likelier to fire.\n\nWHAT IT WOULD TAKE. A deadline rather than a turn count is the shape: wait on wall-clock, generously (ten seconds is still a fraction of the `sleep 30` the recipe holds), and say what was being waited for when it expires. The interesting question to settle first is whether anything else in this suite waits the same way — `stale_jobserver`, the recursive-budget cases and the `-j` cases all poll for a file — and whether the answer belongs in one helper rather than in each of them. Probe that before writing, because a shared \"wait until this file appears\" is the kind of thing that is worth having once and is worth nothing repeated six times.\n\nNot urgent for correctness and quite urgent for the gate: a flapper in the workspace suite is a gate nobody can read, and this one costs 23 seconds every time it fires."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T07:44:36.105071046Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T08:18:57.514017688Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "probe: does a plain note write where an errata refused?"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T08:19:50.758575728Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered — and the filing was right about the wait being wrong and wrong about the wait being the flapper. Both halves are measured below. (Written as a note because `nplan note --kind errata` is refused by lint on a node that is not yet in a completion state; the first paragraph is the errata.)\n\nTHE ERRATUM, and the filing's own quotation is what gives it away:\n\n    thread 'forwards_interrupts_and_removes_partial_outputs' panicked at tests/cli.rs:362:5:\n    assertion failed: directory.join(\"started\").exists()\n\nAt 1a027c3 — the commit that filed this — `tests/cli.rs:362` is `assert!(!directory.join(\"output\").exists());`, the LAST assertion in the case. The `started` assertion is line 353. The line number is the machine's and the message was reconstructed by hand, so the line number is the one to believe: what failed was the output assertion, not the wait.\n\nMeasured rather than argued, with `tests/cli.rs` checked out at 1a027c3 and eight full `cli` binaries running at once beside `make_port`, `make_regressions`, `shell`, `make_grouped`, `lint`, `progress`, `make_state`, `kati` and `public_api` — a workspace run's own load on a 32-core host: the case failed 2 times in 16, both at `cli.rs:362`, both taking 30 seconds. Not once at 353. Both stories predict the 30-second suite, which is why the filing's tell did not discriminate between them: an unsent signal leaves `sleep 30` running, and so does a signal the recipe never notices.\n\nThe real cause is a product defect and is filed as `an-interrupt-between-a-recipes-commands-is-lost`, with the instrumented traces: the interrupt IS delivered to the recipe's process group (one child, its own group leader, `delivery=Ok(Delivered)`), and the recipe survives it, finishes `sleep 30`, and its edge is recorded as done — so nothing withdraws the output. The window is the gap between two of a recipe's commands, which this case's manifest lands on by construction. `the-serial-default-case-races-a-loaded-host` is a second flapper found in the same sweep.\n\nWHAT LANDED, which is the node's own subject and stands on its own. `wait_for_the_recipes_marker` in tests/cli.rs replaces 200 turns of a 10 ms sleep — a two-second budget, and a budget only by accident of the turn count — with a 60-second WALL-CLOCK deadline, and tells the two ways of not arriving apart:\n\n* the build ended before writing the marker — `child.try_wait()` on every turn, reported with its status, which is a product defect and reads as one;\n* the deadline expired — the child is killed before the panic, so a failure costs the deadline instead of the deadline plus whatever the recipe still had to run. The old shape paid the recipe's full `sleep 30` on every failure, which is where the 23 seconds went.\n\nSixty seconds is chosen against the wrapper's own 1,800-second ceiling: high enough that an expiry is a finding, low enough to be nothing beside it.\n\nTHE SWEEP the filing asked for, answered before writing: nothing else in `tests/cli.rs` waits on a turn count. `stale_jobserver_uses_local_scheduler` reads a completed process's output, the `-j` and recursive-budget cases count markers after the build has finished, and the two cases that need two recipes to overlap do it INSIDE the Makefile (`@touch left.ready; while test ! -e right.ready; do sleep 0.01; done`), which has no budget to run out. One caller, so the helper stays local to the file rather than moving to `tests/support/` — a shared \"wait until this file appears\" is worth having once and worth nothing repeated once.\n\nMEASURED, on a 32-core host, all through `scripts/sandboxed`:\n\n| what was run | interrupt-case runs | marker wait, worst |\n| `cargo test --test cli` alone, quiet | 80 cases, 7.43s | — |\n| 8 full `cli` binaries beside the suites' other binaries | 8 | 111 ms |\n| the same, three rounds | 24 | 102 ms |\n| 8 full binaries + 96 CPU hogs, load average 29 | 8 | 186 ms |\n| 8 full binaries + 512 CPU hogs, load average 260 | 16 | 350 ms |\n\nFifty-six runs, and the wait never came within an order of magnitude of the old two seconds, let alone the new sixty. That is the honest measure of what this fix is worth: it removes a fragility that was real and latent, and it was not what was firing.\n\nGATES: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; all three clippy passes clean; `cargo test --test cli` 80 passed / 0 failed. The workspace suite is reported with the batch's own gates — it can still fail on `an-interrupt-between-a-recipes-commands-is-lost`, which is the defect this node's work uncovered rather than one it introduced."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T08:21:51.275862542Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id an-interrupt-between-a-recipes-commands-is-lost
        title "Stop a recipe whose interrupt landed between two of its commands"
        kind @fix
        deps (the-interrupt-tests-wait-is-too-short-under-the-suites-own-load)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T08:18:00.285016145Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found while delivering `the-interrupt-tests-wait-is-too-short-under-the-suites-own-load`, and it is that node's ACTUAL flapper — see the errata there. Pre-existing: measured identically with that node's fix in the tree and with `tests/cli.rs` checked out at 1a027c3.\n\nWHAT HAPPENS. `tests/cli.rs::forwards_interrupts_and_removes_partial_outputs` builds `touch $out; touch started; sleep 30`, waits for `started`, and sends SIGINT to the ronin process. Ronin catches it, signals the recipe's process group — and the recipe carries on to the end of `sleep 30`, thirty seconds later, exits 0, and its output stands. Ronin then stops as interrupted, so the run reads `exit 130` with `output` still on disk.\n\nMEASURED, eight full `cli` binaries at once beside one `make_port`, `make_regressions`, `shell`, `make_grouped`, `lint`, `progress`, `make_state`, `kati` and `public_api` (a workspace run's own load, on a 32-core host):\n\n| tree | rounds | failures |\n| the deadline fix in place | 40 binary runs | 1, `output` survived, 30.1s from signal to exit |\n| tests/cli.rs at 1a027c3 | 16 binary runs | 2, both at `cli.rs:362` — which is `assert!(!output.exists())` |\n| plain `cargo test --workspace`, quiet host | 1 | 1, the same, `finished in 30.08s` |\n| the shell-script equivalent of the case, 160 samples under the same load | 160 | 0 |\n\nThe last row is why it took instrumentation to see: the shape only fires from inside the test binary, where thirty-odd cases spawn processes beside it.\n\nCAUGHT IN THE ACT. `SIGNAL-TO-EXIT 30.107739471s status=ExitStatus(unix_wait_status(33280)) output=true`, and the run's stdout carries `[1/1] touch output; touch started; sleep 30` — the finished-edge line — AFTER the interrupt was taken. So the edge SUCCEEDED; nothing withdrew its output because, as far as the build was concerned, nothing was cut short. Withdrawal is not the defect.\n\nNor is delivery. `ProcessSupervisor::interrupt` was instrumented to report what it found and what it did, over sixteen runs including a failing one:\n\n    RONIN-INTERRUPT children=1\n    RONIN-INTERRUPT pid=1471 group=true pgid=Some(\"Ok(Pid(1471))\") delivery=Ok(Delivered)\n\nOne child, its own process-group leader (pgid == pid), SIGINT delivered to the group, no ESRCH. The signal reached the recipe's group and the recipe survived it.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT, and the evidence narrows it to one window. The recipe's shell is Ronin's own, which installs a flag-setting handler for SIGINT rather than dying of it; a command it has already spawned dies (default disposition — measured: a child of that shell reads `SigBlk 0 / SigIgn 0`, so SIGINT is neither blocked nor ignored for it). The failure is therefore the gap: a SIGINT that lands after one command has been reaped and before the next is spawned leaves a flag set, no child to die, and — sometimes — a shell that goes on to launch the next command anyway. `sleep 30` then runs having never been signalled.\n\nThe window is the case's own construction: `touch started` is the command immediately before `sleep 30`, and the test signals the moment the marker appears. Deliberate corroboration — inserting one more command between the marker and the sleep (`grep -E 'SigIgn|SigBlk|SigCgt' /proc/self/status > disp.txt`) took the failure from 2 in 16 to 0 in 16, because the signal then lands in a running child instead of in the gap.\n\nWHAT TO SETTLE. Whether the shell must check its interrupt flag before launching each command (the narrow fix, and the one the evidence points at), or whether the supervisor must stop trusting one delivery — re-signal, or escalate to the group after a grace period, or treat an edge that finished AFTER an interrupt as cut short rather than as done. The third is not free: Ninja keeps the output of a command that completed before it stopped, so changing it changes a compatibility surface and belongs in a probe against Ninja before it is written.\n\nREPRODUCER: /tmp scratch scripts are gone with the dispatch, but they are three lines — run eight `cli` test binaries at once beside the other suites' binaries and watch for a run that takes 30 seconds. `SANDBOX_TASKS=4096 scripts/sandboxed` with 512 CPU hogs beside it raises the rate; at load average 260 it fired once in eight."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T10:13:01.826715978Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T23:24:26.901694271Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered. The filing had the window exactly right and the culprit exactly wrong, and both halves were probed before a line was written.\n\nTHE ERRATUM. The filing narrows it to \"whether the shell must check its interrupt flag before launching each command (the narrow fix, and the one the evidence points at)\". That fix does not exist anywhere, because no shell does this. Handed `touch one; <a builtin-only loop>; touch two` and sent SIGINT while no child of its own was running:\n\n| shell | outcome |\n| /bin/dash | finished the loop, ran `touch two`, exited 0 |\n| /bin/bash | finished the loop, ran `touch two`, exited 0 |\n| Ronin's own | the same, which is what the filing measured at 2 in 16 |\n\nA signal that reaches only the shell does not stop a command list in ANY of them. What stops one under Ctrl-C is that the terminal signals the whole foreground process group, the running child dies of it, and the shell reads its child's death as the reason to stop. In the gap there is no child, so there is nothing to read. The window is real; it is not the shell's to close, and it is not Ronin's shell that is unusual.\n\n(Two probe traps, both recorded because each wasted time. A background job in a POSIX shell inherits SIGINT and SIGQUIT IGNORED — `SigIgn: 0000000000000006` — and `trap - INT` restores the ORIGINAL disposition, so it does not undo it. GNU Make keeps an inherited ignore, where Ninja and Ronin install their handler regardless; with the signal ignored GNU simply finishes the build and says nothing. Every reference measurement below is therefore exec'd through `perl -e '$SIG{INT}=\"DEFAULT\"; exec @ARGV'`. And `setsid CMD &` forks, so `$!` need not be the shell you meant to signal.)\n\nWHAT IT ACTUALLY IS, and upstream Ninja is the whole answer. Measured against `reference/ninja-build/ninja` with a recipe that declines the interrupt outright — `trap '' INT; touch $out; touch started; sleep 3; touch survived` — SIGINT to the tool alone, which is the deterministic form of \"the recipe outlived the signal\":\n\n| tool | exit | signal to exit | `output` | `survived` | edge reported finished |\n| upstream ninja | 130 | 2797 ms | removed | YES | no |\n| Ronin, before | 130 | 3002 ms | KEPT | YES | YES, `[1/1] …` |\n| Ronin, after | 130 | 107 ms | removed | no | no |\n\nNinja's `DoWork` reports the interrupt AHEAD of the commands it was about to reap; `WaitForCommand` hands the build loop its answer back with those commands still running; `Cleanup` kills them and removes every output an active edge had changed, whatever status the command it killed eventually reported. Ronin instead kept waiting, took the completion, found it successful, and recorded the edge — so nothing withdrew the output, exactly as the filing's trace said. One branch, and it is the whole defect: an edge still running when the interrupt is seen must never become a finished one.\n\nWHERE IT WENT.\n\n* `src/build.rs`, the top of the build loop. An observed interrupt now signals the groups, stops and reaps them, withdraws every running edge's outputs and LEAVES the loop, where it used to raise the failure limit and go back to waiting. Withdrawal is after the reap and not before, because a command still running is a command that can still write the file being taken back — the order Ninja's `Clear()`-then-`Cleanup()` uses for the same reason.\n* `src/subprocess.rs`, `ProcessSupervisor::stop` and `discard`, plus `INTERRUPT_GRACE`. Ninja's wait has no bound, so a command that declines the signal holds it forever. `stop` gives the children 100 ms to take the signal `interrupt` already sent — polled on the supervisor's own reap backoff, so a child that dies at once costs microseconds — and kills what is still standing. The number is a courtesy rather than a deadline: whether a command dies of the signal within it or of the kill after it, the edge is not reported and its outputs are withdrawn either way, so no assertion anywhere depends on its value.\n* `src/build/command.rs`, `continue_recipe`. No further command line of a recipe is launched once the interrupt has been observed. Its window is the instant between `wait()` handing back a queued completion and the loop reaching its top, so it is defence in depth rather than the fix — and it is honest to say that it is not separately regression-tested, because it cannot be hit from outside on purpose.\n\nTHE DEPARTURE, and it is written into the rule rather than left in the code. `[spec:ronin:req:compat.process-integration]` said only \"signal and interrupt handling … match Ninja\"; at +2 it now says what an interrupt DOES (Ninja's, throughout: nothing further launched, no running edge reported or recorded, every changed output withdrawn) and names the one deliberate difference, which is about how long that takes and not about what it is. Ronin exits in 107 ms where Ninja takes 2797 ms, and the recipe that declined the signal does not get to finish. Every reference to the rule floats, so nothing went stale; `nplan spec` reports no stale references and no uncovered rules.\n\nGNU'S HALF, which is effects and was already right. Oracle at `reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make`, one Makefile with `.PRECIOUS: kept` beside an ordinary target, `-j2`:\n\n| tool | exit | ordinary target | `.PRECIOUS` target |\n| GNU make 4.4.1 | 130, by dying of the signal | removed, `make: *** Deleting file 'gone'` | kept |\n| Ronin make mode | 2 | removed | kept |\n\nBoth files match GNU exactly, before and after, because `withdraw_outputs` already narrows through `graph.withdrawal` and a Makefile's `.PRECIOUS` already empties that set. Ronin says nothing about the deletion, which `[spec:ronin:req:make.narration+1]` allows. The exit status matches nothing — GNU says 130, Ninja says 130, `[spec:ronin:req:product.build-outcome]` says 130, and Make mode flattens every build failure to `ABANDONED`. That is a separate claim from this node's and is filed as `make-mode-leaves-an-interrupt-with-2-where-both-references-say-130`, with the measurement; the Make-mode regression here deliberately asserts only `!status.success()` so that it does not enshrine the 2.\n\nREGRESSIONS, deterministic and three of them, in `tests/cli.rs`. The `trap` is what makes the window a certainty instead of a 1-in-16 race: a recipe that cannot be killed by the signal it was sent is stopped by the build or by nothing, so no timing is being read.\n\n* `an_interrupt_stops_a_recipe_that_declines_it_and_records_nothing` — exit 130, `output` gone, the rest of the command line never ran, and no `[1/1]` line, which is the assertion that says the edge was not recorded.\n* `make_mode_interrupt_deletes_the_target_and_spares_a_precious_one` — the two files, oracle-recorded above. Both recipes decline the signal, which is what makes it a statement about the build rather than about the shell.\n* `make_mode_launches_no_further_recipe_line_after_an_interrupt` — pins the contract and catches nothing today, said so in its own doc comment: before the fix the line WAS launched and then signalled where it stood, which killed it before it could leave a mark. What changed is that there is now nothing to kill.\n\nTEETH, by parking the three source files and keeping the tests:\n\n    test an_interrupt_stops_a_recipe_that_declines_it_and_records_nothing ... FAILED\n      assertion failed: !directory.join(\"survived\").exists()\n    test make_mode_interrupt_deletes_the_target_and_spares_a_precious_one ... FAILED\n      assertion failed: !directory.join(\"gone\").exists()\n    test result: FAILED. 2 passed; 2 failed; finished in 30.03s\n\nThirty seconds of that is the recipe outliving the signal, which is the defect measuring itself. The suite runs the four interrupt cases in 0.14 s with the fix in.\n\nAND A FLAPPER OF MY OWN, found by soaking rather than by reading, which is the whole point of soaking. The first shape of the `.PRECIOUS` case waited only for the OTHER recipe's marker, so under eight concurrent `cli` binaries the precious target had not always been written when the signal landed and the case asserted that a file nothing ever made had been spared: 5 failures in 40. It now waits for that target itself — the recipe writes it and then sleeps, so the target IS the marker. Re-soaked at 48 runs: all four interrupt cases 48/48, and zero failures of any of the 84 cases across the 48 binary runs.\n\nGATES, serial, through `scripts/sandboxed`, all against the final tree: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` both passes clean; workspace 981 passed / 0 failed (978 plus the three new); `--no-default-features` 760 / 0 (759 plus the one that is not Make's); make_port 4 of 4 over 968, all eight `divergence` sidecars live and none rewritten; conformance 336 normalised identical / 51 differing and 286 raw, classified 17 defect + 2 recorded + 31 extension + 1 artefact, nothing unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles / 280 graphs / 75 make-rejected / 0 disagreements; upstream inventory regenerated with `--update` and diffed — 1285 cases at 132 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1129 narration, md5 `2f09deebd5a3495ea06bfcaedc092bf5` before and after, not one row moved, compiler still zero; `check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 15 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 27 s at -j8; ninja conformance 425 of 425 upstream plus 10 CLI, 86 build-outcome, 3 invocation, persistence round trip and log content; `scripts/check-release.sh` green end to end."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T23:24:33.895136308Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T23:28:31.864272064Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @errata
                text "The three regression cases landed under shorter names than the completion note above gives them. The commit gate's `function-name` check caps a function name at six words and forty-five characters, and all three were written past it; the refusal was fixed rather than bypassed. Read the note's names as:\n\n* `an_interrupt_stops_a_recipe_that_declines_it_and_records_nothing` → `an_interrupt_stops_an_outliving_recipe`\n* `make_mode_interrupt_deletes_the_target_and_spares_a_precious_one` → `make_interrupt_spares_precious_targets`\n* `make_mode_launches_no_further_recipe_line_after_an_interrupt` → `make_interrupt_launches_no_further_line`\n\nEach case's doc comment carries what the name no longer spells out, and the teeth transcript quoted in the note names the two that failed by their original spelling."
                after_done true
