            {
                at 2026-08-21T23:48:20.59190101Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @errata
                text "The completion note says the rule bump left nothing stale. That was true of the SOURCE side only once the references were pinned, and it was not the whole picture; the correction is recorded here rather than by rewriting it.\n\nBumping `compat.process-integration` to +2 had two consequences the note does not mention.\n\nFirst, the twenty-one references that predate the bump were recorded against v0 and went stale the moment `nplan sync` wrote version 2 into `plan/.state.styx` — after this node's own commit, so the release gate that ran green before it did not see them. Each was re-read against the new body and pinned to +2 in a follow-up commit; coverage is back to 225 of 225 with no stale references, and the release gate is green on the final tree. What the note reports as \"no stale references\" describes the tree after that commit, not after this one.\n\nSecond, `ronin-process-integration` implements the bumped rule and has been Done since long before it, so it now reads Stale — recorded evidence at version 0 against a rule at version 2. `nplan sync` will not clear it and `nplan start` refuses a Stale node, which is the no-reopen invariant working as intended. Filed as `carry-process-integrations-evidence-to-the-bumped-rule`, with the evidence that already exists, rather than stamped by the dispatch that caused it.\n\nNeither is a product defect and no gate is red over either."
