    {
        id the-serial-default-case-races-a-loaded-host
        title "Prove the serial default without racing a loaded host"
        kind @test
        deps (the-interrupt-tests-wait-is-too-short-under-the-suites-own-load)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T08:18:22.315007759Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found under the load raised to reproduce `the-interrupt-tests-wait-is-too-short-under-the-suites-own-load`, and it is the same family: a case that measures wall-clock ordering with a tenth of a second between the answers.\n\n`tests/cli.rs::make_mode_runs_one_recipe_at_a_time_unless_asked_otherwise` writes\n\n    all: a b c\n    a: ; @sleep 0.3; echo a\n    b: ; @sleep 0.2; echo b\n    c: ; @sleep 0.1; echo c\n\nand asserts `abc` with no switch and `cba` under `-j4`. The second assertion is the one that moves: under `-j4` the three recipes start together and finish in the order their sleeps end, 100 ms apart. A host that cannot schedule three shells within 100 ms of each other reorders them, and the case reads `left: \"bca\" / right: \"cba\"`.\n\nMEASURED, eight full `cli` binaries at once beside the other suites' binaries on a 32-core host:\n\n| load average | binary runs | failures of this case |\n| ~5 (the suites alone) | 24 | 1 |\n| ~29 (96 CPU hogs beside them) | 8 | 0 |\n| ~260 (512 CPU hogs beside them) | 16 | 5 |\n\nNothing about the product is in question in any of them: every failure is an ordering swap of two recipes whose sleeps are 100 ms apart, and the serial assertion above it never moved.\n\nWHAT IT WOULD TAKE. The case wants to say two things — that the default runs one recipe at a time, and that `-j4` really does run them at once — and it currently says both with the same stopwatch. The first is provable without timing at all: a recipe that writes into a shared file and fails if the file is already claimed says \"one at a time\" outright. The second wants overlap rather than order: two recipes that each wait for the other's marker (the `left.ready`/`right.ready` shape already used by `make_grouped`'s output-sync case in this same file) DEADLOCK under a serial build and complete under a parallel one, which is a bound rather than a race. Either way the 100 ms sleeps go.\n\nWorth doing together with any other timing-shaped case in the suite: the sweep of `tests/cli.rs` done for the interrupt node found this one and no others polling on a stopwatch, but the `-j` cases that count markers were not re-read with this question in mind."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T09:41:21.028576106Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T10:12:00.824275497Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered. The case says its two things without a stopwatch anywhere, and both halves were measured with teeth before the load was measured at all.\n\nWHAT IT SAID BEFORE, and why it moved. Three recipes sleeping 0.3, 0.2 and 0.1 seconds, asserted `abc` with no switch and `cba` under `-j4`. The second assertion is a stopwatch reading: under `-j4` the three start together and finish in the order their sleeps end, 100 ms apart, so a host that cannot start three shells within 100 ms of each other reorders them and the case reads `left: \"bca\" / right: \"cba\"`.\n\nWHAT IT SAYS NOW, in two halves that share no Makefile and no timing.\n\n* The serial half. Each recipe runs `alone.sh`: `mkdir claim` — one system call, and it refuses a name already taken — then two appends to `order`, then `rmdir claim`. A build that runs one recipe at a time takes the claim three times and writes `a in / a out / b in / b out / c in / c out`, in that order, on any scheduler and any host. Both are properties of running one at a time rather than of running fast.\n* The parallel half. Each recipe runs `together.sh`: touch its own marker, then wait for all three markers. No recipe can finish until the other two have started, so a build that finishes AT ALL ran three recipes at once — the assertion is the exit status, not an ordering. The wait ends on a deadline (6,000 turns of 10 ms, the same minute `wait_for_the_recipes_marker` gives the interrupt case) so a build that runs them one at a time fails rather than hangs. That is a bound on the failure, not a stopwatch on the pass.\n\nTEETH, by parking the property rather than by argument, all through `scripts/sandboxed`:\n\n| probe | result |\n| the serial half's Makefile under `-j4`, 40 rounds | claim refused 40/40, `order` wrong 40/40 |\n| the parallel half's Makefile, no `-j` | exit 2 |\n| the parallel half's Makefile under `-j2` — two slots, three recipes | exit 2 |\n| the parallel half's Makefile under `-j4` | exit 0 |\n\nBoth halves bite absolutely, which the old pair did not: the old serial assertion could not tell a parallel build from a serial one at all when the three recipes happened not to overlap, and it was the parallel assertion that moved under load.\n\nLOAD, which is what the node was filed for. The first attempt at an A/B was WRONG and is recorded because it misled for half an hour: 512 spinning shells beside the case, 16 rounds, old 0/16 and new 0/16. CPU load alone does not reproduce this. The filing's condition was not load, it was SPAWN CONTENTION — eight full `cli` binaries at once, each spawning processes for thirty-odd cases beside this one — which is the same thing the interrupt node found when its shell-script equivalent fired 0 times in 160.\n\nMeasured again in the filing's own condition, on a 32-core host, alternating old and new rounds so both see the same machine, with the parked binary built from `tests/cli.rs` at HEAD:\n\n| rounds of 8 concurrent `cli` binaries | old | new |\n| 3 rounds, 512 CPU hogs beside them, load 210 rising to 485 | 2 of 24 failed | 0 of 24 failed |\n| plus the earlier 16 single runs at 512 hogs | — | 0 of 16 failed |\n\nZero in forty for the new shape against two in twenty-four for the old, in one session on one host, and consistent with the filing's own 5 in 16.\n\nCOST: the case runs in 0.06 s where it ran in 0.96 s — the second of those is the 1.8 seconds of `sleep` it no longer pays.\n\nTHE SWEEP the filing asked for: `tests/cli.rs` was re-read for the other `-j` cases that count markers. None of them times anything. `make_mode_serialises_only_the_makefile_that_declared_notparallel` stamps start and end times into a log and asserts OVERLAP with a tolerance, which is a bound rather than a race; the recursive-budget and jobserver cases count markers after the build has finished. This was the one case reading an ordering off a stopwatch, and it is gone.\n\nGATES: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` (both passes) clean; `cargo test --workspace` 978 passed / 0 failed."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T10:12:05.025555798Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id make-mode-leaves-an-interrupt-with-2-where-both-references-say-130
        title "Leave a Make-mode interrupt with 130 as both references do"
        kind @fix
        deps (an-interrupt-between-a-recipes-commands-is-lost)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T10:32:01.539379409Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found while probing `an-interrupt-between-a-recipes-commands-is-lost`, and it is a separate claim from that node's: what the files look like after an interrupt is one question and what the tool exits with is another. The files are already right; the status is not.\n\nWHAT HAPPENS. Ninja mode leaves an interrupt with 130 — `main.rs` asks `signal_handlers.interrupted()` on the error path and exits `INTERRUPTED_EXIT_CODE`. Make mode never reaches that: `crate::make::load_with_subninjas` maps every build failure to `RunResult { exit_code: ABANDONED }`, `main` sees `Ok(result)` and exits with the 2 it carries. The interrupt is not one of the ways of not building that `ABANDONED` was written for — `src/make/report.rs` lists them, and they are all refusals to start rather than a build cut short.\n\nMEASURED, on a 32-core host through `scripts/sandboxed`, one Makefile with a `.PRECIOUS` target beside an ordinary one, `-j2`, SIGINT sent to the tool once the first recipe had started. A background job inherits SIGINT ignored, and GNU Make KEEPS an inherited ignore where Ninja and Ronin install their handler regardless, so the tool is exec'd through `perl -e '$SIG{INT}=\"DEFAULT\"; exec @ARGV'` — without that GNU simply finishes the build and the probe says nothing:\n\n| tool | exit | signal to exit | ordinary target | `.PRECIOUS` target |\n| GNU make 4.4.1 | 130, by dying of the signal | 2694 ms | removed, `make: *** Deleting file 'gone'` | kept |\n| Ronin make mode | 2, a normal exit | 2 ms | removed | kept |\n| upstream ninja | 130, a normal exit | 2997 ms | removed | — |\n| Ronin ninja mode | 130, a normal exit | 2 ms | removed | — |\n\nSo the effects already match GNU on both files and the status does not match anything: GNU says 130, Ninja says 130, and `[spec:ronin:req:product.build-outcome]` says \"An interrupt leaves with Ninja's 130 rather than re-raising the signal\". Make mode says 2.\n\nWHAT IT WOULD TAKE. The Make front end has to tell an interrupted build apart from an abandoned one where it maps the failure. `BuildError::exit_code` already answers 130 for `Interrupted`, and `BuildStop::from_failure` already short-circuits on it, so the information is present at the boundary and is being flattened by the `map_err` that mints `ABANDONED`. Ronin exits 130 normally rather than re-raising, per the rule above, so the fix is a status and not a signal.\n\nWATCH FOR: `-q`'s exit 1 must not become 130, and `ABANDONED` must stay 2 for every one of the refusals `src/make/report.rs` enumerates — the conformance corpus records success and failure per case and would not see a 2 becoming a 130, so the regression has to be an explicit case.\n\nREPRODUCER: /tmp scratch is gone with the dispatch. Three lines — a Makefile whose recipe is `@trap '' INT; touch $@; touch started; sleep 30`, run it through the perl exec above, wait for `started`, `kill -INT` the tool, read `$?`.\n\nRegression coverage nearby: `tests/cli.rs::make_mode_interrupt_deletes_the_target_and_spares_a_precious_one` asserts the two files and deliberately asserts only `!status.success()`, so that it does not enshrine the 2 this node is here to change."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-21T23:28:39.684034379Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "The regression case named at the end of the note above landed as `tests/cli.rs::make_interrupt_spares_precious_targets` — the commit gate caps a function name at six words and the original spelling was past it. Its contract is unchanged: it asserts the two files and deliberately asserts only `!status.success()`, so that fixing this node's status does not have to move it."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T00:25:43.615936348Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T00:48:29.022588907Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered. The filing was right about the defect and right about where it lived; what the probe added is that the matrix is wider than one case, that the two references PART on one row of it, and that the number cannot be used to recognise an interrupt.\n\nTHE FIX, in one place. `src/frontend/execute.rs` gains `Outcome::interrupted()`, which reads `BuildStop::Interrupted` off the stop the build already recorded, and `src/make/report.rs` grows `stopped_status`, which `complained_of` now calls instead of flattening inline. A build that finished leaves zero; a build the user cut short leaves `INTERRUPTED_EXIT_CODE`; every other way of not finishing leaves `ABANDONED`. Nothing else moved: `abandoned`, `refused_makefile`, `answered`, `no_makefile` and `duplicate_standard_input` keep their 2, and the `-q` path is not on this route at all.\n\nTHE REASON IS READ, NEVER THE NUMBER. `Outcome::exit_code()` already answered 130 for an interrupt — and answers 130 just as readily for a recipe that exited 130 of its own accord, which is an ordinary failed recipe and must stay at 2. That is why `interrupted()` asks `BuildStop` rather than comparing statuses, and it is the difference the guard cases below were written to hold.\n\nTHE MATRIX, measured on a 32-core host, all through `scripts/sandboxed`, every tool exec'd through `perl -e '$SIG{INT}=\"DEFAULT\"; exec @ARGV'` — a background job inherits SIGINT ignored and GNU Make KEEPS an inherited ignore where Ninja and Ronin install their handler regardless, so without that GNU finishes the build and the probe says nothing. The signal goes to the tool alone, never to the process group, which is the deterministic form of what a terminal does to the foreground group.\n\n| case | GNU 4.4.1 | upstream ninja | Ronin ninja | Ronin make before | after |\n| SIGINT during a recipe that declines it | 130 | 130 | 130 | 2 | 130 |\n| SIGINT during the read phase, `$(shell)` running | 130 | — | — | 2 | 130 |\n| SIGTERM during a recipe | 143 | 130 | 130 | 2 | 130 |\n| SIGINT under `-k -j2`, a second recipe running | 130 | — | — | 2 | 130 |\n| recursive make, SIGINT to the outer tool | 130 | — | — | 2 | 130 |\n| `-q`, no signal | 1 | — | — | 1 | 1 |\n| no makefile | 2 | — | — | 2 | 2 |\n| a recipe that exits 130 itself, with and without `-k` | 2 | — | — | 2 | 2 |\n| a recipe that dies of SIGINT, tool never signalled | 2 | — | — | 2 | 2 |\n| a recipe that dies of SIGTERM, tool never signalled | 2 | — | — | 2 | 2 |\n| a target with no rule | 2 | — | — | 2 | 2 |\n\nWHERE THE REFERENCES PART, and why Ninja wins. SIGTERM is the one row where they disagree: GNU leaves 143, because it kills its children, deletes the target and then dies of the signal it caught, while upstream Ninja leaves 130 for every signal it treats as an interrupt. `[spec:ronin:req:product.build-outcome]` settles it in so many words — \"an interrupt leaves with Ninja's 130 rather than re-raising the signal, so the status does not depend on how far the build had got; C samurai re-raised here, and Ninja is the contract\" — and `[spec:ronin:req:make.question-status]` names that same rule as governing every Make invocation's status but `-q`'s. Reproducing GNU's 143 would mean re-raising in Make mode and not in Ninja mode, which is runtime emulation in the one front end that exists to avoid it. So Make mode leaves 130 for SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGHUP and SIGQUIT alike, as Ronin's own manifest front end already did, and `make_termination_leaves_ninjas_status` states that as a decision rather than leaving it to be discovered.\n\nWHAT THE PARENT REPORTS FOR A COMPOSED CHILD, which the dispatch asked about. GNU, signalled at the sub-make alone rather than at the tree: `make[1]: *** Deleting file 'inner'`, `make: *** [Makefile:2: all] Interrupt`, and the parent exits TWO — 130 is what GNU leaves when GNU ITSELF caught the signal, and a child that was interrupted is reported like any other failed child. Ronin has no separate process to signal here — `$(MAKE) -C sub` is compiled into the graph as a subninja, so the recursive case collapses onto the ordinary one — but the boundary it stands for is real and is now pinned from the other side: a recipe that dies of SIGINT or SIGTERM while the tool was never signalled leaves 2 in both tools, which is what `options.recipe_signal_fails` and `options.command_status_interrupts = false` were set for.\n\nFIXTURES, five of them, three tightened or new for the status and two guarding the boundary, all in `tests/cli.rs`:\n* `make_interrupt_spares_precious_targets` and `make_interrupt_launches_no_further_line` were written deliberately asserting only `!status.success()` so that this node would not have to move them. They now assert `Some(130)` and `signal() == None` outright, which is the hole they were left with being closed rather than a contract changing.\n* `make_termination_leaves_ninjas_status` is new: SIGTERM, 130, and the target withdrawn — the divergence from GNU's 143, stated.\n* `a_recipes_own_130_stays_two` and `a_signalled_recipe_abandons_with_two` are new, deterministic, and send no signal at all.\n\nTEETH, by parking rather than by argument, each parked build run against all five:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the fix itself (`stopped_status` returns ABANDONED again) | 3 of 5 — the three interrupt-status cases, each `left: Some(2) / right: Some(130)` |\n| the WRONG fix: `outcome.exit_code() == 130` instead of the reason | 2 of 5 — `a_recipes_own_130_stays_two` (`left: Some(130) / right: Some(2)`) and `a_signalled_recipe_abandons_with_two` |\nEvery case bites against something, and the two halves bite against opposite mistakes: the first parking says the fix is needed, the second says it has to read the reason.\n\nA PROBE TRAP, recorded because it cost a full re-measurement. The first harness ran each tool inside a command substitution. A recipe that outlives the tool inherits the pipe's write end, so `$(...)` blocked on the orphan for as long as it slept, and the file checks afterwards reported a 30-second wait as `survived=yes` for GNU and upstream Ninja. Every number above comes from the rewritten harness, which sends the tool's streams to a file. A second trap is on the new node beside this one: `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` leaves a Make-less binary at the shared debug path, and a probe that runs it afterwards gets exit 1 from every case.\n\nFILED, not fixed, both with deps on this node and both measured to the millisecond:\n* `question-mode-answers-up-to-date-after-an-interrupt` — `-q` interrupted while a `+` line runs answers ZERO where GNU answers 130, three rounds each. Left alone deliberately: `[spec:ronin:req:make.question-status]` owns `-q`, points at 2, and does not mention interrupts, while `product.build-outcome` points at 130 — a spec decision, not a code change, and not this node's to take.\n* `a-read-phase-interrupt-waits-for-the-shell-function` — the status is now right during the read phase and the wait in front of it is not: 10,007 ms against GNU's 8 ms, the whole delta being the `$(shell)` child. The fix is in the kati submodule, which is our code.\n\nGATES: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` both passes clean; workspace 984 passed / 0 failed (981 + 3 new cases); conformance 336/51 normalised, 286 raw, zero unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles, 280 graphs compared, 75 refused, 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 @ 132 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1129 narration, `tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv` byte-identical — no row moved; make_port 4/4 with all eight recorded divergences still live; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 23 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 45 s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus 10 differentials, 86 build-outcome cases, 3 invocation-boundary cases, persistence round trip and log content; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 760 passed / 0 failed."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T00:48:37.662169967Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T00:56:35.839087406Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "ERRATUM to the note above, on two counts, both from the commit gate refusing and being fixed rather than bypassed.\n\nTHE SUITE SPLIT. `tests/cli.rs` reached 3001 LOC against the file-size gate's 3000, so the interrupt group moved to `tests/interrupts.rs` — the readiness gate `wait_for_the_recipes_marker`, the five cases this node touched, and the two that were already there. It is one subject rather than a handful of leftovers: every case starts a build, waits for it to reach a recipe, sends one signal, and reads back a status and the files on disk, and they are the cases that must run under `scripts/sandboxed`. No assertion moved with them, so the teeth measured above are measurements of the same five cases; the per-item `#[cfg(unix)]` attributes became a file-level `#![cfg(unix)]`, and `test_directory`, `make_case`, `invoked_as` and `make_command` are local copies, which is what every other integration test in this tree already does with them.\n\nTHE COUNT. The workspace total is 985 passed / 0 failed, not the 984 the note above records. The extra one is `scratch_directory::a_scratch_goes_with_its_test`: `tests/support/scratch.rs` carries its own unit test and is included by `#[path]`, so a new integration binary that includes it runs one more copy of it. Baseline 981, plus three new cases, plus that copy."
                after_done true
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id carry-process-integrations-evidence-to-the-bumped-rule
        title "Carry process integration's evidence forward to the bumped interrupt rule"
        kind @test
        deps (an-interrupt-between-a-recipes-commands-is-lost)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-21T23:48:03.349957661Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Opened by `an-interrupt-between-a-recipes-commands-is-lost`, which bumped `[spec:ronin:req:compat.process-integration]` from unversioned to +2 to state what an interrupt does and to name the one bounded departure from Ninja's unbounded wait. `ronin-process-integration` implements that rule and had been Done since long before the bump, so it is now Stale: its recorded evidence in `plan/.state.styx` reads `rule_versions_seen {id compat.process-integration, version 0}` against a rule at version 2.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY SETTLED, so that this node is not read as larger than it is. The SOURCE side is finished and committed: every one of the twenty-one references was re-read against the new body and pinned to +2, and `nplan spec` reports 225 of 225 rules covered with no stale references. What is left is only the NODE's evidence.\n\nWHY SYNC WILL NOT DO IT. Three separate `nplan sync` runs applied nothing, and `nplan start ronin-process-integration` is refused — \"node is Stale; start only transitions Todo → Doing\". That is the no-reopen invariant working as designed: a Done node whose rule moved under it returns to Done by being worked again, which forms the epoch the append-only log is built around. The precedent is in the tree — `make-ninja-narration` and `make-recipe-command-narration` both record `{id make.narration, version 1}` and both read Done, so evidence at a bumped version does get carried forward; it is carried by a fresh cycle rather than by sync alone.\n\nWHAT RE-VERIFICATION MEANS. The rule's claims are command spawning, working directories, inherited environment, signal and interrupt handling, jobserver participation, child exit interpretation and terminal ownership matching Ninja — plus, at +2, what an interrupt does (nothing further launched, no running edge reported or recorded, every changed output withdrawn) and the bounded departure (Ronin stops what declined the signal instead of waiting for it without bound, as Ninja does).\n\nEVIDENCE THAT ALREADY EXISTS, measured on 2026-08-21/22 against the tree this node would re-record: ninja conformance 425 of 425 upstream tests plus 10 CLI differentials, 86 build-outcome cases, 3 invocation-boundary cases, the persistence round trip and log content; workspace 981 passed / 0 failed, which is every `subprocess` and `signal` unit test; and the three new interrupt regressions in `tests/cli.rs` — `an_interrupt_stops_an_outliving_recipe`, `make_interrupt_spares_precious_targets`, `make_interrupt_launches_no_further_line`. The +2 half was probed against upstream Ninja and GNU Make 4.4.1 directly; both probes are recorded on the interrupt node.\n\nSO THE WORK IS: decide whether that evidence is accepted as the re-verification or whether the integration wants a fresh pass, then move the node through a cycle so its `rule_versions_seen` records version 2. It is bookkeeping over a P0 integration node, which is exactly the kind of thing that should be deliberate rather than silently stamped by the agent that caused the bump — which is why it was filed rather than forced.\n\nNOT URGENT AND NOT A PRODUCT DEFECT: nothing about the build is wrong, no gate is red, and `nplan lint` is clean. The visible cost is one node reading Stale on the frontier."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T01:00:42.66874282Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T01:06:00.389311571Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered. The cycle the filing declined to take for itself was ordered by the dispatcher, and it was run as a verification rather than as a restamp — which was the right call, because the tree it verified is not the tree the bump left behind.\n\nWHAT THE MACHINERY ACTUALLY WANTS, since the filing left this as the open question. `nplan sync` applies nothing for a Stale node because evidence is not derived from the working tree — it is rebuilt from the commits that NAME a node. d645a55 states the mechanism where the precedent used it: \"Evidence for a node is rebuilt from the rule trailers on a commit that names it, so the versions a revalidation observed can only be written once that commit exists.\" The precedent pair is 9ee428b (the revalidation, naming `make-graph-equivalence` and `make-ninja-narration`) followed by d645a55 (the reconciliation stamp), and `make-ninja-narration` never left Done across either. So the no-reopen invariant is not stronger than the precedent suggested and there was nothing to force: a Done node whose rule moved under it is brought back by re-verifying it and committing in its name, and `nplan start` refusing a Stale node is not in the way of that at all. This node is the cycle; `ronin-process-integration` is what the cycle is about, and both are named on the landing commit.\n\nTHE VERIFICATION ITSELF is recorded on `ronin-process-integration`, where it belongs — all three of the rule's +2 clauses and its one bounded departure probed fresh against upstream Ninja and GNU Make 4.4.1 at this tree, plus ninja conformance 425/425 with its 10 differentials, 86 build-outcome and 3 invocation-boundary cases, and `cargo test --workspace` at 985 passed / 0 failed. Two findings worth repeating here:\n\n* GNU Make does not forward an interrupt it alone receives to the recipe's process group — measured with a recipe whose trap records the signal rather than dying of it, `caught` absent for GNU and present for upstream Ninja, Ronin's ninja mode and Ronin's make mode alike. Ronin matching Ninja and departing from GNU is the rule as written, and it is worth having measured rather than assumed.\n* The departure the rule names is visible as a pure latency: upstream Ninja takes 20,071 ms and 29,998 ms to leave a declining recipe — the recipes' own sleeps, to the millisecond — where Ronin takes 121 to 141 ms. What the two do is identical; only how long it takes differs.\n\nWHY THE ORDER MATTERED. This cycle ran last, over a tree that includes `make-mode-leaves-an-interrupt-with-2-where-both-references-say-130`. On the day the rule was bumped, Make mode left an interrupt with 2 — inside this rule's own \"signal and interrupt handling ... match Ninja\" — so evidence recorded then would have been evidence for a tree in which one clause was false. Five of the rule's twenty verify sites are cases that landing added or tightened, three of them in the `tests/interrupts.rs` suite it created. Re-verifying first and fixing second would have had to be done twice.\n\nWHAT WAS ALREADY SETTLED and needed no work: the source side. All 26 annotations claiming the rule read `+2` — six implementation sites and twenty verify sites — and `nplan spec` reports no stale references. The filing's account of that was accurate.\n\nNO PRODUCT CHANGE. This node's delta is the two revalidation notes and the commit that names both nodes; the implementation of `compat.process-integration` was not touched, because the rule at +2 describes what it already does."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T01:06:08.17817441Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T01:17:23.331971626Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @errata
                text "CORRECTION to the mechanism described in the delivery note above, measured rather than inferred, because the first attempt half-worked and the difference is the useful part.\n\nThe note says evidence is rebuilt \"from the commits that NAME a node\". Naming is necessary and is NOT sufficient. Observed here, in order:\n\n1. `07c6b05` named both nodes and touched nothing but `plan/`. It cleared `ronin-process-integration`'s stale `rule_versions_seen {compat.process-integration, version 0}` and the node went Stale → Done. TWO subsequent `nplan sync` runs then wrote nothing but a timestamp, and the node was left with NO `rule_versions_seen` at all.\n2. `83fa098` named both nodes and touched `tests/interrupts.rs`, which carries `[spec:ronin:req:compat.process-integration+2/test]`. `nplan_commit` derived `nplan-rule: [spec:ronin:req:compat.process-integration/test]` from that delta, and the next `nplan sync` wrote `{id compat.process-integration, version 2}`.\n\nSo the rebuild needs a commit that names the node AND carries a rule trailer for the rule — and the trailer is derived from the staged delta's own annotations, which means a plan-only commit can never produce one. State 1 is exactly what d645a55 warned about in the precedent: \"leaving them with nothing to drift from\". A node in that state reads Done and is not Stale, but a future bump of its rule would not mark it Stale either, because there is no recorded version to drift from — a silent hole rather than a visible one.\n\nWHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE NEXT CYCLE OF THIS KIND. Carrying a Done node's evidence across a rule bump cannot be done by bookkeeping alone: the cycle has to produce a real change at a site that annotates the rule, or the version never lands. That is a good property rather than an awkward one — it makes \"re-verified\" mean a verification site was touched — and it is why this cycle went looking for a clause with no always-on verification instead of restamping. The precedent did the same thing for the same reason (`9ee428b` added a verify site for `make.manifest-equivalence`'s new clause); what was not written down there is that it HAD to.\n\nBoth syncs and both commits are on `main` and pushed, so the sequence above is readable in the history rather than only here."
                after_done true
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id question-mode-answers-up-to-date-after-an-interrupt
        title "Refuse the question when an interrupt cut it short"
        kind @fix
        deps (make-mode-leaves-an-interrupt-with-2-where-both-references-say-130)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-22T00:47:18.66760805Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found while probing the full interrupt matrix for `make-mode-leaves-an-interrupt-with-2-where-both-references-say-130`, and deliberately left out of that node's fix: the build's status and the question's status are governed by two different rules, and only the first of them was the subject there.\n\nWHAT HAPPENS. `-q` runs no recipe except a line the makefile marked `+`, which runs anyway. Interrupt the tool while such a line is running and Ronin answers ZERO — \"the goals are already up to date\" — where GNU Make 4.4.1 answers 130. Ronin does not merely mis-number the answer; it gives the affirmative one, so a script branching on `-q` is told there is nothing to do by a run that was cut short before it could find out.\n\nMEASURED, all through `scripts/sandboxed`, the tool exec'd through `perl -e '$SIG{INT}=\"DEFAULT\"; exec @ARGV'` because a background job inherits SIGINT ignored and GNU Make keeps an inherited ignore:\n\n    out:\n    \t+@trap '' INT; touch started; sleep 5\n\n| run | GNU 4.4.1 | Ronin make mode |\n| `-q`, no signal (`+@touch ran`) | 0, line ran | 0, line ran |\n| `-q`, SIGINT once the line had started, 3 rounds | 130, 130, 130 | 0, 0, 0 |\n\nThe uninterrupted baseline agrees, which is what makes this a statement about the interrupt and not about `+` lines. Both tools wait the full five seconds for the trapping child, so the latency is not the difference either — only the answer is.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT SIMPLY 130. `[spec:ronin:req:make.question-status]` says `-q` \"answers in the exit status alone: zero when nothing would run, one when something would, two when the question could not be answered\", and names itself \"the one place a Make invocation's status is GNU Make's rather than the build outcome `compatibility.md` governs\". An interrupted question was not answered, so the rule as written points at TWO, while GNU points at 130 — which it reaches by dying of the signal, the very thing `[spec:ronin:req:product.build-outcome]` refuses (\"an interrupt leaves with Ninja's 130 rather than re-raising the signal\"). The two rules genuinely disagree about which one owns this case, and that is a spec decision rather than a code change: whichever number is chosen, the rule that governs `-q` has to say what an interrupt does to a question, because at present neither rule mentions it.\n\nWHERE IT IS. `src/build/command.rs::question_step` already mints `BuildError::Interrupted` when a questioned line is killed by the signal — but a line that DECLINES the signal exits successfully, `question_step` returns `Ok(true)`, and the recipe carries on as if nothing had happened. The interrupt flag is never consulted on the question path at all: `src/make/cli.rs` returns through `answered(reported, question, keep_going)` (`src/make/report.rs`), which reads only `Ok(up_to_date)` / `Err(failure)`. So the fix is a question asked in `answered`, or one asked before it, and NOT a change to the build-outcome mapping that the parent node moved — `stopped_status` is not on this path.\n\nREGRESSION COVERAGE NEARBY: `tests/cli.rs::question_mode_answers_in_the_status_and_builds_nothing` gates the ordinary 0/1, and the parent node's `a_recipes_own_130_stays_two` gates the other side of the same boundary. Neither touches an interrupted question, so this needs a case of its own — the `wait_for_the_recipes_marker` readiness gate plus a sandboxed `kill_process`, as the three interrupt cases beside them do."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T02:37:21.111568953Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T02:42:11.295948943Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "PROBED FIRST, and the probe is not ambiguous: GNU Make 4.4.1 answers 130 in every interrupted `-q` cell there is, and its TWO appears only where no signal was sent at all. So the rule is amended rather than the disagreement surfaced.\n\nTHE MATRIX, measured on a 32-core host, all through `scripts/sandboxed`, every tool exec'd through `perl -e '$SIG{INT}=\"DEFAULT\"; exec @ARGV'` because a background job inherits SIGINT ignored and GNU Make KEEPS an inherited ignore. The signal goes to the tool alone, never to the process group, which is the deterministic form of what a terminal does to the foreground group. Each interrupted cell measured three rounds; every round of every cell agreed with the one beside it, so the table gives one number.\n\n| case | GNU 4.4.1 | Ronin before |\n| `-q`, no signal, goal up to date | 0 | 0 |\n| `-q`, no signal, goal out of date | 1 | 1 |\n| `-q`, a `+` line that runs to the end, no signal | 0 | 0 |\n| `-q`, a `+` line that FAILS, no signal | 1 | 1 |\n| `-q`, SIGINT while a trapping `+` line runs | 130 | 0 |\n| `-q`, SIGINT while an UNTRAPPED `+` line runs | 130 | 0 |\n| `-q`, SIGINT while a `+` line runs, more work behind it | 130 | 1 |\n| `-q -k`, SIGINT while a trapping `+` line runs | 130 | 0 |\n| `-q -k`, SIGINT while a `+` line runs, more work behind it | 130 | 1 |\n| `-q`, SIGINT during the read phase, a `$(shell)` running | 130 | 130 |\n| `-q`, recursive, SIGINT to the outer tool | 130 | 0 |\n| `-q nosuchtarget`, no signal | 2 | 2 |\n| `-q` over a makefile that will not parse, no signal | 2 | 2 |\n| `-q` with no makefile at all, no signal | 2 | 2 |\n\nWHAT THE TABLE SETTLES. Not one cell answers 2 to an interrupt. Twelve interrupted measurements across four case shapes, plus the read-phase cell, and every one of them 130. The three cells that DO answer 2 are the three the rule's own words describe — a goal with no rule, a makefile that will not parse, no makefile to read — and none of them involves a signal. The 2 is therefore about the QUESTION being unanswerable from the makefile, not about the run not having reached an answer, and the filed note's reading that \"an interrupted question was not answered, so the rule as written points at TWO\" reads a clause that was written about something else.\n\nThe `-q`-with-work-behind-it cells are what make this decisive rather than merely consistent. There, GNU has already learned the answer is ONE — something would run — and still leaves 130. So the interrupt is not being folded into the question's vocabulary at all; it overrides an answer GNU already had. A rule that made an interrupt mean \"could not answer\" would have to explain why GNU discards an answer it holds.\n\nTHE READING, and why the two rules do not actually disagree. `[spec:ronin:req:product.build-outcome]` says an interrupt leaves 130 without re-raising, and `[spec:ronin:req:make.question-status]` names it as governing every Make invocation's status BUT `-q`'s. The exception was written for a reason that is about the ordinary endings: `-q` has three of them where a build has two, and its zero and one mean things a build's statuses do not. An interrupt is not one of `-q`'s endings — it is the run not reaching one — so it was never inside the exception's subject, and the rule is SILENT rather than in conflict. Amending it names the interrupt so the silence stops being read as a claim.\n\nRonin's own read-phase cell already answers 130, delivered by `a-read-phase-interrupt-waits-for-the-shell-function` an hour before this probe: `-q` reaches the compiler through the same path every other invocation does, and `report::cut_short` answers before `answered` is ever called. That cell is therefore evidence for the amendment rather than against it — the front end already treats an interrupted `-q` as an interrupt wherever the interrupt lands before the question is asked, and the remaining cells are the ones where it lands after.\n\nTHE DEPARTURE STAYS THE NINJA ONE. GNU reaches its 130 by dying of the signal; Ronin leaves 130 without re-raising, as `product.build-outcome` requires and as Make mode already does for a build. So the number matches and the mechanism does not, which is the same trade `make_termination_leaves_ninjas_status` records for SIGTERM one node up.\n\nDECIDED: amend `[spec:ronin:req:make.question-status]` to name what an interrupt does to a question — 130, in Ninja's spelling, whatever the question had already been told — take the version bump to `+1`, re-pin its four references, and then fix the code so `-q` stops answering 0 or 1 to a run that was cut short."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T03:05:14.293756964Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered, spec amendment and all. The decision note above holds the probe and the reading; this is what was built on them.\n\nTHE RULE. `[spec:ronin:req:make.question-status]` → `+1`, with a second paragraph that says an interrupt is not one of `-q`'s three answers and overrides whichever of them the run had reached. It gives the reason rather than only the number — two of the three answers are affirmative, and a run cut short before it could finish asking must not report that it finished asking — and it names the three places GNU's TWO is right, none of which involves a signal. It also settles the spelling: the 130 is `product.build-outcome`'s, not GNU's re-raised signal, because the exception the rule carries is about `-q`'s own endings and an interrupt is not one of them.\n\nThe cascade was four sites and is empty: `src/make/report.rs` (`answered`), `src/make/cli.rs` (`touch_does_not_override_question_mode`), `tests/cli.rs` (`question_mode_answers_in_the_status_and_builds_nothing`) re-pinned to `+1` after being read against the new body — each is about the three ordinary answers and none of them moved — and the prose reference inside `stopped_status`'s doc, which said `question-status` governs \"every Make invocation's status but `-q`'s\" and now says \"but `-q`'s THREE ANSWERS\", because that is what the exception was always about and what the amendment makes explicit.\n\nTHE FIX. `answered` takes a fourth argument and returns `cut_short` when it is set, so the whole of `-q`'s status stays in the one function the rule annotates. The three callers — the ordinary question, and the two in `remake.rs` where a question is answered during a makefile update — pass `crate::signal::interrupted().is_some()`.\n\nTHE REASON IS READ, AND HERE THE WALK CANNOT SUPPLY IT. `question_step` mints `BuildError::Interrupted` only for a line KILLED by the signal. The case that was filed is a line that DECLINES it: the `+` line reaches the end of its own script, `question_step` answers `Ok(true)`, and the walk's result is indistinguishable from an uneventful run. So the tool's own flag is the only thing that knows, and it is also the right thing to ask — a `+` line killed by a signal nobody sent this process is an ordinary refusal and keeps its 2, which is the boundary the third fixture holds and which the parent node's `a_signalled_recipe_abandons_with_two` holds on the build side.\n\nTHE MATRIX AFTER, every cell re-measured against the oracle, three rounds each for the interrupted ones:\n\n| case | GNU 4.4.1 | before | after |\n| `-q`, no signal, up to date | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| `-q`, no signal, out of date | 1 | 1 | 1 |\n| `-q`, a `+` line that runs to the end | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| `-q`, a `+` line that fails | 1 | 1 | 1 |\n| `-q`, SIGINT during a trapping `+` line | 130 | 0 | 130 |\n| `-q`, SIGINT during an untrapped `+` line | 130 | 0 | 130 |\n| `-q`, SIGINT during a `+` line, work behind it | 130 | 1 | 130 |\n| `-q -k`, SIGINT during a trapping `+` line | 130 | 0 | 130 |\n| `-q -k`, SIGINT during a `+` line, work behind it | 130 | 1 | 130 |\n| `-q`, SIGINT during the read phase | 130 | 130 | 130 |\n| `-q`, recursive, SIGINT to the outer tool | 130 | 0 | 130 |\n| `-q nosuchtarget` | 2 | 2 | 2 |\n| `-q` over a makefile that will not parse | 2 | 2 | 2 |\n| `-q` with no makefile | 2 | 2 | 2 |\n| `-q`, a `+` line that signals ITSELF | 2 | 2 | 2 |\n| `-q`, a `+` line that exits 130 itself | 2 | 2 | 2 |\n\nONE THING THE PROBE FOUND THAT IS NOT A DEFECT. Both tools WAIT for a trapping `+` line under `-q` — 19,996 ms for GNU against 20,001 ms for Ronin on a twenty-second child. That is the opposite of the read-phase case delivered an hour earlier, and the reason is structural: a `+` line is a JOB child, which `fatal_error_signal` reaps, where a `$(shell)` child is not and is abandoned. So the two nodes' answers differ because the oracle's do.\n\nFIXTURES, three, in `tests/interrupts.rs`, and none of them sends a signal or sleeps. The makefile signals the tool itself — `$$PPID` reaches the shell as `$PPID`, and the shell a recipe line runs in is the tool's own child — so the moment the interrupt arrives is a fact of the makefile rather than a race, and each case runs in milliseconds:\n* `a_stopped_question_answers_neither` — the filed case. 130 where it answered 0, and the status is the whole answer: both streams asserted empty.\n* `an_interrupt_outranks_the_answer` — a line that would run behind the `+` line, so the walk HAS an answer and the interrupt discards it. 130 where it answered 1.\n* `a_signalled_question_line_answers_two` — the boundary from the other side, with no signal sent to the tool at all.\n\nTEETH, by parking:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| `answered` ignores the interrupt | 2 of 3 — `left: Some(0) / right: Some(130)` and `left: Some(1) / right: Some(130)`, the two filed numbers exactly |\n| the WRONG fix: read the walk's failure instead of the tool's flag | 2 of 3, the same two, because a declining line reports no failure at all |\n| — | the boundary case bites neither, RECORDED AS SUCH: a `+` line that signals itself never mints `BuildError::Interrupted`, since `options.command_status_interrupts` is false in Make mode, so both parkings leave it at 2 by accident rather than by the fix. It is a statement of the boundary, as `a_recipes_own_130_stays_two` is one node up, and its value is that a future fix reading a command's status would fail it. |\n\nGATES: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; workspace 1001 passed / 0 failed (998 + 3); conformance 336/51 normalised, 286 raw, one artefact and zero unclassified — no cell moved, which is the point: the corpus has no interrupted `-q` case; equivalence 297 makefiles, 280 graphs compared, 75 refused, 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 @ 132 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1129 narration, `tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv` byte-identical; make_port 4/4 with all eight divergence sidecars still on disk; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 16 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 30 s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus 10 differentials, 86 build-outcome cases, 3 invocation-boundary cases, persistence round trip and log content; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 771 passed / 0 failed, unchanged because the new cases are Make mode's; `scripts/check-release.sh` green end to end; `nplan spec-stale` empty."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T03:06:59.634595734Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id a-read-phase-interrupt-waits-for-the-shell-function
        title "Act on an interrupt during the read phase"
        kind @fix
        deps (make-mode-leaves-an-interrupt-with-2-where-both-references-say-130)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-22T00:47:42.423673323Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found while probing the full interrupt matrix for `make-mode-leaves-an-interrupt-with-2-where-both-references-say-130`. That node fixed the STATUS a read-phase interrupt leaves with; this is the WAIT in front of it, which the status fix does not touch and which is the whole of the remaining difference.\n\nWHAT HAPPENS. A `$(shell ...)` at the top level of a makefile runs during the read, before any edge exists. Send the tool SIGINT while that child is running and GNU Make 4.4.1 is gone in single-digit milliseconds; Ronin runs the shell function to completion, finishes compiling, starts the build, and only then notices the flag the handler set.\n\nMEASURED on a 32-core host through `scripts/sandboxed`, tool exec'd through `perl -e '$SIG{INT}=\"DEFAULT\"; exec @ARGV'`, signal sent once the shell function had written its marker. The child does NOT trap the signal, so this is not the bounded-departure case that `[spec:ronin:req:compat.process-integration+2]` already records — nothing is declining anything:\n\n    X := $(shell touch started; sleep 10; echo done)\n    out:\n    \t@touch $@\n\n| tool | exit | signal to exit |\n| GNU make 4.4.1 | 130 | 8 ms |\n| Ronin make mode (with the parent node's fix) | 130 | 10,007 ms |\n\nThe same shape with a `trap '' INT` on the shell function reads 30,005 ms against GNU's 10 ms. Both numbers are the sleep, to the millisecond: nothing is being waited for except the child, and the wait is the whole delta. The status is right and the outputs are right — `out` is never made — so what is left is a tool that ignores Ctrl-C for as long as a shell function runs.\n\nWHY IT IS SEPARATE. `[spec:ronin:req:compat.process-integration+2]` describes what an interrupt does to a BUILD — nothing further launched, no running edge recorded, every changed output withdrawn — and Ronin honours all three here. The read phase is the compiler, not the build: `crate::make::load_with_subninjas` evaluates the makefile through kati, and kati's shell-function support waits on its child without ever asking `crate::signal::interrupted()`. So this is a claim about the compiler's responsiveness, and the rule that would govern it does not yet say anything about the read.\n\nWHERE IT IS. The wait is inside the kati submodule, which is our code — `kati/` is where the fix belongs, not a workaround in Ronin. The flag to consult already exists and is already process-global (`crate::signal::interrupted()`); what is missing is a caller. Note that the compile is also where `-C`, restarts and recursive subninja compilation live, so a fix wants to be a check the evaluator can make at more than one point rather than one bolted onto the shell function alone.\n\nNOT A REGRESSION AND NOT URGENT: the tool does stop, does report 130, and leaves nothing half-made. What a user sees is Ctrl-C taking as long as the makefile's slowest shell function.\n\nA PROBE TRAP WORTH KEEPING, because it cost a full re-measurement here. `target/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/debug/ronin` is shared by every cargo invocation, and `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` leaves a binary with NO Make front end at that path. A probe that runs it afterwards gets `ronin: this build has no Make front end` and exit 1 from every case, which reads as a plausible answer rather than as an error. Copy the binary somewhere private before probing, and check `--version` against a real Makefile first."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T01:30:34.752076145Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T02:28:05.598664677Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered. The filing was right about the defect, right about where it lived and right that the fix belonged in the submodule. What the probe added is that GNU's answer is ABANDONMENT rather than termination, and that the wait the filing measured is one of THREE, only one of which the filing saw.\n\nWHAT GNU DOES, measured rather than reasoned. Through `scripts/sandboxed`, tool exec'd through `perl -e '$SIG{INT}=\"DEFAULT\"; exec @ARGV'`, SIGINT to the tool alone, `X := $(shell touch started; sleep 10; echo done)`, three rounds each:\n\n| tool | exit | latency | the child, after the tool is gone |\n| GNU 4.4.1 | 130 | 1, 2, 3 ms | ALIVE: `/bin/sh -c ...` and its `sleep`, both reparented to pid 1 |\n| Ronin, before | 130 | 10013, 10015, 10019 ms | gone, because it was waited for |\n| Ronin, after | 130 | 2, 2, 3 ms | ALIVE, reparented — the same two processes GNU leaves |\n\nGNU does not kill the command-substitution child, does not signal its process group, and does not reap it. `fatal_error_signal` (commands.c) waits for the children Make is running as JOBS and knows nothing about the one a `$(shell)` left behind, so it re-raises and is gone while that child runs on. Neither tool leaves a zombie: the abandoned child is the init process's to bury, and a run with a 3-second trapping child left `Z` states at zero for both. So the effect to reproduce is to STOP WAITING, and dropping a `std::process::Child` — which neither waits nor kills — is exactly it.\n\nTHREE WAITS, NOT ONE. The filing measured the read of the command's output. Probing found two more on the same path, and the second is the one that would have left the whole defect standing behind a fixed read:\n* THE READ. `read_to_end` on the command's pipe. 10,007 ms.\n* THE WAIT AFTER IT. `Child::wait`, reached whenever the command closes its standard output before it finishes — a shell told `exec >/dev/null` is the deterministic form. Measured at 3,996 ms of a 4-second child under `SHELL := /bin/bash` against GNU's 2 ms, with the child's `survived` marker present rather than absent. A read that had only been half fixed would answer 3 ms for one makefile and four seconds for the other.\n* THE LAUNCH. Not a wait at all: an interrupt that arrives between two commands leaves the next one to be spawned and then abandoned, which is one more shell command than GNU runs after Ctrl-C. Refused outright now.\n\nTHE FIX, in kati, on `nplan/a-read-phase-interrupt-waits-for-the-shell-function` at 48f13fa024d778bc6612b20a22751d75d8091a2a. A new `interrupt` module: `Interruptible` is what the host answers about a signal it handled — a flag, and optionally a descriptor that becomes readable when one arrives, which is polled and never read from so the host may hand out the same read end it waits on itself. `Session::interrupts` carries it. SESSION-OWNED, not process-global, because `[spec:ronin:req:make.no-ambient-state]` and `tests/no_globals.rs` are a gate rather than a preference — a `static` here would have failed the submodule's own suite, and `[dec:ronin:session-owned-evaluation]` says why. `None`, which is what `rkati` and every kati test leave, runs the code that ran before the module existed.\n\nOn Ronin's side `ReadInterrupts` reads `crate::signal::interrupted()` and hands over a clone of the supervisor's own wake pipe, and it rides on `CompilationContext` beside the diagnostics sink and the census — shared by every session of one compilation, because one Ctrl-C stops the whole invocation and not the one recursive unit that happened to be waiting. That is also the answer to the filing's point about `-C`, restarts and subninja compilation: the watch is set in `compile_unit`, which is the one place every unit passes through.\n\nTHE STATUS IS ASKED OF THE ERROR, NOT ITS TEXT. `kati::interrupt::Interrupted` travels through `anyhow`, `MakeError::evaluate` looks for it in the chain and answers the new `MakeError::Interrupted`, and `evaluated()` turns that into `report::cut_short` — 130, and NOTHING on either stream. Not `abandoned`: `ABANDONED` is the status for every way of not being ABLE to build, and this is the user stopping a read that was going fine, which is the same distinction `stopped_status` draws one screen further down. The silence is measured too — GNU writes nothing here, and Ronin before this fix wrote `ronin: build stopped: interrupted by user.`, which is a BUILD's line reached only because the read had run to the end and started one.\n\nFIXTURES, three, in `tests/interrupts.rs`, all deterministic and none timing-dependent for its verdict:\n* `a_read_phase_interrupt_abandons_the_shell_function` — the filing's own case. `trap '' INT` makes the window a certainty, and `survived`, which the child writes thirty seconds later, is the teeth: a tool that waited would have it.\n* `a_read_phase_interrupt_abandons_a_shell_function_that_closed_its_output` — the wait after the read. `SHELL` is named through a symlink of the test's own, because a `SHELL` spelled `/bin/sh` is stood in for by Ronin's builtin shell, which holds the pipe until the command ends and so reaches the interrupt through the READ instead. Naming the same shell another way takes the stand-in out and puts the case on the path it was written for.\n* `an_interrupted_read_launches_no_further_shell_function` — sends no signal at all: the makefile's first `$(shell)` interrupts the tool itself, so the moment is a fact of the makefile. `exec >/dev/null 2>&1` before the `kill` is what makes the interrupt land where nothing is being read.\nNine unit tests in kati beside them, including one asserting that an abandoned child is STILL RUNNING afterwards rather than killed.\n\nTEETH, by parking, each build run against all three:\n| parked | cases that fail |\n| the read waits for the child again | 1 of 3 — the filing's case, and the suite takes 30.02 s instead of 0.22 s |\n| the wait after the read cannot be stopped | 1 of 3 — the `exec`-closing case, 30.03 s |\n| the session is told nothing (`interrupts = None`) | 3 of 3 |\n| the interrupted read is read as a rejection | 3 of 3, each `left: Some(2) / right: Some(130)` |\n| the launch guard removed | 0 of 3 — RECORDED AS SUCH |\nThe last row is honest rather than convenient. The guard's only observable effect is not launching one command in a state no deterministic fixture can arrange: the read's own check reaches every state a makefile can force, because a `$(shell)` under either shell holds something the tool is waiting on for its whole life, and the only path to \"flag set, nothing running\" is an interrupt landing inside pure evaluation, which needs a timed kill. The guard is kept anyway — it is the difference between \"GNU runs no command after Ctrl-C and nor do we\" being true by construction and being true by the read noticing first, and the command it would run is arbitrary.\n\nA SPEC GAP, FILED not fixed, as `an-interrupted-read-has-no-rule-of-its-own` with a dep on this node. `[spec:ronin:req:compat.process-integration+2]` says what an interrupt does to a BUILD — no further command launched, no running edge recorded, every changed output withdrawn — and a read has no edges; its departure clause describes signalling a process group and then stopping what is still standing, where a read ABANDONS without signalling, which is GNU's behaviour and not Ninja's. So the new sites are annotated `[spec:ronin:req:product.build-outcome]` for the status they leave, which that rule does govern, and the read-phase behaviour itself is left without a rule rather than claimed under one that does not say it.\n\nA PROBE FINDING WORTH KEEPING. Ronin's builtin shell and the system `/bin/sh` agree that `exec >/dev/null` closes the shell's own descriptor — measured directly on a pipe, EOF at 1 ms and 3 ms respectively — so there is no divergence there. What differs is that under a `$(shell)` the builtin path keeps something the tool waits on alive for the command's whole life while `SHELL := /bin/bash` does not, which is why the second fixture needs a shell the stand-in does not answer for. The other trap, recorded on this node before this cycle, was confirmed live: `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` leaves a Make-less binary at the shared debug path, and the first parked measurement of the cycle was rebuilt with `--features make` before use.\n\nGATES: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; workspace 998 passed / 0 failed (986 + 3 fixtures + 9 kati unit tests); conformance 336/51 normalised, 286 raw, one artefact and zero unclassified; equivalence 297 makefiles, 280 graphs compared, 75 refused, 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 1285 @ 132 unclassified / 0 compiler / 24 interface / 1129 narration, `tests/make_upstream_inventory.tsv` byte-identical — no row moved; make_port 4/4 with all eight divergence sidecars still on disk; port ledger 170 symbols; `scripts/check-make-projects.sh all` built vim 9.2 in 16 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 28 s; ninja conformance 425/425 plus 10 differentials, 86 build-outcome cases, 3 invocation-boundary cases, persistence round trip and log content; `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features` 771 passed / 0 failed (762 + the 9 kati tests, which do not depend on the front end); `scripts/check-release.sh` green end to end."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T02:36:40.880979219Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id an-interrupted-read-has-no-rule-of-its-own
        title "Say in the spec what an interrupt does to a read"
        kind @docs
        deps (a-read-phase-interrupt-waits-for-the-shell-function)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-22T02:29:25.180920485Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found while delivering `a-read-phase-interrupt-waits-for-the-shell-function`, and deliberately left out of it: the behaviour that node fixed is now measured, tested and shipped, and there is no rule that says it.\n\nWHAT HAS NO RULE. An interrupt during the READ PHASE — the compiler, before any edge exists — now does four things, each measured against GNU Make 4.4.1:\n1. It stops the wait for a running `$(shell)` command, instead of waiting for the command to end (10,007 ms before, 2-3 ms after, GNU 1-3 ms).\n2. It stops the wait for a command that closed its standard output early, which is a second wait behind the first (3,996 ms before under `SHELL := /bin/bash`, GNU 2 ms).\n3. It ABANDONS that command rather than signalling it: the child and its own children are left running, unsignalled, reparented, and never reaped — which is what GNU leaves, and it is NOT what Ronin's build does to an edge it stops.\n4. It launches no further command, so an interrupt between two `$(shell)` calls means the second does not run at all.\n\nWHY THE EXISTING RULES DO NOT COVER IT. `[spec:ronin:req:compat.process-integration+2]` is the natural candidate and does not fit twice over. It says what an interrupt does to a BUILD — \"no edge that was running is reported as finished or recorded\", \"every output such an edge had already changed is withdrawn\" — and a read has no edges and withdraws no outputs. And its one deliberate departure is written the other way round from what a read does: \"Ronin sends the same signal to the same groups and gives them the same chance to take it, and then stops what is still standing rather than being held by it\", where a read sends NO signal and stops nothing, because abandoning is what the oracle does. The rule is also a claim that the behaviour matches NINJA, and Ninja has no read phase to match; the reference for point 3 is GNU, measured.\n\n`[spec:ronin:req:product.build-outcome]` does cover the status the interrupted read leaves with — 130, without re-raising — and the new sites are annotated against it for that and only that. Point 4's wording is very close to `compat.process-integration`'s \"No further command is launched\", which is why the guard in `kati::fileutil::run_command` was written to it, but the clause sits inside a paragraph about edges and recipes and cannot be read as covering the read without saying so.\n\nWHAT THIS NODE IS. A spec amendment with the established bump discipline, not a code change: the code is delivered and the fixtures are in `tests/interrupts.rs`. The shape to decide is whether the read's behaviour belongs in `compat.process-integration` as a paragraph of its own — which makes a rule about matching Ninja carry a clause whose oracle is GNU — or in `make.md` as a rule of the Make front end's, beside `[spec:ronin:req:make.compiler-boundary]`, which is where the read phase is otherwise described. The second reads better and is what the delivering node's author leant towards without taking the decision.\n\nWEIGHT. Low urgency and real: the behaviour is right, tested and measured, and what is missing is the sentence that makes it a promise rather than an observation. What it costs today is that a later change to `run_command`'s waiting could take all of it away and no rule would notice — the fixtures would, which is why this is not a defect.\n\nONE VERIFICATION SITE HAS NOWHERE TO POINT. `ReadInterrupts` in `src/make.rs` and `kati::interrupt` carry no rule annotation at all, because there is none to carry. Whichever rule this node writes wants them, and so do the two abandonment fixtures, which currently claim only the status."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T03:14:30.09382341Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T03:18:34.854287535Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered as the node proposed, and the placement question it left open was taken the way it leant: the clause is a rule of the Make front end's, `[spec:ronin:req:make.read-interrupt]` in `docs/spec/ronin/make.md`, written directly under `[spec:ronin:req:make.compiler-boundary]` in the Graph construction section. A rule whose oracle is GNU Make does not belong inside `[spec:ronin:req:compat.process-integration]`, which is a claim that the behaviour matches Ninja, and Ninja has no read phase to match. THE OPERATOR MAY OVERRIDE THIS PLACEMENT: moving the same text into `compat.process-integration` as a paragraph of its own is a spec edit and a version bump, and every site annotated here would move with it.\n\nWHAT THE RULE SAYS, in three paragraphs answering the node's four measured points. The wait: an interrupt during the read stops it, whether the wait is on the command's output or — the output having ended — on the command itself, which is the second wait the delivering node found and the one a half-fixed read leaves standing. The launch: nothing further is launched, so an interrupt between two `$(shell)` calls means the second does not run. The status: the invocation leaves with what `[spec:ronin:req:product.build-outcome]` gives it and writes nothing to either stream, because no build was reached and a build's narration belongs to a build. And the abandonment, as a MUST NOT: the command that was running is not signalled, not killed and not reaped, and its own children are left running to be reparented and buried by init — GNU Make 4.4.1's behaviour, cited to `fatal_error_signal` waiting only for the children Make runs as jobs. The last paragraph is the boundary itself, saying in the spec what the node said in its filing: a read has no edge to leave unreported and no changed output to withdraw, and where a build sends Ninja's signal to the same process groups and then stops what is still standing, a read sends nothing at all.\n\nTEN SITES, five impl and five verify, and the node's \"one verification site has nowhere to point\" is closed. Impl: `kati::interrupt::stopped` (the launch promise), `kati::interrupt::read_to_end_or_abandon` (the first wait), `kati::interrupt::wait_or_abandon` (the second), `kati::fileutil::run_command` (where the guard sits and where both waits are reached), and `ReadInterrupts` in `src/make/interrupts.rs`. That last one carried `[spec:ronin:req:compat.process-integration+2]` before and no longer does — it is the read's seam and nothing else, and the node's own reading is that the build rule does not cover it; `compat.process-integration` keeps fifteen other sites, so nothing was left uncovered by the swap. Verify: the three fixtures in `tests/interrupts.rs` — `a_read_interrupt_abandons_its_command`, `an_interrupted_read_launches_nothing_further` (which likewise gave up its `compat.process-integration+2/test` claim), `an_interrupt_ends_the_second_wait` — and two kati unit tests, `an_interrupt_ends_a_read_the_writer_never_closes` and `an_interrupt_ends_a_wait_for_a_child_that_is_still_running`, the second of which asserts the child is STILL RUNNING afterwards and so is the only site that holds the MUST NOT directly. Every site keeps its `[spec:ronin:req:product.build-outcome]` claim for the status, which is what the delivering node annotated them for and what that rule does govern.\n\nOne comment went with it. `make::report::cut_short`'s doc ended \"See the node beside this one\", which was a pointer at a plan node standing in for a rule that did not exist; it now names the rule.\n\nVERSION MECHANICS. A new rule starts unversioned, so every site here is an unpinned float and nothing needed a bump. `compat.process-integration` was NOT bumped: its text is unchanged, and the two sites that stopped claiming it did so by dropping an annotation rather than by the rule saying something new. The mechanism the dispatch named holds — this commit touches annotating sites in `src/`, `tests/` and `kati/src-rs/`, so `nplan-rule` trailers for the new rule's impl and verify facets are derived from the diff rather than asserted.\n\nkati carries the three submodule annotations on `nplan/an-interrupted-read-has-no-rule-of-its-own` at 2301917d597287abfcf0832ecc8c05bac43c193a, pushed before this pin.\n\nGATES, run for a change that is comments and markdown and can move nothing else: `cargo fmt --check` clean in both trees; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `nplan_spec_validate --deny-warnings` no orphan refs; `nplan_lint` clean; workspace tests 1001 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, the baseline exactly; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean. Conformance, equivalence, the upstream inventory, make_port, the real-project gate and the release gate were not re-run for this node — no compiled behaviour changed — and they are run in full at the end of the dispatch."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-22T03:18:41.060220735Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id an-oversized-composed-segment-cannot-write-its-response-file
        title "Give a composed recipe's oversized preceding segment a directory for its response file"
        kind @fix
        deps (a-composed-recipe-segment-arrives-without-its-lines)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-24T02:07:23.668531774Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found while delivering `a-composed-recipe-segment-arrives-without-its-lines`, whose own note asked to probe a segment holding a line over the response-file threshold before trusting the fallback. Pre-existing rather than introduced by that work: the fallback path is the one an oversized segment always took, and it never worked.\n\nWHAT IS WRONG. A composed recipe's lines ahead of a `$(MAKE)` are staged as their own edge, whose output is an invented name — `.ronin_recipe_stage/N` (src/make/sink/invented.rs). When such a preceding segment holds a line too long to be a shell argument, `CommandLayout::launches_line_by_line` refuses the per-line split and the segment falls back to the whole assembled script read from a response file, named after the edge's output: `.ronin_recipe_stage/N.rsp`. But `Builder` deliberately does NOT create the directory for an invented output — src/build.rs (the loop that skips `is_virtual_output`, with the comment \"a name the graph invented to be asked for by has no file behind it ... Making it anyway left an empty `.ronin_recipe_stage/` in the build root of every tree whose recipe composed a `$(MAKE)`\"). So the response-file write lands in a directory that was never made.\n\nMEASURED against reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make, a recipe whose preceding segment is one ~120,000-byte line beside a `$(MAKE)`:\n\n    goal:\n    \\t@echo <120000 A's> > big.out\n    \\t@$(MAKE) --no-print-directory -C sub child\n    \\t@echo trailing > trailing.out\n\nGNU Make writes big.out (120001 bytes), runs the child, writes trailing.out, exits 0. Ronin exits 2 with `ronin: error: WriteFile(.ronin_recipe_stage/0.rsp): Unable to create file. No such file or directory`. Nothing is built.\n\nNARROW. Only the PRECEDING segment reaches this — the RESIDUAL segment's response file is named after the wrapper edge's real output (e.g. `goal.rsp`), whose directory exists, so an oversized trailing segment builds fine. And only a line over the threshold: a preceding segment of short lines now launches line by line (the sibling node's delivery) and needs no file at all.\n\nTHE TENSION A FIX HAS TO OWN, which is why this is filed rather than folded into the sibling. The build skips the invented output's directory ON PURPOSE, to keep an empty `.ronin_recipe_stage/` out of every composed tree — under `-n` as well, where nothing may reach the disk. So the fix cannot just create the directory for every invented output; it has to create it ONLY when there is a response file to write into it (the write path in src/build.rs around `write_response_file`, or the directory loop made to consider the rspfile's own parent). Gate it in a test that generates the oversized line rather than a corpus case — a 120 kB Makefile costs a quarter megabyte in the repository, the reason `an-oversized-recipes-marked-lines-cannot-be-split-out` records none either. The build-intent gate would catch it: today it errors where GNU builds."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-24T02:58:52.395119689Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-24T03:26:54.3487484Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered, and the filing's central claim held under the probe: reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make builds the reproducer whole (big.out 120001 bytes, the -C child, trailing.out, exit 0) where Ronin exited 2 with `WriteFile(.ronin_recipe_stage/0.rsp): Unable to create file. No such file or directory` and built nothing — the invented preceding proxy's response-file directory was never made.\n\nTHE SKEPTICISM PAID OFF, and it moved the fix. My first cut created the response file's directory for any rspfile whose directory was missing. That broke `ninja_build_makes_depfile_dir_only` (src/build/tests.rs, `[spec:ronin:req:compat.command-runtime/test]`), which pins Ninja's DELIBERATE asymmetry: the build tool makes a depfile's directory but NOT a response file's — a response file the manifest placed somewhere absent fails with `WriteFile … No such file or directory` and the directory stays unmade. That is Ninja's contract for a user-authored manifest and must not change. The defect is narrower: only a name the COMPILER invented has this problem, because the output loop in `prepare_edge` skips exactly the invented outputs (`is_virtual_output`, set only by `mark_invented_output`, which only `recipe_stage_proxy` calls). So the fix is scoped to invented outputs.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. `prepare_edge` computes `output_is_invented = completion_outputs.any(is_virtual_output)` — true only for a recipe-stage proxy, false for every manifest output and for the grouped-join proxy (which is not marked invented and carries no response file). The response-file write moved into `prepare_response_file`, which for an invented output (a) makes the response file's directory, because the tool that chose the path is the one that can place it, and (b) writes nothing under `-n`, so the invented directory is never made where nothing may reach the disk. A manifest's response file keeps Ninja's behaviour byte-for-byte: no directory made, and its `-n` handling left exactly as it was. Because an invented output never exists in Ninja mode, the Ninja code path is provably untouched.\n\nPROBE, GNU on the left, the filing's reproducer (a ~120 kB preceding segment `@echo <120000 A's> > big.out`, then `@$(MAKE) -C sub child`, then `@echo trailing > trailing.out`):\n\n| mode | GNU Make 4.4.1 | Ronin before | Ronin after |\n| build | big.out 120001, child, trailing, exit 0 | exit 2, WriteFile error, nothing built | matches (empty `.ronin_recipe_stage/` left, see residue) |\n| `-n`  | prints the commands, writes nothing, exit 0 | exit 2, WriteFile error | writes nothing, exit 0 |\n\nAlso probed: the residual oversized segment still builds (its rspfile is named after the wrapper's real output, whose directory exists — untouched); `-j4` builds identically; the response file itself is removed on drop (only the directory persists).\n\nRESIDUE, recorded honestly. A real build of an oversized PRECEDING segment leaves an empty `.ronin_recipe_stage/` in the build root; GNU leaves nothing. It is the invented staging directory, dot-hidden, and it occurs only for a preceding segment over the 100 kB response-file threshold — a shape the sibling `an-oversized-recipes-marked-lines-cannot-be-split-out` measured as occurring in NOTHING (longest recipe line: corpus 424 B, vendored kati 876 B, vim/zsh under 1 kB). This is a strict improvement over the state the original comment guarded against — an empty `.ronin_recipe_stage/` in EVERY composed tree — now narrowed to essentially never. Removing the directory would be racy across proxies sharing it and disproportionate; renaming the proxy (to drop the slash) would ripple through every composed recipe's manifest and the equivalence gate's 297 graphs, far beyond this node. The node endorsed \"give that proxy a real directory,\" which is what landed.\n\nFIXTURE, oracle-recorded, teeth measured on the base-state binary. `tests/shell.rs::an_oversized_preceding_segment_writes_its_response_file` generates the 120 kB Makefile (no corpus case — a quarter megabyte echoed back, the reason the sibling records none either) and gates both effects: a real build writes big.out (120001 bytes), child.out (`childran\\n`) and trailing.out (`trailing\\n`) and exits 0; `-n` writes none of them and exits 0. Teeth: on the base-state binary both exited 2 with the WriteFile error, writing nothing.\n\nGATES (node-scoped; the make-corpus and ninja gates are provably unmoved — no corpus recipe reaches the threshold and the Ninja code path is untaken, both run in the dispatch's final battery): fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean (the fix's line count pushed `prepare_edge` over the density limit, fixed by extracting `prepare_response_file`, not by suppressing); workspace tests 1085 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored (1084 + this fixture); `--no-default-features` 834 / 0; `ninja_build_makes_depfile_dir_only` green (the asymmetry preserved). Ronin-only change, no kati."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-24T03:27:02.36684404Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id drop-the-proc-macro-escape-hatch
        title "Drop the proc-macro escape hatch, and take nsh's closed surface with it"
        kind @build
    }

    {
        id make-mode-has-never-been-measured-against-gnu-make
        title "Measure Ronin's Make mode against GNU Make, and gate the ratio"
        kind @test
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-27T15:24:40.508551782Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE HOLE. `scripts/check-performance.sh` compares NINJA mode against pinned stock ninja across eight synthetic manifests, and Ronin wins seven of them. MAKE MODE HAS NEVER BEEN COMPARED WITH GNU MAKE AT ALL. There is no recorded number, no gate, and no workload — which is how a large regression could sit uncontested through a hundred-odd dispatches of correctness work without anything saying so.\n\nWHAT PROMPTED IT. The operator asked what the speedup is for using Ronin as `make`. A hand measurement, interleaving both tools so they saw the same conditions, said there is none and the sign is the other way: zsh 5.9.2 no-op ~1.55 s GNU against ~4.2 s Ronin, and vim 9.2 no-op ~80 ms GNU against ~130 ms Ronin. The zsh ratio did not move when Make mode stopped writing `.ninja_log`/`.ninja_deps`, so the build-state writes are not the cost.\n\nWHAT THIS NODE IS. The gate, not the fix. `scripts/check-make-performance.sh`, modelled on `scripts/check-performance.sh` — same recorded-ratio-with-tolerance shape, same interleaved sampling, same CSV provenance header — comparing ronin-as-`make` against `reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make` on the same trees, same targets, same `-j`, and wired into `scripts/check-release.sh`. Workloads: vim no-op and zsh no-op (the two real hand-written build systems the project already keeps configured), a synthetic wide graph in one directory (graph construction with no recursion), a synthetic deep recursive graph (recursion and composition with almost no graph), and a clean build of vim (the number that matters to a real user, too slow to run on every release pass, so opt-in and recorded rather than gated).\n\nTHE RECORDED RATIO IS WHAT IS MEASURED, NOT WHAT IS WISHED FOR. If Ronin is 2.7x slower on zsh the recorded ratio is 2.7 and the gate's job is to stop it getting worse while the profiling work brings it down. A gate whose baseline is aspirational fails on the day it is written and gets disabled.\n\nWALL TIME ON A QUIET HOST IS THE METRIC. Every number recorded here is taken with the box's load checked first; a measurement taken under load is junk and is re-taken rather than written down."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-27T15:24:46.11839316Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-27T15:50:53.779253084Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE GATE IS IN, AND IT MEASURED SOMETHING THE FILING DID NOT KNOW.\n\n`scripts/check-make-performance.sh` runs `examples/make_baseline.rs` the way `scripts/check-performance.sh` runs `examples/baseline.rs`: build, symlink, `--validate`, exec. Wired into `scripts/check-release.sh` AFTER `scripts/check-make-projects.sh`, and the order is load-bearing — two of the workloads measure vim and zsh at their up-to-date steady state, and there is nothing up to date until that gate has built one. Nine interleaved samples by default rather than the Ninja gate's fifteen, because these workloads are seconds where those are milliseconds.\n\nRECORDED, on a host at load 1.41, nine interleaved samples each, `-j8` both sides, GNU Make 4.4.1 against ronin 0.1.0 release:\n\n| workload | GNU | Ronin | ratio |\n| `wide-noop` (4,000 explicit rules, one directory, no recursion) | 904.664 ms | 2126.803 ms | 2.35x |\n| `recursive-noop` (259 Makefiles, fan-out 6, depth 3) | 81.209 ms | 1119.070 ms | 13.78x |\n| `vim-noop` (vim 9.2.0957, from the top) | 15.240 ms | 35.054 ms | 2.30x |\n| `zsh-incremental` (zsh 5.9.2 steady state) | 1355.675 ms | 2369.092 ms | 1.75x |\n| `vim-clean-build` (vim from empty, opt-in) | 16063.362 ms | 16082.989 ms | 1.00x |\n\nTHE CLEAN BUILD IS A WASH. Sixteen seconds either way. On the run that dominates a real user's day one, the front end's cost is lost inside the compiler's, and there is nothing here to fix.\n\nTHE COST IS PER MAKEFILE READ, AND `recursive-noop` NAMES IT. GNU forks 259 processes in 81 ms — 0.31 ms each. Ronin composes the same 259 units in 1119 ms — 4.3 ms each. Fourteen times the cost per unit against a tool that pays for a fork and an exec where Ronin pays for neither. That is where the profiling work goes.\n\nTWO THINGS THE FILING GOT WRONG, BOTH FOUND BY BUILDING THE THING.\n\nFirst: ZSH'S BUILD DOES NOT SETTLE, and not because of Ronin. A whole-tree `make` in an already-built zsh 5.9.2 recompiles one object, updates `stamp-modobjs` and relinks `Src/zsh` — every run, under GNU Make 4.4.1 itself, verified by running GNU four times in a row and watching it relink four times. So there is no such thing as a zsh no-op, the workload is named `zsh-incremental`, and its shape assertion requires the sentinel to CHANGE. This also reframes the operator's original 2.7x: roughly 1.3 s of that 1.36 s GNU figure is `gcc` and `ld`, work both tools do identically, so the front end's own share is about 55 ms GNU against about 1070 ms Ronin — the same order `recursive-noop` reports, not 1.75x. The dispatch's headline ratio was diluted by compiler time.\n\nSecond: EVERY WORKLOAD ASSERTS ITS OWN SHAPE, and it earns its keep immediately — the very first smoke run refused, because GNU rebuilt `Src/zsh` on what the harness had been told was a no-op. Without that check the gate would have recorded a number for a run that did a different amount of work than the one it was compared against, and it would have recorded it forever. A no-op that builds the sentinel, or an incremental run that does not, is refused rather than timed.\n\nTWO MORE THINGS THE HARNESS DOES THAT THE NINJA ONE DOES NOT.\n\nIt REFUSES TO MEASURE ON A BUSY HOST: one-minute load average above `--max-load` (4.00 by default) and the run stops before sampling instead of producing a number somebody will paste into a table. Checked only at the start, deliberately — once sampling begins the average includes the harness's own `-j8` workloads, and an after-check on that basis refused a completed clean-build run during development, which is how the design got fixed rather than the threshold raised.\n\nAnd it WAITS RATHER THAN POLLS. The Ninja harness reads `/proc` every 100 us to sample peak RSS, and that cost lands on the slower tool in proportion to how much slower it is. Here the only question is wall time, so nothing runs between the spawn and the wait, and no memory claim is made.\n\nNO ABSOLUTE THRESHOLD, deliberately, and this is the difference from the Ninja gate. Ronin does not beat GNU Make on these workloads. A gate that demanded it would fail on the day it was written and be switched off on the day after. What is recorded is what was measured and what is gated is the direction of travel: a Ronin/GNU ratio more than 20% worse than recorded is refused.\n\nThe pinned tree versions are matched by prefix under `reference/make-projects/` rather than named here, so `scripts/check-make-projects.sh` stays the one place a version lives; two matching trees is an error rather than a coin toss.\n\nGATES: fmt clean; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; `nplan spec uncovered` reports none, the new rule covered by the example and its three tests; workspace tests 1176 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, which is the base state's 1173 plus exactly the three tests this node adds. NOTE FOR THE RECORD: the dispatch's stated workspace baseline of 1111 does not match `cargo test --workspace --all-targets` at e73d5ab, which is 1173 before this change — the stale number is the dispatch's, not the tree's. The gate itself was then run end to end through `scripts/check-make-performance.sh` at five samples and passed, reproducing every ratio within a few percent (2.45x / 13.88x / 2.13x / 1.76x)."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-27T16:13:50.412401648Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id make-mode-hashes-a-symbol-index-with-siphash
        title "Stop hashing kati's internal lookup keys with SipHash"
        kind @perf
        deps (make-mode-has-never-been-measured-against-gnu-make)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-27T16:13:07.716958913Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "WHAT THE PROFILE SAID. `perf record` over a Make-mode no-op, release build with debug info, on a quiet host. Two workloads from the gate `make-mode-has-never-been-measured-against-gnu-make` just landed, both dominated by the same thing:\n\n- `recursive-noop` (259 Makefiles): `kati::evaluate::evaluate` 87.96% inclusive, `kati::dep::make_dep` 83.61%, `DepBuilder::pick_pattern_rule` 72.87%, `implicit_chain_exists` 63.11%, `ordered_candidates` 28.06%, `DepBuilder::exists` 23.89%, of which `std::fs::exists` (the `stat` itself) is 18.94%.\n- `wide-noop` (4,000 explicit rules, no recursion): the same shape harder — `pick_pattern_rule` 87.53%, `implicit_chain_exists` 78.55%, `ordered_candidates` 31.26%, `exists` 23.89% with 18.18% in `stat`.\n\nSo the Make front end's cost is not \"compiling the whole makefile every run\" — GNU Make reads the whole makefile every run too. It is ONE algorithm, the implicit (pattern and suffix) rule search, running roughly ten to fourteen times slower than GNU Make's. That is a defect in an implementation, not a consequence of the compile-to-graph model, and it is worth saying plainly because the dispatch that ordered this work expected the answer to be architectural.\n\nWHAT THIS NODE TAKES, which is the cheapest and safest of the three costs the profile named. Sixteen per cent of the whole run was inside SipHash and the probing around it: `DefaultHasher::write` 5.36%, `hash_one::<&Symbol>` 4.75%, `hash_one::<&Bytes>` 1.91%, `hash_one::<&(usize, Symbol)>` 1.29%, plus inserts and rehashes. Rust's default `HashMap` hashes with a keyed cryptographic permutation and a per-process random key, which is exactly right for keys a stranger chooses and buys nothing at all for these: every one of them is a `Symbol` — an interner index, a `NonZeroUsize` — or a byte string interned out of a Makefile on the machine's own disk. GNU Make hashes the same keys with a handful of multiplies.\n\n`kati::fasthash` is rustc's `FxHasher` (rotate, xor a word, multiply by an odd constant) behind `FastMap`/`FastSet` aliases, and it replaces the default in the twenty tables the profile put on the hot path: `Symtab::index`, `Evaluator::rule_vars`, and in `DepBuilder` `rules`, `rule_vars`, `expanded`, `chaining`, `rules_in_use`, `impossible`, `tried_implicit`, `exists_cache`, `intermediates`, `declared_intermediate`, `secondary`, `not_intermediate`, `mentioned`, `phony`, `ignore_errors`, `precious`, `precious_patterns`, and `candidate_pool`'s per-call `seen`.\n\nTHE ONE WAY THIS COULD HAVE CHANGED BEHAVIOUR, and it was audited rather than hoped about. Swapping a `BuildHasher` changes the order a `HashMap` iterates — unspecified either way, but stable within a build, so code that had come to lean on the old order would change behaviour silently rather than fail to compile. Every table above was checked for an iteration whose order reaches output. All but one are membership or lookup only. The exception is `DepBuilder::rules`, iterated once under `--gen_all_targets`, and that site already collects the keys and calls `sort_by_cached_key` on the interned name before using them — the hash order never survives to anything observable. The module's own doc says so, and says not to reach for this hasher where that is not true.\n\nMEASURED, seven interleaved samples per tool on a host at load under one, against the numbers the gate recorded three hours earlier on the same host:\n\n| workload | Ronin before | Ronin after | change | ratio before | ratio after |\n| `wide-noop` | 2126.803 ms | 1834.255 ms | -13.8% | 2.35x | 2.16x |\n| `recursive-noop` | 1119.070 ms | 977.857 ms | -12.6% | 13.78x | 12.16x |\n| `vim-noop` | 35.054 ms | 32.487 ms | -7.3% | 2.30x | 2.07x |\n| `zsh-incremental` | 2369.092 ms | 2231.678 ms | -5.8% | 1.75x | 1.71x |\n\nGNU Make's own figures moved by under 4% across the same run, which is what says the host was the same host.\n\nCORRECTNESS, run in full because a hasher swap is exactly the kind of change that moves something quietly, and every gate is where it was: conformance 354 runs, 326 identical / 28 differing normalised, 276 raw — the recorded baseline to the case; equivalence 274 makefiles, 276 graphs, 8 respellings, 56 make-rejected, 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 20 interface; make_port 4/4; ninja 425/425 upstream, 10 differential, 92 build-outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence round trip; vim and zsh both build from their own Makefiles (15 s and 26 s at -j8, 36 modules); `--no-default-features` clean; fmt and `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; workspace tests 1186 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored, which is the gate commit's 1176 plus the six harness tests the test-footprint gate asked for there and the four this module adds.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT, for whoever takes the next one. The profile's other two costs are still standing: `std::fs::exists` at 18-19% of the run, where GNU Make answers the same question from a directory it read once into a hash (`dir.c`), and `ordered_candidates` at 28-31%, which rebuilds and re-sorts the whole candidate list — allocating a `Vec`, a `HashSet` and a `Pattern` per candidate — for every name at every depth. The second is the larger and the more delicate: the `rules_in_use` filter sits ahead of `specific_rule_matched`, so the list cannot simply be memoised by name without changing which rules the match-anything retain step drops."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-27T16:13:54.835848932Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-27T16:14:26.749436714Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id the-implicit-rule-search-stats-every-candidate-name
        title "Answer the implicit search's existence questions from a directory read"
        kind @perf
        deps (make-mode-hashes-a-symbol-index-with-siphash)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-27T16:45:49.221581318Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE SECOND OF THE THREE COSTS THE PROFILE NAMED, and the largest. `DepBuilder::exists` was 23.89% of a Make-mode no-op with 18.94% of the whole run inside `std::fs::exists` itself — the `stat` syscall, not the code around it.\n\nCOUNTED RATHER THAN INFERRED, which is what turned a suspicion into a fix. `strace -c` over the 4,000-rule `wide-noop`, both tools, same Makefile:\n\n| | Ronin | GNU Make 4.4.1 |\n| `statx` | 480,137, of which 476,122 ENOENT | 0 |\n| `newfstatat` | 8,002 | 8,010 |\n| `getdents64` | 0 | 6 |\n| total | 488,146 | 8,028 |\n\nSixty times the filesystem syscalls, and 476,122 of them — 99.2% of the stats — asking after a name that was never going to be there. The mtime stats the two tools share are 8,002 against 8,010, so the whole difference is the implicit-rule search's existence questions. GNU Make answers every one of those from a hash of what it read with six `getdents64` calls (`dir.c`), and has done since forever.\n\nThe names are the built-in catalogue's inventions: for `build/0: src/0` the search asks after `src/0.c`, `src/0.cc`, `src/0.p`, `src/0.web`, `src/0.ym`, `src/SCCS/s.0.c`, `src/RCS/0.c,v` and about a hundred more.\n\n`kati::dircache::DirectoryCache` is that hash, and it is deliberately narrower than GNU Make's in one way and wider in another.\n\nNARROWER: IT ONLY EVER PROVES ABSENCE. `certainly_absent` returning true is a proof and the caller stops; false means \"ask the filesystem\" and the `stat` happens as before. That is not timidity, it is a correctness requirement — a directory entry is not a file. A symlink whose target has been removed is listed by `readdir` and does not exist, and answering \"present\" from the listing would report it there where `stat` reports it gone. The confirming `stat` costs one call per name that really is there: four thousand, against the four hundred and seventy-six thousand removed.\n\nWIDER: A DIRECTORY THAT IS NOT THERE IS AN ANSWER. The first cut treated every `read_dir` failure alike and left HALF the syscalls in place — 244,078 statx, all of them under `src/SCCS/` and `src/RCS/`, directories that do not exist. Nothing can be inside a directory that is not there, so `NotFound` and `NotADirectory` — the same two conditions `std::fs::exists` reports as absent — now prove absence for every name under them. A directory that EXISTS and cannot be READ is a different thing and proves nothing; there is a test for each of the three.\n\nTHE COHERENCE RULE, AND THE ARCHITECTURAL GUARD THAT CORRECTED IT. A `$(shell)` in a second expansion can create a file the search has already looked for, so a listing may only be trusted across a stretch in which nothing ran. My first cut kept the count in a `static AtomicU64` in `fileutil`, bumped inside `run_command`. `kati/tests/no_globals.rs::test_no_mutable_process_globals` refused it — mutable process globals are forbidden under `src-rs`, evaluation state belongs on the session (`plan/decisions/session-owned-evaluation.md`) — and it was right to, because the counter already existed and I had built a second one. `Session::note_command_ran` bumps `GlobCache`'s epoch from all four places a makefile can reach the disk, and its doc comment already cites GNU Make's `command_count`. The cache now stamps itself with `Session::filesystem_epoch()`, handed in on every call, and gives up everything it holds when that moves. One counter, already correct, already maintained.\n\nAFTER, 12,039 syscalls against GNU Make's 8,028 — from 60x to 1.5x. Nine interleaved samples per tool on a quiet host:\n\n| workload | Ronin at the gate's baseline | after FxHash | after this | total | ratio then | ratio now |\n| `wide-noop` | 2126.803 ms | 1834.255 ms | 1398.889 ms | -34.2% | 2.35x | 1.60x |\n| `recursive-noop` | 1119.070 ms | 977.857 ms | 785.509 ms | -29.8% | 13.78x | 9.75x |\n| `vim-noop` | 35.054 ms | 32.487 ms | 29.508 ms | -15.8% | 2.30x | 1.89x |\n| `zsh-incremental` | 2369.092 ms | 2231.678 ms | 2111.164 ms | -10.9% | 1.75x | 1.63x |\n| `vim-clean-build` | 16082.989 ms | — | 15105.164 ms | — | 1.00x | 1.01x |\n\nTHE RECORDED BASELINE MOVES WITH IT. `benchmarks/make-baseline-v1.csv` now holds these numbers rather than the ones the gate was born with. A gate left anchored at the slow figure would sit there letting the whole gain be given back without a word, which is the failure mode the gate exists to prevent.\n\nCORRECTNESS, run in full because this one changes what the evaluator believes about the disk: conformance 354 runs, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw; equivalence 274 makefiles, 276 graphs, 8 respellings, 56 make-rejected, 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 20 interface; make_port 4/4; ninja 425/425 upstream, 10 differential, 92 build-outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence round trip; vim and zsh both build from their own Makefiles (15 s, 26 s, 36 modules); `--no-default-features` clean; fmt and lints clean; workspace tests 1196 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored — 1186 plus this node's ten (nine `dircache` cases and the session-epoch one).\n\nWHAT IS LEFT. `ordered_candidates` is now the largest single item the profile named and is untouched: it rebuilds and re-sorts the whole candidate list — a `Vec` from the trie, a `FastSet` for de-duplication, a `Pattern` and a `Bytes` clone per candidate — for every name at every depth of the search. It is delicate rather than large: the `rules_in_use` filter sits AHEAD of `specific_rule_matched`, so the list cannot be memoised by name without changing which rules the match-anything retain step drops. Caching the pattern-matching work per name while leaving the filter and the specificity decision to each call would preserve that exactly, and is what I would take next."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-27T16:46:16.821871361Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-27T16:46:51.414171065Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id the-candidate-walk-allocates-a-vector-per-character
        title "Stop rebuilding the implicit-rule candidate list one allocation per character"
        kind @perf
        deps (the-implicit-rule-search-stats-every-candidate-name)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-27T17:12:03.448139647Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "A SMALL WIN, AND THE NOTE SAYS SO RATHER THAN DRESSING IT UP.\n\nWHAT THE PROFILE SAID after the two fixes before this one. On the recursive no-op, allocation and reference counting had become the largest thing left: `Bytes::shared_clone` 6.47% and `shared_drop` 6.35% — 12.8% between them — with `_int_malloc` 5.00%, `malloc` 2.28%, `free` 2.27%, `malloc_consolidate` 2.26% and `unlink_chunk` 1.50%, another 13.3%. `RuleTrie::get` was 4.54% on its own and `ordered_candidates` 5.90%.\n\nThe candidate walk is where that lands. `RuleTrie::get` descends a level per byte of the name and returned a fresh `Vec` from every level; `candidate_pool` called it twice and joined the two into a third; and the loop cloned each candidate's pattern `Bytes` to hand to `Pattern::new` while keeping the original only to compare it with `%`. All three are rebuilt from scratch for every name the search asks about, tens of thousands of times.\n\nTHREE CHANGES, none of them touching what the search decides. `get` appends into the caller's buffer. `candidate_pool` fills one buffer with both walks and de-duplicates the second walk's additions where they lie, keeping the walk's order exactly — the order the caller's stem-and-definition tie-break reads. And the `%` comparison is asked before the pattern is handed over, so `Pattern::new` takes it instead of taking a copy; the answer is still only used once the match has succeeded, which is where it was used before.\n\nMEASURED, and it is not what the profile promised. Nine interleaved samples on a host at load 0.64:\n\n| workload | before | after | ratio before | ratio after |\n| `wide-noop` | 1398.889 ms | 1376.408 ms | 1.60x | 1.51x |\n| `recursive-noop` | 785.509 ms | 780.529 ms | 9.75x | 9.69x |\n| `vim-noop` | 29.508 ms | 30.086 ms | 1.89x | 1.96x |\n| `zsh-incremental` | 2111.164 ms | 2297.914 ms | 1.63x | 1.57x |\n\nThe absolute zsh figures are not comparable between the two runs — GNU Make's own zsh time moved 12% between them, because that workload relinks and the link time varies — which is exactly why the gate records ratios. On ratios: `wide-noop` improved about 6% and held at 1.49–1.51 across three separate runs, `zsh-incremental` and `recursive-noop` moved a little the right way, and `vim-noop` a little the wrong way. Call it 2–6% where it shows and nothing where it does not.\n\nTHE PROFILE MOVED MORE THAN THE CLOCK, which is the finding. Re-profiled after: `shared_clone` 6.47% → 4.28%, `RuleTrie::get` 4.54% → 3.93%, the allocator 13.3% → 11.2%. Real work removed, and it did not buy proportionate wall time — the freed cycles were not the ones on the critical path.\n\nI WROTE THE JUSTIFICATION WRONG THE FIRST TIME and the comment now says so. The draft claimed the old `get` cost \"seven allocations and seven `Arc` clone-and-drop passes\" for a seven-byte name. Both halves are false: `extend` from an owned `Vec` MOVES its elements rather than cloning them, and `Vec::new` does not allocate, so a trie level with no matching rule cost nothing at all. What was actually saved is the allocation at each level that DID match and the copying between them, which is a far smaller quantity — and is why the measurement came in far under what the profile's allocator share suggested. The comment carries the corrected reasoning and the measured 2.5%, because a wrong reason in a comment is worse than no comment: the next person profiles this path, reads that allocation was the problem, and repeats the mistake.\n\nKEPT RATHER THAN REVERTED, deliberately, and it is a close call. It removes real work, the code is simpler than it was (one buffer instead of three, one clone fewer per candidate), the correctness battery is untouched, and `wide-noop`'s 6% reproduced three times. What it is not is the third big win, and the recorded baseline moves by a correspondingly small amount.\n\nCORRECTNESS: conformance 354 runs, 326/28 normalised, 276 raw; equivalence 274 makefiles, 276 graphs, 8 respellings, 56 make-rejected, 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 18/0/20; make_port 4/4; ninja 425/425 upstream, 10 differential, 92 build-outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence; vim and zsh build (16 s, 27 s, 36 modules); `--no-default-features` clean; fmt and lints clean; workspace tests 1196/0/1, unchanged, because this node adds no test — there is no new behaviour to gate, and the existing corpus is what proves the candidate order did not move.\n\nWHERE THE REMAINING 9.7x ON `recursive-noop` LIVES, for whoever takes the next one. `ordered_candidates` is still 37.7% of the run inclusive, and the reason is now measured rather than guessed: I instrumented the call and it runs 80,000 times over only 180 distinct names on the recursive workload — 440 calls per name — but 160,000 times over 78,056 distinct names on the wide one, barely two calls per name. So memoising by name is worth almost everything on recursion and almost nothing on breadth, and an unbounded memo would cost something like 280 MB on `wide-noop`. A BOUNDED cache — the last few dozen names, which is where the recursion's locality is — is the shape that fits both, and it must keep the `rules_in_use` filter and the `specific_rule_matched` decision outside whatever it caches, because the filter runs ahead of the specificity test and moving it would change which rules the match-anything retain step drops. The other standing item is `DepBuilder::new`/`install_builtin_rules` at 11.5%, paid once per composed unit — 259 times on that workload."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-27T17:12:29.164883909Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-27T17:12:56.834485417Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
