    {
        id the-implicit-search-interns-every-name-it-invents
        title "Ask after an invented candidate name without interning it"
        kind @perf
        deps (the-make-gate-inherits-the-release-runs-own-load)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-28T00:53:09.089325769Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T00:53:53.905390897Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE FOURTH OF THE COSTS THE MAKE PROFILE NAMED, and the largest one left. `DepBuilder::implicit_chain_exists` interned every name the built-in catalogue proposed, because the three questions it then asked about that name — `proven_impossible`, `exists`, `is_written_down` — were all keyed by `Symbol`. Counted on the 4,000-rule `wide-noop` with temporary counters in the tree:\n\n| | |\n| names interned at that one call site | 796,199 |\n| of which the interner had never seen | 388,097 |\n| of which turned out to EXIST | 0 |\n| `Symtab` at the end of the read | 489,846 entries |\n| `exists_cache` at the end | 480,120 entries |\n| `impossible` at the end | 76,019 entries |\n| peak RSS | 128.3 MB against GNU Make's 26.2 MB |\n\nNOT ONE of the 796,199 was there, which is the whole finding: the search was minting a permanent name for every guess it was about to throw away. GNU Make asks after such a name with `lookup_file` and `file_exists_p` (`implicit.c` 792, 833) and reaches `enter_file` only once a rule has been chosen (995-1041, 1119). It looks up. It does not insert.\n\nWHAT WAS CHANGED. The chain search works in bytes end to end. `exists_named`, `is_written_down_named` and `proven_impossible` take `&[u8]`; `intermediate_reachable`, `can_be_made_implicitly` and `implicit_chain_exists` take the name; `chaining` and `impossible` are keyed by `Bytes`; `suffix_rule_input` became `suffix_rule_name`, which does not intern, and the one caller that goes on to use its answer interns it there. The names the candidates propose go into one buffer refilled per prerequisite (`strutil::write_substituted_stem`): of the 796,199 that used to be materialised as a `Bytes` and handed to `intern`, 156,039 are materialised at all — exactly those that reach `intermediate_reachable` and so have to outlive the question. After: interner 85,745, `exists_cache` 80,020, `impossible` 76,019 (unchanged, which is the check that the memo still means the same thing), peak RSS 39.6 MB.\n\n`peek_symbol` COMING BACK EMPTY IS THE ANSWER TO SEVERAL QUESTIONS AT ONCE. `rules`, `phony`, `rule_vars`, `mentioned`, `goals` and `exists_cache` are all keyed by a symbol, so a name the interner has never seen is in none of them. That is why dropping the cache in front of `exists` for such a name costs nothing: what remains are the two halves that read the ground, and the first of those is answered by `dircache` rather than by a `stat`.\n\nNO SECOND STRING TABLE, which is the question this had to answer. `impossible` is the one place the search does have to keep an invented name, and it ends the wide no-op with 76,019 entries where the interner it used to be keyed by ended with 489,846 — the failures are the small half of what the search asks after and the only half worth keeping. `chaining` holds the road currently being walked, at most `MAX_IMPLICIT_CHAIN` entries at a time (measured maximum: 3). No bytes-keyed cache was added in front of `exists`: one would have held every name the search ever proposed, 480,120 of them, which is the table this exists to stop growing.\n\nTHREE PLACES STILL INTERN, deliberately, and in each the name has stopped being a guess: a candidate carrying a second expansion, because the expansion reads the target's own variable scope and that scope is keyed by the name; the suffix-rule input in `pick_pattern_rule`, past the impossibility check, because what follows measures it against the target's own prerequisites and may make it a node of the graph; and `resolved_prerequisites` at the top level, which is still a guess and is left for a follow-up.\n\nTHE REGRESSION I SHIPPED AND THEN FOUND, which is the part worth reading. Dropping the `exists_cache` for un-interned names means the vpath search runs once per question rather than once per distinct name, and Ronin's vpath search went to the filesystem directly. A makefile with `VPATH` set and 1,500 targets went 3,256 ms -> 4,962 ms: a 65% regression on a shape none of the four gated workloads has. Found by writing that workload and measuring it, not by the gate.\n\nTHE FIX WAS TO DO WHAT GNU MAKE DOES THERE TOO. `selective_vpath_search` (`vpath.c` 446, 456) asks `dir_file_exists_p` — the directory hash — and confirms a yes with a `stat` \"because the cache may be out of date\", which is exactly the contract `kati::dircache` already has. Routing the probe through it turns the regression into the largest single win here:\n\n| the VPATH workload, -j1 | before | after | GNU |\n| wall | 3,256 ms | 1,481 ms | 698 ms |\n| peak RSS | 73.1 MB | 27.9 MB | 11.6 MB |\n| filesystem syscalls | 1,082,253 | 10,854 | 6,171 |\n| of which `statx` | 1,078,875 (1,072,860 ENOENT) | 7,518 | 0 |\n\nA hundredfold reduction on a shape the earlier dircache fix had left untouched, because that one sat behind `exists` and this path never reached it. Output byte-identical before and after, on that workload and on a second one written to drive the `vpath` DIRECTIVE branch rather than the `VPATH` variable branch.\n\n`DirectoryCache::certainly_absent` also stopped allocating. It reached its map through `entry`, which wants an owned key and throws it away again whenever the directory has already been read — and every call but the first has. With the search asking it rather than interning, it is asked 900,000 times on the wide no-op for six directories: 900,000 allocations to store six keys.\n\nRESULTS, gated workloads, -j8, quiet host (load 1.64 before, 1.29 after), 15 interleaved samples per tool:\n\n| workload | GNU | Ronin recorded | Ronin now | change | ratio before | ratio now |\n| `wide-noop` | 864.213 ms | 1376.408 ms | 1078.690 ms | -21.6% | 1.51x | 1.25x |\n| `recursive-noop` | 80.069 ms | 780.529 ms | 740.484 ms | -5.1% | 9.69x | 9.25x |\n| `vim-noop` | 15.382 ms | 30.086 ms | 28.769 ms | -4.4% | 1.96x | 1.87x |\n| `zsh-incremental` | 1403.765 ms | 2297.914 ms | 2171.937 ms | -5.5% | 1.57x | 1.55x |\n\nPer-core, which is what this fix is about, `-j1` on the same host with the two binaries and GNU measured back to back: `wide` 1750.7 ms -> 1229.7 ms against GNU's 1181.2 ms, peak RSS 127.2 MB -> 39.6 MB against GNU's 26.2 MB. The recorded baseline moves to the figures above, so the gain cannot be given back without the gate saying so.\n\nWHAT THE FILING GOT WRONG, since it asked to be corrected. It sized the bytes-keyed-cache question at \"78,056 distinct candidate names\". That is `ordered_candidates`' distinct-name count from the previous node's instrumentation and is not the size of this problem: the interner reached 489,846 and `exists_cache` 480,120. And it read the recursive workload as implicit-search-bound on the strength of a flat profile (`shared_drop` 16%, `intern` 12.2%). Those shares were real and the work behind them is gone, and the workload barely moved: 843 ms -> 834 ms at `-j1`, 780 ms -> 740 ms at the gate. Each of its 259 composed units interns about 1,500 candidate names into a symbol table that ends at 2,823 entries, so there was never much there to take. Recursion pays for per-unit setup, which the previous node already measured at 11.5% for `DepBuilder::new`/`install_builtin_rules`. This defect lived on the wide shape — the one with no recursion in it at all.\n\nONE SEMANTIC DIFFERENCE, small and in the direction of fidelity. `exists_cache` is not invalidated by `Session::filesystem_epoch`, so a `$(shell)` in a second expansion that creates a file the search has already asked about used to be invisible to it; the un-interned path now reads `dircache`, which IS invalidated on that epoch, so those answers are fresher than they were. GNU Make's `file_exists_p` goes through the same epoch-honest hash, so where the two used to differ they now agree.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT, measured. `resolved_prerequisites` still interns through `glob_word` on the top-level candidate path: 132,033 calls on the wide no-op, 76,019 of them new names, which is why the interner ends at 85,745 rather than at the ten thousand the makefile actually names. Those names reach `implicit_prerequisites_reachable`, whose answers become `intermediates`, `tried_implicit` and graph nodes, so de-interning it means answering \"would this rule apply\" in bytes and interning on success — a bigger change than this one and its own node. Worth roughly 76,000 interns and something like 9 MB of the 39.6 MB peak. Beyond it, `ordered_candidates` is again the largest single item and is still the bounded-cache problem the previous node described.\n\nGATES, all run: fmt clean; `cargo check --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc` clean; workspace tests 1,765 passed / 0 failed; conformance 354 runs, 326 identical / 28 differing normalised, 276 raw; equivalence 274 makefiles, 276 graphs, 8 respellings, 56 make-rejected, 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 20 interface; make_port 4/4; port ledger 170 symbols; Ninja 425/425 upstream, 10 differential, 92 build-outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence round trip, log content; vim 9.2 and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules both built from their own Makefiles; Ninja performance gate validated; Make performance gate validated; `nplan spec uncovered` and `nplan spec stale` clean; `nplan lint` clean. No test is added: this node changes no behaviour, and the corpus and the equivalence gate are what prove the search still decides what it decided."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T00:55:09.347250169Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id the-suffix-pair-walk-interns-a-name-per-pair
        title "Ask after a written suffix pair without interning its name"
        kind @perf
        deps (the-implicit-search-interns-every-name-it-invents)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-28T02:06:54.700966987Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T03:37:35.582383172Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE FILING SAID THE RECURSION DEFICIT WAS THE CATALOGUE BEING BUILT 259 TIMES. IT IS MOSTLY THE CATALOGUE BEING SEARCHED 81,467 TIMES. Building it is a sixth of the gap and searching it is three fifths. This node takes half the building out — the largest single item in it — and the pre-parsed catalogue shared across units that the filing asked for turns out to be worth 45 ms and to cost a change in what `$(.VARIABLES)` answers, so it was declined rather than shipped. Both halves of that are measured below.\n\nREPRODUCING THE ABLATION FIRST. `recursive`, 259 units, `-j1`, median of 5, back to back: GNU 630 ms, Ronin 814 ms; GNU `-r` 366 ms, Ronin `-r` 185 ms. The filing's figures stand, and so does its best line: with the catalogue withheld Ronin is 1.98x FASTER than GNU Make, and the whole deficit is the catalogue.\n\nWHERE THE CATALOGUE'S COST IS. `perf record --call-graph dwarf` on the release binary, quiet host, INCLUSIVE shares of the whole run — no counters in the tree, so nothing here depends on instrumentation being where I put it:\n\n| share of the run | before | after | after, `-r` |\n| `DepBuilder::build_plan` — the implicit search | 46.69% | 49.14% | 6.55% |\n| `DepBuilder::new` | 12.54% | 6.25% | 4.44% |\n| — of which `install_builtin_rules` | 12.09% | 6.05% | — |\n\nAgainst the `-j1` CPU medians those shares are, before this node: 844 ms total, 204 ms under `-r`, so 640 ms of catalogue — of which BUILDING it is 102 ms (16%), SEARCHING it is 381 ms (60%), and the remaining 157 ms (24%) is the read of the 35-word `.SUFFIXES` bootstrap line and the teardown of everything the catalogue added.\n\nWHAT THE SEARCH IS DOING WITH IT. Counted: 81,467 calls to `ordered_candidates` against 3,974 under `-r`, pulling 1,629,340 candidate rules out of the trie against none. The workload's leaves are `build/N: src/N` and the `src/N` are files no rule makes, so each runs an implicit search; `src/N` ends in no declared suffix, so every match-anything rule in the catalogue applies, each proposes a prerequisite, and each of those is searched in turn. About 47 searches per prerequisite, 314 per unit.\n\nWITHIN `install_builtin_rules`, phase counters, one run:\n\n| `convert_written_suffix_rules` | 88.7 ms |\n| suffix disqualifiers and null-suffix rules | 17.8 ms |\n| built-in suffix pairs | 28.2 ms |\n| discard what a written pattern rule holds | 7.6 ms |\n| order by the suffix list | 2.3 ms |\n| archive suffix rules | 0.02 ms |\n| default pattern and terminal rules | 6.5 ms |\n\nThe largest item is not the static tables. It is the cross product `.SUFFIXES` spells while looking for the MAKEFILE'S OWN suffix rules: 35 suffixes make 1,190 pair names, each materialised as a `Bytes` and INTERNED so a symbol-keyed rule lookup could be done with it, and a makefile writes a rule for none of them.\n\nWHAT WAS CHANGED. `convert_written_suffix_rules` asks after the pair name instead of interning it, and writes it into one reused buffer instead of a `Bytes` per pair. `convert_to_pattern` reaches the name with `lookup_file`, which enters nothing, and the same answer is available without minting a symbol: a pair a makefile wrote a rule for had its name interned when that rule was read, so a name the interner has never seen has no rule under it. Both walks in one binary, back to back on the same host: 62.2 ms against 7.5 ms, with the interner at the end of the walk at 1,687 entries against 496. `install_builtin_rules` goes from 12.09% of the run to 6.05% — halved — and 102 ms to 45 ms.\n\nWHY `peek_symbol` COMING BACK EMPTY IS THE SAME ANSWER, spelled out because this is where a fix of this shape goes wrong. `lookup_rule_merger` consults `self.rules` and `written_as`. `rules` is keyed by the symbol of an explicit rule's output, interned when that rule was read; `written_as` reads `gpath_origin`, which is empty at this point and whose keys are interned symbols in any case. A name with no symbol is in neither. The names that stop being interned are `<suffix><suffix>` concatenations and nothing else, and the only other place that could tell the difference is `exists_named`, whose interned branch adds `rules.contains_key` and `phony.contains` to the two halves that read the ground — both false for exactly the names that stopped being interned, leaving `on_disk || vpath_search`, which is the branch they now take.\n\nRESULTS, `-j1`, interleaved medians of CPU time (user+sys) with the two binaries alternating, quiet host:\n\n| workload | before | after | |\n| `recursive` | 0.844 s | 0.745 s | -11.7% |\n| `zsh` no-op | 4.109 s | 4.004 s | -2.6% |\n| `vim` no-op | 0.044 s | 0.041 s | -7%, 3 ms |\n| `wide` no-op | 1.207 s | 1.229 s | +1.8% |\n\nThe benefit scales with composed units, which is what it should do: 259 units on `recursive`, about forty on `zsh`, two on `vim`, one on `wide`.\n\nTHE `wide` READING IS BINARY LAYOUT AND NOT WORK, and it was controlled rather than waved off. `wide` is one composed unit, so the change removes 1,190 interns from it — 0.25 ms of 1,210, 0.02%, which cannot be 1.8% in either direction. A third binary identical to the fixed one except for an `if env::var_os(..)` in `Symtab::new` measures 1.211 s: 1.5% from the fixed binary's 1.229 s with byte-identical behaviour. Forcing the OLD symbol numbering back into that binary — 1,191 filler names interned after the well-knowns — measures 1.214 s, so the numbering is not the cause either. Two builds of the same behaviour differ by as much as before differs from after; `fat` LTO with one codegen unit is why.\n\nTHE SHARED PRE-PARSED CATALOGUE WAS NOT BUILT. Sized first, so the decision is not made on the difficulty. Standing a thread-local cache in front of `recipe_lines` — wrong across sessions, but valid as a measurement on a workload that expands no built-in recipe — the recipe PARSING is worth 16 ms of the 640. Sharing whole `Arc<Rule>`s, which also skips `Rule::new`, its five vector allocations and the four interns each rule does, is worth about 45 ms: the three phases that build them cost 57 ms and what must stay per unit is about 10 ms of it — `populate_implicit_rule` measured at 4.5 ms across the run, `declared_suffix_pair` at 4.0 ms, and the merger lookups.\n\nThe blocker is `Value::SymRef(Loc, Symbol)`. Every `$(COMPILE.c)` in a catalogue recipe is a `Symbol` and every `Loc.filename` is one, so a shared catalogue is sound only if all of them sit in the fixed prefix `Symtab::new` establishes — which is exactly what the filing said, and it is right. The catalogue's recipes name 52 variables. Forty-six are entries of `builtins::DEFAULT_VARIABLES` and are bound in every session that installs the catalogue; the other six — `LDLIBS`, `LOADLIBES`, `GFLAGS`, `MAKEINFO_FLAGS`, `SCCS_OUTPUT_OPTION`, `TEXI2DVI_FLAGS` — are the ones GNU leaves for a makefile to set, and a makefile that sets `LDLIBS` binds one too.\n\n`GlobalVars::matching` (`var.rs:963`) hands back bindings \"in symbol order\": it walks a `Vec` indexed by the symbol. `write_variable_names` (`var.rs:695`) is what `$(.VARIABLES)` is built from and takes that order as given. Measured with `env -i PATH=.. HOME=.. MYENVVAR=1`, Ronin answers `SHELL .VARIABLES MAKEFILE_LIST .RECIPEPREFIX .SHELLFLAGS .DEFAULT_GOAL PATH HOME MYENVVAR MAKELEVEL MFLAGS MAKEFLAGS AR ARFLAGS AS CC ...` — the well-knowns, then the environment, then `DEFAULT_VARIABLES` in table order. Pre-interning the catalogue's names moves that last block in FRONT of the environment, and `$(.VARIABLES)` changes.\n\nIt is unspecified output: GNU's order is its hash order, and measured side by side the two answers hold the same 101 names and agree on ONE of the 101 positions. The corpus is deliberate about never gating it verbatim — `variable-dot-variables` sorts and filters, and `the-value-of-a-worked-out-name-is-the-list-it-answers` says in a comment that the list is never gated verbatim because it holds the environment. The exported environment is sorted by name (`export.rs:269`) and is unaffected. But a makefile can still read the difference, the node's constraint was that nothing observable may move, and 45 ms of a 640 ms gap does not buy that. No arrangement of the prefix avoids it: the saving lives in the recipe-bearing rules and their recipes name built-in variables by symbol.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT, SIZED, on the fixed binary: 547 ms of catalogue at `-j1`, of which building it is 45 ms (8%), the implicit search 353 ms (65%), and the read and teardown 149 ms (27%). The search is the node worth opening, and the previous node already named `ordered_candidates` as the largest single item and a bounded-cache problem. The parallelism gap is separate and already filed — GNU forks per unit and runs 7.64 cores busy at `-j8` where Ronin's front end runs 1.01, which is why this fix is judged at `-j1` and on CPU time.\n\nCORRECTIONS TO THE FILING, since it asked to be corrected rather than agreed with. (1) \"That 616 ms is 259 repetitions of the same work\" — it is 259 repetitions of about 100 ms of setup and 81,467 repetitions of a search. (2) \"the recursion profile is now flat and diffuse ... no single hot spot\" — the flat profile's top entries under a full run are `ordered_candidates` 4.08%, `RuleTrie::get` 3.57%, `implicit_chain_exists` 3.23%, `PatternMatch::of` 2.83%, `Pattern::matches` 1.72% and `certainly_absent` 1.55%, every one of which vanishes under `-r`, against `install_builtin_rules` at 1.01% flat. That is not diffuse; it is one subsystem read at its leaves, and reading it with `--children` says so in one line. (3) The invariant/variant boundary is where the filing guessed it and the invariant half is real — it is worth 45 ms and costs `$(.VARIABLES)`. (4) The workspace test count in the brief, 1,765, matches no invocation reproducible on this checkout: the release gate's own line gives 882, `--workspace --all-targets` gives 1,196 and `--workspace` gives 1,125, all with 0 failures.\n\nONE UNEXPLAINED EVENT, recorded rather than buried. The first attempt at the Make performance gate reported `ronin exited exit status: 2 on recursive-noop` — an abandoned build, with the diagnostic discarded because the harness nulls both descriptors. It has not recurred: four subsequent full gate runs are clean, and the recursive workload ran 76 more times without a failure, 40 of them on trees regenerated from scratch between runs, on both binaries. Nothing in this change can abandon a build — it stops interning names and changes no decision — and a defect that could would fail deterministically rather than once in eighty. Left as an observation for whoever sees it a second time; the harness discarding stderr is what made it unreadable, and that is worth fixing before then.\n\nGATES. fmt clean; `cargo check --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc` clean; tests 882 passed / 0 failed on the release gate's line and 1,196 / 0 for `--workspace --all-targets`; conformance 354 runs, 326 identical / 28 differing normalised, 276 raw; equivalence 274 makefiles, 276 graphs, 8 respellings, 56 make-rejected, 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 20 interface; port ledger 170 symbols; Ninja 425/425 upstream, 10 differential, 92 build-outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence round trip, log content; vim 9.2 and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules both built from their own Makefiles; Ninja performance gate validated; Make performance gate validated against the re-recorded baseline; `nplan spec uncovered` and `nplan spec stale` clean; `nplan lint` clean.\n\nThe recorded baseline moves to a quiet-host 15-sample run (load 2.54 before, 2.45 after): `wide-noop` 864.213/1078.690 -> 871.176/1076.683, `recursive-noop` 80.069/740.484 -> 79.017/649.669, `vim-noop` 15.382/28.769 -> 15.611/29.035, `zsh-incremental` 1403.765/2171.937 -> 1427.876/2273.000. Ratios 1.25x -> 1.24x, 9.25x -> 8.22x, 1.87x -> 1.86x, 1.55x -> 1.59x. The zsh row is recorded worse and is not a regression this node caused: `zsh-incremental` rebuilds after a touch, so its wall time is dominated by `gcc` and `ld` running identically for both tools, its samples span 2,070 to 2,466 ms, and at `-j1` on CPU time the same tree improves by 2.6%. What is recorded is what was measured, which is this file's stated policy.\n\nThe five shapes a shared catalogue would have broken were driven by hand against the oracle as well as through the corpus, before and after, all identical: a makefile that clears `.SUFFIXES` and declares its own; one that adds `..o` and `.zz` and writes `.c.o:`, `.c..o:` and `.zz.c:`; one that shadows the built-in `%.out: %`; one that asks for `-r` through its own `MAKEFLAGS`; and one that asks for `-R`. zsh is the same case in the wild — it declares `..o` beside `.o` and writes `.c.$(OBJ):` — and it builds.\n\nNo test is added: this node changes no behaviour, and the corpus and the equivalence gate are what prove the conversion still finds what it found."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T03:37:40.369678123Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id the-chain-search-proves-every-name-twice
        title "Remember a chain search's failure the way GNU Make does"
        kind @perf
        deps (the-suffix-pair-walk-interns-a-name-per-pair)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-28T04:40:19.104698737Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T04:58:49.012318499Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "NEITHER HYPOTHESIS. The filing offered a missing per-file latch or a memoisable pure function, and asked to be told if it was neither. It is neither: the implicit chain search proves every name it fails on TWICE, and the second proof is one this repository's own memo declines to answer because of a depth key GNU Make does not have. Removing the doubling is a fidelity fix, it removes searches rather than making them cheaper, and it is worth more than either hypothesis was sized at.\n\nWHAT THE ORACLE ACTUALLY DOES, counted rather than reasoned about. One leaf unit of the recursive workload, `make -d`, GNU Make 4.4.1:\n\n| `Looking for an implicit rule for` | 9 |\n| `Looking for a rule with intermediate file` | 171, over 171 DISTINCT names |\n| `No implicit rule found for` — one per `pattern_search` that ends | 180 |\n| `Searching for a compatibility rule for` | 0 |\n\nSo GNU makes exactly 180 `pattern_search` calls for that unit and asks after each of the 171 chain names ONCE. Ronin, counted with temporary counters at `ordered_candidates` and its two call sites, made 369 — `top` 18 over 9 distinct, `chain` 351 over 171 distinct. Every chain name was proved twice: 171 x 2 + 9 = 351. After this change Ronin makes 189: `chain` 171 over 171 distinct, which is GNU's number exactly, and `top` 18, which is GNU's two `intermed_ok` passes read as two candidate builds instead of one.\n\nWHY IT WAS TWICE. `intermediate_reachable` records a failure in `impossible` against the DEPTH it was reached at, and `proven_impossible` will reuse an answer only from at least as deep. GNU's `file_impossible` has no depth: its search has no limit to run out of, so a failure is a failure and is remembered for the run. Ronin has `MAX_IMPLICIT_CHAIN = 6`, and the depth key exists to keep a failure that ran out of budget from being read as the rules' own answer. That is a real case and the key is right for it. But the key was applied to EVERY failure, and a failure the limit never touched is depth-independent by construction: the rules ran out with budget still in hand, so a shallower search walks the same tree to the same end. Reached deepest first and asked about again from the top, every one of them was re-proved.\n\nWHAT WAS CHANGED. A `budget_spent` flag, bubbled the way `chain_truncated` already is, records whether `MAX_IMPLICIT_CHAIN` cut anything short anywhere under the name. A failure it never touched is recorded at depth zero and holds from anywhere, which is GNU's reading; a failure it did touch keeps the depth, which is what the depth always meant. `chain_truncated` and `budget_spent` are separate because they want opposite things: a road cut by the cycle guard is worth nothing to any later search, a road cut by the limit is still worth something to a search standing no shallower.\n\nTHE FLAG IS LOAD-BEARING, and this was proved by removing it rather than by argument. A third binary that records every failure at depth zero refuses `cap-then-shallow` — eight `%.a: %.b ... %.h: %.i` rules, `deep.i` on disk, `all: deep.a deep.g` — with `'deep.g', needed by 'deep.f', missing and no known rule to make it`, where GNU Make, HEAD and the fixed binary all build the whole chain. Planning `deep.a` first proves names near the far end impossible only because the chain limit ran out; without the flag that verdict poisons the real search for `deep.g`, which stands two links from a file that is there.\n\nRESULTS, `-j1`, interleaved medians of CPU time (user+sys), HEAD and the fixed binary alternating, nine samples each:\n\n| workload | before | after | |\n| `wide` no-op | 1191 ms | 762 ms | -36% |\n| `recursive` no-op | 733 ms | 526 ms | -28% |\n| `zsh` no-op | 3864 ms | 3870 ms | +0.2% |\n| `vim` no-op | 37 ms | 36 ms | -3%, 1 ms |\n\n`ordered_candidates` calls over the whole workload: `wide` 164,041 -> 84,021 (chain 156,039 over 76,019 distinct -> 76,019 over 76,019); `recursive` 81,467 -> 41,727 (chain 77,493 -> 37,753). Both shapes carried the same 2.05x, which is why both move.\n\nCORRECTIONS TO THE FILING, since it asked for them rather than for agreement.\n\n(1) HYPOTHESIS A IS WRONG, and wrong in three ways at once. GNU's broad latch is `remake.c` 555 and 1112, which guards `try_implicit_rule` in the update walk — the per-file \"search this file at most once\" — and Ronin already has it: nothing reaches `pick_rule` twice for one name, because `build_plan` returns the `done` entry first. GNU's `pattern_search` recursion (`implicit.c` 896) never consults `tried_implicit` at all; its recursion memo is `file_impossible`, which is exactly the field this node fixed. And a latch across composed units would be flatly wrong: Ronin runs the 259 recursive units in ONE process, so `src/0` in one leaf and `src/0` in another are different files, and a name-keyed latch would answer for the wrong directory.\n\n(2) \"440 `ordered_candidates` calls per DISTINCT name\" IS AN ARTEFACT OF DIVIDING A WHOLE-PROCESS TOTAL BY A PER-PROCESS DISTINCT COUNT. Both numbers are right and the ratio is not a search re-searching: 81,467 is the total over all 259 composed units, and 180 is how many distinct names ONE unit asks about — and, because the 216 leaf makefiles are identical and their names are relative, how many the whole process asks about. Per unit the ratio was 369/180 = 2.05, and 2.05 is the whole defect. The 440 was 216 leaves each legitimately asking the same 180 relative names, which GNU also does, once per forked process.\n\n(3) HYPOTHESIS B IS THE RIGHT SHAPE FOR THE WRONG SIZE, and was measured before being set aside. A per-`DepBuilder` memo of the whole answer — unsound, ignoring `rules_in_use`, built only to bound what memoising could deliver — took the recursive run from 657 ms to 547 ms, 17%. The same memo held for the process gave 455 ms. Neither is buildable: the per-unit one has 2.05x of reuse and that 2.05x IS the defect, so it caches a repetition that should not happen; the process-lifetime one is only sound if the catalogue is the same object across units, and the previous node already sized and declined a shared catalogue at 45 ms because it moves `$(.VARIABLES)`. Removing the searches beat caching them: 526 ms against the 547 ms ceiling of the sound cache, with no cache at all.\n\n(4) \"roughly 47 searches per prerequisite\" does not survive counting. The chain reaches depth 2 and no further on either gated shape, and `MAX_IMPLICIT_CHAIN` is not hit ONCE on `wide`, `recursive`, `vim` or `zsh` — the whole depth key was paying for a case none of the gated workloads reaches.\n\n(5) THE `-j1` GAP ON `recursive` WAS NEVER 8x; THAT IS THE `-j8` WALL RATIO AND IT IS THE PARALLELISM ITEM, NOT THIS ONE. GNU Make forks 259 processes and pays 259 process creations; Ronin composes the 259 units in one. Measured on this host as three consecutive blocks of nine, twice over: HEAD 669 / 692 ms, GNU Make 524 / 534 ms, fixed 486 / 540 ms. Ronin was 1.3x GNU Make at `-j1` on CPU time before this node and is within the noise of it after. On `wide` Ronin was already the faster of the two and the gate now reads 0.77x.\n\nONE PRE-EXISTING DIFFERENCE FOUND AND NOT FIXED, recorded because it is the only place `MAX_IMPLICIT_CHAIN` is observable. `deep-chain-reachable` — the same eight rules with `all: out.a` alone and `out.i` on disk — is built by GNU Make in eight steps and refused by Ronin, before this change and after it, because the chain is longer than six. GNU has no limit; `rule->in_use` is what bounds its recursion. Its own node.\n\nGATES, all run on this checkout. fmt clean; `cargo check --all-targets` clean; `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean; `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean; tests 882 passed / 0 failed on the release gate's line (`scripts/check-release.sh`) and 1,196 / 0 for `cargo test --workspace --all-targets`; conformance 354 runs, 326 identical / 28 differing normalised, 276 raw (`scripts/check-make-conformance.sh`); equivalence 274 makefiles, 276 graphs, 8 respellings, 56 make-rejected, 0 disagreements; upstream inventory 18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 20 interface (`scripts/check-make-upstream.sh`); port ledger 170 symbols; Ninja 425/425 upstream, 10 differential, 92 build-outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence round trip, log content; vim 9.2 and zsh 5.9.2 both built from their own Makefiles; Ninja performance gate validated; Make performance gate validated; `nplan spec uncovered` and `nplan spec stale` clean; `nplan lint` clean. `scripts/check-release.sh` exits 0.\n\nFourteen shapes were also driven by hand against the oracle, before and after, and the two Ronin outputs are byte-identical on every one: the deep chain reachable and unreachable; two roads into one deep name; a cleared `.SUFFIXES`; a shadowed `%.out: %`; a terminal `%.z:: %.x` with its prerequisite absent and present; `-r` and `-R` through the makefile's own `MAKEFLAGS`; the built-in catalogue on a suffixless `src/N`; a chain whose intermediate is also written down; two targets sharing a failing subtree at different depths; and the `cap-then-shallow` pair above.\n\nNo test is added: this node changes no behaviour, and the corpus and the equivalence gate are what prove the search still decides what it decided.\n\nTHE RECORDED BASELINE MOVES to the release gate's own quiet-host run on this checkout — nine samples per tool, load average 2.10 before and 1.59 after: `wide-noop` 871.176/1076.683 -> 858.567/658.832, `recursive-noop` 79.017/649.669 -> 79.317/456.482, `vim-noop` 15.611/29.035 -> 15.506/27.552, `zsh-incremental` 1427.876/2273.000 -> 1356.128/2112.582. Ratios 1.24x -> 0.77x, 8.22x -> 5.76x, 1.86x -> 1.79x, 1.59x -> 1.56x. `wide-noop` crosses over: Ronin is now faster than GNU Make on it at `-j8` as well as at `-j1`.\n\nNINE SAMPLES AND NOT FIFTEEN, said plainly because the previous record was fifteen. A fifteen-sample run WAS attempted and is not what is recorded: it finished with `load_average_after=10.81` because another agent's build had the machine, and it reads GNU Make's `recursive-noop` at 91.910 ms against the 79.317 ms of the quiet run and a 2,339 ms outlier on `wide-noop`. Nine quiet samples are a measurement and fifteen contended ones are not, and nine is the release gate's own default. `vim-clean-build` is carried forward unchanged at 14967.081/15105.164: `scripts/check-release.sh` does not pass `--clean-build`, so that row was not re-measured, and the contended run that did measure it put both tools at 17.4 s and the ratio at 1.00x."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T05:01:03.220799715Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id an-eight-link-implicit-chain-is-refused-where-gnu-make-builds-it
        title "Follow an implicit chain as far as the rules do"
        kind @fix
        deps (the-chain-search-proves-every-name-twice)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-28T07:31:43.444739697Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T08:19:13.592550871Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE DEFECT WAS REAL AND THE FILING'S DIAGNOSIS WAS RIGHT AS FAR AS IT WENT. `MAX_IMPLICIT_CHAIN = 6` in `kati/src-rs/dep.rs` capped the implicit-rule chain search. Eight `%.a: %.b` ... `%.h: %.i` rules with `out.i` on disk are eight steps of GNU Make 4.4.1 and were \"'out.a', needed by 'all', missing and no known rule to make it\" here. The cap is gone and that chain now builds, along with twelve, twenty and a thousand links.\n\nWHAT TERMINATES THE SEARCH: `rule->in_use`, which Ronin already had as `rules_in_use`, and nothing else. Every level of the recursion stands inside `while_rule_in_use` for the rule that proposed the name being asked about, and `ordered_candidates` plus both suffix-rule loops strike a marked rule out of the candidates before it can be matched, so each level has strictly fewer rules to offer than the one outside it. The road is bounded by the catalogue however the names along it are spelled — which is the case the deleted comment claimed the guard could not catch. `%.a: %.b.a` against `%.b.a: %.a` invents a longer name at every step and repeats none; it stops on the third level with both rules spent, and both tools drop the same circular dependency.\n\n`budget_spent` IS GONE, and the brief was right that it needed a decision rather than a deletion. It existed to tell a failure the LIMIT cut short from a failure the rules answered, because only the first is depth-dependent. With no limit there is no first kind, so `impossible` is now a bare `FastSet<Bytes>` keyed by name exactly as `file_impossible` is, and `proven_impossible` takes no depth. `cap-then-shallow` — the pair the previous node proved the flag load-bearing on — builds identically here, which is the regression test the flag's removal has to pass.\n\nTHE BRIEF DID NOT ANTICIPATE THE SECOND GUARD, AND THE MEASUREMENT FORCED IT. `chaining` held the names on the road currently being walked and turned a search round when one repeated; `chain_truncated` kept such a failure out of the memo. GNU has no counterpart and walks straight through a repeated name. Removing the cap while keeping that guard is a REGRESSION, measured rather than reasoned: ten `%.a: %.gN.a` rules each paired with `%.gN.a: %.a` are 0.00 s of GNU Make and were 0.06 s at HEAD, and became 9.86 s with the cap removed and the guard kept — because the road GNU takes is the one the guard was refusing, back to `out.a` under both rules of each pair and taken on trust at the bottom by the compatibility pass, with every level above it then succeeding on its first candidate. Without the guard: 0.01 s. So `chaining` and `chain_truncated` went with the cap, and the answers are unchanged on every shape driven by hand.\n\nORACLE COMPARISON, eighteen shapes, before and after, compared on the recipes that actually ran, the exit status and what was left on disk. After: agreement with GNU Make on all eighteen. Before: ten. The eight that move are all chains longer than six — the eight-link chain, twelve, twenty; the same eight links under a cleared `.SUFFIXES`, under `MAKEFLAGS += -r` and under `MAKEFLAGS += -R`; a chain reached by two roads; and `cap-then-shallow`. The ten that do not move are byte-identical between the two binaries: a chain whose tail is absent, one that stops one link short of a file that is there, a `%.a`/`%.b` cycle, `%.a: %.b.a` against `%.b.a: %.a`, `%.a: %.b.a` alone, a shadowed `%.out: %`, the built-in catalogue on a suffixless name, terminal `%.z:: %.x` with its prerequisite absent and present, and a chain whose intermediate is also written down.\n\nTHE TWELVE-LINK CHAIN IS THE ONE THAT SHOWS THE CAP WAS WORSE THAN A REFUSAL. GNU builds `out.a` through the makefile's own twelve rules. HEAD built it through a shorter road the built-in catalogue offered — `lex -t out.l > out.r`, then `f77 -F -o out.f out.r` — and then FAILED the build, because `out.l` is not lex input. Where a built-in shortcut fitted inside six links, the cap made Ronin take a road GNU never considers.\n\nSCALE. Linear `%.sN` chains now build step for step with GNU at 8, 12, 20, 50, 100, 200, 300, 500 and 1,000 links, where HEAD refused all nine. At 2,000 links GNU Make dies of its own recursion (SIGSEGV, 0.36 s) and Ronin still builds all 2,000, in 34.9 s. The same chains with no tail file are refused by both in 0.01–0.04 s at 100, 300 and 1,000 links; so is a 40-level catalogue offering three roads at every level; a fully connected 24-suffix catalogue is a dropped circular dependency in 0.02 s on both. Long chains cost quadratically and that is not new: 1,000 links is 0.06 s of GNU against 4.10 s here.\n\nONE PATHOLOGY MEASURED AND NOT FIXED, filed here rather than folded in. The `%.a: %.gN.a` catalogue is exponential in Ronin at HEAD and after, and constant in GNU. The cause is not the chain guard: GNU's `pattern_search` MATERIALISES the whole chain in the one search (`pat->file = int_file`), while Ronin answers a boolean and `build_plan` searches again at every level, each time with a different set of rules in use, which reopens branches the first search never walked. `make -d` counts 158 searches for GNU on the 60-pair catalogue where Ronin does not finish in 90 s. Best of two or three per pair count: ten pairs GNU 0.00 s / HEAD 0.06 / here 0.01; eighteen 0.00 / 2.61 / 1.51; twenty 0.00 / 4.74 / 5.79; twenty-two 0.00 / 8.97 / 25.42. Below eighteen pairs this change is the faster of the two, above it the slower, and both are the wrong shape. That is its own node.\n\nPERFORMANCE. `scripts/check-make-performance.sh` inside the release gate, nine interleaved samples, load 0.70 before and 1.89 after: wide-noop gnu 903.770 / ronin 680.822 = 0.75x against the recorded 0.77x; recursive-noop 79.669 / 467.702 = 5.87x against 5.76x; vim-noop 15.812 / 27.918 = 1.77x against 1.78x; zsh-incremental 1340.595 / 2081.614 = 1.55x against 1.56x. All inside the gate's 120% band, and wide-noop — the shape this search dominates — is the one that moved forward. The recorded baseline is left alone: nothing here justifies moving it.\n\nGATES. `scripts/check-release.sh` exits 0. Tests 882/0 on the release gate's own `cargo test --all-targets --no-fail-fast` line and 1,196/0 for `cargo test --workspace --all-targets`; conformance 354 runs, 326 identical / 28 differing normalised, 276 raw; equivalence 274 makefiles, 276 graphs, 8 respellings, 56 make-rejected, 0 disagreements; upstream 18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 20 interface; port ledger 170; Ninja 425/425 + 10 + 92 + 3 with the persistence round trip and log content. Not one moved: the corpus reaches depth 2 and never met the cap, which is why removing it moves nothing there and everything on the shapes above.\n\nTWO CORRECTIONS TO THE BRIEF. (1) The brief said no invocation on this checkout produces \"1,765 tests\". One does: summing every `test result` line in a full `scripts/check-release.sh` log gives exactly 1,765/0 — 882 from the gate's own `cargo test` step and 883 from the test binaries the later gates run. The 882 figure is the gate's own line and both are right. (2) The brief asked whether `rules_in_use` alone terminates every search, and it does — but \"terminates\" is not the whole question. It terminates on every shape tried, including the ones GNU cannot survive, and it leaves one catalogue shape exponential that GNU answers in constant time for a reason that has nothing to do with either guard. A cap is not the fix for that; materialising the chain in one search is, and that is filed separately.\n\nTWO PROCESS FAULTS OF MY OWN, recorded because both nearly produced false results. A binary was built from HEAD's source into `target/release/ronin` to make a \"before\" comparator and the working-tree binary was not rebuilt afterwards, so a block of scaling measurements labelled \"after\" were taken with the \"before\" binary — caught because a shape the fixed binary demonstrably builds was reported as refused; every affected measurement was re-taken with binaries kept at separate paths. And a `timeout 120` on one of the pathological shapes was orphaned when the outer tool call timed out first, leaving a `make` burning a core for 58 minutes across several measurement blocks and the first release-gate run; it was found, killed, and every number it could have touched re-measured on a quiet host."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T08:20:23.293203433Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id the-chain-search-answers-a-boolean-and-build-plan-derives-it-again
        title "Keep the chain the search found instead of deriving it again"
        kind @fix
        deps (an-eight-link-implicit-chain-is-refused-where-gnu-make-builds-it)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-28T11:01:19.569967927Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T11:26:01.932983307Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "DIAGNOSIS HOLDS, WITH TWO CORRECTIONS THAT CHANGED THE FIX. The filing said `implicit_chain_exists` answers a boolean and `build_plan` searches again \"at every level, with a different `rules_in_use` set each time\". Counted rather than assumed: on the ten-pair catalogue `build_plan` runs FIVE times for the whole run and `pick_pattern_rule` eight, while the chain search runs 1,103 times. So the re-search is not repeated per level — it happens once per materialised intermediate — and repetition is not what costs. What costs is that the re-search runs with a DIFFERENT set of rules marked in use. Traced: the first search proves `out.g0.a` with `%.a: %.g0.a` marked, so `%.g0.a: %.a` is its first candidate and succeeds; `build_plan(out.g0.a)` searches again with `%.g0.a: %.a` marked instead, which strikes out that same candidate and opens the `%.a: %.gJ.a` branches — `out.g0.g1.a`, `out.g0.g1.g2.a`, an exponential family of invented names the first search never proposed. The second correction is that materialising the road as the search stood was NOT SAFE, and that is the whole of why this is a bigger change than the filing implies."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T11:26:35.635243413Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "THE ROAD THE SEARCH WALKED WAS NOT THE ROAD TO KEEP, AND KEEPING IT AS IT STOOD WOULD HAVE BEEN A WRONG ANSWER. `implicit_chain_exists` ran ONE loop over its candidates in which each was allowed to invent its prerequisites as it was reached. `pattern_search` runs its rule list twice — `for (intermed_ok = 0; intermed_ok < 2; ++intermed_ok)` — so every rule is offered the chance to be satisfied by what is already on disk before ANY rule may invent. While the answer was a boolean the difference was invisible, because both orders answer yes. It is not invisible in a road.\n\nThe shape that shows it: `%.a: %.b`, `%.b: %.d`, `%.d: %.a`, `%.b: %.c`, with `out.c` on disk. GNU Make and Ronin at HEAD both copy `out.c` to `out.b` and `out.b` to `out.a`. The merged pass settles `out.b` on `%.b: %.d` instead, reaching `out.d` by walking back to `out.a` and taking it on trust. That road was invisible at HEAD because `build_plan` threw it away and re-derived a different one.\n\nMEASURED, NOT REASONED. A binary was built with the road kept and the pass left merged. On that shape it drops `Circular out.d <- out.a`, runs `cp  out.d` with one operand and fails the build, where GNU Make exits 0. So the two-pass restructure is a prerequisite of materialising, not an improvement bundled with it, and both halves land together or neither does."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T11:30:20.891256997Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "EVIDENCE THAT THE ROAD KEPT IS THE ROAD GNU MAKE TAKES, beyond the corpus. 1,500 random pattern-rule catalogues were driven through both tools and compared on the recipes that ran, the exit status and the files left: two to seven rules over an eight-suffix alphabet, a quarter of them terminal, grouped or prerequisite-less, with a random subset of the suffixes seeded on disk at a fixed mtime and `out.a` as the goal. Twenty of the 1,500 differ from GNU Make, and the SAME twenty differ before and after this change — case for case, on every one of six seeds. Nothing here moves an answer.\n\nThe twenty are one pre-existing shape and are not this node's: a multi-target pattern rule whose prerequisite pattern is also one of its own target patterns, `%.e %.a: %.e`, with `out.e` on disk. GNU matches `%.a` against `out.a`, reads the prerequisite as `out.e`, finds it and runs the recipe with `$<` set. Ronin matches the same rule and leaves `$<` empty, so the recipe copies nothing and the build fails. Present identically at HEAD; filed here because the sweep found it, not fixed here.\n\nA first version of the sweep also reported a divergence that was its own fault: it seeded `out.a` beside `out.f` and then asked whether `out.a` was out of date, which is a filesystem timestamp race and not a search answer. The goal's own suffix is now never seeded and every seeded file is stamped to a fixed time."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T11:33:10.017631908Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE DIVERGENCE IS A WRONG ANSWER AND NOT ONLY A SLOW ONE, AND THE SWEEP MEASURED HOW OFTEN. The same random-catalogue sweep run over DEEPER catalogues — eight to twenty rules instead of two to seven, which is where a road has room to be more than one link — moves answers, in one direction only. Seven seeds, 200 cases each:\n\n  seed 11  14 differ before, 13 after   case 23 fixed\n  seed 12   8 before,  8 after          none move\n  seed 13  15 before, 12 after          77, 124, 195 fixed\n  seed 14  14 before, 12 after          37, 193 fixed\n  seed 15  14 before, 12 after          70, 109 fixed\n  seed 16  18 before, 16 after          10, 116 fixed\n  seed 17  17 before, 14 after          8, 65, 69 fixed\n\nThirteen cases stop disagreeing with GNU Make and NOT ONE starts. On the 1,500 shallow cases the differing sets are identical case for case.\n\nWhat the fixed cases look like, seed 11 case 23: eleven rules, `out.f` and `out.h` on disk, goal `out.a`. GNU builds the chain `T g<-h`, `P e<-g`, `P a<-e` and exits 0. HEAD settled `out.e` on `%.e: %.a` instead — the rule written above `%.e: %.g` — ran it with `$<` empty and failed the build. The road that proved `out.e` reachable was proved with `%.a: %.e` marked in use, which strikes `%.e: %.a` out; `build_plan` searched again without that mark, `%.e: %.a` was back in the list and first, and it won. That is the same mechanism as the exponential catalogue, showing itself as a wrong build rather than as a slow one.\n\nA first reading of this sweep reported seed 11 case 36 as a NEW divergence. It was not: the reporter printed only the first five differing cases, so dropping one earlier case shifted which five were shown. Every comparison here is over the full list of differing case ids."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T11:34:01.192233123Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE SAME SWEEP WITH THE BUILT-IN CATALOGUE LEFT IN, which is where the suffix-rule half of the search lives and where the one regression this change did produce was found. Two seeds, 150 deep catalogues each, run without `-r` so the ninety-two built-in pattern rules and the suffix map are candidates beside the makefile's own: seed 21 goes from 37 differing to 32, seed 22 from 45 to 39. Eleven more disagreements with GNU Make removed, none added. The baseline is high because these catalogues reach `cc`, `f77` and `tangle` on files holding the word \"seed\" and the comparison is over the exact recipe log; what matters is the direction, which is the same in all four families of the sweep.\n\nTotals across the whole sweep, before against after: 1,500 shallow catalogues under `-r`, identical differing sets; 1,400 deep catalogues under `-r`, thirteen disagreements removed and none added; 300 deep catalogues with the built-in catalogue, eleven removed and none added."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T13:23:00.969202848Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "WHAT CHANGED. `implicit_chain_exists` is now `implicit_chain_search` and settles on a road instead of reporting that one exists. A `SettledChain` — the rule, which of its target patterns matched or which stem a suffix rule left, the candidate's position and the relaxation it was proved under — is recorded per name in a `settled` map, installed by `can_pick_implicit_rule` and by the suffix branch of `pick_pattern_rule` at the moment the rule that wanted the name is the rule chosen, and applied by `pick_rule` through a new `settled_rule_for` instead of searching again. A name a chosen road already settled answers `intermediate_reachable` at once, which is what `df->is_target` does in GNU Make once the recursion's `int_file` has been handed to `enter_file`. The road is flat and outermost-first, so a name reached twice keeps the rule the outer search chose and a road that walks back on itself stops there.\n\nTHE SEARCH COUNT, which is the diagnosis measured rather than argued. Entries into the recursive search on the `%.a: %.gN.a` catalogue, GNU Make counted from `make -d` and Ronin from a counter:\n\n  pairs   GNU   before   after\n     10    62    1,103      59\n     22    86       —       83\n     60   162       —      159\n\nBefore does not finish at sixty pairs. After tracks GNU to within three at every size, and the three are the top-level `try_implicit_rule` calls, which reach `pick_pattern_rule` here and are not counted.\n\nWALL TIME on the same catalogue, quiet host, load 0.34 to 1.13:\n\n  pairs    GNU   before    after\n     10   0.01     0.01     0.01\n     14   0.01     0.09     0.01\n     18   0.01     1.42     0.01\n     20   0.01     5.91     0.01\n     22   0.01    24.11     0.01\n     30   0.01    >150      0.01\n     60   0.01    >150      0.01\n\nand, after only, 100 pairs 0.01 against GNU's 0.02, 200 pairs 0.03 against 0.03, 400 pairs 0.09 against 0.10. There is no crossover left: Ronin is at or below GNU Make at every size measured. Before, killed at sixty pairs after two minutes, had reached 1.4 GB of resident memory and was still growing — the search invents an exponential family of distinct names and `impossible` keeps every one of them.\n\nLONG CHAINS GOT FASTER, because `build_plan` no longer re-derives a link it was already handed: 300-link chain GNU 0.33 / before 0.93 / after 0.73; 1,000-link chain GNU 1.11 / before 16.87 / after 12.79. Twenty-one and twenty-four per cent off.\n\nTHE FRONT END COSTS ABOUT THREE PER CENT MORE, and that is the price of GNU Make's search order rather than of keeping the road. Nine interleaved `-j1` CPU-time samples per tool on a quiet host at load 0.34: wide, GNU 887 ms, before 689, after 709 — 0.777x of GNU becomes 0.799x; recursive, GNU 520 ms, before 469, after 483 — 0.902x becomes 0.929x. Ronin still beats GNU Make on both. The cost is the second candidate walk, which `pattern_search` also pays; it can be fused into one walk that remembers where each candidate stalled, and that is a separate change with its own validation, not one to fold in here.\n\n`scripts/check-make-performance.sh` inside the gate, nine interleaved samples at load 3.88 (its own earlier workloads decaying): wide-noop 0.78x against the recorded 0.77x, recursive-noop 6.17x against 5.76x, vim-noop 1.84x against 1.78x, zsh-incremental 1.59x against 1.56x. All four inside the gate's 120% band; the gate validates and exits 0. The recorded baseline is left alone — nothing here justifies moving it.\n\nGATES. `scripts/check-release.sh` exits 0. 882/0 on the gate's own `cargo test --all-targets --no-fail-fast` line and 1,765/0 summing every `test result` line in the full log; 1,196/0 for `cargo test --workspace --all-targets` run separately. Conformance 354 runs, 326 identical / 28 differing normalised, 276 raw. Equivalence 274 makefiles, 276 graphs, 8 respellings, 56 make-rejected, 0 disagreements. Upstream 18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 20 interface. Port ledger 170. Ninja 425/425 upstream + 10 differential + 92 build-outcome + 3 invocation-boundary, with the persistence round trip and log content. Not one figure moved.\n\nTHE EIGHTEEN ORACLE SHAPES the previous node drove are byte-identical to GNU Make before and after. Sixteen more were written for the road itself — suffix-rule chains, the built-in catalogue's own chain, a grouped link, second expansion, VPATH, a wildcard prerequisite, `.SECONDARY`/`.INTERMEDIATE`/`.PRECIOUS` over an invented name, two roads into one name, a terminal rule at the bottom, an intermediate that is also a goal, target- and pattern-specific variables on a settled name, a directory in the stem, and a written-down middle — and before and after agree with each other on all sixteen, including the two where both already differ from GNU.\n\nTWO CORPUS CASES ADDED, both recorded from GNU Make: `implicit-chain-prefers-what-is-already-there` locks the pass order, and `implicit-chain-through-mutually-chaining-rules` is twenty pairs of the pathological catalogue, 0.02 s now and minutes before.\n\nONE REGRESSION WAS FOUND AND FIXED BEFORE LANDING, by the corpus. `include-remade-by-a-multi-target-pattern-rule` failed: the replay's suffix-rule branch returned the rule without recording the name it reads as one the search invented, so `Makefile.p` and `Makefile.web` were not intermediate, their absence stopped being excused, and Ronin built a chain through `tangle` that GNU Make finds, materialises and then declines to build — `if (d_mtime == NONEXISTENT_MTIME && !d->file->intermediate)` in `update_file_1`. The suffix replay now does the same \"ought to exist\" reckoning the ordinary suffix path does."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T13:25:23.47631789Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id the-make-front-end-reads-one-makefile-at-a-time
        title "Read a recursive Make's sibling child Makefiles at the same time"
        kind @perf
        deps (the-chain-search-answers-a-boolean-and-build-plan-derives-it-again)
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-28T14:51:58.925715335Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T15:39:11.176908138Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "TWO THINGS THE DISPATCH'S DESIGN GOT WRONG, both found by checking it rather than by building on it, and both worth keeping because the next parallelism attempt will meet them again.\n\nFIRST, AND IT IS THE ACTUAL BLOCKER: the process working directory. The brief named three obstacles — the shared `&mut GraphSink`, the shared `&mut CompilationState`, and diagnostics ordering — and did not mention that `compile_unit` reads every unit inside `in_directory` (src/make.rs), which is a process-wide `chdir`. Kati resolves relative names against it everywhere that matters: `$(shell)` spawns with no `current_dir` of its own (fileutil.rs), `$(wildcard)` globs relative patterns, `$(abspath)`/`$(realpath)`/`CURDIR` read `current_dir()`, the find emulator takes its root from it, and — the expensive one — every existence test the implicit-rule search makes. There is a static `COMPILATION_DIRECTORY: RwLock<()>` at src/make.rs:67 held for the whole of `load_with_subninjas` precisely because of this, and `plan/decisions/session-owned-evaluation.md` defers concurrent evaluation in as many words: \"Evaluating two sessions concurrently is not a goal of this change, only evaluating them in one process.\" So the design as briefed could not have been built: two units reading at once would each have read against the other's directory.\n\nWhat unblocked it was `unshare(CLONE_FS)` on each worker, which gives the thread its own filesystem context. That is a per-thread working directory, so the existing `chdir` code is correct unchanged, and the process never moves. Proven directly by a test rather than assumed. It is Linux-only, which is why the pool declines to exist elsewhere.\n\nSECOND: per-child sub-sinks merged in source order — the shape the brief called obvious — are not available at any reasonable cost. `Node`, `Edge`, `Rule`, `Pool`, `Scope` and `Binding` are all arena indices into one `BuildGraph`, numbering is insertion order, insertion order is observable, and there is no merge, import or remap machinery anywhere in src/frontend.rs or src/graph.rs. A merge would have to renumber six id types and rewrite them inside `Node.generator`/`Node.uses`, `Edge.out`/`input`/`validation`/`dyndep`/`rule`/`pool`/`env`, the edge binding keys, `BuildGraph.defaults` and about fifteen side tables, one of them keyed by an `(EdgeId, NodeId)` pair. There are also two counters that would collide: kati's `rule{N}` restarts per unit and is kept apart only by the child scope, and the sink's own `.ronin_grouped_join/{n}` and `.ronin_recipe_stage/{n}` are sink-global. And `GraphSink::interned` is keyed by kati `Symbol`, which is a per-`Session` interner index — safe today only because `begin_subninja` clears the map, so two units emitting at once would silently alias symbols from different interners.\n\nWHAT THAT LEFT, and why it is the right cut rather than a consolation. Reading a unit and emitting it are separable, and `evaluate(session)` touches no graph at all. Sampling a `recursive` run put ~63% of stacks inside `kati::dep` alone, all of it under `evaluate`, against ~2% touching the sink. So the expensive half is exactly the half that can leave the thread, and the ordering-critical half — every sink write, every `CompilationState` mutation, every boundary decision — stays on one thread in the order it already ran. Determinism is then structural rather than argued: nothing about the graph can depend on scheduling because nothing about the graph is built off it.\n\nWHAT IS STILL SERIAL, for whoever takes the next one. Composition itself, which is the ceiling: `-j16` and `-j32` measure the same as `-j8` (2.8 cores busy). Going past it means parallel composition, which means the graph merge above. Also serial by choice: invocations within one recipe (each stages the next's compiler input), any unit whose recipes could stop the composition at a boundary, and reporting runs.\n\nTHE ONE BEHAVIOURAL EDGE, stated rather than buried. Resolving an invocation stats the child directory for a default Makefile, and reading a Makefile runs its `$(shell)` calls; both now happen earlier for later siblings. A sibling's `$(shell)` that writes another sibling's Makefile would therefore be raced. This needs `-j` above one to happen at all, and at `-j` above one GNU Make has several recursive children doing exactly the same thing concurrently. At `-j1`, and with no `-j` at all, there is no pool and nothing moves.\n\nMEASUREMENT, AND WHAT COULD NOT BE DONE. The host carried another project's fuzzing and builds for the whole dispatch at load 8-17, and `make_baseline` refuses above 4.0 by design (\"Wall time from a busy machine is not a measurement\"). Interleaved A/B/C sampling still separates the arms cleanly: `recursive` at `-j8` went 560 ms / 1.00 cores busy to 266 ms / 2.82 cores, `-j1` 553 to 556 ms, `wide` unmoved at 780 ms. The gate itself passes with `--max-load` raised deliberately, at `recursive-noop` 2.06-2.68x against a recorded 5.76x. benchmarks/make-baseline-v1.csv was NOT re-recorded: the absolute medians this host yields are inflated (GNU's own `wide-noop` measured 1467 ms against a recorded 858 ms), and writing those into the record would corrupt it while only appearing to tighten the gate. The re-record is the one deliverable left, and it wants a quiet host."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T17:23:45.422150205Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Where the composing thread stands after taking the read and the teardown off it, and what is left.\n\nTHE BRIEF'S RANKING WAS BACKWARDS, and the correction is worth more than the fix. Folding the composing thread's DWARF stacks and classifying each sample by the INNERMOST frame naming a phase (outermost-first files every nested compile_unit under compose_subninjas, which is how the first reading went wrong) gave: reading Makefiles 28%, freeing 17%, composing and resolving 17%, emitting 11%. Reading was the largest item, not freeing. And within the 17% that was freeing, only 5 points was the per-unit teardown the brief described; 12 points was the retained recipe sessions, freed in one go inside make::cli after the build had already answered — a place the brief did not look at.\n\nWHAT WAS DONE. (1) read_ahead dispatches the first recipe too, before the recipes behind it are resolved; the win is not that the worker is faster but that this thread has the remaining resolutions, disk freshness questions and wrapper staging to get on with while the worker reads. (2) parallel::Reaper, a joined thread, frees the dependency nodes and the session per unit. (3) parallel::Released frees the retained recipes on a thread nothing waits for, gated on more than one unit because an unconditional spawn cost a one-Makefile build 0.23 ms a run.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT, and it is a different shape of problem. The composing thread is now 20% of the run's samples (was 34%) and about 130 ms of CPU against a 192 ms wall — it is only two thirds BUSY, so a third of the wall is now the thread WAITING. What it waits on is the first recipe's read, once per unit that has recursive recipes: it dispatches and then has only that unit's remaining resolve/stage work to overlap, which is about a third of a read. Removing that wait needs the read dispatched a full read-duration before the composition reaches it, and it cannot be: a unit's children can only be resolved after that unit's own emission into the sink, and emission is in recipe order on one thread. So the next real step is either a cheaper read (DirectoryCache::certainly_absent, implicit_chain_search and PatternMatch::of dominate a worker) or more than one thread writing the sink — the latter blocked by typed-graph-arenas (arena indices are observable) and by GraphSink::interned being keyed on a per-Session symbol.\n\nRESIDUAL MAIN-THREAD ITEMS worth knowing about: compose/dispatch is now the largest at 34% of a much smaller thread, and Session::new / RuntimeState::new inside `resolve` are a real part of it — resolve could move to a worker, but it raises diagnostics and can refuse, and both have to land at the recipe's own position. std::hash::random::DefaultHasher::write is still ~2% of the composing thread (CompilationState's cache/units_read/completed_recipes are RandomState maps); left alone because their iteration order reaches Groundwork and I did not want to change map order in the same commit as a scheduling change.\n\nMEASUREMENT CONDITIONS. This host ran an LLVM build for most of the session; load went 3.6 to 168. The numbers in the commit are from a load 3.6-4.5 window with the before and after binaries interleaved run for run. On a saturated host the change measures WORSE than the baseline (more threads, no free cores) — do not judge it from a loaded window. benchmarks/make-baseline-v1.csv is still NOT re-recorded and still holds pre-parallel figures; make_baseline refuses above load 4.0 and the window never lasted. scripts/check-make-performance.sh was not run: it needs the vim and zsh trees that scripts/check-make-projects.sh builds, and target/make-performance holds only recursive and wide in this checkout. GNU Make on this host at -j8 is 87 ms, not the 150 the brief quoted.\n\nGATES RUN: lints, fmt, cargo check --all-targets, cargo doc -D warnings, cargo test --all-targets 887/0, cargo test --workspace --all-targets 1201/0, conformance 354/326/28/276, upstream 18/0/20, port ledger 170, Ninja 425/425 + 10 + 92 + 3, equivalence 24 runs all 274/276/8/56/0, standalone determinism 125 runs. check-release.sh not run end to end for the two performance stages above."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T19:15:50.483315056Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "WHAT `parallel::discard` ACTUALLY COSTS, because the brief this node was handed said 68-117 ms on the composing thread and 0.26-0.45 ms per unit, and asked for it to be fixed first. It is not a regression and nothing was changed there. Two independent measurements say so. (1) `size_of` of what is handed over: the `nodes` discard is a `Vec<NamedDepNode>`, 24 bytes; the session discard is `(Evaluator, CommandLayout)`, 2464 bytes. The proposed mechanism — \"a memcpy of the whole struct per unit\" — cannot cost 0.26 ms for 2.4 KB. (2) `perf record -F 4999 -e cpu-clock` over ten `-j8` runs, 46,146 samples, classified by innermost frame: `mpmc::Sender::send` fifteen samples and `box_new_uninit` one, all sixteen on the composing thread, out of 9728 composing-thread samples. 0.16% of that thread, 0.035% of the run, about 0.3 ms a run for both call sites and all 259 units. `parallel::discard` does not appear as a symbol at all. Note that the brief's own `-j1` figure of 0.37 ms is essentially identical to this: the `-j1` number was the honest one and the `-j8` number was the artefact. The likely mechanism for the artefact is that at `-j1` `discard` is `drop(value)` and its cost is attributed to its callees (`drop_in_place`, `free`), while at `-j8` its whole body inlines, so the two numbers were never measuring the same thing. THE REAPER EARNS ITS KEEP AND SHOULD STAY: it is 1.8-2.0% of the run, and 44% of its own samples are `free`/`_int_free_chunk`, all of which the composing thread used to pay.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY ON THE COMPOSING THREAD. Same profile, self time, share of the composing thread: malloc/free family 20.0%, mem{cpy,move,set,cmp} 8.7%, kernel page-fault and TLB work 5.1%, `kati::export::environment` 14.1% as a subtree, `Session::new` 15.5% as a subtree, `RuntimeState::new` 6.8%, SipHash 6.0%. Two of those were doing work no answer needed and are what this commit fixes: the child-environment walk collected the entire variable table into a `HashMap<Symbol, Var>` — an `Arc` clone, a hash, a probe and an `Arc` drop per rejected binding — to keep the handful of names that export, twice per unit; and `Symtab::new` allocated the 255 one-byte names twice each (a `Vec`, then the shared refcount its first clone promotes it to) and filed them in a map whose only two readers answer a one-byte name from its byte and return before they reach it.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT ON IT, ranked, for whoever takes the next one. `Session::new` and `RuntimeState::new` inside `resolve` are still the largest single item — a session per compilation unit, built on the composing thread. Moving `resolve` to a worker is the obvious next step and the previous note already flagged the obstacle: it raises diagnostics and it can refuse, and both have to land at the recipe's own position. Second: the sink-free PREFIX of `read_unit` — everything from `refused_makefiles` down to `evaluated_flag_variables`, which is the whole of `exported_environment` and `command_line_environment` — touches only `ev` and `nodes` and no `GraphSink` at all, so it could join `evaluate_unit` on the worker exactly as the read itself did. That is a genuine \"move it off the serial thread\" and it was not attempted here. Third: SipHash is still ~6% of the composing thread and the previous note's reason for leaving it (map iteration order reaches Groundwork) still stands.\n\nWHY WORKER CPU IS THE LOWER-LEVERAGE TARGET, which the brief had the other way round. The composing thread is serial and only two-thirds busy, and after this change it is 133 ms of CPU against a 196 ms wall at `-j8`. Cutting a millisecond there is worth roughly five times a millisecond cut on a worker. Worth having anyway, and measured: on the workers the biggest single leaf is `__memcmp_avx2_movbe` at 6.9%, of which 41% is `RuleTrie::get`'s `name.ends_with(&ent.suffix[1..])` — a `bcmp` call for a two-byte suffix, on a walk that scans every rule filed at every prefix level. A last-byte guard before the call is the obvious cheap fix and was not taken here. `Symbol::as_bytes` is the other one: it returns an OWNED `Bytes` and its doc comment says \"as borrowed from `names`\", which is false — 2.9% of worker stacks are inside it, all of them from the implicit-rule search, and `Symtab::name_bytes` already exists as the borrowing form.\n\nMEASUREMENT METHOD, since two nodes running have now been given a wrong ranking. Flat `perf record -F 4999 -e cpu-clock` with NO call graph is enough for the innermost-frame question and is far more reliable than a DWARF record: `perf script -F comm,ip,sym` gives one line per sample, and counting them per `comm` gives CPU milliseconds directly (samples / 4999). Cross-check any `perf report` percentage against that count — `perf report --comms=make` matches `ronin-make-read` and `ronin-make-free` too, because it substring-matches, and `--percentage=relative` on top of that produced numbers three times too large in this session before the counts caught it. Thread names are `make` for the composing thread, `ronin-make-read` for workers, `ronin-make-free` for the reaper.\n\nTHE BASELINE DEBT IS STILL NOT DISCHARGED, and the reason is now narrower than it was. `reference/make-projects/vim-9.2.0957/src/vim` and `zsh-5.9.2/Src/zsh` are both BUILT (2026-08-28), so the prerequisite `scripts/check-make-projects.sh` stage no longer has to run and the performance gate's workloads are all present. The only remaining blocker is host load, and `make_baseline` refuses above 4.0 by design. Load over this whole session was 7 to 182 — another project's LLVM and pkg builds early, another agent's Ninja conformance suite and clang builds late — and never once went below 4.0; a watcher armed for the entire session never fired. `benchmarks/make-baseline-v1.csv` still holds pre-parallel figures and `scripts/check-release.sh` is still unverified end to end. Whoever gets a quiet host should do that before anything else; everything needed for it is now on disk.\n\nMEASUREMENT CONDITIONS FOR THE NUMBERS IN THE COMMIT. The two binaries were kept at separate paths and run interleaved round for round, so host drift hits both arms equally. The `-j` matrix was taken at load 7.6, where GNU Make's own `-j8` measured 92 ms against the 87 ms a quiet host gives it — close enough that the absolutes are nearly right and the ratios are solid. `bash`'s `time` reports wall at 1 ms and CPU at 10 ms, so the CPU medians are coarse; the per-thread figures come from sample counts instead. Determinism: 25 consecutive runs of `scripts/check-make-equivalence.sh`, every one 274 makefiles / 276 graphs / 8 respellings / 56 make-rejected / 0 disagreements.\n\nONE THING THE GATE CAUGHT AND SHOULD HAVE. `kati/tests/no_globals.rs` refused the static byte table on the first run. It is the sanctioned exemption path and the table took it, registered in the test's expected set as well as in the source — 256 constant bytes hold no evaluation state, which is the line `session-owned-evaluation` draws. Worth saying because the commit had already been pushed when the gate ran; it was amended rather than followed up, so no commit in the branch fails its own tests."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T21:54:27.789256628Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "WHAT IS ACTUALLY ON THE COMPOSING THREAD, and the brief's ranking was inverted. The brief ranked (1) `Session::new`/`RuntimeState::new` inside `resolve`, (2) the sink-free prefix of `read_unit`, (3) descendant read-ahead. Measured — `perf -F 2999 --call-graph dwarf` over five `-j8` runs, 2216 samples on the composing thread, classified by INNERMOST frame naming a phase, and counting only that thread's own tid: `resolve` (the whole of `cli::subninja::compile`) 54.6%, of which the session is only 12.8 points; the rest is `resolve:OTHER` 19.0 (the environment and context clones and the allocator traffic inlined into `compile` itself), directory resolution 7.9, context/recipe-environment 6.0, `extend_compilation_key` 5.3, `named_makefiles` 1.7, `shell_words` 1.3, switch parsing 0.8. Then the read prefix 12.7, the build phase 6.5 (not compilation at all), `emit_build` 13.5 as three pieces (sink emission 6.4, other 4.9, `populate_ninja_node` 2.2), freshness/stat 4.4, the rest of `read_unit` 4.3, the read handoff 2.5.\n\nTHE RANKING IS INVERTED BECAUSE THE THREAD IS NOT UNIFORMLY BUSY, and this is the finding worth more than the fix. A unit's composition has exactly one place where the thread waits: after `read_ahead` has dispatched the unit's children, the thread asks for the first child's read and blocks. Work done BEFORE that point is hidden inside the wait and costs the wall nothing; work done AFTER it is one-for-one. `resolve` runs entirely before it — every one of the 54.6% — so moving `resolve` to a worker buys nothing on this workload today. The read prefix runs entirely after it, so all 12.7% of it converts. That is why this commit moved the second item and not the first.\n\nMEASURED, NOT MODELLED. Three trees of near-equal unit count and different shape, `-j8`, nine interleaved rounds each, medians, load ~25:\n  fan-out 6, depth 3, 259 units:    Ronin 192, GNU  99  →  0.741 ms/unit\n  fan-out 16, depth 2, 273 units:   Ronin 169, GNU 104  →  0.619 ms/unit\n  fan-out 258, depth 1, 259 units:  Ronin 160, GNU 191  →  0.618 ms/unit\nThe per-unit cost falls 16% from fan-out 6 to 16 and then stops. On the flat tree every read is dispatched in one burst, the composition never waits, and the run is spine-bound at 160 ms — where it beats GNU Make by 31 ms. So PERFECT READ-AHEAD IS WORTH ABOUT 32 MS OF THE 192, it is the largest single item left, and it is not enough on its own: reaching GNU's 92 needs the composing thread to shed `resolve` as well, and `resolve` only begins to pay once the wait it hides in has gone. Both, in that order.\n\nAND THE `-j16`/`-j32` PLATEAU IS NOT WHAT THE BRIEF SAYS IT IS. The brief reads it as \"the wall is the serial spine, full stop\". It is not: the number of reads in flight is bounded by the TREE'S FAN-OUT, not by `-j`, because `read_ahead` dispatches one unit's children and then the composition consumes them. At fan-out 6 the pool is never more than six deep however many workers it has, so raising `-j` adds workers with nothing to take. The metric workload is adversarial to the current design in a way the `wide` workload is not — two of eight workers are idle by construction.\n\nWHAT DESCENDANT READ-AHEAD ACTUALLY NEEDS, because the brief's premise for it is false. The brief says a child's identity — directory, command, MAKE, shell — is known from the parent's READ result before any emission. It is not. `sink::SubninjaInvocation` is minted in `GraphSink::declare_edge` out of the `SinkRule.subninjas` that kati fills in `NinjaGenerator::sink_node`, from `nn.commands[].recursive_make`; and `nn.commands` is produced by `CommandEvaluator::eval` inside `populate_ninja_node`. The identity is created by RECIPE EXPANSION, which runs `$(shell)` and must therefore happen exactly once. Reading it off the dep nodes early would be expanding twice.\n\nTHE OPENING IS REAL THOUGH, and it is in kati. `kati::ninja::emit_build` is already two phases: `populate_ninja_nodes(nodes)` takes NO SINK AT ALL, and `emit(sink)` is the only half that writes one. Split them and the worker can evaluate AND populate, learn its own children, and dispatch their reads before the composition has reached it — which is the whole of item 3. Cost, surveyed but not paid here: `NinjaGenerator<'a>` borrows `&mut Evaluator` through `CommandEvaluator`, so the split needs the generator's owned state (`nodes`, `done`, `rule_id`, `shell`, `used_envs`, `phony_aliases`, `deferred_recipes`) and the command evaluator's carry-over (`current_dep_node`, `found_new_inputs`, `recipe_shell`) lifted into one `Send` value, with `CommandEvaluator::{suspend,resume}` so the autocommand globals are registered once rather than twice. The HARD part is the eligibility guard: `reads_may_start_early` reads `PendingSubninja::{always_dirty, invocations.len(), preceding_rule, evaluation_inputs}`, every one of them post-emission, and re-deriving them from the populated nodes would be a second implementation of the same judgement — the kind that diverges. The way to avoid that is to move the subninja translation itself out of `sink_node` into the populate phase, so one computation feeds both the early dispatch and the emission. That is the shape of the next node, and it is a real kati refactor rather than a tweak.\n\nTWO MEASUREMENT TRAPS, one of them new. (1) `RealDiskInterface::stat_many` SPAWNS THREADS AND NEVER RENAMES THEM, so they carry the `make` comm and a comm-based count files them under the composing thread. At `-j8` they were 9.1 ms of the 126.8 ms such a count calls the composing thread — 7% overstated. Count by TID, not by comm; this is a second trap on top of the `perf report --comms=make` substring match the previous note documented. (2) A DWARF record truncated at 16384 bytes loses the outer frames: `ronin::main` appeared in only 160 of 2360 composing-thread samples, so INCLUSIVE counts for anything near the root are unusable. Only the innermost-marker classification survives truncation, which is the classification the brief asks for anyway.\n\nWHAT THIS COMMIT MEASURED. Per thread, both arms profiled in ONE window at load 14.5-14.9, six runs at 4999 Hz, by tid: composing thread 117.7 ms of CPU to 94.7, workers 620.6 to 598.9 (77.6 to 74.9 per core over eight), reaper 11.9 to 12.2. Against that window's minimum `-j8` wall the stall went 69 ms to 85 — the thread gave up 23 ms of work and got 16 ms of waiting back, because part of what it gave up sat before the wait rather than after it. Walls, two windows, forty paired rounds each, arms alternated round by round: at load 6.0-7.6, before 163/175 (min/median) against after 152/169, GNU 80/84, after faster in 27 of 40, median paired delta -6.5 ms; at load 14.7-16.4, before 187/221 against after 180/202, GNU 90/95, faster in 31 of 40, median paired delta -11.6 ms. `-j1`, fourteen paired rounds: before 460/522, after 444/527, GNU 526/594, faster in 6 of 14, median delta +2.3 ms — a coin flip, which is what the mechanism predicts because at `-j1` there is no pool and nothing moves.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT ON THE THREAD AFTER THIS, ranked by what it would actually be worth. Everything after the wait is one-for-one and totals about 33 ms: `emit_build` 20 (of which only `populate_ninja_node`'s share can leave, and only via the split above), freshness/stat 6, the rest of `read_unit` 4, the read handoff 3. Everything before the wait is free until the wait is gone, and that is `resolve`'s 65. So the order is: kill the wait (item 3, worth ~32 ms and needing the kati split), then `resolve` becomes worth its full 65, and only then is the anti-target list — `RuleTrie::get`'s `bcmp`, `Symbol::as_bytes` returning an owned `Bytes` against its own doc comment — worth taking. Note that the anti-target's stated reason is wrong in one respect: while the composing thread is BLOCKED on one specific worker's read, that worker's milliseconds are on the critical path at full weight, not at one eighth.\n\nGATES: lints, fmt, cargo check --workspace --all-targets, cargo test --all-targets --no-fail-fast 887/0, cargo test --workspace --all-targets 1201/0, conformance 354/326/28/276, upstream 18/0/20, port ledger 170, Ninja 425/425 + 10 + 92 + 3, equivalence 25 runs on the committed tree and 50 more on the same change before one private function moved between files — all 75 reading 274 makefiles / 276 graphs / 8 respellings / 56 make-rejected / 0 disagreements. `cargo doc -D warnings` fails on the same 14 private intra-doc links, all in kati, none added.\n\nTWO THINGS THE HOST DID. `user@1000.service` died twice mid-gate and took a whole gate run with it silently — the sandbox wrapper answers \"Failed to connect to user scope bus\" and every script under it produces no output, so a gate loop reports nothing rather than failing. Check `systemctl is-active user@1000.service` before trusting an empty gate log. And the session scratchpad under /tmp was DELETED mid-session by another Claude process's startup cleanup, taking two measurement binaries and a gate log with it; durable working state for this campaign belongs in samurai-lanes, which is where the rest of this node's artefacts went."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T22:01:46.087759203Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE BASELINE DEBT IS DISCHARGED. The host finally went quiet — one-minute load 0.74 — and `benchmarks/make-baseline-v1.csv` is re-recorded on a CLEAN tree at 551d574, all five workloads including the clean build, `ronin_dirty=false`. It had been holding pre-parallel figures since before the read pool existed.\n\nWHAT IT SAYS, medians over five samples with the two tools interleaved, `-j8`, and the whole of the recursive campaign's numbers finally measured on a machine that was not doing anything else:\n\n                        GNU 4.4.1   Ronin    ratio    was (pre-parallel)\n  wide-noop                 918      749     0.82x    858 / 659\n  recursive-noop             79      163     2.07x     79 / 456\n  vim-noop                   16       27     1.72x     16 /  28\n  zsh-incremental          1360     2078     1.53x   1356 / 2113\n  vim-clean-build         15185    15126     1.00x  14967 / 15105\n\nRECURSIVE-NOOP WENT 456 TO 163 MS across the parallel-read campaign, against a GNU number that has not moved (79 both times). That is the campaign's actual scoreboard and it had never been recorded — the row said 456 for four commits. The remaining gap is 84 ms and its shape is in the note above: about 32 ms of it is the composition waiting on reads it could have started earlier, and most of the rest is `resolve` on the composing thread, which only starts costing wall once that waiting is gone.\n\nTWO ROWS WORTH SAYING OUT LOUD. `wide-noop` is a win — 749 against 918, Ronin 18% FASTER than GNU Make on a four-thousand-target flat makefile — and `vim-clean-build` is a dead heat at 1.00x, which is the number that matters to somebody actually using this as their `make`, because a real build is dominated by the compiler and not by the tool starting it. The two Ronin loses on are both recursion-shaped: `recursive-noop` and `zsh-incremental`.\n\nA TRAP IN THE RECORDER, for whoever re-records next. `--clean-build` is not just \"slower and more thorough\": WITHOUT it the recorder writes a four-row baseline and silently drops `vim-clean-build`, because that workload is deliberately outside `GATED_WORKLOADS` and only measured under the flag. `--validate` still passes on the four, so nothing complains — the row is simply gone from the file. The first record of this session did exactly that and had to be thrown away. Record with `--clean-build`; validate without it."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-28T22:22:55.582610626Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE RELEASE GATE IS VERIFIED END TO END, and it took two runs plus one separate invocation to get there. It had been unverified for five commits.\n\nFIRST RUN, EXIT 101. `cargo test --all-targets` failed on three of `make_baseline`'s own tests with \"recorded Make baseline has an invalid header\", because the baseline re-record an hour earlier had pointed `make_baseline --output` at `benchmarks/make-baseline-v1.csv`. Those are two different files: `--output` writes the six-column measurement record with provenance comments, and the v1 CSV is the three-column reference the harness compiles in with `include_str!`. Fixed in 9d6eab9; the measurement itself was fine and is written up in `benchmarks/make-baseline-2026-08-29.md`. WORTH SAYING PLAINLY: the fast gates could not have caught this. `cargo test --all-targets` passed on the tree an hour before the file changed, and nothing between then and the push reads that file. The end-to-end release run is the only thing that would have, and it found it on the first pass it has had in five commits.\n\nSECOND RUN, EXIT 1, AND THE FAILURE IS THE GATE'S OWN SHAPE. Every stage passed — fmt, check, lints, `cargo doc -D warnings`, `cargo test --all-targets`, equivalence 274/276/8/56/0, `check-make-projects.sh` (built vim 9.2 in 17 s and zsh 5.9.2 with 36 modules in 28 s, both from their own Makefiles under Ronin at `-j8`), the port ledger, Ninja conformance 425/425 + 10 + 92 + 3, and the Ninja performance gate — and then the LAST stage refused: \"one-minute load average is still 9.30 after waiting 300 s\". The load it refused to measure on was its own. `scripts/check-performance.sh` runs immediately before it and saturates thirty-two cores, and a one-minute average of 9.3 does not decay through 4.0 inside `make_baseline`'s 300 s wait. Measured on the way down here: 9.32 at the refusal, 8.78 a minute later, 2.81 about fifteen minutes after that. So `check-release.sh` CANNOT pass in one invocation on this host, and the reason is structural rather than anything about the code under test.\n\nRun separately once the host was quiet, the stage passes: `scripts/check-make-performance.sh --warmups 1 --repetitions 9` at load 2.81, EXIT 0, every ratio inside the 120% tolerance — wide-noop 0.78x, recursive-noop 2.02x (GNU 80.7 against Ronin 163.0, which reproduces the recorded 78.7/162.8), vim-noop 1.91x, zsh-incremental 1.57x.\n\nNOT FIXED, AND DELIBERATELY LEFT TO THE OPERATOR. The obvious repairs are to lengthen `WAIT` in `make_baseline`, to put a quiescence step between the two performance gates, or to order them the other way round. All three are a judgement about how much wall clock a release pass may spend idling rather than a correctness question, and the source comment beside the constant shows the author already weighed exactly this case and thought 300 s was enough — it is enough on a host whose Ninja gate has fewer cores to saturate. Loosening a measurement gate's tolerance without being asked is the wrong kind of quiet. What the next node needs to know is only this: run `check-release.sh`, expect it to stop at the last stage, and run that stage on its own afterwards."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-29T09:17:22.782140579Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Chaining the read-ahead through the subtree: what it bought, and the brief's error.\n\nTHE WALLS. `recursive-noop` at `-j8`, interleaved run for run, GNU Make 4.4.1 and both Ronin arms in one window at one-minute load 6.35: GNU 82.2 ms, Ronin before 164.2 ms, Ronin after 118.7 ms. Medians of 11 samples each, rotating which tool goes first. At `-j1` in the same window: GNU 609.6, before 485.2, after 482.8 — no regression, which was the standing requirement. A second window at load 9.73 agreed on the delta: 91.8 / 190.5 / 131.9. The host carried another tenant's builds for the whole dispatch (load 6-27 the entire time; it touched 0.11 once, for about four minutes, before I had a before-binary to compare against), so these are paired deltas rather than absolutes. benchmarks/make-baseline-v1.csv is therefore STILL not re-recorded and still holds `recursive-noop,78.654,162.805`; make_baseline refuses above load 4.0 and the window never came. That remains the one deliverable outstanding, and it is a five-minute job on a quiet host — the gate will pass comfortably, since the recorded ratio is 2.07x and the measured one is now about 1.44x.\n\nTHE BRIEF'S ERROR, which it invited me to look for in the wrong place. It said \"a child's SubninjaInvocation is minted by CommandEvaluator::eval inside populate_ninja_node\". It is not. `eval` mints `Command::recursive_make` — the raw lifted invocation — and the `SubninjaInvocation` the composition resolves is minted in the SINK, in `GraphSink::declare_rule`, during the emission. That matters because it decides what a worker can know: after populating, a worker has the invocation's command text, the make path, the node's shell and the script's shell flags, and it has to build the composition's own key out of those four rather than read a `SubninjaInvocation` that does not exist yet. `PopulatedBuild::liftable_recursions` is where that reconstruction lives, and `adopted` matches on the four values rather than on position for exactly this reason: two lists built in two places.\n\nThe brief also predicted the likeliest error would be \"something in populate_ninja_node that is not safe to run before the parent's sink write\". Checked first, as instructed, and it is not that either — nothing in the population touches a sink, which is why the split works at all. What IS there, and the brief did not name it, is that `GraphSink` answers `FileEvaluation::Expansion` and `OutputEvaluation::Expansion`: a recipe the compiler must read at construction performs its `$(file ...)` and prints its `$(info ...)` while it is populated. Those effects move to the worker with the population. They are bounded — under `RecipeExpansion::Launch` the only recipes read at construction are the ones whose text the graph's shape depends on — and what they SAY still lands in recipe order, because the worker's collected descriptor is drained where the composition reaches the child. What they DO to the disk happens earlier, and that is the same edge reading a Makefile early already took.\n\nThe two smaller ones: `scripts/check-lints.sh` is clean, not \"14 pre-existing doc-link errors\" — `cargo doc --no-deps` is clean too. And the load figures the brief quoted for the record (163/79 at load 0.74) are a window this host did not offer again.\n\nWHAT WAS DONE, in the three steps the brief set. (1) kati c5b8e8e splits `emit_build` into `populate_build` + `emit_populated` across a `PopulatedBuild` value, with `BuildEvaluation` carrying the sink's five evaluation answers to the half that cannot see the sink and `CommandEvaluator::resumed` handing the autocommand cells back rather than redefining them. (2) `prepare_read` runs the population on the worker, so `Prepared` now carries a populated build instead of dependency nodes, and the nodes are freed on the thread that built them rather than by the reaper. (3) `chained_reads` is the new thing: a worker that finishes a unit resolves the invocations that unit names and starts their reads, so `Session::new`/`RuntimeState::new` left the composing thread with them. Steps 2 and 3 are one commit because 2 buys nothing alone — the population is cheap on this workload, since every leaf recipe defers — and its whole value is that it tells the worker what to read next.\n\nMECHANISM, by TID at 999 Hz (perf's own overhead is in the wall, so read the CPU columns): composing thread 140.1 ms → 108.1 ms. The eight workers' CPU went from 105-128 samples each to 104-110 — the same total work, spread evenly, which is the signature of reads no longer waiting to be handed out. The reaper's own thread went 45 ms → 26 ms.\n\nWHAT IS STILL SERIAL, and it is now the whole story. The composing thread does the emission and the composition for all 259 units and nothing else. Its CPU is the floor: at ~108 ms measured under load, against workers at ~107 ms/core, the two are balanced for the first time — so the next honest step is not more read parallelism but a cheaper or parallel EMISSION, and parallel emission is blocked by typed-graph-arenas exactly as the previous note said. The root unit is still read and resolved on the composing thread, which is 1 read and 6 resolutions out of 259 and 258.\n\nTHE PROPERTY GATE. `one_graph_however_many_threads_read_it` now asserts both halves: all twenty reads per parallel run are started exactly once, AND sixteen of them are started by a worker rather than by the composition. A run where chaining silently stopped firing would fail on the second number rather than merely get slower. Run 60 times over this dispatch (600 compilations at -j1/-j8/-j16), no failure.\n\nGATES: equivalence 35 runs, every one 274/276/8/56/0 disagreements. cargo test --all-targets 887/0; cargo test --workspace --all-targets 1201/0; conformance 354/326/28/276; upstream 18/0/20; port ledger 170; Ninja 425/425 + 10 + 92 + 3; check-lints clean; cargo doc --no-deps clean. check-release.sh not run end to end — its two performance stages want the vim and zsh trees and a quiet host, and neither was available.\n\nONE HAZARD WORTH RECORDING for whoever instruments this next. An `eprintln!` from a worker deadlocks the whole run, hard and every time: the composition holds Rust's stderr lock for the length of the compilation, so a worker that prints blocks forever and the composing thread then waits on an answer that never comes. It cost an hour to find and it looked exactly like a bug in the chaining. Instrument with an atomic counter, never with a print."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-29T09:48:42.252576163Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "The quiet-host window arrived after the commit. Clean numbers, and one correction to the mechanism I reported from the loaded ones.\n\nTHE WALLS, `recursive-noop`, interleaved run for run, all three arms in one window. `-j8`: GNU Make 4.4.1 **78.2 ms**, Ronin before **151.5 ms**, Ronin after **104.2 ms**. `-j1`: GNU 526.2, before 450.1, after 417.7. Medians of 11 and 7 samples, rotating which tool goes first.\n\nThe window is trustworthy on GNU's own evidence rather than on the load average: GNU measured 78.2 ms with min 77.8 and max 79.7 — a spread of 1.9 ms — against the 78.654 ms in benchmarks/make-baseline-v1.csv. The one-minute average read 6.5 while this ran, but it was decaying from an earlier tenant's burst and the run queue was empty (nothing above 5% CPU); the load average lags, GNU does not. Ronin/GNU on this workload is now **1.33x**, from 1.94x in the same window and 2.07x in the recorded baseline.\n\nTHE CORRECTION, and it is the brief's second error. The brief modelled the composing thread as \"CPU 94.7 ms + stall 85 ms ~ wall 163\" and concluded that moving `resolve` off it \"buys zero while the spine's only block is the first-child read\", because resolution runs before that block. Measured by TID on a quiet host, the before arm's composing thread is **137.1 ms of CPU against a 151.5 ms wall** — it is 90% BUSY, not a third idle. There was no 85 ms of stall to hide the resolution in. So moving population and resolution off it paid immediately and paid nearly one-for-one: spine CPU 137.1 -> 79.1 ms, wall 151.5 -> 104.2 ms. The brief's own decomposition does not add up either (94.7 + 85 = 180, not 163), which is the tell I should have followed sooner instead of sequencing the work around it.\n\nTHE REST OF THE MECHANISM, same window, 999 Hz, counted strictly by TID. The eight read workers' per-core share 95.6 -> 78.4 ms, and their spread tightened from 90-104 samples to 76-81 — the same work, evenly fed. Total process CPU **938 -> 764 ms**, which is 174 ms LESS work overall, not merely relocated: the dependency nodes and the expansion's allocations are now freed on the thread that allocated them instead of crossing to the composing thread and its reaper, and a thread-caching allocator charges much less for that. The reaper's own thread went 36 -> 58 ms because it now carries only the retained recipe sessions.\n\nWHERE THE FLOOR IS NOW. Composing thread 79.1 ms CPU, workers 78.4 ms/core: the two are balanced, so neither is the single thing to attack next. The composing thread's 79 ms is emission plus composition for 259 units and essentially nothing else — the reads and resolutions have left it. Making that smaller means a cheaper or a parallel emission, and parallel emission is blocked by typed-graph-arenas (arena indices are observable and a merge would renumber six id types) exactly as the previous note recorded.\n\nTHE BASELINE RE-RECORD IS BLOCKED FOR A SECOND REASON, which is worth stating precisely because \"wants a quiet host\" has been the standing explanation for three dispatches. `target/make-projects` is EMPTY in this checkout: the vim and zsh trees `scripts/check-make-projects.sh` fetches, configures and builds are not there, and `make_baseline --validate` refuses a run missing any of its four gated workloads. So even in this quiet window the record could not be re-taken. Whoever takes it next needs check-make-projects.sh to run first, and that build is itself what saturates the host — which is why the same gate's own documentation names a second invocation for the last stage as the accepted workaround."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-29T10:52:22.364298462Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Cutting both sides of the balance: what moved, and the three numbers I got wrong on the way.\n\nTHE WALLS. `recursive-noop` at `-j8`, GNU Make 4.4.1 and both Ronin arms interleaved run for run with rotating order, medians of 11 to 15 samples. The host carried another tenant's cargo builds for this entire dispatch — one-minute load 5 to 38, never once below 4.0 — so what is solid here is the PAIRED RATIO, measured five times across loads 5.1 to 23.1, and the absolutes belong to their window:\n\n| load | GNU | Ronin before | Ronin after | before | after |\n| 6.32 | 78.9 | 115.2 | 104.8 (mid-change) | 1.46x | 1.33x |\n| 5.06 | 85.8 | 120.7 | 109.0 | 1.41x | 1.27x |\n| 7.09 | 86.6 | 125.3 | 110.9 | 1.45x | 1.28x |\n| 16.43 | 88.7 | 121.1 | 111.9 | 1.37x | 1.26x |\n| 23.11 | 94.6 | 138.3 | 119.1 | 1.46x | 1.26x |\n\nThe last row is the committed tree. Ronin was 1.44x GNU Make and is 1.26x: 12.5% off the wall. At the load the previous node recorded its 82.2/118.7 pair (6.35), that ratio puts the after-wall near 104 ms, and I say NEAR because no window below load 5 came and I will not report a scaled number as a measured one.\n\n`-j1` is faster too, which was the standing requirement rather than a target: 494.9 ms to 428.6 ms in the same window, against GNU's 667.1. At `-j16`, 96.4 to 89.0.\n\nBOTH SIDES, BY TID, paired and interleaved on one host (`perf record -F 4999 -e cpu-clock`, no call graph, samples counted per TID — thread names `make`, `ronin-make-read`, `ronin-make-free`, and the three unrenamed `stat_many` threads that also carry the `make` comm counted separately):\n\n| | before | after |\n| composing thread | 87.6 ms | 77.6 ms (-11.5%) |\n| workers, per core | 108.6 ms | 96.5 ms (-11.2%) |\n| `stat_many` helpers | 10.9 ms | 10.7 ms |\n| reaper | 11.5 ms | 20.4 ms (it frees more now, by design) |\n| total CPU | 979 ms | 879 ms (-10.2%) |\n\nThat block was taken at load 36 and both arms are inflated by it; an earlier paired block at load 7 gave 59.8 -> 55.0 and 72.0 -> 62.2 for an intermediate tree. Two rotations of the load-36 block agreed to 1.7 ms.\n\nWORKER SIDE — four cuts, all in kati 9d26cd6. `RuleTrie::get` compared a pattern's tail with `ends_with`, which reaches `bcmp` through a call for what is almost always a two- or three-byte file extension; comparing last byte first took `__memcmp_avx2_movbe` from 6.77% of worker samples to 4.29%. `PatternMatch::of` cut two `Bytes` out of the name BEFORE asking whether the pattern matched, so every one of ~1.6 million rejected candidates paid a shared-refcount pair it never read. `ordered_candidates` reads nothing of that match but the one number it ranks by, so `PatternMatch::stem_length` measures the two halves without building either — which also stops the first of them promoting the interned name's buffer to a shared one. And the pattern itself is now read through `Symtab::name_bytes` rather than taken out as a `Bytes`, with `Pattern::parse_borrowed`/`parsed_matches` doing that parse against bytes the caller keeps; only a pattern that escapes its `%` needs the text rewritten, and only that one is given somewhere to put it. `bytes::shared_drop` 3.29% -> 1.58%, `shared_clone` 1.99% -> 1.38%. Two allocations went with them: `candidate_pool` borrows its buffer from the builder and gives it back, and the de-duplication set is not built when the second walk added nothing to de-duplicate.\n\nSPINE SIDE — four cuts, in 98d867c. The biggest was not on anybody's list: a started read arrives holding a `CompilationContext` and the composition CLONED it and then freed the original, both on the serial thread, once per unit. Dropping a `ChildRead` was 11.95% of the composing thread's samples and the clone another 2.2%; `collect` now hands the context back and gives the read's own leavings to the reaper. `freshness_disk` stood up a disk interface per unit, each one a `realpath` walk plus a `stat` plus an `openat` of a build root that is the same directory for the whole compilation — 5.12%, now held in the compilation state. `edge_dirty_with` sized a `RuntimeState` to the WHOLE graph to read one edge's ancestors, once per unit, against a graph that grew with every unit before it — quadratic in the tree; the sink keeps one and `reset_asked` restores it to exactly what `RuntimeState::new` would have made. Composing-thread leaves: allocator family 25.2% -> 21.8%, `RuntimeState::new` 4.35% -> `RuntimeState::reset` 2.94%.\n\nWHAT THE BRIEF GOT WRONG, and the largest one is its central claim.\n\n(1) \"Composing-thread CPU ~108 ms, worker per-core share ~107 ms — balanced ... the wall now falls only if BOTH sides shrink; cutting either alone re-binds the wall to the other at ~108.\" The two numbers are equal only at one load. Measured on this host at load 6.3 the before-tree ran a 67 ms composing thread and 78 ms/core of workers against a 125 ms wall — so NEITHER side was the wall, and about 45 ms of it was neither. The decisive evidence is that the same before-binary at `-j16` measured 96.4 ms against 115-125 at `-j8`: a run bound by a serial composing thread cannot get faster when you add workers. What binds the `-j8` wall on this workload is the read dependency chain plus worker throughput, and the composing thread is under it, not at it. Cutting both was still right; the reason given for it was not.\n\n(2) \"`Symbol::as_bytes` ... 2.9% of worker stacks when measured.\" That is an inclusive call-graph figure. `as_bytes` is fully inlined and has no self time at all; what is measurable is `bytes::shared_clone`/`shared_drop`, 6.3% of worker samples across ALL sources, of which the implicit search's pattern lookup is one. The doc comment was as false as the brief said and now says what the function does.\n\n(3) \"Allocator traffic ~11% of worker samples; `bytes` atomic refcount clone/drop ~5%.\" Understated: 17.1% and 6.3% respectively on the before-tree, and the composing thread's allocator share was 25.2%. The \"41% of the workers' memcmp\" for `RuleTrie::get` was right — removing it took 37% of the memcmp away.\n\n(4) The brief lists `adopted` matching of lifted reads among the spine's costs. It has no samples at all, at any frequency I recorded. `dependency_ordered` has 1.1% of `compile_unit`.\n\nERRATA ON MY OWN TWO COMMIT MESSAGES, recorded here because the commits are pushed and the history is append-only. kati 9d26cd6 says memcmp falls \"from 6.7% of the workers' samples to 3.5%\": the 3.5% divided by the run's TOTAL sample count instead of the workers', and the correct paired figure is 6.77% -> 4.29%. Its `shared_drop` \"4.2% to 1.8%\" and `shared_clone` \"2.1% to 1.4%\" mix two windows the same way; the paired figures on the committed tree are 3.29% -> 1.58% and 1.99% -> 1.38%. And 98d867c's \"cloning a context another 3.0%\" is 3.0% of `compile_unit`'s samples, which is 2.2% of the composing thread. The directions and the conclusions are unchanged; the digits above are the ones to read.\n\nWHAT IS NEXT, ranked, with the audit already done for the top one. SipHash is now the largest single leaf on the composing thread at 7.13%, and it comes from exactly two places: `hash_one::<&Vec<u8>>` (62% of it) for the four cache-key maps — `CompilationState::cache`, `compiling`, `units_read`, `read_claims` — and `hash_one::<&RecursiveRecipe>` (35%) for `completed_recipes`/`finished_recipes`. The reason two previous nodes left it alone is that a hasher swap changes iteration order and that order could reach output. It does not here: of those five, the only iteration anywhere is `finished_recipes.iter().any(...)` at src/make.rs:691, whose boolean result is order-independent. Everything else is insert, get, remove or contains. `crate::htab::RapidHashMap` is the house form and is already used by `RuntimeState`. I did not take it because it landed after the commit was pushed and the host was at load 37, where I could not have measured whether it helped.\n\nAfter that, on the workers: `install_builtin_rules` is 14.2% and `recipe_lines` — parsing the built-in catalogue's recipes — is 30% of that, for recipes this workload never expands. Lazy recipe parsing is the shape, and `Rule::cmds.is_empty()` being consulted in the hot path is the thing to design around. And glibc's `sysmalloc`/`mprotect` churn is 9.6%+7.5% of worker samples — each worker's arena trims after a unit and re-grows for the next. `mallopt(M_TRIM_THRESHOLD, ...)` would stop it, and I deliberately did NOT do that: the shipped configuration is musl-static, where the knob is inert, so it would improve the number I report here and nothing that ships. Whoever wants that saving should take it as an allocator decision, not as a tuning call.\n\nTHE BASELINE AND RELEASE DEBT IS STILL NOT DISCHARGED, for the third dispatch running, and for the same reason: `make_baseline` refuses above one-minute load 4.0, and the host never reached it. Load over this dispatch ran 5 to 38 with another tenant's cargo builds running continuously; two armed watchers, one for 40 minutes and one for the rest of the session, never fired. `benchmarks/make-baseline-v1.csv` still holds `recursive-noop,78.654,162.805` and `scripts/check-release.sh` has not been run end to end here. Everything else in that gate WAS run individually and is green (see below); what is missing is `check-make-projects.sh`, the two performance stages, and the single invocation that chains them. The gate will pass comfortably: the recorded ratio is 2.07x and the measured one is now about 1.26x.\n\nGATES, with invocations. `scripts/sandboxed cargo test --all-targets --no-fail-fast` 887 passed / 0 failed / 2 ignored; `scripts/sandboxed cargo test --workspace --all-targets` 1201 / 0 / 2. Equivalence: 35 runs of `scripts/check-make-equivalence.sh`, 25 mid-change and 10 on the committed tree, every one 274 makefiles / 276 graphs / 8 respellings / 56 make-rejected / 0 disagreements. `one_graph_however_many_threads_read_it` 60 consecutive runs, 0 failures. Conformance 354 runs, 326 identical normalised / 28 differing, 276 raw. Upstream inventory 18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 20 interface. Port ledger 170 symbols. Ninja 425/425 upstream, 10 differential, 92 build-outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence round trip, log content. `cargo fmt --check`, `scripts/check-lints.sh`, `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` all clean. `nplan spec uncovered` and `nplan spec stale` clean; `nplan lint` 0 violations; `nplan audit` no proposals, on_ref.\n\nTWO THINGS WORTH KNOWING FOR NEXT TIME. The `eprintln!`-deadlocks-a-worker hazard is real and I did not have to learn it again — every measurement here is a sample count or an atomic, never a print. And the function-density gate refuses `src/make.rs` at 51 functions: extracting a helper out of `compile_unit` to satisfy `too_many_lines` immediately breaks it, and the way through is to put the helper where its subject lives (`ChildUnit::claim` belongs in `parallel`, beside `prepare_session`) rather than to bypass either gate.\n"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-29T12:12:53.178363997Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Cutting the composition's SipHash and the catalogue's eager recipe parse (ronin 8f17a87, kati 1bb4eb4).\n\nMEASUREMENT. Recursive workload (259 Makefiles, fanout 6, depth 3), -j8, interleaved with rotating order, separate tree per tool, all on disk under target/. Headline at load 10.05: GNU Make 4.4.1 101.7 ms, Ronin 121.0 ms, 1.190x (25 reps). Paired before/after across load points, after/before wall: 0.922 (load 12.7, 21 reps), 0.920 (13.7, 21), 0.942 (13.9, 21), 0.976 (13.9, 21), 0.955 (10.0, 31). Ronin/GNU went 1.26-1.35x before to 1.18-1.25x after. -j1 did not regress: before 433.9 ms, after 414.2 ms, GNU 684.3 ms (load 8.75, 7 reps).\n\nWHAT EACH BOUGHT. Isolated at load 21.4, 9 reps: hash alone 0.921x. At load 10.0, 31 reps: hash alone 0.993x, parse alone 0.962x. The split between the two is inside the noise on a host at load 10-38; the combined ~0.93 is the figure that survives repetition. Profile evidence is cleaner than wall: composing-thread hashing 6.74% -> 3.87% of that thread; install_builtin_rules 14.66% -> 10.10% of worker samples, with recipe_lines gone from 5.68% to absent.\n\nERRATA AGAINST THE BRIEF I WAS GIVEN.\n1. \"the only iteration over those five maps is finished_recipes.iter().any() at make.rs:691\" — REFUTED. There are five: make.rs:691, make.rs:696, cli.rs:1761, cli/remake.rs:1171, and cli/remake.rs:883 (a real `for (unit, journal) in take_units_read()`). All five are order-free, so the conclusion held, but the audit that produced that sentence was not exhaustive.\n2. \"install_builtin_rules at 14.2% of worker samples\" — holds, 14.66% at 9e86c5b.\n3. \"30% of it parsing recipe text\" — UNDERSTATED. recipe_lines is 5.68% of worker samples against install_builtin_rules' 14.66%, i.e. 38.7% of the installer.\n4. \"this workload never expands them\" — CONFIRMED and stronger than stated: a counter on the lazy path reports 14,763 built-in recipes deferred per run and 0 forced, at both -j1 and -j8.\n5. \"SipHash ... its largest leaf, 7.13%\" — it is the largest leaf, at 6.05% of composing-thread samples at 9e86c5b. The composing thread is 9.57% of all samples, so this leaf is 0.58% of process CPU; it still bought measurable wall because composition is serial and on the critical path.\n6. Standing debt \"target/make-projects absent\" — WRONG PATH and ALREADY CLEAR. The trees live at reference/make-projects and both are built (vim src/vim, zsh Src/zsh). target/make-projects-bin is the symlink dir the brief was thinking of.\n7. Standing debt \"make-baseline-v1.csv needs re-recording\" — ALREADY DONE before this node, at 551d574 by dd85183 + 9d6eab9, on 2026-08-29 at load 0.74, in the three-column format the harness include_str!s.\n\nBASELINE NOT RE-RECORDED, AND WHY. The recorded CSV is now three commits stale and its recursive-noop row (2.07x) badly understates the tool: measured 1.21x. Re-recording needs load < 4.0; the host sat at 8-38 for this whole node and make_baseline waited out its full 300 s patience at 12.06 and gave up at 13.35. Not forced with --max-load, because a recorded baseline taken on a busy host is the thing the gate exists to prevent. The gate itself is green against the stale CSV with margin: wide-noop 0.51x, recursive-noop 1.21x, vim-noop 1.71x, zsh-incremental 1.59x, all inside the 1.20 tolerance on the recorded ratio.\n\nSAFETY WORK THE CHANGE NEEDED. Deferring the parse defers the interning parse_expr does, and $(.VARIABLES) is every interned name in intern order (var.rs:977 <- session.rs:489) — the one channel where a Symbol's ordinal reaches output. Symbol has no Ord, so no sort anywhere keys on an id; the exposure is exactly six names the catalogue's recipes refer to and nothing else in session startup interns. They are now interned where the parse used to intern them, and builtin_rules::recipes_intern_no_name_outside_the_catalogue_list parses every table recipe and fails if the list drifts from the tables. Also moved: --enable_stat_log's \"*kati*: N symbols\" line will read lower, and a parse warning from a table defect would now surface at first expansion rather than at install.\n\nGATES. cargo test --all-targets --no-fail-fast 887/0 in 24 invocations; cargo test --workspace --all-targets 1203/0 in 30 invocations (1201 + the 2 new builtin_rules tests); make conformance 354/326/28/276; upstream 18/0/20; port ledger 170; Ninja 425/425 + 10 + 92 + 3; check-lints.sh and cargo doc --no-deps clean. Make equivalence 274/276/8/56/0 over 40 runs of the gate; one_graph_however_many_threads_read_it 60/60 in bulk (it is #[ignore]d by default — it must be driven with --ignored --exact, which the release gate does)."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-29T12:56:32.605064054Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "By-TID profile of the recursive workload at -j8, and the ranking it supports. 2026-08-29, host load 4.4-9.2 throughout; no quiet window came, so the baseline was NOT re-recorded (make_baseline refuses above 4.00).\n\nMETHOD. perf_event_max_sample_rate is 1000 on this host, as warned. Frequency mode (-F 249, -F 999) throttled 8-15% of events because short-lived threads burst during period adjustment — 1200 thread starts over 100 runs. A FIXED period (-e cycles:u -c 5000000) over 200 runs gave 69,852 samples with ZERO throttle events. Total sampled cycles calibrate to 582 ms CPU/run against bash `time`'s 581 ms, so the sampling is trustworthy. Threads are already named, so `perf script -F comm` aggregates by role without TID bookkeeping. Profiling binary: release flags plus `-C force-frame-pointers=yes -C debuginfo=1` AND `-C target-feature=+crt-static`, because a bare RUSTFLAGS overrides .cargo/config.toml and silently drops the static link — it measured 102 ms against the shipped binary's 108, close enough to use.\n\nWHERE THE CPU IS, each percentage against its own thread's samples:\n- workers (`ronin-make-read`, 8 of them): 62,566 samples = 89.6% of the run, 521 ms CPU\n- composing thread (`make`): 5,384 = 7.71%, 44.9 ms CPU\n- reaper (`ronin-make-free`): 1,896 = 2.71%, 15.8 ms\n\nWHERE THE WALL IS, and this is the finding the ranking turns on. Binning each run into 20 slices and averaging over 200 runs, the run is TWO NON-OVERLAPPING PHASES:\n- 0-68 ms: workers pinned at 8.4 cores with all 8 distinct workers active every bin; composing thread at 0.13 cores, i.e. blocked.\n- 73-105 ms: workers at 0.00 cores; composing thread alone at ~0.95 cores.\nPhase A wall = worker_cpu/8 = 521/8 = 65 ms, observed 68. Phase B is 30.5 ms of composer CPU at 1:1. So a worker-side cut divides by eight and a composer-side cut does not. That divisor is the whole of why the ranking below looks the way it does.\n\nThe tail is NOT the Ninja build phase: classifying composer samples by module puts only 4.63 ms/run in build/graph/runtime/deps code. The tail is composition itself plus the allocator.\n\nRANKING BY EXPECTED WALL-MS, with the arithmetic:\n1. Environment-size work — 0.137 ms of wall per environment variable per run, measured end to end by padding the environment (50 vars 106 ms, +50 109, +150 129, +300 147; slope is linear). At this host's 50 variables that is ~7 ms of the 106 ms wall. THIS IS AN END-TO-END WALL MEASUREMENT, not a sample.\n2. Composer allocator traffic — 37.4% of the 30.5 ms serial tail = 11.4 ms of wall at 1:1 (malloc_consolidate 2.95, _int_malloc 2.42, free 1.87, unlink_chunk 1.65, then malloc/realloc/free_chunk).\n3. Worker implicit-rule search — implicit_chain_search is 50.55% INCLUSIVE of worker samples, ordered_candidates 24.95%, make_dep 78.37%. 0.5055 x 521 / 8 = 33 ms of wall sits behind it; a 20% cut is 6.6 ms.\n4. Worker allocator cluster — 20.25% of worker samples x 521 / 8 = 13.2 ms if wholly removed.\n5. Bytes refcounting — 5.85% of worker samples = 3.8 ms.\n\nWHAT THIS CONTRADICTS, all with the correcting number:\n- \"install_builtin_rules is 10.10% of worker samples\" — it is 1.78% SELF and 8.53% INCLUSIVE. The 10.10% was an inclusive figure read as a self figure. Worth 1.2 ms of wall at the divisor, not 10.\n- \"the spine's hashing is now wide_hash at 2.68%\" — 0.54% of worker samples = 0.35 ms of wall. On the composer it is 5.39% of the serial tail = 1.64 ms, which is the only place it is worth naming.\n- \"two workers idle by construction on fanout-6 trees\" — FALSE. All 8 workers are active in every time bin from 10 ms to 57 ms; mean distinct active workers is 8.00. Nothing is idle by construction.\n- \"-j16/-j32 measure the same as -j8, composition is the ceiling\" (the 2026-08-28 note) — stale. -j1 377, -j2 247, -j4 151, -j8 119, -j16 93, -j32 100. -j16 is a real 1.28x over -j8.\n- Parallel overhead is NOT +50%. CPU-ms says 388 at -j1 against 582 at -j8, but a single core turbos to ~3.85 GHz while eight run at ~2.98. In CYCLES — the honest unit — it is 1.49e9 against 1.75e9, i.e. +17%. Any future comparison across -j must be in cycles.\n- Depth is not the problem. A flat 216-leaf tree and the fanout-6 depth-3 tree cost the SAME per unit (0.394 vs 0.405 ms) at the same 5.5 cores, so the composer's starvation in phase A is not a BFS-dispatch-vs-DFS-composition ordering artifact. The flat tree's composer overlaps at 0.5 cores and still leaves a 25 ms tail.\n\nWHAT THE CUT BOUGHT. Sharing the environment instead of deep-copying it per unit: CPU 570 -> 544 ms at -j8 paired, and 787 -> 739 with 300 extra variables, so the saving scales with the environment and the mechanism is confirmed. Wall 107 -> 106 ms, which is inside the noise — 26 ms of worker CPU divided by eight is 3 ms and this host will not resolve that. -j1 401 -> 399 against GNU 609.\n\nNotably the environment-size SLOPE was unchanged by that cut (11 ms before, 12 ms after). So the ~7 ms is not the vector copies at all — it is kati's per-session IMPORT of the environment into the variable table, 259 sessions x 50 variables. A differential profile with +300 variables confirms it: _int_free_chunk +2.74pp, _int_malloc +1.79pp, memcmp +1.31pp, parse_expr_impl_ext +0.73pp. read_invocation_state never appears as a frame because it is inlined, which is why sampling missed it and only the padding experiment found it.\n\nNEXT TARGETS, in order, with the arithmetic already done:\n(a) The per-session environment import, ~7 ms of wall. Every session parses all 50 environment values as Make expressions eagerly, though the comment at kati/src-rs/evaluate.rs already observes that parsing is not expanding. Deferring the PARSE while keeping the INTERN eager would preserve $(.VARIABLES) order exactly and is the shape the decisions permit (lazier is safe; earlier is not).\n(b) ordered_candidates, kati/src-rs/dep.rs:5107. It is the top allocator caller on the workers (20% of their allocator samples). candidate_pool already borrows a scratch buffer from candidate_scratch, but `matched` and the returned Vec still allocate per call, AND the chain walk is RECURSIVE — its own doc says a nested search \"finds the cell empty and allocates\", which is exactly the hot path. A small stack of scratch buffers rather than one cell would hit where the single cell misses.\n(c) The composer's serial tail, 30.5 ms at 1:1 and 37.4% allocator. Anything removed here is wall.\n\nMETHOD NOTES FOR THE NEXT NODE. DHAT cannot profile the shipped binary — a static link gives valgrind no malloc to intercept, and it reports 0 blocks with an empty PP table. Build a dynamic one for allocation counts. perf `fp` unwinding cannot attribute allocator samples at all (glibc has no frame pointers; 74% came back [unknown]); `--call-graph dwarf,16384` attributes them correctly but is ~23 KB per sample, so use a sparse period. And the padding experiment — hold the code fixed and vary an input the cost should scale with — found in one command what two sampling profiles had missed."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-29T14:40:07.65262979Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Why the composition waited, and what serving the reads in its own order was worth (ronin 158fecd).\n\nTHE WALLS. `recursive-noop` at `-j8`, one tree on disk, GNU Make 4.4.1 and both Ronin arms rotating rep for rep, 61 reps at one-minute load 13.4 to 15.5: GNU 107.0 ms, Ronin before 122.8, Ronin after 100.4. That is 1.147x GNU before and 0.938x after. Six alternating rounds of nine reps each, same tree, load 13 to 15, agree: GNU 118.3, before 135.3, after 115.9. An earlier window at load 11 to 14 gave GNU 97.2, before 115.9, after 96.6. The absolutes belong to their window; the paired ratio does not, and it is 0.82 to 0.86 after over before in every window measured.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, AND IT IS NOT WHAT THE BRIEF SAID. The reads are STARTED breadth-first and CONSUMED depth-first, and the queue served them first-come-first-served. That is the whole of it.\n\nA worker that finishes reading a unit resolves every recursive child that unit names and hands all of them to the queue at once, so what arrives is a breadth-first wave: the root's six children, then thirty-six, then two hundred and sixteen. The composition walks depth-first — a unit, then the whole subtree under its first recipe, then the subtree under its second — so the third unit it asks for is the leftmost leaf, which on a fan-out-six depth-three tree is dispatched behind about sixty other reads and read about sixteen milliseconds later than it is wanted.\n\nINSTRUMENTATION. Five atomic timestamps per read — dispatched, started on a worker, read finished, chained children resolved, and the two ends of the composition's wait in `ChildRead::collect` — written into a static table indexed by a dispatch sequence number and dumped to a file named by an environment variable when the pool is joined. Never a print from a worker; the composition holds the stderr lock and an `eprintln!` there deadlocks the compilation. Twenty runs, 258 reads each, the before-tree at `-j8`:\n\n- composing thread: 59.9 ms BLOCKED and 64.7 ms busy, of a 127.5 ms wall.\n- of that wait, 47.7% (29.0 ms, 3.7 units a run) was on a read NO WORKER HAD PICKED UP YET, and 51.6% (31.4 ms, 23.3 units) on one in flight. Only 0.7% was on a read already finished, which is what a wait should be.\n- reads dispatched and not yet started peaked at 198 a tenth of the way in and drained linearly for the rest of the run.\n- all eight workers were busy at 7.9 of 8 for 90% of the wall. Nothing was idle. The workers were reading the WRONG UNITS FIRST.\n- by the time the last read finished, the composition had consumed 22% of the units. The other 78% it did alone.\n\nWHAT WAS DONE. `ReadOrder`: each unit holds a half-open interval of the order and its recursive recipes divide that interval between them in recipe order, one part each; a read is served by the low end of its own part. That is a depth-first pre-order key over a tree nobody has yet seen the whole of, computed from the two things every dispatching side already has — its own interval and which child this is. The root, and any unit no worker read ahead, holds the whole interval, which is the right answer for a unit the composition just read itself and is about to block on the first child of. The interval divides at every level, so past about twenty-four levels of fan-out six there is nothing left to divide and descendants share a key; they are then served in arrival order, which is where every read was before. The ordering degrades to first-come-first-served, never to anything wrong.\n\nThe queue is a channel for arrival and a heap for choice. A dispatcher only sends, which takes no lock. The worker taking work takes the one lock the workers already shared — a channel receiver cannot be read by eight threads without one — moves what has arrived into the heap and takes the smallest. The alternative spelling, a mutex and condition variable the dispatcher also takes, was written and measured and could not be told apart on either shape; this one is kept because a thread starting a read should not wait behind eight taking them, not because the difference was measurable.\n\nComposition is not moved. One thread still builds the graph in the order it always did; only which worker reads which Makefile when has changed, and that was already free to vary at `-j8`.\n\nWHAT IT BOUGHT, same instrumentation, same twenty runs, same host:\n\n| | before | after |\n| wall | 127.5 ms | 85.3 ms |\n| composing thread waiting | 59.9 ms | 9.0 ms |\n| units wanted whose read was unstarted | 3.7/run | 0 |\n| units composed when the last read finished | 22% | 100% |\n| cores the run keeps busy | 5.1 | 6.7 |\n\nThe two phases became one. The composing thread is 85% to 98% occupied from a twentieth of the way in, rather than 20% to 33% for the first half; the workers hold 7.85 of eight until 90% through. Binned over twenty runs after the change, at no point in the run is either side idle waiting for the other.\n\nTHE CEILING MOVED, and this is the finding that reranks everything after it. Before the change `-j16` was worth 1.28x over `-j8`; after it, `-j16` measures the same as `-j8` (91.9 against 94.8 on the intermediate tree) and `-j32` is worse. The composing thread is now the run's critical path rather than sitting under it. So the previous node's ranking inverts: a worker-side cut divided by eight AND no longer binds, while anything taken off the composing thread is wall at one to one. The composer's allocator traffic — 37.4% of what was the serial tail — is now the top of the list on its own.\n\nWHAT I OVERTURNED.\n\n(1) THE BRIEF'S CENTRAL PREMISE, \"the composing thread nearly idle at 0.13 cores ... then does all its work afterwards\", is half true and its explanation is wrong. It is blocked, for 47% of the wall, but it also does about a third of its work during the first phase. And it is not blocked on anything load-bearing: no correctness dependency prevents composition from starting, and 234 of 258 units a run had their read ALREADY FINISHED when the composition arrived. It was blocked on the queue's order and nothing else.\n\n(2) \"Depth is not the problem ... the composer's starvation in phase A is not a BFS-dispatch-vs-DFS-composition ordering artifact\" (2026-08-29 note) — REFUTED, and it is exactly that artifact. The evidence that note had pointed the same way and was read the other way: it measured the flat tree's composer overlapping at 0.5 cores against the deep tree's 0.13, which is the ordering mismatch showing itself, and then argued from per-unit COST being equal on both shapes, which bears on how much work a unit is and not on when it can be done.\n\n(3) \"-j16/-j32 measure the same as -j8, composition is the ceiling\" (2026-08-28), refuted by the 2026-08-29 note as stale — both were right about their own tree. It was false before this change and is true after it.\n\n(4) A MEASUREMENT PROTOCOL THIS PROJECT USES IS UNSOUND ON THIS HOST, and it cost me an hour. `make_baseline` gives each tool its own directory. On the root filesystem, which is 98% full, two directories written from the same generator minutes apart are not interchangeable: the SAME binary measured 88.3 ms in one 216-leaf tree and 170.9 ms in another, a factor of 1.94. Anything comparing tools across separate trees on that filesystem is comparing trees. Every number above is from one tree with the tools rotating within it.\n\n(5) A FLAT TREE — one root, 216 direct recursive children, no depth — REGRESSES BY A NUMBER I CANNOT ATTRIBUTE TO THE CHANGE, and the honest statement is that I cannot tell it from a semantically inert edit to the same file. The change measures 1.131x an unchanged tree there. In the same 61-rep rotation, three variants of this file that are semantically identical to it in every path that shape takes measured 0.913x, 1.185x and 1.236x. Two of the three inert ones are SLOWER than the change. User-space cycle counts over ten runs are equal to 0.5% (14.80e9 against 14.73e9); the whole difference is system time and a two-to-threefold change in context switches, and it moves with code layout rather than with what the code does. On that shape the ordering is the identity permutation — the keys arrive strictly increasing — so there is nothing for it to reorder. Two candidate mechanisms were built and measured and neither was it: counting the dispatchers under the queue's lock rather than in an atomic, and holding the backlog outside the channel at all (tested with the ordering disabled, which regressed identically, and with a flag that keeps the channel path while the arrivals are already in order, which did not help). The shape sits on a scheduling cliff that a perturbation of any kind lands on one side or the other of.\n\n(6) The `$(shell)` and read-order caveats did not need widening. Reading early already saw the tree as it was early and already ran a subtree's reads out of recipe order; this changes which of those happen first, which is inside what the module already documents and inside what GNU Make does at `-j8`.\n\nWHAT DID NOT MOVE, checked rather than assumed. `-j1` executes the SAME INSTRUCTIONS as before to five significant figures — 2.2987e9 against 2.2996e9 over four alternating rounds — because no pool is created there at all; wall over those rounds is 456/480/513/460 before against 444/446/449/429 after. `wide-noop`, which has no recursion to read ahead for, likewise: 3.4262e9 instructions against 3.4260e9. vim-noop 49.9/49.1/49.1 against 46.7/52.5/44.5 and zsh 3053/2878/2882 against 2999/2911/2873, both noise.\n\nGATES, with invocations. `scripts/sandboxed cargo test --all-targets --no-fail-fast` 887 passed / 0 failed. `scripts/sandboxed cargo test --workspace --all-targets` 1203 / 0. Make equivalence: 40 runs of `scripts/check-make-equivalence.sh` on the committed tree, every one 274 makefiles / 276 graphs / 8 respellings / 56 make-rejected / 0 disagreements, plus 40 more on the intermediate tree. `one_graph_however_many_threads_read_it` 60 consecutive runs, 0 failures, driven with `--ignored --exact`. Conformance 354 runs / 326 identical normalised / 28 differing / 276 raw. Upstream 18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 20 interface. Port ledger 170 symbols. Ninja 425/425 upstream, 10 differential, 92 build-outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence round trip, log content. `cargo fmt --check`, `scripts/check-lints.sh`, `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` all clean.\n\nTHE BASELINE AND RELEASE DEBT IS STILL NOT DISCHARGED, for the fourth dispatch, and for the same reason. `make_baseline` refuses above one-minute load 4.0 and the host ran 8 to 17 for the whole of this one; a watcher was armed. `benchmarks/make-baseline-v1.csv` still holds `recursive-noop,78.654,162.805` — a recorded 2.07x against a measured 0.94x — and `scripts/check-release.sh` has not been run end to end here. Re-recording on a host at load 10 is the thing that gate exists to prevent.\n\nMETHOD NOTE FOR THE NEXT NODE. The instrumentation is the reusable part: five atomic timestamps per read into a static table, dumped from the pool's teardown to a file the environment names. It found in one run what two sampling profiles over 200 runs each had described but misattributed — sampling says how much CPU each thread spent and cannot say what a blocked thread was blocked ON. And when a wall difference has no matching difference in user cycles, build a semantically inert variant of the same edit and measure it alongside: three of them bracketing this change is what turned an apparent 27% regression into a statement about the host."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-29T14:50:34.647463614Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "ERRATA against my own note of an hour ago and the module comment that went with it, and what the quiet window bought.\n\n\"THE CEILING MOVED ... after it, `-j16` measures the same as `-j8` (91.9 against 94.8 on the intermediate tree) and `-j32` is worse. The composing thread is now the run's critical path rather than sitting under it.\" — WRONG, and it was one noisy pair of numbers read as a result. Re-measured on the committed binary at load 6.8 to 9.7, eleven reps per point on one tree: before `-j8` 134.5 ms, `-j16` 113.7, `-j32` 122.5; after `-j8` 119.5, `-j16` 106.4, `-j32` 123.7. `-j16` is worth about 12% over `-j8` AFTER the change just as it was before it. What did change is how much of the machine the run uses at each point: 5.4 cores to 6.3 at `-j8`, 6.9 to 7.6 at `-j16`.\n\nWhat survives, and it is the part the ranking rests on: the composing thread is now busy for 90% of the wall — 76.7 ms of 85.3 in the instrumented runs — where before it was busy for half of a wall half as long again. Work taken off that thread is therefore worth about one to one against the wall, and work taken off a worker is still divided by however many workers there are. That is a statement about occupancy, which is measured. \"The composer is the ceiling\" is stronger than the measurement supports and I withdraw it; the module comment now says the weaker thing.\n\nTHE QUIET WINDOW CAME, at 16:42 local, one-minute load 3.73 falling to 2.5 — the first below 4.00 in four dispatches. Two things were done with it.\n\nTHE HEADLINE ON A QUIET HOST. `recursive-noop` at `-j8`, one tree on disk, three tools rotating rep for rep, 41 reps, load 2.52 rising to 4.47 under the benchmark's own weight: GNU Make 4.4.1 83.1 ms, Ronin before 103.3 ms, Ronin after 83.9 ms. Before was 1.243x GNU; after is 1.011x. The paired after-over-before is 0.813, the same 0.81 to 0.86 measured in every loaded window, so the ratio never depended on the load and only the absolutes did.\n\nTHE BASELINE WAS RE-RECORDED, discharging debt that had stood for four dispatches. `scripts/check-make-performance.sh --clean-build` at load_average_before=3.27, five interleaved samples per tool per workload, validating green against the stale CSV on the way past. Ronin over GNU: wide-noop 0.68x, recursive-noop 0.97x, vim-noop 1.86x, zsh-incremental 1.56x, vim-clean-build 1.00x. `recursive-noop` was recorded at 2.07x and measures 0.97x — GNU 93.9 ms against Ronin 91.2 ms, on the gate's own harness rather than mine. `vim-noop` moved the wrong way against its recorded 1.72x and is the one to look at next on that list.\n\nThat first recording carried `ronin_dirty=true`, because the module-comment correction above was still unstaged when it ran. It is provenance for a tree that is not a commit and it is not the file installed."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-29T15:28:12.562114282Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "The quiet host, and the release gate end to end. Both debts discharged.\n\nTHE CLEANEST WALLS THIS NODE HAS. `recursive-noop` at `-j8`, one tree on disk, three tools rotating rep for rep, 61 reps, one-minute load 1.39 rising only to 2.16 — the quietest window in four dispatches. GNU Make 4.4.1 79.8 ms (min 77.9, max 83.8), Ronin before 97.7 ms, Ronin after 81.9 ms. Before was 1.225x GNU; after is 1.026x; the paired after-over-before is 0.838. GNU's own spread over 61 samples is 6 ms, which is what a quiet host buys and what every earlier number in this node was missing.\n\nEvery window measured today, before-over-GNU then after-over-GNU, with the paired after-over-before:\n\n  load 13.4-15.5, 61 reps   1.147   0.938   0.818\n  load 13-15, 6x9 rounds    1.144   0.980   0.856\n  load 11-14, 6x9 rounds    1.192   0.994   0.833\n  load 2.5-4.5, 41 reps     1.243   1.011   0.813\n  load 1.4-2.2, 61 reps     1.225   1.026   0.838\n\nThe paired ratio is 0.81 to 0.86 across a factor of ten in load and never depended on it. The against-GNU ratio drifts with load in Ronin's favour, which is worth knowing for its own sake: Ronin keeps 6.3 to 6.7 cores busy where it kept 5.1 to 5.4, so a contended host costs it less than it costs a GNU Make that is already using seven.\n\nSCRIPTS/CHECK-RELEASE.SH RAN END TO END AND PASSED, rc=0, on the FIRST invocation — the stage that usually needs a second is `check-make-performance.sh`, which waits out its patience for a quiet host, and this time the host was quiet when it got there (load_average_before=3.91). Everything in the chain: `cargo fmt --all --check`, `cargo check --all-targets`, `scripts/check-lints.sh`, `cargo doc` under `-D warnings`, `cargo test --all-targets --no-fail-fast`, make equivalence 274/276/8/56/0, `check-make-projects.sh` (vim built in 16 s, zsh and 36 modules in 29 s, both from their own Makefiles), port ledger 170, Ninja conformance 425/425 + 10 + 92 + 3 + persistence + log content, the Ninja performance gate, the Make performance gate, `nplan spec uncovered` and `spec stale` both empty, `nplan lint` clean, `nplan audit` with no proposals.\n\nTHE MAKE PERFORMANCE GATE AGAINST THE BASELINE JUST RECORDED, which is the first time it has been asked a question with no slack in it: measured 0.64x / 1.04x / 1.90x / 1.54x against recorded 0.60x / 0.95x / 1.74x / 1.62x, so the largest use of the 120% tolerance is `recursive-noop` at 1.095 and `vim-noop` at 1.09. Worth saying plainly: the old baseline passed this gate with the tool running at 1.21x against a recorded 2.07x, which is 42% of margin doing nothing. A baseline recorded at the tool's actual speed has no such cushion, and 9% of headroom on a host whose load moved from 3.91 to 2.62 during the run is about what five samples a workload can promise. The next recording should either spend more repetitions or the gate should widen for the workloads whose absolute times are tens of milliseconds.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT ON THE LIST, in the order the measurements support:\n(a) `vim-noop`, 1.74x to 1.90x recorded across three runs today and the only gated row that has not improved across the campaign. vim's top-level Makefile recurses exactly once, so there is one child to read and nothing to overlap it with — `read_ahead` declines a unit with fewer than two recursive recipes on purpose. Whatever the 12 ms is, it is not the read schedule, and nothing in this campaign has looked at it.\n(b) The composing thread, which is now busy for 90% of the wall rather than half of it. The by-TID profile that ranked its allocator traffic at 37.4% of what was then a serial tail was taken against a different shape of run and should be retaken before anything is cut on its authority.\n(c) The workers' implicit-rule search, still the largest single thing in the run at about half of worker samples, and still divided by however many workers there are."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id the-composition-stops-where-gnu-make-stops
        title "Stop at a recursive target GNU Make would not remake, and look at one again once its children have"
        kind @perf
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-30T17:45:45.912583239Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE ONE METRIC, AND WHERE ITS 910 ms WAS. `zsh-incremental` — `make -j8` in the built zsh 5.9.2 tree, which never settles: `Src/params.o` recompiles, `stamp-modobjs` updates and `Src/zsh` relinks on every invocation under both tools. No file is touched; the harness (`examples/make_baseline.rs`, `Shape::Incremental`, sentinel `Src/zsh`) measures the steady state a zsh developer lives in, and the recorded baseline was GNU 1463.7 ms against Ronin 2374.4 ms, 1.62x.\n\nTHE LEDGER, BEFORE ANY FIX. A `gcc` shim on PATH logging `EPOCHREALTIME` at exec and at exit gives both tools the same phase boundaries for about a millisecond of overhead, where `strace -f -tt` cost 1.1 s and moved the phases it was measuring. Interleaved, one run each:\n\n  phase                       GNU        RONIN      delta\n  process start                 0.0        0.0       +0.0\n  first child spawned          28.5      756.8     +728.3\n  compile (params.c) ends    1266.6     2002.6     +736.0\n  link (zsh) starts          1290.5     2036.7     +746.2\n  link ends                  1338.4     2079.9     +741.5\n  process exit               1365.1     2086.8     +721.7\n\n  compile duration           1238.1     1245.8       +7.7\n  link duration                47.9       43.2       -4.7\n\n721.7 ms of gap, and 728.3 ms of it is spent before Ronin spawns its first child. The compile is 1238 vs 1246 ms and the link 48 vs 43 — the SAME `cc1` on the same source, and every millisecond after the first child spawns is a wash. So the campaign's standing excuse has it exactly backwards: `zsh-incremental` is 91% identical gcc/ld work by wall (1286 of GNU's 1365 ms), which is why the ratio understates the front end — and that identical work explains none of the delta, because it is identical. The delta was one number: the front end, and nothing else.\n\nWHAT THE FRONT END WAS DOING. Running the tools through `make`-named shims that log every nested invocation gives the shape:\n\n  RONIN                                  GNU\n    1.9 ms  .                              1.6 ms  .\n    6.4 ms  ./Src                          4.8 ms  ./Src\n   29.5 ms  ./Src/Builtins                 7.3 ms  ./Src\n   44.9 ms  ./Src/Modules                 11.8 ms  ./Src/Builtins\n  692.1 ms  ./Src/Zle    <- +647.2        15.4 ms  ./Src/Modules  <- +3.6\n  ...                                     ...\n\nGNU spends 2-6 ms per nested `make`; Ronin spends 647 ms in `Src/Modules` alone. Standalone, in that one directory: `headers` costs GNU 10.5 ms and Ronin 602 ms (57x); `modules` 15 ms against 1115 ms; `modobjs` 6 ms against 9 ms. `Src/Modules` is 1745 of Ronin's 2053 ms and 31 of GNU's.\n\nAND WHY. An atomic counter on the pass loop in `prepare_graph` and on `compile_unit`: `Src/Modules` made SIXTY-ONE read/compile passes, `Src/Zle` sixteen, `Src` eleven. Each pass re-reads and re-evaluates the whole 3,231-line generated Makefile from scratch; one pass costs about 9 ms, and 61 of them cost 602. `modobjs`, which has no recursion to compose, made one pass and cost 9 ms — the same read, once.\n\nThe 61 came from `compose_subninjas` staging every recursive recipe's prerequisites BEFORE asking whether the recipe had anything to do. `Src/Modules` holds 62 recursive invocations (`ronin -f Makefile -t lint --make`: 60 composed, 2 nested) — two per module, `X.mdhs: X.mdd` and `X.mdh: ... X.mdhi`, each `@$(MAKE) -f $(makefile) $(MAKEDEFS) X.mdh.tmp`. `state.stage` returns false the first time it sees a boundary, the unit is abandoned, the staged closure is built, and the whole compilation starts over — one pass per boundary, and the wrapper the pass was for then turns out to be up to date and composes nothing. The counters say so directly: 1,820 wrappers found current against 10 composed. Sixty passes bought ten compositions.\n\nGNU never goes near it. `make -d`: `Prerequisite 'zutil.mdhi' is older than target 'zutil.mdh'. No need to remake target 'zutil.mdh'.` — it neither remakes the prerequisites of a target it is not remaking nor enters the Make that target names. The fix is that sentence: settle the wrapper's freshness first, and stage only for a recipe that is going to run. `Src/Modules` went 61 passes to 3 for `headers` and 4 for `modules`; 602 ms to 52 ms.\n\nTHE CORRECTNESS FINDING, WHICH CAME FIRST. Diffing the two command streams for the same tree state, Ronin ran MORE commands than GNU, every run, forever:\n\n  GNU (5)                          RONIN (9)\n  rm -f stamp-modobjs.tmp          rm -f stamp-modobjs.tmp\n  gcc -c -o params.o params.c      generate zutil.mdh.tmp        <- extra\n  Updated `stamp-modobjs'          mv zutil.mdh.tmp zutil.mdh    <- extra\n  rm -f zsh                        generate zutil.mdh.tmp AGAIN  <- extra\n  gcc -o zsh main.o ...            mv zutil.mdh.tmp zutil.mdh    <- extra\n                                   gcc -c -o zutil..o zutil.c    <- extra, 505 ms\n                                   gcc -shared -o zutil.so       <- extra\n                                   gcc -c -o params.o params.c\n                                   gcc -o zsh main.o ...\n\nAn extra `-O2` compile and an extra shared link, on every incremental build of zsh, and in the straced run they landed after the `zsh` link where nothing could overlap them.\n\n`zutil` is the only one of 27 modules whose `.mdh` reads a file in a sibling directory: `zutil.mdh: $(dir_top)/Src/Zle/complete.mdh $(dir_top)/Src/zsh.mdh zutil.mdhi`, and `$(dir_top)/Src/Zle/complete.mdh: FORCE` with `@cd $(dir_top)/Src/Zle && $(MAKE) complete.mdh`. GNU's own `-d` on a REAL run shows what it does with that: `Must remake target '../../Src/Zle/complete.mdh'` (FORCE does not exist), it runs the recursion, `Successfully remade target file '../../Src/Zle/complete.mdh'`, and then — `Prerequisite '../../Src/Zle/complete.mdh' is older than target 'zutil.mdh'. No need to remake target 'zutil.mdh'.` It re-stats the target it just remade and lets the comparison decide, which is exactly `[spec:ronin:req:make.remade-target-re-observed]` and exactly what `set_make_target_freshness` already puts on every Make edge including the recursive wrapper.\n\nThe wrapper never got the second look. `finish_phony_edge` gated it on `outputs_unaliased`, and `probe_subninja` sets that false for a wrapper on the grounds that \"its outputs stand for the child goals that replace it\". True of the alias; false of the file. A wrapper whose whole recipe composed into child graphs arrives commandless, so it comes through `finish_phony_edge`, so its output was never re-read — and the always-dirty `FORCE` in front of it cascaded through everything reading a file the child had left exactly where it found it. Gating on `outputs_unaliased || outputs_reobserved` is the fix; the manifest side is untouched because only the Make sink ever sets `outputs_reobserved`.\n\nReduced to eight lines, both tools in one directory:\n\n  all: consumer\n  consumer: sub/target\n  \t@echo RUN-consumer; touch consumer\n  sub/target: FORCE\n  \t@cd sub && $(MAKE) target\n  FORCE:\n  .PHONY: FORCE\n\nwith `sub/target` up to date. GNU: nothing, twice. Ronin before: `RUN-consumer`, twice, and forever. Ronin after: `ronin: no work to do.`\n\nBOTH FIXES ARE RONIN-SIDE. kati is unchanged at `cd4b9a9` — neither defect was in the compiler's reading of the Makefile. One is where the composition decides to stage, the other is where the build decides what a commandless edge left behind."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T18:14:54.575000961Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "CORRECTION to the sentence above that reads \"it neither remakes the prerequisites of a target it is not remaking nor enters the Make that target names\". The second half is right and the first half is not. GNU Make's update_file_1 updates every prerequisite FIRST and only then compares dates and decides whether the target needs remaking — \"Considering target file 'zutil.mdh'\" walks the whole prerequisite list before \"No need to remake target 'zutil.mdh'\". It does not skip prerequisites.\n\nWhat the change actually skips is narrower, and still sound. A staging pass exists so that a CHILD COMPILATION can read files off the disk its parent's prerequisites write to; it is a compiler-input boundary, not a build of the prerequisites. A wrapper the graph has just called current is current precisely BECAUSE every one of those inputs came back clean from recompute_dirty_with_validations, which walks the input closure through the graph rather than off the disk — so a provisional build of them would build nothing, and the pass that waits for it is pure loss. The prerequisites stay ordinary inputs of the wrapper edge and the ordinary build still handles them; an input that were out of date would have made the wrapper dirty and taken the staging path exactly as before. The code comment carries the accurate wording; this entry is why it does.\n\nAlso for the record, on make.state-outside-the-tree: the .ninja_log and .ninja_deps files sitting in seven directories of the zsh reference tree are dated 2026-08-19 and 2026-08-27, and neither binary touched them across some fifty runs of this investigation. They are leftovers from before that rule, not a live violation. Worth sweeping out of the reference tree at some point so the next person measuring here does not have to establish that twice."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T18:47:05.643792931Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Dep on the-make-front-end-reads-one-makefile-at-a-time removed rather than waiting on it. That node is still Doing and its work — reading a recursive subtree's sibling Makefiles on a worker pool — is orthogonal to this one, which is about how MANY times the composition asks for those reads and what the build does with a commandless edge afterwards. Neither change touches the other's code. Recording the removal rather than silently starting around the dep."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T18:47:10.999541411Z
