            {
                at 2026-08-30T19:11:55.827008571Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "WHAT THE TWO CHANGES BOUGHT, PAIRED. Four binaries at four paths — `before` (0624ffa), `fixonly` (the re-observation fix alone), `stageonly` (the pass-count fix alone), `after` (both) — plus GNU Make 4.4.1, interleaved with rotating order, one tree per workload with the tools rotating inside it rather than a tree each.\n\nzsh-incremental, 17 samples a tool, load 5.06 rising to 9.23 (two other agent sessions held this host between 6 and 12 for the whole dispatch; the absolute times are inflated by about 11% against the recorded GNU figure and the RATIO is what survives it — `before` reads 1.610x against the 1.62x recorded on a quiet host, which is the calibration):\n\n  gnu       1630.5 ms   1.000x\n  before    2624.8 ms   1.610x\n  fixonly   2582.5 ms   1.584x\n  after     1711.2 ms   1.049x\n\n  re-observation fix alone   -42.3 ms\n  pass-count fix on top      -871.3 ms\n  both                       -913.6 ms   gap to GNU +80.7 ms\n\nAn earlier 16-sample round at load 2.69, before the phony exclusion below, read GNU 1391.6 / after 1440.0 — 1.035x, +48.4 ms. Both rounds agree the ratio is between 1.03x and 1.05x where it was 1.62x.\n\nThe two are not additively separable at -j8 and the ordering flatters the second. The extra `zutil.c` compile the re-observation fix removes is 505 ms of gcc that OVERLAPS `params.c` when the scheduler has room, so removing it buys only what it was blocking — 42 ms here, and in the straced run where it landed after the `zsh` link it was 1.1 s of pure tail. Applied the other way round it would score higher and the pass-count fix lower. What is not order-dependent: 913.6 ms of 994.3 ms of gap, gone.\n\nStandalone `Src/Modules`, which is where it all was: `headers` GNU 10.5 ms / before 602 ms / after 52 ms; `modules` GNU 15 ms / before 1115 ms / after ~560 ms then lower again once the spurious zutil work went; `modobjs`, the goal with no recursion in it, GNU 6 ms / before 9 ms / after 9 ms.\n\nA REGRESSION THIS NODE CAUSED AND CAUGHT BEFORE LANDING, which is the whole reason the synthetics exist. The first cut of the re-observation fix gated on `outputs_unaliased || outputs_reobserved` and took `recursive-noop` from 1.01x to 1.40x of GNU — through the 20% band over the recorded 0.95x, a red gate. `perf stat` said +304 M instructions and +58 ms task-clock for identical passes (1) and identical units (259) and identical makefile reads (259), and the profile diff put it in `DirtyEvaluator::evaluate` under `Builder::add_target` plus allocator churn.\n\nThe cause was a correctness error, not a cost. That workload's recursion is `$(SUBDIRS): ; @$(MAKE) --no-print-directory -C $@ all` with `.PHONY: all $(SUBDIRS)`. The wrapper's outputs are `sub0`..`sub5` — names the Makefile declared phony that are ALSO directories on the disk. `outputs_reobserved` is set on every Make edge, so the fix made the build stat a phony target, find the directory, prune, and recompute every consumer: 259 dirty recomputations for an answer GNU Make never asks for. `build/freshness.rs::reobserves`, the command path beside `finish_phony_edge`, has carried `&& !edge.always_dirty` all along for exactly this. Adding the same exclusion put `recursive-noop` at 0.98x and left the zsh fix intact.\n\nThat is the seventh consecutive wrong pre-ranking's lesson applied in the small: the first measurement said \"the fix costs 35 ms\", and the second said \"the fix was stating a phony target\". Cost was the symptom.\n\nMEASURED ELSEWHERE, one tree, tools rotating, 13 or 11 samples a tool:\n  recursive-noop -j8   gnu 91.5   before 92.4 (1.010x)   after 89.7 (0.981x)\n  wide-noop      -j8   gnu 1100.9 before 659.0 (0.599x)  after 663.9 (0.603x)\n  recursive-noop -j1   gnu 704.5  before 460.1 (0.653x)  after 448.8 (0.637x)\n  wide-noop      -j1   gnu 1279.1 before 722.8 (0.565x)  after 692.9 (0.542x)\n\nNothing shifted outside noise and both -j1 rows are the same or better, so no re-record is owed for the two synthetics. One IS owed for zsh-incremental: the recorded 1463.741/2374.419 would now let a 1.94x regression back in without the gate saying a word. Not taken in this dispatch because the host never went quiet enough to record honestly — flagged here rather than recorded badly.\n\nBEHAVIOUR, WHICH CAME FIRST AND IS THE STRONGER RESULT. What each tool WRITES into the already-built zsh tree, by mtime over the whole tree:\n\n  GNU    4 files: Src/params.o  Src/patchlevel.h  Src/stamp-modobjs  Src/zsh\n  after  4 files: Src/params.o  Src/patchlevel.h  Src/stamp-modobjs  Src/zsh\n  before 7 files: those four, plus Src/Modules/zutil.mdh, zutil..o, zutil.so\n\nIdentical sets. And the compiled read is byte-identical before and after — `ronin -f Makefile -t lint --make` in `Src/Modules` reports the same 62 recursive invocations, the same 60 composed and 2 nested, and the same diagnostics either way; only how many times the compilation runs to produce it changed.\n\nGATES, with invocations. `cargo test --all-targets --no-fail-fast` 888/0 (887 before, +1 for the new regression case). `cargo test --workspace --all-targets` 1204/0. `scripts/check-make-conformance.sh` 354 cases, 276 raw identical, 326 normalised identical, 28 differing. `scripts/check-make-equivalence.sh` 274 makefiles / 276 graphs / 8 respellings / 56 make-rejected / 0 disagreements, FORTY consecutive runs all identical. `scripts/check-make-upstream.sh` 18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 20 interface. `scripts/check-ninja-conformance.sh` 425/425 upstream + 10 differential + 92 build-outcome + 3 invocation-boundary, persistence round trip and log content. `scripts/check-port-ledger.sh` 170. `scripts/check-lints.sh` clean. `make::equivalence::one_graph_however_many_threads_read_it --ignored --exact` 30/30 in bulk. `scripts/check-release.sh` ran through lints, tests, equivalence, the vim and zsh clean-build project gate, the port ledger and the whole Ninja conformance gate, and stopped in the NINJA-mode performance gate on `path-canonicalization` at 0.76x against a recorded 0.59x — on a host at load average 12.92, which the Ninja gate does not refuse to measure at the way the Make gate does. Ronin is still faster than Ninja there; nothing in this change reaches that path, because `outputs_reobserved` is set by the Make sink alone and a manifest-built graph reads the same `outputs_unaliased` it always did. Re-running that gate on a quiet host is the outstanding item."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T19:17:28.887013856Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE MAKE PERFORMANCE GATE, TWICE, AND IT VALIDATES. `scripts/check-make-performance.sh --max-load 16` — the ceiling raised deliberately and said so here, because two other agent sessions held this host between 8 and 16 for the whole dispatch and it never went under 4.00. Both runs `rc=0`:\n\n  workload          recorded   run A (7 reps)   run B (5 reps)\n  wide-noop           0.60x        0.55x            0.53x\n  recursive-noop      0.95x        0.95x            0.96x\n  vim-noop            1.74x        1.56x            1.75x\n  zsh-incremental     1.62x        1.07x            1.03x\n\n`recursive-noop` lands on its recorded figure to two decimal places in both runs, which is the row the 9.5% cushion warning was about; nothing there needs re-recording. The gate's own harness puts zsh-incremental at 1.07x and 1.03x, against my four-binary rounds' 1.049x and 1.035x — four independent measurements agreeing the workload is at parity where it was 1.62x.\n\nAbsolute times are inflated by the host (GNU zsh-incremental 1606 ms and 1463 ms recorded), which is exactly why the gate is written on ratios and why the paired rounds carry a `before` binary: `before` reads 1.610x against the recorded 1.62x, so the ratio is the quantity that survives this load and the ratio is what moved.\n\nRE-RECORD OWED, NOT TAKEN. The recorded 1463.741/2374.419 for zsh-incremental would now admit a 1.94x regression without the gate saying a word. Re-recording it needs a quiet host and `--clean-build` (dropping the `vim-clean-build` row would break the next `--clean-build` run), and baking load-11 absolutes into the provenance header would be worse than leaving the row honest-but-stale. Left for a quiet window and flagged here.\n\nTHE NINJA-MODE GATE IS NOISE ON THIS HOST, and nothing in this node reaches it. `scripts/check-performance.sh` run twice back to back at load 8.6:\n\n  run 1  rc=0   path-canonicalization ronin 4.446 ms against ninja 6.910 ms — 0.64x, inside the band over the recorded 0.59x\n  run 2  rc=1   deep-graph-evaluation regressed to 0.91x from a recorded 0.72x\n\nA different workload each time and one run green outright. The release gate's earlier stop named a third combination — `path-canonicalization` at 0.76x, on a host at load 12.92 — and that gate, unlike the Make one, has no quiet-host refusal to protect it.\n\nIt cannot be this change in any case, and that is checkable rather than arguable: `outputs_reobserved` is written by `set_make_target_freshness` and by nothing else, and its only two callers are in `src/make/sink.rs`. A graph built from a manifest carries it false on every edge, so `finish_phony_edge`'s new disjunct folds away and the gate reads the identical `outputs_unaliased` it always did. `src/make.rs` is not on the Ninja path at all. The Ninja binary's behaviour is bit-for-bit what it was; only its neighbours on the machine changed."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id the-symbol-maps-hash-an-index-with-siphash
        title "Hash an interned Symbol with the crate's own hasher instead of SipHash"
        kind @perf
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-30T21:03:14.520534759Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T21:05:11.35128233Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE BRIEF'S PREMISE ABOUT vim-noop IS WRONG, and it is the finding that matters most here. The brief says vim \"builds from one large `src/Makefile` (plus `src/auto/config.mk`), essentially no recursion — so the worker pool, read-ahead, staging order, and composition machinery are all irrelevant\". The gated workload is `make -j8` from the TOP of the vim tree, and vim's top-level Makefile is a recursive stub whose `first:` recipe is `cd src && $(MAKE) first`. An execve trace of one gated run shows NINE Ronin process images: the top Make, six `/bin/sh -c` shells (the `config.mk` test, two `echo` banners, two `po/Makefile` tests, a `wayland-scanner` probe), and a composed sub-Make. `src/Makefile` recurses again into `src/auto/wayland`. GNU Make runs eleven images over the same tree: it spawns a separate `make` for `src` where Ronin composes it, and it spawns dash where Ronin execs itself.\n\nWHERE THE 12 ms ACTUALLY IS, measured by running the sub-trees standalone with both tools rotating inside one directory, 21 paired interleaved repetitions each at load 23:\n\n  scope                        GNU      Ronin    paired ratio    share of the gap\n  whole vim-noop            27.033     49.193       1.790x       100%  (22.2 ms)\n  src/ sub-make alone       16.840     34.570       1.972x        80%  (17.7 ms)\n  src/auto/wayland alone     4.085      5.192       1.246x         5%  ( 1.1 ms)\n\nEighty per cent of the gap is inside the `src` sub-make, and the wayland recursion is nearly at parity. The top-level Makefile, three of the shells and the composition together account for the other 4.5 ms.\n\nTHE PERF TRAP, STATED BEFORE THE NUMBERS BECAUSE IT INVALIDATED MY FIRST PROFILE. This host caps `perf_event_max_sample_rate` at 1000/s. At a fixed period of 100,000 cycles a 30 ms run interrupts about 40,000 times a second, so the kernel throttles: `perf record` captured 258.1M cycles where `perf stat` counted 3,183.4M over the same 40 runs — EIGHT PER CENT of the truth, with \"Total Lost Samples: 0\" reported the whole time. Throttling is not uniform; it resumes at each tick boundary, which oversamples whatever runs just after a process starts. That fake profile put `_dl_relocate_static_pie` at 5.23% and had me measuring static-PIE relocation costs that do not exist (4,722 relative relocations, ~18 us a process). At `-c 4000000` the recording captured 32.388G against an expected 31.83G and `_dl_relocate_static_pie` vanished. The brief warned that frequency mode drops 8-15%; fixed-period mode drops 92% unless the period is chosen against the cap. Any period below ~2.2M cycles is unusable here.\n\nTHE PHASE LEDGER for the `src` sub-make, from 2,753 DWARF-unwound samples over 150 runs at 4M cycles:u each, attributed to the outermost frame naming a phase. Denominator 73.4M cycles:u per run (perf stat says 79.6M cycles:u + 44.6M cycles:k, 90.7M instructions, 1,723 page faults; GNU is 41.2M + 32.0M, 43.6M instructions, 833 faults — Ronin retires 2.08x the instructions for the same job):\n\n  phase                                        %      cyc/run\n  dep graph (kati::dep)                    34.65%       25.4M\n  rule and variable eval (eval/expr/rule)  23.39%       17.2M\n  shell: Ronin running as /bin/sh          13.51%        9.9M\n  makefile parse (parser/file/file_cache)  12.28%        9.0M\n  ninja generation (kati::ninja)           10.39%        7.6M\n  symtab/dircache/fileutil                  1.20%        0.9M\n  unattributed                              4.43%        3.3M\n\nWHAT THE RUN ACTUALLY DOES, from atomic counters in a throwaway build (sampling cannot give call counts): 4,059 `build_plan` calls, 4,057 `populate_ninja_node` calls, 12,487 `Symtab::intern` calls, 9,797 `Vars` lookups, 6,016 expression parses, 1,588 `can_pick_implicit_rule` calls, 748 candidate-pool walks, 636 `eval_rule` calls, 3,280 `DirectoryCache::certainly_absent` calls. Nothing pathological: 4,059 nodes is the graph vim's `first` actually reaches, and GNU walks the same one. The 2x is a constant factor spread across parse, eval and dep, not an algorithm doing extra work.\n\nNO WORKER POOL STARTS HERE, which the brief asked to be checked rather than assumed. `strace -f -e trace=clone,clone3` on the `src` sub-make: zero `clone`, eight `clone3`, and every one of them carries `CLONE_VM|CLONE_VFORK` — they are `posix_spawn` of children, not threads. The run is single-threaded, so no `eprintln!`-from-a-worker hazard applied and no cross-thread allocator arena thrash is available as an explanation.\n\nTHE BUILTIN SHELL IS 13.5% OF THE SUB-MAKE AND IT IS UPSTREAM'S. Profiling `ronin` invoked as `sh -c 'if test -n \"\" -a -f po/Makefile; then echo x; fi'` over 1,200 invocations: 1.06M cycles:u each, of which `nsh::builder::Builder::build` is 47.78% INCLUSIVE — the shell is twice as expensive to construct as the command is to run. Inside that: `variables::set_bytes` 17.39%, `set_entry` 12.21% and `std::env::vars_os` 7.50% (importing 53 environment variables into a `BTreeMap<BString, Entry>`), and `Locale::new` + `__newlocale` + `__wcsmbs_load_conv` + `mbrtowc` + `__uselocale` together about 17%. Against dash: 748k instructions and 168 page faults per invocation versus 269k and 127. Six shells a gated run, so roughly 6M cycles of the gap. Per [dec:ronin:builtin-shell] nsh is a normal git dependency and a fix there arrives by lock bump, not from a Ronin dispatch — this is the number to hand upstream, not something to work around here.\n\nA LEAD I CHASED AND KILLED, recorded so nobody spends the hour again. `reference/make-projects/vim-9.2.0957/.ninja_log` is 2.6 MB, 38,002 lines, and holds 161 UNIQUE outputs — a 236:1 ratio. Ninja recompacts at 3:1 (`kMinCompactionEntryCount` 100, `kCompactionRatio` 3 in `build_log.cc`), and the zsh tree is worse: `Src/Modules/.ninja_log` is 5.3 MB and 79,934 lines for 305 unique outputs, ~8.5 MB of stale log across that tree. An unpaired A/B at load 23 said emptying vim's log saved 23.7 ms a run. It saves nothing: `strace -e trace=openat` shows Make mode NEVER OPENS `.ninja_log` — `Persistence::none()` is what a Makefile-derived graph gets, and the file's mtime has not moved since 27 August. The 23.7 ms was the host, and it is exactly the mistake the mandate warns about. THE REAL DEFECT IS STILL THERE THOUGH, and it is Ninja-mode-only: `BuildLog::open` in src/log.rs counts nothing and sets no `needs_recompaction`, and `crate::log::logrecompact` has exactly one caller, the explicit `-t recompact` tool. Ninja recompacts automatically inside `OpenForWrite`. Filed here rather than fixed: it is a Ninja-mode conformance defect, not a vim-noop cost.\n\nWHAT LANDED, AND WHY IT DOES NOT MOVE THE ROW. Thirty-nine `Symbol`-keyed maps were hashing an interner index with SipHash-1-3. Converting them to the crate's existing `FastMap`/`FastSet` retires 3.20% of the sub-make's instructions (90.702M to 87.797M per run) and about 3.4% of its user cycles, and no gated workload does more work after it (wide -0.42%, recursive -0.51%, zsh -0.12%). It does not show on the clock: 3.4% of 79.6M cycles:u is ~0.75 ms against a 3.6 ms per-repetition standard deviation, so 41 paired interleaved repetitions with rotating order return 31.429 ms before and 31.957 ms after, paired median ratio 0.998. Instruction counts are the evidence; the clock at this scale is not.\n\nWHAT THE NEXT DISPATCH SHOULD KNOW. The remaining gap has no hotspot to attack. The largest single self-time category in the flat profile is glibc `malloc`/`free`/`malloc_consolidate` at 15.3%, and the second is `bytes` reference counting — `shared_drop` 4.26% and `shared_clone` 2.57%, roughly 100k atomic increment/decrement pairs a run — but the callers are spread across dozens of sites with none above 0.5%, so there is no targeted fix, only a representation change or an allocator. There is already a `ronin-allocation-discipline` tree and an `examples/alloc_metrics.rs` harness whose strategy is removing allocations rather than swapping allocators, and kati carries `tikv-jemallocator` as an off-by-default feature for `rkati` alone with a written rationale; changing the global allocator for the `ronin` binary would be an architectural decision with its own record, not a perf node. Process teardown is NOT worth chasing — only 0.18% of samples have a drop as their outermost kati/ronin frame."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:27:01.078642698Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id the-build-starts-only-after-the-last-makefile-is-read
        title "Start ready work while the composition is still reading"
        kind @perf
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-30T22:10:34.244017878Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "FILED 2026-08-31 on the operator's direction (\"wbs those 3 things\"). The structural item: GNU Make's top-level make starts children immediately, so compiles in early directories run while later directories' Makefiles are still being read; Ronin composes the ENTIRE graph before the scheduler runs anything, so the whole front end is a pure wall-clock prefix. At zsh scale the prefix is ~100ms; at kernel scale (thousands of Makefiles) it is the difference that makes a cold build feel slower at compile parity. The machinery is half-present: the boundary passes already stage and build mid-compilation for $(shell)/regen, so \"begin building completed subgraphs while composition continues\" extends an existing mechanism rather than inventing one. Hazards known in advance: the sink is single-writer in run order (determinism is structural — preserve it); a started edge must never race a composition boundary that would have withheld it; -B/-n/-q phase semantics (made_for_a_makefile) decide which staged work may run early. Sequencing per the operator-approved queue: after the front-end constant-factor node lands and the host's measurement contention clears; the scheduler node (the-scheduler-picks-ready-edges-without-looking-down-the-graph) goes first as self-contained."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T08:00:49.397580604Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T08:28:12.355248637Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE PREMISE IS WRONG IN BOTH HALVES, AND MEASURING IT FOUND SOMETHING WORTH TWENTY TIMES WHAT THE NODE PROPOSED.\n\nThe node says the build starts only after the last Makefile is read, and that the whole front end is therefore a pure wall-clock prefix. Neither holds. Builds already run mid-composition — that is what `prepare_graph` (src/make/cli.rs:1793) does, and it is not an embryo, it is the dominant front-end cost on a zsh clean build. And the prefix that is left is a fifth of a percent of a clean build.\n\nHOW THE PREFIX WAS MEASURED, because nothing here is inferred. A `gcc` shim first on PATH stamps the first compile's start with a hard link (atomic under `-j8`), and the prefix is that stamp minus the process's own start. Same shim, same tree, both tools, so it measures the identical thing about each. Beside it, an env-gated `RONIN_PHASE_TRACE` — six `eprintln!`s on the phase-driving thread only, never a worker — timestamped `enter`, every `compose-begin`/`compose-end`, every `stage-build-begin`/`stage-build-end`, `plan-end` and `run-end`, in every process of the tree including recursive children. The instrument is reverted; it is not in the tree.\n\nTHE PREFIX, wall clock, host at one-minute load 9 to 13 throughout (so the absolutes are inflated and the fractions are what to read):\n\n                       ronin prefix        ronin wall        gnu prefix     gnu wall\n    zsh-incremental    101 / 107 / 127 ms  1.59 / 1.63 / 1.85 s  33 / 35 / 35 ms  1.45 / 1.54 / 1.61 s\n    vim clean           33 /  31 ms        17.7 / 18.6 s        17 / 17 ms       18.5 / 19.4 s\n    zsh clean           84 /  55 ms        29.0 / 31.2 s        13 / 12 ms       43.1 / 46.9 s\n\nAND WHAT THE PREFIX IS MADE OF, which is the number the node actually needed and did not have. From the phase trace, composition against staged command execution:\n\n                    procs  compose passes   composition   staged builds   front end\n    wide-noop           1        1            776.1 ms        0.0 ms        848.5 ms\n    recursive-noop      1        1             92.7 ms        0.0 ms        103.1 ms\n    vim-noop            2        3             23.6 ms        3.5 ms         28.9 ms\n    vim clean           3        4             22.6 ms        5.1 ms         29.2 ms\n    zsh-incremental    12       20            195.4 ms       85.4 ms        288.6 ms summed\n    zsh clean          15       99           4903.0 ms         —          16 s+ summed\n\nOn the zsh-incremental CRITICAL PATH — top-level unit then the `Src` child, which is where the first `gcc` waits — the front end is 112.9 ms and it splits 35.6 ms of composition against 76.8 ms of staged commands actually running. GNU's whole prefix on the same tree is 34 ms. So of the ~70 ms Ronin is behind at the first compile, roughly half is work GNU also does and the other half is composing four times over.\n\nTHE CEILING ON WHAT THIS NODE PROPOSED, stated per workload, which is what the brief asked for either way.\n\nOverlap can only ever recover composition that stands between process start and the first command the process could have run. Staged builds are commands; they are not recoverable. So:\n\n    vim clean          20.5 ms of 17,700 ms                        0.12%\n    zsh clean          the prefix entire is 55-84 ms of 29-31 s    0.19-0.29%, composition alone less\n    zsh-incremental    35.6 ms of ~1,600 ms                        2.2% absolute ceiling\n                       and only the FINAL pass's 13.9 ms is safely\n                       overlappable without starting edges off a\n                       graph a later pass may still change          0.9% realistic\n    wide-noop / recursive-noop / vim-noop                          0%, exactly — a no-op runs no\n                                                                   commands, so there is nothing\n                                                                   for composition to hide behind\n\nAT KERNEL SCALE, from the per-Makefile numbers rather than from a feeling. Synthetic recursive trees, fan-out and depth varied, every `$(MAKE) -C` lifted so the whole tree composes into one graph in one process — which is the favourable case for Ronin and the one a kernel-shaped build would want:\n\n      259 Makefiles     85.4 ms      0.330 ms each\n      400 Makefiles    127.4 ms      0.319 ms each\n    1,555 Makefiles    537.7 ms      0.346 ms each\n    2,801 Makefiles  1,386.4 ms      0.495 ms each\n\nLinear to about fifteen hundred and drifting up after. A kernel config reads on the order of 1,500 to 2,800 Makefiles, so composition would be 0.5 to 1.4 s. Against a cold `-j8` kernel build of 300 to 600 s that is 0.1 to 0.5 per cent, and against a kernel NO-OP it is the whole run — but a no-op has no commands to overlap with, so the ceiling there is zero as well. There is no workload, at any scale reachable from these numbers, where this change is worth a tenth of what the surgery would risk. It is not built. See the decision entry.\n\nWHAT THE MEASUREMENT FOUND INSTEAD, and it is large. On a zsh CLEAN build one process — a `Makemod` unit at MAKELEVEL 3 — made SIXTY compose passes, spending 4,547 ms composing to do 2,062 ms of staged work and 169 ms of goal build. Its own wall is 6.80 s of a 33.7 s build. Per-pass composition grows from 11 ms to 130 ms as the graph accumulates, and what each pass BUYS is one boundary: `compose_subninjas` returns `incomplete_unit` at the first recipe that needs staging (src/make.rs:1226 and :1245), so a unit holding sixty independent recursive recipes costs sixty reads of itself. The shape is already named in the comment at src/make.rs:1216 — \"zsh's generated `Src/Modules/Makefile` holds two recursive recipes per module and made 61 passes over its 3,231 lines to compose ten children\" — but it had never been weighed, and it is 13.5% of that build's wall against this node's 0.2%. Filed as `a-unit-with-many-recursive-recipes-is-read-once-per-recipe` with this evidence.\n\nGATES, on a tree whose only delta is this plan file. `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, `scripts/check-lints.sh` and `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps` clean, no new warnings. `cargo test --all-targets --no-fail-fast` 893 / 0. `cargo test --workspace --all-targets --no-fail-fast` 1209 / 0."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T08:29:24.456325756Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "NOT BUILT, AND THE DESIGN IS WRITTEN DOWN ANYWAY SO THAT REOPENING IT COSTS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A REDERIVATION.\n\nTHE DECISION. Composition does not begin publishing completed subgraphs to a scheduler running beside it. The ceiling is 0.12% on a vim clean build, 0.19-0.29% on a zsh clean build, 0.9% realistically and 2.2% absolutely on zsh-incremental, exactly 0% on all three no-op rows because a no-op runs no command for composition to hide behind, and 0.1-0.5% on an extrapolated kernel-scale cold build. The measurements are in the note above. Nothing in that column pays for touching the one place in this program where a single writer, a fixed emission order and a conformance-visible edge numbering all meet.\n\nWHAT THE DESIGN WOULD HAVE BEEN, because it was worked out before it was declined and half of it is reusable by the node this one found.\n\nA COMPLETED FRONTIER, NOT A LOCK. The sink is a single writer on the composing thread, and that is structural rather than incidental: `[dec:ronin:frontend-graph-boundary]` rejects a second writer into the arenas outright, because `BuildGraph`'s invariants — a node's generating edge and use list, an edge's input partitions and explicit output count, the validation side table, the `dyndep` binding naming one of the edge's own inputs — live in its mutating functions and nowhere else. A scheduler reading arenas a composer is still growing is that second writer's read half, and it is refused for the same reason. The contract that works instead is a published frontier: composition stays the only writer and, at points it chooses, hands the scheduler an immutable snapshot of the edges whose whole input cone is composed AND whose composition can no longer change — which means a unit composed whole, on a pass that will not be repeated, with no boundary staged anywhere beneath it. The scheduler draws only from snapshots.\n\nHOW DETERMINISM SURVIVES IT. It survives because the frontier is derived from composition's own order and changes nothing about it: the final graph is still built by one thread in exactly the order it is built in today, so every node, every edge and every invented name falls where it falls now. What moves is WHEN an edge starts, which is scheduling — with one exception that is not negotiable. `[dec:ronin:...]` on `the-scheduler-picks-ready-edges-without-looking-down-the-graph` established that a SERIAL Make run's recipe order is a conformance criterion, pinned by `tests/make/shuffled-goals-are-built-back-to-front`, and that the order is carried by nothing but the edge identifier — the order of creation. An overlapped start reorders exactly that. So overlap would have to be off wherever the serial arm is on, which is the default `make` invocation, which is most of them.\n\nPHASE SEMANTICS. An edge started early has to carry the phase it was composed under, not the phase the process happens to be in when it runs. `made_for_a_makefile` (src/make.rs:1404) already answers per recipe which phase a recursive recipe belongs to, `remaking_options` (src/make/cli/remake.rs:520) turns `-n`, `-t` and `-q` off for the makefile half, `Passes::stage_all` (remake.rs:441) splits the staged work into the two halves, and `-B`/`-W` are gated on `restarts == 0` (remake.rs:912). A frontier snapshot is the natural carrier for that answer, because it is built where both facts are in hand. Started without it, a goal edge would run under makefile-update switches, which is a different build and a silent one.\n\nTHE JOBSERVER, AND ONE CONSTRAINT NOBODY HAD WRITTEN DOWN. The budget is settled BEFORE the read — `settle_jobserver` from `build_options` (src/make/cli.rs:1543 and :1630), deliberately, because the address has to exist before a unit bakes `MAKEFLAGS` into its recipes. Every build over the graph gets the same `options.jobserver`, staged and goals alike, so staged work is already charged correctly. But `JobserverClient` is per-`Builder` (src/build.rs:2192) and each one takes an implicit slot first. Overlapping a goal build with composition puts two `Builder`s alive in one process — the staged one and the overlapped one — and each takes its own implicit slot, so the budget is over-drawn by one token per concurrent builder. Whoever builds this needs a single long-lived `Builder`, or the implicit slot lifted out of `Builder` and owned by the process. Noted here rather than discovered later.\n\nAND A SEPARATE OBSERVATION FROM THE SAME READING, not acted on: `$(shell)` forks `/bin/sh` during evaluation (kati/src-rs/func.rs:682) and is charged against no budget at all. A composition reading a recursive tree on `ReadPool` workers can therefore have as many shells running as it has workers, on top of whatever the scheduler is running. It has never been measured and may well be nothing; it is written here because this is the reading that found it.\n\n`RuntimeState` is positionally indexed against arenas that grow during composition. `synchronize` (src/runtime.rs:321) exists for exactly that and returns the newly grown range — but it was built for a dyndep growth with the build stopped at a barrier, not for growth concurrent with a live scan, and reusing it for the second would be reusing a name rather than a mechanism."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T08:35:52.301239777Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "GATES. The tree's only delta is this plan file — the instrument used to take the measurements was reverted before any of these ran — so these are HEAD's numbers, run to say so rather than to discover anything.\n\n    cargo fmt --all -- --check                          clean\n    scripts/check-lints.sh                              clean\n    RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc --no-deps        clean, no new warnings\n    cargo test --all-targets --no-fail-fast             893 / 0\n    cargo test --workspace --all-targets --no-fail-fast 1209 / 0\n\n    Make equivalence   274 makefiles, 276 graphs, 8 manifest respellings,\n                       56 make-rejected, 0 disagreements — TWENTY runs, every\n                       column identical on all twenty, and\n                       `one_graph_however_many_threads_read_it` with it each time\n    Make conformance   354 runs against GNU Make 4.4.1; 276 identical raw,\n                       326 identical normalised, 28 differing\n    Upstream           18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 15 interface\n                       (1,243 narration, discovery differences 1,276)\n    Port ledger        170 symbols\n    Ninja conformance  425 / 425 upstream across 33 suites, 10 differential,\n                       92 build-outcome, 3 invocation-boundary; persistence\n                       round trip passed, log content matched\n\nThe perf gates are separate and are recorded in their own entry: the host has sat between 8 and 13 for this whole dispatch and `check-make-performance.sh` will not measure above 4.00, correctly."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T08:39:34.941901368Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "ERRATUM. \"A `Makemod` unit at MAKELEVEL 3\", in the measurement note above, is `Src/Modules` — the Makefile `Src/mkmakemod.sh` generates, which is the same `Src/Modules/Makefile` the comment at src/make.rs:1216 names. Established by sampling `/proc/PID/cwd` for every live process through a whole zsh clean build rather than inferred from the level. See the matching entry on `a-unit-with-many-recursive-recipes-is-read-once-per-recipe`; no number changes."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T09:25:29.638871308Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
