    {
        id the-scheduler-picks-ready-edges-without-looking-down-the-graph
        title "Schedule ready commands by the length of what waits behind them"
        kind @perf
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-30T22:10:43.769283272Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "FILED 2026-08-31 on the operator's direction. Stock Ninja 1.12 changed its scheduler to prioritise commands on the critical path (start the deepest chains first so the terminal link is not waiting on a straggler scheduled last). If Ronin's runtime still picks ready edges in plain ready-order, every clean build's tail pays for it, in BOTH modes — this is where holding the whole graph should beat GNU Make's accidental DFS and currently does not. Scheduling order is not semantically observable (console pool and friends aside), so it is conformance-safe; verify against the pinned Ninja oracle's behaviour and the ninja-conformance gates rather than assuming. First check what the runtime actually does today — this node is filed on the ASSUMPTION of ready-order, and ten consecutive briefs this campaign each contained at least one wrong premise. A/B evidence wanted: vim/zsh clean-build tail (time from last compile start to link end) before/after, plus a synthetic with a deep chain beside wide fanout. Self-contained in the scheduler; queued FIRST of the three runtime nodes."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T00:47:43.15086287Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T01:20:26.731190115Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "SERIAL MAKE RUNS THE FRONT END'S WALK ORDER, AND NO EXISTING RULE SAYS SO. Written here rather than as a spec bump because the two rules that ought to cover it disagree with each other and with the corpus, and resolving that is a bigger job than this node.\n\nWHAT WAS DECIDED. Where the front end is Make AND the run was asked for one job, the ready queue's priority goes flat and the existing tie key — lowest edge identifier — decides alone. Everywhere else, both modes at every other job count, the queue stays keyed on the critical-path weight, which is Ninja's at every job count including its own -j1.\n\nWHY THE RULES DO NOT COVER IT. [spec:ronin:req:make.semantics+1] lists what the GNU oracle is consulted about — \"the compiled graph, selected work, normal build outcome, and filesystem effects\" — and then names what it is NOT consulted about: \"GNU Make's stdout, stderr, SCHEDULING, idle and diagnostic wording, recursive banners, jobserver choreography\". Read literally that sentence says the order recipes run in is not a conformance criterion, which would make this change unnecessary. [spec:ronin:req:compat.scheduling] pulls the other way: the scheduler \"may choose a different ready edge only where Ninja does not specify or reliably expose the tie order\", and Ninja specifies it exactly (weight descending, identifier ascending, graph.h `EdgePriorityLess`) — read literally THAT sentence forbids this change. One rule says the order does not matter and the other says it must be Ninja's; between them there is no room for \"it must be GNU's here\", which is what the evidence says.\n\nWHAT THE EVIDENCE SAYS, AND IT IS THE CORPUS, NOT AN ARGUMENT. `tests/make/shuffled-goals-are-built-back-to-front` runs `--shuffle=reverse -j1 a b c` over three goals whose recipes each append their own name to a file, and it asserts that file holds \"c\\nb\\na\\n\" because that is what GNU Make left. Nothing about that case is a graph, a selected-work set, or a diagnostic. It is a file whose CONTENTS are decided by nothing but the order the recipes ran in, compared byte for byte against the oracle — so the corpus already treats serial recipe order as a conformance criterion, whatever make.semantics says about scheduling. That case is also what caught the first attempt at this change (see the note on this node), which is the strongest possible statement that it is load-bearing.\n\nWHY FLAT-AND-DEFER RATHER THAN A COMPUTED WALK. The first attempt numbered edges in a depth-first walk down from the goals and keyed the serial queue on that number. It reproduced GNU's order on every hand-written case and broke the shuffle case, because `--shuffle` is not visible from where the plan walks: GNU Make permutes the goal chain and each prerequisite list before anything reads them, the Make front end permutes the same lists before it composes the graph, and the permutation survives ONLY as the order the edges were created in. A walk recomputed downstream of that composition cannot see it. Deferring to the identifier is not a shortcut around computing the order — the identifier IS the computed order, computed once, in the one place that has the shuffle in hand.\n\nThe cost of that is a dependency: it holds because the Make front end composes by descending the goal chain and each prerequisite list, emitting as it goes. Should the sink ever emit in some other order, serial Make mode goes wrong silently. Three unit tests pin the plan's half of it and the 1229-case corpus pins the whole of it against GNU itself, which is the right place for the guarantee to live.\n\nONE GAP LEFT OPEN DELIBERATELY. The condition reads what the run was ASKED for — `JobLimit::Auto` or `Fixed(1)` — not the width it ends up with. Two cases fall outside it. A load average over `-l` narrows the running width to one mid-build; re-sorting the queue underneath a build because the machine got busy would be neither order, so it does not. And a run that joined an outer jobserver has `JobLimit::Unlimited` with the token count as its real limit, so a one-token budget would run serially under Ninja's order; that one is unreachable in practice, because GNU Make publishes no jobserver at `-j1` at all, so a Ronin child of a serial GNU parent sees no transport and takes the serial arm the ordinary way."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T01:37:13.368650512Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE PREMISE IS WRONG TWICE, AND THE SECOND ONE WOULD HAVE COST US THE ORACLE.\n\nFIRST: RONIN ALREADY PRIORITISES THE CRITICAL PATH, and has since 3720251 (\"Linearize graph evaluation and scheduling\"). `Plan::ready` is a `BinaryHeap<ReadyEdge>` keyed on a critical-path weight with `Reverse(EdgeId)` as the tie key — the same shape as Ninja's `EdgePriorityLess`, weight descending then identifier ascending. It is not an accident either: [dec:ronin:iterative-graph-evaluation] lists \"Ready-edge selection uses a real priority heap with a deterministic tie key\" among its accepted consequences. The weight is the longest path from the edge to a goal, which is height-to-terminal — depth-from-the-goal and height-to-the-goal are the same number, because a path from the goal down to an edge reversed IS a path from the edge up to the goal, and `add_target` takes the max over goals. On the shape the node names as where ready-order loses worst — a 12-deep chain beside a 48-wide fanout, fanout declared first so ready-order would take it first — Ronin at `-j4` starts `c00` before any `w`, exactly as stock Ninja does. Nothing had to be built for that.\n\nSECOND, AND THIS IS THE ONE THAT MATTERED: THE PINNED ORACLE DOES NOT USE BUILD-LOG DURATIONS. The node says to implement \"log-informed durations where a build log exists\". Ninja tried that and threw it away before it shipped. On the PR branch there was `AssignEdgeRuntime(BuildLog*)`, `run_time_ms`, a 75th-percentile duration for edges with no log entry, and a per-target ordering bias; commit 29fe3ef, \"Simplify scheduler to not use build log/execution time\", deleted all of it, and 460dff8 merged what was left. The oracle at b51a1e3 (1.14.0.git) has no `BuildLog*` in `Plan` at all. What ships is `EdgeWeightHeuristic`: a phony edge is worth 0, everything else 1, relaxed once backwards over a topological order. So the design the node asked for would have moved Ronin AWAY from the tool the conformance suite compares it against. It is not built, and the reason is written into `CriticalPathWeight`'s doc comment so the next person does not re-derive it.\n\nWHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG, both found by doing the verification the node demanded instead of the implementation it proposed.\n\n(1) PHONY EDGES COST ONE, AND NINJA SPENDS ZERO ON THEM. Ronin incremented the weight per graph hop; Ninja adds `is_phony() ? 0 : 1`. So Ronin's weight counted bridges, not commands, and a branch reached through more phony edges outranked a branch with more real work. Constructed to show it: branch A is four commands with no bridge, branch B is two commands under four phony bridges. Counting commands, A is 4 and B is 2. Counting hops, A is 5 and B is 7 — so the tool started the SHORT branch and left the long one running alone at the end, which is the precise failure prioritising the critical path exists to prevent, produced by the heuristic meant to prevent it. At `-j1` on that manifest: stock ninja `a0 a1 a2 b0 a3 b1`, Ronin before `b0 b1 a0 a1 a2 a3`, Ronin after `a0 a1 a2 b0 a3 b1` — byte-identical to the oracle.\n\nWeights now match Ninja's numerically, sentinel included: the arena's \"not computed yet\" is `-1` as Ninja's is, because a phony edge with no consumer legitimately weighs 0 and zero can no longer double as absence. `ninja_plan_priority_without_build_log`, which is Ronin's port of Ninja's own `PlanTest.PriorityWithoutBuildLog` and asserts `out=1 a0=2 b0=2 c0=2 a1=3`, passes UNCHANGED — the first attempt shifted every weight by one and that test caught it, which is why the sentinel was worth the trouble.\n\n(2) A SERIAL MAKE RUN — WHICH IS THE DEFAULT `make` INVOCATION — DID NOT RUN GNU'S ORDER. Ninja prioritises the critical path at every job count and its own `-j1` does too, verified: Ronin and stock ninja agree at `-j1`. GNU Make is different. `update_goal_chain` walks the goals in turn, `update_file` walks each prerequisite list left to right, and a target runs after what is under it — a fixed order, at one job, which makefiles with an undeclared dependency quietly rely on. Ronin's heap overrode it. On `all: a b c` with `b: b1`, GNU runs `a b1 b c` and Ronin ran `b1 a b c`, because `b1` is deeper and the weight pulled it forward. No `-j` at all, the ordinary invocation. Now it matches.\n\nThe fix is that where the front end is Make AND the run asked for one job, the priority goes flat and the queue's existing tie key decides alone. Nothing new computes the order, and that is the point — see the decision entry on this node for why a computed walk was tried, why the corpus refused it, and what the identifier already carries.\n\nEVIDENCE.\n\nNinja mode, wall clock, the mechanism isolated: a 10-command chain beside 40 branches of one command under 12 phony bridges, `-j4`, every command a 0.3 s sleep so the number is scheduling and not the host. Interleaved, rotating order, five repetitions, load 11.5 throughout — and the spread is +-0.01 s BECAUSE it is sleep-bound, which is the whole reason the fixture is built that way.\n\n  stock ninja    3.96 s\n  Ronin before   6.08 s\n  Ronin after    3.96 s      1.54x, and identical to the oracle\n\nFrom the build log on the same fixture: total 6082 ms -> 3953 ms, 50 commands both sides, tail (last command start to build end) 304 ms -> 304 ms. The tail is unchanged and the total is not, which says exactly what happened: nothing about the last command moved, the long pole simply stopped being scheduled last.\n\nvim and zsh clean builds: NO WALL-CLOCK A/B IS QUOTED, because the schedule barely moves and a wall figure would be reporting the host. Compared instead where it is exact — `make -n -B -j8`, the whole clean-build schedule as commands in scheduling order, both arms invoked through ONE path so the binary's own path cannot show up in a recipe:\n\n  zsh      427 lines, schedule byte-identical before and after\n  vim/src  475 lines, exactly two of 156 commands swap places\n\nThe two that swap in vim are positions 1 and 2 — `cd auto/wayland; make` and the `osdef.sh` generator — both configuration steps that run before any compile starts. No compile and no link edge moves in either project. So the clean-build tail latency cannot have changed, and that is a stronger statement than fifteen repetitions of a noisy difference would have been. It also says plainly that this change is not a speedup for vim or zsh: those graphs are a flat fanout of compiles into one link, every compile the same height, and a heuristic that counts commands has nothing to separate them by. Where it pays is graphs with unequal chains, which is what the synthetic is.\n\nGATES. `cargo test --all-targets --no-fail-fast` 891/0 (888 + the three added); `--workspace --all-targets` 1207/0 (1204 + three). Make equivalence 274 makefiles / 276 graphs / 8 respellings / 56 refused / 0 disagreements, TWENTY runs, byte-identical summary on every one. Make conformance 354 runs, 276 identical raw, 326 normalised, 28 differing. Upstream 18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 20 interface. Port ledger 170. Ninja conformance 425/425 upstream, 10 differential, 92 build-outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence round trip passed, log content matched — console pool and `-j1` ordering ride in there. `cargo fmt`, `scripts/check-lints.sh` and `cargo doc` clean, no new warnings.\n\nONE CORPUS CASE FAILED ON THE WAY AND IT IS THE MOST USEFUL THING THAT HAPPENED — `shuffled-goals-are-built-back-to-front`, in the decision entry."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T01:51:13.614625313Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE PERF GATES, AND WHY ONLY ONE OF THEM HAS A CLEAN ANSWER. Said plainly rather than buried, because the node's own constraint was that BOTH must exit 0 and one of them has not.\n\nWHAT THE HOST WAS DOING THE WHOLE TIME. Another campaign — nplan silo-5bf32a6a, the `backends.*` worktrees — held this machine between 4.5 and 42 for the entire node. A watcher was armed at the start and its minimum over the first half hour was 5.72; it never once reached the 4.00 that `scripts/check-make-performance.sh` measures at. That gate has its own quiet-host guard and it refused, twice, in the words it is supposed to: \"one-minute load average is still 5.14 after waiting 300 s, above the 4.00 this gate will measure at. Wall time from a busy machine is not a measurement.\" That refusal is the gate working, and it is not being argued with.\n\n  scripts/check-performance.sh   exit 0 at load 10.96 (pre-refactor binary)\n                                 exit 1 at load 10.54 (final binary), on dependency-log-load\n  scripts/check-make-performance.sh  refused at load 11.04 and again at 5.14, quiet-host guard\n\nA combined retry runs detached and fires both gates the moment the one-minute average goes under 3.5. It had not caught a window when this was written.\n\nTHE ONE FAILURE, RUN DOWN RATHER THAN WAVED AT. `dependency-log-load` is a four-millisecond workload about reading `.ninja_deps`; nothing in this change is within reach of it. The gate compares Ronin against pinned Ninja and then against a ratio recorded on a quiet host, allowing 120% of it — 0.73x recorded, so 0.876x is the line. Measured directly, same host, same minutes, both binaries through `--ronin`, fifteen repetitions each, two alternating passes at load 8.8:\n\n    2ad7da8 (before)  ronin 3.650 / ninja 4.604 = 0.793      ronin 3.598 / ninja 4.500 = 0.799\n    c4441b2 (after)   ronin 3.756 / ninja 4.732 = 0.794      ronin 3.797 / ninja 4.470 = 0.850\n\nThe binary from BEFORE this change measures the same band as the one after, and neither reproduces the recorded 0.73x on a machine at load 9. The gate saw 0.86x on one pass and 0.93x on another with no code difference between them worth a thousandth. So the failure is the host, demonstrated rather than asserted — and the way to close it is a quiet window, which the retry is waiting for, not a change to anything here.\n\nWHAT CAN BE SETTLED WITHOUT A QUIET HOST, AND IS. The gated Make rows are no-op and incremental builds, and an instruction count does not care what else the machine is doing. vim-noop, the row worth 1.71x recorded, `perf stat -e instructions:u -r 10`, both binaries interleaved, two passes, on a tree settled first so neither arm is measuring the other's work:\n\n    before  103,420,905    103,402,061\n    after   103,418,682    103,433,262\n\nSpread inside an arm is 19k and 15k; the gap between arms is 2k one way and 31k the other. Means differ by 0.014%. There is no change to find.\n\nAnd the schedules themselves, which is the tighter argument because it is exact rather than statistical: `make -n -B -j8` prints the whole clean-build schedule as commands in scheduling order, and running both arms through ONE path so the binary's own location cannot appear inside a recipe, zsh's 427 lines are byte-identical and vim/src's 475 differ in exactly two — commands 1 and 2 of 156, `cd auto/wayland; make` and the `osdef.sh` generator, swapping with each other before any compile starts. A no-op build schedules nothing at all, so the ordering question does not even arise on three of the four gated rows.\n\nBetween the instruction count, the byte-identical zsh schedule and the two-command vim swap, there is no mechanism left by which a recorded Make row could move, and none of the five rows was re-recorded — correctly, since nothing that could be recorded on this host would be worth having."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T02:21:07.02300198Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE QUIET WINDOW ARRIVED AND BOTH GATES ARE GREEN. Supersedes the pending state in the note above; that note's account of the contention stands, its \"not yet caught a window\" does not.\n\nThe retry that had been armed all night caught the host on its sixteenth attempt, after fifteen refusals it logged with the load that caused each (10.04, 8.85, 6.59, 4.60, 4.41, 3.65, 5.25, 5.83, 8.86, 10.36, 10.25, 10.60, 10.24, 4.66, 3.55):\n\n  scripts/check-performance.sh        exit 0   load 2.10\n  scripts/check-make-performance.sh   exit 0   load 1.49\n\nTHE FAILURE FROM THE BUSY HOST DID NOT SURVIVE THE QUIET ONE, which is what the direct A/B had already predicted. `dependency-log-load` was the row that refused at load 10.54 with 0.93x against a 0.876x line; measured at load 2.10 it is ronin 3.303 / ninja 4.228 = 0.781x. Nothing was changed in between. The rest of the Ninja gate on that same quiet run, for the record: manifest-command-evaluation 0.540x, large-manifest-parse 0.474x, path-canonicalization 0.612x, clean-tree-noop 0.677x, dependency-log-load 0.781x, deep-graph-evaluation 0.822x, wide-noop-build 0.852x, scheduler-barrier 1.069x — that last one being the only row where Ronin is behind Ninja, and it is the row this node's subject actually touches, so it is the one to watch next.\n\nTHE MAKE ROWS, AND THEN THE SAME ROWS A/B'd AGAINST THE BINARY FROM BEFORE THE CHANGE. The gate reported wide-noop 0.60x, recursive-noop 1.03x, vim-noop 1.76x, zsh-incremental 1.12x. Two of those sit above what the node was told not to regress (recorded 0.62 / 1.01 / 1.71 / 1.03), so the ratios alone do not settle whether this change caused it. Both binaries were therefore run through the harness on the quiet host, `--ronin-make` pointed at each, nine repetitions, BASE first then NEW:\n\n                      2ad7da8 (before)   c4441b2 (after)   recorded\n    wide-noop              0.62x             0.56x           0.62x\n    recursive-noop         1.04x             0.99x           1.01x\n    vim-noop               1.72x             1.72x           1.71x\n    zsh-incremental        1.09x             1.11x           1.03x\n\nThe binary from BEFORE this change already measures 1.09x on zsh against a recorded 1.03x, so that gap is the recording not reproducing on today's tree, not something this change did. What is attributable here is 1.09 to 1.11 — two hundredths on a 1.4-second workload, against a gate run that put the same arm at 1.12, so it is inside the noise floor. vim-noop is identical to the thousandth, and wide-noop and recursive-noop are both BETTER after. The two arms ran sequentially rather than interleaved and the load climbed from 1.19 to 4.66 across the pair, so the NEW arm ran under the worse half of the window — which makes the two improvements more credible and the zsh two-hundredths less.\n\nNothing was re-recorded. No row moved for a reason in this change, and the row that most disagrees with its recording disagrees identically without it."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id an-unlifted-recursion-brings-its-own-job-budget
        title "Share one job budget with a child the graph could not lift"
        kind @fix
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-30T22:10:54.610415373Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "FILED 2026-08-31 on the operator's direction. Open question, cheap to answer, filed as fix because the likely defect is a compat one: when a $(MAKE) invocation cannot be composed statically, a real child ronin runs. GNU Make coordinates -j across the whole tree through one jobserver (--jobserver-auth in MAKEFLAGS); if Ronin's escape-path child brings its own -j8 instead of joining the parent's budget, kernel-shaped builds oversubscribe the machine — and a GNU child make invoked UNDER ronin (mixed trees) is entitled to read a jobserver from MAKEFLAGS too. Verify both directions: (a) child ronin under parent ronin — one budget or two? (b) GNU make under ronin — is a jobserver offered, and must one be per make compat? (c) ronin under GNU make — do we JOIN an offered jobserver, which GNU's own sub-makes are required to? The census/carried-switches machinery already threads MAKEFLAGS; the question is whether job tokens ride it. Answer with process-count evidence under a constrained -j, then fix whichever directions are wrong. Cheap to check inside whichever runtime node runs first."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T01:08:10.108992338Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "ANSWERED WITH PROCESS COUNTS, from the scheduler node (the-scheduler-picks-ready-edges-without-looking-down-the-graph), which ran first as queued. Findings only; nothing fixed here — see the design at the end and why it is not small.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT. A parent makefile with twelve 0.25s recipes and one recursive line placed FIRST in the prerequisite list, a child makefile with eight more of the same, every recipe bracketing its sleep with a flock'd counter that records the peak number in flight. Run at -j2 throughout, so a shared budget shows as 2 and a doubled one shows as 3 — the parent spends one of its two slots on the child make process itself, leaving one for its own recipes beside the child's two. Oracle is reference/make-oracle/make-4.4.1/make. The recursion is unliftable by construction (`@if true; then $(MAKE) -C sub; fi`), and confirmed to fork a real child: the child prints its own pid and its own rewritten MAKEFLAGS.\n\n  (a) child ronin under parent ronin, Make mode      peak 3     GNU->GNU reference: peak 2\n  (b) GNU make under parent ronin, Make mode         peak 3     GNU->GNU reference: peak 2\n  (c) ronin under GNU make -j2                       peak 2     GNU->GNU reference: peak 2\n\n(a) TWO BUDGETS, and the same for any child whatever it is. Make mode sets `options.serve_jobserver = false` outright (src/make/cli.rs:1539) — the rationale beside it is that recursive Make units are already compiled into the graph and use the Ninja scheduler directly, which is true of every recursion that CAN be lifted and false of exactly the ones that cannot. What the child receives is `MAKEFLAGS=\" -j2 --no-print-directory -- ...\"`: the width, with no authorization behind it. The child re-reads `-j2` as `ArgumentSource::Inherited` (src/make/cli.rs:493), which lands in `inherited_jobs` rather than `jobs` and so becomes the unshared fallback (src/make/cli.rs:1530) — an independent 2-wide scheduler. N wide at depth d is N*d.\n\n(b) NO JOBSERVER IS OFFERED, and GNU is entitled to one. Same mechanism as (a) and the same number; a GNU child reads the `-j2` it was handed and runs two of its own. It does not warn, because there is no unusable auth for it to complain about — there is no auth at all. `.FEATURES` advertises `jobserver` and `jobserver-fifo` in Make mode regardless (src/make/cli.rs:1428), which is a makefile-visible claim the mode does not honour.\n\n(c) JOINING WORKS, and it is the one direction that is right. `normalize_runtime_options` (src/cli.rs:869) attaches an inherited transport whenever no explicit `-j` was typed on this argv and this is not a dry run, then sets `jobs = Unlimited` so the tokens are the only limit; Make mode reaches it at src/make/cli.rs:1519. All three spellings parse (src/jobserver.rs:59): `--jobserver-auth=R,W`, `=fifo:PATH`, the Win32 name, and the legacy `--jobserver-fds=`. Measured: GNU -j2 -> ronin holds at 2, and the FIFO GNU published was visible in the MAKEFLAGS ronin received.\n\nA FOURTH THING, NOT ASKED FOR AND WORSE THAN ANY OF THEM. The budget ronin joins in (c) stops at ronin. Three levels deep:\n\n  GNU -j2 -> GNU -> GNU     peak 2\n  GNU -j2 -> ronin -> GNU   peak 3\n\nMake mode rebuilds MAKEFLAGS for recipe environments from its own switch census (src/make/cli/interface.rs:222, `compiler_flag_variables`), and the comment at :216 says the jobserver authorization is deliberately absent. So a grandchild under a joined ronin is handed the width with no tokens and runs unconstrained — the outer tool's budget is silently doubled by a tool in the middle that was itself well behaved. Worth saying plainly because (c) looks correct measured one level down and is not correct measured two. Note also BUGS.md:565-568 still describes a splice that re-inserts an inherited auth into a rewritten MAKEFLAGS; that code exists (`Transport::publish_into`, src/jobserver.rs:242-264) and is unit-tested (src/jobserver.rs:851), but its only production call site is src/build.rs:2151, which runs on an empty base env and skips the splice for an inherited transport. The BUGS.md claim describes the state before 6c20a85.\n\nTHE DESIGN, AND WHY IT IS NOT SMALL. The leak in the fourth finding is the cheapest and most clearly right: Make mode should splice an inherited `--jobserver-auth` back into the MAKEFLAGS it hands recipes, reusing the splice that is already written and tested. That is a change to MAKEFLAGS bytes, and MAKEFLAGS bytes are read by makefiles and compared by the conformance corpus, so it needs the corpus run behind it rather than a one-line edit — small, but not small enough to ride along inside a scheduler node's diff without its own evidence.\n\n(a) and (b) are one change and it is not small at all: Make mode would have to SERVE a jobserver for the recursions it could not lift, which is the second scheduling mechanism [spec:ronin:req:make.jobserver+1] currently forbids in as many words (\"Ronin does not create a GNU Make jobserver for recursive Make execution\"). Doing it right means serving only when the graph actually holds an unliftable recursion — the census already knows which ones those are (src/make/parallel.rs:726, `liftable_recursions`) — so the common case keeps one scheduler and no FIFO, and only a tree that genuinely forks pays for a token server. That is a spec amendment plus a conditional server plus the `.FEATURES` claim in (b) becoming true, and it wants its own node.\n\nEvery number above is peak concurrent recipes, not wall time; the host was loaded and it does not matter, because what is being counted is how many processes were allowed to exist at once."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T01:55:16.799360246Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T02:55:34.018810297Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "FIXED, ALL FOUR PAIRINGS AT THE GNU BASELINE. Peak concurrent recipes under -j2, flock'd counter, same harness the diagnosis node used:\n\n                            before   after   GNU->GNU baseline\n  (a) ronin -> ronin           3       2            2\n  (b) ronin -> GNU             3       2            2\n  (c) GNU   -> ronin           2       2            2\n  (d) GNU -> ronin -> GNU      3       2            2   (GNU->GNU->GNU: 2)\n      ronin -> ronin -> ronin  -       2            2\n\nTHE DESIGN IS GNU'S OWN, AND IT IS ONE MECHANISM FOR ALL FOUR. GNU holds a single `jobserver_auth` string: `main` either validates the one a parent published or replaces it with the one this run created, and `define_makeflags` republishes whatever is left. Ronin now does exactly that. `Invocation::jobserver_auth` is a switch-table field; `--jobserver-auth` and `--jobserver-fds` write it (one variable between them, as GNU's table has it, republished under the new spelling alone because the legacy row has `toenv` clear); `settle_jobserver` (src/make/cli.rs) settles it after `normalize_runtime_options` and before the read; `compiler_flag_variables` emits it between `--debug` and `--trace`, which is where GNU's switch table puts it. Three outcomes, GNU's three: joined -> republish the address unchanged; not joined and more than one slot -> `Transport::serve`, publish our own; one slot, a bare `-j`, or a dry run -> nothing.\n\nTOKENS ARE ACQUIRED AND RELEASED WHERE THEY ALREADY WERE. Nothing in build.rs changed. The served fifo holds `j-1` tokens; `JobserverClient` gives the scheduler the implicit slot for its first concurrent edge and a token for every one past it (build.rs:2261); a forked child reads the address out of MAKEFLAGS and draws on the same fifo. Composed recursions cost no token beyond the slot their edge already holds, so the in-process path is unchanged and still takes no lock per edge — `maxjobs` and the token count agree at `j`, so the scheduler never asks for a slot it cannot have and never starves in the common case. What was wrong was one line: Make mode set `serve_jobserver = false` and the machinery below it had nothing to serve.\n\nTHE MAKEFLAGS SPELLING, AND WHAT THE CORPUS SAID. GNU's bytes, verbatim: ` -j2 --jobserver-auth=fifo:<path> --no-print-directory`, in MAKEFLAGS and MFLAGS alike, one word so a `$(patsubst --jobserver-auth=%,...)` sees one word. The corpus said the byte question does not arise where it was feared: the kati conformance corpus never passes `-j` at all (examples/make_conformance.rs:445-478) and strips MAKEFLAGS from the environment; the build-intent corpus has 21 `-j` cases and not one dumps `$(MAKEFLAGS)` above `-j1` — the ones that touch it use `$(findstring)`; equivalence executes nothing. Conformance came back 354/326/28/276, unmoved. The gate that DID move is upstream discovery, and it moved the right way.\n\nTWO THINGS GNU DOES THAT THE FIRST CUT DID NOT, both found by upstream's own suite and both now done: a jobserver that cannot be created is not a refusal (GNU falls back from fifo to pipe and builds; Ronin has no second style, so it falls back to its own scheduler and publishes no address — the same forgiveness the inherited side already had), and the fifo goes in `MAKE_TMPDIR` before `TMPDIR` before `/tmp`, GNU's `get_tmpdir` order, passing over a name that does not stat as a directory.\n\nDIVERGENCE ACCEPTED AND WRITTEN INTO THE RULE: the address is settled BEFORE the read, where GNU settles it after. Ronin bakes each unit's MAKEFLAGS into the recipes it emits, so it cannot be later. Two consequences: a `$(MAKEFLAGS)` expanded while the makefile is being read carries the address where GNU's does not yet, and a makefile's own `MAKEFLAGS += -jN` cannot resize a budget that already exists. What a recipe finally reads is GNU's value.\n\nAlso measured and left alone: under `--jobserver-style=pipe` GNU appends `--jobserver-auth=-2,-2` to a line it does not think recursive, and its child answers with `jobserver unavailable: using -j1`. Ronin reads the dead pair, drops it, and masters its own group at the width it was told — the pre-existing `stale_jobserver_uses_local_scheduler` behaviour, which this change improves rather than introduces (that chain went from as much as 5 concurrent to 3). Not GNU's -j1 clamp; a separate question."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T02:56:01.850160824Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "GATES. Equivalence 274/276/8/56/0, and `one_graph_however_many_threads_read_it` 20/20 in bulk (200 compilations, the schedule is 10 loads per invocation) — unaffected because it drives `load_makefile` directly and never reaches the CLI's runtime options, which is exactly why the address is settled there and not in the compiler. Conformance 354/326/28/276 unmoved (defect 17 / recorded 2 / extension 9 / artefact 0). Port ledger 170. Ninja 425/425 + 10 + 92 + 3, plus the log round trip. `cargo test --all-targets` 892/0 and `--workspace --all-targets` 1208/0, one more than the recorded 891/1207: the new regression test. Real projects: vim in 18s, zsh + 36 modules in 29s, ninja's own CMake Makefile in 27s, all at -j8 with the budget now in play.\n\nUPSTREAM MOVED FROM 18/0/20 TO 18/0/15, five rows and every one a win. `features/jobserver.diff`, `.1`, `.2`, `.3` went interface -> narration: each prints `$(MAKEFLAGS)` in a parent and a recursive child through the suite's own `$(patsubst --jobserver-auth=%,--jobserver-auth=<auth>,...)`, the values now agree word for word, and what is left is Ninja's progress line and the recipe echo. `.15` left the inventory entirely — `MAKE_TMPDIR=.` beside `TMPDIR=nosuchdir` asserting `/--jobserver-auth=fifo:\\./`, which now passes outright. Unclassified stayed at 18 and compiler at 0.\n\nThe classifier had to move with it, and honestly. `jobserver-auth` recognised its case by deleting the switch from GNU's line alone, which was right while only GNU wrote one and wrong the moment both did. Both sides' addresses are now anonymised to `<auth>` first — the value names a temporary file minted per process and there is nothing byte-comparable in it — and the one-sided deletion fires only where Ronin published none. `jobserver-narration` picked up GNU's two `TMPDIR value X` / `using default temporary directory` lines, which are the same category as the three it already held. Two new classifier unit tests pin both halves: two tools that both published agree whatever the files are called, and a `-j2` against a `-j4` beside them is still unexplained.\n\nBOTH PERFORMANCE GATES PASSED ON A QUIET HOST, load 3.39 at the start of the make gate. check-make-performance.sh (warmups 1, repetitions 9, -j8, interleaved): wide-noop 0.63x against 0.618x recorded, recursive-noop 0.95x against 1.009x, vim-noop 1.70x against 1.710x, zsh-incremental 0.88x against 1.033x — three of the four at or better than recorded, none near the 120% ceiling. wide-noop is 4000 targets at -j8 and is where a per-edge token would show; it did not, because a token is one non-blocking read on a fifo against a process spawn. check-performance.sh (warmups 2, repetitions 15) passed its three thresholds unchanged; Ninja mode was not touched.\n\nTHIS BRIEF'S ERROR, and it was in the hard constraints rather than the diagnosis. \"Graph byte-identical\" and \"MAKEFLAGS bytes are corpus-compared ... before deciding the spelling\" cannot both hold: in Make mode the recipe's MAKEFLAGS IS graph command text, baked as an `exec env 'MAKEFLAGS=...'` prefix, so any address a child can read is a per-process path inside the graph. The brief read those as two constraints on one artefact and they are constraints on two — the compiled graph a test compares across thread counts, and the command text a run emits. The reconciliation is that `one_graph_however_many_threads_read_it` compares graphs from `load_makefile`, which never sees the runtime options, so the graph it pins is genuinely identical while the graph a `-j8` run emits carries an address that changes every run. Had that not been true the whole design would have had to move to the executor boundary, where `Launch::Shell` — every makefile that sets `SHELL := /bin/bash` — would have been unreachable. Worth saying because the next brief in this area will pose the same pair."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id siphash-still-hashes-a-byte-a-word-and-a-name
        title "Take SipHash off the maps the Symbol pass did not reach"
        kind @perf
        deps (the-symbol-maps-hash-an-index-with-siphash)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:27:05.926154395Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:27:22.33664714Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "FOUR TABLES THE SYMBOL PASS DID NOT REACH, and three of them are hotter per lookup than the thirty-nine it did. `RuleTrie::children` keyed by ONE BYTE, walked a level per byte of every implicit-rule candidate (`RuleTrie::get` alone was 1.12% of the vim `src` sub-make). `Evaluator::expanding_vars`, the recursion guard keyed by a `Var`'s address, asked on every expansion. `FUNC_INFO_MAP`, read by name for every `$(...)` the parser sees. And two `HashSet<Symbol>` built per node inside `populate_ninja_node`, 4,057 nodes a run, only to ask `contains`. On Ronin's side, `make/sink.rs` held eight `std::collections` maps keyed by `RuleId` (a `usize`) and `Symbol`: `hash_one::<&u64>` was 1.38% and `hash_one::<&Symbol>` 0.60% of the run, both instantiated in this crate rather than in kati.\n\nWHAT WAS DELIBERATELY LEFT ALONE, said because the next person will see it and wonder: `fileutil`'s glob cache. `FileCache::recorded()` iterates it into the regeneration stamp, so its ITERATION ORDER IS AN OUTPUT — a faster hasher there changes stamp bytes. The entries are few and the lookup is cold. Same question asked of every table converted: `children` is read by `len` (a sum) and `retain`, the ninja sets are membership only, the rest are lookups.\n\nWORTH -2.66%: 87.666M instructions to 85.335M on the vim `src` sub-make, `perf stat -e instructions:u -r 20`. Ratio against GNU Make 4.4.1 on that sub-make 2.010x to 1.956x.\n\nTHE FORMAT GATE WAS ALREADY RED WHEN THIS STARTED. `cargo fmt --all -- --check` fails at kati aa64baa — verified in a worktree at that revision, eight files, all of them the `use crate::fasthash::...` line the `Symbol` pass inserted in the wrong sort position. So 659ac76 landed with the check failing and nothing said. Fixed in the same kati commit as this change rather than filed, because it is one command; the point worth keeping is that a red format gate survived a whole node."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:27:30.706075184Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id the-expression-scan-questions-every-literal-byte
        title "Step the expression scan over the text no arm of it can act on"
        kind @perf
        deps (siphash-still-hashes-a-byte-a-word-and-a-name)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:28:57.723663282Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:29:17.347016183Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE TOP SELF-TIME SYMBOL IN THE PROFILE WAS A BYTE LOOP. `parse_expr_impl_ext` at 4.95% of the vim `src` sub-make, ahead of `_int_malloc` and of `build_plan`, and doing nothing complicated: read a byte, ask up to six questions of it, `i += 1`. Around 500k iterations a run at ~6.6 cycles each. A makefile is mostly text where all six answers are no.\n\nWHAT MAKES THE SKIP SAFE, because a parser is where a wrong optimisation is silent. The set of bytes an arm can act on is a pure function of four things — `opt`, `terms`, `terms_ignored`, `save_paren` — and the last two move at exactly two statements in the loop, both of which now recompute. The set has to be a SUPERSET of what the arms match, never a subset: an extra stop costs one iteration that decides to do nothing, a missing one reads a `$` as literal text. Enumerated arm by arm: the terminator break, the `#` comment (only when `terms.is_none()`), `$`, `(`/`{` under `Func`, the `save_paren` close, and `\\\\` under anything but `Command`. Plain newlines are NOT special here -- `loc.line` only moves inside the two fold arms, both reached through `$` or `\\\\` -- so skipping cannot lose a line number.\n\n`memchr` for a set of three or fewer, a 256-bit table above that. The two common shapes fall in the fast half: `Normal` stops on `$ # \\\\`, `Command` on `$` alone.\n\n`Symtab::intern` came with it because it is the same mistake in a different place: it did `s.into()` to get a `Bytes` BEFORE the lookup, so a caller passing `&str`, `&[u8]` or `Vec` bought an allocation and a copy to ask a question whose answer is usually \"already interned\". Probing borrowed and converting only on a miss.\n\nWORTH -4.0%: 85.335M to 81.926M instructions on the vim `src` sub-make. `parse_expr_impl_ext` 4.95% self to 2.23%. Equivalence 274/276/8/56/0, `$(.VARIABLES)` byte-identical, `make -n -B` identical over vim `src` (475 lines) and zsh (427).\n\nWHAT IS LEFT IN THAT FUNCTION, for whoever comes next: it is now allocation-bound rather than scan-bound. 8.43% inclusive against 2.72% self, the difference being one `Arc<Value>` per fragment and a `Vec<Arc<Value>>` per list. A parse of a line with no `$` still costs one `Vec` allocation and one `Arc`. An inline first-element slot, or an evaluation-scoped arena for the `Value` tree, is where the next cut is -- and `drop_glue<Value>` is 1.98% on its own."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:29:21.619424483Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id the-shell-costs-more-to-build-than-the-command-costs-to-run
        title "Take upstream's cheaper shell construction, six invocations a build"
        kind @perf
        deps (the-c-librarys-allocator-is-a-sixth-of-a-make-run)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:56:03.746480965Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:56:23.214767069Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE NUMBER `the-symbol-maps-hash-an-index-with-siphash` HANDED UPSTREAM, taken. That node measured `nsh::builder::Builder::build` at 47.78% INCLUSIVE of every `sh -c` this binary runs as its own shell and filed it rather than working around it, per [dec:ronin:builtin-shell]. It is fixed upstream and arrives here as a lock bump and nothing else: cd93991 to 9c3f343.\n\nWHAT UPSTREAM FOUND, because the number alone did not say it. `Locale::is_alpha` and `is_alphanumeric` wrap the C library's `isalpha`/`isalnum` in a `uselocale` in and a `uselocale` back — per byte. `valid_name` asks per byte of a name, the environment import asks `valid_name` per variable, and `set_entry` asks it AGAIN for the same name, so importing fifty variables was on the order of a thousand locale selections establishing that ordinary letters are letters. A locale object is immutable after `newlocale`, so the answer is a property of the handle: it is now a 256-entry table filled under one selection, lazily, because building a shell constructs seven locales and keeps one. Beside it, `set_entry` cloned the whole variable — value included — to read three flags, then inserted it over itself with a fresh copy of the name; and `Locale::new` issued a separate `newlocale` per category, so a shell selecting all six through one `LANG` loaded the same locale data six times.\n\nPAIRED, both arms identical but for the lock rev, both through this binary's multicall so the allocator is the same on each side, `perf stat -e instructions:u`:\n\n  sh -c 'true'                          816,940 -> 663,912   -18.7%\n  if test -n \"\" -a -f po/Makefile; ...  890,314 -> 738,759   -17.0%\n  dash 0.5.13.5, sh -c 'true'           217,423\n\nAbout 152k instructions of constant off every shell this tool starts. Six process images a gated vim no-op are shells, so roughly 0.9M of that row; a build whose recipes are one line each pays it once per line.\n\nA FOURTH CUT WAS MEASURED AND NOT TAKEN, upstream's decision and worth knowing here: taking the environment snapshot's bytes out of the owned `OsString` rather than copying them is worth about 10k an invocation, and it added a function to a file nsh's `function-density` gate holds at its current count. It needs that file split first.\n\nSTILL 3.0x DASH. What is left, from a profile of the new shell: `__memcmp_avx2_movbe` at 7.6% is `BTreeMap<BString, Variable>` probing — `set_bytes` takes four probes per variable and two of them exist only to discover that an ordinary name carries no Bash attributes. An 8,209-byte zeroed input frame and ten `fcntl` descriptor snapshots are allocated and never read by a `-c` invocation."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:56:31.402983937Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id the-c-librarys-allocator-is-a-sixth-of-a-make-run
        title "Install an allocator built for what evaluating a Makefile allocates"
        kind @perf
        deps (the-expression-scan-questions-every-literal-byte)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:34:27.307351023Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:34:49.227167023Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "THE ONE THE CAMPAIGN HAS BEEN PARKING, unparked by the operator and written up as [dec:ronin:allocator-is-not-the-c-librarys].\n\nWHY THERE WAS NOTHING ELSE TO DO. A flat profile of the vim `src` sub-make after the two cuts before this one: `free` 4.56%, `_int_malloc` 3.96%, `malloc_consolidate` 2.99%, `malloc` 2.66%, `_int_free_chunk` 1.57%, `unlink_chunk` 1.57%, `realloc` 0.91%, `_int_realloc` 0.71% — 18.9% of user cycles, and the DWARF call graph attributes them to dozens of callers with none above 1%. `parse_expr_impl_ext` is the largest at 0.86% of `_int_malloc`. There is no site to fix. The traffic IS the work: an expression tree per line, a dependency node per target, a name per symbol, built and dropped inside a process that lives milliseconds. `malloc_consolidate` at 3.0% is glibc merging free chunks back into its bins and is pure overhead of that shape.\n\nTHE THREE-WAY MEASUREMENT, same tree, same host, `perf stat -e instructions:u -r 20`, vim-noop from the top which is the gated row:\n\n  GNU Make 4.4.1     48.658M    1.000x\n  glibc             104.3M      2.14x\n  jemalloc           93.986M    1.932x\n  mimalloc           89.037M    1.830x\n\nand the vim `src` sub-make, 80% of that row's gap: glibc 81.926M, jemalloc 75.404M, mimalloc 71.608M. jemalloc was measured because kati already vendors it behind an off-by-default feature, so it was the dependency already in the audited set; it is better than glibc on both and worse than mimalloc on both.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS, AND IT IS NOT NOTHING. `sh -c 'true'` goes 437.7k instructions to 653.8k, +49%. All of it is mimalloc's `_mi_options_init`, which walks about fifty options on process load calling `getenv` once or twice each, and each call scans the whole environment with a case-insensitive compare — so the constant rises with the size of the environment the recipe inherited. Profiled to be sure: `getenv` 2.28%, `_mi_strnicmp` 2.07%, `_mi_strlcpy` 1.21% of the shell's run. Nothing in the shell's own work got slower, and there is no compile-time switch for it in the vendored sources. Ronin runs as its own `/bin/sh` once per recipe line, so this is the decision's weak point and it is written into the record rather than left to be discovered.\n\nEvery workload measured still comes out ahead, including vim-noop, which starts SEVEN shells and improves by 15.2M instructions net of all of them.\n\nTWO THINGS THE CHANGE BROKE THAT THE MEASUREMENT DID NOT SHOW, both found by the gates. `scripts/check-ninja-conformance.sh` type-checks the library for `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu`, and mimalloc is the only dependency here that compiles C: an unqualified `[dependencies]` entry made that gate die in `cc-rs` looking for `x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc`. It is under `[target.'cfg(unix)'.dependencies]` beside `kati` and `nsh` for that reason, with `#[cfg(unix)]` on the static. And the closure had to be proven proc-macro-free for crt-static — `cargo tree -e normal,build` finds no `syn`, `quote` or `proc-macro2`, and the release build succeeding under `+crt-static` is the harder proof."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:34:57.794168123Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T00:17:03.000272585Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @errata
                text "THIS NODE'S WORK WAS LANDED AND THEN REVERTED IN THE SAME SESSION, and the reason is the finding.\n\n`scripts/check-performance.sh` — the NINJA-mode gate — refuses mimalloc twice over, and this campaign had not been running it because the campaign is about Make mode. It samples each coordinator's peak RSS from `/proc` and rejects above 200% of Ninja's, and it rejects a Ronin/Ninja wall ratio above 120% of the recorded one. On `clean-tree-noop`, all four arms measured in the same window on the same host:\n\n  build               wall      Ronin/Ninja   peak RSS     of Ninja\n  glibc (recorded)    8.747 ms    0.675x       4,596 KiB     81%\n  jemalloc, tuned     9.536 ms    0.75x        8,180 KiB    145%\n  jemalloc, default   8.954 ms    0.70x       11,996 KiB    212%\n  mimalloc           10.538 ms    0.84x       23,640 KiB    402%\n\nThe wall refusal is a consequence of the footprint one: these workloads run for eight milliseconds, so pages an allocator commits before doing any work are a measurable fraction of the run. mimalloc's RSS is FIVE TIMES the C library's across every Ninja workload — manifest-command-evaluation 27,372 KiB against 11,888 for jemalloc, wide-noop 21,488, canonicalization 19,308.\n\nThe tuning was tried rather than assumed. jemalloc with `narenas:2,dirty_decay_ms:0,muzzy_decay_ms:0` — a build tool's lifetime instead of a server's — brings clean-tree-noop to 8,180 KiB, but the wide no-op still sits at 11,868 against Ninja's 5,924, which is 200.3%. No margin. Note for whoever tries this again: the symbol tikv-jemallocator reads is `_rjem_malloc_conf`, not `malloc_conf`; exporting the unprefixed name silently does nothing and the RSS does not move.\n\nWHAT THE MEASUREMENT IS STILL WORTH, because it was not wasted. On the gated vim no-op, `perf stat -e instructions:u -r 20`, GNU Make 4.4.1 at 48.658M: glibc 103.205M (2.120x), jemalloc 93.986M (1.932x), mimalloc 87.938M (1.806x). The allocator is genuinely 18.9% of a Make-mode evaluation's user cycles and 14.6% of the gated row is genuinely available there. It is simply not available at this price. That makes the number an argument FOR the representation change rather than a substitute for it: removing the allocations removes the allocator's share of them and costs nothing in footprint.\n\n[dec:ronin:allocator-is-not-the-c-librarys] is rewritten from @approved to @rejected with all four arms in it, rather than deleted, so that the next person to profile this reaches the record before the experiment. `the-allocator-swap-was-refused-by-the-ninja-gate` carries the follow-up.\n\nAND THE PROCESS FINDING. The Make campaign has run `check-make-equivalence`, `check-make-conformance`, `check-make-upstream` and `check-make-performance` for forty nodes and never `check-performance.sh`, because Make mode does not touch Ninja mode. A change to the BINARY — the allocator, the linkage, a global — is not a Make change or a Ninja change, and the only gate that caught this one was the mode nobody was working in."
                after_done true
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id the-allocator-swap-was-refused-by-the-ninja-gate
        title "Reduce the allocation itself, since replacing the allocator costs footprint"
        kind @perf
        deps (the-c-librarys-allocator-is-a-sixth-of-a-make-run)
        children (
            {
                id symtab-name-hands-out-an-owner-where-a-borrow-would-do
                title "Return the interned name borrowed, not as a second Bytes owner"
                kind @perf
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-31T17:43:24.42278619Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "FILED 2026-08-31 from the profile on `the-allocator-swap-was-refused-by-the-ninja-gate`, as the first of the three representation leads that node's log carried. It is filed as its own node because the allocator swap that was supposed to make these unnecessary has now been measured twice and refused twice — see that node's errata of this date — so removing the allocations is the only lever left, not the slow alternative to a fast one.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE. Flat profile of the vim `src` sub-make at the landed revision, `perf record -e cycles:u -c 4000000` over 150 runs:\n\n    glibc malloc/free/consolidate/realloc  ~18%   (no caller above 1%)\n    bytes shared_drop + shared_clone         7.55%\n    DepBuilder::build_plan                   4.58%\n    parse_expr_impl_ext                      3.72%\n    memmove + memcmp                         4.15%\n    Symtab::intern                           2.53%\n\nThe allocator's share and the reference-counting share are one problem seen twice: every `Bytes` handed out is an atomic pair, and every one that is not a slice of something already alive is a malloc. `bytes shared_drop + shared_clone` at 7.55% is this node's share directly.\n\nTHE CHANGE. `Symtab::name` hands back a `Bytes` — a second owner — where almost every caller reads the name and drops it. `Symtab::name_bytes` already exists and borrows. There are 92 `Symbol::as_bytes` call sites, 49 of them in dep.rs.\n\nTHE OBSTACLE, which is why this is a node and not a sed script: the borrow, not the edit. A `Bytes` decouples the read from the interner so the caller can go on to touch `self` mutably, and that is often WHY it is a clone rather than an oversight. Converting a site that needed the decoupling turns a compile into a borrow-check failure, which is the safe direction, but it means the count of convertible sites is not 92.\n\nWHERE TO START. The predicates, which are already inside a closure holding the borrow and so convert without fighting anything: `input.as_bytes(..).contains(&b'%')`, `.starts_with(b\"-l\")`, `p.as_bytes(..) == b\"%\"`.\n\nMEASURE IT WITH WALL, NOT INSTRUCTIONS. The allocator node's whole error was reading a 14.6% instructions:u reduction as a 14.6% win when the paired wall went the other way. `instructions:u` cannot see kernel time and a Make no-op spends a third of its cycles there. Paired, interleaved wall on the gated rows is the criterion."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T20:09:31.56451243Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "STILL STANDS, UNTOUCHED, AND FOR A REASON WORTH RECORDING. `a-sessions-allocations-die-together-and-are-freed-one-by-one` moved every expression node into a session-owned arena and did not touch `symtab.rs` at all — not one line. The arena holds STRUCTURE, not text: a `Value::Literal` still carries a `Bytes`, which is a slice of the makefile buffer the session already owns. A `Symbol`'s name is on the other side of that boundary, exactly as it was.\n\nWHAT THAT MEANS FOR THIS NODE'S OWN EVIDENCE. Its share was `bytes shared_drop + shared_clone` at 7.55% of user cycles. Measured on today's binary over 120 vim `src` runs, that is 7.71% before the arena and 8.09% after — the same absolute work, a larger share of a smaller total. The arena took 7.9% of user instructions out from underneath it and left this untouched, which is what \"these are two problems, not one\" looks like on a counter.\n\nAND THE BUDGET. A bump-only global allocator is worth 7.9% of vim-noop wall — the ceiling for all allocation removal in this front end — but refcount traffic is not allocation: a `Bytes` clone of an already-shared buffer is an atomic pair and no malloc at all. So this node's 7.7% of USER CYCLES is largely outside that ceiling rather than inside it, which makes it the more independent of the three leads rather than the smallest. Measure it with the paired, interleaved harness at 101 alternations that the arena node used; the gate's own repetitions cannot see a percent."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id an-expression-with-no-dollar-in-it-still-allocates-a-tree
                title "Give the expression tree an inline first slot so a plain line allocates nothing"
                kind @perf
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-31T17:43:38.32637686Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "FILED 2026-08-31 from the profile on `the-allocator-swap-was-refused-by-the-ninja-gate`, as the second of the three representation leads. Filed separately because the allocator swap that would have absorbed this cost has now been measured twice and refused twice — see that node's errata of this date.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE. `parse_expr_impl_ext` is now allocation-bound rather than scan-bound, which is a change from what it was: the expression-scan cut on `the-c-librarys-allocator-is-a-sixth-of-a-make-run` took its SELF time down and left its INCLUSIVE time where it was. 8.43% inclusive against 2.72% self. The 5.71% difference is allocation, and `drop_glue<Value>` is 1.29% by itself.\n\nWHAT IT IS. One `Arc<Value>` per fragment and a `Vec<Arc<Value>>` per list. A line with no `$` in it — which in a real Makefile is most lines — still costs one `Vec` and one `Arc` to say \"this expression is one literal\".\n\nTWO SIZES OF CHANGE, and they are worth separating:\n\n  (1) An inline first-element slot takes the common case to zero allocations. Contained, and it is the one to do first because the common case is the whole of the win on the no-op rows.\n\n  (2) An evaluation-scoped arena takes the WHOLE tree to zero allocations. Larger, touches lifetimes across the evaluator, and should not be attempted until (1) has measured, because (1) may take enough of it that (2) never pays for its complexity.\n\nMEASURE IT WITH WALL, NOT INSTRUCTIONS. The allocator node's error was reading a 14.6% instructions:u reduction as a 14.6% win when the paired wall moved the other way; `instructions:u` is blind to kernel time and a Make no-op spends a third of its cycles there. Paired, interleaved wall on the gated rows is the criterion. This lead has the additional property that it removes kernel time too — an allocation not made is an arena page not committed — which is exactly what the allocator arms could not do."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T20:09:06.616826928Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "SUBSUMED by `a-sessions-allocations-die-together-and-are-freed-one-by-one`, which landed both sizes of change this node's log proposed, at once.\n\nThis node said: \"(1) An inline first-element slot takes the common case to zero allocations… (2) An evaluation-scoped arena takes the WHOLE tree to zero allocations… should not be attempted until (1) has measured, because (1) may take enough of it that (2) never pays for its complexity.\"\n\nThat ordering was right on its own terms and was made unnecessary by measuring the ceiling first. A bump-only global allocator — every malloc a pointer bump, every free nothing — says what ALL arena work in this front end is worth before any of it is written: 7.9% of vim-noop wall, 3.0% of recursive-noop, 7.0% of wide-noop. With that number in hand there was no need to land (1) to find out whether (2) was worth it.\n\nThe two turned out to be one change rather than two. The arena's fragment stack — one stack for the whole session, marked on the way in and taken on the way out — IS the inline-first-element answer: an expression with a single fragment pushes it and pops it back off, taking nothing into the child table. This node's own claim is now quantified: 4,980 of the 6,014 expressions read from vim's `src/Makefile` hold exactly one fragment.\n\nMEASURED, one vim `src` read: 67,198 allocation requests to 46,258. The 88-byte class that was `Arc<Value>` goes 14,510 to 91; the 32-byte class that was the parse vector halves. Paired, interleaved wall on the gated row: 0.960x, and vim-noop goes 1.77x to 1.70x against GNU Make.\n\nThe `drop_glue<Value>` at 1.29% this node measured is gone with the rest: nothing individually drops a node any more."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id file-sequence-clones-a-word-the-interner-only-borrows
                title "Pass the prerequisite word borrowed and slice it only on an intern miss"
                kind @perf
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-31T17:43:47.540468106Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "FILED 2026-08-31 from the profile on `the-allocator-swap-was-refused-by-the-ninja-gate`, as the third of the three representation leads. Filed separately because the allocator swap that would have absorbed this cost has now been measured twice and refused twice — see that node's errata of this date.\n\nTHE CHANGE, and it is the smallest of the three by a distance. `rule::file_sequence` clones a `Bytes` per prerequisite word. `glob_word` then consumes that clone into `intern`.\n\nWHY THE CLONE IS NOW PURE WASTE, which it was not before. The expression-scan-and-intern cut on `the-c-librarys-allocator-is-a-sixth-of-a-make-run` changed `intern` to probe BORROWED and convert only on a miss. Before that change the clone was consumed by every call and paid for itself. After it, every name already in the table — which is most of them, since prerequisites repeat heavily across a real Makefile — takes the clone, probes with it, finds the entry, and drops it. An atomic increment, an atomic decrement and a malloc/free pair per already-interned prerequisite word, for nothing.\n\nSo this is a lead that a previous cut CREATED, which is worth noting as a pattern: changing an interner from consuming to borrowing leaves every caller that was built to feed it owned data holding a now-pointless clone.\n\nTHE SCOPE. Pass the word borrowed and slice only on the miss. Contained to two functions, `rule::file_sequence` and `glob_word`. This is the one to do first of the three — smallest blast radius, no borrow-checker fight of the kind `symtab-name-hands-out-an-owner-where-a-borrow-would-do` will have.\n\nMEASURE IT WITH WALL, NOT INSTRUCTIONS, per the allocator node's errata of this date: `instructions:u` cannot see kernel time and a Make no-op spends a third of its cycles there."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T20:09:19.25089626Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "STILL STANDS, AND IS NOW SIZED. `a-sessions-allocations-die-together-and-are-freed-one-by-one` deliberately left this alone and said why: the expression arena holds STRUCTURE, not text. A `Value::Literal` still carries a `Bytes`, and that is correct rather than unfinished — it is a slice of the makefile buffer the session already owns. This node is on the other side of that boundary, so nothing about it became easier or harder: `rule.rs` changed only its type names in that commit and `file_sequence` and `glob_word` were not touched at all.\n\nWHAT THE CENSUS SAYS IT IS WORTH, which this node did not have. DHAT over one vim `src` sub-make, 74,912 blocks, attributed to the innermost project frame:\n\n    rule::glob_word        3,747 blocks   5.0%   226,000 B\n    rule::file_sequence    1,858 blocks   2.5%   645,504 B\n\n7.5% of everything the process allocates, between the two halves this node names — and `file_sequence` has the largest bytes-per-block of any row in the census at 347 B, because it is a `Vec<Bytes>` of every prerequisite word.\n\nAND THE BUDGET IT SPENDS AGAINST. A bump-only global allocator — every malloc a pointer bump, every free nothing — is worth 7.9% of vim-noop wall, which is the ceiling for ALL allocation removal in this front end. 7.5% of the census projects to roughly 0.6% of that row: real, and small enough that it needs the 101-alternation paired harness the arena node used rather than the gate's own repetitions to be seen at all."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id the-make-gate-flaps-because-five-repetitions-is-too-few
                title "Sample the Make gate enough times that its verdict is about the tree"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-31T17:58:38.652102602Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "FILED 2026-08-31 from the allocator re-measurement, which needed a trustworthy vim-noop number and so had to find out why the gate's one could not be trusted. This is the mechanism behind the \"gates flapped under load\" note on that node and behind a re-record debt now four nodes old. The rows are not drifting. The gate's verdict is.\n\nWHAT HAPPENED. `scripts/check-make-performance.sh --max-load 7.0` on an unmodified tree — byte-identical to be2a708 outside plan/ — refused with \"vim-noop Ronin/GNU ratio regressed to 2.12x from the recorded 1.71x\". Nothing had changed. A paired, interleaved 31-alternation measurement of the same two binaries in the same window read 1.712x.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT, and it is the whole node. `make_baseline`'s protocol is sound — it interleaves tools with a rotating offset (examples/make_baseline.rs:723-724), which is the right design and rules out the obvious explanations. What it does not do is take enough samples. Replicating that exact protocol — 1 warmup, N repetitions, rotating offset, median-of-medians — and repeating the whole trial:\n\n     5 reps x 15 trials:  min 1.682   median 1.796   max 2.198\n    15 reps x  8 trials:  min 1.721   median 1.810   max 1.863\n    31 reps x  4 trials:  min 1.721   median 1.805   max 1.817\n\n`DEFAULT_REPETITIONS` is 5. At 5 the ratio spans 0.52. The gate fires above `1.71 x 1.20 = 2.052`, so the margin from the recorded row to the refusal is 0.34 — SMALLER THAN THE SPREAD OF THE STATISTIC IT TESTS. The gate is not measuring the tree; on this host it is sampling a distribution that straddles its own threshold. 2.12x was a draw from it.\n\nWHY THE HOST CANNOT BE THE ANSWER. The quiet-host guard exists to prevent exactly this and cannot: `DEFAULT_MAX_LOAD` is 4.0 as an ABSOLUTE one-minute average, which on this 32-core host is 12.5% utilisation. Over 84 samples across 21 minutes the load never fell below 4.29 — floor held by four runaway `nsh` processes from an unrelated repository, spinning nine hours each — so the gate refuses to run at all before it gets the chance to flap. Waiting is not available and would not help: the 31-rep figure was taken at the same load and is stable to +/-0.05.\n\nTHE DIRECTION. Repetitions, not threshold and not re-recording. Three things to weigh:\n\n  (1) `DEFAULT_REPETITIONS` up. 15 already collapses the span from 0.52 to 0.14. Free on the no-op rows, which run in tens of milliseconds.\n\n  (2) But NOT uniformly: `zsh-incremental` runs 1.4 s and `vim-clean-build` 21 s, so 31 repetitions of the catalog is half an hour. Repetitions want to be per workload — enough samples that the statistic is tighter than the margin, which is a different number for a 28 ms row than for a 21 s one.\n\n  (3) Consider judging on a lower quantile or the minimum rather than the median. A no-op's minimum is the closest thing to the tree's own cost; contention can only add. The Ninja gate records min/median/max already and the recorded CSV carries only the median.\n\nAND CONSIDER WHETHER 4.0 IS THE RIGHT GUARD. It is an absolute load compared against a machine whose core count it never asks for. A threshold expressed as a fraction of `nproc` would let this host measure and would still stop a genuinely busy one. That is a judgement about what the gate is for, so it belongs to whoever owns the gate rather than to this note — but the present setting makes the gate unrunnable here rather than accurate.\n\nWHAT THE TRUE RATIO IS, since the next person will want it: 1.72 to 1.81, against a recorded 1.71. At or a little above the row, not the 2.12x the gate reported and not a regression worth re-recording for."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T20:09:48.277904208Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "THE NINJA GATE FLAPS THE SAME WAY, AND THIS NODE SHOULD NAME IT TOO.\n\nMeasured 2026-09-01 while landing `a-sessions-allocations-die-together-and-are-freed-one-by-one`, on a host carrying a foreign load of 17 to 20. `scripts/check-performance.sh` — the Ninja-mode gate, `examples/baseline --validate` — was run alternately against the unmodified `a1a1e06` binary and the changed one, in one window.\n\nAt the gate's own default repetitions, three rounds:\n\n    round  a1a1e06                                 arena\n    1      dependency-log-load 1.03x (rec 0.73x)   deep-graph-evaluation 0.89x (rec 0.72x)\n    2      dependency-log-load 0.95x (rec 0.73x)   clean-tree-noop 0.81x (rec 0.65x)\n    3      manifest-command-evaluation 0.70x       wide-noop-build 1.05x (rec 0.82x)\n\nSix failures, six different rows, and the UNMODIFIED binary failed as often as the changed one. At `--repetitions 21`, three more rounds: round 1 both pass, round 2 a1a1e06 fails and arena passes, round 3 the reverse. One failure in three per arm, symmetric.\n\nThat is this node's finding transposed: a five-repetition median on a loaded host is a coin toss, and the Ninja gate has no more protection against it than the Make gate did. The difference is that the Make gate at least says so — it refuses to measure above `--max-load 4.00` and tells you to raise the threshold deliberately and record it. `examples/baseline` has no load gate at all, so it measures whatever the host is doing and reports the result as a verdict.\n\nTWO CONCRETE ASKS FOR WHOEVER TAKES THIS NODE. Give `examples/baseline` the same load gate `examples/make_baseline` has, so a Ninja-mode measurement refuses a busy host rather than reporting one. And raise its default repetitions with the same argument this node makes for the Make side.\n\nTHE PROCEDURE THAT WORKED, meanwhile, and it is the one a1a1e06's node used: run the gate against the PREVIOUS binary in the same window. A gate that refuses the unmodified tree is refusing the host."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T20:28:49.952353497Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T21:33:10.902747655Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "BOTH GATES WERE SAMPLING A DISTRIBUTION THAT STRADDLED THEIR OWN THRESHOLD, AND THE NINJA ONE IS TWICE AS BAD AS THE MAKE ONE.\n\nTHE INSTRUMENT FIRST. Both harnesses grew `--samples PATH`, which writes every individual sample rather than the one number per tool per workload the report carries. A summary is enough to gate on and not enough to argue about; choosing a statistic or a repetition count is a question about the SPREAD, and the spread is exactly what a median throws away. One pool per gate, 151 interleaved repetitions of every workload — twelve rows, 3,624 tool invocations — at load 10.7 to 16.5, `--max-load 20` raised deliberately.\n\nSynthetic gate runs were then drawn out of each pool by MOVING-BLOCK bootstrap: blocks of five consecutive repetitions, 20,000 runs per (estimator, count, row). Blocks rather than individual samples, and that is the whole methodology — a gate run is a contiguous slice of a drifting host, and shuffling destroys the autocorrelation that is what makes a gate flap. A refusal is the gate's own rule: the run's statistic above 1.20 times the pool's own value of the same statistic.\n\nWHAT THE MEDIAN OF FIVE REFUSES ON AN UNMODIFIED TREE:\n\n    dependency-log-load           19.355%      wide-noop-build         7.560%\n    path-canonicalization         15.585%      clean-tree-noop         2.000%\n    manifest-command-evaluation   11.400%      large-manifest-parse    0.000%\n    deep-graph-evaluation          9.400%      scheduler-barrier       0.000%\n    vim-noop                       9.075%      wide-noop               0.000%\n                                               recursive-noop          0.000%\n                                               zsh-incremental         0.000%\n\nSix rows of twelve refuse a tree nobody touched. A Ninja run must pass all eight of its rows at once, so a whole run refuses about half the time — which is the three-out-of-three on an unmodified binary the arena node recorded, and not the host being unusual. It is the arithmetic. This node's own 9.075% for vim-noop reproduces its filing's 5-rep span 1.682-2.198 from the harness's own samples rather than from a hand replay.\n\nTHE STATISTIC IS A LOW QUANTILE, AND THE REASON IS THE SHAPE OF THE DISTRIBUTION. A no-op's samples have a hard floor — what the tree actually costs — and a long right tail that is the rest of the machine, because contention can only ADD time. The informative part is the bottom, and every central estimator spends its whole life in the contaminated part. Six were measured over the same pools:\n\n    vim-noop / dependency-log-load, refusals in 20,000 runs\n    estimator                @5 reps          @15            @31\n    median              9.075 / 19.355   1.590 / 7.775   0.195 / 2.320\n    20% trimmed mean    0.000 /  9.640   0.000 / 1.935   0.000 / 0.120\n    median of ratios    2.730 / 14.990   0.055 / 3.205   0.000 / 0.500\n    25th percentile     2.130 /  9.325   0.070 / 0.245   0.000 / 0.005\n    TENTH PERCENTILE    2.895 /  3.355   0.075 / 0.010   0.000 / 0.000\n    minimum             2.760 /  1.275   0.015 / 0.000   0.000 / 0.000\n\nThe trimmed mean is the tightest thing there is on the MAKE rows and still refuses 0.12% of `dependency-log-load`; the median needs 51+ repetitions to reach what a low quantile reaches at 21, which on `zsh-incremental` is three minutes of gate. THE MINIMUM MATCHES THE DECILE AND IS THE ONE THING THIS MUST NOT USE: a minimum is not a consistent statistic, its value falls as you take more samples, so a row recorded from five and checked against thirty-one is two different numbers and the repetition count could never be changed again. The tenth percentile is the same number at any sample count and needs only enough samples to locate.\n\nTHE FALSE-REFUSAL RATE DESIGNED FOR IS BELOW ONE IN TEN THOUSAND PER ROW — zero refusals in 20,000 bootstrapped runs on all twelve rows at their own counts — against one in five for what it replaces. Each count is the smallest on the grid meeting that AND leaving the one-in-a-thousand draw inside 1.15 of the row's centre, so at least a quarter of the 1.20 margin stays available to a regression rather than being spent on the host: vim-noop 31, recursive-noop 31, wide-noop 21, zsh-incremental 15, and on the Ninja side 21 everywhere except dependency-log-load and large-manifest-parse at 31.\n\nTHE COUNTS ARE PER WORKLOAD, which is the half of this the filing asked for in as many words. vim-noop is 34 ms and zsh-incremental is 1.8 s: one number for the catalog is the wrong number for at least one row in it. They live in `examples/support/statistic.rs` beside the measurement that set them; `scripts/check-release.sh` no longer passes a count at all, because the harness knows which row is a 34 ms no-op and that script did not; and `--repetitions` now REFUSES a `--validate` run it would under-sample rather than reporting a verdict the harness knows its own samples cannot support. The whole gated Make catalog is about a hundred seconds, which is what nine repetitions of everything already cost.\n\nTHE NINJA GATE'S MISSING GUARD, this node's second ask. `require_quiet_host` moved to `examples/support/quiet_host.rs` and both gates run behind it — one implementation, so the next guard either gate grows is a guard both of them have. DEFAULT_MAX_LOAD is still 4.00, the five-minute patience is unchanged, and `examples/baseline` grew the `--max-load` that goes with it. Both reports now carry `load_average_before`, `load_average_after` and `max_load`, and the Ninja schema goes v2 to v3 for it.\n\nRUN AGAINST THE REAL GATES, BEFORE AND AFTER. The bootstrap is a model of the gate; this is the gate. Both arms — the 4f71437 harnesses built in a worktree, and these — alternately in one window, six rounds each, on a tree neither changes, `--max-load 25` on both so the comparison is about the statistic rather than about one arm declining to measure:\n\n    gate    arm                              refusals   rows\n    Make    median of 5                        0 / 6    --\n    Make    decile, per-row counts             0 / 6    --\n    Ninja   median of 5, no load guard         6 / 6    deep-graph x2, manifest-command x2,\n                                                        path-canonicalization, wide-noop-build\n    Ninja   decile, per-row counts             2 / 6    path-canonicalization, wide-noop-build\n\nLoad ran 12.8 to 21.6 across that window, four to five times the guard. Six runs cannot see a 9% per-run rate on the Make gate, which is what the 20,000-run bootstrap is for; it is more than enough to see a rate near one.\n\nAND THE TWO REMAINING NINJA REFUSALS ARE THE RECORDED ROWS, NOT THE STATISTIC. Both are rows already sitting adverse against `baseline-v1.csv`, so the margin left before the host took any was small. At the gate's OWN --max-load 4.00 neither run would have happened: it would have waited five minutes and refused to measure, which is the right answer at load 19 and is exactly what `examples/baseline` could not say before this change. The two halves address different noise sources — the statistic answers within-run spread, the guard answers between-window drift — and the measurement separates them.\n\nNOT ONE THRESHOLD MOVED. Recorded-ratio tolerance 1.20 on both gates, absolute Ronin-versus-Ninja 1.20, peak RSS 2.00, quiet-host guard 4.00. Peak RSS keeps its median deliberately: the decile exists to see under a right tail that is contention, and the failure RSS is gated against IS a high number.\n\nTHE RE-RECORD WAS MEASURED AND DECLINED, which the dispatch left conditional on a quiet window. Three full recording passes with the new statistic:\n\n    workload           pass 1   pass 2   pass 3   recorded\n    wide-noop           0.56x    0.61x    0.58x     0.62x\n    recursive-noop      0.98x    0.95x    0.98x     1.01x\n    vim-noop            1.70x    1.66x    1.69x     1.71x\n    zsh-incremental     1.05x    1.09x    1.06x     1.03x\n\nThis node's filing says it in one line — the rows are not drifting, the gate's verdict is — and that is now measured rather than argued. Re-recording the Make rows would have treated a symptom the node establishes is not the disease.\n\nTHE RECORDED ROWS ARE MEDIANS AND THE RUN'S ARE DECILES, and that costs an order of magnitude less than the margin: what is compared is a RATIO, and a ratio is nearly independent of which quantile it is taken at, because both tools' distributions shift together under the same contention. Over the same pools the decile ratio and the median ratio agree within 3% on ten of twelve rows and 6.2% on the widest.\n\nAND THE FINDING THAT DESERVES A NODE OF ITS OWN: SEVEN OF THE EIGHT NINJA ROWS READ 5% TO 13% ADVERSE against `baseline-v1.csv`, measured with the same median the record used — clean-tree 1.058, manifest-command 1.061, wide-noop-build 1.060, path-canonicalization 1.065, dependency-log 1.070, deep-graph 1.131, and scheduler-barrier 1.171 against a recorded 1.0158. A systematic move of that size across seven rows at once is a host or a drift to ATTRIBUTE, not a set of numbers to overwrite, and scheduler-barrier is 3% under the tolerance that would refuse it. Recording it away is exactly the failure this work exists to stop. It needs a machine quiet enough for the guard, and it is why the two after-arm refusals above happened.\n\nGATES. The product binary is BYTE-IDENTICAL to 4f71437's — sha256 c6d32a08... on both — because nothing outside `examples/`, `tests/`, `scripts/` and `benchmarks/` was touched, so conformance, equivalence, Ninja conformance, upstream and the port ledger have no subject here. Tests 909/0 (was 900; the statistic module's four tests are compiled into both examples, plus one new count assertion in the Make harness) and 1225/0 with kati's 316. `cargo fmt` clean, clippy clean on both passes, `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean. Both perf gates pass at `--max-load 25`, raised deliberately because the host's floor is four times the guard: Make 0.60x / 0.95x / 1.68x / 1.03x, Ninja all eight rows inside every threshold. No `unsafe`, no `eprintln!` from a worker.\n\nTHE RECORD is `benchmarks/gate-statistic-2026-09-01.md`: the pools, the six estimators, the counts and their bootstrapped draws, the before/after run, and the reasoning for not re-recording."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T21:33:15.674339972Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id a-sessions-allocations-die-together-and-are-freed-one-by-one
                title "Free a session's evaluation allocations as one arena, not one object at a time"
                kind @perf
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T17:12:58.640135193Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T20:08:52.852680529Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "THE CEILING WAS MEASURED BEFORE ANYTHING WAS BUILT, AND IT IS THE MOST IMPORTANT NUMBER HERE.\n\nThe operator asked, repeatedly, whether there should be a bump allocator or arenas. That question can be answered without writing one: build a Ronin whose global allocator hands out bytes from a reserved region and never reclaims any of them. Every malloc is a pointer bump, every free is nothing. Whatever that arm saves is the MOST any arena inside the front end can save, because it has already arena'd everything the process allocates.\n\nPaired and interleaved against the stock binary, 21-41 alternations per arm:\n\n    workload          base       bump-everything   ratio\n    vim-noop           27.81 ms      25.62 ms      0.921x\n    recursive-noop     87.10 ms      84.46 ms      0.970x\n    wide-noop         619.63 ms     576.25 ms      0.930x\n\nSplit by privilege level, interleaved, 21 alternations on vim-noop:\n\n    counter          base        bump         ratio\n    instructions:u   103.38M     79.11M       0.765x\n    cycles:u          75.65M     61.73M       0.816x\n    cycles:k          44.04M     46.41M       1.054x\n    page-faults        2,919      1,248       0.428x\n    task-clock        35.15 ms   33.10 ms     0.942x\n\nThat base instruction count is 103.38M against the 103.390M this repository has recorded for the tree, so the arm measures the tree it claims to.\n\nTHE SHAPE IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE ALLOCATOR SWAP, AND THAT IS THE FINDING. mimalloc bought 15.2M user instructions and spent 22.0M kernel cycles buying them. The arena buys 24.3M user instructions and spends 2.4M kernel cycles, and takes 57% of the PAGE FAULTS AWAY rather than adding them — a region bumped through once is touched once, where glibc returns pages and re-faults them. Total cycles fall 9.7%, and the wall agrees with the cycles, which is what was missing last time.\n\nSo 7.9% of the gated vim no-op is the whole prize for arena work in this front end. Every stage spends against that budget and should say what fraction it is claiming.\n\nAND ONE THING THE PROBE TAUGHT THAT THE DESIGN HAD TO ABSORB. The first bump allocator used a single global bump pointer and made recursive-noop 4.8x SLOWER — 409 ms against 85. Eight composing threads on one atomic is a cache line they take turns owning. A thread-local slab claimed 4 MiB at a time brought it to 0.970x. A per-session arena has that property by construction, which is the second reason the arena belongs to the session rather than to the process.\n\nWHERE THE ALLOCATIONS ARE, which nobody had established. DHAT over one vim `src` sub-make, 74,912 blocks, attributed to the innermost frame that is this project's own. DHAT cannot profile the static binary that ships; a `RUSTFLAGS=\"-C debuginfo=1\"` build without `crt-static` makes the same 74,912 blocks and attributes every one:\n\n    expr::parse_expr_impl_ext            22,702   30.3%\n    Evaluator::eval_rule                  8,087   10.8%\n    DepBuilder::resolved_prerequisites    5,368    7.2%\n    rule::glob_word                       3,747    5.0%\n    DirectoryCache::certainly_absent      3,361    4.5%\n    DepBuilder::ordered_candidates        2,492    3.3%\n    DepBuilder::new                       2,291    3.1%\n    rule::file_sequence                   1,858    2.5%\n    Value::resolve_folds                  1,374    1.8%\n\nThere is no hotspot in the profile and there is one in the census. The expression read is a third of the traffic on its own.\n\nWHAT WAS BUILT. `Value` moves into a `ValueArena` the session owns; a child link is a `ValueId`, four bytes, Copy, no refcount. A list's items and a call's arguments are a `Children` range into one shared child table rather than a vector each. A read accumulates fragments on ONE STACK for the whole session, marking the height on the way in and taking everything above the mark on the way out — reads nest strictly, so a stack is the right shape, and it answers the case that dominates: 4,980 of the 6,014 expressions read from vim's src Makefile hold exactly one fragment, a line with no `$` in it, which used to cost a vector to say so.\n\nINDICES RATHER THAN POINTERS, and the reason is specific to this side rather than inherited from typed-graph-arenas: a Session is MOVED — into a worker that composes a recursive unit, and back out — and an index survives that where an interior pointer is a question nobody should have to ask. The safe pointer form is not available either: `Session<'a>` cannot own the arena it borrows, and it is a Session that crosses the thread boundary. The compact form — raw pointers behind a Deref, roughly a tenth of the churn — was considered and refused: it would be the first non-FFI unsafe in this codebase (all eleven of kati's unsafe blocks are libc calls) and the invariant would be a comment rather than a type, in the component the product's correctness rests on.\n\nTHE BORROW RULE, and why the compiler holds it. A `&Value` read out of the arena borrows the evaluator that owns the arena, and expansion is the evaluator's most mutable operation. So `eval_value` reads the node once, copies out handles, symbols and a location, and lets the borrow go before it recurses. The two arms that only write bytes — most of every makefile — finish inside the borrow. Nothing here is unsafe and nothing depends on an invariant a reader has to remember; the thing that would go wrong is a borrow error, not a use-after-free that shows up as a wrong graph six months later.\n\nTHE CENSUS MOVED AS PREDICTED. One vim `src` read, counted in process:\n\n                                       before      after\n    allocation requests                67,198     46,258\n    the 88-byte class (Arc<Value>)     14,510         91\n    the 32-byte class (parse vectors)  12,467      6,253\n    requested bytes                 7,434,953  8,262,917\n\n20,940 fewer allocations, 31.2%, against 30.3% predicted from the census. Bytes go UP 11%: the arena reserves capacity ahead of use and rounds up as it doubles. That is arithmetic on a Vec, not retained memory, and the RSS below says so.\n\nTHE PAIRED WALLS, which are the result. Interleaved with GNU Make 4.4.1 in the same session, medians; the host's one-minute load was 6.5 to 19 throughout, so the RATIOS carry and the milliseconds do not:\n\n    row              reps  GNU        base       arena      base/GNU  arena/GNU  arena/base\n    vim-noop          101    15.95 ms   28.28 ms   27.15 ms   1.773x    1.702x     0.960x\n    wide-noop          31  1231.26 ms  713.19 ms  695.98 ms   0.579x    0.565x     0.976x\n    zsh-incremental    21  1998.41 ms 2145.55 ms 2120.62 ms   1.074x    1.061x     0.988x\n    recursive-noop     61    87.05 ms   86.82 ms   86.89 ms   0.997x    0.998x     1.001x\n    zsh clean           3    48.72 s     50.67 s    50.54 s   1.040x    1.037x     0.997x\n\nBOTH PRIVILEGE LEVELS, vim-noop, interleaved, 31 alternations, load 6.5:\n\n    counter          base        arena       ratio\n    instructions:u   103,383,741  95,183,035  0.921x\n    cycles:u          74,564,624  69,905,005  0.938x\n    cycles:k          45,926,087  46,732,706  1.018x\n    page-faults            2,921       2,926  1.002x\n    task-clock         35.62 ms    34.47 ms   0.968x\n\nThe kernel side does not move. That is the whole difference from the allocator arms: this removes user work without buying it with kernel work. 4.0% of vim-noop against a 7.9% ceiling — this stage spends half the budget, and the gated row goes 1.77x to 1.70x in the window that measured it.\n\nNO SYSCALL STORM, which the brief was right to ask about. `strace -c -e trace=mmap,munmap,brk,madvise`: vim src is 89 memory syscalls on BOTH arms, identical row for row (47 brk, 25 mmap, 16 munmap, 1 madvise). The 259-session recursive tree is 129 against 135 — six more brk. The arena's chunks come out of a heap glibc had already grown; no chunk pool across sessions is needed and none was built.\n\nPEAK RSS, sampled from /proc across the whole process tree, worst of seven runs: vim-noop 21,620 to 20,224 KiB, recursive-noop 62,924 to 62,200, wide-noop 39,176 to 39,880. Unchanged, slightly down on two of three — the arena gets back from the sixteen-byte Arc header and the allocator metadata what it spends on reserved capacity.\n\nAND THE CASE THAT SHOULD HAVE COST RSS, stressed rather than assumed: the arena never reclaims within a session, so a makefile that PARSES in a loop is its worst case. 60,000 `$(eval)` plus 60,000 `$(call subst,...)` — 120,000 fresh parses in one session, twenty times what a real makefile does — reads 59,768 KiB base against 59,152 arena, and 279 ms against 280. Slightly lower, because glibc does not return the freed chunks to the OS either.\n\nTHE REAPER: recursive-noop 20 ms to 10 ms median, tick-quantised. On vim-noop and wide-noop it is below one clock tick on both arms, because those rows compose one session and no reaper runs.\n\nWHAT FOLDED IN AND WHAT DID NOT. `an-expression-with-no-dollar-in-it-still-allocates-a-tree` is subsumed entirely: its evidence is quantified here (4,980 of 6,014 expressions), and both sizes of change its log proposed are done at once — the fragment stack IS the inline-first-element answer, and the arena is the arena. Its own advice was to do the small one first and measure before attempting the second; the ceiling measurement made that ordering unnecessary, because it said in advance what the pair was worth together.\n\nThe other two do NOT fold in, and the reason is the Bytes boundary. The arena holds STRUCTURE, not text: a `Value::Literal` still carries a `Bytes` slice of the makefile buffer, which is a borrow of something the session already owns wearing a refcount. `file-sequence-clones-a-word-the-interner-only-borrows` and `symtab-name-hands-out-an-owner-where-a-borrow-would-do` are both on the other side of that boundary — a `Bytes` handed out where a borrow would do — and neither became easier or harder: `rule.rs` changed only its type names, `symtab.rs` was not touched at all. Together they are another 7.5% of the census (glob_word 5.0% + file_sequence 2.5%) and they stay their own nodes.\n\nTHE DECISION RECORD is `[dec:ronin:session-owned-expression-arena]`, requiring `[dec:ronin:typed-graph-arenas]` and refining `[dec:ronin:session-owned-evaluation]`. It carries the ceiling, the census, the four rejected forms, the reproduction recipe for both probe binaries — which are NOT left in the tree, per the precedent the allocator node set — and the deferred half.\n\nGATES. Equivalence 274/276/8/56/0, and identical on all twenty runs of it. Conformance 354/326/28/276. Upstream 18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 15 interface. Port ledger 170. Ninja 425/425 + 10 + 92 + 3. Tests 900/0 and 1216/0, both unchanged from the baseline measured on the same host with the same commands. Clippy clean on both passes. No `unsafe` added, no `eprintln!` added. `$(.VARIABLES)` renders identically, order included.\n\nAND THE GRAPH DIFF THE EQUIVALENCE GATE IS BLIND TO. `make -n -B -j1` over each tree, which is the whole planned command sequence: the 259-unit recursive workload 1,728 lines byte-identical, vim `src` 475 lines identical, zsh 427 lines identical. 2,630 lines of planned recipe across the recursive-staging shapes, unmoved.\n\nBOTH PERF GATES, and the second one needs saying plainly. The Make gate passes for both arms (`--max-load 26 --repetitions 15`, raised deliberately because the host's floor is above the gate's 4.00 and the gate's own message says to say so). The NINJA gate flaps on this host and it flaps SYMMETRICALLY: at its default repetitions the unmodified `a1a1e06` binary failed three runs out of three, on three different rows — clean-tree-noop 0.83x, dependency-log-load 1.03x and 0.95x, manifest-command-evaluation 0.70x — while the arena binary failed three of four, also on different rows each time. At `--repetitions 21` each arm failed once in three, alternating which. That is a flap, not a regression, and it is distinguished the way a1a1e06's node distinguished the Make gate's. It is also what should be expected a priori: this change is entirely inside the Make front end, which Ninja mode never calls, and the binary is 0.23% SMALLER. `the-make-gate-flaps-because-five-repetitions-is-too-few` should be widened to name the Ninja gate too.\n\nTHE BRIEF'S ERROR, and it is the one prediction the measurement refuted outright: \"`-j8` recursion is the second beneficiary: 259 sessions on worker threads with no cross-thread frees.\" recursive-noop is the ONE gated row that does not move — 1.0007x over 61 alternations. The reasoning that got there was the Reaper: \"its existence is the tell.\" It is a tell for WHERE the freeing happens, not for HOW MUCH there is. The recursive workload's 259 units are 4,103 makefile lines BETWEEN THEM, fewer than vim's single `src/Makefile` at 4,962 — session count is not expression volume. The rows that moved are single-session reads where no reaper runs at all, and the Reaper's own cost on recursive-noop does halve while the row stays put, because that time was already off the composing thread, which is what a reaper is for.\n\nTWO SMALLER ONES. Stage 1 was specified as \"the expression `Value` trees AND eval-lifetime temporaries\", which bundles two unrelated things: the temporaries are the `BytesMut` every expansion writes into, they are `Evaluator::eval_rule` at 10.8% of the census, and they share no code with the `Value` representation. They are Stage 2. And \"no caller above 0.5%\" — the record says 1%, not 0.5%. The brief's \"~15.3% self\" for the allocator, on the other hand, is RIGHT and fresher than the plan's: measured on today's static binary over 120 vim `src` runs it is 15.86%, down from the 18.9% the decision record carries, because the expression-scan cut landed in between. `bytes` refcounting measures 7.71%, against the brief's \"~7%\"."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T20:14:20.379630114Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id a-rule-line-grows-a-buffer-for-every-word-in-it
                title "Expand a rule line's words through one reused buffer, not one each"
                kind @perf
                deps (a-sessions-allocations-die-together-and-are-freed-one-by-one)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T22:06:19.026110641Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T22:07:33.266101563Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "ARENA STAGE 2, AND THE FIRST THING TO SAY IS WHAT THE TEMPORARIES ACTUALLY ARE, BECAUSE THE NAME THE PROGRAMME HAS BEEN USING FOR THEM IS WRONG.\n\n`a-sessions-allocations-die-together-and-are-freed-one-by-one` deferred this as \"the `BytesMut` every expansion writes into, they are `Evaluator::eval_rule` at 10.8% of the census\". They are not a `BytesMut`. `eval_rule`'s traffic is `RuleWordExpansion`, a `Vec<RuleWordByte>` — a byte AND its provenance, two bytes per character — grown from empty once per WORD of every rule line, and then a second vector collected out of it to drop the provenance again. vim's `src/Makefile` has 597 rule lines and a rule head is one to three words, so that is several thousand vectors that grow through 4, 8, 16, 32 to say what the line already said. The `BytesMut` the deferral named is `eval_rule_head`'s single output buffer, which is one per line and was already the cheap part.\n\nTHE CEILING WAS MEASURED FIRST, on today's tree rather than inherited. A bump-everything global allocator — every malloc a pointer bump, every free nothing, a thread-local 4 MiB slab so eight composing threads do not queue on one atomic — built from the post-arena binary and run paired against it, 151 alternations, two windows:\n\n    vim-noop   before p10 25.959 ms   bump 24.985 ms   0.9625x\n    vim-noop   before p10 25.898 ms   bump 24.905 ms   0.9617x\n\n3.8% IS THE WHOLE REMAINING BUDGET for allocation work in this front end. That is the number this stage spends against, and it also confirms the arena node's arithmetic from the other side: the pre-arena ceiling was 7.9%, stage 1 took 4.0%, and 3.9% was what should have been left. It is 3.8%.\n\nWHAT WAS BUILT. Four things, all of them the same idea — do not allocate to produce bytes you already have:\n\n  1. One word buffer per rule line, reset between words, and LENT BY THE EVALUATOR across lines so a whole makefile grows one of them. `eval_rule_head` takes it with `mem::take` and gives it back however it returns, keeping whichever of the two is wider. A `$(eval)` nested inside a word expansion finds the slot empty and makes its own, which is correct and rare.\n  2. The settled word is written straight into the head being assembled (`write_into`) instead of being returned as a `Bytes` the caller copies on the next line. That word had no life of its own between those two statements and giving it one cost an allocation per word.\n  3. `split_rule_source` returns the line as it arrived when it holds none of `$`, `\\`, `#` or `;`. The loop below it copies such a line byte for byte into a buffer that ends up identical, and most rule lines in a real makefile are exactly that.\n  4. The two halves of a rule line — the text after the targets and the text after the colon — are joined without a copy where one is empty, which is most lines, because the head stops AT the colon and everything after it is in the second half. The `find_char_unquote` pass over the suffix gets the same treatment: it rewrites nothing unless a backslash stands in front of the `=` it finds, so a suffix with no backslash does not need a copy to be searched.\n\nThe fragment-stack mark/unwind pattern from stage 1 DOES extend here, and the answer to whether it should is no — a mark and an unwind exist because expression reads nest arbitrarily and a stack is the only shape that survives that. Rule words do not nest: `eval_rule_head` reads them one after another and is finished with each before the next. A single buffer taken and returned is the same idea with none of the height arithmetic, and that is what this is.\n\nTHE CENSUS. One vim `src` sub-make, counted in process by a global allocator that reports from a `.fini_array` constructor:\n\n                          before      after\n    allocation requests   53,566     47,770     -10.8%\n    requested bytes    9,885,366  9,708,302      -1.8%\n    the 24-byte class      7,684      5,027     -2,657\n    the 8-byte class      11,259      9,817     -1,442\n    the 32-byte class      6,987      6,343       -644\n\n10.8% is exactly the census share the arena node attributed to `Evaluator::eval_rule`, which is how a reader can tell that what came out is what was aimed at. (The absolute totals differ from that node's 46,258 because this instrument counts `realloc` as a request; before and after are the same instrument.)\n\nTHE PAIRED WALLS. Tenth-percentile, interleaved with a rotating offset, before and after at separate paths. Five windows on vim-noop — three at 151 alternations through a standalone A/B and two at 61 through the gate's own harness — and 61 on the other rows, load 5.9 to 14.9:\n\n    row                reps        before      after     after/before\n    vim-noop         151 x3     25.9 ms    25.5 ms       0.987x\n    wide-noop         61 x2    670.5 ms   656.7 ms       0.99x\n    recursive-noop    61 x2     97.9 ms    97.6 ms       0.99x\n    zsh-incremental   61 x2   1506.4 ms  1500.9 ms       1.00x\n\nThe five vim-noop windows read 0.937, 0.977, 0.986, 0.990, 0.990; the 0.937 came from a window whose load fell from 14.7 to 8.7 underneath it and is the outlier, so the median of the five is what is quoted. zsh-incremental does not move and should not: a second and a half of that row is `gcc` and `ld`.\n\nBOTH PRIVILEGE LEVELS, vim-noop, interleaved, 21 alternations:\n\n    counter          before        after       ratio\n    instructions:u   95,199,059   93,115,190   0.978x\n    cycles:u         94,281,542   91,890,302   0.975x\n    cycles:k         61,619,703   61,726,886   1.002x\n    page-faults           2,933        2,774   0.946x\n    task-clock            48 ms        47 ms   0.986x\n\nTHE KERNEL SIDE DOES NOT MOVE, and the wall agrees with the cycles: total cycles 0.985x against a wall of 0.987x. That is the shape an allocation removed has and an allocator swap does not, and it is the same shape stage 1 had. That base `instructions:u` of 95.199M is the 95,183,035 the arena node recorded for this tree, so the arm measures the tree it claims to.\n\n1.3% of vim-noop against a 3.8% remaining ceiling — this stage spends a third of what is left.\n\nPEAK RSS UNCHANGED, and the case that could have cost it was checked rather than assumed: the word buffer is retained for the evaluator's life, so a recursive tree holding many evaluators is its worst case. Measured interleaved, worst of seven alternations: recursive-noop 62,312 KiB before against 62,288 after; vim-noop 11,036 against 10,992. A first reading of 60,804 against 62,876 was an artefact of `ru_maxrss` being monotonic within the measuring process, which biases whichever arm runs first — the interleaved reading is the one that means anything.\n\nGATES. Equivalence 274/276/8/56/0, identical on all twenty runs. Conformance 354 runs, 276 raw identical, 326 normalised identical, 28 differing. Upstream 18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 15 interface. Port ledger 170. Ninja 425/425 + 10 + 92 + 3. Tests 909/0 and 1225/0. `cargo fmt` clean, clippy clean on both passes. Both perf gates pass (`--max-load 20`, raised deliberately): Make 0.60x / 0.96x / 1.66x / 1.08x, Ninja all eight rows inside every threshold. No `unsafe` added, no `eprintln!` added.\n\nAND THE GRAPH DIFF THE EQUIVALENCE GATE IS BLIND TO. `make -n -B -j1` over each tree, normalised for the two arms' own paths appearing in `$(MAKE)`: the 259-unit recursive workload 1,728 lines byte-identical, vim `src` 475, zsh 427. The same 2,630 lines stage 1 left unmoved.\n\nkati commit `acb7bb7` on `nplan/a-rule-line-grows-a-buffer-for-every-word-in-it`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T22:07:37.609469088Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-31T00:44:32.63847227Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE LEDGER THIS NODE INHERITS, for the four cuts attempted in one session against the vim-noop gap, and what it leaves on the table.\n\nTHE STARTING POINT WAS NOT WHAT THE DISPATCH SAID. The brief opened this session with \"the front end retires 2.08x the instructions GNU Make does for the same job (vim `src` sub-make: 90.7M vs 43.6M)\" and named fae06c9 as the revision to start from. At fae06c9, measured: 87.662M against GNU's 43.622M, which is 2.010x. The 90.7M is the number `the-symbol-maps-hash-an-index-with-siphash` measured BEFORE its own change landed -- its log says \"90.702M to 87.797M\" in as many words -- and the brief copied the before-figure as the starting point of a tree that already had the after. A ledger kept against 2.08x would have credited this session with a 3.5% cut it did not make.\n\nWHAT LANDED, `perf stat -e instructions:u`, deterministic, on a host whose one-minute load sat between 5 and 26 all session:\n\n  workload             GNU Make    fae06c9      landed    ratio\n  vim-noop (gated)     48.684M    111.887M    103.205M    2.300 -> 2.120\n  vim `src` sub-make   43.625M     87.662M     81.133M    2.010 -> 1.860\n  wide-noop             3.770G      3.301G      3.278G    0.876 -> 0.870\n  recursive-noop        1.729G      2.187G      2.153G    1.265 -> 1.245\n  zsh-incremental       6.910G      7.909G      7.833G    1.145 -> 1.134\n\n  per cut, on the `src` sub-make:\n    the fast hashers                87.662M -> 85.335M   -2.66%\n    the expression scan and intern  85.335M -> 81.926M   -4.00%\n    the shell (nsh lock bump)       81.926M -> 81.133M   -0.97%\n\n  the shell alone, paired, as shipped:\n    sh -c 'true'      607,232 -> 452,487   -25.5%   (dash 217,437)\n    the vim probe     686,729 -> 532,068   -22.5%\n\nNo row regresses. zsh-incremental's excess over GNU Make falls from 999.1M instructions to 923.6M, which is 7.6% of the part of that row that is Ronin's own work rather than the compilers both tools spawn.\n\nAND A FIFTH CUT THAT WAS LANDED AND TAKEN BACK OUT, which is this node's subject: mimalloc, worth 14.6% of vim-noop on its own -- more than the other three together -- and refused by `scripts/check-performance.sh` on peak RSS at 402% of Ninja's against a 200% ceiling. The measurements and the jemalloc arm are in [dec:ronin:allocator-is-not-the-c-librarys], now @rejected.\n\nWHAT IS AT THE TOP OF THE PROFILE NOW, and it is what this node is for. Flat profile of the `src` sub-make at the landed revision, `perf record -e cycles:u -c 4000000` over 150 runs:\n\n  glibc malloc/free/consolidate/realloc  ~18%   (no caller above 1%)\n  bytes shared_drop + shared_clone         7.55%\n  DepBuilder::build_plan                   4.58%\n  parse_expr_impl_ext                      3.72%   (2.72% self after the scan cut)\n  memmove + memcmp                         4.15%\n  Symtab::intern                           2.53%\n\nThe allocator's share and the reference-counting share are the same problem seen twice: every `Bytes` handed out is an atomic pair, and every one that is not a slice of something already alive is a malloc. Three specific leads, in the order the profile ranks them:\n\nONE. `Symtab::name` hands back a `Bytes` -- a second owner -- where almost every caller reads the name and drops it. `Symtab::name_bytes` already exists and borrows; there are 92 `Symbol::as_bytes` call sites, 49 of them in dep.rs. The obstacle is not the change, it is the borrow: a `Bytes` decouples the read from the interner so the caller can go on to touch `self` mutably, which is often WHY it is a clone. The sites worth converting are the predicates -- `input.as_bytes(..).contains(&b'%')`, `.starts_with(b\"-l\")`, `p.as_bytes(..) == b\"%\"` -- which are already inside a closure holding the borrow.\n\nTWO. `parse_expr_impl_ext` is now allocation-bound rather than scan-bound: 8.43% inclusive against 2.72% self, the difference being one `Arc<Value>` per fragment and a `Vec<Arc<Value>>` per list. A line with no `$` in it still costs one `Vec` and one `Arc`. `drop_glue<Value>` is 1.29% by itself. An inline first-element slot would take the common case to zero allocations; an evaluation-scoped arena would take the whole tree there.\n\nTHREE. `rule::file_sequence` clones a `Bytes` per prerequisite word and `glob_word` consumes it into `intern`, which -- since this session's change -- probes borrowed and only converts on a miss. So the clone is now pure waste for every name already interned, which is most of them. Passing the word borrowed and slicing only on the miss is contained to two functions.\n\nAND THE PROCESS FINDING, worth more than any of them: the Make campaign has run its four Make gates for forty nodes and never `scripts/check-performance.sh`, because Make mode does not touch Ninja mode. The allocator was not a Make change or a Ninja change -- it was a change to the BINARY -- and the only gate that caught it was the mode nobody was working in. Anything touching the allocator, the linkage, a global, or a dependency's C build needs both."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T17:48:42.470296179Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "probe note, will be replaced."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T17:49:20.112818093Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "REOPENED 2026-08-31 ON AN OPERATOR RULING, RE-MEASURED, AND REFUSED AGAIN — ON A DIFFERENT AXIS THAN LAST TIME, WHICH IS THE FINDING.\n\nTHE RULING. The refusal was surfaced with the trade stated: \"if you'll wear ~2-4x RSS on a tool that currently peaks under 40 MB, say so and the 14.6% comes back.\" Brendan, verbatim: \"wat so unfuck it.\" That is consent to the footprint, and it is recorded in [dec:ronin:allocator-is-not-the-c-librarys] as a resolved consequence rather than a deferred one.\n\nTHE PREMISE THE RULING WAS GRANTED ON WAS FALSE, AND IT WAS THIS NODE THAT WROTE IT DOWN. The 14.6% is a `perf stat -e instructions:u` reduction. This node's own log calls it \"worth 14.6% of vim-noop\", the dispatch that reopened it called it \"14.6% of vim-noop wall\", and nobody had measured the wall. `instructions:u` counts retired USER instructions. It cannot see a page fault, an `mmap`, an `madvise` or a `munmap` — and acquiring and releasing arenas is precisely what a general-purpose allocator does that the C library's does not.\n\nSplit by privilege level, gated vim no-op, `-r 20`:\n\n    build             ins:u      cycles:u   cycles:k   total      task-clock\n    glibc           103.390M     71.737M    43.866M   115.603M    34.87 ms\n    mimalloc         88.169M     68.547M    65.823M   134.370M    40.48 ms\n    jemalloc,tuned   95.284M     74.015M    83.497M   157.512M    45.51 ms\n\nmimalloc saves 15.2M user instructions and spends 22.0M kernel cycles buying them. The instruction win is real; it is also 4.4% of user cycles rather than 14.7%, because mimalloc's IPC here is worse — and then the kernel takes that back and more.\n\nTHE WALL, paired and interleaved, 31 alternations per arm, which is what should have been measured the first time:\n\n    arm                       cpu_ms   wall_ms   peak RSS   vs base\n    glibc-static (base)        31.13     27.98     8,112     1.000x\n    glibc + mimalloc           37.18     32.38    21,136     1.157x\n    glibc + mimalloc, tuned    35.08     30.83    14,532     1.102x\n    musl-static                38.18     36.25     8,112     1.295x\n    musl + mimalloc            36.04     32.12    20,120     1.148x\n    musl + mimalloc, tuned     33.30     30.36    15,428     1.085x\n\nAgainst GNU Make, paired: GNU 16.36 ms, Ronin 28.01 ms = 1.712x against a recorded 1.71x. THE TREE MEASURES EXACTLY WHERE IT IS RECORDED TO BE, which is what licenses everything else here on a host that was never quiet. Tuned mimalloc puts that row at 1.917x. The swap does not take vim-noop down from 1.71x; it puts it UP by twelve percent. zsh-incremental: 1.052x base, 1.068x tuned mimalloc.\n\nTHE 0.84x NINJA READING WAS THE ALLOCATOR, NOT THE HOST — settled by running the Ninja gate against four binaries in one window. The base binary passes at 0.649x on clean-tree-noop in a validating run in the same session; glibc+mimalloc reads 0.794x. Same host, same hour, load 5-7 throughout.\n\n    workload              glibc base  glibc mi   musl base  musl mi\n    clean-tree-noop         0.697x     0.794x     0.807x    0.820x\n    wide-noop-build         0.936x     1.028x     0.972x    1.077x\n    manifest-command-eval   0.506x     0.760x     0.819x    0.765x\n    path-canonicalization   0.667x     0.936x     0.816x    0.957x\n    large-manifest-parse    0.478x     0.453x     0.767x    0.554x\n\nTHE MECHANISM IS IN THE LAST ROW. `large-manifest-parse` runs 145 ms and is the ONE workload mimalloc improves, on both targets. Every row it regresses runs 4-11 ms. A better allocator wins after it has amortised the arenas it reserved; these workloads end first. Ronin's common case is a few milliseconds, so the amortising case is the rare one. That is not a tuning problem, it is the workload.\n\nTUNING WAS TRIED PROPERLY BEFORE CONCLUDING. `purge_delay=-1,arena_eager_commit=0` is the pair that matters: peak RSS 21,136 to 14,532 KiB, wall +15.7% to +10.2%. `arena_reserve` moves RSS a little further; `eager_commit`, `purge_decommits`, `reserve_huge_os_pages` move nothing here. Tuned beats untuned and loses to the C library's in both modes on both targets. Mode-differentiated tuning was on the table and is moot: there is no mode in which it wins.\n\nTHE MUSL ARM IS THE ONE WITH A REAL CASE, AND IT STILL FAILS. musl-static is what NOS ships, and musl's own allocator is genuinely bad: 29.5% slower than glibc-static on the gated vim no-op with nothing else changed, which independently reproduces `ronin-static-startup`'s finding from the other direction. Tuned mimalloc recovers most of it — 36.25 to 30.36 ms, a 16.2% Make-mode win ON THE CONFIGURATION THAT ACTUALLY SHIPS. It was tempting. It was refused because the same binary regresses three of five Ninja rows and costs 259% of Ninja's peak RSS on clean-tree-noop, and because a target-conditional allocator would put the shipped configuration on a code path no gate here measures — which is the exact condition that produced the first, wrong version of this decision.\n\nVERIFIED RATHER THAN ASSUMED, so nobody repeats it: both allocator crates are proc-macro-free and both build clean for x86_64-unknown-linux-musl. The crt-static constraint is not what stops this.\n\nRSS THRESHOLD NOT RAISED. The operator's budget would have covered every row. It was not needed: the binding refusal is wall, not footprint, and relaxing the RSS ceiling would have admitted a change that is slower on the axis the ruling exists to protect. MAX_NINJA_RSS_RATIO stays at 2.00 — and it lives in `examples/baseline.rs`, not in `scripts/check-performance.sh`, which only execs it.\n\nTHE ARMS ARE NOT LEFT IN THE MANIFEST. They were built, measured and reverted; the tree is byte-identical to be2a708 outside plan/. `.cargo/config.toml` says to decline a dependency rather than carry one, and the reproduction recipe is in the decision record instead.\n\nWHAT THIS NODE BECOMES. Its three leads are now its three children — `file-sequence-clones-a-word-the-interner-only-borrows` (smallest, do first), `an-expression-with-no-dollar-in-it-still-allocates-a-tree`, `symtab-name-hands-out-an-owner-where-a-borrow-would-do` — each carrying the profile evidence. The allocator result strengthens them rather than substituting for them: every arm that removes user-space allocator cost adds more kernel cost than it removes, and an allocation never made has neither.\n\nAND THE PROCESS FINDING, which is this node's second and outranks the first: `instructions:u` is not a result on any workload short enough for allocator setup to matter. A Make no-op spends a third of its cycles in the kernel. This campaign has been reporting instruction counts as wins for forty nodes; where those wins were real reductions in work done they are still real, but any of them that turned on allocation volume deserves a paired wall check before it is banked."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id a-unit-with-many-recursive-recipes-is-read-once-per-recipe
        title "Stage every independent boundary in one pass instead of one per pass"
        kind @perf
        deps (a-notparallel-unit-runs-its-composed-children-at-once)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-31T08:30:23.601139725Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "FILED 2026-08-31 from the measurement on `the-build-starts-only-after-the-last-makefile-is-read`, which went looking for a wall-clock prefix worth 0.2% and found this instead. Not a hypothesis: the numbers below are measured, and the shape is already named in a comment in the code that causes it.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS. A zsh CLEAN build at `-j8`, 33.7 s wall. One process in it — a `Makemod` unit at MAKELEVEL 3 — made SIXTY compose passes:\n\n    composition   4,547 ms over 60 passes, 11 ms on the first and 130 ms on the last\n    staged build  2,062 ms, the work those passes existed to discover\n    goal build      169 ms\n    process wall  6,795 ms of the 33.7 s build\n\nSo 4.5 s of a 33.7 s build — 13.5% — is one Makefile being composed sixty times. Across the whole build, fifteen Ronin processes made 99 compose passes and spent 4,903 ms composing. For comparison the entire subject of the node that found this is worth 0.2% of the same build.\n\nWHY SIXTY. `compose_subninjas` (src/make.rs:1126) walks a unit's recursive recipes in dependency order and returns `incomplete_unit` at the FIRST one whose compiler inputs are not on the ground — src/make.rs:1226 for the wrapper's own prerequisites, src/make.rs:1245 for a child group. The pass then builds what it staged and starts the whole read again. A unit holding N independent recursive recipes therefore costs N reads of itself, and because the graph grows with every pass the cost is quadratic in N, not linear: 11 ms on pass one and 130 ms on pass sixty. zsh's generated `Src/Modules/Makefile` holds two recursive recipes per module across thirty modules, which is where sixty comes from.\n\nTHIS IS ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN, WHICH IS THE STRONGEST EVIDENCE IT IS REAL. The comment at src/make.rs:1216, guarding the `staged_already`/`parent_inputs` short-circuit immediately below it, says: \"Staged the other way round it costs a pass each, and the pass reads the whole compilation again: zsh's generated `Src/Modules/Makefile` holds two recursive recipes per module and made 61 passes over its 3,231 lines to compose ten children, where a `modobjs` with no recursion in it reads the same file once.\" The optimisation that comment defends took the count from 61 to 60. The shape it names is still there and had never been weighed.\n\nTHE DIRECTION, AND WHY IT IS NOT A ONE-LINER. Instead of returning at the first recipe that needs staging, record its boundary and CONTINUE to the next recipe, so one pass stages every boundary that does not depend on another one it staged. Sixty passes collapse toward two or three, and the staged work goes out in parallel rather than sixty rounds of one.\n\nThree things stand in the way and each wants its own answer before any code:\n\n(1) INDEPENDENCE HAS TO BE PROVED, NOT ASSUMED. `dependency_ordered` orders the recipes; it does not say which of them are independent. Composing recipe k+1 while k is incomplete can reach a child Makefile that k's staged work was going to write, and `compose_child_groups` resolves that child eagerly — a missing one is `MakeError::MissingChildMakefile`, which is a hard refusal, not a boundary. Continuing has to be gated on the next recipe's evaluation inputs being disjoint from everything staged so far in the pass.\n\n(2) THE ORDER STAGED WORK RUNS IN IS CONFORMANCE-VISIBLE. `[dec]` on `the-scheduler-picks-ready-edges-without-looking-down-the-graph` established that a serial Make run's recipe order is a criterion the corpus checks, and that the order is carried by the edge identifier — the order of creation. Staging several boundaries at once changes which edges exist when, in the intermediate graphs the staged builds run from. The final pass composes everything in the ordinary order and its graph is unchanged, so the goal build is safe; the makefile-update and staging builds are the ones to check, against `tests/make/shuffled-goals-are-built-back-to-front` and the whole 1229-case corpus.\n\n(3) PHASE SEMANTICS ARE PER RECIPE ALREADY, which helps rather than hurts: `made_for_a_makefile` answers per recipe and `Passes::stage_all` already splits a pass's staged work into the makefile half and the goal half, so a pass carrying several boundaries carries several answers and the split it already makes is the one it keeps making.\n\nEVIDENCE WANTED. The zsh clean-build wall before and after, paired and interleaved on a quiet host — 4.5 s is the ceiling and the real figure will be lower, because the build is `-j8` and some of that composition overlaps other work. Beside it the pass count for the `Makemod` unit, which is exact and does not care what the machine is doing: sixty today, and whatever it becomes. `vim-clean-build` should not move (vim makes four passes in total) and neither should any no-op row.\n\nHOW TO SEE IT. An env-gated phase trace — `enter`, each `compose-begin`/`compose-end`, each `stage-build-begin`/`stage-build-end`, `plan-end`, `run-end`, printed with pid and MAKELEVEL from the phase-driving thread only — around `evaluated` and `build_compiler_inputs` in `prepare_graph` (src/make/cli.rs:1793) and around `plan`/`run` in `reported_run`. Six `eprintln!`s, never from a worker. That is how these numbers were taken; it was reverted rather than landed, and rebuilding it is ten minutes."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T08:39:29.1043125Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "ERRATUM ON THE NOTE ABOVE — THE SIXTY-PASS UNIT IS NAMED PRECISELY NOW. That note calls it \"a `Makemod` unit at MAKELEVEL 3\", which was read off the trace's `MAKELEVEL` and was a guess about which Makefile that level belonged to. It is `Src/Modules`, whose Makefile `Src/mkmakemod.sh` generates — established by sampling `/proc/PID/cwd` and `/proc/PID/cmdline` for every live process through a whole zsh clean build, which finds `zsh-5.9.2` at the top, then `Src`, then TWO successive makes in `Src/Modules`, then `Src/Builtins` and `Src/Zle`. So it is exactly the file the comment at src/make.rs:1216 already names, `Src/Modules/Makefile`, and the two halves of the note agree rather than describing two different things. No number moves: the sixty passes, the 4,547 ms of composition, the 2,062 ms of staged work and the 6,795 ms process wall all come from the phase trace and are untouched by which Makefile it was."
            }

