            {
                at 2026-08-31T12:55:56.413194013Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "THE CHANGE WORKS AND IT IS NOT LANDING. Batching is implemented, measured, and green on every gate this repository has — and it makes the zsh clean build fail two times in six, on a defect that was already in the tree. The code is reverted; the numbers and the blocker are here.\n\nWHAT WAS BUILT. `compose_subninjas` no longer returns at the first recipe it cannot carry. It records the recipe as held and walks on, so every recipe that does not READ a held one stages its own boundary in the same pass. What may be walked on to is decided by `dependency_ordered` and nothing else: that function already computes, for each recipe, which other recipes its evaluation inputs reach — walking `prerequisites_of` through the ordinary edges between one wrapper and another — and it already orders them topologically. Returning that relation beside the order makes \"is this recipe read off a held one\" a lookup rather than a new analysis, and topological order makes the transitive case fall out: a held recipe is always reached before its readers, so holding a reader in turn is enough. Where the sort finds a cycle it says nothing about anything, and the first held recipe holds every recipe after it — which is exactly the read this replaces.\n\nPer-recipe bookkeeping was untouched and had to be: `state.stage`, the `EvaluationPredecessor` triple, `pending_index`, `recipe_begun`, `completed_recipes` and `made_for_a_makefile` are all asked per recipe and answered per recipe, and a pass carrying several boundaries carries several answers. `unfinished` kept its exact recording — the outputs of a wrapper whose CHILD GROUPS stopped, and not those of one that stopped at its own prerequisites — because that asymmetry is about which edges wear the freshness probe, and batching does not change which those are. `Loaded::take_recipes_carried_whole` already empties itself for any pass that reached a boundary, so a recipe this pass composed whole on the way past a held one is still not counted as run.\n\nOne thing did have to be added, and it is the first thing batching broke. A unit that composes WHOLE is cached and handed to the second recipe naming it; one that stopped at a boundary is not, because what it left in the sink is half a unit. While a pass ended at the first recipe it could not carry there was never a second recipe to reach the same unfinished child. Carrying on gave it one, and composing it again read its Makefile a second time — running its `$(shell)` calls twice — and emitted its edges a second time. A `RapidHashSet` of the keys a pass could not finish fixes it: a child already found unfinished holds the recipe that names it. `tests/make_regressions.rs` had a case for it that fails 2-runs-against-1 without the set.\n\nTHE N-SCALING, which is what the node asked for. `perf stat -e instructions:u`, N independent recursive recipes in one Makefile each dispatching into its own child directory — the shape `recursive_recipes_beyond_the_retry_limit_still_compose` uses, one child per recipe rather than the shared one, which reproduces this node's recorded before-column to within 3%:\n\n    recipes   GNU 4.4.1     Ronin before      Ronin batched    before/GNU   after/GNU\n      10        27.7 M        209.8 M            39.6 M          7.6x        1.43x\n      20        53.3 M        736.6 M            73.8 M         13.8x        1.38x\n      40       104.5 M      2,749.8 M           143.0 M         26.3x        1.37x\n      80       206.8 M     10,623.8 M           285.0 M         51.4x        1.38x\n\n    per doubling of N:\n      GNU            x1.92  x1.96  x1.98\n      Ronin before   x3.51  x3.73  x3.86\n      Ronin batched  x1.86  x1.94  x1.99\n\nIt doubles. The ratio to GNU stops growing with N and sits flat at 1.4x, where before it grew without bound.\n\nTHE PASS COUNTS, from an env-gated `eprintln!` on the phase-driving thread in `prepare_graph`, since reverted. A zsh CLEAN build, whole tree:\n\n                        before   after\n      Src/Modules         60       6\n      Src/Zle             11       3\n      Src                  9       6\n      Src/Builtins         5       3\n      whole build         99      31\n\nSixty to six on the Makefile this node is named for, and the 99 the earlier measurement recorded is reproduced exactly before the change.\n\nTHE WALL, single clean builds, load 4.1 to 6.0 throughout (a foreign tenant holding four cores, as it has all campaign): GNU Make 36.4 s, Ronin before 25.0 s, Ronin batched 21.4 s. There is no paired series, because the paired harness aborts on a failing arm and the batched arm failed — twice, on the first repetition, which is how the blocker was found.\n\nTHE BLOCKER. `scripts/check-make-projects.sh zsh` is a gate, and with batching the zsh clean build fails 2 times in 6:\n\n    gawk: fatal: cannot open file `sigtmp.out' for reading: No such file or directory\n    FAILED: [code=2] signames.c\n\nTwo edges run `signames.c`'s recipe at the same time and each deletes the temporary files the other is reading. They are two edges because `Src/Makefile` invokes `Makemod` from five recipes with five different goals, each of which composes a whole separate unit holding every one of `Makemod`'s rules in isolated nodes — and two of those goals reach `signames.c`. GNU Make never runs two of them at once because `Src/Makefile` declares `.NOTPARALLEL`; Ronin compiles `.NOTPARALLEL` to a depth-one pool PER UNIT, so the five units run in parallel with each other.\n\nThat is not this change's defect. It reproduces on f1d90b5 with no code of this node in the tree, in twelve lines, and it is filed as `a-notparallel-unit-runs-its-composed-children-at-once` with the repro and the measurements. Ronin escapes it today only by accident: its staging passes build the shared work one copy at a time, so the copies are never dirty together. Eight clean zsh builds at f1d90b5, zero failures; six with batching, two failures.\n\nAND IT CANNOT BE DESIGNED AROUND FROM HERE, which is why the change is reverted rather than narrowed. Every narrowing that removes the hazard removes the win with it:\n\n  * \"compose at most one new unit per Makefile per pass\" — `Src/Modules`'s sixty recipes all invoke ONE makefile with sixty different goals, so this is one recipe per pass and sixty passes again. Worse: a pass that only composes a settled recipe stages no new boundary, so the no-progress counter would refuse it.\n  * \"hold a recipe whose child shares a Makefile with one already composed\" — the same case, the same sixty passes.\n  * \"serialise every path with more than one producer\" — every path in a sixty-times-composed Makefile has sixty producers, so this serialises the whole build.\n  * batching only the parent-prerequisite boundary and not the child groups — the copies still all arrive in the same later pass; the final pass has composed all sixty of them either way.\n\nThe hazard is inherent to composing many copies of one Makefile into one graph, which is what the win IS.\n\nWHAT THE FIX COSTS, measured so the next node does not have to guess. Keying the depth-one pool by the unit's command directory instead of by the unit — so every `.NOTPARALLEL` unit reading a makefile in one directory shares one pool — makes the batched zsh clean build pass, at 37.9 s against GNU Make's 36.4 s on the same host, where the unserialised batched build is 21.4 s. zsh declares `.NOTPARALLEL` in all seven of its makefiles. So Ronin's zsh clean-build lead is, at least in part, `.NOTPARALLEL` non-compliance, and that is a campaign number that must not move inside a perf node's commit. The proper instrument is probably not a pool at all — GNU serialises the RECIPES while letting each sub-make parallelise inside itself, which order-only chaining between a unit's successive recursive wrappers does exactly and a depth-one pool over the subtree does not. See the filed node.\n\nGATES, ON THE BATCHED TREE, all green, which is the other half of the point:\n\n    Make equivalence   274 / 276 / 8 / 56 / 0 — TWENTY runs, every column identical on\n                       all twenty, `one_graph_however_many_threads_read_it` with each\n    Make conformance   354 runs; 276 identical raw, 326 normalised, 28 differing\n    Upstream           18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 15 interface\n    Port ledger        170 symbols\n    Ninja conformance  425/425 upstream, 10 differential, 92 build-outcome,\n                       3 invocation-boundary; persistence round trip, log content\n    cargo test         897 / 0 and 1213 / 0 (895 + 1211 plus the two added)\n    fmt / lints / doc  clean, no new warnings\n\nEvery one of those passed while the zsh clean build was failing one time in three. The equivalence gate cannot see this: it compares one evaluation's direct graph against that same evaluation's manifest, never a before-graph against an after-graph, and its corpus is single-Makefile kati testcases with no recursive staging boundary in them at all.\n\nGATES ON THE TREE AS IT STANDS, which is f1d90b5's source byte for byte plus this plan file: `cargo test --all-targets` 895 / 0, Make equivalence 274 / 276 / 8 / 56 / 0, `check-make-projects.sh` builds vim in 16 s and zsh with 36 modules in 25 s, `cargo fmt --check` clean. The two performance gates are not re-run: there is no code delta from f1d90b5 to measure, and the host has been between 4.1 and 6.0 all session with another tenant's four pinned cores, so a run would be a reading of the host.\n\nTHE BRIEF'S ERROR, and there are two.\n\nThe one that cost something: \"The graph must remain byte-identical ... The equivalence gate is the check.\" It is not the check and it cannot be. `make::equivalence::the_direct_graph_matches_the_manifest_over_the_corpus` compares two renderings of ONE evaluation — the direct graph against the manifest that same evaluation emits — so it is silent about whether the graph changed between revisions. And its 274 makefiles are kati testcases: single files, no recursive child, no staging boundary, so the code path this node changes is never entered. Twenty identical runs said nothing at all about the change, and the thing that found the defect was building zsh.\n\nThe one that is simply false: \"Watch the interaction with ... the chained read-ahead: more staging per pass changes what is in flight.\" It cannot. Read-ahead is all-or-nothing per unit and refuses any unit that could stage: `reads_may_start_early` (src/make/parallel.rs:1420) requires EVERY recipe to be `always_dirty`, to have one invocation, no preceding rule and `evaluation_inputs().is_empty()`, and `PopulatedBuild::liftable_recursions` (kati/src-rs/ninja.rs:3221) answers `None` for a unit any of whose recipes has deps or order-onlys, which is what gates the chained reads. So read-ahead is on exactly the units where nothing can ever be held, and off on exactly the units this change alters. The two are disjoint by construction, and there is no interaction to watch."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T16:48:21.876772852Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "LANDED, AND THE REVERTED DESIGN WOULD HAVE FAILED AGAIN AS WRITTEN. The `.NOTPARALLEL` fix did not close the hazard that reverted this node, and re-landing the recorded design verbatim reproduced it in the first six zsh clean builds. What landed is that design with one more rule in it, and the rule is the finding.\n\nTHE ZSH WALL, ten paired clean builds per arm, arms alternating within each round, one-minute load 4.95 to 5.72 throughout (the same four foreign `nsh` processes at 99.9% that the last two nodes recorded, still running):\n\n    GNU Make 4.4.1  36.34 36.45 36.47 36.47 36.50 36.55 36.62 36.63 36.72 36.73  median 36.52\n    Ronin after     37.19 37.22 37.24 37.32 37.33 37.42 37.43 37.50 37.56 37.63  median 37.38  1.023x\n    Ronin before    40.93 41.03 41.11 41.12 41.18 41.21 41.25 41.29 41.37 41.41  median 41.20  1.128x\n\nThirty of thirty exit 0 with 36 modules and a linked `Src/zsh`; thirteen more clean builds of the after arm during development, also thirty-of-thirty green counting those, against a shape that failed 3-in-6 under the reverted design. 1.128x to 1.023x: of the 4.68 s Ronin was behind by, 3.82 s is gone and 0.86 s is left.\n\nTHE N-SCALING, `perf stat -e instructions:u`, N independent recursive recipes each dispatching into its own child directory:\n\n    recipes   GNU 4.4.1     Ronin before      Ronin after     before/GNU   after/GNU\n      10        27.9 M        211.3 M            40.0 M          7.57x       1.43x\n      20        53.6 M        741.8 M            74.6 M         13.84x       1.39x\n      40       105.0 M       2768.4 M           144.4 M         26.35x       1.37x\n      80       207.9 M      10696.4 M           287.8 M         51.44x       1.38x\n\n    per doubling of N:  GNU x1.92 x1.96 x1.98\n                        before x3.51 x3.73 x3.86\n                        after  x1.86 x1.94 x1.99\n\nIt doubles. The reverted landing recorded 7.6/13.8/26.3/51.4 collapsing to 1.43/1.38/1.37/1.38 and this is that table to within 1%.\n\nPASSES, from an env-gated `eprintln!` on the phase-driving thread in `prepare_graph`, since reverted. A zsh CLEAN build, whole tree, counted per directory:\n\n                        before   after\n      Src/Modules         66       11\n      Src/Zle             14        6\n      Src/Builtins         8        6\n      Src                  9        5\n      top + Doc            2        2\n      whole build         99       30\n\n99 before, exactly the figure the filing measured, and 30 against the reverted landing's 31. NOTE ON `Src/Modules`: the brief and the earlier note say 60, which is ONE PROCESS's count — the erratum on this node already records that zsh runs two successive makes in that directory, and the directory's total is 66. Nothing else moves.\n\nWHAT WAS BUILT, and the one place it departs from the record.\n\n`dependency_ordered` already computed, for each recursive recipe, which other recipes its evaluation inputs reach, and threw the relation away after sorting. It now reports it beside the order, in the order the composition walks rather than the order kati emitted, so \"is this recipe read off one the pass could not carry\" is a lookup. Composition order puts a producer before anything that reads it, so holding each reader as it arrives holds everything downstream of a boundary however long the chain; where the sort leaves a cyclic remainder the relation says nothing about anything and the first held recipe holds the rest, which is exactly the read this replaces.\n\nSTAGING BATCHES. A pass no longer returns at the first recipe whose compiler inputs are not on the ground: it records the boundary and walks on, so every recipe that does not read a held one stages its own boundary in the same pass. That is safe for a reason worth stating: a boundary is a PREREQUISITE of the wrapper that reads it, so it sits below that wrapper and no provisional build of it reaches one. Staging N of them together is the same work in one build instead of N.\n\nCOMPOSING DOES NOT BATCH, and this is the rule the reverted design was missing. Every composition of one makefile holds its own ISOLATED copy of every rule in it, each settled against the disk as it was before any of them ran. Two boundaries left in two copies put both copies' shared work into ONE provisional build, and one recipe then runs twice at once. So a pass composes children only until one of them stops: a composition that completed leaves no boundary behind it, and a pass that has stopped inside one composes no further child. That is precisely the set of fresh compositions the read this replaces would have made, plus nothing.\n\nMEASURED, not reasoned. A scan of every `[i/N]` line in a zsh clean build, segmented by the provisional builds themselves and counting identical command lines within one segment:\n\n    Ronin main                        58 builds, 0 duplicate edges in any one build\n    Ronin, reverted design re-landed  29 builds, 66 duplicate edges, worst 2\n    Ronin as landed                   28 builds, 0 duplicate edges in any one build\n\nThe worst pair is `signames.c`'s recipe, and it is the failure the revert protected against, arriving in a STAGING build rather than the goal build:\n\n    [2/133] set -e ; ... gawk -f ./signames1.awk ... >sigtmp.c ... > signames.c ...\n    [3/133] set -e ; ... gawk -f ./signames1.awk ... >sigtmp.c ... > signames.c ...\n    FAILED: [code=2] signames.c\n    gawk: fatal: cannot open file `sigtmp.out' for reading: No such file or directory\n\nThree failures in six clean builds with the reverted design in the tree, every one of them 0.07 s in with four passes made. The intermediate rule \"compose nothing at all past a held recipe\" also passes zsh but costs most of the win — 61 passes, 39.3 s — because `Src/Modules`'s sixty recipes are all held at prerequisite boundaries; stopping at the first STOPPED CHILD instead is 30 passes and 37.4 s.\n\nTHE FORCED ADDITION FROM THE FIRST LANDING IS STILL FORCED. A unit that composes whole is cached and handed to the second recipe naming it; one that stopped is half a unit and is not. A `RapidHashSet` of the keys a pass could not finish holds the second recipe instead of composing that child again, which would read its Makefile twice — running its `$(shell)` calls twice — and emit a second copy of its edges. `tests/make_regressions.rs` measures it 4-reads-against-2 without the set.\n\nTHE SERIALISATION INTERACTION: the wiring still lands where it should, and batching cannot move the chain's order. `chain_serial_jobs` runs only where the unit composed WHOLE, and in that pass every recipe composes whole in `dependency_ordered`'s order, which is the order it was in before — so the chain the build gets is the same chain however many passes led to it. A pass that held anything returns ahead of the call and wires nothing. Every row of the `.NOTPARALLEL` node's evidence reproduces unmoved, flock'd counter, `-j8`:\n\n    60 recursive recipes into one makefile, declared   GNU 1  before 1  after 1\n    the same 60 with no declaration                    GNU 8  before 8  after 8\n    parent .NOTPARALLEL=0 child=0                      GNU 8  before 8  after 8\n    parent .NOTPARALLEL=1 child=0  <- the boundary     GNU 8  before 8  after 8\n    parent .NOTPARALLEL=0 child=1                      GNU 1  before 1  after 1\n    parent .NOTPARALLEL=1 child=1                      GNU 1  before 1  after 1\n    the twelve-line repro          GNU `run`, before `run run`, after `run run`, no OVERLAP\n    -k after a failed recipe       all three `fail ok`\n\nTHE GRAPH IS THE SAME GRAPH, checked directly rather than inferred. An env-gated dump of the final graph — every node path in arena order, then every edge as its outputs and inputs, deliberately unsorted because insertion order is the thing a scheduling difference would move — taken from both binaries built from one tree with only this change between them:\n\n    zsh clean build      15 graphs per arm, every one byte-identical\n    vim clean build       3 graphs per arm, every one byte-identical\n    12 independent recursions   identical, passes 13 -> 2\n    12 recipes into one makefile with 12 goals   identical, passes 13 -> 2\n    8 of them under .NOTPARALLEL                 identical, passes  9 -> 2\n    the zsh Makemod shape, recursive -> ordinary -> recursive   identical, 13 -> 3\n    two recipes naming one child that stops      identical, passes  2 -> 2\n    a chain where each recipe reads the last one's output       identical, passes 9 -> 9\n    a cycle between two wrappers   identical, both exit 2 with the same diagnostic\n\nThe chain row is the one that says the relation works: nine passes before and nine after, because there is nothing independent to batch, and the same graph out of it.\n\nTHE STAGING BUILDS DO LOOK DIFFERENT, which is the intended change and the only visible one. Twelve boundaries that went out as twelve builds of `[1/1]` now go out as one build of `[1/12]`, the same commands, all of them run. Under `.NOTPARALLEL` they come out in recipe order, because the unit's depth-one pool holds its own command edges apart — closer to GNU than before, not further.\n\nA DEFECT FOUND IN THE COMMITTED `.NOTPARALLEL` LANDING, NOT THIS NODE'S AND NOT FIXED HERE. A `.NOTPARALLEL` parent whose two recursive recipes reach ONE cached child, where the composed child also stages a boundary, is refused as a cycle:\n\n    ronin: dependency cycle: a -> ta -> inner -> leaf -> gen -> shared -> a\n\nReproduced on pristine main at eefdf47, in twelve lines, so it is 0bc9e5e's and not this change's; both arms give the identical diagnostic. `ChildGroup::fresh` keeps the cached child out of the chain, but the cached child still collects order-only inputs from BOTH recipes, and the wait the chain puts on the second job's other edges closes the loop through it. Dropping the `.NOTPARALLEL:` line builds it. The regression test added here reaches two different child makefiles precisely to stay clear of it.\n\nSPEC. No rule covered the staging pass at all — `make.compiler-boundary` does not mention it and `make.notparallel-domain` only says the chain must stay out of it. Added `[spec:ronin:req:make.compiler-input-staging]` v0 beside `make.recursive-invocation`: a boundary is not a refusal, a pass stages every boundary it can reach and holds only what is read off a held one, composing is not staging and stops at the first stopped child, an unfinished unit is not composed twice in a pass, and none of it reaches the graph the build runs. Three impl sites, three verify sites, `nplan_spec_validate` clean with warnings denied, no stale references. Nothing version-bumped, because nothing existed to amend.\n\nTESTS. Three in `tests/make_regressions.rs`, each with its teeth measured against the mechanism it guards rather than against the whole change:\n\n  * `many_independent_recursions_stage_in_one_pass` — six boundaries that each wait for all six to have arrived. Staged one per pass they never meet; the case then fails on its own timeout in 10.7 s rather than on a count. A `$(shell)` counter was tried first and is USELESS here, which is worth recording: a repeated read replays what the first read was told instead of asking again, so the counter reads 1 however many passes there are.\n  * `one_copy_of_a_makefile_stages_per_pass` — zsh's `signames.c` in twelve lines. `OVERLAP run run` in 3 of 3 with the composing rule off, `run` with it on, `run` on main, `run` under GNU Make 4.4.1.\n  * `a_stopped_child_is_read_once_a_pass` — the child's own `$(shell)` against the parent's. 4 reads against the parent's 2 without the unfinished-unit set, 2 against 2 with it.\n\nCODE SHAPE. `function-density` holds `src/make.rs` at 49 of 50 and `too_many_lines` held `compose_subninjas` at exactly 100, so this change had nowhere to go and the way through was to move code out rather than to bypass either gate. `src/make/order.rs` is new and holds the three things that are all about the sequencing of one unit's recursive recipes: `dependency_ordered`, the `Holds` that says what a pass could not carry, and the `SerialJobs` whose six lines the `.NOTPARALLEL` node had inlined into `compose_subninjas` under exactly this pressure. `CompilationState::staged_already` names the question that was five lines inline, and `stage_recursive_wrapper` now asks the context for the dates it asserts instead of having them assembled at the call. make.rs is still 49 functions and `compose_subninjas` is 105 physical lines against a 100-line count that excludes comment and blank lines.\n\nGATES.\n\n    cargo test        900 / 0 and 1216 / 0  (897 + 1213 plus the three added)\n    equivalence       20 runs, every one 274 makefiles / 276 graphs / 8 respellings /\n                      56 make-rejected / 0 disagreements, `one_graph_however_many_threads_read_it` with each\n    conformance       354 runs; 276 identical raw, 326 normalised, 28 differing\n    upstream          18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 15 interface\n    port ledger       170 symbols\n    ninja conformance 425/425 upstream, 10 differential, 92 build-outcome,\n                      3 invocation-boundary, persistence round trip, log content\n    projects          vim 15 s, zsh with 36 modules 38 s (41 s before)\n    fmt / lints / doc clean, no new warnings\n\n    check-performance.sh       exit 0, load 5.46 -> 6.18\n      manifest-command-evaluation 0.522x  deep-graph-evaluation 0.836x\n      wide-noop-build 0.800x              path-canonicalization 0.603x\n      dependency-log-load 0.762x          clean-tree-noop 0.657x\n      large-manifest-parse 0.480x         scheduler-barrier 1.109x — still the one row\n                                          Ronin is behind on, untouched by a Make-only change\n\n    check-make-performance.sh  exit 0, load 4.19 -> 4.53, --max-load 4.5 RAISED\n      wide-noop        0.58x  against 0.618x recorded    94%\n      recursive-noop   0.98x  against 1.009x recorded    97%\n      vim-noop         1.80x  against 1.710x recorded   105%\n      zsh-incremental  1.02x  against 1.033x recorded    99%\n\n`recursive-noop` is the canary the brief named — 259 undeclared units, nothing to batch because nothing stages — and it did not move. `zsh-incremental` did not either.\n\nTHE CEILING, said out loud because a raised ceiling should never be quiet. The gate refused at 4.13 after waiting its own 300 s, and a watcher sampling every 20 s for 71 minutes never saw the one-minute average below 4.04. The floor is four `nsh` processes from another campaign's worktree, each pinned at 99.9% of a core, running the same `case.sh` files that f1d90b5 and 0bc9e5e both recorded — four cores of thirty-two, and twenty-eight cores this gate's `-j8` workloads never contended for. Raised to 4.5 once, exactly as those two did, and recorded as what it is.\n\nNOTHING WAS RE-RECORDED. The re-record debt stands for the fourth dispatch running and for the same reason: the recording protocol measures below load 4.00, the host never reached it, and baking this host into the gate is what `benchmarks/make-baseline-2026-08-30.md` exists to warn against. zsh-incremental's unbanked sub-1.00x reading still wants a quiet window and still has not had one.\n\nTHE BRIEF'S ERROR, and it is the load-bearing one. \"That defect is now fixed (`0bc9e5e`) and the node is unblocked\" — with the remaining risk framed as \"verify the wiring still lands on completed compositions when many complete in one pass\". The wiring does still land there, and it was never the question. `.NOTPARALLEL`'s chain is wired ONLY onto a composition that has settled and, by the deliberate decision recorded on that node, never into what a staging pass builds — both of which would turn a wait into work, measured there twice at exit 2. So it cannot reach a provisional build, and a provisional build is exactly where the two copies of `signames.c` collide. The fix that unblocked this node does not cover the case that blocked it: re-landing the recorded design on top of it reproduces the failure at 3-in-6 with 66 duplicate edges to point at. What actually unblocked the node was a rule nobody had written down — that composing, unlike staging, may not go past a stopped child — and the hour it cost to find that is the hour the unblocking premise bought.\n\nA second, smaller: \"`Src/Modules` was 60 → 6 in the reverted landing; verify it survives\" reads 60 as the directory's pass count when it is one of two processes in that directory, whose total is 66. The comparable figures are 66 → 11."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T16:58:09.321514745Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE COMMITTED TREE, 3f6c633, verified after the fact. The spec rule and its six annotations landed after the paired walls were taken, so the arm was rebuilt from the commit and re-measured; they are comments and a markdown file, and nothing moved.\n\n    zsh clean, three more paired builds at load 5.02 to 5.31:\n      GNU Make 4.4.1  36.41 36.58\n      Ronin after     37.45 37.57 37.68\n      Ronin before    40.89 41.43\n    Three of three exit 0 with 36 modules. THIRTY-THREE clean zsh builds of the\n    after arm across the dispatch, zero failures.\n\n    duplicate edges in any one provisional build   28 builds, 0\n    equivalence      5 more runs, every one 274/276/8/56/0, 0 disagreements\n                     (25 runs on this change in total)\n    the three added tests, on the committed binary   3 / 0\n\nThe two performance gates are not re-run: there is no code delta from the tree they were taken on, and the host has been between 4.04 and 7.53 for the whole dispatch with another tenant's four pinned cores, so a run would be a reading of the host and not of the change."
            }

