    {
        id a-notparallel-unit-runs-its-composed-children-at-once
        title "Serialise the recursive children a .NOTPARALLEL unit composes"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-31T12:54:55.088780426Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "FILED 2026-08-31 from inside `a-unit-with-many-recursive-recipes-is-read-once-per-recipe`, which could not land because of it. Measured on the UNMODIFIED tree at f1d90b5, so it is not that node's doing — that node only made it bite.\n\nTHE DEFECT. `.NOTPARALLEL` is compiled as a per-unit Ninja pool of depth one (`serialise_unit`, src/make/sink.rs:386, landed as bcd200e under make-subninja-recursion). That serialises a unit's own command edges against each other and nothing else. GNU Make's `.NOTPARALLEL` also serialises the unit's RECIPES, and a recursive recipe's duration is the whole sub-make it starts — so two `$(MAKE)` recipes of a `.NOTPARALLEL` makefile never overlap in GNU and always may in Ronin.\n\nTWELVE LINES, run against GNU Make 4.4.1 and against `target/release/ronin` through a `make`-named symlink, `-j8`, no code of the open node in the tree:\n\n    # Makefile\n    .NOTPARALLEL:\n    .PHONY: all a b\n    all: a b\n    inA: mk.sh ; @sh mk.sh inA\n    inB: mk.sh ; @sh mk.sh inB\n    a: inA ; @$(MAKE) -f child.mk ta\n    b: inB ; @$(MAKE) -f child.mk tb\n\n    # child.mk\n    shared:\n            @if [ -e busy ]; then echo OVERLAP >> ranlog; fi; : > busy; sleep 1; \\\n             rm -f busy; echo run >> ranlog; : > shared\n    ta: shared ; @:\n    tb: shared ; @:\n\n    GNU Make 4.4.1   ranlog = \"run\"                 — one run, the second sub-make finds `shared` current\n    Ronin (f1d90b5)  ranlog = \"OVERLAP run run\"     — both run, and they overlap\n\nDrop the `.NOTPARALLEL:` line and both tools give `OVERLAP run run`, which is the control: Ronin is not wrong about two independent sub-makes, it is wrong about a makefile that said not to run two at once.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND TIDINESS. A unit composed twice from one Makefile gets two ISOLATED copies of every rule (`GraphSink::node` takes `isolated_node` for a non-root unit, src/make/sink.rs:768), so the same recipe exists twice in one graph and both copies may be dirty at once. zsh does this five times over in `Src`, whose `Makefile` invokes `Makemod` from five recipes with five different goals; two of those goals reach `signames.c`, whose recipe writes and then deletes `sigtmp.c`/`sigtmp.out`. Two copies running at once is:\n\n    gawk: fatal: cannot open file `sigtmp.out' for reading: No such file or directory\n    FAILED: [code=2] signames.c\n\nRonin does not hit it today only because its staging passes happen to build the shared work one copy at a time — 0 failures in 8 clean zsh builds at f1d90b5. Any change that composes more of a unit per pass hits it: 2 failures in 6 with the batching from the open node.\n\nAND WHAT THE FIX COSTS, measured rather than guessed, because it is the reason this is its own node. A one-line experiment keyed the depth-one pool by the unit's command directory instead of by the unit, so every `.NOTPARALLEL` unit reading a makefile in one directory shares one pool. zsh's clean build then went from 21.4 s to 37.9 s against GNU Make's 36.4 s on the same host — because zsh declares `.NOTPARALLEL` in its top `Makefile`, `Src/Makefile`, `Src/Makemod`, `Src/Modules/Makefile`, `Src/Zle/Makefile`, `Src/Builtins/Makefile` and `Doc/Makefile`, which is to say everywhere. So RONIN'S ZSH CLEAN-BUILD LEAD OVER GNU MAKE IS, AT LEAST IN PART, `.NOTPARALLEL` NON-COMPLIANCE, and honouring it puts the two tools level. That is a number the campaign reports and it should not move by accident inside another node's commit.\n\nWHAT THE NODE HAS TO ESTABLISH FIRST, none of which the experiment above did. Probe 4.4.1 for: whether `.NOTPARALLEL` reaches a sub-make at all (it should not — the child's own declaration is what governs the child); whether the serialisation is per makefile INSTANCE or per makefile TEXT, which is what decides whether two compositions of one file share a constraint; and what the narrow `.NOTPARALLEL: targets` form does, which make-notparallel-scheduling explicitly did not claim. The pool is probably the wrong instrument for the answer: GNU serialises the recipes while letting each sub-make parallelise INSIDE itself, which a depth-one pool over the whole subtree does not, and which order-only chaining between one unit's successive recursive wrappers — the mechanism `stage_completed_group` already is — does exactly."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T12:59:22.946810563Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T14:05:17.453124848Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "THE OVERLAP IS GONE AND IT COST WHAT IT WAS ALWAYS GOING TO COST. Ronin's zsh clean build is now 41.3 s against GNU Make's 36.6 s on the same host in the same window, where the unserialised tree is 25.0 s. The 16 s Ronin was ahead by was `.NOTPARALLEL` non-compliance, all of it, and it is gone.\n\nTHE REPRO, the node's own twelve lines, `-j8`, alternating on one host:\n\n    GNU Make 4.4.1        run\n    Ronin at d9de77a      OVERLAP run run\n    Ronin fixed           run run          — no OVERLAP, in 3 of 3 and in every later run\n\nThe OVERLAP line is the defect and it is gone. The second `run` is NOT this node's and never was: with the `.NOTPARALLEL:` line removed both tools write `OVERLAP run run`, so the count of runs is not something the declaration governs. It is that two compositions of one makefile hold two ISOLATED copies of its rules, both settled against the disk as it was before either ran, where GNU's second process re-stats `shared` and finds it current. Serialising the copies makes them sequential; it cannot make the later one's compile-time freshness question be asked later. Filed below.\n\nWHERE THE DOMAIN IS, AND THE BRIEF IS WRONG ABOUT IT. The brief directs that \"the pool must be keyed by the makefile identity (all compositions of one Makefile share one serialisation domain), not by the composed unit\". GNU has no such domain. `not_parallel` is a plain global of one make PROCESS (main.c:591), written once in `snap_deps` (file.c:881) after every makefile, include and `-f` has been read, and read in exactly two places: `job.c:1962` and `shuffle.c:225`. Five `make -f Makemod` invocations are five processes and five domains that constrain each other not at all — what holds them apart is that their PARENT, `Src/Makefile`, declared it and runs its five recipes one at a time.\n\nA compiled unit is that process, so the key Ronin already had is the right one. The defect was never the key; it was the EXTENT. GNU's serialised unit is a JOB, and a recursive recipe's job is the whole sub-make: `new_job` starts it and then blocks in `reap_children` until the child has exited. Ronin's depth-one pool holds a slot only while one command runs, and the composition has dissolved that sub-make into edges of the same graph whose wrapper is a phony that runs at the END of it. The pool could never have said this, whatever it was keyed by.\n\nThe brief's key is also unimplementable on the corpus it cites. zsh's `.NOTPARALLEL` is not in seven makefiles; it is in ONE — `Config/defs.mk`, which all seven include (`Src/Makemod` declares it as well). \"The makefile that declared it\" is a single file for the whole tree, so keying on it would fuse every unit of zsh into one domain and serialise the entire build. Keying on the unit's own top makefile is closer and still wrong, for the reason above.\n\nWHAT WAS BUILT. Each recursive recipe of a `.NOTPARALLEL` unit is a job: its wrapper edge, plus every edge of the children THAT recipe composed. Taken in `dependency_ordered`'s order, each job's edges get the previous job's wrapper targets as order-only inputs. The wait is FORGIVEN (`graph::forgiven`, built for double-colon chains): GNU blocks on the job finishing and not on it succeeding, so `-k` still reaches the recipe after a failed one. `dependency_ordered` now also reports how much of its order it actually settled, and the chain runs only across that prefix — a cyclic remainder stands in no relation to anything and Make's own cycle diagnostics answer for it. A child reached from the composition cache is not chained: two recipes naming one cached child are two names for a single copy of work the build runs once, and sequencing it behind the recipe that made it is a cycle. That is `ChildGroup::fresh`.\n\nAND THE THING THAT NEARLY LANDED BROKEN, which is the finding worth more than the fix. `.NOTPARALLEL` is a SCHEDULER constraint — `not_parallel` appears nowhere in `remake.c` — and threading it into the graph while the composition is still running breaks the build twice over. Both were measured on zsh, both times exit 2:\n\n  * Into the FRESHNESS question. The previous recipe's targets are `.PHONY` names never on disk; an order-only prerequisite that cannot be found is a reason to remake; every wrapper carrying one is dirty for good.\n  * Into what a STAGING PASS builds. A boundary is built from the graph as it stands, so a wait threaded into that graph drags the previous recipe's whole subtree into a provisional build that had no reason to want it. The subtree runs there, is composed again afterwards, and runs twice. `Src/Makefile`'s `modobjs` recursion went from one staged append to three, `stamp-modobjs` held all 31 objects three times, and `gcc -o zsh` died on `multiple definition of 'utils.o'` symbols — with the clean build making 96 provisional graphs where it makes 52.\n\nSo the chain is wired once, at the end of a composition that completed, over the edges the build will actually schedule. With that, the clean build is back to 52-58 graphs and 550 edges — main's numbers — and `stamp-modobjs` is 31.\n\nEVIDENCE. Peak concurrency inside one domain, flock'd counter, GNU beside Ronin, `-j8`:\n\n    60 recursive recipes into one makefile, .NOTPARALLEL      GNU 1   Ronin 1\n    the same 60 with no declaration                           GNU 8   Ronin 8\n    parent .NOTPARALLEL=0 child=0                             GNU 8   Ronin 8\n    parent .NOTPARALLEL=1 child=0                             GNU 8   Ronin 8   <- the boundary\n    parent .NOTPARALLEL=0 child=1                             GNU 1   Ronin 1\n    parent .NOTPARALLEL=1 child=1                             GNU 1   Ronin 1\n\nThe third row is the one the brief asked for and the one a pool inherited by the child would have failed: a `.NOTPARALLEL` parent hands its children `-jN` and `--jobserver-auth` untouched — `define_makeflags` has no case for `not_parallel`, and the jobserver is created at main.c:2217 BEFORE `snap_deps` has discovered the declaration at all — so the child parallelises unless its own makefiles say otherwise (doc/make.texi:4416). Under `-k`, a recipe after a failed one still runs: both tools write `fail ok`.\n\nZSH, nine paired clean builds, alternating arms, one-minute load 4.32 to 5.35 throughout (the same four foreign `nsh` processes at 99.9% that f1d90b5 recorded, still running):\n\n    GNU Make 4.4.1     36.5 36.5 36.6 36.6 36.6 36.6 36.6 36.7 36.8   median 36.6\n    Ronin fixed        40.8 40.9 41.2 41.2 41.3 41.3 41.3 41.3 41.5   median 41.3   1.13x\n    Ronin at d9de77a   24.8 24.9 25.0 25.1                            median 25.0   0.68x\n\nNine of nine exit 0 with 36 modules and a linked `Src/zsh`, against the shape that failed 2-in-6 under batching. The 4.7 s Ronin is now behind by is composition: the same serial critical path GNU walks, plus the ~4.9 s this tree spends composing zsh 99 times over. That is `a-unit-with-many-recursive-recipes-is-read-once-per-recipe`, which depends on this node and takes those 99 passes to 31.\n\nGATES. Equivalence 274/276/8/56/0, TWENTY runs, every column identical on all twenty, `one_graph_however_many_threads_read_it` with each. Conformance 354 runs, 276 identical raw, 326 normalised, 28 differing — no row moved. Upstream 18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 15 interface. Port ledger 170. Ninja conformance 425/425 upstream, 10 differential, 92 build-outcome, 3 invocation-boundary, persistence round trip and log content. `cargo test --all-targets` 897/0 and `--workspace` 1213/0 (895 + 1211 plus the two added). fmt, `scripts/check-lints.sh` and `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc` clean. `check-make-projects.sh` builds vim in 16 s and zsh with 36 modules in 41 s.\n\nBOTH PERFORMANCE GATES PASSED, NOTHING RE-RECORDED.\n\n    scripts/check-performance.sh        exit 0  load 4.13 -> 4.26\n    scripts/check-make-performance.sh   exit 0  load 4.26 -> 5.24, --max-load 4.5 RAISED,\n                                                for f1d90b5's reason and no other\n\n    Make gate     recorded   measured\n      wide-noop      0.618x    0.61x\n      recursive-noop 1.009x    0.96x   <- the canary: 259 units, no declaration, not serialised\n      vim-noop       1.710x    1.95x   (114% of recorded, in band; a 15 ms workload)\n      zsh-incremental 1.033x   1.09x   (105% of recorded, in band; moved for the honest reason)\n\n    Ninja gate  manifest-command-evaluation 0.551x, deep-graph-evaluation 0.807x,\n                wide-noop-build 0.861x, path-canonicalization 0.628x,\n                dependency-log-load 0.840x, clean-tree-noop 0.662x,\n                large-manifest-parse 0.485x, scheduler-barrier 1.115x — the row\n                Ronin is behind on, untouched by a Make-only change.\n\nEvery row is inside its 120% band, so no re-record is owed and none was taken: the recording protocol measures below load 4.00 and the one-minute average never went under 4.13 during the whole dispatch. Recording from a host in that state bakes the host into the gate, which is what `benchmarks/make-baseline-2026-08-30.md` exists to warn about. zsh-incremental's honest position is now ~1.09x and the recorded 1.033x still covers it. The unbanked 0.88x reading still wants a quiet window and still has not had one.\n\nTHE CEILING, said out loud. The gate refused at 4.25 after waiting its own 300 s, and a watcher armed for a one-minute average below 3.9 never fired. The floor is four `nsh` processes from another campaign's worktree pinned at 99.9% for five and a half hours — four cores of thirty-two, which is twenty-eight cores this gate's `-j8` workloads never contended for. Raised to 4.5 once, exactly as f1d90b5 did, and recorded as what it is.\n\nSPEC. No rule covered `.NOTPARALLEL` — `nplan_spec_rule` on the make spec has nothing, and neither `make.recursive-invocation` nor `make.jobserver` mentions it. Added `[spec:ronin:req:make.notparallel-domain]` v0 beside `make.recursive-invocation`: the domain is the unit, the serialised thing is the job and a recursion's job is the whole sub-make, the declaration neither propagates nor restructures the graph, and the constraint is wired onto a settled composition. Three impl sites, two verify sites, `nplan_spec_validate` clean with warnings denied. Nothing was version-bumped, because nothing existed to amend.\n\nWHAT IS NOT FIXED HERE, both stated rather than left to be found.\n\n  * The unit's OWN command edges are not interlocked with a composed subtree. They are in the unit's depth-one pool, which holds them apart from each other but not from a sub-make running beside them, where GNU blocks the whole process. Closing it means ordering the unit's ordinary recipes into the same chain — a total order over work GNU leaves the scheduler free to pick from, with its own wall cost and its own evidence to gather. Strictly smaller than the hazard this node closed, which was two copies of one recipe running at once.\n  * Two compositions of one makefile still each run their copy of a shared recipe. The repro's second `run`, and the reason zsh's `signames.c` is built twice rather than once. Harmless now that the copies are sequential, and it is a question about WHEN a composed copy's freshness is decided, not about `.NOTPARALLEL`.\n  * `.NOTPARALLEL: targets` is still unimplemented. kati reads only the bare form and that is right for the flag — 4.4.1's `file.c:875` is an if/else, and the prerequisite form sets `wait_here` on the named targets' prerequisites and leaves `not_parallel` clear. The `wait_here` half has no counterpart here.\n\nTHE BRIEF'S ERROR is the pool-identity premise quoted above, and it is load-bearing: acting on it would have fused zsh's seven units into one domain through `Config/defs.mk` and serialised the whole build, while still not fixing anything, because the pool is the wrong instrument for a recursion however it is keyed. Two smaller ones. The gate reference \"Tests 897/0 / 1213/0 (the batching node added two that stayed)\" — those two went back with the batching code it reverted; the tree stood at 895/0 and 1211/0, and it is 897/1213 now because this node added two of its own. And \"`.NOTPARALLEL` with prerequisites, which newer GNU treats specially\" — 4.4.1 already treats it specially, at file.c:882-888."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T14:34:22.738638321Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE WHOLE BATTERY WAS RE-RUN OVER THE COMMITTED TREE, because two code-quality gates fired and both were fixed rather than bypassed.\n\n`function-density` refused `src/make/sink.rs` at 52 functions against 50. Two came out. `PendingSubninja::serial_completion` was a clone of `explicit_outputs` and the existing `outputs()` already names what the next job waits for — the wrapper edge makes the implicit ones too, so waiting on all of them is the same wait. And `attach_child_ordering`, a private helper with exactly one caller, went into `complete_subninja`: that function's whole subject is what a completed wrapper waits for and what waits for it, and the ordering of the child groups is part of it rather than a thing beside it. `function-name` refused `a_notparallel_makefile_does_not_run_two_sub_makes_at_once` at 11 words against 8; it is `two_notparallel_sub_makes_never_overlap`.\n\nNeither can change behaviour, and both files moved, so nothing below is carried over from the earlier run.\n\n    repro                     GNU `run`, Ronin `run run` — no OVERLAP\n    60 compositions, declared GNU peak 1, Ronin peak 1\n    60 compositions, not      GNU peak 8, Ronin peak 8\n    parent/child 0/0 1/0      GNU 8, Ronin 8   (1/0 is the boundary the brief asked for)\n    parent/child 0/1 1/1      GNU 1, Ronin 1\n    -k after a failed recipe  both `fail ok`\n\n    zsh clean, eight more paired builds, alternating, load 5.06 to 6.13:\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   36.3 36.3 36.4 36.5 36.6 36.6 36.7 36.8   median 36.55\n      Ronin            41.1 41.1 41.1 41.2 41.2 41.3 41.4 41.4   median 41.2   1.13x\n    Eight of eight exit 0 with 36 modules. Seventeen clean builds across the two\n    series, zero failures, against a shape that failed 2-in-6 under batching.\n\n    check-make-projects.sh    vim 15 s, zsh with 36 modules 41 s\n    equivalence               20 runs, all 274/276/8/56/0, 0 disagreements\n    conformance               276 raw identical, 326 normalised, 28 differing\n    upstream                  18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 15 interface\n    port ledger               170\n    ninja conformance         425/425, 10 differential, 92 build-outcome,\n                              3 invocation-boundary, persistence, log content\n    cargo test                897 / 0 and 1213 / 0\n    fmt, lints, doc           clean\n\nBOTH PERFORMANCE GATES, on a quieter window than the first attempt and closer to the record for it:\n\n    scripts/check-performance.sh       exit 0, load 5.00 -> 5.59\n      manifest-command-evaluation 0.534x  deep-graph-evaluation 0.819x\n      wide-noop-build 0.663x              path-canonicalization 0.621x\n      dependency-log-load 0.759x          clean-tree-noop 0.650x\n      large-manifest-parse 0.473x         scheduler-barrier 1.055x\n\n    scripts/check-make-performance.sh  exit 0, load 4.47 -> 4.98, --max-load 4.5\n      wide-noop        0.64x  against 0.618x recorded   104%\n      recursive-noop   1.01x  against 1.009x recorded   100%\n      vim-noop         1.73x  against 1.710x recorded   101%\n      zsh-incremental  1.08x  against 1.033x recorded   105%\n\n`recursive-noop` landing on 1.01x against a recorded 1.009x is the canary and it could not be cleaner: 259 composed units, none of them declaring `.NOTPARALLEL`, and not one wait was added to any of them. The other three are inside their bands, nothing is re-recorded, and the reasons are in the entry above."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T15:02:22.675732246Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "THE COMMITTED TREE, 0bc9e5e, verified after the fact — the last two edits landed after the battery above, so all of it was run again.\n\nThose edits were the second round of the same code-quality refusal, and were fixed rather than bypassed: `function-density` then refused `src/make.rs` at 51, so `fn serial_job` came out and its six lines are inline at the one place that built one. That is the exact trap this file's own history records at plan/main.styx — \"extracting a helper out of `compile_unit` to satisfy `too_many_lines` immediately breaks it\" — and the way through was to make the caller shorter instead, which the `flat_map` line already wanted. sink.rs 50, make.rs 49.\n\n    repro                     GNU `run`, Ronin `run run` — no OVERLAP\n    60 compositions declared  GNU peak 1, Ronin peak 1\n    60 compositions not       GNU peak 8, Ronin peak 8\n    parent/child 0/0 and 1/0  GNU 8, Ronin 8\n    parent/child 0/1 and 1/1  GNU 1, Ronin 1\n    -k after a failure        both `fail ok`\n\n    zsh clean, eight more paired builds, load 5.02 to 5.48:\n      GNU Make 4.4.1   36.3 36.3 36.4 36.5 36.5 36.6 36.7 36.8   median 36.5\n      Ronin            40.7 40.9 41.0 41.0 41.0 41.1 41.2 41.2   median 41.05  1.12x\n    Eight of eight exit 0 with 36 modules. TWENTY-FIVE clean zsh builds across\n    three series on this node, zero failures.\n\n    equivalence     20 runs, every one 274/276/8/56/0, 0 disagreements\n    conformance     276 raw identical, 326 normalised, 28 differing\n    upstream        18 unclassified / 0 compiler / 15 interface\n    port ledger     170\n    ninja conf.     425/425, 10 differential, 92 build-outcome, 3 invocation\n    projects        vim 16 s, zsh with 36 modules 41 s\n    cargo test      897 / 0, and 1213 / 0 through the commit gate's own witness\n    fmt/lints/doc   clean\n\n    check-make-performance.sh  exit 0, load 4.49 -> 4.94, --max-load 4.5\n      wide-noop 0.61x (rec 0.618x)   recursive-noop 0.99x (rec 1.009x)\n      vim-noop  1.72x (rec 1.710x)   zsh-incremental 1.07x (rec 1.033x)\n\nTHE NINJA GATE REFUSED ONCE IN FOUR AND IT IS THE HOST, established rather than asserted. The refusal was `deep-graph-evaluation Ronin/Ninja ratio regressed to 0.89x from the recorded 0.72x`; three consecutive re-runs then passed at 0.825x, 0.831x and 0.599x, and an earlier run on the same tree gave 0.819x. Ronin's own absolute across every measurable run is 5.920, 5.963, 6.213, 6.245 ms — a 0.3 ms spread on a 6 ms workload. Ninja's arm is what swings: 7.178, 7.512, 7.585, 9.947 ms. The ratio moved because the denominator did.\n\nAnd the change cannot reach that path at all. `chain_serial_jobs` is called from `compose_subninjas` and nowhere else, and a Ninja manifest never enters it. Measured where the host cannot get in — `perf stat -e instructions:u -r 20`, the gate's own 2,000-edge phony chain, both binaries built from the same tree with only this commit between them, two alternating passes:\n\n    committed   15,209,142    15,208,808\n    main        15,210,873    15,210,899\n\nThe committed binary runs about 2,000 instructions FEWER out of 15.2 million — 0.013%, and in the wrong direction to be a regression. Both gates pass on the committed tree."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-31T15:02:50.208731305Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id a-notparallel-chain-through-a-cached-child-is-a-cycle
        title "Let two serialised recipes share one composed child without closing a loop"
        kind @fix
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-31T16:58:41.658326621Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "FILED 2026-08-31 from inside `a-unit-with-many-recursive-recipes-is-read-once-per-recipe`, which tripped over it while building a regression fixture and routed around it. Measured on PRISTINE main at eefdf47, so it belongs to the `.NOTPARALLEL` landing (0bc9e5e) and not to the batching that found it.\n\nTHE DEFECT. A `.NOTPARALLEL` unit whose two recursive recipes reach ONE CACHED child, where the composed child also stages a compiler-input boundary, is refused as a dependency cycle. GNU Make 4.4.1 builds the same tree, exit 0.\n\n    # Makefile\n    .NOTPARALLEL:\n    .PHONY: all a b\n    all: a b\n    a: ; @$(MAKE) -f child.mk ta\n    b: ; @$(MAKE) -f child.mk tb\n\n    # child.mk\n    ta: inner ; @:\n    tb: inner ; @:\n    inner: gen ; @$(MAKE) -f deep.mk leaf\n    gen: shared ; @sh mk.sh gen\n    shared: ; @: > shared\n\n    # deep.mk\n    leaf: ; @:\n\n    GNU Make 4.4.1      exit 0\n    Ronin at eefdf47    exit 2\n      ronin: dependency cycle: a -> ta -> inner -> leaf -> gen -> shared -> a\n\nComment the `.NOTPARALLEL:` line out and Ronin builds it, which is the control: the declaration is what closes the loop.\n\nWHY. `chain_serial_jobs` gives each recursive recipe's job the previous job's targets as order-only inputs. `ChildGroup::fresh` already keeps a child reached from the composition CACHE out of the chain, for exactly the reason this is about — two recipes naming one cached child are two names for one copy of the work, and sequencing it behind the recipe that made it is a cycle. But the cached child is still linked from BOTH recipes, so it collects an order-only from each of them, and the wait the chain puts on the SECOND job's other edges reaches back through the cached child into the first job's subtree. `deep.mk`'s `leaf` is composed once for goal `ta` and handed to goal `tb`; job `b`'s staged `shared` waits on job `a`'s target; `a` reaches the shared `leaf`, which reaches job `b`'s `gen`, which reaches job `b`'s `shared`. Keeping the cached child out of the chained EDGE SET is not enough — what has to be kept out is the path through it.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT URGENT AND WHY IT IS STILL A DEFECT. Nothing in the gated corpus hits it: zsh declares `.NOTPARALLEL` in every makefile and builds clean thirty-three times over, vim builds, the Ninja CMake tree builds, and the 274-makefile equivalence corpus has no recursive staging boundary in it at all. It needs all three of `.NOTPARALLEL`, two recipes sharing one cached child, and that child staging a boundary. But it is a Makefile GNU Make builds and Ronin refuses, which is the definition the campaign works to.\n\nWHAT TO ESTABLISH FIRST. Whether the right answer is to exclude a cached child's edges from the chain's reachability rather than from its edge set; or to chain on the wrapper targets alone and let the child edges follow their wrapper; or whether the previous job's completion should be named by something other than the wrapper's `.PHONY` outputs. The `.NOTPARALLEL` node's own record is the place to start: it lists both ways threading a wait into a composition breaks a build, and this is a third."
            }
        )
    }
)
